<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) &#58;-), expressing happiness or amusement.</li></ol><p>Evolved from &#58;-). In the Russian keyboard layout, one has to press Shift+6 to type a colon, which is deemed inconvenient, particularly in instant messaging, as it requires both hands, resulting in the decline of &#58;-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging,&#32;humorous) LOL, an expression of laughter or amusement.</li></ol><p>Evolved from ), ultimately from &#58;-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Noun-forming suffix used to form diminutives of proper names&#59; used after a hard (unpalatalized) consonant.</li><li>Noun-forming suffix used to form abstract nouns and agent nouns from prefixed stems ending in a consonant that becomes a hushing consonant (ш ж ч щ) when iotated. The final consonant of the stem is iotated before this suffix.</li><li>Noun-forming suffix used to form abstract nouns and agent nouns from prefixed stems. The final consonant of the stem is not iotated before this suffix.</li></ol><p>From early Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-able, -ible, -uble, -ed (<i>present participle passive</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-age (used to form nouns indicating a measurement in specific units, a process or action, or a collective term)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>-age</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-я́к</i> (-ják) (<i>suffix usually denoting a person with a given characteristic, or an inanimate noun of various meanings; often of a colloquial nature</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>added to adjectives</i>)</li><li>(<i>added to nouns</i>)</li><li>(<i>added to verbs</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-акъ</i> (-akŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-akъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from verbs, especially those in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) added to a noun stem, produces a masculine noun with emotional colouring&#47;coloring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs&#59; added especially to verbs ending in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-я́нин, -яни́н</i> (-jánin, -janín)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka) (<i>used after hushing consonants (ш ж ч щ) and ц</i>)</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ка</i> (-ka) added to adjectives ending in <i>-аный</i> (-anyj) or <i>-ано́й</i> (-anój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form past passive participles from verbs in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ), which are frequently lexicalized into adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój) used after a hushing consonant (<i>ш</i> (š), <i>ж</i> (ž), <i>ч</i> (č), <i>щ</i> (šč)) or <i>ц</i> (c)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to create semelfactive verbs</li></ol><p>An expressive addition to <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form passive-participle-like adjectives from verbs in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</li></ol><p>Compare the past-passive-participle suffix <i>-анный</i> (-annyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ание</i> (-anije): -tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs&#59; added especially to nouns ending in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns, verbs and adjectives to form masculine agent nouns, sometimes inanimate: -er, -or</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ařь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to nouns (especially body parts), indicating having the property of that noun, particularly to a large extent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-atъ</i> / <i>&#42;-astъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form agent nouns (both animate and inanimate) from verbs in <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ) and <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ): -ator, -er</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>-ātor</i>, from <i>-tor</i> added to verbs in <i>-āre</i>. Influenced by French <i>-ateur</i>, itself a borrowing from the same Latin suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives from nouns, meaning "having X".</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-атъ</i> (-atŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-atъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive suffix for many verbs. Usually indicates an imperfective verb.</li><li>Used in place of <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) after the hushing sibilants ш щ ч ж and after жд, when used to derive imperfective verbs from (normally but not always prefixed) perfective class 4 verbs (in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)) following one of т д с з ш ж ч щ. Always stressed. Triggers iotation of the final stem consonant, but it is unpredictable whether the iotation of т results in ч or щ and whether the iotation of д results in ж or жд. Very occasionally found with class 5 verbs in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ).</li><li>Used to derive imperfective verbs from certain classes of prefixed perfective verbs, where the stem ends in a non-palatal consonant, e.g. class 3 (<i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)), class 6 (some <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ), particularly where the present tense does not end in <i>-аю</i> (-aju)), class 7 (<i>-ть</i> (-tʹ) after a consonant and <i>-ти</i> (-ti)), class 8 (<i>-чь</i> (-čʹ)). Always stressed (which may be the only difference between the perfective and imperfective forms, esp. for class 6 perfectives). It is added to the underlying stem, minus the infinitive suffix. In some cases, the underlying stem is not apparent in the infinitive. In such a case, the stem may appear in the present tense (in class 7 verbs such as <i>напа́сть</i> (napástʹ), underlying stem напа́д- visible in present 1st singular <i>нападу́</i> (napadú), or in class 8 verbs such as <i>отсе́чь</i> (otséčʹ), underlying stem отсе́к- visible in present 1st singular <i>отсеку́</i> (otsekú)) or may not (in class 3 verbs in -нуть, e.g. <i>поки́нуть</i> (pokínutʹ), underlying stem поки́д-).</li><li>Occasionally, irregularly used to derive imperfective verbs from (normally but not always prefixed) perfective class 4 (<i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)) and class 5 (some <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)) verbs where the stem ends in a paired palatal consonant. Always stressed. Does not trigger iotation.</li><li>Used in place of <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ) after the hushing sibilants ш щ ч ж, when used to derive class-5 imperfective verbs from nouns, especially onomatopoeic verbs. Probably no longer productive.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ati</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ati</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;-ěti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ēˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₁-</i> (stative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ation</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>-ātiō</i>, an alternative form of <i>-tiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Usually denoting occupation or agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing, -ent, -ive <i>(present participle active: "which is...-ing")</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing, -ent, -ive (<i>active reflexive present participle: "which is...-ing"</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a noun forming suffix</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) -maniac, -mane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-бѣ́съ</i> (-bě́sŭ), calque of Ancient Greek <i>-μανής</i> (-manḗs) and <i>-μαργος</i> (-margos). Cognate with <i>беси́ться</i> (besítʹsja, “to rage”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) -mania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-бѣ́сїе</i> (-bě́sije), calque of Ancient Greek <i>-μανία</i> (-manía) and -<i>μάργος</i> (márgos). Cognate with <i>беси́ться</i> (besítʹsja, “to rage”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbs and adjectives to form nouns indicating locations (relatively uncommon suffix).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbs to form nouns indicating locations.</li></ol><p>Generalized from nouns ending in <i>-ба́</i> (-bá) to which <i>-ище</i> (-išče) had been added. Compare <i>стрельба́</i> (strelʹbá, “shooting”) + <i>-ище</i> (-išče) → <i>стре́льбище</i> (strélʹbišče, “shooting range”), later reinterpreted as <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ) + <i>-бище</i> (-bišče); similarly with <i>мо́льбище</i> (mólʹbišče, “prayer ground”), originally derived from <i>мольба́</i> (molʹbá, “entreaty, prayer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to derive imperfective verbs from perfective verbs whose unprefixed stem consists of a single syllable ending in a vowel. Also sometimes formed from class-1 verbs in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), which can also form imperfectives using <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Affixed to the end of numerals to mean "in or on &#43; (date)"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) -hedron; forms the names of three-dimensional solids</li></ol><p>From <i>грань</i> (granʹ, “facet; side; face”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, object suffix), when combined with a numerical prefix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used to indicate a shift in the speaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to stems to show that it does something; a doer.</li></ol><p>Combining form of де́ять (déjatʹ, “to do”), Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѣꙗти</i> (dějati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dějati</i>, an extension of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děti</i> (“to do, to say”) (whence <i>деть</i> (detʹ)), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dḗˀtei</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to do”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-о</i> (-o): Suffix added to adjectives to produce adverbs&#59; highly productive&#59; used in place of <i>-о</i> (-o) after palatal consonants, hushing consonants (ч ш щ ж) and ц</li><li>Used to form comparatives from adjectives and adverbs ending in a velar, i.e. х г к.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming adjectives from nouns.</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of words such as <i>сва́дебный</i> (svádebnyj), where -еб- is part of the radical (<i>сва́дьба</i> (svádʹba) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>'s. (<i>possessive suffix</i>)</li><li>-son, -s. (<i>suffix used in many last names</i>)</li><li>s’, of <i>(genitive <b>plural</b> ending for some masculine nouns)</i>.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-evъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ish, rather, a bit too (attenuates adjectives)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form verbs from nouns and adjectives whose stem ends in a palatal consonant, a hushing consonant (ш ж ч щ) or ц.</li><li>Used to form imperfective verbs from prefixed class-4 perfective verbs based on a non-syllabic root&#59; variant of <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-овик</i> (-ovik) (<i>used after palatalized or formerly palatalized consonants</i>)</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ик</i> (-ik) added to adjectives ending in <i>-евый</i> (-evyj) or <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-овина</i> (-ovina) (<i>used after hushing consonants (ш ж ч щ) and ц</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A masculine patronymic suffix added to a given name ending in a soft (palatalized) consonant&#58; son of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja) / <i>-овня́</i> (-ovnjá)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja) / <i>-овня́</i> (-ovnjá) after soft consonants and ш ж ч щ ц.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form adjectives from nouns, often relational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival suffix forming nouns meaning "related to X"&#59; variant of <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffixed used to form adjectives from nouns, often specifically relational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to stems to show that it eats something; an eater.</li></ol><p>From есть (jestʹ, “to eat”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěsti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ēˀstei</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁édti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; used to denote a greater degree, the comparative.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; used to denote a greater degree, the superlative.</li><li>-ate; characterized by the specified thing.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>added to certain adjectives and adverbs</i>) More; used to form the comparative.</li></ol><p>A combination of a euphonic Proto-Slavic yat (ѣ (jě), inherited from &#42;ě) and -ший (-šij) (itself from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-jьjь</i> (“<i>comparative suffix</i>”)). This suffix is used with adjectives that used to have a rising tone in the root syllable in Proto-Slavic, whereas the simpler form -ший (-šij) was used with adjectives that used to have a falling tone in the root syllable in Proto-Slavic; as tone has been lost in Russian, such a rule has become muddier.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ок</i> (-ok) (<i>after the unpaired sibilants ш ж ч щ ц and unpaired palatal й</i>)</li></ol><p>Regular phonological variant of <i>-ок</i> (-ok) after unpaired palatal or originally palatal sounds.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Unproductive suffix forming action nouns</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ělь</i>. Compare <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to verbs, adjectives, proper nouns, etc. to form an agent noun.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs, and occasionally from other parts of speech)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-nьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form past passive participles from verbs in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) and <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), which are frequently lexicalized into adjectives</li><li>used to form adjectives from nouns, with the meaning "having the property of X"</li><li>used to form adjectives from other adjectives, with the colloquial meaning "very X"</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-еньнъ</i> (-enĭnŭ), from <i>енъ</i> (enŭ) + <i>ьнъ</i> (ĭnŭ). Originally used to form adjectives from past passive participles in Old Church Slavonic, later reinterpreted as a past passive participle suffix within the Russian literary language. Compare the native past participle suffix <i>-еный</i> (-enyj), which developed into an adjective suffix. For the same development compare <i>-ячий</i> (-jačij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form passive-participle-like adjectives from verbs that normally form past passive participles in <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</li></ol><p>Compare the past-passive-participle suffix <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ение</i> (-enije): -tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs&#59; added especially to verbs ending in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), and certain other endings, but not <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-онька</i> (-onʹka) (<i>used after soft consonants, the hushing consonants ш ж ч щ as well as ц</i>): suffix forming diminutive, often endearing, nouns from other nouns and adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to form diminutive adjectives from other adjectives&#59; often the diminutive meaning is lost, or an intensifying meaning is imparted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to form (often colloquial) diminutive adverbs from other adverbs&#59; often, the diminutive meaning is lost, or an intensifying meaning is imparted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly, -ally (adverbial suffix&#59; the adverb form of -еский)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns or sometimes other adjectives, often relational (meaning "related to X")&#59; a variant of <i>-ский</i> (-skij): -ian, -ic, -ish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ьскъ</i> (-ĭskŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>. Doublet of <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns and adjectives to create a neuter noun, usually an abstract noun denoting a relation, social status, scientific discipline, quality or state: -hood, -ness, -ity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>-ество</i> (-estvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ness, -ity (<i>used to form abstract nouns from adjectives</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive suffix for some verbs&#59; often indicates an intransitive or perfective verb. Becomes <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) after hushing consonants (ж ч ш щ) or ц. Becomes <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ) after vowels. Forms both class-5 and class-1 verbs. Probably not productive in class-5 verbs except possibly for onomatopoeic verbs.</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;-ěti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ēˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₁-</i> (stative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er, -an, -ian <i>(nominal suffix for agent nouns or nouns designating persons)</i>.</li><li>Diminutive suffix.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-очка</i> (-očka) (<i>after the unpaired sibilants ш ж ч щ ц and unpaired palatal й</i>)</li></ol><p>Regular phonological variant of <i>-очка</i> (-očka) after unpaired palatal or originally palatal sounds, or from <i>-ек</i> (-ek) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); in both cases, originally a double diminutive. Related to -чка (-čka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ling (forming diminutive nouns, usually endearing)</li><li>Forms diminutive adverbs, usually endearing.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing, -ent, -ive <i>(present participle active: "which is...-ing")</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>times</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Used to denote frequency.</li><li>multiplied by</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-жды</i> (-ždy), from Old Church Slavonic <i>шьды</i> (šĭdy), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьdъ</i>. Compare with Russian шедший (šedšij) and шедши (šedši), forms of идти (idti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to verbs to create nouns denoting act, state</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-znь</i>. The spelling in the nominative case may have been influenced by Old Church Slavonic <i>-знь</i> (-znĭ), as <i>-зень</i> would probably be expected, compare Czech <i>bázeň</i>, Polish <i>bojaźń</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>бојазан</i>, Slovene <i>bojazen</i> and Bulgarian <i>боя́зън</i> (bojázǎn) exhibiting <i>-zьnь</i> while deriving from <i>&#42;bojaznь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form imperfective verbs from prefixed perfective verbs in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) and sometimes <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), with iotation of the final consonant.</li><li>Used as a spelling variant in place of -ывать (-yvatʹ) to form imperfective verbs from prefixed perfective verbs in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ), <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) and sometimes <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) with root-final velars (к г х) and hushing consonants (ш ж ч щ), without iotation of the final consonant.</li><li>Used to form frequentative verbs from non-prefixed verbs in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) and sometimes <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), with iotation of the final consonant.</li><li>Used as a spelling variant in place of -ывать (-yvatʹ) to form frequentative verbs from non-prefixed verbs in <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ), <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) and sometimes <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) with root-final velars (к г х) and hushing consonants (ш ж ч щ), without iotation of the final consonant.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ous, etc. (Used to form adjectives from nouns and verbs, with the meaning "having the characteristic of X".)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) -id; used to form names of an individual organism of a taxonomic family.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>-ida</i> (“member of a taxonomic family”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Abstract noun suffix&#59; forms part of larger suffixes such as <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije), <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije), <i>-тие</i> (-tije), and exists on its own especially as a suffix added to compound words.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ие</i> (-ije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ize</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>-isieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>-isme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a noun forming suffix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms relational and possessive adjectives from nouns describing animals and people: -'s, -ine, -ian</li><li>-ed</li><li>forms adjectives from verbs, verbal nouns and compounds: -ous, -al, etc.</li><li>forms adjectives and adjectival nouns from some nouns referring to people</li><li>forms names of chemical elements, especially metallic ones: -ium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ии</i> (-ii), further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьjь</i>, whose native descendant <i>&#42;-ей</i> (&#42;-jej) was displaced (compare <i>и́ней</i> (ínej) [ˈinʲɪj] from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьnьjь</i> and <i>пыре́й</i> (pyréj) [pɨˈrʲej] from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pyrьjь</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ыи</i> (-yi), displaced expected <i>-ой</i> (-oj) in unstressed positions, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъ</i> + <i>&#42;jь</i>, doublet of <i>-о́й</i> (-ój).</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ии</i> (-ii), displaced native <i>&#42;-ей</i> (&#42;-jej) (compare <i>и́ней</i> (ínej) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьnьjь</i>), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-jьjь</i>.</p><p>Reanalysis of Latin and New Latin terms suffixed with Latin <i>-um</i> or <i>-ium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly, -cally, -ishly (<i>adverbial suffix</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival suffix forming nouns meaning "related to X"&#59; variant of <i>-ский</i> (-skij): -ian, -an, -ish</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from <i>-ский</i> (-skij) added to nouns ending in stressed <i>-и́я</i> (-íja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to normally masculine nouns to form diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning.</li><li>Suffix added to normally masculine names to form diminutives, often shortening the name in the process.</li><li>Suffix appended to words, especially adjectives, to create masculine agent nouns, usually denoting a profession, performer, place, object, tool or a feature: -er, -or, -ant, -ic, -ist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to words to create a feminine noun, usually denoting a berry plant.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-́лище</i> (-́lišče)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from verbs, especially those in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the present passive participle of imperfective conjugation-II verbs, i.e. class 4 verbs (ending in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)) and class 5 verbs (ending in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ), <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)), but not including class 1, class 2 or other verbs in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ), or <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</li><li>-able, -ible (added to both imperfective and perfective conjugation-II verbs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-'s (<i>possessive suffix</i>)</li><li>Forms surnames</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-инъ</i> (-inŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-inъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns to form concrete nouns having the quality of the original noun</li><li>-age; suffix appended to verbs to form a noun denoting a result</li><li>suffix appended to mass nouns to form a noun denoting an individual item</li><li>suffix appended to nouns or adjectives to form a noun denoting a kind of meat</li><li>suffix forming an augmentative, usually informal</li><li>-ness, -th; suffix appended to adjectives to form a (usually abstract) noun denoting a state or quality</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-an, -ian, -er; nominal suffix for nouns designating persons&#59; variant of <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival suffix forming nouns meaning "related to X"&#59; variant of <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns and adjectives to create a neuter abstract noun denoting a quality or state: -ity, -hood, -ness</li></ol><p>Expanded form of <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to nouns denoting animals, typically forming relational adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ыня</i> (-ynja)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-yni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a productive verbal suffix, also used to create new verbs, especially from loanwords</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>-ieren</i> (ultimately from Latin <i>-āre</i>) + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) (or <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) in its final-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to nouns, indicating having the property of that noun</li><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to verbs, indicating being in the state of that verb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-itъ</i> / <i>&#42;-istъ</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>-ysty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) -itis</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) -ite</li><li>-ite (follower or adherent)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>-ite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>the infinitive suffix for many verbs&#59; usually indicates a perfective verb</li><li>a suffix used to form factitive verbs from nouns, i.e. verbs having the meaning "to make X(er)" for X an adjective or noun&#59; usually used with a prefix such as <i>о-</i> (o-), <i>об-</i> (ob-) or <i>у-</i> (u-)</li><li>A suffix used to form negative factitive verbs from <i>без-</i> (bez-, “without, -less”) (or <i>бес-</i> (bes-)) &#43; a noun, i.e. verbs having the meaning "to deprive of X" for X a noun. Usually used with <i>о-</i> (o-) and commonly corresponding to English verbs in <i>de-</i> or <i>dis-</i>.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-iti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ify</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns referring to feminine animals.</li><li>Forms nouns referring to feminine people, often of a colloquial nature.</li><li>Forms nouns referring to objects, of a decidedly colloquial and often pejorative nature.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms diminutives of generally feminine nouns</li><li>forms abstract nouns from adjectives</li><li>forms writing system names from nouns and adjectives</li><li>forms abstract nouns from prefixes &#43; nouns</li><li>forms nouns referring to plants, animals, tools or other objects from nouns and adjectives</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A masculine patronymic suffix added to a given name ending in a soft (palatalized) consonant&#58; son of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ичь</i> (-ičĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-iťь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns or sometimes other adjectives, often relational (meaning "related to X"). Often equivalent to -al, -ic, or -ical.</li></ol><p><i>-и́к</i> (-ík) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(added to (normally feminine) nouns, forms diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning)</li></ol><p>Originally a double diminutive, from <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare <i>води́чка</i> (vodíčka) with synonymous <i>води́ца</i> (vodíca); <i>части́чка</i> (častíčka), diminutive of <i>части́ца</i> (častíca, “fraction, particle”), originally itself a diminutive of <i>часть</i> (častʹ, “part, piece”); <i>пти́чка</i> (ptíčka), diminutive of <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca, “bird”), etymologically a diminutive; etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffixed used to form adjectives from nouns.</li></ol><p><i>-и́к</i> (-ík) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to normally masculine nouns, forms diminutives, often with a pejorative meaning.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Аttached to nouns to form augmentatives.</li><li>Attached to nouns and verbs to form neuter nouns indicating locations.</li><li>Attached to verbs to form neuter, often colloquial, nouns indicating objects having the quality of the verb.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-išče</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ion (frequently found as part of foreign borrowings, where it represents <i>-ion</i>, especially as part of <i>-ция</i> (-cija, “-tion”))</li><li>Suffix used to create abstract nouns from nouns denoting persons.</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>-ia</i> or Ancient Greek <i>-ία</i> (-ía), <i>-εία</i> (-eía); found only in words borrowed from other languages (compare German, French <i>-ie</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>just, (now) would you? (<i>appended to an imperative verb to soften the command somewhat</i>)</li><li>now, (real) quick, just (<i>appended to an imperative verb to express urgency or a pressing request</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(added to (normally feminine) nouns, forms diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning)</li><li>(added to nouns denoting classes of people, forms the feminine equivalent&#59; replaces the suffix <i>-ец</i> (-ec) if present)</li><li>(added to verbs, forms abstract nouns or nouns indicating the result of the action)</li><li>(added to verbs, forms instruments or tools)</li><li>(added to adjectives or numerals, forms colloquial nouns with the meaning of a carrier of the adjective's or numeral's properties)</li></ol><p>From a conflation of two suffixes Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьka</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъka</i>. The former always came to be used after velars (hence the occurrence of the Slavic first palatalization), while the latter always came to be used after -л- (hence the lack of palatalization of -л- before -ка).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to create verbs denoting onomatopoeic sounds or an expressive action.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-kati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ous: Used to form qualitative adjectives from verbs and nouns, with the meaning "having the characteristic of X".</li><li>Used to form primary qualitative adjectives from adjectival roots, which often occur without the suffix in related words.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьkъ</i>, <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>, <i>&#42;-kъ</i>?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates diminutives of normally neuter nouns and adverbs, normally having a colloquial register.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(anime and manga fandom,&#32;fandom slang) appended to a young man's name or nickname to indicate familiarity.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese くん (<i>kun</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to verbs or occasionally nouns to create a colloquial or slang animate agentive noun.</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-year-old (suffix used to indicate the age of something or someone, in terms of years)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>some-, any- (indefinite particle used in pronouns)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ous, etc. (Used to form adjectives from nouns and verbs, with the meaning "having the characteristic of X".)</li></ol><p>Probably rebracketed from <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj) following roots ending in л. Cognate with Belarusian <i>-лі́вы</i> (-lívy). See also <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj) (presumably rebracketed from roots ending in к or ч).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form neuter nouns from verbs, usually denoting a place and sometimes having an archaic or literary feel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-лище</i> (-lište). Equivalent to <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er (forms nouns denoting objects, generally tools or instruments, from verbs)</li><li>(forms nouns denoting places, generally of a colloquial nature, from verbs)</li><li>(forms nouns, denoting women performing an occupation, from verbs)</li></ol><p>In the meaning of a tool or instrument, may originate from a combination of the Proto-Slavic tool suffix <i>&#42;-dlo</i> (Russian <i>-ло</i> (-lo), e.g. <i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo, “soap”), <i>ра́ло</i> (rálo, “plow”), <i>ши́ло</i> (šílo, “awl”)) + the diminutive <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to verbs to create a neuter noun, normally denoting a tool.</li><li>Suffix appended to verbs or sometimes other words to create a colloquial or slang neuter noun with various meanings.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-dlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-logist</li><li>-logue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos). Similar to Ukrainian <i>-лог</i> (-loh) and Belarusian <i>-ляг</i> (-ljah)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-logy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives, usually qualitative, from verbs and nouns.</li><li>Suffix used to form primary qualitative adjectives from adjectival roots, which may occur without the suffix in related words.</li><li>Suffixed used to form adjectives from (normally intransitive) verbs with the meaning "having X'ed".</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-lъ</i>. Historically identical to the past tense suffix -л (-l).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Unproductive suffix forming action nouns</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-lь</i>, from <i>&#42;-lъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ь</i>. Compare <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ), <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to verbal stems to create masculine nouns, animate or inanimate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form nouns referring to places from verbs.</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from <i>-ня</i> (-nja) attached to stems ending in л; compare <i>маслоде́льня</i> (maslodélʹnja, “creamery”) from <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo, “butter”) + <i>-о-</i> (-o-) + <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ, “to make”) + <i>-ня</i> (-nja), and similarly <i>мукомо́льня</i> (mukomólʹnja, “flour mill”) (based on <i>мука́</i> (muká, “flour”) and <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ, “to mill”)) and <i>сукнова́льня</i> (suknoválʹnja, “fullery”) (based on <i>сукно́</i> (suknó, “broadcloth”) and <i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ, “to roll”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er (forms nouns denoting persons from verbs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ous, etc. (<i>used to form adjectives from nouns and verbs, with the meaning "having the characteristic of X"</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-teen (&#43;10)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;na desęte</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>на десѧте</i> (na desęte), Polish <i>-naście</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>десять</i> (desjatʹ), literally “on ten”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>any-, some- (as in <i>anything</i>, <i>something</i>); a particle used to form compound indefinite pronouns</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>будь-</i> (budʹ-) or Polish <i>bądź</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form adjectives from nouns, often relational (meaning "related to X")&#59; a less common variant of <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>-ний</i> (-nyj), Polish <i>-ny</i>, Belarusian <i>-ні</i> (-ni), Ukrainian <i>-ній</i> (-nij).</p><p>Soft variant of <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to nominal stems to create a masculine noun, usually denoting a professional, performer, adherent, place, object, tool or a feature.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьnikъ</i>, itself originally by nominalization of adjectives in <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i> with the suffix <i>&#42;-ikъ</i> (whence <i>-ик</i> (-ik)). The suffix originates from the Proto-Balto-Slavic period; compare dialectal Lithuanian <i>lauk-inykas</i> (“peasant, farmer”) (from <i>laũkas</i> (“field”)) and Old Prussian <i>lauk-inikis</i> (“vassal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Productive verbal suffix used to indicate showing a certain behaviour</li></ol><p>Extracted from words such as <i>безде́льничать</i> (bezdélʹničatʹ) and <i>у́мничать</i> (úmničatʹ). By surface analysis, <i>-ник</i> (-nik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ish, -ic, -al, -ed; having a particular quality of, pertaining to, associated with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ьнъ</i> (-ĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming abstract nouns from adjectives. A less productive alternative to <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), except after щ, where it is the regular suffix.</li><li>Suffix forming nouns with the meaning of a group of things characterized by the quality designated by the adjectives from which the nouns are formed.</li><li>Suffix forming nouns of various sorts from miscellaneous parts of speech.</li></ol><p>Probably originated from the suffix <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ) added to words ending in <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), and was then extracted by rebracketing as a productive suffix. The same thing happened with e.g. <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj) and English <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to create primary imperfective intransitive verbs</li><li>suffix used to create derived perfective verbs, most of which are semelfactive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-nǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ish, -ic, -al, -ed; having a particular quality of, pertaining to, associated with&#59; forms adjectives from nouns, and sometimes from verbs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ьныи</i> (-ĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьnъjь</i>. Doublet of <i>-ной</i> (-noj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms nouns denoting the place where an occupation or office is performed</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) forms nouns denoting amassment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>forms nouns denoting material objects taking their shape from a particular application on them</li><li>forms nouns for groups of people</li></ol><li>forms nouns denoting amassment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>forms nouns denoting an over-repetitive action</li><li>forms nouns denoting material objects taking their shape from a particular application on them</li><li>(rude,&#32;derogatory) forms nouns for groups of people</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьňa</i>, from <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix usually denoting a person with a given characteristic, or forming inanimate nouns of various meanings&#59; often of a colloquial nature</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from <i>-я́к</i> (-ják) following adjectives ending in <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare <i>горня́к</i> (gornják, “miner”) from <i>го́рный</i> (górnyj, “mining (<i>relational</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to adjectives to produce adverbs&#59; highly productive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms possessive adjectives</li><li>Forms patronymic surnames</li><li>Genitive plural desinence of most hard-stem masculine nouns</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-овъ</i> (-ovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ovъ</i>.</p><p>From old u-stems, where Old East Slavic -<i>овъ</i> was a combination of the final syllable of the stem and the genitive plural desinence <i>-ъ</i> (-ŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ish, rather, a bit too (attenuates adjectives)</li><li>-y, -like (creates denominal adjectives with qualities of the noun)</li></ol><p>Compounding/rebracketing of <i>-о́вый &#43; -а́тый</i> (-óvyj &#43; -átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives, or foreign verbs.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns to form related nouns of various sorts, both animate and inanimate, generally referring to more specific objects than the base lemma</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ик</i> (-ik) added to adjectives ending in <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) or <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns, adjectives, etc. to form nouns of various sorts, generally referring to more specific objects than the base lemma</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ина</i> (-ina) added to adjectives ending in <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) or <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A masculine patronymic suffix added to a given name ending in a hard (unpalatalized) consonant&#58; son of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns to form related nouns of various sorts, both animate and inanimate, generally referring to more specific objects than the base lemma</li><li>suffix appended to nouns referring to males, to form the corresponding noun referring to females</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ка</i> (-ka) added to adjectives ending in <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) or <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a feminine patronymic suffix added to a given name ending in a hard (unpalatalized) consonant&#58; daughter of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ня</i> (-nja) (<i>in all senses, but especially referring to places</i>)</li></ol><p>Presumably rebracketed from <i>-ня</i> (-nja) attached to stems ending in -ов.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form adjectives from nouns, often relational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-'s, -ian, -ic, -ish (<i>adjectival suffix forming relational or possessive adjectives, i.e. meaning "related to X"</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) or <i>-о́в</i> (-óv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Cognate with Czech <i>-ovský</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-'s, -ian, -ic, -ish (<i>adjectival suffix forming relational or possessive adjectives, i.e. meaning "related to X"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form adjectives from nouns, often relational</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>-овъ</i> (-ovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>masculine nominative singular adjective ending</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъ</i> + <i>&#42;jь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>highly&#32;productive</i>) takes masculine nouns, produces diminutive masculine nouns, sometimes with further sense development</li><li>(highly&#32;productive,&#32;takes given name, produces given name,&#32;diminutive) paired with <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>-ук</i> (-uk), <i>-юк</i> (-juk)</li><li>(productive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;unstressed,&#32;takes verbs, produces nouns) an object that remains after process is done</li><li>(animate,&#32;takes verbs, produces nouns) agent</li><li>(<i>takes verbs, produces nouns</i>) a tool or means of an action</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;takes verbs, produces nouns) subject of an action</li><li>(<i>takes verbs, produces nouns</i>) place of an action</li><li>(productive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;stressed,&#32;takes verbs, produces nouns) one occurrence of an action; separate act</li><li>(<i>takes adjective, produces nouns</i>)</li><li>(with недо-,&#32;productive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns) undeveloped or transitional object</li><li>(<i>takes nouns, produces nouns</i>) paired with <i>-иц</i> (-ic), <i>-ица</i> (-ica), <i>-ак</i> (-ak), <i>-ака</i> (-aka), <i>-як</i> (-jak), <i>-яка</i> (-jaka); a piece of, one of them</li><li>(<i>takes numeral, produces nouns</i>)</li><li>(<i>takes prepositions, produces nouns</i>)</li><li>(unproductive,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns)</li><li>(with за-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns) an object behind something or in the background</li><li>(with за-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns) located behind and look alike</li><li>(with о-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns) adjacent</li><li>(with от-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;takes nouns, produces nouns) separated from, produced by</li><li>(with па-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) alike, but not equal</li><li>(with пере-,&#32;slightly productive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-и</i> (-i); in between, during break (about location or time)</li><li>(with пере-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-и</i> (-i), <i>-ица</i> (-ica); crossing, intersection</li><li>(with по-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns)</li><li>(with под-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) beneath</li><li>(with под-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>-ник</i> (-nik); undeveloped animal or object</li><li>(with пред-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns)</li><li>(with при-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-ник</i> (-nik), <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ); adjacent</li><li>(with при-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-ка</i> (-ka); adjacent</li><li>(with про-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) in between</li><li>(with су-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns)</li><li>(with о- и об-,&#32;unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) paired with <i>-ки</i> (-ki) (plurale tantum), <i>-j</i>, <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</li><li>(unproductive,&#32;produces nouns) overdone, with a negative outcome</li><li>(unproductive,&#32;animate&#32;or&#32;inanimate,&#32;produces nouns) subject with a qualifier in the first stem</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) when added to verbs or other parts of speech, produces masculine nouns related in meaning to the original word.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) when added to nouns, produces stylistically reduced synonyms.</li></ol><p>It has been formed as a suffix since the beginning of the 20th century from <i>опрокидо́н</i> (oprokidón), with simplification from <i>опрокидо́нт</i> (oprokidónt), from <i>опроки́д(ывать)</i> (oprokíd(yvatʹ)) + Ancient Greek <i>-ων</i> (-ōn). It is first found in the works of Nikolai Leskov, Anton Chekhov and Mikhail Saltykov-Shchedrin. In the future, words also appear related to the consumption of alcoholic beverages, for example, <i>выпиво́н</i> (vypivón) and <i>закусо́н</i> (zakusón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka) (<i>particularly after hushing consonants, including those caused by the Slavic first palatalization</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ional, etc. (forms adjectives, especially relational adjectives, from nouns ending in <i>-ия</i> (-ija, “-ion”), especially <i>-ция</i> (-cija, “-tion”))</li></ol><p>Apparently extracted from -ion + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok) (<i>used after the hushing consonants ч ж ш щ</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming diminutive, often endearing, nouns from other nouns and adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to form diminutive adjectives from other adjectives&#59; often the diminutive meaning is lost, or an intensifying meaning is imparted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ness, -ity (<i>used to form abstract nouns from adjectives</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ostь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to adjectives to create a feminine noun, usually an abstract noun denoting a quality.</li><li>Suffix appended to verbs to create a feminine noun, usually an abstract noun denoting a quality.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ota</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ня</i> (-nja) (<i>in all senses, but especially forming abstract nouns; often with a colloquial flavor</i>)</li></ol><p>Presumably rebracketed from <i>-ня</i> (-nja) attached to stems ending in -от.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(added to (normally feminine) nouns, forms diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning)</li><li>(added to nouns and adjectives, forms colloquial nouns referring to women with endearing qualities)</li></ol><p>Originally a double diminutive Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъčьka</i> formed from <i>&#42;-ъka</i> + <i>&#42;-ьka</i>. Related to -чка (-čka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a Russian suffix that was formerly used to emphasize the speaker’s politeness or servility before a person of higher social status, created as an abbreviation of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ)/<i>суда́рыня</i> (sudárynja) (sir, ma'am).</li></ol><p>A contracted form of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ, “sir”), <i>суда́рыня</i> (sudárynja, “ma'am”). Typologically compare English <i>'m</i> (contraction of ma'am).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a polite suffix added to surnames</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese さん (<i>san</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix used in place names.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>, like the adjectival endings <i>-ский</i> (-skij) and <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly, -cally, -ishly (adverbial suffix&#59; the adverb form of -ский)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns or sometimes other adjectives, often relational (meaning "related to X"): -ian, -ic, -ish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ьскъ</i> (-ĭskŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns, often relational (meaning "related to X"): -ian, -ic, -ish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-iskos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-stan suffix to form country and province names, especially when added to names of traditionally Muslim ethnic groups.</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive) -stan suffix to form derisive place names.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian ـِسْتَانْ (-estân, “place of”). Doublet of <i>стан</i> (stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns and adjectives to create a neuter noun, usually an abstract noun denoting a relation, social status, scientific discipline, quality or state: -hood, -ness, -ship, -ity, -y</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form verbs from nouns, and sometimes from adjectives.</li></ol><p><i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>contraction of -ся (-sja) <i>(reflexive suffix appended to finite verbs and infinitives to make a reflexive, reciprocal, or intransitive verb)</i></li><li>this, last (<i>found in a few, mostly obsolete or dialectal, terms referring to time</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sь</i> (“this”). Doublet of <i>сий</i> (sij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. See also <i>сей</i> (sej), the inherited independent form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Reflexive suffix appended to finite verbs and infinitives to make a reflexive, reciprocal, intransitive or passive verb.</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сѧ</i> (sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sę</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sen</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i>. It is considered the shortened form of <i>себя́</i> (sebjá). Compare Belarusian and Ukrainian <i>-ся</i> (-sja), Italian <i>-si</i>, Spanish <i>se</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>after all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er (a suffix appended to words to create a masculine noun, usually denoting a profession, performer of an action or a human relation)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-teľь</i>. Cognate to Old Church Slavonic <i>-тель</i> (-telĭ), Serbo-Croatian <i>-telj</i> and Czech <i>-tel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives from verbs.</li><li>(productive,&#32;takes verbs,&#32;produces adjectives) Performing an action or able to do so</li><li>(productive,&#32;takes verbs,&#32;produces adjectives) subject</li><li>(<i>produces adjectives</i>)</li><li>(takes nouns,&#32;produces adjectives)</li><li>(unproductive,&#32;with без-, не-,&#32;takes verbs,&#32;produces adjectives) subject</li><li>(productive,&#32;produces adjectives) intended for</li></ol><p>Derives from the gerundive (<i>причастодѣтїе</i> (pričastodětije)) suffix found in late Old Church Slavonic <i>-тельно, -тельнѣ</i> (-telĭno, -telĭně).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives from verbs.</li></ol><p><i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ence, -ance, -tion, -ity, -ment, etc. (<i>suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive ending of a minority of Russian verbs&#59; most verbs instead use <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-tьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) particle used to give intonational emphasis to a part of speech</li><li>some-: an indefinite particle used in the various compound pronouns and adverbs</li></ol><p>From the pronoun <i>тот</i> (tot), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to). Akin to the Macedonian definite article <i>-от</i> (-ot), <i>-та</i> (-ta), <i>-то</i> (-to), and Bulgarian definite article <i>-ът</i> (-ǎt), <i>-та</i> (-ta), <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive ending of the majority of Russian verbs (some use <i>-ти</i> (-ti) or <i>-чь</i> (-čʹ)).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ti</i>, through the variant <i>*-tь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anime and manga fandom</i>) appended to one's name or nickname to indicate cuteness and affection.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ちゃん</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Accusative case suffix for feminine nouns ending with -а or -я.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form nouns.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) -gon; -angle; forms the names of two-dimensional solids</li></ol><p>From <i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “angle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, object suffix), when combined with a numerical prefix. Doublet of <i>уго́льник</i> (ugólʹnik, “square; tool used to place objects or draw lines at right angles”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form nouns, often colloquial and endearing, from nouns or adjectives. Sometimes a new meaning is imparted.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to verbs to create a masculine noun, normally denoting an actor.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-unъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Combining form used to form verbs meaning approximately "to shoe".</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form pejorative nouns.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjective-forming suffix, added to intransitive verbs, indicating being in the state of that verb.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ǫťь</i>, a present active participial suffix. Doublet of <i>-у́щий</i> (-úščij), the normal present participle suffix, a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(added to nouns, adjectives, and verbs&#59; forms a feminine noun, usually with ironic or pejorative meaning)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(added to (normally feminine) nouns, forms diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning)</li><li>(added to verbs, forms instruments or tools)</li><li>(added to adjectives or numerals, forms colloquial nouns with the meaning of a carrier of the adjective's or numeral's properties)</li></ol><p>Extracted from nouns with the suffix <i>-уха</i> (-uxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) by rebracketing: <i>стару́ха</i> (starúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) → <i>стару́шка</i> (starúška) → <i>стар</i> (star) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;diminutive,&#32;produces particles or nouns)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-phobe</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix appended to words to create a feminine noun, usually a diminutive form expressing endearment.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ling forming diminutive nouns</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьce</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun-forming suffix: -tion</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>-tiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic -<i>чьскъ</i> with assimilation -<i>чс</i>- &gt; -<i>ц</i>-. See <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>neuter noun forming suffix</i></li><li>-y, -ling (forming diminutive nouns)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-а́нин</i> (-ánin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to nouns, indicating having the characteristic of that noun&#59; sometimes a relational adjective is formed instead</li></ol><p>Extracted by rebracketing of words such as <i>хлопча́тый</i> (xlopčátyj) (from <i>хло́пок</i> (xlópok) + <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)), where -ч- (&lt; -к- with palatalisation) is part of the stem.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns or sometimes other adjectives, often relational (meaning "related to X").</li></ol><p>Extended form of <i>-еский</i> (-eskij), probably extracted by rebracketing from cases where this suffix was added to nouns ending in <i>к</i> (k), which was then palatalized to <i>ч</i> (č) in the Slavic first palatalization.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives from nouns, verbs, and other adjectives with the meaning "having the characteristic of X".</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Diminutive suffix.</li><li>alternative form of <i>-щик</i> (-ščik) after coronal obstruents</li></ol><p><i>-ец</i> (-ec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to stems to create a feminine noun, to make something more diminutive, affectionate, or pejorative.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьka</i>. May be related to -очка (-očka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive ending of some Russian verbs whose root ends in &#47;k&#47; or &#47;g&#47;.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ťi</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;-kti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Forms female equivalents of nouns denoting males, or nouns denoting the wife of the male equivalent.</li><li>(<i>masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine</i>) Used to make diminutive forms of words, including personal names. When added to personal names, the name is normally truncated, typically down to a single syllable consisting of a consonant &#43; vowel.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ство</i> (-stvo): -hood, -ness, -ity</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i> following roots ending in х and ш.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives from adverbs and prepositions.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-чьнии</i> (-čĭnii), cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>-щьнь</i> (-štĭnĭ), Serbo-Croatian <i>-ħӣ</i>, Czech <i>-ci</i>, Slovene <i>-č</i>, Kashubian <i>-ci</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>-cy</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>-cny</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ťь</i>, cognate with Sanskrit <i>-त्य</i> (-tya), e.g. in <i>अमात्य</i> (amā́tya, “companion, member of household”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to normally masculine nouns to form diminutives, sometimes with a further change in meaning.</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from the suffix <i>-о́к</i> (-ók) added to diminutives whose roots end in х or ш.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns, verbs and sometimes adjectives to form masculine agent nouns: -er, -or</li></ol><p><i>-ский</i> (-skij) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to stems to show that it is a collective or a condition; -hood, -dom, -ness, -ism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form imperfective verbs from prefixed perfective verbs. Normally used with stems ending in paired non-palatal consonants, but occasionally found with stems ending in paired palatal consonants, with the consonant becoming non-palatal before this suffix.</li><li>Used to form frequentative verbs from non-prefixed imperfective verbs.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>masculine nominative singular adjective ending</li><li>masculine inanimate accusative singular adjective ending</li><li>-ed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ыи</i> (-yi), displaced expected <i>-ой</i> (-oj) in unstressed positions, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъ</i> + <i>&#42;jь</i>, doublet of <i>-о́й</i> (-ój).</p><p>The same as Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ин</i> (-in): 's (<i>possessive suffix</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms feminine equivalents of masculine animate nouns</li><li>forms abstract nouns from adjectives</li><li>forms place nouns from nouns and adjectives</li><li>forms nouns of various senses&#59; see <i>-ня</i> (-nja) for specifics</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-yni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A masculine patronymic suffix added to a given name ending in a hard (unpalatalized) consonant&#58; son of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Diminutive suffix forming nouns from adjectives, with a colloquial character.</li></ol><p>Apparently composed of the diminutive suffixes <i>-ы́ш</i> (-ýš) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates diminutives of neuter nouns, sometimes with a particularizing sense.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(forms nouns denoting objects or (usually) abstract concepts from verbs, or occasionally from adjectives)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ь</i> (-ĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract or action nouns.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Abstract noun suffix&#59; forms part of larger suffixes such as <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje), <i>-а́нье</i> (-ánʹje), <i>-тье</i> (-tʹje), and exists on its own especially as a suffix added to compound words.</li><li>Suffix used to create names of locations in conjunction with prefixes.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ие</i> (-ije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Forms stylistically neutral female equivalents of nouns denoting males.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje) (stressed).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>-ие</i> (-ije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Noun-forming diminutive suffix used after a soft (palatalized) consonant.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form adjectives from nouns or other adjectives, usually describing characteristics&#59; approximately equivalent to -y.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming nouns denoting a possessor of a certain quality, often intensifying, and usually colloquial or informal.</li><li>Suffix forming augmentatives or low colloquial forms of a noun.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>added to adjectives</i>) a being&#47;thing with a given characteristic&#59; often of a colloquial nature: -er</li><li>suffix forming nouns of various meanings</li><li>(<i>added to nouns</i>) inhabitant or native</li></ol><p>Probably from soft stem + <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-tion, -ence, -ance, -ity, etc. (suffix used to form abstract nouns from verbs&#59; added especially to verbs ending in <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)&#59; variant of <i>-ание</i> (-anije))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form nouns from other nouns, usually referring to inhabitants of a given place or occupations.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix appended to nouns to form related nouns of various sorts, both animate and inanimate, generally referring to more specific objects than the base lemma, often plants or animals</li></ol><p>Originally from <i>-ка</i> (-ka) added to adjectives ending in <i>-яный</i> (-janyj) or <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój); later extracted out as its own suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to materials, indicating being composed of or related to that material&#59; normally forms relational adjectives</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adjective-forming suffix, added to materials, indicating being composed of or related to that material&#59; normally forms relational adjectives</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Usually denoting occupation or agent. Compare to <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form agent nouns (both animate and inanimate) from verbs in <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ) and <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) whose stem ends in <i>-л</i> (-l): -ator, -er</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>-ātor</i>; see <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The infinitive suffix for many verbs. Usually indicates an imperfective verb. Often used to derive imperfective verbs from prefixed perfective verbs in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), and triggers iotation.</li></ol><p>From <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjective-forming suffix, added to intransitive verbs, particularly class 4 verbs (in <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)) and class 5 verbs (in <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ) or <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)), indicating being in the state of that verb.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ęťь</i>, a present active participial suffix. Doublet of <i>-я́щий</i> (-jáščij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>-ѧщи</i> (-ęšti) which replaced this suffix as the normal present participle suffix. See also the present active participle section of Template:cu-conj-i-type.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to form diminutive nouns from nouns and adjectives, often indicating a (small) object with a given quality</li><li>suffix used to form colloquial singular terms for identical siblings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing, -ent, -ive <i>(present participle active: “which is...-ing”)</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffixed used to form adjectives from nouns, often specifically relational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming abstract nouns from verbs. Many such nouns are distinctly colloquial.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ežь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-о́к</i> (-ók) (<i>after consonants followed by the soft sign ь and after the unpaired palatal й</i>)</li></ol><p>Regular phonological variant of <i>-о́к</i> (-ók) after mostly paired palatal sounds.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns to form diminutives, sometimes with pejorative force.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The young of an animal; -let, -ling.</li><li>Baby, child, youngster.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form passive-participle-like adjectives from verbs that normally form past passive participles in <i>-ённый</i> (-jónnyj).</li></ol><p>Compare the past-passive-participle suffix <i>-ённый</i> (-jónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns and verbs to form agent nouns.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>-eur</i>. The suffix was originally borrowed as a part of French-derived words such as <i>жонглёр</i> (žongljór, “juggler”) from French <i>jongleur</i> and <i>дублёр</i> (dubljór, “understudy, stunt double”) from French <i>doubleur</i>, and later extracted out as a productive suffix. In some cases the suffix replaces French <i>-aire</i> in words borrowed from French, e.g. <i>волонтёр</i> (volontjór, “volunteer”) from French <i>volontaire</i>, and English <i>-er</i> in words borrowed from English, e.g. <i>маркёр</i> (markjór, “marker (various senses)”) from English <i>marker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Indicates the genitive plural of first-declension and neuter second-declension nouns&#58;</li><li>Deverbal suffix&#58; (for some verbs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>нулево́й</i> (nulevój): 0th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj): 1st</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>деся́тый</i> (desjátyj): 10th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>оди́ннадцатый</i> (odínnadcatyj): 11th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>двена́дцатый</i> (dvenádcatyj): 12th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>трина́дцатый</i> (trinádcatyj): 13th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>четырнадца́тый</i> (četyrnadcátyj): 14th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пятна́дцатый</i> (pjatnádcatyj): 15th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój): 2nd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a KIA, a soldier killed in action</li></ol><p>From the Soviet military code. See две́сти (dvésti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>тре́тий</i> (trétij): 3rd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>четвёртый</i> (četvjórtyj): 4th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>пять ба́ллов</i> (pjatʹ bállov)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пя́тый</i> (pjátyj): 5th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>шесто́й</i> (šestój): 6th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>седьмо́й</i> (sedʹmój): 7th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>восьмо́й</i> (vosʹmój): 8th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>девя́тый</i> (devjátyj): 9th</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>&#64;</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom quark, beauty quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) C minor</li></ol><p>Orthographic borrowing from German <i>c-Moll</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;music) in C minor</li></ol><p>From <i>c-moll</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) IP address</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>IP address</i>. By surface analysis, English <i>IP</i> +&lrm; <i>а́дрес</i> (ádres).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) IP address</li></ol><p>From <i>IP-а́дрес</i> (IP-ádres) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) n-hedron (polyhedron with an arbitrary number of faces)</li></ol><p>From <i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) n-gon (polygon with an arbitrary number of vertices)</li></ol><p>From <i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>-уго́льник</i> (-ugólʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) PDF</li><li>(<i>Internet slang, euphemistic</i>) a pedophile</li></ol><p>PDF + файл. 2nd meaning - calque from English PDF file.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>QR code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strange quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top quark, truth quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>Т9</i> (T9), <i>with a Latin T</i>; autocorrect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>up quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>the Roman letter V, v</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) a dogwhistle for supporting the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Sense 2 was derived from <i><b>в</b>осток</i> (<b>v</b>ostok, “east, eastern Ukraine”). Like the Z sign, the V sign was initially used for vehicles targeted towards Kyiv, and later popularised by the Russian government on social media as a rallying symbol.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>the Roman letter Z, z</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) A symbol representing support for the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Sense 2 was derived from <i><b>з</b>апад</i> (<b>z</b>apad, “west”). The Z sign was initially used for vehicles targeted towards southeastern Ukraine, in a manner similar to invasion stripes used by the Allies in World War II. Later popularized by the Russian government on social media as a rallying symbol.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>Encloses a quotation.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>multiplication sign, usually read as умно́жить (umnóžitʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha particle, α-particle (positively charged nucleus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Yekaterinburg (a city, the administrative center of Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>Екатеринбу́рг</i> (Jekaterinbúrg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Yolkin, Elkin, or Yelkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Japanese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>吉<rp>(</rp><rt>よし</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby><ruby>田<rp>(</rp><rt>だ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>Yoshida</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Иерусали́м</i> (Ijerusalím).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Иису́с</i> (Iisús).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Иису́с Христо́с</i> (Iisús Xristós).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Иорда́н</i> (Iordán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Иорда́ния</i> (Iordánija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ио́сиф</i> (Iósif).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Андре́й Анато́льевич Зализня́к</i> (Andréj Anatólʹjevič Zaliznják, “Andrey Zaliznyak”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>абсци́зовая кислота́</i> (abscízovaja kislotá): ABA (abscisic acid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>авари́йное выключе́ние дви́гателя</i> (avaríjnoje vyključénije dvígatelja, “emergency engine shutdown”)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) abbreviation of <i>авиацио́нная диви́зия</i> (aviaciónnaja divízija, “aviation division”)</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) abbreviation of <i>агрега́т высо́кого давле́ния</i> (agregát vysókovo davlénija, “high pressure unit”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>авиация Военно-Морского флота</i> (aviacija Vojenno-Morskovo flota, “naval aviation”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>авиа́ция да́льнего де́йствия</i> (aviácija dálʹnevo déjstvija): strategic aviation, long-range aviation, long haul aircraft</li><li>initialism of <i>артилле́рия да́льнего де́йствия</i> (artillérija dálʹnevo déjstvija): long-range artillery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas station, petrol station, filling station</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>автозапра́вочная ста́нция</i> (avtozaprávočnaja stáncija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>AK-47, Kalashnikov assault rifle</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>автома́т Кала́шникова 1947</i> (avtomát Kalášnikova 1947), from the name of the designer Mikhail Kalashnikov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automatic transmission</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>автомати́ческая коро́бка переключе́ния переда́ч</i> (avtomatíčeskaja koróbka pereključénija peredáč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Аге́нтство национа́льной безопа́сности</i> (Agéntstvo nacionálʹnoj bezopásnosti, “Agency of National Security”): NSA, National Security Agency (US)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(business,&#32;law) JSC, joint-stock company</li><li>autonomous oblast</li><li>autonomous okrug</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>акционе́рное о́бщество</i> (akcionérnoje óbščestvo).</p><p>Acronym of <i>автоно́мная о́бласть</i> (avtonómnaja óblastʹ).</p><p>Acronym of <i>автоно́мный о́круг</i> (avtonómnyj ókrug).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>артиллерийский полк большой мощности</i> (artillerijskij polk bolʹšoj moščnosti, “heavy artillery regiment”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>артилле́рия резе́рва гла́вного кома́ндования</i> (artillérija rezérva glávnovo komándovanija, “general reserve artillery”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ASEAN (Association of Southeast Asian Nations)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ASEAN</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) initialism of <i>Автоно́мная Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Avtonómnaja Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic”): ASSR</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>азиатско-тихоокеа́нский регио́н</i> (aziatsko-tixookeánskij región): APR (Asia Pacific Region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>APEC, Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Азиа́тско-Тихоокеа́нское экономи́ческое сотру́дничество</i> (Aziátsko-Tixookeánskoje ekonomíčeskoje sotrúdničestvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>AUE; an informal, well hidden, and vaguely defined organization of Russian criminals, mainly consisting of children and teenagers, who are often indirectly ruled by adult criminals</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>ареста́нтский укла́д еди́н</i> (arestántskij uklád jedín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear power station</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>а́томная электроста́нция</i> (átomnaja elektrostáncija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aarau (a town and municipality, the capital of the canton of Aargau, Switzerland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Aaron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אהרן</i> (Ahărōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>А́хен</i> (Áxen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abadan (a city in Iran)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian آبادان (âbâdân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abaddon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אבדון</i> (Ǎḇaddōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abakan (the capital city of Khakassia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Abalkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abashiri (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>網走</i> (あばしり, Abashiri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Abaturov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Abbas, from Arabic, of Muslim usage</li><li>a surname, Abbas, from Arabic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَبَّاس (ʕabbās).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abbasid</li></ol><p><i>Аббас</i> (Abbas) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abgar (name of a number of kings of Osroene)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Arabic, Abd al-Rahman, Abdul Rahman</li></ol><p>From Arabic عَبْدُ الرَّحْمٰنِ (ʕabdu r-raḥmāni, literally “servant of the merciful”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Arabic, of Muslim usage, equivalent to English Abdul</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Arabic, of Muslim usage, equivalent to English Abdullah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَبْدُ اللّٰهِ (ʕabdu llāhi, literally “Servant of God”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Abel; also a surname</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aberdeen (a city in Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abidjan (the economic capital of Ivory Coast)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abydos (ancient city in Egypt)</li><li>Abydos (ancient city in Mysia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄβυδος</i> (Ábudos), from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./15cb8bb5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Ab" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Dw&#58;O49</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ꜣbḏw).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Abyssinia (former name of <i>Ethiopia</i>: a country and former empire in East Africa; used as an exonym until the mid 20th century)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) area with a high concentration of petty criminals</li></ol><p>From a European language. See English <i>Abyssinia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Abovyan, from Armenian</li><li>Abovyan (a town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Աբովյան</i> (Abovyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Abrahamian, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Աբրահամյան</i> (Abrahamyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Abram, equivalent to English Abraham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Abramov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Abram's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Abramsky, a Russian surname</li><li>(son) of Abraham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;derogatory) Absurdistan, any country seen as absurd.</li></ol><p><i>абсурд</i> (absurd) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abu Dhabi (an emirate of the United Arab Emirates)</li><li>Abu Dhabi (the capital city of the United Arab Emirates; the capital city of Abu Dhabi emirate, United Arab Emirates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abuja (the capital city of Nigeria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abkhazia (a geographic region and de-facto independent but largely unrecognized country in the Caucasus on the border of Europe and Asia; considered part of Georgia's territory by the vast majority of the international community)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>абха́з</i> (abxáz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an autonomous republic of the Soviet Union within the Georgian SSR</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Avakov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Ավագյան</i> (Avagyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Avanesov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Ավանեսյան</i> (Avanesyan), a by-form of <i>Հովհաննիսյան</i> (Hovhannisyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Avvakum, equivalent to English Habakkuk</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) clipping of <i>Книга пророка Аввакума</i> (Kniga proroka Avvakuma, “the Book of Habakkuk”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>Αββακούμ</i> (Avvakoúm), from Hebrew <i>חֲבַקּוּק</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>Авакум</i> (Avakum).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abgar (name of a number of kings of Osroene)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Avdeyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avdiivka (a city in Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Avdey, equivalent to English Obadiah</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Avdotya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>A form of the name <i>Евдоки́я</i> (Jevdokíja), from Ancient Greek <i>εὐδοκία</i> (eudokía, “good will”). Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Овъдотья</i> (Ovŭdotĭja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Avdyusha, of the male given name Авде́й (Avdéj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abel (<i>biblical character</i>)</li><li>a male given name, Avel, from Ancient Greek &lt; Hebrew, equivalent to English Abel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἅβελ</i> (Hábel).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abner (biblical cousin of Saul)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Averin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Averroes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Absalom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avesta (the sacred texts of Zoroastrianism, composed in the Avestan language.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avignon (a city and commune of Vaucluse department, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Avignon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avicenna (a Persian polymath)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Aulis (<i>ancient port-town in Greece</i>)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Αὐλίς</i> (Aulís).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abraham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Avrely or Avreli, from Latin, equivalent to English Aurelius</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Avrorin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Avrora's, Aurora's</li></ol><p><i>Авро́ра</i> (Avróra) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australasia (a continental region in Oceania, consisting of Australia, New Zealand, New Guinea and neighbouring islands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australian Capital Territory (a federal territory in southeastern Australia, containing the capital, Canberra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Commonwealth of Australia (official name of <i>Australia</i>: a country in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australia (a country consisting of a main island, the island of Tasmania and other smaller islands, located in Oceania; historically, a collection of former colonies of the British Empire)# (<i>geology</i>) Australia (a continent consisting of the land on the Australian tectonic plate, i.e. Australia, New Guinea and intervening islands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Австра́лия</i> (Avstrálija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Austria (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>Austria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Austria-Hungary (a former multi-ethnic empire and country in Central Europe existing from 1867 to 1918)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>А́встро-Ве́нгрия</i> (Ávstro-Véngrija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Austronesia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>А́встрия</i> (Ávstrija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avtovo (Saint Petersburg subway station on the Kirovsko-Vyborgskaya line)</li><li>Avtovo (a historic district)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volga German Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1918 to 1941)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Agadzhanov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Աղաջանյան</i> (Aġaǰanyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Աղաջանյան</i> (Aġaǰanyan)</li><li>a surname, Aghajanyan or Agadzhanyan, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Աղազարյան</i> (Aġazaryan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Agamemnon</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγαμέμνων</i> (Agamémnōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agapovka (a village in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>an abandoned village in Omsk Oblast, Russia</li></ol><p>(<i>village in Chelyabinsk Oblast</i>): From <i>Ага́пов</i> (Agápov) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), the last name of Pavel Osipovich Agapov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agarak (a village in Aragatsotn Province, Armenia)</li><li>Agarak (a village in Lori Province, Armenia)</li><li>Agarak (two villages in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ագարակ</i> (Agarak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agartala (a city in India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Agatha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγαθή</i> (Agathḗ), from ἀγαθός (agathós, “good”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Agatha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγαθή</i> (Agathḗ), from ἀγαθός (agathós, “good”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ageyenko, Aheyenko, Ageenko, Aheenko, Aheienko, or Ageienko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Aglaia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγλαΐα</i> (Aglaḯa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agni, Hindu god of fire.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अग्नि</i> (agni, “fire”). Doublet of <i>огонь</i> (ogonʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agra (a city in India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Agrafena</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrinion (a city in Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrippa (cognomen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agryz (a town in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aguascalientes (a state of Mexico)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Adam</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Adam (the first man and the progenitor of the human race)</li><li>(<i>Islam</i>) first man and first nabi of Islam</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>אָדָם</i> (adám, “earth, man, soil, light brown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adam and Eve (the first man and woman (according to Genesis))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Adamovich</li></ol><p><i>Ада́м</i> (Adám) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adana (a city and province of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adachi (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>足立</i> (あだち, Adachi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Addis Ababa (the capital city of Ethiopia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Adelaide</li><li>Adelaide (the capital city of South Australia)</li><li>Adelaide (a river in the Northern Territory, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adjara, Adzharia (an autonomous republic of Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Аджа́рия</i> (Adžárija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adirondacks, Adirondack Mountains (a mountain range in the state of New York, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from German, equivalent to English Adolf</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adonis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄδωνις</i> (Ádōnis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Adrammelech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, equivalent to English Adrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adriatic Sea (a sea and arm of the Mediterranean Sea, stretching north from the Ionian Sea to Venice, separating the Italian and Balkan Peninsulas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adygeysk (a city in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adyghe Autonomous Oblast (a former autonomous oblast of Krasnodar Krai, Soviet Union, existing from 1922 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adygea (a republic and federal subject of south-west Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adıyaman (a city in Adıyaman Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a unisex given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>Probably from Arabic عَزَاء (ʕazāʔ, “solace”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azawad (a former unrecognized state in Mali)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Azazel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azerbaijan (a region in northwestern Iran, where ancient Atropatene was located, encompassing the modern provinces of Ardabil, East Azerbaijan and West Azerbaijan)</li><li>[from 20th c.] Azerbaijan (a country in the South Caucasus in Asia and Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آذربایجان (Azerbaycan), from Classical Persian آذَرْبَایْجَان (āzarbāyjān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azerbaijan SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1920 to 1991)</li></ol><p><i>Азербайджа́нская</i> (Azerbajdžánskaja, Azɛrbajdžánskaja, “Azerbaijan”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Азербайджа́н</i> (Azerbajdžán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asian Games (sport event)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Asimov</li></ol><p>From <i>озимый</i> (ozimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Asimova</li></ol><p>From <i>озимый</i> (ozimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова</i> (-ova).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asia (the largest continent, located between Europe and the Pacific Ocean)# (<i>historical</i>) Asia (an ancient province of the Roman Empire in western Anatolia, existing between 133 BC (during the Roman Republic) and the 7th century AD)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Aznavour, from French</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Aznavour</i>, an adaptation of Armenian <i>Ազնավուրյան</i> (Aznavuryan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azov (a city in Rostov Oblast, in southern Russia)</li><li>a surname, Azov</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kipchak <i>azaq</i> (“lowlands”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sea of Azov (a sea in Eastern Europe, connected to the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>From the adjectival form of Азо́в (Azóv, “Azov, an adjacent town”) + мо́ре (móre, “sea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azores (an archipelago and autonomous region of Portugal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azores (an archipelago and autonomous region of Portugal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>А́зия</i> (Ázija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aizawl (the capital city of the state of Mizoram, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ayvazovsky, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Slavicized Armenian <i>Այվազյան</i> (Ayvazyan); Ayvazovsky was an ethnic Armenian born in Crimea, Russian Empire.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idaho (a state in the western United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aidos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uluru, Ayers Rock (a rock in the Northern Territory, Australia, near Alice Springs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aizawl (the capital city of the state of Mizoram, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iowa (a state in the Midwestern region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ayrum (a town in Tavush Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Այրում</i> (Ayrum).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aichi (a prefecture in southern Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>愛知</i> (あいち, Aichi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Aitmatov, from Kyrgyz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic, equivalent to English Aisha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَائِشَة (ʕāʔiša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town in Tuva, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akabira (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>赤平</i> (あかびら, Akabira).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aquitaine (a former region of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acoetes, an attendant of Bacchus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acoetes, father of Laocoön</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀκοίτης</i> (Akoítēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akita (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Akita (the capital city of Akita Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>秋田</i> (あきた, Akita).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akkad (ancient kingdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Accra (the capital city of Ghana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acoetes, an attendant of Bacchus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acoetes, father of Laocoön</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀκοίτης</i> (Akoítēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Akopov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Հակոբյան</i> (Hakobyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Akopyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Հակոբյան</i> (Hakobyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acropolis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Aksinia</li></ol><p>A form of the name <i>Ксе́ния</i> (Ksénija), from Ancient Greek <i>ξενία</i> (xenía, “hospitality”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Aksyonov or Aksenov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Akunin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akutagawa (surname)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alaverdi (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ալավերդի</i> (Alaverdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aladdin (the fictional young man)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَلَاء الدِّين (ʕalāʔ ad-dīn, “nobility of the faith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alazani (a tributary of the Kura River, in eastern Georgia and Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Åland Islands (an archipelago of the Baltic Sea)</li><li>Åland Islands (an autonomous province of Finland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Åland Islands (an archipelago of the Baltic Sea)</li><li>Åland Islands (an autonomous province of Finland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Alania (a medieval kingdom of the Alans (proto-Ossetians) in the Northern Caucasus, roughly in the location of the modern republics of Adygea, Chechnya, Ingushetia and North Ossetia-Alania in modern Russia, existing from the 9th to 13th centuries <small>CE</small>)</li><li>Alania, North Ossetia-Alania (a republic and federal subject of Russia, located in the Northern Caucasus mountains; in full, North Ossetia-Alania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alanya (a city and district of Antalya Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alapayevsk (a town in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alaric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albania (a country in Southeast Europe; official name: Респу́блика Алба́ния (Respúblika Albánija))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Алба́ния</i> (Albánija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Alburov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Alevtina</li></ol><p>The name of St. Valentina of Caesarea, corresponds to Ancient Greek <i>Οὐαλεντίνη</i> (Oualentínē). A similar misspelling is found in the Syriac translation of Eusebius’ On the Martyrs of Palestine, but it is doubtful that Slavic speakers could be familiar with it.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alexander</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Олександръ</i> (Oleksandrŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀλέξανδρος</i> (Aléxandros), compounded from <i>ἀλέξω</i> (aléxō, “to defend”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνδρ-</i> (andr-), stem of <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”): "defender of men". Cognate with English <i>Alexander</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexander the Great</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexander the Great</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Alexandra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀλεξάνδρα</i> (Alexándra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lighthouse of Alexandria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Alexandrinsky Theatre</li></ol><p><i>александри́н(ский)</i> (aleksandrín(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexandria (the second-largest city in Egypt)</li><li>Alexandria (a governorate of Egypt)</li><li>Alexandria (the capital city of Teleorman County, Romania)</li><li>Oleksandriia (a city in Kirovohrad Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Alexandria (a former town in the United Counties of Stormont, Dundas and Glengarry, Ontario, Canada; now a part of the township of North Glengarry)</li><li>Alexandria (a ghost town in British Columbia, Canada)</li><li>Alexandria (a town in Sarah Baartman district municipality, Eastern Cape, South Africa)</li><li>Alexandria (a town in Ehlanzeni district municipality, Mpumalanga, South Africa)</li><li>Alexandria (a census-designated place in Calhoun County, Alabama, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city in Monroe Township, Madison County, Indiana, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city in Campbell County, Kentucky, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city, the parish seat of Rapides Parish, Louisiana, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city, the county seat of Douglas County, Minnesota, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city in Clark County, Missouri, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a village in Thayer County, Nebraska, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a town in Grafton County, New Hampshire, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a town in Jefferson County, New York, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a village in Licking County, Ohio, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (an unincorporated community in Scioto County, Ohio, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a borough of Huntingdon County, Pennsylvania, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a town in DeKalb County, Tennessee, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (an independent city in Virginia, United States)</li><li>Alexandria (a city in Hanson County, South Dakota, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Aleksandrov or Alexandrov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Alexander's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Aleksandrovich or Alexandrovich</li></ol><p><i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic</li></ol><p><i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleksandrovskaya Sopka (a mountain in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia, Ural Mountains)</li></ol><p><i>алекса́ндровский</i> (aleksándrovskij, “Alexander's; Alexandrian”) +&lrm; <i>со́пка</i>&nbsp;f (sópka, “mound”), literally “Alexander's mound”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexandroupoli (a city in Evros regional unit, Eastern Macedonia and Thrace region, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Ալեքսանյան</i> (Alekʻsanyan), Aleksanian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ալեքսանյան</i> (Alekʻsanyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Alekseyev</li></ol><p><i>Алексе́й</i> (Alekséj) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Alexey, Aleksey, or Alexei, equivalent to English Alex or Alexis</li><li>Alpha (<i>A</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>From older Russian (Church Slavonic form) <i>Алекси́й</i> (Aleksíj), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀλέξιος</i> (Aléxios). Cognate to English <i>Alexius</i> and <i>Alex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Alexievich or Alexiyevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Алексіе́віч (Aljeksijévič)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleppo (a city in Syria)</li><li>Aleppo (a governorate of Syria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Aleppo</i>, from French <i>Alep</i>, from Ottoman Turkish حلب (haleb), from Arabic حَلَب (ḥalab).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleutian Islands (an archipelago in western Alaska, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Algeria (a country in North Africa)</li><li>Algiers (the capital and largest city of Algeria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>People's Democratic Republic of Algeria (official name of <i>Algeria</i>: a country in North Africa)</li></ol><p>Calque of Arabic الْجُمْهُورِيَّة الْجَزَائِرِيَّة الدِّيمُقْرَاطِيَّة الشَّعْبِيَّة (al-jumhūriyya l-jazāʔiriyya d-dīmuqrāṭiyya š-šaʕbiyya) or calque of French <i>République algérienne démocratique et populaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ali Baba (a fictional character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَلِيّ بَابَا (ʕaliyy bābā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Alik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Alina</li></ol><p>Hypocoristic of <i>Альби́на</i> (Alʹbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Alice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alice in Wonderland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Scottish Gaelic, equivalent to English Alistair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alytus (a city in Lithuania)</li></ol><p>From Lithuanian <i>Alytus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Alla, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Probably from Ancient Greek <i>ἄλλη</i> (állē, “other, different”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allah (God, in Islam)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اللّٰه (allāh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) Allahu akbar, Allah akbar (Muslim takbir - proclamation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اَللّٰهُ أَكْبَر (allāhu ʔakbar), from nominative form of اَللّٰه (allāh, “God”) + أَكْبَر (ʔakbar), elative form of كَبِير (kabīr, “great”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allahabad (a city in Uttar Pradesh, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Azerbaijani surname, Allahverdiyev, feminine equivalent Аллахвердиева (Allaxverdijeva)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>Allahverdi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) Allahu akbar, Allah akbar (Muslim takbir - proclamation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اَللّٰهُ أَكْبَر (allāhu ʔakbar), from nominative form of اَللّٰه (allāh, “God”) + أَكْبَر (ʔakbar), elative form of كَبِير (kabīr, “great”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alma-Ata (the old Soviet-era name of modern-day Almaty, the largest city in Kazakhstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Almazna (a city in Alchevsk Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Almaty (the largest city in Kazakhstan; formerly Alma-Ata)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Алматы</i> (Almaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Almere (a city in Flevoland province, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rose-Red (character from the fairy tale <i>Snow-White and Rose-Red</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Altay, Altai (a mountain range in Central Asia); see Altai Mountains</li><li>Altai Republic (a republic of Russia)</li><li>Altai Krai (a krai of Russia)</li><li>a prefecture of the Xinjiang autonomous region, China</li><li>a city in Altay prefecture, Xinjiang autonomous region, China</li><li>a town in the East Kazakhstan Region, Kazakhstan</li><li>the capital city of Govi-Altai Province, Mongolia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Altai Krai (a krai of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alupka (a city in Crimea, in southern Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Greek <i>Αλούπκα</i> (Aloúpka) which derived from Greek <i>αλεπού</i> (alepoú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alushta (a city in Crimea, in southern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Альфре́д</i> (Alʹfrɛ́d), alternative form of <i>А́льфред</i> (Álʹfrɛd): a male given name from English, equivalent to English Alfred</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alchevsk (a city, the administrative center of Alchevsk Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>al-Qaeda</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اَلْقَاعِدَة (al-qāʕida).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Albatz</i></li><li>a surname, Albats, from German, equivalent to English Albatz</li><li>a Jewish surname, Albats</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Albert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alberta (a province in western Canada)</li><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Alberta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Albina, from Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Albina</i>, female form of <i>Albinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albion (poetic term for England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albireo (star in the constellation of Cygnus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albuquerque (a city in New Mexico, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alvíssmál (tenth book of the Poetic Edda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aldebaran (star in the constellation of Taurus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Almetyevsk (a city in Tatarstan, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Альме́т</i> (Alʹmét), from Tatar <i>Әлмәт</i> (Älmät), after the founder of the village.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Alps (a mountain range in Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alpes-de-Haute-Provence (a department of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alpha Centauri (brightest star in the southern constellation of Centaurus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Alfvén, from Swedish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Alfred</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Alzheimer, from German</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Alzheimer's disease (mental disorder from brain tissue changes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Alya, of the female given names Алекса́ндра (Aleksándra), Али́на (Alína), Али́са (Alísa), Альби́на (Alʹbína), or Алевти́на (Alevtína)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alaska (a state of the United States, formerly a territory)</li></ol><p>Derived from Aleut <i>alaxsxaq</i> (“a mainland”, literally “that toward which the action of the sea is directed”), composed of <i>alag</i> (“pertaining to the sea”) + <i>-sxa</i> (nominalizing suffix, yields 'the objective of action expressed by the root') + <i>-q</i> (nominalizing suffix). Commonly misattributed to Russian <i>ала</i> (ala) + <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Alyona, equivalent to English Ellen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Alyokhin, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Алёха</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim (Aljóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i>&nbsp;m (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Alyosha, of the male given name Алексе́й (Alekséj)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) an idiot or irresponsible person</li></ol><p><i>Алексей</i> (Aleksej) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Amazon basin (a geographic region of South America, drained by the Amazon river)</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>Río Amazonas</i> (“Amazon River”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija), said to have been named by Francisco de Orellana, early explorer of the river, due to an attack on his party by a mixed group of male and female warriors. See English <i>Amazon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>From Arabic أَمَل (ʔamal, “hope”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amasya (the capital city of Amasya Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>America, the Americas (a supercontinent consisting of North America, Central America and South America regarded as a whole; in full, the Americas)# America, United States of America (a country in North America; in full, United States of America)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>America</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Samoa (an archipelago and overseas territory of the United States in Polynesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amersfoort (a city in Utrecht, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amman (the capital city of Jordan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Amos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amritsar (a city in India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amsterdam (the capital city of the Netherlands; a city in North Holland)</li><li>Amsterdam (a city in New York)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amu Darya (a major river in Central Asia, flowing along the borders of Afghanistan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan, emptying into the (now mostly dry) Aral Sea.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amu Darya (a major river in Central Asia, flowing along the borders of Afghanistan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan, emptying into the (now mostly dry) Aral Sea.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amursk (a town in Khabarovsk Krai, in the Far East of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Аму́р</i> (Amúr, “Amur (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amurskaya (a river on Sakhalin island in Sakhalin Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amur, Amur Oblast (an oblast of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amiens (a city and commune, the capital of Somme department, Hauts-de-France region, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Amiens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anadyr (a town, the administrative center of the Chukotka Autonomous Okrug, in extreme northeastern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaxagoras</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaxarchus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaximander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaximenes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anapa (a town in Krasnodar Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anastasi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Anastasia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀναστασία</i> (Anastasía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Anatoli or Anatoly, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Anatolius or Anatole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>ανατολή</i> (anatolí, “east”, “dawn”, “sunrise”), from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνατολή</i> (anatolḗ, “sunrise”). Akin with Latin <i>Anatolius</i> and English and French <i>Anatole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anatolia</li><li>Anatolia (a region of Turkey in Western Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Анато́лий</i> (Anatólij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Iranian mythology</i>) Anahita (old Iranian goddess)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angara (a river in Irkutsk Oblast and Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li><li>Angara (family of Russian launch vehicles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from German, equivalent to English Angela</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Angelina</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anguilla (an archipelago and overseas territory of the United Kingdom in the Caribbean Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angkor Wat (Cambodian temple complex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angliysky Prospekt (a street in Saint Petersburg, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>England (a constituent country of the United Kingdom)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Great Britain (a large island of the United Kingdom in Northern Europe)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) United Kingdom (a kingdom and country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Anglia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>А́нглия</i> (Ánglija).: England (a constituent country of the United Kingdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angola (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in Poland and Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a river in Poland and Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andalusia (a historical region and autonomous community in southern Spain, the most populated and second largest of the seventeen autonomous communities that constitute Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>Andalusia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andalusia (a historical region and autonomous community in southern Spain, the most populated and second largest of the seventeen autonomous communities that constitute Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>Andalusia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andaman and Nicobar Islands (an archipelago and union territory of India in the Bay of Bengal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andaman Islands (an archipelago of India in the Bay of Bengal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from English, equivalent to English Angela</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andorra (a microstate in Southern Europe, between Spain and France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andorra la Vella (the capital city of Andorra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andorra la Vella (the capital city of Andorra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Andreyev, equivalent to English Anderson or Andrews</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Andrei's, Andrew's</li></ol><p><i>Андре́й</i> (Andréj) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Andrey or Andrei, equivalent to English Andrew, Belarusian Андрэ́й (Andréj), or Ukrainian Андрі́й (Andríj)</li><li>a transliteration of the Belarusian male given name <i>Андрэ́й</i> (Andréj), equivalent to Andrew</li><li>a transliteration of the Ukrainian male given name <i>Андрі́й</i> (Andríj), equivalent to Andrew</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀνδρέας</i> (Andréas), cognate with <i>ἀνδρεῖος</i> (andreîos, “manly”), both from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Andrian, from Latin, equivalent to English Adrian</li></ol><p>A variant of <i>Адриа́н</i> (Adrián).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Andromeda</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Andromeda</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Andromeda Galaxy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Andropov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Andryusha, of the male given name Андре́й (Andréj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Андре́й</i> (Andréj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andhra Pradesh (a state in southeastern India, where Telugu is the primary language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Andes (a mountain range in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anzhela, equivalent to English Angela</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anzhelika, equivalent to English Angelica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anjou (a historical province of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Anikin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anisia, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀνυσία</i> (Anusía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anisya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ankara (the capital city and province of Turkey)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Turkish <i>Ankara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anchorage (a city in Alaska)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Ann, Anna, or Ana</li><li>Alpha (<i>A</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) Order of Saint Anna</li><li>Anna (a sailing frigate of the Russian Imperial Navy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Annam (the former name of Vietnam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Annapolis (the capital city of Maryland, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Annapolis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antalya (the capital city of Antalya Province, Turkey)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>Antalya</i>, from Ottoman Turkish آنطالیه (Antalya), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀττάλεια</i> (Attáleia). Doublet of <i>Атта́лия</i> (Attálija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antananarivo (the capital city of Madagascar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antarctica (the southernmost continent, south of the Southern Ocean, containing the South Pole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antarctic (a continental region, one of the major ecozones of the world, covering the south polar regions, especially those south of the Antarctic Convergence; or, in accordance with the Antarctic Treaty System, the 60th parallel south)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antarctic Ocean (an ocean surrounding Antarctica)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antigua and Barbuda (a country consisting of two islands in the Caribbean, Antigua and Barbuda, and numerous other small islands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anti-Lebanon (a mountain range in Syria and Lebanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antiochus (one of the thirteen kings)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antioch, Antioch on the Orontes (ancient Greek city on the Orontes River, in modern Turkey)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀντιόχεια</i> (Antiókheia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anthony</li><li>Alpha (<i>A</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Antonius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Latin, equivalent to English Antonina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Antonov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anton's, Anthony's</li></ol><p><i>Анто́н</i> (Antón) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Антио́хия</i> (Antióxija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anubis (Ancient Egyptian god of the underworld)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἄνουβις</i> (Ánoubis), from Egyptian <i>jnpw</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
