</td></tr></table>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Anfertiev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Anfisa, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀνθοῦσα</i> (Anthoûsa), from ἀνθοφόρος (anthophóros, “bearing flowers, flowery”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Spanish, equivalent to English Angela</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anchises</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Anchīsēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anhui (a province in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Аньхо́й</i> (Anʹxój): Anhui (a province in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Аньхо́й</i> (Anʹxój): Anhui (a province in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anshan (a prefecture-level city in northeastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Anyuta, of the female given name А́ня (Ánja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name А́нна (Ánna), equivalent to English Anya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aomori (the capital city of Aomori Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Aomori (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>青森</i> (あおもり, Aomori).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macau (a city, special administrative region, and peninsula in China, west of Hong Kong)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macau (a city, special administrative region, and peninsula in China, west of Hong Kong)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aparan (a town in Aragatsotn Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ապարան</i> (Aparan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apennine Peninsula, Italian Peninsula (a peninsula consisting of most of Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apennines (a mountain range running along the spine of Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apia (the capital city of Samoa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Apollinary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Appalachians (a mountain range in eastern North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Apresyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ապրեսյան</i> (Apresyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Absheron Peninsula (a peninsula in Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arab Republic of Egypt (official name of <i>Egypt</i>: a country in North Africa and West Asia)</li></ol><p>Calque of Arabic جمهورية مصر العربيه.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arab Spring (movement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ара́б</i> (aráb).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabian Peninsula (a peninsula of West Asia between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf; includes Jordan, Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, and the United Arab Emirates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabian Sea (a sea in the northern Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabia (a peninsula of West Asia between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf; includes Jordan, Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, and the United Arab Emirates)# (<i>broader sense</i>) Arabia (the region of West Asia within or adjacent to the Arabian Plate; Yemen, Oman, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, Iraq, Saudi Arabia, Jordan, the Egyptian Sinai, Palestine and the United Arab Emirates)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>Аравиꙗ</i> (Aravija), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀραβία</i> (Arabía). Compare <i>араб</i> (arab) and related forms with /b/, which are later borrowings (through Latin and French).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ара́вия</i> (Arávija).:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Arabia (a peninsula of West Asia between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf; includes Jordan, Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, and the United Arab Emirates)# (<i>broader sense</i>) Arabia (the region of West Asia within or adjacent to the Arabian Plate; Yemen, Oman, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, Iraq, Saudi Arabia, Jordan, the Egyptian Sinai, Palestine and the United Arab Emirates)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aragatsotn (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Արագածոտն</i> (Aragacotn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aragvi (a river in the country of Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aragon (a medieval kingdom, now an autonomous community, in northeastern Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arakawa (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>荒川</i> (あらかわ, Arakawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aras, Araxes (a river in Turkey, Armenia, Iran and Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aral Sea (a former lake lying between Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ararat (a mountain in the Armenian Highlands, in modern-day Turkey)</li><li>Ararat (a province of Armenia)</li><li>Ararat (a city in Ararat Province, Armenia)</li><li>Ararat (a village in Ararat Province, Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arachosia (an ancient Achaemenid and Seleucid satrapy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aargau (a canton of Switzerland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argentina (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argentine Republic (official name of <i>Argentina</i>: a country in South America)</li></ol><p>Calque of Spanish <i>República Argentina</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argos (a city in Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Argus (giant with hundred eyes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ardennes (forest, geographic region and low mountain range in France, Belgium, Germany and Luxembourg; in full, Ardennes Forest, Ardenne Forest, or Forest of Ardennes)# Ardennes (a department of Grand Est, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ardèche (a department in Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes, south-central France)# Ardèche (a right tributary of the Rhône in the departments of Ardèche and Gard, south-central France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ares</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Arastovich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Арасто́віч (Arastóvič)</li><li>a surname, Arestovych or Arestovich, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arezzo (a province of Tuscany, Italy)</li><li>Arezzo (a city, the capital of the Province of Arezzo, Tuscany, Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Arezzo</i>, from Latin <i>Ārrētium</i>, from Etruscan <i>𐌀𐌓𐌉𐌕𐌉𐌌</i> (aritim).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arzamas (a town in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariana (an ancient region of Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arizona (a state in the southwestern United States, established 1912)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arica (a city in Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Ahriman (the Zoroastrian equivalent of the Devil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arinj (a village in Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristotle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀριστοτέλης</i> (Aristotélēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristophanes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Ariel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arkady or Arkadi, equivalent to English Arcady</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρκάδιος</i> (Arkádios), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρκάς</i> (Arkás, “resident of Arcadia”). Via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ŕ̥tḱos</i> cognate with <i>Урсу́ла</i> (Ursúla).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>Ли́дия</i> (Lídija), <i>Рома́н</i> (Román).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arcadia (an ancient region in the Peloponnese, in modern Greece)</li><li>Arcadia (a regional unit of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρκαδία</i> (Arkadía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Арка́дия</i> (Arkádija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arkansas (a state in the South Central region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arctic (a continental region consisting of the portion of the Earth north of the Arctic Circle, containing the North Pole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armavir (an ancient city and historical capital of the kingdom of Armenia, in the modern country of Armenia)</li><li>Armavir (a province of Armenia)</li><li>Armavir (a town, the capital of Armavir Province, Armenia)</li><li>Armavir (a village in Armavir Province, Armenia)</li><li>Armavir (a city in Krasnodar Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Արմավիր</i> (Armavir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armageddon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenia (a country in the South Caucasus region of Asia, sometimes considered to belong politically to Europe)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Armenia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρμενίᾱ</i> (Armeníā), from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎷𐎡𐎴</i> (a-r-mi-i-n /⁠Armina⁠/).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Арме́ния</i> (Arménija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United States Army</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1936 to 1991)</li></ol><p><i>Армя́нская</i> (Armjánskaja, “Armenian”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenian Apostolic Church</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenian Highland (a plateau in Western Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arnhem (the capital city of Gelderland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Aaron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arran (an island off the coast of Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arsenyev (a town in Primorsky Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artaxerxes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artashat (a town, the capital of the municipality of Artashat and the province of Ararat, Armenia, located on Araks River in the Ararat plain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Արտաշատ</i> (Artašat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Artemis (Greek goddess)</li><li>a female given name, Artemida, from Ancient Greek</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Artemyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artik (a town in Shirak province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Արթիկ</i> (Artʻik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Artina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Arthur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artyom, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Artemius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρτέμιος</i> (Artémios), cognate with Belarusian <i>Арцём</i> (Arcjóm) and Ukrainian <i>Артем</i> (Artem).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Russian politics) Bakhmut, a city in Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>Артём</i> (Artjóm, “Artyom”) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), in honor of the Soviet political figure Fyodor Sergeev, whose pseudonym was <i>Comrade Artyom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aruba (an island, dependent territory, and constituent country of the Netherlands, in the Caribbean Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arunachal Pradesh (a state in northeastern India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arkhangelsk (a seaport city, the administrative center of Arkhangelsk Oblast, in northwest Russia), also known as Archangel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic. By surface analysis, <i>арха́нгел</i> (arxángel, “archangel”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), after Михаило-Архангельский монастырь, the Archangel Michael Monastery.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arkhangelsk Oblast (an oblast of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>арха́нгельский</i> (arxángelʹskij, “related to Arkhangelsk”) +&lrm; <i>о́бласть</i> (óblastʹ, “oblast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Archilochus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Архило́х</i> (Arxilóx).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Archimedes (an ancient Greek mathematician)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρχιμήδης</i> (Arkhimḗdēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Архиме́д</i> (Arximéd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Armenia</i>) Nagorno-Karabakh (a region of the South Caucasus) (preferred by Armenians)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Արցախ</i> (Arcʻax).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Warsaw (the capital city of Poland)</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of the phrase <i>в Варша́ву</i> (v Varšávu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariège (a department of Occitania, in southwestern France)</li><li>Ariège (a river in southern France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asahikawa (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>旭川</i> (あさひかわ, Asahikawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Асуа́н</i> (Asuán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashibetsu (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>芦別</i> (あしべつ, Ashibetsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Askania-Nova (an urban-type settlement in Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Аска́нія-Но́ва</i> (Askánija-Nóva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asclepius (Greek god of medicine and healing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asmara (the capital city of Eritrea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asmodeus (the prince of demons in the Book of Tobit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asmara (the capital city of Eritrea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assam (a state in northeastern India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Асса́м</i> (Assám).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assen (the capital city of Drenthe, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Assyria (an ancient Semitic Akkadian kingdom and at times an empire, extant from the mid-23rd century <small>BCE</small> to 608 <small>BCE</small> and centred on the Upper Tigris river in northern Mesopotamia (present day northern Iraq, but at its maximum extent including parts of modern Iran, Syria, Israel, Palestine and Egypt))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Асси́рия</i> (Assírija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Асуа́н</i> (Asuán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Асси́рия</i> (Assírija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astana (the capital city of Kazakhstan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Астана</i> (Astana), from Classical Persian آسْتَانَه (āstāna, “a threshold”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asterix (fictional Gaulish warrior)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astrakhan (a city, the administrative center of Astrakhan Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asturias (an autonomous community and province of Spain, on the Spanish north coast facing the Cantabrian Sea)# Asturias (a former kingdom existing from c. 718 to 924 <small>CE</small>, in modern northwest Spain and Portugal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astghik (Armenian goddess)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Աստղիկ</i> (Astġik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aswan (city)</li><li>Aswan (governorate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic أسوان (Aswān), itself borrowed from Coptic <i>ⲥⲟⲩⲁⲛ</i> (souan), from Egyptian <i>swnw</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
