</td></tr></table>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given names Анастаси́я (Anastasíja) or Анастасія (Anastasija), equivalent to English Asya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atari (video game company or system)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Atari</i>, in turn from Japanese <i>当たり</i> (atari), referring to the traditional board game of Go.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Turkish surname <i>Atatürk</i></li></ol><p>From Turkish <i>Atatürk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Atkinson</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlanta (a city in Georgia, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Atlanta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantis (a mythical lost island and mythical continent)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀτλαντίς</i> (Atlantís), from <i>Ἄτλας</i> (Átlas, “Atlas”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic Ocean (the ocean lying between the Americas to the west and Europe and Africa to the east)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Atropatene (ancient kingdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attalia (an ancient city in modern Turkey; modern Antalya)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀττάλεια</i> (Attáleia). Doublet of <i>Анта́лья</i> (Antálʹja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attica (a historical region in modern Greece)</li><li>Attica (an administrative region in the modern country of Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aukštaitija (an ethnographic region of Lithuania, located in the north-east of the country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aurangabad (a city in India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auschwitz (concentration camp)</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) Auschwitz (a city in Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Auschwitz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Afanasy, equivalent to English Athanasius</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀθανάσιος</i> (Athanásios), from <i>ἀθάνατος</i> (athánatos, “immortal”). Compare Ukrainian <i>Опана́с</i> (Opanás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Afanasiya, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Athanasia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀθανασία</i> (Athanasía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Афана́сий</i> (Afanásij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afghanistan (a landlocked country between Central Asia and South Asia)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) The Soviet–Afghan War.</li></ol><p>From Classical Persian اَفْغَانِسْتَان (afġānistān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Athena.</li><li>a female given name, Afina, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Athena</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athens (the capital city of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀθῆναι</i> (Athênai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Africa (the continent south of Europe and between the Atlantic and Indian Oceans)# (<i>historical</i>) Africa (a former province of the Roman Empire, containing what is now Tunisia and portions of coastal Algeria and Libya)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horn of Africa (a peninsula in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>African Union (multinational organisation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aphrodite</li><li>a female given name, Afrodita, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀφροδίτη</i> (Aphrodítē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afsharid</li></ol><p><i>Афшар</i> (Afšar) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahab (biblical king of Israel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Akhedzhakov, from West Circassian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achelous (a river in western Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaemenes (the founder of the Achaemenid dynasty)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaemenid (a member of the dynasty which ruled the Persian empire c. 550–330 BCE)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aachen (a city in Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acheron (a river in the Epirus region, in northwestern Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Acheron</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀχέρων</i> (Akhérōn, “lamentation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhetaten</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Egyptian <i>ꜣḫt-jtn</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
