</td></tr></table>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Achilles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Achilles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahmedabad (a city in Gujarat, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female surname, Akhmadullina, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ахмадулла</i> (Axmadulla) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Akhmatov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhtala (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ախթալա</i> (Axtʻala).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okhtyrka (a city in Okhtyrka Raion, Sumy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahura Mazda (the one God of Zoroaster)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Acestes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Acestēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkmenistan standard form of <i>Ашхаба́д</i> (Ašxabád): Ashgabat (the capital city of Turkmenistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ashkenazi or Ashkenazy, from Hebrew</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>אַשְׁכְּנַז</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashtarak (a town in Aragatsotn Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Աշտարակ</i> (Aštarak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ashurkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashgabat (the capital city of Turkmenistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Aelita</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀήρ</i> (aḗr, “wind, atmosphere”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Airbus (a European aircraft manufacturer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeroflot airlines</li></ol><p><i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>флот</i> (flot)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male Buryat surname, Ayusheyev, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Аюше́й</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim (Ajušéj) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i>&nbsp;m (-ev).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Ayushin, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠠᠶ᠋ᠤᠰᠢ ᠶᠢᠨ</i> (ayusi-yin), genitive of <i>ᠠᠶ᠋ᠤᠰᠢ</i> (ayusi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Афи́ны</i> (Afíny).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>бру́тто</i> (brútto): gross weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Бюро по демократическим институтам и правам человека</i> (Bjuro po demokratičeskim institutam i pravam čeloveka): ODIHR, Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>больша́я кольцева́я ли́ния</i> (bolʹšája kolʹcevája línija, “Big Circle Line”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>боева́я маши́на пехо́ты</i> (bojevája mašína pexóty); IFV, infantry fighting vehicle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>без на́шей отве́тственности</i> (bez nášej otvétstvennosti): without our responsibility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) initialism of <i>Белору́сская Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Belorússkaja Naródnaja Respúblika): Belarusian People's Republic (a former country that controlled most of modern Belarus from 1918–19, and a government-in-exile to the present day)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>большо́й противоло́дочный кора́бль</i> (bolʹšój protivolódočnyj koráblʹ, “large anti-submarine ship”), a class of ships of the navies of the USSR and modern-day Russia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>BRIC (Brazil, Russia, India, China)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Брази́лия, Росси́я, И́ндия, Кита́й</i> (Brazílija, Rossíja, Índija, Kitáj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa&#59; BRICS</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) initialism of <i>бе́ло-си́не-бе́лый</i> (bélo-síne-bélyj, “white-blue-white”): a protest flag used against the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Great Soviet Encyclopedia, GSE (<i>published from 1926 to 1990</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Больша́я Сове́тская Энциклопе́дия</i> (Bolʹšája Sovétskaja Enciklopédija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бронетранспортёр</i> (bronetransportjór, “APC”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i><u>б</u>роне<u>т</u>а́нковый <u>р</u>емо́нтный <u>з</u>аво́д</i> (<u>b</u>rone<u>t</u>ánkovyj <u>r</u>jemóntnyj <u>z</u>avód, literally “armoured tank repair factory”); BTRZ.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) BCG (tuberculosis vaccine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>BCG</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) initialism of <i>бе́ло-червоно-белый</i> (bélo-červono-belyj, “white-scarlet-white”): the former flag of the Republic of Belarus, now used as a protest flag used against the government of Alexander Lukashenko, President of Belarus.</li></ol><p>Derived from Belarusian <i>бела-чырвона-белы сцяг</i> (bjela-čyrvóna-bjely scjah). червоный, which corresponds to Belarusian чырвона (čyrvóna), is considered archaic in modern Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) abbreviation of <i>безэкипа́жный ка́тер</i> (bezɛkipážnyj káter); USV, drone boat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baalbek (the capital city of Baalbek-Hermel Governorate, Lebanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Babajanyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Բաբաջանյան</i> (Babaǰanyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Babaryka or Babariko, from Belarusian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Baburov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Ukrainian: Babchenko</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bavaria (a historic region, former duchy, former kingdom, and modern state of Germany; the modern state includes parts of historical Swabia and Franconia as well as historical Bavaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bavarian Soviet Republic (a former republic existing from 13 April 1919 to 3 May 1919)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bahamas (an archipelago and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bahamas (an archipelago and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baghdad (the capital city of Iraq)# Baghdad (a governorate of Iraq)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bagdasarov or Baghdasarov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Բաղդասարյան</i> (Baġdasaryan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Baghramyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Բաղրամյան</i> (Baġramyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baden-Württemberg (a state in southwest Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Baden-Württemberg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Bazaron, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠪᠠᠵᠠᠷ ᠤᠨ</i> (baǰar-un), genitive case of <i>ᠪᠠᠵᠠᠷ</i> (baǰar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Biden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baikal (a lake in Siberia in Russia)</li><li>Baikal (Soviet and Russian non-alcoholic beverage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bayraktar (combat drone)</li></ol><p>Internationalism, from Bayraktar UAV, from Turkish <i>bayraktar</i> (“flag-bearer”), from Ottoman Turkish بیراقدار (bayrakdar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baku (the capital city of Azerbaijan)</li></ol><p>From Classical Persian بَاکُو (bākū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balakliya (a city in Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Baldano, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠪᠠᠯᠳᠠᠨ ᠤ</i> (baldan-u), genitive case of <i>ᠪᠠᠯᠳᠠᠨ</i> (baldan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bali (an island and province of Indonesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Balkans (a cultural region and peninsula in Southeast Europe, roughly equivalent to the area covered by Albania, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Bulgaria, Croatia, Greece, Kosovo, North Macedonia, Montenegro and Serbia, sometimes including Romania, Slovenia, and European Turkey)</li><li>the Balkan Mountains (a mountain range in the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baltiysk (a town in Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From its location by the Baltic Sea.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baltic Sea (a sea in Northern Europe, an arm of the Atlantic enclosed by Denmark, Estonia, Finland, Germany, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Russia and Sweden)</li></ol><p><i>Балти́йское</i> (Baltíjskoje, “Baltic”) +&lrm; <i>мо́ре</i> (móre, “sea”), from Medieval Latin <i>Mare Balticum</i>; Latin being the standard language of medieval European cartography, understood by explorers and navigators across different vernacular tongues. Tradition holds that the name was coined by Adam of Bremen in 1075, deriving from Latin <i>balteus</i> (“belt”) due to the shape of the sea extending around the continent like a belt.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bamako (the capital city of Mali)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bangalore (the capital city of Karnataka, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bangui (the capital city of the Central African Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bangkok (the capital city of Thailand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bangladesh (a country in South Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bandar Seri Begawan (the capital city of Brunei)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>Банде́ра</i> (Bandéra, “[Stepan] Bandera”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Banjul (the capital city of the Gambia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete spelling of <i>Бангко́к</i> (Bangkók) (= Bangkok): the capital city of Thailand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barbados (an island and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barbuda (an island of the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barents Sea (a marginal sea of the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barnaul (a city, the administrative center of Altai Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Barrès</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Barrès</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barcelona (the capital city of Catalonia, Spain)</li><li>Barcelona (a province of Catalonia, Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Barkhudarov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Բարխուդարյան</i> (Barxudaryan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barczewo (a town in Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Baryshnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Baryakin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>ბასილაშვილი</i> (basilašvili)</li><li>a surname, Basilashvili, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Basov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basseterre (the capital city of Saint Kitts and Nevis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bastrykin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Batalov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Batken (the capital of the Batken Region, Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Batna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Batoin, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠪᠠᠲᠤ ᠶᠢᠨ</i> (batu-yin), genitive of <i>ᠪᠠᠲᠤ</i> (batu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baton Rouge (the capital city of Louisiana, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Baton Rouge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Batumi (the capital city of Adjara, Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Batyushkov</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́тюшка</i> (bátjuška, “priest, father”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bautzen (a town and rural district of Saxony, Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bautzen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baphomet (occult deity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baffin Island (a large island of Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bakhmut (a city in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bahrain (an archipelago, island, and country in West Asia in the Persian Gulf)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bakhchisaray (a city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bashkortostan (a republic and federal subject of Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bashkir <i>Башҡортостан</i> (Başqortostan). Can be analysed as <i>башкорт</i> (baškort) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bayamo (a city in Cuba)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bezrukov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bezukhov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beirut (the capital city of Lebanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Beketov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belarus (a country in Eastern Europe; official name: Респу́блика Белару́сь (Respúblika Belarúsʹ)) (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>Белару́сь</i> (Bjelarúsʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bila Tserkva (a city in Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belgorod (a city, the administrative center of Belgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Бѣлъгородъ</i> (Bělŭgorodŭ, literally “white city”). Doublet of <i>Белгра́д</i> (Belgrád).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi (a city in Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belgrade (the capital city of Serbia)</li></ol><p>Borrowing from a South Slavic language. Doublet of <i>Бе́лгород</i> (Bélgorod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belize (an English-speaking country in Central America, formerly called British Honduras)</li><li>Belize City (the largest city in Belize)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Belkovsky, from Polish, equivalent to English Białkowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) Belovodye (in Russian folklore, a legendary country east of Russia or in eastern Russia)</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of two settlements in Russia&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Belogorovka (a khutor in Dyogtevo urban settlement, Millerovsky Raion, Rostov Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Belogorovka (a village in Radishchevo urban settlement, Radishchevsky Raion, Ulyanovsk Oblast, Volga Federal District, Russia)</li></ol><li>The name of a few settlements in Ukraine&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Bilohorivka (a village in Lysychansk hromada, Severodonetsk Raion, Lugansk Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1720)</li><li>Bilohorivka (a village in Soledar hromada, Bakhmut Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Bilohorivka (a former village in Krasnyi Lyman, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine, disestablished 24th July 2008)</li><li>Bilohorivka (a village in Kozyn hromada, Dubno Raion, Rovno Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></ol><p><i>белая</i> (belaja, “white”) +&lrm; <i>гора</i> (gora, “hill”) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka).</p><p>The village in Lugansk Oblast named after coastal marl formation of white color near the Donets River.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White Sea (a sea, an inlet of the Barents Sea and an arm of the Arctic Ocean, in northwestern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Belozyorov or Belozerov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belarus (a country in Eastern Europe) (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>From historical <i>Бе́лая Русь</i> (Bélaja Rusʹ, “White Ruthenia or White Rus”), from Old East Slavic <i>Бѣла Русь</i> (Běla Rusĭ, “White Ruthenia or White Rus”). Cognate with Belarusian <i>Белару́сь</i> (Bjelarúsʹ), later borrowed into Russian <i>Белару́сь</i> (Bjelarúsʹ) as an alternative name, now official. Compare with Ukrainian <i>Білору́сія</i> (Bilorúsija) and <i>Білору́сь</i> (Bilorúsʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) Belarusian People's Republic (a former country that controlled most of modern Belarus from 1918–19, and a government-in-exile to the present day; abbreviation БНР (BNR))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byelorussian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1919 to 1991)</li></ol><p><i>Белору́сская</i> (Belorússkaja, “Byelorussian”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Snow White (fairy tale by the Brothers Grimm)</li><li>Snow White (character from the fairy tale <i>Snow White</i>)</li><li>Snow White (character from the fairy tale <i>Snow-White and Rose-Red</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Białystok (the capital and largest city of Podlaskie Voivodeship, Poland; largest city in northeastern Poland)</li><li>Bilostok, Bilyi Stik (a river in Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Partial calque of Polish <i>Białystok</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Belousov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belfast (a city, the capital of Northern Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White House (the official residence and workplace of the President of the United States of America)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the government of the United States of America</li></ol><li>the House of the Government of the Russian Federation (also known as "the Russian White House" in English)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Federal Assembly of the Russian Federation</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belgium (a country in Western Europe that has borders with the Netherlands, Germany, Luxembourg and France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Бе́льгия</i> (Bélʹgija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belmopan (the capital city of Belize)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Belyavsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Belyayev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Benghazi (a city in Libya)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bengal (a region of South Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bay of Bengal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Benin (a country in West Africa, formerly Dahomey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Benoît</i></li><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Benoît</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berd (a town in Tavush Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Բերդ</i> (Berd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berdichev (a city in Zhytomyr Oblast, in north-western Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berd (a river in Altai Krai and Novosibirsk Oblast, Russia, a right tributary of the Ob)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berdiansk (a city, the administrative centre of Berdiansk Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Ivory Coast (a country in West Africa)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>Côte d'Ivoire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berehove&#47;Beregovo (a city, the administrative center of Zakarpattia Oblast, in western Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Berezovets, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berezivka (a city, the administrative center of Berezivka Raion, Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Byarozawka (a city in Hrodna Oblast, Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Berezovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Brest (a city in Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bering Sea (a marginal sea of Pacific Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beryslav (a city, the administrative centre of Beryslav Raion, Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berytus (an ancient city in modern Lebanon; modern Beirut)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Beria, from Mingrelian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mingrelian <i>ბერია</i> (beria).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Berkovich</li></ol><p>Likely stems from Berko, a diminutive of the Yiddish male name Ber (which means bear), or Baruch.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Italian surname <i>Berlusconi</i></li></ol><p>From Italian <i>Berlusconi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bern (de-facto capital of Switzerland)</li><li>Bern (a canton of Switzerland)</li><li>Berne (a town in Indiana, United States)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of German <i>Bern</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Beethoven</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bekhterev</li></ol><p>Perhaps somehow related with пе́хтерь (péxterʹ) or бе́хтерец (béxterec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Bzyb (a river in Abkhazia, Georgia).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pronunciation spelling of the English initialism <i>BBC</i> (the British Broadcasting Corporation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byblos (a port city in Keserwan-Jbeil Governorate, Lebanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>биг-мэ́к</i> (big-mɛ́k)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Biysk (a city in Altai Krai, Russia, on the Biya River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bikin (a town in Khabarovsk Krai, in the Far East of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burma (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Burmese <i>ဗမာ</i> (ba.ma), probably through another European language (compare German <i>Birma</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Birmingham (a major city and metropolitan borough of the West Midlands, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Birobidzhan (a town, the administrative center of the Jewish Autonomous Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bissau (the capital city of Guinea-Bissau)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bismarck (the capital city of North Dakota, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Bismarck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bitola (the second largest city in North Macedonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bishkek (the capital city of Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blagoveshchensk (a city, the administrative center of Amur Oblast in Siberia, Russia, on the border with China)</li></ol><p>From <i>благове́щение</i> (blagovéščenije, “Annunciation”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blagoevgrad (the capital city of Blagoevgrad province, Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geopolitics</i>) Middle East (the geopolitical region at the intersection of Asia, Africa and Europe, comprising West Asia excluding the South Caucasus and additionally including all of Egypt and all of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Gemini (constellation of the zodiac containing the stars Castor and Pollux, in the shape of a pair of twins)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Gemini (zodiac sign for the twins, covering May 21 – June 21)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Blokhin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bloemfontein (a city in South Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bogatyryov or Bogatyrev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bogdan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Bogъdanъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bogdanov</li><li>Bogdan's, Bohdan's</li></ol><p><i>Богда́н</i> (Bogdán) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bohemia (a cultural region in the west of the former Czechoslovakia and present-day Czech Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Боге́мия</i> (Bogémija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bohodukhiv (a city, the administrative center of Bohodukhiv Raion, Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bogomolov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bogota (the capital city of Colombia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bulgaria (a country in Southeast Europe; official name: Респу́блика Болга́рия (Respúblika Bolgárija))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Болга́рия</i> (Bolgárija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolhrad (a city, the administrative center of Bolhrad Raion, Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Boleslav</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolivia (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bolkonsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bollywood (the Hindi-language film industry located in Mumbai)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Bollywood</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bologoye (a city, a district center in Tver Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Derived from the name of nearby Lake <i>Бологое</i> (Bologoje), from Old East Slavic <i>болого</i> (bologo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolgo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ursa Major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;politics) the Group of Seven, G7 (the commonly used way of referring to the seven economically most important capitalist countries, which are the United States, Japan, Germany, France, United Kingdom, Italy and Canada, whose finance ministers meet several times a year to discuss economic policy)</li></ol><p>Literal mistaken translation of the English <i>the Great Seven</i>, instead of the actual "Group of Seven" (G7).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Salt Lake</li></ol><p>From <i>большо́е</i> (bolʹšóje) +&lrm; <i>солёный</i> (soljónyj) +&lrm; <i>о́зеро</i> (ózero).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Canis Major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;historical) the Great Purge</li></ol><p>Literally, “(The) Great Terror”. Akin to Polish <i>Wielki terror</i> and Ukrainian <i>Великий терор</i> (Velykyj teror).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bombay (former name of Mumbai, the capital city of Maharashtra, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bondarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bondarchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Бондарчу́к</i> (Bondarčúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bonn (an independent city in North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany, on the Rhine River; the former capital of West Germany and (until 1999) seat of government of unified Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Bonner, from Yiddish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname</li></ol><p>Derived from Ukrainian <i>Бордило</i> (Bordylo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borzunov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Boris, equivalent to English Boris</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Борисъ</i> (Borisŭ), the name of Boris I of Bulgaria, from Bulgar (Byzantine Greek <i>Βόγορις</i> (Bógoris)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borisov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Borisovich</li></ol><p><i>Бори́с</i> (Borís) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Borisovna</li></ol><p><i>Бори́с</i> (Borís) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borisoglebsk (a city in Voronezh Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Бори́с и Глеб</i> (Borís i Gleb, “Boris and Gleb”). By surface analysis, <i>Бори́с</i> (Borís) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Глеб</i> (Gleb) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boryspil (a city in Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>The first part is from the name of St.Boris. The other part is probably a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>πόλις</i> (pólis) (like in <i>Mariupol</i>, <i>Sevastopol</i> etc.)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borneo (a large island of Southeast Asia, politically divided between Malaysia, Brunei and Indonesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borovikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borodin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borodino (a town in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li><li>Borodino (several dozen villages across Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bortnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Borya, of the male given name Бори́с (Borís)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Бори́с</i> (Borís) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosnia (a geographic region of Bosnia and Herzegovina, consisting of the northern three fourths of the country)</li><li>Bosnia (a country in southeast Europe; in full, Бо́сния и Герцегови́на (Bósnija i Gercegovína))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosnia and Herzegovina (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Бо́сния</i> (Bósnija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosphorus (a strait in Turkey, which passes through Istanbul)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Botswana (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>бру́тто</i> (brútto): gross weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brazzaville (the capital city of the Republic of the Congo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brasília (a municipality, the capital city of Brazil; the state capital of Distrito Federal, Brazil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brazil (a large Portuguese-speaking country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Brian or Bryan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Braille (writing system for the blind invented by Louis Braille)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bratislava (the capital city of Slovakia)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Slovak <i>Bratislava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bratsk (a city in Irkutsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Брат</i> (Brat) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Brown</i></li><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Braun</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Breda (a city in North Brabant, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Brezhnev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bremen (a state of Germany)</li><li>Bremen (the capital city of the state of Bremen, Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brest (a city in Belarus)</li><li>Brest (a city in Brittany, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Brest</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brittany (a cultural region, historical province, and peninsula in northwest France)# Brittany (an administrative region of northwest France, including most of the historic region of Brittany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Bretagne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bridgetown (the capital city of Barbados)</li><li>Bridgetown (a town in Western Australia)</li><li>Bridgetown (a community in Nova Scotia, Canada)</li><li>Bridgetown (a village in Devon, England)</li><li>Bridgetown (a village in Somerset, England)</li><li>Bridgetown (a small village in County Clare, Ireland)</li><li>Bridgetown (a small rural village in County Wexford, Ireland)</li><li>Bridgetown (a census-designated place in Hamilton County, Ohio, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brisbane (a city in Queensland, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bristol (a city, county, and unitary authority in southwest England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>loosely</i>) Britain (the United Kingdom, a kingdom and country in Northern Europe including the island of Great Britain as well as Northern Ireland on the northeastern portion of the island of Ireland)# Britain (great Britain, a large island (sometimes also including some of the surrounding smaller islands) off the north-west coast of Western Europe, made up of England, Scotland, and Wales; especially (but not exclusively) during antiquity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) British Empire (a former empire ruled by the United Kingdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>British Isles (an archipelago of Northern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>British Commonwealth of Nations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Брита́ния</i> (Británija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brno (a city in Moravia, Czech Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brovary (a city, the administrative centre of Brovary Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Brodsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bronx (a borough of New York City, New York, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brooklyn (a borough of New York City)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brunei (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brunei (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brussels (the capital city of Belgium)</li></ol><p>From French <i>Bruxelles</i>, ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōk</i> (whence also English <i>brook</i>) + Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sali</i> (whence also <i>зал</i> (zal), <i>сало́н</i> (salón)) &lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saliz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bryansk (a city, the administrative center of Bryansk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Дьбрꙗньскъ</i> (Dĭbrjanĭskŭ), from the word <i>дъбрь</i> (dŭbrĭ), <i>дьбрь</i> (dĭbrĭ, “valley”) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьbrь</i> (“valley, ravine”) now Russian <i>де́бри</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (débri), the first consonant of which in the oikonym had to be lost to simplify the cluster resulting from Havlík's law.</p><p>Other formations from the same noun include <i>Брынь</i> (Brynʹ), the name of a river in a neighbouring province, a historical <i>Брыньскъ</i> (Brynĭskŭ) in Volhynia, apparently the current village <i>Бринь</i> (Brynʹ) in the Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, and various Polish settlement and waterbody names <i>Bryń</i>, <i>Brynica</i> <sup>WP</sup>, <i>Breń</i> <sup>WP</sup>, whence the family name <i>Bryński</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>бру́тто</i> (brútto): gross weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bug, Western Bug (a river in Poland, Belarus and Ukraine)</li><li>Bug, Southern Bug (a river in Ukraine)</li><li>Bug (a small tributary of the Mata River in Bashkortostan, Russia)</li></ol><p>Of Germanic origin, borrowed from Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (biugan, “to bend”), as Goths lived near the river. Compare Ukrainian <i>Буг</i> (Buh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Бу́г</i> (Búg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Budanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Budapest (the capital city of Hungary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bujumbura (the capital city of Burundi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucalemu (a town in Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bukovina (a historical region of Eastern Europe on the northern slopes of the northeastern Carpathian Mountains and adjoining plains, currently split between Romania and Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic, from Old Polish <i>Bukowina</i> or perhaps from Romanian <i>Bucovina</i> from Old Polish <i>Bukowina</i>, from <i>bukowina</i> (“beech forest”), probably independently formed from <i>buk</i> (“beech”) +&lrm; <i>-ow-</i> +&lrm; <i>-ina</i> (a now-obsolete suffix appended to trees to form nouns meaning "forest of...").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bulgakov</li><li>The former name, from its founding until 1917 (or before), of <i>Плюще́вка</i> (Pljuščévka).</li></ol><p>From <i>Булгак</i> (Bulgak)</p><p>From the surname.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bulychov or Bulychev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Belarus.</li></ol><p><i>бу́льба</i> (búlʹba, “potato”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan, land of”). Referring to the popularity of potatoes in Belarus.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bunin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bunkyō (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>文京</i> (ぶんきょう, Bunkyō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buratino (the main character of the book "The Golden Key, or the Adventures of Buratino")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burgas (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burgundy (a historical region and former administrative region of France; since 2016, part of the administrative region of Bourgogne-Franche-Comté)# Burgundy (an early-medieval kingdom and later former duchy in France and the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Бургу́ндия</i> (Burgúndija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Burdasov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burkina Faso (a country in West Africa, formerly Upper Volta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burkina Faso (a country in West Africa, formerly Upper Volta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bursa (a city and province of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burundi (a country in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buryatia (a republic and federal subject in southeast Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Butin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English or Maltese surname <i>Buttigieg</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Buttigieg</i>, from Maltese <i>Buttiġieġ</i>, or directly from Maltese. The pronunciation of the ending roughly corresponds to Maltese.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Butusov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bukhara (a city in Uzbekistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucharest (the capital and largest city of Romania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bukharin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bouches-du-Rhône (a department of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France; located in the historical region and province of Provence)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Bouches-du-Rhône</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buenos Aires (a seaport and capital of Argentina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia (former long form of <i>North Macedonia</i>: a country in Southeast Europe, on the Balkan Peninsula; used in diplomatic circles before the 2019 agreement that produced the name <i>North Macedonia</i> due to the naming dispute with Greece over the former name of the country, <i>Macedonia</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Byvshev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bydgoszcz (the capital and largest city of Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship, Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Bykov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byurakan (a major village in Aragatsotn Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Բյուրական</i> (Byurakan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byureghavan (a town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Բյուրեղավան</i> (Byureġavan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byoryolyokh (a river in the Sakha Republic, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Бөрөлөөх</i> (Börölööq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Белору́ссия</i> (Belorússija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>GDP; gross domestic product</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>валово́й вну́тренний проду́кт</i> (valovój vnútrennij prodúkt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air force</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>вое́нно-возду́шные си́лы</i> (vojénno-vozdúšnyje síly).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>Возду́шно-деса́нтные во́йска Росси́и</i> (Vozdúšno-desántnyje vójska Rossíi, “Russian Airborne Forces”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military slang,&#32;colloquial) paratrooper</li></ol><p><i>ВДВ</i> (VDV) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>вы́ставка достиже́ний наро́дного хозя́йства</i> (výstavka dostižénij naródnovo xozjájstva): VDNKh (USSR, all-Soviet Union exhibition centre), now called <i>Всеросси́йский вы́ставочный центр</i> (Vserossíjskij výstavočnyj centr) (ВВЦ - vɛ-vɛ-cé)</li><li>Name of the corresponding Metro station in Moscow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>вока́льно-инструмента́льный анса́мбль</i> (vokálʹno-instrumentálʹnyj ansámblʹ, literally “vocal and instrumental ensemble”); a pop or rock band</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>всеросси́йский институ́т нау́чной и техни́ческой информа́ции</i> (vserossíjskij institút naúčnoj i texníčeskoj informácii) (<i>All-Russian Institute of Scientific and Technical Information</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>HIV</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ви́рус иммунодефици́та челове́ка</i> (vírus immunodeficíta čelovéka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>всесоюзный ленинский коммунистический союз молодёжи</i> (vsesojuznyj leninskij kommunističeskij sojuz molodjóži, “All-Union Leninist Young Communist League”) (Komsomol)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navy</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>вое́нно-морско́й фло́т</i> (vojénno-morskój flót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residence permit</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>ви́д на жи́тельство</i> (víd na žítelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>GNP</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>валово́й национа́льный проду́кт</i> (valovój nacionálʹnyj prodúkt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Вели́кая Оте́чественная война́</i> (Velíkaja Otéčestvennaja vojná): the Great Patriotic War (WW II)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>вое́нно промы́шленный ко́мплекс</i> (vojénno promýšlennyj kómpleks, “Military–industrial complex (MIC)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>military revolutionary committee</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>вое́нно-революцио́нный комите́т</i> (vojénno-revoljuciónnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>armed forces</i>; abbreviation of <i>вооружённые силы</i> (vooružónnyje sily)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Высший совет народного хозяйства</i> (Vysšij sovet narodnovo xozjajstva, “Supreme Soviet of the National Economy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armed Forces of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Вооружённые си́лы Украи́ны</i> (Vooružónnyje síly Ukraíny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A member of the Armed Forces of Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>ВСУ</i> (VSU) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Всеми́рная торго́вая организа́ция</i> (Vsemírnaja torgóvaja organizácija): WTO (World Trade Organization)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>всеросси́йская фаши́стская организа́ция</i> (vserossíjskaja fašístskaja organizácija, “All-Russian Fascist Organization”) (<i>Former a Russian white émigré group led by Anastasy Vonsyatsky. It was based in Putnam, Connecticut, United States and was founded on May 10, 1933.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ARCEC (All-Russian Central Executive Committee)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Всеросси́йский центра́льный исполни́тельный комите́т</i> (Vserossíjskij centrálʹnyj ispolnítelʹnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheka, Vecheka (<i>forerunner of the KGB</i>)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Всеросси́йская чрезвыча́йная коми́ссия</i> (Vserossíjskaja črezvyčájnaja komíssija, “All-Russian Extraordinary Commission”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Armenian mythology</i>) Vahagn (god)</li><li>a male given name from Armenian, equivalent to English Vahagn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վահագն</i> (Vahagn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vavilov; particularly, a Soviet physicist who co-discovered the Vavilov-Cherenkov radiation in 1934 Sergey Vavilov, 24 March, 1891 – 25 January, 1951</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylon (an ancient city, the ancient capital of Babylonia in modern Iraq, built on the banks of the Euphrates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vagharshapat (a city in Armavir Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վաղարշապատ</i> (Vaġaršapat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vadim</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Вадимъ</i> (Vadimŭ), diminutive of <i>Вадимиръ</i> (Vadimirŭ); compare <i>вадить</i> (vaditʹ, “to argue; to tame”) (see Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vaditi</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vaduz (the capital city of Liechtenstein)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vayk (a town in Vayots Dzor Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վայք</i> (Vaykʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wyoming (a state of the United States, formerly a territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vayots Dzor (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վայոց Ձոր</i> (Vayocʻ Jor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wakayama (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Wakayama (the capital city of Wakayama Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>和歌山</i> (わかやま, Wakayama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wakkanai (a city in Sōya subprefecture, on the north coast of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>稚内</i> (わっかない, Wakkanai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) Bacchus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Bacchus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Βάκχος</i> (Bákkhos). Doublet of <i>Ба́хус</i> (Báxus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wallachia (a historical region and former principality in Eastern Europe, now part of southern Romania)# Wallachia, Moravian Wallachia (a cultural region in northeast Moravia, in the Czech Republic; in full, Moravian Wallachia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valday (a town and the administrative center in the Valdaysky District, Novgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Wałęsa</i></li></ol><p>From Polish <i>Wałęsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valencia (the capital city of the autonomous community of Valencia, Spain)</li><li>Valencia, Valencian Community (an autonomous community of Spain; in full, Valencian Community)# Valencia (a province of the autonomous community of Valencia, Spain)</li></ol><p>See Valencia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Valentine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Valentinus</i>, from <i>valeō</i> (“to be strong, healthy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Latin, of 20th century usage, equivalent to English Valentina</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Valentina</i>, female form of <i>Valentinus</i>, from valēns (“strong, healthy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Valera, of the male given names Вале́рий (Valérij), Валериа́н (Valerián), or Валерья́н (Valerʹján), equivalent to English Val</li><li>a diminutive, Valera, of the female given name Вале́рия (Valérija), equivalent to English Val</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Вале́рий</i> (Valérij) and related names + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Valery or Valeri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Valerius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Valeria, from Latin, equivalent to English Valerie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Valeria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Swedish surname <i>Wallenberg</i></li></ol><p>From Swedish <i>Wallenberg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valletta (the capital city of Malta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wallonia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Валло́ния</i> (Vallónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Val-d'Oise (a department of the Île-de-France region, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Val-d'Oise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Valya, of the male given name Валенти́н (Valentín), equivalent to English Val</li><li>a diminutive, Valya, of the female given name Валенти́на (Valentína), equivalent to English Val</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Валенти́н</i> (Valentín) or <i>Валенти́на</i> (Valentína) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Dutch surname <i>van Gogh</i></li><li>Vincent van Gogh, Dutch painter (1853-1890)</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>van Gogh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vanadzor (the capital city of Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վանաձոր</i> (Vanajor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Wanda</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Wanda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vendée (a department of Pays de la Loire, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vaneyevo (a village in Cherepovetsky district, Vologda Oblast, Russia).</li><li>Vaneyevo (a village in Sheksninsky district, Vologda Oblast, Russia).</li><li>Vaneyevo (a village in Vokhomsky district, Kostroma Oblast, Russia).</li><li>Vaneyevo (a village in Neysky district, Kostroma Oblast, Russia).</li><li>Vaneyevo (a village in Torzhoksky district, Tver Oblast, Russia).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vancouver (a seaport, the largest city in British Columbia, Canada)# Vancouver (a city, the county seat of Clark County, Washington, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vanuatu (a country and archipelago of Melanesia in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Vanya, of the male given names Ива́н (Iván) or Іва́н (Iván), equivalent to English Johnny or Jack</li><li>A Russian man.</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;specifically) An online troll who promotes views supportive of Russian nationalism or the 2022 invasion of Ukraine; a vatnik.</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p><p>From Ва́ня being a common Russian name for men.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varash (a city, the administrative center of Varash Raion, Rivne Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Varvara, equivalent to English Barbara</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros, “foreign”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Varvara's, Barbara's</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Varvarin</li></ol><p><i>Варва́ра</i> (Varvára) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vardenis (a town in Gegharkunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վարդենիս</i> (Vardenis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Varenka, of the female given name Ва́ря (Várja)</li></ol><p><i>Ва́р(я)</i> (Vár(ja)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Varlamov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varna (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Warta (a river in Poland)</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>Warta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vartanov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Վարդանյան</i> (Vardanyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Varfolomey or Varfolomei, equivalent to English Bartholomew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Warsaw (the capital city of Poland; the capital city of Masovian Voivodeship)</li><li>(politics,&#32;metonymic) the Polish government</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>Warszawa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian Sea (former name of the Baltic Sea, a sea in Northern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Vasilevsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vasily, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Basil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βασίλειος</i> (Basíleios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Vasilisa, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>βασίλισσα</i> (basílissa, “queen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vasilyev, Vasiliev, or Vasilliev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vasylivka (a city, the administrative centre of Vasylivka Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Named after a Russian general Vasiliy Popov (Василий Попов)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vasnetsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Vasya, of the male given name Васи́лий (Vasílij)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Васи́лий</i> (Vasílij) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vatican City (a city-state in Southern Europe, an enclave within the city of Rome, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ватика́н</i> (Vatikán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Washington, Washington, D.C. (the capital city of the United States)</li><li>Washington, Washington State (a state in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States)</li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Washington</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Washington</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Вашингто́н</i> (Vašingtón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Veduta</li></ol><p>Unknown. However, there are two highly questionable etymologies:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vedas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>वेद</i> (véda, “knowledge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vedyaykin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vesuvius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Vesuvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Veles (god of earth, livestock, and the underworld)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Велесъ</i> (Velesŭ). Likely cognate with <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) (compare <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) → <i>беле́сый</i> (belésyj)), thus ultimately deriving from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velь</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Greater Armenia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Great Patriotic War (1941–1945)</li></ol><p>Literally, “great war of the fatherland”. The term <i>Оте́чественная война́</i> (Otéčestvennaja vojná, “patriotic war”, literally “war of the fatherland”) (<i>оте́чество</i> (otéčestvo), Russia), was meant to represent a defensive war against an invader.</p><p>It was first used in 1844 to refer to Russian resistance against the French invasion in 1812. On the centenary of the French defeat, it became known as Вели́кая Оте́чественная война́. This term reappeared in the Pravda, the official newspaper of the Communist Party, on 23 June 1941, just a day after Nazi Germany invaded the Soviet Union, to motivate Soviet citizens to unite and fight against the invasion.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) "Great Russia", Muscovy (the predecessor of the Russian Empire as a successor state of Kyivan Rus)</li><li>(poetic,&#32;endearing) Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veliky Novgorod, formerly known simply as Novgorod (a city, the administrative center of Novgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>The medieval Novgorod Republic used the appellation <i>Господи́н Вели́кий Но́вгород</i> (Gospodín Velíkij Nóvgorod, “Lord Novgorod the Great”). The form <i>Вели́кий Но́вгород</i> (Velíkij Nóvgorod, “Novgorod the Great”) continued to be occasionally used over the following centuries to distinguish Novgorod from Nizhny Novgorod, and was adopted as Novgorod's official name in 1999.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Britain (a large island (sometimes also including some of the surrounding smaller islands) off the north-west coast of Western Europe, made up of England, Scotland, and Wales)# (<i>loosely</i>) Great Britain (the United Kingdom, a kingdom and country in Northern Europe including the island of Great Britain as well as Northern Ireland on the northeastern portion of the island of Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Великобрита́ния</i> (Velikobritánija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Grand Duchy of Moscow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Great Russia (the territories of "Russia proper", the land that formed the core of Muscovy and, later, Russia)</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>Μεγάλη Ῥωσσίᾱ</i> (Megálē Rhōssíā), attested since 1347. This term was coined by the Patriarch of Constantinople to differentiate it from "Little Russia" (Μικρὰ Ῥωσσίᾱ). Can be analysed as <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij, “greater”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Ро́ссия</i> (Róssija, “Rus, Russia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wellington (the capital city of New Zealand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beelzebub</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hungary (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol><p>Attested since 1698, from <i>венгр</i> (vengr) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija). Displaced Middle Russian <i>Венгры</i> (Vengry), a direct borrowing from Polish <i>Węgry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ве́нгрия</i> (Véngrija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Venedikt, from Latin, equivalent to English Benedict</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Byzantine Greek <i>Βενέδικτος</i> (Benédiktos), from Latin <i>Benedictus</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Venediktov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venus (planet)</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Venus (Roman god)</li><li>a female given name, Venera, from Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Venus, Veneris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venezuela (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venice (a port city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Venice and the region of Veneto, Italy; former capital of an independent republic)# Venice (a metropolitan city of Veneto, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Venice)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Вене́ция</i> (Venécija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Veniamin, from Byzantine Greek, equivalent to English Benjamin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Βενιαμίν</i> (Beniamín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ventimiglia (a city in Liguria, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Verbitskiy or Verbitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Verzilov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Wehrmacht</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wehrmacht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vermont (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Verona (a city in Veneto, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Veronica</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βερονίκη, Βερενίκη</i> (Beroníkē, Bereníkē), from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō) + <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē), as if bringer of victory.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Versailles (a city, commune, and prefecture of Yvelines department, Île-de-France, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Versailles</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hautes-Alpes (a department of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Verkhovyna (a town, the administrative centre of Verkhovyna Raion, Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Supreme Soviet (a bicameral parliament of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>Literally, “supreme council”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Verkhovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Verkhoyansk (a town in Sakha, Russia, known for being one of the coldest inhabited places on Earth)</li></ol><p>From <i>верх</i> (verx, “top”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Я́на</i> (Jána, “Yana (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Veselnitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Indies (a the islands of the Caribbean Sea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>West-Indië</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) Vesta (Roman god)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Vesta (asteroid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Merry Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vetluga (a river in Russia)</li><li>Vetluga (a town in Russia). The town is placed on the river.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Testament</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ве́тхий Заве́т</i> (Vétxij Zavét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vyzhnytsia (a city, the administrative center of Vyzhnytsia Raion, Chernivtsi Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Byzantium (an ancient Greek city situated on the Bosporus in modern Turkey, renamed Constantinople in 330 <small>C.E.</small>; modern Istanbul)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βῡζᾰ́ντῐον</i> (Būzắntĭon). Compare Ukrainian <i>Віза́нтій</i> (Vizántij), Belarusian <i>Віза́нтый</i> (Vizántyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Byzantine Empire (an ancient Greek empire in Southeastern Europe and Western Asia, with its capital at Constantinople)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wikimedia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wikinews</li></ol><p>Calque from English <i>Wikinews</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wikipedia</li></ol><p>From English <i>Wikipedia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wiktionary</li><li>Russian Wiktionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Viktor, equivalent to English Victor</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>victor</i>. Cognate with English <i>Victor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Victoria, from Latin, equivalent to English Victoria</li><li>Victoria (a state of Australia, located in the southeastern part of the continent; a former British colony from 1851 to 1901)# Victoria (a city, the capital of British Columbia)# Victoria (a city, the capital of Seychelles)# Victoria (a town and local council, the capital of the Gozo Region)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Victoria</i>, from <i>victōria</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Dutch surname <i>Wilders</i></li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>Wilders</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vilnius (the capital city of Lithuania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Windhoek (a city, the capital city of Namibia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Afrikaans/Dutch <i>Windhoek</i>, from <i>wind</i> (“wind”) +&lrm; <i>hoek</i> (“corner, angle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Winnie the Pooh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Winnie the Pooh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Winnipeg (the capital city of Manitoba, Canada)# Winnipeg (a large lake in Manitoba, Canada; in full, Lake Winnipeg)# Winnipeg (a major river in Ontario and Manitoba, Canada; in full, Winnipeg River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vinnytsia Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vinnytsia (a city, the administrative center of Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Virginia (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wisconsin (a state in the Upper Midwest region of the United States)</li><li>Wisconsin (a river in the United States that flows from northern Wisconsin into the Mississippi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vistula (the longest river in Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Wisła</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vissarion, equivalent to English Bessarion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vitali or Vitaly, from Ancient Greek &lt; Latin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>Βιτάλιος</i> (Vitálios), from Latin <i>Vītālis</i>, from Latin <i>vītālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Vitalina</li></ol><p>Feminine form of Виталий, from Latin <i>Vītālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vitebsk (the capital city of the Vitebsk region, Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vitkov</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Григо́рий Ива́нович Витко́в (1902–1975), Soviet state-security officer</li><li>Заха́р Ароно́вич Витко́в (1910–2006), Soviet historian and major in the Red Army</li><li>Бори́с Моисе́евич Витко́в (<i>fl.</i> 1949), Soviet photojournalist; father of Алекса́ндр Бори́сович Витко́в</li><li>Анато́лий Серге́евич Витко́в (1937–2009), Soviet association-football player in the defender position</li><li>Алекса́ндр Бори́сович Витко́в (<i>a.</i>&nbsp;1986–<i>p.</i>&nbsp;2008), Soviet photojournalist and museum director; son of Бори́с Моисе́евич Витко́в</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Czech surname <i>Vítková</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ве́роника Ви́ткова (born 1988), Czech former biathlete</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Витко́в</i> (Vitkóv).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Italian male given name <i>Vito</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>Ви́ктор</i> (Víktor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem (a city in Palestine)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>Βηθλεέμ</i> (Bēthleém), from Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bēṯ lɛ́ḥɛm, literally “house of bread”). Doublet of <i>бедлам</i> (bedlam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vikhryov or Vikhrev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Wiśniewski</i></li><li>a Russian surname, Vishnevsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vlad</li><li>a diminutive, Vlad, of the male given name Владисла́в (Vladisláv)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vladivostok (a city and seaport in the Russian Far East, on the Sea of Japan, near North Korea; the administrative centre of Primorsky Krai)</li></ol><p><i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ, “to possess, to be master of”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>восто́к</i> (vostók, “the East”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;diminutive) clipping of <i>Владивосто́к</i> (Vladivostók)</li><li>(colloquiall,&#32;diminutive) clipping of <i>Владикавка́з</i> (Vladikavkáz)</li><li>(colloquiall,&#32;diminutive) clipping of <i>Владисла́в</i> (Vladisláv)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vladikavkaz (the capital city of North Ossetia-Alania, Russia)</li></ol><p>From the imperative of the verb <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ, “to possess, to be master of”) + <i>Кавка́з</i> (Kavkáz, “Caucasus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a male given name, equivalent to English Vladimir</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Vladimir (a city, the administrative center of Vladimir Oblast, Russia)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Transliteration of Volodymyr (a city, the administrative center of Volodymyr Raion, Volyn Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Владимѣръ</i> (Vladiměrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Voldiměrъ</i> (where further etymology is listed). By surface analysis, <i>влад(еть)</i> (vlad(etʹ), “to master, to possess”) +&lrm; <i>мир</i> (mir, “peace”), with the second component changed by folk etymology from Old Church Slavonic <i>мѣръ</i> (měrŭ, “great”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vladimirov or Vladimiroff</li><li>Vladimir's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Vladimirovich</li></ol><p><i>Влади́мир</i> (Vladímir) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Vladimirovna</li></ol><p><i>Влади́мир</i> (Vladímir) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Влади́мир</i> (Vladímir).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vladislav</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Владиславъ</i> (Vladislavŭ), from Old Polish <i>Władysław</i>, from Old Czech <i>Vladislav</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Voldislavъ</i>, compare <i>&#42;volsti</i> (“to rule”) and <i>&#42;slava</i> (“fame, glory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vladlen</li></ol><p>Coined during Soviet times as an abbreviation of Влади́мир Ле́нин (Vladímir Lénin, “Vladimir Lenin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inner Mongolia (a Mongolian autonomous region of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Vova, of the male given name Влади́мир (Vladímir)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;specifically) Vladimir Putin, President of Russia</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Vovan, of the male given name Влади́мир (Vladímir)</li></ol><p><i>Во́ва</i> (Vóva) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Voevodin or Voyevodin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vojvodina (an autonomous province of Serbia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Voynovich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Вайно́віч (Vajnóvič)</li><li>a surname, Wojnowicz, from Polish</li><li>a surname, Vojnović, from Serbo-Croatian, equivalent to Serbo-Croatian Војновић &#47; Vojnović</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volga (a major river in Russia, the longest river in Europe)</li><li>Volga (mid-size Russian automobile often used as a taxi)</li><li>Volga (Russian rocket upper stage)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlga</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlgъkъ</i>, in which case related to <i>во́лглый</i> (vólglyj, “damp, humid”), <i>вла́га</i> (vlága, “moisture, humidity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Volgin</li></ol><p><i>Во́лга</i> (Vólga) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volgograd (an oblast in southwestern Russia)</li><li>Volgograd (a city, the administrative center of Volgograd Oblast, Russia); formerly Stalingrad, renamed in 1961 during the de-Stalinization campaign; situated on the west bank of the Volga River</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Volkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Volkonsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volnovakha (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vologda (a city, the administrative center of Vologda Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Вологда</i> (Vologda), ultimately probably from Proto-Uralic <i>&#42;wëlketa</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Volodin</li><li>Volodya's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Wołodyjowski</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Wołodyjowski</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Volodya, of the male given name Влади́мир (Vladímir)</li></ol><p>Clipping of Old East Slavic <i>Володимѣръ</i> (Volodiměrŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Voloshin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vovchansk (a city in Kharkiv Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volyn (an oblast in western Ukraine)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Volhynia (a geographic region of Eastern Europe, now divided between southeastern Poland, northwestern Ukraine, and southwestern Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vorkuta (a coal mining town in Komi, Russia, situated just north of the Arctic Circle)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the root whence Tundra Nenets <i>варк</i> (wark°, “bear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vorobyov or Vorobyev, equivalent to English Sparrow</li></ol><p><i>воробе́й</i> (vorobéj, “sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Voronezh (an oblast of Russia)</li><li>Voronezh (a city, the administrative center of Voronezh Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Voronezh (a left tributary of the Don in Tambov, Lipetsk and Voronezh Oblasts, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Воронажь</i> (Voronažĭ), <i>Воронѧжь</i> (Voronęžĭ), <i>Воронежь</i> (Voronežĭ). Vasmer connects with <i>вороно́й</i> (voronój, “black”) and compares with <i>Воронега</i> (Voronega), a river flowing into Lake Ladoga.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Voronin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vorontsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Voroshilov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>East Asia (a geographic region comprising the eastern part of Asia, inclusive of China proper, Japan, North Korea, South Korea, and Taiwan and sometimes inclusive of Mongolia and Tibet and some or all of Southeast Asia and the Philippines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;1949&ndash;1990) East Germany (a former Communist country of Central Europe, existing between 1949 and 1990)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eastern Europe (a socio-political geographical area of eastern Europe usually including the European countries to the east of Germany, Austria and Italy, and to the west of the Urals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>East China Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the East Siberian Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Samoa (an archipelago and overseas territory of the United States in Polynesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrénées-Orientales (a department in Occitania, France)</li></ol><p>From <i>восто́чный</i> (vostóčnyj, “eastern”) +&lrm; <i>Пирене́и</i> (Pirenéi, “Pyrenees”), a calque of French <i>Pyrénées-Orientales</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>East Timor (a country and island of Southeast Asia; capital: Dili)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>East Turkestan (a geographic region in northwestern China; in modern usage, a synonym of Xinjiang used by the East Turkestan independence movement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>International Women's Day</li></ol><p>Literally, “8th of March”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Wrangel, from German</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vračar (an affluent urban area and municipality of the city of Belgrade, Serbia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wrocław (the capital and largest city of Lower Silesian Voivodeship, Silesia, in what is now southwestern Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Wrocław</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vsevolod</li></ol><p>"Possessing all". From <i>все</i> (vse, “all, everybody”), <i>всё</i> (vsjo, “all, everything”) + -волод- (stem of <i>володе́ть</i> (volodétʹ), Old Russian form of <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ, “to possess”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>All-Russian Extraordinary Commission, Cheka (forerunner of the KGB)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) The Second World War, World War II (<i>the war from 1937/39 to 1945</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vyborg (a city in Leningrad Oblast, Russia, 130 kilometers northwest of Saint Petersburg)</li></ol><p>From Swedish <i>Viborg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vysotsky, equivalent to Polish Wysocki</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vyshhorod (a city, the administrative centre of Vyshhorod Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vientiane (the capital city of Laos)</li></ol><p>From French <i>Vientiane</i>, from Lao <i>ວຽງຈັນ</i> (wīang chan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vietnam (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vyatka (former name of Kirov, a city in Russia)</li><li>Vyatka (river in Kirov Oblast and Tatarstan, Russia, passing through the city of Kirov (formerly Vyatka))</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, presumably related to the ethnonym Vyada found on the upper Vyatka River and possibly the original name of the river, which may go back to Finnish <i>vento</i> (“slow, calm, deep”), used as a hydronym.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Vyacheslav</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Вѧчесла́въ</i> (Vęčeslávŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Vęťeslàvъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vęťьjь</i> (“greater, more”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;slàva</i> (“glory, fame”).</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>Вячасла́ў</i> (Vjačasláw), Ukrainian <i>В'ячеслав</i> (Vʺjačeslav), Old Novgorodian <i>Вѧцьсла́ве</i> (Vęcʹĭsláve), English <i>Wenceslaus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ве́на</i> (Véna).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Госуда́рственная автомоби́льная инспе́кция</i> (Gosudárstvennaja avtomobílʹnaja inspékcija): State Vehicle Inspection (State Patrol), road/traffic police</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>State Duma</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Госуда́рственная ду́ма</i> (Gosudárstvennaja dúma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>GDR, DDR, East Germany</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>GIBDD (State Automobile Inspectorate)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Госуда́рственная инспе́кция безопа́сности доро́жного движе́ния</i> (Gosudárstvennaja inspékcija bezopásnosti doróžnovo dvižénija, “State Traffic Safety Inspectorate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) civil code</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>гражда́нский ко́декс</i> (graždánskij kódeks, “civil code”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) State committee on the state of emergency (18 August to 21 August, 1991)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i><b>г</b>осуда́рственный <b>к</b>омите́т по <b>ч</b>резвыча́йному <b>п</b>оложе́нию</i> (<b>g</b>osudárstvennyj <b>k</b>omitét po <b>č</b>rezvyčájnomu <b>p</b>oložéniju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>ГЛОба́льная НАвигацио́нная Спу́тниковая Систе́ма</i> (GLObálʹnaja NAvigaciónnaja Spútnikovaja Sistéma): GLONASS, Global Navigation Satellite System (GPS)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>municipal department of people's education</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Городско́й отде́л наро́дного образова́ния</i> (Gorodskój otdél naródnovo obrazovánija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>госуда́рственный общеросси́йский станда́рт</i> (gosudárstvennyj obščerossíjskij standárt): ISO</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Госуда́рственная электрифика́ция Росси́и</i> (Gosudárstvennaja elektrifikácija Rossíi, “State Electrification of Russia”): GOELRO</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>гей-по́рно</i> (gej-pórno, “gay porn”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Гла́вное Разве́дывательное Управле́ние</i> (Glávnoje Razvédyvatelʹnoje Upravlénije, “Chief Intelligence Agency”): GRU (in Russia or Mongolia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuels and lubricants</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>горю́че-сма́зочные материа́лы</i> (gorjúče-smázočnyje materiály).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ready for Labour and Defence (of the USSR) (physical culture training program in the USSR)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>гото́в к труду́ и оборо́не</i> (gotóv k trudú i oboróne, literally “ready for labor and defense/defence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Гру́зия, Украи́на, Азербайджа́н, Молдо́ва</i> (Grúzija, Ukraína, Azerbajdžán/Azɛrbajdžán, Moldóva): GUAM (Georgia, Ukraine, Azerbaijan, Moldova)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Гуам)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guam (an island and overseas territory of the United States in Micronesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business&#32;law</i>) initialism of <i>госуда́рственно-ча́стное партнёрство</i> (gosudárstvenno-částnoje partnjórstvo, “public–private partnership”, literally “state–private partnership”): PPP</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general staff; abbreviation of <i>генштаб</i> (genštab, “<i>генера́льный штаб</i> (generálʹnyj štab)”)</li><li>main headquarters; abbreviation of <i>главного штаба</i> (glavnovo štaba)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydroelectric power station</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>гидроэлектроста́нция</i> (gidroelektrostáncija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Hague (a city and capital of South Holland, Netherlands; the administrative capital of the Netherlands)</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) The International Criminal Court, located in the Hague</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Dutch <i>Den Haag</i>, shortening of 's-Gravenhage.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from the Kipchak languages</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Габи́т Бикмухаме́тович Аргынба́ев (1856–1921), Bashkir sėsėn, quray-player, and collector of Bashkir folklore</li><li>Габи́т Махму́тович Мусре́пов (1902–1985), Soviet Kazakh writer and playwright, author of the libretto to the Kazakh opera <i>Kyz-Zhibek</i></li><li>Габи́т Анабаевич Кадырбе́ков (1906–1984), Soviet Kazakh statesman</li><li>Габи́т Абду́ллович Ахме́ров (1921–1996), Soviet Bashkir lieutenant in the Red Army, recipient in 1944 of the gold-star medal of the Hero of the Soviet Union</li><li>Габи́т Габду́ллович Мейрамов (born 1947), Soviet Kazakh doctor of sciences in medicine and diabetologist</li><li>Габи́т Бе́йбутович Койшыба́ев (born 1972), Kazakh diplomat</li><li>Габи́т Абдимажитович Сыздыкбеков (born 1980), Kazakh politician and diplomat</li></ol></ol><p>Adapting from Kazakh <i>Ғабит</i> (Ğabit), Tatar <i>Габит</i> (Ğäbit), Bashkir <i>Ғәбит</i> (Ğəbit), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Gabitovich, meaning “son of Габи́т (Gabít)”</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Дми́трий Габи́тович Абза́лов (born 1984), Russian political scientist, economist, and analyst</li></ol></ol><p><i>Габи́т</i> (Gabít) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gabon (a country in Central Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaborone (the capital city of Botswana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>Гава́йи</i> (Gaváji)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hawaii (an insular state of the United States, formerly a territory)</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) Hawaii, Hawaii Island (an island of Hawaii archipelago, Pacific Ocean, of Hawaii County, State of Hawaii, United States)</li><li>Hawaii, Hawaiian Islands (an archipelago of the Pacific Ocean, between North America and Oceania)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hawaiian <i>Hawaiʻi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hawaii, Hawaiian Islands (an archipelago of the Pacific Ocean, between North America and Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Havana (the capital city of Cuba)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gavar (a town, the capital of Gegharkunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Գավառ</i> (Gavaṙ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Le Havre (a city in France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Le Havre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gavriil, equivalent to English Gabriel</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Gabriel</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>Гаврїилъ</i> (Gavriilŭ), ultimately from Hebrew <i>גַּבְרִיאֵל</i> (gaḇrīʾēl). Doublet of <i>Гаври́ла</i> (Gavríla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gavrilov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gavril's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Гаври́л</i> (Gavríl), <i>Гаври́ла</i> (Gavríla), <i>Гаврии́л</i> (Gavriíl), etc. + <i>-овна</i> (-ovna, feminine patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gavrilyuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Гаврилюк</i> (Havryljuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gavrish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gagarin</li><li>Gagarin (a town in Gagarinsky district, Smolensk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) baker's bun, sixty-one in the lotto game</li></ol><p><i>гагара</i> (gagara) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gagauzia (an autonomous territorial unit of Moldova)</li></ol><p>From Gagauz <i>gagauz</i> (“Gagauz”) + <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gades (former name of <i>Ка́дис</i> (Kádis) (= Cadiz): a city in Andalusia, in southwestern Spain)</li><li>Hades (Ancient Greek god of the dead and king of the underworld)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gādēs</i>.</p><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaza (a city in Palestine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gazmanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haiti (a country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guyana (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gaidai, Gayday, Haidai, or Hayday, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gaydar or Gaidar, from Crimean Tatar &lt; Arabic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haisyn (a city, the administrative center of Haisyn Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Galina, from Ancient Greek</li><li>golf (<i>G</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>Γαλήνη</i> (Galíni) (a given name), from the Ancient Greek adjective <i>γαλήνη</i> (galḗnē, “calm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galicia (an autonomous community of Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halych (a city in Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Galich (a town in Kostroma Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Галичь</i> (Galičĭ), derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;galica</i> (“jackdaw”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galicia (former kingdom and geographical area in Central Europe, now divided between Poland and Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Га́лич</i> (Gálič) +&lrm; <i>-ина́</i> (-iná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Galkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gallyamov, from Tatar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Galya, of the female given name Гали́на (Galína)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Galyamin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gambia (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hamburg (a city in, and a state of, Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hamburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Гамсаху́рдия</i> (Gamsaxúrdija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>გამსახურდია</i> (gamsaxurdia)</li><li>a surname, Gamsakhurdia, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ghana (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ganges (a river in India and Bangladesh)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>गङ्गा</i> (gaṅgā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ganymede, moon of Jupiter</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ganymede</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanover (the capital city of Lower Saxony, Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gansu (a province in northwestern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kaohsiung (a city and special municipality in southern Taiwan, and the third largest city in Taiwan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Harvard</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fiction</i>) Gargantua (a giant with a very large appetite in François Rabelais' <i>Gargantua and Pantagruel</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>ღარიბაშვილი</i> (ɣaribašvili)</li><li>a surname, Garibashvili or Gharibashvili, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Garik, of the male given name Гео́ргий (Geórgij)</li><li>a diminutive, Garik, of the male given name И́горь (Ígorʹ)</li><li>a male given name, Garik, from Armenian</li></ol><p>From Armenian <i>Գարիկ</i> (Garik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Garin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harrisburg (the capital city of Pennsylvania, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Harrisburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Spanish, English, or Portuguese surname <i>García</i> or <i>Garcia</i></li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>García</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Garuda</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>गरुड</i> (garuḍa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Garfield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gascony (a region, former province, and medieval duchy in France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guadeloupe (an island at the northeastern edge of the Caribbean)# Guadeloupe (an archipelago, overseas department, and administrative region of France, including the island of Guadeloupe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guatemala (a country in northern Central America)</li><li>Guatemala City (the capital city of Guatemala)</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed directly from Spanish <i>Guatemala</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guinea (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guinea-Bissau (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gdańsk (the capital city of Pomeranian Voivodeship, Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gdynia (a city and port in northern Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Spanish surname <i>Guevara</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Guevara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heidelberg (a city and urban district of Baden-Württemberg, Germany; the administrative seat of Rhein-Neckar-Kreis district, Baden-Württemberg)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Heidelberg</i>, following the now dated German–Russian practical transcription system.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) Europe, "Gayrope" (<i>a derogatory term ridiculing Europe's tolerance toward gays</i>)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>гей</i> (gej, “gay”) +&lrm; <i>Евро́па</i> (Jevrópa, “Europe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hecate</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἑκάτη</i> (Hekátē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gelderland (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gelendzhik (a resort town in Krasnodar Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heliogabalus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>clipped compound of <i>Генера́льная Ассамбле́я ОО́Н</i> (Generálʹnaja Assambléja OÓN)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>General Winter, personification of the harsh Russian winter as an enemy of invading armies.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Nations General Assembly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Henichesk (a city, the administrative centre of Henichesk Raion, Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gennady or Gennadi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γεννάδιος</i> (Gennádios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Genrikh, equivalent to English Henry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Heinrich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Genoa (a port city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Genoa and the region of Liguria, Italy)</li><li>Genoa (a metropolitan city of Liguria, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Genoa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Georgiev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Georgy, equivalent to English George</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γεώργιος</i> (Geṓrgios, “farmer, earth-worker”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hera (wife of Zeus)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) heroin</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἥρα</i> (Hḗra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Heracles</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heraclitus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gerasim, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γεράσιμος</i> (Gerásimos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gerasimov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gerashchenko or Herashchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Gerda</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly from some Germanic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Gerdt, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Herdt</li><li>a surname from German, equivalent to English Herdt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herculaneum (a Roman town in Italy, buried under a pyroclastic flow in the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Pillars of Hercules</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Herman, equivalent to English Herman or German Hermann</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Герма́ния</i> (Germánija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hermes</li><li>Hermes (family of modularly designed guided missiles produced in Russia)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Герме́с</i> (Germés).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Геродо́т</i> (Gerodót).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herzegovina (a historical region consisting of the southern one-fourth of Bosnia and Herzegovina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Herzen</i></li><li>a surname, Gertsen, from German, equivalent to English Herzen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesiod (an ancient Greek epic poet and rhapsodist of the 8th century <small>BCE</small>, known as the first author of didactic verse)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἡσίοδος</i> (Hēsíodos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hestia (goddess of hearth and household)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἑστία</i> (Hestía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hephaestus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἥφαιστος</i> (Hḗphaistos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Гефе́ст</i> (Gefést).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gegharkunik (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Գեղարքունիք</i> (Geġarkʻunikʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Gaea, Gaia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γαῖα</i> (Gaîa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gibraltar (a peninsula, city, and overseas territory of the United Kingdom, at the southern end of Iberia)# Gibraltar, Strait of Gibraltar (a strait connecting the Mediterranean to the Atlantic between Gibraltar and Morocco; in full, Strait of Gibraltar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Иерако́нполь</i> (Ijerakónpolʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gilgamesh</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Akkadian <i>𒄑𒂆𒈦</i> (Gilgameš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Himalayas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyperborea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gipuzkoa (a province and historical region of the Basque Country, Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Guipúzcoa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Girenko or Hirenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jilin (a province of China)# Jilin, Jilin City (a prefecture-level city of Jilin, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Girkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Hitler</i></li><li>Adolf Hitler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hitler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gifu (a prefecture in southern Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>岐阜</i> (ぎふ, Gifu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulag (Chief Administration of Corrective-Labor Camps, or Main Directorate for Places of Detention, or Main Administration of Corrective Labor Camps, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glasgow (a major city and council area of Scotland, largest city in Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gleb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Гълѣбъ</i> (Gŭlěbŭ), from Old Norse <i>Guðleifr</i> (literally “god's heir”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gliwice (a city in Silesian Voivodeship, in southern Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Glikeriya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γλυκερία</i> (Glukería), from γλῠκεῖᾰ (glŭkeîă, “sweet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Glinka; the surname of the Russian composer Mikhail Glinka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gluzsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Glukhovsky</li><li>a surname from Polish, equivalent to Polish Głuchowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goa (a state in western India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gobi (desert)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Godunov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorzów Wielkopolski (one of two capitals of Lubusz Voivodeship, Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Gozman, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Hosmann</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Golan Heights</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gold Coast (a city in Queensland, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Golitsyn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holland (a historical province of the Netherlands)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) Holland, the Netherlands (a country in Western Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Голла́ндия</i> (Gollándija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hollywood (an area of Los Angeles, California, United States)</li><li>Hollywood (the American motion picture industry)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Ukrainian surname, Holoborodko or Goloborodko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holovanivsk (an urban-type settlement, the administrative center of Holovanivsk Raion, Kirovohrad Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Goldshtayn, from German, equivalent to English Goldstein</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>Goldstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gulf Stream (warm ocean current)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goliyanovo (a district of Moscow, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gomel (a city in Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Хомс</i> (Xoms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Гомс</i> (Goms).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gondwana, Gondwanaland (hypothetical ancient supercontinent in the Southern Hemisphere, evolved from Laurasia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Honduras (a country in Central America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hong Kong (a city, island and special administrative region in southeastern China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Hong Kong</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Honolulu (a city in the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Goncharenko or Honcharenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Goncharov, originating as an occupation</li></ol><p>From <i>гончар</i> (gončar, “potter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gorbachev or Gorbachov</li></ol><p>From <i>горба́ч</i> (gorbáč, “humpback”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gordeyev or Gordeev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Gordias</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γορδίας</i> (Gordías).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Gordon</i></li><li>a surname from English, equivalent to English Gordon</li><li>a Jewish surname, Gordon, equivalent to English Gordon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gorinov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goris (a town in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Գորիս</i> (Goris).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horishni Plavni (a city in Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horki (a town in Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horlivka (a city in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorno-Altaysk (the capital city of the Altai Republic, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gornozavodsk (a town in Perm Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>го́рный</i> (górnyj, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>заво́д</i> (zavód, “factory”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk, toponymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>State Duma (lower house of Russian national parliament)</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>Госуда́рственная ду́ма</i> (Gosudárstvennaja dúma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gosplan</li></ol><p>Short form from <i><b>Гос</b>уда́рственный <b>пла́н</b>овый комите́т</i> (<b>Gos</b>udárstvennyj <b>plán</b>ovyj komitét, “State Planning Committee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hostomel (a rural settlement, the administrative centre of the settlement hromada of the same name of Bucha Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine, first mentioned in 1494, designated a hero city on the 6th of March 2022)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>State Vehicle Inspection (State Patrol), road/traffic police</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>State of Israel (official name of <i>Israel</i>: a country in Western Asia, in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>State of Libya (official name of <i>Libya</i>: a country in North Africa)</li></ol><p>Calque of Arabic دولة ليبيا.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Grabski</i></li><li>a surname, Grabsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gradsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grasse (a city, commune, and subprefecture of Alpes-Maritimes department, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Grasse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Grachov or Grachev, equivalent to English Rook</li></ol><p><i>грач</i> (grač, “rook”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Grebenshchikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grenada (an island and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grenelle (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Grenelle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greenland (a large self-governing island and dependent territory of Denmark, in North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greece (a country in Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>Греція</i> (Grecija) and <i>Грецыя</i> (Grecyja) (mid-17th c.), from Middle Polish <i>Grecja</i>, from Latin <i>Graecia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Γραικός</i> (Graikós), of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Гре́ция</i> (Grécija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gribov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Griboyedov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Grigory, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Gregory</li><li>Golf (<i>G</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γρηγόριος</i> (Grēgórios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greenwich (a city in England, famous for the global time standard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Grinko or Hrynko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Grisha, of the male given name Григо́рий (Grigórij), equivalent to English Greg</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Григо́рий</i> (Grigórij) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grodno (the capital city of the Grodno region, Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Grozev, from Bulgarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gromov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hromokliia (a river, a right tributary of the Inhul, having its course entirely within the Kropyvnytskyi Raion of Kirovohrad Oblast and the Bashtanka Raion of Mykolaiv Oblast of Ukraine)</li><li>a Jewish agricultural settlement in Yelysavethrad povit, Kherson Governorate, the Russian Empire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gromyko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>A Ukrainian surname.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Groningen (the capital city of Groningen, Netherlands)</li><li>Groningen (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Grudinin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1936 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgia (a transcontinental country in the Caucasus region of Europe and Asia, on the coast of the Black Sea, often considered to belong politically to Europe)</li></ol><p>Modern formation from <i>грузи́н</i> (gruzín) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Гру́зия</i> (Grúzija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Гру́ша (Grúša)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) oh my God, OMG</li></ol><p>Shortening of Го́споди (Góspodi, “Oh God”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guangdong (a populous province of China, located on the southern coast)</li></ol><p>From Mandarin <i>廣東</i>&nbsp;/ <i>广东</i> (Guǎngdōng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guangxi (a Zhuang autonomous region of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guangzhou (the capital city of Guangdong, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Guberman, from Yiddish</li><li>a surname from German, equivalent to English Huberman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Hoover</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Hoover</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Google</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gudermes (a city in Chechnya, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chechen <i>Гутӏермаӏас</i> (Gutʼermaˀas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gujarat (a state in western India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>dated form of <i>Гуджара́т</i> (Gudžarát)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish گجرات (Gücerât), from Persian گجرات (gojarât).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gudkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guizhou (a province in southwestern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Huliaipole (a small city in Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Humenyuk or Gumenyuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gunma (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Gunma (the capital city of Gunma Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>群馬</i> (ぐんま, Gunma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gundyayev or Gundyaev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Gurevich</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Guriev or Guriyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gurchenko or Hurchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Gusev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gusinoozyorsk (a town in Selenginsky district, Buryatia, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>гуси́ный</i> (gusínyj, “goose”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>о́зеро</i> (ózero, “lake”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk, toponymic suffix), named after Гуси́ное о́зеро (Gusínoje ózero), a nearby lake.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gandalf</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Gandalf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gyumri (a city, the administrative center of Shirak Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Գյումրի</i> (Gyumri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gothenburg (a city in Sweden)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>Göteborg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>DNA</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>дезоксирибонуклеи́новая кислота́</i> (dɛzoksiribonukleínovaja kislotá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Доне́цкая Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Donéckaja Naródnaja Respúblika, “Donetsk People's Republic”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Voluntary Society for Assistance to the Army, Air Force, and Navy</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Доброво́льное о́бщество соде́йствия а́рмии, авиа́ции и фло́ту</i> (Dobrovólʹnoje óbščestvo sodéjstvija ármii, aviácii i flótu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>диверсио́нно-разве́дывательная гру́ппа</i> (diversiónno-razvédyvatelʹnaja grúppa, “sabotage and reconnaissance group”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Договор о ликвидации ракет средней и меньшей дальности</i> (Dogovor o likvidacii raket srednej i menʹšej dalʹnosti): Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>traffic accident</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>доро́жно-тра́нспортное происше́ствие</i> (doróžno-tránsportnoje proisšéstvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English David</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) David</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dagestan (a republic and federal subject of Russia, located in the Caucasus region)</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish داغستان (Dağıstan), from Ottoman Turkish داغ (dağ, “mountain”) +&lrm; Classical Persian ـِسْتَان (-istān, “-land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dadin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Dažbog (Slavic god of the sun)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Дажьбогъ</i> (Dažĭbogŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Daďьbogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dakar (the capital city of Senegal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dhaka (the capital city of Bangladesh)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hulun (a lake in Inner Mongolia, China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolian <i>Далай нуур</i> (Dalaj nuur), from <i>далай</i> (dalaj) and <i>нуур</i> (nuur, “lake”). Doublet of <i>Чжалайнор</i> (Čžalajnor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dallas (a major city in Texas, United States)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Dalnegorsk, Primorski Krai, a city in Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatovo (a town in Dalmatovsky district, Kurgan Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Далма́тов</i> (Dalmátov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatia (a historical region of Croatia, on the eastern coast of the Adriatic Sea)# (<i>historical</i>) Dalmatia (a province of the Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Dalmatia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Far East (a geographical region of Asia, consisting of East Asia (Korea, Japan, China, the Russian Far East, etc.) and Southeast Asia, taken as a whole)</li><li>the Russian Far East</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalian (a city in Liaoning, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Damascus (the capital city of Syria; an ancient settlement, the ancient capital of various polities, most notably the Umayyad Caliphate from 661 to 744 <small>CE</small> and Aram-Damascus, existing from the 12th to 8th centuries <small>BCE</small>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Damian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Δαμιανός</i> (Damianós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Danelaw</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Danish <i>Danelagen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Daniil, equivalent to English Daniel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Дании́л</i> (Daniíl)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Дании́л</i> (Daniíl)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Denmark (a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Attested ca. 1702. Borrowed from Polish <i>Dania</i> (“Denmark”). Displaced Old East Slavic <i>Донь</i> (Donĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Да́ния</i> (Dánija).: Denmark (a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dar es Salaam (the largest city and former capital of Tanzania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Darwin</i></li><li>Darwin (the capital city of the Northern Territory, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dardanelles (strait)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Darya, equivalent to English Daria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dastakert (a village in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Դաստակերտ</i> (Dastakert).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Date (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>伊達</i> (だて, Date).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daugavpils (a city in Latvia)</li></ol><p>From Latvian <i>Daugavpils</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) The Donetsk People's Republic.</li><li>(Ukraine,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) The pro-Russian separatists of the Donbass region in eastern Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From да́ун ("a person with Down's syndrome, retard, stupid") and Донба́сс (Donbáss, “Donbass, Ukrainian area held by Russian separatists”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Dasha, of the female given name Да́рья (Dárʹja)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Да́рья</i> (Dárʹja) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Да́ша (Dáša)</li></ol><p><i>Да́ш(а)</i> (Dáš(a)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daugava (a river in Russia, Belarus and Latvia)</li><li>Northern Dvina (a river in Russia)</li></ol><p>Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvina</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰweynā</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dvořák</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Czech <i>Dvořák</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Des Moines (the capital city of Iowa, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Des Moines</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Devon (a county of England)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Devon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Degtyaryov or Degtyarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Father Christmas, Father Frost (equivalent to Santa Claus).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing</i>) Grandfather Frost, Father Frost (equivalent to our Saint Nicholas).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Dezhnyov or Dezhnev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Désiré</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delaware (the first state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delhi (a megacity and union territory of India, containing the national capital New Delhi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dementyev or Dementiev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Demeter</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Democratic Republic of the Congo (a country in Central Africa, the larger of the two countries named <i>Congo</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Democritus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Demyan</li></ol><p>Vernacular form of <i>Дамиа́н</i> (Damián).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Denver (the capital city of Colorado, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Denver</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Denikin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dennis</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Διόνυσος</i> (Diónusos).</p><p>Compare typologically:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Denisov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Denis's, Dennis's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Knowledge Day, the start of the school year observed in Russia on September 1.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Victory Day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thanksgiving, Thanksgiving Day (a holiday)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>April Fools' Day (First day of April)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>April Fools' Day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Дербе́нт</i> (Derbént)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Дербе́нд</i> (Derbénd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derbent (a city in Dagestan, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دربند (Derbend, Derbent), from Persian دربند (darband, literally “bar of a door, bolt; a barrier”), from در (dar, “door, gate, passage”) +&lrm; بند (band, “band, tie, fastening”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Deripaska, from Belarusian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Detroit (a city in Michigan, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Detroit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Juggernaut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jakarta (the capital city of Indonesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Jalil; particularly, Soviet Tatar poet and resistance fighter, Hero of the Soviet Union Musa Jalil, February 15, 1906 – August 25, 1944</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>Җәлил</i> (Cälil).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English James</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jack the Ripper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Italian.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Gemma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English female given name <i>Jenny</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jermuk (a town, a spa town in Vayots Dzor Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ջերմուկ</i> (J̌ermuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Jefferson</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Djibouti (a country in East Africa)</li><li>Djibouti (the capital city of Djibouti)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Ջիգարխանյան</i> (J̌igarxanyan)</li><li>a surname, Jigarkhanyan or Dzhigarkhanyan, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Everest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tibetan <i>ཇོ་མོ་གླང་མ</i> (jo mo glang ma, “mother of the universe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English John</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>John</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Johnson</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Johnson</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unknown-gender given name from English, equivalent to English George</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgia (a state in the Southern United States)</li><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;ironically) Georgia (a transcontinental country in the Caucasus region of Europe and Asia, on the coast of the Black Sea, often considered to belong politically to Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgetown (the capital city of Guyana)</li><li>Georgetown, a district in Washington, D.C.</li><li>Georgetown, a city in Kentucky, USA, with a population of about 21,000.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Juba (the capital city of South Sudan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>ჯუღაშვილი</i> (ǯuɣašvili)</li><li>a surname, Jughashvili or Dzhugashvili, from Georgian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ჯუღაშვილი</i> (ǯuɣašvili).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literature</i>) Juliet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Giulietta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dzungaria (a geographical region in northwestern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jackson (the capital city of Mississippi, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Jackson</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Dzaudzhikau (former name of <i>Владикавка́з</i> (Vladikavkáz) (= Vladikavkaz): the capital city of North Ossetia-Alania, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ossetian <i>Дзӕ́уджыхъӕу</i> (Ʒǽuǵyqæw).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dzerzhinsky, from Polish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Dzierżyński</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dzyadko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Diana</li><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Diana</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>Diāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Dianochka, of the female given name Диа́на (Diána)</li></ol><p><i>Диа́на</i> (Diána) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dido</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dijon (a city in Côte-d'Or department, Bourgogne-Franche-Comté, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Dijon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dykanka, Dikanka (a rural settlement in Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Dickens</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Dickens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dili (the capital city of East Timor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dilijan (a town in Tavush Province, Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Dima, of the male given names Дми́трий (Dmítrij) or Дими́трий (Dimítrij)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Дими́трий</i> (Dimítrij) + <i>-а</i> (-a, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dima, Dima's</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dimin (or Dimina)</li></ol><p><i>Ди́м</i> (Dím) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dimon, (<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) a diminutive of the male given name Дми́трий (Dmítrij)</li></ol><p><i>Ди́ма</i> (Díma) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diodorus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek Ancient Greek <i>Διόδωρος</i> (Diódōros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Dione</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Dione, moon of Saturn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Dionysus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Διόνυσος</i> (Diónusos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dietzgen, from German; particularly, the German socialist philosopher, marxist and journalist Joseph Dietzgen, October 28, 1828 – April 15, 1888</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dmitriev</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dmitriy's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Dmitry or its forms, equivalent to English Demetrius, Ukrainian Дмитро (Dmytro), or Belarusian Дзмітрый (Dzmitryj)</li><li>a transliteration of the Belarusian male given name <i>Дзмітрый</i> (Dzmitryj)</li><li>a transliteration of the Ukrainian male given name <i>Дмитро</i> (Dmytro)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Δημήτριος</i> (Dēmḗtrios), from <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr, “Demeter”).</p><p>Compare typologically:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dmitrov (city in Russia, near Moscow)</li></ol><p>From <i>Дми́трий</i> (Dmítrij) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov); the patron saint of the city's founder was Saint Demetrios.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Дми́триев</i> (Dmítrijev).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dnieper (a river in Russia, Belarus and Ukraine)</li><li>Dnipro (a city in Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Дънѣпръ</i> (Dŭněprŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Dъněprъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>former name of <i>Каменское</i> (Kamenskoje, “Dniprodzerzhynsk”)</li></ol><p><i>Днепр</i> (Dnepr, “Dnieper”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Дзержи́нск(ий)</i> (Dzeržínsk(ij), “Felix Dzerzhinsky”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dnipropetrovsk (former name of <i>Днепр</i> (Dnepr) (= Dnipro): a city in Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Днепр</i> (Dnepr, “Dnieper”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Петро́вск(ий)</i> (Petróvsk(ij), “Grigory Petrovsky”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dnipropetrovsk Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dniester (a river in Ukraine and Moldova)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Дънѣстръ</i> (Dŭněstrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Dъněstrъ</i> of Iranian origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Дне́пр</i> (Dnépr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Дне́стр</i> (Dnéstr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Dobronravov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dobrokhotov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dover (the capital city of Delaware, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Dover</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dovlatov, from Bashkir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dodoma (the capital city of Tanzania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dodonovo, a village near Zheleznogorsk, Krasnoyarsk Krai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dolgikh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dovzhansk (a city, the administrative center of Dovzhansk Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Dolores, from Spanish</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>Dolores</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palace of Culture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dominica (an island and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dominican Republic (a country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Domodedovo (an airport in Moscow, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Donald</i></li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Donald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donald Duck (Disney character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Donald Duck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;politics) Donetsk People's Republic</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Доне́цк</i> (Donéck, “Donetsk”) +&lrm; <i>Зимба́бве</i> (Zimbábve, “Zimbabwe”). References either the poor living conditions in Donetsk People's Republic (akin to those of poor African countries) or the "primitive mind" of its anti-Ukrainian inhabitants.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donbas or Donbass (Donets Basin)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) the DPR and the LPR separatist states as a collective.</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>доне́цкий бассе́йн</i> (donéckij basséjn/bassɛ́jn, “Donets Basin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donetsk Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of several rivers in Eastern Europe, including but not limited to:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Donets (a river, the Seversky Donets, which flows through Russia and Ukraine into the Don River)</li></ol></ol><p>Of Iranian origin, related to the Proto-Sarmatian origin of the river <i>Dniester</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donetsk (a city in Ukraine)</li><li>clipping of <i>Доне́цкая Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Donéckaja Naródnaja Respúblika, “Donetsk People's Republic”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donetsk People's Republic (a self-proclaimed quasi-state, internationally recognized as part of country of Ukraine; one of two members of the Confederation of Novorossiya.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donetsk–Krivoy Rog Soviet Republic (a Donetsk Oblast in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dordrecht (a city in South Holland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Dorofey, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Δωρόθεος</i> (Dōrótheos, “God’s gift”). Compare <i>Фёдор</i> (Fjódor), which uses the same roots in the reverse order.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dortmund (a major city in Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Dostoevsky or Dostoyevsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dauphiné</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Dauphiné</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drava (a tributary of the Danube River in Italy, Austria, Slovenia, Croatia and Hungary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Dragunov</i>: a masculine surname; masculine of <i>Драгунова</i> (Dragunova)</li></ol><p><i>драгу́н</i> (dragún) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a feminine surname, Dragunova</li></ol><p><i>Драгунов</i> (Dragunov) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ancient Rome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ancient Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drenthe (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>Drenthe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drina (a river in Serbia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drohobych (a city, the administrative center of Drohobych Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Drozdov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dubai (an emirate of the United Arab Emirates)</li><li>Dubai (a city in the United Arab Emirates; the capital of Dubai emirate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dubinsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dublin (the capital city of Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dubna (a city in Moscow Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Substantivisation of Old East Slavic <i>дубьнъ</i> (dubĭnŭ), from <i>дубъ</i> (dubŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dubno (a city, the administrative center of Dubno Raion, Rivne Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dubrovnik (a city and port in southern Croatia, on the Adriatic Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dubrovsky or Dubrovski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dover (a town and port in Kent, in southeast England)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Douvres</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Dugin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Chechen surname, Dudayev or Dudaev, from Chechen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danube (The Danube (or the Danube River) is a river that flows along its course through 10 countries: Germany, Austria, Slovakia, Hungary, Croatia, Serbia, Bulgaria, Romania, Moldova and Ukraine; the second-longest river in Europe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Дунаи</i> (Dunai), <i>Доунаи</i> (Dunai), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Dunavь</i>, from Gothic <i>&#42;𐌳𐍉𐌽𐌰𐍅𐌹</i> (&#42;dōnawi), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Dōnawjaz</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Dānowyos</i> (whence also <i>Danubius</i>), an extended form of the river-name <i>&#42;Dānu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₂nu</i> (“river goddess”), akin to <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i> (“to set in motion; to flow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Duntsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar&#32;slang,&#32;humorous) Rosie Palmer and her five sisters; an imaginary woman's name used as a euphemism for male masturbation.</li></ol><p><i>Дунька</i> (Dunʹka) is the pejorative form of the peasant woman’s name <i>Евдокия</i> (Jevdokija); <i>Кулакова</i> (Kulakova) is the feminine form of the surname <i>Кулаков</i> (Kulakov), which itself is formed from the possessive of <i>кулак</i> (kulak, “fist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Durban (a city in South Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Durnovo, a number of placenames in Russia</li><li>a gender-neutral surname, Durnovo</li></ol><p>From <i>дурной</i> (durnoj) +&lrm; <i>-ово</i> (-ovo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Durov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dushanbe (the capital city of Tajikistan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tajik <i>Душанбе</i> (Dušanbe).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dyakonov or Diakonoff, originating as an occupation</li></ol><p>From <i>дья́кон</i> (dʹjákon, “deacon”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dyachenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Дяче́нко</i> (Djačénko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English David</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thumbelina</li></ol><p><i>дюймо́вый</i> (djujmóvyj, “inch”) (<i>relational</i>) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Düsseldorf (a city in Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dyachok</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Deux-Sèvres (a department of the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Dyomushkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Евре́йская автоно́мная о́бласть</i> (Jevréjskaja avtonómnaja óblastʹ, “Jewish Autonomous Oblast”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Еврази́йский сою́з</i> (Jevrazíjskij sojúz): Eurasian Union, EAU</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) initialism of <i>еди́ный госуда́рственный экза́мен</i>&nbsp;m (jedínyj gosudárstvennyj ekzámen): USE, Unified State Exam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Екатеринбу́рг</i> (Jekaterinbúrg)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>EU</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Европе́йский сою́з</i> (Jevropéjskij sojúz, “European Union”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>European Court of Human Rights</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Европе́йский суд по права́м челове́ка</i> (Jevropéjskij sud po pravám čelovéka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) initialism of <i>Европейское экономическое сообщество</i> (Jevropejskoje ekonomičeskoje soobščestvo, “European Economic Community”); EEC.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) initialism of <i>единая энергетическая система</i> (jedinaja energetičeskaja sistema, “unified energy system”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;vulgar) Belarus</li></ol><p>Deliberate misspelling of <i>Белару́сь</i> (Belarúsʹ). Can be interpreted as a pun: <i>ебло́</i> (jebló, “(<i>vulgar</i>) a face, a mug, fuckface”) +&lrm; <i>Белару́сь</i> (Belarúsʹ, “Belarus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek &lt; Hebrew, equivalent to English Eva or Eve</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Eve (the first woman)</li><li>(<i>cryptography</i>) Eve (an agent attempting to intercept a message)</li><li>(<i>fandom slang</i>) Neon Genesis Evangelion, a Japanese science fiction animation series.</li><li>(fandom slang,&#32;science fiction) The "Evangelions", giant artificial humanoids used to fight supernatural monsters.</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Εὔα</i> (Eúa), from Biblical Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (ḥawwā).</p><p>Clipping of Евангелион (Jevangelion), from Japanese <i>エヴァンゲリオン</i>, then English <i>Evangelion</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good news, gospel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yevgeny, equivalent to English Eugene</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Евге́ний Ива́нович Замя́тин (Yevgeny Ivanovich Zamyatin, 1884–1937), Russian author of science fiction and political satire</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Εὐγένιος</i> (Eugénios, “noble, high-born”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yevdokim</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Εὐδόκῐμος</i> (Eudókĭmos, “in good repute, honoured”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yevdokimov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yevdokia, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Eudocia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>εὐδοκία</i> (eudokía, “goodwill, kindness”). Name of a second century martyr, Latinized and Anglicized as Eudocia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yevpatoria (a resort city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian Union</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasia (a supercontinent consisting of Europe and Asia)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Европа</i> (Jevropa) +&lrm; <i>Азия</i> (Azija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jewish Autonomous Oblast (an autonomous oblast of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Эврипид</i> (Evripid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurovision</li></ol><p>From <i>Евро-</i> (Jevro-, “Euro”) +&lrm; <i>видение</i> (videnije, “vision”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euromaidan (series of anti-government protests in Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Евро́па</i> (Jevrópa, “Europe”) +&lrm; <i>майда́н</i> (majdán, “square”), from Ukrainian <i>Євромайда́н</i> (Jevromajdán), named after Ukrainian <i>Майда́н Незале́жності</i> (Majdán Nezaléžnosti, “Independence Square”) in Kiev.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Europe (a continent located west of Asia and north of Africa)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>informal</i>) Europe outside the Russian sphere of influence, in particular Western Europe.</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) the European Union</li></ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Europa (a moon in Jupiter)# (<i>Greek mythology</i>) Europa (several characters, most notably a Phoenician princess abducted to Crete by Zeus)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>Еѵропа</i> (Jevropa), <i>Европа</i> (Jevropa) and <i>Эѵропа</i> (Evropa) (17th c.), likely through Old Ruthenian from Polish <i>Europa</i>, from Latin <i>Eurōpa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Εὐρώπη</i> (Eurṓpē). Displaced earlier forms like Еѵропіа, Европїя etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>European Union</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>abbreviation</i>) European Union, "Eurounion"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yevsey, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Εὐσέβιος</i> (Eusébios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yevstigneyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euphrates (a river in Iraq, Syria and Turkey in Mesopotamia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Евфра́т</i> (Jevfrát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yeghvard (a town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Եղվարդ</i> (Eġvard).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt (a country in North Africa and West Asia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Егѵпетъ</i> (Jegüpetŭ), <i>Егѵпьтъ</i> (Jegüpĭtŭ), from Latin <i>Aegyptus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Еги́пет</i> (Jegípet).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yegor</li></ol><p>Vernacular form of <i>Гео́ргий</i> (Geórgij). Doublet of <i>Юрий</i> (Jurij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yegorov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yegor's</li></ol><p><i>Его́р</i> (Jegór) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Еги́пет</i> (Jegípet).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yezhov</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ežèvъ</i>, accent paradigm <i>b</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yezuch (a river in Sumy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably the origin is associated with the hydronym Russian <i>Я́зучая</i> (Jázučaja), Ukrainian <i>Я́зуча</i> (Jázuča). Compare Ukrainian <i>Є́зуч</i> (Jézuč), Polish <i>Jezucz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yeysk (a resort town in Krasnodar Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Е́я</i> (Jéja) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ekaterina, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Catherine</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Αἰκατερίνη</i> (Aikaterínē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yekaterinburg (a city, the administrative center of Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Екатери́на</i> (Jekaterína) +&lrm; <i>-бу́рг</i> (-búrg), referring to Catherine I of Russia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yekaterinoslav (a former name of Dnipro / Dnepr, Ukraine's fourth-largest city, also formerly known under the name Dnipropetrovsk / Dnepropetrovsk)</li></ol><p>From <i>Екатери́на</i> (Jekaterína, “Catherine (the Great)”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-слав</i> (-slav), combining form of <i>сла́вить</i> (slávitʹ, “to glorify”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ossetian male given name</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ossetian</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Elena, Yelena, or Jelena, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Helen</li><li>a transliteration of the Belarusian female given name <i>Алена</i> (Aljena)</li><li>a transliteration of the Ukrainian female given name <i>Олена</i> (Olena)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἑλένη</i> (Helénē, “Helen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yeleninka (a village in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Елена</i> (Jelena) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), in memory of Grand Duchess Elena Pavlovna of Russia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yelenovka or Olenivka (a number of place names in Russia, Ukraine, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>Еле́на</i> (Jeléna) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) Zelensky, specifically Volodymyr Zelenskyy, President of Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>Зеленский</i> (Zelenskij), derived thus due to various European governments banning the use of the letter <i>Z</i> as a symbol of support for Russia's war on Ukraine. See <i>Z</i> for more information about the ban on the Latin <i>Z</i> letter.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yelizaveta, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Elizabeth</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλισάβετ</i> (Elisábet), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱלִישֶׁבַע</i> (ʾĕlîšeḇaʿ, “my God is an oath”), equivalent to English <i>Elizabeth</i>,.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>genitive form of Елизавета</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yelisei, equivalent to English Elisha</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Elisha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Єлїссє́й</i> (Elisséj), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλισσαῖος</i> (Elissaîos), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱלִישָׁע</i> (ʾĕlîšāʿ, literally “My God is salvation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yeltsin, particularly Russian and former Soviet politician Boris Yeltsin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yenakiyeve (a city in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yenilin</li></ol><p>Probably of Chuvash origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yenisei (a major river in Russia)</li></ol><p>Russified from Kyrgyz <i>Эне-Сай</i> (Ene-Say, “mother river”), from <i>эне</i> (ene) + <i>Сай</i> (Say), or from Evenki <i>Ионэсси</i> (Ionəssi, “big water”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yerbogachen, Erbogachen (selo)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Evenki <i>Нэрбэкэчэ̄н</i> (Nərbəkəçə̄n, literally “Hill with Woods”), which is probably a local-dialect variant from <i>нербэкэ</i> (ņerbəkə, “hill”) +&lrm; <i>тэ̄н</i> (tə̄n, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yerevan (the capital city of Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yermak or Ermak, from Russian or Ukrainian</li><li>a male given name, Yermak or Ermak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yermolay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἑρμόλαος</i> (Hermólaos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yerofeyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalem</li></ol><p>Transliterated from Hebrew <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yerushaláyim) by Mikhail Bulgakov in his novel <i>The Master and Margarita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yesenin or Esenin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yessentuki (a city in Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esther (biblical figure)</li><li>a female given name, Yesfir, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Esther</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἐσθήρ</i> (Esthḗr), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֶסְתֵּר</i> (ʾestēr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yefan, equivalent to English Ethan</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>אֵיתָן</i> (ʾêṯān, literally “firmness, strong, or long-lived”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yefim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Εὔφημος</i> (Eúphēmos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The former name, from its founding until 1945, of <i>Плюще́вка</i> (Pljuščévka)&#58; Yefinhar.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The <i>Shorter Jewish Encyclopedia</i> (volume 2, 1982) states that the name derives from the Hebrew <i>יָפֶה נָהָר</i> (Yafẹ́ Nahár, literally “Beautiful River”), which accords with the settlement’s founding near the River Inhul as an agricultural colony by Jews from the Russian Empire’s Lithuania Governorate(-General). However, <i>יָפֶה נָהָר</i> (yafẹ́ nahár) reverses the usual position of the adjective (akin to “the river beautiful” in English), though this is not strictly incorrect (compare Biblical Hebrew <i>יְפֵה נוֹף</i> (Yəp̄ē Nōp̄, “Jerusalem”, literally “Beautiful View”) in the third verse of Psalm 48).</p><p>The toponym’s vowels also present a difficulty. The area’s and settlers’ Ashkenazi dialects of Hebrew would be expected to give penultimate stress to <i>יָפֶה נָהָר</i> (yáfẹ náhar) and to realise its <i>a</i> vowels (<i>kamatzim</i>) as [ɔ], [u], or [ʌ], yet no such spellings as <i>&#42;Ёфенгор</i> (&#42;Jófengor) or <i>&#42;Ю́фенгур</i> (&#42;Júfengur) are attested. Moreover, the first vowel of <i>נָהָר</i> (nahár) is absent in all the Russian toponym’s spellings; even assuming Sephardi stress and vowel qualities, this absence is unexplained.</p><p>Inflecting <i>יָפֶה נָהָר</i> (yafẹ́ nahár) for its construct state addresses this phonological difficulty: the resulting <i>יְפֵה־נְהַר</i> (y’fe-n’har) would be expected to yield a Russian borrowing ending in <i>-фенгар</i> (-fengar), whilst the Ashkenazi pronunciation of the <i>e</i> vowel (<i>tzere</i>) as [ej] also accounts for the <i>фен–фин</i> (fen–fin) variation in the spelling of the second syllable. (Deriving from a [j] sound without a following vowel, it is indeterminate what form the <i>beginning</i> of the Russian borrowing would be expected to take; <i>И-</i> (I-) or any iotated vowel&nbsp;– whereof <i>Е-</i> (Je-) is one&nbsp;– might be expected, whereas <i>Э-</i> (E-) is not readily explicable.) However, the construct form <i>יְפֵה־נְהַר</i> (y’fe-n’har) denotes a “beautiful river <i>of</i>” something or someone, but of <i>what</i> or of <i>whom</i> it is unclear. Thus, the phonological difficulty is exchanged for a grammatical one.</p><p>Cognate with the Ukrainian <i>Єфінга́р</i> (Jefinhár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yefremov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yeghegnadzor (a town, the capital of Vayots Dzor Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Եղեգնաձոր</i> (Eġegnajor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>definitely, beyond doubt</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>железобетонно</i> (železobetonno, “reinforced concrete”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>жили́щно-коммуна́льное хозя́йство</i> (žilíščno-kommunálʹnoje xozjájstvo, “housing and communal services, housing and community amenities”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name meaning “sea”, of Buryat usage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠵᠠᠮᠰᠤ</i> (ǰamsu), from Classical Tibetan <i>རྒྱ་མཚོ</i> (rgya mtsho).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zhanna, from French, equivalent to English Jean or Joan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Jeanne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zharov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Zhvanetsky</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Михаи́л Миха́йлович Жване́цкий (<i>Mixaíl Mixájlovič Žvanéckij</i>, 1934–2020), Soviet writer, satirist, and performer</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhdanov</li></ol><p>Apparently from the Slavic male name <i>Ждан</i> (Ždan), <i>cf.</i> Old Novgorodian <i>Жьданъ</i> (Žĭdanŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zheleznogorsk</li></ol><p>From <i>жезезн(ый)</i> (žezezn(yj), “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гор(а)</i> (gor(a), “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) "Zheltorossiya" or "Yellow Russia", a historical plan in the Russian Empire to colonise northern China.</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja, “Russia”). The original Russian term was first used in 1905 by a journalist, Ilya Levitov, during the Russo-Japanese War.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samogitia (an ethnographic region of Lithuania, located in the north-west of the country)</li></ol><p>From <i>жема́йт</i> (žemájt, “Samogitian; inhabitant of Samogitia”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geneva (the capital of Geneva canton, Switzerland)</li><li>Geneva (a canton of Switzerland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Zhenya, of the male given name Евге́ний (Jevgénij), equivalent to English Gene or Genie</li><li>a diminutive, Zhenya, of the female given name Евге́ния (Jevgénija), equivalent to English Genie</li></ol><p>From a clipping of French <i>Eugène</i> +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rzeszów (the capital and largest city of Subcarpathian Voivodeship, in southeastern Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhzhenov, Zhzhyonov, or Zhzhonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zhigulyov or Zhigulev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Israel</li></ol><p><i>жид</i> (žid, “a Jewish person, a kike”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhizdra (a river in Kaluga Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Zhizdra (a city in Kaluga Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Golyad <i>&#42;Žeizdrā</i> (literally “river with sandy banks and bottom”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źeigsdrāˀ</i> (“sand”), related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kesul</i> (“pebble, gravel”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>Žiẽzdris</i>, <i>Žiẽzdras</i>, Lithuanian <i>žiegždrà</i>, <i>žiezdrà</i>, Old Prussian <i>sixdo</i> (“sand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhirinovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zhirnov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gironde (the largest department in the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, France)# Gironde (an estuary in the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, France, formed by the merger of the rivers Garonne and Dordogne; in full, the Gironde estuary)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Gironde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhytomyr (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vinnytsia Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhmerynka (a city, the administrative center of Zhmerynka Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;anime,&#32;fandom slang) <i>JoJo's Bizarre Adventure</i> (the manga and anime series by Hirohiko Araki)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>JoJo's Bizarre Adventure</i>, from English <i>JoJo</i>, itself a clipping of <i>Jonathan Joestar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zhozefina, from French, equivalent to English Josephine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Joséphine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Zhora, of the male given names Его́р (Jegór) or Гео́ргий (Geórgij), equivalent to English Georgie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhukov</li><li>Zhukov (a town in Kaluga Oblast, Russia, named after Georgy Zhukov)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhukovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zhuravlyov or Zhuravlev, equivalent to English Crane</li></ol><p><i>жура́вль</i> (žurávlʹ, “crane”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yellow River, Huang He (a major river in northern China, passing through Qinghai, Sichuan, Gansu, Shaanxi, Shanxi, Henan and Shandong provinces as well as Ningxia and Inner Mongolia Autonomous Regions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yellow Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>за́пад</i> (západ, “west”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post scriptum</li></ol><p>Arose as a result of typing the abbreviation P.S. in the wrong keyboard layout, using the Cyrillic keyboard layout ЙЦУКЕН instead of the Latin keyboard layout QWERTY, since the letters З and Ы in ЙЦУКЕН are in the same places as the letters P and S in QWERTY, respectively.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>заболева́ние, передаю́щееся половы́м путём</i> (zabolevánije, peredajúščejesja polovým putjóm): STD (sexually transmitted disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>зени́тный раке́тный ко́мплекс</i> (zenítnyj rakétnyj kómpleks): anti-aircraft missile complex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) abbreviation of <i>зени́тный раке́тно-пу́шечный ко́мплекс</i> (zenítnyj rakétno-púšečnyj kómpleks, “anti-aircraft rocket-gun complex”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>знание — сила</i> (znanije — sila, “knowledge is power”)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) <i>The Green Elephant</i>, a 1999 Russian horror film set in an abusive Soviet army base used as a source for many humorous Internet memes.</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Зелёный сло́ник</i> (Zeljónyj slónik, “green elephant calf”). The movie's title was from a song sang by one of the characters in the film.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>закры́тый сегме́нт переда́чи да́нных</i> (zakrýtyj segmént peredáči dánnyx, “a closed data transmission segment”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) anti-aircraft vehicle, SPAD, SPAA, SPAAG</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>зени́тная самохо́дная устано́вка</i> (zenítnaja samoxódnaja ustanóvka, literally “anti-aircraft self-propelled apparatus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transbaikal</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>Байка́л</i> (Bajkál) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, equivalent to English Zavolokin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic ظَاهِر (ẓāhir, “radiant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zagreb (the capital and largest city of Croatia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zayeltsovsky District (one of the 10 districts of Novosibirsk, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>Ельцо́в(ка)</i> (Jelʹcóv(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) Bumfuck, Podunk; a place in the middle of nowhere</li></ol><p>From <i>за</i> (za, “beyond”) +&lrm; <i>жопа</i> (žopa, “ass”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Zaire (former name of the <i>Democratic Republic of the Congo</i>: a country in Central Africa; used from 1971–1997)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transcaucasian SFSR, TSFSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, the largest republic within the USSR, existing from 1922 to 1936)</li></ol><p><i>Закавка́зская</i> (Zakavkázskaja, “Transcaucasian”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Федерати́вная Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Federatívnaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Federative Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transcaucasia, South Caucasus (a mountainous region of West Asia and Eastern Europe, lying between the Black Sea and the Caspian Sea, comprising the republics of Georgia, Armenia and Azerbaijan)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-, “beyond”) +&lrm; <i>Кавка́з</i> (Kavkáz, “Caucasus”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje, “suffix forming names of locations”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zakarpattia (an oblast in western Ukraine in the Carpathian Mountains)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) A historical region of Russia located near Moscow.</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;by extension) Russia.</li></ol><p>From за (za) and лес (les, “forest”). The original sense referred to the forests around the Principality of Vladimir-Suzdal, a predecessor of the Tsardom of Moscow and modern Russia.</p><p>Sense 2 refers to Russia's forests and Moscow's position from Ukraine in the perspective of a Ukrainian. Compare Ukrainian <i>Залі́сся</i> (Zalíssja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zaliznyak, from Ukrainian; particularly, Soviet and Russian linguist, an expert in historical linguistics, accentology, dialectology and grammar Andrey Zaliznyak, 29 April 1935, to 24 December 2017 (aged 82)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Залізня́к</i> (Zaliznják), from <i>залі́зо</i> (zalízo, “iron”), <i>cf.</i> <i>Железня́к</i> (Železnják) of the same origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Ukrainian surname, Zaluzhnyi or Zaluzhny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zambia (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>From Arabic زَمِيلَة (zamīla, “colleague”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zamyatin; famously held by:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Евге́ний Ива́нович Замя́тин (Yevgeny Ivanovich Zamyatin, 1884–1937), Russian author of science fiction and political satire</li></ol></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Замѧтьнѧ</i> (Zamętĭnę) + <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Asia (the westernmost geographic region of Asia, which includes Anatolia, the Arabian Peninsula, Iran, the Levant, Mesopotamia, the Sinai Peninsula, and Transcaucasia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Bengal (a state in eastern India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Западная Вирджиния</i> (Zapadnaja Virdžinija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Virginia (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;1949&ndash;1990) West Germany (a former country in Central Europe, distinguished from the German Democratic Republic, commonly known as East Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Western Europe (a sociopolitical region in the west of Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Western Sahara (a territory and former Spanish colony in North Africa; about 70% is occupied by Morocco, which claims ownership over the territory as its Southern Provinces, while 30% is governed by its native inhabitants as the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic)</li><li>Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic (a country in North Africa, a partially recognised state in Western Sahara)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) West Berlin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Bank (territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Bank (territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Arctic (region north of the Arctic circle)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>поля́р(ный)</i> (poljár(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zaporizhzhia (an industrial city, the administrative centre of Zaporizhzhia urban hromada, Zaporizhzhia Raion and Zaporizhzhia Oblast, in south-central Ukraine, on the River Dnipro)</li><li>Zaporizhzhia (a raion of Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)# Zaporizhzhia (an oblast in southeast Ukraine, also illicitly claimed by Russia)# (<i>historical</i>) Zaporizhzhia (a geographic region south-central Ukraine, “beyond the rapids” in the lower reaches of the Dnipro River, base of the Zaporozhian Cossacks)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>поро́г</i> (poróg) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Forbidden City (the palace complex located in Beijing, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoroaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoroaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zarema, of Turkic and North Caucasian usage</li><li>(dated,&#32;rare) a female given name, Zarema, of occasional Soviet usage</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Persian زر (zar, “gold”). Made widely known in Russian by Pushkin's <i>The Fountain of Bakhchisaray</i> (1823).</p><p>A new name of Soviet origin, acronym of <i>за револю́цию ми́ра</i> (za revoljúciju míra, “for a world revolution”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zarina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zarubin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zakhar, equivalent to English Zachary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zakharenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zakharov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>From Arabic زَهْرَة (zahra, “flower, blossom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zayarsky</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>яр</i> (jar) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Literally, “beyond the ravine”. Derived from the Polish surname Zajarski or Zajerski.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zvenyhorodka (a city, the administrative center of Zvenyhorodka Raion, Cherkasy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zwolle (the capital city of Overijssel, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zvonaryov or Zvonarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zviahel (a city, the administrative center of Korosten Raion, Zviahel Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Star City (a small town to the northeast of Moscow, Russia with the Gagarin Cosmonauts Training Center («Российский государственный научно-исследовательский испытательный центр подготовки космонавтов имени Ю. А. Гагарина»))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Zeus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zealand (Danish island)</li><li>Zeeland (Dutch province)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zelenograd (a city in Moscow Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Ukrainian surname, Zelenskyy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Зеле́нський</i> (Zelénsʹkyj). Related to зелёный (zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) Volodymyr Zelenskyy, president of Ukraine.</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Зеле́нский</i> (Zelénskij), the surname of Volodymyr Zelenskyy, +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zielona Góra (the capital and largest city of Lubusz Voivodeship, in western Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pays de la Loire (an administrative region of France, near Brittany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Franz Josef Land (an archipelago of Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia in the Arctic Ocean, north of Novaja Zemlya, east of Svalbard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zemlyachka; particularly, Rosalia Zemlyachka was a Russian revolutionary and Soviet politician.</li></ol><p>From <i>земля́чка</i> (zemljáčka, “female compatriot, countrywoman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zemfira</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zeno</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Zēnō</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ζήνων</i> (Zḗnōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zimbabwe (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol><p>From Shona <i>dzimba-dze-mabwe</i> (“house of stones”), in reference to Great Zimbabwe.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Zina, of the female given name Зинаи́да (Zinaída)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zinaida, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ζηναΐς</i> (Zēnaḯs, “belonging to Zeus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zinovy or Zinovi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ζηνόβῐος</i> (Zēnóbĭos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zinovyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Golden Horde (a medieval Turco-Mongol khanate existing from the 13th century in much of modern Russia and modern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zolotov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zolotonosha (a city, the administrative center of Zolotonosha Raion, Cherkasy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Zolotukhin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Golden Gate Bridge</li><li>Golden Gate strait</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Золотые ворота)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Golden Gate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zolochiv (a city, the administrative center of Zolochiv Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cinderella</li></ol><p><i>зола́</i> (zolá) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoroaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zosya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zoya, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Zoë or Zoe</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ζωή</i> (zōḗ, “life”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zubarevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Зубарэ́віч (Zubarévič)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zubenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zubko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zuyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>ზურაბიშვილი</i> (zurabišvili)</li><li>a surname, Zurabishvili or Zourabichvili, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Arabic, of Muslim usage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic ظُفَار (ẓufār).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic</li></ol><p><i>Зуфа́р</i> (Zufár) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic</li></ol><p><i>Зуфа́р</i> (Zufár) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zyuganov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ISIS, ISIL</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Исла́мское госуда́рство Ира́ка и Лева́нта</i> (Islámskoje gosudárstvo Iráka i Levánta, “Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) initialism of <i>иностра́нный инве́стор</i> (inostránnyj invéstor); foreign investor</li><li>(<i>information technology</i>) initialism of <i>иску́сственный интелле́кт</i> (iskússtvennyj intellékt); artificial intelligence</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) initialism of <i>ишеми́ческий инсу́льт</i> (išemíčeskij insúlʹt); ischaemic stroke</li><li>initialism of <i>исто́рия иску́сств</i> (istórija iskússtv); art history, history of art, history of the arts</li><li>initialism of <i>информацио́нная инфраструкту́ра</i> (informaciónnaja infrastruktúra); information infrastructure</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) initialism of <i>ионизи́рующее излуче́ние</i> (ionizírujuščeje izlučénije); ionising radiation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Информационно-издательский центр</i> (Informacionno-izdatelʹskij centr, “Information and Publishing Center”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>инфракра́сный</i> (infrakrásnyj)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) initialism of <i>интонацио́нная констру́кция</i> (intonaciónnaja konstrúkcija)</li><li>initialism of <i>иисправительная колония</i> (iispravitelʹnaja kolonija, “corrective labor colony”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole trader, sole proprietor</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>индивидуа́льный предпринима́тель</i> (individuálʹnyj predprinimátelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>инфе́кция, передаю́щаяся половы́м путём</i> (infékcija, peredajúščajasja polovým putjóm): STI (sexually transmitted infection)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>information-psychological special operation</li><li>psyop, psyops</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>информацио́нно-психологи́ческая специа́льная оперáция</i> (informaciónno-psixologíčeskaja speciálʹnaja operácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>IRL, in real life</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>HPD, histrionic personality disorder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>IRL</i>.</p><p>Initialism of <i>истери́ческое расстро́йство ли́чности</i> (isteríčeskoje rasstrójstvo líčnosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) initialism of <i>информацио́нные техноло́гии</i> (informaciónnyje texnológii): IT (information technology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibaraki (a prefecture in eastern Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>茨城</i> (いばらき, Ibaraki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iberia (a peninsula and region of Europe south of the Pyrenees, consisting of Andorra, Spain, Portugal, and Gibraltar)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Iberia, Caucasian Iberia (an antique and Byzantine name for the geographic region and ancient Georgian kingdom of Kartli in Eastern Europe, located east of Colchis, corresponding roughly to the eastern parts of present-day Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avicenna (a Persian polymath)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iburi (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>胆振</i> (いぶり, Iburi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iwamizawa (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>岩見沢</i> (いわみざわ, Iwamizawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ivan, equivalent to English John</li><li>an Ivan (a Russian everyman)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;rare) clipping of <i>Ива́н-дура́к</i> (Iván-durák)</li><li>India (The cyrillic letter <i>и</i> in spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Иваннъ</i> (Ivannŭ), from Old East Slavic and Old Church Slavonic <i>Іѡаннъ</i> (Ioannŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωάννης</i> (Iōánnēs), from Hebrew <i>יוֹחָנָן</i> (Yôḥānān, “God is gracious”). Compare Иоанн (Ioann), a borrowing from either Old Church Slavonic Іѡаннъ (Ioannŭ) or German Johann.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Russian equivalent of John Smith, John Doe, Joe Q. Public, etc.</li></ol><p>Ива́н (Iván) is a popular name among Russian men.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ivanenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Іване́нко</i> (Ivanénko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>ივანიშვილი</i> (ivanišvili)</li><li>a surname, Ivanishvili, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ivano-Frankivsk (a city, the administrative center of Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ivanov (one of the most common Russian surnames)</li><li>a surname, Ivanov (less common stress variant or non-Russian Slavic surname, e.g. Bulgarian)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ivan's</li></ol><p>From <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv) (end-stressed variant) or <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) (stem-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Ivanovich or Ivanovitch</li><li>a surname, Ivanovich or Ivanovitch, equivalent to Serbo-Croatian Ивановић or Ivanović</li></ol><p>From <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Ivanovna</li></ol><p><i>Ива́н</i> (Iván) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ivanovo (an oblast of Russia)</li><li>Ivanovo (a city, the administrative center of Ivanovo Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iwate (a prefecture in north-eastern Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>岩手</i> (いわて, Iwate).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yvelines (a department of the Île-de-France region, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Yvelines</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibiza (island in the Balearic Islands of Spain, in the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Ibiza</i>, from Catalan <i>Eivissa</i>, from Arabic يَابِسَة (yābisa), from Latin <i>Ebusus</i>, from Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤉𐤁𐤔𐤌</i> (ʾybšm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Ivleyev or Ivleev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ignat, equivalent to English Ignatius</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname</li></ol><p>From <i>Игна́т</i> (Ignát) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Игна́т</i> (Ignát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ignatyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Igor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Игорь</i> (Igorĭ), from Old Norse <i>Yngvarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ijevan (a town, the capital of Tavush Province, Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idlib (a city in Syria)</li><li>Idlib (a governorate of Syria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic إِدْلِب (ʔidlib).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idrisid</li></ol><p><i>Идрис</i> (Idris) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Jehovah (the personal name of God in the Hebrew Scriptures)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hierakonpolis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἱεράκων πόλις</i> (Hierákōn pólis, “city of hawks, city of falcons”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalem (the claimed (and de-facto) capital city of Israel; the claimed capital city of Palestine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jesse</li><li>a male given name, Iyessey, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Jesse</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰεσσαί</i> (Iessaí), from Hebrew <i>יִשַׁי</i> (Yishai)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Biblical Hebrew male given name <i>יְהוֹשֻׁעַ</i> (Yehoshúa')</li><li>(<i>Biblical, rare</i>) Jesus or Joshua</li><li>(<i>fictional</i>) The character <i>Yeshua haNozri</i> from The Master and Margarita, whose name is the Russian transliteration of the Hebrew appellation of Jesus from Nazareth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוֹשֻׁעַ</i> (Yehoshúa', “Joshua, Jesus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Izhevsk (the capital city of Udmurtia, Russia)</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>иже́вский</i> (ižévskij), from <i>Ижевский заво́д</i> (Iževskij zavód, “Izh plant”). By surface analysis, <i>Иж</i> (Iž, “Izh (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-евск</i> (-evsk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Izabella, from Spanish, equivalent to English Isabel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Isabel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Izvestia ("The News"), a long-running high-circulation daily newspaper in Russia.</li></ol><p>From earlier form <i>извѣстія</i>&nbsp;pl (izvěstija, “news”), plural form of <i>изве́стие</i> (izvéstije, “news”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Isis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Izmail (a city in Odesa Oblast, in southern Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>יִשְׁמָעֵאל</i> (yishma'él (yišmāʿēl), “God hears, God will hear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Izmir (the capital city of İzmir Province, Turkey)</li><li>Izmir (a province of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Israel (a country in Western Asia in the Middle East, at the eastern shore of the Mediterranean)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) a male given name, Izrail, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Israel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσρᾱήλ</i> (Isrāḗl, “Israel”), from Hebrew <i>יִשְׂרָאֵל</i> (yisra'él, “Israel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesus (<i>biblical character</i>)</li><li>Joshua (<i>biblical character</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic, and borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Исоусъ</i> (Isusŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Joshua (<i>biblical character</i>)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰησοῦς του Ναυή</i> (Iēsoûs tou Nauḗ), from Hebrew <i>יהושע</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰησοῦς Χριστός</i> (Iēsoûs Khristós), from Hebrew <i>יֵשׁוּעַ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ica (a city in Ica department, Peru)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Ica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iqaluit (the capital city of Nunavut, Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Icarus</li><li>Ikar (Russian rocket upper stage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ikaria (an island in the northern Aegaean Sea)</li><li>Ikaria (a regional unit of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ῑ̓κᾰρῐ́ᾱ</i> (Īkărĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elijah, Elias (<i>biblical character</i>)</li><li>a male given name, Iliya, from Ancient Greek &lt; Hebrew, equivalent to English Elijah or Elias</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἠλίας</i> (Ēlías), from Hebrew <i>אֱלִיָּה</i> (eliyáh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Illarionov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Illinois (a state of the United States, named for the people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Illyria (a vaguely-defined geographic region in Southeast Europe in the western part of the Balkan Peninsula, approximately coincident with modern Albania)# (<i>historical</i>) Illyria, Illyricum (a much larger former province of the Roman Empire, covering the whole of Dalmatia and Pannonia, existing from 27 BC to c. 70 AD and reconstituted in 293 AD by Diocletian)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ilona</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Île-de-France (an administrative region in north-central France, including Paris)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ilyin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ilya's, Elijah's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Ilyinichna</li></ol><p><i>Илья́</i> (Ilʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-инична</i> (-inična)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Ilyinsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Ilyich</li></ol><p><i>Илья́</i> (Ilʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ич</i> (-ič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ilnitsky</li><li>a surname from Polish, equivalent to Polish Ilnicki</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ilya, equivalent to English Elijah or Elias</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Iman (former name of Dalnerechensk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from dialectal Manchu <i>ᠨᡳᠮᠠᠨ</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Immanuil, from Ancient Greek &lt; Hebrew, equivalent to English Immanuel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inazawa (a city in Aichi Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>稲沢</i> (いなざわ, Inazawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Inga</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ingwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ingria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ingushetia (a republic and federal subject of Russia in the Caucasus)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ინგუშეთი</i> (ingušeti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indiana (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indianapolis (the capital city of Indiana, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Indianapolis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indigirka (a river in Sakha, Russia)</li></ol><p>Related to Yakut <i>Индигиир</i> (Indigiir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian Ocean (the ocean separating Africa, southern Asia, Australia and Antarctica)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>India (a country in South Asia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Индиꙗ</i> (Indija) and <i>Индѣꙗ</i> (Inděja). Judging by the initial stress, from Middle High German <i>Indien</i> or Old Polish <i>Indie</i>, originally used alongside with Old East Slavic <i>Индикиꙗ</i> (Indikija). Ultimately, from Latin <i>Indus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós), from Sanskrit <i>सिन्धु</i> (sindhu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Indochina (a peninsula and geographic region of Southeast Asia, consisting of the mainland portion, not including islands such as those of Indonesia)# (<i>historical</i>) Indochina (a former French colony in Southeast Asia comprising the peninsula containing Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos; in full, French Indochina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indonesia (a country and archipelago in maritime Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hindustan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindustani <i>हिंदुस्तान</i> (hindustān) / ہندوستان (hindūstān), from Classical Persian هندوستان (hindūstān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>इन्द्र</i> (indra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>И́ндия</i> (Índija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Inessa</li></ol><p>Probably from the name of the Greek town of <i>Ἴνησσα</i> (Ínēssa) in Sicily. Alternately, derived from the name <i>Agnes</i>, a doublet of <i>Inez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inkerman (a city in Crimea, in southern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Inna, from Bulgarian, Macedonian</li></ol><p>Uncertain; possibly from Macedonian <i>И́на</i> (Ína). (One suggestion is Ancient Greek <i>ἴννα</i> (ínna), from <i>ἴννην</i> (ínnēn, “little girl”).)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Incheon (a port city in South Korea, near Seoul)</li></ol><p>Korean <i>인천</i> (incheon) (<i>仁川</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inya (a tributary of the Ob in Kemerovo Oblast and Novosibirsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Either:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) John</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a male given name, Ioann, equivalent to English John, used mainly for religious figures and monarchs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Іѡаннъ</i> (Ioannŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωάννης</i> (Iōánnēs), from Hebrew <i>יוֹחָנָן</i> (Yôḥānān, “God is gracious”). Also used, due to similarity with German Johann, to render Western monarch names equivalent to John (e.g. Иоанн XXIII, that is Pope John XXIII). Compare Иван (Ivan), instead inherited through Old East Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ioanna, of old usage, equivalent to English Joanna or Johanna</li><li>Joanna (biblical figure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yokohama (the capital city of Kanagawa Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>横浜</i> (よこはま, Yokohama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Iolanta, equivalent to English Yolande</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jordan (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)# Jordan (a river in West Asia in the Middle East, that empties into the Dead Sea, flowing through Israel, the Golan Heights, the West Bank and Jordan)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰορδάνης</i> (Iordánēs), ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>יַרְדֵּן</i> (yardén, “Jordan (river)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jordan (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol><p><i>Иордан</i> (Iordan) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan (official name of <i>Jordan</i>: a country in West Asia, in the Middle East)</li></ol><p>Calque of Arabic المملكة الأردنية الهاشمية.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yosif or Iosif, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Joseph</li><li>Joseph (various Biblical characters)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Ἰωσήφ</i> (Iōsḗph), ultimately from Hebrew <i>יוֹסֵף</i> (yoséf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ypres (a city and municipality of West Flanders, Belgium)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Ypres</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Ирина (Irina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Iraida</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iraq (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ира́к</i> (Irák).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iran (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian ایران (irân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ира́н</i> (Irán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irgen (a lake in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li><li>Irgen (a selo in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠡᠷᠭᠡᠩ</i> (ergeng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Irina, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Irene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Εἰρήνη</i> (Eirḗnē), from <i>εἰρήνη</i> (eirḗnē, “peace”). Cognate with English <i>Irene</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Irenaeus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Εἰρηναῖος</i> (Eirēnaîos), from <i>εἰρήνη</i> (eirḗnē, “peace”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irkutsk (a city, the administrative center of Irkutsk Oblast in Siberia, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ирку́т</i> (Irkút, “Irkut (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ireland (an island and country in northwestern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irish Sea (a sea between the islands of Great Britain and Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ирла́ндия</i> (Irlándija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irpin (a city in Irpin municipality, Bucha Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine, on the Irpin River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irtysh (a major river in Russia, Kazakhstan and c&#47;China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Isaak, equivalent to English Isaac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esau (son of Isaac and Rebekah)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡσαῦ</i> (Hēsaû).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iset (a river in western Siberia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Isis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ishikawa (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>石川</i> (いしかわ, Ishikawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ishikari (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Ishikari (a city in Ishikari subprefecture, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>石狩</i> (いしかり, Ishikari).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islamabad (the capital city of Pakistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islamic Republic of Iran (official name of <i>Iran</i>: a country in West Asia, in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iceland (an island and country in the North Atlantic Ocean in Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Исла́ндия</i> (Islándija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spain (a country in Southern Europe, including most of the Iberian peninsula)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Испа́ния</i> (Ispánija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Itabashi (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>板橋</i> (いたばし, Itabashi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ithaca (island in Greece)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰθάκα</i> (Itháka), Doric form of <i>Ἰθάκη</i> (Ithákē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italy (a country in Southern Europe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Италиꙗ</i> (Italija), from Old Church Slavonic <i>Италиꙗ</i> (Italija), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰταλία</i> (Italía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ита́лия</i> (Itálija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iturup, the largest and northernmost island in the southern Kuril Islands</li></ol><p>From Ainu <i>エツ゚ヲロㇷ゚シㇼ</i> (Etuworop-sir, “island with many capes”), probably under the influence of the neighboring island of <i>Уру́п</i> (Urúp).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Judas (one of the Apostles)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰούδας</i> (Ioúdas), from</p><p>Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (yəhūḏā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰουδαία</i> (Ioudaía), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (Yəhūḏā(h), “Judah”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iphigenia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ῑ̓φιγένεια</i> (Īphigéneia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ikhshidid</li></ol><p>Arabic إِخْشِيد (ʔiḵšīd, “[Muhammad b. Tughj al-]Ikhshid”) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ichkeria (a geographic region encompassing the highlands of eastern Chechnya, Russia)# (<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) Ichkeria (a former separatist state that controlled most of the modern republics of Chechnya and Ingushetia, Russia, from 1991 to 2000)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Iya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάς</i> (Iás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>JCUKEN (the standard keyboard layout for Russian and other languages using the Cyrillic script)</li></ol><p>From the first six letters on the upper row of such a keyboard; compare to English <i>QWERTY</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yemen (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Йе́мен</i> (Jjémen).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fictional character, Jedi Master in the Star Wars franchise, a small, green, elderly humanoid alien who is powerful with the Force and typically speaks in an inverted phrase order</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Yoda</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yule</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yonne (a department of Bourgogne-Franche-Comté, France)# Yonne (a left tributary of the Seine in central France, flowing through the departments of Nièvre, Yonne and Seine-et-Marne)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yorkshire (a county of England)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Yorkshire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Johannesburg (a city, the provincial capital of Gauteng, South Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yoshkar-Ola (the capital and largest city of Mari El, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Eastern Mari <i>Йошкар-Ола</i> (Joškar-Ola).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Гё̀тебо́рг</i> (Gjòtebórg, “Gothenburg”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overhaul</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>капита́льно-восстанови́тельный ремо́нт</i> (kapitálʹno-vosstanovítelʹnyj remónt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Комите́т госуда́рственной безопа́сности</i> (Komitét gosudárstvennoj bezopásnosti, “State Security Committee”): KGB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>кагэбэ́шник</i> (kagɛbɛ́šnik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>констру́кторская документа́ция</i> (konstrúktorskaja dokumentácija); design documentation, construction documentation</li><li>initialism of <i>«Ки́ровец» ди́зельный</i> («Kírovec» dízelʹnyj); Kirovets diesel (tractor model prefix, as in the КД-35 model)</li><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) initialism of <i>кулда́ун</i> (kuldáun); cooldown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Кра́ткая Евре́йская Энциклопе́дия</i> (Krátkaja Jevréjskaja Enciklopédija, “Shorter Jewish Encyclopedia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>коро́ткое замыка́ние</i> (korótkoje zamykánije); short circuit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Куро́рты Краснода́рского кра́я</i> (Kurórty Krasnodárskovo krája, “resorts of the Krasnodar Krai territory”): KKK</li><li>initialism of <i>Ку-клукс-клан</i> (Ku-kluks-klan, “Ku Klux Klan”): KKK</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>DPRK (initialism of <i>Коре́йская Наро́дно-Демократи́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Koréjskaja Naródno-Demokratíčeskaja Respúblika, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea”), official name of <i>North Korea</i>: a country in East Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>PRC (initialism of <i>Кита́йская Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Kitájskaja Naródnaja Respúblika, literally “Chinese People's Republic”) (= People's Republic of China): a country in East Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pocket PC</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Communist Party of China; CPC; CCP</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>карма́нный персона́льный компью́тер</i> (karmánnyj personálʹnyj kompʹjútɛr).</p><p>Initialism of <i>Коммунисти́ческая па́ртия Кита́я</i> (Kommunistíčeskaja pártija Kitája).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checkpoint</li><li>gearbox</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>контро́льно-пропускно́й пу́нкт</i> (kontrólʹno-propusknój púnkt).</p><p>Initialism of <i>коро́бка переключе́ния переда́ч</i> (koróbka pereključénija peredáč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) initialism of <i>Коммунисти́ческая Па́ртия Росси́йской Федера́ции</i> (Kommunistíčeskaja Pártija Rossíjskoj Federácii, “Communist Party of the Russian Federation”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>CPSU</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Коммунисти́ческая па́ртия Сове́тского Сою́за</i> (Kommunistíčeskaja pártija Sovétskovo Sojúza, “Communist party of the Soviet Union”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Коммунисти́ческая па́ртия Украи́ны</i> (Kommunistíčeskaja pártija Ukraíny, “Communist Party of Ukraine”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>Корпус стражей исламской революции</i> (Korpus stražej islamskoj revoljucii, “Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps”): IRGC</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Kaaba</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kabayev or Kabaev, from a Turkic language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kabardia (a former country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from East Circassian <i>Къэбэрд</i> (Qɛbɛrd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kabardino-Balkaria (a republic and federal subject of Russia, located in the Northern Caucasus mountains)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Verde (an archipelago and country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kabul (the capital and largest city of Afghanistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Japanese</li></ol><p>Derived from Japanese <i>川口</i> (Kawaguchi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kawasaki (a city in Kanagawa Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>川崎</i> (かわさき, Kawasaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasus (an a region in Eurasia)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Καύκασος</i> (Kaúkasos), ultimately of Iranian origin.</p><p>Was already attested in Old East Slavic as <i>Кавкасийскыѣ горы</i> (Kavkasijskyjě gory), a calque of Καυκάσια ὄρη, however according to Vasmer and Trubachyov the current form <i>Кавказ</i> (Kavkaz) stems from French <i>Caucase</i> and German <i>Kaukasus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a prefecture of Japan</li><li>a surname from Japanese</li></ol><p>Derived from Japanese <i>香川</i> (Kagawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kagoshima (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Kagoshima (the capital city of Kagoshima Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>鹿児島</i> (かごしま, Kagoshima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kajaran (a town in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Քաջարան</i> (Kʻaǰaran).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadiz (a port city and municipality, the capital of the province of Cadiz, Andalusia, Spain)</li><li>Cadiz (a province of Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kadochnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Chechen surname, Kadyrov, from Chechen</li></ol><p><i>Кадыр</i> (Kadyr) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kazakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the name of Kazakhstan until 1936, see modern <i>Казахста́н</i> (Kazaxstán).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Қазақстан</i> (Qazaqstan). Can be analysed as <i>казак</i> (kazak) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kazan (the capital and largest city of the Republic of Tatarstan, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kazakh SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1936 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kazakhstan (a country in Central Asia and Eastern Europe)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>Казакста́н</i> (Kazakstán), which is from Kazakh <i>Қазақстан</i> (Qazaqstan). Can be analysed as <i>казак</i> (kazak) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kazbek</li><li>A male given name.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qazvin (a city in Iran)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian قزوین.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ка́ин</i> (Káin).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cairo (the capital city of Egypt)</li><li>Cairo (a governorate of Egypt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cayenne (the capital city of French Guiana department)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kailastui (a village in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠬᠠᠶᠢᠯᠠᠰᠤᠲᠠᠢ</i> (qayilasutai, “[place] with elms”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qalamjar (a river in Uzbekistan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>Qalamjarsoy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Kazakhstan.</li></ol><p><i>калби́т</i> (kalbít, “Central Asian person, a yokel”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan, land of”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calgary (the largest city in Alberta, Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kaledin; particularly, a Don Cossack Cavalry General who led the Don Cossack White movement in the opening stages of the Russian Civil War Alexey Kaledin.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borneo (the world's third-largest island, in Southeast Asia, politically divided between Malaysia, Brunei and Indonesia)</li></ol><p>From Indonesian <i>Kalimantan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Kalinin (former name, from 1930 to 1990, of Tver (Russian <i>Тверь</i> (Tverʹ)), a city, the administrative center of Tver Oblast, Russia)</li><li>a Mikhail Kalinin surname, Mikhail Kalinin - a Russian revolutionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kaliningrad (a city, the administrative center of Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia, formerly Königsberg, capital of East Prussia)</li></ol><p>From Russian <i>Кали́нин</i> (Kalínin, “Mikhail Ivanovich Kalinin”) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad, “town”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>California (the most populous state of the United States)</li><li>California (a town in Pennsylvania, United States)</li><li>California (a town in Missouri, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>California</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Calliope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalmykia (a republic and federal subject in southwestern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Калмы́кия</i> (Kalmýkija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalush (a city, the administrative centre of Kalush Raion, Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolkata (a city in India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Calcutta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalmiuske (a city in Kalmiuske Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kama (a left tributary of the Volga River in Russia)</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin; possibly related to <i>коми</i> (komi, “Komi”), a Permian tribe, or otherwise from an Uralic word for "water" or "river," though exact ancestors are difficult to construct.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamasutra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>कामसूत्र</i> (kā́ma-sūtra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kambar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Қамбар</i> (Qambar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cambodia (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol><p>Possibly via German <i>Kambodscha</i> from French <i>Cambodge</i>, ultimately from Khmer <i>កម្ពុជា</i> (kampuciə), from Sanskrit <i>काम्बोज</i> (kāmboja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chortkiv (a city, the administrative center of Chortkiv Raion, Ternopil Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamensk-Uralsky, Kamensk-Uralskiy (a town in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamianske (a city in Kamianske Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cameroon (a country in Central Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kampala (the capital city of Uganda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Kampuchea (former name of <i>Cambodia</i>: a country in Southeast Asia, used from 1975 to 1989)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamchatka (a long peninsula forming part of Kamchatka Krai in the Far East of Russia, lying between the Pacific Ocean and the Sea of Okhotsk)# Kamchatka (a krai and federal subject of Russia, in the Russian Far East)# (<i>with <i><b>the</b></i></i>) Kamchatka (a river in Kamchatka Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>Named after the Kamchadal, an indigenous people inhabiting the Siberian peninsula. The ethnonym is derived from Koryak <i>кончачал</i> (končačal), perhaps meaning “men of the far end”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kanagawa (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>神奈川</i> (かながわ, Kanagawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canada (a country in North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canary Islands (an archipelago and autonomous community of Spain, off the coast of northwestern Africa, near Morocco)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canary Islands (an archipelago and autonomous community of Spain, off the coast of northwestern Africa, near Morocco)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canberra (the capital city of Australia; located in the Australian Capital Territory)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of English <i>Canberra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gangdong (a district of Seoul, South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean 강동구 (江東區, Gangdonggu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kansas (a state of the midwest United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cannes (a city and commune in southeastern France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kantō (one of the traditionally used regional divisions of Japan)</li><li>Kantō (a variety of Japanese dialect)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>関東</i> (かんとう, Kantō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kangchenjunga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kanygin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kapan (a town, the capital of Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Կապան</i> (Kapan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Kapitolina, from Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Capitolina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kapitsa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cappadocia (a historical region and ancient kingdom in central Asia Minor, in present-day Turkey)# Cappadocia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing from 18 AD until the 7th century)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kapustin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kara-Murza, from Tatar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karabakh</li><li>Nagorno-Karabakh</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Nagorno-Karabakh Republic</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language. Compare Ottoman Turkish قراباغ (Karabağ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karabulak (a town in Ingushetia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karaganda (a city in Kazakhstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karakalpakstan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caracas (the capital city of Venezuela)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Karakulov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karakum (desert)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Karamzin, from Tatar or Crimean Tatar</li></ol><p>From Tatar <i>Карамирза</i> (Qaramirza) or an equivalent Crimean Tatar name, and the suffix <i>-ин</i> (-in). See <i>кара</i> (qara, “black”) and <i>мирза</i> (mirza, “a noble title”) for the constituent parts of the name; the latter derives from Persian میرزا (mirzâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Karaulov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karachay-Cherkessia (a republic of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karachi (the capital of Sindh province, Pakistan, the most populous city in Pakistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cardiff (the capital city of Wales)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karelia (a historical region of Northern Europe, located to the north of Saint Petersburg and politically split between Russia and Finland)</li><li>Karelia (a republic and federal subject of Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>Carelia</i>, ultimately from a Finnic endonym; see Finnish <i>Karjala</i> for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karelo-Finnish SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1956)</li></ol><p><i>Каре́ло-Фи́нская</i> (Karélo-Fínskaja, “Karelo-Finnish”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karelo-Finnish Soviet Socialist Republic (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1956)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English <i>Karen</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Կարեն</i> (Karen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Caribbean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Karina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karlovac (a city and municipality in central Croatia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karnataka (a state in southern India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>कर्नाटक</i> (karnāṭak), from Tamil <i>கருப்பு</i> (karuppu, “black”) + <i>நாடு</i> (nāṭu, “country”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Karolina, equivalent to English Caroline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Karpov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kara Sea</li></ol><p><i>Кара</i> (Kara) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) <i>море</i> (more)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carthage (an ancient city in North Africa, in modern Tunisia)</li><li>Carthage (an ancient civilisation in North Africa, centred on the city of Carthage)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Carthāgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karelia (a republic of Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Karelian or Finnish <i>Karjala</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kasatkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kashiwa (a city in Chiba Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>柏</i> (かしわ, Kashiwa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Armenian surname, Kasparov</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Կասպարյան</i> (Kasparyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kaspersky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caspian Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Castries (the capital city of Saint Lucia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kasyanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Katayev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catalabutte (a character in the ballet <i>The Sleeping Beauty</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Catalabutte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catalonia (an autonomous community in northeast Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Katenka, of the female given name Екатери́на (Jekaterína)</li></ol><p><i>Ка́тя</i> (Kátja) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Katerina, equivalent to English Catherine</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>Екатери́на</i> (Jekaterína); compare English <i>Catherine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kathmandu (the capital city of Nepal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Katowice (a city in southern Poland; the capital and largest city of Silesian Voivodeship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Katori (a city in Chiba Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>香取</i> (かとり, Katori).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in the Russian part of the Altai mountains. This river is running from south down to north, and, by merging with river Biya, creates the river Ob - one of the longest rivers of Siberia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Katyn, Katyń (a town in Smolensk Oblast, near Belarus). Located not far from the Gnezdovo archaeological site. The forest of Katyn is primarily known as the place of executions made by GPU and NKVD, such as the massacre of several thousands Polish officers in 1940.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Katya, of the female given names Екатери́на (Jekaterína) or Катери́на (Katerína), equivalent to English Katie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kaunas (a city in Lithuania)</li></ol><p>From Lithuanian <i>Kaunas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kakhovka (a historical region of Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Katz or Kats, from Hebrew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a village in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ка́цбах</i> (Kácbax) (note the different stress), from German <i>Katzbach</i>, after the Battle of Katzbach.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Katsushika (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>葛饰</i> (かつしか, Katsushika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kachalov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Kaczyński</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kashgar, Kashi (a prefecture of the Xinjiang autonomous region, China)</li><li>Kashgar, Kashi (a county-level city of Kashgar Prefecture, Xinjiang autonomous region, China)</li></ol><p>From Persian کاشغر (kâšġar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kashmir (a contested geographic region of South Asia in the northern part of the Indian subcontinent, located between (and de facto divided between) India, Pakistan and China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quebec (a province in eastern Canada)# Quebec, Quebec City (the capital city of the province of Quebec, Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Queens (a borough of New York City, New York, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Queensland (a state of Australia, located in the northeastern part of the continent; a former British colony from 1859 to 1901)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Quintus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Quīntus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cebes of Thebes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ке́бет</i> (Kébet).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Malayalam, equivalent to English Kezhkepat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Мадхаван Кежкепат Палат (born 1947), Indian historian</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Keynes</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Keynes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Town (the legislative capital of South Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kelmentsi (a town, the administrative center of Dnistrovskyi Raion, Chernivtsi Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>Ке́льменці</i> (Kélʹmenci).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kielce (the capital and largest city of Holy Cross Voivodeship, in central Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ке́льце</i> (Kélʹce)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kemerovo (a city in Siberia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kenya (a country in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kentucky (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Քեոսայան</i> (Kʻeosayan)</li><li>a surname, Keosayan, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kerala (a state in southern India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kerensky; (especially when referring to Alexander Kerensky)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ке́ренск</i> (Kérensk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kerch (a city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cybele</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Κυβέλη</i> (Kubélē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kigali (the capital city of Rwanda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyiv (the capital city of Ukraine)</li><li>(politics,&#32;metonymic) the Ukrainian government.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Кꙑевъ</i> (Kyjevŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kiyevskaya (Moscow metro stations on the Arbatsko–Pokrovskaya line, Filyovskaya line and Koltsevaya line)</li></ol><p>From <i>ки́евский</i> (kíjevskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Kievan Rus (a medieval principality in Eastern Europe centred around Kyiv; at its maximum extent covering much of modern Ukraine, Belarus and western Russia and considered an early predecessor of each country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyiv Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a North-Caucasian female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>კიკაბიძე</i> (ḳiḳabiʒe)</li><li>a surname, Kikabidze, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Cilicia (an ancient region in southeastern Asia Minor in modern Turkey, famed for cilice, i.e. haircloth made from the hair of Cilician goats)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κιλικία</i> (Kilikía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kim, from Korean</li><li>a male given name of occasional Soviet Union usage, equivalent to English Kim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>김</i> (gim).</p><p>Acronym of <i>Коммунисти́ческий интернациона́л молодёжи</i> (Kommunistíčeskij intɛrnacionál molodjóži, “Young Communist International”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kingstown (the capital city of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kingston (the capital city of Jamaica)</li><li>Kingston (a large city in eastern Ontario, Canada)</li><li>Kingston (a community in Kings County, New Brunswick, Canada)</li><li>Kingston (a rural settlement in Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada)</li><li>Kingston (a village in Kings County, Nova Scotia, Canada)</li><li>Kingston (a community in Queens County, Prince Edward Island, Canada)</li><li>Kingston (a large number of villages in England)</li><li>Kingston (several cities, towns, villages, unincorporated communities and census-designated places in the United States)</li><li>Kingston (several towns and suburbs in Australia)</li><li>Kingston (a hamlet, an inner-city area and a coastal village in Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kingston upon Hull (a city and unitary authority of the East Riding of Yorkshire, England)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Kingston upon Hull</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kinshasa (the capital city of the Democratic Republic of the Congo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyoto (the capital city of Kyoto Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Kyoto (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>京都</i> (きょうと, Kyōto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyprus (an island and country in the Mediterranean Sea, normally considered politically part of Europe but geographically part of West Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cypris (a name for Aphrodite)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Kira</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Sanskrit unisex given name <i>किरण</i> (kiraṇa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyzstan (a country in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyz SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1936 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Кыргызста́н</i> (Kyrgyzstán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kirill, equivalent to English Cyril</li><li>Saint Cyril (apostle to the Slavs and inventor of the Glagolitic alphabet, which served as the basis for the Cyrillic alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κύριλλος</i> (Kúrillos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, equivalent to English Kirillov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Kirillovich</li></ol><p><i>Кири́лл</i> (Kiríll) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Kirillovna</li></ol><p><i>Кири́лл</i> (Kiríll) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kirkorov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>From Bulgarian <i>Кирко́ров</i> (Kirkórov), a Slavicized form of the Armenian <i>Գրիգորյան</i> (Grigoryan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kirov (a city, the administrative center of Kirov Oblast, Russia)</li><li>a Sergey Kirov surname, Sergey Kirov, a Soviet revolutionary, assassinated in 1934</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kislovodsk (a city in Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj, “sour”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kislyak, from Ukrainian</li><li>a village in Ukraine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Кисля́к</i> (Kyslják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Kissinger</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Kissinger</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kistyakovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>China (a large country in East Asia, occupying the region around the Yellow, Yangtze, and Pearl Rivers; the People's Republic of China, since 1949)</li><li>China (a cultural region and civilization in East Asia, occupying the region around the Yellow, Yangtze, and Pearl Rivers, taken as a whole under its various dynasties)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Китаи</i> (Kitai), from a Turkic source, ultimately from Old Turkic <i>𐰶𐰃𐱃𐰪</i> (Qïtaǰ, “Khitan”), from Khitan <i>𘱿𘱤</i> (&#42;qid i) or <i>𘱿𘲫</i> (&#42;qid ún, “Khitan”), the Mongolic people who ruled Manchuria, then Northern China, then Central Asia from the 9th to 13th centuries.</p><p>Doublet of <i>кида́нь</i> (kidánʹ). Compare with Kazakh <i>Қытай</i> (Qytai), Kyrgyz <i>Кытай</i> (Kıtay), Belarusian <i>Кітай</i> (Kitaj), Karachay-Balkar <i>Къытай</i> (Qıtay), Uyghur خىتاي (xitay), Uzbek <i>Xitoy</i>, Turkmen <i>Hytaý</i> (“China”), Mongolian <i>Хятад</i> (Xjatad, “China”), Chinese <i>契丹</i> (Qìdān), English <i>Cathay</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>People's Republic of China (official name of <i>China</i>: a country in East Asia)</li></ol><p>Literally, “Chinese People's Republic”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of China (official name of <i>Taiwan</i>: a partly-recognized country in East Asia; the rump state left over from the Republic of China on the mainland after 1949)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kitami (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>北見</i> (きたみ, KItami).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Kitezh, the legendary sunken city not far from Nizhny Novgorod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quito (the capital city of Ecuador)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chișinău (the capital and largest city of Moldova)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Quicherat</i>.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Луи́-Мари́ Кишра́ (1799–1884), French Latinist and librarian; elder brother of Жюль-Этье́н-Жозе́ф Кишра́</li><li>Жюль-Этье́н-Жозе́ф Кишра́ (1814–1882), French historian and archaeologist; younger brother of Луи́-Мари́ Кишра́</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>C, coulomb</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>куло́н</i> (kulón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Klavdia, from Latin, equivalent to English Claudia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Klaipėda (a city in Lithuania)</li></ol><p>From Lithuanian <i>Klaipėda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Klara, equivalent to English Clara</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Klevan (a rural settlement of the settlement hromada of the same name in Rivne Raion, Rivne Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1458).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a settlement hromada of Rivne Raion, Rivne Oblast, Ukraine, established in August 2017.</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Кле́вань</i> (Klévanʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cleopatra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κλεοπάτρα</i> (Kleopátra), from κλέος (kléos, “glory”) + πατήρ (patḗr, “father”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cleveland (a city in Ohio, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Klim, equivalent to English Clement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Klimenko or Klymenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Klimov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Clinton, from English (for a male)</li><li>a surname, Clinton, from English (for a female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Clytemnestra</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Κλυταιμνήστρα</i> (Klutaimnḗstra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Ukrainian surname, Klichko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Klyuev or Klyuyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Knesset (the Israeli parliament)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>כְּנֶסֶת</i> (k'néset).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Knorozov, particularly, a Soviet Ukrainian and Russian linguist, epigrapher and ethnographer, who is particularly renowned for the pivotal role his research played in the decipherment of the Maya script</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Knyazev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coahuila (a state of Mexico)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Coahuila</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kobe (the capital city of Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>神戸</i> (こうべ, Kōbe).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kovalevsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kovalski, Kovalsky, or Kowalski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kovalchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kovalyov, equivalent to English Smith or Kovalev</li></ol><p><i>кова́ль</i> (koválʹ, “smith”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kovel (a city, the administrative center of Volyn Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kogan, from Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>כֹּהֵן</i>. Equivalent to English <i>Cohen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kozhevnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kozlov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kozlovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kozyrev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kolesnikov</li></ol><p>From <i>коле́сник</i> (kolésnik, “wheel maker”) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colosseum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>Colisēum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Kolya's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolkata (the capital city of West Bengal, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colombo (the commercial capital and largest city of Sri Lanka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolomna (a city in Moscow Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from dialectal <i>коло́мень</i>&nbsp;f (kolómenʹ, “neighborhood”), related to <i>о́коло</i> (ókolo, “near, around”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolomyia (a city, the administrative centre of Kolomyia Raion, Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colorado (a state in the western United States, the 38th state)</li><li>Colorado, Colorado River (a major river of western North America, running from the Rocky Mountains in the United States to the Gulf of California in Mexico)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kolosov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colossus of Rhodes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colombia (a country in South America)</li><li>Columbia (a river in the western United States and Canada)</li><li>Columbia (numerous cities, towns, unincorporated communities and census-designated places in the United States)</li><li>Columbia (a federal district of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Columbus (the capital city of Ohio, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Columbus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Colchis (a historical region and ancient Georgian kingdom on the coast of the Black Sea in the South Caucasus on the border between Europe and Asia, corresponding to what is now western Georgia (including Abkhazia), as well as parts of Russia and Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kolchak, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolyma (a region of the Russian Far East)</li><li>Kolyma (a river in the Russian Far East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kola Peninsula (a peninsula in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Kolya, of the male given names Никола́й (Nikoláj) or Микола́й (Mikoláj), equivalent to English Nick</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Никола́й</i> (Nikoláj) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Kolyan, of the male given name Никола́й (Nikoláj), equivalent to English Nick</li></ol><p><i>Ко́ля</i> (Kólja) +&lrm; <i>-я́н</i> (-ján)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Komarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comintern</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>коммунисти́ческий интернациона́л</i> (kommunistíčeskij intɛrnacionál, “communist international”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Komissarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the former Soviet "State Security Committee": the KGB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Communist Party of China</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Komolov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comoros (an archipelago and country in East Africa in the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comoros (an archipelago and country in East Africa in the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Komsomolsk-na-Amure, Komsomolsk-on-Amur (a city in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia, on the Amur River)</li></ol><p>From <i>комсомо́л</i> (komsomól, “a youth organization”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conakry (the capital city of Guinea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Konashenkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Congo (a major river in Central Africa which flows for about 4,380 km (2,720 miles) to the Atlantic Ocean in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, at times forming the border with the Republic of the Congo and Angola)</li><li>Democratic Republic of the Congo (a country in Central Africa, larger and to the east of the Republic of the Congo)</li><li>Republic of the Congo (a country in Central Africa, smaller and to the west of the Democratic Republic of the Congo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Konev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Konkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Concord (the capital city of New Hampshire, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Concord</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Conley</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Conley</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Connacht (a province of Ireland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Connacht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Connecticut (a state in the northeastern United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Connelly</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Connelly</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Konstantin, equivalent to English Constantine</li></ol><p>Via Koine Greek <i>Κωνσταντῖνος</i> (Kōnstantînos) from Latin <i>Cōnstantīnus</i>, from <i>cōnstāns</i> (“constant, steadfast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Konstantinov</li></ol><p><i>Константи́н</i> (Konstantín) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kostiantynivka (a city in Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Константи́н</i> (Konstantín) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Constantinople (the former name, from 330–1930 <small>C.E.</small>, of Istanbul, the largest city in Turkey; the former capital of the Ottoman Empire and of the Byzantine Empire before that)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Confiturembourg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Confiturembourg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Confucius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Confucius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Конфу́ций</i> (Konfúcij, “Confucius”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Konya (the capital city of Konya Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kopelyan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Copenhagen (the capital city of Denmark)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Korablyov or Korablev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) The Qur'an</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اَلْقُرْآن (al-qurʔān), from قِرَاءَة (qirāʔa, “reading, recitation”), from قَرَأَ (qaraʔa, “to declaim, recite”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Democratic People's Republic of Korea (official name of <i>North Korea</i>: a country in East Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Korenev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Korea (two countries in East Asia, North Korea and South Korea)</li></ol><p>From Sino-Korean <i>고려(高麗)</i> (Goryeo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kornilov</li></ol><p>From <i>Корни́л</i> (Korníl) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov), from <i>Корни́лий</i> (Kornílij), from <i>Корне́лий</i> (Kornélij), borrowing from Latin <i>Cornelius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Korovin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Königsberg (the former capital of East Prussia)</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евец</i> (-evec), a calque of German <i>Königsberg</i> (literally “King's Hill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Kingdom of Italy (a former country in Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of Italian <i>Regno d'Italia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Korolyov or Korolev</li></ol><p><i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Korosten (a city, the administrative center of Korosten Raion, Zhytomyr Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Kazakhstan.</li></ol><p><i>корса́к</i> (korsák, “a corsac fox, Central Asian person, a yokel”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan, land of”). Mocking Kazakhstan's name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corsica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corfu (an island of Greece)</li><li>Corfu (a city in Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Korchnoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Korshunov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koriukivka (a city in Koriukivka Raion, Chernihiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Goryeo (a Korean dynasty, a kingdom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>고려(高麗)</i> (Goryeo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kosiv (a city, the administrative centre of Kosiv Raion, Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kosovo (a partly-recognized country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>Косово</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Costa Rica (a country in Central America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kostroma (a city, the administrative center of Kostroma Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kostylyov or Kostylev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Kostya, of the male given names Константи́н (Konstantín) or Костянти́н (Kostjantín)</li></ol><p>Compare to Greek <i>Κώστας</i> (Kóstas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ivory Coast, Côte d'Ivoire (a country in West Africa)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Côte d'Ivoire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Côte-d'Or (a department of Bourgogne-Franche-Comté, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kotayk (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Կոտայք</i> (Kotaykʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kochi (a prefecture in southern Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>高知</i> (こうち, Kōchi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kōtō (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>江東</i> (こうとう, Kōtō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kotov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kotovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cotonou (the de facto capital of Benin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>კოტრიკაძე</i> (ḳoṭriḳaʒe)</li><li>a surname, Kotrikadze, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from German, equivalent to German Koch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Koschei (also Koshchei, Koshchey, Kashchei or Kashchey)</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>кость</i> (kostʹ, “bone”). Compare with Polish <i>Kościej</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) K (king)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kravchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kravchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Кравчу́к</i> (Kravčúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Extreme North, the Far North (region of Russia north of the Arctic Circle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krakow (a city on the Vistula River, the capital of the Lesser Poland Voivodeship in southern Poland and the former capital (until 1596) of Poland as a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kramarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kramatorsk (a city, the de facto administrative center of Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Krasikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Little Red Riding Hood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Red Army</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Red Square</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Krasnov</li></ol><p>Related to кра́сный (krásnyj, “beautiful, red”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Polish surname, Krasnovsky, equivalent to English Krasnowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city in Moscow Oblast, Russia</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krasnohrad (a city, the administrative center of Krasnohrad Raion, Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krasnodar (a krai of Russia)</li><li>Krasnodar (a city, the administrative center of Krasnodar Oblast, Russia); called Yekaterinodar before December 1920</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Red Sea, a sea between the continents of Africa and Asia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) the city of Hurghada in Egypt.</li></ol><p><i>Красного́рск</i> (Krasnogórsk) +&lrm; <i>Кра́сное мо́ре</i> (Krásnoje móre, “Red Sea”). Named due to its popularity among Russian tourists, especially divers.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krasnoyarsk (a krai of Russia)</li><li>Krasnoyarsk (a city, the administrative center of Krasnoyarsk Krai in Siberia, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, connecting vowel) +&lrm; <i>яр</i> (jar, “steep bank”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Red Cross</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Red Crescent (a branch of the Red Cross that operates in Muslim countries)</li><li>Red Crescent (the symbol of this branch)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Crassus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Crassus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Krasheninnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kremenets (a city, the administrative center of Kremenets Raion, Ternopil Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kreminna (a city in Sievierodonetsk Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kremenchuk (a city in Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kryvyi Rih (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Kristina, equivalent to English Christina</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Χριστίνα</i> (Khristína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crete (an island and administrative region of Greece in the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Крит</i> (Krit).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Hinduism</i>) Krishna</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कृष्ण</i> (kṛṣṇa) through English <i>Krishna</i>. Used at least since the first Russian translation of Bhagavad Gita made from English in 1788.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krk (an island of Croatia in the Adriatic Sea)</li><li>Krk (a town on the island of Krk, Croatia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Cromwell</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Cromwell</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Кромвель</i> (Kromvelʹ)</li></ol><p>From English <i>Cromwell</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kronstadt, a city in Russia</li></ol><p>From German <i>Kronstadt</i> (“crown city”).</p><p>Probably inspired by the name of an older fortification, now known as <i>Кроншлот</i> (Kronšlot, “crown castle”) (see Dutch <i>slot</i> or Swedish <i>slott</i>), before also known as <i>Кроншлоссъ</i> (Kronšloss) (from German <i>Schloß</i>) (by folk etymology used even in school books, <i>шлот(ъ)</i> has meaning <i>lock</i>, while <i>шлосс(ъ)</i> means <i>key</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kropyvnytskyi (a city, the administrative center of Kirovohrad Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>a Ukrainian surname</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Krylov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crimea (a geographic region and peninsula in Eastern Europe, jutting out into the Black Sea; de facto occupied and annexed in 2014 as a republic of Russia, but internationally recognized as an autonomous republic of Ukraine :)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>qırım</i> or Crimean Tatar name of the peninsula <i>Qırım</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crimean peninsula</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Крым</i> (Krym).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kryuchkov, transferred from the nickname</li></ol><p><i>крючо́к</i> (krjučók) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Xavier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ксаве́рий</i> (Ksavérij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Ksena, of the female given name Ксе́ния (Ksénija), equivalent to English Xena</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ksenia, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ξενία</i> (xenía, “hospitable”). Cognate with English Xenia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xenophanes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ξενοφᾰ́νης</i> (Xenophắnēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xerxes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ξέρξης</i> (Xérxēs), from Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠</i> (x-š-y-a-r-š-a /⁠Xšayāršā⁠/, “ruler among kings”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cthulhu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuala Lumpur (the capital city of Malaysia)</li><li>Kuala Lumpur (a federal territory of Malaysia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay <i>Kuala Lumpur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cuba (a country, the largest island (based on land area) in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuban (a river in southern Russia)</li><li>Kuban (a region in southern Russia, surrounding the Kuban River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kuvayev or Kuvaev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuwait (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li><li>Kuwait City (the capital city of Kuwait)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kudryavtsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) derogatory name for <i>Kyiv</i>: the capital and largest city of Ukraine</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics,&#32;metonymic) the Ukrainian government</li></ol><p>Possibly from English <i>Kyiv</i>, the transliteration of Ukrainian <i>Ки́їв</i> (Kýjiv), as the Latin letter wye (y) looks like the Cyrillic letter u (у).</p><p>Alternatively from similarity with the vulgar word ху́ев (xújev), if not influenced by both.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuznetsov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>кузнец</i> (kuznec, “blacksmith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) of Kuzma, Russian: <i>Кузьма́</i> (Kuzʹmá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kuzma, equivalent to English Cosmo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuzmin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kuzminov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Queens (a borough of New York City)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuybyshev, most notably Valerian Kuybyshev, a Russian Bolshevik revolutionary</li><li>Kuybyshev (former, Soviet name of Samara, a large city in Samara Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Kuybyshev (any of various settlements in Russia and other former Soviet states)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuytun (a county-level city of Ili prefecture, Xinjiang autonomous region, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuleba, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kulibin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kulbakin</li></ol><p>From <i>Кульбака</i> (Kulʹbaka) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kumamoto (the capital city of Kumamoto Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Kumamoto (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>熊本</i> (くまもと, Kumamoto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kumasi (a city in Ghana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Confucius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese 孔子 (<i>Kǒngzǐ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kunming (a prefecture-level city, the provincial capital of Yunnan, in southwestern China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>昆明</i> (Kūnmíng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuopio (a city and municipality of Northern Savonia, Finland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Cupid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cupīdō</i>, personification of <i>cupīdō</i> (“desire, desire of love”), from <i>cupidus</i> (“eager, greedy, passionate”), from <i>cupiō</i> (“to desire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuprin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kupiansk (a city, the administrative centre of both the urban hromada and the raion of the same name of Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1655)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ку́пянск</i> (Kúpjansk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kuravlyov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kurbanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kurbyko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Курби́ко</i> (Kurbýko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kurdistan (a cultural region in West Asia inhabited mostly by the Kurds, encompassing parts of Turkey, Iraq, Iran and Syria)# (<i>by extension</i>) Kurdistan (an autonomous region in northern and northeastern Iraq, lying within the aforementioned Kurdish-majority region)# (<i>by extension</i>) Kurdistan (a province in northwestern Iran, lying within the aforementioned Kurdish-majority region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuril Islands, Kurils (an archipelago of Sakhalin Oblast, Russia, some of which are claimed by Japan)</li></ol><p>One theory suggests that the islands' name was ultimately from Ainu <i>クㇽ</i> (“a person”).</p><p>Russian folk etymology claims that this name was also from курить (kuritʹ), which means "to smoke", referring to smoke produced by the volcanoes.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kuril Islands (an archipelago of Sakhalin Oblast, Russia, some of which are claimed by Japan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kurkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Courland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname: surname Kurnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kurnosov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kursk (a city, the administrative center of Kursk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Кур</i> (Kur, “Kur River”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk). Possibly related to <i>курья́</i> (kurʹjá) or <i>ку́рица</i> (kúrica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kurchatov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kushiro (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Kushiro (the capital city of Kushiro subprefecture, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>釧路</i> (くしろ, Kushiro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kutaisi (the capital of the region of Imereti in the country of Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kutuzov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Kuczyński</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the name of the settlement that would become the city of Moscow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kushnar, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Kushner</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyzyl (the capital city of the Tuva Republic, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a legendary settlement of first Russian colonists in Alaska</li></ol><p>Probably from Inupiaq</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyz Republic (official name of <i>Kyrgyzstan</i>: a country in Central Asia)</li></ol><p>Calque of Kyrgyz <i>Кыргыз Республикасы</i> (Kırgız Respublikası).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyzstan standard form of <i>Кирги́зия</i> (Kirgízija): Kyrgyzstan (a country in Central Asia, bordering on Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kyrgyz <i>Кыргызстан</i> (Kırgızstan). Can be analysed as <i>кыргыз</i> (kyrgyz) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Japanese, equivalent to English Keisuke</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>けいすけ</i> (Keisuke).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cairns (a city in Queensland, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyushu (the third-largest island of Japan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kütahya (the capital city of Kütahya Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyuyorelyakh (a rural locality in Gorny district, Sakha, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Кюерелях</i> (Kuyerelaq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cologne (the largest city in North Rhine-Westphalia, in northwestern Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Köln</i>. Further from Latin <i>colōnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Königsberg (the former capital of East Prussia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Königsberg</i>, from <i>König</i> (“king”) + <i>Berg</i> (“mountain”), named in honour/honor of Ottokar II, king of Bohemia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ки́ев</i> (Kíjev).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) LNR and DNR combined (Luhansk People's Republic and Donetsk People's Republic combined)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>лесбия́нки, ге́и, бисексуа́лы и трансге́ндеры</i> (lesbijánki, géi, bisɛksuály i transgéndɛry): LGBT (Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Transgender&#47;Transexual)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) LNR and DNR combined (Luhansk People's Republic and Donetsk People's Republic combined)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) initialism of <i>Либера́льно-демократи́ческая па́ртия Росси́и</i> (Liberálʹno-demokratíčeskaja pártija Rossíi, “LDPR, Liberal Democratic Party of Russia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>лакокра́сочный материа́л</i> (lakokrásočnyj materiál)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) initialism of <i>лева́я кно́пка мы́ши</i> (levája knópka mýši, “left mouse button”): LMB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Луга́нская Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Lugánskaja Naródnaja Respúblika, “Luhansk People's Republic”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;ecology,&#32;Moscow) forest park protective ring (<i>1935‒2020</i>)</li><li>green belt</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>лесопа́рковый защи́тный по́яс</i> (lesopárkovyj zaščítnyj pójas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>LSD, acid (диэтилами́д d-лизерги́новой кислоты́ (dietilamíd d-lizergínovoj kislotý))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>LSD</i>, initialism of <i>lysergic acid diethylamide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Любля́нский университе́т</i> (Ljubljánskij universitét, “UL”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>ли́ния электропереда́чи</i> (línija elektroperedáči): electric power transmission line</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>English Channel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>La Manche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>La Paz (the capital city of Bolivia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Elbe (European river)</li><li>Laba (a tributary of the Kuban River, Krasnodar Krai and Adygea in Russia)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olba</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;albī</i>. Doublet of <i>Эльба</i> (Elʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laurasia (hypothetical ancient supercontinent in the Northern Hemisphere, evolved from Pangaea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lavrov</li></ol><p><i>Лавр</i> (Lavr, “a given name”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lagos (a city in Lagos state, Nigeria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ladoga (a lake in Russia, east of St. Petersburg). The largest freshwater lake in Europe.</li><li>Staraya Ladoga (a rural locality and former town in Volkhovsky district, Leningrad Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Ладога</i> (Ladoga), through liquid metathesis probably from Old Norse <i>&#42;Aldoga</i> (variant <i>Aldeigja</i>), of uncertain origin. Theories include:</p><p>Theories</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lazarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lazarevskoye (a remote microdistrict of Sochi, Russia)</li></ol><p>Named after <i>Андре́й Петро́вич Ла́зарев</i> (Andréj Petróvič Lázarev, “Andrey Lazarev”), a Russian vice-admiral, 1787-1849.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lanovoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from French</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Lanceray</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lansing (the capital city of Michigan, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Lansing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laos (a country in Southeast Asia)</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;neologism) Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lapland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Лари́са (Larísa), equivalent to English Lara</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Larin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Larisa, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Larissa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Λάρισα</i> (Lárisa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lhasa (the capital city of the Tibet Autonomous Region, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latakia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Latvia (official name of <i>Latvia</i>: a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latvian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latvia (a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ла́твия</i> (Látvija).: Latvia (a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latin America (a continental region consisting of those parts of the Americas located south of the United States where Spanish or Portuguese is predominantly spoken; sometimes including areas speaking French or French-based creoles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Latynin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lahore (the capital of Punjab province, Pakistan, the second-most populous city in Pakistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Le Pen</i></li></ol><p>From French <i>Le Pen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lebedev, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Levant (a cultural region of West Asia consisting of the eastern Mediterranean countries, i.e. Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Palestine, Jordan and Cyprus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Leviyev or Leviev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arctic Ocean (the smallest of the five oceans of the Earth, on and around the North Pole, bordered by the three continents of Asia, Europe and North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leiden (a city in South Holland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic, equivalent to English Leila</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic لَيْلَى (laylā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leinster (a province of Ireland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Leinster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leipzig (an independent city in Saxony, Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lelystad (the capital city of Flevoland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Еле́на (Jeléna), equivalent to English Lena</li><li>Lena (a major river in Irkutsk Oblast and the Sakha Republic, flowing 2,734 miles from Lake Baikal to the Arctic Ocean Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>Лє́на</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;sg (Léna), from Evenki <i>Елюенэ</i> (Jeljujenə, literally “the Large River”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>masculine relational adjective of <i>Лена</i> (Lena)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lenin (the famous alias of Russian revolutionary leader and politician Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov, April 22, 1870, to January 24, 1924)</li></ol><p>From <i>Лена</i> (Lena) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p><p>After the workers' revolt in the Lena Gold Mining Partnership, Ulyanov adopted the nom de guerre of "Lenin" in December 1901, taking the River Lena as a basis (Лена + possessive-adjective suffix -ин), thereby imitating the manner in which Georgi Plekhanov had adopted the pseudonym on "Volgin" after the River Volga.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feminine relational adjective of <i>Ле́на</i> (Léna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhalgori, Leningori (a town in South Ossetia, a de-facto independent state internationally recognized as part of the country of Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Leningrad (the name of a major city in Russian SFSR, from 1924 to 1991)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin, “Lenin”) +&lrm; <i>гра́д</i> (grád, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Several rivers in Germany, Italy and France</li><li>a district municipality of Holzminden district, Lower Saxony, Germany</li><li>a transliteration of the Swedish male given name <i>Lenne</i></li><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Lenne</i></li><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Lenné</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Lenochka, of the female given name Ле́на (Léna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leinster (a province of Ireland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Leinster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Leonid, equivalent to English Leonidas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Λεωνίδης</i> (Leōnídēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname: surname Leonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Leontyev, also written Leontief, Leontiev, etc.; surname of various notable Russians and Soviets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lermontov, a prominent family in Russia, most notably Mikhail Lermontov, a Russian Romantic writer, poet and painter</li><li>Lermontov (a town in Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lesotho (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the river Lethe, the river of oblivion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Λήθη</i> (Lḗthē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flying Spaghetti Monster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical&#32;folklore</i>) Flying Dutchman (mythical ghost ship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leeuwarden (the capital city of Friesland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>False Dmitry (the name refers to various impostors who passed themselves off as Tsarevich Dmitry Ivanovich)</li></ol><p><i>лже-</i> (lže-) +&lrm; <i>Дми́трий</i> (Dmítrij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liberia (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libreville (the capital city of Gabon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebanon (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Livanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Лива́н</i> (Liván).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libya (a country in North Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Livorno (a province and city in Tuscany, Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Livorno</i>, from Old Italian <i>Legorno</i>, from Latin <i>Liburnus</i>, from <i>Liburnī</i>, name of the ancient population of Liburnians.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arab League (an international organization of Arabic-speaking nations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Lida, of the female given name Ли́дия (Lídija)</li><li>name of a city in Belarus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lidiya, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Lydia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Lydia (a historical region and ancient kingdom in western Asia Minor, in modern-day Turkey)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Λυδία</i> (Ludía).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>Арка́дий</i> (Arkádij), <i>Рома́н</i> (Román).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Елизаве́та (Jelizavéta), equivalent to English Liza or Eliza</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gänseliesel, the "Goose-Girl", "Goose-Lizzy"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Licopid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycurgus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Лику́рг</i> (Likúrg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Liliana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lilongwe (a city, the capital city of Malawi)</li></ol><p>From Chichewa <i>Lilongwe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lima (the capital city of Peru)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Limburg (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lincoln (the capital city of Nebraska, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Lincoln</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Linna; particularly, Finnish author, Väinö Linna, December 20, 1920 – April 21, 1992. He gained literary fame with his third novel, <i>Tuntematon sotilas</i> (The Unknown Soldier, published in 1954)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>Linna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>software</i>) Linux</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Linux</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lipetsk (a city, the administrative center of Lipetsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>ли́пец</i> (lípec) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lisitsyn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lysychansk (a city in Luhansk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lisbon (a port city on the Iberian Peninsula, at the mouth of the Tagus River on the Atlantic Ocean; the capital city of Portugal)</li></ol><p>Most likely from German and Dutch <i>Lissabon</i>, ultimately from Arabic لِشْبُونَة (lišbūna), from Latin <i>Olisipo</i>, further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Лиссабо́н</i> (Lissabón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Listyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Litvinenko, from Ukrainian or Lytvynenko</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Little Rock (the capital city of Arkansas, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Little Rock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Lithuania (official name of <i>Lithuania</i>: a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lithuanian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Likhachev, Likhachov, or Likhachyov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>"Wild Nineties", the decade of the 1990s, characterised in Russia by economic difficulties, crime epidemic, regional separatism and social decadence.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liechtenstein (a microstate in Central Europe)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Austrian German <i>Liechtenstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lobamba (the capital city of Eswatini)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lobanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Loginov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lodz (the capital and largest city of Lodz Voivodeship, Poland, also spelled Łódź)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lozova (a city in Lozova Raion, Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lombardy (an administrative region in northern Italy, where its capital and the largest city Milan is founded in the Po Valley)</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>Lombardia</i> (“Lombardy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lomé (the capital city of Togo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lomonosov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London (the capital city of the United Kingdom; the capital city of England)</li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>London</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lori (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Լոռի</i> (Loṙi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Los Angeles (a major city and seaport in southern California, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Los Angeles</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Losev; particularly Oleg Losev, was a Soviet scientist and inventor who made significant discoveries in the field of semiconductor junctions and the light emitting diode (LED).</li></ol><p>From Middle Russian <i>Ло́севъ</i> (Lósev). First attested in 1626. By surface analysis, <i>лось</i> (losʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lostiuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lorraine (a cultural region, former administrative region, and former duchy in eastern France; since 2016 part of the region of Grand Est region)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Lotharingia (a historical region, early medieval kingdom and former duchy in Western Europe; a successor kingdom of the Carolingian empire, now politically split between Lorraine in France, western Germany, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Lotharingien</i> or French <i>Lotharingie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luanda (the capital city of Angola)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Loire (the longest river in France, passing (from southeast to northwest) through the departments of Ardèche, Haute-Loire, Loire, Saône-et-Loire, Allier, Nièvre, Cher, Loiret, Loir-et-Cher, Indre-et-Loire, Maine-et-Loire and Loire-Atlantique)# Loire (a department of Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Loire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lubny (a city, the administrative center of Lubny Raion, Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lubyanka Square</li><li>Lubyanka Building, the headquarters of the Soviet intelligence agency KGB and Russian FSB</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the intelligence agencies themselves; KGB, FSB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luga (a river in Russia)</li><li>Luga (a town in Leningrad Oblast, Russia). The town is placed on the river.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;online slang,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;politics) Luhansk People's Republic</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Луга́нск</i> (Lugánsk, “Luhansk”) +&lrm; <i>Уга́нда</i> (Ugánda, “Uganda”). References either the poor living conditions in Luhansk People's Republic (akin to those of poor African countries) or the "primitive mind" of its anti-Ukrainian inhabitants.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) Luhansk People's Republic</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Луга́нск</i> (Lugánsk, “Luhansk”) +&lrm; <i>гандо́н</i> (gandón, “a condom, a contemptible person”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luhansk (a city in Ukraine)</li><li>clipping of <i>Луга́нская Наро́дная Респу́блика</i> (Lugánskaja Naródnaja Respúblika, “Luhansk People's Republic”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luhansk People's Republic (a self-proclaimed quasi-state, internationally recognized as part of country of Ukraine; one of two members of the Confederation of Novorossiya.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Lugansk Oblast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the Lugansk People's Republic</li></ol><p><i>Луга́нск</i> (Lugánsk) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lugo (a city in Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Lugo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Italian male given name <i>Luigi</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Louisiana (a state in the Deep South and South Central regions of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lukashenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lukin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(folklore,&#32;Slavic mythology) Lukomorye (in medieval Russian folklore, a magical shore in northern Russia along the Arctic coast)</li></ol><p><i>лука</i> (luka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мо́ре</i> (móre, “sea”), literally “bend of the sea”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lukyanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Lumumba</i>; particularly, was a Congolese politician and independence leader Patrice Lumumba.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Lumumba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Lupercus (Roman god)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lupercus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Luperca (Roman goddess)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Luperca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lusaka (the capital city of Zambia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luzon (the largest island of the Philippines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lutsk (a city, the administrative center of Volyn Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lhasa (the capital city of the Tibet Autonomous Region, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lykin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(folklore,&#32;Slavic mythology) Bald Mountain (mythical mountain or hill where witches gather)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lviv (a city, the administrative center of Lviv Oblast, in western Ukraine)</li><li>a surname, Lvov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lviv Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lgov (a town in Kursk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liège (a province of Wallonia, Belgium)</li><li>Liège (the capital of the province of Liège, Wallonia, Belgium)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Luba, of the female given name Любо́вь (Ljubóvʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyubavichi (one of two villages in the Smolensk Oblast of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lyubimov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lublin (the capital and largest city of Lublin Voivodeship, in southeast Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ljubljana (the capital city of Slovenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>University of Ljubljana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lyubshin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ludmila or Lyudmila</li></ol><p>Probably from Czech <i>Ludmila</i>. Popularized by Russian writers of the early 19th century. May be analyzed as <i>люд</i> (ljud) or <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) + <i>мила́</i> (milá), short feminine form of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj), i.e. dear to the people, beloved by the people.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luxembourg (a small country in Western Europe)# Luxembourg (a province of Wallonia, Belgium)# Luxembourg, Luxembourg City (the capital city of Luxembourg)# Luxembourg (one of the twelve cantons of the country of Luxembourg, which includes its capital city)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lyusya's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Liusya, of the female given name Людми́ла (Ljudmíla)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) LSD</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Lucifer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liaoning (a province in northeastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liaoyang (a city in Liaoning, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Lionya, of the male given names Леони́д (Leoníd) or Леоні́д (Leoníd)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Леони́д</i> (Leoníd) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Lyokha, of the male given name Алексе́й (Alekséj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Lyosha, of the male given name Алексе́й (Alekséj)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Алёша</i> (Aljóša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>IAEA (International Atomic Energy Agency)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Междунаро́дное аге́нтство по а́томной эне́ргии</i> (Meždunaródnoje agéntstvo po átomnoj enɛ́rgii, “International Atomic Energy Agency”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>межконтинента́льная баллисти́ческая раке́та</i> (mežkontinentálʹnaja ballistíčeskaja rakéta, “ICBM, intercontinental ballistic missile”)</li><li>(<i>prostitution</i>) initialism of <i>мине́т без рези́нки</i> (minét bez rezínki, “head without rubber”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ministry of Internal Affairs, Interior Ministry</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Министе́рство вну́тренних де́л</i> (Ministérstvo vnútrennix dél).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>IMF, International Monetary Fund</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>междунаро́дный валю́тный фо́нд</i> (meždunaródnyj valjútnyj fónd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Моско́вский госуда́рственный университе́т</i> (Moskóvskij gosudárstvennyj universitét, “MSU”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>министе́рство иностра́нных дел</i> (ministérstvo inostránnyx del): ministry of foreign affairs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microcomputer</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>микрокомпью́тер</i> (mikrokompʹjútɛr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ISS, international space station</li><li>(<i>international&#32;law</i>) initialism of <i>междунаро́дный кримина́льный суд</i> (meždunaródnyj kriminálʹnyj sud): International Criminal Court (ICC)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>междунаро́дная косми́ческая ста́нция</i> (meždunaródnaja kosmíčeskaja stáncija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>MOD</i> (“ministry of defence”), <i>DOD</i> (“department of defense”); abbreviation of <i>министерства обороны</i> (ministerstva oborony)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) initialism of <i>мѐжотраслево́й бала́нс</i> (mèžotraslevój baláns): input-output table, input-output model</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>магни́тно-резона́нсная томогра́фия</i> (magnítno-rezonánsnaja tomográfija); MRI, magnetic resonance imaging</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá)</li><li>initialism of <i>Моско́вское вре́мя</i> (Moskóvskoje vrémja, “Moscow Time, Moscow Standard Time”) (the time zone in western Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>STS (Space Transportation System)</li><li>Space Shuttle, shuttle</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>многора́зовый тра́нспортный косми́ческий кора́бль</i> (mnogorázovyj tránsportnyj kosmíčeskij koráblʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>international&#32;law</i>) acronym of <i>междунаро́дный уголо́вный суд</i> (meždunaródnyj ugolóvnyj sud): International Criminal Court (ICC)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>междунаро́дный фонети́ческий алфави́т</i> (meždunaródnyj fonɛtíčeskij alfavít, “IPA, International Phonetic Alphabet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Moscow Artistic Academic Theatre</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Моско́вский Худо́жественный академи́ческий теа́тр</i> (Moskóvskij Xudóžestvennyj akademíčeskij teátr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>моско́вские центра́льные диа́метры</i> (moskóvskije centrálʹnyje diámetry, “Moscow Central Diameters”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>моско́вское центра́льное кольцо́</i> (moskóvskoje centrálʹnoje kolʹcó, “Moscow Central Circle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business&#32;law</i>) initialism of <i>муниципа́льно-ча́стное партнёрство</i> (municipálʹno-částnoje partnjórstvo, “public–private partnership”, literally “municipal–private partnership”): PPP</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ministry of Emergency Situations</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>министе́рство по чрезвыча́йным ситуа́циям</i> (ministérstvo po črezvyčájnym situácijam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maastricht (the capital city of Limburg, Netherlands)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>Maastricht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mauritius (a country in the Indian Ocean, east of East Africa and Madagascar)# Mauritius (the main island of the country of Mauritius)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Маври́кий</i> (Mavríkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mauritania (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magadan (a port city, the administrative center of Magadan Oblast, Russia, on the Sea of Ohkotsk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magas (the capital city of Ingushetia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magnitogorsk (an industrial city in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>магни́т</i> (magnít, “magnet”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, connecting vowel) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk); named after a mountain called <i>магни́тная</i> (magnítnaja, “magnetic”) because it consisted almost completely of iron.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Magnitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Magomedov, originating as a patronymic, feminine equivalent Магоме́дова (Magomédova)</li></ol><p><i>Магоме́д</i> (Magoméd, “Muhammad”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Muhammad, the person who introduced Islam</li><li>a male given name, Magomet, from Arabic, equivalent to English Muhammad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Mahomet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madagascar (an island and country off the east coast of Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Majuro (the capital city of the Marshall Islands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madison (the capital city of Wisconsin, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Madison</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madras (former name of Chennai, a major city, the capital of Tamil Nadu, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madrid (the capital city of Spain)</li><li>Madrid, Community of Madrid (an autonomous community of Spain)</li><li>Madrid (a province of the Community of Madrid, Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mazandaran (a province in northern Iran)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maizuru (a city in Kyoto Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>舞鶴</i> (まいづる, Maizuru).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Mike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Maykl, from English, equivalent to English Michael</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Michael</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maykop (the capital city of Adygea, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mayotte (an overseas department of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macau (a city, special administrative region, and peninsula in China, west of Hong Kong)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Makarevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Макарэ́віч (Makarévič)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>Макарэ́віч</i> (Makarévič), from the common Slavic given name <i>Мака́р</i> (Makár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Makarenko, in particular, a Soviet Ukrainian/Russian educator, social worker and writer Anton Makarenko</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Макаре́нко</i> (Makarénko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Makary, equivalent to English Macarius</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μακάριος</i> (Makários), from <i>μακάριος</i> (makários, “blessed, happy, fortunate”). Doublet of <i>Мака́р</i> (Makár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Makarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>McDonald's <i>(American fast-food chain)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedonia (a geographic region in Southeast Europe in the Balkans, including North Macedonia and parts of Greece, Bulgaria, Albania and Serbia)# (<i>historical</i>) Macedonia (an ancient Greek kingdom in Southeast Europe)# Macedonia (former name of <i>North Macedonia</i>: a country in Southeast Europe in the Balkans)</li><li>Macedonia (a geographic region and former administrative region of Greece)# Macedonia (a geographic region in southwestern Bulgaria; in full, Pirin Macedonia or Bulgarian Macedonia)# (<i>historical</i>) Macedonia (a former constituent republic of Yugoslavia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μακεδονία</i> (Makedonía, “Macedonia”), from <i>μακεδονία</i> (makedonía, “highland”), from <i>μακεδνός</i> (makednós, “high, tall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Македо́ния</i> (Makedónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Makiyivka (a city in Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>McNamara</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>McNamara</i>, from Irish <i>Mac Conmara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Maks, of the male given name Макси́м (Maksím)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Макси́м</i> (Maksím).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Maksim, from Latin, equivalent to English Maximus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Maximus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Maksimov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Maksimovich or Maximovich</li></ol><p><i>Макси́м</i> (Maksím) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic</li></ol><p><i>Макси́м</i> (Maksím) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Makurazaki (a city in Kagoshima Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>枕崎</i> (まくらざき, Makurazaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malabo (a city in Equatorial Guinea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malawi (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malaysia (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Мала́йзия</i> (Malájzija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Malakhovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asia Minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Lesser Armenia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ursa Minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malé (the capital city of the Maldives)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Malevich</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Malenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Malenkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mali (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Malkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Little Russia</li><li>(dated,&#32;now considered&#32;offensive) Ukraine</li></ol><p>Originally <i>Малая Россия</i> (Malaja Rossija), itself a calque of Ancient Greek <i>Μικρὰ Ῥωσσίᾱ</i> (Mikrà Rhōssíā), attested since 1292. Coined by the Patriarch of Constantinople to differentiate it from "Great Russia" (Μεγάλη Ῥωσσίᾱ). Can be analysed as <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj, “small”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Ро́ссия</i> (Róssija, “Rus, Russia”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Canis Minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Malyshev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Malgin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maldives (a country and archipelago of South Asia in the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Malkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malmö (a city in Scania, Sweden; the third-largest city in the country, located on the south-west coast)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>Malmö</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malta (an archipelago and country in Southern Europe, in the Mediterranean Sea)# Malta (the largest island in the Maltese Archipelago)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Maltsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Managua (the capital city of Nicaragua)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manama (the capital city of Bahrain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an abandoned city in Siberia. One of the oldest cities of Siberia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Mandelshtam, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Mandelstamm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manila (the capital city of the Philippines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Munster (a province of Ireland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Munster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manchuria (a region of northeastern China comprising the three provinces of Liaoning, Jilin, and Heilongjiang and the northeastern part of Inner Mongolia)</li><li>Manzhouli, Manchouli (a city in the Inner Mongolia autonomous region, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Маньчжу́рия</i> (Manʹčžúrija, “Manchuria; Manzhouli”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Manya, of the female given names Мари́я (Maríja) or Ма́рья (Márʹja)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja) or <i>Ма́рья</i> (Márʹja) + <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Mandarin surname <i>毛</i> (Máo)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 毛 (<i>Máo</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maputo (the capital city of Mozambique)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mar-a-Lago (a beach resort in Palm Beach, Florida, primary residence of Donald Trump)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maralik (a town in Shirak Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Մարալիկ</i> (Maralik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Margarita or Margaret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Margarita</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μαργαρίτης</i> (margarítēs, “pearl”); see English <i>Margaret</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic</li></ol><p>From Arabic مَرْجَان (marjān, “corals”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Mariinsky Theatre</li></ol><p><i>марии́н(ский)</i> (mariín(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mari El (a republic of Russia; capital: Yoshkar-Ola)</li></ol><p>From Eastern Mari <i>Марий</i> (Marij, “Mari”), from <i>марий</i> (marij, “man, husband”), and <i>эл</i> (el, “country, land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Marina, from Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Marina</i>, feminine form of <i>Marinus</i>, from <i>marīnus</i> (“of the sea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Spanish male given name <i>Mario</i></li><li>a transliteration of the Italian male given name <i>Mario</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mariupol (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Greek <i>Μαριούπολη</i> (Marioúpoli), from being named after the Virgin Mary, Empress Maria Feodorovna, the Crimean Greek city of Mariampol, from <i>Μαρία</i> (María) +&lrm; <i>πόλη</i> (póli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Maria or Mary</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Mary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Маріа</i> (Maria), from Ancient Greek <i>Μαρία</i> (María), from Aramaic <i>מרים</i> (maryam); doublet of <i>Ма́рья</i> (Márʹja); cognate with English <i>Mary</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ма́рья Ива́новна</i> (Márʹja Ivánovna); a typical Russian woman with a typical feminine mentality</li></ol><p>From the common given name <i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja) and patronymic <i>Ива́новна</i> (Ivánovna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marijampolė (a city in Lithuania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mark</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Mārcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Markelov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Markov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Marx, from German, particularly, the great German philosopher, economist, historian, sociologist, political theorist, journalist and socialist revolutionary Karl Marx, 5 May, 1818 – 14 March, 1883</li><li>Marks, Marx (a city in Saratov Oblast in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marne (a right tributary of the Seine, in eastern France, flowing 319 miles east and southeast of Paris through the departments of Haute-Marne, Marne, Aisne, Seine-et-Marne, Seine-Saint-Denis and Val-de-Marne)# Marne (a department of Grand Est, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morocco (a country in North Africa)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Marokko</i> or possibly another Germanic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Campus Martius (a field in Rome, Italy)</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>Campus Mārtius</i>, through <i>Марс</i> (Mars) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) and поле (pole).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Martirosov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Մարտիրոսյան</i> (Martirosyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Martuni (a town in Gegharkunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Մարտունի</i> (Martuni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Martynov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Мари́я (Maríja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Marfa, equivalent to English Martha</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Martha</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μάρθα</i> (Mártha), from Aramaic <i>מַרְתָּא</i> (martā, “the mistress”), from <i>מרה</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Martsinkevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Марцінке́віч (Marcinkjévič)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marshall Islands (a country and archipelago of Micronesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>Ма́рья Ива́новна</i> (Márʹja Ivánovna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>Ма́рья Ива́новна</i> (Márʹja Ivánovna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maryinka (a city in Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Mar'ya, equivalent to English Mary or Maria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Маріа</i> (Maria), from Ancient Greek <i>Μαρία</i> (María), from Aramaic <i>מרים</i> (maryam); doublet of <i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja); cognate with English <i>Mary</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a typical Russian woman with a typical feminine mentality</li></ol><p>From the common given name <i>Ма́рья</i> (Márʹja) and patronymic <i>Ива́новна</i> (Ivánovna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maseru (the capital city of Lesotho)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Masis (a town in Ararat Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Մասիս</i> (Masis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muscat (the capital city of Oman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Maslov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Massachusetts (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Maskhadov, from Chechen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Matanzas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Matveyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Matvey, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Matthew</li></ol><p>From <i>Матфе́й</i> (Matféj), from Ancient Greek <i>Ματθαῖος</i> (Matthaîos), from Hebrew <i>מַתִּתְיָהוּ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Матве́й</i> (Matvéj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Matilda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Matrosov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Matryona, from Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>mātrōna</i> (“matron”), from <i>māter</i> (“mother”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(endearing,&#32;poetic) Russia.</li><li>(endearing,&#32;poetic) The personification of Russia as a mother.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Matfey, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Matthew: alternative form of <i>Матве́й</i> (Matvéj)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Matthew (Matthew the Evangelist, one of the twelve Apostles)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Matthew (the Gospel of St. Matthew, the first book of the New Testament of the Bible&#59; attributed to Matthew the Evangelist)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Матфе́й</i> (Matféj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maharashtra (a state in western India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Makhachkala (the capital city of Dagestan, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Avar <i>Махӏачхъала</i> (Maḥʳačqxala).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Masha, of the female given names Мари́я (Maríja) or Ма́рья (Márʹja)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja), <i>Ма́рья</i> (Márʹja), or Ukrainian <i>Марія</i> (Marija) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Ма́ша (Máša)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mashkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mayakovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mbabane (the capital city of Eswatini)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mealhada (a city and municipality in Aveiro district, Portugal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megion (a town in the Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Eastern Khanty, then derived from Eastern Khanty <i>мехи</i> (mjehi, “river bend”) or Eastern Khanty <i>мегин</i> (mjegin, “bend”, adjective) and Eastern Khanty <i>оӈ</i> (oṇ, “river mouth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meghri (a town in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Մեղրի</i> (Meġri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Medan (a city in Indonesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Medvedev</li></ol><p><i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ, “bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Medvedchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Medea</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μήδεια</i> (Mḗdeia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>International Women's Day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Mezentsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in Russia, flows into the White Sea</li><li>a town in Russia, located on mouth of this river</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mecca (a city in the Hejazi region, Saudi Arabia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mexico (a country in North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gulf of Mexico</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>მელაძე</i> (melaʒe)</li><li>a surname, Meladze, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melanesia (a continental region of Oceania, consisting of New Guinea, the Bismarck Archipelago, the Solomon Islands, New Caledonia, Vanuatu and Fiji)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Melenty or Melentius, from Ancient Greek, variant of Меле́тий (Melétij), equivalent to Serbo-Croatian Мелентије (Melentije)</li></ol><p>From <i>Меле́тий</i> (Melétij), then uncertain, then Ancient Greek <i>Μελέτιος</i> (Melétios)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Melety or Meleti, equivalent to English Meletius</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Melissa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μέλισσα</i> (Mélissa), from μέλισσα (mélissa, “bee, honey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melitopol (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melchizedek, Melchisedech (priest and king of Salem in the time of Abraham, mentioned in Genesis 14&#58;18 and Hebrews 7&#58;1-3)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melbourne (the capital city of Victoria, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Méliès</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Méliès</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mendeleev or Mendeleyev; particularly Dmitri Mendeleev, creator of the first version of the periodic table of elements</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Menelaus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μενέλαος</i> (Menélaos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Minsk (the capital city of Belarus)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Мѣньскъ</i> (Měnĭskŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Menkov, feminine equivalent Менько́ва (Menʹkóva)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Menshikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Merkuryev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>toponym</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a mountain in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia</li><li>a mountain in Chukotka Autonomous Okrug, Russia</li></ol><li>A brand of vodka originated from Ukraine.</li><li>a female surname, Mernaya, masculine equivalent Ме́рный (Mérnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mercedes (Mercedes-Benz)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Spanish, equivalent to English Mercedes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Local Bubble</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́стный</i> (méstnyj) +&lrm; <i>пузы́рь</i> (puzýrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Mephistopheles, Mephisto</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mefody, equivalent to English Methodius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μεθόδιος</i> (Methódios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mexico City (the capital city of Mexico)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Metsamor (a town in Armavir Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Մեծամոր</i> (Mecamor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mechnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Мефо́дий</i> (Mefódij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mihranyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Միհրանյան</i> (Mihranyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Midas</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μίδᾱς</i> (Mídās), from Phrygian <i>Μιδας</i> (Midas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Middelburg (the capital city of Zeeland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Mizulin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Мико́ла</i> (Mykóla)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mikoyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Միկոյան</i> (Mikoyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) Microsoft</li></ol><p>Distorted spelling of Микросо́фт (Mikrosóft), ultimately borrowed from English <i>Microsoft</i>. Compare with English <i>Micro$oft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Micronesia (a continental region in Oceania in the northwestern Pacific Ocean consisting of around 2,000 small islands)# Micronesia (the Federated States of Micronesia (a country consisting of about 700 islands in the Micronesia region))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Mila</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milan (a city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Milan and the region of Lombardy, Italy)# Milan (a metropolitan city of Lombardy, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Milan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milików (a village in Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miletus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Миле́т</i> (Milét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Serbo-Croatian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>Ми́лица</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Milov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Slavic female given name, Miloslava</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minato (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Minato (a ward of Osaka, Japan)</li><li>Minato (a ward of Nagoya, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>港</i> (みなと, Minato).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mineralnye Vody (a small town in Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Minin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ministry of Internal Affairs (MVD)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minnesota (a state in the Upper Midwest region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Ministry of Science and Higher Education (Russia): a Russian federal executive body responsible for state control over scientific institutions and higher-level education in Russia.</li></ol><p><i>мин</i> (min, “ministry”) +&lrm; <i>обр</i> (obr, “education”) +&lrm; <i>нау́ки</i> (naúki, “sciences”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Minos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ми́нос</i> (Mínos).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minsk (the capital city of Belarus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ministry of energy, department of energy</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Министе́рство энерге́тики</i> (Ministérstvo energétiki, “Ministry of energy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrhorod (a city, the administrative center of Myrhorod Raion, Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mironov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mississippi (a state of the United States)</li><li>Mississippi, Mississippi River (a major river in the United States, flowing southward from north-central Minnesota through Wisconsin, Iowa, Illinois, Missouri, Kentucky, Tennessee, Arkansas, Minnesota and Louisiana, emptying into the Gulf of Mexico)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Missouri (a state in the Midwestern region of the United States)</li><li>Missouri, Missouri River (the longest river in North America, rising in the Rocky Mountains of western Montana and flowing roughly southeast over 2,300 miles through North Dakota, South Dakota, Nebraska, Iowa, Kansas and Missouri, before joining the Mississippi at St. Louis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mitaka (a city in Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>三鷹</i> (みたか, Mitaka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mitrofan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Mitrofanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mitrokhin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Mitja, of the male given name Дми́трий (Dmítrij)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Дми́трий</i> (Dmítrij) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mikhail, from Ancient Greek &lt; Hebrew, equivalent to English Michael</li></ol><p>From Byzantine Greek <i>Μιχαήλ</i> (Mikhaḗl), from Hebrew <i>מִיכָאֵל</i> (mikha'él, literally “Who is like God?”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mikhailenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Михайле́нко</i> (Myxajlénko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mikhailov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mikhalkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from English, equivalent to English Michaelson</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Michaelson</i>, and other similar Germanic patronymics.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mickievič, Belarusian and Polish surname</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Michigan (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Misha, of the male given name Михаи́л (Mixaíl), equivalent to English Mike</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Михаи́л</i> (Mixaíl) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English female given name <i>Michelle</i></li><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Michel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the male given name Ми́ша (Míša)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mishustin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mie (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>三重</i> (みえ, Mie).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miyagi (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>宮城</i> (みやぎ, Miyagi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miyazaki (a prefecture in southern Japan)</li><li>a surname from Japanese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>宮崎</i> (みやざき, Miyazaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miyake (one of 4 subprefectures of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>三宅</i> (みやけ, Miyake).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milky Way</li></ol><p>A calque of Latin <i>Via Lactea</i>.</p><p><i>мле́чный</i> (mléčnyj) is a stilted/archaic form of моло́чный (molóčnyj, “milky”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mogadishu (the capital city of Somalia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mogilev (a city in Belarus)</li><li>a surname, Mogilyov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mohyliv-Podilskyi (a city, the administrative center of Mohyliv-Podilskyi Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Modestus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mozambique (a country in East Africa and Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Moses</li><li>a male given name, Moisei or Moisey, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Moses</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μωϋσῆς</i> (Mōüsês), from Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōše).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moynaki Lake (a lake in Yevpatoria, Yevpatoria Raion, Crimea, Ukraine, or Russia)</li></ol><p><i>мойна́кский</i> (mojnákskij) +&lrm; <i>о́зеро</i>&nbsp;n (ózero, “lake”), a calque of Crimean Tatar <i>Moynaq gölü</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Mokosh</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old East Slavic <i>Мокошь</i> (Mokošĭ). By surface analysis, (<i>dialectal</i>) <i>моква́</i> (mokvá) +&lrm; <i>-ошь</i> (-ošʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moldavia (former name of Moldova, a country in Eastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moldavian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moldova standard form of <i>Молда́вия</i> (Moldávija): Moldova (a country in Eastern Europe; official name: Респу́блика Молдо́ва (Respúblika Moldóva))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in Russia</li><li>an evacuated city once located on the mouth of this river. Capital of the Mologa principality, which existed in the 14th century. Now the city is located under the water of the Rybinsk Artificial Sea.</li><li>a village located on the left side of river Kostroma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Molotov</li></ol><p>From <i>мо́лот</i> (mólot, “hammer”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov). Originally the pseudonym of Vyacheslav Molotov, real surname <i>Скря́бин</i> (Skrjábin), chosen for its proletarian connotations.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Momus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mombetsu (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>紋別</i> (もんべつ, Mombetsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monaco (a city-state in Western Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mont Blanc</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Mont Blanc</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mongolia (a country in East Asia)# (<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) Mongolia (a Mongolian-speaking geographic region in East Asia, including the country of Mongolia (Outer Mongolia), the autonomous region of Inner Mongolia in China, and sometimes also Buryatia, Tuva and the Altai Republic in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Монго́лия</i> (Mongólija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monza (a river in Russia), tributary of Kostroma</li><li>a railroad station in Russia, located on this river</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montmartre (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Montmartre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montparnasse (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Montparnasse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montreal (an island on which is situated the largest city in Quebec, Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monrovia (the capital city of Liberia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montana (a state in the Mountain West region of the United States, formerly a territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monte Carlo (a part of Monaco famous for its casinos)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montevideo (the capital city of Uruguay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montpelier (the capital city of Vermont, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Montpelier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moravia (a historic region in the eastern Czech Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Мора́вия</i> (Morávija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mordovia (a republic and federal subject of southwestern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mordor.</li><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism) Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Mordor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morkoka (a village in Sakha, Russia)</li><li>Morkoka (a river in Sakha, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Моркуока</i> (Morkuoka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Morozov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol><p><i>мороз</i> (moroz) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moroni (the capital city of the Comoros)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Moskalyov or Moskalev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moscow (a federal city, the capital of Russia)</li><li>Moskva (a river in Russia)</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the government of the Russian Federation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;Москꙑ</i> (&#42;Mosky), originally referring to the river, probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mosky</i> of unknown origin. Perhaps related to Czech <i>moskva</i> (“raw bread”), Slovak <i>mozga</i> (“puddle”), Polish <i>Mozgawa</i>, and more distantly Lithuanian <i>mazgóti</i> (“to wash, rinse”), Sanskrit <i>मज्जति</i> (májjati, “to sink”), Latin <i>mergō</i> (“to dive”). Doublet of <i>Моско́вия</i> (Moskóvija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) derogatory name for <i>Moscow</i>: the capital and largest city of Russia, seen as a city with a high migrant population (mainly referring to migrants from either Central Asian countries or the Caucasus)</li></ol><p>From <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá) +&lrm; <i>-абад</i> (-abad), the suffix likely from Persian آباد, an ending commonly found in Central Asian place names, for example <i>Ашхабад</i> (Ašxabad, “Ashgabat”), <i>Исламабад</i> (Islamabad, “Islamabad”), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) derogatory name for <i>Moscow</i>: the capital and largest city of Russia, seen as a city with a high migrant population (mainly referring to migrants from either Central Asian countries or the Caucasus)</li></ol><p>From <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Muscovy (Grand Duchy of Moscow).</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;especially&#32;Ukraine) modern Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Moscovia</i>, which is in turn from Old East Slavic <i>Московь</i> (Moskovĭ) or <i>Москъвь</i> (Moskŭvĭ), 1st mentioned in the 12th century, a form of <i>Москꙑ</i> (Mosky). Doublet of <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Московия</i> (Moskovija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mossad (the national intelligence agency of Israel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>המוסד</i> (ha-mosád), Arabic اَلْمُوسَاد (al-mūsād). Literally, “the Institute”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moscow City Council</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>моско́вский городско́й сове́т</i> (moskóvskij gorodskój sovét, “Moscow City Council”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Motya, of the female given names Матрёна (Matrjóna) or Матильда (Matilʹda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mudanjiang (a city in China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mukachevo (a city, the administrative center of Mukachevo Raion, Zakarpattia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mumbai (the capital city of Maharashtra, India, formerly known as Bombay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Muravyov</li></ol><p><i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Muratov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Murashko</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Murmansk (a city, the administrative center of Murmansk Oblast, in far northern Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Му́рман</i> (Múrman, “Murman Coast”), itself a clipping of <i>Му́рманский бе́рег</i> (Múrmanskij béreg), from Middle Russian <i>му́рмане</i> (múrmane, “Norwegians”) attested from the 14th century, probably a borrowing from Old Norse <i>norðmaðr</i> (“Norseman”).</p><p>Renamed from <i>Рома́нов-на-Му́рмане</i> (Románov-na-Múrmane) in 1917.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) synonym of <i>Ба́ренцево мо́ре</i> (Bárencevo móre, “the Barents Sea”), a sea in the Arctic Ocean</li></ol><p>From <i>му́рманский</i> (múrmanskij, “of Murmansk”) +&lrm; <i>мо́ре</i> (móre, “sea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Murom (a city in Vladimir Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Musashino (a city in Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>武蔵野</i> (むさしの, Musashino).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Musorgsky or Mussorgsky; particularly Modest Mussorgsky, Russian composer, March 21, 1839 – March 28, 1881</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muhammad the rasul to whom Quran was revealed.</li><li>47th sura (chapter) of Quran.</li><li>a male given name, Muhammad, from Arabic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مُحَمَّد (muḥammad).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) Muhammad, the person who introduced Islam</li><li>a male given name, Mukhammed, from Arabic, equivalent to English Muhammad</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Arabic مُحَمَّد (muḥammad, “praised, commendable, laudable, (as a proper name) Muhammad”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) Bumfuck, Podunk</li></ol><p>From <i>му́ха</i> (múxa, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to shit”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mutsu (a city in Aomori Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Mutsu (a historical province of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>陸奥</i> (むつ, Mutsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;derogatory</i>) alternative form of <i>Мико́ла</i> (Mikóla, “a Ukrainian”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Мико́ла</i> (Mykóla). Mocking the Ukrainian pronunciation of the letter и (y) as "ы".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Myskin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Myshkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myanmar (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Burmese <i>မြန်မာ</i> (mranma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meguro (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>目黒</i> (めぐろ, Meguro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mels</li></ol><p>From the initial letters of surnames <i>Маркс</i> (Marks, “Marx”), <i>Э́нгельс</i> (Éngelʹs, “Engels”), <i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin, “Lenin”), <i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin, “Stalin”).</p><p>First appears in the 20s of the 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maine (a state of the United States; probably named for the province in France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maryland (a state of the United States; named for Queen Mary, queen consort of England, Scotland and Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Müller</i></li><li>a transliteration of the German or English surname <i>Mueller</i></li></ol><p>From German <i>Müller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Munich (the capital city of Bavaria, Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Myagkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dead Sea (a highly saline lake in Israel, Palestine and Jordan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ме́рная</i> (Mérnaja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>не́тто</i> (nétto, nɛ́tto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>NASA (National Aeronautics and Space Administration)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>NASA</i>, acronym for National Aeronautics and Space Administration.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;international law) NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organisation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>NATO</i>. Acronym for North Atlantic Treaty Organisation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Национа́л-большеви́стская па́ртия</i> (Nacionál-bolʹševístskaja pártija); NBP, National Bolshevik Party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet slang) initialism of <i>Но́вый год</i> (Nóvyj god)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>нало́г на доба́вленную сто́имость</i> (nalóg na dobávlennuju stóimostʹ): VAT, value-added tax</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(scientific) research institute</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>нау́чно-иссле́довательский институ́т</i> (naúčno-isslédovatelʹskij institút).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Scientific Research Institute of Calculating Mechanical Engineering (NIIschetmash)</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Нау̀чно-иссле́довательский институ́т счётного машинострое́ния</i> (Naùčno-isslédovatelʹskij institút sčótnovo mašinostrojénija, “Scientific Research Institute of Calculating Mechanical Engineering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>NKVD (<i>a forerunner of the KGB</i>)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т вну́тренних де́л</i> (Naródnyj komissariát vnútrennix dél, “People’s Commissariat for Interior Affairs”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т госуда́рственной безопа́сности</i> (Naródnyj komissariát gosudárstvennoj bezopásnosti, “People's Commissariat for State Security”): NKGB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-commercial organisation, nonprofit organisation, NPO</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>некомме́рческая организа́ция</i> (nekommérčeskaja organizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Наго́рно-Караба́хская Респу́блика</i> (Nagórno-Karabáxskaja Respúblika): Nagorno-Karabakh Republic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>UFO</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>неопо́знанный лета́ющий объе́кт</i> (neopóznannyj letájuščij obʺjékt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil refinery</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>нефтеперераба́тывающий заво́д</i> (neftepererabátyvajuščij zavód).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>NGO, non-governmental organization</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>неправи́тельственная организа́ция</i> (nepravítelʹstvennaja organizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil pumping station</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>нефтеперека́чивающая ста́нция</i> (nefteperekáčivajuščaja stáncija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>наиме́нее развиты́е стра́ны</i> (naiméneje razvitýje strány, “least developed countries”): LDC</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>нача́льник шта́ба</i> (načálʹnik štába, “CSA”), a Chief of Staff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naberezhnye Chelny (a city in Tatarstan, Russia)</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>на́бережный</i> (náberežnyj, “riverbank”) + <i>чёлн</i> (čoln, “boat”), a sound-like adaptation of the Tatar <i>Яр Чаллы</i> (Yar Çallı, “riverbank stone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Tatar surname, Nabiullin or Nabioullin</li><li>a Bashkir surname, Nabiullin or Nabioullin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nabokov, transferred from the nickname</li></ol><p>From <i>набо́кий</i> (nabókij, “crooked”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov), a nickname referring to its bearer's physical appearance.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nowruz (the New Year in the Iranian and Zoroastrian calendars, celebrated on the spring equinox)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagano (the capital city of Nagano Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Nagano (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>長野</i> (ながの, Nagano).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagasaki (the capital city and prefecture of Nagasaki Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>長崎</i> (ながさき, Nagasaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagorno-Karabakh Republic (de-facto independent country, internationally recognized as part of Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast, NKAO (a former autonomous oblast of Azerbaijan SSR, existing from 1923 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagorno-Karabakh (a region of the South Caucasus)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Nagorno-Karabakh Republic (de-facto independent country, internationally recognized as part of Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagoya (the capital city of Aichi Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>名古屋</i> (なごや, Nagoya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Warming Island, Uunartoq Qeqertaq (an island off the east central coast of Greenland, created in 2005 as a result of global warming)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nadvirna (a city, the administrative center of Nadvirna Raion, Ivano-Frankivsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nadezhdin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Nadia, of the female given name Наде́жда (Nadéžda), equivalent to English Nadia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nazarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nairobi (the capital city of Kenya)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nakano (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>中野</i> (なかの, Nakano).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nalchik (the capital city of the Kabardino-Balkarian Republic, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Namibia (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Namur (a city in Belgium)</li></ol><p>From French <i>Namur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nanjing (a large city in China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nara (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Nara (the capital city of Nara Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>奈良</i> (なら, Nara).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Narita (a city in Chiba Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>成田</i> (なりた, Narita).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of Народный комиссариат внутренних дел (Narodnyj komissariat vnytrennikh del)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomzdrav</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т здравоохране́ния</i> (Naródnyj komissariát zdravooxranénija, “People's Commissariat for Health Care”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomzem</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т земледе́лия</i> (Naródnyj komissariát zemledélija, “People's Commissariat for Agriculture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkompochtel</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т почт и телегра́фов</i> (Naródnyj komissariát počt i telegráfov, “People's Commissariat for Posts and Telegraphs”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkompros (1917‒1946)</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т просвеще́ния</i> (Naródnyj komissariát prosveščénija, “People's Commissariat for Education”).</p><p>Directly translated as the “People's Commissariat for Enlightenment”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomput</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т путе́й сообще́ния</i> (Naródnyj komissariát putéj soobščénija, “People's Commissariat of Communication Routes”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomsviaz</li></ol><p>syllabic abbreviation of <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т свя́зи</i> (Naródnyj komissariát svjázi, “People's Commissariat for Communications”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomtankoprom</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т та́нковой промы́шленности</i> (Naródnyj komissariát tánkovoj promýšlennosti, “People's Commissariat of the Tank Industry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomtrud</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т труда́</i> (Naródnyj komissariát trudá, “People's Commissariat for Labour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomtiazhprom</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т тяжёлой промы́шленности</i> (Naródnyj komissariát tjažóloj promýšlennosti, “People's Commissariat for Heavy Industry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomfin</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т фина́нсов</i> (Naródnyj komissariát finánsov, “People's Commissariat for Finance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narkomyust</li></ol><p>Short form from <i>Наро́дный комиссариа́т юсти́ции</i> (Naródnyj komissariát justícii, “People's Commissariat for Justice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>NKVD, the People’s Commissariat for Interior Affairs (a forerunner of the KGB).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naruto (a city in Tokushima Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>鳴門</i> (なると, Naruto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naryn (the capital of the Naryn Region, Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naryan-Mar (a port town, the administrative center of the Nenets Autonomous Okrug, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nassau (the capital city of the Bahamas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Nastasya, of the female given name Анастаси́я (Anastasíja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Nastenka, of the female given name На́стя (Nástja)</li></ol><p><i>На́ст(я)</i> (Nást(ja)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (<i>hypocorism</i>) diminutive, Nastia, of the female given name Анастаси́я (Anastasíja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Natalya, from Latin, equivalent to English Natalie or Natalia</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>Natalia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given names Ната́лья (Natálʹja) or Ната́лия (Natálija), equivalent to English Natasha</li><li>a Russian or Slavic woman who has sexual relations with non-white men, a mud shark</li><li>(Siberia,&#32;Yakutia) an insulated hood for a passenger car</li></ol><p>From Ната́ша being a common Russian name for women, and a racist stereotype depicting Russian women going to foreign countries for sex tourism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Natashenka, of the female given name Ната́ша (Natáša)</li></ol><p><i>Ната́ш(а)</i> (Natáš(a)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Naumov or Naumoff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nauru (a country and island of Oceania, in the Pacific Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nafanail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naha (the capital city of Okinawa Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>那覇</i> (なは, Naha).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nakhichevan (an autonomous republic of Azerbaijan)</li><li>Nakhichevan (the capital city of the Nakhichevan Autonomous Region, Azerbaijan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>banking</i>) central bank (the principal monetary authority of a polity or monetary union, which normally implements monetary objectives by controlling interest rates, issuing currency, managing the national debt, overseeing banking and financial activities, and regulating the supply of money)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>национа́льный банк</i> (nacionálʹnyj bank, “national bank”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nashville (the capital city of Tennessee, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Nashville</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nayoro (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>名寄</i> (なよろ, Nayoro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ngerulmud (the capital city of Palau)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Ngerulmud</i>, Palauan <i>Ngerulmud</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Vietnamese surname <i>Nguyễn</i></li><li>a surname from Vietnamese, equivalent to English Nguyen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Vietnamese <i>Nguyễn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>N'Djamena (the capital city of Chad)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naples (a port city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Naples and the region of Campania, Italy)# Naples (a metropolitan city of Campania, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Naples)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nebraska (a state in the Midwestern region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neva (a river in Russia) (the river on which the Saint Petersburg city is placed)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Either:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nevada (a state in the western United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nevzlin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nevzorov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nevsky Prospect (the main street in Saint Petersburg, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Нью́то́н</i> (Nʹjútón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neglinka (an underground river in Moscow)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neglinnaya (an underground river in Moscow)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhyn (a city in Chernihiv Oblast, in northern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;derogatory,&#32;humorous) Ukraine (<i>as an independent country</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>незале́жна</i> (nezaléžna, “independent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nijmegen (the capital city of Gelderland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naypyidaw (the capital city of Myanmar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Burmese <i>နေပြည်တော်</i> (ne-pranytau).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nekrasov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative name of Instagram</li></ol><p>From <i>нельзя́</i> (nelʹzjá) +&lrm; <i>Инстагра́м</i> (Instagrám), ending in -гра́м, similar to the Instagram and Telegram social media names in Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nemesis</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Νέμεσις</i> (Némesis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nemuro (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Nemuro (the capital city of Nemuro subprefecture, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>根室</i> (ねむろ, Nemuro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nemtsov</li></ol><p>From <i>не́мец</i> (némec) - in modern Russian a "German (man)", originally "foreigner", later any European foreigner.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nenetsia, Nenets Autonomous Okrug (an autonomous okrug of Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neonila</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nepal (a country in South Asia, located between China and India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Neptune</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Neptune</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neretva (a river in Croatia and Bosnia and Herzegovina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unknown or mythological river somewhere in southern Chukotka-area, rich on silver.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nersesov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Ներսիսյան</i> (Nersisyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Netrebko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nefed or Nefyod</li></ol><p>From <i>Мефо́дий</i> (Mefódij), borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Μεθόδιος</i> (Methódios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nechayev or Nechaev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nechepurenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niamey (the capital city of Niger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nilin</li></ol><p><i>ни́ва</i> (níva) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niger (a country in West Africa, situated to the north of Nigeria)# Niger (a major river in West Africa that flows into the Gulf of Guinea in Nigeria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nigeria (a country in West Africa, south of the country of Niger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nidwalden (a canton of Switzerland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Netherlands (the main constituent country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, located primarily in Western Europe bordering Germany and Belgium)# Netherlands (a country in Western Europe, consisting of four constituent countries: the Netherlands per se, Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten the Kingdom of the Netherlands)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Niederlande</i>, perhaps through Polish <i>Niderlandy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Нидерла́ндия</i> (Niderlándija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) Nidhogg</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>Níðhǫggr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhnevartovsk (a city in the Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Нижне-Вартовский</i> (Nižne-Vartovskij), then <i>ни́жний</i> (nížnij, “lower”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ва́ртовский</i> (vártovskij), then uncertain.</p><p>Possibly cognate Eastern Khanty <i>варта</i> (warta), Eastern Khanty <i>варт</i> (wart)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhnekamsk (a city in Tatarstan, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>ни́жний</i> (nížnij, “lower”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>Ка́ма</i> (Káma, “Kama (<i>river</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhneleninskoye</li></ol><p><i>ни́жний</i> (nížnij, “lower”) +&lrm; <i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Literally, “lower Leninsky District”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basses-Alpes (a department of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basses-Pyrénées (a department of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhny Novgorod (an oblast and federal subject of Russia)# Nizhny Novgorod (the capital city of Nizhny Novgorod Oblast, Russia; the fourth-largest in the country by population)</li></ol><p>Literally, “Lower Novgorod”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nizhny Tagil (a city in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lower Saxony (a state in northwestern Germany)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Niedersachsen</i>, as <i>ни́жний</i> (nížnij, “lower”) +&lrm; <i>Саксо́ния</i> (Saksónija, “Saxony”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nizovtsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niigata (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Niigata (the capital city of Niigata Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>新潟</i> (にいがた, Nīgata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nike</li><li>a unisex given name, Nika</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Νίκη</i> (Níkē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nikanorov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nicaragua (a country in Central America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nika's</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nikin</li></ol><p><i>Ни́ка</i> (Níka) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nikita</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Νικήτας</i> (Nikḗtas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nikitin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nikita's</li></ol><p><i>Ники́та</i> (Nikíta) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nikifor, equivalent to English Nicephorus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nikkō (a city in Tochigi Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>日光</i> (にっこう, Nikkō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nikodim, equivalent to English Nicodemus</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Νῑκόδημος</i> (Nīkódēmos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a surname, Nikolayev</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Nikolayev (a khutor in Znamenka rural settlement, Morozovsk Raion, Rostov Oblast, Southern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a village in Bobrovytsia urban hromada, Nizhyn Raion, Chernihiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1587)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a village in Chornyi Ostriv settlement hromada, Khmelnytskyi Raion, Khmelnytskyi Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1555)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a village in Lopatyn settlement hromada, Chervonohrad Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine, founded before 1772)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a village in Davydiv rural hromada, Lviv Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine, first mentioned in 1670)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a city, the administrative centre of Mykolaiv urban hromada, Stryi Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1570 near the River Dniester)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Mykolaiv (a city, the administrative centre of Mykolaiv urban hromada, Mykolaiv Raion and Mykolaiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1789 at the confluence of the Inhul and the Southern Buh)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nikolai’s, Nicholas’s</li></ol><p><i>Никола́й</i> (Nikoláj) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Very many settlements in various countries, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Nikolayevka (an an urban-type settlement (a workers’ settlement), the administrative centre of in Nikolayevka urban settlement and Nikolayevka Raion, Ulyanovsk Oblast, Volga Federal District, Russia, founded in 1839)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numerous settlements in various countries, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Mikałajeva (a hamlet, the administrative centre of Mikałajeva sielsaviet, Šumiłina Raion, Viciebsk Oblast, Belarus)</li><li>Nikolaevo (a town, the administrative centre of Nikolaevo municipality, Stara Zagora Oblast, Bulgaria)</li><li>Nikolayevo (a hamlet in Podbolotye rural settlement, the Raion imeni Babushkina, Vologda Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Nikolayevo (a hamlet in Vizma rural settlement, Belozersk Raion, Vologda Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Mykolaieve (a former village in Danylivka silrada, Krasnohvardiiske Raion, Crimea Oblast, Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic; it disappeared somewhen 1960–1968)</li><li>Mykolaieve (a former rural-type settlement in Podillia silrada, Veselynove Raion, Mykolaiv Oblast, Ukraine, disincorporated in March 2013)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Nikolayevsk, Nikolayevsk-on-Amur (a town in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia, on the Amur River)</li><li>Nikolayevsk (a town, the administrative center of the Nikolayevsky District, Volgograd Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Никола́й</i> (Nikoláj) +&lrm; <i>-евск</i> (-evsk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nikolayevsk-on-Amur (a town in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia, on the Amur River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Several settlements in Russia, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Nikolayevskaya (a stanitsa, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskaya rural settlement, Naurskaya Raion, Chechen Republic, North Caucasian Federal District, Russia, founded in 1848)</li><li>Nikolayevskaya (a stanitsa, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskaya rural settlement, Uspenskoye Raion, Krasnodar Krai, Southern Federal District, Russia, founded in 1825)</li><li>Nikolayevskaya (a stanitsa, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskaya rural settlement, Digora Raion, Republic of North Ossetia–Alania, North Caucasian Federal District, Russia, founded in 1838)</li><li>Nikolayevskaya (a stanitsa, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskaya rural settlement, Konstantinovsk Raion, Rostov Oblast, Southern Federal District, Russia, incorporated in 1836)</li><li>Nikolayevskaya (a former hamlet in Tarnoga rural settlement, Tarnoga Okrug, Vologda Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia, annexed to the village and okrug centre of Tarnogsky Gorodok in 2022)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numerous settlements in Russia, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Nikolayevskoye (a village, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskoye rural settlement, Yadrin Raion, Chuvash Republic, Volga Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Nikolayevskoye (a village, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskoye rural settlement, Prikubanye Raion, Karachay-Cherkess Republic, North Caucasian Federal District, Russia, incorporated in 1964)</li><li>Nikolayevskoye (a village, the administrative centre of Nikolayevskoye rural settlement, Ulyoty Raion, Zabaykalye Krai, Far Eastern Federal District, Russia)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(fully <i>Се́льское поселе́ние «Никола́евское»</i>) Nikolayevskoye rural settlement (a rural settlement of Ulyoty Raion, Zabaykalye Krai, Far Eastern Federal District, Russia, established in 2004)</li></ol></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nikolai or Nikolay, equivalent to English Nicholas</li><li>November (<i>N</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Νικόλαος</i> (Nikólaos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Влади́мир Адамо́вич Николаёнок (1923–2007), Soviet-Belarusian artillery officer and rocket scientist, Deputy Head of Baikonur Cosmodrome 1969–1976</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Николовски</i>&nbsp;m (Nikolovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Николоски</i>&nbsp;m (Nikoloski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nikonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nikopol (a city, the administrative center of Nikopol Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Greek <i>Νικόπολη</i> (Nikópoli), from <i>νίκη</i> (níki) + <i>πόλη</i> (póli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nicosia (the capital city of Cyprus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nikulin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nile (a major river in Africa)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Нилъ</i> (Nilŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Νεῖλος</i> (Neîlos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nilin</li></ol><p><i>Ни́л</i> (Níl) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ни́л</i> (Níl).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Nina</li><li>a diminutive, Nina, of the female given name Антони́на (Antonína)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ninilchik (a town in Alaska, United States)</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from Dena'ina <i>niqnilchint</i> (“place where a lodge is built”) when settled by Russian colonists in 1847.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nina's</li></ol><p><i>Ни́на</i> (Nína) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ningxia, Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region (a Hui autonomous region of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Nisa, of the female given name Ани́сия (Anísija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nisa's</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nisin</li></ol><p><i>Ни́са</i> (Nísa) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niue (an island and country in Polynesia in Oceania, a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nice (a coastal city, the capital of Alpes-Maritimes department in the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region in southeast France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Nizza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Swedish surname <i>Nobel</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Nobel prize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New England (a geographic region of the northeastern United States, consisting of Connecticut, Maine, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Rhode Island and Vermont)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>New England</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Guinea (the world's second-largest island, politically divided between Indonesia and Papua New Guinea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Holland Island (artificial triangular island in the city of Saint Petersburg, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Zealand (a country and archipelago of Oceania; capital: Wellington)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novaya Zemlya (an archipelago of Arkhangelsk Oblast, in the north of Russia in the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol><p><i>но́вая</i> (nóvaja, “new”) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “land, soil”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Caledonia (an archipelago and overseas territory of France in Oceania; capital: Nouméa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nova Kakhovka (a city in Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novgorod (a city, the administrative center of Novgorod Oblast, Russia); in full, <i>Veliky Novgorod</i>; one of the oldest and most important historic cities in Russia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Новъгородъ</i> (Novŭgorodŭ), from <i>новъ</i> (novŭ, “new”) +&lrm; <i>городъ</i> (gorodŭ, “town”). By surface analysis, <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novhorod-Siverskyi (a city in Novhorod-Siverskyi Raion, Chernihiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Но́вгород</i> (Nóvgorod).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Novikov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novo-Arkhangelsk or New Archangel (current Sitka in Alaska, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novoaltaysk (a town in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novodvinsk (a town in Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>Двина́</i> (Dviná, shortening of Се́верная Двина́ (Sévernaja Dviná, “Northern Dvina”), the river it is situated next to) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk, placename suffix). Coined in 1977, when the town was granted town status.</p><p>The town was originally named <i>Вороши́ловский</i> (Vorošílovskij), and was later changed to <i>Первома́йский</i> (Pervomájskij) in the late 1950s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Novodvorsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Novozhilov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novokuznetsk (a city in Kemerovo Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, connecting vowel) +&lrm; <i>кузне́ц</i> (kuznéc, “blacksmith”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novomoskovsk (a city, the administrative center of Novomoskovsk Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novorossiysk (a port city in Krasnodar Krai, in southern Russia, the main Russian port on the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, connecting vowel) +&lrm; <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja, “Russia”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Novorossiya, the former Russian province in southern Ukraine formed during a war with the Ottoman Empire.</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) Novorossiya, a proposed confederation of ethnic Russians in southern Ukraine.</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja, “Russia”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novosibirsk (a city, the administrative center of Novosibirsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Сиби́рь</i> (Sibírʹ, “Siberia”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Siberian Islands (an archipelago of the Sakha Republic, Russia in the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novoslobodskaya (Moscow metro stations on the Koltsevaya line)</li><li>Novoslobodskaya (a street)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>слобода́</i> (slobodá) +&lrm; <i>-ская</i> (-skaja). The newest, colloquial stress comes from the re-analyzed using the compound <i>но́во-</i> (nóvo-) +&lrm; <i>слободска́я</i> (slobodskája) with loose attachment of morphemes.</p><p>Сompare with <i>Электрозаво́дская</i> (Elektrozavódskaja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of a few settlements in the Ukraine&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Novostav (a village in Berestechko urban hromada, Lutsk Raion, Volyn Oblast)</li><li>Novostav (a village in Boratyn rural hromada, Lutsk Raion, Volyn Oblast)</li><li>Novostav (a village in Zoria rural hromada, Rivne Raion, Rivne Oblast)</li><li>Novostav (a village in Shumsk urban hromada, Kremenets Raion, Ternopil Oblast)</li><li>Novostav (a village in Iziaslav urban hromada, Shepetivka Raion, Khmelnytskyi Oblast)</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Новоста́в</i> (Novostáv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novoukrainka (a city, the administrative center of Novoukrainka Raion, Kirovohrad Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Testament</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Но́вый Заве́т</i> (Nóvyj Zavét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Orleans (a port city in Louisiana, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the New World (the Americas and Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Year, New Year's Day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah's ark (the vessel built by Noah under God's instructions, as described in Genesis and the Quran)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noyemberyan (a town in Tavush Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Նոյեմբերյան</i> (Noyemberyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Naomi</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a female given name, Noyemin, equivalent to English Naomi</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>נָעֳמִי</i> (no'omí, literally “my pleasantness”), from <i>נועם &#47; נֹעַם</i> (nó'am, “pleasantness”) + <i>־ִי</i> (-í, “my, of me”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah (biblical figure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nome (a city in Alaska)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nord (a department of the Hauts-de-France region, France)</li><li>Nord (a department of Haiti)</li><li>Nord (a region of Burkina Faso)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Nord</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nor Hachn (a town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Նոր Հաճն</i> (Nor Hačn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norway (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe; capital and largest city: Oslo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Норве́гия</i> (Norvégija).: Norway (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norilsk (a city in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Нори́лка</i> (Norílka, “Norilka (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Normandy (an administrative region, historical province, and medieval kingdom in northwest France, on the English Channel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Normandie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Northumbria (an Anglo-Saxon kingdom in the Northeast of England; in full, Kingdom of Northumbria)# (<i>informal</i>) Northumbria (a vaguely defined geographic area of England, often synonymous with North East England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nosov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>не́тто</i> (nétto, nɛ́tto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nouakchott (the capital city of Mauritania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nubia (a historical region in the upper Nile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ну́бия</i> (Núbija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nuzhin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nuku'alofa (the capital city of Tonga)</li></ol><p>From Tongan <i>Nukuʻalofa</i>, from <i>nuku</i> (“abode”) + <i>alofa</i> (“love”). Literally, “abode of love”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nouméa (the capital city of New Caledonia, an overseas territory of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nunavut (a territory in northern Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nur-Sultan (former name of <i>Astana</i>: the capital city of Kazakhstan, used from 2019 to 2022)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nureyev, notably borne by Rudolf Nureyev</li></ol><p>From Tatar <i>Нуриев</i> (Nuriyew).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nuuk (the capital city of Greenland)</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from Danish <i>Nuuk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Нью-Йо́рк</i> (Nʹju-Jórk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Hampshire (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Delhi (the capital city of India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Jersey (a state in the northeastern United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New York, New York City (the largest city in the state of New York, and the largest city in the United States, a metropolis extending into neighboring New Jersey in addition to Pennsylvania and Connecticut)# New York (a state of the United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>New York</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Mexico (a state in the southwestern United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) Njord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nerima (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>練馬</i> (ねりま, Nerima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nyx</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Νύξ</i> (Núx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nuremberg (a major city in Bavaria, Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hauts-de-Seine (a department of the Île-de-France region, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Hauts-de-Seine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Объединённые Ара́бские Эмира́ты</i> (Obʺjedinjónnyje Arábskije Emiráty) (= United Arab Emirates): a country in West Asia, in the Middle East: UAE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) Health and Safety <i>(a subject taught in Russian schools that should provide knowledge of healthy life skills and personal safety)</i></li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>осно́вы безопа́сности жизнеде́ятельности</i> (osnóvy bezopásnosti žiznedéjatelʹnosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Организа́ция по безопа́сности и сотру́дничеству в Евро́пе</i>&nbsp;f (Organizácija po bezopásnosti i sotrúdničestvu v Jevrópe): OSCE, Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prostitution</i>) initialism of <i>оконча́ние в рот</i> (okončánije v rot, “finishing into the mouth”)</li><li>initialism of <i>окисли́тельно-восстанови́тельная реа́кция</i> (okislítelʹno-vosstanovítelʹnaja reákcija, “oxidation–reduction”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>операти́вный дежу́рный</i> (operatívnyj dežúrnyj, “OD, operational duty”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Организа́ция Догово́ра о коллекти́вной безопа́сности</i>&nbsp;f (Organizácija Dogovóra o kollektívnoj bezopásnosti): Collective Security Treaty Organisation, CSTO</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>OPCW (Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Организа́ция по запреще́нию хими́ческого ору́жия</i> (Organizácija po zapreščéniju ximíčeskovo orúžija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>OKB; initialism of <i>о́пытно-констру́кторское бюро́ or о́пытное констру́кторское бюро́</i> (ópytno-konstrúktorskoje bjuró or ópytnoje konstrúktorskoje bjuró)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>обсессивно-компульсивное расстройство</i> (obsessivno-kompulʹsivnoje rasstrojstvo, “obsessive-compulsive disorder”): OCD</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) acronym of <i>Олимпийский комитет России</i> (Olimpijskij komitet Rossii, “Russian Olympic Committee”): ROC</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>OMON, special police units</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Отря́д Моби́льный Осо́бого Назначе́ния</i> (Otrjád Mobílʹnyj Osóbovo Naznačénija, “mobile special-purpose detachment”); before 2011, <i>Отря́д Мили́ции Осо́бого Назначе́ния</i> (Otrjád Milícii Osóbovo Naznačénija, “special-purpose police detachment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Организа́ция Объединённых На́ций</i> (Organizácija Obʺjedinjónnyx Nácij): UNO, United Nations Organization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) initialism of <i>о́бщество с ограни́ченной отве́тственностью</i>&nbsp;n (óbščestvo s ograníčennoj otvétstvennostʹju): LLC, a limited liability company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military-industrial complex</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>оборо́нно-промы́шленный ко́мплекс</i> (oborónno-promýšlennyj kómpleks).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) acronym of <i>о́страя респирато́рная ви́русная инфе́кция</i> (óstraja respiratórnaja vírusnaja infékcija): URTI (viral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The parts of Donetsk and Luhansk oblasts in Ukraine that were <i>de facto</i> controlled by the Donetsk People's Republic and the Luhansk People's Republic between 2015 and 2022.</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>отде́льные райо́ны Доне́цкой и Луга́нской областе́й</i> (otdélʹnyje rajóny Donéckoj i Lugánskoj oblastéj, literally “separate districts of Donetsk and Luhansk regions”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Organization of Ukrainian Nationalists, OUN</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Организа́ция украи́нских национали́стов</i> (Organizácija ukraínskix nacionalístov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Special Economic Zone</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>осо́бая экономи́ческая зо́на</i> (osóbaja ekonomíčeskaja zóna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Obama</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Obidchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Obihiro (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>帯広</i> (おびひろ, Obihiro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regional department of people's education</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Областно́й отде́л наро́дного образова́ния</i> (Oblastnój otdél naródnovo obrazovánija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>Гора́ о́блачная</i> (Gorá óblačnaja), a mountain in Chuhuyivka district, Primorsky Krai, Russia.</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>Гора́ о́блачная</i> (Gorá óblačnaja, literally “cloudy mountain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Obolensky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Obukhiv (a city, the administrative centre of Obukhiv Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Arab Emirates (a country in Western Asia; capital: Abu Dhabi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Nations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ob (a major river in western Siberia, Russia)</li><li>Ob (Russian icebreaker)</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auvergne (a former region and historical province of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Overijssel (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ovsyannikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ohio (a state of the United States)</li><li>Ohio (a river in the United States, flowing 981 miles from the confluence of the Allegheny and Monongahela at Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania through Ohio, West Virginia, Kentucky, Indiana and Illinois along state borders and emptying into the Mississippi at Cairo, Illinois)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Hovhannisyan or Oganessian, from Armenian</li></ol><p>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Հովհաննիսյան</i> (Hovhannisyan)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ogaryov or Ogarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ogasawara (one of 4 subprefectures of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>小笠原</i> (おがさわら, Ogasawara).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augusta (the capital city of Maine, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Augusta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Firefox</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>Firefox</i>. From <i>огонь</i> (ogonʹ, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>лис</i> (lis, “fox”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odaenathus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odessa (a city, the administrative center of Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ὀδησσός</i> (Odēssós), the name of an ancient Greek colony which supposedly was located near the present location in Ukraine and which itself was named after a larger, better known city located at modern Varna, Bulgaria. The name was given to the city in 1795 at the suggestion of the Russian Academy of Sciences.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Odessa SR (a former republic existing from 17 January 1918 to 13 March 1918)</li></ol><p><i>Оде́сская</i> (Odésskaja, “Odessa”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Odysseus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ὀδυσσεύς</i> (Odusseús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ozhegov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oita (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Oita (the capital city of Ōita Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大分</i> (おおいた, Ōita).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oymyakon (a village in Sakha, Russia, known for being one of the coldest inhabited places on Earth)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Өймөкөөн</i> (Öymököön), in turn derived from Even <i>хэюм</i> (həjum, “place of non-freezing water; a place where fish spend the winter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oka (the largest right tributary of the Volga, in central Russia)</li><li>Oka (a left tributary of Angara river in Siberia, Russia)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ekʷeh₂</i> (“water”). Alternatively, of Baltic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okazaki (a city in Chiba Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>岡崎</i> (おかざき, Okazaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okayama (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Okayama (the capital city of Okayama Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>岡山</i> (おかやま, Okayama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oceania (a continent or continental region composed of Australia, New Zealand and the islands of Melanesia, Micronesia and Polynesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okinawa (an island and prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Okinawa (a city in Okinawa Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>沖縄</i> (おきなわ, Okinawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oklahoma (a state in the central United States, formerly a territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auckland (a city in New Zealand)</li><li>Oakland (a city in California, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Oksana or Oxana</li></ol><p>A form of the name <i>Ксе́ния</i> (Ksénija), from Ancient Greek <i>ξενία</i> (xenía, “hospitality”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Окса́на (Oksána)</li><li>a Ukrainian woman</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) a Ukrainian woman who is a refugee or immigrant, perceived to be vulgar or uncouth</li></ol><p>Ukrainian <i>Оксана</i> (Oksana) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), referring to Оксана being one of the most common names among Ukrainian women.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Occitania (a cultural region in southwestern Europe where Occitan has historically been spoken, consisting of southern France, parts of Catalonia in Spain, and some valleys in northwestern Italy)# Occitania (an administrative region in southern France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Occitānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oxford (a city in Oxfordshire, England)</li></ol><p>From English <i>Oxford</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) October Revolution, traditionally dated to October 25, 1917 (Julian calendar), which is November 7, 1917 according to the new Gregorian calendar.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Okudzhava, from Mingrelian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mingrelian <i>ოჸუჯავა</i> (oʾuǯava).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albany (the capital city of New York)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oleg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old East Slavic <i>Ольгъ</i> (Olĭgŭ), from Old Norse <i>Helgi</i>. Reintroduced in the 19th century after Oleg of Novgorod, displacing inherited <i>Олёг</i> (Oljóg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Olegovich</li></ol><p><i>Оле́г</i> (Olég) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Olegovna</li></ol><p><i>Оле́г</i> (Olég) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Oleynikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Olesya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olympic Games</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olympia (a small town in Elis regional unit, Western Greece region, Greece, site of the ancient Olympic Games)</li><li>Olympia (the capital city of Washington, United States)</li><li>Olímpia (a municipality of São Paulo state, Brazil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Hollande</i></li></ol><p>From French <i>Hollande</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olonets (a city, the administrative center of Olonetsky district, Karelia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aalborg (a city in Denmark)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Olga or Helga, Ukrainian Ольга (Olʹha), or Belarusian Вольга (Vólʹha)</li><li>Oscar (<i>O</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Ольга</i> (Olĭga) (compare <i>Оле́г</i> (Olég)). The Belarussian version for Olga is Volha (Вольга). The term Olga/Volha could be connected with the term for the Russian river Volga (Волга). Olga could also be connected to Old Norse <i>Helga</i>, from <i>heilagr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ulster (a province of Ireland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Ulster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olsztyn (the capital and largest city of Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship, in northeastern Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Olya, of the female given name О́льга (Ólʹga)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Umayyad</li></ol><p><i>Омейя</i> (Omejja) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Omsk (an oblast of Russia)</li><li>Omsk (a city, the administrative center of Omsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Омь</i> (Omʹ, “name of a river”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Om River</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ontario, Lake Ontario (a large lake in North America, one of the Great Lakes)</li><li>Ontario (a province in eastern Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Opole (the capital and largest city of Opole Voivodeship, in southern Poland in Silesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Opishnia (a rural settlement in Poltava Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Related to Ukrainian <i>Опі́шня</i> (Opíšnja) and Ukrainian <i>Опі́шне</i> (Opíšne).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German or English surname <i>Oppenheimer</i></li></ol><p>From German <i>Oppenheimer</i>, through Yiddish pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetry</i>) Pskov</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of the phrase во Пско́ве (vo Pskóve).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orange County (counties in California, Florida, Indiana, New York, North Carolina, Texas, Vermont and Virginia, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Nations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oregon (a state in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orenburg (a city, the administrative center of Orenburg Oblast, Russia, on the Ural River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Orestes</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ὀρέστης</i> (Oréstēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Oreshkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Orion (a giant-hunter)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Orion (a constellation)</li><li>Orion (Soviet and Russian manufacturer of high technology military and aerospace electronics)</li><li>Orion (a family of Russian unmanned aerial vehicles)</li><li>Orion (a spacecraft used in NASA's Artemis program)</li><li>Orion (an American turboprop anti-submarine and maritime surveillance aircraft)</li><li>Orion (a group of four British dreadnought battleships)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odisha (a state in eastern India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orleans (a city in France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Orlov</li></ol><p>From <i>орёл</i> (orjól, “eagle”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahura Mazda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Orontes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Оро́нт</i> (Orónt).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Orpheus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ὀρφεύς</i> (Orpheús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aarhus (a city in Denmark)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Osaka (the capital city of Osaka Prefecture, Japan)# Osaka (a prefecture on the island of Honshū, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大阪</i> (おおさか, Ōsaka, literally “large hill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auschwitz (a city in Poland)</li><li>Auschwitz (concentration camp)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Oświęcim</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ossetia (a region of the North Caucasus, consisting of the Republic of North Ossetia-Alania in Russia and the de-facto independent country of South Ossetia, internationally recognized as part of the country of Georgia)</li></ol><p>From Georgian <i>ოსეთი</i> (oseti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Osechkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ōshima (the biggest island in the Amami Islands)</li><li>Ōshima (one of 4 subprefectures of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Oshima (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大島</i> (おおしま, Ōshima).</p><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>渡島</i> (おしま, Oshima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oskol, Oskil (a river in Russia and Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oslo (the capital city of Norway)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Osman I</li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Osman</i></li><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Haussmann</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ottoman (member of the Ottoman dynasty)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish عثمان (Osman), from Arabic عُثْمَان (ʕuṯmān).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>Osman</i>, from Old Norse <i>Ásmundr</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>Haussmann</i>, from German <i>Haussmann</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ottoman Empire (a large former Turkish empire centered in modern Turkey that ruled over much of Southeast Europe, West Asia and North Africa from 1299 to 1922)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>East Indies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ostankino (a district of Moscow)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Austin (capital of Texas&#59; male given name&#59; surname&#59; make of car)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ostia (a city in Lazio, Italy and the Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin and Italian <i>Ostia</i>. Cognate with устье (ustʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prince Edward Island (a province and island in eastern Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ostrovsky</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>остров</i> (ostrov, “island”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare <i>Ostrowsky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ōta (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Ōta (a city in Gunma Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大田</i> (おおた, Ōta).</p><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>太田</i> (おおた, Ōta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Otaru (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>小樽</i> (おたる, Otaru).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Book of Revelation</li></ol><p>Literally, “Revelation of John the Theologian”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riot squad, riot police, special police squad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ottawa (a city in Ontario, Canada; the capital city of Canada)# Ottawa (a river flowing southeast between Ontario and Quebec, Canada into the St. Lawrence River)# Ottawa (a city, the county seat of LaSalle County, Illinois)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Ottawa</i>, from Ojibwe <i>odaawaa</i> (“an Odawa; a trader”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>Осма́н</i> (Osmán), the name borne by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Osman I (d. 1323/4), founder of the Ottoman Empire.</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>Ottomanus</i>, from Italian <i>Ottomano</i>, from Arabic عُثْمَان (ʕuṯmān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Оттома́н</i> (Ottomán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auteuil (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Auteuil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okhotny Ryad (Moscow metro stations on the Sokolnicheskaya line)</li><li>Okhotny Ryad (a street)</li><li>Okhotny Ryad (a shopping centre in Moscow)</li></ol><p>The name of the 17th century, given by one of the Moscow trading rows, in which it was allowed to trade game brought by hunters near Moscow.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okhotsk (a settlement in Khabarovsk krai)</li></ol><p>From river name <i>Охо́та</i> (Oxóta), from Even <i>окат</i> (okat, “river”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sea of Okhotsk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Okhotsk (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>オホーツク</i> (Ohōtsuku), from Russian <i>Охо́тск</i> (Oxótsk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Osh (a city in Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) air defense</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>противовозду́шная оборо́на</i> (protivovozdúšnaja oboróna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;humorous) Absurd, exaggerated Orthodox Christian beliefs and practices, resulting in irrational actions, immorality, violence or unfair politics.</li><li>(slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) any form of Orthodox Christianity.</li></ol><p>initialism of <i>правосла́вие головно́го мо́зга</i> (pravoslávije golovnóvo mózga, “Orthodoxy of the brain”): a wordplay between the sound of the РПЦ-abbreviation ("Russian Orthodox Church") and the medical terms like e.g. <i>опухоль головного мозга</i> (<i>a brain tumor</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics,&#32;colloquial) initialism of <i>Па́ртия жу́ликов и воро́в</i> (Pártija žúlikov i voróv): Party of crooks and thieves (sarcastic reference to the United Russia party of Vladimir Putin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>постоя́нное ме́сто жи́тельства</i> (postojánnoje mésto žítelʹstva): (place of) permanent residence (used in reference to people moving to another place&#47;country, as opposed to those going for a visit)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) residence permit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Приднестро́вская Молда́вская Респу́блика</i> (Pridnestróvskaja Moldávskaja Respúblika, “Pridnestrovian Moldavian Republic”) (PMR)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i> Палестинская национальная администрация</i> (Palestinskaja nacionalʹnaja administracija, “Palestinian National Authority”): PNA</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) abbreviation of <i>пошёл на́ хуй</i> (pošól ná xuj): GFY, "go fuck yourself"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>пистоле́т-пулемё́т Суда́ева</i> (pistolét-pulemjót Sudájeva); PPS, Sudayev's submachine-gun</li><li>initialism of <i>По́льская па́ртия социали́стов</i> (Pólʹskaja pártija socialístov); PPS, Polish Socialist Party</li><li>initialism of <i>парите́т покупа́тельной спосо́бности</i> (paritét pokupátelʹnoj sposóbnosti); PPP, purchasing power parity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>пистоле́т-пулемё́т Шпа́гина</i> (pistolét-pulemjót Špágina); PPSh-41, Shpagin's machine-pistol-41</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;cliché) The Beautiful Russia of the future</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>«прекра́сная Росси́я бу́дущего»</i> («prekrásnaja Rossíja búduščevo»). First used in the presidential campaign of Alexey Navalny in 2017.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Петрозаво́дск</i> (Petrozavódsk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;vulgar) abbreviation of <i>Пу́тин — хуйло́</i> (Pútin — xujló): "Putin is a dickhead".</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>профессиона́льное техни́ческое учи́лище</i> (professionálʹnoje texníčeskoje učílišče, “professional technical college”): institution for adult education at a basic or intermediate level, college</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strait of Dover</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Pas-de-Calais</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Paul</li><li>Papa (<i>P</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Павьлъ</i> (Pavĭlŭ), from Byzantine Greek, from Ancient Greek <i>Παῦλος</i> (Paûlos), from Latin <i>Paulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pavlenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Павле́нко</i> (Pavlénko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pavlov, a common Slavic surname</li><li>Pavlov (several rural localities in Russia)</li><li>Pavlov (two villages in Ukraine)</li><li>Pavlov (several villages and municipalities in the Czech Republic)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pavel's, Paul's</li></ol><p><i>Па́вел</i> (Pável) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Pavlovich</li><li>a surname, Pavlovich, equivalent to Serbo-Croatian Павловић &#47; Pavlović</li></ol><p><i>Па́вел</i> (Pável) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female patronymic, Pavlovna</li></ol><p><i>Па́вел</i> (Pável) +&lrm; <i>-овна</i> (-ovna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pavlohrad (a city, the administrative center of Pavlohrad Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Named after the emperor Paul I of Russia (Павел I), equivalent to <i>Па́вел</i> (Pável) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Padua (the capital of Padua province, Veneto region, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pakistan (a country in South Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palau (a country consisting of around 340 islands in Micronesia, in Oceania)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Palauan <i>Belau</i> (“Palau”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ecology,&#32;geography) The Palearctic (an an ecological region).</li></ol><p>From English <i>Palearctic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palermo (a port city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Palermo and the region of Sicily, Italy)# Palermo (a metropolitan city in Sicily, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Palermo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palestine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palikir (the capital city of the Federated States of Micronesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palmyra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palmyrena (a historical region of Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pamir (a mountain range in Central Asia)</li><li>Pamir (a river located in Afghanistan and Tajikistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pamfilov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pangaea (a hypothetical ancient supercontinent of the Mesozoic era)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pandora</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Πανδώρα</i> (Pandṓra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Panfilov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Пан</i> (Pan).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Papanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papeete (the capital city of the overseas collectivity of French Polynesia, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papua New Guinea (a country in Melanesia; capital: Port Moresby)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paraguay (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paramaribo (the capital city of Suriname)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paris (the capital and largest city of France)</li><li>Parizh (a village in Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>a hamlet in Bashkortostan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Paryż</i>, attested since 1696. Displaced earlier Middle Russian <i>Парисъ</i> (Paris).</p><p>(<i>village</i>): Named after the French city.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Paris (<i>Trojan prince</i>)</li><li>Paris (several cities, towns, unincorporated communities and census-designated places in the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parmenides</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Пармени́д</i> (Parmeníd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pars (a province and historical region in southwestern Iran, homeland of the Persians; known to the ancient Greeks as <i>Περσίς</i> (Persís) or <i>Περσική</i> (Persikḗ), to the Romans as <i>Persis</i> or <i>Persia</i>, and to the Arabs as فَارِس (fāris).)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Parthia (a region in northeastern Iran)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Παρθία</i> (Parthía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Parfyonov or Parfenov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Parkhomenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pasargadae</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pasir Ris</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Passy (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Passy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pastukhov, equivalent to English Sheppard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Patrushev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Paustovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Paufoshima</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>Paufošima</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Powell</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Powell</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Powers</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Powers</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pakhmutov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pachelma (an urban locality in Pachelmsky district, Penza Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pevchikh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beijing (a direct-administered municipality, the capital city of China)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Nanking court dialect Mandarin <i>北京</i> (Běijīng), from before the modern palatalization of /k/ to /tɕ/. Compare Portuguese <i>Pequim</i>, Spanish <i>Pekín</i>, Dutch <i>Peking</i>, English <i>Peking</i>, French <i>Pékin</i>, German <i>Peking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Pelageya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πελαγία</i> (Pelagía), from πέλᾰγος (pélăgos, “the sea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pelevin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pemzashen (a village in Shirak Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Պեմզաշեն</i> (Pemzašen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Пендаровски</i>&nbsp;m (Pendarovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Пендароски</i>&nbsp;m (Pendaroski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Punjab (a geographical region in South Asia, politically divided between India and Pakistan)# Punjab (a state in northern India)</li><li>Punjab (a province in Pakistan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پَنْجاب (pencâb), from Classical Persian پنجاب (panjāb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Penza (a city, the administrative center of Penza Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pennsylvania (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pentagon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ash Wednesday (a Christian day of penitence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Père Noël (French Father Christmas)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Père Noël</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) World War I</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pervouralsk (a city in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj, “first”) +&lrm; <i>Ура́л</i> (Urál, “Ural”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk, placename suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pereverzev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Places in Ukraine&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Perekop (a former fortress-city on the Crimea, founded in antiquity as Taphros, believed to have first been fortified by the Crimean Scythians, rendered uninhabitable in November 1920 by the Perekop–Chonhar Operation of the Russian Civil War; the fortress ruins are known by their Crimean Tatar name, <i>Or Qapı</i>)</li><li>Perekop (a village in Suvorove silrada, Perekop Raion, the Crimea, Ukraine, founded in the wake of the fortress-city’s destruction and named for it)</li><li>Perekop (a village in Henichesk urban hromada, Henichesk Raion, Kherson Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1926)</li><li>Perekip (a village in Valky urban hromada, Bohodukhiv Raion, Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1643)</li></ol><li>Places in Russia&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Perekop (an uninhabited hamlet in Verkhnyaya Uftyuga rural settlement, Krasnoborsk Raion, Arkhangelsk Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia, first mentioned in 1670)</li><li>Perekop (a posyolok in Churovichi rural settlement, Klimovo Raion, Bryansk Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Perekop (a posyolok in Byakovo rural settlement, Navlya Raion, Bryansk Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Perekop (a hamlet in Ozernitsa rural settlement, Slobodskoy Raion, Kirov Oblast, Volga Federal District, Russia, first mentioned in 1891)</li><li>Perekop (a hamlet in Turbino rural settlement, Okulovka Raion, Novgorod Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia)</li></ol><li>Places in Belarus&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Pierakop (an uninhabited hamlet in Kurhannie sielsaviet, Rahačoŭ Raion, Homiel Oblast, Belarus, founded in the early-1920s, last inhabited 2019–2021)</li><li>Pierakop (a pasyolak in Taŭkačy sielsaviet, Škłoŭ Raion, Mahiloŭ Oblast, Belarus, founded in 1920; it had two inhabitants in 2007)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Переко́п</i> (Perekóp).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pereslavl-Zalessky (a town in Yaroslavl Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pericles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perm (a historical region of northeastern Russia)</li><li>Perm (a krai of Russia)</li><li>Perm (a city, the administrative center of Perm Krai, Russia, near the Ural Mountains)</li></ol><p>Of Uralic origin, from Komi-Permyak <i>Перем</i> (Perem) or Komi-Zyrian <i>Перым</i> (Perym), meaning something like "far-away land." Compare Finnish <i>perämaa</i> (“backwoods”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Perseus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Περσεύς</i> (Perseús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Persephone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Περσεφόνη</i> (Persephónē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian Gulf (a gulf between Iran and Arabian Peninsula)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persia (former name of Iran, a country in West Asia)</li></ol><p>From <i>перс</i> (pers, “Persian”) under influence of Latin <i>Persia</i>. Displaced Old East Slavic <i>Персида</i> (Persida, “Persia”) (> <i>перси́дский</i> (persídskij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Пе́рсия</i> (Pérsija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perth (a city in Australia)</li><li>Perth (a city in Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peru (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>Санкт-Петербу́рг</i> (Sankt-Peterbúrg): Saint Petersburg (a federal city of Russia, known between 1914 and 1924 as Petrograd and between 1924 and 1991 as Leningrad; the former capital of Russia, from 1713–1728 and 1732–1918)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petrarch (Italian poet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Petrarca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Петревски</i>&nbsp;m (Petrevski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Петрески</i>&nbsp;m (Petreski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Peter's School (secondary school in St. Petersburg)</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from German <i>Petrischule</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Peter’s day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Petrovich, meaning “son of Пётр (Pjotr) [=Peter]”</li></ol><p><i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petrovsky Island (an island in Saint Petersburg, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Petrograd (short-lived name of Saint Petersburg, a major city in Russia, from 1914 until 1924, when it became Leningrad)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Петрогра́д</i> (Petrográd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petrozavodsk (the capital city of the republic of Karelia, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr, “Peter”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заво́д</i> (zavód, “factory”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), named after Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Peter and Paul Fortress</li></ol><p><i><b>Петропа́влов</b>ская кре́пость</i> (<b>Petropávlov</b>skaja krépostʹ, “Peter and Paul Fortress”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petropavlovsk, the name of several inhabited localities in Russia.</li></ol><p><i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr, “Peter”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Па́вел</i> (Pável, “Paul”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky (a city, the administrative center of Kamchatka Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Петроски</i>&nbsp;m (Petroski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Պետրոսյան</i> (Petrosyan)</li><li>a surname, Petrosyan, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Petrushevsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Petya, of the male given name Пётр (Pjotr), equivalent to English Pete</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peshawar (the capital city of North-West Frontier Province, Pakistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pivovarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;sometimes&#32;humorous) St. Petersburg.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>пидор</i> (pidor, “insulting term for a male homosexual, a bastard, a disliked person”) and <i>Петербу́рг</i> (Peterbúrg, “[St.] Petersburg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pisa (a city in Tuscany, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Picardy (a former administrative region and historical province of France; since 2016, part of the administrative region of Hauts-de-France region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humuhumunukunukuapuaa (either of two species of triggerfish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Ukrainian surname <i>Пі́куль</i> (Píkulʹ)</li><li>a Russian surname, Pikul, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Polish surname <i>Piłsudski</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) US, USA, America</li></ol><p>See пиндо́с (pindós)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) US, USA, America</li></ol><p><i>пиндо́с</i> (pindós, “an American, a Yankee”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Piontkovsky, from Polish, equivalent to Polish Piątkowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piraeus (a municipality and port in Greece, part of Athens agglomeration, the chief port of Athens, located on the Saronic Gulf)</li><li>Piraeus (a regional unit of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πειραιεύς</i> (Peiraieús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrenees (European mountain range)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pȳrēnaeī montēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iberian Peninsula</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pirka (a municipality of Austria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pirogov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pirozhkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) Saint Petersburg (a federal city of Russia, known between 1914 and 1924 as Petrograd and between 1924 and 1991 as Leningrad; the former capital of Russia, from 1713–1728 and 1732–1918)</li><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;informal) Leningrad; (<i>1914-1924</i>) Petrograd</li><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Peter</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Питербу́рг</i> (Piterbúrg), alternate spelling of Петербу́рг (<i>Peterbúrg</i>). Unclipped form from Dutch <i>Pieter</i>, the petname used in reference to the Russian tsar Peter the Great, the founder of Saint Petersburg. Attested since at least 1772.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>Peter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pittsburgh (a port city in Pennsylvania)</li><li>Pittsburg (cities in California, Kansas, Texas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pittacus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pythagoras</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πυθαγόρας</i> (Puthagóras).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plato</li><li>a male given name, Platon, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πλάτων</i> (Plátōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Platonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Плато́н</i> (Platón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pleven (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plesetsk (a settlement, the administrative center of Plesetsky district, Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pletnyov or Pletnev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Plekhanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Плифо́н</i> (Plifón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plymouth (a city in Devon, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Plisetsky, Plisetski, or Plisiecki</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plethon (the Hellenic-styled alias of Georgios Gemistos, a 14th-century Greek scholar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plovdiv (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ploiești (a city in Romania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plotinus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Плоти́н</i> (Plotín).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Plotnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plutarch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Plyushchev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pliushchivka (a village in Bashtanka urban hromada, Bashtanka Raion, Mykolaiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1803)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phnom Penh (the capital city of Cambodia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pobedonostsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volga region</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-, “along”) +&lrm; <i>Во́лга</i> (Vólga) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Podgorica (the capital city of Montenegro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Podgorny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>informal name given to the Moscow suburbs or Moscow oblast as a whole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) China, Celestial Empire (a country in East Asia)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>небеса́</i> (nebesá) +&lrm; <i>-ная</i> (-naja). Literally, “(the country) under Heavens”; calque of Chinese <i>天下</i> (Tiānxià, “Under Heaven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Podilsk (a city, the administrative center of Podilsk Raion, Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Podolyak, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pozharsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pozarikha (a village in Kamensky district, Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poznan (the capital and largest city of Greater Poland Voivodeship, west-central Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Poznań</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Poklonsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pokrovsk (a city in Pokrovsk Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pokromkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from French, equivalent to English Paulette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Paulette</i>, a diminutive feminine form of <i>Paul</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Polyidus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πολύιδος</i> (Polúidos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Polina, equivalent to English Pauline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Pauline</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polynesia (a continental region of Oceania, including Easter Island, Hawaii, New Zealand, and most of the islands between them)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Politkovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polyphemus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Polyphēmus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polohy (a city, the administrative centre of Polohy Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Polozkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poltava (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poltava Oblast (an oblast of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Polish People's Republic (former official name of <i>Poland</i>: a country in Central Europe; used from 1952 to 1989 when it was a one-party, Marxist-Leninist state; officially known as the Republic of Poland from 1947 to 1952, but under the same form of government)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poland (a country in Central Europe; official name: Респу́блика По́льша (Respúblika Pólʹša))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>Польша</i> (Polʹša) (att. 1560s), likely through Old Ruthenian (cf. Ukrainian <i>По́льща</i> (Pólʹšča) and Belarusian <i>По́льшча</i> (Pólʹšča) but Carpathian Rusyn <i>По́льско</i> (Pólʹsko)) from Middle Polish <i>w Polszcze</i> (“in Poland”), from <i>Polska</i>, from Old Polish <i>Polska</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poľьska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Polyakov or Poliakoff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Polyansky or Polansky</li><li>a surname, Polanski, from Polish</li><li>a surname, Polanský, from Czech</li><li>a surname, Polanský, from Slovak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Polaris, North Star</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pomerania (region of Europe on the southern shores of the Baltic Sea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pomerania</i>, further from a Slavic word akin to <i>помо́рье</i> (pomórʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pompeii (a Roman town in Italy, buried under volcanic ash and pumice in the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pompeiī</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ponomarenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Пономаре́нко</i> (Ponomarénko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ponomaryov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Poplavsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Popov, particularly Alexander Popov, Russian physicist who first demonstrated the practical application of electromagnetic (radio) waves</li></ol><p>From <i>поп</i> (pop, “priest”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Poroshenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Port Louis (the capital city of Mauritius)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Port Moresby (the capital city of Papua New Guinea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Port Said (a large city in Egypt)</li><li>Port Said (a governorate of Egypt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Port-au-Prince (the capital city of Haiti)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Port of Spain (the capital city of Trinidad and Tobago)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portland (a city in Oregon, United States)</li><li>Portland (a city in Maine, United States)</li><li>Portland (an island in the English Channel, Dorset county, England)</li><li>Portland (several cities, towns, unincorporated communities and census-designated places in the United States and elsewhere)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Portnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porto-Novo (the official capital of Benin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portugal (a country in Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portuguese Republic (official name of <i>Portugal</i>: a country in Southern Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of Portuguese <i>República Portuguesa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Португа́лия</i> (Portugálija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Porfiry or Porfiri; Porphyry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Poseidon</li><li>Poseidon (Russian nuclear-powered unmanned underwater vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Potanin, feminine equivalent Пота́нина (Potánina)</li></ol><p><i>Пота́ня</i> (Potánja) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Potemkin or Potyomkin</li></ol><p>From <i>Потёмка</i> (Potjómka, a nickname), from <i>потёмки</i> (potjómki, “darkness”), from <i>по-</i> (po-, a comparative or delimitative prefix) + <i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj, “dark”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьmьnъ</i>, + <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix) + <i>-ин</i> (-in, possessive suffix). This surname came from the nicknames <i>Потёма</i> (Potjóma)/<i>Потёмка</i> (Potjómka) that were given to a child born at twilight or at night. They may also mean "a secretive man".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prague (the capital and largest city of the Czech Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Praskovya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>παρασκευή</i> (paraskeuḗ, “the day of Preparation, before the Passover”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Praia (the capital city of Cape Verde)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pretoria (the administrative capital of South Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baltic, the Baltics, the Baltic states (a geographic region of Northern Europe, consisting of the three countries to the east of the Baltic Sea, i.e. Latvia, Lithuanian and Estonia; in full, the Baltics or the Baltic states)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-, “by, near, next to”) +&lrm; <i>Балт</i> (Balt, “Balt”) +&lrm; <i>-ика</i> (-ika), literally “near the Baltic [Sea]”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Prigozhin, transferred from the nickname or place name</li></ol><p>From <i>приго́жий</i> (prigóžij, “good-looking”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p><p>In case of Jewish persons, a toponymic surname from <i>Приго́жее</i> (Prigóžeje, “a village in what is now the Vitebsk Region of Belarus”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transnistria (a geographic region and de-facto independent but unrecognized country in Eastern Europe that claims statehood as the Pridnestrovian Moldavian Republic; internationally considered an autonomous territorial unit within Moldova)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>Днестр</i> (Dnestr, “Dniester”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Prilepin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pryluky (a city in Pryluky Raion, Chernihiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Primakov</li></ol><p><i>прима́к</i> (primák) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alpes-Maritimes (a department of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Primorsky Krai (a krai of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prince Albert (piercing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pripyat (a river in Eastern Europe, flowing east through Ukraine and Belarus, draining into the Dnieper)# Pripyat (an abandoned city and ghost town in northern Ukraine, near the border with Belarus)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Припѧть</i> (Pripętĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the) Prysyvashshia (a geographic region of southern Ukraine, surrounding the Syvash; it occupies those parts of eastern left-bank Kherson Oblast outside the Dnieper basin and the northeastern coastal strip of the Crimea very approximately east of Highway M17 from the Isthmus of Perekop as far as Vladyslavivka in Theodosia Raion)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-, “next to”) +&lrm; <i>Сива́ш</i> (Siváš, “the Syvash”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje, “region”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Prikhodko or Prykhodko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Черномо́рье</i> (Černomórʹje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Prishvin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Priština (the capital city of Kosovo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur (an administrative region of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prodicus</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Πρόδικος</i> (Pródikos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Proklov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Prokopchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Prokofyev or Prokofiev, particularly Sergei Prokofiev, Russian composer, April 27, 1891 — March 5, 1953</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Procrustes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Προκρούστης</i> (Prokroústēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Prometheus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Προμηθεύς</i> (Promētheús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Völuspá, the Old Norse prophecy</li></ol><p>Literally, “Prophecy of the Vølva”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Protagoras</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Protasevich or Pratasevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Пратасе́віч (Pratasjévič)</li><li>a surname from Polish, equivalent to Polish Protasewicz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Prokhorov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) Prussia (a geographical area on the Baltic coast of Northeast Europe)# (<i>historical</i>) Prussia (a former duchy, kingdom and (after German unification in 1871) a province of Germany, existing from 1525 to 1947 in parts of modern Germany, Poland and Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) Psyche</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ψυχή</i> (Psukhḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pskov (a city, the administrative center of Pskov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Пльсковъ</i> (Plĭskovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plьsky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a village in Leningrad Oblast, Russia</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Like most of toponyms in the Luga river basin, probably of Slavic or Finno-Ugric origin. The area had also influence of Varangians, but Germanic etymology is doubtful.</p><p>By surface analysis, from <i>пёс</i> (pjos, “dog”) and <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”); literally, “dog eater place”. This explanation is common in folk etymology used in local legends; the official website of the village is using a dachshund as its logo; one of the older spellings of the village's name is "<i>Псоѣди</i>" (probably referring to the folk etymology).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censored spelling of <i>Путин</i> (Putin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a surname, Pugachev or Pugachov</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Pugachyov (a city of oblast significance, the administrative centre of Pugachyov urban settlement and Pugachyov Raion, in Saratov Oblast, Volga Federal District, Russia, founded as a sloboda in 1764)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Пугачёв</i> (Pugačóv).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pudahuel (a city and commune of Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Pulkheriya or Pulkheria, equivalent to English Pulcheria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πουλχερία</i> (Poulkhería), from Latin <i>pulcher</i> (“beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Busan (a major port city in the Yeongnam region, South Korea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an abandoned city located 12.5 miles from Naryan-Mar in Russia. The oldest city of Nenetsia. The place of immolation of the protopope Avvakum.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Putin</li></ol><p>From <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “way, path, road”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in, possessive suffix), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫtь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pont-</i>, <i>&#42;pint-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pónteh₁s</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) Putler (derogatory name of Vladimir Putin)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin) +&lrm; <i>Ги́тлер</i> (Gítler, “Hitler”); in use since the early 2000s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Putorana (a plateau in Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Evenki.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Pushilin, feminine equivalent Пуши́лина (Pušílina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pushkin; particularly, a Russian poet, playwright, and novelist of the Romantic era Alexander Pushkin</li><li>Pushkin (a municipal town in Pushkinsky district)</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́шка</i> (púška, “cannon”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in, “'s”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Puebla (a city in Mexico)</li><li>Puebla (a municipality of Mexico)</li><li>Puebla (a state of Mexico)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Puebla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Puerto Rico (a commonwealth, island and dependent territory of the United States in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyongyang (the capital city of North Korea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phuket (an island and province of Thailand)</li><li>Phuket (the capital of Phuket Province, Thailand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>Пхенья́н</i> (Pxenʹján, “Pyongyang”)</li></ol><p>From the Kontsevich Cyrillization system of Korean <i>Пхёнъян</i> (Pxjonʺjan) of Korean <i>평양(平壤)</i> (Pyeong'yang, “flat land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Pcholkin or Pchelkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) Putin</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory,&#32;obsolete) libtard, NPC</li></ol><p>From the name of a character of a web animation series first published in 2011. Originally used by pro-Putin internet activists to mock opposition supporters and then reclaimed by anti-Putin internet activists due to phonetic similarity to <i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piedmont</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pyanykh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>彭</i></li><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Pesne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Puy-de-Dôme (a department of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyatigorsk (a city in Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), referring to the nearby Mount Beshtau in the northern Caucasus.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Pentateuch (the first five books of the Bible)</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>книга</i> (kniga) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pyotr, equivalent to English Peter</li><li>Peter (the Apostle)</li><li>a transliteration of the Ukrainian male given name <i>Петро́</i> (Petró)</li><li>a transliteration of the Belarusian male given name <i>Пятро</i> (Pjatró)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>Πέτρος</i> (Pétros) (cf. also Old Church Slavonic <i>Петръ</i> (Petrŭ)), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Πέτρος</i> (Pétros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>расстро́йство аутисти́ческого спе́ктра</i> (rasstrójstvo autistíčeskovo spéktra, “autism spectrum disorder”): ASD</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Респу́блика Башкортоста́н</i> (Respúblika Baškortostán): Republic of Bashkortostan</li><li>initialism of <i>Респу́блика Белару́сь</i> (Respúblika Belarúsʹ): Republic of Belarus</li><li>initialism of <i>Респу́блика Болга́рия</i> (Respúblika Bolgárija): Republic of Bulgaria</li><li>initialism of <i>Респу́блика Буря́тия</i> (Respúblika Burjátija): Republic of Buryatia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Railways <i>or</i> <b>Rossiyskie Zheleznye Dorogi</b> (RZhD) (<i>state-owned railway company in Russia</i>)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Росси́йские желе́зные доро́ги</i> (Rossíjskije želéznyje dorógi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>ру́сский же́стовый язы́к</i> (rússkij žéstovyj jazýk, “Russian Sign Language”): RSL.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Росси́йское информацио́нное аге́нтство</i> (Rossíjskoje informaciónnoje agéntstvo): Russian Information Agency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>RIC (Russia, India, China)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Росси́я, И́ндия, Кита́й</i> (Rossíja, Índija, Kitáj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Kazakhstan (RK, an informal abbreviation for the name of the modern (since 1991) country, yet widely used in official documents).</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Респу́блика Казахста́н</i> (Respúblika Kazaxstán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Workers’ and Peasants’ Inspectorate</li><li>RCC, Radio Communications Company</li><li>Rocket and Space Complex</li><li>Raketno-kosmičeskaja korporacija (Rocket and Space Corporation)</li><li>RRC, Russian Red Cross</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>рабо̀че-крестья́нский контро́ль</i> (rabòče-krestʹjánskij kontrólʹ).</p><p>Initialism of <i>Радиокоммуникацио́нная компа́ния</i> (Radiokommunikaciónnaja kompánija).</p><p>Initialism of <i>раке́тно-косми́ческий ко́мплекс</i> (rakétno-kosmíčeskij kómpleks).</p><p>Initialism of <i>Раке́тно-косми́ческая корпора́ция</i> (Rakétno-kosmíčeskaja korporácija).</p><p>Initialism of <i>Росси́йский Кра́сный Кре́ст</i> (Rossíjskij Krásnyj Krést).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Red Army</i> (“USSR”); abbreviation of <i>Рабоче-крестьянская Красная армия</i> (Raboče-krestʹjanskaja Krasnaja armija, “Workers' and Peasants' Red Army”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>RNA</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>рибонуклеи́новая кислота́</i> (ribonukleínovaja kislotá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work permit</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>разреше́ние на рабо́ту</i> (razrešénije na rabótu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Defence Sports-Technical Organization</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Росси́йская оборо́нная спорти́вно-техни́ческая организа́ция</i> (Rossíjskaja oborónnaja sportívno-texníčeskaja organizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portable rocket launcher; rocket-propelled grenade</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>ручно́й противота́нковый гранатомёт</i> (ručnój protivotánkovyj granatomjót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ROC, the Russian Orthodox Church</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Ру́сская правосла́вная це́рковь</i> (Rússkaja pravoslávnaja cérkovʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>расши́ренная реа́льность</i> (rasšírennaja reálʹnostʹ); extended reality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Баллисти́ческая раке́та сре́дней и меньшей да́льности</i> (Ballistíčeskaja rakéta srédnej i menʹšej dálʹnosti): intermediate-range ballistic missile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) RSFSR (Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic, 1917 to 1991)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Росси́йская Сове́тская Федерати́вная Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Rossíjskaja Sovétskaja Federatívnaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Federation (RF, an informal abbreviation for the name of the modern (since 1991) country, yet widely used in official documents).</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Росси́йская Федера́ция</i> (Rossíjskaja Federácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>радиоэлектро́нная борьба́</i> (radioelektrónnaja borʹbá, “electronic warfare”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rabat (the capital city of Morocco, and capital city of the region of Rabat-Sale-Kenitra, Morocco)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Раба́т</i> (Rabát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Russia.</li></ol><p><i>ра́бский</i> (rábskij, “slave-like”) +&lrm; <i>вымира́ть</i> (vymirátʹ, “to die out, to become extinct”) +&lrm; <i>эмира́т</i> (emirát, “emirate”); literally "dying slave emirates", influenced by the similar-sounding Объединённые Ара́бские Эмира́ты (Obʺjedinjónnyje Arábskije Emiráty, “United Arab Emirates”).</p><p>"Рабский" refers to the practice of autocracy in Russian politics and supposed lack of visible dissent according to the people using this word; "вымирать" refers to the claimed poorer quality of life in Russia; and the second word is mixed with "Эмираты" to refer to Russia's economy being primarily based on oil.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ragusa (a city in Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Radishchev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Radnain, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠷᠠᠳᠨ᠎ᠠ ᠶᠢᠨ</i> (radn-a-yin), genitive of <i>ᠷᠠᠳᠨ᠎ᠠ</i> (radn-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Radom (a city in central Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Razvozzhayev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hrazdan (a town, the capital of Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li><li>Hrazdan (a major river in Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Հրազդան</i> (Hrazdan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rozdilna (a city, the administrative center of Rozdilna Raion, Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Raznochinovka (a village in Narimanovsky district, Astrakhan Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Razumihin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Raisa, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Probably from Ancient Greek <i>ῥήϊστος</i> (rhḗïstos), superlative of <i>ῥᾴδιος</i> (rhāídios, “easy-going”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Raikin or Raykin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Wright</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Muslim female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Ralph</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rangoon (former name of Yangon, the capital of Yangon Region and the largest city in Burma)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Burmese <i>ရန်ကုန်</i> (rankun /⁠jàɴɡòʊɴ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;derogatory&#32;or&#32;poetic) Russia (country)</li></ol><p>From <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja), <i>Россѣ́я</i> (Rossě́ja). The presence of «ѣ» instead of «и» is a consequence of rethinking under the influence of the word <i>рассеять</i> (rassejatʹ), Old East Slavic <i>россѣяти</i> (rossějati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fictional Russian surname, Raskolnikov</li></ol><p><i>раско́льник</i> (raskólʹnik) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rasputin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Rasya, of the male given name Гера́сим (Gerásim)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Ratnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Rachel</li><li>a female given name, Rakhil, equivalent to English Rachel</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ῥαχήλ</i> (Rhakhḗl), from Biblical Hebrew <i>רָחֵל</i> (rāḥēl, “ewe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rakhmaninov or Rachmaninoff, particularly Sergei Rachmaninoff, Russian composer, April 1, 1873 — March 28, 1943</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rakhiv (a city, the administrative center of Rakhiv Raion, Zakarpattia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;derogatory,&#32;ironic) Russia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Russia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Raška, Rascia, a historical region located in modern Serbia</li><li>Raška (a city in Serbia)</li><li>(slang,&#32;chiefly&#32;derogatory) Russia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>Рашка</i>.</p><p><i>Ра́ша</i> (Ráša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rashkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Latin, equivalent to English Regina</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rēgīna</i> (“queen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rezh (a town in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Reznik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Reznikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Reykjavík (the capital city of Iceland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rhein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Repin, particularly Ilya Repin a leading Russian painter and sculptor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Altai Republic (a republic and federal subject of Russia, in southern Siberia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Bulgaria (official name of <i>Bulgaria</i>: a country in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Guinea-Bissau (official name of <i>Guinea-Bissau</i>: a country in West Africa)</li></ol><p>Calque of Portuguese <i>República da Guiné-Bissau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Kazakhstan (official name of <i>Kazakhstan</i>: a country in Central Asia and Eastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of the Congo (a country in Central Africa, the smaller of the two countries named <i>Congo</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Korea (official name of <i>South Korea</i>: a country in East Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Tajikistan (official name of <i>Tajikistan</i>: a country in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Uzbekistan (official name of <i>Uzbekistan</i>: a country in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of South Sudan (official name of <i>South Sudan</i>: a country in East Africa)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>Republic of South Sudan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Rzeczpospolita, Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</li></ol><p>Phono-semantic matching of Polish <i>Rzeczpospolita</i>, itself a calque of Latin <i>rēs pūblica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Riau (a province in Sumatra, Indonesia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Indonesian <i>Riau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rome (a major city, the capital of Italy and the Italian region of Lazio, located on the Tiber River; the ancient capital of the Roman Empire)# Rome (a metropolitan city of Lazio, Italy)# (<i>historical</i>) Rome, Ancient Rome (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Римъ</i> (Rimŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>Римъ</i> (Rimŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Rimъ</i> (“Rome”), from Late Latin <i>Rōma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Bulgarian</li></ol><p>Probably from Bulgarian <i>Ри́ма</i> (Ríma), feminine form of Ри́мен (Rímen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Рим</i> (Rim).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Buryat surname, Rinchino, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠷᠢᠨᠴᠢᠨ ᠦ</i> (rinčin-ü, patronymic and grammatically possessive of <i>ᠷᠢᠨᠴᠢᠨ</i> (rinčin, given name)), from Classical Tibetan <i>རིན་ཆེན</i> (rin chen, given name). By surface analysis, <i>Ринчи́н</i> (Rinčín) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó).</p><p>Compare also <i>Ринчи́нов</i>&nbsp;m (Rinčínov) and <i>Ринчи́нова</i>&nbsp;f (Rinčínova), variants of the surname which adopted Russian possessive suffixes.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male Buryat surname, Rinchinov, from Tibetan &lt; patronymics</li></ol><p>From <i>Ринчи́н</i> (Rinčín, Mongolian name of Tibetan origin) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov, possessive suffix for patronymic surnames). From Classical Mongolian <i>ᠷᠢᠨᠴᠢᠨ</i> (rinčin), from Classical Tibetan <i>རིན་ཆེན</i> (rin chen, “treasure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rio de Janeiro (a municipality, the state capital of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil)</li><li>Rio de Janeiro (a state of the Southeast Region, Brazil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Маргари́та (Margaríta), equivalent to English Rita</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Richard, from English</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Richard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Richmond (the capital city of Virginia, United States)</li><li>Richmond (several cities, towns, unincorporated communities and census-designated places in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom and other English-speaking countries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Richmond</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;derogatory,&#32;humorous,&#32;politics,&#32;Internet slang) Russia, especially the personification of an entity which is formed of militarist "Russian world" supporters; Ruzzia.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja, “Russia”) +&lrm; <i>Z</i> (“Z, nationalist symbol”). Compare Ukrainian <i>РоZZі́я</i> (RoZZíja) and English <i>Ruzzia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Robert, from English</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Robin</li><li>a female given name from English, equivalent to English Robin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Robin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rovenky (a city, the administrative center of Rovenky Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rogov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rogozin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhode Island (the smallest state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Rhodesia (former name of Zimbabwe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rodinia (a hypothetical ancient supercontinent of the Mesoproterozoic era, which gave way to Pannotia)</li></ol><p>From <i>ро́дина</i> (ródina, “motherland”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rodion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rodionov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rodnin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas</li><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) the Nativity of Jesus</li></ol><p>Literally, “the Birth of Christ”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roseau (the capital city of Dominica)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;humorous,&#32;Internet slang) Russia.</li></ol><p>A humorous misspelling of <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja), possibly playing with the word <i>рои́ться</i> (roítʹsja, “to swarm, to crowd”).</p><p>This misspelling first appeared from a 2008 photo of a belt buckle. It became popular as a mockery of Dmitry Medvedev's article "Росси́я, вперёд (Rossíja, vperjód)" and the patrotic slogan "вперёд, Росси́я (vperjód, Rossíja)" (both meaning "Forward, Russia!").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;politics,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) "Forward, Russia!"; "Russia is going down the drain"</li></ol><p>A humorous misspelling of <i>Росси́я, вперёд</i> (Rossíja, vperjód). The first part, <i>Рои́сся</i> (Roíssja), is a pun on <i>рои́ться</i> (roítʹsja, “to swarm”). The other part, <i>вперде́</i> (vperdé), can be read as <i>в перде́</i> (v perdé), derived in turn from <i>перде́ть</i> (perdétʹ, “to fart”). Therefore, the phrase can be read as "Rssuia/Ruissa is in ass" or "Rssuia/Ruissa is in deep shit".</p><p>This misspelling first appeared from a 2008 photo of a belt buckle. It became popular as a mockery of Dmitry Medvedev's article "Росси́я, вперёд (Rossíja, vperjód)" and the patrotic slogan "вперёд, Росси́я (vperjód, Rossíja)" (both meaning "Forward, Russia!").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Roizman, from Yiddish</li><li>a surname, Reusmann, from German</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rockall (an uninhabited islet in the north Atlantic Ocean, claimed by the United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Rockall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rokossovsky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Rokossowski</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rockport (a community in Leeds and the Thousand Islands township, Ontario, Canada).</li><li>Rockport (a number of places in the United States):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a small city in Hot Spring County, Arkansas.</li><li>a former settlement in Mendocino County, California.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Pike County, Illinois.</li><li>a city, the county seat of Spencer County, Indiana.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Parke County, Indiana.</li><li>a small city in Ohio County, Kentucky.</li><li>a town in Knox County, Maine.</li><li>a town in Essex County, Massachusetts.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Copiah County, Mississippi.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Allen County, Ohio.</li><li>a city, the county seat of Aransas County, Texas.</li><li>a census-designated place in Skagit County, Washington.</li></ol><li>(<i>fiction</i>) Rockport (a fictional city serving as the primary setting for Need for Speed: Most Wanted (2005))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Rockport</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Roksana or Roxana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Rockefeller</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Rockefeller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a number of places in the United States:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a small town, the county seat of Coosa County, Alabama.</li><li>a city, the county seat of Winnebago County, Illinois.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Jackson County, Indiana.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Wells County, Indiana.</li><li>a small city in Floyd County, Iowa.</li><li>a city in Kent County, Michigan.</li><li>a city in Wright County and Hennepin County, Minnesota.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Chariton County, Missouri.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Surry County, North Carolina.</li><li>a village in Mercer County, Ohio.</li><li>an unincorporated community in Tuscarawas County, Ohio.</li><li>a small city in Blount County, Tennessee.</li><li>a small town in Spokane County, Washington.</li><li>A number of townships in the United States, listed under Rockford Township.</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Rockford</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Romanov; the Romanovs were the second and last imperial dynasty of Russia, which ruled the country from 1613 to 1917</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Roman's</li></ol><p><i>Рома́н</i> (Román) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literature</i>) Romeo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Romeo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romny (a city, the administrative center of Romny Raion, Sumy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romulus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ро́мул</i> (Rómul).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Ronya, of the female given name Верони́ка (Veroníka)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a diminutive, Ronya, of the male given name Верони́к (Veroník)</li><li>a diminutive, Ronya, of the male given names Софро́ний (Sofrónij), Софро́н (Sofrón), or Сафро́ний (Safrónij)</li><li>a diminutive, Ronya, of the female given names Софро́ния (Sofrónija) or Сафро́ния (Safrónija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>National Guard of Russia (the internal military force of the Russian government)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>росси́йская гва́рдия</i> (rossíjskaja gvárdija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roskomnadzor: The Federal Service for Supervision of Communications, Information Technology and Mass Media: a Russian federal executive body responsible for censorship in media and telecommunications.</li></ol><p><i>Рос</i> (Ros) (<i>Росси́я</i>) +&lrm; <i>ком</i> (kom) (<i>коммуника́ции</i>) +&lrm; <i>надзо́р</i> (nadzór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) Roskomnadzor</li></ol><p><i>Роскомнадзо́р</i> (Roskomnadzór) +&lrm; <i>позо́р</i> (pozór, “shame”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roscosmos: a Russian state corporation responsible for space flights, cosmonautics programs, and aerospace research.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Roslyakov</li></ol><p>From <i>росля́к</i> (roslják, “tall, lanky person; a strapping youth”), from <i>рослы́й</i> (roslýj, “tall, well-grown, fully developed”) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov), literally “son of the tall one; belonging to Roslyak”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Russian SFSR, RSFSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, the largest republic within the USSR, existing from 1917 to 1991)</li></ol><p><i>Росси́йская</i> (Rossíjskaja, “Russian”) + initialism of <i>Сове́тская Федерати́вная Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Federatívnaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Soviet Federative Socialist Republic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic, RSFSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, the largest republic within the USSR, existing from 1917 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Federation (official name of <i>Russia</i>: a transcontinental country in Eastern Europe and North Asia; abbreviation РФ (RF))</li><li>(historical,&#32;informal) Russian Federation (alternative name of the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic, a former republic of the Soviet Union)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Russian Empire (a former empire in Eastern Europe and Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russia (a transcontinental country in Eastern Europe and North Asia; official name: Росси́йская Федера́ция (Rossíjskaja Federácija))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Роси́ꙗ</i> (Rosíja) (see <i>Русь</i> (Rusĭ, “inhabitants of Rus”)). It is the first known spelling of the name in Cyrillic in the ascetical treatise The Ladder of Divine Ascent by John Climacus transcribed by Cyprian, Metropolitan of Kiev on April 24, 1387.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja, “Russia”), a country in Eastern Europe and Asia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Federal Service for Environmental, Technological and Nuclear Supervision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rostislav</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Rostislava</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rostov (a city in Yaroslavl Oblast, Russia); also called <i>Росто́в Вели́кий</i> (Rostóv Velíkij)</li><li>Rostov-na-Donu (a large city, the administrative center of Rostov Oblast, Russia); normally called <i>Росто́в-на-Дону́</i> (Rostóv-na-Donú)</li><li>a surname, Rostov</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Ростовъ</i> (Rostovŭ). The city Rostov-on-Don is called after the Rostov city in Yaroslavl oblast.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rostov-on-Don, Rostov-na-Donu (a large city, the administrative center of Rostov Oblast, in southern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Belarusian surname, Rostropovich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Растрапо́віч (Rastrapóvič)</li><li>a Polish surname, Rostropowicz, from Polish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rotterdam (a city in South Holland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rothstein, from Yiddish</li></ol><p>From Yiddish <i>רוטשטיין</i> (rutshteyn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rokhlin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Roshchin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rouen (a city, commune, and subprefecture, the capital city of Normandy in the Seine-Maritime department, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Rouen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rublyov or Rublev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Roosevelt</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Roosevelt</i>, from Dutch <i>van Rosevelt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rumoi (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Rumoi (the capital city of Rumoi subprefecture, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>留萌</i> (るもい, Rumoi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romania (a country in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Румы́ния</i> (Rumýnija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rumyantsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ruse (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ruslan, feminine equivalent Русла́на (Ruslána)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>арыслан</i> (arıslan), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;arsïlan</i> (“lion”). Cognate with English <i>Aslan</i>/English <i>Arslan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ruslana, masculine equivalent Русла́н (Ruslán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;politics) The protests and separatist rebellions in Russian-majority areas in eastern Ukraine, held against the Euromaidan protests of 2014.</li></ol><p>From ру́сский (rússkij, “Russian”) ве́сна (vésna, “spring”). Based on the term Арабская весна (Arabskaja vesna, “Arab Spring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Rus (region in Eastern Europe, land of the Rus, East Slavic lands)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Russia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Русьскаꙗ землꙗ</i> (Rusĭskaja zemlja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Orthodox Church</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Ruth</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a female given name, Ruf, equivalent to English Ruth</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>רות</i>, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rybnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Pisces (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a pair of fishes)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Pisces (zodiac sign for the fish, covering February 19 — March 20)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>), (<i>colloquial</i>) a person who was born under this sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ryzhkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rykov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Rykovtsev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ryukyu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ryurik, equivalent to English Rurik</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Рюрикъ</i> (Rjurikŭ), from Old Norse <i>Rørik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþirīks</i>. Cognate with English <i>Roderick</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) member of the Rurik dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>Рю́рик</i> (Rjúrik) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ryazanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ryazan (a city, the administrative center of Ryazan Oblast, Russia, 196 kilometres south of Moscow)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Рѣзанъ</i> (Rězanŭ). Doublet of <i>ре́занный</i> (rézannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ryōtsu (a city in Niigata Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>両津</i> (りょうつ, Ryōtsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;euphemistic) special military operation (used in the context of the Russian invasion of Ukraine)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) consolidated military band</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>специа́льная вое́нная опера́ция</i> (speciálʹnaja vojénnaja operácija).</p><p>Initialism of <i>сво́дный вое́нный орке́стр</i> (svódnyj vojénnyj orkéstr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neurology,&#32;pathology,&#32;psychiatry,&#32;psychology) ADHD (initialism form of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>синдро́м дефици́та внима́ния и гиперакти́вности</i> (sindróm deficíta vnimánija i giperaktívnosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сре́дство индивидуа́льной защи́ты</i> (srédstvo individuálʹnoj zaščíty, “an item of personal protective equipment”): PPE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remand centre, jail (for suspects), detention facility</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>с(ле́дственный) изо(ля́тор)</i> (s(lédstvennyj) izo(ljátor)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сре́дство индивидуа́льной защи́ты о́рганов дыха́ния</i> (srédstvo individuálʹnoj zaščíty órganov dyxánija, “an item of respiratory protective equipment”): RPE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сле́дственный комите́т</i> (slédstvennyj komitét, “investigative committee”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Caucasian Federal District</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Северо-Кавка́зский федера́льный о́круг</i> (Severo-Kavkázskij federálʹnyj ókrug).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>SMERSH</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>«Сме́рть шпио́нам&#33;»</i> («Smértʹ špiónam&#33;», “Death to spies!”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>сре́дства ма́ссовой информа́ции</i> (srédstva mássovoj informácii): mass media</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Short Message Service</li><li>SMS, message, text (a text message sent on a cell phone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>SMS</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strategic (offensive) arms</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>стратеги́ческие наступате́льные вооруже́ния</i> (strategíčeskije nastupatélʹnyje vooružénija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>CIS (Commonwealth of Independent States)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Содру́жество Незави́симых Госуда́рств</i> (Sodrúžestvo Nezavísimyx Gosudárstv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Сове́т наро́дных комисса́ров</i> (Sovét naródnyx komissárov, “Council of People's Commissars”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovnarkhoz</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>сове́т наро́дного хозя́йства</i> (sovét naródnovo xozjájstva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Стратеги́ческая оборо́нная инициати́ва</i> (Strategíčeskaja oborónnaja iniciatíva, “Strategic Defense Initiative”): SDI</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint venture</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>совме́стное предприя́тие</i> (sovméstnoje predprijátije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) acronym of <i>синдро́м приобретённого иммунодефици́та</i> (sindróm priobretjónnovo immunodeficíta): AIDS</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Са́нкт-Петербу́рг</i> (Sánkt-Peterbúrg)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) initialism of <i>са́нкт-петербу́ргский</i> (sánkt-peterbúrgskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>SRV (initialism of <i>Социалистическая Республика Вьетнам</i> (Socialističeskaja Respublika Vʹjetnam) (= Socialist Republic of Vietnam): a country in East Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) SS, Schutzstaffel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>SS</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>сове́тская социалисти́ческая респу́блика</i> (sovétskaja socialistíčeskaja respúblika): SSR (Soviet Socialist Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>SSR Abkhazia (a country in Transcaucasia in Asia and Europe; capital: Sukhumi) (<i>from March 31, 1921 to February 19, 1931</i>)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Сове́тская Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika, “Socialist Soviet Republic”) + <i>Абха́зия</i> (Abxázija, “Abkhazia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) USSR (a former transcontinental country in Europe and Asia (1922–1991), now split into Russia and 14 other countries; the Soviet Union; in full, Сою́з Сове́тских Социалисти́ческих Респу́блик (Sojúz Sovétskix Socialistíčeskix Respúblik))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>SFSR (Soviet Federative Socialist Republic)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Сове́тская Федерати́вная Социалисти́ческая Респу́блика</i> (Sovétskaja Federatívnaja Socialistíčeskaja Respúblika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Соединённые Шта́ты Аме́рики</i> (Sojedinjónnyje Štáty Amériki) (= United States of America): a country in North America: USA</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>სააკაშვილი</i> (saaḳašvili)</li><li>a surname, Saakashvili, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Sablin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sava (a river in southern Europe, a right side tributary of Danube at Belgrade)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Savvin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Savely</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sabellius</i> (“Sabine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Savelyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Savitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Savoie (a department of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region, France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Savchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Sazonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saitama (the capital city of Saitama Prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Saitama (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>埼玉<rp>(</rp><rt>さいたま</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>saitama</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;endonym) Georgia (a country in the South Caucasus)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>საქართველო</i> (sakartvelo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zacatecas (a state of Mexico)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Zacatecas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saxony (a state of modern Germany, located in the east, far from historical Saxon lands)# Saxony (a historical region and former duchy in north-central Germany)</li></ol><p>Likely from Latin <i>Saxonia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salekhard (a city, the administrative center of the Yamalo-Nenets Autonomous Okrug, Russia, located on the Arctic Circle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;specifically) Ukraine after the Euromaidan protests, seen as an enemy of Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>са́ло</i> (sálo, “salo, lard”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ре́йх</i> (réjx, “Reich”), literally <i>Lard Regime</i>, referring to the use of lard in Ukrainian cuisine and accusations of Nazism by the Russian government.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Saltykov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>El Salvador (a country in Central America)</li><li>Salvador (the capital city of the state of Bahia, Brazil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Salye, from French, equivalent to French Saliès or Salié</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samantha</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samara (a city, the administrative center of Samara Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Samara (a tributary of the Volga in Russia)</li><li>Samara (a tributary of the Dnieper in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samaria (the ancient capital of the northern Kingdom of Israel, in the modern West Bank)</li><li>Samaria (an ancient region of the region of Palestine, mostly in the northern part of the modern West Bank)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samarkand (a city in Uzbekistan)</li></ol><p>From Persian سمرقند (samarqand).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Samael</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sambir (a city, the administrative center of Sambir Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Summerside (a city in Prince Edward Island)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samoa (a country consisting of the western part of the Samoan archipelago in Polynesia, in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Samoylenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Само́йленко</i> (Samójlenko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samos (an island in the eastern Aegaean Sea)</li><li>Samos (a regional unit of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σᾰ́μος</i> (Sắmos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sampsigeramid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samsø (an island of Denmark, lying within the Kattegat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Danish <i>Samsø</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samuel (<i>biblical character</i>)</li><li>a male given name, Samuil, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Samuel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שְׁמוּאֵל</i> (shmuel); see <i>Samuel</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Belarusian surname, Samutsevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Самуцэ́віч (Samucévič)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Vicente de Tagua Tagua (a commune and city in Cachapoal Province, O'Higgins Region, Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Diego (a city in California)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Marino (a landlocked microstate in Southern Europe, located within the borders of Italy)</li><li>San Marino (the capital city of San Marino)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>São Paulo (a municipality, the state capital of São Paulo, Brazil)</li><li>São Paulo (a state of the Southeast Region, Brazil; capital: São Paulo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Salvador (the capital city of El Salvador)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>São Tomé (the capital city of São Tomé and Príncipe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>São Tomé and Príncipe (a country and archipelago of Central Africa in the Atlantic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Francisco (a major coastal city and county in California, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>San José (the capital city of Costa Rica)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sanaa (the capital and largest city of Yemen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>St (saint)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Petersburg (a federal city of Russia, known between 1914 and 1924 as Petrograd and between 1924 and 1991 as Leningrad; the former capital of Russia, from 1713–1728 and 1732–1918)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sankt Peterburg</i> (“Saint Peter's City”), referring to Saint Peter the Apostle and alluding to its founder Tsar Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Санкт-Петербу́рг</i> (Sankt-Peterbúrg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sanssouci</li></ol><p>From German <i>Sanssouci</i>, from French <i>sans souci</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Santa Claus (a fictional figure said to bring presents to people (especially children) at Christmas time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Santo Domingo (the capital city of the Dominican Republic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Santiago (the capital city of Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sanya (a resort city, a prefecture-level city on Hainan island, Hainan province, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Sanya, of the male given name Алекса́ндр (Aleksándr)</li><li>(<i>less commonly</i>) a diminutive, Sanya, of the female given name Алекса́ндра (Aleksándra)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) or <i>Алекса́ндра</i> (Aleksándra) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sapega, Sapeha, or Sapieha, from Belarusian</li><li>a surname, Sapiega, from Polish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sapporo (the capital city of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>札幌</i> (さっぽろ, Sapporo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarajevo (the capital city of Bosnia and Herzegovina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saransk (the capital city of Mordovia, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Erzya <i>Саран</i> (Saran) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saratov (a port city, the administrative center of Saratov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;siarïg</i> (“yellow, white”) and Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tāg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardanapalus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardinia (an island and autonomous region of Italy, in the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sargsyan, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarny (a city, the administrative center of Sarny Raion, Rivne Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarov (closed city in Russia, 120 miles from Nizhny Novgorod&#59; a center of nuclear research)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarthe (a department of the Pays de la Loire region, France)</li><li>Sarthe (a tributary of the Maine River, in western France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sasebo (a city in Nagasaki Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>佐世保</i> (させぼ, Sasebo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Saturn</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Saturn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saudi Arabia (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Сау́довская Ара́вия</i> (Saúdovskaja Arávija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Safin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Safronov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sakhalin (a large island and region in eastern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sahara</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sakharov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic (a country in North Africa, a partially recognised state in Western Sahara)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Sasha, of the male given name Алекса́ндр (Aleksándr), equivalent to English Sasha, Alex, or Xander</li><li>a diminutive, Sasha, of the female given name Алекса́ндра (Aleksándra), equivalent to English Sasha, Alex, or Sandy</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) or <i>Алекса́ндра</i> (Aleksándra) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Sasha's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Swaziland (former name of <i>Eswatini</i>: a country in Southern Africa; used until 2018)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Svalbard (an archipelago, territory, and unincorporated area of Norway northeast of Greenland, in the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Norwegian Bokmål <i>Svalbard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>სვანიძე</i> (svaniʒe)</li><li>a surname, Svanidze, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Svarog (Slavic god of the sun, fire, and the forge)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Сварогъ</i> (Svarogŭ), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Sъvarogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Сваро́г</i> (Svaróg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Svarozhits, Svarozhich (Slavic god of fire)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Сваро́жиць</i> (Svaróžicĭ). Can be analysed as <i>Сваро́г</i> (Svaróg) +&lrm; <i>-ич</i> (-ič), meaning "son of Svarog".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Svatove (a city in Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sverdlov; particularly, Russian revolutionary, Soviet politician and statesman Yakov Sverdlov, 3 June 1885, to 16 March 1919 (aged 33)</li></ol><p>The surname is of Jewish origin, derived from the name of the settlement of Sverdly in the Vitebsk region (North of Belarus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sverdlovsk, a name for Yekaterinburg from 1924 to 1991.</li></ol><p>From <i>Свердло́в</i> (Sverdlóv) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), from the surname of Yakov Sverdlov, full name <i>Я́ков Миха́йлович Свердло́в</i> (Jákov Mixájlovič Sverdlóv), a Bolshevik party administrator and chairman.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Sveta, of the female given name Светла́на (Svetlána)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Svetlana</li></ol><p>From <i>свет</i> (svet, “light”). Coined by Alexander Vostokov in 1802 and popularized by Vasily Zhukovsky's eponymous ballad (see).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Svetlov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Svetov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Svetovid (west Slavic god of war, fertility, and prosperity)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Svętovitъ</i> (“holy lord”), made of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svętъ</i> (“holy”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vitъ</i> (“lord”). Corresponds to Polish <i>Świętowit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Svetogorsk (a town in North-Western Russia, near the Finnish border, called Enso until 1949)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá, “pig”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рейх</i> (rejx, “Reich”). Russian jokes often stereotype Ukrainians as eating salo (salted pig fat); after the War in Donbas and the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine, in Russian nationalist discourse, this portrayal progressed to a dehumanizing association of enemy Ukrainian soldiers with pigs (as opposed to merely pig-eaters).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sviridov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Holy Rus (religious concept which depicts Russia as a land chosen by God)</li><li>(poetic,&#32;endearing) Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holy Spirit, Holy Ghost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Svyatoslav</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Свѧтославъ</i> (Svętoslavŭ). Equivalent to <i>свят</i> (svjat, “holy, sacred”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́в(а)</i> (sláv(a), “fame, glory”)</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>Ἱεροκλῆς</i> (Hieroklês) (<i>ἱερός</i> (hierós) + <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês) (cognate with <i>сла́ва</i> (sláva))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ceará (a state of the Northeast Region, Brazil; capital: Fortaleza)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cebu (an island and city in the Philippines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Seva, of the male given name Все́волод (Vsévolod)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Все́волод</i> (Vsévolod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sevan (the largest lake in Armenia)</li><li>Sevan (a town in Gegharkunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սեւան</i> (Sewan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sevastopol (a port city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol><p>Via Greek <i>Σεβαστούπολη</i> (Sevastoúpoli), from Koine Greek <i>σεβαστός</i> (sebastós, “venerable, august”) and Ancient Greek <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Sevastyanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Asia (the northern region of Asia, also referred to as Siberia, geographically coextensive with the Asian part of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North America (the continent forming the northern part of the Americas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Africa (a geographic region comprising the northern part of Africa, usually referring to specifically the regions north of the Sahara Desert where Arabic is widely spoken as a mother tongue)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Holland (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Dakota (a state in the Upper Midwest region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northern Dvina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Severnaya Zemlya (an archipelago of Krasnoyarsk Krai, in the north of Russia in the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northern Ireland (a constituent country and province of the United Kingdom, situated in the northeastern part of the island of Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Carolina (a state of the United States, situated on the east coast of the North American mainland north of South Carolina and south of Virginia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Korea (a country in East Asia; capital: Pyongyang)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Macedonia (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Ossetia (a republic and federal subject of Russia, located in the Northern Caucasus mountains; in full, Се́верная Осе́тия-Ала́ния (Sévernaja Osétija-Alánija); short form Ала́ния (Alánija))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Се́верная Осе́тия</i> (Sévernaja Osétija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northern Palmyra (nickname for <i>Saint Petersburg</i>: a federal city of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Brabant (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>northern Vietnam</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) North Vietnam (a former Communist country in Southeast Asia that governed northern Vietnam from 1945 to 1975)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Caucasus (a region in southern Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northern Cyprus (a geographic region and de facto unrecognized country comprising the northeastern part of the island of Cyprus, internationally recognized as part of the country of Cyprus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arctic Ocean (the smallest of the five oceans of the Earth, on and around the North Pole, bordered by the three continents of Asia, Europe and North America)</li></ol><p><i>северный</i> (severnyj, “Northern”) +&lrm; <i>ледовитый</i> (ledovityj, “ice”) +&lrm; <i>океа́н</i> (okeán, “ocean”).</p><p>Coined by Fyodor Litke in the 1820s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northern Sea Route</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>Соединённые Шта́ты Америки</i> (Sojedinjónnyje Štáty Ameriki) United States of North America (archaic (pre-1778) term for the United States of America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sievierodonetsk (a city, the de facto administrative center of Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donets River, or Seversky Donets, which flows 1053 km through Russia and the Ukraine into the Don River.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Szeged (the county seat of Csongrád-Csanád County, Hungary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Seifulla, from Arabic, of Muslim usage</li></ol><p>From Arabic سَيْفُ ٱللَّٰهِ (sayfu llāhi, literally “Sword of Allah”), via various languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Tatar and Kazakh surname, Seifullin, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Сейфулла́</i> (Sejfullá, male given name from Arabic) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in, possessive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seychelles (a country and archipelago of East Africa in the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seychelles (a country and archipelago of East Africa in the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Sextus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Sextus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Seleznyov or Seleznev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selena (goddess of the moon)</li><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Selena or Selene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σελήνη</i> (Selḗnē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Sementsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a group of Russian nobles who deposed Tsar Vasily Shuisky on 17 July 1610</li></ol><p>From <i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>боя́рин</i> (bojárin) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina), literally, “group of seven boyars”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Semey, Semipalatinsk (a city in Kazakhstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Semiramis (name of an Assyrian queen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σεμίραμις</i> (Semíramis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Semyon, equivalent to English Simon or Simeon</li><li>Simon (name of various people in the New Testament)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Συμεών</i> (Sumeṓn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Semyonov or Semenov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Semyon's, Simeon's, Simon's</li></ol><p><i>Семён</i> (Semjón) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seine-Saint-Denis (a department of Île-de-France, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Seine-Saint-Denis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seine (a former department of France, split into 4 departments in 1968)</li><li>Seine (river in France that flows through Paris)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Seine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sendai (the capital city of Miyagi Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>仙台</i> (せんだい, Sendai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Senegal (a country in West Africa)# Senegal (a river on the border between Senegal and Mauritania in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Senkevich, from Belarusian, equivalent to Belarusian Сянке́віч (Sjankjévič)</li><li>a surname from Polish, equivalent to Polish Sienkiewicz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines (an archipelago and country in the Caribbean, comprising the islands of Saint Vincent and the island chain of the Grenadines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. John's (the capital city of Antigua and Barbuda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. George's (the capital city of Grenada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Kitts and Nevis (a country comprising the islands of Saint Kitts and Nevis in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Louis (a city in Missouri, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Saint Louis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Lucia (an island and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sentsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Serafimov</li></ol><p><i>Серафи́м</i> (Serafím) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serbia (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Serbia and Montenegro (a former country in Southeast Europe on the Balkan Peninsula, existing from 1992 to 2006, previously part of Yugoslavia; composed of modern Serbia and Montenegro and known until 2003 as the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Се́рбия</i> (Sérbija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sergeyev or Sergeev</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sergey's, Sergei's</li></ol><p><i>Серге́й</i> (Sergéj) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sergey or Sergei, from Latin, equivalent to English Sergius</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sergiyev Posad (a city in Sergiyevo-Posadsky district, Moscow Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Serebrennikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Serebryakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Seryozha, of the male given name Серге́й (Sergéj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seto (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>瀬戸</i> (せと, Seto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seoul (the capital city of South Korea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Siam (former name of <i>Thailand</i>: a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Thai <i>สยาม</i> (sà-yǎam, “Siam, historical name”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xi'an (the capital city of Shaanxi, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberia (the region of Russia in Asia, stretching from the Pacific Ocean to Ural Mountains; the southern border of this area may be not the same as the modern borders of the Russian Federation, especially if this term is used in a historical or dated context)</li><li>Siberia (the Asian part of Russia not including the Russian Far East)</li></ol><p>Derived from the name of the Siberian Tatar tribe <i>себер</i>, from earlier <i>сыбыр</i>, meaning locals (where ыр = men, nation, people; сибэ = alluvial deposit, throw to the ground; literally, "scattered [living] here people").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shibuya (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>渋谷</i> (しぶや, Shibuya, literally “harsh valley”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shizuoka (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Shizuoka (the capital city of Shizuoka Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>静岡</i> (しずおか, Shizuoka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sydney (the capital of New South Wales, and the largest city in Australia)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of English <i>Sydney</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sydney</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sidon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σιδών</i> (Sidṓn) from Phoenician <i>𐤑𐤉𐤃𐤅𐤍</i> (ṣydwn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sidor, equivalent to English Isidore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσίδωρος</i> (Isídōros), originally <i>Иси́дор</i> (Isídor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sidorov, one of the very common Russian surnames</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sidor's</li></ol><p><i>Си́дор</i> (Sídor) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siena (a city in Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Sisyphus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Σίσυφος</i> (Sísuphos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shikoku (one of the five main islands of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>四国</i> (しこく, Shikoku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Silesia (a cultural region of Central Europe, now chiefly in Poland, with small portions in the Czech Republic and Germany, from early modernity until 1945 predominantly German-speaking with Slavic-speaking minorities (and local majorities))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sealand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Silin</li><li>Xiling (a district of Yichang, Hubei, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Silviya, from Latin, equivalent to English Silvia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Silvia</i>, feminine form of Silvius, from Latin <i>silva</i> (“forest”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shimane (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>島根</i> (しまね, Shimane).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Simeonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simonides of Ceos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Симони́д</i> (Simoníd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Simonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>Симоня́н</i> (Simonján)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Simonyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սիմոնյան</i> (Simonyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simferopol (the capital city of the Autonomous Republic of Crimea, Ukraine, and of the Republic of Crimea, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cimiez (a neighborhood of Nice, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Cimiez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shinagawa (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>品川</i> (しながわ, Shinagawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Singapore (an island and city-state in Southeast Asia, located off the southernmost tip of the Malay Peninsula; a former British crown colony and state of Malaysia (1963-1965))</li></ol><p>From French <i>Singapour</i>, from Malay <i>Singapura</i>, from Sanskrit <i>सिंहपुर</i> (siṃhá-pura, “lion city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sindh (a populated province in southeastern Pakistan, notably the region in which the ancient Indus Valley Civilisation flourished)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sindeyev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Japanese, equivalent to English Shinji</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>しんじ</i> (Shinji).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shinjuku (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>新宿</i> (しんじゅく, Shinjuku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ciney (a municipality of Belgium)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Synelnykove (a city, the administrative center of Synelnykove Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Named after a Russian general Ivan Sinelnikov (Иван Синельников).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Sintiya, equivalent to English Cynthia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xinjiang (an Uyghur autonomous region of China, located in the sparsely populated northwest)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>新疆</i> (Xīnjiāng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Zion (a hill in Jerusalem, Israel, on which ancient Jerusalem was partly built; a centrepiece to Biblical accounts of old days and future eschatological events)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Сионъ</i> (Sionŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>Сионъ</i> (Sionŭ), from Koine Greek <i>Σῑών</i> (Sīṓn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>צִיּוֹן</i> (ṣiyyôn). Compare Ukrainian <i>Сіо́н</i> (Sión) and Belarusian <i>Сіён</i> (Sijón), <i>Сыён</i> (Syjón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shiribeshi (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>後志<rp>(</rp><rt>しりべし</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>Shiribeshi</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrian Arab Republic (official name of <i>Syria</i>: a country in West Asia, in the Middle East)</li></ol><p>Calque of Arabic الجمهورية العربية السورية.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Cyril</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Cyril</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Sirius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sīrius</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σείριος</i> (Seírios), usually taken from <i>σείριος</i> (seírios, “scorching; scorcher”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syria (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Си́рия</i> (Sírija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sisian (a town in Syunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սիսիան</i> (Sisian).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sitka (a consolidated city and borough in Alaska, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tlingit <i>Sheetʼká</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tibet (a geographic region in Central Asia, the homeland of the Tibetan people)# Tibet (an autonomous region of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicily (the largest island in the Mediterranean Sea, an autonomous region of Italy, close to Africa and separated from Tunisia and Libya by the Strait of Sicily)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sicilia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σικελία</i> (Sikelía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Skadovsk (a city, the administrative centre of Skadovsk Raion, Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scandinavia (a geographic region of Northern Europe, consisting of Denmark, Norway, and Sweden collectively and sometimes Finland, Iceland, Åland and the Faroe Islands)# Scandinavia (a peninsula in Northern Europe, consisting of Norway, Sweden and most of northern Finland; in full, Scandinavian Peninsula)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Scandināvia</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>Скандина́вія</i> (Skandynávija), Belarusian <i>Скандына́вія</i> (Skandynávija), Polish <i>Skandynawia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scaramouche (a roguish clown character from the 16th-century Italian commedia dell'arte)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Scaramouche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Skobov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Skolkovo (a village in Odintsovsky district, Moscow Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Skolkovo (two distinct villages in Vachsky district, Nizhny Novgorod Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Skolkovo (a village in Kinelsky district, Samara Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Skopje (the capital city of North Macedonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Skochilenko or Skochylenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Skripal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Skryabin or Scriabin, particularly Alexander Scriabin, Russian composer, January 6, 1872 — April 27, 1915</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Slavin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavonia (a region of Croatia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sloviansk (a city in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li><li>Slavyansk-on-Kuban (a city in Krasnodar Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from the Kipchak languages</li></ol><p>Adapting from Kazakh <i>Сләмжан</i> (Slämjan), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Slepakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>"Slobodka;" a common toponym for various settlements.</li><li>The name of many villages throughout Russia&#59; see <b>Slobodka, Russia</b> on Wikipedia.</li></ol><p>From <i>слобо́дка</i> (slobódka). By surface analysis, <i>слобода́</i> (slobodá, “sloboda”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slobodskoy (a district of Kirov Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Slobodskoy (a town, the administrative center of Slobodskoy district, Kirov Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Slobodskoy (a settlement in Kotlassky district, Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Slobodskoy (a settlement in Zhukovsky district, Bryansk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Slobodskoy (a hamlet in Kromskoy district, Oryol Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Slobodskoy (a hamlet in Semikarakorsky district, Rostov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slovakia (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Слова́кия</i> (Slovákija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slovenia (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Central Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Слове́ния</i> (Slovénija, “Slovenia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Slutsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Smekhov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Smirnov or Smirnoff, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol><p>From <i>сми́рный</i> (smírnyj, “quiet, mild, meek”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Smith</i>; particularly, the Scottish economist and pioneer of political economy Adam Smith</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Smith</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Smoktunovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smolensk (a city, the administrative center of Smolensk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Smolensk (an oblast of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Смо́льня</i> (Smólʹnja, “Smolnya (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), possibly from <i>смола́</i> (smolá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Smolyaninov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smolyan (a town and ski resort in southern Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Snow Maiden</li></ol><p>From <i>Снегу́рка</i> (Snegúrka, “Snow Maiden”, from <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-у́рка</i> (-úrka, a rare diminutive suffix for females)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); compare <i>дочу́рка</i> (dočúrka), diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Snezhana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian and Macedonian <i>Снежана</i> (Snežana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Snizhne (a city in Horlivka Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sobakevitch (a character of <i>Dead Souls</i> by Nikolai Gogol)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sobchak, from Polish, equivalent to Polish Sobczak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Sobczak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Security Council</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>Сове́т безопа́сности</i> (Sovét bezopásnosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Nations Security Council</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sovetsk (a city in Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Sovetsk (a city in Kirov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>сове́т</i> (sovét) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sovetskaya Gavan (a town in Khabarovsk Krai, in the Far East of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Soviet Union (a former transcontinental country in Europe and Asia (1922–1991), now split into Russia and fourteen other countries)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Советское Информационное Бюро</i> (Sovetskoje Informacionnoje Bjuro, “Soviet Information Bureau”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Сове́т мини́стров СССР</i> (Sovét minístrov SSSR, “Council of Ministers of the Soviet Union”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Сове́т наро́дных комисса́ров</i> (Sovét naródnyx komissárov, “Council of People's Commissars”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Federation Council (upper house of Russian national parliament)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Сове́т Федера́ции</i> (Sovét Federácii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seogwipo (a city in Jeju Province, South Korea)</li></ol><p>Korean <i>서귀포</i> (seogwipo) (<i>西歸浦</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) <i>(made up of eleven former Soviet republics, including Russia)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Commonwealth of Nations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seodaemun (a district of Seoul, South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean 서대문구 (西大門區, Seodaemun'gu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Kingdom (a kingdom and country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>United Kingdom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland (official name of the <i>United Kingdom</i>: a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United States (a country in North America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>United States of America (a country in North America, also including Hawaii in Oceania; short form Соединённые Шта́ты&nbsp;m&nbsp;inan&nbsp;pl (Sojedinjónnyje Štáty) or Аме́рика&nbsp;f&nbsp;inan (Amérika); abbreviation США&nbsp;m&nbsp;inan&nbsp;pl (SŠA))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sokolov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Socrates</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σωκράτης</i> (Sōkrátēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sokurov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soledar (a city in Bakhmut Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine, with an extensive salt mine)</li></ol><p><i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>дар</i> (dar), literally “gift of the salt”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Solzhenitsyn, particular Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, Russian novelist and dissident, December 11, 1918 — August 3, 2008</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solar System</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Solovetsky Islands (an archipelago of the White Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Solovyev, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Solodov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Solomin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solomon Islands (a country and archipelago of Melanesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solon of Athens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Соло́н</i> (Solón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solvychegodsk (a city in Kotlasky district, Arkhangelsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Somalia (a country in East Africa, in the Horn of Africa)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Somalie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Somaliland (a geographic region and de facto independent unrecognized state in East Africa, internationally recognized as part of Somalia; formerly the Somali Coast Protectorate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a male given name, Soniril</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Sonerilas</i>, in turn from Ancient Greek <i>Σουηρίλλας</i> (Souēríllas). Originally named after a Gothic Christian saint, who was burned to death under orders of King Jungerich around 375 AD.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sorachi (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>空知</i> (そらち, Sorachi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common surname, Sorokin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sosnowiec (a city in Silesian Voivodeship, Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Sotnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sofia (the capital city of Bulgaria)</li><li>Sofia (a female given name from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Sophia)</li><li>(<i>Gnosticism</i>) Sophia (<i>Aeon of wisdom</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σοφία</i> (sophía, “wisdom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Sofya, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Sophia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σοφία</i> (sophía, “wisdom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Софи́я</i> (Sofíja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Socialist Republic of Vietnam (official name of <i>Vietnam</i>: a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sochi (a city in Krasnodar Krai, Russia, which hosted the 2014 Winter Olympics)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Georgian <i>სოჭი</i> (soč̣i, “fir”); the city belonged to Georgia during the XI-XV centuries.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (a former transcontinental country in Europe and Asia (1922–1991), now split into Russia and 14 other countries; short form Сове́тский Сою́з (Sovétskij Sojúz); abbreviation Сою́з ССР (Sojúz SSR) or СССР (SSSR))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spalnoye (a hamlet in Borkovsky selsoviet, Sudzhansky Raion, Kursk Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia, situated on the banks of the River Psyol)</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>спа́льный</i> (spálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sparta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Spartacus, a Thracian gladiator who was notable for leading an uprising of slaves against the Roman Republic.</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) shortening of Футбо́льный клуб «Спарта́к» Москва́ (FC Spartak Moscow, a football team from Moscow, Russia).</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>Spartacus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Speransky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Սպերցյան</i> (Spercʻyan)</li><li>a surname, Spertsyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սպերցյան</i> (Spercʻyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spiridon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Σπυρίδων</i> (Spurídōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spitak (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սպիտակ</i> (Spitak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Split (a port city in Croatia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sprouse (transliteration of the English surname)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sleeping Beauty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) Joseph Stalin.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>сра́л(ьник)</i> (srál(ʹnik), “toilet”), itself from <i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to shit”), and <i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin, “Stalin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mediterranean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>region of the Mediterranean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Средиземномо́рье</i> (Sredizemnomórʹje)</li><li>Middle-earth, the setting of works by J.R.R. Tolkien.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Central Asia (a landlocked geographic area of continental central Asia comprising Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stabiae (a Roman city in Italy, largely buried under tephra ash in the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Stabiae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stavropol (a city, the administrative center of Stavropol Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>σταυρός</i> (staurós, “cross”) + <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Stalin; usually referring to the Russian revolutionary and Soviet Secretary of the Central Committee of the CPSU(b) Joseph Stalin</li></ol><p>From <i>сталь</i> (stalʹ, “steel”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in, “’s”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Stalinabad (name used for Dushanbe, a capital of Tajikistan, from 1929 to 1961, in the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin) +&lrm; <i>абад</i> (abad), Named after Soviet&#32;leader&#32;Joseph Stalin</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Stalingrad (former name of <i>Volgograd</i>: a city, the administrative center of Volgograd Oblast, Russia, and site of a famous World War II battle; used from 1925–1961)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad), named after Joseph Stalin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Istanbul (the largest city in Turkey)</li><li>Istanbul (a province of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Stanislav</li></ol><p>Of Slavic origin. By surface analysis, <i>стать</i> (statʹ, “to stand, to become”) +&lrm; <i>слава</i> (slava, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Stanislava</li></ol><p>From <i>Станислав</i> (Stanislav).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Stanislavsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stara Zagora (a city in Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ста́ра Заго́ра</i> (Stára Zagóra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Starobilsk (a city, the administrative center of Starobilsk Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Starovoytov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old New Year, the Orthodox New Year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stary Oskol (a city in Belgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”) +&lrm; <i>Оско́л</i> (Oskól, “Oskil”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Old World (the Eastern Hemisphere, especially Europe, Africa and Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Stas, of the male given names Анаста́с (Anastás) or Станисла́в (Stanisláv)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Stasya, of the female given names Анастаси́я (Anastasíja) or Станисла́ва (Stanisláva)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Staten Island (an island and borough of New York City)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Statkevich, from Belarusian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Stakhanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Statsenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Stepan, equivalent to English Stephen or Steven</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Στέφανος</i> (Stéphanos), from <i>στέφανος</i> (stéphanos, “crown, wreath”), from <i>στέφω</i> (stéphō, “to put round, to surround”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stepanavan (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ստեփանավան</i> (Stepʻanavan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stepanakert (the capital city of Artsakh, a former breakaway state internationally recognized as part of Azerbaijan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ստեփանակերտ</i> (Stepʻanakert). By the decree of the Central Executive Committee of the Azerbaijan SSR of August 10, 1923, the village of Khankendi was renamed the city of Stepanakert in honor of the head of the Baku Commune Stepan Shahumyan. First attested in the August 10, 1923.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Stepanov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Stepan's, Stephen's</li></ol><p><i>Степа́н</i> (Stepán) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sterzh (a lake in Tver Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sterlitamak (a city in Bashkortostan, Russia)</li></ol><p>Compare Bashkir <i>Стәрлетамаҡ</i> (Stərletamaq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Stephen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Στεφανός</i> (Stephanós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stockholm (the capital city of Sweden)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stolichnaya vodka, Stoli (a brand of (Russian) vodka made of wheat and rye grain)</li></ol><p>Nominative feminine singular form of <i>столи́чный</i> (stolíčnyj, “of capital city”), i.e. "capital vodka", "vodka from the capital city".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Stolypin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stonehenge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Stonehenge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Stravinsky, particularly Igor Stravinsky, Russian composer, pianist and conductor, June 17, 1882 — April 6, 1971</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>Strawiński</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Holy Week</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Good Friday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Holy Week</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Maundy Thursday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Streisand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Stribog (Slavic god of the wind)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Стрибогъ</i> (Stribogŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Strybogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Стри́бо́г</i> (Stríbóg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Stroganoff</i>: a surname, Stroganov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Strugatsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Studenkin, originating as an occupation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Styopa, of the male given name Степа́н (Stepán), equivalent to English Steve</li><li>a diminutive, Styopa, of the female given name Степани́да (Stepanída), equivalent to English Steph</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Степа́н</i> (Stepán) or <i>Степани́да</i> (Stepanída) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;rare) Caturday, Saturday being dedicated to posting pictures of cats.</li></ol><p>From <i>суббо́та</i> (subbóta, “Saturday”) and <i>кот</i> (kot, “cat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suva (the capital city of Fiji)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Suvorov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suginami (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>杉並</i> (すぎなみ, Suginami).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sudan (a country in North Africa and East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suzdal (a town in Vladimir Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Суждаль</i> (Suždalĭ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sukkot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>סוכות \ סֻכּוֹת</i> (sukót), plural of <i>סוכה \ סֻכָּה</i> (suká).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sulawesi (an island of Indonesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sultanbekov</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Сұлтанбеков</i> (Sūltanbekov). By surface analysis, <i>султан</i> (sultan) +&lrm; <i>бек</i> (bek) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sumatra (the largest island entirely in Indonesia and the world’s sixth largest island)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sumida (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>墨田</i> (すみだ, Sumida).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sumy (a city, the administrative center of Sumy Oblast, in northeastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Song; the Song dynasty (Chinese dynasty that lasted from 960–1279 <small>C.E.</small>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>宋</i> (Sòng)</li><li>a surname, Song, from Chinese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>宋</i> (Sòng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;endonym) Finland (a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>Suomi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Surikov, particularly Vasily Surikov the foremost Russian painter of large-scale historical subjects</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suriname (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Surovikin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Susanna or Susan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Соусана</i> (Susana), from Ancient Greek <i>Σουσάννα</i> (Sousánna), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שׁוֹשַׁנָּה</i> (šôšannâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sufiyan or Sufyan, from Arabic, of Muslim usage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic سفيان (sufyān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sukhona (a tributary of the Northern Dvina River in Vologda Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Probably from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;Суходъна</i> (&#42;Suxodŭna, literally “a river with a dry (hard) bottom”). See <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) and <i>дно</i> (dno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Суху́ми</i> (Suxúmi) (used in Russian and Abkhazian texts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sukhumi (the capital city of Republic of Abkhazia, a de-facto independent country internationally recognized as part of the country of Georgia (and capital city of its Autonomous Republic of Abkhazia))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>სოხუმი</i> (soxumi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suzhou (a major city in Jiangsu, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suez (a port,city in Egypt)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English Suez.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Merry Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syktyvkar (the capital city of the Komi Republic, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Komi-Zyrian <i>Сыктывкар</i> (Syktyvkar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syr Darya (river)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syr Darya (river)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sichuan (a province in central China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siena (alternative form of <i>Сие́на</i> (Sijéna): a city in Tuscany, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sierra Leone (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Setagaya (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>世田谷</i> (せたがや, Setagaya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syunik (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Սյունիք</i> (Syunikʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Гонко́нг</i> (Gonkóng): Hong Kong (a city, island and special administrative region in southeastern China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 香港 (<i>Xiānggǎng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Се́веро-Америка́нские Соединённые Шта́ты</i> (Sévero-Amerikánskije Sojedinjónnyje Štáty).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Си́рия</i> (Sírija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) autocorrect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>T9</i>, the brand name of a late-1990s predictive text technology by Tegic Communications for mobile phones with a 3×4 numeric keypad (although the meaning has been extended to cover any autocorrect).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) alternative form of <i>ТАКР</i> (TAKR)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>тяжёлый авианесущий крейсер</i> (tjažólyj avianesuščij krejser, “heavy aircraft-carrying cruiser”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) the Soviet Navy classification for aircraft carriers</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>тяжёлый авианесущий крейсер</i> (tjažólyj avianesuščij krejser, “heavy aircraft-carrying cruiser”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) acronym of <i>Телегра́фное аге́нтство Советского Союза</i> (Telegráfnoje agéntstvo Sovetskovo Sojuza): TASS (“Telegraph Agency of the Soviet Union”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV, television</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>телеви́дение</i> (televídenije) or <i>телевизио́нный</i> (televiziónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Optimal Functioning Theory for a Socialist Economy</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Тео́рия оптима́льного функциони́рования социалисти́ческой эконо́мики</i> (Teórija optimálʹnovo funkcionírovanija socialistíčeskoj ekonómiki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TORFL</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Те́ст по ру́сскому языку́ как иностра́нному</i> (Tɛ́st po rússkomu jazykú kak inostránnomu, “Test of the Russian Language as a Foreign (Language)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) labor theory of value</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>трудова́я тео́рия сто́имости</i> (trudovája teórija stóimosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tabakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Taurica (an ancient geographic region and peninsula, the name used by the Greeks and Romans for the modern Crimean Peninsula in Eastern Europe, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but occupied by Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tavush (a province of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Տավուշ</i> (Tavuš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tagil (a river in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Nizhny Tagil (a city in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Verkhny Tagil (a town in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>type of Russian main battle tank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tahir</li></ol><p>From Arabic طَاهِر (ṭāhir, “pure, virtuous”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tajikistan (a country in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tajik SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1929 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thailand (a country in Southeast Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Taisiya</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Θαΐς</i> (Thaḯs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Variant of Russian <i>Таи́сия</i> (Taísija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tahiti (the largest island in French Polynesia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Tahiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taipei (the capital city of Taiwan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taiwan (a partly-recognized country in East Asia consisting of a main island and 167 smaller islands; official name: Кита́йская Респу́блика (Kitájskaja Respúblika))</li><li>Taiwan (an island between the Taiwan Strait and Philippine Sea in East Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>臺灣</i>&nbsp;/ <i>台湾</i> (Táiwān), from Literary Chinese <i>臺灣</i>, from Hokkien 大員／大员 (<i>Tāi-oân</i>), 大圓／大圆, 臺員／台员, 大灣／大湾, 臺灣／台湾, etc., from possibly Siraya.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>Таила́нд</i> (Tailánd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Taymura (a river in Evenkiysky district, Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taimyr (a large peninsula in northern Russia)</li><li>Taymyr (Soviet and Russian nuclear-powered icebreaker)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taitō (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>台東</i> (たいとう, Taitō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taichung (a city in Taiwan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talakivka (a settlement in Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talas (a region in northern Kyrgyzstan)# Talas (a town, the capital of the Talas Region, Kyrgyzstan)# Talas (a river in Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taliban</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Pashto طالبان (tālibān, “students, seekers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talin (a town in Aragatsotn Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Թալին</i> (Tʻalin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tallinn (the capital and largest city of Estonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Estonia standard form of <i>Та́ллин</i> (Tállin): Tallinn (the capital city of Estonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Talkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Tamara</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) Tango (<i>T</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>תָּמָר</i> (tamár, “Tamar”, literally “date palm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tambov (a city, the administrative center of Tambov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tanzania (a country in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Tanyusha, of the female given name Та́ня (Tánja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Tanya, of the female given name Татья́на (Tatʹjána)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Taras</li><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Тара́с</i> (Tarás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Taras'es</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tarasov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Taratuta, from Ukrainian</li><li>a Jewish surname, Taratuta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarbaldzhey (a mountain in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li><li>the Tarbaldzhey (a river in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li><li>Tarbaldzhey (a selo in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠲᠠᠷᠪᠠᠯᠵᠢ</i> (tarbalǰi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tariverdiev, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Թարիվերդյան</i> (Tʻariverdyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ев</i> (-ev).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tarkovsky, from Polish, equivalent to English Tarkowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) Tartarus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tartary, an old name for Central Asia and the Eurasian Steppe used by Europeans.</li><li>(<i>pseudoscience</i>) A supposed ancient civilization according to some Russian conspiracy theories</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Та́ртар</i> (Tártar).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tartu (a city in Estonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tartus (a city on the Mediterranean coast of Syria)# Tartus (a governorate of Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tasmania (an island group and state of Australia; a former British colony, from 1856 to 1901)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tatarstan (a republic and federal subject of south-central Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tatarstan (a republic and federal subject of south-central Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>Татарстан</i> (Tatarstan). Can be analysed as <i>тата́р</i> (tatár) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Tatiana, from Latin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Tatiana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tashir (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Տաշիր</i> (Tašir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tashkent (the capital city of Uzbekistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Taya, of the female given name Таи́сия (Taísija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tbilisi (the capital of the country of Georgia)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Georgian <i>თბილისი</i> (tbilisi), from <i>თბილი</i> (tbili, “warm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tver (a city, the administrative center of Tver Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Тьхвѣрь</i> (Tĭxvěrĭ). Compare Russian <i>Тверца́</i> (Tvercá), Russian <i>Ти́хвин</i> (Tíxvin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) Twitter (social-networking website)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Twitter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teberda (a town in Karachayevsk, Karachay-Cherkessia, Russia)</li><li>Teberda (a river in Karachay-Cherkessia, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Karachay-Balkar <i>Теберди</i> (Teberdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tehran (the capital and largest city of Iran)</li><li>Tehran (a province of Iran)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tegucigalpa (the capital city of Honduras)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lake Teletskoye (a lake in the Altai Mountains, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tieling (a prefecture-level city of Liaoning, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tel Aviv (a city in Israel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Тель-Ави́в</i> (Tɛlʹ-Avív).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thames (a river in southeast England in the United Kingdom, flowing through London)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tengri, god of the sky and supreme deity in Turkic and Mongol religions.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language; ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;teŋri</i> (“sky, heaven, god”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tennessee (a state of the United States)</li><li>Tennessee, Tennessee River (a river in Tennessee, Alabama, Mississippi and Kentucky, United States, that flows from eastern Tennessee into the Ohio River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Terebeychuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Tereza, equivalent to English Theresa or Teresa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Terenty or Terenti</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tereshkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terjola (a town in Georgia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>თერჯოლა</i> (terǯola).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Turner</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ternopil (a city, the administrative center of Ternopil Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) <i>Tetris</i></li></ol><p>Coined by the creator of the game Alexey Pajitnov from the Greek numerical prefix <i>тетра-</i> (tetra-, “tetra-”) (as all of the game’s pieces contain four segments) and <i>те́ннис</i> (tɛ́nnis, “tennis”), his favourite/favorite sport.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Texas (a state in the south-central region of the United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Tejas</i>, itself from Caddo <i>táyshaʔ</i> (“friend, ally”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chiba (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Chiba (the capital city of Chiba Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>千葉</i> (ちば, Chiba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tibet (a geographic region in Central Asia, the homeland of the Tibetan people)# Tibet (an autonomous region of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Тибе́т</i> (Tibét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiber (a major river in Italy)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tiberis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Tigris <i>(Thor Heyerdahl’s 60-ft. reed boat)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Тигр</i> (Tigr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tilburg (a city in North Brabant, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Timofey, equivalent to English Timothy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tymoshenko or Timoshenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Timchenko or Tymchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Tinkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tirana (the capital and largest city of Albania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiraspol (the capital city of Transnistria, a de-facto independent state internationally recognized as part of Moldova)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tisiphone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Titus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Titus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Titarev or Titaryov</li></ol><p>From <i>кти́тор</i> (ktítor) or <i>ти́тор</i> (títor)/<i>ти́тар</i> (títar, “ktetor”, also “ktitor”), from Ancient Greek <i>κτήτωρ</i> (ktḗtōr) — someone who provides the funds for construction or reconstruction of an Orthodox church or monastery, a church elder.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Titov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tiflis (historical name of Tbilisi, the capital city of the country of Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tikhanovsky or Tsikhanouski, equivalent to Belarusian Ціхано́ўскі (Cixanówski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pacific Ocean (an ocean, the world's largest body of water, to the east of Asia and Australasia and to the west of the Americas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Tikhomirov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tikhon</li></ol><p>Through Old East Slavic from Old Church Slavonic <i>Тѵхѡнъ</i> (Tüxonŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Τύχων</i> (Túkhōn, “male name, originally an epithet of Hermes”) (whence also Latin <i>Tycho</i>), akin to <i>τύχον</i> (túkhon, “lucky”), from <i>τύχη</i> (túkhē, “fortune”) (whence <i>Τῠ́χη</i> (Tŭ́khē, “Tyche, Fortuna, Lady Luck”)) and <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewgʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tikhonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tishchenko or Tyshchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chiyoda (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>千代田</i> (ちよだ, Chiyoda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tobol (a river in Kazakhstan and Russia)</li></ol><p>Of Turkic origin. From the same root whence Bashkir <i>тубылғы</i> (tubılğı, “meadowsweet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tōgane (a city in Chiba Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>東金</i> (とうがね, Tōgane).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tokachi (a river in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan; in full, Tokachi River)# Tokachi (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan, named after the river; in full, Tokachi Subprefecture)# Tokachi (a former province in modern Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan; in full, Tokachi Province)# Tokachi (a district of Tokachi subprefecture, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan; in full, Tokachi District)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>十勝</i> (とかち, Tokachi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tokyo (a prefecture, the capital city of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>東京<rp>(</rp><rt>とうきょう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>Tōkyō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tokushima (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Tokushima (the capital city of Tokushima Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>徳島</i> (とくしま, Tokushima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Tolik, of the male given names Анато́лий (Anatólij) or Анато́лій (Anatólij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tolokonnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Tolstoy, particularly Leo Tolstoy, Russian writer, September 9, 1828 — November 20, 1910</li></ol><p>A variant of <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj, literally “fat”) with archaic accent. Compare dialectal <i>Талсто́ј</i> (Leka village).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tolyatti, Togliatti (a city in Samara Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Named after Italian&#32;politician&#32;Palmiro Togliatti&nbsp;(1893–1964), leader of the Italian Communist Party.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Tolya, of the male given names Анато́лий (Anatólij) or Анато́лій (Anatólij), equivalent to English Anatolius or Anatole</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Анато́лий</i> (Anatólij) + <i>-я</i> (-ja, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) a diminutive, Tolyan, of the male given name Анато́лий (Anatólij)</li></ol><p><i>То́ля</i> (Tólja) +&lrm; <i>-я́н</i> (-ján)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tomigusuku (a city in Okinawa Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>豊見城</i> (とみぐすく, Tomigusuku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tomsk (a city, the administrative center of Tomsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, from <i>Томь</i> (Tomʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Tom River</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tonga (a country and archipelago of Polynesia in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tondi (a subdistrict of Kristiine district, Tallinn, Estonia)</li><li>Stongdey, Thonde (a monastery in Zanskar, Ladakh, India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Estonian <i>Tondi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dongdaemun (a district of Seoul, South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean 동대문구 (東大門區, Dongdaemun'gu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Torah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>תורה</i> (torá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toronto (the capital city of Ontario, Canada and largest city Canada)# Toronto (a lakeside suburb of Greater Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia, in the local government area of the City of Lake Macquarie)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toropets (a city, the administrative center of Toropetsky district, Tver Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toruń (the capital city of Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship, in north-central Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Toruń</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toshima (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>豊島</i> (としま, Toshima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tochigi (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>栃木</i> (とちぎ, Tochigi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tottori (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>鳥取</i> (とっとり, Tottori).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Totma (a city, the administrative center of Totemsky district, Vologda Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toyama (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Toyama (the capital city of Toyama Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>富山</i> (とやま, Toyama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Трајковски</i>&nbsp;m (Trajkovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Трајкоски</i>&nbsp;m (Trajkoski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Trump</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transylvania (a historical region in western Romania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Трајановски</i>&nbsp;m (Trajanovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Трајаноски</i>&nbsp;m (Trajanoski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(history,&#32;politics,&#32;theology) Third Rome (political concept of Moscow as the successor of the Byzantine Empire and the Roman Empire, later extended to Russia in nationalist discourse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Third Reich (Nazi Germany)</li></ol><p>Partial calque of German <i>Drittes Reich</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>Тре́тій Рейх</i> (Trétij Rejx). Doublet of <i>Тре́тья импе́рия</i> (Trétʹja impérija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tretyak, from Ukrainian, equivalent to Ukrainian Третя́к (Tretják)</li><li>a surname from Polish, equivalent to Polish Trzeciak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tretyakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Tretyakov Gallery</li></ol><p>From <i>третьяко́вский</i> (tretʹjakóvskij) [in <i>Третьяко́вская галере́я</i> (Tretʹjakóvskaja galeréja, “Tretyakov Gallery”)] + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trieste (the capital city of Friuli-Venezia Giulia, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trinidad and Tobago (a country consisting of two main islands and several smaller islands in the Caribbean, off the coast of Venezuela)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tripoli (the capital city of Libya)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Triptolemus</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Τριπτόλεμος</i> (Triptólemos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arc de Triomphe (the Parisian monument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tryphon or Trifon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Trifonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Trotsky, particularly Leon Trotsky, Russian-Ukrainian revolutionary, November 7, 1879 — August 21, 1940</li></ol><p>Toponymic surname from the Polish <i>Troki</i>, from the Lithuanian city <i>Trakai</i>, from <i>trakai</i> (“glade”).</p><p>Or could be from <i>трость</i> (trostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij) with the toponymic root <i>трость</i> (trostʹ), like in <i>Тростянець</i> (Trostjanecʹ, “reedbed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Trubachov or Trubachev</li></ol><p><i>труба́ч</i> (trubáč, “trumpeter”) +&lrm; <i>-ёв</i> (-jóv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Trubetskoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Trudeau</i></li></ol><p>From French <i>Trudeau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuapse (a resort town in Krasnodar Krai, in southern Russia, on the Black Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuva (a republic of Russia)</li></ol><p>The name likely refers to a Uralic, probably Samoyedic, tribe, referred to in 7th-century Chinese sources as <i>Dubo</i> or <i>Tupo</i>. The Tuvan language reflects Samoyedic influences in much of its vocabulary.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuvalu (a country and archipelago of Polynesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A river valley on the left bank of the Lena (Yakutia, Russia), where the city of Yakutsk is located</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Туймаада</i> (Tuymaada).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tula (an industrial city, the administrative center of Tula Oblast, Russia, 165 km south of Moscow)</li></ol><p>Per Dahl and after him Vasmer, possibly from <i>тулить</i> (tulitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tulchyn (a city, the administrative center of Tulchyn Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tumanyan (a town in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Թումանյան</i> (Tʻumanyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Swedish surname <i>Thunberg</i></li></ol><p>From Swedish <i>Thunberg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tunguska (a river in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tunisia (a country in North Africa)</li><li>Tunis (the capital city, since 1159, and largest city of Tunisia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tupolev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Turan (a region of Central Asia, originally populated by Iranian Central Asian nomads; later, Iranians came to identify Turkic neighbours as Turanians)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian توران (turân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Turgenev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Türkiye (official name of <i>Turkey</i>: a country in West Asia and Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>République turque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turin (a city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Turin and the region of Piedmont, Italy)# Turin (a metropolitan city of Piedmont, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Turin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Shroud of Turin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Turkestan (a historic region of Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkmenistan (a country in Central Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkmen <i>Türkmenistan</i>. Can be analysed as <i>туркме́н</i> (turkmén) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkmenistan (a country in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkmen SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1921 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Turkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tursunov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town in Siberia, former known as <i>Монасты́рское</i>, a former city. Located by the river Yenisei.</li><li>a village in the same municipality, now known as <i>Старотуруха́нск</i>, a former city. One of the oldest cities in Siberia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkey (a country located in Eastern Thrace in Southeastern Europe and Anatolia in West Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>Turcia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ту́рция</i> (Túrcija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tusculum (a city of Latium said to have been founded by Telegonus, the son of Ulysses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Tusculum</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thimphu (the capital city of Bhutan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) Tyva (a republic of Russia)</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from Tuvan <i>Тыва</i> (Tıva). Doublet of <i>Тува́</i> (Tuvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daegu, Taegu (a city in South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>대구(大邱)</i> (Daegu, literally “large hill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chūbu region (of Honshu, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>中部</i> (ちゅうぶ, Chūbu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyumen Oblast (an oblast of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>тюме́нский</i> (tjuménskij, “related to Tyumen”) +&lrm; <i>о́бласть</i> (óblastʹ, “oblast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyumen (a city, the administrative center of Tyumen Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tümen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyung (a tributary of the Vilyuy in Yakutia, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Yakut <i>түҥ</i> (tüŋ, “dark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chūō (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>中央</i> (ちゅうおう, Chūō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) Tyr, the Norse god of war and warriors.</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>Týr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thuringia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Тюри́нгия</i> (Tjuríngija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tyutenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tyutchev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tyaglov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tian Shan (a mountain range in Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tianjin (a direct-administered municipality and major city in northern China, between Beijing and the Bohai Bay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiachiv (a city, the administrative center of Tiachiv Raion, Zakarpattia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tyorkin or Terkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Тернер</i> (Terner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Turpin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Тели́н</i> (Telín).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>универсальная десятичная классификация</i> (universalʹnaja desjatičnaja klassifikacija, “Universal Decimal Classification”); UDC</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) acronym of <i>у̀льтразвуково́е иссле́дование</i> (ùlʹtrazvukovóje isslédovanije): ultrasound examination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) criminal code</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>уголо́вный ко́декс</i> (ugolóvnyj kódeks, “criminal code”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ukrainian Insurgent Army, UPA</li></ol><p>abbreviation of <i>Украи́нская повста́нческая а́рмия</i> (Ukraínskaja povstánčeskaja ármija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ouagadougou (the capital city of Burkina Faso)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Uvarov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uganda (a country in East Africa)</li></ol><p>From Swahili <i>Uganda</i> (“Buganda, subnational kingdom”), from Luganda <i>Buganda</i> (“land of the Ganda”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vuhledar (a city in Volnovakha Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>дар</i> (dar), literally “gift of the coal”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Uglov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Udaltsov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Udmurtia (a republic and federal subject of Russia, on a plain to the west of the Ural Mountains)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uzhhorod (A city in the Carpathian Mountains, the administrative centre of Zakarpattia Oblast in western Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uzbekistan (a country in Central Asia)</li></ol><p>From Classical Persian اُزْبَکِسْتَان (uzbakistān). Can be analysed as <i>узбек</i> (uzbek) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan). See Uzbekistan for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uzbek SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1924 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Uzlov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English William</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Williams</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Williams</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Witkoff</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uqaylid</li></ol><p><i>Укайл</i> (Ukajl) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukraine (a country in Eastern Europe, bordering on the north shore of the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оукраина</i> (ukraina, “borderland”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ukrajina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ukrainian People's Republic (a former country in Eastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1917 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>укро́п</i> (ukróp, “a Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija), or alternatively as <i>укро́п</i> (ukróp, “a Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>уто́пия</i> (utópija, “a utopia”). Compare <i>Се́рбия</i> (Sérbija) — <i>серб</i> (serb), <i>Че́хия</i> (Čéxija) — <i>чех</i> (čex), <i>Слова́кия</i> (Slovákija) — <i>слова́к</i> (slovák) and etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>укр</i> (ukr, “Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рейх</i> (rejx, “regime”)</p><p>Derived from accusations against the Ukrainian government of being a "Nazi government" due to the usage of the Azov Battalion into its armed forces, which used fascistic symbols; or the popularity of Stephan Bandera as a symbol used by some Ukrainian nationalist groups.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncommon spelling of <i>Ула́н-Ба́тор</i> (Ulán-Bátor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ulaanbaatar (the capital city of Mongolia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolian <i>ᠤᠯᠠᠭᠠᠨᠪᠠᠭᠠᠲᠤᠷ</i> (Ulaɣanbaɣatur). The spelling pre-dates the development of the Cyrillic Mongolian spelling <i>Улаанбаатар</i> (Ulaanbaatar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ulan-Ude (the capital city of the Republic of Buryatia, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Buryat <i>Ulaan-Yde</i> (created in 1934; later since 1939 in Cyrillic as <i>Улаан-Үдэ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ulanov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Ulysses (Latin name form of Odysseus)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ulyssēs</i>, a frequent alternative form of <i>Ulixēs</i> (“Odysseus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ulitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uluru, Ayers Rock (a rock in the Northern Territory, Australia, near Alice Springs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ulyan</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>Iūliānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ulyana</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>Iūliāna</i>, feminine form of <i>Iūliānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ulyanov, notably the original name of Lenin (<i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin))</li></ol><p><i>Улья́н</i> (Ulʹján) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ulyanovsk (a city, the administrative center of Ulyanovsk Oblast, Russia, on the Volga River); birthplace of Vladimir Lenin</li></ol><p>From <i>Улья́нов</i> (Ulʹjánov) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), from the surname of Lenin &ndash; full name <i>Влади́мир Ильи́ч Улья́нов</i> (Vladímir Ilʹíč Ulʹjánov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uman (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Crimean Tatar surname, Umerov, from Crimean Tatar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Unicode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wokingham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Walter Mitty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Urals <i>(mountain range)</i></li><li>Ural <i>(river)</i></li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, with several theories proposed, including Turkic/Bashkir and Ugric; see Urals.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Urartu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;uncommon,&#32;Internet slang) Ukraine, perceived as a country of criminals</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;specifically) Ukraine under former president Viktor Yanukovych</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>у́рка</i> (úrka, “a criminal”) +&lrm; <i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína), originally from Ukrainian <i>Уркаї́на</i> (Urkajína). Coined in 2004 after the Orange Revolution, and popularised in 2011 from its usage in the satirical novel <i>S.N.U.F.F.</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Urozhaynoye (a village in Urozhayny rural settlement, Sovetsky Raion, Altai Krai, Siberian Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Urozhaynoye (a village in Dalnevostochnoye rural settlement, Romnensky Raion, Amur Oblast, Far Eastern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Urozhaynoye (a settlement in Guryevsky Raion, Kaliningrad Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia, founded in 1258)</li><li>Urozhaynoye (a settlement in Slavsky Raion, Kaliningrad Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia, founded before 1893)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Bobrovytsia urban hromada, Nizhyn Raion, Chernihiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Simferopol Raion, Crimea, Ukraine, occupied by Russian forces since February 2014)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Theodosia Raion <i>de jure</i>, in Sovietskyi Raion <i>de facto</i>, in Crimea, Ukraine, occupied by Russian forces since February 2014)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Tomakivka settlement hromada, Nikopol Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a rural settlement in Velyka Novosilka settlement hromada, Volnovakha Raion, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Beryslav urban hromada, Beryslav Raion, Kherson Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a deserted rural settlement in, at time of last population, the former Novoarkhanhelsk Raion, now in Holovanivsk Raion, Kirovohrad Oblast, Ukraine, abandoned sometime after 2001 &#91;before the introduction of hromady&#93;)</li><li>Urozhaine (a rural settlement in Khrustalnyi urban hromada, Rovenky Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine, controlled by the Luhansk People’s Republic since 2014)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Melnytsia-Podilska settlement hromada, Chortkiv Raion, Ternopil Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Hnivan urban hromada, Vinnytsia Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Urozhaine (a village in Tokmak urban hromada, Polohy Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>The neuter of <i>урожа́йный</i> (urožájnyj, “of harvest, messorious; bountiful, rich”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uruguay (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ürümqi (a prefecture-level city, the capital of the Xinjiang autonomous region, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ussuriysk (a city in Primorsky Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Уссу́ри</i> (Ussúri, “Ussuri (river)”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ustinov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ustinia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ust-Luga (a town in Leningrad Oblast, Russia); the town is placed on mouth of river Luga, where this river falls into the Gulf of Finland</li></ol><p>From <i>устье</i> (ustʹje, “river mouth”) +&lrm; <i>Луга</i> (Luga, “the river Luga”). The toponyms starting with <i>Усть-</i> (Ustʹ-) are very common in Russia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ustyuzhna (a city, the administrative center of Ustyuzhensky district, Vologda Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Utkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Utrecht (a province of the Netherlands)# Utrecht (a city and municipality, the capital of Utrecht province, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uttar Pradesh (a state in northern India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>उत्तर प्रदेश</i> (uttar pradeś, literally “north country”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Utyosov or Utesov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ufa (the capital and largest city of Bashkortostan, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wuhan (the capital city of Hubei, China)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;humorous) Ukraine</li></ol><p>Sense 2 derives from a mispronunciation by US House of Representatives Speaker Nancy Pelosi of "<i>Сла́ва Украї́ні</i> (Sláva Ukrajíni)" as "<i>Сла́ва <b>Уха́ни</b></i> (Sláva <b>Uxáni</b>)" in a speech held in March 2022.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Ushakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ushkovo (a municiple settlement in Kurortny district, Saint Petersburg, Russia)</li></ol><p>From the surname <i>Ушко́в</i> (Uškóv). The settlement was renamed from Тюрисевя (Tjurisevja) in 1948 after Dmitrij Konstantinovič Uškov (Russian Wikipedia article), who was killed in action on the Eastern Front in World War II and awarded the title Hero of the Soviet Union post mortem. It was thought that he died near the settlement.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uelen, Whalen (coastal Yupik village in Chukotsky district, Chukotka Autonomous Okrug, Russia, in eastern Siberia, and the easternmost settlement in Russia)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Chukchi <i>увелен</i> (“thawed patch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wellington (the capital city of New Zealand); alternative spelling of <i>Веллингто́н</i> (Vellingtón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wales (a constituent country of the United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Wales</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) abbreviation of <i>ферзь</i> (ferzʹ): Q (queen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>фами́лия, и́мя, о́тчество</i> (famílija, ímja, ótčestvo): surname, first name, patronymic (<i>on a form or an ID</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Фонд борьбы́ с корру́пцией</i> (Fond borʹbý s korrúpcijej): "FBK", Anti-Corruption Foundation (was a Russian non-profit organization based in Moscow established in 2011 by activist and politician Alexei Navalny.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Федера́льное бюро́ рассле́дований</i> (Federálʹnoje bjuró rasslédovanij): FBI, Federal Bureau of Investigation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>фами́лия, и́мя, о́тчество</i> (famílija, ímja, ótčestvo): surname, first name, patronymic (<i>on a form or an ID</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>federal migration service</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>федера́льная миграцио́нная слу́жба</i> (federálʹnaja migraciónnaja slúžba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Федера́льная нало́говая слу́жба</i> (Federálʹnaja nalógovaja slúžba): Federal Tax Service (of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>FSB (successor organization to the Soviet KGB)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Федера́льная слу́жба безопа́сности Росси́йской Федера́ции</i> (Federálʹnaja slúžba bezopásnosti Rossíjskoj Federácii, “Federal Security Service of the Russian Federation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>эфэсбэ́шник</i> (efɛsbɛ́šnik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Federal Penitentiary Service in Russia (successor organization to the Soviet <i>ГУЛА́Г</i> (GULÁG) and Russian <i>ГУИ́Н</i> (GUÍN)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Федера́льная слу́жба исполне́ния наказа́ний</i> (Federálʹnaja slúžba ispolnénija nakazánij, “Federal Penitentiary Service”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>federal customs service</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>федера́льная тамо́женная слу́жба</i> (federálʹnaja tamóžennaja slúžba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Fabergé, from French (a surname)</li></ol><p>Of Huguenot French origin, having evolved since c. 17th century from <i>Favri</i>. Compare <i>Favre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Fadeyev or Fadeev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phazania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Phazānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Faina, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>φᾰεινή</i> (phăeinḗ, “bright, shining”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thales of Miletus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faroe Islands (an archipelago and self-governing autonomous territory of Denmark, in the North Atlantic Ocean between Scotland and Iceland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faroe Islands (an archipelago and self-governing autonomous territory of Denmark, in the North Atlantic Ocean between Scotland and Iceland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Farida, from Arabic</li></ol><p>From Arabic فَرِيدَة (farīda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vasmer, from German</li><li>a surname, Fasmer, from German</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fastiv (a city, the administrative centre of Fastiv Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Fateyev or Fateev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Arabic, equivalent to English Fatima</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic فَاطِمَة (fāṭima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fatimid</li></ol><p><i>Фатима</i> (Fatima) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;politics) The government of the United States of America.</li></ol><p>From <i>фаши́зм</i> (fašízm) +&lrm; <i>Вашингто́н</i> (Vašingtón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) February Revolution, traditionally dated to February 23, 1917 (Julian calendar), which is 8 March, 1917 according to the new Gregorian calendar.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Federal Security Service of the Russian Federation (successor organization to the Soviet KGB)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Federal Security Service of the Russian Federation (successor organization to the Soviet KGB)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Fedora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Fedya, of the male given name Фёдор (Fjódor), equivalent to English Theo</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Фёдор</i> (Fjódor) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Feygin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Felgengauer, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Felgenhauer</li><li>a surname from German, equivalent to English Felgenhauer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Felshtinsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Themis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Θέμῐδᾰ</i> (Thémĭdă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Feodosia (a port and resort city and raion in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Θεοδοσία</i> (Theodosía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Fesenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessaly (a historical region and modern administrative region of north-central Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fibonacci (surname)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thebes (a city in Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fiji (a country and archipelago of over 300 islands in Melanesia in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Filipov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Filipp, equivalent to English Phillip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philippines (a country and archipelago of Southeast Asia; capital: Manila)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Filippov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Finland (a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>Finland</i> (“Finland”). Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>Сумь</i> (Sumĭ, “Finland”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. explain the Latinate -ия ending if this is directly from Swedish</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Финля́ндия</i> (Finljándija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gulf of Finland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Fishman, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Fishman</li><li>a surname, Fischman, from German</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Flaminius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Flāminius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flanders (a cultural region in the north of Belgium)# Flanders (a historical county of Western Europe; in full, County of Flanders)# Flanders (two provinces in Belgian Flanders, West Flanders and East Flanders)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>Flandria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flevoland (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Florence (a city and comune, the capital of the Metropolitan City of Florence and the region of Tuscany, Italy)# Florence (a metropolitan city of Tuscany, established in 2015; in full, the Metropolitan City of Florence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Florestan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Florida (a state of the United States)</li><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Florida</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Fox</li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Fox</i></li></ol><p>Phonetic transliteration of <i>Fox</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Faulkner</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Faulkner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Foma, equivalent to English Thomas</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Thomas</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>Ѳѡма</i> (Θoma), from Ancient Greek <i>Θωμάς</i> (Thōmás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Fomin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Foma's, Thomas's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic, Fomich, meaning “son of Фома́ (Fomá)”</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ѳедоръ Ѳомичъ Петрушевскій (1828–1904), Russian physicist, professor emeritus at the Saint Petersburg Imperial University, and (from 1878) an Active State Councillor</li></ol></ol><p><i>Фома́</i> (Fomá, “Thomas”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ч</i> (-íč, “son of”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Fonarev or Fonaryov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fort Worth (a city in northeastern Texas, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fortunatus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fortūnātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Fortuné</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Franklin</i></li><li>a surname, Franklin, from English, equivalent to English Franklin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Franklin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Franconia (a cultural region and former duchy in Germany, comprising the northern area of modern Bavaria and lands to its immediate west)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Франко́ния</i> (Frankónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frankfurt, Frankfurt-am-Main (the largest city in Hesse, in central Germany)</li><li>Frankfurt, Frankfurt-an-der-Oder (a sizable town in Brandenburg, in eastern Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Frantsisk, equivalent to English Francis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Франци́ск</i> (Francísk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>France (a country located primarily in Western Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Francja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>French Guiana (an overseas department and administrative region of France in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>French Polynesia (an overseas collectivity of France in Polynesia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>French Republic (official name of <i>France</i>: a country in Western Europe)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>République française</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) French Revolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Фра́нция</i> (Fráncija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Freddy.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Freddy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Frenkel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Phrygia (a geographic region and ancient kingdom in the west central part of Asia Minor, in what is now modern-day Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from German, equivalent to English Friedman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Friedmann</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friesland (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Freetown (the capital city of Sierra Leone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friuli</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Frunze</li><li>Frunze (former name of Bishkek, capital of Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romanian <i>Frunză</i>, from <i>frunză</i> (“leaf”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fukui (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>福井</i> (ふくい, Fukui).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fukuoka (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Fukuoka (a city in Fukuoka Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>福岡</i> (ふくおか, Fukuoka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fukushima (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Fukushima (the capital city of Fukushima Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Funafuti (the capital city of Tuvalu)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fuchū (a city in Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li><li>Fuchū (a city in Hiroshima Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>府中</i> (ふちゅう, Fuchū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fujian (a province of the People's Republic of China (mainland China), located on the southeastern coast)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Nazism) Führerbunker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Führerbunker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Fyodor, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Theodore</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) Foxtrot (<i>F</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Fedorov</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Fyodor's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Fiokla, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Thecla</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Θέκλα</i> (Thékla), from θεός (theós, “god”) + κλέος (kléos, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Христо́с воскре́с</i> (Xristós voskrés, “Christ has risen”): Happy Easter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khabarovsk (a city, the administrative center of Khabarovsk Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Хаба́ров</i> (Xabárov) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk), from the surname of Yerofey Khabarov, in Russian <i>Ерофе́й Па́влович Хаба́ров-Святи́тский</i> (Jeroféj Pávlovič Xabárov-Svjatítskij), a Cossack explorer.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khabarovsk Krai (a krai of Russia in the Russian Far East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Habomai (an island of the Pacific Ocean)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>歯舞</i> (はぼまい, Habomai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Khava, from Hebrew</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (Khava).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hagåtña (the capital of Guam, a United States island territory)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Hagåtña</i>, from Chamorro <i>Hagåtña</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyderabad (the capital city of Telangana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hainan (an island and province of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khakassia (a republic and federal subject of central south Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hakodate (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>函館</i> (はこだて, Hakodate), in turn possibly borrowed from Ainu <i>ハㇰ チャシ</i> (hak casi), meaning "shallow fort".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Huxley</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Huxley</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalcedon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Khalkha Mongolia, Outer Mongolia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠬᠠᠯᠬ᠎ᠠ</i> (qalk-a). Compare modern Mongolian <i>халх</i> (xalx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hama</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hamas (a Palestinian Islamist militant group and political organization)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Iranian Persian surname خامِنِه‌ای (xâmene-i)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Swedish surname <i>Hammarskjöld</i></li></ol><p>From Swedish <i>Hammarskjöld</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Armenian surname <i>Խանջյան</i> (Xanǰyan)</li><li>a surname, Khanjian, from Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanoi (the capital city of Vietnam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khanty-Mansiysk (a town, the administrative center of the Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>ха́нты</i> (xánty, “Khanty”) +&lrm; <i>ма́нси</i> (mánsi, “Mansi”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khantia-Mansia, Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug, Yugra (an autonomous okrug of Tyumen Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanukkah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>חנוכה</i> (ḥānukkâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hangzhou (the capital city of Zhejiang, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harare (the capital city of Zimbabwe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kharatyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Խառատյան</i> (Xaṙatyan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harbin (a subprovincial city, the capital of Heilongjiang, China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>哈爾濱</i>&nbsp;/ <i>哈尔滨</i> (Hā'ěrbīn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Khariton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kharitonov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Kharlamov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haarlem (the capital city of North Holland, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Harris</i></li></ol><p>Transliteration of English <i>Harris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khartoum (the capital city of Sudan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kharkiv, Kharkov (a city in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hachijō (one of 4 subprefectures of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>八丈</i> (はちじょう, Hachijō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Бори́с Исаа́кович Хаце́т (1921–2008), mathematician</li></ol><li>a transliteration of the Lithuanian surname <i>Chacetas</i>.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>О. Хацет (1920–?), Lithuanian professional chess player who represented the Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic at the 1st Soviet Team Chess Championship in 1948</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khachanov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of Armenian <i>Խաչանյան</i> (Xačʻanyan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khachaturyan, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Adaptation of Armenian <i>Խաչատրյան</i> (Xačʻatryan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Хезболла́</i> (Xezbollá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hezbollah (a Lebanese Shi'a Islamist political party and paramilitary group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حِزْب اللّٰه (ḥizb allāh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Хезболла́</i> (Xezbollá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helsinki (the capital city of Finland)</li><li>(politics,&#32;metonymic) the government of Finland.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>Helsinki</i>, itself derived from Swedish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Sanskrit female given name <i>हेमा</i> (hemā)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chemnitz (a city in Saxony)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Chemnitz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heerlen (a city in Limburg, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kherson (a city, the administrative center of Kherson Oblast, in southern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>'s-Hertogenbosch (the capital city of North Brabant, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chilon of Sparta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Spanish, equivalent to English Gil</li><li>a Russian surname, Khil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hino (a city in Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>日野</i> (ひの, Hino).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kumamoto (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>兵庫</i> (ひょうご, Hyōgo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chios (an island in the eastern Aegaean Sea)</li><li>Chios (a regional unit of Greece)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Χῐ́ος</i> (Khĭ́os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hirado (a city in Nagasaki Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>平戸</i> (ひらど, Hirado).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hirosaki (a city in Aomori Prefecture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>弘前</i> (ひろさき, Hirosaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hiroshima (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Hiroshima (the capital city of Hiroshima Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>広島</i> (ひろしま, Hiroshima).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hiyama (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>檜山</i> (ひやま, Hiyama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Khlebnikov</li></ol><p>From <i>хлеб</i> (xleb) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khmilnyk (a city, the administrative center of Khmilnyk Raion, Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khmelnytskyi (a city, the administrative center of Khmelnytskyi Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>a surname, Khmelnytsky or Khmelnitsky, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hobart (the capital city of Tasmania, Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khovansky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khodorkovsky</li></ol><p>From <i>Хо́дорков</i> (Xódorkov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hokkaido (an island of Japan)</li><li>Hokkaido (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>北海道<rp>(</rp><rt>ほっかいどう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>Hokkaidō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hokuto (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>北斗</i> (ほくと, Hokuto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kholodov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homs (a city, the capital of the governorate of Homs, in western Syria)# Homs (a governorate in western Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Хомс</i> (Xoms).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Japanese automotive manufacturer.</li><li>a surname from Japanese</li></ol><p>Derived from Japanese <i>本田</i> (Honda) or Japanese <i>ホンダ</i> (Honda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Honiara (the capital city of the Solomon Islands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Honshu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>本州</i> (ほんしゅう, honshū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khorasan, Khurasan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Хораса́н</i> (Xorasán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croatia (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>From <i>хорва́т</i> (xorvát) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Хорва́тия</i> (Xorvátija, “Croatia”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A nickname borne by Ива́н Мстисла́вич, князь Кара́чевский (Iván Mstislávič, knjazʹ Karáčevskij, “Ivan Mstislavich, prince Karachevsky”, <i>c.</i> 1370–1417), whence the princes Khotetovsky took their name.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a The capital city of the princes Khotetovsky (identified with Bolshaya Slobodka).</li><li>Chotětov (a market town in Mladá Boleslav District, Central Bohemian Region, the Czech Republic, first mentioned in 1057)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Czech <i>Chotětov</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khotetova (a hamlet in Koptevskoye rural settlement, Znamensky Raion, Oryol Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khatsyetava (a hamlet in Harbavichy sielsaviet, Chavusy Raion, Mahilyow Oblast, Belarus, first mentioned in 1619)</li><li>Khotetovo (a village in Yamskoye rural settlement, Bolkhovsky Raion, Oryol Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia, founded in or before the fourteenth century)</li><li>Khotetovo (a hamlet in Yakovlevskoye rural settlement, Sverdlovsky Raion, Oryol Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia, first mentioned in 1696)</li><li>Khotetovo (a hamlet in Voskresenskoye municipal formation, Dubensky Raion, Tula Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia, founded in the fifteenth century, first mentioned in 1616)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a princely surname, Khotetovsky</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Князь (“Prince”) Ива́н Степа́нович Хотето́вский (17th C.–18th C.): twenty-third–generation Rurikid from the Khotetovsky princely family; stolnik, voivode, and okolnichy during the reign of Tsar Fyodor III Alekseyevich Romanov (1676–1682), regency of Tsarevna Sophia Alekseyevna Romanova (1682–1689), and reign of Tsar-Emperor Pyotr I Alekseyevich Romanov (1682–1725)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a historical polity in Eastern Europe, one of the Upper Oka Principalities, existing <i>p.</i>&nbsp;1383–<i>p.</i>&nbsp;1490 (<i>no English name for the state is attested</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Khokhlov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “khokhol”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól) +&lrm; <i>-ляндия</i> (-ljandija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ho Chi Minh City (the largest city in Vietnam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Khristina, equivalent to English Christina</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Χριστίνα</i> (Khristína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Христо́с</i> (Xristós).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khromykh</li></ol><p><i>хромой</i> (xromoj) +&lrm; <i>-ых</i> (-yx)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khrushchev, particular Nikita Khrushchev (April 17, 1894, to September 11, 1971) (First Secretary of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union following the death of Joseph Stalin, presiding from 1953 to 1964)</li></ol><p>From <i>хрущ</i> (xrušč, “May beetle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;Internet slang) Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>хрю</i> (xrju, “oink, sound of a pig”) +&lrm; <i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína). Russian jokes often stereotype Ukrainians as eating salo (salted pig fat); after the War in Donbas and the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine, in Russian nationalist discourse, this portrayal progressed to a dehumanizing association of enemy Ukrainian soldiers or Ukrainians in general with pigs (as opposed to merely pig-eaters).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Mandarin surname <i>胡</i> (Hú)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 胡 (<i>Hú</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yellow River, Huang He (a major river in northern China, passing through Qinghai, Sichuan, Gansu, Shaanxi, Shanxi, Henan and Shandong provinces as well as Ningxia and Inner Mongolia Autonomous Regions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hubei (a province in central China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Isle of Youth (an island of Cuba)</li></ol><p>Spanish <i>(Isla de la) Juventud</i> (literally “Isle of Youth”), from Spanish <i>juventud</i> (“youngness; the youth”, abstract noun and adjectival noun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khujand (a city, the capital of Sughd, Tajikistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Khudik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory&#32;or&#32;self-deprecatory,&#32;politics) Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>хуйло́</i> (xujló, “asshole; Putin”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan”). See Putin khuylo for more information on "хуйло́ (xujló)" becoming a slang term referring to Vladimir Putin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hulagu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hunan (a province of China, located in the middle reaches of the Yangtze River and south of Lake Dongting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khust (a city, the administrative center of Khust Raion, Zakarpattia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hohhot (the capital city of the Inner Mongolia autonomous region)</li></ol><p>From Mongolian <i>ᠬᠥᠬᠡᠬᠣᠲᠠ</i> (kökeqota)/<i>Хөх хот</i> (Xöx xot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Hubert</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Hubert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hebei (a province in northern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heilongjiang (a province in northeastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Хеллоуи́н</i> (Xɛllouín)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Henan (a province of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>целева́я аудито́рия</i> (celevája auditórija, “target audience”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Центра́льно-Африка́нская Респу́блика</i> (Centrálʹno-Afrikánskaja Respúblika, “Central African Republic”): CAR</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulp and paper mill</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>целлюло́зно-бума́жный комбина́т</i> (celljulózno-bumážnyj kombinát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>центра́льная дире́кция пассажи́рских обустро́йств</i> (centrálʹnaja dirékcija passažírskix obustrójstv): Central Directorate of Passenger Facilities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Центра́льная Избира́тельная Коми́ссия</i> (Centrálʹnaja Izbirátelʹnaja Komíssija): Central Election Commission</li><li>acronym of <i>Центра́льный исполни́тельный комите́т</i> (Centrálʹnyj ispolnítelʹnyj komitét): Central Executive Committee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>central committee</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Центра́льный комите́т</i> (Centrálʹnyj komitét, “Central committee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>центра́льный проце́ссор</i> (centrálʹnyj procéssor): central processor</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) child pornography, CP</li></ol><p>From English <i>CP</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Центра́льное разве́дывательное управле́ние</i> (Centrálʹnoje razvédyvatelʹnoje upravlénije): CIA, Central Intelligence Agency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) initialism of <i>Центра́льный спорти́вный клуб а́рмии</i> (Centrálʹnyj sportívnyj klub ármii, “Central Sports Club of the Army”) (a professional football team in Moscow, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Tsarin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tsaritsyn (the Russian city which was renamed Stalingrad in 1925 and then Volgograd in 1961)</li><li>a surname, Tsaritsyn</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Цари́ца</i> (Caríca, “Tsaritsa river, now called Pionerka”) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in, “'s”). The river's name itself is from Turkic <small>[Term?]</small> (Sary-su), meaning "Yellow Water".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) Constantinople</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Царьградъ</i> (Carĭgradŭ). By surface analysis, <i>царь</i> (carʹ, “emperor”) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Tsaryov or Tsarev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tsaghkadzor (a town, a spa town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ծաղկաձոր</i> (Caġkajor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsvetaev or Tsvetayev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsvetkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsvetov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine (as a pro-European nation)</li></ol><p>From the Ukrainian phrase Украї́на це Євро́па (Ukrajína ce Jevrópa, “Ukraine is Europe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ceylon (former name of <i>Sri Lanka</i>: an island country in South Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ceylon</i>, <i>Zeylon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Celsius (surname)</li><li>Celsius, centigrade (temperature unit)</li></ol><p>A surname. Named after Swedish&#32;astronomer&#32;Anders Celsius&nbsp;(1701–1744) in the case of the unit of temperature.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Central America (a continental region in North America, consisting of the countries lying between Mexico and South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Central African Republic (a country in Central Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Central Intelligence Agency, CIA</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(astronomy,&#32;inanimate) Ceres, dwarf planet</li><li>(Roman mythology,&#32;animate) Ceres</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Zetkin; particularly, German Marxist theorist, advocate for women’s rights Clara Zetkin.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zetkin</i>. Through morphophonological analysis.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jilin (a province of China)# Jilin, Jilin City (a prefecture-level city of Jilin, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jiangxi (a province in southern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jiangsu (a province of China, northeast of the mouth of the Yangtze River)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Qing; the Qing dynasty (the last dynasty of China, lasting from 1644 to 1912 <small>C.E.</small>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>清</i> (Qīng)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>清</i> (Qīng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qingdao (a major subprovincial city in eastern Shandong, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qinghai (a province in northwestern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cincinnati (a city in Ohio, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Cincinnati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsiolkovsky, from Polish, equivalent to Polish Ziółkowski, particularly Konstantin Tsiolkovsky an Imperial Russian and Soviet rocket scientist and pioneer of the astronautic theory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Ziółkowski</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cicero</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Цицеро́н</i> (Cicerón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsoi, Tsoy, Choi, Choy, or Choe, from Korean</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>최(崔)</i> (Choe), assimilated in Russian in relation to Koreans living in the former USSR.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tsolak, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ցոլակ</i> (Cʻolak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Црвенковски</i>&nbsp;m (Crvenkovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Црвенкоски</i>&nbsp;m (Crvenkoski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Цхинва́ли</i> (Cxinváli)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tskhinvali (the capital city of South Ossetia, a de-facto independent country internationally recognized as part of the country of Georgia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ცხინვალი</i> (cxinvali).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common-gender Buryat surname, Tsybigon, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠴᠡᠪᠡᠭ ᠦᠨ</i> (čebeg-ün), genitive of <i>ᠴᠡᠪᠡᠭ</i> (čebeg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tsyhanenko or Tsyganenko, from Ukrainian, equivalent to Ukrainian Цигане́нко (Cyhanénko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male Buryat surname, Tsyrenov, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Цыре́н</i> (Cyrén, Mongolian name of Tibetan origin) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov, possessive suffix for patronymic surnames).</p><p>From Classical Mongolian <i>ᠴᠡᠷᠢᠩ</i> (čering), from Classical Tibetan <i>ཚེ་རིང</i> (tshe ring, “longevity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zürich (the capital of Zürich canton, and the largest city in Switzerland)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of German <i>Zürich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>FAQ, frequently asked questions</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ча́сто задава́емые вопро́сы и отве́ты</i> (částo zadavájemyje voprósy i otvéty, “frequently asked questions and answers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private military company, PMC</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>ча́стная вое́нная компа́ния</i> (částnaja vojénnaja kompánija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) abbreviation of <i>что́ за хуйня́</i> (štó za xujnjá): WTF, "what the fuck?"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheka (<i>forerunner of the KGB</i>)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Чрезвыча́йная коми́ссия</i> (Črezvyčájnaja komíssija), short for <i>Всеросси́йская чрезвыча́йная коми́ссия</i> (Vserossíjskaja črezvyčájnaja komíssija, “All-Russian Extraordinary Commission”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;often&#32;humorous) abbreviation of <i>чу́вство со́бственной ва́жности</i> (čúvstvo sóbstvennoj vážnosti, “feeling of self-importance”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) initialism of <i>члены семьи изменника Родины</i> (členy semʹi izmennika Rodiny), family members of political prisoners and defectors during World War II in the Soviet Union</li></ol><p>The full form of this term, "члены семьи изменника Родины", literally means "members of families of traitors to the Motherland".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Chaadayev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Tchaikovsky, from Polish; particularly, Pyotr Tchaikovsky, Russian composer of the Romantic period</li></ol><p>Russian variant of the Polish surname <i>Czajkowski</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chambarak (a town in Gegharkunik Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Ճամբարակ</i> (Čambarak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Çanakkale (a city, district, and province of Turkey)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>Çanakkale</i>, from Ottoman Turkish چناق قلعه (Çanak Kalʼe, literally “Fortress of Pots”), so named from the great manufacture of coarse earthenware carried on in and around the town.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Sanskrit unisex given name <i>चन्द्र</i> (candra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Changchun (the capital of Jilin, China)</li></ol><p>From Chinese <i>長春</i>&nbsp;/ <i>长春</i> (Chángchūn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Changsha (a prefecture-level city, the provincial capital of Hunan, in south-central China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>長沙</i>&nbsp;/ <i>长沙</i> (Chángshā)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chanyshev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chapayev or Chapaev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charentsavan (a town in Kotayk Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Չարենցավան</i> (Čʻarencʻavan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Romanian surname <i>Ceaușescu</i></li></ol><p>From Romanian <i>Ceaușescu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, equivalent to English Chebaturkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheboksary (the capital and largest city of Chuvashia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheburashka (a character in Soviet children's literature and the protagonist of an animated film), Topple (a former English translation).</li></ol><p>Jocular formation, from <i>чебура́хнуться</i> (čeburáxnutʹsja, “to topple”) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška).</p><p>Attested in Vladimir Dal's <i>Explanatory Dictionary of the Live Great Russian language</i> &lsqb;1882&rsqb; as a generic noun synonymous to <i>ванька-встанька</i> (vanʹka-vstanʹka, “roly-poly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jeju (a province of South Korea)</li><li>Jeju (an island of South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>제주도</i> (jejudo) (<i>濟州道</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>제주도</i> (jejudo) (<i>濟州島</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spider-Man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chelyabinsk (an oblast of Russia, near the Ural Mountains)</li><li>Chelyabinsk (a city, the administrative center of Chelyabinsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Чэнду́</i> (Čɛndú, “Chengdu”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chennai (a major city, the capital of Tamil Nadu, India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Częstochowa (a city in southern Poland, on the Warta river, with 240,027 inhabitants as of June 2009)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chervonohrad (a city, the administrative center of Chervonohrad Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Chervochkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Cherkasov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cherkasy (a city, the administrative center of Cherkasy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cherkessk (the capital city of the Karachay-Cherkess Republic, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>черке́с</i> (čerkés, “Circassian”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chernenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chernihiv&#47;Chernigov (a city, the administrative center of Chernihiv Oblast, in northern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chernov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city, the administrative centre of Chernivtsi Oblast in the Bukovyna region, Ukraine: Chernivtsi</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>Чернівці́</i> (Černivcí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montenegro (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>Calqued from the original Serbo-Croatian name of the country Црна Гора; <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Черного́рия</i> (Černogórija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Black Sea region</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chernomyrdin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chernykh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chernyshevsky; particularly, a Russian literary and social critic, journalist, revolutionary democrat, materialist philosopher, and socialist Nikolay Chernyshevsky, 24 July 1828 – 17 October 1889</li></ol><p>Derived from the name of the village of <i>Чернышёво</i> (Černyšóvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Chernyshov or Chernyshev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, equivalent to English Cherniak or Chernyak</li></ol><p>May in some cases be of Ukrainian, Jewish or Polish origin. By surface analysis, <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak). Compare Polish <i>Czerniak</i> and Ukrainian <i>Черняк</i> (Černjak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Czech Republic, Czechia (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chekhov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Czechoslovakia (a former country in Central Europe, now the Czech Republic and Slovakia; Carpathian Ruthenia, at the eastern end, became a part of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Че́хия</i> (Čéxija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chechnya (a republic and federal subject of Russia, in the northern Caucasus)</li></ol><p>Of Northwest Caucasian origin, from East Circassian <i>шашан</i> (šašan, “Chechen”). The term was formerly thought to come into use after being associated with <i>Chechen</i> <i>Aul</i>, a mountainous area where the Chechens defeated a Russian army contingent in 1732, but use of the republic's name and its appearance on maps predate this.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Czech Republic (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jalainur (a city in Inner Mongolia, China)</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>Далайнор</i> (Dalajnor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhejiang (a province in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chiang Mai (the capital city of Chiang Mai Province, Thailand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chicago (a major city in Illinois, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Chicago</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Shymkent (old name used before 8 September 1992)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>Chimkent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Genghis Khan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chirikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chistopol (a town in Tatarstan, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chita (a city, the administrative center of Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia in Siberia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Chicherin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jongno (a district of Seoul, South Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean 종로구 (鍾路區, Jongnogu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chortkiv (a city, the administrative center of Chortkiv Raion, Ternopil Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Chubais or Chubays</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chuvashia (a republic and federal subject of Russia, located in Eastern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>чува́ш</i> (čuváš, “Chuvash”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Чува́шия</i> (Čuvášija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chuhuiv (a city, the administrative center of Chuhuiv Raion, Kharkiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lake Chudskoye, Lake Peipsi (lake in Pskov Oblast, Russia and in Estonia, part of the Peipus lake system)</li></ol><p>Literally, “Chud lake”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chukotka (a peninsula and region of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chukchi Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chongqing (a direct-administered municipality and city in western China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>重慶</i>&nbsp;/ <i>重庆</i> (chóngqìng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Churapcha (a town in Yakutia)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Yakut <i>Чурапчы</i> (Čurapčï).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) synonym of <i>Чуркиста́н</i> (Čurkistán)</li></ol><p><i>чу́рка</i> (čúrka, “slur for a Central Asian person”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan, land of”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) A Central Asian country.</li></ol><p><i>чу́рка</i> (čúrka, “slur for a Central Asian person”) +&lrm; <i>-стан</i> (-stan, “-stan, land of”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chukhloma (a city, the administrative center of Chukhlomsky district, Kostroma Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chengdu (a subprovincial city, the provincial capital of Sichuan, China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>成都</i> (Chéngdū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Black Sea</li></ol><p>Calque of Ottoman Turkish قره دكز (Kara Deñiz); see Black Sea for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) 1993 Russian constitutional crisis, dated to October 4, 1993 according to the Gregorian calendar.</li></ol><p>Literally, “Black October”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solitary confinement cell</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ш(трафно́й) изо(ля́тор)</i> (š(trafnój) izo(ljátor), literally “punishment isolation cell”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shaveddinov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shadrin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shainsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaillot (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Chaillot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shalamov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Humpty Dumpty (fairy tale character)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shalyapin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shamlugh (a village in Lori Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Շամլուղ</i> (Šamluġ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shanghai (a direct-administered municipality and major port city in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shandong (a coastal province in eastern China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shanxi (a province in the northern part of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shapovalov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shaposhnikov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sharapov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Sharlotta, from French, equivalent to English Charlotte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Charlotte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charlottetown (a city in Prince Edward Island)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French male given name <i>Charles</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Charles</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Shakhla</li></ol><p>Likely from Persian شهلا (šahlâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shakhnazarov, from Armenian</li></ol><p>Russification of the Armenian <i>Շահնազարյան</i> (Šahnazaryan), by substituting the Armenian ending <i>-յան</i> (-yan) with Russian <i>-ов</i> (-ov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Shakhriyar or Shahryar, from Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian شَهْرْیَار (šahryār).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shvedov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Switzerland (a country in Western Europe and Central Europe)</li></ol><p>Attested since 1701, from Polish <i>Szwajcaria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Швейца́рия</i> (Švejcárija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sweden (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>A literary borrowing from Medieval Latin <i>Suecia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Шве́ция</i> (Švécija).: Sweden (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shebekino (a city in Belgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Georgian surname <i>შევარდნაძე</i> (ševardnaʒe)</li><li>a surname, Shevardnadze, from Georgian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shevchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shevchuk, from Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Шевчу́к</i> (Ševčúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in Russia. The city Cherepovets is located on the lowest parts of this river.</li><li>a town located on this river</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Shakespeare</i></li><li>Shakespeare (English playwright)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Shakespeare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Shenderovich, from Yiddish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shepetivka (a city, the administrative center of Shepetivka Raion, Khmelnytskyi Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sheremetyevo (a famous airport in Moscow, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shestakov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Hinduism) Shiva, the Hindu god of destruction, time, dance, and eternity.</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>शिव</i> (śiva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the song "Venus" by Shocking Blue.</li></ol><p>Mondegreen, based on the first lines of the song's chorus: "<i><b>She's got it!</b> Yeah, baby, she's got it!</i>"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shikotan (an island in the Kurils)</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>色丹</i> (Shikotan), from Ainu <i>シコタン</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shirak (a province of Armenia)</li><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Chirac</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Շիրակ</i> (Širak).</p><p>From French <i>Chirac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Schleswig-Holstein (a state of Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schleswig-Holstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shlisselburg (a town in Kirovsky district, Leningrad Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlüsselburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Shlosberg, from Yiddish</li><li>a surname from German, equivalent to English Schlossberg or German Schlossberg or Schloßberg</li><li>Schlossberg (previously spelled Schloßberg in German) (various place names in Germany, Austria, Switzerland, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shmelyov or Shmelev</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Shoigu, from Tuvan</li><li>a common-gender patronymic surname, Shoigu, from Tuvan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tuvan <i>Шойгу</i> (Şoygu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sholokhov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shostakovich, from Belarusian; particularly Dmitri Shostakovich, Russian composer and pianist</li></ol><p>A rare Belarusian patronymic surname: <i>Шоста́к</i> (Šosták) +&lrm; <i>-о́вич</i> (-óvič), where <i>шоста́к</i> (šosták, “sixth”) is a nickname for the sixth child in the family.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shostka (a city in Shostka Raion, Sumy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scotland (a constituent country of the United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schottland</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shpanberg (an island in the Nordenskiöld Archipelago in the Kara Sea)</li><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;disputed</i>) synonym of <i>Шикотан</i> (Šikotan), Shikotan, an island in the Kurils</li></ol><p>From <i>Шпанберг</i> (Španberg) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), from Martin Spanberg, a Danish naval officer who served as one of Vitus Bering's lieutenants in the early Russian exploration of the Pacific Ocean. The suffix provides agreement with an implicit <i>остров</i> (ostrov, “island”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shpaniv (a village in Shpaniv hromada, Rivne Raion, Rivne Oblast)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shpilkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Svalbard, Spitsbergen (an archipelago, territory, and unincorporated area of Norway northeast of Greenland, in the Arctic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte (the administrative capital of Sri Lanka, located next to the executive and judicial capital of Colombo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sri Lanka (an island and country in South Asia, off the coast of India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Schröder</i></li><li>a transliteration of the German or English surname <i>Schroeder</i></li></ol><p>From German <i>Schröder</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>Соединённые Шта́ты Аме́рики</i> (Sojedinjónnyje Štáty Amériki)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>States</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stuttgart (a city in Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shukshin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Shulgin, originating as a patronymic, feminine equivalent Шульги́на (Šulʹgína)</li></ol><p><i>Шу́льга</i> (Šúlʹga) +&lrm; <i>-и́н</i> (-ín)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female surname, Shulgina, masculine equivalent Шульги́н (Šulʹgín)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Jewish surname, Shulman, from Yiddish, equivalent to English Schulman</li><li>a surname from German, equivalent to German Schulman or Schulmann</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Shura, of the male given name Алекса́ндр (Aleksándr), equivalent to English Alex</li><li>a diminutive, Shura, of the female given name Алекса́ндра (Aleksándra), equivalent to English Alex</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Сашу́ра</i> (Sašúra), a dated diminutive of <i>Са́ша</i> (Sáša), itself a clipping of <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) or <i>Алекса́ндра</i> (Aleksándra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Shurik, of the male given names Алекса́ндр (Aleksándr) or Олекса́ндр (Oleksándr)</li></ol><p><i>Шу́ра</i> (Šúra) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), from a clipping of <i>Сашу́ра</i> (Sašúra), a dated diminutive of <i>Са́ша</i> (Sáša), from a clipping of <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shymkent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Шымкент</i> (Şymkent). Replaced <i>Чимке́нт</i> (Čimként) from 8 September 1992.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shaanxi (a province of China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shenzhen (a major subprovincial city in southern Guangdong, China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shenyang (the capital of Liaoning, China)</li></ol><p>From Chinese <i>瀋陽</i>&nbsp;/ <i>沈阳</i> (Shěnyáng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shchedrin, particularly Rodion Shchedrin, a Russian composer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Shchekochikhin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;humorous) the national anthem of Ukraine.</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;humorous,&#32;Internet slang) Ukraine.</li></ol><p>Derived from Ukrainian <i>Ще не вмерла України і слава, і воля</i> (Šče ne vmerla Ukrajiny i slava, i volja, “Ukraine's glory and will has not yet died”), the first line in Ukraine's national anthem.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Szczecin (the capital and largest city of West Pomeranian Voivodeship, in what is now Poland; Stettin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Szczytno (a town in Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship, Poland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Shchukin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shchuchinsk (a city in Aqmola, Kazakhstan)</li></ol><p>From <i>Щу́чье</i> (Ščúčʹje, literally “Pike Fish Lake”) +&lrm; <i>-инск</i> (-insk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ygyatta river in Sakha (Yakutia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ynykchan, Ynykchansky (a former town in the Sakha Republic, Russia, abandoned in 2008)</li></ol><p>From Yakut <i>Ыныкчаан</i> (Ïnïkčaan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) computer</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>электро́нно-вычисли́тельная маши́на</i> (elektrónno-vyčislítelʹnaja mašína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>электрокардиогра́мма</i> (elektrokardiográmma): (<i>cardiology</i>) electrocardiogram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>экстракорпора́льное оплодотворе́ние</i> (ekstrakorporálʹnoje oplodotvorénije, “in vitro fertilization”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) initialism of <i>Энциклопедический слова́рь Брокга́уза и́ Ефро́на</i> (Enciklopedičeskij slovárʹ Brokgáuza í Jefróna, “Brockhaus and Efron Encyclopedic Dictionary”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ebetsu (a city in Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>江別</i> (えべつ, Ebetsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ebola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euboea (an island and regional unit of Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Everest.</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;also lowercase, эверест) Everest; summit.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eurydice</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Εὐρυδίκη</i> (Eurudíkē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euripides (a Greek tragedian)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Εὐριπίδης</i> (Euripídēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aigaleo (a city and municipality of West Athens regional unit, Attica, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from German, equivalent to German Eggert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegina (island)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Edik, of the male given names Эдуа́рд (Eduárd) or Едуа́рд (Jeduárd), equivalent to English Ed or Eddie</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Эдуа́рд</i> (Eduárd) + <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edinburgh (the capital city of Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oedipus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Οἰδίπους</i> (Oidípous).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edirne (a city and province of Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edmonton (the capital city of Alberta, Canada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Edo (former name of Tokyo)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>江戸<rp>(</rp><rt>えど</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>Edo</i>, literally “cove entrance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edogawa (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>江戸川</i> (えどがわ, Edogawa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Eduard, from English, equivalent to English Edward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aesop (ancient Greek author)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Αἴσωπος</i> (Aísōpos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Abel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Abraham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Eisenhower</i></li><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Eisenhauer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from German, equivalent to German Eisenstein</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>Eisenstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eindhoven (a city in North Brabant, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Einstein (Albert Einstein)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>Einstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Airbus (a European aircraft manufacturer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eiffel Tower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ecuador (a country in South America)</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>Ecuador</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Equatorial Guinea (a country in Central Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elagabalus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Alvin</i></li><li>a male given name from Old English, equivalent to English Alvin</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Elvin</i></li><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Elvin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Alvin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elektrozavodskaya (Moscow metro stations on the Arbatsko–Pokrovskaya line and Bolshaya Koltsevaya line)</li><li>Elektrozavodskaya (a street)</li></ol><p>From <i>электрозаво́д</i> (elektrozavód, “electric factory”) +&lrm; <i>-ская</i> (-skaja). From a project name and an electric trust.</p><p>The newest, colloquial stress comes from the re-analyzed using the compound <i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>заводска́я</i> (zavodskája) with loose attachment of morphemes. Whereas the stress <i>заводска́я</i> (zavodskája) comes from the obsolete stress <i>заво́дская</i> (zavódskaja). Also, there can be no banalization process with a hypothetical re-analysis in the form of <i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>заво́д</i> (zavód) +&lrm; <i>-ская</i> (-skaja), because the wordform <i>заво́д</i> (zavód) already has a final stress; compare with <i>великово́зрастный</i> (velikovózrastnyj, “overage”), where the word form <i>во́зраст</i> (vózrast) has an initial stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Eleonora, from Italian, equivalent to English Eleanor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Eleonora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Elis, from English, equivalent to English Alice</li><li>a transliteration of the English female given name <i>Alice</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elista (the capital city of Kalmykia, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Ella</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;endonym</i>) Hellas, Greece (a country in Southeast Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>Ελλάδα</i> (Elláda) or Ancient Greek <i>Ἑλλᾰ́δᾰ</i> (Hellắdă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elohim (the name used for God in Hebrew scriptures)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אֱלֹהִים &#47; אֱלוֹהִים</i> (elohím).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laayoune (the capital and largest city of Western Sahara)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>El Niño (a global coupled ocean-atmosphere phenomenon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>El Salvador (a country in Central America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elbe (a river in the Czech Republic and Germany in Central Europe)</li><li>Elba (an island of Tuscany, Italy in the Tyrrhenian Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elbrus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Spanish, equivalent to English Elvira</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>Elvira</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Eldar</li><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) Eldar, a race of elves from the writings of Tolkien.</li><li>(<i>science fiction</i>) Eldar, a race of elf-like aliens from <i>Warhammer 40,000</i> now known as "Aeldari".</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>Eldar</i></p><p>Borrowed from English <i>Eldar</i>, from the fictional language Quenya.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alsace (a cultural region, former administrative region and historical province of France; since 2016, part of the administrative region of Grand Est)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emesa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἔμεσα</i> (Émesa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Emiliya, from Latin, equivalent to English Emily</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aemilia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Emil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Emma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Empedocles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Engels, from German, particularly, the German philosopher, historian, political scientist, one of the founders of Marxism and revolutionary socialist Friedrich Engels, 28 November, 1820, to 5 August 1895</li><li>Engels (a city in Saratov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Endryu, from English, equivalent to English Andrew</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Andrew</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the <i>Aeneid</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aenēis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aeneas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin Latin <i>Aenēās</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enerhodar (a city in Vasylivka Raion, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>эне́ргия</i> (enérgija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дар</i> (dar), literally “gift of the coal”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Enn, from English, equivalent to English Ann</li><li>a transliteration of the English female given name <i>Ann</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Ann</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enschede (a city in Overijssel, Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Entoni, from English, equivalent to English Anthony</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Anthony</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aeolus (character in Greek mythology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epictetus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Эпикте́т</i> (Epiktét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epicurus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apple (Apple Inc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Riyadh (the capital city of Saudi Arabia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Turkish surname <i>Erdoğan</i></li></ol><p>From Turkish <i>Erdoğan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erebuni</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erzurum (the capital city of Erzurum Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) the name of the city Yerevan in Russian transcription till 1936</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old Armenian <i>Երևան</i> (Erewan). Compare dialectal Armenian <i>Էրևան</i> (Ērewan) and Ottoman Turkish اروان (Erivan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eris</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἔρις</i> (Éris).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eritrea (a country in East Africa, on the Red Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermitage (museum)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ermitage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eros</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἔρως</i> (Érōs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;humorous,&#32;Internet slang) Russia.</li></ol><p>Derived from РФ (RF), an acronym for Росси́йская Федера́ция (Rossíjskaja Federácija, “Russian Federation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eswatini (a country in Southern Africa, formerly called Swaziland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aesculapius (Roman god)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Essonne (a department of the Île-de-France region, France)</li><li>Essonne (a tributary of the Seine River in France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Essonne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Estemirov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Estonia (a country in northeastern Europe, on the southeastern coast of the Baltic Sea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>Estonia</i>. Compare also <i>Эстля́ндия</i> (Estljándija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of Estonia (official name of <i>Estonia</i>: a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Estonian SSR (a former republic of the Soviet Union, existing from 1940 to 1991)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Эсто́ния</i> (Estónija).: Estonia (a country in northeastern Europe, on the southeastern coast of the Baltic Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esther (biblical figure)</li><li>a female given name, Esfir, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Esther</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἐσθήρ</i> (Esthḗr), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֶסְתֵּר</i> (ʾestēr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeschylus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aetolia (a region of Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephialtes</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ἐφῐάλτης</i> (Ephĭáltēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopia (a country in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ахетато́н</i> (Axetatón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ehime (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>愛媛</i> (えひめ, Ehime).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Эфио́пия</i> (Efiópija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Ю́жно-Африка́нская Респу́блика</i> (Júžno-Afrikánskaja Respúblika, literally “South-African Republic”), official name of <i>South Africa</i>: a country in Southern Africa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Southeast Asia</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>Ю́го-Восто́чная А́зия</i> (Júgo-Vostóčnaja Ázija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>UNESCO</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>UNESCO</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Southeast Asia (a geographic region of Asia, comprising the territories of Brunei, Cambodia, East Timor, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar (Burma), the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, and Vietnam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Yugoslavia (a former country in Southeast Europe in the Balkans, now split into 6 countries: Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, North Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia, and Slovenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>Jugoslavija</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yudenich, from Belarusian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, equivalent to English Yudin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Asia (a geographic region of Asia, comprising all the countries of the Indian subcontinent (principally India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, and Bhutan) and the Indian Ocean (principally Sri Lanka, Maldives))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South America (the continent forming the southern part of the Americas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Africa (a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Holland (a province of the Netherlands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Dakota (a state in the Upper Midwest region of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Carolina (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Korea (a country in East Asia; capital: Seoul)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Ossetia (a geographic region and de-facto independent but largely unrecognized country in the South Caucasus on the border of Europe and Asia; considered part of Georgia's territory by the vast majority of the international community)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Republic of South Africa (official name of <i>South Africa</i>: a country in Southern Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South China Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk (a city, the administrative centre of Sakhalin Oblast, on Sakhalin Island, in the Far East of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>ю́жный</i> (júžnyj, “southern”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, connecting vowel) +&lrm; <i>Сахали́н</i> (Saxalín, “Sakhalin”) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i> (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yuzhne (a city in Odesa Raion, Odesa Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yuzhnoukrainsk (a city in Mykolaiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>southern Vietnam</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) South Vietnam (a former country in Southeast Asia that governed Vietnam south of the 16th parallel from 1955 to 1975)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Sudan (a country in Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Southern Ocean (the portion of the World Ocean surrounding Antarctica, variously taken as the waters south of the Antarctic Circle, the waters south of 60°&nbsp;S, or the waters bounded by the Antarctic Convergence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yulian, equivalent to English Julian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yuliana, equivalent to English Juliana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yuli, equivalent to English Julius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iulius</i>. Cognates with Belarusian <i>Ю́лій</i> (Júlij), Bulgarian <i>Юлий</i> (Julij), Polish <i>Juliusz</i>, Ukrainian <i>Ю́лій</i> (Júlij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Julia, from Latin, equivalent to English Julia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Yulya, of the female given name Ю́лия (Júlija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Young Army Cadets National Movement, Yunarmiya</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ю́ная а́рмия</i> (júnaja ármija, literally “the Young Army”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Unicode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Unicode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Juno (equivalent to Greek Hera)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iūnō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yunnan (a province in southwestern China, bordering Myanmar (Burma), Laos and Vietnam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Yura, of the male given name Ю́рий (Júrij)</li><li>Yura (a river in Russia)</li><li>Jura (a mountain range on the border of France and Switzerland, extending into Germany; in full, Jura Mountains)</li></ol><p>Possibly from German <i>Jura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yuri or Yury, equivalent to English George</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Гюрги</i> (Gjurgi), vernacular form of <i>Георгий</i> (Georgij). Doublet of <i>Георгий</i> (Georgij) and <i>Егор</i> (Jegor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yurii's, Jurij's, Yuri's</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A surname</li></ol><p><i>Ю́рий</i> (Júrij) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from English, equivalent to English Eustace</li></ol><p>From English <i>Eustace</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yusupov</li></ol><p><i>Юсуп</i> (Jusup) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Utah (a state in the western United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Yushenkov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yushchenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) nuclear weapons</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>я́дерное ору́жие</i> (jádernoje orúžije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Java (an island of Indonesia, the world's most populous island)</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) Java (programming language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yavlinsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yavoriv (a city, the administrative center of Yavoriv Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yadviga or Jadwiga, from Polish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Jadwiga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Yazov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Ясо́н</i> (Jasón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yakov, equivalent to English Jacob or James</li><li>Я (<i>ya</i> in the Russian spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάκωβος</i> (Iákōbos), from Hebrew <i>יעקב</i> (<i>ya'aqov</i>, heel-grabber), from <i>עקב</i> (<i>‘akev</i>, heel of the foot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yakovenko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yakutia, Sakha Republic (a republic and federal subject of eastern Russia; official name: Респу́блика Саха́ (Яку́тия) (Respúblika Saxá (Jakútija)))</li></ol><p>From <i>яку́т-</i> (jakút-, “Yakut”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija, “-ia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yakutsk (the capital city of the Sakha Republic, also known as Yakutia in Russia)</li></ol><p>From Middle Russian <i>Ꙗ҆́кꙋцкои҆́</i> (Ja҆́kuckoi҆́). By surface analysis, <i>яку́т-</i> (jakút-, stem of <i>яку́ты</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim&nbsp;pl (jakúty, “Yakuts”)) +&lrm; <i>-ск</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;sg (-sk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yalta (a resort city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yalutorovsk (a town in Tyumen Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamagata (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Yamagata (the capital city of Yamagata Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>山形</i> (やまがた, Yamagata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamaguchi (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Yamaguchi (the capital city of Yamaguchi Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>山口</i> (やまぐち, Yamaguchi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jamaica (an island and country in the Caribbean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamal (a large peninsula and a region in the Russian North)</li><li>Yamal (Russian nuclear-powered icebreaker)</li><li>Yamal (a communication and broadcasting system developed and operated by Gazprom Space Systems)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamanashi (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>山梨</i> (やまなし, Yamanashi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Yamato (ancient name for Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大和</i> (Yamato).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iamblichus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Я́мвлих</i> (Jámvlix).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yampil (a city in Vinnytsia Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamuna, Jumna (a river in the states of Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh and Haryana and union territory of Delhi, India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>यमुना</i> (yamunā), from <i>यम</i> (yama, “twin”), since the Yamuna runs in parallel with the Ganges.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yamoussoukro (the capital city of Ivory Coast)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yana</li><li>a river in the Sakha Republic, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yangon, Rangoon (the capital of Yangon Region and the largest city in Burma)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Burmese <i>ရန်ကုန်</i> (rankun /⁠jàɴɡòʊɴ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yandex (Russian Internet company)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) Yandex (the largest search engine in Russia and which popularized the company of the same name)</li></ol><p>The name initially stood for “Yet Another iNDEXer”. The Russian word <i>я</i> (ja) corresponds to the English personal pronoun “I”, making <i>Яndex</i> a bilingual pun on <i>index</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname from Serbo-Croatian</li></ol><p>Serbo-Croatian <i>Janković, <i>Јанковић</i></i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yankovsky, equivalent to Polish Jankowski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yanukovych or Yanukovich</li></ol><p>A Ukrainian/Belarusian/Russian patronymic surname: <i>Яну́к</i> (Janúk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вич</i> (-óvič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Janus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yangtze or Chang Jiang</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Japan (a country and archipelago of East Asia)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from New Latin <i>Iaponia</i>. By surface analysis, <i>япо́н</i> (japón) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Japanese Orthodox Church</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sea of Japan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Япо́ния</i> (Japónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Jordan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Yarilo (Slavic god of spring and plants)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jaro</i> (“spring”) or Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarъ</i> (“strong, fierce”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yarmolnik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yarmysh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yaropolk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Ꙗропълкъ</i> (Jaropŭlkŭ), equivalent to <i>я́рый</i> (járyj, “ardent, violent”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полк</i> (polk, “regiment”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yaroslav</li><li>Jarosław (a city, villages in southeastern Poland)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Jaroslavъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́рый</i> (járyj, “ardent, violent”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́в(а)</i> (sláv(a), “fame, glory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yaroslava</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yaroslavl (a city, the administrative center of Yaroslavl Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Ꙗрославль</i> (Jaroslavlĭ, adj., literally “Yaroslav's”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Jason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Yastrebov</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yaoundé (the capital city of Cameroon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jaffa (a port city in western Israel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Yasha, of the male given name Я́ков (Jákov), equivalent to English Jake</li><li>(computing,&#32;Internet) <i>nickname for</i> Yandex (<i>calque, Russian slang</i>)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Я́ков</i> (Jákov) + <i>-ша</i> (-ša, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, Yashin, originating as a patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>Я́ша</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim (Jáša) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i>&nbsp;m (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>А</i>/<i>а</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>A</i>/<i>a</i>.</li><li><i>The sound expressed by the letter A</i>.</li><li>The first item in a list of similar items.</li><li>The first item among those numbered with the same number as a pre-existing one.</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, and, while, although (deviates from the preceding idea in some manner&#59; introduces a new or different meaning, as opposed to <i>но</i> (no), which usually indicates contrarity)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh! (expressing surprise or realization)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>colloquial particle used to seek confirmation: eh</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) he, she or it (used to point to a particular person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>а</i> (a, “and, but”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;a</i> (“and, but”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ōd</i> (presumed to be the ablative of <i>&#42;éy, h₁e</i>). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>õ</i> (“and, but”).</p><p>Possible link with Lithuanian <i>ar̃</i> (“question particle”). Compare Russian <i>а</i> (a, “and, but”), <i>и</i> (i, “and”) and <i>да</i> (da, “and, but”) with Lithuanian <i>ar̃</i> (“if, whether”), <i>ir̃</i> (“and”) and <i>dár</i> (“still, yet”).</p><p>Compare Lithuanian <i>à</i>, <i>õ</i>, <i>oà</i> (“expression of pain, surprise”), as well as Gothic <i>𐍉</i> (ō, “ah!”), Latin <i>ā</i>, <i>āh</i> (“expression of pain, anger”), Ancient Greek <i>ἆ</i> (â, “ah!”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: А)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A, ampere</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>ампер</i> (amper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>although, nevertheless, nonetheless</li></ol><p>Idiomatic use of <i>а</i> (a, “but, and”) + <i>ведь</i> (vedʹ, “as, because”). Perhaps related to Carpathian Rusyn <i>авадь</i> (avadʹ, “or”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sure!, of course!</li><li>what about, how about? (<i>used to ask someone to consider something or someone that they apparently have not considered</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>а</i> (a, “and”) +&lrm; <i>как</i> (kak, “how”) +&lrm; <i>же</i> (že, “emphatic/protesting particle”) with ellipsis of <i>ина́че</i> (ináče, “otherwise”), i.e. "how else?".</p><p>Same as above, but with no ellipsis.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a cappella (performed by a choir with no instrumental accompaniment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) why the fuck</li></ol><p>From <i>а</i> (a) + <i>на хуя</i> (na xuja). Literally, “and/but on [the] dick”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, and now, but now <i>(beginning a sentence or phrase to indicate a change or contrast from the foregoing)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) other than that</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> а (a),&lrm; так (tak).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as well as (and in addition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>otherwise, or else</li><li>because, since</li><li>and/but in fact, and/but in reality</li><li>(with <i>и</i> (i),&#32;in parenthetical remarks) if not, or even, and even, or indeed</li><li>(<i>introductory</i>) Used at the beginning of a remark containing a polite wish, offer or persuasion.</li><li>(<i>introductory</i>) on second thoughts (used to indicate agreement after a refusal or hesitation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ah, aah, oh (expressing understanding or realisation)</li><li>aah (a cry of horror, the sound of screaming)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>а́-а</i> (á-a)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>a la</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>à la</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffet with light refreshments eaten standing</li></ol><p>From French <i>à la fourchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhinebarge, a single-masted flat-bottomed barge used on the Rhine for wine transportation.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>aak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aam, an old Dutch and German unit of measure for liquids.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>aam</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Kamchatka) long-tailed duck <i>(<i>Clangula hyemalis</i>)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Itelmen <i>аʼӈичӽ</i> (a'ngičch), of onomatopoeic origin. Cognate to Tundra Nenets <i>ӈаʼʼӈо</i> (nga"ngo, “pochard, long-tailed duck”) and Nivkh <i>ауӈк</i> (auŋk, “type of duck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Aaron's (<i>in collocations</i>)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ааронов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aaron's</li></ol><p><i>Ааро́н</i> (Aarón) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aaron's rod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) follower of Aaron's confession</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aaron's confession (minor confession of Russian Old Believers which split from the Filippians in the second half of the 18th century)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) Aaron's confession</li></ol><p>From <i>ааро́нов</i> (aarónov, “Aaron's (confession)”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aaron's confession, a minor group of old believers in the second half of the 18th century</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aba, a coarse woven cloth</li><li>a cloak-like garment made from aba</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Omsk) elder sister</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Omsk) father's sister</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Ural) Well, I never! (expression of surprise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish عبا (abâ) (Turkish <i>aba</i>), from Arabic عَبَاءَة (ʕabāʔa).</p><p>Limited spread suggests Bashkir <i>аба</i> (aba, “expression of surprise, indignation”), Chuvash <i>апа</i> (ap̬a, “expression of surprise, indignation”).</p><p>From dialectal Tatar <i>аба</i> (aba). According to Anikin, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;aba</i> (“father, bear”), Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;aba</i> (“father”) &gt; Mongolian <i>ав</i> (av). See also <i>а́ва</i> (áva), <i>апа</i> (apa).</p><p>Also compare Old East Slavic <i>абба</i> (abba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) abat-vent (strips of wood or metal used as windbreaks, particularly in belfries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abat-vent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) abat-voix (an overhang over a pulpit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abat-voix</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) malevolent spirit (among the Yakut people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native or inhabitant of Abadan (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Абада́н</i> (Abadán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female equivalent of <i>абада́нец</i> (abadánec); female native or inhabitant of Abadan</li></ol><p><i>Абада́н</i> (Abadán) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from or relating to Abadan</li></ol><p>From <i>Абада́н</i> (Abadán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lampshade</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a visor, worn on the forehead to protect one's eyes from the sun</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) head</li><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) order, norm (pun on <i>ажу́р</i> (ažúr))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abat-jour</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lampshade</li></ol><p>From <i>абажу́р</i> (abažúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>абажу́р</i> (abažúr): small lampshade</li></ol><p><i>абажу́р</i> (abažúr) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) abbasi, abazi (a Persian and Georgian coin, worth around twenty kopeks)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;obsolete) A twenty kopek coin.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ingush <i>аьпӏаьз</i> (äpʼäz) or Chechen <i>эппаз</i> (eppaz), from Georgian <i>აბაზი</i> (abazi), from Persian عباسی ('abbâsi, “20 dinar coin”); see that entry for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea,&#32;Caucasus,&#32;vulgar,&#32;dated) a stupid infidel</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea,&#32;Caucasus) a type of horse in the Caucasus</li><li>a strong wind in the Black Sea coming from the Caucasus region</li></ol><p>All meanings most likely derive from the ethnonym <i>абази́ны</i> (abazíny, “Abazins”). The meaning of stupid infidel was borrowed through Turkish <i>abaza</i> (“stupid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abaza, Abazin (member of the Abaza people living in the northwest Caucasus)</li></ol><p>18th century, from <i>абазы</i> (abazy, “Abazins”) + suffix <i>-ин</i> (-in), denoting nationality. From Abaza <i>абаза</i> (abaza, “Abazins, Abkhaz”).</p><p>Cognate with the Abkhaz endonym <i>аԥсуа</i> (apswa), West Circassian <i>абадзә</i> (“Abazins”), Turkish <i>abaza</i> (“Abkhaz”), also perhaps Ancient Greek <i>Ἀψῑλαι</i> (Apsīlai, “Apsilae”) and Old East Slavic <i>обезы</i> (obezy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Abaza (a people living in the northwest Caucasus)</li></ol><p><i>абази́н</i> (abazín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abasia</li></ol><p>Adaptation of German <i>Abasie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abacus (board for doing calculations)</li><li>abacus (uppermost member of a column)</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed through French <i>abaque</i>. See <i>абака</i> (abaka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) abacus (uppermost member of the capital of a column)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) abacus (cupboard for holding cups, bottles, etc.)</li><li>abaca, Manila hemp</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἄβαξ</i> (ábax, “board for drawing, calculating”).</p><p><i>Аба́ка</i> (Abáka), in its culinary sense, originally referred to a perforated board for holding cups (compare cupboard, from cup + board).</p><p>Ultimately from Tagalog <i>abaká</i>. Compare Spanish <i>abacá</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Абака)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Persian, equivalent to English Abaqa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;historical</i>) abacost (style of jacket, promoted in Zaïre by Mobutu Sese Seko)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abacost</i>, a contraction of <i>à bas</i> <i>le</i> <i>costume</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) abactinal (on the side opposite to the mouth in animals with radial symmetry)</li></ol><p>Ultimately a New Latin construction from <i>ab-</i> (“without”) + <i>ἀκτίς</i> (aktís, “ray”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a small crowbar for breaking through doors</li></ol><p>From the patronimic <i>Абаку́мыч</i> (Abakúmyč), from the given name <i>Абакум</i> (Abakum), <i>Аввакум</i> (Avvakum), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀββακούμ</i> (Abbakoúm), from Hebrew <i>חֲבַקּוּק</i> (khavakúk, “Habakkuk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abalone (edible univalve mollusc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;humorous) Expression of annoyance or frustration.</li></ol><p>Coined by Sergei Dovlatov in his book <i>Наши</i> (<i>Naši</i>, "Ours"). Apparently a child's misinterpretation of the vulgarism <i>ёбана мать</i> (jóbana matʹ, “motherfucker”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) abandonment (the relinquishment of a property by the insured)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abandon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abandonment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person from Aban of Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Аба́н</i> (Abán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Baikal) kaluga (<i>Huso dauricus</i>) (predatory sturgeon).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Buryat <i>абарга</i> (abarga, “large, powerful”); <i>абарга загаһан</i> (abarga zagahan, “mythical fish”). From Mongolian <i>аварга</i> (avarga, “gigantic, enormous”), from Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;aburγa</i> (“large: of fish, serpents”). Kaluga may be the largest freshwater fish in the world.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abarognosis (loss of the ability to judge the weight of objects)</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>abarognosis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) abaton (a part of a temple in Ancient Greece which it was forbidden to enter.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄβατον</i> (ábaton), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “tread”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abachi, obeche (an African hardwood of the tree <i>Triplochiton scleroxylon</i>).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>абаша́</i> (abašá)</li><li>alternative form of <i>аба́чи</i> (abáči)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Muslim cleric; Mullah &lsqb;17th c.&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to <i>бакша</i> (bakša, “elder amongst Kalmyk priests”), from Kalmyk <i>бакшы</i> (bakşy, “shaman”). From Old Chinese <i>博士</i> (&#42;paːɡ zrɯʔ, “teacher, mentor”) &gt; Mandarin <i>博士</i> (bóshì, “doctor, academician”). The suffix <i>-ша</i> (-ša) is apparently from <i>士</i> (shì, “scholar”), or <i>師</i>&nbsp;/ <i>师</i> (shī, “tutor”). Compare <i>тайша</i> (tajša, “Kalmyk leader, elder”).</p><p>The origin of <i>аба-</i> (aba-) is unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>аба́</i> (abá, “coarse woven cloth”)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative spelling of <i>аба́</i> (abá, “interjection of surprise”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abbasi, a Persian coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Abbasid</li></ol><p><i>Аббаси́д</i> (Abbasíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) abbot, priest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>abbate</i>, or Middle High German <i>abbāt</i>, from Latin <i>abbās</i> (“abbot”), oblique stem <i>abbat-</i>. Further (via Ancient Greek <i>ἀββα</i> (abba) and Aramaic <i>אבא</i>) from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔabw-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) abbot, priest</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) abbey</li></ol><p><i>абба́т</i> (abbát, “abbot”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) abbey</li></ol><p><i>абба́т</i> (abbát, “abbot”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation (shortened or contracted form of a word or phrase)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>abbreviatura</i>, from Late Latin <i>abbrevio</i> (“to shorten”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation (the process of abbreviating)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) abbreviation (loss of ancestral ontogenetic pattern during evolution).</li></ol><p>See <i>аббревиату́ра</i> (abbreviatúra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abbreviate; to reduce a word or phrase by means of contraction or omission to a shorter recognizable form</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Abwehr (German military intelligence service from 1921 to 1944)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Abwehr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upholder (rope)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) abhomotopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abdal (a sect of Persian monks)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a fraud, crook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ابدال (abdal, aptal, “pauper, Dervish”), whence Turkish <i>aptal</i> (“idiot”); see for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Abderite, citizen of the Ancient Greek city Abdera.</li><li>a naive or stupid person</li></ol><p>From <i>Абдеры</i> (Abdery, “Abdera”), the citizens of which had a reputation for being simple-minded. Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄβδηρα</i> (Ábdēra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) abdest, wudu (Islamic procedure of ablution)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آبدست (âbdest), from Classical Persian آبدست (ābdast).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abjad (writing system)</li></ol><p>From Arabic أَبْجَد (ʔabjad); see English <i>abjad</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdication</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>abdicatiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abdomen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abdōmen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abdominal</li></ol><p>French <i>abdominal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abdominohysterotomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abdominohysterectomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abdominopericardiostomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdominoplasty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abdominoscopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Kazan,&#32;Orenburg) a fright, fear</li></ol><p>Apparently from Turkic, whence also Kazakh <i>абыржыған</i> (abyrjyğan), Tatar <i>аптыраган</i> (aptırağan, “confused, bewildered”), <i>аптырау</i> (aptıraw, “mystify, be confused”), Chuvash <i>аптӑрат</i> (aptărat, “confuse, torment, frustrate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abductor, one who abducts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abductor muscle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abduction (movement of a limb away from the midline of the body)</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) abduction (form of argument)</li><li>alien abduction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abductio</i>, from <i>abducere</i> (“to lead away”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) alphabet, ABC</li></ol><p>Constructed from the modern names of the first four letters of the Russian alphabet, <i>а</i> (a), <i>бэ</i> (bɛ), <i>вэ</i> (vɛ) and <i>гэ</i> (gɛ). Compare <i>а́збука</i> (ázbuka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) alphabet, ABC</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) hepatitis</li></ol><p>From the names of the first five letters of the Russian alphabet, <i>а</i> (a), <i>бэ</i> (bɛ), <i>вэ</i> (vɛ), <i>гэ</i> (gɛ) and <i>дэ</i> (dɛ) + suffix <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare <i>а́збука</i> (ázbuka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) abelian</li></ol><p>Name of the Norwegian mathematician Niels Henrik Abel + possessive adjectival suffix <i>-ев</i> (-ev).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) abelian group</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) abelian ring, commutative ring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commutativity</li></ol><p><i>а́белев</i> (ábelev) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abelianization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abelite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abelia (a genus in the honeysuckle family)</li></ol><p>From the genus name <i>Abelia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Abelmoschus</i> (genus in the mallow family)</li><li>abelmosk (the plant <i>Abelmoschus moschatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From the genus name, translingual <i>Abelmoschus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aberrant (deviating from the ordinary or natural type&#59; exceptional&#59; abnormal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) aberrator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aberrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) aberration (the distortion of an image from optical devices)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) aberration (deviation from the norm or reality)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) aberration (change of position in the stars)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) aberration (atypical development in structure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Aberration</i>, or French <i>aberration</i>. Both from Latin <i>aberrātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) abaisse (pastry used for non-food purposes in cooking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abaisse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abessive case</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>abesse</i> (“to be absent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abessive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>abessive</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abessive (the abessive case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) abetalipoproteinemia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an adaptation of the Polish alphabet used periodically in Ukraine in the 17th-19th centuries.</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) the musical alphabet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>abecadło</i> (“alphabet”) of 18th century, from Old Polish <i>obiecadło</i> adapted with suffix <i>-dło</i>. From Middle High German <i>abecede</i>. From the first four letters of the Latin alphabet. Compare Latin <i>abecedārium</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>абеца́дло</i> (abecádlo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) abecedary, abecedarium (book to help learn the alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abécédaire</i> attested 1791, under influence of older <i>абецедариум</i> (abecedarium), from the Latin <i>abecedārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) paragraph</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) an indentation at the start of a paragraph</li><li>(<i>euphemism</i>) for <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Absatz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) euphemism for <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc).</li></ol><p>From the name of the Ukrainian punk-rock group Абздольц (<i>Abzdolʹc</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>building</i>) stacker (large machine used to stockpile bulk materials)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Absetzer</i>, from <i>absetzen</i> (“set down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abzyme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) at once, straight away &lsqb;11th c.&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>абие</i> (abije, “now, suddenly”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>абиѥ</i> (abije, “now, immediately”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;abьje</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>अह्नाय</i> (ahnāya, “instantly, at once”), Ancient Greek <i>ἄφαρ</i> (áphar, “at once”), <i>ἄφνω</i> (áphnō, “suddenly”), possibly from a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;abʰ-</i>.</p><p>Compare also Old Novgorodian <i>абьно</i> (abĭno, “if, when”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) abient (characterised by avoidance or withdrawal)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>abiens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) abietane (a diterpene hydrocarbon)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>abies</i> (“silver fur”), from which the substance is extracted.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abietic (containing abietane)</li></ol><p><i>абиети́н</i> (abijetín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) ability, special ability</li></ol><p>English <i>ability</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) abiogenesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abiosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abioseston</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abiota</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abiotrophy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abiophysiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abiocoen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) abyssal zone, abyssopelagic zone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abyssal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abyssal, abyssopelagic</li></ol><p><i>абисса́ль</i> (abissálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Abyssinian (citizen of Abyssinia)</li></ol><p><i>Абисси́ния</i> (Abissínija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abyssinian cat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;historical) Abyssinian (of or pertaining to Ethiopia or its inhabitants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Time spent taking university entrance examinations</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>абитурие́нт</i> (abiturijént, abituriént). with final <i>-a</i> by analogy with other nouns ending in <i>-тура</i> (-tura). Compare German <i>Abitur</i>.</p><p>Compare also collective suffixes <i>-я́та</i> (-játa) (e.g. <i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta)), <i>-ота́</i> (-otá) (e.g. <i>школота</i> (školota), <i>гопота</i> (gopota)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graduate, school-leaver</li><li>university applicant, enrollee, candidate for a university entrance examination. &lsqb;from mid 20 c.&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Abiturient</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) school-leaver, university entrant</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) graduation (from school)</li></ol><p><i>абитурие́нт</i> (abiturijént, abituriént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) school-leaver, university entrant</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) graduation (from school)</li></ol><p><i>абитурие́нт</i> (abiturijént, abituriént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablactation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablactation, weaning</li></ol><p><i>аблакти́ровать</i> (ablaktírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ablactate, to wean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablactation, weaning</li></ol><p><i>аблакти́ровать</i> (ablaktírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) ablastics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ablativus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>абляти́вный</i> (abljatívnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) ablaut, gradation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ablaut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) ablepharia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) ablepharon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablutomania</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ablativus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ablative (<i>in various senses</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablative case (grammar case used to indicate movement away from something, removal, separation, source)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablaut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ablation; ablative</li></ol><p><i>абля́ция</i> (abljácija) +&lrm; <i>-онный</i> (-onnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(meteorology,&#32;geography) ablation (removal of snow or ice)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) ablation (removal of a part of biological tissue)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) ablation (removal of material from a solid surface)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ablātiō</i> (“removal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abmigration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abolitionism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>abolitionism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abolitionist</li></ol><p>From English <i>abolitionist</i>, early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abolitionist (relating to abolitionism or abolitionists)</li></ol><p><i>аболициони́ст</i> (abolicioníst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abolition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abohm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subscription, season ticket, membership (such as to a gym)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abonnement</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subscription, loan</li><li>prepaid</li></ol><p><i>абонеме́нт</i> (abonemént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subscriber</li></ol><p>From German <i>Abonnent</i>, either directly or perhaps via Polish <i>abonent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subscriber; subscriber's</li></ol><p><i>абоне́нт</i> (abonént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subscriber; subscriber's</li></ol><p><i>абоне́нт</i> (abonént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to subscribe (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) boarding (attacking another ship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abordage</i>, from French <i>aborder</i> + <i>-age</i> (&lt; Latin <i>-āticum</i>). Further from French <i>a-</i> (&lt; Latin <i>ad-</i>) + French <i>bord</i> (&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdą</i>, whence also English <i>board</i>; Dutch <i>boord</i>, German <i>Bord</i>, whence Russian <i>борт</i> (bort)) + French <i>-er</i> (&lt; Latin <i>-āre</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boarding (<i>of a ship</i>)</li></ol><p><i>аборда́ж</i> (abordáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aborigine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>aborīginēs</i>, from Latin <i>ab</i> <i>orīgine</i> (from the beginning).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aboriginal, indigenous, local, native, native-born</li></ol><p><i>абориге́н</i> (aborigén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abortion</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) abort</li></ol><p>From German <i>Abort</i>, itself derived from Latin <i>abortiō</i>.</p><p>In the computing sense: from English <i>abort</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;medicine) abortive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abort forceps</li></ol><p>From German <i>Abortzange</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrasive (substance used for cleaning, smoothing, or polishing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abrasif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrasiveness</li></ol><p><i>абрази́вный</i> (abrazívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abrasive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrasion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsense, gibberish</li><li>abracadabra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abracadabra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abrek</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a native of the Caucasus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from North Caucasian languages, specifically West Circassian <i>абрэгь</i> (abrɛgʲ) (standart Adyghe <i>абрэдж</i> (abrɛdž)). The term is widely distributed across the Caucasus; compare Ossetian <i>абырӕг</i> (abyræg), Chechen <i>обург</i> (oburg), Georgian <i>აბრაგი</i> (abragi). Compare Armenian <i>ավազակ</i> (avazak, “robber”). V.I. Abaev suggests the ultimate origin is Middle Persian <i>&#42;āparak</i> (“robber”), related to <i>āpartan</i> (“to rob”) and <i>āpar</i> (“robbery”). This implies a connection to Persian آواره (âvâre, “vagabond, wanderer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apricot (<i>tree or fruit</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;vulgar) testicles, balls, nuts, nads, gonad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>abrikoos</i>, from Middle French <i>abricots</i>, from Catalan <i>albercoc</i>, from Arabic اَلْبَرْقُوق (al-barqūq). Further from Latin <i>praecox</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>абрико́совый</i> (abrikósovyj, “apricot (<i>relational, color</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>абрико́с</i> (abrikós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) apricot</li><li>apricot (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>абрико́с</i> (abrikós) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apricot brandy, apricot schnapps, crème d'abricot, apricotine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contour, outline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Abriss</i> (“outline”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>а́брис</i> (ábris).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrogation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abrogare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) ejective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ABS plastic</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>акрилонитрил бутадиен стирол</i> (akrilonitril butadijen stirol) + <i>пла́стик</i> (plástik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) absence seizure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>absence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absinth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>absinthe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absenteeism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absinthism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apse, apsis, exedra</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἁψίς</i> (hapsís).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute, something absolute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>absolutus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolutisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a making of something absolute</li></ol><p><i>абсолютизи́ровать</i> (absoljutizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to absolutise, to make absolute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolutism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>absolutisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolutist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absolutist</li></ol><p><i>абсолюти́ст</i> (absoljutíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute value (numerical value of a real number)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute humidity (mass of water vapour in a given volume of air)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute monarchy (rule)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>absolutely</li></ol><p><i>абсолю́тный</i> (absoljútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute majority (a true numerical majority, more than 50% of the items being counted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absoluteness</li></ol><p><i>абсолю́тный</i> (absoljútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absolute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>absolutny</i>, from Latin <i>absolūtus</i> (“unrestricted, unconditional”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute zero</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) absolutive, absolutive case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfect pitch (ability to identify a note by name on hearing it, or to re-create a specified note from memory, without the benefit of an externally provided reference pitch)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>oreille absolue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorbate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorbent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorbent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption</li></ol><p><i>абсорби́ровать</i> (absorbírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to absorb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>absorbēre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorbable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorbing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorptive (able to absorb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorption, absorptive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>absorbēre</i>, from <i>ab</i> + <i>sorbeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstentionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstainer, one who abstains</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) abstinence; abstinent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withdrawal symptoms, withdrawal</li></ol><p>Literally, “abstinence syndrome”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstinence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstraction</li></ol><p><i>абстраги́ровать</i> (abstragírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abstract</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, Latin <i>abstrahō</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abstractus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) abstract algebra (a branch of mathematics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun (a noun that denotes an abstract concept)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstractedness, abstractiveness</li></ol><p><i>абстра́ктный</i> (abstráktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abstract</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abstractus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract verb (type of verb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstractionism, abstract art</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstractionist, abstract artist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abstract, relating to abstractionism or abstractionists</li></ol><p><i>абстракциони́ст</i> (abstrakcioníst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstraction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>abstraction</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absurdity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absurdism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absurdist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absurdity</li></ol><p><i>абсу́рдный</i> (absúrdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absurd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abscess</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>abscessus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) abscisic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;relational) abscission; abscisic</li></ol><p>English <i>abscis(ic)</i> +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) abscissa, absciss</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>abscissus</i> (p.p.p. of <i>abscindō</i>). At the PIE level from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeyd-</i> (an extension of <i>&#42;skey-</i>, an extension of <i>&#42;sek-</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>odcięta</i> (calqued from French).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abugida (a syllabary-like writing system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) abuse (violation of the rules of or misuse of a service for personal gain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>abuse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;medicine,&#32;rare) abuse, overuse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aboulia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀβουλία</i> (aboulía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abkhazian, Abkhaz person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>აფხაზი</i> (apxazi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Abkhazia; Abkhazian</li></ol><p><i>Абха́зия</i> (Abxázija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so that</li><li>if only</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;often in the negative) it doesn't matter (<i>what/which/who/how</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aby</i>. By surface analysis, <i>а</i> (a) +&lrm; <i>бы́</i> (bý)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) abuse (physically, psychologically, financially or otherwise, typically against a loved one)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;figuratively,&#32;Internet slang) abuse (violation of the rules of an Internet service)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;LiveJournal) clipping of <i>абью́з-тим</i> (abʹjúz-tim); abuse team (team of moderators combatting rule violations on LiveJournal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>abuse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) abuser (one who shows physical, psychological, financial or other abuse)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>abuser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;relational) abuser, abuser's, typical/characteristic of an abuser</li></ol><p><i>абью́зер</i> (abʹjúzer) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) abusive (physically, psychologically, financially or otherwise)</li></ol><p><i>абью́з</i> (abʹjúz) +&lrm; <i>-и́вный</i> (-ívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to abuse (physically, psychologically, financially or otherwise)</li></ol><p><i>абью́з</i> (abʹjúz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) woof, arf (sound a dog makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) pfp, profile pic, user pic</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>авата́р</i> (avatár, “avatar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) aval, bank guarantee, surety</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aval</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vanguard, avant-garde, advance guard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avant-garde</i> (“vanguard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avant-gardist, avant-garde artist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avant-garde (innovative, pioneering, especially when extremely or obviously so)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avant-garde</li></ol><p><i>аванга́рд</i> (avangárd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forebay, outport, forechamber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outer harbour, outport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) outpost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avant-poste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advance (<i>of money</i>), prepayment, payment on account</li><li>(figurative,&#32;in the plural) advances, overtures</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ава́нс</i> (aváns)</li></ol><p><i>ава́нс</i> (aváns) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advance</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) to advance money (to somewhere)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) advance (of money), prepayment</li></ol><p><i>ава́нс</i> (aváns) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up front, in advance (esp. of payments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proscenium, forestage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gamble (risky, uncertain and occasionally successful event or action)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) adventure, venture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aventure</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;adventūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adventurism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aventurine (quartz)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adventurist, gambler (someone who takes excessive risks); an unscrupulous person</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) adventurer (someone who enjoys adventures)</li></ol><p>From French <i>aventuriste</i> or <i>aventurier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) adventure; adventurous</li><li>risky</li></ol><p><i>авантю́ра</i> (avantjúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Avar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avar (person)</li></ol><p><i>ава́р</i> (avár) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crash landing, emergency landing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) emergency, crash</li></ol><p><i>ава́рия</i> (avárija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency exit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage, breakdown, fault, failure, crash (<i>rarely</i>)</li><li>accident, crash, wreck, emergency (situation caused by damage, breakdown or failure)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) misfortune</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>avaria</i>, from Arabic عَوَارِيَّة (ʕawāriyya, “damaged goods”), from عَوَار (ʕawār, “fault, blemish, defect, flaw”), from عَوِرَ (ʕawira, “to lose an eye”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Avar</li></ol><p><i>ава́р</i> (avár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) avatar (a digital representation of person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>avatar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avatar (the earthly incarnation of a deity, particularly Vishnu)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) avatar</li></ol><p><i>авата́р</i> (avatár) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy) Abba</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>а́вгуст</i> (ávgust) or <i>а́вгуста</i> (ávgusta, “August”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augur, soothsayer (diviner who foretells events by unusual occurrences)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>augur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>August</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>авъгустъ</i> (avŭgustŭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>Αὔγουστος</i> (Aúgoustos), from Latin <i>Augustus</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Август)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augustus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>august (<i>of a monarch</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>augustus</i> +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij); formally a superlative.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) August</li></ol><p><i>а́вгуст</i> (ávgust) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avenue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avenue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) obverse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avers</i>, from Latin <i>adversus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Avestan (relating to the Avesta and its language)</li></ol><p><i>Аве́ста</i> (Avésta) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>air-, aero-</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>авиа́ция</i> (aviácija, “aviation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airbase, airforce base (military airport)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>ба́за</i> (báza)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviation fuel, jet fuel</li></ol><p>From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>бензи́н</i> (benzín), an abbreviation of <i>авиацио́нный бензи́н</i> (aviaciónnyj benzín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airplane ticket</li></ol><p>From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>биле́т</i> (bilét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) wing (air force organization between the levels of an air division and a group)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>брига́да</i> (brigáda)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial horizon (an instrument indicating orientation)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-, “air-”) +&lrm; <i>горизо́нт</i> (gorizónt, “horizon”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) air group, air squadron</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airplane engine</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гатель</i> (dvígatelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air division</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>диви́зия</i> (divízija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) flight, squadron</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>звено́</i> (zvenó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane crash, aircraft accident</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>катастро́фа</i> (katastrófa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) airline (company that flies airplanes)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>компа́ния</i> (kompánija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envelope (for airmail)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>конве́рт</i> (konvért).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) air command</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>ко́рпус</i> (kórpus)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airliner (large, multi-passenger aircraft)</li></ol><p>Partial calque of English <i>airliner</i>; see <i>авиа-</i> (avia-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airline (company that flies airplanes)</li><li>(<i>used in names of airlines, including translation of foreign companies</i>);... <i>Air</i>,... <i>Airlines</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air route, airway</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-, “air-”) +&lrm; <i>ли́ния</i> (línija, “line”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air route</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>магистра́ль</i> (magistrálʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air route</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>маршру́т</i> (maršrút)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) air route</li></ol><p><i>авиамаршру́т</i> (aviamaršrút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;nautical) aircraft carrier, seaplane tender</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;aviation) airborne aircraft carrier, mother ship</li></ol><p>First attested in 1923. From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>ма́тка</i> (mátka, “womb”); an inexact calque of German <i>Flugzeugmutterschiff</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerial model</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>модель</i> (modelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aerial model</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airstrike, air raid, aerial attack (attack by military aircraft)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-, “air-”) +&lrm; <i>налёт</i> (naljót, “raid”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aircraft carrier, flattop</li></ol><p>First attested in 1927. From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), an inexact calque of German <i>Flugzeugträger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shipborne</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>авиацио́нный отря́д</i> (aviaciónnyj otrjád, “air detachment”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air force detachment, air detachment</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>отря́д</i> (otrjád)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) air carrier</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>перево́зчик</i> (perevózčik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air regiment (<i>equivalent to U.S. group or air wing</i>)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>полк</i> (polk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) airmail</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>по́чта</i> (póčta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) airmail</li></ol><p><i>авиапо́чта</i> (aviapóčta) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) airline (company that flies airplanes) (<i>more common in the USSR of a state-owned airline</i>)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>предприя́тие</i> (predprijátije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviation industry</li></ol><p>From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>пром</i> (prom), abbreviation of <i>авиацио́нная промы́шленность</i> (aviaciónnaja promýšlennostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aviation industry</li></ol><p><i>авиапро́м</i> (aviapróm) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) air traffic, air connection</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>сообще́ние</i> (soobščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;aviation) airman, aviator, pilot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aviator</li></ol><p><i>авиа́тор</i> (aviátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airstrike (attack by military aircraft)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-, “air-”) +&lrm; <i>уда́р</i> (udár, “strike, hit”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviation college</li></ol><p>From <i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>учи́лище</i> (učílišče, “specialized school, college”). Compare Ukrainian <i>авіаучи́лище</i> (aviaučýlyšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) abbreviation of <i>общество друзей авиационной и химической обороны и промышленности</i> (obščestvo druzej aviacionnoj i ximičeskoj oborony i promyšlennosti, “Society of the Friends of Air and Chemical Defense and Industry”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airbase (military airport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air show (a public display of stunt flying, aerobatics, and historical aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aviation, aircraft</li></ol><p><i>авиа́ция</i> (aviácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviation</li><li>air force</li><li><i>(collectively):</i> aircraft</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aviation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strategic aviation, long-range aviation</li><li>long haul aircraft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air show (a public display of stunt flying, aerobatics, and historical aircraft)</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>шо́у</i> (šóu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air squadron</li></ol><p><i>авиа-</i> (avia-) +&lrm; <i>эскадри́лья</i> (eskadrílʹja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>commerce</i>) letter of advice</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) aviso, advice-boat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviculture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>avicultūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aulos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avocado</li><li>avocado tree</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>avocado</i> or Spanish <i>avocado</i>, from Spanish <i>aguacate</i>, from Nahuatl <i>āhuacatl</i> (“testicle, avocado”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) avocado</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) perhaps, maybe, possibly, may still, might yet (<i>low certainty</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blind trust in divine providence; blind faith in sheer luck; counting on a miracle</li><li>faith in serendipity; counting on pure luck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>авосе</i> (avose), from <i>а</i> (a, “but”) +&lrm; <i>осе</i> (ose, “here”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shopping net, string bag</li></ol><p><i>аво́сь</i> (avósʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), referring to the notion of having a bag one one's person in case one happened to see something good for sale, i.e. a “maybe bag”; attested early 1920s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) work involving all hands</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hectic/frantic rush, all-out effort <i>(to get a job done)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>overal</i>. Attested since the early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;relational) emergency</li><li>urgent</li></ol><p><i>авра́л</i> (avrál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) aurora, dawn</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Аврора)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Aurora (goddess of dawn)</li><li>a female given name from Latin, equivalent to English Aurora</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aurora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a male Aussie (an Australian man)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australian (native or inhabitant of Australia) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Австра́лия</i> (Avstrálija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female equivalent of <i>австрали́ец</i> (avstralíjec); female Australian (female native or inhabitant of Australia)</li></ol><p><i>Австра́лия</i> (Avstrálija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Australian (of, from or relating to Australia)</li></ol><p><i>Австра́лия</i> (Avstrálija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australian dollar (official currency of Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australian rules football, Aussie rules (variety of Australian football)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) australopith</li></ol><p>Most likely derived from translingual <i>Australopithecus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Austrian (man)</li></ol><p><i>А́встрия</i> (Ávstrija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Austrian</li></ol><p><i>А́встрия</i> (Ávstrija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Austro-Hungarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Austronesian</li></ol><p>From <i>А̀встронези́я</i> (Àvstronezíja) +&lrm; <i>-ийский</i> (-ijskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autarky, autarchy (state of self-sufficiency)</li><li>autarchy (condition of absolute power)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>αὐτάρκεια</i> (autárkeia, “self-sufficiency”), from <i>αὐτ-</i> (aut-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρκέω</i> (arkéō, “to be sufficient”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia, feminine occupation suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) car, wheels</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Auto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>auto-, self- (regarding oneself)</li><li>auto- (automatic)</li><li>auto- (relating to cars or the driving of cars)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>αὐτός</i> (autós) in classical compounds, originally through mostly German and Polish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor-service station, motor depot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autobahn, highway, motorway <i>(esp. in Germany)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Autobahn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autobiographical</li></ol><p>From <i>автобиогра́фия</i> (avtobiográfija, “autobiography”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autobiography</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-), from <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self”) + <i>βίος</i> (bíos, “life”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automatic blocking system, self-locking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) automatic blocking system, self-locking</li></ol><p><i>автоблокиро́вка</i> (avtoblokiróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bus, motorbus</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>autobus</i>, from German <i>Autobus</i>, from English <i>autobus</i> or French <i>autobus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>авто́бус</i> (avtóbus): (small) bus</li></ol><p><i>авто́бус</i> (avtóbus) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bus, coach</li></ol><p><i>авто́бус</i> (avtóbus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automobile railway car, a railway carriage with its own engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car, bike and motorbike race</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car owner, motorist</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>владе́лец</i> (vladélec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bus station, bus terminal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autogamy (self-fertilization)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) autogenic welder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;technical) autogenic welder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>own goal</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>гол</i> (gol, “goal”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto racing (sport)</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-, “auto-”) +&lrm; <i>го́нки</i> (gónki, “race, racing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autograph, signature</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>αὐτόγραφον</i> (autógraphon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>авто́граф</i> (avtógraf).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autodidact (a self-taught person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor trolley</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vehicle testing point, motor-racing circuit, automobile racetrack, autodrome, motordrome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autogiro</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car factory, motor-car factory, automobile plant, automobile works, motor works</li></ol><p>First attested in 1926. <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>заво́д</i> (zavód), calque of German <i>Autowerk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paddy wagon, police van for transportation of suspects and prisoners</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>автомоби́ль для заключённых</i> (avtomobílʹ dlja zaključónnyx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas station, petrol station</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>запра́вка</i> (zaprávka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas station</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serving for refueling vehicles</li></ol><p><i>автозапра́вка</i> (avtozaprávka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spare parts for cars</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocorrect, autocorrection, auto-fixing</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>исправле́ние</i> (ispravlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocannibalism (the act of eating oneself)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car crash, auto accident</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>катастро́фа</i> (katastrófa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) autocephalous</li></ol><p><i>автокефа́лия</i> (avtokefálija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autoclave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor club (group of people with interest in cars)</li></ol><p><i>а́вто-</i> (ávto-, “auto-”) +&lrm; <i>клуб</i> (klub, “club”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto-oscillation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor transport convoy, motorcade</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>коло́нна</i> (kolónna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocorrect, autocorrection, self-correction</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>корре́кция</i> (korrékcija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mobile crane</li></ol><p>From <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>кран</i> (kran).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocrat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autocratic</li></ol><p><i>автокра́тия</i> (avtokrátija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motorist, motor-car enthusiast, motoring fan</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>люби́тель</i> (ljubítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dual carriageway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automagical (automatic, but with an apparent element of stage magic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automaton</li><li>slot machine</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) automaton</li><li>submachine gun, assault rifle</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) automatic transmission</li><li>circuit breaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalashnikov assault rifle (machine-gun), AK-47</li></ol><p>Developed by Mikhail Kalashnikov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automation (converting the controlling of a machine to an automatic system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automated; automatic; automatized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to automatize, to render automatic</li><li>to introduce automation, to automate</li></ol><p>From <i>автома́т</i> (avtomát) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automatism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>automatisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automation; automatics, automatic control engineering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automatic transmission (a mechanical transmission which shifts gears automatically in response to speed and&#47;or load)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automatic transmission (a mechanical transmission which shifts gears automatically in response to speed and&#47;or load)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>automatically, mechanically (in an automatic manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automatic</li></ol><p><i>автома́т(ика)</i> (avtomát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automatic</li></ol><p><i>автома́т(ика)</i> (avtomát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) submachine gun</li></ol><p><i>автома́т</i> (avtomát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automaton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>submachine gunner; Tommy gunner</li></ol><p><i>автома́т</i> (avtomát) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automobile, car, motorcar, motor vehicle</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>маши́на</i> (mašína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto mechanic</li></ol><p>From <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>механик</i> (mexanik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automotive industry</li></ol><p><i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>строе́ние</i> (strojénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motorist</li></ol><p>From French <i>automobiliste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car, automobile, motorcar</li><li>vehicle</li><li>lorry, truck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>automobile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car bomb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) car, automobile</li></ol><p><i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ, “automobile”): (small) automobile</li></ol><p><i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ, “automobile”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automorphic number (number whose square ends in itself)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automorphic (describing a number whose square ends in the number itself&#59; circular)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric/motorized railway engine, self-propelled railway carriage or coach, railcar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonomy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>autonomia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>αὐτονομία</i> (autonomía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonomous oblast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonomy</li></ol><p><i>автоно́мный</i> (avtonómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autonomous</li></ol><p><i>автоно́мия</i> (avtonómija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reviewer of cars</li></ol><p>From <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>обозрева́тель</i> (obozrevátelʹ). Literally, “autoreviewer”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answering machine (device that automatically records voice mail)</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>ответ</i> (otvet) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vehicle fleet</li><li>company or complex for storing, maintaining and repairing a vehicle fleet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Autopark</i>; also derivable as <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>парк</i> (park).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autopilot, mechanical pilot, robot pilot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car tire</li></ol><p>First attested in 1922. <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>покры́шка</i> (pokrýška), calque of German <i>Autodecke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-portrait</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>autoportrait</i>. By surface analysis, <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>портре́т</i> (portrét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor race, motor rally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automaker, automobile manufacturer</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>производи́тель</i> (proizvodítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car rental</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>прока́т</i> (prokát, “rental”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) car rental</li></ol><p><i>автопрока́т</i> (avtoprokát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automotive industry</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>автомоби́льная промы́шленность</i> (avtomobílʹnaja promýšlennostʹ, “automotive industry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>author</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) the head of a criminal group</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>autor</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>auctor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car defrosting chemical</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-, “auto-”) +&lrm; <i>размораживать</i> (razmoraživatʹ, “to defrost”, verb) +&lrm; <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ, suffix forming active nouns).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract (summary of a scientific work)</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>рефера́т</i> (referát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorization (act of authorizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authorized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to authorize/authorise (to grant someone the power or authority to do something specific)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to authorize/authorise (to grant someone the power or authority to do something specific)</li></ol><p><i>авторизова́ть</i> (avtorizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authoritarianism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>autoritarisme</i>, from Latin <i>auctōritās</i>. With <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm) suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authoritarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>autorytarny</i>?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority, prestige</li><li><i>(person)</i> authority</li><li>underworld leader</li></ol><p>From German <i>Autorität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authoritativeness; authoritativity (the quality of being authoritative)</li></ol><p><i>авторите́т</i> (avtoritét, “authority”) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authoritative</li><li>competent</li><li>prestigious</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>authoritative</i>, or some other European equivalent. By surface analysis, <i>авторите́т</i> (avtoritét, “authority”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royalties, money paid to the author by the publishing company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>author's</li></ol><p><i>а́втор</i> (ávtor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copyright</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorship</li></ol><p><i>а́втор</i> (ávtor) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fountain pen</li></ol><p><i>автору́чка</i> (avtorúčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fountain pen</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>ру́чка</i> (rúčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto show, motor show</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>сало́н</i> (salón), since the 1960s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garage, car service (place where cars are serviced and repaired)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electrical engineering</i>) autosyn (a type of synchro, used as part of a system to reproduce angular rotation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>autosyn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person who repairs cars</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>сле́сарь</i> (slésarʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto racing, autosport, motorsport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hitchhiking</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-, “related to cars”) +&lrm; <i>стоп</i> (stop, “stop”). From French <i>auto-stop</i>, possibly through Polish <i>autostop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parking lot, car park</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motorway, highway, freeway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>autostrada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autotimer</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>та́ймер</i> (tájmer)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autotomy (invertebrate self-defense mechanism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor transport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motor transport</li></ol><p><i>автотра́нспорт</i> (avtotránsport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expressway</li></ol><p><i>авто</i> (avto, “car”) +&lrm; <i>трасса</i> (trassa, “line; track”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autochory (self-dispersal of seeds)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indigene, native</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aboriginal, indigenous</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</td></tr></table></p><p><i>автохто́н</i> (avtoxtón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driving school</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto show, motor show, car show</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automotive electronics</li></ol><p><i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>электро́ника</i> (elektrónika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endoethnonym, autoethnonym (an ethnonym used by those who belong to the ethnic group it describes)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>авто-</i> (avto-) +&lrm; <i>этно́ним</i> (etnónim).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;stilted</i>) assets, holdings</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>avoirs</i>, plural of <i>avoir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cane toad (<i>Bufo marinus</i>, now <i>Rhinella marina</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;Turkey,&#32;Central Asia) agha, master, patron</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) uh-huh (yes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) aha! (upon figuring something out)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ahah (laughter)</li></ol><p>Compare Turkish <i>ağa</i>, Kazakh <i>аға</i> (ağa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) agalite (a fibrous variety of talc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agami heron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay <i>agar</i> or <i>agar-agar</i>, both meaning jelly.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Muslim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) alternative form of <i>ага́</i> (agá); uh-huh (yes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) agate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) agate-like</li></ol><p><i>агат</i> (agat, “agate”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ага́т</i> (agát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albanian (inhabitant Caucasian Albania)</li></ol><p>From Armenian <i>աղվան</i> (aġvan) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Caucasian Albania (historical region in the Eastern Caucasus)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasian Albanian (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallen angel; devil; demon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agglutinative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agglutination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>аге́нтство</i> (agéntstvo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agent</li><li>envoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agent provocateur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agency, dealership</li></ol><p><i>аге́нт</i> (agént, “agent, envoy”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group/community of agents, collectively (<i>in various senses of "agent", especially but not exclusively referring to secret or intelligence service agents</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) agent (legal actor, secret agent, or police agent)</li></ol><p><i>агенту́ра</i> (agentúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hagiography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitator, propagandist</li><li>canvasser, teller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;politics) agitation, propaganda, campaigning</li><li>agitational, propagandistic</li></ol><p><i>агита́ция</i> (agitácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation, propaganda</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>agitātiō</i> (“movement, agitation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation, propaganda</li></ol><p><i>агити́ровать</i> (agitírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate</li><li>to campaign</li><li>to carry on propaganda, to make propaganda</li><li>to stump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation leaflet</li><li>piece of propaganda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitprop</li></ol><p>From “<i>отде́л агита́ции и пропага́нды</i> (otdél agitácii i propagándy, “Department for Agitation and Propaganda”)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation centre</li><li>canvassing centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;colloquial) English</li></ol><p>From <i>А́нглия</i> (Ánglija, “England”) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agglomerate, agglomeration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agglomeration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) lamb</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) lamb (a meek, docile, innocent person)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Lamb, the host (the portion of bread consecrated in the Eucharist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>агньць</i> (agnĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agnьcь</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;àgnę</i>; eventually from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʷn-</i>. Compare native <i>ягнёнок</i> (jagnjónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agnonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) agnostic</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>agnostic</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “I know”); influenced by existing agentive suffix <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agnosticism</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>agnosticism</i>. By surface analysis, <i>агно́стик</i> (agnóstik) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agnostic</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>agnost(ic)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agonize</li><li>to collapse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agony, death pangs</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀγωνία</i> (agōnía, “emulation, competition, struggle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agoraphobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrarian, landowner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agrarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>agrarius</i> (“agrarian”), from <i>ager</i> (“field, farm”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unit, set</li><li>aggregate, aggregation</li><li>outfit, plant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agrégat</i>, further from Latin <i>aggregātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggregator (someone or something which aggregates)</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) aggregator (an online feed reader used to keep track of updates to blogs, etc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggregation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggregation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aggressively (in an aggressive manner)</li></ol><p><i>агресси́вный</i> (agressívnyj, agrɛssívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggressiveness, animus, bellicosity, belligerence</li></ol><p><i>агресси́вный</i> (agressívnyj, agrɛssívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aggressive (tending or disposed to aggress)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agressif</i>, futher from Latin <i>aggressus</i> (&lt; <i>aggredior</i> &lt; Latin <i>gradior</i>) + Latin <i>-īvus</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>наступа́тельный</i> (nastupátelʹnyj), <i>напо́ристый</i> (napóristyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggression</li><li>(<i>international law</i>) war of aggression</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agression</i>. Further from Latin <i>aggressiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggressor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agriculture, agricultural technique (the art or science of cultivating the ground)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) to aggro (to cause (a non-player character) to become aggressive towards the player's character)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;by extension) to provoke; to deliberately anger; to pick a fight</li></ol><p>From English <i>aggro</i> (verb) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to be angry</li></ol><p>From <i>а́грить</i> (ágritʹ, “to provoke; to deliberately anger”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), originally gaming slang from English <i>aggro</i> (“to cause a non-player character to become aggressive towards the player's character”, verb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrobusiness, agribusiness (big business connected to agriculture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrobiologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrobiology</li></ol><p>From <i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>биоло́гия</i> (biológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) agrogorod, agricultural workers' city (a type of comfortable urban settlement for housing agricultural workers proposed in the 1930s–1950s Soviet Union as an alternative to kolkhozes)</li></ol><p><i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agriculture (the art or science of cultivating the ground)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agronomist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agronomy, agronomics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) agriculture and industry; agroindustrial</li></ol><p><i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agricultural technician, agrotechnician, agricultural engineer, agriculturist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrotechnology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrochemist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agrochemical</li></ol><p><i>агрохи́мия</i> (agroxímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrochemistry</li></ol><p>From <i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>хи́мия</i> (xímija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) An agroindustrial holding company.</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>а̀гропромы́шленный</i> (àgropromýšlennyj, “agroindustrial”) +&lrm; <i>хо́лдинг</i> (xólding, “holding company”). Equivalent to <i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>хо́лдинг</i> (xólding).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agroecological</li></ol><p><i>агроэколо́гия</i> (agroekológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agroecology</li></ol><p>From <i>агро-</i> (agro-) +&lrm; <i>эколо́гия</i> (ekológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;agriculture) agricultural export</li></ol><p><i>агро-</i> (agro-, “agro-”) +&lrm; <i>э́кспорт</i> (éksport, “export”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>used to call a baby to attract its attention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make the "agu" sound (to calm infants)</li></ol><p><i>агу́</i> (agú) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man of Aghul background</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aghul (ethnic group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aghul</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aghul (language)</li></ol><p><i>агу́л</i> (agúl) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agouti</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agouti</i>, from Old Tupi <i>akuti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aghori (an order of Hindu ascetics)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अघोर</i> (aghora), <i>अघोरी</i> (aghorī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aghori (an order of Hindu ascetics)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अघोर</i> (aghora), <i>अघोरी</i> (aghorī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) hell, Hades (the abode of the damned)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Siberia) bog, unpassable mud</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>адъ</i> (adŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>адъ</i> (adŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) adagio</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a movement or piece that starts as adagio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>adagio</i>, perhaps through French <i>adagio</i>, based on the pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Adam's (<i>in collocations</i>)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Адамов)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Adamov, equivalent to English Adams</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Adam's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Adam's apple (<i>formal term</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) Osage orange (<i>Maclura pomifera</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adapalene (medicine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptation</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>adaptatio</i>, from Latin <i>adapto</i> (“to adapt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adapter (device to allow compatibility)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) pick-up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>adapter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptability</li></ol><p><i>адапти́вный</i> (adaptívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adaptive (relating to, characterized by or showing adaptation&#59; making or made fit or suitable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adapted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt (to make suitable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt (to change to make oneself suitable)</li></ol><p><i>адапти́ровать</i> (adaptírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Advent (season before Christmas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Adventism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Adventism</i>. By surface analysis, <i>адве́нт</i> (advént) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adventist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adverbial (word or phrase modifying a verb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawyer, attorney, barrister, solicitor, advocate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Advokat</i>, itself from Latin <i>advocātus</i>.</p><p>Attested since the time of Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) devil's advocate (debater)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lawyer, attorney</li><li>lawyer's, attorney's</li><li>legal</li></ol><p><i>адвока́т</i> (advokát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advocacy, bar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adhesive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adhesion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>addictive (causing or tending to cause addiction&#59; habit-forming)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) add-on (extension of core application)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>add-on</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>adequately</li></ol><p><i>адеква́тный</i> (adɛkvátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adequacy</li></ol><p><i>адеква́тный</i> (adɛkvátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) corresponding to norm, acceptable, normal, proper, appropriate, reasonable, sane, rational, having common sense</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) adequate, exact (accurately corresponding to something)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) proportionate, commensurate (sufficient, but not excessive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adelaidean</li></ol><p><i>Аделаи́д(а)</i> (Adelaíd(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adenovirus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) adenovirus; adenoviral</li></ol><p><i>аденови́рус</i> (adenovírus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adenosine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) adenosine diphosphate (a nucleotide, the result of adenosine triphosphate losing a phosphate group in metabolism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biochemistry,&#32;biochemistry) adenosine triphosphate (a nucleotide that is used as a source of energy in cellular reactions, in the synthesis of nucleic acids, and as a neurotransmitter recognized by purinergic receptors)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) adenoid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adenoma (benign tumour)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adherent, follower</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>adeptus</i>, past participle of <i>adipīscor</i> (“reach, arrive at”), possibly via French <i>adepte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adessive (the adessive case, or a word in that case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) adessive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>adessive</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adessive (the adessive case, or a word in that case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) adephagan</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Adephaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ajapsandali (Georgian dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>აჯაფსანდალი</i> (aǯapsandali).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adjaran (person from Adjara, Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjika, adzhika</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Abkhaz <i>аџьы́ка</i> (adžə́kʼa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Adidas</li></ol><p>From <i>адида́с</i> (adidás) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>administrative territorial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administrative law (laws that pertain to the administrative agencies of government)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>administrative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administrator</li><li>business manager</li><li>receptionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administration, management</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>administrō</i>, <i>administrātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administering, administration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) admiral</li><li>red admiral (<i>Vanessa atalanta</i>, butterfly)</li></ol><p>From Old French <i>admiral</i> etc., from Medieval Latin <i>admiralis</i> etc., from Arabic أَمِير (ʔamīr, “commander”) + <i>-alis</i> (“-al”) under influence from <i>admīrārī</i> (“to admire, to respect”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) admiralty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiralty law (area of law that deals with ships at sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiralty (office or jurisdiction of an admiral)</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>admiraliteit</i> +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admin. dept.</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>администрати́вный отде́л</i> (administratívnyj otdél).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hellish, infernal (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ад</i> (ad) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) adoption (act of adopting, or state of being adopted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) adrenaline (epinephrine, the hormone and neurotransmitter)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from English <i>adrenaline</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>address (a description of the location of a property, usually with at least a street name and number, name of a town, and now also a postal code; such a description as superscribed for direction on an envelope or letter)</li><li>address (the location of a property)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the recipient or direction of a statement, claim, allegation etc.</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) address (a number identifying a specific storage location in computer memory)</li><li>(networking,&#32;Internet) address (a string of characters identifying a node or range of nodes on a network (especially the Internet), such as an e-mail address, IP address or URL)</li><li>a ceremonial written greeting or congratulation</li><li>(dated,&#32;diplomacy,&#32;politics) address (a formal approach to a sovereign or head of state, especially an official appeal or petition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>adres</i>, from French <i>adresse</i>. Further from Latin <i>ad-</i> + Latin <i>dīrēctus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sender, consignor, shipper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addressee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Adressat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>address book (small book with addresses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;graphical user interface) address bar (bar in a web browser that displays the address)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) address</li><li>selective, targeted</li></ol><p><i>а́дрес</i> (ádres) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address (something to someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to contact (<i>personally or in writing</i>) [<i>with</i> locational adverb <small>‘somewhere’</small> <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>адресова́ть</i> (adresovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>а́дрес</i> (ádres): address</li></ol><p><i>а́дрес</i> (ádres) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Adriatic</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>Adriāt(icus)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) hadron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hellishly (in a hellish manner)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;as an intensifier) as hell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hellish, infernal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) diabolical, fiendish (evil and cunning)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;as an intensifier) hellish, damned (extreme, very unpleasant)</li></ol><p><i>ад</i> (ad) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) adsorbent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;physical chemistry,&#32;physics) to adsorb (to accumulate on a surface, by adsorption)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>adsorbieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), from French <i>adsorber</i>; see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>adsorbēre</i>, from <i>ad</i> + <i>sorbeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjectival, adjective</li></ol><p><i>адъекти́в</i> (adʺjektív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), borrowed from Latin <i>adjectīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) aide-de-camp, adjutant, aide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Adyghe</li></ol><p><i>Адыге́я</i> (Adygéja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>адыге́йский</i> (adygéjskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) adieu (farewell)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>adieu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) adultery (sexual intercourse by a married person with someone other than their spouse)</li></ol><p>From French <i>adultère</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literary) adultery</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) adulterous</li></ol><p><i>адюльте́р</i> (adjulʹtér, “adultery”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) even</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) as many as, as much as, all the way to (<i>a word used for emphasis</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>аже</i> (aže), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aže</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policeman (French)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agio</li></ol><p>From French <i>agio</i>, from Italian <i>aggio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stockjobbing, agiotage, rush (speculative fever on stock exchanges and markets&#59; increased demand for something caused by some events or rumours)</li><li>stir, hullabaloo (strong excitement&#59; a struggle of interests around an affair)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>agiotage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;financial) related to a stock-market rush or panicked economic demand</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) feverish, panicky (associated with excessive, unreasonably large interest or demand associated with panic)</li></ol><p>From <i>ажиота́ж</i> (ažiotáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(psychology,&#32;dated) agitation; anxiety</li></ol><p>From French <i>agitation</i> + <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>openwork <i>(cloth)</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>accounting</i>) up-to-date</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) openwork</li><li>delicate, finely-wrought</li></ol><p>From <i>ажу́р</i> (ažúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) name of the Cyrillic letter А, а</li><li>(plural only,&#32;dated) letters</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) basics, fundamentals</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) I (first person singular nominative pronoun)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) yours truly, your humble servant (<i>as a self-deprecating, third-person reference to oneself</i>)</li></ol><p>From the pronoun with the same spelling, see below.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>азъ</i> (azŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(j)azъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azalea</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Азалия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Azalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) adhan (call to prayer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic أَذَان (ʔaḏān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excitement, fervour, passion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hazard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game of chance</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>jeu de hasard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>heatedly, excitedly</li></ol><p><i>аза́ртный</i> (azártnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fervent, passionate</li><li>impassioned, heated</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gambling, chance</li></ol><p><i>аза́рт</i> (azárt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li><li>ABC book, abecedary; primer</li><li>ABC (the rudiments of any subject)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>азъбукꙑ</i> (azŭbuky); borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>азъбоукꙑ</i> (azŭbuky), from the names of the first two letters of the Cyrillic alphabet, <i>азъ</i> (azŭ) +&lrm; <i>боукꙑ</i> (buky), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀλφάβητος</i> (alphábētos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Braille</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morse code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alphabetical, especially of the Cyrillic alphabet</li><li>basic; commonly known</li></ol><p>From <i>а́збука</i> (ázbuka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Azeri, Azerbaijani person</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>азербайджа́нец</i> (azerbajdžánec, azɛrbajdžánec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azeri, Azerbaijani person</li></ol><p><i>Азербайджа́н</i> (Azerbajdžán, Azɛrbajdžán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Azeri</li></ol><p><i>Азербайджа́н</i> (Azerbajdžán, Azɛrbajdžán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asian (person from Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Asian (of, relating to or from Asia)</li></ol><p><i>азиа́т</i> (aziát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive flounder (<i>Paralichthys olivaceus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Azeri, Azerbaijani person</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>азербайджа́нец</i> (azerbajdžánec, azɛrbajdžánec) + <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azimuth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>azimut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azitromycin (azalide antibiotic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Azov (town in Russia, located near the Sea of Azov)</li></ol><p><i>Азо́в</i> (Azóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nitrogen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>azote</i>, coined from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) + Ancient Greek <i>ζωή</i> (zōḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nitrous acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) nitric acid</li></ol><p>From <i>азо́тный</i> (azótnyj, “of nitrogen”) +&lrm; <i>кислота́</i> (kislotá, “acid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nitrogen</li></ol><p><i>азо́т</i> (azót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nitrogen-bearing, nitrogenous</li></ol><p>From <i>азо́т</i> (azót) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>содержа́щий</i> (soderžáščij), a calque of German <i>stickstoffhaltig</i>. First attested in 1859.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azu (Tatar dish, meat pieces in spicy sauce)</li></ol><p>From <i>‘азу</i> (‘azu), Qasim Tatar variant of Tatar <i>казы</i> (qazı).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azurite (blue, vitreous mineral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) ABC, nuts and bolts, elements</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>азъ</i> (azŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of champagne from Ay, Marne, France</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Ay</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Jewish&#32;slang</i>) Yid, Jew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>אַ ייִד</i> (a yid, “a Jew”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Аид)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hades</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀΐδης</i> (Aḯdēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Jewish&#32;slang</i>) Jewish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>אַ ייִדישע</i> (a yidishe, “a Jewish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flagroot, sweet flag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stork</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>а́истъ</i> (áist), <i>а́гистъ</i> (ágist), known since the first half of 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stork</li></ol><p><i>а́ист</i> (áist) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby stork</li></ol><p><i>а́ист</i> (áist) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ouch! (it hurts!)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) maybe, or</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> I, i</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) good old, what a (<i>an expression of admiration or approval</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;ironic,&#32;sarcastic) truly, indeed (<i>an expression of admiration or approval; or alternatively, of mockery or disapproval</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aye-aye (nocturnal quadruped)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>tsk tsk! (shame on you!)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>ayva</i> (“quince”), borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آیوا (ayva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) quince</li></ol><p><i>айва́</i> (ajvá) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) let's go, c'mon</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) made off; dashed away; vanished</li></ol><p>Supposedly from Tatar <i>әйдә</i> (äydä) or a cognate term from some other Turkic language. Related to Serbo-Croatian <i>hajde</i>.</p><p>Not to be confused with ай да (aj da), which is a compliment.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aikido</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese 合気道&#32;(あいきどう,&#32;&#8203;<i>aikidō</i>), from Middle Chinese <i>合</i> (hop, “meet, unite”) + <i>氣</i> (kʰjɨ̀j, “spirit; breath”) + <i>道</i> (dáu, “way”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aimag (various historical or modern Turkic or Mongolian provinces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic or Mongolian <i>аймаг</i> (ajmag). Cognates include Kyrgyz <i>аймак</i> (aymak), Kazakh <i>аймақ</i> (aimaq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aymara (language of South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ainu people, an ethnic group in northern Japan and eastern Russia.</li></ol><p>From Ainu <i>アイヌ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ainu</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ainu language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ainu language</li></ol><p>From Ainu <i>アイヌ</i> (aynu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) IP address</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>IP</i>, an ellipsis of <i>IP address</i>, from <i>Internet Protocol address</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airan</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language; compare to Tatar <i>әйрән</i> (äyrän), Turkish <i>ayran</i>.</p></html>:/aɪ̯s/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cool, good</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ice</i>, from Middle English <i>is</i>, from Old English <i>īs</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;īs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsą</i> (“ice”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eyH-</i> (“ice, frost”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iceberg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Eisberg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) iceberg</li></ol><p><i>а́йсберг</i> (ájsberg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ice hockey</i>) icing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>icing</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Syriac, Assyrian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>ասորի</i> (asori).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian Neo-Aramaic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian Neo-Aramaic language, a Neo-Aramaic dialect of Modern Aramaic</li></ol><p><i>айсо́р</i> (ajsór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) IT guy</li></ol><p>From English <i>IT</i> +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iPhone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>iPhone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>academician, academic</li><li>graduate</li></ol><p><i>акаде́мия</i> (akadémija) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>academic</li></ol><p><i>акаде́мия</i> (akadémija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>academy</li><li>college</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>academia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀκαδημία</i> (Akadēmía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) acalypha (<i>Acalypha</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Acalypha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akan (Niger-Congo language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Akan (people and language group in Ghana)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akan (language group spoken in Ghana)</li></ol><p><i>ака́н</i> (akán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) acanthus</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) acanthus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) the standard reduced pronunciation of unstressed <i>о</i> as <i>а</i> (i.e., [ɐ] or [ə]), as heard in Central Russian and Southern Russian and Belarusian.</li></ol><p><i>а́кать</i> (ákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;zoology) acariform (relating to the Acariformes)</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Acariformes</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce an unstressed <i>о</i> like an <i>а</i> (as seen in Central and Southern Russia, considered standard)</li></ol><p><i>а</i> (a) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) akathist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀκάθιστος</i> (akáthistos, “unseated, standing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acacia</li></ol><p><i>акация</i> (akacija, “acacia”) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) acacia (shrub or tree)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Akazie</i>, from Latin <i>acacia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀκακία</i> (akakía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ака́ция</i> (akácija, “acacia”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ака́фист</i> (akáfist).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aquabike (motorised personal vehicle that travels on water&#59; jetski)</li></ol><p>From English <i>aquabike</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aquaculture</li></ol><p>Internationalism ultimately from Latin <i>aqua</i> + <i>cultura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aqualung</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>aqualung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scuba diver</li></ol><p><i>акваланг</i> (akvalang, “aqualung”) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aquamarine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Aquamarin</i>, from Latin <i>aqua marīna</i> (“sea water”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aquapark (an amusement park with waterplay areas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watercolour/watercolor painter, watercolourist/watercolorist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watercolour/watercolor (a painting technique, paint or painting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aquarelle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aquarium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aquatic (relating to water&#59; living in or near water, taking place in water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water area, waters, harbour water</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>аквато́рія</i> (akvatórija), Belarusian <i>аквато́рыя</i> (akvatóryja), Polish <i>akwatorium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang,&#32;Ukraine) the flag of Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Aquafresh</i>, referring to the flag's similar colours to the toothpaste brand.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aqueduct (an artificial channel conveying water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;ironic) like, as</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>акы</i> (aky).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>akim, a leader of a local administration in Kazakhstan or Kyrgyzstan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>әкім</i> (äkım) or Kyrgyz <i>аким</i> (akim).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Аким)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Akim</li></ol><p>From <i>Иоаким</i> (Ioakim), from Hebrew <i>יְהוֹיָקִים</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>local administration in Kazakhstan or Kyrgyzstan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>акка́унт</i> (akkáunt); account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Akkadian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account (an authorization to use a service)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>account</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acclimatization (the act of acclimatizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acclimatization (the act of acclimatizing)</li></ol><p><i>акклиматизи́ровать</i> (akklimatizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acclimatize, to acclimate (to habituate to a climate not native)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acclimate, to acclimatize (to get used to a new climate)</li></ol><p><i>акклиматизи́ровать</i> (akklimatizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accolade (a salutation marking the conferring of knighthood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accommodation, adjustment (adjustment of the eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) accompaniment (that which gives support or adds to the background)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accompany</li></ol><p><i>аккомпани́ровать</i> (akkompanírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to accompany (to perform an accompanying part next to another instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) chord</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>accord</i>. Further from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;accordāre</i>, from <i>ac-</i> + Latin <i>cor</i> (see <i>concordō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accordion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>accordéon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) accordionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accreditation (giving of credentials)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) letter of credit, bill of credit, letter of undertaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accredit (to send with letters credential, as an ambassador, envoy, or diplomatic agent&#59; to authorize, as a messenger or delegate.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>akredytować</i>, from French <i>accréditer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) accretion</li></ol><p>From <i>аккре́ци(я)</i> (akkréci(ja)) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accretion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to accumulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulator, (car) battery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) accumulator</li></ol><p><i>аккумуля́тор</i> (akkumuljátor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) exactly, precisely</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>accuratus</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>аккура́тно</i> (akkurátno); carefully, neatly, gently, easy does it</li></ol><p><i>аккура́тно</i> (akkurátno) +&lrm; <i>-енько</i> (-enʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>neatly, smartly</li><li>carefully, cautiously, prudently</li></ol><p><i>аккура́тный</i> (akkurátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tidiness, neatness</li><li>exactness, accuracy</li></ol><p><i>аккура́тный</i> (akkurátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accurate, punctual, exact, thorough</li><li>tidy, neat</li><li>careful, cautious</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Polish <i>akuratny</i> or German <i>akkurat</i>, from Latin <i>accūrātus</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acmeism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀκμή</i> (akmḗ, “top, highest degree of something”) + <i>-изм</i> (-izm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Ural) <i>Sophora</i> alopecuroides</li></ol><p>Compare with Kazakh <i>ақ мия</i> (aq miä, “white liquorice; liquorice root”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) acne, comedone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolfsbane, aconite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acre (unit of surface area)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) acridid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>акрида</i> (akrida), itself borrowed from Latin <i>acrīda</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓κρῐ́ς</i> (ăkrĭ́s, “grasshopper, locust, cricket”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) acrylamide (amide of acrylic acid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acrylonitrile butadiene styrene</li></ol><p><i>акрилонитрилбутадиенстиро́л</i> (akrilonitrilbutadijenstiról) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrobat (an athlete who performs acts requiring skill, agility and coordination), tumbler</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting Ancient Greek <i>ἀκροβάτης</i> (akrobátēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrobatism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrobatics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acrobatic</li></ol><p><i>акроба́т</i> (akrobát) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym, initialism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrostic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀκροστιχίς</i> (akrostikhís).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aqsaqal or aksakal (a village elder in some Central Asian cultures)</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) an elder, a chief, an old important man</li></ol><p>From Turkic languages, cognates include Tatar <i>аксакал</i> (aqsaqal), Kazakh <i>ақсақал</i> (aqsaqal) or Kyrgyz <i>аксакал</i> (aksakal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) velvet, expensive silk fabric</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἑξάμιτος</i> (hexámitos, “velvet; of six threads”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accelerator (accelerator pedal)</li></ol><p>Likely from English <i>accelerator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceleration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accelerometer (instrument for measuring acceleration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accessory (that which belongs to something else deemed the principal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>accessoire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(logic,&#32;mathematics) axiom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axolotl (amphibian of the salamander tribe)</li></ol><p>From Classical Nahuatl <i>āxōlōtl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axon (a nerve fibre)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἄξων</i> (áxōn, “axis”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>axonometric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act</li><li>nude, painting of a nude person</li><li>receipt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group of activists, active members or leading players</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) asset</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) successes, achievements</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology,&#32;computing) activator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activation (making active)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to activate</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>акти́вный</i> (aktívnyj, “active”) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more active, to liven up</li></ol><p><i>активизи́ровать</i> (aktivizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to activate (to encourage development or induce increased activity&#59; to stimulate)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to activate (to put a device, mechanism or system into action or motion&#59; to enable)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;chemistry) to activate (to render more reactive)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;computing) to activate (to remove the limitations of demoware by providing a license&#59; to unlock)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;computing) to focus (to transfer the input focus to a visual element)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activist, active worker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active vocabulary (words and phrases that someone commonly uses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>actively</li></ol><p><i>акти́вный</i> (aktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activity</li></ol><p><i>акти́вный</i> (aktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>active, proactive, energetic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active voice (the form in which the subject of a verb carries out some action)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active vocabulary (words and phrases that someone commonly uses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active vocabulary (words and phrases that someone commonly uses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actinium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actinia, sea anemone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cancellation of school or work due to weather</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) exemption due to illness or disability</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly</li></ol><p><i>акт</i> (akt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>update (action of making something up to date)</li><li>actualization (action of making actual)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to update (make something up to date)</li><li>to actualize (convert into reality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relevance, urgency, topicality</li></ol><p><i>актуа́льный</i> (aktuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relevant, urgent, timely, topical, current</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actuary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actor, player, performer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>acteur</i>, further from Latin <i>āctor</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Macedonian <i>глумец</i> (glumec), Serbo-Croatian <i>глумац</i> (akin to <i>глум</i> (glum), <i>глуми́ться</i> (glumítʹsja)); Slovene <i>igralec</i>, Czech <i>herec</i> (akin to <i>игра́</i> (igrá), <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>histrionic, actorish</li></ol><p><i>актёр</i> (aktjór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shark</li></ol><p>First attested as <i>а́ккула</i>&nbsp;f (ákkula) in 1747 and 1789, <i>а́ккулъ</i>&nbsp;m (ákkul) in 1788, <i>акулъ</i>&nbsp;m (akul) in 1755. Likely inherited from Middle Russian (or possibly a back-formation), attested only as a derivative <i>акулий</i> (akulij) used to describe liver oil of a Greenland shark in a source going back to late 16th or early 17th century.</p><p>Borrowed from Sami language (compare Skolt Sami <i>akkli</i>, Kildin Sami <i>а̄һклэӈӈк</i> (āhkleŋŋk), Northern Sami <i>áhkạlakkis</i> (“Greenland shark”)), further borrowed Old Norse <i>hákarl</i> (compare Icelandic <i>hákarl</i>, Faroese <i>hákallur</i>, dialectal Norwegian <i>håkall</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acupuncture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acupoint (a specific point on the body where acupuncture is applied)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acupuncture</li></ol><p><i>акупункту́ра</i> (akupunktúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acoustics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acoustic guitar (hollow-body guitar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acoustic</li></ol><p><i>аку́ст(ика)</i> (akúst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acute accent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accoucheur, obstetrician</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>accoucheur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstetrician-gynaecologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwife</li></ol><p><i>акуше́р</i> (akušér) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstetrics, midwifery</li></ol><p><i>акуше́р</i> (akušér) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>акселера́ция</i> (akselerácija): acceleration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(grammar)</i> stress, accent</li><li>accent <i>(foreign or local)</i></li><li>accent, emphasis, stress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accentuation</li></ol><p><i>акценти́ровать</i> (akcentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accent, to accentuate</li><li>to emphasize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) accentology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accentuation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>accentuatio</i> and its descendants. According to Bulgarian Etymological Dictionary, derived from French <i>accentuation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(business,&#32;finance) acceptance (an assent and engagement by the person on whom a bill of exchange is drawn)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) acceptor, acceptor impurity</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) scavenger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;politics&#32;or&#32;literary) accession</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from Latin <i>accessiō</i>, likely through German <i>Akzession</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accidental; incidental, unimportant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excise, excise tax</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>accise</i>, from Latin <i>accisus</i>. Attested since the time of Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shareholder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>actionnaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint-stock company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shareholder</li></ol><p><i>акционе́р</i> (akcionér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corporatization</li></ol><p><i>акциони́ровать</i> (akcionírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corporatize</li></ol><p><i>акцио́н-</i> (akción-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) share, stock</li><li>(politics,&#32;diplomacy) action, move, demarche</li><li>(<i>marketing</i>) promotion, campaign; special offer</li><li>organized public event</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>āctiō</i>, probably through German <i>Aktie</i> and <i>Aktion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Leningrad Oblast) leftovers from the heating of fat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Veps <i>ahkud, aškud, akšud</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alabama (tribe)</li><li>Alabama (language)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Алабама)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alabama (a state of the United States)</li><li>Alabama (a river in Alabama)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Kostroma, Ryazan, Tver, Perm, Penza, Tambov) to speak unclearly, to mumble, to burr</li></ol><p>Uncertain, most likely an imitation of mumbling.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>алема́нн</i> (alemánn)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) greedy, grasping</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alan (member of a Sarmatian tribe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarmism (production of needless alarms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarmist (one who causes others to become alarmed without cause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) alatyr, a magic stone with healing powers</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The name has been compared to English <i>altar</i> or Russian <i>янта́рь</i> (jantárʹ, “amber”). Viktor Martynov derives the name from Iranic <i>*al-atar</i>, literally “white-burning”, the synonym <i>си́ний ка́мень</i> (sínij kámenʹ, “blue stone”) being a calque.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ала́тир</i> (alátyr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albanian (inhabitant of Caucasian Albania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albanian (person from Albania)</li></ol><p><i>Алба́ния</i> (Albánija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albanian language</li><li>an online slang based on deliberate misspelling of the Russian language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Albanian</li></ol><p><i>Алба́ния</i> (Albánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alviri-Vidari (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;colloquial) forward</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bashkir <i>алға</i> (alğa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) algebra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>algebraic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>algebraic equation (mathematical equation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Algonquin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Algonquin</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Algonquian</li></ol><p>From <i>алгонки́н</i> (algonkín, “Algonquin”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>algorithm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>algorithmic</li></ol><p><i>алгори́тм</i> (algorítm) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halberd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alabaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alabaster</li></ol><p><i>алеба́стр</i> (alebástr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) long-tailed duck, <i>Clangula hyemalis</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami (allokaj, “long-tailed duck”), akin to Finnish <i>alli</i>, in the end onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leukemia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Alexandrian (referring to the city of Alexandria in Egypt or to Alexander the Great)</li><li>alexandrine (<i>verse, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Александри́я</i> (Aleksandríja) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij) when referring to Alexandria; from <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij) when referring to Alexander the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Alexander's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Alexandrov (referring to anyone with this last name, or to anything named after such a person)</li></ol><p>From <i>Алекса́ндр</i> (Aleksándr) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij) or <i>Алекса́ндров</i> (Aleksándrov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of the Alemanni tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) aleocharine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Aleocharinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aleppine</li></ol><p><i>Але́ппо</i> (Aléppo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>але́ппский</i> (aléppskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become crimson, scarlet, vermilion</li></ol><p><i>алый</i> (alyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleutian <i>(person)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aleut (of the Aleutian Islands)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleut (language)</li></ol><p><i>алеу́т</i> (aleút) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Eastern Aleut, Fox Islands dialect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aleph (first letter of the Proto-Canaanite alphabet and descendant script)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Algerian (citizen of Algeria or of Algiers)</li></ol><p><i>Алжи́р</i> (Alžír) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Algerian (of Algeria or its people)</li></ol><p><i>Алжи́р</i> (Alžír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) or</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Али)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ali</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alibi (criminal legal defense)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>alibi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) alim (Islamic scholar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَالِم (ʕālim).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alimony, maintenance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aleph (first letter of the Proto-Canaanite alphabet and descendant script)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aliyah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עלייה</i> (aliyá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alkaloid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) alkane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to crave, to thirst (for), to be greedy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>алъкати</i> (alŭkati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olkati</i>. Doublet of <i>лака́ть</i> (lakátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) alkie, wino (an alcoholic)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>алкого́лик</i> (alkogólik, “alcoholic”) +&lrm; <i>-аш</i> (-aš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) alcoholic beverages</li></ol><p><i>алка́ш</i> (alkáš) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) alkies</li></ol><p><i>алка́ш</i> (alkáš) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) alkyne (hydrocarbon containing at least one carbon-carbon triple bond)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alco-</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>алкого́ль</i> (alkogólʹ, “alcohol”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alcogel</li></ol><p><i>алко-</i> (alko-) +&lrm; <i>гель</i> (gelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) alcoholism (chronic disease)</li></ol><p><i>алкого́ль</i> (alkogólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alcoholic (a person addicted to alcohol)</li></ol><p><i>алкого́ль</i> (alkogólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female alcoholic, a female dipsomaniac</li><li>clipping of <i>ма́йка-алкоголи́чка</i> (májka-alkogolíčka); wife-beater (sleeveless shirt)</li></ol><p><i>алкого́лик</i> (alkogólik) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Bulgarian <i>алкохоли́чка</i> (alkoholíčka), Ukrainian <i>алкоголі́чка</i> (alkoholíčka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethanol, ethyl alcohol</li><li>alcohol</li><li>alcoholic beverage, alcoholic drink, booze</li></ol><p>From English <i>alcohol</i>, from Latin <i>alcohol</i>, from Andalusian Arabic اَلْكُحُول (al-kuḥūl), اَلْقُحُول (al-quḥūl), earlier اَلْكُحْل (al-kuḥl, “kohl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alcohol poisoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alcohol, alcoholic</li></ol><p><i>алкого́ль</i> (alkogólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) alkie, drunkard</li></ol><p>From <i>алко-</i> (alko-) +&lrm; <i>-навт</i> (-navt), on the pattern of <i>космона́вт</i> (kosmonávt, “cosmonaut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) allative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>allative</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) allative case (case used to indicate movement onto, or to the adjacency of something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>allegorically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allegorical</li></ol><p>From <i>аллего́рия</i> (allegórija, “allegory”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allegory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀλληγορία</i> (allēgoría).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>але́йка</i> (aléjka, “long-tailed duck”)</li><li>diminutive of <i>але́я</i> (aléja, “allée”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allele</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allergen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone allergic, an allergic person</li></ol><p><i>аллерги́я</i> (allergíja, “allergy”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allergic</li></ol><p><i>аллерги́я</i> (allergíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allergic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allergy</li></ol><p>From German <i>Allergie</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “other”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work, activity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alley, path <i>(in a park)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alligator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alleluia; hallelujah</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) alleluia; hallelujah (a choral composition incorporating <i>alleluia</i> or <i>hallelujah</i> in its text)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀλληλούϊα</i> (allēloúïa), from Biblical Hebrew <i>הַלְּלוּיָהּ</i> (hal'luyáh, “praise God!”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alliteration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>telephony</i>) hello!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>allô</i>, ultimately from English <i>hello</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allodium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allocasuarina (<i>Allocasuarina</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i><i>Allocasuarina</i></i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) allomorph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allonge (attachment to negotiable instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>allonge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allopathy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allotropy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allophone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alluvial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alluvium, alluvion, wash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allusion (indirect reference, hint)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pace, gait</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>allure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diamond</li><li>glass cutter, glazier's diamond</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>алмаз</i> (almaz), from Persian الماس (almâs, “diamond”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diamond</li></ol><p><i>алма́з</i> (almáz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>алма́з</i> (almáz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aloe</li></ol><p><i>ало́й</i> (alój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) alocasia (<i>Alocasia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Alocasia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) alonsoa (<i>Alonsoa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Alonsoa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alopecia (deficiency of the hair)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) aloe</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀλόη</i> (alóē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) alprazolam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Altaicist</li></ol><p>From <i>алтаи́стика</i> (altaístika) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Altay (referring to the Altay region or its people)</li></ol><p><i>Алта́й</i> (Altáj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>altāre</i> (often attested in plural form <i>altāria</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) altyn (a three-kopek coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed either from Tatar <i>алты</i> (altı, “six”), referring to six dengas (six half-kopeks), or from Tatar <i>алтын</i> (altın, “gold”).</p><p>These small coins were never minted in gold; however, compare to the semantic evolution of Latin <i>solidus</i> from a Roman gold coin to a medieval silver shilling.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alunite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li><li>alphabetical index</li></ol><p>From Byzantine Greek <i>ἀλφάβητον</i> (alphábēton). The pronunciation with stress on the second syllable comes directly from the Greek, while the pronunciation with stress on the third syllable was altered, possibly by analogy to French, and gained prestige. Compare Ukrainian <i>алфа́віт</i> (alfávit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alphabetic, alphabetical</li></ol><p><i>алфави́т</i> (alfavít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabetical order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alchemist</li></ol><p><i>алхи́мия</i> (alxímija) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alchemic</li></ol><p><i>алхи́м(ия)</i> (alxím(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alchemy</li></ol><p>From Arabic اَلْكِيمِيَاء (al-kīmiyāʔ), from Ancient Greek <i>χυμείᾱ</i> (khumeíā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greedily, covetously, rapaciously, avidly, avariciously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed, greediness, avidity, cupidity</li></ol><p><i>а́лчный</i> (álčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) greedy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>алъчьнъ</i> (alŭčĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;òlčьnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;álˀkinas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crimson, scarlet, vermilion</li><li>reddish, blood-red, ruby-colored</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>алъ</i> (alŭ, “bright red; crimson”), from a Turkic source. Compare Bashkir <i>ал</i> (al, “bright red”), Turkish <i>al</i> (“crimson”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherry plum (the fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>alça</i>, from Persian آلوچه (âluče), diminutive of آلو (âlu, “plum”). Attested since 1836.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) shortening of <b><i>а́ли</i> (áli)</b>; or</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Anglicanism,&#32;Lutheranism,&#32;Roman Catholicism) alb (long white robe worn by ministers at religious ceremonies)</li><li>(historical,&#32;poetry) alba (genre of Old Occitan lyric poetry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>alba</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Occitan <i>alba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;only in reference to Spain) basil, sweet basil (<i>Ocimum basilicum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>albahaca</i>, from Arabic الْحَبَق (al-ḥabaq, “basil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>albatross</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>albatros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>albedo</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>albēdō</i> (“whiteness”); borrowed through some European language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>albinism (lack of melanin pigmentation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>albino</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>albinos</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) albizia (<i>Albizia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Albizia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>album</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>album</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>albumin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) albumin</li></ol><p><i>альбуми́н</i> (alʹbumín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) elf</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>alfr</i>. Related to Ukrainian <i>альв</i> (alʹv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>algologist (one who studies algology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) algological (of or pertaining to algology (the study of algae))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>algology (the branch of botany dealing with algae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) aldehyde</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alcove, oriel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>alcove</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alma mater</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>alma mater</i> "nourishing mother".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literary miscellany, anthology</li><li>almanac (book or table listing astronomical, nautical or other events for the year)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almandine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>альпа́ка</i> (alʹpáka)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>alpaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpaca</li><li>alpaca hair, textile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpine chough</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alpine</li></ol><p><i>А́льпы</i> (Álʹpy) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphorn (musical instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountaineering</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>alpinisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountaineer, alpinist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) alstroemeria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Alstroemeria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alto <i>(voice)</i></li><li>viola <i>(stringed instrument)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alter ego (alternate personality or persona)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>alter ego</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) alternanthera (<i>Alternanthera</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Alternanthera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative (a situation which allows a choice between two or more possibilities)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>alternatywa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative medicine (any of various medical methods and practices used in place of conventional medicine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative (relating to a choice)</li></ol><p><i>альтернати́ва</i> (alʹternatíva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altimeter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altruism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>altruisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altruist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>altruistic</li></ol><p><i>альтруи́ст</i> (alʹtruíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alt-girl</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) girl with a standout appearance, possibly from any subculture, not necessarily the alt one</li></ol><p>From English <i>alt-</i> +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha</li><li>Alfa (class of Soviet nuclear-powered attack submarines)</li><li>Alpha (Soviet and Russian military counterterrorist organizations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha and omega (the first and the last)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha rays</li></ol><p>First attested in 1913. <i>а́льфа</i> (álʹfa) +&lrm; <i>лучи́</i> (lučí), calque of German <i>Alphastrahlen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha male (dominant male in a group of animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha helix (secondary structure found in many proteins)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha particle (positively charged nucleus)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1913. <i>а́льфа</i> (álʹfa) +&lrm; <i>части́ца</i> (častíca), calque of German <i>Alphateilchen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>virology</i>) alphavirus</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>alphavirus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alfvén wave (a hydromagnetic shear wave in a charged plasma)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man, who is dependent on a woman (or women)</li></ol><p>From <i>Monsieur Alphonse</i>, a book by Alexandre Dumas <i>fils</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alliance (state of being allied)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>alliance</i>. Further from Latin <i>alligō</i> (<i>ad-</i> + <i>ligō</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) (akin to <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ), <i>у́зы</i> (úzy)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aluminium/aluminum (metal)</li></ol><p><i>алюми́ний</i> (aljumínij) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aluminium, aluminum</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>aluminium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a novice prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>алло́</i> (alló)</li><li>used to confirm that the person being addressed is paying attention</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>allô</i>; see <i>алло́</i> (alló) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Amazon</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) horsewoman</li><li>(<i>clothing</i>) riding habit (<i>dress</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀμαζών</i> (Amazṓn, “Amazon (<i>mythology</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Амазонка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Amazon (a major river in Brazil, Colombia and Peru)</li></ol><p><i>Амазо́ния</i> (Amazónija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Amazon (referring to the Amazon River or Amazon basin)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Amazon (referring to the Amazons of Greek mythology)</li></ol><p><i>Амазо́ния</i> (Amazónija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>амазо́н(ка)</i> (amazón(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) amanat (a hostage from a local tribe taken by Russian troops during reconnaissance or exploration, e.g. in Siberia during the 17th century's colonization)</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic language from Arabic أَمَانَة (ʔamāna, “surety”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a prostitute (with experience)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) amaranth (herb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational&#32;or&#32;color</i>) amaranth</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) pink (progressive, supportive of revolutionary ideas)</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) amaranthaceous</li></ol><p><i>амара́нт</i> (amaránt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amaranth (color&#47;dye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amaretto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>amaretto</i> (“little bitter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) strong, healthy man</li><li>(dated,&#32;dialectal) Persian longshoreman working in Caspian Sea and lower Volga ports</li><li>(dated,&#32;dialectal) loader, unskilled worker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حَمَّال (ḥammāl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barn, granary, granestore, warehouse, storehouse</li></ol><p>Via Turkic (compare Tatar <i>амбар</i> (ambar)) from Persian انبار (anbâr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) granary, barn</li></ol><p><i>амба́р</i> (ambár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambivalence</li></ol><p><i>амбивале́нтный</i> (ambivaléntnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) ambivalent</li></ol><p>German <i>ambivalent</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambigram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ambitiously</li></ol><p><i>амбицио́зный</i> (ambicióznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-conceited, proud, vain, arrogant, pretentious, showy</li><li>ambitious, aspiring, emulous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-conceit, pride, vanity, arrogance</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pretensions, claims ( to ), ambitions</li><li>ambition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amblyopia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>amblyōpia</i>, itself a borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓μβλῠωπῐ́ᾱ</i> (ămblŭōpĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambergris</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>ambra</i>, from Arabic عَنْبَر (ʕanbar), from Middle Persian <i>ʾmbl</i> (ambar, “ambergris”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embrasure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>embrasure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) odor, smell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ambré</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambrosia <i>(food of gods or delicious foods)</i></li><li>ambrosia, hogweed, ragweed, richweed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(outpatient) clinic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambulatory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) embouchure (use of the lips, facial muscles, tongue, and teeth when playing a wind instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>embouchure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulpit, ambo (raised platform)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>амъвонъ</i> (amŭvonŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ἄμβων</i> (ámbōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amenorrhoea (lack of a menstrual period)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;derogatory) an American, a Yankee</li></ol><p>Shortening of америка́нец (amerikánec) or америка́нский (amerikánskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male American <i>(citizen, resident)</i></li></ol><p><i>Аме́рика</i> (Amérika) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Americanisation (assimilation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Americanism (linguistic feature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>а sort of high-wheeled sulky, first presented in Russia in 1889 by American sportsmen</li><li>free pyramid (American pyramid), a variant of billiards</li><li>a sort of jobbing press</li></ol><p><i>америка́н(ец)</i> (amerikán(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>americano (coffee)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>americano</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Tachycineta</i>-genus bird</li></ol><p><i>америка́нская</i> (amerikánskaja, “American”) +&lrm; <i>ла́сточка</i> (lástočka, “swallow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Dream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rollercoaster (amusement ride)</li></ol><p>Literally, “American small mountains”, or more loosely translated as “American hills”. Compare French <i>montagnes russes</i> and its derivatives in other languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) American</li></ol><p><i>Аме́рика</i> (Amérika) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Bulldog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Indian (a member of some indigenous peoples of the Americas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afro-American (a resident of the United States who is of African heritage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American football</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Sign Language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Americophilia (an affection for, or interest in things related to the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnic slur</i>) an American, a Yank</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>américas</i> (“the Americas, North and South America”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Americophilia (an affection for, or interest in things related to the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Americophobia (a fear of the United States of America and&#47;or its culture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Americocentrism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>americium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amethyst</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;humorous,&#32;often&#32;derogatory) atheist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀμέθυστος</i> (améthustos)</p><p>Deliberate misspelling of атеист</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) amethyst</li><li>amethyst (having a colour similar to that of the gemstone)</li></ol><p><i>амети́ст</i> (ametíst) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amylase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) amine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(organic chemistry,&#32;biochemistry) amino acid</li></ol><p>First attested in 1877. <i>ами́н</i> (amín) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кислота́</i> (kislotá), calque of French <i>acide aminé</i> or German <i>Aminosäure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;chemistry) amino acid</li></ol><p><i>аминокислота́</i> (aminokislotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>amen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>аминъ</i> (aminŭ), from Koine Greek <i>ἀμήν</i> (amḗn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אמן</i> (amén, “certainly, truly”); cognate with Arabic آمين (ʔāmīn), Classical Syriac <i>ܐܡܝܢ</i> ('āmēn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amiodarone (medicine used against arrhythmia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) amitriptyline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ammonia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) ammonium</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) ammonium carbonate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ammoni(um)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amnesia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀμνησία</i> (amnēsía, “forgetfulness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amnesty</li><li>free pardon, let-off</li><li>act of grace</li></ol><p>From obsolete Polish <i>amnistja</i> (now <i>amnestia</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amoxicillin (antibiotic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) amoralism</li></ol><p>Possibly from French <i>amoralisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amoral</li><li>immoral</li></ol><p>Most likely based on either French <i>amoral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or German <i>amoral(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). The latter has both meanings "immoral" and "amoral".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(device to absorb shocks) shock absorber, cushion, dashpot</li></ol><p>From French <i>amortisseur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) depreciation, amortization (process of distributing asset cost)</li><li>shock absorption, cushioning</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) dampening</li><li>buffer action</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>amortisation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amorpha (<i>shrub</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Amorpha</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἄμορφος</i> (ámorphos, “shapeless”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amorphous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampere</li></ol><p>Named after the French physicist André-Marie Ampère.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampere-turn</li></ol><p>First attested in 1900. <i>ампе́р</i> (ampér) +&lrm; <i>вито́к</i> (vitók), calque of German <i>Amperewindung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampere-hour</li></ol><p>First attested in 1900. <i>ампе́р</i> (ampér) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas), calque of German <i>Amperestunde</i> or French <i>ampère-heure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ammeter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampersand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Empire style</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>empire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amplitude (maximum absolute value)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>amplitūdō</i>, from Latin <i>amplus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amplitude modulation (method of sending information)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) amplitude</li><li>(rare,&#32;by extension) pronounced, of large amplitude</li></ol><p><i>амплиту́да</i> (amplitúda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type, theatrical character, line, (figuratively) part, role</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>emploi</i>. Further from Latin <i>implicō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleḱ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampoule</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amputation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amputate</li></ol><p>From German <i>amputieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Sign Language (language that uses hands, facial expressions, and other bodily behavior to communicate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amsterdamer, resident of Amsterdam</li></ol><p><i>Амстерда́м</i> (Amstɛrdám, Amsterdám) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amulet, charm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military equipment, accouterments</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupid (representation of the god Cupid as a boy with a bow and arrow)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) handsome boy</li><li>(plural only,&#32;dated,&#32;colloquial) love affairs</li><li>amur (carp-like fish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>amour</i>.</p><p>From <i>Аму́р</i> (Amúr, “Amur river”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Амур)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amur (a river on the border between China and Russia)</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Cupid (God of love)</li></ol><p>Of Tungusic origin. From the same root whence Solon <i>amur</i> (“river”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>Amour</i>, calque of Latin <i>Cupīdō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of Amursk or Amur Oblast</li></ol><p>From <i>Аму́р</i> (Amúr) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec) (for "Amur Oblast") or from <i>Аму́р(ск)</i> (Amúr(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec) (for "Amursk").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amorous</li></ol><p><i>аму́р</i> (amúr, “Cupid”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Amur</li></ol><p><i>Аму́р</i> (Amúr) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) love affair</li></ol><p>From <i>Аму́р</i> (Amúr, “Cupid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amuzgo (indigenous language of Mexico)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) amphetamine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(zoology,&#32;animate) amphibian</li><li>(transport,&#32;inanimate) amphibian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphibole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphibrach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphiprostyle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphitheatre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) amphora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Amharic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amharic (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) amoeba (a genus of unicellular protozoa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amoebic</li></ol><p><i>амёба</i> (amjóba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nevertheless; but in fact</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;adversative) Used at the beginning of a sentence to express opposition to a remark.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anabasis (military march up-country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀνάβασις</i> (Anábasis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anabiosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anabolism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anabolic steroid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anagram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anadromous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anacoluthon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anaconda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anacrusis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anal sex (sex involving the anus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analemma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analysis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>analyse</i>&nbsp;f.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analyzer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to analyse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analyst</li><li>(<i>media</i>) pundit, commentator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analytics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>analytically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analytic</li></ol><p><i>анали́т(ика)</i> (analít(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analogue</li><li>counterpart</li><li>equivalent</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάλογος</i> (análogos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a similar / like manner, in much the same way / manner / fashion (as), similarly / analogously (to)</li></ol><p><i>аналоги́чный</i> (analogíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analogous, similar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analogy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀναλογίᾱ</i> (analogíā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;offensive) Wunderwaffe (secret weapon design claimed to be revolutionary and capable of winning the war)</li><li><i>(by extension)</i> any technology claimed to be revolutionary</li></ol><p>From <i>ана́логов</i> (análogov, “analog, comparison”) +&lrm; <i>нет</i> (net, “no, not”). Or alternatively interpreted as <i>ана́л</i> (anál, “anal”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-ет</i> (-et).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analog (represented by a continuously variable physical quantity)</li></ol><p><i>ана́лог</i> (análog) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lectern (in church)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analgesic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analgesic</li></ol><p><i>анальге́т(ик)</i> (analʹgét(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) metamizole, dipyrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anus, anal opening (lower opening of the digestive tract)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anal fin (fin on the underside of a fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anal sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) buttfucker, bummer</li></ol><p><i>ана́л</i> (anál) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anamnesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) the other day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pineapple <i>(fruit or plant)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anapest</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάπαιστος</i> (anápaistos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;politics) anarchism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anarchist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>anarchiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anarchistic</li></ol><p><i>анархи́ст</i> (anarxíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anarchically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anarchic, anarchical</li><li>chaotic, without law or order</li></ol><p>From <i>ана́рхия</i> (anárxija), from French <i>anarchie</i> or German <i>Anarchie</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>anarchia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀναρχία</i> (anarkhía), from <i>ἄναρχος</i> (ánarkhos, “without a leader”), from <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) + <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anarchy (various senses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀναρχία</i> (anarkhía), from <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>anarcho- (denoting anarchy or anarchism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) anarcho-communism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anatolian</li></ol><p><i>Анато́лия</i> (Anatólija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anatomist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anatomy, dissection</li></ol><p><i>анатоми́ровать</i> (anatomírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to dissect, to anatomize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anatomical</li></ol><p><i>анато́мия</i> (anatómija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anatomy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>anatomia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνατομή</i> (anatomḗ). Further from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνατέμνω</i> (anatémnō), from <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) + Ancient Greek <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;temh₁-</i> (whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęti</i>, Latin <i>temnō</i>, <i>tondeō</i>, and possibly <i>tempus</i>, <i>templum</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) anathema, excommunication</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;humorous) ostracism, ban</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) A profanity that expresses one's displeasure with the situation; damn, dammit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάθεμα</i> (anáthema).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>immunology</i>) anaphylaxis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anaphylactic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anaphylactic shock (severe allergic reaction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anaphora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchorite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anachronism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anachronistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anachronistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a type of) cannabis, hemp; marijuana, hashish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tajik <i>нашъа</i> (naš'a) or Uzbek <i>nasha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retainer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;formal) to engage; to book (an actor, a speaker)</li></ol><p>French <i>engag(er)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hangar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hangar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angel</li><li>an innocent</li><li>dear, darling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos). Doublet of <i>а́ггел</i> (ággel).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angel of death (a personification of death)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian angel (spirit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angelica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>а́нгел</i> (ángel): little angel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) little angel, little darling (child with a sweet disposition)</li></ol><p><i>а́нгел</i> (ángel) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angellike</li></ol><p><i>а́нгел</i> (ángel) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>ангело́к</i> (angelók): dear little angel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) little angel, little darling (child with a sweet disposition)</li></ol><p><i>ангело́к</i> (angelók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(recreational drug,&#32;slang) phencyclidine, angel dust</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>angel dust</i>. Apparently already in 1978 from the United States language and finally in 1985.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angelic</li></ol><p><i>а́нгел</i> (ángel) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tonsillitis (an (inflammatory) infection of the throat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(oncology,&#32;pathology) angioma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) Eng., eng. (<i>English</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglicisation/anglicization (process of anglicising)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anglicise (make English)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be anglicised (become English)</li></ol><p><i>англизи́ровать</i> (anglizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safety pin (<i>lit.: English pin</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>по-англи́йски</i> (po-anglíjski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>English</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>English language, ellipsis of <i>англи́йский язы́к</i> (anglíjskij jazýk)</li></ol><p><i>А́нглия</i> (Ánglija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cor anglais, English horn (musical instrument related to the oboe)</li></ol><p>From English <i>English horn</i>. See cor anglais for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglicanism (beliefs and practices of the Anglican Church)</li></ol><p><i>англика́нин</i> (anglikánin) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humanities</i>) English studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglicisation/anglicization (process of anglicising)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglicism</li></ol><p>Probably from Polish <i>anglicyzm</i> or German <i>Anglizismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Englishman, English people</li></ol><p><i>А́нглия</i> (Ánglija) +&lrm; <i>-ча́нин</i> (-čánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Anglo- (relating to England or English)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-American (of relating to England and USA)</li></ol><p>From <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>америка́нский</i> (amerikánskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-Egyptian</li></ol><p><i>а́нгло-</i> (ánglo-) +&lrm; <i>еги́петский</i> (jegípetskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-Norman (Norman who settled in England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-Norman (relating to the Norman rule of England)</li></ol><p><i>а́нгло-норма́нн</i> (ánglo-normánn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-Russian (relating to both England and Russia)</li></ol><p><i>а́нгло-</i> (ánglo-) +&lrm; <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>English–Russian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglophone (English-speaking)</li></ol><p>From <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>говоря́щий</i> (govorjáščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglomania</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Anglomaniac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Anglo-Saxon (historical Germanic people in Britain)</li><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) English-speaking Western countries; especially the United States and Britain.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Anglo-Saxon (related to the Anglo-Saxon peoples or the historical Germanic language in Britain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglosphere (set of anglophone countries)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>сфе́ра</i> (sféra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglophile</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>-фи́л</i> (-fíl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglophobe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>-фо́б</i> (-fób).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglophobia</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>-фо́бия</i> (-fóbija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglophone (English-speaking)</li></ol><p>From <i>англо-</i> (anglo-) +&lrm; <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Polish <i>anglojęzyczny</i>, Czech <i>anglojazyčný</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>англи́йский</i> (anglíjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Angolan (citizen, resident)</li></ol><p><i>Анго́ла</i> (Angóla) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Angolan</li></ol><p><i>Анго́ла</i> (Angóla) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angstrom (a very small unit of length, 10<sup>-10</sup> m)</li></ol><p>From the name of the Swedish physicist Anders Jonas Ångström.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andalusian (a native or inhabitant of Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>From <i>Андалу́з(ия)</i> (Andalúz(ija), “Andalusia”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>andalusite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Andalusian</li></ol><p><i>Андалу́зия</i> (Andalúzija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andalusian (a native or inhabitant of Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>From <i>Андалу́с(ия)</i> (Andalús(ija), “Andalusia”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andalusian (a native or inhabitant of Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>From <i>Андалу́си(я)</i> (Andalúsi(ja), “Andalusia”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Andaman</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>Andaman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground (artistic or social movement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>underground</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>androgen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) androgen; androgenic</li></ol><p><i>андроге́н</i> (androgén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>android</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>androphobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aneurysm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joke in the form of a story with a funny punchline/ending</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) funny incident from one's life resembling such a joke</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) joke (something fake or fictional)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) anecdote (not of a comedic nature)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>anecdote</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνέκδοτος</i> (anékdotos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anemic</li></ol><p><i>анеми́я</i> (anemíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anemia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anemone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anemophobe, someone suffering from anemophobia.</li></ol><p>Related to анемофо́бия (anemofóbija, “anemophobia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahnenerbe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aneroid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) anaesthetist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anesthetizing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anesthesia (loss of bodily sensation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anesthetic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enjambment (technique in poetry whereby a sentence is carried over to the next line without pause)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>enjambement</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resident or native of the Ani region of Armenia (around Aniavan and Maralik)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) resident or native of the Kingdom of Ani (Bagratuni Kingdom of Armenia in the Middle Ages)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ани</i> (Ani) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anisotropy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) aniline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anilingus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anilingus (form of oral sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animatronics (a form of robotics)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>animatronics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animation (causing images to appear to move)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>animātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anime</i>) anime</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>アニメ</i> (anime).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anime</i>) otaku</li></ol><p>From <i>аниме́</i> (animɛ́) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anime</li><li>anime-like</li></ol><p>From <i>аниме́</i> (animɛ́) +&lrm; <i>-шный</i> (-šnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anime</i>) alternative form of <i>аниме́</i> (animɛ́) anime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) anion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anise</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>anīsum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of a clockwork, ensuring uniformity of its stroke, a type of escapement</li><li>old measure of volume, used for wine, brandy and oil</li><li>fastener for fixing of structures and equipment</li><li>a device that serves to transmit the pull out forces from building structures to the ground layer</li><li>a device with known coordinates, the coordinates of which are determined relative to markers used in positioning systems</li><li>truss rod (metal rod inside the neck of a guitar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Anker</i> (“anchor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>questionnaire, form</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>enquête</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surveying, questioning (<i>collection of information through a survey or questionnaire</i>)</li></ol><p><i>анкети́ровать</i> (anketírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survey (to collect information through a survey or questionnaire)</li></ol><p><i>анке́та</i> (ankéta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) questionnaire, form</li><li>questionnaire-like; characteristic of a questionnaire</li></ol><p><i>анке́та</i> (ankéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankylosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankh (cross shaped like a T with a loop at the top)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) anlaut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Anlaut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) to annex (a territory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) annexation (the act of annexing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>annexion</i>. First attested in 1771.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) annihilation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>annihilation</i>, from Latin <i>annihilātio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annotation (comment added to a text)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annotation (process of writing a comment or commentary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annul, to cancel, to nullify</li><li>to abrogate, to rescind, to repeal, to revoke, to abolish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anoa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) anode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anodic</li></ol><p><i>ано́д</i> (anód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>anolis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anomaly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνωμαλία</i> (anōmalía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anomalously, abnormally (in an abnormal manner)</li></ol><p><i>анома́льный</i> (anomálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anomalous</li></ol><p><i>анома́лия</i> (anomálija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) clipping of <i>анони́м</i> (anoním): anon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anonymous, especially anonymous author</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) anonymous letter</li></ol><p>From <i>анони́м(ный)</i> (anoním(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from an ellipsis of <i>анони́мное письмо́</i> (anonímnoje pisʹmó, “anonymous letter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anonymously</li></ol><p><i>анони́мный</i> (anonímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anonymity</li></ol><p><i>анони́мный</i> (anonímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anonymous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>announcement, notice, advertisement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>annonce</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>nūntius</i>. Doublet of <i>денонса́ция</i> (denonsácija), <i>ну́нций</i> (núncij), and <i>проно́нс</i> (pronóns).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to announce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>annoncieren</i>, French <i>annoncer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parka (long jacket)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Greenlandic <i>annoraaq</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anorexia, anorexia nervosa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anopheles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ensemble (<i>various senses</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ensemble</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) A person or a video game character which doesn’t have/require skill.</li></ol><p>From English <i>unskill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antagonism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antagonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Antarctic</li></ol><p><i>Анта́ркт(ика)</i> (Antárkt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerial, antenna</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) antenna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>anti-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-alcohol</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>алкого́льный</i> (alkogólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-Americanism (the fear or dislike of the United States or American people, policies, culture or government)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-American</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anti-Belgian</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>Бе́льгия</i> (Bélʹgija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) antibiotic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antibiotic</li></ol><p><i>антибио́т(ик)</i> (antibiót(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) antivaxer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>antivaxer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antimatter</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>вещество́</i> (veščestvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antivirus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antiviral</li></ol><p><i>антиви́рус</i> (antivírus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-war</li></ol><p>First attested in 1926. <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj), possibly, a half-calque of German <i>Antikriegs-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthelmintic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antigen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) antigen; antigenic</li></ol><p><i>антиге́н</i> (antigén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antihero (protagonist who proceeds in an unheroic manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antihistamine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allies of World War II</li></ol><p>Literally, “Anti-Hitler coalition”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antidepressant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antidote</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀντίδοτος</i> (antídotos, “antidote, remedy”), from <i>ἀντιδίδωμι</i> (antidídōmi, “I give in return, repay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Antiquar</i>, from Latin <i>antiquarius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valuable antique goods such as old maps and books.</li><li>trade in antiques.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) antiquark</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>кварк</i> (kvark).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antique, ancient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anticlericalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticoagulant</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>коагули́рующий</i> (koagulírujuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anticoagulant (substance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticoagulant</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>коагуля́нтный</i> (koaguljántnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-colonial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anticommunism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anticonstitutionally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticonstitutional, unconstitutional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antelope (any of several African mammals of the family Bovidae)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>antilope</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnu, wildebeest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Antillean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antimatter (matter composed of antiparticles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) antimetabole</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀντιμεταβολή</i> (antimetabolḗ). By surface analysis, <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>μετᾰβολή</i> (metăbolḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antitrust, antimonopoly</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>монопо́лия</i> (monopólija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antinational, antipopular</li></ol><p>First attested in 1862. <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>наро́дный</i> (naródnyj), calque of French <i>antipopulaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anti-NATO</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antinomian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antisocial</li></ol><p>From <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>обще́ственный</i> (obščéstvennyj), a calque of French <i>antisocial</i>. First attested in the 1900s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antioxidant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>antioxidant</i>. By surface analysis, <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>оксида́нт</i> (oksidánt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antiochian</li></ol><p><i>Антио́хия</i> (Antióxija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Antiochian (of or pertaining to ancient Antioch)</li></ol><p><i>Антио́хия</i> (Antióxija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antipathy (contrariety or opposition in feeling)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀντιπάθεια</i> (antipátheia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antipatriotism</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>патриоти́зм</i> (patriotízm). Compare Ukrainian <i>антипатріоти́зм</i> (antypatriotýzm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame retardant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antipyretic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>antipyretic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antipode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antipodean (situated at opposite sides of the Earth)</li></ol><p><i>антипо́д</i> (antipód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) antigovernment</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>прави́тельственный</i> (pravítelʹstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-vaxxer (someone who is against vaccinations)</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>приви́вок</i> (privívok) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstick (<i>of cookware</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antipsychotic (any of a group of drugs used to treat psychosis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) anti-Putin</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-Russian (against Russia, the Russian government or state)</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsanitary, counter to sanitary standards</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-Semite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-Semitism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-Semitic (exhibiting anti-Semitism)</li></ol><p><i>антисеми́т</i> (antisemít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiseptic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anti-Soviet</li></ol><p>From <i>анти-</i> (anti-, “anti-”) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij, “Soviet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) an anti-Soviet (dissident, opponent of Soviet Russia)</li></ol><p><i>антисове́т(ский)</i> (antisovét(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staple remover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antithesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antithesis (proposition that is opposite to other proposition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antitheism (active opposition to theism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) antibody (protein that binds to a specific antigen)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1895. <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo), calque of French <i>anticorps</i> or German <i>Antikörper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiterrorism (activity aiming at opposing terrorism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antiterrorist</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>antiterroriste</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antiplatelet (destroying blood platelets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anti-Ukrainian</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dystopia (an imagined state or society with dreadful quality of life, typically one that is totalitarian)</li><li>dystopia (a genre or work of fiction depicting such a society)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) antifascism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antifascist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antifascist</li></ol><p><i>антифаши́ст</i> (antifašíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antifibrinolytic (serving to inhibit fibrinolysis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antifreeze</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>antifreeze</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antichrist (someone that works against the teachings of Christ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>антихристъ</i> (antixristŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>антихрьстъ</i> (antixrĭstŭ), from Koine Greek <i>ἀντίχριστος</i> (antíkhristos).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Антихрист)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antichrist</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>antichristus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀντίχριστος</i> (antíkhristos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticellulite</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>целлюли́тный</i> (celljulítnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weather</i>) anticyclone</li></ol><p>From English <i>anticyclone</i> through German <i>Antizyklone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antigypsyism (prejudice or hostility against Romani people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) antiparticle</li></ol><p>From <i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>части́ца</i> (častíca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquity</li></ol><p><i>анти́чный</i> (antíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antique, ancient, classical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) antienzyme</li></ol><p><i>анти-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>энзи́м</i> (enzím)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antienzymatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antienzymatic</li></ol><p><i>антиэнзи́м</i> (antienzím) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthology (collection of literary works)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antonym</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>antonyme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antonymy (semantic relation between antonyms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antonovka (an apple cultivar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a guerrilla movement active in 1920–22 mainly in regions east and south of Tambov city during the Russian Civil War</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interval; intermission</li><li>interlude, entr'acte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entracte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthracite (form of carbon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anthracite</li><li>anthracite (color)</li></ol><p><i>антраци́т</i> (antracít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrechat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entrechat</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private entertainment company (<i>of the theater, circus, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) beginning, venture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entreprise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attic</li><li>mezzanine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entresol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) attic</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) mezzanine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entresol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthropogenic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>anthropogen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthropologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthropological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthropology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthropomorphism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthropomorphic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthroponym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthroponymy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthropochory (the dispersal of seeds, spores, or fruit by humans)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>environment, surroundings, setting (set of environmental conditions)</li><li>entourage (retinue of attendants, associates or followers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>entourage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>антиохи́йский</i> (antioxíjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) anus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ānus</i> (“ring, anus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anusvara (diacritic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अनुस्वार</i> (anusvāra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>full-face, face on (with the face to the viewer, pointing straight ahead)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>en face</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>in</i> + Latin <i>faciēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;military) enfilade</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) successively alternating series of events</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>enfilade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankh (cross shaped like a T with a loop at the top)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botanical</i>) upas (<i>Antiaris</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchovy</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>ansjovis</i> (attested as <i>анчовес</i> in 1724), from Spanish <i>anchoa</i>, from Ligurian <i>anciôa</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;apiuva</i>, from Latin <i>aphyē</i> (“small fry”), from Ancient Greek <i>ἀφύη</i> (aphúē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anchovy</li></ol><p><i>анчо́ус</i> (ančóus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anchovy</li></ol><p><i>анчо́ус</i> (ančóus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) little devil, imp (in East Slavic mythology, an evil spirit or imp, one of the most ancient names of the devil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the "sold out" notice, full house (e.g. on a theatre performance, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Anschlag</i> (“poster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anschluss (the annexation of Austria into Germany in 1938 or an analogy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Anschluss</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anestrus (sexual respite in between breeding periods)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macanese (person from Macau)</li></ol><p>From <i>Аомы́нь</i> (Macau) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) aorist, aorist aspect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀόριστος</i> (aóristos, “not done, not completed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aorist (relating to the aorist aspect or aorist tense in verbs)</li></ol><p><i>ао́рист</i> (aórist) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) aorta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀορτή</i> (aortḗ, “the arteries springing from the heart”), from <i>ἀορτέω</i> (aortéō), lengthened form of <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “I lift, raise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>renal-aortic (relating to the place of origin of the renal arteries from the abdominal aorta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aortal, aortic</li></ol><p>Perhaps ultimately from Latin <i>aortālis</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from <i>aorta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apadana (a large hypostyle hall in ancient Persian palaces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Tatarstan,&#32;Bashkortostan,&#32;Chelyabinsk,&#32;Central Asia) A (generally Turkic-speaking or Asian and typically older or married) woman</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>Especially</i>, a female teacher</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language, such as Kazakh <i>апай</i> (apai), with addition of <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) apartment</li></ol><p>First attested in 1705. Borrowed from Polish <i>apartament</i>, from French <i>appartement</i>, from Italian <i>appartamento</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apartheid (policy of racial separation in South Africa)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Afrikaans <i>apartheid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) apatheism (apathy concerning the existence of God)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>апа́тия</i> (apátija, “apathy”) +&lrm; <i>теи́зм</i> (tɛízm, “theism”), calque of English <i>apatheism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apatite (type of mineral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apathetically</li></ol><p><i>апати́чный</i> (apatíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apathetic (void of feeling)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apathy, indifference</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπάθεια</i> (apátheia, “freedom from suffering”), from <i>ἀπαθής</i> (apathḗs, “without feeling”), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) + <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “pain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A stateless person, apatride</li></ol><p>From French <i>apatride</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἄπατρις</i> (ápatris).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to bump (to raise a discussion thread to the top of the list)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;video games) to level up, to upgrade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>up</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>апа́тия</i> (apátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apache (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apache (language)</li><li>Apache (people)</li><li>Apache (person)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Апачи)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apache (a town in Oklahoma, United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upgrade (an improved component or replacement item)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>upgrade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;media) update (action of making something up to date)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>update</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appealing</li></ol><p><i>апелли́ровать</i> (apellírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) to appeal <i>(to)</i></li><li>to address, to appeal <i>(to)</i></li><li>to make reference <i>(to)</i></li><li>to appeal '<i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>apellieren</i> (variant of <i>appellieren</i>) + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appellate</li></ol><p><i>апелля́ция</i> (apelljácija) +&lrm; <i>-онный</i> (-onnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appeal (application for the removal of a cause to a superior judge for reexamination)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>appellātiō</i> via Polish <i>apelacja</i>. In use since the early 1700s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orange tree</li><li>orange (fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>appelsien</i>, calqued from French <i>pomme de Sine</i> (“Chinese apple”).</p><p>For semantic parallels compare <i>пе́рсик</i> (pérsik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;familiar,&#32;ironic) to be or act gentle, tender, nice or cutesy</li></ol><p>From <i>апельси́н</i> (apelʹsín, “orange”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ). Coined by Russian novelist Fyodor Dostoyevsky in the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orange (tree)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) orange</li><li>bright orange, Indian orange (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>апельси́н</i> (apelʹsín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orange juice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appetizer, apéritif</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>apéritif</i> (whence multiple borrowings: Bulgarian <i>аперитив</i> (aperitiv), Czech <i>aperitiv</i>, Polish <i>aperitif</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>аперитив</i>, and many more), from Latin <i>aperitīvus</i> (variant of <i>apertīvus</i>) &lt; <i>aperiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aperture (something which restricts the diameter of the light path through one plane in an optical system)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>apertura</i>. Doublet of <i>увертю́ра</i> (uvertjúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;botany) apical</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>apical</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>аппле́т</i> (applét)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicant (one who applies)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>applicant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to applaud, to cheer, to clap</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>applaudere</i>, presumably through French <i>applaudir</i> or German <i>applaudieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applause, clapping, cheers</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>applaudissement</i> (generally used in the plural).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formerly</i>) aplomb, self-confidence</li><li>excessive confidence, arrogance</li><li>(<i>ballet</i>) aplomb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aplomb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to bump (to raise a discussion thread to the top of the list)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;video games) to level up, to upgrade</li></ol><p>From English <i>up</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;video games) to level up (to progress to the next level)</li></ol><p><i>а́пнуть</i> (ápnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apogee</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the highest point</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apograph (perfect copy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocalypse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀποκάλυψις</i> (apokálupsis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>апока́липсис</i> (apokálipsis).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocalyptic (genre of literature set in an apocalypse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>апока́липсис</i> (apokálipsis).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocope (loss or omission of a sound or syllable from the end of a word)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) apocryphal work; Apocrypha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπόκρυφος</i> (apókruphos, “hidden, obscure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apoliticism, apoliticality</li></ol><p><i>аполити́чный</i> (apolitíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apolitical</li></ol><p>French <i>apolit(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apollo (a very handsome young man)</li><li>apollo butterfly</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Аполлон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Apollo</li><li>(NASA,&#32;space science) Apollo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apologetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) apologia, apology (formal, written defense of a position or belief)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπολογία</i> (apología).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;relational) apoplexy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) irritative</li></ol><p><i>апоплексия</i> (apopleksija, “apoplexy”) +&lrm; <i>-ический</i> (-ičeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) apoplexy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) apoptosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aporia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a posteriori (in a manner that deduces theories from facts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a posteriori (involving deduction of theories from facts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostille (a special sign)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>apostille</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostilb (unit of luminance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>апостолъ</i> (apostolŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>апостолъ</i> (apostolŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓πόστολος</i> (ăpóstolos), from <i>ἀποστέλλω</i> (apostéllō, “I send off”), from <i>ἀπό-</i> (apó-, “from”) and <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “I set”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apostolic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>апостольскъ</i> (apostolĭskŭ). By surface analysis, <i>апо́стол</i> (apóstol) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) apostrophe (the character ’)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>apostrophe</i>, which in turn is borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπόστροφος</i> (apóstrophos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apotheosis (a glorified example or ideal&#59; the apex or pinnacle (of a concept or belief))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀποθέωσις</i> (apothéōsis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apophysis (outgrowth)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπόφυσις</i> (apóphusis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apophysis (branch)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) apoenzyme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apparatus, instrument, device</li><li>camera</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) apparat, State organ, the machinery of state bureaucratic administration</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>apparātus</i>; compare German <i>Apparat</i>, Dutch <i>apparaat</i> (“bureaucracy”). Cognate to English <i>apparatus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morse transmitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) hardware</li></ol><p>From <i>аппара́тное</i> (apparátnoje, “apparatus”) +&lrm; <i>обеспе́чение</i> (obespéčenije, “supply”) (or <i>обеспече́ние</i> (obespečénije, “supply”); the position of the accent has no effect on the meaning).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) device, apparatus, hardware</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) staff, personnel</li><li>hardware-controlled</li></ol><p><i>аппара́т</i> (apparát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apparatus, equipment, gear (assortment of tools or instruments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>operator of a mechanical device (apparatus)</li><li>person in charge of an apparatus</li><li>(politics,&#32;slang) apparatchik, loyal member of a Communist apparat (State organ), bureaucrat</li></ol><p><i>аппара́т</i> (apparát, “apparat, apparatus”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik, “doer”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendix (vermiform appendix)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) appendicitis</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>appendicitis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendectomy (surgical procedure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uppercut (in boxing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>uppercut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appetite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>appetītus</i>. Compare typologically <i>жор</i> (žor) + <i>напа́сть</i>&nbsp;pf (napástʹ) collocations.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appetizing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) attractive, seductive (<i>of a woman</i>)</li></ol><p><i>аппети́т</i> (appetít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) applet (a small program module that runs under the control of a larger application)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>applet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicant (one who applies)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>applicant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applique, appliqué (decorative design)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) appointment (arrangement for a meeting&#59; an engagement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>appointment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>апре́ль</i> (aprélʹ) or <i>апре́ля</i> (aprélja, “April”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apraxia (disorder of motor planning)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>April</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>апрѣль</i> (aprělĭ), earlier априль (aprilĭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>Ἀπρίλιος</i> (Aprílios), from Latin <i>Aprīlis</i> from (perhaps) Etruscan <i>𐌀𐌐𐌓𐌖</i> (apru) from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀφροδίτη</i> (Aphrodítē). The form with <i>ѣ</i> in the second syllable is explained by Sobolevsky as a loan from the Novgorodian written language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) April</li></ol><p><i>апре́ль</i> (aprélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a priori</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a priori</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approval, approbation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>approbātiō</i>. Also derivable as <i>апроб(и́ровать)</i> (aprob(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approve</li><li>to test</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>approbieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) upsail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>aap-zeil</i> (“monkey sail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apse, apsis, exedra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἁψίς</i> (hapsís).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemist (store); drugstore; pharmacy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>аптека</i> (apteka), from Old Ruthenian <i>аптека</i> (apteka), from Middle Polish <i>apteka</i> (16th c.). Perhaps from Old Polish <i>apoteka</i> (att. 1484) and Middle High German <i>apothēke</i>, but more likely from Middle Low German <i>abteke</i>. Either way, from Latin <i>apothēca</i> (“repository”) and from Ancient Greek <i>ἀποθήκη</i> (apothḗkē), itself from <i>ἀποτίθημι</i> (apotíthēmi, “to put away”), from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) + <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epó</i> and <i>&#42;dʰé-dʰeh₁-ti</i>. Doublet of <i>бути́к</i> (butík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pharmacist, pharmacy</li></ol><p><i>апте́карь</i> (aptékarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacist's wife</li></ol><p>From <i>апте́карь</i> (aptékarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacist, chemist, druggist (male or female)</li></ol><p><i>апте́ка</i> (aptéka) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first-aid box; medicine chest</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>апте́ка</i> (aptéka): small pharmacy</li></ol><p><i>апте́ка</i> (aptéka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pharmacy, chemist's, drugstore</li></ol><p><i>апте́ка</i> (aptéka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>achoo (the sound of a sneeze)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male inhabitant of Apsheronsk or Apsheronsky District</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>are, measurement of area<i>(100 sq. m.)</i></li><li>R, r (English letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) macaw</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;ironic,&#32;ethnic slur) an Armenian male</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Brazilian Portuguese <i>ará</i>, in turn borrowed from Old Tupi <i>ará</i>.</p><p>From Armenian <i>արա</i> (ara, “an informal way of addressing a familiar male person”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arab (by ethnicity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Arabe</i>, from Latin <i>Arabus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arabesque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arabesque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arabesque <i>(ornament)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arabesque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabisation/Arabization (process of arabising)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Arabize (to make Arab)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anthropology,&#32;linguistics) Arabist (a scholar who studies Arab or Arabic culture, politics, or language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabic studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabic script</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabophobia (the fear, hate or dislike of Arabs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabic numeral (any of the ten symbols from 0 through 9)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arabic, Arabian</li><li>Arab</li></ol><p><i>ара́б</i> (aráb) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Arab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrack</li></ol><p>From Arabic عَرَق (ʕaraq)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aramaean (person)</li></ol><p><i>араме́й</i> (araméj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aramaean (people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aramaic</li></ol><p><i>араме́й</i> (araméj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An aranga (burial scaffold traditionally used by Yakuts, Buryats, Tofalars, Evenks, Orochons and some other native nations of Siberia and the Russian Far East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(zoology,&#32;taxonomy) araneomorph</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Araneomorphae</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to arrange (to adapt an existing composition for presentation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) arrangement</li></ol><p>From <i>аранжи́ровать</i> (aranžírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Negro</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) an African servant, especially one who served Russian nobility during the 18th-early 19th centuries.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a swindler, someone who cheats in card gambling games</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>арапъ</i> (arapŭ), from Turkic; compare Turkish <i>Arap</i> (“Arab”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ararat</li></ol><p><i>Арара́т</i> (Ararát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arachidonic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arachidonic</li></ol><p>Possibly from English <i>arachidon(ic)</i> +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peanut, goober</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀράχιδνα</i> (arákhidna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peanut</li></ol><p><i>ара́хис</i> (aráxis) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arachnophobia (an abnormal or irrational fear of spiders)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>araba (a horse- or bullock-drawn cart with two large wheels)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source. See <i>araba</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossbow (mechanized weapon based on the bow), ballista</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arbalète</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Arbat (historic street in Moscow)</li></ol><p><i>Арба́т</i> (Arbát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) arbiter, arbitrator (<i>to resolve controversy</i>)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) referee, umpire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arbitre</i>, from Latin <i>arbiter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arbitration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arbitrage</i>. Further from Latin <i>arbitror</i> + <i>-āticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) arbitration; arbitral</li></ol><p><i>арбитра́ж</i> (arbitráž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arboretum (place of many varieties of trees)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watermelon</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) milliard, billion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>арбузъ</i> (arbuzŭ), via Turkic from Persian خربز (xarboz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) watermelon</li></ol><p><i>арбу́з</i> (arbúz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argamak (fine horse)</li></ol><p>See also</p><p>Uzbek <i>argʻumoq</i>,</p><p>Kazakh <i>арғымақ</i> (arğymaq),</p><p>Turkmen <i>argamak</i>,</p><p>Tatar <i>аргамак</i> (arğamaq),</p><p>Bashkir <i>арғымаҡ</i> (arğımaq),</p><p>Southern Altai <i>аргымак</i> (argïmak),</p><p>Uyghur ئارغىماق (arghimaq),</p><p>Mongolian <i>аргамаг</i> (argamag).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Argand lamp (a lamp with a circular hollow wick and glass chimney)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any tick of the genus <i>Argas</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Argas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argentinian (person from Argentina)</li></ol><p><i>Аргенти́на</i> (Argentína) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Argentinian (relating to Argentina)</li></ol><p><i>Аргенти́на</i> (Argentína) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver (element)</li></ol><p>Latin argentum.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arginine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) argot, slang (secret language of thieves, tramps and vagabonds)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>argot</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Арго)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) <i>Argo</i> (mythical ship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argon</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>argon</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ᾱ̓ργόν</i> (ārgón), the neuter substantive form of <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós, “idle, lazy”) because of the element's inertness.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argonaut</li><li>argonaut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument, reason (fact or statement used to support a proposition)</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;programming) argument</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argumentation, reasoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argumentation</li></ol><p><i>аргументи́ровать</i> (argumentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to argue, to justify, to motivate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by means of a justification’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>argumentieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) area</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) angry old curmudgeon</li></ol><p>From <i>А́ред</i> (Áred), variant of <i>Иа́ред</i> (Iáred, “Jared (Old Testament patriarch who lived 962 years)”), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάρετ</i> (Iáret), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יָרֶד</i> (yā́reḏ), pausal form of <i>יֶרֶד</i> (yéreḏ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old curmudgeon's</li></ol><p><i>а́ред</i> (áred) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arena</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>arēna</i> (<i>harēna</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ателье́</i> (atelʹjé) (<&lt; Latin <i>assula</i>), another example of a metonymic shift from a matter to a place (a common noun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) contemporary R&B</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>R'n'B</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lease, rent</li><li>leasehold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>аренда</i> (arenda) (att. 1665), from Old Ruthenian <i>аренда</i> (arenda) (att. 1550s; whence Ukrainian <i>оренда</i> (orenda)), from Middle Polish <i>arenda</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>arrenda</i>. Ultimately from <i>ad-</i> + Vulgar Latin <i>rendo</i> (“give back”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenant (one who pays a fee in return for the use of land, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>arrenda</i>, from <i>reddo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rent</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rental</li></ol><p><i>аре́нда</i> (arénda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lease; to rent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>arendować</i>, from <i>arenda</i>, from Latin <i>arrenda</i>, from <i>arrendare</i>, from <i>&#42;adrendare</i>, from <i>reddere</i>; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>areola (circle around the nipple)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrestee, prisoner, jailbird (a person who is under arrest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) convict, prisoner; convict's, prisoner's</li></ol><p><i>ареста́нт</i> (arestánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrested, apprehended</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrestee (a person who is under arrest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrest</li></ol><p><i>арестова́ть</i> (arestovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arian</li></ol><p><i>ариа́н(ин)</i> (arián(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aryan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Aristarch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male aristocrat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(imagined) aristocratic behavior (subtlety, nobility, elegance, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>аристокра́т</i> (aristokrát) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aristocratic</li></ol><p><i>аристокра́тия</i> (aristokrátija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aristocratic</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) grand</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>аристокра́т</i> (aristokrát) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aristocracy, nobility</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>aristocratia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀριστοκρατία</i> (aristokratía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>аристокра́тия</i> (aristokrátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristotelianism</li></ol><p><i>Аристо́тель</i> (Aristótelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) arrhythmia</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρρυθμία</i> (arrhuthmía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arithmetic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arithmetic progression (sequence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arithmetic, arithmetical</li></ol><p><i>арифме́т(ика)</i> (arifmét(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) aria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>aria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>арифме́тика</i> (arifmétika).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arch, archway, arc</li><li>Any clam of the genus <i>Arca</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arcade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arcade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lasso, lariat</li><li>noose</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source. Compare Bashkir <i>арҡан</i> (arqan), Kazakh <i>арқан</i> (arqan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harquebus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) arccosine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>arcus</i> + <i>complementi sinus</i> (literally “sine of the complement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) arctangent</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>arcus</i> + <i>tangens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arctic</li></ol><p><i>А́ркт(ика)</i> (Árkt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arctic hare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armada</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armagnac (brandy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>armagnac</i>, from <i>Armagnac</i>, a region and former county in France.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fittings</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) armature</li><li>steel-concrete reinforcement, rebar</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>armatura</i>. First attested in 1703.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) soldier, serviceman</li><li>(historical,&#32;pre-Revolutionary Russia) army officer or infantryman</li></ol><p><i>а́рмия</i> (ármija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) army</li></ol><p><i>а́рмия</i> (ármija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenologist (one who engages in Armenology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenology (field of study of the Armenian history, language, religion and culture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenologist (one who engages in Armenology)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>арменове́дение</i> (armenovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenology (field of study of the Armenian history, language, religion and culture)</li></ol><p><i>Арме́ния</i> (Arménija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Armenological (relating to Armenology)</li></ol><p><i>арменове́д(ение)</i> (armenovéd(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenology (field of study of the Armenian history, language, religion and culture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) armoury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, armed forces (the complete military organization of a nation, including land, sea and air forces)</li><li>army (specifically the land-based forces of a nation's military)</li><li>army, field army (a large military formation subdivided into several corps, divisions, or brigades)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>армѣя</i> (arměja)/<i>армея</i> (armeja), directly from French <i>armée</i> (“army; armed forces”) or via German <i>Armee</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm wrestling (a strength sport with two participants)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>armwrestling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) kind of long garment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenian (citizen, resident, or of heritage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>армѣнинъ</i> (arměninŭ), <i>рамѣнинъ</i> (raměninŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρμένιος</i> (Arménios, “Armenian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenophile (someone with unusual admiration or partiality for the Armenian people, its culture, language or history)</li></ol><p><i>армя́не</i> (armjáne) +&lrm; <i>-фил</i> (-fil)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenophilia (unusual admiration or partiality for the Armenian people, its culture, language or history)</li></ol><p><i>армя́не</i> (armjáne, “Armenians”) +&lrm; <i>-филия</i> (-filija, “-philia”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenophobe (a person having a fear, dislike of, hatred or aversion to the Armenians, Republic of Armenia and the Armenian culture)</li></ol><p><i>армя́не</i> (armjáne, “Armenians”) +&lrm; <i>-фоб</i> (-fob, “-phobe”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenophobia (the fear, dislike of, hatred or aversion to the Armenians, Republic of Armenia and the Armenian culture)</li></ol><p><i>армя́не</i> (armjáne, “Armenians”) +&lrm; <i>-фобия</i> (-fobija, “-phobia”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>а́рмия</i> (ármija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arity (number of arguments)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>arity</i>, formed analogously from adjectives ending in <i>-арный</i> (-arnyj) (e.g. <i>уна́рный</i> (unárnyj, “unary”), <i>бина́рный</i> (binárnyj, “binary”), <i>терна́рный</i> (ternárnyj, “ternary”)) + <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aroma, fragrance, scent, perfume</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ароматъ</i> (aromatŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ἄρομα</i> (ároma), genitive <i>ἀρομάτος</i> (aromátos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aromatherapy</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ἄρωμα</i> (árōma) + <i>терапи́я</i> (terapíja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aroma compound, odorant</li><li>air freshener, deodorizer</li><li>flavoring (food)</li><li>attractant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aromatize</li></ol><p>From <i>арома́т</i> (aromát, “aroma, fragrance”) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) aromatic, euodic</li><li>fragrant, scented</li></ol><p><i>арома́т</i> (aromát) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aromatic, fragrant</li></ol><p><i>арома́т</i> (aromát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian (language)</li></ol><p><i>арому́н</i> (aromún) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrondissement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arrondissement</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) arpeggio (notes of a chord played individually)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>arpeggio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) arsenal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arsenal</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>zbrojnice</i> «збройница», Polish <i>zbrojownia</i> «зброёвня».</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) arsenide (compound with arsenic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arsenic (chemical element)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art dealer (someone who buys and sells paintings and&#47;or sculptures)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>art dealer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art director</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>art director</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an artel: a worker or peasant cooperative.</li></ol><p>Unknown or disputed: borrowed from Italian <i>artiere</i> or from Turkish or Uralic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arterial</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>artériel</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artery</li><li>through-passage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artifact (archaeological object)</li><li>visual artifact, distortion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Artefakt</i>. Further from Latin <i>ars</i> + Latin <i>factus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) article <i>(part of speech)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>article</i>, from Latin <i>articulus</i>. Doublet of <i>арти́кул</i> (artíkul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>article, item, commodity</li><li>model number, item code</li><li>(law,&#32;dated) article (<i>of a statute or legal document</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Artikel</i>, from Latin <i>articulus</i>; attested 1704. Displaced the earlier form <i>арты́кул</i> (artýkul), which was borrowed from Polish <i>artykuł</i>. Doublet of <i>арти́кль</i> (artíklʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;music) articulation</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>articulatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artillery preparation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) artillery</li></ol><p><i>артилле́рия</i> (artillérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artillery fire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artilleryman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Artillerist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artillery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>artillerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artist, actor, performer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>artiste</i>. First attested in 1775. Ultimately from Latin <i>ars</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) an unfortunate, a loser (someone who has not lived up to expectations in some activity)</li></ol><p>Literally, “actor (artist) of the burned-out theater”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artistic skills, artistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artistic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>арти́ст</i> (artíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artistic</li></ol><p>French <i>artisti(que)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) artificial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>artificiālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artichoke</li></ol><p>From Northern Italian <i>articiocco</i>, from Provençal <i>archichaut</i>, <i>arquichaut</i>, from Old Spanish <i>alcarchofa</i>, from Andalusian Arabic الْخَرْشُوف (al-ḵaršūf), from Arabic الْخُرْشُوف (al-ḵuršūf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) artichoke</li></ol><p><i>артишо́к</i> (artišók) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) artichoke</li></ol><p><i>артишо́к</i> (artišók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) artillery bombardment/shelling</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>артиллери́йский обстре́л</i> (artilleríjskij obstrél).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arthritis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aromanian (language)</li></ol><p><i>арумы́н</i> (arumýn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Harfe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harpist</li></ol><p><i>а́рфа</i> (árfa) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) harp (a stringed musical instrument)</li></ol><p><i>а́рфа</i> (árfa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) archaic</li></ol><p>French <i>archa(ïque)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>archaic (old-fashioned or antiquated)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arkhalig</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, such as Azerbaijani <i>arxalıq</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archangel</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>арханг҄елъ</i> (arxangʹjelŭ), and Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχᾰ́γγελος</i> (arkhắngelos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Arkhangelsk</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) archangelic</li></ol><p><i>Арха́нгель(ск)</i> (Arxángelʹ(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>арха́нгел</i> (arxángel) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arhat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaeologist (someone who is skilled in, professes, or practices archaeology, male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>archaeological (relating to the science or research of archaeology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaeology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archeopteryx (ancient bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;literature,&#32;psychology) archetype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with nouns</i>) archi-, arch- (chief, highest, most extreme)</li><li>(<i>with adjectives</i>) arch-, highly, extremely</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Archiv</i>, from Latin <i>archīvum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχεῖον</i> (arkheîon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) paramount, of utmost importance</li></ol><p><i>архи-</i> (arxi-) +&lrm; <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archivist, recorder, registrar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archiver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archiving</li></ol><p><i>архиви́ровать</i> (arxivírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to archive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) archive, archival</li></ol><p><i>архи́в</i> (arxív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archdeacon</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιδιάκονος</i> (arkhidiákonos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιεπίσκοπος</i> (arkhiepískopos). By surface analysis, <i>архи-</i> (arxi-) +&lrm; <i>епи́скоп</i> (jepískop). Piecewise doublet of <i>арциби́скуп</i> (arcibískup).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishopric (jurisdiction of an archbishop)</li></ol><p><i>архиепи́скоп</i> (arxijepískop) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishop (Orthodox church)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιερεύς</i> (arkhiereús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;religion) bishop; bishop's</li></ol><p><i>архиере́й</i> (arxijeréj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archimandrite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Archimedes'; Archimedean</li></ol><p><i>Архиме́д</i> (Arximéd) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a respectful term for a bishop in the Orthodox church</li></ol><p>From <i>архи-</i> (arxi-) +&lrm; <i>па́стырь</i> (pástyrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archipelago</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>arcipelago</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Judaism,&#32;historical) archisynagogue (head of a synagogue)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>архисѵнаго́гъ</i> (arxisünagógŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχισῠνᾰ́γωγος</i> (arkhisŭnắgōgos). By surface analysis, <i>архи-</i> (arxi-) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>συνάγωγος</i> (sunágōgos, “synagogue”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>liturgical</i>) Epithet/title used by the Russian Orthodox Church to refer to Archangel Michael (compare to архангел)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>στρατηγός</i> (stratēgós)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>architect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) architect</li></ol><p><i>архите́ктор</i> (arxitéktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>architecture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>architektura</i>, from German <i>Architektur</i>, from Latin <i>architectura</i>; ultimately derived from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιτέκτων</i> (arkhitéktōn, “master builder”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>architectural, architectonic</li></ol><p><i>архитекту́ра</i> (arxitektúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>architrave (the lowest part of an entablature)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-) (whence Russian <i>архи-</i> (arxi-)) + Latin <i>trabs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄρχων</i> (árkhōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) dwarf mistletoe (<i>Arceuthobium</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Arceuthobium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a Catholic or Uniate archbishop, especially in Poland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>арцыбискꙋпъ</i> (arcybiskupŭ), from Old Polish <i>arcybiskup</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιεπίσκοπος</i> (arkhiepískopos). Piecewise doublet of <i>архиепи́скоп</i> (arxijepískop).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper (various <i>Juniperus</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>арча</i> (arça), from Middle Mongol (/⁠arča⁠/), from Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;arča</i>, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;artuč</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Archi</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Archi (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper forest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arshin</li><li>yardstick (standard to which other measurements or comparisons are judged)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آرشون &#47; آرشین (arşın) (Turkish <i>arşın</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aryk (in Central Asia, a relatively small aqueduct supporting agriculture and providing water to inhabitants of the area)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Common Turkic <i>&#42;ārïk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) rearguard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>arrière-garde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) ace (a pilot or tank commander who has destroyed a large number of enemy aircraft&#47;tanks)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ace (expert)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) as (Roman coin)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) as, ace (obsolete Prussian and Dutch small unit of mass&#59; a fraction of a grain)</li><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) one of the Æsir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>as</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>as</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>áss</i> through another language not before 18th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>açai (South American palms of the genus <i>Euterpe</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Brazilian Portuguese <i>açaí</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asana, yoga position</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>आसन</i> (āsana, “sitting down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asbestos</li></ol><p>Cognate with Russian <i>и́звесть</i> (ízvestʹ, “quicklime”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) asbestos</li></ol><p><i>асбе́ст</i> (asbést) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asexuality</li></ol><p><i>асексуа́льный</i> (asɛksuálʹnyj, “asexual”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asexual (having no interest in sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aseptic (free of microbes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asymmetric, asymmetrical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asymmetry</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀσυμμετρία</i> (asummetría).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) asymptote (a straight line which a curve approaches arbitrarily closely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asymptotic</li></ol><p>From <i>аси́мптота</i> (asímptota) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij) or from <i>асимпто́та</i> (asimptóta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asyndeton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Asyndeton</i>, from Latin <i>asyndeton</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀσύνδετον</i> (asúndeton). Compare with <i>бессою́зие</i> (bessojúzije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>asynchronously (in an asynchronous manner)</li></ol><p><i>асинхро́нный</i> (asinxrónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asynchronous (not synchronous)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asceticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascetic</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀσκητής</i> (askētḗs), from <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō, “to work, form”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asceticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ascetic</li></ol><p><i>аске́т</i> (askét) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ascetically</li></ol><p><i>аскети́чный</i> (asketíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ascetic (related to Ascetics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) ascomycete, a fungus of the taxon Ascomycota.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Ascomycetes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vitamin C</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascorbic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>aspectus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elapidae, asp</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) An evil person; asp, bloodsucker</li><li>black slate</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>аспида</i> (aspida), from Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓σπίς</i> (ăspís), ᾰ̓σπῐ́δος (ăspĭ́dos, “asp”).</p><p>Ancient Greek <i>ἴασπις</i> (íaspis, “jasper”). Doublet of <i>яспис</i> (jaspis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postgraduate, graduate student (a person continuing to study after completing a degree)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aspirant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graduate school (a school that awards advanced degrees)</li><li>graduate study</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspiration (burst of air that follows the release of some consonants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspirin</li></ol><p>German <i>Aspirin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aspirate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunken disk lichen (<i>Aspicilia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Aspicilia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) asplenia (absence of normal spleen function)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly (a congregation of people in one place for a purpose, particularly a ball)</li><li>assembly (a legislative body)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>assemblée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assamese</li></ol><p><i>Асса́м</i> (Assám) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>programming</i>) assembler</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) assembly language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;historical</i>) banknote (particularly, paper money in Russia from 1769 to 1849 and in France during the French Revolution)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) payment order</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>asygnacja</i>, from Latin <i>assignātiō</i> (“allocation, designation, appointment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assignment, allocation, allotment, appropriation</li></ol><p><i>ассигнова́ть</i> (assignovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assign, to appropriate</li><li>to allocate, to allot</li><li>to ear-mark, to budget</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assimilate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assimilate <i>(to, with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ассимили́ровать</i> (assimilírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assimilation; assimilational, assimilative</li></ol><p>From <i>ассимиля́ция</i> (assimiljácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>асиміляці́йний</i> (asymiljacíjnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assimilation</li></ol><p>Derived from Medieval Latin <i>assimilatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian</li></ol><p><i>Асси́рия</i> (Assírija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian (related to Assyria, ancient region on the Upper Tigris river)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian (language)</li></ol><p><i>Асси́рия</i> (Assírija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Assyrian (a citizen of an ancient nation and empire, including the northern half of Mesopotamia, with capital city of Nineveh)</li></ol><p><i>Асси́рия</i> (Assírija) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assist (help)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assonance (repetition of similar or identical vowel sounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assortment, range</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>assortiment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(psychology,&#32;mathematics,&#32;philosophy,&#32;computing,&#32;etc.) associative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>assoziativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>association (group of persons associated for a common purpose)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>association</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to associate</li></ol><p>Perhaps German <i>assoziieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to associate <i>(with)</i>, to form an association <i>(with)</i></li><li>to be associated <i>(with)</i>, to bring up an association <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ассоции́ровать</i> (associírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astatine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) astatine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asthenia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>asthénie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀσθένεια</i> (asthéneia). Compare Ukrainian <i>астені́я</i> (asteníja), Belarusian <i>астэні́я</i> (asteníja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asterism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asterisk (symbol used to highlight words or sentences)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>asteriscus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asteroid</li><li>minor planet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astigmatism (defect of a lens)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) astilbe (<i>Astilbe</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Astilbe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asthma (chronic respiratory disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aster (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astral (relating to or resembling the stars)</li><li>astral (relating to a supposed supersensible substance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Astrakhan</li><li>from Astrakhan</li></ol><p><i>А́страхань</i> (Ástraxanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waxbill; any bird of the genus <i>Estrilda</i> and a few related genera in the Estrildidae.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Afrikaans <i>astrild</i> or its descendants.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrobiology (exobiology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrobleme (pit-like structure created by an impacting meteoroid, asteroid or comet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Asteraceae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>а́стровый</i> (ástrovyj, “asteraceous”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astroid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astrological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>астроло́гия</i> (astrológija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrolabe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronaut (usually of American astronauts)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>astronaut</i>, influenced by <i>космона́вт</i> (kosmonávt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomical unit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astronomical</li></ol><p><i>астроно́мия</i> (astronómija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;astronomy) astroscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrophysicist</li></ol><p>From <i>астро-</i> (astro-) +&lrm; <i>фи́зик</i> (fízik). Compare Ukrainian <i>астрофі́зик</i> (astrofízyk), Belarusian <i>астрафі́зік</i> (astrafízik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrophysics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astrophysical</li></ol><p><i>астрофи́з(ика)</i> (astrofíz(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Asterales.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>астроцве́тный</i> (astrocvétnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Asturian (relating to Asturias in Spain)</li></ol><p><i>Асту́рия</i> (Astúrija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asura (in Hinduism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphalt</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) asphalt-covered ground, a road, a walkway, a parking etc.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>asphalte</i>, from Latin <i>asphaltum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἄσφαλτος</i> (ásphaltos, “asphalt, bitumen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to asphalt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) asphalt</li><li>asphalt grey, stone grey (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>асфа́льт</i> (asfálʹt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphyxia (condition with extreme decrease of oxygen and increase of carbon dioxide)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;humorous) alternative form of <i>а</i> (a); huh? eh?</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) asiento</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>asiento</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) ICQ (<i>instant messenger</i>)</li></ol><p>Noun derived from the Russian transcription of ICQ, ай-си-кью́ (<i>aj-si-kʹjú</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) att <i>(Lao coin)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) abbreviation of <i>атмосфе́ра абсолю́тная</i> (atmosféra absoljútnaja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atavism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>atavistic (of the recurrence of a trait after an absence of generations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, assault</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Attacke</i> or directly from French <i>attaque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attack, to charge, to assault</li></ol><p><i>ата́ка</i> (atáka) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ataxia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attacker (someone who attacks someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ataman</li><li>gang-leader, robber chief</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Has been connected with <i>ге́тман</i> (gétman).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) watch out!, cheese it! (<i>warning of danger, police, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) lookout (a vantage point)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>attendez</i> (“wait!”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ataraxia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) watch out!, cheese it!, cave! (<i>warning of danger, authorities, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) lookout (a vantage point)</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>ата́нда</i> (atánda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atayal (person or people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Atayal (relating to the Atayal people)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atayal (language)</li></ol><p><i>атая́л</i> (atajál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) atheism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheist</li></ol><p>First attested in 1726. Ultimately from Latin <i>atheista</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἄθεος</i> (átheos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>atheistic</li></ol><p><i>атеи́ст</i> (atɛíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>studio, atelier</li><li>atelier, dressmaking and tailoring establishment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>atelier</i>. Further from Latin <i>assula</i> (a diminutive of <i>axis</i> / <i>assis</i> (“axis, wheel, axle”)). Can be calqued as **стру́жечная. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱs-</i> (whence also <i>ось</i> (osʹ)).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>arēna (harēna)</i> (whence <i>аре́на</i> (aréna)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheophobia (fear or hatred of atheists)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheroma (abnormal fatty deposit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) atherosclerosis</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>athérosclérose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) atlas <i>(uppermost vertebra of the neck)</i></li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) telamon, atlantes</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Атлант)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atlas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic</li></ol><p>Latin <i>ātlant(icus)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cartography,&#32;topology) atlas</li><li>satin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Atlas</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄτλας</i> (Átlas).</p><p>From Arabic أَطْلَس (ʔaṭlas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) atlas</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) satin; satiny</li></ol><p><i>а́тлас</i> (átlas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>атла́с</i> (atlás) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>а́тлас</i> (átlas).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>атла́с</i> (atlás).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athlete (a person who actively participates in physical sports, possibly highly skilled in sports)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athletics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>athletic</li></ol><p><i>атле́т</i> (atlét) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atmosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atmospheric pressure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>atmospheric, aerial</li></ol><p><i>атмосфе́ра</i> (atmosféra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atoll</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄτομος</i> (átomos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;historical) atomgrad (a planned city built to facilitate the construction and operation of a nuclear power plant or other nuclear facility in the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>а́том</i> (átom, “atom”) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atom bomb, atomic bomb</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>atomic bomb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic mass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic energy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic number (number of protons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) atom; atomic</li><li>nuclear</li></ol><p><i>а́том</i> (átom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic number (number of protons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>атомгра́д</i> (atomgrád)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>а́том</i> (átom, “atom”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomoxetine (drug)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic-powered vessel</li></ol><p><i>а́том</i> (átom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) atomic-powered vessel</li></ol><p><i>атомохо́д</i> (atomoxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic physicist</li></ol><p><i>а́том</i> (átom) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) atony</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀτονία</i> (atonía, “languor”), from <i>ἄτονος</i> (átonos, “languid”), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “privative”) + <i>τόνος</i> (tónos, “accent”), from <i>τείνειν</i> (teínein, “to stretch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attribute</li><li>modifier, property</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attributive (a word or phrase that modifies a noun and is part of the noun's noun phrase)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attributive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>set of attributes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atrium (square hall lit from above)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atrium (square hall lit from above)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atropine (alkaloid extracted from the plant deadly nightshade)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atrophy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>atrophia</i> from Ancient Greek <i>ἀτροφία</i> (atrophía, “lack of food, hunger”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attalea (<i>Attalea</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Attalea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attaché</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>attaché</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certificate, diploma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>attestātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certification, attestation, certification procedure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to certify</li><li>to recommend</li><li>to rate, to evaluate (someone's) knowledge (<i>usually on a specific topic for a specific period</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>atestować</i>, from French <i>attester</i>, from Latin <i>attestārī</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic (style); a type of decorative wall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attitude</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>attitude</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Greece</i>) Attic (<i>relating to Attica or Attic Greek</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>А́ттика</i> (Áttika, “Attica”) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attometre (10<sup>-18</sup> metres)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an amusement park entertainment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(special,&#32;psychology,&#32;linguistics,&#32;rare) attraction</li></ol><p>Latin <i>attractio</i> from past participle of <i>attrahō</i> (= <i>ad</i> + <i>trahō</i>)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ateji (kanji used phonetically)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>当て字</i> (あてじ, ateji).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) huh? (used as a response to one's name being called or to indicate not quite having heard something)</li><li>hello? (is anyone there?)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audience (a formal meeting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>audientia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technology</i>) audio</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>audio (of or relating to audible sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>аудиокни́га</i> (audiokníga)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>audiovisual (containing both aural and visual elements)</li></ol><p><i>аудио-</i> (audio-) +&lrm; <i>визуа́льный</i> (vizuálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audiobook (a recording, usually made available for sale, of a reading of the full text or of an abridgement of a book)</li></ol><p><i>аудио-</i> (audio-) +&lrm; <i>кни́га</i> (kníga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audiologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audiology (study of hearing and hearing defects)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audiotape (magnetic tape that stores analog sound)</li></ol><p><i>аудио-</i> (audio-) +&lrm; <i>плёнка</i> (pljónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice message</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>аудио-</i> (audio-) +&lrm; <i>сообще́ние</i> (soobščénije). Compare Ukrainian <i>аудіоповідо́млення</i> (audiopovidómlennja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) audio file</li></ol><p><i>а́удио</i> (áudio) +&lrm; <i>фа́йл</i> (fájl)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>listening, listening comprehension, audition (skill or ability for nonnatives of a language of perceiving the spoken language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audit (judicial examination)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>audītor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditorium</li><li>audience</li><li>lecture hall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>auditoire</i>&nbsp;m, mistaken as a feminine like <i>histoire</i>&nbsp;f &gt; <i>история</i> (istorija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) audit</li><li>auditor's</li></ol><p><i>ауди́тор</i> (audítor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>AUE member; underage thug, young hooligan</li></ol><p><i>АУЕ</i> (AUJe) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ahuyama (<i>Cucurbita maxima</i>), a winter squash of the West Indies similar to butternut or Hubbard.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>ahuyama</i>, from Taíno.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yell/shout out (for help) by shouting "ау" (halloo!)</li></ol><p><i>ау́</i> (aú, “halloo, call for help”) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) auction</li></ol><p><i>аукцио́н</i> (aukción) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aul (a type of village in the Caucasus or Central Asia)</li></ol><p>From West (Kypchak) Turkic <i>awul, awïl</i>; related to Ottoman Turkish آغل (“penfold”). Compare Karachay-Balkar <i>ауул</i> (awul), Bashkir <i>ауыл</i> (awıl), Kazakh <i>ауыл</i> (auyl) and Turkish <i>ağıl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aul</li></ol><p><i>ау́л</i> (aúl) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aura (the distinctive atmosphere or quality associated with someone or something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>aura</i> (“a breeze, a breath of air, the air”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any mushroom of the genus <i>Auriscalpium</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Auriscalpium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an ID, a document issued by German authorities, e.g. during the occupation of a large area of the Soviet Union during World War II</li><li>(<i>humorous&#32;or&#32;Germany</i>) any passport; any ID</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ausweis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auscultation (diagnosis by stethoscope or similar auditory device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authenticity (the quality of being genuine or not corrupted from the original)</li></ol><p><i>аутенти́чный</i> (autɛntíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authentic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) autism (abnormal self-absorption)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Autismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outing (public revelation of homosexuality)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>outing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autist (person with autism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) autistic</li></ol><p>From <i>аути́зм</i> (autízm) +&lrm; <i>-ический</i> (-ičeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) autoantagonism (a situation where one substance acts on different receptors, inducing opposite forces in one pharmacological effect)</li></ol><p><i>ауто-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>антагони́зм</i> (antagonízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autogynephilia (male's propensity to be sexually aroused by the thought of himself as female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auto da fe (public announcement of the sentences imposed by the Inquisition on supposed heretics&#59; public execution by burning at the stake)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>auto da fé</i> (“act/sentence of faith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autopsy (a dissection performed on a cadaver)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autotomy (invertebrate self-defense mechanism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outsider</li><li>(politics,&#32;sports) underdog</li><li>person or team in the last place; loser</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>outsider</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outsourcing (the transfer of a business function to an external service provider)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>outsourcing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>auf Wiedersehen (goodbye)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>auf Wiedersehen</i> (“goodbye”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) ouch (expression of one's own physical pain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ouch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) diminutive of <i>ау</i> (au); huh? eh?</li></ol><p><i>а́у</i> (áu) +&lrm; <i>-ушки</i> (-uški, plural of diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aphasia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottlenose dolphin</li></ol><p>From Turkish <i>afalina</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>φάλλαινα</i> (phállaina, “whale”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Afar</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afar (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afghan Hound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afghan (person from Afghanistan)</li><li>Afghan (breed of dog)</li><li>(in the former Soviet Union) A veteran of the 1979-89 Soviet–Afghan War</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) afghani (currency of Afghanistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afghan bitch, female Afghan hound</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) afganka (a Soviet military field uniform developed in the late 1970s and adopted in 1984)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) Soviet kepi from the Afghan war era</li><li>Afghani (a strain of cannabis)</li></ol><p>From <i>афга́н(ец)</i> (afgán(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afghan hound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Afghan, Afghani</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pashto language</li></ol><p><i>афга́н(ец)</i> (afgán(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) aphelion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speculation, fraud, shady deal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>affaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraud, swindle</li></ol><p>Most probably related to Ancient Greek ἀφαίρεσις (aphaíresis, “a taking away”) with -изм (-izm, “-ism”) attached (also from Ancient Greek -ισμός (-ismós)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindler, trickster, rook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>affairiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Athenian (relating to Athens, Greece)</li></ol><p><i>Афи́ны</i> (Afíny) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athenian (an inhabitant, resident, or citizen of Athens, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bill, poster, placard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>affiche</i>. Further from Latin <i>ad-</i> + Latin <i>fīgō</i> (via Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;fīg(i)cāre</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advertise</li><li>to flaunt; to make noticeable</li></ol><p><i>афи́ша</i> (afíša) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morris column</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) aphodiine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Aphodiinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A scarab beetle of the genus <i>Aphodius</i>.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Aphodius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aphorism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aphoristic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Afroasiatic (relating to the language family)</li></ol><p><i>афр-</i> (afr-) +&lrm; <i>А́зия</i> (Ázija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afrikaans language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afrikaner (member of ethnic group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afrikaner (member of ethnic group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>African <i>(person)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Africanist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) African (of, relating to or from Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>African wolf (<i>Canis lupaster</i>)</li></ol><p><i>африка́нский</i> (afrikánskij, “African”) +&lrm; <i>во́лчий</i> (vólčij, “lupine”) +&lrm; <i>шака́л</i> (šakál, “jackal”), literally “African wolf jackal”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>African elephant (Loxodonta africana or Loxodonta cyclotis found in Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afro (hairstyle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>afro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Afro- (African)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic spelling of <i>афроамерика́нец</i> (afroamerikánec)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afro-American (a resident of the United States who is of African heritage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aphrodisiac (food or drug)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afrophobia (the fear, hate or dislike of Africans)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aphthous ulcer (ulcer in the mouth caused by break in mucous membrane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aftershock (earthquake that follows in the same vicinity as another)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>aftershock</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>dotřes</i> “дотряс” (akin to <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí), <i>встря́ска</i> (vstrjáska)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aphthous (relating to aphtha)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) temporary insanity, irresistible impulse</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) affect (feeling in response to a stimulus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) affective</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>affektiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>afferent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) affix</li></ol><p>From Early Medieval Latin <i>affixus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;medicine) affinity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affine (relating to transformations that preserve parallel lines and finite points)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) affricate (a sound produced using a combination of a plosive and a fricative)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) author (especially of an online creative work, in particular using Padonkaffsky jargon)</li></ol><p>Deliberate misspelling of <i>а́втор</i> (ávtor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) nonstandard form of <i>афе́ра</i> (aféra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ah!, oh!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ah (an instance of the interjection <i>ах</i> (ax))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;axъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclaim, to gasp, to sigh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;axati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ах</i> (ax) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Achaemenid (relating to the dynasty which ruled the Persian empire c. 550–330 BCE)</li></ol><p><i>Ахемени́д</i> (Axemeníd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achilles heel (vulnerability in an otherwise strong situation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Achilles' tendon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drivel</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Thought to derive either from dialectal <i>охи́нить</i> (oxínitʹ, “to scold”) (related to <i>хинь</i> (xinʹ, “nonsense”) and <i>хи́нить</i> (xínitʹ, “to abuse”)) or from a seminarian usage of the word <i>афинейский</i> (afinejskij) in the sense of 'falsely learned', from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀθηναῖος</i> (Athēnaîos, “Athenian”). Compare Belarusian and Ukrainian <i>ахіне́я</i> (axinéja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclaim, to gasp, to sigh</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>а́хать</i> (áxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) or <i>ах</i> (ax) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ); since the 18th century. Compare Polish <i>achnąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>achromatism (achromatopia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afterpeak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>achterpiek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) strong surprise or bewilderment</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a state of numbness due to a strong emotional shock</li></ol><p>Formed from the verb <i>охуе́ть</i> (oxujétʹ, “to be shocked”), further from the noun <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “dick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Japanese fiction</i>) ahegao (an exaggerated facial expression consisting of flushed cheeks, rolled-back eyes, and the tongue hanging out, intended to depict a person having an orgasm)</li></ol><p>From Japanese アヘ<ruby>顔<rp>(</rp><rt>がお</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>ahegao</i>), from アヘアヘ (<i>aheahe</i>, “panting”) + <ruby>顔<rp>(</rp><rt>かお</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>kao</i>, “face”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetylene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) acetylcholinesterase (an enzyme that catalyzes the hydrolysis of the neurotransmitter acetylcholine into choline and acetic acid)</li></ol><p>Possible borrowed from English <i>acetylcholinesterase</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetone (the organic compound (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CO)</li><li>nail polish remover</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>оцет</i> (ocet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosquito orchid; any plant of the genus <i>Acianthus</i>.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Acianthus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) acidophilic</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>acidophilic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acinus (one of the granular masses which constitute a racemose or compound gland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aztec</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aztec</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>H</i>/<i>h</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>âche</i>, <i>ache</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashanti</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ашанти)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashanti (one of 16 administrative regions of Ghana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashugh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>աշուղ</i> (ašuġ). Derivations from Turkic are untenable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) if</li></ol><p>From аче, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ače</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>aero-, air (referring to air or (usually) aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerobics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>aerobics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) aerobiology (the study of the dispersion of airborne biological materials, as pollen, spores, microorganisms, or viruses)</li></ol><p><i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>биоло́гия</i> (biológija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aerobic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airport terminal</li></ol><p>First attested in 1927. <i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>вокза́л</i> (vokzál), possibly, a half-calque of French <i>aérogare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerogram (a thin piece of foldable and gummed paper for writing a letter and serving as its own envelope for transit via airmail)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aérogramme</i>, influenced by <i>телегра́мма</i> (telegrámma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerodynamics</li></ol><p>From <i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>дина́мика</i> (dinámika), modeled on French <i>aérodynamique</i> or English <i>aerodynamics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aerodynamic</li></ol><p><i>аэродина́м(ика)</i> (aerodinám(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airfield, aerodrome, airdrome</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>aérodrome</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerosol</li><li>spray</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aerosol</li></ol><p><i>аэрозо́ль</i> (aerozólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerosol can (pressurized canister)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) aerolite, aerolith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerology (branch of meteorology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerometer (instrument used to measure mass and density of gases)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerometry</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>aerometry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aeronavigation, navigation</li></ol><p><i>аэронавига́ция</i> (aeronavigácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navigation light <i>(airplane)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air navigation</li></ol><p><i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>навига́ция</i> (navigácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aeronautics (mechanics and science of aircraft)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>aéronautique</i>, English <i>aeronautics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) aeroplane, airplane</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>aeroplane</i> or French <i>aéroplane</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airport</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>aeroport</i> or French <i>aéroport</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerosledge</li></ol><p><i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>са́ни</i> (sáni)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hot-air balloon, aerostat</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>aerostat</i> or French <i>aérostat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air show (a public display of stunt flying, aerobatics, and historical aircraft)</li></ol><p><i>аэро-</i> (aero-) +&lrm; <i>шо́у</i> (šóu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>express train to airport</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>аэропо́рт</i> (aeropórt, “airport”) +&lrm; <i>экспре́сс</i> (ekspréss, eksprɛ́ss, “express”).</p><p>First attested in 2004.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>а</i> (a); huh? eh?</li></ol><p><i>а</i> (a) +&lrm; <i>-юшки</i> (-juški, plural of diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) ayatollah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian آیت‌الله (âyatollâh), from Arabic آيَةُ اللّٰه (ʔāyatu llāh, “sign of God”), from آيَة (ʔāya, “sign”) + اللّٰه (allāh, “God”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ayahuasca (any of various psychoactive infusions or decoctions prepared from the ayahuasca vine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>ayahuasca</i>, from Quechua <i>ayawaska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бы</i> (by)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój, “big”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>бы́вший</i> (bývšij, “former”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего го́да</i> (búduščevo góda): of next year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего ме́сяца</i> (búduščevo mésjaca): of next month, prox.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего го́да</i> (búduščevo góda): of next year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего ме́сяца</i> (búduščevo mésjaca): of next month, prox.</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Б.М.)</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>Бо́жьей Милостью</i> (Bóžʹjej Milostʹju, “by the Grace of God”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бы́вший в употребле́нии</i> (bývšij v upotreblénii, “secondhand”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего го́да</i> (búduščevo góda): of next year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>бу́дущего ме́сяца</i> (búduščevo mésjaca): of next month, prox.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>бы́вший в употребле́нии</i> (bývšij v upotreblénii, “secondhand”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) why!, well I never!, wow! (an exclamation of surprise, especially at an unexpected meeting)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal&#32;term of address</i>) granny, gran</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ba</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>ба́бушка</i> (bábuška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>kaboom (sound of an explosion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baathism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baathist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Baathist</li></ol><p><i>баасист</i> (baasist) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) grandma, granny (short for <i>ба́бушка</i> (bábuška))</li><li>(<i>informal&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) old woman (short for <i>ба́бушка</i> (bábuška))</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) (any) woman; countrywoman</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) woman, female, broad; countrywoman</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) peasant’s wife, peasant woman</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) milksop, old woman (weak man)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) ram (<i>of a pile driver</i>)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) drop weight</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) baba (tall, cylindrical cake), bundt cake, Gugelhupf</li><li>in expressions</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;baba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Ба́ба-Яга́</i> (Bába-Jagá)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Баба-Яга)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Baba Yaga</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) old witch, hag</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;baba ęga</i> (literally “hag of terror”).</p></html>

<h3>Russian (Capitalized: Баба-яга)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Ба́ба-Яга́</i> (Bába-Jagá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bogeyman <i>(monster used to scare misbehaving children)</i></li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) A suspiciously-looking old man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>kaboom (sound of an explosion)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive) to give a resounding slap, to go off with a bang, to pop, to explode</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive) to give a resounding slap, to go off with a bang, to pop, to explode</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babbitt metal (a soft white alloy of variable composition used in bearings to diminish friction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>babbitt</i>, after the American inventor Isaac Babbitt (1799–1862).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>womanish, effeminate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;babьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ба́ба</i> (bába) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Novgorod,&#32;Pskov,&#32;Smolensk,&#32;Kaluga,&#32;Oryol,&#32;Kursk,&#32;Nizhegorod,&#32;Arkhangelsk,&#32;Perm) to work as a midwife</li><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Kursk,&#32;Oryol,&#32;Nizhegorod,&#32;Perm) to heal, to cure</li><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Oboyan,&#32;Kursk,&#32;Orlov) to do magic, to enchant</li><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Vologda) to be married</li></ol><p>From баба + -ить. The various definitions are derived from the traditional roles that older women took on with the exception of the last, which relates to a secondary meaning of баба.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granny, grandma</li><li>old woman, crone, granny</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) midwife, a simplification of повивальная бабка, or "midwife old woman"</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) medicine woman</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) mandrel</li><li>pastern</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) knucklebones (usually in the plural)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;babъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́ксы</i> (báksy) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money (means of exchange and measure of value)</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́бки</i> (bábki) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money; dough</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady's man, philanderer, womanizer</li></ol><p><i>ба́ба</i> (bába) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money; dough</li></ol><p>A variation of <i>ба́бки</i> (bábki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) butterfly</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bowtie</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feisty, spirited woman</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prostitute, hooker</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́бка</i> (bábka, “grandmother, old woman”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), originally a diminutive; from the folk belief that spirits of the dead live on as butterflies.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) tiger</li><li>a chimaera between a tiger and a beaver, used as symbol of Irkutsk, often depicted with a sable in his mouth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian بَبْر (babr). The chimaera was created as result of confusion in describing of a coat of arms, originally having a tiger, which was a popular symbol of many East Siberian cities, while the Russian name of beaver is <i>бобр</i> (bobr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow baboon (baboon of the species <i>Papio cynocephalus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бабу́ля</i> (babúlja, “granny”)</li></ol><p><i>бабу́ля</i> (babúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;endearingly) granny (grandmother)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;endearingly) granny (old lady)</li></ol><p><i>ба́ба</i> (bába) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Tula) to talk</li></ol><p>Uncertain. One possibility is a derivation from баба as a reference to a perceived female inclination towards talking.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granny, grandma</li></ol><p>Endearing diminutive of баба.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandmother, grandma, granny</li><li>old lady</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Gandhi's breakfast, eighty in the lotto game</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;babušьka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ба́ба</i> (bába) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granny's</li></ol><p><i>ба́бушка</i> (bábuška) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian summer (summerlike days in early autumn)</li></ol><p>Literally, “old woman's summer”. Cognate with Polish <i>babie lato</i>. See also German <i>Altweibersommer</i> and Hungarian <i>vénasszonyok nyara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ба́ба</i> (bába): dame, babe, chick, broad</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) looker</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) feisty woman or girl</li></ol><p><i>ба́ба</i> (bába) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bavarian (of, from or relating to the state of Bavaria, Germany)</li></ol><p><i>Бава́рия</i> (Bavárija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;Ukraine) explosions (of Russian war infrastructure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>баво́вна</i> (bavóvna), itself a mistranslation of Russian <i>хлопо́к</i> (xlopók, “crack, snap, pop”), a euphemism for an explosion, as <i>хло́пок</i> (xlópok, “cotton”) by automated translation tools.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) bug (a problem that needs fixing; especially, an error in a computer program)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bug</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luggage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bagage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk (<i>US</i>), boot (<i>UK</i>) (luggage storage compartment of a sedan&#47;saloon style car)</li><li>rack, carrier (<i>of a bicycle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бага́ж</i> (bagáž, “luggage”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baggage, luggage</li></ol><p><i>бага́ж</i> (bagáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bahamian</li></ol><p><i>Бага́мы</i> (Bagámy) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) baghatur (Turkic and Mongol honorific title)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baguette, French bread, French stick (a variety of bread that is long and narrow in shape)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) baguete</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>baguette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baguette (French bread) or fillet (detail of a frame)</li></ol><p>From <i>баге́т</i> (bagét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baguette (French bread) or fillet (detail of a frame)</li></ol><p>From <i>баге́т</i> (bagét) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) A (male) inhabitant of Baghlan or Baghlan Province, Afghanistan.</li></ol><p><i>Ба́глан</i> (Báglan, “Baghlan”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec, demonymal suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pike pole</li><li>gaff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bagrъ</i> (“hook”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow/turn crimson/purple, to redden, to flush</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>багрити</i> (bagriti, “to redden, to become scarlet-coloured/colored”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>багръ</i> (bagrŭ, “purple”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bagrъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₁-</i> (“to heat up; bathe”). Cognates include English <i>bake</i>, German <i>bähen</i> (“to bake”) and Ancient Greek <i>φώγω</i> (phṓgō, “to roast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crimson, purple (deep, bluish shade of red)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>багръ</i> (bagrŭ) + <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blood-red</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bagrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pigsqueak (<i>Bergenia</i> spp.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) turtleneck sweater</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) badminton (a racquet sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badminton player</li></ol><p>From <i>бадминто́н</i> (badmintón, “badminton”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>бадмінтоні́ст</i> (badmintoníst) and Belarusian <i>бадмінтані́ст</i> (badmintaníst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tub, bucket, basin, pail (broad, flat-bottomed vessel)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бадия</i> (badija), via Tatar <i>бадъя</i> (badʺya) from Persian بادیه (bâdye, “a capacious earthen vessel, in which wine is kept; a large deep jug, cup, bowl”), from Akkadian <i>𒁀𒋾𒌝</i> (baṭium, “vessel, basin, place to hold; tray, platter”), also found in Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>בָּטִיתָא</i> (bāṭīṯā), Classical Syriac <i>ܒܳܛܺܝܬܴܐ</i> (bāṭīṯā), Arabic بَاطِيَة (bāṭiya) and Ancient Greek <i>βατιάκη</i> (batiákē). Compare Ukrainian <i>баддя</i> (baddja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бодя́га</i> (bodjága)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bazhenov (referring to anyone with this last name, or to anything named after such a person)</li></ol><p><i>Баже́нов</i> (Bažénov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>base</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) something, mostly opinion or action, viewed as being based (admirable or praiseworthy)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) something seen as accurate, relatable or common for a given situation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>base</i>.</p><p>From English <i>based</i> (4chan slang).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) database</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>database</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physiology,&#32;geology) basal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>basal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basalt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>basaltes</i>, a misspelling of Latin <i>basanites</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βασανίτης</i> (basanítēs), from <i>βάσανος</i> (básanos, “touchstone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>market, bazaar</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) row, uproar</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) talk, chatter</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) (irresponsible) words</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, from Persian بازار (bâzâr, “market, bazaar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk loudly, to babble</li></ol><p><i>база́р</i> (bazár) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) market, marketplace</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) noisy, loud, rude, prone to shouting or arguing (over insignificant matters)</li></ol><p><i>база́р</i> (bazár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>база́р</i> (bazár, “bazaar, market”)</li></ol><p><i>база́р</i> (bazár) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basil <i>(plant)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basilica (Christian church building having a nave)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>βασιλικός</i> (basilikós, “royal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basing</li><li>stationing</li></ol><p><i>бази́ровать</i> (bazírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) based</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to base, to found, to ground (on or upon)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>basieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), since early 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be based</li><li>to be based somewhere, to set up base</li></ol><p><i>бази́ровать</i> (bazírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) basis (linearly independent set of vectors)</li><li>base, basis, foundation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;general&#32;or&#32;mathematics) basis</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) base, foundation</li><li>basic, foundational</li></ol><p><i>ба́зис</i> (bázis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) base</li><li>basic</li></ol><p><i>ба́за</i> (báza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used when putting a baby to sleep.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;predicative,&#32;impersonal,&#32;usually with young children) time to sleep</li></ol><p><i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-иньки</i> (-inʹki, plural of diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Central Asia) bay or bai, a rich man, lord, (by extension) exploiter</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bye</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source. Cf Turkish <i>bay</i>, Azerbaijani <i>bay</i>, Kazakh <i>бай</i> (bai), Kyrgyz <i>бай</i> (bay), Tatar <i>бай</i> (bay), Bashkir <i>бай</i> (bay), Turkmen <i>baý</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>bye</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hushaby; used when putting a baby to sleep.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bye-bye</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́ю-ба́ю</i> (báju-báju).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>bye-bye</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marmot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, rubbish</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) fishing boat (<i>on the Caspian Sea, Black Sea, and Sea of Azov</i>)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) go-fast boat (<i>on the Caspian Sea, Black Sea, and Sea of Azov</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bajьda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional) large fishing boat, cargo boat (<i>used on the Black Sea, Sea of Azov, and Caspian Sea</i>)</li><li>(large) canoe, umiak, bidarrah (a large, seaworthy open boat traditionally used by the native inhabitants of Eastern Siberia and Alaska)</li></ol><p>Perhaps an augmentative of <i>ба́йда</i> (bájda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kayak</li></ol><p><i>байда́ра</i> (bajdára) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kayakist, kayaker</li></ol><p>From <i>байда́рка</i> (bajdárka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) baydzharakh (a roughly cone shaped rock formation, usually consisting of siltstone, peat or loam)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>бадьараах</i> (bajaraaq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) motorcycle (an open-seated motor vehicle with handlebars instead of a steering wheel, and having two (or sometimes three) wheels)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bike</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fable, fairy-tale, old wives' tales</li><li>baize, (thick) flannelette</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bajьka</i>.</p><p>Per Vasmer, probably borrowed from Dutch <i>baai</i>, from French <i>baie</i> (“woolen matter”), from <i>bai</i> (“chestnut-colored, bay”), from Latin <i>badius</i> (“chestnut-colored, bay”). First attested in 1720.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Baikal (a large lake in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Байка́л</i> (Bajkál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biker (a person whose lifestyle is centered on motorcycles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) biker (a person whose lifestyle is centered on motorcycles)</li></ol><p><i>ба́йкер</i> (bájker) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baize, (thick) flannelette</li></ol><p><i>ба́йка</i> (bájka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) Eid, Bayram (a Muslim religious festival), abbreviation of <i>курбан-байра́м</i> (kurban-bajrám, “Eid al-Adha”)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any Islamic festival</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source.</p><p>Compare Bashkir <i>байрам</i> (bayram), Tatar <i>бәйрәм</i> (bäyräm), Khakas <i>пайрам</i> (payram), Kazakh <i>мейрам</i> (meiram), Kyrgyz <i>майрам</i> (mayram), Azerbaijani <i>bayram</i>, Turkish <i>bayram</i> etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) byte</li><li>(<i>music&#32;slang</i>) bite, biting (plagiarism in rap)</li><li>(<i>video games&#32;slang</i>) bait (a provocative act or a seemingly easy target intended to lure a player into a trap)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>byte</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>bite</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>bait</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;computing) byte</li></ol><p>From <i>байт</i> (bajt, “byte”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>byte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;computing) byte</li></ol><p>From <i>байт</i> (bajt, “byte”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), from English <i>byte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose, loose-leaf (<i>tea</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, possibly from Chinese <i>白花</i> (báihuā); compare to English <i>pekoe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baijiu, clear Chinese alcoholic beverage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 白酒 (<i>báijiǔ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) forecastle</li><li>tank, cistern, can, bin (a large vessel for storing liquids, typically cylindrical)</li><li>boiler</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a large serving bowl for a crew of sailors or workmen</li><li>sideburn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>bakboord</i> or German <i>Backbord</i>.</p><p>Derived from French <i>bac</i>, likely through Dutch <i>bak</i> or German <i>Back</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>бакенба́рда</i> (bakenbárda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bachelor's degree</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>baccalaureus</i>, semantically influenced by <i>bachelor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bachelor's degree course, undergraduate programme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) groceries</li><li>grocery store, grocery department</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>bakkal</i>, from Arabic بَقَّال (baqqāl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buoy, a moored float, sea mark, beacon</li><li>sideburn (see also бакенбарда)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>baken</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sideburn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Backenbart</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(plural of бакенбарда)</i> sideburns, side whiskers</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Backenbart</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bakuvian</li></ol><p><i>Баку́</i> (Bakú, “Baku”) +&lrm; <i>-и́нец</i> (-ínec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bakuvian</li></ol><p><i>Баку́</i> (Bakú) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>баки́нский</i> (bakínskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baklava (<i>dessert</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بقلوا (<i>baklava</i>). Doublet of <i>пахлава́</i> (paxlavá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of flask for carrying liquids with hooks on the sides for straps</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any flask for carrying liquids</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Attested since 16th century.</p><p>May be from some Turkic language, compare Crimean Tatar <i>bakla</i> (“wooden measure for grain”).</p><p>May be from some South Slavic language, compare Bulgarian <i>бъ́кел</i> (bǎ́kel), <i>бъ́клица</i> (bǎ́klica, “bottle”), Macedonian <i>баклица</i> (baklica, “water vessel”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ба̏кла</i> (“wooden vessel for water or alcohol”), and also Byzantine Greek <i>μποῦκλα</i> (mpoûkla, “wooden wine wessel”) (on the other hand, Greek <i>μπουκάλι</i> (boukáli, “bottle”), <i>μπουκάλι</i> (boukáli, “large bottle”) are less likely to be related).</p><p>Compare also Polish <i>bukłak</i> (“wineskin”) and dialectal Romanian <i>butlagă</i>, <i>bătlag</i> (“keg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aubergine, eggplant (plant and vegetable)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بادلجان (bâdlicân) under influence of <i>бакла́га</i> (baklága), from Classical Persian بَادِنْجَان (bādinjān), from earlier بَادِنْگَان (bādingān), from Indo-Aryan, likely ultimately from Dravidian. Compare Sanskrit <i>भण्डाकी</i> (bhaṇḍākī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aubergine paste</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aubergine</li></ol><p><i>баклажа́н</i> (baklažán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aubergine, eggplant</li><li>aubergine (a dark purple colour&#47;color)</li></ol><p><i>баклажа́н</i> (baklažán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carboy, demijohn</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) plastic water bottle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бакла́га</i> (baklága)</li></ol><p><i>бакла́га</i> (baklága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cormorant</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female cormorant or gannet</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) violent, drunken brawl or beating (usually committed by teenagers who cannot yet be tried as adults)</li></ol><p><i>бакла́н</i> (baklán) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buck, dollar</li></ol><p>From English <i>bucks</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bacterial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacteriologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bacteriological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacteriology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacteriophage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacterium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bactrian camel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) tip (money)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>bahşiş</i>, from Classical Persian بخشیش (baxšiš, “present; an honorary or pecuniary gratuity; drink-money”) or بخشش (baxšiš), from بخشیدن (baxšīdan, “to give, grant, bestow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball, dance</li></ol><p>First attested in 1705. Borrowed from French <i>bal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballabile</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>ballabile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>балабо́лка</i> (balabólka)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>балабо́лка</i> (balabólka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive) to babble, to talk nonsense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chatterbox, windbag</li></ol><p>From <i>балабо́лить</i> (balabólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive) to babble, to talk nonsense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booth, show-booth</li><li>show; farce; low farce; buffoonery</li><li>fuss</li><li>(<i>Siberia and the Far East</i>) a summer hut, a saeter</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Persian بالاخانه (bâlâxâne, “upper room”), via a Turkic language. Doublet of <i>балахана</i> (balaxana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play the buffoon; to fool around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) showman; organiser of a show-booth</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buffoon; someone who fools around</li></ol><p><i>балага́н</i> (balagán) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a man, who speaks in a joking manner, a joker</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ) (or related to <i>балабо́л</i> (balaból), <i>балабо́лить</i> (balabólitʹ)) + <i>гу́ркать</i> (gúrkatʹ) (related to <i>го́вор</i> (góvor), <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)).</p><p>For the similar meaning development compare Latin <i>iocus</i> (“a joke, a jest”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yek-</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk merrily and jokingly</li></ol><p><i>балагу́р</i> (balagúr) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;colloquial) to speak, to talk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;balakati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balaclava (headgear)</li></ol><p>From <i>Балакла́ва</i> (Balakláva, “Balaklava”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) balalaika</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>балаба́йка</i> (balabájka), from onomatopoeic <i>балабай-</i> (balabaj-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare with <i>балабо́нить</i> (balabónitʹ), <i>балагу́рить</i> (balagúritʹ), <i>бала́кать</i> (balákatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troublemaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;obsolete) skillygalee</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very thin, usually tasteless soup, prison swill</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) meal</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;rare) idle talk, nonsense, unnecessary or disingenuous chatter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Lithuanian <i>balánda</i> (“orach, <i>Atriplex</i> spp.”). The colloquial and slang meanings are probably implying a poor taste of meal cooked with this plant.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) balanitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance</li><li>pulpwood</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>balance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to balance, to keep one's balance</li><li>to balance, to equipoise</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>balancieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) balance (<i>in various senses</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) balance sheet</li></ol><p>From <i>бала́нс</i> (baláns) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) loose peasant overgarment made of sackcloth or twill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) loose-fitting robe</li></ol><p>Unclear. Formerly unsuccessfully derived from Persian بالا جامه (bâlâ jâme, “upper dress”) or Turkic. May be related to <i>бала́хтать</i> (baláxtatʹ, “splash”), <i>бала́хтаться</i> (baláxtatʹsja, “gad”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Balachka (South Russian and Ukrainian regional dialects in Don and Kuban regions)</li><li>(regional,&#32;Southern Russia) conversation, talk; gossip</li></ol><p><i>бала́кать</i> (balákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fool, idiot, simpleton, booby</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Czech <i>blb</i>, <i>blbý</i>, <i>blbec</i>. Alternatively from a Turkic language, compare Kazakh <i>билбес</i> (bilbes), Tatar <i>билмәс</i> (bilmäs, “he won't know”), and thus perhaps connected to бельмес (belʹmes).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>балбе́с</i> (balbés).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sledgehammer</li><li>(animate,&#32;masculine&#32;and&#32;feminine) blockhead, dolt, dunderhead</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, compare Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;baltu</i> (“axe”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>дуби́на</i> (dubína), <i>оря́сина</i> (orjásina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldacchin (canopy suspended over an altar or throne)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to stop thinking clearly, to take leave of one's senses</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get/be high;</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to have fun; to lead a fun life, to be idle</li></ol><p><i>балда́</i> (baldá) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) euphoria; (extreme) fun; enjoyment; idleness</li></ol><p><i>балде́ть</i> (baldétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballerina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ballet</i>. Further from Italian <i>balletto</i>, from Latin <i>ballō</i> + <i>-ittus</i> (whence Italian <i>-etto</i>, French <i>-et</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fairy ballet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ballet-féerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballet choreographer, ballet master</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>Ballettmeister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ballet</li></ol><p><i>бале́т</i> (balét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balitoridae (a biological family that includes hillstream loaches and river loaches of the eastern part of Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam, girder, bar, baulk</li><li>gully, (narrow) gorge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;balъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Balkan</li></ol><p><i>Балка́ны</i> (Balkány) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) bulk carrier, bulker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bulker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balcony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>balcon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) balcony</li></ol><p><i>балко́н</i> (balkón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>балко́н</i> (balkón, “balcony”)</li></ol><p><i>балко́н</i> (balkón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point</li><li>mark, grade, score</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a ball used in the process of voting</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>balle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ballade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballast (heavy material placed in the hold of a vessel)</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) dead weight</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>ballast</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballistics (science of the study of falling objects)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballistic missile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ballistic</li></ol><p><i>балли́ст(ика)</i> (ballíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cylinder (<i>of compressed gas</i>)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) envelope (<i>of a balloon</i>)</li><li>inner tube (<i>of a tire</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) large jar, carboy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ballon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gas cartridge containing incapacitating liquid, for self-defense (e.g. pepper spray)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>балло́н</i> (ballón, “gas or liquid canister”)</li></ol><p><i>балло́н</i> (ballón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to put (a proposal) to a vote</li><li>to elect by secret ballot</li></ol><p>From German <i>ballot(ieren)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ) or from French <i>balloter</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), from Italian <i>ballottare</i> (“to vote with balls”), from Italian <i>ballotta</i> (“ball”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run for an election, to stand for election</li></ol><p><i>баллоти́ровать</i> (ballotírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) spoiled, indulgent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indulge, to spoil, to pet, to pamper</li><li>to give a treat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>баловати</i> (balovati, “to flatter, to heal”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;badlovati</i>. Per Vasmer, cognate to Slovene <i>balováti</i> and probably not to Sanskrit <i>बाल</i> (bāla, “kid, infant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frolic, to play about, to fool around, to get up to monkey tricks</li><li>to amuse oneself <i>(with)</i>, to dabble <i>(in/at)</i></li><li>to indulge <i>(in)</i>, to treat oneself <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have sex, to fool about <i>(with)</i>, to mess <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ба́лова́ть</i> (bálovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) favorite, darling, pet, coddle, cosset (a person or animal especially cherished, indulged or spoiled)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) mischievous child</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>балова́ть</i> (balovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mischievous child, imp, naughty child or animal</li><li>piddler, frolicker, someone who screws around, someone who is being naughty</li></ol><p>From <i>балова́ть</i> (balovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) or from <i>ба́ловать</i> (bálovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik); compare also <i>ба́ловень</i> (bálovenʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naughtiness, mischievousness</li><li>petting, spoiling, over-indulgence</li><li>something not serious, e.g. not a serious hobby, infatuation, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>балова́ть</i> (balovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó) or from <i>ба́ловать</i> (bálovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Baltic</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>balt(isch)</i> (“Baltic”) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balyk (smoked or cured meat from the spine of the sturgeon or large salmon)</li></ol><p>From an indeterminate Turkic language, see <i>&#42;bālïk</i> (“fish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) balyk</li></ol><p><i>балы́к</i> (balýk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>балык</i> (balyk)</li></ol><p><i>балык</i> (balyk) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Balzacian</li></ol><p><i>Бальза́к</i> (Balʹzák) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balsam, balm (soothing ointment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Balsam</i>, from Latin <i>balsamum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βάλσαμον</i> (bálsamon, “balsam”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music to one's ears (unexpected good news)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) balsam</li></ol><p><i>бальза́м</i> (balʹzám) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) balsam</li></ol><p><i>бальза́м</i> (balʹzám) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ball, ballroom</li></ol><p><i>бал</i> (bal) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) balustrade</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) banisters</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>balustrade</i>, from Italian <i>balaustrata</i>. First attested in the 18th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baluster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bang (percussive sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bamboo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bambou</i> or Dutch <i>bamboe</i>, from Portuguese <i>bambu</i>, from Malay <i>bambu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bamboo</li></ol><p>From <i>бамбу́к</i> (bambúk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) bamboo woodpecker (any <i>Gecinulus</i> sp. or <i>Gecinulus viridis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бамбу́ковый</i> (bambúkovyj, “bamboo”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>дя́тел</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim (djátel, “woodpecker”, noun), literally “bamboo woodpecker”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>okra, <i>Abelmoschus esculentus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بامیه (bamya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) bumper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bumper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bumper</li></ol><p><i>ба́мпер</i> (bámper) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ban (a noble title)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) ban</li><li>a block towards another online user</li><li>ban (small coin of Romania and Moldavia)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea,&#32;Odessa,&#32;slang) a railway station</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea,&#32;slang) a pier</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea,&#32;Odessa,&#32;slang,&#32;by extension) a place where a lot of people gather</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;banъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>ban</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bannaną</i> (whence also Latin <i>bannus</i>, whence Latin <i>bannālis</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”).</p><p>From German <i>Bahn</i> (“a road”), probably through Yiddish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banality, triteness</li><li>commonplace, platitude, banal remark</li></ol><p><i>бана́льный</i> (banálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>banal, trivial (common, ordinary)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>banal</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Latin <i>bannālis</i>, from Latin <i>bannus</i> (whence also English <i>abandon</i>). Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bannaną</i> (whence also English <i>ban</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>euphemistic form of <i>ана́льный се́кс</i> (análʹnyj sɛ́ks, “anal sex”)</li></ol><p><i>бана́льный</i> (banálʹnyj, “banal, trivial”) +&lrm; <i>ке́кс</i> (kéks, “sponge cake”); literally "trivial sponge cake" or "banal sponge cake", from a wordplay on ана́льный се́кс (análʹnyj sɛ́ks, “anal sex”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banana (tree or fruit)</li><li>joint of marijuana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>banane</i>, from Spanish or Portuguese <i>banana</i>, from Wolof <i>banaana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;idiomatic,&#32;derogatory) banana republic</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>banana republic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) banana</li></ol><p><i>бана́н</i> (banán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bangladeshi person</li></ol><p><i>Бангладе́ш</i> (Bangladɛ́š) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bangladeshi</li></ol><p><i>Бангладе́ш</i> (Bangladɛ́š) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gang</li></ol><p>From French <i>bande</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surgical corset, <i>(abdominal)</i> support</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) tyre, band</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bandage</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandana (large kerchief)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bandana</i>. Further from Sanskrit <i>बध्नाति</i> (badhnāti). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) A person who is easily-influenced or brainwashed by another; sheeple, a lemming.</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) An unremarkable, boring person; a conformist.</li><li>(prison slang,&#32;derogatory) someone who spends too much time alone in their cell, refusing to speak with others; a recluse.</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) A member of the special forces of the GRU, especially one trained in acrobatics and advanced knowledge of foreign languages.</li><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) An opponent of Vladimir Putin, president of Russia.</li><li>(offensive,&#32;derogatory) A Banderite, a supporter of Stepan Bandera.</li><li>(offensive,&#32;derogatory,&#32;by extension) A Ukrainian ultranationalist.</li></ol><p>Corruption of бандар-лог (bandar-log, “a tribe of monkeys from "The Jungle Book"”), from English <i>Bandar-log</i>, ultimately Hindi <i>बन्दर-लोग</i> (bandar-log, “monkey people”). All the other meanings of this word were influenced by senses 1 and 2.</p><p>For senses 6 and 7, a blend of <i>банде́ровец</i> (bandérovec, “a Banderite, supporter of Stephan Bandera”) +&lrm; <i>бандар-ло́г</i> (bandar-lóg, “Bandar-log, a tribe of monkeys from "The Jungle Book"”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) Banderite (a member of Stepan Bandera's movement)</li><li>(by extension,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) a Ukrainian nationalist</li></ol><p><i>Банде́ра</i> (Bandéra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Compare with Ukrainian <i>банде́рівець</i> (bandérivecʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periodical wrapper, post package, small parcel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>banderole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banjo (a musical instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>banjo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banjoist (person playing or performing on the banjo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandicoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandit, gangster, brigand</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangsterism, banditry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thug, bandit, highwayman</li><li>thug's, bandit's, highwayman's</li><li>like a bandit, thug, or highwayman</li></ol><p><i>банди́т</i> (bandít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Azerbaijan</i>) anti-organized crime unit</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>отде́л по борьбе́ с бандити́змом</i> (otdél po borʹbé s banditízmom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandura <i>(old Ukrainian music instrument)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a large, bulky object</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>банду́ра</i> (bandúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armed gang</li></ol><p><i>ба́нда</i> (bánda) +&lrm; <i>формирова́ние</i> (formirovánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>banzai</li></ol><p>From Japanese <ruby>万歳<rp>(</rp><rt>ばんざい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>banzai</i>, “hurrah”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to ban</li></ol><p><i>бан</i> (ban) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank (financial institution)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bank</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bankiz</i> (whence ultimately also English <i>bench</i>, Russian <i>банке́тка</i> (bankétka)), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeg-</i>. Doublet of <i>ба́нка</i> (bánka).</p><p>For the bench–bank (<i>банке́тка</i> (bankétka)–<i>банк</i> (bank)) relation, compare typologically <i>ла́вка</i> (lávka), <i>ла́вочник</i> (lávočnik), <i>прила́вок</i> (prilávok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar, pot</li><li>can, tin</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cupping glass</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;figuratively,&#32;slang) guns (muscles)</li><li>(<i>marine</i>) sandbank, shoal</li><li>(<i>marine</i>) устричная банка oyster bed</li><li>(<i>boats</i>) thwart <i>(oarsman’s transverse seat)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;banъka</i>, from <i>&#42;baňa</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ъka</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bank</i> or Dutch <i>bank</i>. Doublet of <i>банк</i> (bank).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banquet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>banquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of seat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) banquet</li></ol><p><i>банке́т</i> (bankét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bankier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banknote, bill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banknote</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Banknote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank statement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bank</li></ol><p><i>банк</i> (bank) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ATM, automated teller machine, cash machine</li></ol><p>Former trade name; see <i>bank</i> and <i>automatic</i>, and compare <i>automat</i> and <i>Bankomat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bankrupt, defaulter, insolvent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bankrott</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bankruptcy (legally declared or recognized condition of insolvency)</li></ol><p><i>банкро́т</i> (bankrót, “bankrupt, defaulter, insolvent”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banksia, <i>Banksia</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Banksia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banner (large sign)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>banner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bannik (a bathhouse spirit in Slavic mythology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bathhouse, bath</li></ol><p><i>ба́ня</i> (bánja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauna whisk or bath broom used in Finnish saunas and Russian banyas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ба́нка</i> (bánka): a small jar, a small can/tin</li></ol><p><i>ба́нка</i> (bánka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow; bowknot, ribbon (long, narrow strip of material)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Band</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bandiz</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бант</i> (bant): a (small) ribbon; a (small) bow</li></ol><p><i>бант</i> (bant) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banshee (in Irish folklore, a female spirit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>banshee</i>, from Irish <i>bean sí</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i> (whence also <i>жена́</i> (žená)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathhouse attendant</li></ol><p><i>ба́ня</i> (bánja) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ба́ня</i> (bánja): a bath (place or action), a (small) bathhouse</li></ol><p><i>ба́ня</i> (bánja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banshee (in Irish folklore, a female spirit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>banshee</i>, from Irish <i>bean sí</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banya</li><li>(public) bath</li><li>Turkish bath, steam bath</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;baňa</i>, from Latin <i>baneum</i> (plural <i>banea</i>), variant of <i>balneum</i>, from Latin <i>balneum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βαλανεῖον</i> (balaneîon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bonan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relaional</i>) Bonan</li></ol><p>From <i>баоа́нь</i> (baoánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baobab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baozi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 包子 (<i>bāozi</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Baptist (member of a Baptist church or denomination)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Baptist</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βαπτιστής</i> (baptistḗs). By surface analysis, Ancient Greek <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saloon, bar, snack bar</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) bar, jib</li><li>sandbar</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) bar (unit of pressure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bar</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>barre</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>bar</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βάρος</i> (báros).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бар)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bar (a port city in Montenegro)</li><li>Bar (a city in Ukraine)</li><li>Bar (historical French duchy of the Holy Roman Empire, in modern eastern France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>Бар</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>Bar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) drum</li><li>drum, barrel, cylinder</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat a drum</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drum, to thump</li></ol><p><i>бараба́н</i> (barabán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drum</li></ol><p><i>бараба́н</i> (barabán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll, drumbeat, rataplan, rub-a-dub (the uniform beating of a drum)</li><li>A drum signal, used to issue relay an officer's command throughout a unit.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drummer</li></ol><p><i>бараба́н</i> (barabán) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a barrabora.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Itelmen.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) goat-fish, king-mullet, surmullet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bunkhouse, barrack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>baraque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram (male domestic sheep)</li><li>sheep (an animal from the <i>Ovis</i> genus)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mule, donkey (a very stubborn and foolish person)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>баранъ</i> (baranŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;baranъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sheep, ram</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) sheepskin</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mutton</li></ol><p><i>бара́н</i> (barán) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutton, lamb (the meat of sheep)</li></ol><p><i>бара́н</i> (barán) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baranka (a ring-shaped cracknel), bagel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) steering wheel</li></ol><p>From <i>обвари́ть</i> (obvarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нка</i> (-ánka). Compare Russian <i>заварно́й</i> (zavarnój) (<i>заварно́е те́сто</i>, <i>заварно́й хлеб</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baranka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>барахло́</i> (baraxló): junk, belongings</li></ol><p><i>барахло́</i> (baraxló) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) goods and chattels, belongings</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) junk, trash, odds and ends</li></ol><p>Derived from dialectal or Old East Slavic <i>борошень</i> (borošenĭ, “travel belongings”), through an intermediate form <i>*борохъло</i> before undergoing akanye, itself from <i>*борох</i>, from the same origin <i>*borxъ</i>, whence Czech <i>brašna</i> (“travel bag”), a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i>, from where беру (beru, “I carry”), брать (bratʹ, “to carry”) and Ancient Greek <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “I carry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flea market</li></ol><p><i>барахло́</i> (baraxló) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flounder, to roll, to wallow</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>бара́н</i> (barán): little sheep, little ram, lamb</li><li>lambskin</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) wing nut, thumbscrew</li></ol><p>From <i>бара́н</i> (barán) +&lrm; <i>-шек</i> (-šek), with unexpected loss of н.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barberry, barbery <i>(berries or shrub)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barberry</li></ol><p><i>барбари́с</i> (barbarís) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barberry</li></ol><p><i>барбари́с</i> (barbarís) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbecue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>barbecue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbershop (for men only)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) barbershop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>barbershop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barbershop</li></ol><p>From <i>барбершо́п</i> (barberšóp) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periwinkle, vinca</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>barwinek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bard</li><li>author singer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brothel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) disorder, jumble, clutter, mess</li></ol><p>Was also spelled as <i>бордак</i> (bordak). Likely from <i>бордель</i> (bordelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak).</p><p>Compare French <i>bordel</i> for the figuative sense of "jumble, terrible mess".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) messy, cluttered, disorganised, chaotic</li></ol><p><i>барда́к</i> (bardák, “mess”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>барда́к</i> (bardák, “mess”)</li><li>glove compartment, glove box</li></ol><p><i>барда́к</i> (bardák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barège</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barège</li></ol><p><i>баре́ж</i> (baréž) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bas relief (a low or mostly-flat sculpture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bas-relief</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>barge</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>barca</i>. Doublet of <i>ба́рка</i> (bárka) and <i>барка́с</i> (barkás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) boyar, barin, nobleman, gentleman, landlord (a rank of aristocracy in Russia)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sir, master (a term of address used by a lower-class person for a nobleman, or a third-person reference to a nobleman)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) freeloader, gentleman of leisure (a person who doesn't like to work or who makes others do his job)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>боя́рин</i> (bojárin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) baryon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barista (person who prepares coffee in a coffee shop for customers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>barista</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baritone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of a barge, specifically a wooden flat-bottomed river boat, usually undecked</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>барка</i> (barka), from Middle Low German <i>barke</i> (“shallow wide boat, usually flat-bottomed”), from Middle Dutch <i>barke</i>, from Middle French <i>barque</i>, from Old Occitan <i>barca</i>, from Late Latin <i>barca</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;bārica</i>, from Latin <i>bāris</i> (“Egyptian shallow wide flat-bottomed river boat”), from Ancient Greek <i>βᾶρις</i> (bâris), from Demotic Egyptian <i>br</i>, from Egyptian <i>bꜣjr</i>, further origin uncertain. Doublet of <i>ба́ржа</i> (bárža) and <i>барка́с</i> (barkás).</p><p>Attested since the 14th-15th centuries (Novgorod 4th chronicle).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>launch <i>(a big rowing boat)</i></li><li>longboat</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the second half of 18th century (first attested in 1785, form <i>барказ</i> (barkaz) in 1762) from Dutch <i>barkas</i> (“a large or the largest row-boat on a ship”) (att. since 1718), Swedish <i>barkasse</i> (since 1765) or possibly another Western European language, from some Romance language. Compare Italian <i>barcaccia</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>barca</i>. Doublet of <i>баржа́</i> (baržá) and <i>ба́рка</i> (bárka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;childish) an evil-looking person, a thug, a villain</li><li>(military slang,&#32;ethnic slur) Islamic terrorist, jihadist, towelhead</li></ol><p>From <i>Бармале́й</i> (Barmaléj), an evil pirate from Korney Chukovsky's eponymous children's poem (1925).</p><p>The military slang sense first appeared among Russian military servicemen deployed in Syria.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barman, bartender</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>barman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) kind of fur coat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bar (establishment)</li></ol><p><i>бар</i> (bar) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baroque</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baroque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>barocco</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Baron</i> or directly from French <i>baron</i>, further from Latin <i>barō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barony</li></ol><p><i>баро́н</i> (barón) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air patrol</li></ol><p>From French <i>barrage</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrel (unit of liquid measure equal to one-quarter of a pipe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barricade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>barricade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;bars</i>, also related to Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;bars</i>.</p><p>See Turkish <i>pars</i>, Kazakh <i>барыс</i> (barys), Mongolian <i>бар</i> (bar), Dongxiang <i>basi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>master's, gentleman's</li><li>lordly, aristocratic</li><li>haughty</li></ol><p><i>ба́р(ин)</i> (bár(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>ба́рский</i> (bárskij), lordliness</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;collective) gentry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badger (mammal)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source (compare Turkish <i>porsuk</i>, Kazakh <i>борсық</i> (borsyq, “badger”)), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;borsuk</i> (“badger”). Compare Ukrainian <i>борсу́к</i> (borsúk, “badger”). Displaced native <i>язве́ц</i> (jazvéc), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jàzvьcь</i> (“badger”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) badger</li></ol><p><i>барсу́к</i> (barsúk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a baby badger, a young badger</li></ol><p><i>барсу́к</i> (barsúk) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barter (an equal exchange)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>barter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barchan, sand dune (<i>formed by the wind into a crescent shape</i>)</li><li>(slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;western&#32;Kazakhstan,&#32;ethnic slur) Kazakh individual, perceived as rude, uncultured, rural or lacking fluency in Russian.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>барқан</i> (barqan, “sand dune”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>velvet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>barchât</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>barracanus</i>, from Arabic بَرَّكَان (barrakān, “a kind of black gown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marigold (plant) (<i>Tagetes</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) hair ornament in the form of a velvet ribbon or a small bouquet of artificial velvet flowers</li></ol><p><i>ба́рхат</i> (bárxat, “velvet”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>velvety; silky</li></ol><p><i>ба́рхат</i> (bárxat) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) velvet</li><li>velvety</li></ol><p><i>ба́рхат</i> (bárxat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Barczewo</li></ol><p><i>Барче́во</i> (Barčévo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corvee, statute labor, gavel work (unpaid labor due to a feudal lord)</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́рин</i> (bárin) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina). Compare Ukrainian <i>панщина</i> (panščyna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) huckster, profiteer, fraud, scalper</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) fence (dealer in stolen goods)</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>бары́ш</i> (barýš, “profit”), a Turkic borrowing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) lady, madam, boyar's wife</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) madam, ma'am (a form of address to a noble woman or landlady)</li></ol><p><i>ба́р(ин)</i> (bár(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ыня</i> (-ynja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) profit, income</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>барышъ</i> (baryš, “factor's commission; trade profit”) attested since 1568, through Old Ruthenian <i>барышъ</i> (baryš) (since 1539) or directly from Ottoman Turkish بارش (“peace; reconciliation, agreement”) (whence Turkish <i>barış</i> (“peace”)). Anikin quotes Strakhov, who suggests borrowing through 16th-century horse trade jargon.</p><p>Historical hypotheses include Chuvash <i>&#42;парӑш</i> (&#42;parăš, “gift”) and <i>varış</i>, from the verb <i>varmak</i> (to arrive, come).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profiteer</li></ol><p>From <i>бары́ш</i> (barýš) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) young lady, miss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrier (structure that bars passage)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) hurdle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>barrière</i>&nbsp;f. Several steps omitting from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;barra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bass (voice)</li><li>bass (singer)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: БАС)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neurology,&#32;pathology) initialism of <i>боково́й амиотрофи́ческий склеро́з</i> (bokovój amiotrofíčeskij skleróz, “amyotrophic lateral sclerosis”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basenji</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Basenji</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bass player</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or sing in a deep voice, to speak or sing in a bass voice</li></ol><p>From <i>бас</i> (bas) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basque (member of a people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basketball <i>(sport only)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>basketball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basketball player, basketballer</li></ol><p>From <i>баскетбо́л</i> (basketból, “basketball”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>баскетболі́ст</i> (basketbolíst), Belarusian <i>баскетбалі́ст</i> (baskjetbalíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) basketball</li></ol><p><i>баскетбо́л</i> (basketból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretty</li><li>well-dressed</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) good</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;basъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Basque (relative to the Basque people or their language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Basque</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basque (language)</li></ol><p><i>баск</i> (bask) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mujahid (a Muslim engaging in jihad, Central Asia during 1917–26)</li><li>Basmachi (Muslim anti-Bolshevik fighter in Central Asia during 1917-26)</li><li>(humorous,&#32;endearing) a term of endearment, commonly used in reference to young people</li></ol><p>A back-formation from Uzbek <i>bosmachi</i>, from <i>bosma</i> (“raid”) + the agent suffix <i>-chi</i>. It was conceived as a plural form by Russian and the final vowel was dropped to create a singular form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basmachi movement</li></ol><p><i>басма́ч</i> (basmáč) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabulist (writer of fables)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1798. <i>ба́сня</i> (básnja) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), an inexact calque of German <i>Fabeldichter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fabulously, amazingly</li></ol><p><i>басносло́вный</i> (basnoslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) legendary</li><li>fabulous, incredible</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>баснословити</i> (basnosloviti, “to tell tales”), calque of Ancient Greek <i>μυθολογέω</i> (muthologéō, “to tell tales”). By surface analysis, <i>ба́сня</i> (básnja, “fable”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) alternative form of <i>ба́сня</i> (básnja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary genre</i>) fable</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fable, untruth, cock-and-bull story, fib</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;basnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basolia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming pool</li><li>basin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bassin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) basin</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) swimming pool</li></ol><p><i>бассе́йн</i> (basséjn, bassɛ́jn) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basset hound</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>basset hound</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>basta (that's enough, stop)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>basta</i> or Spanish <i>basta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) bastard (person born to unmarried parents)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) bastard (mongrel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bastard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bastion; bulwark (also figuratively)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bastion</i>&nbsp;f. Several steps omitting from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bastijan</i> (whence also <i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja) (via Italian) and <i>Басти́лия</i> (Bastílija)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>опло́т</i> (oplót) (akin to <i>плести́</i> (plestí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go on strike</li></ol><p>Constructed in the 19th century from <i>баста</i> (basta) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bastirma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>բաստուրմա</i> (basturma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;derogatory) Muslim</li><li>(dated,&#32;derogatory) a person of a different religion (non-Christian or non-Orthodox)</li></ol><p>Probably the same as <i>мусульма́нин</i> (musulʹmánin) via a minority language in Russia, cf. Kumyk <i>бусурман</i> (busurman)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baht (currency of Thailand)</li><li>Russian name for a dought canoe traditionally used by Itelmen and Udege people</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thai <i>บาท</i> (bàat).</p><p>May be clipping of <i>ботни́к</i> (botník), from verb <i>ботать</i> (botatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) battle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) fight, clash, row, squabble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>battaglia</i> or French <i>bataille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battalion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bataillon</i>, from Italian <i>battaglione</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) battalion</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) batallion commander</li></ol><p><i>батальо́н</i> (batalʹón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>батаре́я</i>&nbsp;f (bataréja): small electric battery, baby battery</li></ol><p>From <i>батаре́я</i> (bataréja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electricity</i>) battery (electric power source consisting of electrochemical cells)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) battery (group of artillery pieces)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) battery (an array of similar things)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) radiator (metal fixture for heating a room)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) battery</li></ol><p>From French <i>batterie</i>, likely via Dutch <i>batterij</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet potato</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing of</i> батя) dad</li><li><i>familiar and affectionate term of address</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) bathymetry (measurement of the depths of oceans, seas and other water bodies using special devices of different systems)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>-ме́трия</i> (-métrija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathyscaphe</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>βάθος</i> (báthos, “depth”) and <i>σκάφος</i> (skáphos, “ship”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>batiste, cambric</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>batiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cambric</li></ol><p><i>бати́ст</i> (batíst) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathysphere</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bathysphère</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βάθος</i> (báthos, “depth”) and <i>σφαῖρα</i> (sphaîra, “sphere”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional) cane, stick, walking stick</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) riding crop, horsewhip</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;batogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loaf (of bread), bread stick</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bâton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Georgia</i>) Sir, friend, mate (form of address to a man)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ბატონო</i> (baṭono).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confectionery bar, bar of chocolate or other confectionery</li></ol><p><i>бато́н</i> (batón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farm labourer; cotter</li></ol><p>Unknown, maybe related to ба́тырь (bátyrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy farmhand, child farm laborer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>swimming</i>) butterfly, butterfly stroke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>butterfly</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) modern dance competition (such as rap, hip-hop, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>battle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бату́т</i> (batút)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trampoline</li><li>bouncy castle, bounce house</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>batoude</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) father, dad</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) "father", boss, master, lord, kingpin, number one, pro</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a nickname for president of Belarus Alyaksandr Lukashenka</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́тя</i> (bátja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), originally a diminutive. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>ба́тько</i> (bátʹko), Belarusian <i>ба́цька</i> (bácʹka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;dated&#32;elsewhere) father, dad</li></ol><p>From <i>ба́тя</i> (bátja) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko); originally a diminutive. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>ба́тько</i> (bátʹko), Belarusian <i>ба́цька</i> (bácʹka). This spelling is probably influenced by Ukrainian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) father (<i>parent</i>)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) father (<i>term of address for a priest</i>), priest</li><li>dear friend; old boy (<i>respectful and affectionate term of address</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ба́тя</i> (bátja) +&lrm; <i>-юшка</i> (-juška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slightly&#32;dated,&#32;colloquial) goodness me! oh my goodness!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) dad</li><li><i>familiar term of address for an elderly man</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>батѧ</i> (batę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;batę</i>, compare to Bulgarian <i>баща́</i> (baštá). Alternatively from <i>&#42;brat(r)ъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portmanteau</li><li>trunk, a large travel bag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>baule</i>, from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bang, kaboom (sound of a gunshot or an explosion)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бах)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the German surname <i>Bach</i>; particularly, the great German composer and musician of the Baroque period Johann Sebastian Bach</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Baháʼí</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian بهائی (bahâ'i).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ) + <i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bragging, boasting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wader (boot)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) workboot (especially one that is tough or rough)</li><li>overshoe</li><li>shoecover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small, strong horse</li><li>klepper, pony</li></ol><p>It is considered a borrowing from a Turkic (Crimean Tatar, or rather Nogai) language <i>paχn at</i>, in which it is explained (according to Lokotsch and Miklosich, for example) as a compound word formed from Persian پهن (pahn, “wide, broad”) (cf. Ottoman Turkish پهن (pehn)) + <i>at</i> (“horse”) of Common Turkic origin (cf. Nogai <i>at</i>, Crimean Tatar <i>at</i>). Vasmer considers this highly unlikely.</p><p>Cognates include Ukrainian <i>бахма́т</i> (baxmát) and Polish <i>bachmat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бахма́т</i> (baxmát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bahraini</li></ol><p><i>Бахре́йн</i> (Baxréjn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe (decorative border)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a fringe (decorative border) or resembling a fringe</li></ol><p><i>бахром(а́)</i> (baxrom(á)) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a garden/field/plantation for watermelons, melons or pumpkins</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic source, borrowed from Persian باغچه (bâġče). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂g-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bang!, bam!, whack! (a sudden percussive sound, as from an impact, a gunshot, etc.)</li><li>bam! (a sudden or abrupt occurrence)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacillus (any bacteria in the genus <i>Bacillus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>bacillum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacilli-carrier</li></ol><p>First attested in 1912. From <i>баци́лла</i> (bacílla) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́тель</i> (nosítelʹ), calque of German <i>Bazillenträger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ба́ки</i> (báki): (small) sideburns, side whiskers</li></ol><p><i>ба́ки</i> (báki) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бак</i> (bak): a (small) cistern; a (small) tank</li></ol><p><i>бак</i> (bak) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja); a (small) tower, a turret, a mirador, a bartisan</li></ol><p><i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tower</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) turret</li></ol><p><i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) pate, noggin, bonce (slang word for head, part of the body)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, probably a borrowing of a dative singular form attested in Kipchak languages; compare Tatar <i>баш</i> (baş, “head”), Bashkir <i>башҡа</i> (başqa, literally “head.SG.DAT”).</p><p>Initial (in 17th century) meaning 'head of a large fish' implies an origin related to fish trade in South Russia (e.g. on Volga and Don).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bashkir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bashkir <i>башҡорт</i> (başqort). Compare with Chagatai باجقیر (bacqır).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bashkir</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bashkir (language)</li></ol><p><i>башки́р</i> (baškír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clever, smart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money (usually a large sum)</li></ol><p>Originally used by Cossacks, likely of Turkic origin. Compare Tatar <i>баш</i> (baş). Also see башловка (bašlovka, “the top share of Cossacks' loot, given to the leader or bravest Cossack.”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bashlyk, hood (<i>cone-shaped</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source — compare Bashkir <i>башлыҡ</i> (başlıq, “piece of headwear”), Turkish <i>başlık</i> (“hat”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bashlyk</li></ol><p><i>башлы́к</i> (bašlýk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>башлы́к</i> (bašlýk): (small) bashlyk</li></ol><p><i>башлы́к</i> (bašlýk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to pay for someone else</li></ol><p>From <i>башли</i> (bašli, “money”) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe</li><li>boot</li><li>chock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source (perhaps Ottoman Turkish باشمق (başmak)), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;baĺmak</i> (“boot, shoe”). Compare Tatar <i>башмак</i> (başmaq, “shoe”), dialectal Turkish <i>başmak</i> (“shoe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) shoemaker, cobbler</li></ol><p>From <i>башмач-</i> (bašmač-) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boot, shoe</li></ol><p><i>башма́к</i> (bašmák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li><li>(<i>military</i>) turret (rotating gun installation)</li></ol><p>First recorded in the 1st Pskov manuscript. Often used in the 17th century. Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>башта</i> (bašta) from Italian <i>bastia</i> via Polish <i>baszta</i> or Czech <i>bašta</i>. The ending was influenced by the suffix <i>-ня</i> (-nja), which creates nouns of place, as in <i>па́шня</i> (pášnja), <i>тамо́жня</i> (tamóžnja). Doublet of <i>Басти́лия</i> (Bastílija) and <i>бастио́н</i> (bastión).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>опло́т</i> (oplót), <i>плете́нь</i> (pleténʹ) (akin to <i>плести́</i> (plestí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivory tower (sheltered, overly-academic existence or perspective, implying a disconnection or lack of awareness of reality or practical considerations)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>tour d'ivoire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lull, to rock to sleep</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bayan, button accordion</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) an old joke</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) syringe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bajanъ</i>. Related to <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ).</p><p>The "old joke" sense originated from a joke (Internet meme) at http://www.anekdot.ru — <i>Хорони́ли тёщу — порва́ли два <b>бая́на</b></i> (Xoroníli tjóšču — porváli dva <b>bajána</b>, “when we buried my mother-in-law, we broke two accordions”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bayan player; bayanist</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) poster of old jokes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to speak, to tell (<i>also used in folklore or jocularly</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bàjati</i> (“to tell”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bā́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéh₂ti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging,&#32;humorous) LOL, an expression of laughter or amusement.</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, to represent wheezing or chuckling.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vigil, wakefulness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be awake, not sleep</li><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to keep watch, to keep vigil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бъдѣти</i> (bŭděti, “to be awake”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъděti</i> (“to be awake”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;budḗˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бди́тельный</i> (bdítelʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бди́тельно</i> (bdítelʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бди́тельный</i> (bdítelʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бди́тельно</i> (bdítelʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>watchfully, vigilantly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vigilance, watchfulness</li></ol><p><i>бди́тельный</i> (bdítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigilant, watchful</li></ol><p><i>бдеть</i> (bdetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) baa (sound a sheep makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) baby (very young child)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) baby (sweetheart, darling, also as a form of address)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>baby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babysitter (baby or child caretaker)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>babysitter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>run</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) running (as in athletics or track and field)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jog (exercise)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>races, horse-race</li><li>hiding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běgati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰegʷ-</i>, cognate with Lithuanian <i>bėgti</i> and Ancient Greek <i>φέβομαι</i> (phébomai, “to flee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of eyes</i>) characterized by nystagmus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) behemoth</li></ol><p>From German <i>Behemoth</i>, from Hebrew <i>בְּהֵמוֹת</i> (behemót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hippopotamus</li></ol><p><i>бегемо́т</i> (begemót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hippopotamus</li></ol><p><i>бегемо́т</i> (begemót) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to run (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runaway, fugitive</li><li>jailbreaker</li></ol><p><i>бе́глый</i> (béglyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fluently (expressing oneself easily)</li><li>superficially, cursorily</li></ol><p><i>бе́глый</i> (béglyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fluent, quick, fast</li><li>superficial, cursory</li><li>fugitive</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fugitive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běglъ</i>. Equivalent to <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) running</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) race, racing</li><li>cross-country</li></ol><p><i>бег</i> (beg) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>running, at a run</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) hastily, urgently</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>run!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>begonia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running about</li><li>trouble, bustle, cares</li></ol><p><i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight, escape (act of fleeing)</li></ol><p><i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runner, one who runs</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a kind of an Old Believer with a vagabond lifestyle as part of the ideology</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běgũnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bembidion (<i>Bembidion</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleeing, running</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, trouble, disaster, mischief</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běda</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeydʰ-</i>, whence English <i>bide</i>, <i>faith</i> and <i>fidelity</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) travel guidebook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Baedeker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badge (nameplate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>badge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) bedlam (a lunatic asylum)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chaos, mess, madhouse (a place or state of complete disorder)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bedlam</i>. Doublet of <i>Вифлеем</i> (Viflejem).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj): poor, pitiable</li></ol><p><i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become poor, to become difficult</li></ol><p><i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>poorly</li></ol><p><i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty, poorness</li><li>indigence, penury</li></ol><p>From <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj, “poor”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) the poor</li></ol><p><i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor (having no possessions or money)</li><li>poor, scanty (having little of a desirable thing)</li><li>poor, miserable, wretched</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bědьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>беда́</i> (bedá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor relation</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>parent pauvre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor fellow, poor devil, wretch</li></ol><p><i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-яга</i> (-jaga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>бедня́га</i> (bednjága): poor thing, poor fellow, poor devil</li></ol><p><i>бедня́га</i> (bednjága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor man, pauper</li><li>small farmer, poor peasant</li></ol><p><i>бе́да</i> (béda) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) poor devil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>бідолага</i> (bidolaha, “poor thing, poor person”), from an earlier бідолаха related to Ukrainian <i>біда</i> (bida), which is a cognate with Russian <i>беда</i> (beda). The word has been adapted to the Russian phonetics with the change of "і" in Ukrainian <i>біда</i> (bida) to "е" like in Russian <i>беда</i> (beda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimpernel (<i>Pimpinella</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bedrьnьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) thigh, hip</li><li>(<i>meat</i>) leg, round, loin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bedro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) disaster</li><li>disastrous</li><li>extremely serious, extremely severe</li><li>dangerous</li></ol><p><i>бе́дствие</i> (bédstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calamity, disaster</li></ol><p>From <i>беда́</i> (bedá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije) or from Old Church Slavonic <i>бѣдьство</i> (bědĭstvo), remodeled after other slavonicisims featuring <i>-ствие</i> (-stvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live in poverty, to be in misery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бѣдьствовати</i> (bědĭstvovati). By surface analysis, <i>бе́дствие</i> (bédstvije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedouin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic بَدَوِيُّون&nbsp;pl (badawiyyūn), from بَدَوِيّ (badawiyy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bedouin</li></ol><p>From <i>бедуи́н</i> (beduín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beige</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>beige</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective&#32;only</i>) to run, to be running</li><li>to flee, to be fleeing</li><li>to avoid, to be avoiding, to shun, to be shunning</li><li>to run after [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běžati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bēˀgjā́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰégʷ-y-eh₂-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰegʷ-</i> (“to flee, run”). 1-st person singular and 3-rd person plural, as well as imperative forms derive from <i>бечь</i> (bečʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be hasty, to act prematurely, to get ahead of oneself, to put the cart before the horse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beige</li></ol><p><i>беж</i> (bež) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refugee</li><li>asylum seeker (one who seeks asylum)</li></ol><p><i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without, aside from, except, minus, free of/from (signifying absence)</li><li>minus, less</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bez</i> (“without”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeǵʰs</i> (<+ <i>&#42;h₁éǵʰs</i> (“out”), whence also <i>из</i> (iz)).</p><p>Cognate with Lithuanian <i>bè</i> (“without; besides”), Latvian <i>bez</i> (“without”), Old Prussian <i>bhe</i> (“without”), Sanskrit <i>बहिस्</i> (bahís, “outside”), Persian بی (bi, “without”).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>without</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) no kidding, seriously, for real</li></ol><p>From без балды́ (bez baldý).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) no kidding, seriously, for real</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unbeknownst to, without the knowledge of [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with nothing (having lost everything)</li><li>naked, with nothing on, without anything on (with no clothes on)</li></ol><p>Literally, “without everything”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for a very short time, less than (normally) required or expected</li></ol><p>Literally, “(for) a week minus/without a year, i.e. an impossibly short or negative duration of time”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>без дополне́ния</i> (bez dopolnénija): without any object(s) or complement(s)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(speech act,&#32;idiomatic) all kidding aside (used to attempt to make a serious point in a jocular conversation, lit.&#58; "without fools")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost, nearly, just short of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hell-for-leather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) no kidding, seriously, for real</li></ol><p>Literally, “without a cunt”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>loose, not leashed, off leash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unaided, alone, single-handed (without outside help)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to no avail, to no purpose, in vain (achieving no result or serving no useful end)</li><li>senselessly, purposelessly (for no good reason)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frankly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>dis-, un-</li><li>-less</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bez-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly (in a careless manner)</li></ol><p><i>безала́берный</i> (bezalábernyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shiftlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>безалаберный</i> (bezalabernyj, “shiftless”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shiftless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alcohol-free (containing no alcohol)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>алкого́льный</i> (alkogólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indispensable, unavoidable, not up for debate</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>альтернати́вный</i> (alʹternatívnyj). Compare Polish <i>bezalternatywny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) peremptorily (not allowing appeal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) peremptorily, dictatorially, imperiously, categorically, absolutely, dogmatically, cavalierly, presumptuously (not allowing objection)</li></ol><p><i>безапелляцио́нный</i> (bezapelljaciónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) peremptory, final (not allowing appeal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) peremptory, dictatorial, imperious, categorical, absolute, dogmatic, cavalier, presumptuously (not allowing objection)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>апелля́ция</i> (apelljácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barrierless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>барье́р</i> (barʹjér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comfortably, fairly well-to-do</li></ol><p><i>безбе́дный</i> (bezbédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stowaway, fare dodger</li><li>gatecrasher</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>биле́т</i> (bilét) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheism</li><li>godlessness</li><li>ungodliness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безбожиѥ</i> (bezbožije). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безбожьникъ</i> (bezbožĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godless</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безбожьнъ</i> (bezbožĭnŭ), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓́θεος</i> (ắtheos). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>painlessly (in a painless manner)</li></ol><p><i>безболе́зненный</i> (bezboléznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painless (without pain or trouble)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>боле́знь</i> (boléznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beardless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezbordъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>борода́</i> (borodá) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>безборо́дый</i> (bezboródyj, “beardless”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celibacy</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shoreless</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) boundless, vast</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>брег</i> (breg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbelief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscurity</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) absence of news</li></ol><p><i>безве́стный</i> (bezvéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscure, unknown</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calm, windlessness (absence of wind)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visa-free (not requiring a visa)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ви́за</i> (víza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tastelessness, lack of taste, bad taste</li></ol><p>First attested in 1813. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вкус</i> (vkus) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of German <i>Geschmacklosigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of good taste, tastelessness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something tasteless, raunch</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вкус</i> (vkus) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tasteless</li></ol><p><i>безвку́сие</i> (bezvkúsije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moistless (without moisture)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вла́га</i> (vlága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anarchy (absence of any form of political authority or government)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerless (lacking legal authority)</li></ol><p><i>безвла́стие</i> (bezvlástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waterless, arid</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water shortage</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>irretrievably, irrevocably</li></ol><p><i>безвозвра́тный</i> (bezvozvrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irretrievable, unrecoverable</li><li>irrevocable, permanent, non-repayable</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>возвра́т</i> (vozvrát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gratis, for free</li></ol><p><i>безвозме́здный</i> (bezvozmézdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gratuitousness</li></ol><p><i>безвозме́здный</i> (bezvozmézdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free (of charge), without compensation; gratuitous, unpaid</li></ol><p>From archaic <i>безме́здный</i> (bezmézdnyj) changed under the influence of <i>возме́здие</i> (vozmézdije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weak will, lack of will, limpness</li></ol><p>First attested in 1863. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of New Latin <i>abūlia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀβουλίᾱ</i> (aboulíā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hairless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) spineless, gutless, weak-willed</li></ol><p><i>безво́л(ие)</i> (bezvól(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmless, innocuous</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вред</i> (vred) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colchicum (<i>Colchicum</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) period of social and cultural stagnation</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) time of unhappiness or misery</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inability to exit</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hopelessness, desperation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) inalterability</li></ol><p><i>безвы́ходный</i> (bezvýxodnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having no way out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hopeless, desperate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unalterable</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>вы́ход</i> (výxod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eyeless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Speechless, mute, without a voice.</li><li>Too timid, not expressing one’s opinion.</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful, calm, good-humoured</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>гнев</i> (gnev, “anger”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voiceless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illiteracy</li><li>ignorance</li></ol><p>From <i>безгра́мотный</i> (bezgrámotnyj, “illiterate”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illiterate</li><li>ignorant</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infinite, boundless, limitless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>грани́ца</i> (graníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinlessness; innocence</li></ol><p>From <i>безгре́шный</i> (bezgréšnyj, “sinless; innocent”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinless, without sin</li><li>pure, innocent</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>talentlessly, ineptly, in a mediocre manner</li><li>pointlessly, uselessly</li></ol><p><i>безда́рный</i> (bezdárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) lack of talent</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) person without talent, dullard</li></ol><p><i>безда́рный</i> (bezdárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talentless, inept</li><li>pointless, useless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дар</i> (dar) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bungler, botcher</li><li>(collective,&#32;inanimate) bunglers, botchers</li></ol><p>From <i>безда́р(ный)</i> (bezdár(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), with expected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inactive, idle</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inaction</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije), a calque of French <i>inaction</i>. First attested in 1788.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inactive, to do nothing, to stand idle, to be inactive/inoperative</li></ol><p><i>безде́йств(ие)</i> (bezdéjstv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knick-knack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knick-knack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knick-knack, trifle</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, inactivity, shirking</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, loafer, lazybones</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do nothing, to idle, to laze, to loaf around</li></ol><p><i>безде́льник</i> (bezdélʹnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futile activity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moneyless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of money</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childlessness (the state of being childless)</li></ol><p><i>безде́тный</i> (bezdétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childless (not having any children)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezdětьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дитя́</i> (ditjá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inactivity, inertia, passivity, lethargy</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>де́ятельность</i> (déjatelʹnostʹ), probably, a calque of French <i>inaction</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abyss, deep (a bottomless or unfathomed depth)</li><li>vast number</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezdъna</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дно</i> (dno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsubstantiated, unfounded, unproved</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>доказа́тельство</i> (dokazátelʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>beweislos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homelessness</li></ol><p>From <i>бездо́мный</i> (bezdómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homeless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeless person</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homelessness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bottomless</li><li>unfathomable, fathomless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дно</i> (dno) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impassability; mud-locked roads, lack or shortage of (good) roads</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>доро́га</i> (doróga) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). Compare with Ukrainian <i>бездорі́жжя</i> (bezdorížžja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessly, unthinkingly</li></ol><p><i>безду́мный</i> (bezdúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtless, unthinking, feckless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ду́ма</i> (dúma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soulless, heartless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezdušьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дух</i> (dux) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifeless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>дыха́ние</i> (dyxánije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meringue (mixture)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) kiss</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>baiser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ruthlessly, cruelly, mercilessly, without remorse</li></ol><p><i>безжа́лостный</i> (bezžálostnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruthless, pitiless, merciless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>жа́лость</i> (žálostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifeless, inanimate</li><li>lifeless, pallid, insipid, lacklustre</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>light-heartedly, in a carefree way/fashion, with nothing to care about</li></ol><p><i>беззабо́тный</i> (bezzabótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insouciance, nonchalance, unconcern, carelessness (lack of care)</li></ol><p><i>беззабо́тный</i> (bezzabótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light-hearted</li><li>carefree, untroubled, insouciant</li><li>careless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>забо́та</i> (zabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>selflessly, devotedly, whole-heartedly</li></ol><p><i>беззаве́тный</i> (bezzavétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole-hearted, selfless, devoted (<i>service, devotion, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>заве́т</i> (zavét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawlessness</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemer, infringer</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) lawlessness</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defencelessness</li></ol><p><i>беззащи́тный</i> (bezzaščítnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defenseless, unprotected</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>защи́та</i> (zaščíta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soundlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>soundlessly, noiselessly, silently</li></ol><p><i>беззву́чный</i> (bezzvúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silent, without sound</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starless (without visible stars)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mirrorless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зе́ркало</i> (zérkalo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirrorless camera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>goodnaturedly</li></ol><p><i>беззло́бный</i> (bezzlóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river mussel (<i>Anodonta</i> spp. etc.)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), calque of New Latin <i>Anodonta</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνόδοντα</i> (anódonta), neuter plural of <i>ἀνόδων</i> (anódōn, “toothless”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toothless</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) insipid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezzǫbъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forestlessness</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-, “-less”) +&lrm; <i>лес</i> (les, “forest; woods”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faceless, featureless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лик</i> (lik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lensless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ли́нз(а)</i> (línz(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leafless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) impersonal (not representing a person)</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj), a calque of Latin <i>impersōnālis</i>. First attested in 1746.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impersonal verb (verb used only in the infinitive or in the third-person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horseless, steedless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ло́шад(ь)</i> (lóšad(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moonless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>луна</i> (luna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpopulated</li><li>abandoned</li><li>empty (<i>of people</i>)</li><li>unmanned</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence of people</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lungless, without lungs</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-, negating prefix) +&lrm; <i>лёгочный</i> (ljógočnyj, “lung, pulmonary”, relational adjective)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maskless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ма́ска</i> (máska) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hive without a queen</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ма́т(ка)</i> (mát(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) gratis, for free</li></ol><p><i>безме́здный</i> (bezmézdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) free (of charge), without compensation; gratuitous, unpaid</li></ol><p>From the Old East Slavic <i>безмьздьнꙑи</i> (bezmĭzdĭnyi). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>мзда</i> (mzda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stilyard</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a weight measure, 2<s style="display:none">+</s><small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>2</sub></small> фу́нта (fúnta) – 1.022 kg</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>безмѣнъ</i> (bezměnŭ), which see for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>infinitely, immensely, immeasurably, beyond all measure</li></ol><p><i>безме́рный</i> (bezmérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immense, immeasurable, boundless, infinite, unbounded</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brainless, stupid</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>мозг</i> (mozg) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безмлъвиѥ</i> (bezmlŭvije). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>молва́</i> (molvá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently</li><li>mutely, speechlessly</li></ol><p><i>безмо́лвный</i> (bezmólvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mute, silent, speechless</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безмлъвьнъ</i> (bezmlŭvĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>молва́</i> (molvá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be silent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безмлъвьствовати</i> (bezmlŭvĭstvovati). By surface analysis, <i>безмо́лвие</i> (bezmólvije) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>serenely, quietly</li></ol><p><i>безмяте́жный</i> (bezmjatéžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insouciance, placidity, serenity</li></ol><p><i>безмяте́жный</i> (bezmjatéžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serene, tranquil, placid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безмѧтежьнъ</i> (bezmętežĭnŭ). Equivalent to <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>мяте́ж</i> (mjatéž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>безме́н</i> (bezmén, “stilyard”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hopelessly</li></ol><p><i>безнадёжный</i> (beznadjóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hopelessness</li><li>despair</li></ol><p>From <i>безнадёжный</i> (beznadjóžnyj, “hopeless”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hopeless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with impunity</li></ol><p><i>безнака́занный</i> (beznakázannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impunity (freedom from punishment or retribution&#59; security from any reprisal or injurious consequences of an action, behavior, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>безнака́занный</i> (beznakázannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpunished</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нака́занный</i> (nakázannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cashless, noncash</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нали́ч(ные)</i> (nalíč(nyje)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tax-free</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нало́г</i> (nalóg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legless</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) apod, apodal</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnumbered</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>но́мер</i> (nómer) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immorally</li></ol><p><i>безнра́вственный</i> (beznrávstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immorality</li><li>dissoluteness</li></ol><p>From <i>безнра́вственный</i> (beznrávstvennyj, “immoral”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immoral</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нра́вственный</i> (nrávstvennyj), a calque of French <i>immoral</i>. First attested in 1809.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bezoar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmless (incapable of causing harm or danger)</li><li>inoffensive</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>оби́да</i> (obída) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cloudless</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unclouded, serene</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>о́блако</i> (óblako) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) without threshing</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>обмоло́т</i> (obmolót, “threshing”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>casing-free, skinless (<i>e.g. sausages</i>)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>оболо́чка</i> (obolóčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ugliness</li><li>deformity</li><li>mess</li><li>disgrace, outrage, scandal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безобразиѥ</i> (bezobrazije). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hooligan, scamp</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave/carry on outrageously/disgracefully, to make a nuisance of oneself</li></ol><p><i>безобра́зник</i> (bezobráznik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ugly, hideous</li><li>disgraceful, scandalous, shameful</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) formless, vague</li><li>(<i>art</i>) nonrepresentational, nonfigurative</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with stress shift.</p><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reckless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unreservedly, without reserve, unconditionally</li></ol><p><i>безогово́рочный</i> (bezogovóročnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreserved, unqualified, unconditional</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>огово́рка</i> (ogovórka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windowless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>окно́</i> (oknó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>safely (in a safe manner)</li></ol><p><i>безопа́сный</i> (bezopásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safety</li><li>security</li></ol><p>From <i>безопа́сный</i> (bezopásnyj, “safe”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ty”). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>bezopasnost</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe, secure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezopasьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>опа́сный</i> (opásnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unarmed</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) defenseless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ору́ж(ие)</i> (orúž(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>groundlessly, without grounds</li></ol><p><i>безоснова́тельный</i> (bezosnovátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baseless, groundless (not grounded in facts, not based on anything true)</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>основа́ние</i> (osnovánije) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj), a calque of German <i>grundlos</i>. First attested in 1842.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nonstop (without stopping)</li></ol><p><i>безостано́вочный</i> (bezostanóvočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unceasing, nonstop</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>остано́вка</i> (ostanóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unanswered, unrequited</li><li>meek, submissive, resigned</li><li>gentle</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отве́т</i> (otvét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irresponsibility</li></ol><p><i>безотве́тственный</i> (bezotvétstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irresponsible</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отве́тственный</i> (otvétstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irrevocable</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отзы́в</i> (otzýv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reliable, faultless, trouble-free, dependable (<i>machine, operation, etc.</i>)</li><li>ever-willing, never refusing a request</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отка́з</i> (otkáz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recoilless gun, recoilless rifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>recoilless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отка́т</i> (otkát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pressing, urgent, immediate</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отлага́ть</i> (otlagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not relatively; irrespective</li></ol><p><i>безотноси́тельный</i> (bezotnosítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irrelative, irrespective, unconditional</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>относи́ть</i> (otnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerless, dreary, dismal</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отра́да</i> (otráda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fatherlessness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fatherless child</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) uneducated person; without a senior, superior, sensei</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-о́вщина</i> (-óvščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not subject to control</li><li>unaccountable, instinctive, unconscious</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>отчётный</i> (otčótnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>correctly, perfectly</li><li>unerringly, infallibly</li><li>unmistakably</li></ol><p><i>безоши́бочный</i> (bezošíbočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unerring, faultless</li><li>correct, exact</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) error-free</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>оши́бка</i> (ošíbka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unemployment</li></ol><p>First attested in 1869. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica), calque of German <i>Arbeitslosigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unemployed, jobless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unemployed person</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the unemployed</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bleak, joyless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ра́дость</i> (rádostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undivided</li><li>whole-hearted</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>разде́л</i> (razdél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indifference, nonchalance</li><li>invariance</li></ol><p><i>без</i> (bez) +&lrm; <i>разли́чие</i> (razlíčije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indifferently</li></ol><p><i>безразли́чный</i> (bezrazlíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indifferent, apathetic, nonchalant</li></ol><p><i>безразли́чие</i> (bezrazlíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reckless, thoughtless, rash</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рассу́д(ок)</i> (rassúd(ok)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recklessness, rashness, folly</li><li>reckless act</li></ol><p><i>безрассу́д(ный)</i> (bezrassúd(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>futilely, to no avail, in vain</li></ol><p><i>безрезульта́тный</i> (bezrezulʹtátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessful, futile</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>результа́т</i> (rezulʹtát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>over-the-counter (able to be obtained without a doctor's prescription)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рецепту́ра</i> (receptúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hornless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without relatives; rootless; not belonging to any race or tribe</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without a murmur, compliantly</li></ol><p><i>безро́потный</i> (bezrópotnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmurmuring, uncomplaining, resigned, submissive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бездръпътьнъ</i> (bezdrŭpŭtĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ро́пот</i> (rópot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeveless jacket, sleeveless pullover, sleeveless cardigan</li></ol><p><i>безрука́в(ный)</i> (bezrukáv(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeveless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рука́в</i> (rukáv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armless (without arms)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awkward, clumsy, all thumbs</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) lack of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ры́б(а)</i> (rýb(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of fish (usually only occurs in expressions)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>break-even</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>убы́точный</i> (ubýtočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) unaccented, unstressed (pronounced with little or no stress)</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>уда́р</i> (udár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrestrained</li><li>impetuous</li><li>unchecked</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>у́держ</i> (úderž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>flawlessly, perfectly, impeccably</li></ol><p><i>безукори́зненный</i> (bezukoríznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfect, impeccable, irreproachable, flawless</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>укори́зна</i> (ukorízna) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj), a calque of French <i>irréprochable</i>. First attested in 1834.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madman, lunatic</li><li>madcap</li></ol><p>From <i>без</i> (bez, “without”) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insanity, madness (state of being insane)</li><li>folly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>безоумьѥ</i> (bezumĭje), which is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀφροσύνη</i> (aphrosúnē). By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>madly, awfully</li><li>terribly, dreadfully, extremely</li></ol><p><i>безу́мный</i> (bezúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crazy, mad, insane</li><li>rash, reckless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezumьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insanity, madness, folly</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perfectly, faultlessly</li></ol><p><i>безупре́чный</i> (bezupréčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameless, faultless</li><li>unstained, stainless, spotless</li></ol><p>First attested in 1835 [RNC]. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>упрёк</i> (uprjók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of French <i>irréprochable</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>certainly, undoubtedly, absolutely</li><li>there is no doubt that...</li></ol><p><i>безусло́вный</i> (bezuslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absolute, unconditional</li><li>undoubted, indisputable</li></ol><p>First attested in 1770. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>усло́вный</i> (uslóvnyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>unbedingt</i> or French <i>inconditionnel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessfully</li></ol><p><i>безуспе́шный</i> (bezuspéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessfulness</li></ol><p>From <i>безуспе́шный</i> (bezuspéšnyj, “unsuccessful”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessful</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>успе́х</i> (uspéx) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moustacheless, mustacheless</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ус</i> (us) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsolable</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>уте́шить</i> (utéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indifferently</li></ol><p><i>безуча́стный</i> (bezučástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indifferent [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something or someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>безуча́стие</i> (bezučástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>без</i> (bez).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) anuclear</li><li>nuclear-free</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ядро́</i> (jadró) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) The absence of the letter ѣ (called <i>ять</i> (jatʹ) - yat) in modern Russian orthography.</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>playless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking in ideas; idealess</li></ol><p>First attested in 1880. <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>иде́я</i> (idéja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>ideenlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscure, unknown, unfamiliar</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anonymous, nameless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>безименьнꙑи</i> (bezimenĭnyi, “nameless”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezimenьnъ</i> (“nameless”), from <i>&#42;bez-</i> (“-less”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;jьmę</i> (“name”). Compare <i>безымённый</i> (bezymjónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring finger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) nameless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezimenьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>имен-</i> (imen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indexless</li></ol><p>From <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>и́ндекс</i> (índeks) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparkless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hopelessness, despair</li></ol><p><i>безысхо́дный</i> (bezysxódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interminable, endless</li><li>hopeless</li><li>desperate</li><li>irreparable</li></ol><p><i>без-</i> (bez-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>исхо́д</i> (isxód, “outcome, way out”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anechoic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bey <i>(Turkish official)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>bey</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) bagel (a toroidal bread roll that is boiled before it is baked)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bagel</i>, from Yiddish <i>בייגל</i> (beygl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beiruti</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Beiruti</li></ol><p><i>Бейру́т</i> (Bejrút) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baseball <i>(game)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baseball player, baseballer</li></ol><p>From <i>́бейсбо́л</i> (́bejsból) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baseball cap</li></ol><p><i>бейсбо́л</i> (bejsból) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baseball (game)</li></ol><p><i>бейсбо́л</i> (bejsból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>title</i>) bey, beg, bek, master</li><li>Turkish or Persian chieftain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بك (beg, “lord”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) natural, cancel (the symbol ♮)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bécarre</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>b quadrātum</i> (“B natural”, literally “square B”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snipe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bécasse</i>. Further from Latin <i>beccus</i> (whence also English <i>beak</i>) + <i>-āceus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snipe; snipe's</li></ol><p><i>бека́с</i> (bekás) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young snipe</li></ol><p><i>бека́с</i> (bekás) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>unit</i>) becquerel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacon (cut of meat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bacon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bacon (cut of meat)</li></ol><p><i>беко́н</i> (bekón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bel (unit of sound intensity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonstandard spelling of <i>белору́с</i> (belorús): Belarusian (person from Belarus or of Belarusian descent)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>белару́с</i> (bjelarús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vodka</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) ellipsis of <i>бе́лая горя́чка</i> (bélaja gorjáčka, “the shakes; the DTs; delirium tremens”, literally “white fever”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;figurative) strange bird, rare bird, black sheep</li></ol><p>Literally, “white crow”. Compare Polish <i>biały kruk</i> and Ukrainian <i>бі́ла воро́на</i> (bíla voróna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) delirium tremens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) blue blood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A northern summer night (when the sky is bright)</li></ol><p>Literally, “white night”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;colloquial) salt, which harms the user's health when eaten too much</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) sugar, which harms the user's health when eaten too much</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;youth slang) cocaine</li><li>(<i>journalistic</i>) an avalanche that kills people in its path</li></ol><p>Literally, “white death”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Belgorod (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Бе́лгород</i> (Bélgorod) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бело́</i> (beló)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belemnite</li></ol><p>Analyzable as Ancient Greek <i>βέλεμνον</i> (bélemnon) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henbane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bel(e)nъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”)</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>белёсый</i> (beljósyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn white, (<i>intransitive</i>) to whiten (inchoative deadjectival verb of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj), compare factitive <i>бели́ть</i> (belítʹ))</li><li>(stative deadjectival verb of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj))</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn gray</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become brighter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to dawn</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to gleam, to be visible (<i>of something white</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělěti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) rubbish, nonsense, balderdash</li></ol><p>The variants <i>билиберда́</i> (biliberdá), <i>кериберда́</i> (keriberdá), <i>кулиберда́</i> (kuliberdá) are also found. Per Vasmer, possibly borrowed from a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whiteness</li><li>bleach</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belizean</li></ol><p><i>Бели́з</i> (Belíz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleachery</li></ol><p><i>бели́ть</i> (belítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blanch, to whiten, to make something white (factitive deadjectival verb of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj), compare inchoative and stative <i>беле́ть</i> (belétʹ))</li><li>to whitewash (<i>literally or figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) squirrel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бѣличии</i> (běličii), from <i>бѣлица</i> (bělica, “squirrel”), diminutive of <i>бѣла</i> (běla, “squirrel”). By surface analysis, recognized as an irregular form of <i>бе́лка</i> (bélka, “squirrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij) (the expected form would be *белочий).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirrel (mammal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бѣлъка</i> (bělŭka, “squirrel”), diminutive of <i>бѣла</i> (běla, “squirrel”) (see <i>-ка</i> (-ka)), from <i>бѣла вѣверица</i> (běla věverica, “white squirrel”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělъka</i>. See <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”), <i>ве́верица</i> (véverica, “squirrel; ermine, stoat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) egg-white, albumen; albuminous</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) protein</li></ol><p><i>бело́к</i> (belók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) belladonna, deadly nightshade (<i>Atropa belladonna</i>)</li><li>(<i>pharmacy</i>) belladonna (an alkaloid))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>belladonna</i> (<i>bella donna</i>, literally “beautiful lady”), altered by folk etymology from Medieval Latin <i>bladona</i> (“nightshade”), from Gaulish <i>&#42;blātōnā</i>, <i>blātunā</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;blātus</i> (“flower”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰléh₃tus</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i> (“blossom, flower”). The folk etymology was motivated by the cosmetic use of nightshade for dilating the eyes.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelist, fiction writer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Belletrist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easy reading, pulp fiction</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) fiction, belles-lettres</li></ol><p>From <i>беллетрист</i> (belletrist) +&lrm; <i>-ика</i> (-ika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white-blue-white flag (Russian anti-war flag)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white, blue, and red (the colors of the Russian flag)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redwing</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бровь</i> (brovʹ, “eyebrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blond, having blond hair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whitish</li></ol><p><i>бе́л(ый)</i> (bél(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fair copy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) white guardsman (soldier in one of the White Armies of the Russian Revolution)</li></ol><p>From <i>бело-</i> (belo-, “white”) +&lrm; <i>гварде́ец</i> (gvardéjec, “guardsman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) delirium tremens</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>горя́чка</i> (gorjáčka, “fever”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having very white teeth</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg-white, albumen</li><li>sclera, white (<i>of an eye</i>)</li><li>(<i>nutrition</i>) protein</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók). The protein sense is a semantic calque of German Eiweiß (“egg-white, (<i>nutrition</i>) protein”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white-skinned (having white skin)</li></ol><p>From <i>бе́л(ый)</i> (bél(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ко́ж(а)</i> (kóž(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leukemia</li></ol><p>First attested in 1859. <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of New Latin <i>leucaemia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) + <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blond, fair-haired</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belarusian, Belarusan (person from Belarus or of Belarusian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belorussian (relating to Belorussia, former state of the Soviet Union)</li><li>Belarusian (relating to Belarus, current nation formed from the same territory)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belarusian language</li></ol><p><i>Белору́ссия</i> (Belorússija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belarusian-speaking, Belarusophone</li><li>written or spoken in Belarusian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person shirking rough or dirty (physical) work</li></ol><p>From <i>белый</i> (belyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ру́чка</i> (rúčka, “small hand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beloribitsa (<i>Stenodus leucichthys</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snow-white</li></ol><p>First attested in 1795. <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>schneeweiß</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) whiteness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělota</i> (“whiteness”), from <i>&#42;bělъ</i> (“white”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ota</i>. Morphologically <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matgrass (<i>Nardus stricta</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>&#42;бѣлоѫсъ</i> (&#42;běloǫsŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běloǫsъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Белоус)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Belous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seamstress</li></ol><p>First attested in 1806. <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>шве́йка</i> (švéjka), calque of German <i>Weißnäherin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beluga (European sturgeon)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) beluga (white whale) (the term was used until the XIX century and is still sometimes confused with "белуха" - the modern term for "white whale")</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-уга</i> (-uga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balochi (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Balochi</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balochi (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>белу́дж</i> (belúdž) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beluga</li></ol><p><i>белу́га</i> (belúga) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beluga (white whale)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Белуха)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belukha (a mountain in the Altai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a weak tea made of re-used (secondary boiled) leaves</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li><li>white skinned</li><li>light, fair</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) a White; a member of the White Guard; an anticommunist</li><li>white person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cep, porcini (<i>Boletus edulis</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “white mushroom”, referring to the fact that the cep's white flesh does not change color when cut and exposed to air.</p><p>Compare typologically Mongolian <i>цагаан мөөг</i> (cagaan möög) (&lt; <i>цагаан</i> (cagaan)). Also compare <i>ры́жик</i> (rýžik) (&lt; <i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Catholicism</i>) white smoke</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white dwarf (white dwarf star)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white clover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(figurative,&#32;historical) one-hundred- or two-hundred-rouble note</li></ol><p>бе́лый ле́бедь (literally, white swan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polar bear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) God's green earth, the world</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Belgian (Belgian, person from Belgium)</li></ol><p><i>Бе́льгия</i> (Bélʹgija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belgian</li></ol><p><i>Бе́льгия</i> (Bélʹgija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) underwear, undergarments, bedclothes, linens</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) laundry</li></ol><p><i>бельё</i> (belʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>бельё</i> (belʹjó)</li></ol><p><i>бельё</i> (belʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leucoma, albugo, walleye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bělьmo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a baby squirrel, a young squirrel</li></ol><p><i>белка</i> (belka) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) piano nobile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bel étage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underwear, skivvies, underclothes, undergarment, undies</li><li>bedclothes, linen</li><li>washing (textiles that have been or are to be washed)</li></ol><p>From <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó). Compare Polish <i>bielizna</i>. Also compare Hungarian <i>fehérnemű</i>, German <i>Weißwaren</i>, and English <i>tighty whities or whites</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Czech <i>prádlo</i> (“laundry (things to be laundered)”), <i>prádélko</i> (“diminutive of prádlo; lingerie”) (note <i>бельи́шко</i> (belʹíško)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white hare, mountain hare, arctic hare</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;history) a male member of the White Army, a white guardsman</li><li>(<i>history</i>) a small duchy in central Volga basin area during late Medieval time</li></ol><p>From <i>белый</i> (belyj) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak)</p><p>From Turkic, compare Ottoman Turkish بكلك.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) belyana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of pie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>бәлеш</i> (bäleş), from Classical Persian بالش (bâliš, “pillow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whitish, off-white</li></ol><p><i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-ёсый</i> (-jósyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) flat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bémol</i> (“b soft”). Ultimately from Latin <i>mollis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bengali</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bengali (language)</li></ol><p><i>Бенга́лия</i> (Bengálija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparkler, Bengal light (hand-held firework)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bengal tiger (Panthera tigris tigris)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) benefit (performance in honor of someone, to raise funds for him or her)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bénéfice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beneficiary</li></ol><p>Possibly from German <i>Benefiziar</i>, or French <i>bénéficiaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) seizing (means of tying together two thin wires or cables)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) paintbrush</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>benzel</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pędzel</i> or <i>pęzel</i>, from German <i>Pinsel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) benzylamine</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>бензил</i> (benzil) +&lrm; <i>ами́н</i> (amín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasoline, petrol</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Benzin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) heating device using gasoline as a fuel</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) benzene, gasoline</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasoline pump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gasoline</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>бензи́н</i> (benzín, “gasoline, petrol”)</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas tank, petrol tank, fuel tank</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín, “gasoline, petrol”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бак</i> (bak, “tank”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petrol / gasoline tanker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(organic chemistry,&#32;pharmacology) benzodiazepine</li></ol><p>Internationalism. See English <i>benzodiazepine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas station, petrol station</li></ol><p><i>бензо-</i> (benzo-) +&lrm; <i>запра́вка</i> (zaprávka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasoline pump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gas station</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas station</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasoline pump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gas station</li></ol><p><i>бензо-</i> (benzo-) +&lrm; <i>колонка</i> (kolonka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benzene, benzol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chainsaw</li></ol><p><i>бензо-</i> (benzo-) +&lrm; <i>пила́</i> (pilá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuel line</li></ol><p><i>бензи́н</i> (benzín) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прово́д</i> (provód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Beninese</li></ol><p><i>Бени́н</i> (Benín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бэнто́</i> (bɛntó, “bento”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berber, a representative of one of the nations that make up the indigenous population in Northwest Africa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic بَرْبَرِيّ (barbariyy, “Berber”), probably from Ancient Greek <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros, “non-Greek, foreign, barbarian”), apparently imitative of foreign speech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>бревно́</i> (brevnó)</li></ol><p>From <i>беревно́</i> (berevnó), <i>бервено́</i> (bervenó), from Old East Slavic <i>бьрьвьно</i> (bĭrĭvĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrvьno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bergamot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bergamot</li></ol><p><i>бергамо́т</i> (bergamót) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berdan rifle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) a cheap or old shotgun or hunting rifle</li></ol><p>English <i>Berdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Berd (a river in Altai Krai and Novosibirsk Oblast, Russia, a right tributary of the Ob)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Berdsk (a town in Novosibirsk Oblast, Russia; a suburb of Novosibirsk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bardiche</li></ol><p>First attested in 1596. Probably from Middle Low German <i>barde</i> + Russian suffix <i>-ыш</i> (-yš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank, shore, coast, beach</li><li>land</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>берегъ</i> (beregŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bergъ</i>. Doublet of <i>брег</i> (breg), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beach, coast; coastal</li></ol><p><i>бе́рег</i> (béreg) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (a wound), causing pain</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disturb, to stir up, to agitate (one's heart or soul, etc.), causing one to relive painful experiences</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, a shortening of <i>&#42;обереди́ть</i> (&#42;oberedítʹ), from <i>&#42;обвереди́ть</i> (&#42;obveredítʹ), from <i>об-</i> (ob-) + <i>&#42;вереди́ть</i> (&#42;veredítʹ, “to harm”) (compare <i>ве́ред</i> (véred), <i>вред</i> (vred), <i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrift, economy</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) care</li></ol><p><i>бережли́вый</i> (berežlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrifty, economical</li></ol><p><i>берег-</i> (bereg-, “to keep safe”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carefully</li></ol><p><i>бе́режный</i> (bérežnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concern (for someone)</li><li>cautiousness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>бе́режный</i> (bérežnyj, “careful, attentive”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, abstract suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful</li><li>caring, attentive</li><li>thrifty, economical</li></ol><p><i>берег-</i> (bereg-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg, “bank, shore, coast”)</li></ol><p><i>бе́рег</i> (béreg) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) March</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berzьnь</i> (“March; April”). The first part is from <i>берёза</i> (berjóza), the second is likely related to <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj), compare <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto), <i>злак</i> (zlak). The meaning is therefore: "the month when birches leaf out".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch forest</li><li>birch wood (the wood of a birch tree)</li></ol><p><i>берёза</i> (berjóza) +&lrm; <i>-ня́к</i> (-nják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>березня́к</i> (bereznják): a small birch forest</li></ol><p>From <i>березня́к</i> (bereznják) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) March</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>березозолъ</i> (berezozolŭ, “April”). The first part is from <i>берёза</i> (berjóza), the second is likely related to <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj), compare <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto), <i>злак</i> (zlak). The meaning is therefore: "the month when birches leaf out".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get pregnant</li></ol><p><i>бере́мен-</i> (berémen-) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pregnancy</li><li>childbearing</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) gravidity, gestation</li></ol><p><i>бере́менный</i> (berémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant (carrying developing offspring within the body)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) filled (with something, usually abstract)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) pregnant (having a profusion of ideas)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bermenьnъ</i>; morphologically, from <i>бере́мя</i> (berémja) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj). Cognate with Polish <i>brzemienny</i> (“burdened”), Macedonian <i>бремен</i> (bremen), Bulgarian <i>бременен</i> (bremenen), Serbo-Croatian <i>бремѐнит</i> / Serbo-Croatian <i>bremènit</i>; compare also Czech <i>březí</i> (“gravid, pregnant [animals only]”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;formal) burden</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) load, heavy weight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>беремѧ</i> (beremę), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bermę</i>. Doublet of <i>бре́мя</i> (brémja), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle tree (<i>Euonymus</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; the plant itself is rarely used, so the name of the plant is also rarely mentioned, resulting in various variants of the original name.</p><p>In its present state the Russian form is a blend of different names for various plant names, including any combination of <i>бе́рест</i> (bérest, “elm”), <i>берёза</i> (berjóza, “birch”), <i>ве́реск</i> (véresk, “heather”), <i>брусни́ка</i> (brusníka, “ligonberry”), <i>клён</i> (kljon, “maple”), <i>хме́ль</i> (xmélʹ, “hop”) and <i>дёрен</i> (djóren, “dogwood”). Akin to Ukrainian <i>берескле́т</i> (beresklét).</p><p>The Russian term may be borrowed from Ukrainian. This is supported by the plant's distribution and the fact that the stressed <i>-е-</i> hasn't become <i>-ё-</i>. However, the latter could be a result of the term's uncommonness. Furthermore, the alternatively stressed variant <i>бере́склет</i> (berésklet), found in the 19th century may have gone against this regular change.</p><p>Cognates and further relations</p><p>The related Slavic terms used for spindle-tree are Belarusian <i>брызглі́на</i> (bryzhlína), Ukrainian <i>брусли́на</i> (bruslýna), Polish <i>trzmielina</i>, Czech <i>brslen</i> and Slovak <i>bršlen</i>. Serbo-Croatian <i>bršlenka</i> is only known from a mention by Bogoslav Šulek.</p><p>Furthermore, similar terms denoting the ivy can be found in various Slavic languages:</p><p>Standard Bulgarian <i>бръшля́н</i> (brǎšlján) (also <i>бръшел</i> (brǎšel), <i>брешлян</i> (brešljan), <i>брешнял</i> (brešnjal), <i>брошлен</i> (brošlen), <i>брушлен</i> (brušlen), <i>бръчнел</i> (brǎčnel), <i>крушнел</i> (krušnel)), Standard Macedonian <i>бршлен</i> (bršlen) (also <i>бршлан</i> (bršlan)), Slovene <i>bršljȃn</i> and Serbo-Croatian <i>br̀šljan</i>, Czech <i>břečťan</i> and Slovak <i>brečtan</i> (see there for other forms), Old Polish <i>brzeszczan</i> (also <i>brzecztan</i>, <i>brzesztan</i>, <i>brzestan</i>, <i>brzostan</i>), Ukrainian <i>бречитан</i> (brečytan) (also <i>беречан</i> (berečan), <i>борощаник</i> (boroščanyk), <i>прочитан</i> (pročytan)). The term has been borrowed early into Hungarian <i>borostyán</i> (“ivy”) and <i>boroszlán</i> (“daphne”) (also <i>boraszlán</i>, <i>boroslán</i>, <i>boroslány</i>, <i>buruslán</i>, <i>boroszlyány</i>, <i>borosnyán</i>, <i>borosnyány</i>, <i>burusnyán</i>), Romanian <i>boroșlean</i> and Macedo-Greek <i>μπρούσλιανη</i> (broúsliani), <i>μπρούσλου</i> (broúslou).</p><p>Skok proposes this complex is an Illyrian borrowing, since a derivation from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъstь</i> (“grazed crop”) is doubtful; Sköld dismisses a Slavic origin, due to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľuščь</i> (“ivy”), which is unrelated but akin in meaning. The <i>Etymological Dictionary of Slavic Languages</i> unifies the terms for ivy and spindle-tree under one form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elm (tree or wood) (<i>Ulmus minor</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berstъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birchbark (bark of the birch tree, especially the paper birch)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бересто</i> (beresto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bersto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berestology</li></ol><p>From <i>береста́</i> (berestá, “birchbark”) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birchbark manuscript, birchbark letter (a document written on pieces of the inner layer of birch bark)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) birchbark</li></ol><p><i>береста́</i> (berestá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>béret</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to take care of, to keep safe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be careful, to guard oneself, to protect oneself</li><li>to beware, to take care, to be on one's guard</li></ol><p><i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beriberi, endemic neuritis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beryl</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>bēryllus</i>, <i>bēryllus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βήρυλλος</i> (bḗrullos, “beryl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beryllium</li></ol><p><i>бери́ллий</i> (beríllij) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beryllium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>beryllium</i>, from <i>bēryll(us)</i> (“beryl”) +&lrm; <i>-ium</i> (“chemical element suffix”). Equivalent to <i>бери́лл</i> (beríll) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beryllium</li></ol><p><i>бери́ллий</i> (beríllij) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Berytian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berkelium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) berkelium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berkelium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) berkovets (an old Russian unit of weight, 10 poods, or approximately 164 kilograms)</li></ol><p>From <i>бе́рковеск</i> (bérkovesk), Old East Slavic <i>бьрковьскъ</i> (bĭrkovĭskŭ, “of Birka”), borrowed from Old Norse <i>Birka</i>, a port city in mediaeval Sweden.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golden eagle</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;bürküt</i> (“Burkut; eagle”). Exact source unknown, possibly Chagatai (börküt) or Kazakh <i>бүркіт</i> (bürkıt), or Tatar <i>бөркет</i> (börket). Compare also Bashkir <i>бөркөт</i> (börköt), Kyrgyz <i>бүркүт</i> (bürküt), Uzbek <i>burgut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) berlin (a four-wheeled carriage with a separate sheltered seat behind the body)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Берлин)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berlin (the capital and largest city of Germany)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of German <i>Berlin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berliner</li></ol><p><i>Берли́н</i> (Berlín) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Berlin</li></ol><p><i>Берли́н</i> (Berlín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lair, burrow, den, cave (for bears)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;humorous) crib, pad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>берлога</i> (berloga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrloga</i>, from <i>&#42;bьrljati</i> / <i>&#42;bьrliti</i> + <i>&#42;-oga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete form of <i>бревно́</i> (brevnó)</li></ol><p>Simplified from <i>бервно́</i> (bervnó), from <i>беревно́</i> (berevnó), <i>бервено́</i> (bervenó), from Old East Slavic <i>бьрьвьно</i> (bĭrĭvĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrvьno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earplugs</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>береги</i> (beregi) +&lrm; <i>уши</i> (uši). Literally, “keep your ears safe”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch <i>(tree or wood)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>береза</i> (bereza), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berza</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;berźas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerHǵós</i>, whence English <i>birch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>берёза</i> (berjóza): (small) birch(-tree); young birch(-tree)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>берёза</i> (berjóza), used for example of a tall, slender, pale-skinned Russian woman</li><li>field bindweed (<i>Convolvulus arvensis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>берёза</i> (berjóza) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) birch; birchen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>березовꙑи</i> (berezovyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berzovъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>берёз-</i> (berjóz-, “birch”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj). Cognate with Belarusian <i>бяро́завы</i> (bjarózavy), Ukrainian <i>бере́зовий</i> (berézovyj), Polish <i>brzozowy</i>, Czech <i>březový</i>, Slovak <i>brezový</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any endromid moth species, especially a caterpillar of such species.</li></ol><p><i>берёзовый</i> (berjózovyj, “birch”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>шелкопря́д</i> (šelkoprjád, “silkworm”), literally “birch silkworm”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demon, devil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běsъ</i> (“evil spirit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>без-</i> (bez-) (<i>before voiceless consonants</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversation, talk</li><li>conference, discussion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;besěda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arbor, gazebo, pavilion (a shady place for sitting)</li></ol><p>From <i>бесе́да</i> (beséda, “conversation”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), meaning "a place to have a conversation".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) talkative, loquacious, garrulous</li></ol><p><i>бесе́да</i> (beséda) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to converse, to talk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;besědovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бесе́да</i> (beséda) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gazebo, arbour, pavilion</li></ol><p><i>бесе́дка</i> (besédka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cradle</li><li>little demon, imp</li><li>the national flag of Russia: "white-blue-red", "WBR".</li></ol><p>From Proto-Common Turkic <i>&#42;bēšik</i>, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;bẹĺčig</i>. See also Turkish <i>beşik</i>.</p><p>From бес (bes, “demon, devil”).</p><p>Acronym of <i>бе́ло-си́не-кра́сный</i> (bélo-síne-krásnyj), referring to the white-blue-red colours/colors of the Russian flag. This acronym was used before the establishment of the Soviet Union, during Tsarist times.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běsiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бес</i> (bes) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become rabid</li><li>to rage, to be furious, to get/fly into a rage, to fly off the handle, to blow one's top</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to horse around, to make whoopee, to roughhouse</li></ol><p><i>беси́ть</i> (besítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>classless</li></ol><p>First attested in 1907. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), calque of German <i>klassenlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;navy) a flat visorless sailor's cap; beskozyrka</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>козыр(ёк)</i> (kozyr(jók)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncompromising</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>компроми́сс</i> (kompromíss) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>endlessly, infinitely, boundlessly</li><li>eternally, indefinitely</li></ol><p><i>бесконе́чный</i> (beskonéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infinity, endlessness</li><li>eternity</li></ol><p><i>бесконе́чный</i> (beskonéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endless, infinite, unlimited, boundless</li><li>eternal</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>коне́чный</i> (konéčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conflictless</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>конфли́кт</i> (konflíkt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disinterestedness, unselfishness</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>коры́сть</i> (korýstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unselfishly</li><li>in a disinterested manner</li></ol><p><i>бескоры́стный</i> (beskorýstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disinterested, unselfish</li></ol><p><i>бескоры́стие</i> (beskorýstije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caffeine-free</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>бескофеи́н</i> (beskofeín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) boundless, endless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodless</li><li>anemic</li><li>very pale, pallid</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) homeless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) possessed by demons</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) furious, frantic, frenzied, raging</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possessed person, person possessed by demons</li></ol><p>16th-century construction from obsolete <i>бе́сный</i> (bésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj), from Old East Slavic <i>бѣсьныи</i> (běsĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běsьnъ</i>; see Russian <i>бес</i> (bes). Ukrainian <i>біснуватий</i> (bisnuvatyj) is a borrowing from Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, to rave, to be possessed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бѣсьновати сѧ</i> (běsĭnovati sę). By surface analysis, (<i>obsolete</i>) <i>бе́сный</i> (bésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); see <i>беснова́тый</i> (besnovátyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) demon, devil; demon's, devil's</li><li>demonic, devilish</li></ol><p><i>бес</i> (bes) +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) inconspicuous, hard to bait out</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>па́левный</i> (pálevnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unconsciousness, swoon</li><li>forgetfulness</li><li>frenzy</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-party, belonging to no party</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>па́ртия</i> (pártija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of German <i>parteilos</i>. First attested in 1901.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passportless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>па́спорт</i> (pásport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninterrupted</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>перебо́й</i> (perebój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hopelessness, futility</li></ol><p><i>бесперспекти́вный</i> (besperspektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having no prospects, unpromising, hopeless, futile, gloomy, dark</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>перспекти́ва</i> (perspektíva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) same as <i>бесперспекти́вность</i> (besperspektívnostʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly (in a careless manner)</li></ol><p><i>беспе́чный</i> (bespéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness, unconcern</li></ol><p><i>беспе́чный</i> (bespéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, carefree, unconcerned</li><li>nonchalant</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>печ(а́ль)</i> (peč(álʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unmanned aerial vehicle</li><li>unmanned ground vehicle (self-driving car etc.)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пило́т</i> (pilót) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-driving</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пило́т</i> (pilót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-driving car</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV), drone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gratis, free of charge</li></ol><p><i>беспла́тный</i> (besplátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free, gratuitous, rent-free</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пла́та</i> (pláta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infertility</li><li>barrenness</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>плод</i> (plod) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain, vainly, fruitlessly, unsuccessfully</li></ol><p><i>беспло́дный</i> (besplódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infertile, fruitless, barren, sterile</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-, “-less”) +&lrm; <i>плод</i> (plod, “fruit; fetus”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, “-ish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorporeal</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>definitively, irrevocably, first last and all the time, irreversibly</li></ol><p><i>бесповоро́тный</i> (bespovorótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irrevocable, final</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>поворо́т</i> (povorót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peerless, incomparable, matchless</li></ol><p>First attested in 1789. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj), possibly, a calque of French <i>incomparable</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invertebrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invertebrate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) spineless, gutless</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>позвоно́к</i> (pozvonók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of German <i>wirbellos</i>. First attested in 1836.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bother, to disturb</li></ol><p>From <i>без</i> (bez, “without”) + <i>поко́й</i> (pokój, “rest”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pokojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry (about), to be anxious (about), to be uneasy (about)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother with, to care about</li></ol><p><i>беспоко́ить</i> (bespokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anxiously</li><li>fitfully</li><li>unevenly</li></ol><p><i>беспоко́йный</i> (bespokójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneasy, restive, anxious</li><li>disturbing</li><li>restless, fidgety</li></ol><p><i>беспоко́ить</i> (bespokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anxiety, concern, uneasiness, nervousness</li><li>trouble, bother, annoyance</li></ol><p><i>беспоко́йный</i> (bespokójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uselessly</li></ol><p><i>бесполе́зный</i> (bespoléznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uselessness</li></ol><p><i>бесполе́зный</i> (bespoléznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless, unavailing, vain</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>по́льза</i> (pólʹza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); see <i>поле́зный</i> (poléznyj) for the phonological development.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asexual (without sexual action&#59; having no distinct sex)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1839. <i>бес-</i> (bes-, “-less”) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol, “sex”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj), calque of French <i>asexuel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>blankly</li></ol><p><i>беспо́мощный</i> (bespómoščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helplessness</li><li>feebleness</li></ol><p>From <i>беспо́мощный</i> (bespómoščnyj, “helpless”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>helpless (unable to defend oneself or to act without help)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>по́мощь</i> (pómoščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad, low-quality, cheapo, tasteless, uninteresting, soggy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;religion) a member of an Old Believer fellowship without a priesthood. See <i>Bezpopovtsy</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>поп</i> (pop) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorder</li><li>confusion</li><li>disarray</li><li>untidiness</li><li>mess</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>поря́док</i> (porjádok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in confusion, in disorder, higgledy-piggledy</li></ol><p><i>беспоря́дочный</i> (besporjádočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disorderly, confused, irregular, random</li></ol><p><i>беспоря́док</i> (besporjádok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a flight</i>) direct, nonstop, without a stopover</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>поса́дка</i> (posádka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baseless, groundless (not grounded in facts, not based on anything true)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1874. From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>по́чва</i> (póčva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj), a calque of German <i>grundlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>duty-free (exempt from duty, especially customs)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>по́шлина</i> (póšlina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mercilessly</li></ol><p><i>беспоща́дный</i> (bespoščádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruthlessness, mercilessness</li></ol><p><i>беспоща́дный</i> (bespoščádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>поща́да</i> (poščáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence of rights; lawlessness</li></ol><p>First attested in 1845. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of German <i>Rechtlosigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deprived of civil rights</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>rechtlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lawlessness, outrage, arbitrary rule</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>преде́л</i> (predél)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boundless, unlimited, infinite</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>преде́л</i> (predél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unconditionally</li></ol><p><i>беспрекосло́вный</i> (besprekoslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconditional</li><li>obeying unconditionally (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>прекосло́вие</i> (prekoslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) by all means, surely, necessarily</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) + the adverbial form of переме́нный (pereménnyj, “transient, variable”). See also <i>переме́на</i> (pereména, “change”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without difficulty, without hindrance, without obstruction, freely</li></ol><p><i>беспрепя́тственный</i> (besprepjátstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhindered, without obstacles</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>препя́тствие</i> (prepjátstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>continuously, uninterruptedly, without a break</li></ol><p><i>беспреры́вный</i> (besprerývnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous, uninterrupted</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>прерыва́ть</i> (preryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incessantly (in a manner without pause or stop)</li></ol><p><i>беспреста́нный</i> (besprestánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incessant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>беспрѣстаньнъ</i> (besprěstanĭnŭ, “ceaseless, incessant”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>беспрѣстани</i> (besprěstani, “ceaselessly, incessantly”). By surface analysis, <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>престан-</i> (prestan-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), based on a combining stem of <i>преста́ть</i> (prestátʹ, “to cease, to quit”); compare 1sg. present <i>преста́ну</i> (prestánu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprecedented, unparalleled</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>прецеде́нт</i> (precedént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waif, stray, street urchin, gamin (homeless child)</li></ol><p><i>беспризорный</i> (besprizornyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homeless</li><li>neglected</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waif, street urchin (homeless child)</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>призор-</i> (prizor-, “to watch over, to supervise”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unscrupulousness, unprincipledness</li></ol><p><i>беспринци́пный</i> (besprincípnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprincipled, unscrupulous</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>при́нцип</i> (príncip) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of German <i>prinzipienlos</i>. First attested in 1870.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>беспристра́стный</i> (bespristrástnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>беспристра́стно</i> (bespristrástno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>беспристра́стный</i> (bespristrástnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>беспристра́стно</i> (bespristrástno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impartially (in an impartial manner)</li></ol><p><i>беспристра́стный</i> (bespristrástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impartiality</li></ol><p><i>беспристра́стный</i> (bespristrástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dispassionate, impartial, unbiased, unprejudiced</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>пристра́стие</i> (pristrástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreasonable, groundless, without cause</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>причи́на</i> (pričína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cordless, wireless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>провод</i> (provod) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitch-black, pitch dark, black</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) cheerless, gloomy, hopeless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>просве́т</i> (prosvét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless, wanton, wayward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Bessarabian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incoherent (<i>of speech, thoughts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>связь</i> (svjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heartless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>less common</i>) alternative form of <i>бессре́бреник</i> (bessrébrenik)</li></ol><p>Same as <i>бессре́бреник</i> (bessrébrenik) but using the inherited word for silver, <i>серебро́</i> (serebró).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) alternative form of <i>бессре́бреник</i> (bessrébrenik)</li></ol><p>Same as <i>бессре́бреник</i> (bessrébrenik) but using the inherited word for silver, <i>серебро́</i> (serebró).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impotence, asthenia, impuissance (loss of strength)</li><li>powerlessness</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>си́ла</i> (síla) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>powerlessly</li><li>feebly, impotently</li></ol><p><i>бесси́льный</i> (bessílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble, powerless; impotent</li></ol><p><i>бесси́лие</i> (bessílije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsystematic</li></ol><p>First attested in 1841. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>систе́ма</i> (sistéma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>systemlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without a trace, tracelessly, completely, utterly (<i>of a disappearance</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бессле́дный</i> (besslédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>traceless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>след</i> (sled) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mute (unable to speak)</li><li>silent</li><li>wordless</li><li>meek, humble, lowly (<i>especially in being unwilling to express one's opinion</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>словеса́</i> (slovesá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Synchronically, based on <i>словеса́</i> (slovesá, “words, verbiage”). Diachronically, <i>словес-</i> (sloves-) is the original stem of <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”), which was originally an <i>s</i>-stem. Compare <i>слове́сный</i> (slovésnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permanent, not temporary (<i>of a worker</i>)</li><li>usual, typical</li><li>continuous</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>сме́на</i> (sména) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immortality</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>смерть</i> (smertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>бессме́ртие</i> (bessmértije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>безсме́ртний</i> (bezsmértnyj), Belarusian <i>бессмяро́тны</i> (bjessmjarótny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>senselessly; foolishly</li></ol><p><i>бессмы́сленный</i> (bessmýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senselessness, inanity, pointlessness</li></ol><p><i>бессмы́сленный</i> (bessmýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meaningless; pointless; nonsensical</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>смысл</i> (smysl) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsense</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>смысл</i> (smysl) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscienceless, lacking a conscience, immoral, inconsiderate</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>со́весть</i> (sóvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrenness, emptiness, insignificance, inanity</li></ol><p><i>бессодержа́тельный</i> (bessoderžátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty; vapid</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>содержа́ть</i> (soderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj), a calque of German <i>inhaltslos</i>. First attested in 1838.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unconsciously, unknowingly</li><li>instinctively, involuntarily</li></ol><p><i>бессозна́тельный</i> (bessoznátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) unconscious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconscious</li><li>involuntary, instinctive</li></ol><p>First attested in 1832. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>созна́ние</i> (soznánije) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj), probably, a calque of German <i>bewusstlos</i> or <i>besinnungslos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insomnia, sleeplessness</li></ol><p><i>бессо́нный</i> (bessónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleepless, wakeful</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>сон</i> (son) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asyndeton</li></ol><p>First attested in 1771. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀσύνδετον</i> (asúndeton), whence also <i>аси́ндетон</i> (asíndɛton).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unquestionably (without doubt)</li></ol><p><i>бесспо́рный</i> (besspórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indisputable, unquestionable</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>спор</i> (spor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) Holy Unmercenary</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) generous person, altruist</li></ol><p>Calque of Byzantine Greek <i>Ἀνάργυρος</i> (Anárguros), from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάργυρος</i> (anárguros, “without money”), from <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “un-”) + <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver, money”). By surface analysis, <i>бес-</i> (bes-, “without”) +&lrm; (<i>poetic</i>) <i>сребро́</i> (srebró, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>less common</i>) alternative form of <i>бессре́бреник</i> (bessrébrenik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perpetual, termless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stemless, acaulescent</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>сте́бель</i> (stébelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of New Latin <i>acaulis</i>. First attested in 1783.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impassively</li></ol><p><i>бесстра́стный</i> (besstrástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impassive, passionless, unimpassioned</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>страсть</i> (strastʹ, “passion; (<i>originally</i>) suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fearlessness (quality of being fearless)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>страх</i> (strax) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fearlessly</li></ol><p><i>бесстра́шный</i> (besstrášnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearless (free from fear)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bezstrašьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бесстра́шие</i> (besstrášije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) twaddler, babbler</li></ol><p>Literally, “stringless balalaika”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stringless</li></ol><p><i>бес</i> (bes) +&lrm; <i>стру́нный</i> (strúnnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stepless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>ступе́н(ь)</i> (stupén(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shameless person</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brazenly, shamelessly, immodestly</li></ol><p><i>бессты́дный</i> (besstýdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brazen, shameless, immodest</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shamelessness, brazenness, impudence, effrontery</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless, brazen</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innumerable, countless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>счёт</i> (sčot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indelicacy, tactlessness; social blunder, faux pas</li><li>indecorum</li></ol><p><i>беста́ктный</i> (bestáktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tactless, indelicate, indiscreet</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>telephoneless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>телефо́н</i> (telefón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rogue, mischievous person, scoundrel</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>bēstia</i> (“beast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stupidly</li><li>incoherently, confusedly</li><li>pointlessly, uselessly</li></ol><p><i>бестолко́вый</i> (bestolkóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>muddle-headed, stupid</li><li>confused, incoherent</li><li>pointless, useless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>толк</i> (tolk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blockhead, dumbass, muddlehead</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) confusion, mess, muddle</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>толк</i> (tolk) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ). The expansion with о is a result of the second pleophony (<i>второе полногласие</i>), compare <i>остолоп</i> from <i>столп</i> or <i>сумеречный</i> from <i>сумерки</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bestseller</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bestseller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bestseller</li></ol><p><i>бестсе́ллер</i> (bestséller) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wickless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>фити́ль</i> (fitílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formless, shapeless</li></ol><p>First attested in 1866. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj), a calque of French <i>amorphe</i> or Latin <i>amorphus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinelessness</li></ol><p><i>бесхара́ктерный</i> (besxarákternyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ) or a calque of German <i>Charakterlosigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak-willed, spineless</li></ol><p>First attested in 1829. <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хара́ктер</i> (xarákter) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>charakterlos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tailless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsophisticated, artless, ingenuous, guileless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хи́трость</i> (xítrostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ownerless, unowned</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хоз(я́ин)</i> (xoz(jáin)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mismanagement, bad management, thriftlessness</li><li>negligence</li></ol><p><i>бесхозя́йственный</i> (besxozjájstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unable to manage the economy; mismanaged</li><li>causing damage to the economy</li></ol><p><i>бесхозя́йствие</i> (besxozjájstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) mismanagement of the economy</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́йство</i> (xozjájstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spineless (also figuratively)</li></ol><p>From <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>хребе́т</i> (xrebét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowerless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colourless/colorless (having no colour&#47;color)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aimlessly</li></ol><p><i>бесце́льный</i> (bescélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aimlessness</li><li>idleness, pointlessness, futility</li></ol><p><i>бесце́льный</i> (bescélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aimless, idle, pointless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>priceless, invaluable</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>за бесце́нок</i> (za bescénok, “for peanuts (very cheap, of a price)”)</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abruptly, brusquely (in an abrupt manner)</li></ol><p><i>бесцеремо́нный</i> (besceremónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unceremoniousness, sans gêne, presumptuousness</li></ol><p><i>бесцеремо́нный</i> (besceremónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unceremonious, brusque, abrupt</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>церемо́ния</i> (ceremónija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhumane, cruel, brutal</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>челове́чный</i> (čelovéčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishonor, disgrace</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, to dishonour/dishonor, to discredit</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dishonorable, dishonest, nefarious</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excess, outrage</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>чин</i> (čin) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>countless, innumerable</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>число́</i> (čisló) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insensible, insensitive</li><li>unfeeling, callous, hard-hearted, heartless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absence of desire to work</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) indifference</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) lack of compassion, heartlessness</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) cruelty, ruthlessness</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reckless, devil-may-care, daredevil</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>ша́баш</i> (šábaš) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>noiselessly (in a quiet manner)</li></ol><p><i>бесшу́мный</i> (besšúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noiseless, soundless</li></ol><p><i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>шум</i> (šum) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imp</li></ol><p><i>бес</i> (bes) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бесе́да</i> (beséda).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beta, the Greek letter, Β, β</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) beta (preliminary version)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) betatron</li></ol><p>Internationalism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) betatronic</li></ol><p>From <i>бетатро́н</i> (betatrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baetyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) bethylid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Bethylidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concrete</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>béton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concrete (to cover with concrete)</li></ol><p>From <i>бето́н</i> (betón) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ). Possibly directly borrowed from German <i>betonieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) concrete (made of concrete)</li></ol><p><i>бето́н</i> (betón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concrete mixer</li></ol><p>First attested in 1917. <i>бето́н</i> (betón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>меша́лка</i> (mešálka), calque of German <i>Betonmischer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>baseball</i>) batter (the player who hits the ball with a bat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>batter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betulin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) betulin</li></ol><p><i>бетули́н</i> (betulín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beef Stroganoff, beef Stroganov</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bœuf Stroganov</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twine, string, pack thread</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beshbarmak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beshbarmak</li></ol><p><i>бешбарма́к</i> (bešbarmák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>madly</li><li>frantically, amok</li></ol><p><i>бе́шеный</i> (béšenyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hydrophobia, rabies</li><li>madness, fury, rage</li></ol><p><i>бе́шеный</i> (béšenyj, “rabid, mad; furious, frantic, wild”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rabid, mad</li><li>furious, frantic, wild</li></ol><p>Adjectivised passive past participle of <i>беси́ть</i> (besítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beshmet (a tightly fitting knee-length outer garment with a short stand-up collar, traditionally worn by Turkic and Caucasian people and adopted by Cossacks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>бишмәт</i> (bişmät).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to fart (silently, as opposed to <i>перде́ть</i> (perdétʹ))</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) to be scared <i>or</i> afraid of something, to panic</li><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;slang,&#32;mostly reflexive) to ventilate, to refresh, to take a walk, to go outside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьzděti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;vernacular,&#32;figuratively) to get scared, to panic</li></ol><p><i>бздеть</i> (bzdetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) farter</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) a person who is scared or who panics</li></ol><p>From <i>бздеть</i> (bzdetʹ, “to fart”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quirk, quirkiness, peculiarity (<i>usually of a personality</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) obsession, mania (an illogical, inexplicable interest or wish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>bzik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> B, b</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a bi person (someone who is bisexual)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>B</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>бисексуа́л</i> (bisɛksuál) or <i>бисексуа́лка</i> (bisɛksuálka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) biathlete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) biathlon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biathlete</li></ol><p><i>биатло́н</i> (biatlón) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) penis, doodle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) car</li></ol><p>Variant of Russian <i>биби́ка</i> (bibíka). Akin to Russian <i>биби́кать</i> (bibíkatʹ),</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honk a car's horn</li></ol><p>First attested in 1969–1970. From onomatopoeic <i>би-би</i> (bi-bi) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ) or <i>биби́ка</i> (bibíka) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honk a car's horn</li></ol><p><i>биби́кать</i> (bibíkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biblical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library (<i>in all senses</i>)</li><li>bookshelf</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>βῐβλῐοθήκη</i> (bĭblĭothḗkē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>librarian</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>библиоте́ка</i> (bibliotéka) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ), ultimately from Latin <i>bibliothēcārius</i>. Since the time of Peter the Great. The exact source of borrowing is disputed, either German <i>Bibliothekar</i> (per Shansky) or Polish <i>bibliotekarz</i> (per Vasmer and Anikin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) library</li></ol><p><i>библиоте́ка</i> (bibliotéka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliophile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bible</li><li>bible</li></ol><p>First attested 1499. Borrowed from Latin <i>biblia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βιβλία</i> (biblía, “books”), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>βίβλος</i> (bíblos, “book”), a variant of Ancient Greek <i>βύβλος</i> (búblos, “papyrus”). The word is derived from the name of the Phoenician city Byblos, which was known for exporting papyri in ancient times.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Библия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bible</li></ol><p>First attested 1499. Borrowed from Latin <i>biblia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βιβλία</i> (biblía, “books”), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>βίβλος</i> (bíblos, “book”), a variant of Ancient Greek <i>βύβλος</i> (búblos, “papyrus”). The word is derived from the name of the Phoenician city Byblos, which was known for exporting papyri in ancient times.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biwa (lute)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>琵琶</i> (びわ, biwa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) bivouac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bivouac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ива́ть</i> (-ivátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tusk</li></ol><p><i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>бивник</i> (bivnik), Macedonian <i>бивна</i> (bivna).</p><p>Compare typologically Polish <i>cios</i> (<&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tesъ</i> <&lt; <i>&#42;tesati</i>). Also compare <i>клык</i> (klyk) (akin to <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bivouac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bivouac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bivouac</li></ol><p><i>бивуа́к</i> (bivuák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>биг-мэ́к</i> (big-mɛ́k)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Big Mac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Big Mac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bigamy (the state of having two spouses simultaneously)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bigos</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair roller, hair curler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bigoudi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cryptozoology</i>) Bigfoot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Bigfoot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bidet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bidet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>can, churn (a large metal or plastic cylindrical container with a secure lid or cover, intended to store and transport liquids, typically milk or liquid fuel)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) boob, breast, tit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bidon</i> in the late 19th century (attested since 1880), from Middle French <i>bidon</i>, further origin uncertain.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бидон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) Joe Biden</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Ба́йден</i> (Bájden, “Joe Biden”) +&lrm; <i>бидо́н</i> (bidón, “a can”). Compare Ukrainian <i>Бідо́н</i> (Bidón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) bijective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) bijection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating, beat, throb, pulse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>биѥниѥ</i> (bijenije). By surface analysis, <i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije). Compare native <i>битьё</i> (bitʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heartbeat <i>(rhythmic pulsation of the heart)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biennale (a biennial celebration or exhibition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>biennale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>costume jewelry (jewelry made from inexpensive materials)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) paraphernalia (<i>e.g. sports</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) imitation, fake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bijouterie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spanker (a fore-and-aft gaff-rigged sail on the aftmost mast)</li><li>ellipsis of <i>биза́нь-ма́чта</i> (bizánʹ-máčta, “mizzenmast”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>bezaan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business (commercial activity&#59; enterprise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>business</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) business association</li></ol><p><i>би́знес</i> (bíznes, “business”) +&lrm; <i>ассоциа́ция</i> (associácija, “association”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business class</li></ol><p><i>би́знес</i> (bíznɛs) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business plan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>business plan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business process</li></ol><p><i>би́знес</i> (bíznɛs) +&lrm; <i>проце́сс</i> (procéss)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business community</li></ol><p><i>би́знес</i> (bíznɛs) +&lrm; <i>соо́бщество</i> (soóbščestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>businessman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>businessman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>businesswoman</li></ol><p>From <i>бизнесме́н</i> (biznesmén) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bison, American buffalo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bison</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) American bison, American buffalo</li></ol><p><i>бизо́н</i> (bizón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Biysk (a city in Altai Krai, Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Biya (a river in the Altai Republic and Altai Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bicameralism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bicameralism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bikini</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Bixa</i> (a plant of the Bixaceae or achiote family)</li><li>biksa (a kind of billiards on a small, inclined table in which the ball rolls back after being hit)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) girl, chick, slut</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>Bixa</i>, from American Spanish <i>bija</i>, <i>bixa</i> (“achiote”), from Taíno <i>bixa</i>.</p><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Bilateria.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billboard (large advertisement along side of highway)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>billboard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ticket (admission to entertainment or transportation)</li><li>banknote</li><li>membership card</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>billet</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>bulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>биле́т</i> (bilét, “ticket”)</li></ol><p><i>биле́т</i> (bilét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>billion (<i>short scale</i>), thousand million, milliard (1,000,000,000, 10<sup>9</sup>)</li><li>billion (<i>long scale</i>), million million (1,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>12</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impact bar, beater</li></ol><p><i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billiards, pool</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>billard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billiard room, room for playing billiards, pool hall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) billiards</li></ol><p><i>билья́рд</i> (bilʹjárd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>binary, binomial</li></ol><p>Late Latin <i>bīnārius</i> + -ный (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bingo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bingo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binoculars</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>binocle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binomial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Binde</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bandage</li></ol><p><i>бинт</i> (bint) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>bio- (pertaining to life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biogeography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biographical (relating to an account of a person's life)</li></ol><p>From <i>биогра́фия</i> (biográfija, “biography”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biodestruction</li></ol><p><i>био-</i> (bio-) +&lrm; <i>дестру́кция</i> (destrúkcija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bioinformatics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biologist (male or female)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>биология</i> (biologija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>biologically</li></ol><p><i>биологи́ческий</i> (biologíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biological</li></ol><p>From <i>биоло́гия</i> (biológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biology</li></ol><p>Adaptation of French <i>biologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biomarker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bioorganic chemist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) biopolitics</li></ol><p><i>био-</i> (bio-, “bio-”) +&lrm; <i>поли́тика</i> (polítika, “politics”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) biodegradable</li></ol><p><i>био-</i> (bio-) +&lrm; <i>разлага́емый</i> (razlagájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ecology</i>) biodiversity</li></ol><p><i>био-</i> (bio-) +&lrm; <i>разнообра́зие</i> (raznoobrázije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bioscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ecology</i>) biosphere</li></ol><p><i>био-</i> (bio-, “bio-”) +&lrm; <i>сфе́ра</i> (sféra, “sphere”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) biotherapy (<i>all senses</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>био-</i> (bio-, “bio-”) +&lrm; <i>терапи́я</i> (terapíja, “therapy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biotechnology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) biotoxicity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biotope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biophysicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biophysics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biochemical</li></ol><p><i>биохи́мия</i> (bioxímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biochemistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(clinical psychology,&#32;psychiatry) bipolar disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bipolar (involving or having both extremes or poles at the same time&#59; relating to both polar regions)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bipolar (relating to or having bipolar disorder)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to take (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;birati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) exchange, market</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>beurs</i>, possibly via German <i>Börse</i>. First attested in 1717, with an early masculine variant of биржъ in 1705. Further from Latin <i>bursa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βύρσα</i> (búrsa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stock exchange operators</li></ol><p>From <i>биржево́й</i> (birževój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stock market</li></ol><p><i>би́ржа</i> (bírža) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nameplate, label, tag</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) tally stick (notched piece of wood used for keeping an account)</li></ol><p>Unclear. There are several hypotheses: a borrowing from a North Germanic word for birch, compare to Danish <i>birk</i>, Norwegian <i>bjerk</i>; a Turkic borrowing, compare to Turkish <i>bir &#43; iki</i> (“one + two”); or a derivation from Old East Slavic <i>събьрати</i> (sŭbĭrati, “to assemble”) (modern Russian <i>собира́ть</i> (sobirátʹ)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrati</i> (“to take”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burmese (a person from Myanmar or of Burmese descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Burmese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turquoise (stone)</li><li>turquoise (colour&#47;color)</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic language from Persian پیروزه (piruze). See فیروزه (firuze) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) turquoise (<i>stone or color</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бирюза́</i> (birjuzá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lone wolf, loner, unsociable person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;bȫrü</i>. Compare Kazakh <i>бөрі</i> (börı), Tatar <i>бүре</i> (büre).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>encore</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>bis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bisexual (person)</li></ol><p>From the Neo-Classical compound bisexual.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bisexuality</li></ol><p>From <i>бисексуа́льный</i> (bisɛksuálʹnyj, “bisexual”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bisexual (sexually attracted to persons of either sex)</li></ol><p><i>бисексуа́л</i> (bisɛksuál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beads</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бисьръ</i> (bisĭrŭ, “pearls”), from Bulgar, from Arabic بُسْر (busr, “unripe dates”) from the root ب س ر (b s r) related to things being early, so applied to pearls because unripe dates can have some tactile and optical properties similar to pearls.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>би́сер</i> (bíser, “beads”); a bead, little bead.</li></ol><p>From <i>би́сер</i> (bíser, “beads”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sponge cake</li><li>biscuit (form of earthenware)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>biscuit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Catholic bishop</li></ol><p>Attested 14th century. Compare Czech <i>biskup</i>, Polish <i>biskup</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>би̏скуп</i>, Ukrainian <i>бі́скуп</i> (bískup). According to Vasmer, borrowed from Old High German <i>biscof</i> or Middle High German <i>bischof</i>, from Latin <i>episcopus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπίσκοπος</i> (epískopos); Vasmer insists the initial /bi/ is of Old High German origin. Doublet of <i>епи́скоп</i> (jepískop).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle bisector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bistro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bistro</i>, according to folk etymology version borrowed from Russian <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro) "quickly" in 1814, when Russian Army was in Paris.</p><p>In actual fact, the word is pure French, first attested only in 1884.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;computing) bit (binary digit)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) beat (time unit of a piece)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) beat music</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) beat (short pause)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bit</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>beat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bat (club)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle, fight, combat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>би́тва</i> (bítva), from Old Polish <i>bitwa</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bitva</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitcoin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bitcoin</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>биткоин</i> (bitkoin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>chockfull</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) The Beatles</li></ol><p>From English <i>The Beatles</i> +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) The Beatles</li></ol><p>From English <i>The Beatles</i> +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) Beatlemania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Beatlemania</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beatnik (beatnik)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>beatnik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics,&#32;computing) bit (binary digit)</li></ol><p>From <i>бит</i> (bit, “bit”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>bit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics,&#32;computing) bit (binary digit)</li></ol><p>From <i>бит</i> (bit, “bit”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), from English <i>bit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cue ball</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) round rissole, meatball</li><li>(<i>cryptocurrencies&#32;slang</i>) Bitcoin</li></ol><p>From <i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p><p>Clipping of <i>битко́йн</i> (bitkójn) + <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitumen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>bitūmen</i>, from Gaulish. Doublet of <i>бетон</i> (beton).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beaten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li><li>to chime (<i>of a clock</i>)</li><li>to churn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;biti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeyh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to loiter, to twiddle one's thumbs</li></ol><p>баклу́ша (baklúša), which is now only used idiomatically, originally meant a stick or plank of split wood that is used for making wooden dishes, spoons, toys, etc.</p><p>Vladimir Dal' claimed that chopping a short wooden block (bolt) in smaller pieces is allegedly an easy task, but modern scholars reject his etymology (even though it's still widespread).</p><p>According to Mokiyenko, dialectal and foreign Slavic material rather indicates that it was a metaphor for playing gorodki.</p><p>Alternatively, Moldavan suggested that the idiom may originally have meant playing music with wooden spoons and similar makeshift percussion instruments.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hammer away at something</li></ol><p>Literally, “to keep striking in one spot”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to struggle</li><li>to hit <i>(against)</i>, to knock <i>(against)</i>, to strike</li><li>(<i>of heart</i>) to beat, to pulsate</li><li>to writhe, to toss about</li><li>to exercise oneself, to drudge, to toil</li><li>to break, to smash</li></ol><p><i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating</li></ol><p><i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тьё</i> (-tʹjó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beetewk (a type of Russian heavy draft horse)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a very strong, heavily built man</li></ol><p>Named after Bityug river near Voronezh, ultimately from a Turkic source.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steak, beefsteak</li><li>hamburger, patty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>beefsteaks</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) biceps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip, scourge</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) scourge (a source of trouble, suffering, or destruction)</li><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) swipple</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bum, hobo (impoverished, homeless person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bičь</i>.</p><p>Probably from English <i>beachcomber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) instant noodle</li></ol><p><i>бич</i> (bič, “hobo, homeless person”) +&lrm; <i>паке́т</i> (pakét, “packet”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to lash, to scourge, to whip, to flagellate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to castigate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live on the streets, to be a bum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bičevati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бич</i> (bič, “whip”) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p><p><i>бич</i> (bič, “hobo, bum”) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) now</li></ol><p>Allegro form of <i>ба́ешь</i> (báješʹ, “you say”), from <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>де́скать</i> (déskatʹ), <i>де</i> (de), <i>мол</i> (mol).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) censored spelling of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) blaberid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Blaberidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good, blessing, benefit</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) good</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) since, now that</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the more so because</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>благо</i> (blago), Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благо</i> (blago), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolgo</i>. Displaced the inherited East Slavic form <i>бо́лого</i> (bólogo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>good-, well-</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благо-</i> (blago-), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-). Bound form of <i>благо́й</i> (blagój), by surface analysis, <i>благо́й</i> (blagój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Annunciation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благовѣщеньѥ</i> (blagověštenĭje). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Благовещение)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>благове́щение</i> (blagovéščenije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) comely</li><li>specious, plausible</li></ol><p><i>благо</i> (blago) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodwill, grace</li><li>condescension</li></ol><p><i>благоволи́ть</i> (blagovolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to favour/favor [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) to do someone a favour/favor by agreeing [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоволити</i> (blagovoliti). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) pleasant aroma, fragrance</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;dated) incense</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вонь</i> (vonʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aromatic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благовон҄ьнъ</i> (blagovonʹĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>благово́ние</i> (blagovónije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politeness</li></ol><p>From <i>благовоспи́танный</i> (blagovospítannyj, “well-mannered”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-mannered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) fitting times</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) well-timed (happening at an opportune or advantageous time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironically</i>) stupidity, foolishness, foolery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) reverentially, respectfully</li></ol><p><i>благогове́йный</i> (blagogovéjnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) reverent, reverential, respectful</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоговѣйный</i> (blagogovějnyj). By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>гове́й-</i> (govéj-, “to respect”, obsolete) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>благогові́йний</i> (blahohovíjnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awe (feeling of fear and reverence)</li></ol><p><i>благогове́ть</i> (blagogovétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revere, to venerate, to be in awe of [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental)]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоговѣти</i> (blagogověti). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гове́ть</i> (govétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thanksgiving (an expression of gratitude)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодар҄еньѥ</i> (blagodarʹjenĭje). By surface analysis, <i>благодари́ть</i> (blagodarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thank</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодарити</i> (blagodariti). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дари́ть</i> (darítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gratefully</li><li>in a worthwhile manner</li></ol><p><i>благода́рный</i> (blagodárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gratitude, gratefulness, thankfulness</li></ol><p><i>благода́рный</i> (blagodárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grateful, thankful (showing thanks)</li><li>worth doing, worthwhile</li></ol><p>From <i>благодари́ть</i> (blagodarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expressing gratefulness; thankful</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодарьствьнъ</i> (blagodarĭstvĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>благодари́ть</i> (blagodarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;low colloquial) to thank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) thank ye</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thanks! (<i>conversational</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thanks! (<i>conversational</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) thanks, thank ye</li></ol><p>Literally, “I humbly thank (you)”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>thanks to, owing to, through (by means of), due to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>thanks to the fact that</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so (has a literary sense), thus, thereby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) full of divine grace, godsent, God-given</li><li>blissful, blessed, happy</li><li>beneficial, godsent</li><li>fertile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодатьнъ</i> (blagodatĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>благода́ть</i> (blagodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divine grace, boon, bliss (a blessing or benefit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодать</i> (blagodatĭ). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) prosperous, happy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welfare, prosperity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодьньствие</i> (blagodĭnĭstvije). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper, to thrive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benefactor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодѣтел҄ь</i> (blagodětelʹĭ), itself a calque of εὐεργέτης. By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benefit, boon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благодѣꙗньѥ</i> (blagodějanĭje). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дея́ние</i> (dejánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benevolence, kindheartedness</li><li>good humor</li><li>idleness, inactivity, lack of proper response to a challenge</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐψυχία</i> (eupsukhía)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good-natured, kindhearted</li><li>in good spirits, pleased (<i>of a person or mood</i>)</li></ol><p><i>благоду́шие</i> (blagodúšije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>benevolent</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>benevolēns</i>, from <i>благо́й</i> (blagój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmony, euphony</li></ol><p>From <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of German <i>Wohllaut</i> or <i>Wohlklang</i>. First attested in 1783.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>благозву́чный</i> (blagozvúčnyj): better sounding, more melodious, more harmonious</li><li>comparative degree of <i>благозву́чно</i> (blagozvúčno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>благозву́чный</i> (blagozvúčnyj): better sounding, more melodious, more harmonious</li><li>comparative degree of <i>благозву́чно</i> (blagozvúčno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>euphoniously, mellifluously, in a pleasant-sounding manner</li></ol><p><i>благозву́чный</i> (blagozvúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>euphonious, euphonic, pleasant-sounding</li></ol><p>From <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of German <i>wohlklingend</i> or <i>wohllautend</i>. First attested in 1780.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(stilted,&#32;ecclesiastic&#32;or&#32;poetic) good</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благъ</i> (blagŭ). Displaced the inherited East Slavic form, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolgъ</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Majesty, splendor, divine beauty.</li></ol><p>Related to Old Church Slavonic благолѣпь (blagolěpĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) extraordinarily beautiful, majestically beautiful, magnificent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благолѣпьнъ</i> (blagolěpĭnŭ), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐπρεπής</i> (euprepḗs). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́пый</i> (lépyj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) reasonable</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) bien pensant, loyal, solid (citizen)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1784. <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слящий</i> (mýsljaščij), calque of German <i>wohlgesinnt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reliableness, trustworthiness</li></ol><p><i>благонадёжный</i> (blagonadjóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trustworthy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good-looking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благообразьнъ</i> (blagoobrazĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-being, prosperity, welfare (state of health, happiness and&#47;or prosperity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благополоучиѥ</i> (blagopolučije). By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>получи́ть</i> (polučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>safely, well, successfully, happily (in a secure manner&#59; without the possibility of injury or harm resulting)</li></ol><p><i>благополу́чный</i> (blagopolúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successful; happy, safe</li></ol><p><i>благополу́чие</i> (blagopolúčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholesome</li></ol><p><i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>потре́бный</i> (potrébnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decency, propriety, decorum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) acquired</li></ol><p>First attested in 1785. <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>приобре́тенный</i> (priobrétennyj), calque of German <i>wohlerworben</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decency, propriety, decorum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decent, modest, seemly</li></ol><p>From <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>присто́йный</i> (pristójnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>auspiciously (in an auspicious way)</li></ol><p><i>благоприя́тный</i> (blagoprijátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>favourable/favorable, auspicious, advantageous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоприѧтьнъ</i> (blagoprijętĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прия́тие</i> (prijátije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to favor, to be favorable towards [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>благоприя́т(ный)</i> (blagopriját(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence, discretion</li></ol><p><i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ра́зум</i> (rázum) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>judicious, wise, prudent</li></ol><p><i>благоразу́мие</i> (blagorazúmije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobleness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благородьѥ</i> (blagorodĭje). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nobly, generously, selflessly</li><li>exceptionally</li></ol><p><i>благоро́дный</i> (blagoródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble, aristocratic, wellborn (of the nobility, of the high-born class, especially in Russia before 1917)</li><li>noble (<i>in spirit</i>), gentle</li><li>selfless, generous</li><li>exceptional, outstandingly elegant</li><li>(sciences,&#32;figurative) noble (having some special quality)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noble, aristocrat (member of the nobility, of the high-born class, especially in Russia before 1917)</li><li>someone noble in spirit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благородьнъ</i> (blagorodĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го-</i> (blágo-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod, “birth”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noble gas, inert gas (the elements of group 18 of the periodic table)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobility, nobleness, generosity</li></ol><p><i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Blooming-branched; blossoming and verdant in a divine and blessed way; (of a plant) having blessed vigor.</li><li>Related to Latin frondens.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>favorably, with favor</li></ol><p><i>благоскло́нный</i> (blagosklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benevolence, favour/favor, goodwill</li></ol><p><i>благоскло́нный</i> (blagosklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kind</li><li>benevolent, favorable</li></ol><p><i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>скло́нный</i> (sklónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benediction, blessing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благословениѥ</i> (blagoslovenije). By surface analysis, <i>благослови́ть</i> (blagoslovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Compare Ukrainian <i>благослове́ння</i> (blahoslovénnja), Belarusian <i>благаславе́нне</i> (blahaslavjénnje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blessed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благословенъ</i> (blagoslovenŭ). By surface analysis, <i>благослове́ние</i> (blagoslovénije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to bless</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give one's blessing, to give permission</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благословити</i> (blagosloviti), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐυλογεῖν</i> (eulogeîn). By surface analysis, <i>бла́го-</i> (blágo-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ, “word”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to bless</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give one's blessing, to give permission</li></ol><p><i>благослови́ть</i> (blagoslovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-being, prosperity, welfare (state of health, happiness and&#47;or prosperity)</li></ol><p><i>бла́го-</i> (blágo-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>состоя́ние</i> (sostojánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mellow, soft and pleasing</li></ol><p><i>бла́гость</i> (blágostʹ, “goodness”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) gift, donation; charity; good deed, benefaction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благостꙑни</i> (blagostyni). Equivalent to <i>благость</i> (blagostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-иня</i> (-inja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) gentleness, kindness</li></ol><p><i>благо́й</i> (blagój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philanthropist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charity, philanthropy, welfare</li></ol><p><i>благотвори́тельный</i> (blagotvorítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charitable, philanthropic</li></ol><p><i>благотворитель</i> (blagotvoritelʹ, “charitable person”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beneficial, salutary, wholesome (helpful or good to something or someone)</li></ol><p><i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve, to equip with modern amenities, <i>(apartment)</i> to face-lift, <i>(city, park)</i> to beautify, <i>(street)</i> to resurface</li></ol><p><i>благоустро́ить</i> (blagoustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become well-equipped</li></ol><p><i>благоустра́ивать</i> (blagoustráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-furnished, well-equipped, comfortable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve, to equip with modern amenities, <i>(apartment)</i> to face-lift, <i>(city, park)</i> to beautify, <i>(street)</i> to resurface</li></ol><p><i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become well-equipped</li></ol><p><i>благоустро́ить</i> (blagoustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improvement of housing</li></ol><p><i>благоустро́ить</i> (blagoustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragrance, aroma (a pleasant smell)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоѫханиѥ</i> (blagoǫxanije), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐωδία</i> (euōdía). By surface analysis, <i>благоуха́ть</i> (blagouxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragrant, sweet-smelling, perfumed, aromatic</li></ol><p><i>благоуха́ние</i> (blagouxánije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fragrant, to smell sweet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благоѫхати</i> (blagoǫxati), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐωδέω</i> (euōdéō), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫxati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>уха́ть</i> (uxátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devout, pious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благочьстивъ</i> (blagočĭstivŭ), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐσεβής</i> (eusebḗs). By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>чести́ть</i> (čestítʹ, “to honor”) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godliness, piety</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>благочьстьѥ</i> (blagočĭstĭje), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐσέβεια</i> (eusébeia). By surface analysis, <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>чести́ть</i> (čestítʹ, “to honor”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>blissfully</li><li>blessedly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wackily</li></ol><p><i>блаже́нный</i> (blažénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blissful</li><li>(<i>church</i>) beatified, saint</li><li><i>(as a noun)</i> "God's fool", holy fool</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>блаженъ</i> (blaženŭ), passive participle of <i>блажити</i> (blažiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolžiti</i>, from <i>&#42;bolgo</i> or <i>&#42;bolgъ</i>, of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bliss, beatitude, felicity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience bliss</li></ol><p><i>блаже́нство</i> (blažénstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whim, caprice, fancy, folly</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>бла́го</i> (blágo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, letterhead, sheet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blank</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blat, cronyism, pull (useful contacts to help in skirting the red tape and other barriers, usually illegal help or assistance to obtain a good post, job, enter the university, etc.)</li><li>bribe</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>blat</i> or Yiddish <i>בלאַט</i> (blat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) joint (a place where people go to have drinks, use drugs or have sex, place where criminals get together and keep weapons or stolen goods)</li></ol><p>Russian <i>блат</i> (blat, “pull”) + Ukrainian <i>ха́та</i> (xáta, “house”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) having good connections, well-connected; having a <i>блат</i> (blat), based on the use of cronyism, etc.</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) thieves' underworld</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) relative or friend of an important person; a representative of the criminal underworld of thieves</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) thief</li></ol><p><i>блат</i> (blat) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) synonym of <i>боло́то</i> (bolóto)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>блато</i> (blato), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolto</i>. Doublet of <i>боло́то</i> (bolóto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) abbreviation of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “damn, fucking (an intensifier)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;low colloquial) to puke, to vomit</li></ol><p><i>блева́ть</i> (blevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;colloquial) to puke, to vomit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бльвати</i> (blĭvati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľьvati</i> (“to vomit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vomit, spew (regurgitated former contents of a stomach)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бле́дный</i> (blédnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бле́дно</i> (blédno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pale, to fade</li><li>to turn white, to grow pale</li></ol><p>From <i>бле́дный</i> (blédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale yellow</li></ol><p><i>бле́дн(ый)</i> (blédn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale green</li></ol><p><i>бле́дный</i> (blédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a pale face (<i>usually in the context of American Indians</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleface (<i>usually in the context of American Indians</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pallor, paleness</li><li>insipidity, colorlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>бле́дный</i> (blédnyj, “pale”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blědьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;blědъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blazer <i>(jacket)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blazer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) blackjack</li></ol><p>From English <i>blackjack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>блёклый</i> (bljóklyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blender</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blender</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brilliance, glitter, lustre, shine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blěskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minnow, spoon-bait</li></ol><p>From <i>блеск</i> (blesk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to glitter, to sparkle, to flash</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be gifted, to shine, to sparkle [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to flash across one's mind (<i>perfective only</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blьsknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to glitter, to sparkle, to flash</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be gifted, to shine, to sparkle [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blьščati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Something shiny; something which shines or sparkles.</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) A sap beetle; a beetle of the family Nitidulidae.</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) ellipsis of <i>оса́-блестя́нка</i> (osá-blestjánka, “cuckoo wasp”)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) A jacamar of the genus <i>Galbula</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>блесте́ть</i> (blestétʹ, “to glitter; to sparkle; to flash; to shine”) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any small sparkling object, spangle</li></ol><p><i>блест(е́ть)</i> (blest(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brilliantly</li></ol><p><i>блестя́щий</i> (blestjáščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shining, glittering, sparkling, flashing</li><li>brilliant</li><li>splendid, magnificent, fine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bluff</li></ol><p>Since the 1920s from English <i>bluff</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) to bluff (also figuratively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleat</li></ol><p><i>бле́ять</i> (bléjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bleat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blějati</i> (“to bleat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₁-</i> (“to bleat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearest</li><li>next</li><li>immediate</li><li>near</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бли́зко</i> (blízko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Near East, Near Eastern; Middle East, Middle Eastern</li></ol><p>From <i>бли́жний</i> (blížnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>восто́к</i> (vostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare <i>Бли́жний Восто́к</i> (Blížnij Vostók, “Near East”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near, nearby, neighboring</li><li>nearest, next</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow creature</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bližьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near, in the vicinity of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blizъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw near, to approach</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bližiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>близ</i> (bliz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(one's) folks, (one's) family, the loved ones</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close, near</li><li>dear, intimate [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>directly concerning [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>having free access [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)]</li><li>similar [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blizъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close, nearby [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>intimately</li></ol><p><i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearby, close by</li><li>contiguous, adjacent, neighboring</li></ol><p>First attested in 1766. From <i>близ</i> (bliz) +&lrm; <i>лежа́щий</i> (ležáščij), calque of German <i>nahegelegen</i> or <i>nächstliegend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twin (male or female)</li><li><i>(colloquial)</i> one born under the zodiac sign of Gemini (Близнецы), from May 21 to June 21</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bliznьcь</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>близна́к</i> (bliznák), Polish <i>bliźniak</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>blizánac</i> / <i>близа́нац</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twins</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearsighted, myopic</li><li>shortsighted, lacking in foresight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>близозоръкъ</i> (blizozorŭkŭ), with haplology, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blizozorkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>short-sightedly</li></ol><p><i>близору́кий</i> (blizorúkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortsightedness, nearsightedness, myopia</li><li>shortsightedness, lack of foresight</li></ol><p><i>близору́кий</i> (blizorúkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nearness, proximity, closeness</li><li>intimacy</li></ol><p><i>бли́з(кий)</i> (blíz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij): close, near</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>близко</i> (blizko): close, nearby</li></ol><p><i>близёхонький</i> (blizjóxonʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij): close, near</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij): very close, very near</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glare, dazzle, flare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Blyxa</i>-genus plant.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Blyxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pancake, crepe, blini (thin batter cake)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) disc, plate (any flat, round object)</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) CD</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding&#32;slang</i>) weight plate</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial,&#32;exclamation) dammit!, darn!, shoot! (expression of any strong emotion)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mlinъ</i>.</p><p>Euphemistic alteration of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore”, a vulgar curse), influenced by the noun <i>блин</i> (blin, “pancake, crepe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) blindage, dugout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>blindage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pancake, crepe, blintsi</li></ol><p>From <i>блин</i> (blin) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc), originally a diminutive of <i>блин</i> (blin); compare Yiddish <i>בלינצע</i> (blintse), English <i>blintz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blini</li></ol><p><i>блин</i> (blin) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folded, stuffed pancake</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>блин</i> (blin): small pancake</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) small lens</li><li>skipping stone</li></ol><p><i>блин</i> (blin) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brilliantly, splendidly, magnificently</li></ol><p><i>блиста́тельный</i> (blistátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) brilliant, splendid, magnificent</li></ol><p><i>блиста́ть</i> (blistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to glitter, to sparkle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make a brilliant display (with), to shine (with), to be radiant (with), to be conspicuous (by) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) flashlight</li><li>quick chess game, sudden-death game</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Blitz</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>молниено́сный</i> (molnijenósnyj) (see more).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blitzkrieg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Blitzkrieg</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blogger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blogger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blogger</li></ol><p>First attested in 2004. Borrowed from English <i>blogger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blogosphere</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blogosphere</i>. By surface analysis, <i>блог</i> (blog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сфе́ра</i> (sféra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block (cuboid object)</li><li>set, unit</li><li>block, section (<i>of a prefabricated building</i>)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) bloc, coalition</li><li>pulley</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) block</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bloc</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Block</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>block</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Блок)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, equivalent to English Blok, particularly, the Russian lyrical poet, writer, publicist, playwright, translator and literary critic Alexander Blok, 28 November, 1880, to 7 August 1921</li><li>Block Island</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) power supply unit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) siege, blockade</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) blockade</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) block; blocking; obstruction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Blockade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Siege of Leningrad (the military blockade of the city of Leningrad (now Saint Petersburg) by German, Finnish and Spanish troops with the participation of volunteers from North Africa, Europe and the Italian naval forces during World War II, which lasted from September 8, 1941, to January 27, 1944)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) under siege, blockade</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) blockade</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) block; blocking; obstruction</li></ol><p><i>блока́да</i> (blokáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) blocker (drug that blocks a receptor)</li></ol><p><i>блокирова́ть</i> (blokirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockbuster (film or book, that sustains exceptional and widespread popularity and achieves enormous sales)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blockbuster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) blocker, clamp (device that locks the movement of something)</li></ol><p><i>блокирова́ть</i> (blokirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blocking</li></ol><p><i>блоки́ровать</i> (blokírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block</li><li>to blockade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blocking, block, blockage</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) lock</li></ol><p><i>блоки́ровать</i> (blokírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blocker (something that prevents the appearance of something)</li></ol><p><i>блокирова́ть</i> (blokirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notebook (book)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bloc-notes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>блокно́т</i> (bloknót, “notebook”)</li></ol><p><i>блокно́т</i> (bloknót) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Blok, Block, Bloch (Russian and Dutch surname, e.g. of Alexander Blok, Russian poet and Adriaen Block, Dutch explorer)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Block Island (an island off the coast of Rhode Island)</li></ol><p><i>Блок</i> (Blok) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) checkpoint; signal box; roadblock</li></ol><p><i>блок</i> (blok) +&lrm; <i>пост</i> (post)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) recorder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Blockflöte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>coding</i>) blockchain code</li></ol><p><i>блокче́йн</i> (blokčéjn, “blockchain”) +&lrm; <i>ко́д</i> (kód, “code”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blond (fair-skinned, fair-haired man)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>блонди́нка</i> (blondínka): blonde (fair-haired girl or woman)</li></ol><p><i>блонди́нка</i> (blondínka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flea</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>блъха</i> (blŭxa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blъxa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;blúšāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰlúseh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flea-ridden</li></ol><p><i>блоха́</i> (bloxá) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) block (cuboid object)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) block (prefabricated piece of a building)</li><li>blocky</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pulley</li></ol><p><i>блок</i> (blok, “block (cuboid object)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>блок</i> (blok, “pulley”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flea</li></ol><p><i>блоха́</i> (bloxá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flea market (an outdoor market selling inexpensive antiques, curios)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licentiousness, lechery, fornication</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>блудъ</i> (bludŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blǫdъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;blandás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰlendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) to wander, to roam</li><li>to fornicate, to whore around</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blǫditi</i>, derived from <i>блуд</i> (blud). Compare with Old Church Slavonic <i>блѫдити</i> (blǫditi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lascivious</li></ol><p><i>блуд</i> (blud) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) rake, lecher, fornicator, immoral person</li></ol><p><i>блуд</i> (blud) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) harlot, whore</li></ol><p><i>блуд</i> (blud) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prodigal</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) adulterous</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) lascivious, lecherous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blǫdьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>блуд</i> (blud) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often plural</i>) wandering</li></ol><p><i>блужда́ть</i> (bluždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roam, to wander, to rove, to ramble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blǫďati</i>. Derived from <i>блуд</i> (blud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constantly changing location or direction of movement; wandering (<i>of a light</i>)</li><li>shifting (<i>of a look or glance</i>)</li><li>intermittent, appearing and disappearing</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) wandering (abnormally mobile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blouse (usually a working blouse)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>blouse</i> or German <i>Bluse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blouse</li></ol><p><i>блу́за</i> (blúza) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) someone wearing a blouse (<i>usually of French workmen and craftsmen</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>блу́за</i> (blúza) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black metal fan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) blackout (a large-scale power failure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blackout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) blackjack (common gambling card game in casinos, where the object is to get as close to 21 without going over)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blackjack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackface (a style of makeup in which a non-black person blackens their face, usually in order to portray a black person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blackface</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>блю́дце</i> (bljúdce): small saucer, small jam dish</li></ol><p><i>блю́дце</i> (bljúdce) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish, plate</li><li>dish, course</li><li>dish, a specific type of prepared food</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľudo</i>.</p><p>Compare the identical meaning expansion (vessel for food, then also content of such a vessel, then also specific type of food): English <i>dish</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>блю́до</i> (bljúdo, “dish, bowl”)</li><li>saucer</li></ol><p><i>блю́до</i> (bljúdo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) blues (musical genre)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) blues (musical composition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>blues</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blues (a music genre and musical form)</li></ol><p><i>блюз</i> (bljuz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to keep</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>блюсти</i> (bljusti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľusti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bjaustei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian, keeper, warden</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>блюститель</i> (bljustitelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>блюсти́</i> (bljustí) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>networking</i>) Bluetooth (personal area wireless network)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Bluetooth</i>, calqued from Old Norse <i>Blátǫnn</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) alternative form of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) augmentative of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ): a bitch, an ugly woman</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang,&#32;by extension) a scoundrel, a bastard</li></ol><p><i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore, slut, prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína). May be related to Ukrainian <i>блєдіна</i> (bljedina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>): an intimate encounter, sexual intercourse on the side or with a casual, non-regular partner</li></ol><p><i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to whore around (to regularly copulate with people that one is not in a relationship with)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) Dictionary of vulgar words.</li></ol><p>From <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ) +&lrm; <i>словник</i> (slovnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) slutty, whorish</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking (offensive, annoying)</li></ol><p><i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) prostitution, depravity</li><li>loose behaviour (of women), sluttiness</li><li>dirty trick</li><li>stupid blunder</li></ol><p><i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore, slut, prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whore, slut, prostitute</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bitch, slut, promiscuous person</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) general-purpose insult; compare shithead, bastard, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) <i>interjection</i> (may be contracted to <i>бля</i> (blja)); compare for fuck's sake!, shit!, etc.</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) used as filler or intensifier</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;intensive) (may be contracted to <i>бля</i> (blja)); compare damn, fucking, etc.</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) discourse particle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>блѧдь</i> (blędĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blędь</i> (“error”), from <i>&#42;blęsti</i> (“to talk nonsense, err”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) slut, whore, tramp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) chatter, talk nonsense</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) fornicate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blęsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>блясти́</i> (bljastí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) misspelling of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badge, plaque</li><li>metal plate used in a buckle or for decoration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>blacha</i>, from Old High German <i>bleh</i> (“metal plate”) (whence ultimately also German <i>Blech</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) alternative form of <i>бля́ха-му́ха</i> (bljáxa-múxa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) Used to express surprise, annoyance or other strong emotion.</li></ol><p>Euphemism of <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ) using similar-sounding words. Literally, “metal plate/badge-fly”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>бля́ха</i> (bljáxa, “badge, name plate”)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) plaque, dental calculus / tartar</li></ol><p><i>бля́ха</i> (bljáxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded</li></ol><p><i>блёк(нуть)</i> (bljók(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade, to wither</li><li>to pale <i>(beside / before)</i>, to pale into insignificance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blьknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparkle, sparklet</li><li>paillette, spangle, tinsel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бле́дный</i> (blédnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>без на́шей отве́тственности</i> (bez nášej otvétstvennosti): without our responsibility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>бНОПНЯ́</i> (bNOPNJÁ)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: бНОПНЯ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mojibake</li></ol><p>The result of decoding the word <i>вопро́с</i> (voprós) using KOI-8. The stress on the last syllable is probably derived through influence from other words with <i>-ня</i> (-nja) ending, especially words with close meaning, like <i>херня</i> (xernja) and <i>фигня</i> (fignja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) boo-boo, owie (an expression that a body part hurts or someone is in pain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boa (snake)</li><li>feather boa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boa</i>, from Latin <i>boa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bean</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) bobsled</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bobъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰabʰ-</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Боб)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name diminutive <i>Bob</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bobbin, spool, reel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bobine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bean; leguminous</li></ol><p><i>боб</i> (bob) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaver (aquatic mammal)</li><li>beaver pelt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бобръ</i> (bobrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bobrъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰébʰrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beaver</li></ol><p><i>бобёр</i> (bobjór, “beaver”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) or <i>бобр</i> (bobr, “beaver”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaverkin; a baby beaver</li></ol><p><i>бобр</i> (bobr) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok) or <i>бобёр</i> (bobjór) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) bobsleigh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bobsleigh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bobsleigh, bobsled</li></ol><p><i>бобсле́й</i> (bobsléj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бобр</i> (bobr, “beaver, beaver pelt”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>God</li><li>god, idol</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>богъ</i> (bogŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bogъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бог)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>God (in monotheistic religions)</li></ol><p>See <i>бог</i> (box).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>any kind of; God knows what kind of; Lord knows what kind of; some kind of</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) unprecedented, unheard-of, unimaginable</li></ol><p>From <i>бог</i> (box, “God”) + весть (<i>vest'</i>) + како́й (kakój, “what, which”) - "God knows what". <b>Весть</b> is the 3rd person singular form of an archaic verb <b>ведети</b> (to see, to know&#59; compare ве́дать).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>any kind of; God knows what kind of; Lord knows what kind of; some kind of</li><li>unprecedented; unheard-of; unimaginable</li></ol><p>From <i>бог</i> (box, “God”) + знать (znatʹ, “to know”) + како́й (kakój, “what, which”) - "God knows what".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deus ex machina</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>deus ex māchinā</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almshouse</li><li>(by extension,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) a place full of incompetent and/or ignorant people, a useless place</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бога дѣлꙗ</i> (boga dělja, “for God's sake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rich man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow rich</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abundantly, prodigally, richly, sumptuously</li></ol><p><i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, riches</li><li>richness, resplendence, gorgeousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bogatьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj, “rich, wealthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, wealthy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>богатъ</i> (bogatŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bogatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bogatyr (type of Russian heroic warrior)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) heroic, Herculean, athletic</li></ol><p><i>богаты́рь</i> (bogatýrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) bogatyr, a medieval heroic warrior in Kievan Rus</li><li>strongly built man, hero</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>богатꙑрь</i> (bogatyrʹ), <i>багатꙑрь</i> (bagatyrʹ), from a Turkic language, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;bagatur</i>. First attested in Russian in 1425. Compare Ukrainian <i>богати́р</i> (bohatýr), Mongolian <i>баатар</i> (baatar), Bashkir <i>батыр</i> (batır), Turkish <i>batur</i>, Persian بهادر, etc. More at bogatyr.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rich man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bogačь</i> (“wealthy man”), from <i>&#42;bogъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ačь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бога́то</i> (bogáto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chinese Emperor</li></ol><p>From Mongolian <i>богд хаан</i> (bogd xaan) "Godly Khan". The term originally referred to the Chinese emperors of the Qing Dynasty who used to rule over Mongolia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bohemia, bohemianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bohemian (<i>lifestyle, behavior, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>боге́ма</i> (bogéma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bohemian (relating to the Bohemia region of the Czech Republic)</li></ol><p><i>Боге́мия</i> (Bogémija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God-fearing</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>боязли́вый</i> (bojazlívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>God-fearingness</li></ol><p><i>богобоя́зненный</i> (bogobojáznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God-fearing</li></ol><p><i>богобоя́знь</i> (bogobojáznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon painter</li></ol><p>From <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-маз</i> (-maz), combining form of <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ, “to smear, to daub”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Богома́терь</i> (Bogomáterʹ)</li></ol><p><i>Бог</i> (Box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ма́терь</i> (máterʹ)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Богоматерь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blessed Virgin, Our Lady</li></ol><p><i>Бог</i> (Box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ма́терь</i> (máterʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devotionalist, pietist</li><li>pilgrim</li><li>praying mantis</li></ol><p>From <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мол</i> (-mol), combining form of <i>моли́ться</i> (molítʹsja, “to pray”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilgrim (traveler to a religious site)</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) God-bearer (someone who carries God within himself&#59; someone who directly expresses the idea of God&#59; someone charged with a great religious mission)</li></ol><p><i>Бог</i> (Box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec); calque of Ancient Greek <i>θεοφόρος</i> (theophóros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godlike</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;archaic) hostile to God, against God</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) disgusting</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>проти́вный</i> (protívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Богоро́дица</i> (Bogoródica)</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Богородица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Virgin Mary, Blessed Virgin, Our Lady, Theotokos</li></ol><p>From <i>Бог</i> (Box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>род(ить)</i> (rod(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica), "God-bearer", calque from Koine Greek <i>Θεοτόκος</i> (Theotókos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Virgin Mary, Blessed Virgin, Our Lady</li></ol><p><i>Богоро́дица</i> (Bogoródica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theologian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theology, divinity</li></ol><p>Might ultimately be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>θεολογία</i> (theología).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theological</li></ol><p><i>богосло́вие</i> (bogoslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) liturgical (related to worship services)</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>служе́бный</i> (služébnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church service, divine service, public worship, worship service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;humorous) prosperous</li><li>(historical,&#32;ecclesiastical) blessed (<i>epithet</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worship, to deify, to idolize, to adore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>боготворити</i> (bogotvoriti), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>θεοποιέω</i> (theopoiéō). By surface analysis, <i>бого-</i> (bovo-) +&lrm; <i>творить</i> (tvoritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deicide <i>(person)</i></li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemer</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хул</i> (xul) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemy, profanity</li></ol><p><i>Бог</i> (Box, “God”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хула́</i> (xulá, “abuse, reviling”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бог</i> (box).</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Богъ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Бог</i> (Box).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to butt, to gore, to hit with horns</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bodati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodysuit</li></ol><p>Pseudo-anglicism, derived from <i>body</i>, a clipping of <i>bodysuit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodybuilding (sport of muscle development)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bodybuilding</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бо́дро</i> (bódro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stimulate, to invigorate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cheerfully, freshly</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vivacity, sprightliness, cheerfulness</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;literary) vivacity</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wakefulness, vigil, arousal, keeping wake</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрствовать</i> (bódrstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay awake</li><li>to keep vigil</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрство</i> (bódrstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awake, wakeful (conscious)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerful</li><li>brisk</li><li>sprightly</li><li>awake</li><li>vigorous, vivacious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бъдръ</i> (bŭdrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъdrъ</i> (“alert, cheerful”), from <i>&#42;bъděti</i> (“to be awake”) + <i>&#42;-rъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vivacity, sprightliness, cheerfulness</li></ol><p><i>бо́дрый</i> (bódryj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>бодря́к</i> (bodrják): vivacity, sprightliness, cheerfulness</li></ol><p><i>бодря́к</i> (bodrják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hangover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) freshwater sponge</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) badyaga (folk medicine derived from the freshwater sponge, used for bruises)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;criminal slang) diluted, surrogate or fake substance sold as the original, pure substance</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) nonsense; long-winded chatter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bodęga</i>, from <i>&#42;bosti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a militant, an insurgent, a fighter (a member of an illegal armed group)</li><li>(animate,&#32;dated) a fighter, a pugilist (a person who engages in tussles and fistfights)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) an action movie</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;dated) a blockbuster (an exceptionally popular movie or performance)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;military,&#32;obsolete) a fighter plane</li></ol><p><i>боево́й</i> (bojevój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martial art (fighting style)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) combat, battle, fighting</li><li>(<i>ammunition</i>) live, lethal (<i>as opposed to dummy, blank, or non-lethal</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) militant, bellicose, belligerent</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) active, energetic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojevъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) unit of fire <i>(set amount of ammunition per weapon)</i></li><li>ammunition allowance, ammunition load</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) combat formation, battle formation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ammunition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warhead</li></ol><p><i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>голо́вка</i> (golóvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) alertness</li></ol><p>First attested in 1912 [see citations]. <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вность</i> (gotóvnostʹ), calque of German <i>Kriegsbereitschaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) unit of fire <i>(set amount of ammunition per weapon)</i></li><li>ammunition allowance, ammunition load</li></ol><p><i>бой</i> (boj, “war”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>компле́кт</i> (komplékt, “kit, set”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) ammunition supply</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explosive ordnance, weapon, shell</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ammunition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ammunition</li><li><i>(singular)</i> explosive ordnance, weapon, shell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight worthiness</li></ol><p>First attested in 1894 [see citations]. <i>бой</i> (boj, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бность</i> (sposóbnostʹ, “capacity, power”), calque of German <i>Kampffähigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>battleworthy</li></ol><p>From <i>бой</i> (boj, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj, “capable, able”), a calque of German <i>kampffähig</i>. First attested in 1887. See the citations.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affair, clash (action or engagement not of sufficient magnitude to be called a battle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior</li><li>fighter, soldier</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-ба</i> (-ba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh my God!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>бог</i> (box)</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deity, divinity (essential nature of a god)</li></ol><p><i>боже́ственный</i> (božéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine, godly, godlike</li></ol><p><i>божество́</i> (božestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deity</li><li>divinity, divine being, godhead</li><li>idol</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>божьство</i> (božĭstvo), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>θεότης</i> (theótēs). By surface analysis, <i>бог</i> (bog, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ество́</i> (-estvó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) goodness, oh my, my word (expression of surprise, delight, indignation, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>бо́же</i> (bóže) +&lrm; <i>-ечки</i> (-ečki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God's, divine</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon box, icon shelf</li><li>chapel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>божьница</i> (božĭnica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;božьnica</i>, from <i>&#42;božьnъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ica</i> or <i>&#42;bogъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьnica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бог</i> (box): (a little) god</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) idol, someone loved by the public</li><li>a figurine or a small statue of a god</li></ol><p><i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladybug, ladybird</li><li>meek creature, lamb (a harmless, quiet person who cannot defend himself)</li></ol><p>Literally, “godly little cow”. Compare archaic Polish <i>boża krówka</i>, Irish <i>bóín Dé</i>, Dutch <i>lieveheersbeestje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бо́жий</i> (bóžij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) elder (<i>a shrub of the genus Sambucus</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъzъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂ǵos</i> (“birch”). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>бъз</i> (bǎz), Serbo-Croatian <i>базга</i>/<i>bazga</i>, Slovak <i>baza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>particle physics</i>) boson</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boson</i>. Named after Indian&#32;physicist&#32;Satyendra Nath Bose&nbsp;(1894–1974).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating, breakage</li><li>battle, combat, engagement</li><li>fight, fighting, struggle</li><li>striking <i>(of a clock)</i></li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) boy (<i>servant</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰoyh₂ós</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeyh₂-</i> (“to strike, hew”). Cognate with <i>бить</i> (bitʹ, “to beat”), with typical Indo-European nominal <i>o</i>-ablaut.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>boy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smart, clever</li><li>sharp</li><li>glib, fluent</li><li>animated, busy, brisk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojьkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>briskly</li><li>glibly, fluently</li></ol><p><i>бо́йкий</i> (bójkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бойко)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Boyko, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boycott</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boycott</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boycott, boycotting</li></ol><p><i>бойкоти́ровать</i> (bojkotírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boycott, to blackball</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiler water heater</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boiler</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>bulliō</i>, from Latin <i>bulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embrasure, loophole (slit in a castle wall)</li></ol><p><i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>massacre (intentional mass killing)</li><li>abattoir (public slaughterhouse)</li></ol><p><i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boy Scout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Boy Scout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) boyfriend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boyfriend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a solid, muscular build (<i>said of fighting cocks, gamecocks</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бое́ц</i> (bojéc, “fighter, warrior”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side (various senses)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>side by side (close to each other, together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bocage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bocage</i>, from Old French <i>boscage</i>, <i>boschage</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;boscāticum</i>, from <i>boscus</i>, a borrowing from Frankish <i>&#42;busk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buskaz</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to grow”) and consequently related to inherited Russian <i>быть</i> (bytʹ). The Latin ending <i>-āticum</i> is composed of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂tos</i>, whence Russian <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj), and <i>&#42;-kos</i>, <i>&#42;-ḱos</i>, whence Russian <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine glass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bocal</i>, from Italian <i>boccale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) side, flank; lateral</li></ol><p><i>бок</i> (bok) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) boxing</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) box (isolation ward in a hospital)</li><li>box cut (men’s flattop hairstyle in which the hair at the temples and nape of the neck is shaved off)</li><li>small box (such as for computer disks)</li></ol><p>From French <i>boxe</i>, from English <i>to box</i>.</p><p>From English <i>box</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boxer briefs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to box</li></ol><p><i>бокс</i> (boks) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bauxite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bauxite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bauxite; bauxitic</li></ol><p><i>бокси́т</i> (boksít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boxer <i>(breed of dog)</i></li><li>boxer <i>(boxing-ring fighter)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boxer</li></ol><p><i>боксёр</i> (boksjór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bok choy (Chinese cabbage) <i>Brassica rapa</i> subsp. <i>chinensis</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bok choy</i>, from Cantonese <i>白菜</i> (baak6 coi3, “white vegetable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>baseball</i>) ball (bad pitch)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) blockhead, dimwit (stupid person)</li><li>dummy (in card games)</li><li>block (<i>for hats or wigs</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;paganism) an idol</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъlvanъ</i>, <i>&#42;balъvanъ</i> (“idol”). Cognate with Polish <i>bałwan</i> (“snowman”), Old Church Slavonic <i>балванъ</i> (balvanŭ, “statue”) and Serbo-Croatian <i>ба̀лван</i> (“beam, log”). Related to Latvian <i>bulvāns</i> (“decoy”). Possibly borrowed from a Turkic language, compare Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;balbal</i> (“gravestone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig, ingot, blank, block of wood, etc., serving as raw material for some product.</li><li>hat block, wig block, blockhead</li><li>(<i>military</i>) solid projectile (<i>either armor-piercing or used as a proof shot</i>); penetrator round; shot</li><li>draft outline, outline</li><li>(<i>Wikimedia jargon</i>) stub</li><li>(<i>office&#32;slang</i>) template, boilerplate</li><li>stupid woman, dummy</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) a blank CD-R /DVD-R / DVD+R <i>etc.</i> disk, or other blank optical media for burning data onto it</li></ol><p><i>болва́н</i> (bolván) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bulgarian (native of Bulgaria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) angle grinder, side grinder</li></ol><p><i>Болга́рия</i> (Bolgárija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>The “angle grinder” sense derives from the fact that the first such tools to become available in the Soviet Union (in the 1970s) were of Bulgarian manufacture.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bulgarian (relating to Bulgaria, its people or the Bulgarian language)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bulgarian language</li></ol><p><i>Болга́рия</i> (Bolgárija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capsicum, sweet pepper, bell pepper</li></ol><p><i>болга́рский</i> (bolgárskij, “Bulgarian”) +&lrm; <i>пе́рец</i> (pérec, “pepper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>большой</i> (bolʹšoj)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>много</i> (mnogo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pain</li><li>painful</li></ol><p><i>боль</i> (bolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more</li><li>more than [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most of all (to a greater extent than anything else)</li><li>most (superlative of many and most)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more-or-less</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>furthermore, moreover, what's more</li><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) nothing more, no more (than that)</li></ol><p>Literally, “more than that”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бо́лее и́ли ме́нее</i> (bóleje íli méneje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>painfully (in a painful manner)</li></ol><p><i>боле́зненный</i> (boléznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickliness, unhealthiness</li><li>painfulness</li></ol><p><i>боле́зненный</i> (boléznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sickly, ailing, unhealthy</li><li>morbid</li><li>painful</li></ol><p><i>боле́знь</i> (boléznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malignant; causing disease</li></ol><p>From <i>боле́знь</i> (boléznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of New Latin <i>pathogenes</i>. First attested in 1786.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease, sickness, illness</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) abnormality</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>болѣзнь</i> (bolěznĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolěznь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-знь</i> (-znʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Addison's disease (Addison's disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alzheimer's disease (mental disorder from brain tissue changes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan, supporter</li></ol><p>From <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be sick, to be ill, to be ailing</li><li>to be anxious, to be apprehensive</li><li>to cheer, to root for</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hurt, to ache</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analgesic, analgetic, anodyne</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analgesic, anodyne, pain-relieving</li></ol><p>First attested in 1834 [see citations]. <i>боль</i> (bolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>утоля́ющий</i> (utoljájuščij), calque of German <i>schmerzlindernd</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bolivarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bolivian</li></ol><p><i>Боли́вия</i> (Bolívija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) bolide, fireball (extremely bright meteor)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;sports) race car</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bolide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) well</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>болого</i> (bologo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolgo</i>. Doublet of <i>бла́го</i> (blágo), which was borrowed from Old Church Slavonic. Probably cognate with Sanskrit <i>बृहस्पति</i> (bṛhaspati), Avestan <i>𐬠𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬔</i> (bər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>g), Avestan <i>𐬠𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬘𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (bər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>jaiie<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulk, a light sled without runners, in the form of a small boat or trough</li></ol><p>From Kildin Sami <i>пуӆӆьк</i> (pul̥l̥’k), from Finnish <i>pulkka</i>. Doublet of <i>пульк</i> (pulʹk).</p><p>According to Vladimir Dal, the word is a derivate from оболокать (obolokatʹ). However, Max Vasmer rejects this theory due to its exclusiveness to Russia's North.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excrescence, gall (on trees)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bichon</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a lapdog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bolonais</i>, Italian <i>bolognese</i> (“from Bologna, a city in Italy”). Can also be analysed as <i>Боло́нья</i> (Bolónʹja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nylon raincoat fabric</li></ol><p>From the city name <i>Боло́нья</i> (Bolónʹja).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Болонья)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bologna (the capital city of Emilia-Romagna, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boloria (<i>Boloria</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Boloria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boggy, marshy, swampy</li></ol><p><i>боло́то</i> (bolóto) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bog, marsh, swamp</li><li>khaki, cossack green, hemp (<i>color/colour</i>)</li><li>(<i>eye color</i>) hazel (mix of brown, green, and gold shades)</li></ol><p><i>боло́то</i> (bolóto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bog, swamp, marsh, quagmire, mire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>болото</i> (boloto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolto</i> (“swamp; mud”). Doublet of <i>бла́то</i> (bláto), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>боло́то</i> (bolóto, “bog, swamp, marsh”)</li></ol><p><i>боло́то</i> (bolóto) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt, screw</li><li>(archery,&#32;historical) crossbow bolt, quarrel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) penis, male member</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dangle</li><li>to shake, to rock, to stir</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to stir, to mix, to beat</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to babble, to talk needlessly or endlessly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to converse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьltati</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>bełtać</i> (“to stir”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dangle, to hang about</li><li>to loaf, to lounge about</li></ol><p><i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) talkative</li><li>blabbing (prone not to keep secrets)</li></ol><p><i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk, chatter, jabber, gossip</li></ol><p><i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овня́</i> (-ovnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prattle, babble, twaddle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bolt-threading</li></ol><p>First attested in 1872. <i>болт</i> (bolt) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>bolzenschneidend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an overly talkative person, chatterbox</li></ol><p><i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omelette</li></ol><p><i>болту́н</i> (boltún) +&lrm; <i>-ья</i> (-ʹja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female chatterbox</li><li>a thin food made of flour and water</li><li>omelette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain, ache</li><li>pang, stab, stitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>боль</i> (bolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bolь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>бо́лее и́ли ме́нее</i> (bóleje íli méneje)</li></ol><p>A colloquial pronunciation of <i>бо́лее-ме́нее</i> (bóleje-méneje), possibly influenced by <i>те́м не ме́нее</i> (tém ne méneje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospital</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>больница</i> (bolʹnica), attested in 16-17ᵗʰ c. By surface analysis, <i>боль</i> (bolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica). Cognate with Old Ruthenian <i>больница</i> (bolʹnica), <i>болница</i> (bolnica), Church Slavonic <i>больни́ца</i> (bolĭníca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hospital</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) medical certificate, sick list</li><li>sick leave</li></ol><p><i>больни́ца</i> (bolʹníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bad, badly</li><li>painfully</li><li>extremely, terribly</li><li>a bit too</li></ol><p><i>больно́й</i> (bolʹnój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick, ill</li><li>painful</li><li>weak</li><li>morbid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) patient (someone receiving treatment for an illness)</li></ol><p><i>боль</i> (bolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;inanimate) a large road in rural areas</li><li>(dated,&#32;animate) male head of a family</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój, “great”) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> большой (bolʹšoj),&lrm; бу́ква (búkva).</li><li>capital letter (upper-case letter A B C (Latin) or А Б В (Cyrillic), etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>machine learning</i>) large language model (a type of neural network specializing in language, typically including billions of parameters)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>large language model</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój), comparative degree of <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo): bigger, larger, more, greater</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бо́льший</i> (bólʹšij)</li><li>any more</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most of all (to a greater extent than anything else)</li><li>most (superlative of many and most)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolshevism</li></ol><p>From <i>большев(и́к)</i> (bolʹšev(ík)) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolshevik</li></ol><p>From <i>бо́льше</i> (bólʹše, “greater”) +&lrm; <i>-еви́к</i> (-evík, “-ist”), literally “majoritarian”, alluding to <i>большинство́</i> (bolʹšinstvó, “majority”, literally “greater-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bolshevistic</li></ol><p>From an ephemeral <i>большеви́ст</i> (bolʹševíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) which has however reached persistent prominency in other languages as German <i>Bolschewist</i>. While the form <i>большеви́стский</i> (bolʹševístskij) has, with help by the Soviet ruling class, attained exclusivity, the exiled enemies of the reds – including in Germany – have continued to use the form <i>большеви́цкий</i> (bolʹševíckij), regularly derived from <i>большеви́к</i> (bolʹševík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bolshevistic</li></ol><p><i>большеви́к</i> (bolʹševík) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>большеви́цкий</i> (bolʹševíckij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big-headed</li></ol><p>From <i>больш(ой)</i> (bolʹš(oj)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>голов(а́)</i> (golov(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-breasted, full-busted</li></ol><p><i>больш(ой)</i> (bolʹš(oj)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>груд(ь)</i> (grud(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heavy-load; heavy</li></ol><p><i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>груз</i> (gruz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something bigger, larger</li><li>the biggest, the largest</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>бо́льший</i> (bólʹšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a large nose</li></ol><p><i>больш(о́й)</i> (bolʹš(ój), “large”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bigmouthed (having a large mouth)</li></ol><p>From <i>больш(ой)</i> (bolʹš(oj)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big-armed</li></ol><p><i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) labia majora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój) and <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) bigger, greater</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;boľьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>majority, most</li></ol><p><i>бо́льше</i> (bólʹše) +&lrm; <i>-инство́</i> (-instvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Khitan large script</li></ol><p>From <i>большо́е</i> (bolʹšóje) +&lrm; <i>кида́ньское</i> (kidánʹskoje) +&lrm; <i>письмо́</i> (pisʹmó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>many thanks, thank you very much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big, large, great</li><li>important</li><li>grown-up</li><li>big, outstanding</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>бо́льший</i> (bólʹšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thumb</li><li>big toe, hallux</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very large, huge, enormous, gigantic</li></ol><p><i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój) +&lrm; <i>-у́щий</i> (-úščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sore</li><li>weak spot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bomb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>бомба</i> (bomba) (att. 1688), from Polish <i>bomba</i> (att. 1670), from German <i>Bombe</i> (early 17th c.), from Italian <i>bomba</i>, from <i>bombo</i> (“buzzing”), from Latin <i>bombus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βόμβος</i> (bómbos), ultimately onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) a military rank in Russia (18-19 centuries)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) bombardier; bomb aimer</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) goal-scorer, shooter, striker</li><li>bombardier beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bomb (attack with bombs)</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to bombard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bombing, bombardment</li></ol><p>From <i>бомбардирова́ть</i> (bombardirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>бомбарди́р</i> (bombardír) or <i>бомба́рда</i> (bombárda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bombardment, bombing</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) bomber</li></ol><p><i>бомбардиро́вка</i> (bombardiróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) bomber</li></ol><p><i>бомбардирова́ть</i> (bombardirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gypsy cab driver</li></ol><p><i>бомби́ть</i> (bombítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bombilla</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>bombilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bomber (bomb maker)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bomb</li><li>(informal,&#32;intransitive) to provide private taxi services without a license</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to rage (<i>sense 1</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;historical) silk paper</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Bombycina</i>, from translingual <i>Bombyx</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бо́мбовый</i> (bómbovyj)</li></ol><p><i>бо́мба</i> (bómba) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bomb</li></ol><p><i>бо́мба</i> (bómba) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bombing, bomb release</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-submarine mortar</li><li>bomb thrower</li></ol><p>First attested in 1911 [see citations]. From <i>бо́мба</i> (bómba) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мёт</i> (-mjót), combining form of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”); calque of German <i>Bombenwerfer</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bomb shelter, air-raid shelter</li></ol><p><i>бо́мба</i> (bómba, “bomb”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>убе́жище</i> (ubéžišče, “refuge”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(air raid) bombing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tramp, bum, hobo, slacker, homeless person</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) poorly dressed person</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>лицо́ <b>б</b>ез <b>о</b>пределённого <b>м</b>е́ста <b>ж</b>и́тельства</i> (licó <b>b</b>jez <b>o</b>predeljónnovo <b>m</b>jésta <b>ž</b>ítelʹstva) - a person without defined place of residence.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) homeless shelter</li></ol><p><i>бомж</i> (bomž) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) beau monde</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>beau monde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voucher</li><li>bon (an early type of paper currency)</li><li>any paper currency collected by notaphilists</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a neo-Nazi skinhead</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bon</i>.</p><p>Shortening of бонхе́д (bonxéd), from English <i>bonehead</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonbonnière, candy box</li></ol><p>From French <i>bonbonnière</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bong</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thai <i>บ้อง</i> (bông).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bongo (animal)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) bongo (drum)</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>bongo</i>.</p><p>From American Spanish <i>bongó</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooperage</li></ol><p>From <i>бо́ндарь</i> (bóndarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja) or from <i>бонда́рь</i> (bondárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cooper (craftsman who manufactures large bowls, barrels or ship masts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) bonze (a Buddhist priest in Japan or other Asian countries)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a haughty high-ranking official; a self-important or pompous person; a bigwig</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>凡僧</i> (bonzō) or <i>坊主</i> (bōzu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notaphilist</li></ol><p><i>бон</i> (bon) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notaphily</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bonistique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) bon mot; witticism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bon mot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonobo (pygmy chimpanzee)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonsai</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>盆栽</i> (ぼんさい, bonsai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;finance) bonus</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>bonus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhuyin fuhao, bopomofo</li></ol><p>From Mandarin, the first letters of the alphabet: ㄅ ㄆ ㄇ ㄈ (bo po mo fo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boron</li><li>pinewood, pine forest</li><li>(<i>dental</i>) drill</li><li>milletgrass</li><li>tidal bore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bore</i> or from German <i>Bor</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>bohren</i> (“to bore, to drill”). Doublet of <i>бур</i> (bur).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bora (wind)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brothel, bordello</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bordell</i> or French <i>bordel</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>bordellum</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>nevěstinec</i>, <i>vykřičený dům</i>, Macedonian <i>јавна куќа</i> (javna kuḱa), <i>курварница</i> (kurvarnica), Serbo-Croatian <i>блудилиште</i>, <i>јавна кућа</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border collie, a herding dog originating near the border between England and Scotland</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>border collie</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bordeaux (type of wine)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maroon, claret (color)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bordeaux</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maroon, wine-colored</li></ol><p><i>бордо́</i> (bordó) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bordeaux</li></ol><p>From <i>Бордо́</i> (Bordó) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curb, kerb, edge</li><li>edging</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) border</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bordure</i>&nbsp;f.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) struggle, fight, confrontation</li></ol><p>Likely a borrowing from Church Slavonic. Composed as of <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije) (an irregular construction, as the expected form based on this verb would be <i>&#42;боро́тие</i> (&#42;borótije)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighter, champion</li><li>wrestler</li><li>monkshood, wolfsbane, aconitum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>борьць</i> (borĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;borti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>боро́ть(ся)</i> (borótʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borjomi (a brand, well-known in the USSR, of naturally carbonated mineral water from Georgia (country)).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ბორჯომი</i> (borǯomi).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Боржоми)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borjomi (a resort town in south-central Georgia, famous for its mineral water)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ბორჯომი</i> (borǯomi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borzoi, Russian Wolfhound</li><li>greyhound</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>борза́я соба́ка</i> (borzája sobáka, “swift dog”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li></ol><p><i>бо́рзый</i> (bórzyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) swift, fleet</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) borzoi, greyhound</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) cheeky, insolent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бързъ</i> (bŭrzŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъrzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) audaciousness, brazenness, pushiness, aggressiveness</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;derogatory) an insolent, nervy person</li></ol><p><i>бо́рзый</i> (bórzyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá) or <i>борзо́й</i> (borzój) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift, brisk</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) impudent, brazen, nervy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъrzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bohrium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;obsolete,&#32;Novgorod,&#32;Pskov,&#32;Tver) carrot</li></ol><p>Possibly from Baltic, see Latvian <i>burkāns</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) drill used by dentists</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bohrmaschine</i>.</p><p>Optionally analyzable<i>бор</i> (bor, “drill”) +&lrm; <i>маши́на</i> (mašína, “machine”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muttering, mumbling</li></ol><p><i>бормота́ть</i> (bormotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, to murmur, to babble, to ramble</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Compare Ukrainian <i>бурмота́ти</i> (burmotáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) low-quality wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bornean</li></ol><p><i>Борне́о</i> (Bornéo, “Borneo”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boron</li></ol><p><i>бор</i> (bor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castrated male pig, barrow (usually fattened)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) obese man</li><li>flue, breeching, chimney intake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borvъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Boletus</i> mushroom genus; boletus or bolete in general</li><li>cep (<i>Boletus edulis</i>) specifically</li></ol><p><i>борово́й</i> (borovój, “of the pine forest”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Compare Polish <i>borowik</i>. Also, compare typologically Czech <i>hřib smrkový</i> (&lt; <i>smrk</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Боровик)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borovik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Borovichi</li></ol><p><i>Боровичи́</i> (Borovičí) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pine forest, pinewood</li></ol><p><i>бор</i> (bor) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Боровой)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Borovoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bordà</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bardā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰardʰéh₂</i>. Doublet of <i>брада́</i> (bradá) borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) wart (type of growth occurring on the skin)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bordavъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A frog of the genus <i>Uperoleia</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>борода́вка</i> (borodávka, “wart”) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca, feminine diminutive suffix). Compare Russian <i>борода́вник</i> (borodávnik, “nipplewort”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warthog (a species of wild pig)</li></ol><p><i>борода́вка</i> (borodávka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bordàtъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>борода́</i> (borodá) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj). Doublet of <i>брада́тый</i> (bradátyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bearded man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bordačь</i> (“bearded man”). By surface analysis, <i>борода́</i> (borodá, “beard”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Borodino</li></ol><p><i>Бородино́</i> (Borodinó) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protrusion at the end of a key</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>борода́</i> (borodá): a (small) beard</li></ol><p><i>борода́</i> (borodá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>борода́</i> (borodá, “beard”)</li></ol><p><i>борода́</i> (borodá) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>борозда</i> (borozda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borzda</i>. Doublet of <i>бразда́</i> (brazdá), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to furrow</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to leave ruts</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to travel around, to gallivant (by car, train or ship, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borzditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>борозда́</i> (borozdá, “furrow”)</li><li>groove</li></ol><p><i>борозда́</i> (borozdá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;relational) Solenogastres</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Solenogastres</i>. From <i>боро́здка</i> (borózdka, “groove”) +&lrm; <i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo, “belly”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harrow, to till</li></ol><p><i>борона́</i> (boroná) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) battle, fight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>боронь</i> (boronĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bornь</i>. Doublet of <i>брань</i> (branʹ), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to wrestle, to struggle, to strive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>боротися</i> (borotisja, “to fight, be at war”). Composed as if <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). See <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ). Ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borti</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>боро́тися</i> (borótysja), Belarusian <i>баро́цца</i> (barócca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rye flour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;boršьno</i>. Doublet of бра́шно (brášno), a Church Slavonic loanword.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man-bag (small bag used by men)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>borsetta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;aviation) board, side</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) aircraft, flight</li><li>breast (<i>of a jacket or coat</i>)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1688 in the nautical sense. Borrowed from Dutch <i>boord</i> or German <i>Bord</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) abreast, side by side</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>борт</i> (bort, “side (<i>of a ship</i>), edge, border (<i>of clothing</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>борт</i> (bort) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(apiculture,&#32;obsolete) beekeeper who works in the forest with wild honeybees</li></ol><p><i>борть</i> (bortʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey hunting, honey harvesting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>onboard</li></ol><p><i>борт</i> (bort) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight attendant</li></ol><p><i>борт</i> (bort) +&lrm; <i>проводни́к</i> (provodník).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beehive in a hollow tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъrtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borscht, borsht, borshch, borsch (a soup made of beets and cabbage)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) hogweed (<i>Heracleum sphondylium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъrščь</i>, originally meaning “hogweed”, then “hogweed soup”, later “beetroot soup”, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bursktis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hogweed (<i>Heracleum</i>, any of a group of umbelliferous plants, some of which are poisonous)</li></ol><p><i>борщ</i> (boršč) +&lrm; <i>-еви́к</i> (-evík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>борщ</i> (boršč)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>борщ</i> (boršč).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>struggle, fight, conflict, combat</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) wrestling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>боро́ться</i> (borótʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm wrestling (a sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fighting, struggling, warring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bosnian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>бо̀санскӣ</i> (bòsanskī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>barefoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bosquet, bosket</li></ol><p>From Borrowed from French <i>bosquet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosnian (person)</li></ol><p><i>Бо́сния</i> (Bósnija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bosnian</li></ol><p><i>Бо́сния</i> (Bósnija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barefoot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bosъ</i>. Cognate to Lithuanian <i>bãsas</i>, Latvian <i>bass</i>, Old High German <i>bar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barefoot, barefooted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bosonogъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>босо́й</i> (bosój, “bare”) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandal (open type of footwear)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of having no shoes, stockings or socks on your feet</li><li>extreme poverty</li><li>poor, beggarly people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boss</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boss</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high-grade woolen cloth</li><li>Boston (old card game)</li><li>Boston (type of dance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Boston</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бостон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boston (the capital and largest city of Massachusetts, United States)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Boston</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Boston (old card game)</li></ol><p><i>босто́н</i> (bostón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hobo, tramp, vagabond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a type of small, single-masted sailing vessel or small motor vessel; boat</li><li>overshoe</li><li>a type of winter shoe with a zipper or Velcro fastener</li><li>(computing,&#32;video games) bot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>boot</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>botte</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>bot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) bot. (<i>abbreviation of botany; botanical</i>)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>бота́ника</i> (botánika), <i>ботани́ческий</i> (botaníčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>botanist</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) nerd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>botany</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>botanical (relating to botany)</li></ol><p><i>бота́ника</i> (botánika) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to knock, to call</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to catch fish with a <i>бо́тало</i> (bótalo)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to speak a (criminal) jargon, to slang</li><li>(<i>university slang</i>) to intensively learn, to prepare for an exam or test</li><li>(<i>university slang</i>) to work hard at school</li><li>(university slang,&#32;rare) clipping of <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ); to work</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaves of root vegetables</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) nonsense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъtъva</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бо́тва</i> (bótva, “beet”), Belarusian <i>бо́тва</i> (bótva, “beet”), Polish <i>boćwina</i>. Further cognates possibly include Ancient Greek <i>φῠτόν</i> (phŭtón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) diminutive of <i>бот</i> (bot); small or miniaturised single-masted sailing vessel</li></ol><p>From <i>бот</i> (bot) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe thrower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lace-boot</li><li>high shoe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>боти́нок</i> (botínok, “lace-boot, high shoe”): (child's or small) lace-boot, high shoe</li></ol><p><i>боти́нок</i> (botínok, “lace-boot, high shoe”) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boat; related to a small, single-masted vessel (<i>used almost exclusively in the following example</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>бот</i> (bot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) Vladimir Putin</li></ol><p>Literally, “botox grandpa”, referring to Putin's seeming obsession with cosmetic procedures.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Botswanan</li></ol><p><i>Ботсва́на</i> (Botsvána) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackboot, Hessian boot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowling (game)</li><li>bowling alley</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bowling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>бустрофедо́н</i> (bustrofedón, “boustrophedon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boatswain (the officer (or warrant officer) in charge of sails, rigging, anchors, cables etc. and all work on deck of a sailing ship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bootsmann</i> or Dutch <i>bootsman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;hydrology) deep pool, small pit filled with water, underwater hole in the bed of a river or lake (any wide or deep cavity of earth that is situated on a riverbed or at the bottom of a small river, a lake, or a swamp)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooper (a craftsman who makes and repairs barrels and similar wooden vessels such as casks, buckets and tubs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrel, keg, drum</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) roll (aerobatic maneuver)</li><li>bochka, ancient Russian measure of liquids equal to 491.96 liters (40 вёдер)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) overweight person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъčьka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бок</i> (bok) flank; side</li></ol><p><i>бок</i> (bok) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small barrel, keg, cask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) head</li><li>(slang,&#32;narcotics,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) inflorescence of a female cannabis plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fearfully, timidly, apprehensively, shyly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timid, shy, fearful</li></ol><p><i>боя́з(нь)</i> (bojáz(nʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, dread</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojaznь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>боя́ться</i> (bojátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-знь</i> (-znʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) boyar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>боꙗринъ</i> (bojarinŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>болꙗринъ</i> (boljarinŭ), from Bulgar, attested in Greek variously as <i>βωυλε</i>, <i>βοιλας</i>, <i>βοηλας</i>, <i>βουληα</i>, <i>βοιλᾶς</i>, <i>βολιᾶς</i>. The form with -<i>j</i>- might have come from contamination with Old East Slavic <i>бои</i> (boi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boyar</li><li>boyar's</li></ol><p><i>боя́р(ин)</i> (bojár(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Боярский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Boyarsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) madam, ma'am (a form of address to a noble woman or landlady)</li></ol><p><i>боя́р(ин)</i> (bojár(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ыня</i> (-ynja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawthorn</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer (and others), from <i>боя́рич</i> (bojárič), in turn from <i>боя́рин</i> (bojárin).</p><p>Compare <i>княжени́ка</i> (knjaženíka, “arctic raspberry”), akin to <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ), <i>кня́жий</i> (knjážij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;dated) young lady, miss, unmarried daughter of a boyar, older form of ба́рышня (báryšnja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, to be afraid [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of someone/something’</small> <i>or</i> (<i>colloquial</i>) accusative <small>‘of someone’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to suffer from, to be vulnerable to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bojàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;informal) a terrorist group of anarchists or Esers in beginning of the 20th century in Tsarist Russia and early Soviet Russia</li><li>(dated,&#32;informal) any terrorist group</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a small tactical group within Ukrainian Insurgent Army</li></ol><p>Short of <i>боевая организация</i> (bojevaja organizacija, “battle organization”) or a similar term. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бої́вка</i> (bojívka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brr!, brrr! (it’s cold!)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ew (expresses disgust)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sconce, light fixture on a wall</li><li>bra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bras</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bravado</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bravade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bravo!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>bravo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gallant, dashing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>brave</i> at the start of the 19th century + <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>home brew <i>(not distilled)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;braga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) beard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брада</i> (brada), itself a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>брада</i> (brada), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borda</i>. Doublet of <i>борода́</i> (borodá), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) bearded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брадатъ</i> (bradatŭ). Doublet of <i>борода́тый</i> (borodátyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) barber</li></ol><p>From <i>брада́</i> (bradá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-брей</i> (-brej), combining form of <i>брить</i> (britʹ, “to shave”) (compare 1sg. present <i>бре́ю</i> (bréju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) reveler, drunkard</li><li>hawk moth, sphinx moth (butterfly of the Sphingidae family)</li></ol><p><i>бра́га</i> (brága) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to carouse, to revel, to drink</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) synonym of <i>борозда́</i> (borozdá)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) reins</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бразда</i> (brazda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borzda</i>. Doublet of <i>борозда́</i> (borozdá), the inherited East Slavic form.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бръзда</i> (brŭzda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъzda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse bit, bridle, reins</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) reins of power</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брозда</i> (brozda, “bridle, horse bit”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>бръзда</i> (brŭzda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъzda</i>, further origin uncertain: perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrusdʰos</i>, but a complicated Slavic etymology has also been proposed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brazilian <i>(person)</i></li></ol><p><i>Брази́лия</i> (Brazílija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Brazilian</li></ol><p><i>Брази́лия</i> (Brazílija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage, matrimony</li><li>flaw, defect</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) defective goods, waste, spoilage, rejects</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бракъ</i> (brakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;borkъ</i>.</p><p>Via Polish <i>brak</i>, from Middle Low German <i>brak</i> (“flaw, defect; breaking”). Compare modern cognates German <i>Bruch</i> and English <i>break</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰreg-</i>, whence also Latin <i>frangō</i> (whence Russian <i>фра́кция</i> (frákcija), English <i>fraction</i>, <i>frail</i>, <i>fragile</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arranged marriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective, flawed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject, to consider defective</li><li>to condemn</li></ol><p><i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poacher (person who trespasses in order to take game illegally)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>braconnier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) divorce</li></ol><p>First attested in 1834 [see citations]. <i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>разво́д</i> (razvód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>Ehescheidungs-</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage; wedding (official); nuptials</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) topgallant sail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>bramzeil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fireship (a vessel full of explosives unleashed against the enemy vessel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brander</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firewall, party wall</li><li>(<i>advertising</i>) wall billboard</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) firewall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brandmauer</i>.</p><p>The computing sense is a semantic loan from English <i>firewall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire pump</li><li>hose mouthpiece</li><li>nose-piece</li><li>water cannon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>brandspuit</i> (<i>brand</i> + <i>spuit</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to scold, to curse (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quarrel (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>брани́ться</i> (branítʹsja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li><li>to abuse, to curse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to call names</li></ol><p><i>брань</i> (branʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel (with), to wrangle (with)</li><li>to abuse one another, to abuse each other</li><li>to swear, to curse</li></ol><p><i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abusive, offensive</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) martial, military; (<i>relational</i>) war; battle</li></ol><p><i>брань</i> (branʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fractious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fractious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;textiles) patterned, adorned with a pattern or design</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scolding, invective, swearing</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) battle, fight</li><li>cloth with fancy patterns</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>брань</i> (branĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bornь</i>. Compare the inherited Old East Slavic <i>боронь</i> (boronĭ).</p><p><i>бра́ный</i> (brányj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bracelet, bangle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the plural) handcuffs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bracelet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>swimming</i>) breaststroke</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother</li><li>old boy, pal</li><li>kind, sort</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>братъ</i> (bratŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bratrъ</i>, <i>&#42;bratъ</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>भ्रातृ</i> (bhrātṛ), Latin <i>frāter</i>, English <i>brother</i> and English <i>pal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) older brother</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brother</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bro, a form of address to a friend</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a member of a братва́ (bratvá) (a criminal group)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бра́танъ</i> (brátanŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraternization</li><li>(<i>military</i>) act of fraternization on the frontline between the fighting sides due to ceasefire, war end or fight refusing, e.g. during the Democratization of the Imperial Russian Army.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) brotherhood (referring to criminal gangs in Russia and other ex-USSR states, especially those involved in organized crime); Russian mafia</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;collectively) friends, pals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) bro, brother, mate (form of address)</li><li>gangster, mobster (member of a <i>братва́</i> (bratvá))</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>брат</i> (brat), influenced by Italian <i>fratello</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brother, bro, old fellow, dear friend (<i>friendly or condescendingly, also as a form of address</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bràtьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>брат</i> (brat): little brother</li><li>used as a friendly form of address to a man or boy</li></ol><p><i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) <i>bratina</i>, a traditional Russian wooden or copper pitcher for dispensing beer or kvass at festivals or large dinners, wine bowl, loving cup</li></ol><p>From <i>брат</i> (brat, “brother”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), meaning ‘brethren's vessel’.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little brother</li><li>buddy</li></ol><p><i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Christian usage) alternative form of <i>бра́тья</i> (brátʹja)</li><li>community of monks</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fraternity, brotherhood, society</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>братиꙗ</i> (bratija), Doublet of <i>бра́тья</i> (brátʹja). By surface analysis, <i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>ия</i> (ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brother’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brother’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brother, man, bro</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mobster</li></ol><p><i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fratricidal</li></ol><p><i>братоуби́йство</i> (bratoubíjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fratricide (killing of one's brother)</li></ol><p><i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fratricide, killer of one's brother</li></ol><p>From <i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass grave</li><li>(politics,&#32;slang) a bill in the Russian State Duma co-sponsored by a very large number of deputies, often to obscure individual responsibility or to demonstrate collective support.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brotherly, fraternal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bratsk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bràtьskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherhood, fraternity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bratrьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>брат</i> (brat, “brother”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brother (sibling)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a form of address to a man</li></ol><p><i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>брат</i> (brat).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete form of <i>бра́тина</i> (brátina)</li></ol><p>From <i>брат</i> (brat) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja); see <i>бра́тина</i> (brátina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бьрати</i> (bĭrati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьràti</i> (“to take”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bírāˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>, whence English <i>bear</i> and Persian بردن (bordan, “to take, to carry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to take the bull by the horns</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to receive exclusive rights to something in exchange for money</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to claim, to take exclusive control over (a project, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the liberty of doing something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;informal) to pull out of thin air, to make up, to pull out of one's ass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull oneself together, to get oneself together, to get it together, to get a grip, to get one's act together, to get ahold of oneself</li></ol><p>Literally, “to take oneself into one's hands”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take something with hands, to take (mutually, together) (e.g. each other's hands)</li><li>to set about; to undertake [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘a task’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appear, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>брать</i> (bratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бра́тия</i> (brátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(web) browser</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>browser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Browning <i>(automatic pistol)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) browningia (<i>Browningia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Browningia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) brachiation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant in the genus <i>Brachylaena</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Brachylaena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matrimonial, marital, conjugal</li></ol><p><i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) bride and groom, couple, persons entering upon marriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bride and groom, couple, persons entering upon marriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) food, sustenance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>брашьно</i> (brašĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;boršьno</i>. Doublet of <i>бо́рошно</i> (bórošno), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of logs, timbered</li></ol><p><i>бревно́</i> (brevnó) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>log, beam (trunk of dead tree, cleared of branches)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) lumber</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) beam, balance beam</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a stupid, heartless, emotionless man</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) dead fish, cold fish (a very passive sexual partner)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бьрьвьно</i> (bĭrĭvĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrvьno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) bank, shore, coast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брѣгъ</i> (brěgŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>брѣгъ</i> (brěgŭ) / <i>ⰱⱃⱑⰳⱏ</i> (brěgŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bergъ</i>. Akin to the inherited doublet <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Breguet watch <i>(vintage Swiss pocket watch renowned for its accuracy...named for Abraham-Louis Breguet)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delirium, ravings</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) nonsense, baloney</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) gibberish</li></ol><p>Related to <i>брести́</i> (brestí).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>заблужде́ние</i> (zabluždénije) (akin to <i>блужда́ть</i> (bluždátʹ)), <i>сумасше́ствие</i> (sumasšéstvije) (akin to <i>ше́ствовать</i> (šéstvovatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) hogwash, bullshit</li></ol><p>Literally, “delirium of a gray mare”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) small dragnet, seine (for use in shallow water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be delirious, to talk deliriously</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to say nonsense</li><li>to rave, to be mad about</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brediti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsense, fantasies, ravings</li></ol><p><i>бред</i> (bred) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), plural</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) dragnet, seine</li></ol><p><i>бре́день</i> (brédenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delirious</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) crazy, insane (<i>plan, idea, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бред</i> (bred) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Leonid Brezhnev</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Brezhnev era</li></ol><p><i>Бре́жнев</i> (Bréžnev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fastidious/squeamish (<i>about</i>)</li><li>to disdain, to shrink (<i>from</i>) (<i>usually negative</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брѣзгати</i> (brězgati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brězgati</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бре́зкати</i> (brézkaty, “to disdain, to be disgusted”), also <i>бре́згати</i> (brézhaty). Proto-Slavic verb derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brězgъ</i> (compare Czech <i>břesk</i> (“tart taste”), Polish <i>brzazg</i> (“tart taste”), dialectal Ukrainian <i>бреск</i> (bresk, “dampness, mold”)), probably cognate with Norwegian <i>brisk</i> (“bitter taste”), <i>brisken</i> (“bitter, tart”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with disgust</li></ol><p><i>брезгли́вый</i> (brezglívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeamishness</li></ol><p><i>брезгли́вый</i> (brezglívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>squeamish, fastidious</li></ol><p><i>бре́згать</i> (brézgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fastidious/squeamish <i>(about)</i></li><li><i>(usually negative)</i> to disdain, to shrink <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>бре́згать</i> (brézgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarpaulin, canvas</li></ol><p>From the earlier form <i>презенинг</i> (prezening), from Dutch <i>presenning</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) canvas, tarpaulin</li></ol><p><i>брезе́нт</i> (brezént) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to dawn</li><li>to flicker faintly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to dawn, to emerge, to begin to manifest (<i>of hope, a thought, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brěždžiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>брезг</i> (brezg, “dawn”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakcore (music genre)</li></ol><p>From English <i>breakcore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) brainstorming</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>brainstorming</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthodontics</i>) a bracket</li><li>(orthodontics,&#32;in the plural) synonym of <i>бре́кет-систе́ма</i> (bréket-sistéma), braces <i>(for correcting teeth)</i></li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>bracket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key fob</li><li>charm, fob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>breloque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden, charge, load</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брѣмѧ</i> (brěmę), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>брѣмѧ</i> (brěmę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bermę</i> (“load, burden”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-men-</i>. Compare the inherited doublet <i>бере́мя</i> (berémja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brand (a specific product, service, or provider distinguished by symbolic identity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>brand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>marketing</i>) brand book</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>brand book</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brandy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>brandy</i> (akin to <i>bren</i>, <i>burn</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>гори́лка</i> (gorílka) (akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) caducity, frailty, transience</li></ol><p><i>бре́нный</i> (brénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) perishable, fleeting, mortal, frail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>брьньнъ</i> (brĭnĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brьnьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jingle, to clink, to chink (to make a noise of metal or glass clattering against itself)</li><li>to strum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bręčati</i>. Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plod, to lag</li><li>to stroll</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брести</i> (bresti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brestì</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Breton <i>person</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Breton</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Breton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a person who deliberately provokes duels; a <i>bretteur</i></li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial,&#32;by extension) troublemaker, rowdy, tough</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bretteur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yelp, to bark</li><li>to lie, to tell lies</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brexati</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Polish <i>brechać</i>, Slovak <i>brechať</i> and Ukrainian <i>бреха́ти</i> (brexáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;usually&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) liar, bullshitter</li></ol><p>From <i>бреха́ть</i> (brexátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo), compare Ukrainian <i>брехло́</i> (brexló), Belarusian <i>брахло́</i> (braxló).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lie (untruth)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rigmarole (nonsense talk)</li></ol><p><i>бреха́ть</i> (brexátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) liar</li></ol><p>From <i>бреха́ть</i> (brexátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breach, gap; break, tear</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>brèche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>brie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brig</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>brig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crew, team, gang (group of people employed to work together on a common task)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) brigade</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) gang, crew (group of criminals)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brigade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brigadier</li><li>foreman, captain (the leader of a crew of workers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brigadier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) team</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) brigade</li></ol><p><i>брига́да</i> (brigáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brigantine <i>(armor)</i></li><li>brigantine <i>(ship)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) bridge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeze (either sea breeze or land breeze)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from some Western European language, most likely from English <i>breeze</i>. Further etymology unknown.</p><p>Initially attested in 1718 in form <i>бри́зами</i> (brízami) in the context of names of different kinds of winds in Portugal. This instrumental plural form is likely not from modern spelling <i>бриз</i>&nbsp;m (briz), but from an exact rendering of Portuguese <i>brisa</i>, which would be <i>&#42;бриза</i>&nbsp;f (&#42;briza) (both have identical case forms in plural).</p><p>Judging by the lack of further attestations in the 18th century, the term didn't settle in the language until 19th century (and from a different source).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brisance</li></ol><p><i>бриза́нтный</i> (brizántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explosive, brisant (<i>of weapons</i>)</li></ol><p>French <i>brisant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briquette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>briquette</i>&nbsp;f.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) briquette</li></ol><p><i>брике́т</i> (brikét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(cut)</i> diamond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diamond</li></ol><p><i>бриллиа́нт</i> (brilliánt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) <i>Entimus imperialis</i>, a species of highly iridescent weevil.</li></ol><p><i>бриллиа́нтовый</i> (brilliántovyj, “diamond”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>жу́к</i> (žúk, “beetle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>бриллиа́нт</i> (brilliánt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any moss of the genus <i>Bryoerythrophyllum</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Bryoerythrophyllum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bristolian, Bristol</li></ol><p><i>Бри́сто́ль</i> (Brístólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briton, Brit <i>(citizen, resident)</i></li></ol><p><i>Брита́ния</i> (Británija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>British</li></ol><p><i>Брита́ния</i> (Británija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor <i>(also figurative)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;britva</i>, from <i>&#42;briti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tva</i>. By surface analysis, <i>брить</i> (britʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Occam's razor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) razor; shaving</li></ol><p><i>бри́тва</i> (brítva, “razor”) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaven-headed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person with a shaven head</li><li>skinhead (extremist, white-supremacist)</li></ol><p><i>бри́тый</i> (brítyj, “shaven”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, “-ed”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briton (member of an ancient tribe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean-shaven</li><li>shaved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shave oneself</li></ol><p><i>брить</i> (britʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shave, shaving</li></ol><p><i>брить</i> (britʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>briefing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;neologism) bro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>бровь</i> (brovʹ): (small) eyebrow</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) nickname of a pet dog</li><li>edge of a road, curb</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) brow</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) track shoulder</li></ol><p><i>бровь</i> (brovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₃bʰrúHs</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бръвь</i> (brŭvĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bry</i>. Cognates include Middle Persian (blwk' /⁠brūg⁠/), Northern Kurdish <i>birû</i>, Ossetian <i>ӕрфыг</i> (ærfyg), Sanskrit <i>भ्रू</i> (bhrū), Old English <i>brū</i> (and thus also English <i>brow</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ford</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brodъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;video games) walking simulator</li></ol><p><i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ, “to wander”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, to roam, to ramble</li><li>to stroll</li><li>to ferment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бродити</i> (broditi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brodìti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrodʰ-eye-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰredʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tramp, vagabond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tramp, vagabond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wander, to ramble</li></ol><p><i>бродя́га</i> (brodjága) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be on the road, to be a tramp, to vagabond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering, itinerant, nomadic</li><li>stray (<i>animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermentation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ferment, rumblings</li></ol><p><i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brocade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brokat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>broker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broccoli (a plant of the cabbage family, Brassicaceae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bromine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mixture with bromide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) bromide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bromic</li></ol><p><i>бром</i> (brom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bromic</li></ol><p><i>бром</i> (brom) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armored car</li></ol><p>First attested in 1923 (see citations). From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ), calque of German <i>Panzerwagen</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) cupola</li></ol><p>First attested in 1911 [see citations]. <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja), calque of German <i>Panzerturm</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armour-piercing</li></ol><p>First attested in 1889 [see citations]. <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>panzerbrechend</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armored car</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) armored train</li></ol><p><i>бронево́й</i> (bronevój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) armor</li><li>armored (clad or equipped with arms or armor)</li></ol><p><i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Броневой)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Bronevoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulletproof vest</li></ol><p>From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>жиле́т</i> (žilét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) armored gunboat</li></ol><p><i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ка́тер</i> (káter)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;inanimate) battleship (pre-dreadnought type), ironclad</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) armadillo</li></ol><p>Attested 1860s. From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), literally “armor-bearer”, an inexact calque of French <i>cuirassé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;nautical,&#32;dated) armored, armor-plated, ironclad</li></ol><p>From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), literally “armor-bearing”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;historical) protected, having an armored deck</li></ol><p>From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>па́лубный</i> (pálubnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armoured train</li></ol><p>From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>по́езд</i> (pójezd), a calque of German <i>Panzerzug</i>, according to Arapova.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armour</li></ol><p>First attested in 1931. <i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>си́ла</i> (síla), calque of German <i>Panzerkräfte</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulletproof glass, bullet-resistant glass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) armored troops, armoured troops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armored</li></ol><p><i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>танк</i> (tank) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) armored equipment, armored vehicles</li></ol><p><i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>те́хника</i> (téxnika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armored carrier, armoured personnel carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>броня́</i> (bronjá, “armor”) and <i>транспортёр</i> (transportjór, “carrier”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bronze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) flower chafer, a scarab beetle of the subfamily Cetoniinae.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Cetoniinae</li></ol><p><i>бро́нза</i> (brónza, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Protaetia speciosissima</i>, a species of flower chafer.</li></ol><p><i>бронзо́вка</i> (bronzóvka, “flower chafer”) +&lrm; <i>гла́дкий</i> (gládkij, “smooth”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bronze</li></ol><p><i>бро́нза</i> (brónza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservation, booking</li><li>armor (armor protection, the protective plating covering military equipment and installations, vehicles, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armored (clad or equipped with armor)</li><li>bulletproof, armor (describing a material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to book, to reserve</li><li>to armour</li></ol><p>From <i>броня</i> (bronja, “armor, booking”).</p><p>From <i>броня</i> (bronja, “armor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) armor</li></ol><p><i>броня́</i> (bronjá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bronchus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>βρόγχος</i> (brónkhos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bronchitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bronchoscopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>бро́ня</i> (brónja, “reservation, booking”)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>броня́</i> (bronjá, “armor”)</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) ripe ear of oat</li></ol><p>Related to obsolete <i>броный</i> (bronyj, “whitish”). Cognate with Old Chech <i>broný</i> (“white (horse)”), Czech <i>brůna</i> (“white horse”) and Sanskrit <i>ब्रध्न</i> (bradhna, “fallow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) armor, armour, armoring, shell</li><li>reservation, booking</li><li>a secured essential worker job providing deferment from conscription</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бръня</i> (brŭnja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъňa</i>, from Gothic <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌾𐍉</i> (brunjō, “breastplate”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunjǭ</i> (“breastplate”).</p><p>Developed from the sense of “armor” during the Soviet period. Sense development was from “armor” → “securing” → “reservation”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to drop</li><li>to send urgently</li><li>to abandon, to forsake</li><li>to give up, to quit, to leave off</li><li>to jilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъsati</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>throw</i> (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i>, whence also <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ), Latin <i>terō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fling <i>(oneself)</i>, to dash, to rush</li></ol><p><i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be plain as the nose on one's face, to stick out a mile, to jump out (to be conspicuous)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>sauter aux yeux</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) to fall on a grenade, to take one for the team (<i>of heroic self-sacrifice; often with the implication that the heroism brought little benefit</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to rush into the embrasure”. From wartime dispatches of a soldier heroically blocking a fortified enemy machine gun emplacement with his body.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw</li><li>to send urgently</li><li>to abandon, to forsake</li><li>to give up, to quit, to leave off</li><li>to jilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brъsiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to cast anchor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fling (oneself), to throw oneself on, to dash, to rush</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to jump down</li></ol><p><i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) to fall on a grenade, to take one for the team (<i>of heroic self-sacrifice; often with the implication that the heroism brought little benefit</i>)</li></ol><p>From wartime dispatches of a soldier heroically blocking a fortified enemy machine gun emplacement with his body.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, conspicuous, catchy</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) nimble, dexterous in hurling</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) swift, nimble, speedy</li></ol><p>From <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p><p>Compary typologically Czech <i>nápadný</i>; Bulgarian <i>биещ на очи</i> (biešt na oči), <i>броса́ться в глаза́</i> (brosátʹsja v glazá). Also compare <i>неотрази́мый</i> (neotrazímyj), Polish <i>rażący</i>; <i>захва́тывающий</i> (zaxvátyvajuščij), English <i>catchy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throw</li><li>rush, spurt</li></ol><p><i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(often preceded by <i>да</i> (da)) come on (don't say that / stop saying that)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>statistical mechanics</i>) Brownian motion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brooch</li></ol><p><i>брошь</i> (brošʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>бро́шкой</i> (bróškoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brooch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>broche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brochure, booklet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>brochure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>брошю́ра</i> (brošúra): (small) brochure, pamphlet, leaflet</li></ol><p><i>брошю́ра</i> (brošúra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brochure</li></ol><p><i>брошю́ра</i> (brošúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usually only in the expression&#58;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brüderschaft or Bruderschaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bruneian</li></ol><p><i>Бруне́й</i> (Brunɛ́j) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) brunnera (<i>Brunnera</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i><i>Brunnera</i></i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squared beam</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brusъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) bruschetta (grilled bread rubbed with garlic and topped with olive oil and salt)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>bruschetta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowberry, red whortleberry, lingonberry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brusьnica</i>, from <i>&#42;brusьnъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ica</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>brusnice</i>, Slovak <i>brusnica</i>, Polish <i>brusznica</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>brùsnica</i>, Slovene <i>brusníca</i>, Bulgarian <i>брусни́ца</i> (brusníca), and more distantly with Lithuanian <i>brùknė</i> and Latvian <i>brūklene</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cowberry, lingonberry</li></ol><p><i>брусни́ка</i> (brusníka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, ingot, slab</li><li>whetstone, hone</li></ol><p><i>брус</i> (brus) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar of soap, cake of soap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breastwork, parapet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Brustwehr</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>przedpiersie</i> (<i>**предпе́рсие</i>, <i>**предпе́рсник</i>, <i>**предпе́рсок</i>), Ukrainian <i>передгруддя</i> (peredhruddja) (<i>**предгру́дие</i>, <i>**предгру́дник</i>, <i>**предгру́док</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paving stone, cobble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pavement, road, etc. paved with such stones</li></ol><p><i>брусча́тый</i> (brusčátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beam</li><li>made of beams</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) paving stone</li><li>paved with paving stones, cobbled</li></ol><p><i>брус</i> (brus) +&lrm; <i>-ча́тый</i> (-čátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) the bars (apparatus on which certain gymnastic events are performed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brutal</li><li>displaying manliness</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>brutalis</i> (“savage, stupid”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gross (<i>of weight</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>brutto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>бры́знуть</i> (brýznutʹ)</li></ol><p><i>бры́з(г)нуть</i> (brýz(g)nutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle, to spray [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to splash, to splatter [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to shed, to exhale, to pour, to spout</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brỳzgati</i>. Of onomatopoeic origin. Compare Bulgarian <i>бръ́згам</i> (brǎ́zgam), Czech <i>brýzhati</i>, Polish <i>bryzgać</i>, Slovene <i>brízgati</i>, Ukrainian <i>бризгати</i> (bryzhaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splash; sputter; spray; (fine) drops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) mudflap, mudguard (flap behind a vehicle wheel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash, to splatter</li><li>to sprinkle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick by the hind legs (horse, bovine etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brykati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>брыка́ть</i> (brykátʹ, “to kick by the hind legs”)</li></ol><p><i>брыка́ть</i> (brykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick by the hind legs</li></ol><p><i>брыка́ть</i> (brykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick by the hind legs</li></ol><p><i>брыка́ться</i> (brykátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bryndza</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romanian <i>brânză</i> (“cheese”).</p><p>In 1937 Kochin erroneously attributed Russian <i>брынец</i> (brynec, “rice”) to bryndza and then was cited by Shansky and Anikin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bryndza</li></ol><p><i>бры́нза</i> (brýnza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>shoo! (to make a cat go away)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>бренд</i> (brɛnd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grumbler, grouch, griper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to growl, to grouch, to be peevish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swede, rutabaga (<i>Brassica napus</i>, sometimes <i>Brassica napus rapifera</i>, plant and vegetable)</li></ol><p>A regional wanderwort, see German <i>Wruke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rutabaga</li></ol><p><i>брю́ква</i> (brjúkva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pants, trousers</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>broek</i>. First attested in the eighteenth century, but became more popular a hundred years later. Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōks</i>, whence also English <i>breeches</i>, whence a doublet <i>бри́джи</i> (brídži). Further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰreg-</i> (whence also English <i>break</i>, Latin <i>frangō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell-bottoms (pants that are flared from the knee downwards)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) flared breeches</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male brunette; brunet (dark-haired male)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>brunet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>urban studies</i>) Brusselization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brussels sprouts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Brussels; Brusselian</li></ol><p><i>Брюссе́ль</i> (brjussélʹ, brjussɛ́lʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brussels lace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to knock up [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>] (to make pregnant)</li></ol><p><i>брюха́тый</i> (brjuxátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) paunchy, potbellied</li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) pregnant, knocked up</li></ol><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) belly</li><li>paunch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>брюхо</i> (brjuxo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;břuxo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gastropods</li></ol><p>From <i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of New Latin <i>Gastropoda</i>. First attested in 1810.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gastropodous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gastropod</li></ol><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>брю́ки</i> (brjúki, “pants, trousers”)</li></ol><p><i>брю́ки</i> (brjúki) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pants, trousers</li></ol><p><i>брю́ки</i> (brjúki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) peritoneum</li><li>flank steak</li></ol><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdomen (the posterior section of an arthropod's body)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo): belly</li></ol><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó)</p><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdominal cavity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abdominal (relating to the abdomen)</li></ol><p><i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clatter, to clang, to clank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bang down, to drop (let fall) with a bang/crash, to drop with a thud, to fling, to hurl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blab, to blurt out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brękati</i>. Cognate with <i>бря́кнуть</i> (brjáknutʹ), <i>бряца́ть</i> (brjacátʹ), <i>бренча́ть</i> (brenčátʹ), Russian Church Slavonic <i>брѧчѫ</i> (bręčǫ), <i>брѧцати</i> (bręcati). Various forms can be reconstructed for Proto-Slavic: <i>&#42;brękati</i>, <i>&#42;bręknǫti</i>, <i>&#42;bręcati</i>, <i>&#42;bręčati</i>. Onomatopoeic in origin. Per Vasmer, cognate with Lithuanian <i>bri̇́nkterėti</i> (“to crash”), <i>bri̇́nkt</i> (“bang!”), Sanskrit <i>भृङ्ग</i> (bhṛ́ṅga-), Sanskrit <i>भृङ्गा</i> (bhṛ́ṅgā, “large black bee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clatter, to clang, to clank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bang down, to drop (let fall) with a bang/crash, to drop with a thud, to fling, to hurl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blab, to blurt out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bręknǫti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;brínktei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bryansk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Брян(ск)</i> (Brjan(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clank, to rattle</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бревно́</i> (brevnó): (small) log/beam</li></ol><p><i>бревно́</i> (brevnó) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dog's barking</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lies, nonsense, empty chatter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tambourine</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) face, mug (often used in phrases involving hitting)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бубьнъ</i> (bubĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bǫbьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jingle bell</li></ol><p><i>бу́бен</i> (búben) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бубене́ц</i> (bubenéc): (small) jingle bell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diamonds (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♦)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>бу́бны</i> (búbny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bagel, bublik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) diamond</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>бу́бны</i> (búbny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to mutter</li><li>to drone on <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bǫbьniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diamonds (suit in card games)</li></ol><p><i>бу́бны</i> (búbny) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of diamonds (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diamonds (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♦)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>bubny</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (“bells in German playing cards”), plural of <i>buben</i>&nbsp;m (“drum”) cognate to the Russian <i>бу́бен</i> (búben, “tambourine”) calquing German <i>Schellen</i>, better known as <i>kule</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;pl (literally “bullets”) in Czech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) uncastrated bull</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bull (a large, strong man)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;buka</i>, compare Uzbek <i>buqa</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>буга́й</i> (buháj), Belarusian <i>буга́й</i> (buháj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bow-shaped device</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) bow collector (type of current collector used on trams, formerly much more than today)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) metal band around a spar to which the rigging is attached</li><li>(<i>skiing</i>) tow gripper (removable device for attaching to a ski tow cable)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) kangaroo bar</li></ol><li>(skiing,&#32;colloquial) ski tow, T-bar, J-bar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>beugel</i> or German <i>Bügel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boogie-woogie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boogie-woogie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) hill, hillock, knoll</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bump (protuberance)</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) big cheese, boss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bugrъ</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewgʰ-</i> (“to bend”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>бугі́р</i> (buhír), Lithuanian <i>baũgurs</i>.</p><p>Non-Slavic cognates include Sanskrit <i>भुग्न</i> (bhugna, “curved, crooked”), Latin <i>fugio</i> (“to flee, to hasten”), English <i>bow</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>буго́р</i> (bugór): hillock, mound; knob, protuberance; tubercle</li></ol><p><i>буго́р</i> (bugór) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bumpy; hilly; uneven</li></ol><p><i>буго́р</i> (bugór) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buddha</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Будда)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buddha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>बुद्ध</i> (buddha).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buddhism</li></ol><p><i>Бу́дда</i> (Búdda) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Buddhist</li></ol><p><i>Бу́дда</i> (Búdda) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buddhist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;relational) Buddhist</li></ol><p><i>будди́ст</i> (buddíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) if</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>cheers! (toast when drinking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarm clock</li></ol><p>From <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ, “to wake up, to awake, to waken”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>будилник</i> (budilnik), Serbo-Croatian <i>будилник</i>, root cognate with Czech <i>budík</i>, Polish <i>budzik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to stir up, to rouse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bouder</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake up, to awake, to waken</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buditi</i>. Causative of <i>бдеть</i> (bdetʹ). Cognate to Ancient Greek <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai), Sanskrit <i>बुद्ध</i> (buddha) (whence Russian <i>Бу́дда</i> (Búdda, “Buddha”)), German <i>bieten</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kennel, doghouse</li><li>booth, cabin, kiosk, box (very small building)</li><li>(<i>history</i>) small police facility (during pre-revolutionary Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́да</i> (búda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), the former of which has been displaced by the suffixed formation and is from Ukrainian <i>бу́да</i> (búda), from Polish <i>buda</i>, from Middle High German <i>buode</i> = German <i>Bude</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōdō</i> as English <i>booth</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weekday(s), working days</li><li>humdrum, colorless existence</li></ol><p>From the non-attested contraction of <i>&#42;будьнъ дьнь</i> (&#42;budĭnŭ dĭnĭ). Compare Bulgarian <i>бу́ддень</i> (búddenʹ), Polish <i>budny dzień</i>. See <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ) and <i>день</i> (denʹ) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ferial; normal (day, non-festival or weekend day)</li></ol><p><i>бу́дни</i> (búdni) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an ordinary, mundane, workaday manner</li></ol><p><i>бу́дничный</i> (búdničnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) weekday</li><li>day-to-day, dull, everyday, ordinary, mundane</li></ol><p><i>бу́дни</i> (búdni) +&lrm; <i>-ичный</i> (-ičnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to agitate, to disturb, to excite</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>раж</i> (raž) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бу́дка</i> (búdka)</li></ol><p><i>бу́дка</i> (búdka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as if, as though</li><li>allegedly, apparently, that (with some uncertainty)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>allegedly, supposedly</li><li>as if</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) I will have, I would like</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boudoir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boudoir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boudoir</li></ol><p><i>будуа́р</i> (buduár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the future</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) future tense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>future</li><li>next, coming, subsequent</li></ol><p><i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ущий</i> (-uščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) future (events to come, or one's expected success or fate)</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́дущий</i> (búduščij) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) please</li></ol><p>Literally, “be a friend”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;to a male) bless you, (US) gesundheit</li><li>(informal,&#32;to a male) goodbye, take care</li></ol><p>Literally, “be healthy”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal&#32;or&#32;to a group</i>) please, would you be so kind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal&#32;or&#32;to a group</i>) bless you, (US) gesundheit (said to someone who has sneezed)</li><li>(<i>formal&#32;or&#32;to a group</i>) goodbye, take care</li></ol><p>Literally, “be healthy”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;history) budyonovka, budenovka (a distinctive type of hat and an essential part of the Red Army uniform during the Russian Civil War and later)</li></ol><p>From Semyon Budyonny (Семён Будённый) + <i>-овка</i> (-ovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;rare,&#32;Northern) open, elevated cemetery or graveyard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iceboat, ice yacht</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>boeier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiled pork (a meat dish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noisy row, uproar, fuss</li><li>boza (a fermented, malted beverage made from millet, buckwheat, oats, or barley, popular in the Crimea and Caucasus. It has a thick consistency and a low alcohol content, usually around 4–6%, and has a slightly acidic sweet flavor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elder <i>(tree)</i></li><li>elderberry</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>буз</i> (buz), <i>боз</i> (boz), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troublemaker</li></ol><p>Popularly believed to be from <i>буза́</i> (buzá) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Buzuluk</li></ol><p><i>Бузулу́к</i> (Buzulúk) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buoy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>boei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffalo</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;byvolъ</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujьvolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) buffalo</li></ol><p><i>бу́йвол</i> (bújvol) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impetuous, wild, violent, vehement</li><li>unbridled, exuberant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rage, outrage, tumult, unruly behavior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бу́й(ный)</i> (búj(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, to rampage</li></ol><p><i>бу́йство</i> (bújstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buky</i>, borrowed from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōkijā</i> (“beech”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) boogieman/bogeyman</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any make-believe threat, boogieman/bogeyman</li><li>surly person, unsociable person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bug, insect</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone insignificant</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bǫkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>letter (a symbol in an alphabet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙋкꙑ</i> (buky), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buky</i>. Doublet of <i>бу́ки</i> (búki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>literally or word for word</li></ol><p><i>буква́льный</i> (bukválʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literal (exactly as stated&#59; read or understood without additional interpretation)</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) +&lrm; <i>-альный</i> (-alʹnyj), a calque of French <i>littéral</i>. First attested in 1780s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ABC book, primer</li></ol><p><i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>буквах</i> (bukvax).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) literal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop cap, versal</li><li>betony</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) a pseudohistorical alphabet based on Cyrillic used by Ynglist neopagans</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bukъvica</i>.</p><p>For sense 3, first coined by Russian neopagan esoteric A. Yu. Khinevich, founder of Ynglism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedant, dryasdust</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́ква</i> (búkva, “letter”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedantry, punctiliousness</li></ol><p><i>буквое́д</i> (bukvojéd) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) (a small) letter (<i>a symbol in an alphabet</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>букв</i> (bukv).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bouquet, bunch of flowers, posy</li><li>bouquet, aroma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>буке́т</i> (bukét): a (small) bouquet, a bunch (of flowers)</li></ol><p><i>буке́т</i> (bukét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>буке́т</i> (bukét, “bouquet”)</li></ol><p><i>буке́т</i> (bukét, “bouquet”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) name of the Cyrillic letter Б, б</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙋкꙑ</i> (buky). Doublet of <i>бу́ква</i> (búkva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookseller (a seller of used or old books), second-hand bookseller, antiquarian bookseller</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bouquiniste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(pornography,&#32;sex) bukkake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>打<rp>(</rp><rt>ぶ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>っ<ruby>掛<rp>(</rp><rt>か</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>け (<i>bukkake</i>, “bukkake”, literally “act of splashing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) old-fashioned agricultural implement for shallow plowing of soil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bucker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booklet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>booklet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;dated) curl of hair</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) ornament composed of a number of rings, sometimes with rosettes in the center</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boucle</i> (“loop; buckle; curl of hair”) in the mid-18th century, from Old French <i>boucle</i> (“buckle”), from Latin <i>buccula</i> (“cheek strap of a helmet”), diminutive of <i>bucca</i> (“puffed cheek; mouth”), of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookmaker, bookie (a person who calculates odds and accepts bets)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bookmaker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) (a small) letter (<i>a symbol in an alphabet</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бу́ква</i> (búkva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beech</li></ol><p><i>бук</i> (buk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>railways</i>) axlebox, journal box</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tug, tugboat, towboat</li><li>tow, towrope, towline</li></ol><p>First attested in 1712. Borrowed from German <i>Bugsierer</i>, from <i>bugsieren</i>, from Dutch <i>boegseren</i>, from Portuguese <i>puxar</i>, from Latin <i>pulsāre</i>.</p><p>Unrelated to Russian <i>бу́кса</i> (búksa). The relation with <i>буксова́ть</i> (buksovátʹ) is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>towing (used for towing)</li></ol><p><i>букси́р</i> (buksír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tow, to tug (to pull something behind one using a line or chain)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1712. Borrowed from Dutch <i>boegseren</i>, from Portuguese <i>puxar</i>, from Latin <i>pulsāre</i>. By surface analysis, <i>букси́р</i> (buksír) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) or <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p><p>Unrelated to Russian <i>бу́кса</i> (búksa). The relation with <i>буксова́ть</i> (buksovátʹ) is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tow truck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to skid, to spin around without traction (<i>of a car or its wheels</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to stall, to be in a gridlock</li></ol><p>Uncertain:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mace (heavy fighting club)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) juggling club</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>buława</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin</li></ol><p>From <i>булава́</i> (bulavá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pin</li></ol><p><i>була́вка</i> (bulávka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulat, wootz steel, Damascus steel</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) sword, blade</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Persian پولاد (pulâd).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Булат)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bulat, from Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian پولاد (pulâd, “steel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;poetic,&#32;relational) bulat</li></ol><p><i>була́т</i> (bulát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bulgakov; Bulgakov's (referring to anyone with this name, especially Mikhail Bulgakov, Russian writer)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bulgakovo (name of several Russian localities)</li></ol><p><i>Булга́ков</i> (Bulgákov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Boolean</li></ol><p>From "Буль" (George Boole).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boolean domain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll of bread, small loaf</li><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) white bread</li><li>(usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;colloquial) buttocks, buns</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Polish <i>bułka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull (<i>document</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) bullying</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bullying</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bun, roll</li></ol><p><i>бу́лка</i> (búlka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bakery, baker's</li></ol><p><i>бу́лка</i> (búlka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker, someone who makes bread and rolls</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́лочный</i> (búločnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) or <i>бу́лочка</i> (búločka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) roll of bread</li></ol><p><i>бу́лка</i> (búlka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobble, cobblestone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>The sound of liquid bubbling or being poured.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) An encrusted table.</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p><p>After André Charles Boulle</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>glug-glug (<i>the sound of liquid bubbling or gurgling</i>)</li><li>The sound of bubbles above an object that sunk and disappeared beneath the water.</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) tuber</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in&#32;reference&#32;to&#32;Belarus</i>) potato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>бу́льба</i> (búlʹba) and Belarusian <i>бу́льба</i> (búlʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnic slur</i>) a Belarusian</li></ol><p>From Belarusian <i>бу́льба</i> (búlʹba, “potato(es)”) + suffix <i>-аш</i> (-aš), referring to the traditionally important role of potatoes in Belarusian cuisine and agriculture; compare to English <i>potato eater</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bulbourethral gland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulbourethral</li></ol><p>Possibly from English <i>bulbourethral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulbophyllum (<i>Bulbophyllum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Bulbophyllum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boulevard, avenue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boulevard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) public walkway, promenade</li><li>tabloid, cheap and sensational</li></ol><p><i>бульва́р</i> (bulʹvár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulldog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bulldog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bulldog</li></ol><p><i>бульдо́г</i> (bulʹdóg) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vehicles</i>) bulldozer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bulldozer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gurgling, gurgle</li></ol><p><i>бу́лькать</i> (búlʹkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gurgle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broth, bouillon (water in which food (meat or vegetable etc) has been boiled)</li><li>clear soup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bouillon</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>bulliō</i> from Latin <i>bulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>media</i>) sensation, racket</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) boom</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thud, crash</li></ol><p>Anglicism (English <i>boom</i>).</p><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paper</li><li>document</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) cotton wool</li><li>(rare,&#32;slang) hundred-ruble banknote</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Vasmer hypothesizes the existence of an earlier form, Old East Slavic <i>&#42;бубага</i> (&#42;bubaga), from which <i>бума́га</i> (bumága) formed through dissimilation, or perhaps a back-formation from <i>бума́жный</i> (bumážnyj). Believed to be ultimately from Middle Persian <i>pmbk'</i> (“cotton”) through the intermediary of some Mediterranean term for cotton wool and/or cloth (most probably Italian <i>bambagia</i>), since the first attestations of the root in the 15th and 16th centuries are mostly with a now obsolete sense, the only remnant of which in modern active vocabulary is хлопчатобума́жный (xlopčatobumážnyj, “made from cotton”). Paper was first imported into Russia from Italian states and the Byzantine Empire in the 14th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribbler (one who scribbles; hasty or untalented writer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton mill</li></ol><p>First attested in 1838 (see citations). <i>бума́га</i> (bumága) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пряди́льня</i> (prjadílʹnja), calque of German <i>Baumwollspinnerei</i> [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бума́га</i> (bumága)</li></ol><p>From <i>бума́га</i> (bumága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallet</li><li>papermaker, worker in the paper industry</li></ol><p><i>бума́га</i> (bumága) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) paper</li><li>(relational,&#32;figuratively) paperwork</li><li>(relational,&#32;dated) cotton, cotton wool (made of cotton fibers or cloth)</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>бума́га</i> (bumága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Vasmer hypothesizes that <i>бума́га</i> (bumága) might be a back-formation, and <i>бума́жный</i> (bumážnyj) the original term derived from Italian <i>bambagino</i> (“made of cotton wool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paper tiger (someone or something that looks powerful but is actually weak)</li></ol><p>Calque of Chinese <i>紙老虎</i>&nbsp;/ <i>纸老虎</i> (zhǐlǎohǔ), Popularized by Mao Zedong.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Beamer (a BMW car)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) boomer; an old person or a member of the baby boomer generation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boomerang</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bungalow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bungalow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a krait; a venomous elapid snake of the genus <i>Bungarus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Bungarus</i>&nbsp;m, itself from Telugu <i>బంగారు</i> (baṅgāru, “golden”, adjective).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bunker, underground shelter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) Vladimir Putin</li></ol><p>Literally, “bunker grandpa”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riot (a spontaneous, often disorganized uprising)</li><li>bale, packet, bundle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bund</i> (“alliance”) via Polish <i>bunt</i> (“uniting for joint action against someone”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bund</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutiny on deck</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) any setting in which adherence to authority is expected but defied</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rebellious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebeldom</li></ol><p>From <i>бунта́рь</i> (buntárʹ, “rebel”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provocateur, someone who "rebels" against prevailing tastes, archetypes, or rules</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) anarchistic rebel of the 1870s and 1880s in Imperial Russia</li></ol><p><i>бунт</i> (bunt) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to revolt, to rebel</li><li>to incite a revolt</li></ol><p><i>бунт</i> (bunt) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebel, to mutiny</li></ol><p><i>бунтова́ть</i> (buntovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rioter</li></ol><p><i>бунтова́ть</i> (buntovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щи́к</i> (-ščík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) buprestid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Buprestidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boer (white South African person of Dutch, or by extension other non-Anglo-Saxon, descent)</li><li>bore, drill, auger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Afrikaans <i>boer</i> (“farmer”).</p><p>Borrowed from a Germanic language, probably Dutch <i>boor</i> (“drill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borax</li></ol><p>From Persian بوره (bure, “borax”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drill, auger (carpenter's tool)</li></ol><p>From a Kipchak source. See Bashkir <i>бырау</i> (bıraw) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бура́в</i> (buráv): little screw, gimlet</li></ol><p><i>бура́в</i> (buráv) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) beet, beetroot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blizzard, snowstorm (<i>especially in the steppe</i>)</li><li>Buran (Soviet spaceplane)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic; compare Bashkir <i>буран</i> (buran), Crimean Tatar <i>boran</i>, Kazakh <i>боран</i> (boran), Kyrgyz <i>бороон</i> (boroon), Tatar <i>буран</i> (buran), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) boraginaceous; related to, characteristic of, or belonging to the borage family (Boraginaceae).</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) A plant of the family Boraginaceae.</li></ol><p>From <i>бура́чник</i> (buráčnik, “borage”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, relational-adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bourbon</li></ol><p>French <i>bourbon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) burger (hamburger)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) An American.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>burger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) burger</li></ol><p><i>бу́ргер</i> (búrger) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burgomaster</li><li>glaucous gull</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>burgemeester</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burgundian (member of a Germanic tribe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dishwater, slops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterskin</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing, from Turkish <i>bardak</i> (“jug, drinking vessel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petrel (type of bird)</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́ря</i> (búrja, “storm”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ве́стник</i> (véstnik, “messenger”); compare <i>storm petrel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) windfall (<i>of trees</i>); deadfall resulting from a storm</li></ol><p>First attested in 1847. Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buřelomъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бу́ря</i> (búrja) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ), calque of German <i>Windbruch</i>, whence also <i>ветроло́м</i> (vetrolóm) [Arapova].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drilling, boring (e.g. of wells, mines)</li></ol><p><i>бури́ть</i> (burítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bourgeois (individual member of bourgeoisie)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bourgeois</i>; doublet of <i>буржуй</i> (buržuj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bourgeoisie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bourgeoisie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bourgeois</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bourgeois</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bourgeois</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bourgeois</i>; doublet of <i>буржуа</i> (buržua).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial,&#32;inanimate) a small metal stove used for heating</li></ol><p><i>буржу́й</i> (buržúj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bouts-rimés</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bout-rimé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore (a hole)</li></ol><p>From <i>бур</i> (bur), from Middle High German <i>boren</i>, cf German <i>bohren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a liver chestnut horse (a horse with a dark reddish or reddish-brown coat)</li><li>burka (male garment from the Caucasus)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) a felt boot with a leather sole</li><li>blasthole (the hole into which an explosive charge is inserted for blasting rock)</li></ol><p><i>бу́рый</i> (búryj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Disputed.</p><p><i>бури́ть</i> (burítʹ, “to bore, to drill”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Saint Petersburg,&#32;obsolete) carrot</li></ol><p>Uncertain; compare Latvian <i>burkāns</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to growl out, to bark out, to say something angrily or with dissatisfaction</li><li>to mumble out, to say something softly and unclearly</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бу́ркати</i> (búrkaty), <i>буркота́ти</i> (burkotáty), Slovene <i>búrkati</i>, Polish <i>burknąć</i>. Possibly onomatopoeic in origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to growl out, to bark out, to say something angrily or with dissatisfaction</li><li>to mumble out, to say something softly and unclearly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) burlak (in old Russia, a man employed in dragging ships upstream)</li></ol><p>From Middle Mongol.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burlesque</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) burlesque</li></ol><p><i>бурле́ск</i> (burlésk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seethe, to boil up</li><li><i>(people)</i> to boil up</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) big pearl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>violently</li></ol><p><i>бу́рный</i> (búrnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stormy, turbulent, violent</li><li>intensive, strong, loud, vivid</li></ol><p><i>бу́ря</i> (búrja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brownish</li></ol><p><i>бу́рый</i> (búryj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boring, drilling</li></ol><p><i>бур</i> (bur) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common shrew; sorex</li></ol><p><i>бу́рый</i> (búryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), such color because of high iron.</p><p>Compare Bulgarian <i>кафявозъбка</i> (kafjavozǎbka) (&lt; <i>кафяв</i> (kafjav)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bourrée</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bourrée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burrito</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>burrito</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) clamp (compact pile of agricultural produce used for temporary storage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaker, surf</li><li>bow wave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Burundian</li></ol><p><i>Буру́нди</i> (Burúndi) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chipmunk</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing, to be compared with Tatar <i>борындык</i> (borındıq, “chipmunk”) or, less likely, a reshaping of Eastern Mari <i>у́рымдо</i> (úrymdo, “chipmunk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chipmunk</li></ol><p><i>бурунду́к</i> (burundúk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chipmunk</li></ol><p><i>бурунду́к</i> (burundúk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to rumble (to make a low growling or rumbling stomach noise)</li><li>to grumble, to complain</li><li>to mutter, to mumble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grayish-brown, dark reddish-brown</li><li>brown (<i>of bears, coal</i>)</li><li>liver chestnut (<i>of horses</i>)</li></ol><p>A post-Mongol invasion – attested first in the Praying of Daniel the Immured – Turkic borrowing of a form <i>bōr</i> or <i>būr</i> applied to bay horses, found in Chagatai بور (bōr, “bay horse”), Turkish <i>mor</i> / مور (“violet”) or <i>boz</i> / بوز (“gray”) reconstructed from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;boŕ</i>, also in Mongolic, Buryat <i>боро</i> (boro, “gray”), etc., also in Tungusic, Evenki <i>боро</i> (boro, “gray”), etc., ultimately of Iranian origin equivalent to Middle Persian (bwl /⁠bōr⁠/, “reddish-brown, bay”), Persian بور (bôr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brown bear (<i>Ursus arctos</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>бу́рый</i> (búryj, “brown”) +&lrm; <i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ, “bear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild grass (steppe); (tall) weeds</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;burьjanъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бур'ян</i> (burʺjan), Bulgarian <i>бурен</i> (buren), Polish <i>burzan</i> and Slovak <i>burina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) storm, tempest (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buřa</i>, akin to Bulgarian <i>бу́ря</i> (búrja), Slovene <i>burja</i>, Polish <i>burza</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>бура</i>/<i>bura</i>, Slovak <i>búrka</i> (“tempest”) and <i>búrať</i> (“to crush”). Non-Slavic cognates include Old Norse <i>byrr</i> (“fair wind”), Latin <i>furō</i> (“to rage, rave”), Sanskrit <i>भुरति</i> (bhurati, “stir, palpitate”), Persian بوران (burân, “storm”), باران (bârân, “rain”). Compare also Turkish <i>bora</i> (“squall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempest in a teapot (big fuss made in a small context)</li></ol><p>Literally, “storm in a glass of water”. Compare French <i>tempête dans un verre d'eau</i> and German <i>Sturm im Wasserglas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) beetroot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buryat (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Buryat <i>буряад</i> (burjaad).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Buryat, Buryatian</li></ol><p><i>буря́т</i> (burját) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) small precipitation</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a drunk man</li><li>(regional,&#32;especially&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Germany) a bus, sometimes a minibus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bushido</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>武士道</i> (ぶしどう, bushidō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bead (pierced small round object)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>бу́сина</i> (búsina): small bead</li></ol><p><i>бу́сина</i> (búsina) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compass</li><li>(<i>military</i>) aiming circle</li><li>(<i>geodesy</i>) circle, azimuth circle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boussole</i>, itself from Italian <i>bussola</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>epigraphy</i>) boustrophedon (text written such that its direction alternates on each line, resulting in a continuous stream of characters spanning multiple lines; frequently also involving the corresponding alternation of letter directionality using mirrored letters, indicating the direction of the text at any given point)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>βουστροφηδόν</i> (boustrophēdón), in reference to the back-and-forth course traced by boustrophedon text resembling the path taken by an ox ploughing a field.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>epigraphy</i>) boustrophedonically (as a boustrophedon or in a boustrophedonic manner, such that the text directionality alternates on each line, resulting in a continuous stream of characters spanning multiple lines)</li></ol><p>From <i>бустрофедо́н</i> (bustrofedón) +&lrm; <i>-и́чески</i> (-íčeski, adverbial suffix), from Ancient Greek <i>βουστροφηδόν</i> (boustrophēdón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;epigraphy) boustrophedonic (written as a boustrophedon, such that the text directionality alternates on each line, resulting in a continuous stream of characters spanning multiple lines)</li></ol><p>From <i>бустрофедо́н</i> (bustrofedón) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij), from Ancient Greek <i>βουστροφηδόν</i> (boustrophēdón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beads</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubble, quarry-stone</li><li>large oak barrel for aging and storing wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) butane</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Бутан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bhutan (a country in South Asia, in the Himalayas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bhutanese</li></ol><p><i>Бута́н</i> (Bután) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) mockup</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sham, fake</li></ol><p>Italian <i>buttafuori</i> (“assistant director, bouncer”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija), from Italian <i>buttare</i> (“to throw”) + Italian <i>fuori</i> (“out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;theater) mockup</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sham, fake</li></ol><p><i>бутафо́рия</i> (butafórija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) sandwich, especially an open sandwich</li></ol><p>Contraction of бутерброд</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterbrot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Butterbrot</i> (“open sandwich, slice of bread with butter”, literally “butter bread”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandwich bar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) butterbrot</li></ol><p><i>бутербро́д</i> (buterbród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бу̀ти-дэ́нс</i> (bùti-dɛ́ns, “twerking”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>booty dance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>booty shake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buthid (scorpion of the Buthidae)</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Buthidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boutique</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) sandwich</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>boutique</i>. Doublet of <i>апте́ка</i> (aptéka).</p><p>From <i>бутер</i> (buter, “sandwich”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, “diminutive suffix”). The first element is itself an informal shortening of бутерброд (buterbrod), with the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boutique</li></ol><p><i>бути́к</i> (butík) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bottle (to seal (a liquid) into a bottle for later consumption)</li><li>to butylate (to react so as to introduce one or more butyl groups into a molecule)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butyl rubber (a synthetic rubber made by the polymerization of isobutene and isoprene)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>бути́л</i> (butíl) +&lrm; <i>каучу́к</i> (kaučúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butyrate, butanoate</li><li>sodium oxybate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(flower)</i> bud</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bouton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) sport boot, usually with hard leather</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>boots</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>butelka</i>, from French <i>bouteille</i> (“bottle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka): a (small) bottle</li><li>spin the bottle</li></ol><p><i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bottle collector (an impoverished person who collects discarded bottles and sells them to recycling facilities)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) bottle maker, bottle blower</li></ol><p><i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka, “bottle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;inanimate) bottle rack, wine rack, bottle cabinet</li></ol><p><i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka, “bottle”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bottle</li></ol><p><i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a large bottle; carboy, demijohn, flask</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bouteille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Butyrsky (district in Moscow, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>Буты́рка</i> (Butýrka, “(name of ancient village)”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffer</li><li>(automotive,&#32;dated) bumper (mechanical device to absorb impact)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) boob</li></ol><p>From English <i>buffer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) buffer</li></ol><p><i>бу́фер</i> (búfer) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sideboard, cupboard</li><li>snack bar, lunchroom, refreshment room, buffet</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) larder, pantry (a room for storing food and dishes and preparing snacks and tea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Büfett</i>, from French <i>buffet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barman, bartender</li></ol><p><i>буфе́т</i> (bufét) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;derogatory) clown, buffoon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slapstick (a style of humor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) slapstick (a style of humor)</li></ol><p><i>буффона́да</i> (buffonáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) buffoonery</li></ol><p><i>буффо́н</i> (buffón) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thud (sound of a dull, forceful impact)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread loaf</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) UAZ-452 four-wheel drive van</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>бухон</i> (buxon, “a kind of bread, a sour flatbread”), a reformulation of a borrowing from Polish <i>bochen</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>focācium</i> (whence multiple descendants), further from Latin <i>focus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucharestian (native or inhabitant of Bucharest) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bucharestian, Bucharest (of, from or pertaining to Bucharest)</li></ol><p>From <i>Бухаре́ст</i> (Buxarést) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Bukharan</li><li>citizen of Bukhara</li><li>Bukharan Jew</li></ol><p><i>Бухара́</i> (Buxará) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bukharan (relating to the city of Bukhara)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Bukharan (relating to the Bukharan ethnic group or their language)</li><li>Bukharan (relating to the Bukharan Jews or their language)</li></ol><p><i>Бухара́</i> (Buxará) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thump, to bang</li><li>to thunder, to thud (of cannons)</li><li>to let fall with a thud</li><li>to blurt out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink, to booze, to tipple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;buxati</i>, originally onomatopoeic.</p><p>According to Vladimir Dal it is related to the homograph <i>бу́хать</i> (búxatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accountant, book-keeper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Buchhalter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookkeeping</li><li>accounting department</li></ol><p>From <i>бухга́лтер</i> (bugálter, buhálter) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account book (a book in which accounts are kept)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bookkeeping</li></ol><p><i>бухга́лтер</i> (bugálter, buhálter) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) diminutive of <i>бухло́</i> (buxló): a small liquor</li></ol><p><i>бухло́</i> (buxló) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective noun) booze, alcoholic drinks</li></ol><p><i>буха́ть</i> (buxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regionalism) see брюква.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thump, to bang</li><li>to thunder, to thud (<i>of cannons</i>)</li><li>to let fall with a thud</li><li>to blurt out</li><li>to swell up, to swell</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) to booze, to drink alcohol</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to fling oneself, to fall to one's knees</li></ol><p><i>бу́хать</i> (búxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p><p>Same as 1.</p><p><i>буха́ть</i> (buxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pissed, drunk</li></ol><p><i>буха́ть</i> (buxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ой</i> (-oj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bay, inlet, cove</li><li>(<i>originally&#32;nautical</i>) coil (<i>of rope, wire, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Bucht</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>bocht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to complain</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) to murmur</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to speak</li><li>(dialect,&#32;slang) to babble</li><li>(<i>dialect</i>) to chatter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turmoil, commotion, disturbance</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Буча)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucha (a city, the administrative centre of Bucha Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>ellipsis of <i>Резня в Буче</i> (Reznja v Buče).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, storm</li></ol><p>Derived from either <i>бу́йный</i> (bújnyj, “wild, unruly”) or <i>бу́хнуть</i> (búxnutʹ, “to beat heavily”). Compare Polish <i>buszować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bushel (dry measure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>bushel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) pea coat</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a warm uniform jacket (<i>worn by soldiers, construction workers, prisoners, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Hinze hypothesized that the term is an alteration of German <i>Buschurunk</i> (“sailor's blouse”), influenced by <i>хала́т</i> (xalát, “smock, robe”); Shansky found Hinze's argument insufficiently convincing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;dialectal,&#32;rare) rutabaga (see <i>брюква</i> (brjukva)) (<i>inanimate</i>)</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;figuratively,&#32;dialectal,&#32;rare) a fat woman (<i>animate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regionalism) rutabaga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowsprit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>boegspriet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) open, elevated cemetery or graveyard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brawler, rowdy, ruffian</li></ol><p><i>бу́йный</i> (bújnyj, “wild, violent”) +&lrm; <i>-ян</i> (-jan)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Буян)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) Buyan (in medieval Russian folklore, a mysterious island with the ability to appear and disappear with the tide&#59; the home of the Zoryas (solar goddesses) and three brothers representing the Northern, Western, and Eastern Winds)</li></ol><p>Various etymologies are proposed for this name.</p><p>Per Vasmer, related to a word that meant "an elevated place open on all sides, a market square, a barn". According to Vladimir Dal, the name was possibly from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;buydan</i>, meaning "elevated space, market square", making it somewhat related to майда́н (majdán).</p><p>According to Potebna and Preobr., related to an obsolete term, буй (buj), which meant "a hill, an open place, a crowded place", making it derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujati</i> (“to grow rapidly, to rage”). Another theory suggests that the name was from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujanъ</i> or Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujьnъ</i> (“stormy, violent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, to rampage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>A verbal particle used to render conditional and subjunctive mood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑ</i> (by), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;by</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) would (often), used to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) worldly-wise, experienced</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) habitual, familiar</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from the past tense of <i>быва́ть</i> (byvátʹ, “to take place, to happen, to be (regularly)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to occur, to happen (repeatedly)</li><li>to be at, to visit, to stay with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;byvati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ex (former female romantic partner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) a past time; that which once was</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>former, the late, ex-</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) ex (former male romantic partner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бы́вший</i> (bývšij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) cattle</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) rabble; uncultured or stupid people</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) sheeple</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>bydło</i>. Ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bydlò</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>би́дло</i> (býdlo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) bull, ox</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) a big, strong, or stubborn man</li><li>(animate,&#32;slang) gangster, mobster, soldier (rank-and-file member of a criminal gang)</li><li>(animate,&#32;finance) bull</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) abutment, pier</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;regional) rocky headland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑкъ</i> (bykŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bykъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бык</i> (byk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) here goes, I can do this, screw it, what the hell, here we go, let's do this (used to express readiness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bylina (traditional East Slavic oral epic narrative poem)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑлина</i> (bylina, literally “something that was”), from <i>бꙑти</i> (byti, “to be”) (Russian <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blade of grass</li><li>diminutive of <i>были́на</i> (bylína): bylina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bylina</li></ol><p><i>были́на</i> (bylína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) blade of grass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a genre of Russian folklore prose: a short narration about a person's allegedly true encounter with evil spirits or people with demonic properties (witches, sorcerers, werewolves, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>быль</i> (bylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>nearly, on the point of, just about</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) the past; yore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bygone, former</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bylъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лой</i> (-loj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>true story, fact</li><li>bylina, epic story</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bylь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ) or <i>было́й</i> (bylój) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;regional,&#32;collective) grass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bylьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing</i>) quickly</li></ol><p><i>бы́стренький</i> (býstrenʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) or <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro) +&lrm; <i>-енько</i> (-enʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fast, quickly, rapidly</li></ol><p><i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj, “fast, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o, “-ly”, adverb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prefabricated (building)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>execution speed, performance (<i>of instruments, devices, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light-footed</li></ol><p><i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quickness (rapidity of movement or activity)</li></ol><p><i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-speed, fast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fast, quick, speedy, rapid</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>быстрыи</i> (bystryi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bystrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>everyday life at home</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>быть</i> (bytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;stilted) existence, being (the state of being, existing, or occurring)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>бꙑтиѥ</i> (bytije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bytьje</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;byti</i>. Doublet of <i>бытьё</i> (bytʹjó), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бытие́</i> (bytijé)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) life, existence</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) stay, sojourn of someone in some place or in the capacity of someone</li></ol><p>From <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) life, existence</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) availability, spread (of species)</li></ol><p><i>бытова́ть</i> (bytovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in respect to social facts</i>) to be current, to exist, to occur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>domestic, household</li><li>common, everyday</li></ol><p><i>быт</i> (byt) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> nominative]</li><li>there be</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑти</i> (byti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;byti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;bū́ˀtei</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>бути</i> (buty), Belarusian <i>быць</i> (bycʹ), Polish <i>być</i>. Other cognates include Sanskrit <i>भू</i> (bhū, “to be”), Latin <i>futūrus</i>, and English <i>be</i>.</p><p>The present forms are suppletive, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(hedge,&#32;literary) alternative form of <i>мо́жет быть</i> (móžet bytʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>бытиё</i> (bytijó)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑтие</i> (bytije). Doublet of <i>бытие́</i> (bytijé), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бытие́</i> (bytijé).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бытиё</i> (bytijó).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bull, ox</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>бꙑчии</i> (byčii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;byčьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>бык</i> (byk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave aggressively (like a bull), to bully (intransitive)</li></ol><p><i>бык</i> (byk, “bull”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>бык</i> (byk): bull calf, steer</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) bullhead, miller's thumb, goby</li><li>(slang,&#32;inanimate) cigarette butt, fag-end</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) ZIL-5301 light truck or related vehicle</li></ol><p><i>бык</i> (byk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biennale (a biennial celebration or exhibition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>biennale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Б</i>/<i>б</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>B</i>/<i>b</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) baa (sound a sheep makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby hatch, baby box (a place where people (typically mothers) can bring babies, usually newborn, and abandon them anonymously)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>baby box</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby boom (an increase in the birth rate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>baby boom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бебиси́ттер</i> (bɛbisíttɛr): babysitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;colloquial) backup (copy of file or record)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>backup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>background, (person's or event's) history, prehistory (relevant information)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) background</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>background</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer security</i>) back door</li></ol><p>From English <i>back door</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bento (Japanese takeaway lunch served in a box, often with the food arranged into an elaborate design)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>弁当<rp>(</rp><rt>べんとう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>bentō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Beamer (a BMW car)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blotter, desk pad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>buvard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blotter, desk pad</li></ol><p><i>бюва́р</i> (bjuvár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>budget</li></ol><p>Via French <i>budget</i> or German <i>Budget</i> from English <i>budget</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) government employee (<i>who is paid from the government budget</i>)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1989. From <i>бюдже́т</i> (bjudžét, “budget”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) budget; budgetary</li><li>budget (appropriate to a restricted budget)</li></ol><p><i>бюдже́т</i> (bjudžét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballot paper</li><li>bulletin (brief report)</li><li>bulletin, journal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) medical certificate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bulletin</i>, from Italian <i>bullettino</i>, from Latin <i>bulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burette (glass tube with fine gradations and a stopcock used for titration, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office, bureau, agency</li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) bureau, writing desk, secretary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bureau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lost and found, lost property (department where items that are found can be claimed by their owners)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bureaucrat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>bureaucrate</i> or German <i>Bürokrat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bureaucracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bureaucratic</li></ol><p><i>бюрокра́тия</i> (bjurokrátija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bureaucratic centralism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) bureaucracy, the excessive power of civil servants</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) officialdom, the officials, the class of civil servants</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) red tape</li></ol><p>From French <i>bureaucratie</i>, likely through German <i>Bürokratie</i>. Stressed the same way as in бюрокра́т.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sculpture</i>) bust (sculptural portrayal of a person's head and shoulders)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bust, bosom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>buste</i>. Further from Latin <i>bustum</i> (via Italian). Further akin to Latin <i>ūrō</i>.</p><p>For the meaning development compare <i>ту́мба</i> (túmba) (&lt; Latin <i>tumba</i>, whence also English <i>tomb</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>poprsí</i>, Polish <i>popiersie</i> «**по́персь», «**попе́рсье».</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brassiere, bra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Büstenhalter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse calico</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, from Arabic بَزّ (bazz), see more there.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) no-no, something disgusting, someone unpleasant, meanie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used to call out a sheep or goat.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>бедро́</i> (bedró): small thigh</li></ol><p><i>бедро́</i> (bedró) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) comb, reed (in a loom)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьrdo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>беда́</i> (bedá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>белору́сский</i> (belorússkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>бес</i> (bes).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>(location)</i> in, at, on</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>(direction)</i> to, into</li><li><i>(time)</i> at, in, on</li><li>per</li><li><i>(indicating aim or purpose)</i> for, as</li><li><i>(indicating exact correspondence, equivalence)</i> for</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> nominative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>(indicating becoming, joining)</i> as</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>V, volt</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁en</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въ</i> (vŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъ(n)</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥</i>, zero-grade form of <i>&#42;h₁én</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>вольт</i> (volʹt).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: В)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>восто́к</i> (vostók, “east”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) in the form of, in the shape of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extremely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>polka-dot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from now on</li><li>later on</li><li>hereinafter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the moment, at the present time (right now)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two shakes, quickly</li></ol><p>Literally, “in two counts”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a nutshell, in short, in a few words, in brief, in simple terms, long story short</li></ol><p>Literally, “in two words”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a stone's throw away (implying a concrete entity being situated very close)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a matter of fact, in reality, in fact, in practical situations, in actuality, in actual fact, actually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at home, on your own</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alive (having life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> в (v),&lrm; жо́па (žópa).</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucked (in trouble&#59; lit.&#58; "in the ass")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in conclusion (as a conclusion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to a marked degree, to a large extent, to a considerable extent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>in the end</li><li>as a result (consequently, effectively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as (in the capacity&#47;role of)</li></ol><p>Possible calque of French <i>en qualité de</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at long last, in the end, finally, ultimately</li><li>after all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the know, aware, abreast, informed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring owls to Athens, to carry coals to Newcastle (to do something that is not needed)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to carry firewood into the woods”. Compare Polish <i>wozić drewno do lasu</i>, Czech <i>nosit dříví do lesa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at best (in the most favorable of conditions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in any case, anyway (in any way), whatsoever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) in the blink of an eye</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) according to one's understanding; as far as one can understand with the data and knowledge available at the moment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, currently, at the present time, today, nowadays, at present, presently, at this time, at this moment</li></ol><p>Literally, “at the present time”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than idiomatically&#58; see <i>в</i> (v) and <i>нату́ра</i> (natúra)&#59; usually means in kind.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) really, indeed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) au naturel, nude, naked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the opposite direction, about (to a reversed position)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in general, generally, all in all, on the whole, by and large</li><li><i>(introductory word used to summarize the preceding statements)</i> to sum up, in short, in a word</li></ol><p>в (v) + о́бщем (óbščem) (о́бщий (óbščij))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by oneself (without company)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around, about (circularly)</li><li>in circumference</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) for the most part, by and large</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>откры́тую</i> (otkrýtuju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in regards to, with regards to, with respect to, as regards, regarding, concerning, in regard of, in respect of</li></ol><p>Literally, “in regard/respect (of)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first, first of all, first and foremost, in the first place, in the first instance, primarily (before anything else in importance or precedence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) to hell with (used to show displeasure or disregard toward the named person or thing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after (in pursuit of, seeking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in every sense of the word</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in order</li><li>all right, in good trim, in kilter (in good health)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>par for the course; standard, expected, normal, not uncommon</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>dans l'ordre des choses</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lately, of late, these days (in recent times)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the nick of time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by the sweat of one's brow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in addition, to boot, into the bargain (also&#59; as well)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>during</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;with verbs of destruction such as <i>разнести́</i> (raznestí) or <i>разби́ть</i> (razbítʹ)) to smithereens, to pieces (<i>completely, totally</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;with verbs related to dressing, especially <i>разоде́ться</i> (razodétʹsja)) to the nines (very smartly)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;with verbs related to arguing or fighting, especially <i>разруга́ться</i> (razrugátʹsja)) ripping (each other) apart (<i>arguing very badly</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “into fluff and dust/ashes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>under, within (<i>of condition</i>)</li><li>in accordance with</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ра́мках</i> (rámkax)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as a result (consequently)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) therefore, as a result</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, truly, indeed, quite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in light of, in the light of (given, considering)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in turn, in (his, her, its, their, etc.) turn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) because of, due to, owing to, by virtue of</li><li>Used other than idiomatically&#58; see <i>в</i> (v) and <i>си́лу</i> (sílu)&#59; usually means "into force", "into effect".</li></ol><p>Literally, “at the force (of)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in case of (in the event of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) what do you mean</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) I mean, you mean etc.</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in terms (of), in a (something) sense</li></ol><p>Literally, “in the sense”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to a T, to perfection, perfectly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in accordance with</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able, in the position</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on average</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at loggerheads ((figuratively) unable to agree&#59; opposing)</li><li>at issue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>essentially</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>including</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>в то́м числе́</i> (v tóm čislé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>during, within</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>while, whereas, when</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time (simultaneously)</li><li>(<i>hedge</i>) on the other hand, at the same time (from the different perspective)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time (simultaneously)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>including</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>то́т же ча́с</i> (tót že čás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(being a) burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>point-blank (so close that a weapon may be aimed directly at its target)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> в (v),&lrm; ход (xod).</li><li>in the course of, in the process of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at worst (in the worst possible alternative)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>generally</li><li>on the whole, all in all</li><li>altogether, entirely</li><li>in total, in toto</li><li>in its entirety</li><li>en masse, en bloc</li><li>by the gross</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) safe and sound; free from danger or injury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in order to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in particular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in honour/honor of, after</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stark naked, mother-naked, in the buff (completely unclothed) (literally, “like when born by mother”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) well-off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>these days (currently, at present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the time, at that time</li><li>meanwhile, in the meantime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;of horses</i>) dappled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in herringbone pattern</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censored spelling of <i>война́</i> (vojná)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>added to verbs, forms verbs with the approximate meaning 'in' or 'into'</li><li>added with <i>-ся</i> (-sja) to verbs, forms verbs with the approximate meaning of thoroughness or completeness of action</li><li>added to adjectives, forms adverbs with the approximate meaning 'in the manner of &#91;the adjective&#93;'</li><li>added to nouns, forms adverbs with the approximate meaning 'in the direction or location of &#91;the noun&#93;'</li><li>added to numerals, forms adverbs based on the numeral</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въ-</i> (vŭ-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъ(n)-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i>. The /n/ persists in <i>внять</i> (vnjatʹ). See also <i>в</i> (v), Old East Slavic <i>въ</i> (vŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъ(n)</i>. Compare the similar development in <i>с-</i> (s-) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thirdly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fourthly, in the fourth place</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>четвёртых</i> (četvjórtyx)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>век</i> (vek, “century”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>вольт</i> (volʹt, “volt”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>восто́к</i> (vostók, “east”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>верста́</i> (verstá, “verst”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: В.)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>east</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>восто́к</i> (vostók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>волостно́е правле́ние</i> (volostnóje pravlénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>волостно́е правле́ние</i> (volostnóje pravlénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>во́инская часть</i> (vóinskaja častʹ, “military unit”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>higher education</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>вы́сшее образова́ние</i> (výsšeje obrazovánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>во́инская часть</i> (vóinskaja častʹ, “military unit number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(with the verbs <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ), <i>пойти́</i> (pojtí) and similar) all in, all out, for broke, doubling down (risking everything, putting everything on the line)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>va banque</i> in the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) to call, to lure with a call (to imitate the sounds of an animal's calls in order to lure it)</li><li>(<i>falconry</i>) to fly a lure</li><li>(dated,&#32;regional) to lure, to entice (<i>a person</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вабити</i> (vabiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vabiti</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>ва́бити</i> (vábyty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) sore, any spot upon the body which hurts</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Babylon; Babylonian</li></ol><p><i>Вавило́н</i> (Vavilón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylonian</li></ol><p><i>Вавило́н</i> (Vavilón) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, scale, weighing machine</li><li>(<i>carriages</i>) splinter bar</li><li>whiffletree</li><li>crowbar, lever</li></ol><p>From Old High German <i>wāga</i>, probably via Polish <i>waga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vagina</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vāgīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vaginal</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>vaginal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaginal sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>surgery</i>) vaginoplasty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) a member of the Wagner Group</li></ol><p><i>Ва́гнер</i> (Vágner) +&lrm; <i>-овец</i> (-ovec). The group "Wagner" was named after the German musician, Wilhelm Richard Wagner.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wagnerian</li></ol><p><i>Ва́гнер</i> (Vágner) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>railway car, coach, railway carriage</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;figurative) much/many, a lot of</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>wagon</i>, from English <i>waggon</i>, from Middle Dutch <i>wagen</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagnaz</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second-class compartment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first-class compartment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>third-class compartment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diner, dining car (on a train)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tram, trolley, carriage, waggon, minecart (vehicle for material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) railway carriage</li></ol><p><i>ваго́н</i> (vagón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tram driver</li></ol><p><i>ваго́н</i> (vagón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вожа́тый</i> (vožátyj), probably, a calque of German <i>Wagenführer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ваго́н</i> (vagón): (small) carriage; trailer, caravan</li></ol><p><i>ваго́н</i> (vagón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>surgery</i>) vagotomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupola furnace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female reindeer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami <i>ва̄жь</i> (vāž’).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ва́жно</i> (vážno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj): the most important; very important; major, paramount, chief, key</li></ol><p><i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put on airs</li></ol><p><i>ва́ж(ный)</i> (váž(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>importantly</li><li>grandly</li></ol><p><i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>importance, significance; relevance</li></ol><p>From <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>important</li><li>proud, majestic, imposing (important-looking or -seeming)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>ważny</i>, from <i>waga</i> (“scales, weight, importance”), from Old High German <i>wāga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vase</li><li>bowl (<i>for fruits, sweets, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vase</i>, from Latin <i>vas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaseline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaeology,&#32;art) vase painting, pottery art</li></ol><p><i>ва́за</i> (váza, “vase”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пис</i> (-pis) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), from a combining form of <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ва́за</i> (váza): a small vase, a small bowl, a condiment plate</li></ol><p><i>ва́за</i> (váza) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) exclamation expressing strong emotion</li></ol><p>From Armenian <i>վայ</i> (vay), Georgian <i>ვაი</i> (vai), Azerbaijani <i>vay</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вайфа́й</i> (vajfáj, “Wi-Fi”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) vibe (atmosphere or aura felt to belong to a person, place or thing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>vibe</i>, from <i>vibration</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wi-Fi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Wi-Fi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waifu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ワイフ</i>, from English <i>wife</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vaisya, Vaishya</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>वैश्य</i> (vaiśya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) frond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacancy (an unoccupied job)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vacance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vacant, unoccupied (<i>of a position or post</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>vakant</i> or French <i>vacant</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe polish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wachs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacuum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vacuum</i>, from Latin <i>vacuus</i> (analyzable as the root of Latin <i>vacō</i> + <i>-uus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vacuum</li></ol><p><i>ва́куум</i> (vákuum) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) waqf (inalienable endowment for charity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic وَقْف (waqf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bacchanalian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bacchanalia (a feast in honor of Bacchus)</li><li>a drunken feast, orgy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bacchanalian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bacchanalian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaccine</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cowpox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaccination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vaccinate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>vacciner</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), or from <i>вакци́на</i> (vakcína) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaccinium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Vaccinium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vaccine; vaccinal</li></ol><p><i>вакци́на</i> (vakcína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billow, roller, large wave (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li><li>rampart, dyke, wall (an earthen embankment, possibly faced with stone, used as a defensive fortification)</li><li>barrage fire</li><li>shaft, axle</li><li>gross output</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>валъ</i> (valŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;valъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wallach</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Walach</i> in the 17th-18th century, itself from Old East Slavic <i>волохъ</i> (voloxŭ), both ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volxъ</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhaz</i> (“foreigner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wallachian (related to Wallachia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Вала́хия</i> (Valáxija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Of or pertaining to Valday.</li></ol><p><i>Валда́й</i> (Valdáj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of a pair of traditional Russian felt boots, sg valenok, pl valenki</li><li>clumsy or stupid person</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) sediment of dust on the fan</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;slang) old age pension, sixty-six in the lotto game</li></ol><p><i>ва́леный</i> (válenyj) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valencian (person from Valencia, Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valencian (person from Valencia, Spain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Valencian (from or relating to Valencia, its people, or its language)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valencian language</li></ol><p><i>Валенсия</i> (Valensija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valency</li></ol><p><i>вале́нтный</i> (valéntnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;linguistics) valent</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>valent</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) felt (made of matted, fulled wool)</li></ol><p><i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valerian (a plant of the genus <i>Valeriana</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>valeriāna</i>; see English <i>valerian</i> for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>валериа́на</i> (valeriána, “valerian”) (a plant of the genus <i>Valeriana</i>)</li><li>valerian root extract (uses as a sedative)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>valeriāna</i>; see English <i>valerian</i> for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) valerian root extract, tincture of valerian; valerian drops (<i>used as a sedative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>валерья́на</i> (valerʹjána) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Valeriana</li></ol><p><i>валерья́н(а)</i> (valerʹján(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) jack, knave (playing card)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>valet</i> (“servant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roller</li></ol><p><i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menthyl isovalerate, validolum (a vasodilatory, sedative and antiemetic drug common in Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Validol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roller, cylinder, platen</li><li>bolster</li></ol><p><i>вал</i> (val) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock down, to kill (e.g. of a disease killing people or animals)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to heap up (<i>transitive</i>)</li><li>to cut, to fell (trees), to make someone fall, to throw down (on the ground)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push the blame or responsibility on someone else</li><li>to throng (<i>e.g. of people</i>)</li><li>to fall heavily (<i>e.g. of snow</i>)</li><li>to belch (<i>e.g. of smoke</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go away, to scram</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;valiti</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>volvō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall (down), to drop, to collapse</li><li>to overturn, to topple (down)</li><li>to come falling down, to roll down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die in great numbers</li></ol><p><i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felling (<i>of trees, etc.</i>)</li><li>felting (production of felt, of production of objects, e.g. shoes, out of felt)</li></ol><p><i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ, “to fell (trees)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ, “to felt, to make out of felt or wool”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsteady, unstable, easily tilting, tending to fall to the side (<i>of a boat, car, gait, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jolting, causing shakiness or unsteadiness (<i>of a road</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ, “to fell, to throw down, to cause to fall”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallaby</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>wallaby</i>, from Dharug <i>wollabi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Welsh</li></ol><p><i>Ва́лл(ис)</i> (Váll(is), “Wales”) (<i>dated</i>) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Walloon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Walloon (Romance language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gross (total before any deductions)</li></ol><p><i>вал</i> (val, “gross output”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cylinder, roller</li></ol><p><i>вал</i> (val) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French horn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>waltornia</i>, from German <i>Waldhorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boulder</li></ol><p>From <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodcock, night peck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Waldschnepfe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valkyrie</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>valkyrja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waltz</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>valse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form sheet metal on a rollforming machine</li></ol><p>From <i>вальц-</i> (valʹc-) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ), based on the root of <i>вале́ц</i> (valéc, “roller”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rollforming (metal industry)</li></ol><p><i>вальц(ева́ть)</i> (valʹc(evátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imposing; impressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>currency (money or other item used to facilitate transactions)</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) foreign currency</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>valuta</i> (“currency”), from Latin <i>valeō</i> (“to be worth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) high-dollar prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) currency; monetary</li></ol><p><i>валю́та</i> (valjúta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fulling, milling (<i>of broadcloth</i>)</li></ol><p><i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag on the floor or ground</li><li>to roll (something) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in flour, bread crumbs, etc., thereby covering the object’</small>]</li><li>to roll (something) (<i>into some form</i>), to shape by rolling</li><li>to knead</li><li>to felt, to make out of felt</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to do (something) quickly, to carelessly and haphazardly; to churn out (a book), to dash off (a painting), to slap together (a proposal), etc.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>only imperative</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> валя́ть (valjátʹ),&lrm; дура́к (durák).</li><li>to fool around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll around, to roll from side to side; to wallow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie about (<i>idly or as a result of being sick; regularly, periodically or for some amount of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie about (<i>in disorder</i>), to be strewn carelessly</li></ol><p><i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vampire (mythological creature, also figuratively)</li><li>vampire bat</li><li>ellipsis of <i>энергетический вампир</i> (energetičeskij vampir)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vampire</i> or German <i>Vampir</i>, from Serbo-Croatian <i>vàmpīr</i>, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫpyrь</i>. Doublet of <i>упы́рь</i> (upýrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vampire</li></ol><p><i>вампи́р</i> (vampír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vampiress</li></ol><p>From <i>вампи́р</i> (vampír) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) king (in Ancient China); prince (in the Chinese Empire)</li><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) one of the Vanir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>王</i> (wáng).</p><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>vanr</i></p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ван)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Van (the capital city of Van Province, Turkey)</li><li>Van (a lake in Van Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vanadium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;joking or ironic) to predict, to prophesy</li></ol><p><i>Ва́нга</i> (Vánga, “Baba Vanga, a Bulgarian mystic, 1911–1996”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vandal (member of a Germanic tribe)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vandal, barbarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vandalism</li><li>indifference to the arts, lack of taste</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) immoral action</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vandalisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Vendean</li></ol><p><i>Ванде́я</i> (Vandéja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vanillin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vanilla (<i>Vanilla planifolia</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of an early-2010s Russian youth subculture that cultivates demonstrative romanticism</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a sensual, vulnerable girl with her head in the clouds</li></ol><p><i>вани́ль</i> (vanílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vanilla</li></ol><p><i>вани́ль</i> (vanílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bath, bathtub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wanne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathroom</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>ва́нная ко́мната</i> (vánnaja kómnata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ва́нна</i> (vánna): small bathtub</li><li>cuvette (laboratory vessel)</li></ol><p><i>ва́нна</i> (vánna) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bath, bathroom</li></ol><p><i>ва́нна</i> (vánna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shroud (tackle supporting a mast)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>want</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunger (device for removing blockages by suction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ventouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar, pitch</li><li>cobbler's wax</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boiling water</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>варъ</i> (varŭ, “boiled liquid”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;varъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>varan, monitor lizard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>varan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros, “foreign, strange”), onomatopoeic (mimicking foreign words, akin to 'blah blah').</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) barbarism (error in language use)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>barbarously</li><li>barbarically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barbarian, barbaric</li><li>barbarous</li></ol><p><i>ва́рвар</i> (várvar) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbarism, barbarity, barbarianism</li></ol><p><i>ва́рвар</i> (várvar) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jew's harp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) broth, slop, liquid food, cooking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mitten (for keeping warm, usually knitted)</li><li>(<i>rude</i>) mouth, yap, trap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>varenets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>process of cooking by boiling</li></ol><p><i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a varenik/varenyk; a single vareniki/varenyky dumpling</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) female genitalia; vagina or vulva</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainian hot beverage (alcoholic or non-alcoholic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jam, varenye, murabba (<i>food; usually not as thick as</i> джем, <i>and with whole pieces of fruit or berry</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vařenьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to boil”) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje). Doublet of <i>варе́ние</i> (varénije, “the process of boiling”), through a cognate suffix, <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>version, revision, edition (of a work)</li><li>version, idea (personal take on a thing or concept)</li><li>option</li><li>variant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>variante</i>. Further from Latin <i>variāns</i>, p.p.p. of <i>variō</i> (&lt; <i>varius</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>provedení</i>, Slovene <i>različica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variation, variability</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) variable, variative</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>variativus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variation (related but distinct thing)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) variation (a musical technique based on an altered repetition of a theme)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook, to boil</li><li>to brew <i>(beer)</i></li><li>to found, to weld</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;variti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to burn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be boiling/cooking</li></ol><p><i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>process of cooking by boiling</li></ol><p><i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a criminal person, a highwayman</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;archaic) an escaped deportee from a penal colony (usually escaped from a penal colony in Siberia to some village also in Siberia or Manchuria)</li></ol><p>Probably from Chuvash <i>вӑрла</i> (vărla, “to steal”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Warsaw</li></ol><p><i>Варша́ва</i> (Varšáva) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varsovian</li></ol><p><i>Варша́ва</i> (Varšáva) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety show, vaudeville</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>variété</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to vary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vary</li></ol><p><i>варьи́ровать</i> (varʹírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian, Viking</li><li>(<i>modern use</i>) an outsider or foreigner brought in to lead or help an organization or company, e.g. local bodies of state administration or a sports team</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>варѧгъ</i> (varęgŭ), from Old Norse <i>væringi</i>. The figurative sense stems from the legendary summoning of Rurik and the Varangians by the East Slavs.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) denim clothing processed (usually at home) to have uneven colouring/coloring</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boiled sausage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boiled condensed milk</li></ol><p><i>варёный</i> (varjónyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiled egg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiled, cooked by boiling (<i>of food</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasabi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>山葵</i> (わさび, wasabi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vasilevsky (name or place)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vasylivka (city in Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Васи́льев</i> (Vasílʹjev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blue bonnet, cornflower</li><li>bright blue, cornflower blue</li></ol><p><i>василёк</i> (vasiljók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cornflower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) small inspection window</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vasistas</i> (“transom window, fortochka”), from German <i>Was ist das&#63;</i> (“What is that?”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vassal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vassal</i>, further from Latin <i>vassallus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorbent cotton, cotton wool, wadding, batting</li><li>(<i>cloth</i>) drugstore cotton</li><li>glass wool</li><li>(<i>slang, derogatory, collective, politics</i>) Russian nationalists (compare ватник)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Watte</i> or French <i>ouate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) gang (big, noisy group of people)</li></ol><p>From Old Chuvash &#42;våtaɣ, &#42;uotaɣ, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;ōtag</i>. The sense developed from “tent, family” to “group of people”. Compare Turkish <i>otağ</i> (“tent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) water closet, lavatory</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) spinning machine in which the threads are continuously pulled through the rollers, twisting and winding</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>water</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>very&#32;rare</i>) level, water level, spirit level</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>waterpas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water polo player</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water polo player</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) water polo (a water sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) raincoat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>waterproof</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person from Vatican City, Vaticanian</li></ol><p><i>Ватика́н</i> (Vatikán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vatican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piece of absorbent cotton</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ва́та</i> (váta, “absorbent cotton”)</li></ol><p><i>ва́та</i> (váta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Whatman paper, wove paper (high-quality drawing paper)</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>ва́тманская бума́га</i> (vátmanskaja bumága), from English <i>Whatman paper</i>, named after James Whatman.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) quilted jacket, vatnik</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism) vatnik, sovok, a zealous Russian jingoist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;specifically) a supporter of Vladimir Putin or the ongoing Russian invasion of Ukraine.</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;by extension) a soldier taking part in the ongoing Russian invasion of Ukraine.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ва́та</i> (váta, “cotton wool”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p><p>For sense 2, from the clothing worn by Soviet citizens, and an online satirical cartoon by the cartoonist Anton Chadsky about a sentient jacket simply known as "ва́тник". The term was popularised by netizens in VKontakte and LIVEJOURNAL, and the Russian opposition in general.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ва́тник</i> (vátnik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cotton wool, batting; wadded, quilted</li><li>(<i>of limbs</i>) powerless</li><li>(<i>slang, derogatory</i>) nationalistic, jingoistic</li></ol><p><i>ва́та</i> (váta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asclepias</li></ol><p><i>ва́тка</i> (vátka, “absorbent cotton”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;relational) cotton wool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vatrushka</li></ol><p><i>ватру́шка</i> (vatrúška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vatrushka (a sort of small, round cottage cheese-filled pastry)</li><li>a round, inflatable snow saucer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ватру́шка</i> (vatrúška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watt</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) mudflat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>watt</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Watt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wow (excitement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>wow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voucher (a piece of paper that entitles the holder to a discount, or that can be exchanged for goods and services)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>voucher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waffle iron (a cooking appliance used to make waffles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waffle, wafer</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) snot, spit</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;criminal jargon) cum, jizz, ejaculate</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) mythical flying dick; by extension, any flying object with an elongated shape, such as a Zeppelin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Waffel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;fandom slang) <i>Warhammer 40,000</i>, a British tabletop military game set in a dystopian future Galaxy torn by war.</li><li>(slang,&#32;fandom slang,&#32;by extension) <i>Warhammer Fantasy</i>, a British tabletop military game set in a dystopian mediaeval fantasy world.</li><li>(slang,&#32;fandom slang,&#32;by extension) <i>Warhammer Age of Sigmar</i>, a British tabletop fantasy game set after <i>Warhammer Fantasy</i> in a mythological setting.</li></ol><p>Shortening of Вархаммер (Varxammer), transliteration of English <i>Warhammer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) wachtmeister (a non-commissioned officer (NCO))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wachtmeister</i> or Dutch <i>wachtmeester</i>, possibly via Polish <i>wachmistrz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watch, shift, duty, vigil</li><li>group of workers working such a shift</li><li>buckbean</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wacht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;nautical) watch</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) guard, watchman, sailor on duty</li></ol><p><i>ва́хта</i> (váxta) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) storekeeper (landlord of a ship's property)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) senior watchman at an institution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter, janitor, watchman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wächter</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вахта</i> (vaxta) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;swear word) A strong feeling of shock ( generally used sarcastically )</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>а́хуй</i> (áxuj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wahhabism (branch of Sunni Islam)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) your, yours (<i>2nd-person singular formal or 2nd-person plural</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вашь</i> (vašĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vašь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wōsyo-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(stilted,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;journalistic) I</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>votre humble serviteur</i>. Literally, “your humble servant”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>your excellency, your honor</li></ol><p>Literally, “your nobleness”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>cheers! (toast when drinking)</li></ol><p>Literally, “[for] your health”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Washingtonian (inhabitant of Washington, D.C., or Washington state)</li></ol><p><i>Вашингто́н</i> (Vašingtón) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Washington; Washingtonian</li></ol><p><i>Вашингто́н</i> (Vašingtón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;nonstandard,&#32;historical) Your Honour/Honor (form of address)</li></ol><p>Corruption of <i>Ва́ше Высокоблагоро́дие</i> (Váše Vysokoblagoródije, “Your Honour”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ваш</i> (vaš).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) pronunciation spelling of <i>вообще́</i> (voobščé)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sculpture (art)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ваꙗньѥ</i> (vajanĭje, “sculpture, idol”). By surface analysis, <i>вая́ть</i> (vajátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sculptor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ваꙗтель</i> (vajatelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>вая́ть</i> (vajátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sculpt</li><li>(figurative,&#32;humorous) to produce, to make something</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ваꙗти</i> (vajati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vajati</i>, possibly related to Old Church Slavonic <i>вити</i> (viti), Russian <i>вить</i> (vitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come running in, to come rushing in, to run <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come running in, to come rushing in, to run <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in, to hammer in (also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>вбить</i> (vbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to absorb, to imbibe</li><li>to suck in, to drink in</li><li>to inhale</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in, to hammer in (also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around, nearby</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>around, near</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sideways, to the side</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by ford</li><li>through the ford</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>брод</i> (brod), from the verb <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throw-in (throw of the ball back into play)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;media,&#32;Internet) publicizing some (often dubious) information in order to create a sensation; making a sensational post in the social media to achieve some effect; clickbaiting</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) stuffing the ballot box</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вбро́сить</i> (vbrósitʹ, “to throw in”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centuries</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>века́</i> (veká).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ве́ка</i> (véka) (<i>genitive singular</i>)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>веко́в</i> (vekóv) (<i>genitive plural</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heave <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>ввали́ть</i> (vvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tumble <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to barge <i>(into)</i>, to burst <i>(into)</i></li><li>to become hollow/sunken</li></ol><p><i>вва́ливать</i> (vválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heave <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tumble <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to barge <i>(into)</i>, to burst <i>(into)</i></li><li>to become hollow/sunken</li></ol><p><i>ввали́ть</i> (vvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing in, leading in</li><li>preamble, preface, introduction, lead-in [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>введ-</i> (vved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring in, to import</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to bring [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something dangerous, unpleasant, or severe’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вве́ргнуть</i> (vvérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to bring [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something dangerous, unpleasant, or severe’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust <i>(with)</i>, to entrust <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upwards</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>верх</i> (verx)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upside down, topsy-turvy (of objects, not humans or animals)</li><li>at sixes and sevens (in a state of confusion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upside down, head over heels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above, overhead (in a higher place)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>верху́</i> (verxú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust <i>(with)</i>, to entrust <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>вве́рить</i> (vvéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce</li><li>to bring in, to usher in</li><li>to input</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to misinform, to deceive, to disorient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make something clear to (someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) in the eve, in the evening</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вечеру́</i> (večerú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in view of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead-in</li><li>input, bringing in</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) enter (key)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce</li><li>to bring in, to usher in</li><li>to input</li><li>to bring into effect</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to misinform, to deceive, to disorient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>вво́дное сло́во</i> (vvódnoje slóvo, “parenthetical word”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>вво́дное словосочета́ние</i> (vvódnoje slovosočetánije, “parenthetical word combination”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parenthetical word, parenthesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>introductory</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) entrance</li><li>parenthetic</li></ol><p>From <i>ввод</i> (vvod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>import</li><li>importation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring in, to import</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (in) plenty, as much as is wanted, a lot</li></ol><p>From <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>во́лю</i> (vólju), accusative of <i>во́ля</i> (vólja, “will”); approximate sense "to (your) will".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upward, skyward (toward an upper level)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>высь</i> (vysʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knit in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mix up, to draw in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get stuck, to get caught (e.g. in a sticky substance)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get involved in something unpleasant or reprehensible</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p><p><i>ввя́знуть</i> (vvjáznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to meddle <i>(in)</i>, to get involved <i>(in)</i>, to put one's oar in</li></ol><p><i>ввяза́ть</i> (vvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get stuck, to get caught (e.g. in a sticky substance)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get involved in something unpleasant or reprehensible</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>вя́знуть</i> (vjáznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knit in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mix up, to draw in</li></ol><p><i>ввяза́ть</i> (vvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to meddle <i>(in)</i>, to get involved <i>(in)</i>, to put one's oar in</li></ol><p><i>ввя́зывать</i> (vvjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend inwards</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into the depths</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>глубь</i> (glubʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peer (at, into)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peer into, to look or observe narrowly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to herd (cattle, etc.) into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hammer in, to force in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/sink one's teeth <i>(into)</i></li><li>to go deep <i>(into)</i>, to bone up <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>вгры́зться</i> (vgrýztʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/sink one's teeth <i>(into)</i></li><li>to go deep <i>(into)</i>, to bone up <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jut out <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вда́ться</i> (vdátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself into (something)</li></ol><p><i>вдави́ть</i> (vdavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вдави́ть</i> (vdavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself into (something)</li></ol><p><i>вда́вливать</i> (vdávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the distance, afar (at a great distance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the distance, afar (at a great distance)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дали́</i> (dalí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into the distance, long way off</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>даль</i> (dalʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jut out <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Cognates include Czech <i>vdát</i> and Polish <i>wdać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>twice</li><li>in half</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дво́е</i> (dvóje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>doubly, twice as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>two (together), both (together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in addition, on top of everything (also&#59; as well)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>доба́вок</i> (dobávok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьdova</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;widewāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁widʰéwh₂</i>.</p><p>Cognate with English <i>widow</i>, German <i>Witwe</i>, French <i>veuve</i>, Spanish <i>viuda</i>, Italian <i>vedova</i>, Sanskrit <i>विधवा</i> (vidhavā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a widower or a widow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widower</li></ol><p><i>вдова́</i> (vdová) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) widow</li></ol><p><i>вдова́</i> (vdová) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in plenty, in abundance</li><li>to one's heart's content</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) + the root of <i>дово́льный</i> (dovólʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widowhood</li></ol><p><i>вдова́</i> (vdová) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a widower or a widow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empress dowager</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вдова́</i> (vdová, “widow”)</li><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Any indigobird of the genus <i>Vidua</i>.</li></ol><p><i>вдова́</i> (vdová, “widow”) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška, diminutive suffix). The ornithological name is a semantic loan from translingual <i>Vidua</i> and Latin <i>vidua</i> (“widow.”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) after (in pursuit of)</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>до</i> (do) +&lrm; <i>го́нку</i> (gónku), or from <i>в</i> (v) + the root of <i>догоня́ть</i> (dogonjátʹ, “to overtake”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhalation, breath (in)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspiration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въдъхновениѥ</i> (vŭdŭxnovenije), which itself is probably a calque of Greek <i>ἔμπνοια</i> (émpnoia, “inspiration”).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>īnspīrō</i> (whence <i>инспири́ровать</i> (inspirírovatʹ)); <i>ве́яние</i> (véjanije), <i>наве́ять</i> (navéjatʹ), <i>навева́ть</i> (navevátʹ) (&lt; <i>веять</i> (vejatʹ)), <i>пове́трие</i> (povétrije) &lt; <i>ве́тер</i> (véter); <i>влия́ть</i> (vlijátʹ), <i>влия́ние</i> (vlijánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inspiredly, with inspiration</li></ol><p><i>вдохнове́нный</i> (vdoxnovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inspired, full of inspiration</li></ol><p><i>вдохнове́ние</i> (vdoxnovénije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspirer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъdъxnoviteľь</i>, from <i>&#42;dъxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire, to mastermind</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>вдохнове́ние</i> (vdoxnovénije).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>влия́ть</i> (vlijátʹ), <i>влия́ние</i> (vlijánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inspired [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘by’</small>], to derive inspiration [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘from’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>вдохнови́ть</i> (vdoxnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire, to mastermind</li></ol><p><i>вдохнови́ть</i> (vdoxnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inhale, to breathe in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to pieces, into smithereens</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>дре́безги</i> (drébezgi)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly, all of a sudden</li><li>what if</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;vernacular) again, (for) a second time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to think over, to ponder <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtfulness</li></ol><p><i>вду́мчивый</i> (vdúmčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serious, profound, thoughtful, penetrating</li></ol><p><i>вду́мать(ся)</i> (vdúmatʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to think over, to ponder <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>вду́маться</i> (vdúmatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow into (something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to palm off onto</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial,&#32;euphemistic) to perform anal sex (on someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have anal sex</li></ol><p>Literally, “to blow into the ass”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inhale, to breathe in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) web (the World Wide Web)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>web</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) web designer, web designer's</li></ol><p>From <i>веб-диза́йнер</i> (veb-dizájner) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) web site</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>website</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) web page</li></ol><p>Partial calque of English <i>web page</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a kind of worm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;webelas</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wibilaz</i> (“weevil”), ultimately from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;webʰ-</i> (“to weave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) squirrel (mammal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣверица</i> (věverica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věverica</i> (“squirrel”). Compare Ukrainian <i>вевери́ця</i> (veverýcja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>vegan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vegan</li></ol><p>From <i>вега́н</i> (vegán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) or <i>ве́ган</i> (végan) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veganism</li></ol><p><i>ве́га́н</i> (végán) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female vegetarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vegetarian</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) harmless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetarianism (practice of following a vegetarian diet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vegetative, autonomic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>vegetativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veda</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वेद</i> (veda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to know (be certain or sure about something or aware of a fact)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to govern, to manage, to control [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> (<i>archaic</i>) accusative <small>‘an organization, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věděti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóyde</i> (“to know”), perfect of <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see, to know”). Cognates include Polish <i>wiedzieć</i>, German <i>wissen</i>, Luxembourgish <i>wëssen</i> and English <i>wit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge, lore</li><li>authority, charge, competence</li><li>directing, leadership</li><li>management, managing</li><li>conducting</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ, “to know, to be in charge”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i>.</p><p>From <i>вед-</i> (ved-, “to conduct, direct”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wedʰ-</i> (“to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>веде́ние</i> (vedénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) name of the Cyrillic letter В, в</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣди</i> (vědi).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Веди)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vedi (a town in Ararat Province, Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>Վեդի</i> (Vedi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable&#32;and&#32;uncountable</i>) witchcraft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>list, register, roll, statement</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gazette, bulletin</li></ol><p><i>ве́домый</i> (védomyj, “known, familiar”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Compare Polish <i>wiadomość</i> (“message, information, news”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>departmental, bureaucratic, institutional</li><li>(figurative,&#32;rare) narrow, bureaucratic</li></ol><p><i>ве́домство</i> (védomstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department, institution, office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supporting</li><li>driven</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) slave (<i>mechanism</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) known, familiar</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) supporting aircraft</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) second wingman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bucket, pail</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) vedro; an ancient Russian measure of liquids equal to 12.3 liters (<small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>40</sub></small> бочки)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rustbucket, bucket of bolts (a run-down automobile or machine)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣдро</i> (vědro), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vědro</i> (“water bucket”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wódr̥</i> (“water”), from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;wed-</i> (“wet”). Akin to Old Armenian <i>գետ</i> (get, “river”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>host, anchor, narrator</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading, main, head</li><li>driving (e.g. wheel)</li></ol><p>From <i>вести́</i> (vestí, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>after all</li><li>indeed, surely</li><li>why, well</li><li>then, you know, you see</li><li>isn’t it?</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as, because</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣдѣ</i> (vědě). Russian <i>ведь</i> (vedʹ) is a former Proto-Indo-European root perfect <i>&#42;wóyde</i> with mediopassive ending <i>-ai</i>, with meaning "I have seen". Cognate with English <i>wit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witch</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) hag, shrew, beldam, harridan, vixen</li><li>Old Maid (card game)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣдьма</i> (vědĭma), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vědьma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) male witch, warlock, vedmak</li></ol><p>A male form of</p><p><i>ве́дьма</i> (védʹma, “witch”) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vědьma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witchiness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practise witchcraft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ведро́</i> (vedró): small bucket, small pail</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flashing light (<i>e.g. on a police car</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ведро́</i> (vedró) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand-held fan</li></ol><p>According to Chernykh, from Dutch <i>waaier</i> in the early 18th century (revocalized under the influence of <i>веять</i> (vejatʹ)), from <i>waaien</i> (“to blow”), from Middle Dutch <i>wâyen</i>, from Old Dutch <i>&#42;wāien</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> (which is also the source of веять).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) diadromous</li><li>fan-shaped</li></ol><p><i>ве́ер</i> (véjer) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of Latin <i>flabelliformis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tent; kibitka (<i>usually of old nomadic tribes</i>)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tower (<i>in Russia of 9-13th c.</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) knowing, versed, learned, educated</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věža</i>. Cognates include Slovene <i>veža</i> (“hallway”), Slovak <i>veža</i> (“tower”). Related to <i>везти́</i> (veztí).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣжа</i> (věža, “knowing, versed”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věďa</i>, from <i>&#42;věděti</i> (“to know”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣжда</i> (věžda). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>невѣжда</i> (nevěžda, “ignorant”) (whence Russian <i>неве́жда</i> (nevéžda, “ignoramus”).</p><p>Related to <i>ве́жество</i> (véžestvo), <i>ве́жливый</i> (véžlivyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) eyelid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣжда</i> (věžda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věďa</i>. Displaced Old East Slavic <i>вѣжа</i> (věža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>politely, civilly</li><li>blandly</li><li>correctly</li><li>gracefully</li><li>obligingly</li></ol><p><i>ве́жливый</i> (véžlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politeness (act of being polite)</li></ol><p><i>ве́жливый</i> (véžlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>courteous, civil, polite</li><li>(about pronoun "Вы") formal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣжливои</i> (věžlivoi, “experienced, versed”), itself from <i>вѣжливои</i> (věžlivoi, “knower, competent”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věďa</i>, from <i>&#42;věděti</i> (“to know”). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>невѣжда</i> (nevěžda, “ignorant”). See also <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li><li>anywhere</li><li>throughout</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьсьде</i> (vĭsĭde), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьsьde</i>. By surface analysis, <i>весь</i> (vesʹ) +&lrm; <i>где</i> (gde).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ubiquity, omnipresence</li></ol><p><i>вездесу́щий</i> (vezdesúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ubiquity, omnipresence</li></ol><p><i>вездесу́щий</i> (vezdesúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>omnipresent, ubiquitous</li></ol><p><i>везде́</i> (vezdé) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>су́щий</i> (súščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ubiquity, omnipresence</li></ol><p>From <i>вездесу́щий</i> (vezdesúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(large) ATV, (large) all-terrain vehicle, landrover, rover, cross-country vehicle, go-anywhere vehicle, four-wheel drive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luck, good luck</li></ol><p><i>вез-</i> (vez-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p><p>For the meaning development compare Latin <i>fortūna</i> (“fortune, luck”) related to <i>ferō</i> (“to bear, to carry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convey, to carry (by vehicle), to deliver, to transport</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be lucky, to have luck, to work out for, to succeed, to be successful [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vezti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>vehō</i>, Sanskrit <i>वहति</i> (vahati), Old English <i>wegan</i> (English <i>weigh</i>).</p><p>(<i>to be lucky</i>): Compare typologically Latin <i>fortūna</i> (akin to <i>ferō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky</li></ol><p><i>везти́</i> (veztí) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vape (electronic cigarette)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>vape</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) to vape</li></ol><p>From English <i>vape</i> +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>century</li><li>lifetime</li><li>age, epoch, era, period</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) age</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>вѣкъ</i> (věkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) eyelid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣко</i> (věko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everlasting, eternal</li></ol><p><i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>centuries-old, age-old, ancient</li></ol><p><i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) bill of credit, bill of exchange, promissory note</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wechsel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bill of credit, bill of exchange, promissory note</li></ol><p><i>ве́ксель</i> (vékselʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vexillology</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>vexillum</i> (“flag”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) vector (a directed quantity)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) vector (a DNA molecule)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vector; vectoral</li><li>vector-valued</li></ol><p><i>ве́ктор</i> (véktor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) squirrel (mammal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣкъша</i> (věkŭša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) loud, loud-voiced, public</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) magnificent, splendid, grand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, decree, injunction, behest</li></ol><p><i>веле́ть</i> (velétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;literary</i>) to order, to enjoin, to command [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>велѣти</i> (velěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velě̀ti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too big; larger than necessary</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bicycle, bike</li></ol><p>From the first syllable of <i>велосипе́д</i> (velosipéd, “bicycle”) + <i>-ик</i> (-ik, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giantess</li></ol><p><i>велика́н</i> (velikán) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter of a grand prince or grand duchess; princess</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large, big</li><li>great, outstanding</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;poetic,&#32;endearing) the Russian language (literally, “the great and mighty”)</li><li>(humorous,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;sarcastic) Russian profanities</li></ol><p>From Turgenev's 1882 prose poem <i>«Русский язык»</i> («Russkij jazyk», “The Russian Tongue”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grand prince, grand duke</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little too big (of clothes or shoes)</li></ol><p><i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;ironic&#32;in modern usage) overage (too old, no longer a child)</li></ol><p>From <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́зраст</i> (vózrast) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>The change in stress from <i>великовозра́стный</i> (velikovozrástnyj) to <i>великово́зрастный</i> (velikovózrastnyj) occurred as a result of the banalization process. The wordform <i>великовозра́стный</i> (velikovozrástnyj) was formed according to the rule of forming compounds, where a <i>complex bracket</i> is formed if the second part is more than one syllable, and the stress is placed on the last syllable of the stem. But as a result of banalization, in modern pronunciation, the second part of the compound does not obey the old rule and retains the stress in the same place as in the non-derivative word. The modern stress pattern was probably first recorded in Ushakov's Dictionary in 1935.</p><p>Also found in colloquial speech when asked to repeat the word <i>велѝковозрастно́й</i> (velìkovozrastnój). This wordform seems to have been re-analyzed using the compound <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>возрастно́й</i> (vozrastnój) with loose attachment of morphemes.</p><p>Compare with <i>одновреме́нный</i> (odnovreménnyj) which in modern usage developed an additional stress pattern <i>одновре́менный</i> (odnovrémennyj) even though the original stress pattern continues to be used.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) great power</li></ol><p><i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnanimity; generosity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>magnanimously, generously</li></ol><p><i>великоду́шный</i> (velikodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnanimous, generous</li></ol><p><i>великоду́шие</i> (velikodúšije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grand principality, grand duchy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splendour, magnificence</li></ol><p>Calque of Greek <i>μεγαλοπρέπεια</i> (megaloprépeia, “inclination for luxury, splendour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>magnificently, marvelously, splendidly</li></ol><p><i>великоле́пный</i> (velikolépnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnificent, splendid, excellent, grand, elegant</li><li>gorgeous</li></ol><p><i>великоле́пие</i> (velikolépije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) great martyr</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>μεγαλομάρτυς</i> (megalomártus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Great Russian (of or pertaining to Great Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Great Russian (of or pertaining to Great Russians, the language of Great Russia, as opposed to Little Russia, White Russia, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) upper class; aristocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>вели́кий князь</i> (velíkij knjazʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>majestically</li></ol><p><i>велича́вый</i> (veličávyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>august, majestic, imposing, stately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij): greatest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to call</li><li>to celebrate (with a ceremony or a song)</li><li>to style (honorifics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>grandly, augustly, sublimely</li></ol><p><i>вели́чественный</i> (velíčestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grand, august, sublime</li></ol><p><i>вели́чество</i> (velíčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>majesty (the quality of being impressive and great)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>величьство</i> (veličĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>вели́к(ий)</i> (velík(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandeur; greatness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>величьѥ</i> (veličĭje). By surface analysis, <i>вели́к(ий)</i> (velík(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size, magnitude (dimensions or magnitude of a thing)</li><li>quantity, amount</li><li>value</li><li>celebrity, coryphaeus, luminary</li><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics) quantity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veličina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>velodrome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) cycling team</li></ol><p>First attested in 1994. From <i>вело-</i> (velo-, “bicycle”) +&lrm; <i>коню́шня</i> (konjúšnja, “stable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bicycle, bike</li><li>(<i>exercise</i>) bicycle crunch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vélocipède</i>, from Latin <i>vēlōx</i> (“fast”) + <i>pedēs</i> (“feet”). Compare English <i>velocipede</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyclist</li></ol><p>From <i>велосипе́д</i> (velosipéd) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bicycle</li></ol><p><i>велосипе́д</i> (velosipéd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bicycle pump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cycling, biking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) velociraptor</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from New Latin <i>Velociraptor</i>, from <i>vēlōx</i> (“swift”) +&lrm; <i>raptor</i> (“thief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>velveteen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) velvet</li></ol><p><i>вельве́т</i> (velʹvét) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) very, much, very much</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velьmi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandee; magnate; alderman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вельможа</i> (velĭmoža), Old Church Slavonic <i>вельможа</i> (velĭmoža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) noble,</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) grand, lordly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>velour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>velours</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) velour</li></ol><p><i>велю́р</i> (veljúr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>velar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vein</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вена)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vienna (the capital and largest city of Austria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Vienna</i> or French <i>Vienne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a Hungarian person</li></ol><p><i>венге́р(ский)</i> (vengér(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a particular Romani dance</li><li>hussar jacket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hungarian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>венгерскои</i> (vengerskoi) (att. 1630; var. вингерскои ca. 1612), from Old Ruthenian <i>венгерскїй</i> (venherskij), венкгерскїй (17th c.), from Middle Polish <i>węgierski</i>, from <i>Węgier</i> + <i>-ski</i>, from Old Polish <i>Węgier</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgъrinъ</i> of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hungarian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>венгръ</i> (vengr) (att. 1692), from Old Ruthenian <i>венкгръ</i> (vengr) (17th c.), from Middle Polish <i>Węgier</i>, from Old Polish <i>Węgier</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgъrinъ</i> of unclear origin. Doublet of <i>у́грин</i> (úgrin) (<i>no-longer extant</i>) and <i>уго́рец</i> (ugórec) (<i>dated or dialectal</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>venereal, sexually transmitted</li></ol><p>Latin <i>venereus</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venereologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Venezuelan</li></ol><p><i>Венесуэ́ла</i> (Venesuéla) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Venetian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venetan (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Viennese</li><li>crown</li><li>wreath, garland</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) corona</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) halo</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) row of beams</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>вѣньць</i> (věnĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věnьcь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;waiˀníkas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;woyh₁níkos</i>.</p><p>Cognate to <i>вен</i> (ven), <i>венок</i> (venok), <i>ве́ник</i> (vénik), <i>вейка</i> (vejka), <i>вить</i> (vitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venetian (man)</li></ol><p><i>Венеция</i> (Venecija, “Venice”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venetian (woman)</li></ol><p><i>Венеция</i> (Venecija, “Venice”) +&lrm; <i>-анка</i> (-anka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Venice; Venetian</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Italian <i>veneziano</i> +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venereal disease</li></ol><p>From a shortening of <i>венери́ческое заболева́ние</i> (veneríčeskoje zabolevánije, “venereal disease”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cipher, device, monogram (intricately interwoven initials)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>węzeł</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫzlъ</i>. Doublet of <i>у́зел</i> (úzel), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom, besom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣникъ</i> (věnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věnikъ</i>, from <i>&#42;věnъ</i> (“wreath”) + <i>&#42;-ikъ</i>, details of further origin unclear (possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;woh₁y-nos</i> or derived later) but ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i> (“to twist, wind, weave”).</p><p>Cognate to <i>вен</i> (ven), <i>вене́ц</i> (venéc), <i>вено́к</i> (venók), <i>ве́йка</i> (véjka), <i>вить</i> (vitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) bride-price</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣно</i> (věno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>venous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wreath, garland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣнъкъ</i> (věnŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věnъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;věnъ</i> (“wreath”) + <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>, details of further origin unclear (possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóh₁y-nós</i> or derived later) but ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i> (“to twist, wind, weave”).</p><p>Cognate to <i>вен</i> (ven), <i>вене́ц</i> (venéc), <i>ве́йка</i> (véjka), <i>вить</i> (vitʹ), also compare dialectal Latvian <i>vaînuks, vaîņuks</i> (“wreath”).</p><p>Cognates in other Slavic languages include Ukrainian <i>віно́к</i> (vinók), Serbo-Croatian <i>vijenac</i>, Czech <i>věnec</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) IV drug user, mainliner, hype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Viennese</li></ol><p><i>Ве́на</i> (Véna) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fyke net</li><li>hoop net</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Lithuanian <i>vénteris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ventilator</li><li>fan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fan, ventilator</li></ol><p><i>вентиля́тор</i> (ventiljátor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ventilation, vent</li></ol><p><i>вентиля́ция</i> (ventiljácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ventilation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>ventilatio</i>, borrowed through the intermediate of another language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Mystery of Crowning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding <i>(marriage ceremony)</i></li><li>coronation</li></ol><p><i>венча́ть</i> (venčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown <i>(for kingdom)</i></li><li>to place a wreath/garland on someone's head, to crown</li><li>to crown; to be the crowning achievement <i>(of)</i></li><li><i>(building)</i> to crown, to top</li><li>to marry <i>(in church)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>вене́ц</i> (venéc) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ), with iotation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be crowned <i>(for kingdom)</i></li><li>to be crowned <i>(with)</i></li><li>to be married <i>(in church)</i>, to get married <i>(in church)</i></li></ol><p><i>венча́ть</i> (venčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вене́ц</i> (venéc)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) corolla</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) whisk</li><li>(<i>church</i>) funeral headband</li></ol><p><i>вене́ц</i> (venéc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(wild) boar (<i>usually of mature males</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вепрь</i> (veprĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veprь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Veps</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veps (language)</li></ol><p><i>вепс</i> (veps) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belief, faith</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trust, faith (belief in someone's honesty and reliability)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) faith, creed (adherence to a religion&#59; a set of religious beliefs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣра</i> (věra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věra</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣра</i> (věra), Ukrainian <i>ві́ра</i> (víra), Bulgarian <i>вя́ра</i> (vjára), Slovene <i>vẹ́ra</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Polish <i>wiara</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>vērus</i> and German <i>wahr</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вера)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Vera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Вѣра</i> (Věra), from Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣра</i> (věra, “faith, belief”); <i>Вѣра</i> (Věra) is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>Πίστις</i> (Pístis); analogous to English <i>Faith</i>. Compare to <i>Любо́вь</i> (Ljubóvʹ), <i>Наде́жда</i> (Nadéžda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verandah</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pussy willow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>verbal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velьb(l)ǫdъ</i>, <i>&#42;vъlьb(l)ǫdъ</i>, from Gothic <i>𐌿𐌻𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (ulbandus). Probably further from Latin <i>elephantus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐλέφας</i> (eléphas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) camel</li></ol><p>From <i>верблю́д</i> (verbljúd, “camel”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlist, to recruit, to hire</li></ol><p>Attested since 1704. Borrowed from Polish <i>werbować</i>, from German <i>werben</i> (“to recruit”), from Middle High German <i>werben</i>, from Old High German <i>werban</i> (“to turn”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwerban</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwerbaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recruitment, enlistment</li></ol><p><i>вербова́ть</i> (verbovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) wergeld</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wergeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verdict</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anbury</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вередъ</i> (veredŭ), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verdъ</i>. Doublet of <i>вред</i> (vred), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>file, chain, row, line, procession</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verenь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) September</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;вересень</i> (&#42;veresenĭ, “September”). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣсень</i> (vrěsenĭ), whence Old East Slavic <i>врѣсень</i> (vrěsenĭ, “September”). Cognates include Belarusian <i>верасень</i> (vjerasjenʹ, “September”) and Polish <i>wrzesień</i>. Related to Russian ве́реск (véresk, “heather”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) common heather (<i>Calluna vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ве́реск</i> (véresk, “heather”) +&lrm; <i>обыкнове́нный</i> (obyknovénnyj, “normal, common”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heather</li></ol><p><i>ве́реск</i> (véresk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowworm</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) a small mythological creature associated with spinning (yarn making), usually looking like a snake or mouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle</li><li>anchor shank</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>веретено</i> (vereteno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verteno</i> (“spindle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any snake of the genus <i>Atractus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>веретенови́дный</i> (veretenovídnyj, “spindle-shaped”) +&lrm; <i>змея́</i> (zmejá, “snake”), a calque of translingual <i>Atractus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fusiform, spindle-shaped</li></ol><p>From <i>веретено́</i> (veretenó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of New Latin <i>fusiformis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>веретено́</i> (veretenó, “spindle”)</li></ol><p><i>веретено́</i> (veretenó) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal (to scream a high-pitched squeal)</li><li>to chirp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verščati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) gatepost</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) latch, bolt (<i>for locking a gate</i>)</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;dialectal) ridge, strip (<i>of land</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;nautical) wherry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>верѣꙗ</i> (verěja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verěja</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>wherry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a big fellow; spindlelegs; maypole; a lanky man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrzidlo</i>, from <i>&#42;vьrzti</i> (“to knit”). Compare <i>-ла</i> (-la).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chains, fetters <i>(worn by ascetics as an act of penance)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>веригꙑ</i> (verigy), the plural of <i>верига</i> (veriga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veriga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>diplomacy</i>) letter of credence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to believe, to trust [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to believe, to have faith [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “faith”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to be believed</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to believe [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vermicelli</li></ol><p><i>вермише́ль</i> (vermišélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vermicelli</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>vermicelli</i> (“little worms”), from <i>verme</i> (“worm”), from Latin <i>vermis</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vermouth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wermut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ве́рный</i> (vérnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ве́рно</i> (vérno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vernissage (private viewing of an exhibition before it opens to the public)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vernissage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>right, correctly</li><li>truly</li><li>faithfully, loyally, devotedly</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) probably, I suppose, most likely</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) indeed, that's right</li></ol><p><i>ве́рный</i> (vérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loyal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loyalist, loyal subject</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́рный по́дданный</i> (vérnyj póddannyj), a calque of German <i>treuer Untertan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faithfulness, fidelity, loyalty</li><li>chastity</li><li>correctness, accuracy, truth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věrьnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ве́рный</i> (vérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to give back</li><li>to recover, to get back</li></ol><p>From <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrtě̀ti</i>. Compare Lithuanian <i>verčiù</i>, <i>ver̃sti</i>, <i>vir̃sti</i>, Latvian <i>vḕrst</i>, Latin <i>verto</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (wairþan), Ancient Greek <i>ῥατάνη</i> (rhatánē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to come back (from)</li><li>to revert (to)</li></ol><p><i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get back to normal, to return to normal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, faithful, loyal</li><li>true, right, correct</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věrьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faith, belief, creed</li></ol><p><i>ве́ровать</i> (vérovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to believe (in, especially to believe in God)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣровати</i> (věrovati). By surface analysis, <i>вера</i> (vera) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religion (especially one's particular religion), denomination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>faithfully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfidious, treacherous</li><li>(rare,&#32;figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) straight; overly straight actions or words</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Polish <i>wiarołomny</i>, itself possibly a calque of German <i>treubrüchig</i>. First attested in 1717. By surface analysis, <i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>віроло́мний</i> (virolómnyj), Belarusian <i>верало́мны</i> (vjeralómny), Bulgarian and Macedonian <i>вероломен</i> (verolomen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfidy, treachery (the act of violating faith or allegiance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veronese</li></ol><p>From <i>Веро́н(а)</i> (Verón(a), “Verona”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Verona</li></ol><p>From <i>Веро́н(а)</i> (Verón(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious toleration</li></ol><p>From <i>веротерпи́м(ый)</i> (veroterpím(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Can be analysed as <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “religion”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>терпи́мый</i> (terpímyj, “tolerance”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;religion) creed, doctrine</li></ol><p><i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уче́ние</i> (učénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) probability, likelihood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>вероя́тный</i> (verojátnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>вероя́тно</i> (verojátno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most likely, most probably, the chances are better than even</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) possibly, perhaps</li></ol><p><i>вероя́тный</i> (verojátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>probabilistic</li></ol><p><i>вероя́тность</i> (verojátnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>probability, likelihood</li></ol><p>From <i>вероя́тный</i> (verojátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>probable, likely</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “faith; belief”) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ, “to take; to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>versifier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>versification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>version</li><li>account, story, theory, narrative (a version of events)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) verst (unit of length equal to 1,066.8 m), used in many expressions</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) verst-post, milepost, milestone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kilometre</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьрста</i> (vĭrsta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrsta</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>верства́</i> (verstvá), Old East Slavic <i>вьрста</i> (vĭrsta, “age, couple, age-mate, verst (measure of length)”), Old Church Slavonic <i>врьста</i> (vrĭsta, “age”), Bulgarian <i>връст</i> (vrǎst, “age”), Serbo-Croatian <i>вр́ста</i> (“row, type”), Slovene <i>vŕsta</i> (“row, string, type, age”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), <i>vȓst</i> (“row, type”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>vrstva</i> (“layer”), Slovak <i>vrstva</i>, Polish <i>warstwa</i> (“row, layer”), Upper Sorbian <i>woršta</i>. Cognate with Russian <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ, “to turn”); per Vasmer, the initial meaning was "turning over of a plow". Further cognate with Lithuanian <i>var̃stas</i> (“verst, distance plowed at one time in one direction”) (also <i>varsnà</i>), participial <i>vir̃stas</i>, Oscan <i>vorsus</i> (“measure of arable land”, literally “turning over”), Latin <i>versus</i> (earlier <i>vorsus</i>), Sanskrit <i>वृत्त</i> (vṛttá, “round, twisted”). Compare <i>све́рстник</i> (svérstnik, “coeval, age-mate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workbench</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Werkstatt</i> (“workshop”) + <i>-ак</i> (-ak), influenced by <i>верста́ть</i> (verstátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>верста́к</i> (versták).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) person who lays out pages</li></ol><p><i>верста́ть</i> (verstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) to lay out (a page)</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrsta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit, skewer</li></ol><p>From <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;biblical</i>) cave, hideout</li><li>(somewhat&#32;dated,&#32;figuratively) den, lair (<i>especially of criminals or vice</i>)</li><li>(somewhat&#32;dated,&#32;figuratively) slum</li><li>(dated,&#32;Ukraine) large box for puppets displaying nativity scenes or comic scenes</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) performance of such puppets</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врьтьпъ</i> (vrĭtĭpŭ, “cave, garden”), instead of expected <i>&#42;вертёп</i> (&#42;vertjóp), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrtъpъ</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>верте́п</i> (vertép), Bulgarian <i>върто́п</i> (vǎrtóp, “whirlpool”), Slovene <i>vrtèp</i>. Per Vasmer, possibly related to Bulgarian <i>врът</i> (vrǎt, “vegetable garden”), Serbo-Croatian <i>вр̏т</i> (“garden”) as well as Lithuanian <i>vérti</i> (“to lock”) (1sg. <i>veriù</i>), Tocharian B <i>warto</i> (“garden, grove”) (also <i>wärto</i>), Old English <i>weorð</i> (“house fence”) (also <i>worð</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn, to twist, to twirl, to spin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьртѣти</i> (vĭrtěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrtěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang,&#32;idiomatic) to not care; to be indifferent; to despise; to not give a fuck</li></ol><p>Literally, “to spin around on a dick”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around, to revolve, to spin</li></ol><p><i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertical, vertical line</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) file</li><li>(politics,&#32;sociology) hierarchy, reporting chain (hierarchical power structure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;sociology) vertical power structure (a hierarchical administrative system providing top-down command and control)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vertically (in a vertical direction or position)</li></ol><p><i>вертика́льный</i> (vertikálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vertical (being perpendicular with the surface of the Earth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verticordia (<i>Verticordia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Verticordia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disparagingly</i>) flirt, coquette (a flirtatious woman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wryneck</li></ol><p>From <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ше́я</i> (šéja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), a calque of German <i>Wendehals</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trochanter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetabulum (Anatomy&#58; bony cup that receives the head of the thigh bone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trochanter; acetabular</li></ol><p><i>вертлу́г</i> (vertlúg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helicopter</li></ol><p>From <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ, “to turn, to twist”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лёт</i> (-ljót), combining form of <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ, “to fly”).</p><p>Sources seem to converge on the coiner being Nikolay Kamov in the minutes of a meeting of the technical commission of the Central Council of OSOAVIAKHIM, dated February 8, 1929.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>вертолёт</i> (vertoljót, “helicopter”)</li></ol><p><i>вертолёт</i> (vertoljót) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) helicopter</li></ol><p><i>вертолёт</i> (vertoljót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spendthrift, weathercock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) thoughtlessness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) a restless, fidgety person, fidget</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) peavy; cant hook; pivot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) turnkey, jailer, prison guard</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) militiaman, policeman</li><li>employee of the "State Vehicle Inspection" / "State Patrol" (ГАИ, or Государственная автомобильная инспекция)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revolving door, turnstile</li><li>revolving stand</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) rotator</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) turntable</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) government switchboard telephone</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) <i>(of a person, esp. a child)</i> fidget</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) helicopter</li></ol><p><i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>believer, a religious person</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>believing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipyard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>werf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top, upper part, roof, dome, height</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) summit, apex</li><li>the highest degree of something</li><li>upper class</li><li>(<i>arm-wrestling</i>) top-roll</li><li>right side (of clothing, fabric), shoe upper, boot top</li><li>hood, top, soft top, rag top of a carriage or convertible automobile</li><li>cap, cover, outside, shell (clothing, as opposed to a lining)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrxъ</i> (whence also Old Church Slavonic <i>врьхъ</i> (vrĭxŭ)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wers-</i>.</p><p>Balto-Slavic cognates include Lithuanian <i>viršus</i> and Latvian <i>virsus</i>. Other Indo-European cognates include Latin <i>verruca</i> (“a steep place, height”), Ancient Greek <i>ἕρμα</i> (hérma, “reef, rock, hill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Upper Sorbian</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́рхний</i> (vérxnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>лу́жицкий</i> (lúžickij), a calque of German <i>obersorbisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) High German</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́рхний</i> (vérxnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>неме́цкий</i> (neméckij), a calque of German <i>hochdeutsch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper</li></ol><p>From <i>верх</i> (verx) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>up quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper lip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) supremacy, domination</li></ol><p>From <i>верхо́вный</i> (verxóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who rides on top of a horse</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) (in an area which has height differences of valley and mountain) a wind that comes from above</li><li>(<i>professional jargon</i>) someone who works at great heights</li><li>(<i>sports jargon</i>) top-roller, i.e. an arm-wrestler who applies the top-roll technique</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supreme, high (dominant)</li></ol><p>Derived from верх (verx)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supreme court (court of law representing the highest legal authority within a jurisdiction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horseback riding</li><li>located upriver</li><li>going along the surface</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse rider, horseman</li></ol><p><i>верх</i> (verx) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper reaches, river head</li></ol><p>From <i>верхово́й</i> (verxovój) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on horseback</li><li>along the top, on high ground</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to the brim, brim-full</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the upper part of something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top, crest, summit, apex</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) elite, establishment</li></ol><p><i>верх</i> (verx) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish-trap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьрша</i> (vĭrša), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrša</i> (“fishing basket, fishing trap made of osiers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak, top, summit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) acme, culmination</li><li>vertex, apex</li></ol><p><i>верх</i> (verx) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) executor, administrator, decider</li></ol><p><i>верши́ть</i> (veršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to manage, to control, to direct, to do, to accomplish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьršiti</i>, cognates include Bulgarian <i>въ́рша</i> (vǎ́rša, “to do”) and, according to some, Bulgarian <i>върше́я</i> (vǎršéja, “to thresh, to flail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to happen, to occur, to take place</li></ol><p><i>верши́ть</i> (veršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an old Russian unit of length approximately equal to 4.445 centimeters or 1/16 of an arshin</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>верх</i> (verx, “top”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), probably originally "length of the upper phalanx of the index finger".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, line</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьрвь</i> (vĭrvĭ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrvь</i> (deverbal of <i>&#42;verti</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to tie, string; rope”) which is ultimately cognate with <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to turn, bend”) whence the two meanings of the verb. Compare Belarusian <i>вяроўка</i> (vjarówka), Ukrainian <i>верве́чка</i> (vervéčka), Old Church Slavonic <i>връвь</i> (vrŭvĭ). Non-Slavic cognates include Lithuanian <i>virvė</i>, Latvian <i>virve</i>, Latgalian <i>vērve</i> (“rope, cord, line”), Ancient Greek <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “I drag, pull”) and Sanskrit <i>वरत्रा</i> (varatrā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>верёвка</i> (verjóvka): (small) rope</li></ol><p><i>верёвка</i> (verjóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rope</li></ol><p><i>верёвка</i> (verjóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight</li><li>influence, authority, importance</li><li>hanging position, suspended position, balanced position; mostly in the expression <i>на весу́</i> (na vesú, “suspended”, literally “in the hanging position”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věsъ</i>. Related to <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ, “to weigh”) and <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ, “to hang”). For the semantic shift compare Latin <i>pondus</i> (“weight”) and Latin <i>pendo</i> (“to hang, to suspend, to weigh”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ве́село</i> (véselo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to become merry/cheerful</li></ol><p><i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to give joy, to gladden</li><li>to exhilarate, to rejoice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>веселити</i> (veseliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veseliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have fun, to rejoice</li></ol><p><i>весели́ть</i> (veselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>merrily, cheerfully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, fun, merriment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veselьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) merry fellow, humourist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vernal equinox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spring (<i>season</i>)</li><li>springlike</li></ol><p><i>весна́</i> (vesná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh (to have a certain weight)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věsiti</i>. Originally a causative form of <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weighty, heavy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) substantial, significant, serious</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) convincing, persuasive (<i>of an argument or reason</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a weighty manner</li><li>significantly, substantially</li><li>convincingly, persuasively</li></ol><p><i>ве́ский</i> (véskij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oar, paddle, scull</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) spoon</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) rifle, especially a sniper rifle (which looks like an oar)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) ala (side petal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>весло</i> (veslo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veslo</i> (“oar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having legs like oars</li></ol><p><i>весло́</i> (vesló) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring <i>(season)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vesna</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Весна)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Vesna</li></ol><p>See весна.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freckled (having freckles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in spring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freckle</li></ol><p>From <i>весну́ха</i> (vesnúxa, “freckle; fever”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Per Vasmer, the original meaning of <i>весну́ха</i> (vesnúxa) was approximately "spring disease", from <i>весна́</i> (vesná, “spring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freckled (having freckles)</li></ol><p><i>весну́шка</i> (vesnúška) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnography</i>) spring song, vesnianka (Eastern Slavic ritual song and dance)</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) stonefly (order Plecoptera)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) spring draba, shadflower, nailwort, whitlowgrass (<i>Draba verna</i>)</li></ol><p><i>весна́</i> (vesná, “spring”) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) weight</li><li>by weight</li></ol><p><i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weigher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ponderable, heavy</li><li>weighty, influential</li><li>considerable, substantial</li></ol><p><i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-о́мый</i> (-ómyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) west</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) wester (west wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>west</i> (“west”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vestal virgin (priestess of Vesta)</li><li>(literary,&#32;often&#32;ironic) female virgin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Visigoth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>film</i>) western</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;Internet slang) a "westerner" or "westoid" (an activist or online user, usually from English-speaking countries, who disregards actual concerns from Eastern European people&#59; or hates Eastern Europeans for ideological reasons)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>western</i>.</p><p>Popularised during the Russian invasion of Ukraine.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) westernization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to preside over, to chair</li><li>to drive</li><li>to keep; see ведение #Noun 2</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vesti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave</li></ol><p>Literally, “to conduct oneself”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) to act like a bull in a china shop</li></ol><p>Literally, “to act like an elephant in a crockery shop”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobby, foyer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vestibule</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) of course (used to confirm that the person being addressed is listening)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) of course</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in progress, to be underway</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be conducted, to be carried on, to be observed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to yield, to succumb, to give in (to someone's) lies, to provocation, etc.</li></ol><p><i>вести́</i> (vestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, herald</li><li>bulletin</li></ol><p><i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) news-bearing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) orderly</li></ol><p><i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) newsmonger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>весть</i> (vestʹ)</li></ol><p><i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>news, piece of news, message; tidings</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) news (presentation of news, e.g. on television)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(God) knows: only used in expressions, often with <i>бог</i> (box)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věstь</i>.</p><p>Third-person singular present of Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣдѣти</i> (věděti, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scales, balance</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Весы)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Libra (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a set of scales)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Libra (zodiac sign for the scales, covering September 23 — October 22)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) all, the whole, all of, the whole of, the entire, the entirety of</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) all, all (of) (<i>about discrete entities</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used to emphasise the size of a numerical figure, especially in estimates&#59; no less than, no fewer than</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) all (of it), the whole (of it), the entirety (of it), (it) all</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) all (of them), every one (of them), (them) all</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) all (completely) [<i>with</i> adjective <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional)]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) covered [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) just like, takes after (<i>used to express a similarity in personality</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) village</li><li>(<i>history</i>) Ves' (medieval Finnic tribe, ancestors of the Vepsians)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьсь</i> (vĭsĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьśь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wišas</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>विश्व</i> (viśva, “all”).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьсь</i> (vĭsĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьsь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wiśis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i>.</p><p>Compare وِيسُو (wīsū) and اِيسُو (īsū) in medieval Arabic sources.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all day (the entire day)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of oneself</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;before an adjective or noun) all, so, such (a) (used to express contempt about a person's supposed pretentiousness in a certain respect)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) very, highly</li><li>quite, rather</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вьсьма</i> (vĭsĭma), from Old Church Slavonic <i>вьсь</i> (vĭsĭ) + <i>-ьма</i> (-ĭma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj, “cheerful, merry; funny”)</li></ol><p><i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheerfulness, merriment</li></ol><p><i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merry, cheerful, happy, convivial, gleeful, lighthearted</li><li>funny</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>railway&#32;slang</i>) post and baggage train</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veselъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wéselas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wésus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) vet (veterinarian)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>ветерина́р</i> (veterinár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>branchy; boughy</li></ol><p><i>ветвь</i> (vetvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to branch out, to ramify</li></ol><p><i>-ветви́ть</i> (-vetvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch, bough, limb (woody part of a tree arising from the trunk and usually dividing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣтвь</i> (větvĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;větvь</i>, <i>&#42;větь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéh₁itis</i>. Cognates include Bulgarian <i>ве́тва</i> (vétva), <i>ве́тка</i> (vétka), <i>ве́я</i> (véja), and <i>ве́йка</i> (véjka), Slovene <i>vȇja</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>větev</i>, Slovak <i>vetva</i>, Ukrainian <i>ві́та</i> (víta). Non-Slavic cognates include Latin <i>vītis</i> (“vine”), German <i>Weide</i> (“willow”), Old Prussian <i>witwan</i> (“willow”), Persian بید (bid, “willow”) and English <i>withe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) wind</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) the wind(s), flatus</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the wind of change (usually in the phrase <i><b>ве́тер</b> переме́н</i> (<b>véter</b> peremén))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;větrъ</i>.</p><p>Cognates include English <i>nirvana</i> from Sanskrit <i>निर्वाण</i> (nirvāṇa, “blown or put out, extinguished”), and English <i>weather</i>, <i>athlete</i>, <i>vent</i> and <i>wind</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veteran (person with long experience)</li><li>veteran (person who has served in the armed forces, especially an old soldier who has seen long service)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veterinarian or veterinary surgeon, vet (<i>colloquial</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>veterinary</li></ol><p><i>ветерина́рия</i> (veterinárija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ве́тер</i> (véter): wind, breeze</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ветвь</i> (vetvʹ, “branch, twig”): small branch, twig</li><li>line, branch line, sideline</li><li>thread (<i>in a discussion forum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ветвь</i> (vetvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ветка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vietka, Vetka (a town in Belarus, near Gomel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white willow</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>ветвь</i> (vetvʹ), <i>вить</i> (vitʹ). Via PIE cognate with Latin <i>vīmen</i>, English <i>withy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vetluga</li></ol><p><i>Ветлу́га</i> (Vetlúga) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veto (a political right, an invocation of that right)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vetō</i> (“to forbid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ве́тка</i> (vétka): a small branch, twig</li></ol><p><i>ве́тка</i> (vétka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag-and-bone man, ragman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag (piece of cloth)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ветъшь</i> (vetŭšĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>ветъхъ</i> (vetŭxŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>old&#32;poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ве́тер</i> (véter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anemone (any plant of genus anemone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessness</li></ol><p><i>ве́треный</i> (vétrenyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windy (<i>of the weather</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) flighty, not serious (<i>of a person or behavior</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣтреный</i> (větrenyj), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;větrьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ветер</i> (veter) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sail</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>ве́тер</i> (véter)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣ́трило</i> (vě́trilo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vě̀tridlo</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Persian بادبان, Tajik <i>бодбон</i> (bodbon) (<i>бод</i> (bod) (cognate via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wéh₁n̥ts</i>) + <i>бон</i> (bon)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>anemo- (relating to wind)</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́тер</i> (véter).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windbreaker, windcheater, anorak</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka) or <i>ветро́вой</i> (vetróvoj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windshield</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wind</li></ol><p><i>ветр-</i> (vetr-) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind turbine; wind generator</li></ol><p>From <i>вѐтро-</i> (vètro-) +&lrm; <i>генера́тор</i> (generátor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scapegrace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windmill</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гатель</i> (dvígatelʹ), calque of German <i>Windmotor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) A windmeter, an anemometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wind-powered</li></ol><p><i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>си́ла</i> (síla) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój), adjectival calque of German <i>Windkraft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>вѐтро-</i> (vètro-) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windsock</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>указа́тель</i> (ukazátelʹ), a calque of German <i>Windzeiger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind energy; wind power</li></ol><p>From <i>вѐтро-</i> (vètro-) +&lrm; <i>энерге́тика</i> (ɛnɛrgétika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wind energy; wind power</li></ol><p>From <i>вѐтроэнерге́тика</i> (vètroɛnɛrgétika) +&lrm; <i>-ический</i> (-ičeskij). By surface analysis, <i>вѐтро-</i> (vètro-) +&lrm; <i>энергети́ческий</i> (ɛnɛrgetíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windmill, windpump (the machine or the structure housing it)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) wind turbine</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) propeller</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) weathercock (an inconstant person)</li></ol><p>From <i>ветряна́я</i> (vetrjanája) +&lrm; <i>ме́льница</i> (mélʹnica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>ветряна́я</i> (vetrjanája) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wind</li></ol><p><i>ве́тер</i> (véter) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veterinary assistant, veterinary paramedic</li></ol><p>From <i>вет</i> (vet, “vet”) +&lrm; <i>фе́льдшер</i> (félʹdšer, “medical assistant, paramedic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ancient, old</li><li>old, dilapidated, shabby, ramshackle, decrepit, rickety</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ветъхъ</i> (vetŭxŭ), instead of expected <i>вётхий</i> (vjótxij), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vetъxъ</i> (“old, ancient”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old Testament, vetero-testamentary</li><li>antiquated, antediluvian</li></ol><p><i>ве́тхий</i> (vétxij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заве́т</i> (zavét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dilapidation, dilapidated state</li></ol><p><i>ве́тхий</i> (vétxij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ham</li></ol><p><i>ветчина́</i> (vetčiná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay; to dilapidate; to become decrepit</li></ol><p><i>ве́тхий</i> (vétxij) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringdove, wood pigeon</li></ol><p>Probably imitative.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вёх</i> (vjox)</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>wiech</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milestone, landmark</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) bath sponge</li></ol><p><i>ве́хоть</i> (véxotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Pskov) to cry, to shout</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věťati</i>. Doublet of <i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ), a borrowing from Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) veche (a popular assembly in medieval Slavic countries).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣче</i> (věče), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věťe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics,&#32;relational) veche</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening (after 8 PM)</li><li>tonight, this evening</li><li>(<i>formal</i>) party, social gathering, soiree</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;wek(ʷ)speros</td></tr></table></p><p>From Old East Slavic <i>вечеръ</i> (večerŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;večerъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wékeras</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wekʷsperos</i>. Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos) (whence <i>Эспе́р</i> (Espér), <i>гесперо́рнис</i> (gesperórnis), <i>геспери́да</i> (gesperída), <i>геспери́дий</i> (gesperídij)), Latin <i>vesper</i>, Old Armenian <i>գիշեր</i> (gišer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to darken, to become dark; to become twilight</li><li>(<i>old&#32;poetic</i>) to approach evening (<i>of the day</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ве́чер</i> (véčer) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soirée, evening party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening dress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) vespers, evensong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ве́чер</i> (véčer)</li></ol><p><i>ве́чер</i> (véčer) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) supper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;večeřa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, forever, eternally</li><li>perpetually, everlastingly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) constantly</li></ol><p><i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evergreen</li></ol><p>From <i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj), a calque of German <i>immergrün</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věčьnostь</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Bulgarian <i>вечност</i> (večnost), Macedonian <i>вечност</i> (večnost), Polish <i>wieczność</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>вечност</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal, everlasting</li><li>perpetual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věčьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternal flame <i>(flame which burns eternally in memory of someone or something)</i></li></ol><p><i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj) +&lrm; <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) perpetual check</li></ol><p>Literally, “eternal check”; compare Polish <i>wieczny szach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coat hanger, peg (device used to hang up coats, shirts, etc)</li><li>rack (for clothes)</li><li>tab (on clothes)</li></ol><p><i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ, “to hang”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging (action)</li></ol><p><i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to hang up</li><li>to hang <i>(execute)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věšati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to deceive</li></ol><p>Literally, “to hang noodles on the ears”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sometimes with the verb omitted</i>) to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to hang (one's) tongue on (one's) shoulder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang oneself</li></ol><p><i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ве́ха</i> (véxa): boundary post; boundary marker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic&#32;or&#32;dialectal,&#32;relational) spring (<i>season</i>)</li></ol><p><i>весна́</i> (vesná) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broadcasting</li></ol><p><i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to broadcast (to transmit a message or signal via radio waves or electronic means)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to prophesy, foretell</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lay down the law, to play the oracle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣщати</i> (věštati, “to say”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věťati</i>. Doublet of <i>веча́ть</i> (večátʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věščati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ве́щий</i> (véščij, “prophetic, oracular”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material evidence, physical evidence, real evidence, exhibit</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>веще́ственное доказа́тельство</i> (veščéstvennoje dokazátelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thing, object, goods</li></ol><p><i>вещь</i> (veščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>real number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corporeal, real, substantial</li></ol><p><i>вещество́</i> (veščestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substance, material, matter</li></ol><p><i>вещь</i> (veščʹ, “thing”) +&lrm; <i>-ество́</i> (-estvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prophetic, oracular</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вещь</i> (veščʹ): a small thing (usually skilfully or artistically made); gizmo, trinket, artefact, object</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вещь</i> (veščʹ): a small thing</li></ol><p><i>вещь</i> (veščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duffel bag, kitbag, holdall (<i>especially military</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>вещево́й мешо́к</i> (veščevój mešók, “clothing sack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing, object</li><li>things, belongings, goods, stuff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вещь</i> (veščĭ), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вещь</i> (veštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;veťь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winnowing</li><li>waft; breathing, blowing (of the wind)</li><li>trend, tendency; current</li></ol><p><i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to blow gently</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wave, to flutter</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to winnow (grain)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вѣꙗти</i> (vějati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vějati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wḗˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strongly press oneself, to shrink down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вжа́ть</i> (vžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strongly press oneself, to shrink down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вжима́ть</i> (vžimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) whoosh (onomatopoeia denoting a quick action or unexpected change in situation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) upwards, up</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) returning, re-</li></ol><p>Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъz-</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;uź-</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>už</i> (“within, instead of, in return for”), Latvian <i>uz</i> (“on, to”). Further etymology disputed. Compare also <i>воз-</i> (voz-), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) back</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>зад</i> (zad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up and down</li></ol><p><i>взад</i> (vzad) +&lrm; <i>и</i> (i) +&lrm; <i>вперёд</i> (vperjód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back and forth, to and fro</li></ol><p><i>взад</i> (vzad) +&lrm; <i>вперёд</i> (vperjód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mutually, reciprocally</li><li>me too (e.g. I'm glad to meet you too)</li><li>the same to you (the same wishes)</li></ol><p><i>взаи́мный</i> (vzaímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reciprocity, mutuality</li></ol><p>взаи́мный (vzaímnyj) + -ость (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mutual, reciprocal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzajьmьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>co- (together, mutually, jointly)</li></ol><p><i>взаи́мный</i> (vzaímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual influence</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>влия́ние</i> (vlijánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mutually beneficial, mutually advantageous</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>вы́годный</i> (výgodnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interaction, interplay</li><li>cooperation, coordination</li></ol><p>From <i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-, “mutual, co-”) +&lrm; <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije, “action, acting”), a calque of German <i>Zusammenwirkung</i> or <i>Wechselwirkung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interact, to act reciprocally, to cooperate</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствовать</i> (déjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interdependency</li></ol><p>From <i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>зави́симость</i> (zavísimostʹ), a calque of French <i>interdépendance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interchange, interchanging</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>заме́на</i> (zaména)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interchangeability</li></ol><p><i>взаимозаменя́емый</i> (vzaimozamenjájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interchangeable</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>заменя́емый</i> (zamenjájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mutually exclusive</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>исключа́ющий</i> (isključájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual relation, correlation, interrelation</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) rapport</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>отноше́ние</i> (otnošénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual support</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>поддержка</i> (podderžka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual assistance</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>по́мощь</i> (pómoščʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual / common understanding, consensus</li><li>rapport (a relationship of mutual trust and respect)</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-, “mutual, co-”) +&lrm; <i>понима́ние</i> (ponimánije, “understanding”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpenetration, interaction (<i>of cultures, technologies, etc.</i>), mutual influence</li></ol><p><i>взаи́мо-</i> (vzaímo-) +&lrm; <i>проникнове́ние</i> (proniknovénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interconnected, interrelated, relative</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>свя́занный</i> (svjázannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interconnection</li><li>correlation, interdependence</li></ol><p><i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-) +&lrm; <i>связь</i> (svjazʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutual destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>взаимо-</i> (vzaimo-, “mutual, co-”) +&lrm; <i>уничтожение</i> (uničtoženije, “destruction”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on loan, on credit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to feel strong hunger</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) to passionately want something; to crave, to hunger for</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>алка́ть</i> (alkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in exchange (as an exchange or replacement&#59; to reciprocate) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>wzajemny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, indeed, in fact</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) passionately</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>засо́с</i> (zasós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) avidly, excitedly, to the fullest, with gusto</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>захлёб</i> (zaxljób, “excitement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stir, to disturb, to muddy (e.g. water)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to disturb, to trouble, to unsettle, to disquiet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unbalanced, extravagant, whimsical, crotchety</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake up</li></ol><p><i>взболта́ть</i> (vzboltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run up (to run in an upward direction, e.g. up a hill)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run up (to run in an upward direction, e.g. up a hill)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become very angry</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>белен(а́)</i> (belen(á)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>беси́ть</i> (besítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become rabid</li><li>to rage, to be furious, to get/fly into a rage, to fly off the handle, to blow one's top</li></ol><p><i>взбеси́ть</i> (vzbesítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;vernacular,&#32;figuratively) to get scared, to panic</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;vernacular,&#32;figuratively) to say something stupid or off-topic</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бздеть</i> (bzdetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whisk, beater (a kitchen implement for mixing)</li></ol><p><i>взбива́ть</i> (vzbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat up, to fluff, to whip, to whisk (e.g. cream)</li></ol><p><i>взбить</i> (vzbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb up, to clamber up</li></ol><p>From <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>взобра́ться</i> (vzobrátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat up, to fluff, to whip, to whisk (e.g. cream)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to invigorate</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бодри́ть</i> (bodrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to climb with difficulty [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>взбред-</i> (vzbred-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to climb with difficulty [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle a little</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle a little</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бры́знуть</i> (brýznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(a show of)</i> restiveness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to agitate, to disturb, to excite</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>будора́жить</i> (budorážitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite to rebellion/mutiny, to incite to revolt</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>бунтова́ть</i> (buntovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolt, to mutiny</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rebel <i>(against)</i>, to protest <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to rise in waves, to surge, to billow</li></ol><p><i>взбунтова́ть</i> (vzbuntovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telling-off, scolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load <i>(on to)</i>, to lift <i>(on to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to burden <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>взвали́ть</i> (vzvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load <i>(on to)</i>, to lift <i>(on to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to burden <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh (to determine the weight of an object)</li><li>to weigh (to consider a subject)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to lead (someone/something) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘up something’</small>]</li><li>to lift up, to cock (a trigger)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to direct (one's eyes, etc.) up</li><li>to unfairly direct (a charge, slander, etc.) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘against someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspension, dredge</li></ol><p>Calque of New Latin <i>suspensio</i>, whence also <i>суспе́нзия</i> (suspénzija); from <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>весь</i> (vesʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weighing (<i>of an object, idea, etc.</i>)</li><li>evaluation (action or result)</li></ol><p><i>взве́шивать</i> (vzvéšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh (to determine the weight of an object)</li><li>to weigh (to consider a subject)</li></ol><p><i>взве́сить</i> (vzvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go up, to rise, to fly up, to soar</li><li><i>(horse)</i> to rear</li></ol><p><i>взви́ться</i> (vzvítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal, to screech, to yelp</li></ol><p><i>взви́згнуть</i> (vzvízgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal, to screech, to yelp</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ви́згнуть</i> (vízgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift up by screwing, to screw (something) upward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to agitate, to upset, to excite (<i>into a nervous state</i>), to work up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate, to jack up (prices)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>винти́ть</i> (vintítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift up by screwing, to screw (something) upward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to agitate, to upset, to excite (<i>into a nervous state</i>), to work up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate, to jack up (prices)</li></ol><p><i>взвинти́ть</i> (vzvintítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise; to soar up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-, “upwards”) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ, “to twist; to weave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go up, to rise, to fly up, to soar</li><li><i>(horse)</i> to rear</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ви́ться</i> (vítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) platoon</li><li>(<i>firearms</i>) cock, state of being cocked</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) readiness to go off, state of being wound up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to lead (someone/something) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘up something’</small>]</li><li>to lift up, to cock (a trigger)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to direct (one's eyes, etc.) up</li><li>to unfairly direct (a charge, slander, etc.) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘against someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) platoon (military unit)</li></ol><p><i>взвод</i> (vzvod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with emotion, with agitation</li></ol><p><i>взволно́ванный</i> (vzvolnóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agitated, uneasy, disturbed, anxious (<i>of a person, voice, or appearance</i>)</li><li>choppy, rough (<i>of the sea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate, to trouble, to disturb, to worry, to alarm, to upset</li><li>to ruffle, to stir</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>волнова́ть</i> (volnovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surge, to ripple</li><li>to worry, to be upset, to be agitated, to work oneself into a tizzy</li><li>to go off the deep end</li></ol><p><i>взволнова́ть</i> (vzvolnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to raise with a lever</li></ol><p>From взвох (vzvox), *взво́ха (cf. взво́шка (vzvóška)), alteration of взвод (vzvod, “raising”) (as in сва́ха (sváxa), пря́ха (prjáxa)), action noun of взводи́ть (vzvodítʹ); compare подво́х (podvóx), подво́ха (podvóxa), перево́ха (perevóxa). The similarity to взва́жить (vzvážitʹ) might be accidental.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to howl, to howl/scream with pain (laughter, delight)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>выть</i> (vytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glance, look</li><li>view, opinion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzględъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vъz-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ględěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glance, to give a glance, to cast a glance</li></ol><p><i>взгляд-</i> (vzgljad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glance at, to look at</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to take a look (at), to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to take upon oneself (a burden)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>гнести́</i> (gnestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load, to pile up, to heap up</li></ol><p><i>взгромозди́ть</i> (vzgromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perch, to clamber up</li></ol><p><i>взгроможда́ть</i> (vzgromoždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load, to pile up, to heap up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>громозди́ть</i> (gromozdítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perch, to clamber up</li></ol><p><i>взгромозди́ть</i> (vzgromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel sad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense</li></ol><p>According to Max Vasmer, comes from деру́, драть.</p><p>Compare otherwise also Polish <i>bzdura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have an argument, fight, spat, falling out, etc., to have words</li></ol><p><i>вздор</i> (vzdor) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absurd, nonsensical, foolish</li><li>ill-tempered, quarrelsome, cantankerous</li></ol><p><i>вздор</i> (vzdor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appreciation (a rise in value&#59;—opposed to depreciation)</li></ol><p><i>вздорожа́ть</i> (vzdorožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise in price, to go up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дорожа́ть</i> (dorožátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigh (a deep and prolonged audible inspiration or respiration of air, as when fatigued, frustrated, or grieved&#59; the act of sighing)</li><li>breath, breathing in</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;anatomy,&#32;plural only) solar plexus</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вздохну́ть</i> (vzdoxnútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, to take breath</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start, to startle, to flinch, to wince</li></ol><p><i>вздро́гнуть</i> (vzdrógnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nap, to snooze</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дрема́ть</i> (dremátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start, to startle, to flinch, to wince</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дро́гнуть</i> (drógnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telling-off, scolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to bloat, to cause to swell</li><li>to blow (something) upward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate (prices)</li></ol><p><i>вздуть</i> (vzdutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swell up</li></ol><p><i>вздува́ть</i> (vzduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think of (doing), to take it into one's head (to), to decide (to)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>to enter someone's head, to come to someone's mind</li></ol><p><i>взду́мать</i> (vzdúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to bloat, to cause to swell</li><li>to blow (something) upward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate (prices)</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;low colloquial) to beat up, to thrash</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swell up</li></ol><p><i>взду́ть</i> (vzdútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (a horse, etc.) to rear up</li><li>to cause (hair, etc.) to stand on end</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дыбы́</i> (dybý) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (a horse, etc.) to rear up</li><li>to cause (hair, etc.) to stand on end</li></ol><p><i>взды́бить</i> (vzdýbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to raise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въздымати</i> (vŭzdymati). Usually explained from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzdymati</i> (“blow up”), from <i>&#42;dymati</i>, iterative of <i>&#42;dǫti</i>. Alternatively, although less likely, it might be a result of rebracketing of <i>подыма́ть</i> (podymátʹ) as <i>по</i>-<i>дыма́ть</i>, at least in the modern sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to heave</li></ol><p><i>вздыма́ть</i> (vzdymátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, to take breath</li><li>to long (for), to sigh (for), to pine (after, for), to yearn (after, for)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въздыхати</i> (vŭzdyxati). By surface analysis, <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>уздыха́ць</i> (uzdyxácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hitch up, to jerk up, to stand up quickly and sharply</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang, to string up</li></ol><p><i>вздёрг-</i> (vzdjorg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise up, to shoot up, to spring up</li></ol><p><i>вздёргивать</i> (vzdjórgivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raised up (<i>of a body part such as the nose or lips</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hitch up, to jerk up</li><li>(<i>colloquial, literary</i>) to hang, to string up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise up, to shoot up, to spring up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang oneself, to commit suicide</li></ol><p><i>вздёрнуть</i> (vzdjórnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>levying, collection (of taxes, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>взима́ть</i> (vzimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, to levy, to take monies (in forms of taxes, payments, duties, tariffs, and fines)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възьмати</i> (vŭzĭmati). By surface analysis, <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be levied, to be collected</li></ol><p><i>взима́ть</i> (vzimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to look <i>(at)</i>, to gaze <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>-зира́ть</i> (-zirátʹ), with reduction of &#42;взз- to вз-; compare <i>взыва́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (vzyvátʹ), <i>воззва́ть</i>&nbsp;pf (vozzvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break open, to force, to burst in, to break <i>(through)</i></li><li><i>(program)</i> to crack</li></ol><p><i>взлома́ть</i> (vzlomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly up, to soar, to take off, to take wing</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to zoom</li></ol><p><i>взлете́ть</i> (vzletétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly up, to soar, to take off, to take wing</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to zoom</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>break-in, breaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break open, to force, to burst in, to break <i>(through)</i></li><li><i>(program)</i> to crack</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burglar, yegg, housebreaker</li><li>hacker</li></ol><p><i>взлома́ть</i> (vzlomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tousle, to dishevel, to ruffle</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>лохма́тить</i> (loxmátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tousle, to dishevel, to ruffle</li></ol><p><i>взлохма́тить</i> (vzloxmátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight, ascension, ascent</li><li>take-off (of airplanes)</li><li>rise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzletъ</i>, deverbal from <i>&#42;vъzletěti</i>, ancestor of <i>взлете́ть</i> (vzletétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) takeoff (<i>of an airplane, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>взлёт</i> (vzljot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stroke, sweep (of oars, etc.), wave, flap, flapping (of wings, etc.), swing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flap, to wave, to strike <i>(with)</i>, to make strokes <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>взмахну́ть</i> (vzmaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flap, to wave, to strike <i>(with)</i>, to make strokes <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fling up, to throw up, to raise (dust, snow, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fling up, to throw up, to raise (dust, snow, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to toss up (<i>in several throws</i>)</li></ol><p><i>взмет-</i> (vzmet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cock (a rifle or gun)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flap (wings)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rise, to rise above others</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw up rapidly with force (as one’s hands)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to hike, to increase, to inflate</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>метну́ть</i> (metnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot up</li></ol><p><i>взметну́ть</i> (vzmetnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get wet/soaked</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be covered with sweat</li></ol><p><i>взмо́кнуть</i> (vzmóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get wet/soaked</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be covered with sweat</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>мо́кнуть</i> (móknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg, to implore</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eelgrass, zostera (<i>Zostera</i>-genus plants)</li></ol><p>From <i>взмо́рье</i> (vzmórʹje, “sandy shore”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seashore, seaside, beach (sandy shore)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roiling, stirring-up</li></ol><p><i>взмучивать</i> (vzmučivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rocket, to soar up upwards, to shoot upwards</li></ol><p><i>взмыть</i> (vzmytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(horse)</i> foamy, lathery</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sweating, dripping with sweat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rocket, to soar up upwards, to shoot upwards</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurl up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment; fee; contribution; instalment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вз-</i> (vz-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb up, to clamber up</li></ol><p><i>взо-</i> (vzo-, “upwards”) + <i>брать</i> (bratʹ, “take”) + <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sublimate</li></ol><p><i>взогна́ть</i> (vzognátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mount, to go up, to climb, to ascend</li><li>to rise (<i>of a heavenly body</i>)</li><li>to come up, to sprout (<i>of a plant</i>)</li></ol><p><i>взо-</i> (vzo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sweat</li></ol><p><i>взо-</i> (vzo-) +&lrm; <i>преть</i> (pretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>look, glance, gaze</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) eyes</li><li>(aviation,&#32;dated) Vzor optical orientation device (used on the early Восток spacecraft, it used mirrors to orient the spacecraft manually using the sun in order to give the best views of the Earth)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to blow up, to detonate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to enrage, to exasperate (<i>also used impersonally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>взо-</i> (vzo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to blow up, to detonate</li><li>to become enraged, to become exasperated, to explode verbally</li></ol><p><i>взорва́ть</i> (vzorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow, to cultivate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring up, to nurture</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) beautiful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>възрачьныи</i> (vŭzračĭnyi), from <i>въ-</i> (vŭ-) +&lrm; <i>зракъ</i> (zrakŭ) +&lrm; <i>-ныи</i> (-nyi); related to <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow, to cultivate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring up, to nurture</li></ol><p><i>взрасти́ть</i> (vzrastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, to utter a roar</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>реве́ть</i> (revétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maturation, growing up</li></ol><p><i>взросле́ть</i> (vzroslétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mature, to grow up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grown, grown-up, adult</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) grown-up, adult</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explosion</li><li>blast</li><li>detonation</li><li>eruption</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>взрыва́ть</i> (vzryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) fuze</li></ol><p><i>взрыва́ть</i> (vzryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to blow up, to detonate, to blast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to enrage, to exasperate (<i>also used impersonally</i>)</li><li>to plough up, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>взрыть</i> (vzrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to blow up, to detonate</li><li>to become enraged, to become exasperated, to explode verbally</li></ol><p><i>взрыва́ть</i> (vzryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) explosive</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) explosive, violent</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) plosive</li></ol><p><i>взрыв</i> (vzryv) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) explosive</li></ol><p><i>взрыв</i> (vzryv) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>опа́сный</i> (opásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combined explosion hazard and fire hazard</li></ol><p><i>взрывопожароопа́сный</i> (vzryvopožaroopásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) explosives, explosive material</li></ol><p><i>взры́вчатый</i> (vzrývčatyj, “explosive”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explosive</li></ol><p><i>взры́в</i> (vzrýv) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough up, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obstinate and angry</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ерепе́ниться</i> (jerepénitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tousle, to ruffle, to rumple</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>еро́шить</i> (jeróšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) telling-off, scolding</li></ol><p>From <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ёб</i> (job).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) telling-off, scolding</li></ol><p>From <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ёб</i> (job) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Siberia) elevated place; uphill section of a road</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Siberia) instep <i>(of a foot or shoe)</i></li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>взнима́ть</i> (vznimátʹ), взымать (vzymatʹ). The combining form -ём is the normal deverbal equivalent of <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)/<i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ); compare archaic first singular present tense <i>емлю́</i> (jemljú) of <i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appeal <i>(to someone for)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ), with reduction of &#42;взз- to вз-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start frolicking, to jump for joy</li><li>to swell (<i>of the sea, wind, a river, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manifest strongly (<i>of a feeling</i>), to swell (<i>of pride</i>), to leap (<i>of the heart</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frolic, to jump for joy</li><li>to swell (<i>of the sea, wind, a river, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manifest strongly (<i>of a feeling</i>), to swell (<i>of pride</i>), to leap (<i>of the heart</i>)</li></ol><p><i>взыгра́ть</i> (vzygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penalty, punishment</li><li>recovery, exaction</li></ol><p><i>взыска́ть</i> (vzyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creditor, claimant</li></ol><p><i>взыска́ть</i> (vzyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demanding, exacting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exact <i>(from, of)</i>, to recover <i>(from)</i></li><li>to make answer <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exact <i>(from, of)</i>, to recover <i>(from)</i></li><li>to make answer <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>взыска́ть</i> (vzyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seizure, capture</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) capture (Russian notation: :)</li></ol><p><i>взять</i> (vzjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bribe, graft, palm-oil</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) trick</li></ol><p><i>взять</i> (vzjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bribetaker (a person who takes bribes)</li></ol><p><i>взя́тка</i> (vzjátka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bribery</li></ol><p><i>взя́точник</i> (vzjátočnik, “bribetaker”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzęti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to take the bull by the horns</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get the better of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to receive exclusive rights to something in exchange for money</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to claim, to take exclusive control over (a project, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the liberty of doing something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;informal) to pull out of thin air, to make up, to pull out of one's ass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull oneself together, to get oneself together, to get it together, to get a grip, to get one's act together, to get ahold of oneself</li></ol><p>Literally, “to take oneself into one's hands”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take something with hands, to take (mutually, together) (e.g. each other's hands)</li><li>to set about; to undertake [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘a task’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appear, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>взять</i> (vzjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> V, v</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transport</i>) viaduct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vibrator (various senses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vibration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vibrate, to oscillate</li><li>to quaver</li><li>to shimmy</li><li>to thrill</li><li>to flutter</li><li>to jar</li><li>to pulsate</li><li>to chatter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vivisection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>vigintillion (10<sup>63</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>look, looks, appearance, air</li><li>sight, view</li><li>kind, sort, species</li><li>form</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) aspect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>видъ</i> (vidŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vidъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wéiˀdas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéydos</i>, from <i>&#42;weyd-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Lithuanian <i>veidas</i> (“face”), Latvian <i>veids</i> (“form, shape”), Sanskrit <i>वेद</i> (veda, “knowledge”), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬉𐬛𐬀𐬵-</i> (vaēdah-, “possession”), Latin <i>videō</i> (“to see”), Ancient Greek <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form, shape”), Old Irish <i>fíad</i>, Welsh <i>gŵydd</i> (“presence”) (from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;weido-</i>), and Old English <i>witan</i> (whence English <i>wit</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>speciēs</i> (&lt; <i>speciō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) videocassette recorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see (<i>many times, frequently</i>); iterative of <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</li><li>to be seen (<i>in the negative sentence, usually in the infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>):</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see, to meet, to see each other</li></ol><p><i>вида́ть</i> (vidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vision, sight</li><li>apparition, dream, ghost</li></ol><p>From <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;viděti</i> (“to see”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to know; to see”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>veidas</i> (“face”), Latvian <i>veids</i> (“form, shape”), Sanskrit <i>वेद</i> (veda, “knowledge”), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬉𐬛𐬀𐬵-</i> (vaēdah-, “possession”), Latin <i>videō</i> (“to see”), Ancient Greek <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form, shape”), Old Irish <i>fíad</i>, Welsh <i>gŵydd</i> (“presence”) (from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;weido-</i>), and Old English <i>witan</i> (English <i>wit</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>video</i>, further from Latin <i>videō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>video- (related to moving images, and especially to television)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>videoblog, vlog (weblog using video as its primary presentation format)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>videoblog</i>. Composed as if from <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>блог</i> (blog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video display, visual display unit</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>диспле́й</i> (displɛ́j, displéj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video(tape) recording</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>за́пись</i> (zápisʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video call</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>звоно́к</i> (zvonók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video game (game controlled by a software)</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>игра́</i> (igrá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video information</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>информа́ция</i> (informácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video camera</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>ка́мера</i> (kámera)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video card</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>ка́рта</i> (kárta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>videocassette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>videocassette</i>. Composed as if from <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>кассе́та</i> (kasséta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) music video (motion picture accompanying a song)</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>клип</i> (klip)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video conference</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>конфере́нция</i> (konferéncija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>videocassette recorder, VCR</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>магнитофо́н</i> (magnitofón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) videocassette recorder</li></ol><p><i>видеомагнитофо́н</i> (videomagnitofón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video material, video, footage</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>материа́л</i> (materiál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video surveillance</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>наблюде́ние</i> (nabljudénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) video message, video address</li></ol><p>From <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>обраще́ние</i> (obraščénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>videotape (tape used to record videos)</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>плёнка</i> (pljónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video message</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>сообще́ние</i> (soobščénije). Compare Ukrainian <i>відеоповідо́млення</i> (videopovidómlennja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video library, videocassette library</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>библиоте́ка</i> (bibliotéka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teletext</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>Videotext</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>video film</li></ol><p><i>видео-</i> (video-) +&lrm; <i>фильм</i> (filʹm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see (perceive by eyesight)</li><li>to see (to be able to perceive by eyesight)</li><li>to meet (someone)</li><li>to understand</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to detect the presence of a component</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;viděti</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>ви́дя</i> (vídja), Latin <i>videō</i>, English <i>wit</i>, <i>wise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see each other, to meet; to date</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to see, to seem</li></ol><p><i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) widget</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>widget</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) videotape recorder</li></ol><p><i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apparently, evidently</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) noticeably, appreciably</li></ol><p><i>ви́димый</i> (vídimyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visibility</li><li>outward appearance, semblance</li></ol><p><i>ви́димый</i> (vídimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vidimъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seen, to be visible</li></ol><p><i>ви́дный</i> (vídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) evidently, obviously, apparently, it looks like</li></ol><p><i>ви́дный</i> (vídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible, conspicuous</li><li>outstanding, eminent, prominent</li><li>stately, portly</li></ol><p><i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aspectual</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) specific</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) landscape</li></ol><p><i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evolve, to transform somewhat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evolve, to transform somewhat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viewfinder (device)</li></ol><p><i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>иска́тель</i> (iskátelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) video (short video production)</li></ol><p><i>видео</i> (video) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>видо́с</i> (vidós); video (short video production)</li></ol><p><i>видо́с</i> (vidós) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</li></ol><p><i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vis-à-vis (counterpart)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vis-à-vis</i> (“face-to-face”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup, cosmetics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>visage</i>; in terms of the sense, back-formation from <i>визажи́ст</i> (vizažíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup artist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>visagiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine (native of Byzantium)</li></ol><p><i>Византи́я</i> (Vizantíja) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Byzantine (relating to the Byzantine Empire)</li></ol><p><i>Византи́я</i> (Vizantíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantism, Byzantinism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scream, squeal, shriek, screech</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>визжа́ть</i> (vizžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shrill, screechy (<i>of a voice or sound</i>)</li><li>screechy, prone to screeching or screaming (<i>of a person or manner</i>)</li></ol><p><i>визг</i> (vizg) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal once</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vizgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal (to scream a high-pitched squeal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vizgati</i>, ultimately of imitative origin, similar to Czech <i>svišť</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visionary, mystic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>visionnaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viewfinder; cursor; sighting device</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Visier</i> (“visor”), French <i>visière</i> (“visor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vizier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish وزیر (vezīr), from Arabic وَزِير (wazīr, “helper, aide, minister”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visit, call</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>vīsitō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business card</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) morning coat</li></ol><p><i>визи́т</i> (vizít) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business card, visiting card, calling card</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) calling card, signature (something distinctly characteristic of someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) visiting</li></ol><p><i>визи́т</i> (vizít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visitor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>visiteur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visualization (the act of visualizing, or something visualized)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>visually</li></ol><p><i>визуа́льный</i> (vizuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visual</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>visualis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) "viy" (a creature with gigantic eyelids and eyelashes from hell who kills by looking at people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>вій</i> (vij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) vetch, tare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вика</i> (vika), from Old Polish <i>wyka</i>, <i>wika</i>, from Middle High German <i>wicke</i>, Old High German <i>wicka</i>, from Latin <i>vicia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vicar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wiki (collaborative website)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>wiki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Viking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Wikipedia</li></ol><p><i>Википе́дия</i> (Vikipédija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wikipedian (a person)</li></ol><p><i>Википе́дия</i> (Vikipédija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Wiktionary, Russian Wiktionary</li></ol><p><i>Викислова́рь</i> (Vikislovárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viscount (a member of the peerage above a baron but below a count or earl)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vicomte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Victorian era (a period in British history)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiz (a test of knowledge)</li></ol><p>First appeared in the Russian magazine Ogoniok in 1928. Mikhail Koltsov, the magazine's founder, applied the term to a selection of puzzles in the magazine. He had named the feature after a fellow employee who prepared the feature, <b>Виктор</b> Микул<b>ин</b> (<b>Viktor</b> Mikul<b>in</b>). Later, people began to associate his puzzle with the Latin term <i>victor</i> (“winner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cutlery,&#32;chess,&#32;cycling) fork</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) plug (<i>with two or more prongs</i>)</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) bracket (the zone between a long and a short shot impact)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) band, range (<i>of prices, values, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ви́лы</i> (víly) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); originally a diminutive of <i>ви́лы</i> (víly), i.e. “small pitchfork”; the modern cutlery sense is probably a semantic loan from French <i>fourchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>villa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>villa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) villein</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>villanus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) head of cabbage</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate) weak, wretched man; pussy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ви́лка</i> (vílka): little fork</li></ol><p><i>ви́лка</i> (vílka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fork</li></ol><p><i>ви́лка</i> (vílka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitchfork, digging fork</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vidla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wag</li><li>to twist, to zigzag, to make sharp turns, <i>(river)</i> to meander</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prevaricate, to be evasive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>виля́ть</i> (viljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vilnius (<i>capital of Lithuania</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Ви́льнюс</i> (Vílʹnjus) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wag</li><li>to twist, to zigzag, to make sharp turns, <i>(river)</i> to meander</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prevaricate, to be evasive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;viljati</i>, imperfective of <i>&#42;viliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guilt, fault; blame</li><li>(<i>music</i>) veena</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вина</i> (vina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vina</i>; cognate with Latvian <i>vaina</i>.</p><p>From a Dravidian language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) Windows</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Ви́ндоуз</i> (Víndouz) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windsurf board</li></ol><p>From English <i>windsurf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windsurfing</li></ol><p>Borrowing from English <i>windsurfing</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegret, a Russian salad</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a mixture, a blending</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vinaigrette</i> (“type of salad dressing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spades (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♠)</li></ol><p>Linked to Belarusian <i>ві́ны</i> (víny). Further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;uncountable) vinyl (<i>the material</i>)</li><li>(music,&#32;countable) vinyl (<i>the phonograph record</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vinyl, vinylic</li></ol><p><i>вини́л</i> (viníl) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) accusative</li></ol><p>From <i>вини́ть</i> (vinítʹ, “to accuse”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Calque of Latin <i>accūsātīvus</i> (“related to accusing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse, to blame</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;viniti</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>виня́</i> (vinjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) wine</li></ol><p><i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) winkel (color and letter coded patch used by Nazis to classify prisoners in concentration camps)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Winkel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vinaceous, vinous</li></ol><p>From <i>ви́нный</i> (vínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj), calque of German <i>weinrot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wine</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) guilt</li><li>guilty</li></ol><p><i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>вина́</i> (viná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Replaced by винова́тый (vinovátyj) in the 19th century due to homonymy with Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) any strong alcoholic beverage (especially vodka)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вино́</i> (vinó), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vīnò</i>, from Latin <i>vīnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>guiltily</li></ol><p><i>винова́тый</i> (vinovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty</li></ol><p>Ostensibly from <i>вина́</i> (viná) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj), but since the suffix is normally attached to adjectives and not nouns, may instead represent a dissimilation of <i>вино́вный</i> (vinóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj). Replaced ви́нный (vínnyj) in the 19th century due to homonymy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culprit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guilt, culpability</li></ol><p><i>вино́вный</i> (vinóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>culpable, guilty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culprit</li></ol><p>From <i>вина</i> (vina) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) grape, grapes</li><li>vintage</li><li>vine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>виноградъ</i> (vinogradŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vinogordъ</i>. Calqued from Germanic (compare German <i>Wingert</i>, English <i>vineyard</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viticulture (agricultural practice of growing grape vines)</li></ol><p><i>виногра́дарь</i> (vinográdarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viticulturist, winegrower</li></ol><p><i>виногра́д</i> (vinográd) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grape</li></ol><p><i>виногра́д</i> (vinográd) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p><i>виногра́д</i> (vinográd) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grape</li></ol><p><i>виногра́д</i> (vinográd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vintner, winemaker (manufacturer of wine)</li></ol><p>From <i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-дел</i> (-del), combining form of <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ, “to do, to make”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winemaking (the act of making wine)</li></ol><p><i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), possibly, a calque of German <i>Weinfabrikation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winery (a place where wine is made, or a company that makes wine)</li></ol><p><i>виноде́лие</i> (vinodélije) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wine-making, wine-producing</li></ol><p><i>виноде́лие</i> (vinodélije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distiller (person who distills, especially alcoholic spirits or hard liquor by a process of distillation)</li></ol><p>From <i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-кур</i> (-kur), combining form of <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ, “to distill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distillery</li></ol><p><i>виноку́р</i> (vinokúr) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vintner (seller of wine)</li></ol><p><i>вино́-</i> (vinó-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>торго́вец</i> (torgóvec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screw</li><li>propeller</li><li>vint (card game, variety of whist)</li><li>(slang,&#32;computing) hard disk (from <i>Winchester</i>, the codename of IBM 3340 drive)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) methamphetamine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>gwint</i>, from German <i>Gewinde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>винт</i> (vint): (small) screw</li><li>cog in the machine</li></ol><p><i>винт</i> (vint) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten or loosen (a screw)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to take to the police</li><li>to play vint</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to reply evasively</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to leave</li></ol><p><i>винт</i> (vint, “screw”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p><p><i>винт</i> (vint, “vint”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiral staircase, screw stair</li></ol><p><i>винтова́я</i> (vintovája, “screw-shaped”) + <i>ле́стница</i> (léstnica, “staircase”). Compare English <i>screw stair</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rifle</li></ol><p><i>винтов(ой)</i> (vintov(oj), “screw; spiral”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) screw</li><li>fitted with a screw</li><li>screw-shaped, helical</li><li>propeller-driven</li></ol><p><i>винт</i> (vint) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rifle</li></ol><p><i>винто́вка</i> (vintóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threading die, T-antenna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>screw-cutting</li></ol><p><i>винт</i> (vint) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>schraubenschneidend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiral-horned</li></ol><p>From <i>винт</i> (vint) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), a calque of New Latin <i>strepsicerus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вино́</i> (vinó)</li></ol><p><i>вино́</i> (vinó) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Winchester <i>(rifle)</i></li><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) hard disk, Winchester <i>(disk)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) vignette (a decorative design that looks like a vine at the beginning/ending of a chapter in a book, manuscript etc.)</li><li>(<i>postal</i>) image on a postage stamp</li><li>(film,&#32;photography) vignetting (effect where qualities vanish toward edges)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) willow</li></ol><p>With doubts inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vīňàga</i> (“grape”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cello</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;dialectal) whirlpool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;virъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) wergeld</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) lift!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вира</i> (vira), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vira</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>vira</i>, second-person singular imperative of <i>virare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, curve</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) banking, turn, horizontal turn</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) toning solution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>virage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virama</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>विराम</i> (virāma, “to stop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a native or resident of the Colony of Virginia</li><li>Virginian (a native or resident of the state of Virginia in the United States of America).</li></ol><p>From <i>Вирги́ния</i> (Virgínija, “Virginia”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vireo (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>vireō</i>. The final 'н' is due to the inflected forms from Latin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virtual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virtuoso</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>virtuose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>masterfully, in a virtuosic manner</li></ol><p><i>виртуо́зный</i> (virtuóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virtuosity</li></ol><p><i>виртуо́зный</i> (virtuóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virtuosic, masterful</li></ol><p><i>виртуо́з</i> (virtuóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) virus (DNA/RNA causing disease)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) computer virus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Virus</i>, from Latin <i>vīrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to go viral</li></ol><p><i>ви́рус</i> (vírus) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) virus</li><li>viral</li></ol><p><i>ви́рус</i> (vírus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) hang</li></ol><p>From висе́ть (visétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallows, gibbet</li><li>hangman <i>(word game)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to be suspended</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>висѣти</i> (visěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;visěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whiskey/whisky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>whiskey</i>, <i>whisky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viscose</li><li><i>(artificial silk)</i> rayon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bismuth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bismuth</li></ol><p><i>ви́смут</i> (vísmut) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to droop, to hang down, to dangle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to hang on (to), to cling (to)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;computing) to freeze up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;visnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) temple</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ, “to hang”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók); original meaning (still found dialectally) "hair hanging at the temples". Compare Belarusian <i>виска́та</i> (viskáta, “slovenly woman with disheveled hair”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leap (referring to leap years, leap days, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>високостныи</i> (visokostnyi), from <i>високостъ</i> (visokostŭ, “leap day”), from Byzantine Greek <i>βίσεξτος</i> (bísextos), from Late Latin <i>bissextus</i>, from Latin <i>bisextus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leap year (366-day year in the Gregorian calendar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neuroanatomy</i>) temporal lobe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) temporal, temple</li></ol><p><i>висо́к</i> (visók, “temple (of the head)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanging Gardens of Babylon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hanging, suspended</li></ol><p><i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitamin</li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>vitamine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitamin B<sub>2</sub></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitamin C</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitamin D</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vitamin</li></ol><p><i>витами́н</i> (vitamín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hover (to float in the air)</li><li>to soar (to fly aloft with little effort&#59; to rise in thought, spirits, or imagination&#59; to be exalted in mood)</li><li>to dwell, to be present invisibly, to be in the air (of smell, of sensation, etc.)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to dwell, to stay somewhere</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to greet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>витати</i> (vitati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vitati</i> (“to dwell; to welcome”). Primary meanings "to hover, to soar, to linger (in air)" arose due to the verb's common usage in Old Church Slavonic to refer to birds in the trees.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vitebsk</li></ol><p><i>Ви́тебск</i> (Vítebsk) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vitebsk</li></ol><p><i>Ви́тебск</i> (Vítebsk) +&lrm; <i>-я́нин</i> (-jánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vitebsk</li></ol><p><i>Ви́тебск</i> (Vítebsk) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) vitex</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Vitex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grandiloquent</li><li>intricate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twisted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coil</li><li>turn (<i>of a spiral</i>)</li><li>round, stage, phase</li><li><i>(at the orbit)</i> circuit</li></ol><p><i>вить</i> (vitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stained glass window</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vitrage</i>. Further from Latin <i>vitrum</i> (whence also <i>витри́на</i> (vitrína)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stained glass</li></ol><p><i>витра́ж</i> (vitráž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shop window</li><li>showcase, vitrine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vitrine</i>. Further from Latin <i>vitrum</i> (whence also <i>витра́ж</i> (vitráž)).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>výkladec</i>, Macedonian <i>излог</i> (izlog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shop window</li></ol><p><i>витри́на</i> (vitrína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind</li><li>to twist, to weave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вити</i> (viti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vī́tī</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(hair)</i> to wave, to curl</li><li><i>(plants)</i> to twine <i>(about, round)</i></li><li><i>(birds, insects etc.)</i> to hover</li><li><i>(sut etc.)</i> to eddy, to whirl</li><li><i>(river, road)</i> to wind, to meander</li><li><i>(snake)</i> to twist, to writhe</li><li>to flutter</li></ol><p><i>ви́ть</i> (vítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ветю́тень</i> (vetjútenʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) hero, knight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vitędzь</i>. Doublet of <i>ви́кинг</i> (víking).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Bethlehem (a city in Palestine)</li></ol><p><i>Вифлее́м</i> (Viflejém) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to zigzag; to stagger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuft, forelock, cowlick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vortex; vortical</li></ol><p><i>вихрь</i> (vixrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swirl, to spin around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlwind</li><li>vortex, swirl</li><li>eddy, twirl</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) personificated whirlwind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вихърь</i> (vixŭrĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vixъrъ</i>. Via PIE cognate with <i>вить</i> (vitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>vice- (deputy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) vice admiral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice-governor, deputy governor</li></ol><p><i>ви́це-</i> (více-) +&lrm; <i>губерна́тор</i> (gubernátor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice-mayor</li></ol><p><i>ви́це-</i> (více-) +&lrm; <i>мэр</i> (mɛr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice president</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vice-presidential</li></ol><p><i>ви́це-президе́нт</i> (více-prezidént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice-presidency</li></ol><p><i>ви́це-президе́нт</i> (více-prezidént) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy prime minister, vice prime minister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy speaker, vice-speaker</li></ol><p><i>ви́це-</i> (více-) +&lrm; <i>спи́кер</i> (spíker)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ВИЧ-инфе́кция</i> (VIČ-infékcija)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ВИЧ-инфекция)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>HIV infection</li></ol><p><i>ВИЧ</i> (VIČ) +&lrm; <i>инфе́кция</i> (infékcija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ВИЧ-инфици́рованный</i> (VIČ-inficírovannyj)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ВИЧ-инфици́рованный</i> (VIČ-inficírovannyj)</li></ol><p><i>вич</i> (vič) +&lrm; <i>инфици́рованный</i> (inficírovannyj)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ВИЧ-инфицированный)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>HIV-infected</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>HIV-infected person</li></ol><p><i>ВИЧ</i> (VIČ) +&lrm; <i>инфици́рованный</i> (inficírovannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icing on the cake, cherry on the cake</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>wisienka na torcie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>вишнёвый</i> (višnjóvyj)</li></ol><p><i>ви́шня</i> (víšnja) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherry, sour cherry (<i>Prunus cerasus</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;višьňa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of liqueur made of cherry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cherry, sour cherry (relating to <i>Prunus cerasus</i> and its fruit)</li><li>(dark) cherry (<i>color</i>), cherry red</li></ol><p><i>ви́шня</i> (víšnja) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>пе́рсиковый</i> (pérsikovyj), <i>лимо́нный</i> (limónnyj), <i>ора́нжевый</i> (oránževyj), <i>грана́товый</i> (granátovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>ви́дишь</i> (vídišʹ)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) see!; (just) look, listen; there; oh; now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inject <i>(to)</i>, to give a shot <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work hard, to sweat <i>(one's guts out)</i></li></ol><p><i>вколо́ть</i> (vkolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig in, to plant in, to dig <i>(into)</i>, to plant <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>вкопа́ть</i> (vkopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>on, to turn on (typical caption on electric switches, "ON")</li><li>inclusion</li><li>(<i>publishing</i>) inset</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on, to turn on (typical caption on electric switches, "ON")</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>including</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>включе́ние</i> (vključénije).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>включи́тельно</i> (vključítelʹno).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>включа́я</i> (vključája).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>вкла́дка</i> (vkládka) or <i>вкле́йка</i> (vkléjka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deposit (money placed in an account), account (<i>in a bank</i>)</li><li>contribution (something given or offered that adds to a larger whole)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>клад</i> (klad, “treasure”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inset, insert, inlay, supplementary sheet</li><li>(browser) tab</li></ol><p><i>вкла́д(ывать)</i> (vklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depositor, investor</li></ol><p><i>вкла́д(ывать)</i> (vklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose, to insert, to put in; to sheathe; to embed</li><li>to invest, to deposit</li><li>to contribute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit (<i>in</i>)</li><li>to invest (<i>in</i>), to buy (<i>into</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be devoted (<i>to</i>), to devote oneself (<i>to</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вкла́дывать</i> (vkládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bushing (type of bearing to reduce friction)</li><li>insert, inset</li><li>bearing shell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to glue, to paste</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to glue, to paste</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to insert with a wedge, to wedge [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something, e.g. a hole or slot’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to insert (a word, remark, etc.) into someone's speech</li></ol><p>From <i>вкли́нить</i> (vklínitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or from <i>вклини́ть</i> (vklinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jut out in a wedge shape</li><li>to rush, to penetrate [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into the middle of something’</small>]</li><li>to interrupt, to meddle [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something, e.g. a conversation or relationship’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вкли́нивать</i> (vklínivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to insert with a wedge, to wedge [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something, e.g. a hole or slot’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to insert (a word, remark, etc.) into someone's speech</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-кли́ни́ть</i> (-klínítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jut out in a wedge shape</li><li>to rush, to penetrate [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into the middle of something’</small>]</li><li>to interrupt, to meddle [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something, e.g. a conversation or relationship’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>вкли́ни́ть</i> (vklínítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to switch on, to power up, to enable (to put a mechanism, device or system into operation)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to include (to bring into as a part or member)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>inclure</i> or German <i>einschließen</i>, ultimately a calque of Latin <i>inclūdō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-ключа́ть</i> (-ključátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be included in, to become part of</li><li>to join, to take part [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v) <small>‘in’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>включа́ть</i> (vključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>including</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclusion (addition or annex to a group, set or total)</li><li>turning on, switching on</li><li>connection</li><li>powering up, starting up</li></ol><p><i>включи́ть</i> (vključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inclusive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to switch on, to power up, to enable (to put a mechanism, device or system into operation)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to include (to bring into as a part or member)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>inclure</i> or German <i>einschließen</i>, ultimately a calque of Latin <i>inclūdō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be included in, to become part of</li><li>to join, to take part [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v) <small>‘in’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>включи́ть</i> (vključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in, hammer in, knock in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat in so that it enters, to slot</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inject <i>(to)</i>, to give a shot <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) entirely, completely, absolutely, utterly</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>коне́ц</i> (konéc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig in, to plant in, to dig <i>(into)</i>, to plant <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die hard; to become hard to change (<i>of a custom or idea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insinuatingly, ingratiatingly</li></ol><p><i>вкра́дчивый</i> (vkrádčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smarmy, ingratiating, insinuating, subtle</li></ol><p><i>вкрад-</i> (vkrad-) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal in, to creep in, to slip in</li></ol><p><i>вкрад-</i> (vkrad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclusion, speck, droplet</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) dissemination, impregnation</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>кропи́ть</i> (kropítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije), calque of German <i>Einsprengung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal in, to creep in, to slip in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сться</i> (krástʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>summarily, in a nutshell, briefly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въкратцѣ</i> (vŭkratcě).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to get a crush on (to gain feelings for, to fall in love with) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>краш</i> (kraš) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>haphazard; at random</li><li>unevenly; awry; crookedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hard-boiled</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>круту́ю</i> (krutúju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) together, coupled</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въкоупѣ</i> (vŭkupě). By surface analysis, <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ку́пе</i> (kúpe, “bunch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taste (one of the sensations produced by the tongue)</li><li>flavour/flavor, fancy</li><li>style, manner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въкоусъ</i> (vŭkusŭ), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kusъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to partake <i>(of)</i>, to taste <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въкоусити</i> (vŭkusiti). By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>вку́сно</i> (vkúsno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>that tastes good, yummy</li><li>that smells delicious</li><li>that smells fragrantly</li></ol><p>From <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abstract noun of <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something delicious and enjoyable to eat (<i>concrete noun</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вку́сн(ый)</i> (vkúsn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abstract noun of <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something delicious and enjoyable to eat (<i>concrete noun</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tasty (having a pleasant flavor), yummy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъkusьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вкус</i> (vkus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abstract noun of <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something delicious and enjoyable to eat (<i>concrete noun</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>вкус</i> (vkus, “taste”), with the suffix <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>food</i>) delicacy, titbit</li></ol><p>From <i>вку́сн(ый)</i> (vkúsn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-яшка</i> (-jaška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) taste; gustatory</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) flavor, flavoring</li><li>(grossly) subjective, arbitrary, based on personal taste</li></ol><p><i>вкус</i> (vkus) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to partake <i>(of)</i>, to taste <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>вкуси́ть</i> (vkusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влага</i> (vlaga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volga</i>. Displaced the Old East Slavic inherited form <i>воло́га</i> (vológa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vaginal</li></ol><p><i>влага́лище</i> (vlagálišče) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to enclose, to insert, to put in; to sheathe; to embed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вълагати</i> (vŭlagati). By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ). Compare it's neutral counterpart <i>вкла́дывать</i> (vkládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;of plants) Requiring high levels of moisture or watering.</li></ol><p>From <i>вла́га</i> (vlága, “moisture”) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ, “to love, to like”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dampproof, moisture-proof</li></ol><p><i>вла́га</i> (vlága) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>владе́льческий</i> (vladélʹčeskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) govern, control</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>владати</i> (vladati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voldati</i>, iterative of <i>&#42;volsti</i>. Doublet of <i>воло́да́ть</i> (volódátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner</li></ol><p><i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) possessory, proprietary</li></ol><p><i>владе́лец</i> (vladélec) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession, ownership</li><li>property, domain, estate</li><li>fluency, knowledge, mastery (of a language)</li></ol><p><i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to own, to possess, to be master of, to be in possession of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to govern, to control [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to master, to manage, to wield [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>владѣти</i> (vladěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volděti</i> (“to rule”).</p><p>Compare the inherited doublet <i>володе́ть</i> (volodétʹ). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>владе́я</i> (vladéja, “to control, possess”), Latin <i>valeō</i> (“to be worth, be strong”), English <i>wield</i>, German <i>walten</i> (“to rule, prevail”), Macedonian <i>владее</i> (vladee, “to govern, be familiar with”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vladimir (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Влади́мир</i> (Vladímir) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereign, lord, arbiter, overlord (judge without control)</li><li>(Eastern Orthodoxy,&#32;respectful) bishop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>владꙑка</i> (vladyka), itself derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>владѣти</i> (vladěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volděti</i> (“to expand”). Cognates include Bulgarian <i>влади́ка</i> (vladíka), Macedonian <i>владика</i> (vladika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, sway, dominion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>владꙑчьство</i> (vladyčĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>влады́ка</i> (vladýka) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to rule, to reign, to dominate, to prevail</li></ol><p><i>влады́чество</i> (vladýčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pre-1918 spelling</i>) abbreviation of <i>владѣ́льческій</i> (vladě́lʹčeskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>владе́льческий</i> (vladélʹčeskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dampish</li></ol><p><i>вла́жный</i> (vlážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humidity (dampness, especially that of the air)</li></ol><p><i>вла́жный</i> (vlážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humid, moist, damp</li></ol><p><i>вла́га</i> (vlága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break into, to forcibly enter [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)]</li></ol><p><i>вломи́ться</i> (vlomítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) hair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>власъ</i> (vlasŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vȏlsъ</i>. Doublet of <i>во́лос</i> (vólos), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule (over), to hold sway (over), to wield power (over)</li></ol><p><i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord (especially feudal), landowner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>властелинъ</i> (vlastelinŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, arbiter, sovereign</li></ol><p><i>власть</i> (vlastʹ, “rule”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>overpoweringly</li><li>in a domineering or peremptory manner</li></ol><p><i>вла́стный</i> (vlástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperious, commanding, authoritative, masterful</li></ol><p><i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust for power, power ambition</li></ol><p><i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority, authorities, power</li><li>rule, regime</li><li>control</li><li>(<i>law</i>) force (legal validity)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Slavic <i>&#42;volstь</i>Old Church Slavonic <i>власть</i> (vlastĭ)bor.Russian <i>власть</i></p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>власть</i> (vlastĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volstь</i>. Doublet of <i>во́лость</i> (vólostʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) to drag, to draw</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влачити</i> (vlačiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volčiti</i>. Doublet of <i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the left</li></ol><p>From <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ле́во</i> (lévo), an old short neuter accusative singular of <i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get into (something, also figurative), to climb into, to scramble into</li><li>to fit in, to go in</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>влезть</i> (vleztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get into (something, also figurative), to climb into, to scramble into</li><li>to fit in, to go in</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick in, to fasten in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slap <i>(on)</i>, to give a smack <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick in, to fasten in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slap <i>(on)</i>, to give a smack <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>влепи́ть</i> (vlepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run into (to enter while running), to burst into</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to get into trouble</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run into (to enter while running), to burst into</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to get into trouble</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strong inclination, attraction</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) attraction, pulling toward</li></ol><p><i>вле́к-</i> (vlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attract, to draw, to pull, to haul</li><li>to entail, to bring about</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влѣщи</i> (vlěšti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velťi</i>. Doublet of <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drag along (<i>e.g. on the floor</i>)</li><li>to move slowly or with difficulty</li><li>to last, to drag out (<i>e.g. of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to strive for, to reach for</li></ol><p><i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infusion, injection, pouring in</li></ol><p><i>влива́ть</i> (vlivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to instill [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>влить</i> (vlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow in/into</li><li>to join</li></ol><p><i>влива́ть</i> (vlivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowing into</li><li>affluent (tributary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get stuck <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to get caught <i>(in)</i>, to get <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вли́пнуть</i> (vlípnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get stuck <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to get caught <i>(in)</i>, to get <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ли́пнуть</i> (lípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) like a glove, fitting perfectly [<i>with</i> <b>как or словно</b> (kak or slovno)]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to instill [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow (in, into)</li><li>to join</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>influence, effect</li><li>weight, credibility, power</li></ol><p><i>влия́ть</i> (vlijátʹ, “to influence”) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ве́яние</i> (véjanije) (&lt; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)), <i>пове́трие</i> (povétrije) &lt; <i>ве́тер</i> (véter); <i>вдохнове́ние</i> (vdoxnovénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>influential</li></ol><p><i>влия́ть</i> (vlijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p><p>Compare cognate: Czech <i>vlivný</i> (i.e. **вливный), Macedonian <i>влијателен</i> (vlijatelen); and noncognate: Polish <i>wpływowy</i>, Slovak <i>vplyvný</i> (akin to <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ), i.e. **вплывовый, **влывный), Serbo-Croatian <i>утицајан</i> (akin to <i>течь</i> (tečʹ), i.e. **утечайный, **утечный, **утечливый).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to pour in</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to influence, to have an influence [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on sb/sth’</small>]</li><li>to affect, to govern</li><li>to have effect, to have action</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въльꙗти</i> (vŭlĭjati). In the modern senses, a semantic loan from French <i>influer</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>наве́ять</i> (navéjatʹ), <i>навева́ть</i> (navevátʹ), <i>ве́яние</i> (véjanije) (&lt; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)), <i>пове́трие</i> (povétrije) &lt; <i>ве́тер</i> (véter); <i>вдохнови́ть</i> (vdoxnovítʹ), <i>вдохновля́ть</i> (vdoxnovljátʹ), <i>вдохнове́ние</i> (vdoxnovénije) (akin to <i>вдохну́ть</i> (vdoxnútʹ), compare Latin <i>īnspīrō</i>, whence <i>инспири́ровать</i> (inspirírovatʹ)); <i>наносно́е</i> (nanosnóje) (&lt; <i>наноси́ть</i> (nanosítʹ)); <i>напускно́е</i> (napusknóje) (&lt; <i>напуска́ть</i> (napuskátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vlog, videoblog (weblog using video as its primary presentation format)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>vlog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure (something enclosed)</li><li>attachment (file sent along with an email)</li><li>investment</li></ol><p><i>вложи́ть</i> (vložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose, to insert, to put in; to sheathe; to embed</li><li>to invest, to deposit</li><li>to contribute</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit (<i>in</i>)</li><li>to invest (<i>in</i>), to buy (<i>into</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be devoted (<i>to</i>), to devote oneself (<i>to</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вложи́ть</i> (vložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit hard, to wallop</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to punish</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;school) to give a negative mark</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to imprison (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to break out, to start doing (something) suddenly and forcefully</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to place (a bet)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to betray</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break into, to forcibly enter [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (someone) to fall in love, to enamor</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in love (to come to have feelings of love) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>влюби́ть</i> (vljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (someone) to fall in love, to enamor</li></ol><p><i>влюби́ть</i> (vljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in love (to come to have feelings of love) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>влюбля́ть</i> (vljubljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, infatuation, being in love</li></ol><p><i>влюблённый</i> (vljubljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in love, enamored (affected with love, experiencing love&#59; of a person)</li><li>(strongly) fond (of) (passionate about some hobby, profession, cause)</li><li>amorous (expressing love&#59; of a look, tone, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a propensity to fall in love</li></ol><p><i>влю́бчивый</i> (vljúbčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amorous (referring to someone who easily falls in love)</li></ol><p><i>влюби́ть</i> (vljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to insert badly, sloppily or inappropriately</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ля́пать</i> (ljápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to step, to plunge (<i>into something dirty</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get into trouble</li></ol><p><i>вля́пать</i> (vljápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to insert badly, to sloppily or inappropriately</li></ol><p><i>вля́пать</i> (vljápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to step, to plunge (<i>into something dirty</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get into trouble</li></ol><p><i>вля́пывать</i> (vljápyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-ую</i> (-uju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>вме́сто</i> (vmésto, “instead of”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix with cement, to mortar, to putty, etc.</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit, to slap</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to use alcohol, to drink</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to make an injection, usually of a narcotic</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix with cement, to mortar, to putty, etc.</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit, to slap</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to use alcohol, to drink</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to make an injection, usually of a narcotic</li></ol><p><i>вма́зать</i> (vmázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get stuck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to regard (something as something else)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въмѣнити</i> (vŭměniti), from <i>въ-</i> (vŭ-) +&lrm; <i>мѣнити</i> (měniti, “to think”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měniti</i> (“to think”). Related to <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ) and <i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be considered [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘as something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘with respect to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вмени́ть</i> (vmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanity, imputability</li></ol><p>From <i>вменя́емый</i> (vmenjájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), calque of French <i>imputabilité</i> coined at the end of the 18th century by Stepan Rumovsky.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) sane (able to act consciously and be responsible for one's actions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to regard (something as something else)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въмѣнꙗти</i> (vŭměnjati). By surface analysis, <i>вмени́ть</i> (vmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be considered [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘as something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘with respect to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вменя́ть</i> (vmenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together, coupled with, along with</li><li>at the same time</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>въ мѣстѣ</i> (vŭ městě), made of Old East Slavic <i>въ</i> (vŭ, “in”) and Old East Slavic <i>мѣсто</i> (město, “a place”); ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъměstě</i> (“along with”). Alternatively, from Old East Slavic <i>въ мѣстѣхъ</i> (vŭ městěxŭ).</p><p>Analyzable as a univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ме́сте</i> (méste), prepositional singular of <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to throw the baby out with the bathwater</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time</li><li>moreover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep (something) (<i>somewhere or into something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí). Compare Czech <i>vmést</i>, Kashubian <i>wmiesc</i>, and Polish <i>wmieść</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container, receptacle</li></ol><p><i>вмести́ть</i> (vmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(holding) capacity</li></ol><p><i>вмести́мый</i> (vmestímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) able to hold, having capacity, capacious</li></ol><p><i>вмести́ть</i> (vmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capacity (ability to hold); capacity (a measure of such ability), volume</li><li>capaciousness, spaciousness, roominess (ability to hold a lot)</li></ol><p><i>вмести́тельный</i> (vmestítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), possibly, a calque of German <i>Geräumigkeit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capacious, roomy, spacious</li></ol><p><i>вмести́ть</i> (vmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contain</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>instead of</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto, “place”). Originated in the early 17th century, where it can be seen in legal documents as a change from <i>в</i> (v) + <i>genitive noun</i> + <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) ("in [something's] place") to <i>вме́сто</i> (vmésto) + <i>genitive noun</i> ("instead [of something]").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sewing</i>) to baste, to tack</li><li>to sweep (something) (<i>somewhere or into something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ). Compare Czech <i>vmetat</i>, Kashubian <i>wmiatac</i>, and Polish <i>wmiatać</i>.</p><p>From <i>вмет-</i> (vmet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interference, meddling, intervention</li></ol><p><i>вмеша́ть</i> (vmešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo), calque of German <i>Einmischung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interfere; to mix in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get someone involved (in trouble etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interfere, to intervene, to meddle</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interfere, to intervene</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>вмеша́ться</i> (vmešátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contain</li></ol><p><i>вмести́ть</i> (vmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вмеща́ть</i> (vmeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in an instant, in a moment, in a flash, in a twinkling, in no time</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>миг</i> (mig)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set into, to build in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>монти́ровать</i> (montírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sewing</i>) to baste, to tack</li><li>to throw in</li></ol><p><i>вмета́ть</i> (vmetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>вметну́ть</i> (vmetnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>на</i> (na), used as a neutral form by bloggers in order to prevent interethnic disputes. Only used in the context of Ukraine.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thrown over the shoulders</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>в нату́ре</i> (v natúre), in the sense "really", "indeed"</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>нату́ре</i> (natúre).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first, first (at the beginning)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>начале</i> (načale)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for hire, for lease</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>наём</i> (najóm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>out of, outside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вънѣ</i> (vŭně). Cognate with <i>вон</i> (von), Slovene <i>vənè</i> (“outside”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), <i>vèn</i> (“out”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>vně</i> (“outside”), <i>ven</i> (“out”), Kashubian <i>wnie</i> (“outside”), <i>wen</i> (“out”). See <i>вон</i> (von) for further Slavic cognates. According to Vasmer, most likely related to Sanskrit <i>वन</i> (vána, “forest”), <i>वने</i> (váne, “in the forest”), although also sometimes compared with Sanskrit <i>विना</i> (vínā, “without, except for”), Old Prussian <i>wins</i> (“air”), <i>winna</i> (“outward”), <i>winnen</i> (“weather”, accusative).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>extra- (outside, beyond)</li></ol><p>From <i>вне</i> (vne).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extramarital, illegitimate (taking place outside marriage)</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>unehelich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>off-budget, extrabudgetary</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>бюдже́т</i> (bjudžét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extradepartmental</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домственный</i> (védomstvennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timeless</li></ol><p><i>вне</i> (vne) +&lrm; <i>временно́й</i> (vremennój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>less common</i>) alternative form of <i>вневременно́й</i> (vnevremennój); timeless</li></ol><p><i>вне</i> (vne) +&lrm; <i>вре́менный</i> (vrémennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any off-road vehicle, all-terrain vehicle</li><li>four-wheel drive, landrover, SUV</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-, “beyond, off”) +&lrm; <i>доро́га</i> (doróga, “road”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) inculcation, intrusion</li><li>introduction (the act or process of introducing)</li></ol><p><i>внедри́ть</i> (vnedrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant <i>(in)</i>, to embed <i>(in)</i></li><li>to introduce, to adopt, to implement</li><li>to inculcate <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>не́дра</i> (nédra, “womb”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become embedded <i>(in)</i></li><li>to penetrate</li></ol><p><i>внедри́ть</i> (vnedrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant <i>(in)</i>, to embed <i>(in)</i></li><li>to introduce, to adopt, to implement</li><li>to inculcate <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>внедри́ть</i> (vnedrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become embedded <i>(in)</i></li><li>to penetrate</li></ol><p><i>внедря́ть</i> (vnedrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly, all of a sudden</li></ol><p><i>внеза́пный</i> (vnezápnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suddenness</li></ol><p><i>внеза́пный</i> (vnezápnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sudden, unexpected</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>вънезапѫ</i> (vŭnezapǫ) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;neza(j)apьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extraterrestrial</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extracellular</li></ol><p>From <i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>кле́точный</i> (klétočnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extracontinental</li></ol><p>From <i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>континента́льный</i> (kontinentálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extrauterine</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-, “extra-; outside”) +&lrm; <i>ма́тка</i> (mátka, “uterus”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, “-ic”), calque of Latin <i>extrauterinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extra, extraordinary, special</li><li>out of order, out of turn</li></ol><p>From <i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>очередно́й</i> (očerednój), a calque of German <i>außerordentlich</i>. Attested since the 19·00s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extrafamilial</li></ol><p>From <i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>семе́йный</i> (seméjnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing in, carrying in</li><li>entry, entering, insertion</li></ol><p><i>внести́</i> (vnestí) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extrasystemic</li></ol><p>From <i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>систе́мный</i> (sistémnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry in, to bring in</li><li>to pay in, to deposit</li><li>to bring in / about, to cause, to introduce</li><li>to enter, to include, to insert</li><li>to move, to bring in, to bring / put forward, to submit, to table</li><li>(in expressions)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush <i>(into)</i>, to run <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>внести́</i> (vnestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extrajudicial (carried out without legal authority)</li></ol><p><i>вне-</i> (vne-) +&lrm; <i>суде́бный</i> (sudébnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) appearance, look (physical appearance)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;politics) foreign policy</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet) external link</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outwardly, externally, outside, outward, outdoors</li></ol><p><i>вне́шний</i> (vnéšnij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foreign policy</li></ol><p><i>вне́шний</i> (vnéšnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>полити́ческий</i> (politíčeskij), possibly a calque of German <i>außenpolitisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foreign trade</li></ol><p><i>вне́шний</i> (vnéšnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>торго́вый</i> (torgóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>external economic, foreign economic</li></ol><p><i>вне́шний</i> (vnéšnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>экономи́ческий</i> (ekonomíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outer, exterior, outside, external</li><li>outward</li><li>superficial, surface</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) external, foreign</li></ol><p>From <i>вне</i> (vne) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij); compare Old Church Slavonic <i>вънѣштьнъ</i> (vŭněštĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical appearance (how someone or something looks from the outside)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, look (physical appearance)</li><li>exterior, outside</li></ol><p><i>вне́шний</i> (vnéšnij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign policy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>external link</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>вне́шняя торго́вля</i> (vnéšnjaja torgóvlja, “foreign trade”): foreign trade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>downwards</li><li>downstairs (direction)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>низ</i> (niz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>below, underneath</li><li>at the foot of, at the bottom of</li><li>downstairs (located downstairs)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>низу́</i> (nizú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate, to delve into, to consider carefully</li></ol><p><i>вни́кнуть</i> (vníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate, to delve into, to consider carefully</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>вруби́ться</i> (vrubítʹsja). Also compare <i>втяну́ться</i> (vtjanútʹsja). Also note <i>вдолби́ть</i> (vdolbítʹ), <i>втемя́шить</i> (vtemjášitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attention</li><li>care, heed</li><li>note, notice</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>attention! <i>(also military)</i></li><li>warning!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въниманиѥ</i> (vŭnimanije). By surface analysis, <i>внима́ть</i> (vnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>attentively, carefully</li></ol><p><i>внима́тельный</i> (vnimátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attentiveness, attention (mental focus)</li></ol><p><i>внима́тельный</i> (vnimátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attentive, observant, observing, mindful, watchful</li></ol><p><i>внима́ть</i> (vnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;stilted) to listen (to); to hear; to hark (to); to hearken; to heed, to pay heed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вънимати</i> (vŭnimati). By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) in a draw, in a tie</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ничью́</i> (ničʹjú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) again, once again</li><li>newly, recently</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>новь</i> (novʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry in, to bring in</li><li>to pay in, to deposit</li><li>to bring in / about, to cause, to introduce</li><li>to enter, to include, to insert</li><li>to move, to bring in, to bring / put forward, to submit, to table</li><li>(in expressions)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush <i>(into)</i>, to run <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вноси́ть</i> (vnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandson</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) grandchildren</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вънукъ</i> (vŭnukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъnukъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>internally, intrinsically</li><li>inwardly</li><li>domestically (as opposed to foreign)</li></ol><p><i>вну́тренний</i> (vnútrennij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inner, interior</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) domestic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вънутрьнии</i> (vŭnutrĭnii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъnǫtrьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtyard (unroofed walled area)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrails, guts, internal organs, intestines</li><li>insides, innards, contents</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interior, inside</li><li><i>pl.</i> internal organs, innards, viscera, entrails, intestines, bowels, garbage, interior</li></ol><p><i>вну́тренний</i> (vnútrennij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domestic policy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internal link</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in or into the interior; inside</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in, inside, within (<i>position</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>intra-</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри́</i> (vnutrí, “inside, within”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intraatomic</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>а́том</i> (átom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>inneratomar</i> or French <i>intra-atomique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intravenous</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>ве́на</i> (véna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of Latin <i>intravenosus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) in-game</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>игрово́й</i> (igrovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intracellular</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of Latin <i>intracellularis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intrauterine</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>ма́тка</i> (mátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of New Latin <i>intrauterinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intramolecular</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>молекуля́рный</i> (molekuljárnyj), calque of French <i>intramoléculaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intramuscular</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>мы́шца</i> (mýšca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of Latin <i>intramuscularis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intraparty, inner-party</li></ol><p><i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>па́ртия</i> (pártija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly, a calque of German <i>innerparteilich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) domestic policy</li></ol><p><i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>полити́ческий</i> (politíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;sex) intrasexual</li></ol><p><i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incretory, endocrine</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>секрето́рный</i> (sekretórnyj), calque of German <i>innersekretorisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intrauterine</li></ol><p>From <i>внутри-</i> (vnutri-) +&lrm; <i>утро́ба</i> (utróba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>into, inside (<i>direction</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъnǫtrь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grandchildren</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) alternative form of <i>внуча́тый</i> (vnučátyj)</li></ol><p><i>внуча́та</i> (vnučáta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of one's grandchildren's generation; grand- (<i>referring to generations downward, not upward</i>)</li></ol><p><i>внук</i> (vnuk) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandnephew, great-nephew (nephew's son)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>внук</i> (vnuk) (little) grandson</li></ol><p><i>внук</i> (vnuk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>внучо́к</i> (vnučók), diminutive of <i>внук</i> (vnuk) little grandson</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) grandchildren</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arouse (fear, etc.), to instill/inspire (respect, etc.)</li><li>to convince, to suggest</li></ol><p><i>внуши́ть</i> (vnušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suggestion, infusion; hypnosis</li><li>reprimand, reproof</li></ol><p><i>внуши́ть</i> (vnušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impressively, imposingly</li></ol><p><i>внуши́тельный</i> (vnušítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressive</li><li>imposing</li></ol><p><i>внуши́ть</i> (vnušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arouse (fear, etc.), to instill/inspire (respect, etc.)</li><li>to convince, to suggest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въноушити</i> (vŭnušiti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sniff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>distinctly, clearly, audibly (in context of being heard)</li></ol><p><i>вня́тный</i> (vnjátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distinct</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>внят-</i> (vnjat-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;stilted) to listen (to); to hear; to hark (to); to hearken; to heed, to pay heed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вънѩти</i> (vŭnjęti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъnęti</i>, from <i>&#42;vъ(n)-</i> (see <i>&#42;vъ(n)</i>) + <i>&#42;ęti</i>. Reanalyzed as (and synchronically analyzable as) <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ), from which other words in <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ) were created, such as <i>наня́ть</i> (nanjátʹ), <i>обня́ть</i> (obnjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>в</i> (v): in, at, on; to, into; etc.</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>вот</i> (vot)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in favor of, for the sake of, for the benefit of</li></ol><p><i>во</i> (vo) +&lrm; <i>бла́го</i> (blágo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>during (within a given time interval)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>во всем мире</i> (vo vsem mire)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>во всем мире</i> (vo vsem mire)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>publicly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the top of one's lungs (very loudly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around the world, in the world</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the name of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at all costs (sparing no effort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>в-</i> (v-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secondly, second (in the second place)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first, first of all, for one thing, firstly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vobla, Caspian roach (<i>Rutilus caspicus</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) an ugly and skinny woman</li><li>(politics,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;euphemistic) war, special military operation</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obьlъ</i>.</p><p>The politics sense derives from a 2022 court case in which an anti-war activist was acquitted of discrediting the Army by claiming that her words <i>нет в&#42;&#42;&#42;е</i> (net v&#42;&#42;&#42;je) meant <i>нет во́бле</i> (net vóble, “no to vobla”), and not <i>нет войне́</i> (net vojné, “no to war”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>во́бла</i> (vóbla): (small) vobla, Caspian roach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to absorb, to imbibe</li><li>to suck in, to drink in</li><li>to inhale</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to involve [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>to drag into, to embroil</li></ol><p><i>вовлек-</i> (vovlek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get involved [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вовлека́ть</i> (vovlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing in, involving, involvement</li></ol><p><i>вовле́к-</i> (vovlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to involve [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>to drag into, to embroil</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get involved [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вовле́чь</i> (vovléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) outside, beyond (the limits)</li></ol><p><i>во</i> (vo) +&lrm; <i>вне</i> (vne)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in time, on time, timely</li><li>opportunely, timely (at the right time)</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at all</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>весь</i> (vesʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) as much or strongly as one can, to one's utmost</li></ol><p>From <i>во</i> (vo) +&lrm; <i>всю</i> (vsju). Literally, “to (one's) all”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to herd (cattle, etc.) into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hammer in, to force in</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concavity (the state of being concave)</li></ol><p><i>во́гнутый</i> (vógnutyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend inwards</li></ol><p>From <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ). Compare Silesian <i>wgnōńć</i> and Polish <i>wgiąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Mansi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li><li>soft drink, soda</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) tide</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) watery prose, fluff</li><li>it (the person who chases the other players in tag and similar games)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вода</i> (voda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voda</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wandō</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wódr̥</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>उद्र</i> (udra), Ancient Greek <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr) → English <i>hydro-</i>, English <i>whisky</i> and <i>water</i>.</p><p>From <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a). Compare <i>воево́да</i> (vojevóda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>водоле́й</i> (vodoléj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle; to be stationed</li><li>to restore order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaudeville</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vaudeville</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (vehicle) driver</li></ol><p><i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide, leader (a person who guides others or leads the way)</li><li>driver, operator (<i>of an automobile or other land vehicle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to drive”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxi driver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) driver's license (documenting permitting this person to drive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) driver (<i>of a vehicle</i>); driver's</li></ol><p><i>води́тель</i> (vodítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driver's license/driver's licence (documenting permitting this person to drive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) leadership, command</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) work of a driver/chauffeur</li></ol><p><i>води́тель</i> (vodítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to conduct, to guide</li><li>to drive</li><li>to move</li><li>to breed</li><li>to be it in tag and similar games</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voditi</i> (“to lead, conduct”). Cognate with English <i>wed</i> and <i>wage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;figurative) to lead by the nose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to associate with, to play with, to consort with</li><li>to be found, to live, to be</li></ol><p><i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”)</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вода́</i> (vodá): water</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) vodka</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) flavored liquor, liqueur</li><li>(pharmacology,&#32;obsolete) tincture (a solution of some substance in alcohol for use in medicine or cosmetics)</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;archaic) aqua, acid</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix). First attested in 1533, at first only in medical context; the beverage sense appeared in the 17th century and became widespread in the 19th, displacing <i>хле́бное вино́</i> (xlébnoje vinó, literally “grain wine”) by the early 20th century. The beverage sense is probably a semantic loan from Polish <i>wódka</i>, which, per Brückner, in turn may be a semantic loan from Medieval Latin <i>aqua vitae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water-transport worker</li><li>Vodnik (Russian military vehicle)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vodьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) water polo (a water sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water skier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water skiing</li></ol><p><i>во́дный</i> (vódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лы́жи</i> (lýži) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water content</li></ol><p><i>во́дный</i> (vódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water transport</li></ol><p><i>во́дный</i> (vódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тра́нспорт</i> (tránsport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) water-skiing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water; aquatic</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear energy</i>) water-moderated</li><li>(nuclear energy,&#32;relational) pressurized water</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>водяно́й</i> (vodjanój), literally “water-water”, referring to the fact that in such reactors, light water plays the roles of both a neutron moderator and a coolant fluid.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrophobia; rabies</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>боя́знь</i> (bojáznʹ), a calque of Latin <i>hydrophobia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Hydraena</i>-genus beetle.</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) hydraenid</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>водоро́д</i> (vodoród, “hydrogen”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix). Likely a calque of translingual <i>Hydraena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water conduit, aqueduct</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>aquaeductus</i>, from <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-воз</i> (-voz), combining form of <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ, “to convey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carrying or holding water</li></ol><p><i>водово́з</i> (vodovóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vortex, swirl, whirlpool</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiler, water heater, kettle</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-грей</i> (-grej), combining form of <i>греть</i> (gretʹ, “to heat”) (compare 1sg. present <i>гре́ю</i> (gréju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heating water, hot water</li></ol><p><i>водогре́й</i> (vodogréj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) room for heating of water</li></ol><p><i>водогре́й</i> (vodogréj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water intake, water extraction</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>забо́р</i> (zabór, “intake, collection”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>displacement</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>измеща́ть</i> (izmeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije), calque of German <i>Wasserverdrängung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumping station, water tower</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frogbit (<i>Hydrocharis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>краса́</i> (krasá, “beauty”), a calque of translingual <i>Hydrocharis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;relational) frogbit (<i>Hydrocharis</i>)</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) hydrocharitaceous, of the Hydrocharitaceae.</li></ol><p>From <i>водокра́с</i> (vodokrás, “frogbit”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diver, frogman, plunger</li><li>Newfoundland dog</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лаз</i> (-laz), combining form of <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ, “to climb, to clamber”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(thin)</i> polo-neck sweater, turtleneck sweater</li></ol><p><i>водола́з</i> (vodoláz) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diver</li></ol><p><i>водола́з</i> (vodoláz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) someone who pours water, water pourer</li><li>Aquarius (someone with a Aquarius star sign)</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лей</i> (-lej), combining form of <i>лить</i> (litʹ, “to pour”) (compare imperative singular <i>лей</i> (lej)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Водолей)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Aquarius (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a water carrier)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Aquarius (zodiac sign for the water carrier, covering January 20 – February 18)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A water vending machine.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) pond-skater (<i>insect</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ, “to measure”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pump-jet</li><li>water cannon</li><li>fountain</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw, to cast”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water cannon</li></ol><p><i>водомёт</i> (vodomjót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water heater</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нагрева́ть</i> (nagrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water pressure</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>напо́р</i> (napór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waterproof</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>water-bearing, aquiferous</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-носи́ть</i> (-nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>во́дка</i> (vódka)</li></ol><p><i>во́дка</i> (vódka) +&lrm; <i>-онька</i> (-onʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drainage system, overflow pipe</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>отво́д</i> (otvód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterfall</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ, “fall”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering hole, pond, horse pond</li><li>watering</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water pipe; water-supply</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>про́вод</i> (próvod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tap water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water pipe</li></ol><p><i>водопрово́д</i> (vodoprovód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumber</li></ol><p><i>водопрово́д</i> (vodoprovód) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watershed</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>разде́л</i> (razdél)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>water-soluble</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>раствори́мый</i> (rastvorímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrogen</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-род</i> (-rod), combining form of <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “to give birth”), a calque of French <i>hydrogène</i>, itself derived from Ancient Greek <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) + <i>γεννάω</i> (gennáō, “I bring forth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hydrogen; hydrogenous</li></ol><p><i>водоро́д</i> (vodoród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydrogen-like</li></ol><p><i>водоро́д</i> (vodoród) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>во́доросль</i> (vódoroslʹ)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>во́доросль</i> (vódoroslʹ), with an unhistoric epenthetic vowel in the nominative singular.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) seaweed, algae</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-росл</i> (-rosl) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), based on a difficult-to-explain combination with <i>расти́</i> (rastí, “to grow”) (compare the past feminine singular <i>росла́</i> (roslá)). A parallel construction occurs in <i>ле́торосль</i> (létoroslʹ, “sprout”), also found as <i>ле́торость</i> (létorostʹ), with cognates Old Church Slavonic <i>лѣторасль</i> (lětoraslĭ), Czech <i>letorost</i>, Polish <i>latorośl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>columbine (<i>Aquilegia</i> genus and species)</li><li>accumulation of water</li><li>a spot in terrain where water accumulates</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i><i>Aquilegia</i></i>, <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сбор</i> (sbor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capybara (<i>Hydrochoerus hydrochaeris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сви́нка</i> (svínka, “little pig; guinea pig”), calque of New Latin <i>Hydrochoerus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeegee</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сгоня́ть</i> (sgonjátʹ, “to drive off”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) squeegee</li></ol><p><i>водосго́н</i> (vodosgón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water supply</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>снабже́ние</i> (snabžénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water content</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>содержа́ние</i> (soderžánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drain, gutter</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сток</i> (stok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gutter</li></ol><p><i>водосто́к</i> (vodostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservoir, storage pond / pool, water storage basin</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>храни́лище</i> (xranílišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>во́дка</i> (vódka): vodka</li></ol><p><i>во́дка</i> (vódka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacturer and/or seller of vodka, strong liquor</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) drunkard, someone who drinks a lot of vodka</li></ol><p><i>во́дка</i> (vódka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) female drunkard, someone who drinks a lot of vodka</li></ol><p><i>во́дка</i> (vódka) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vodka</li></ol><p><i>во́дка</i> (vódka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>water-efficient</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water reservoir, basin, pond, body of water</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ём</i> (-jóm), combining form of <i>имать</i> (imatʹ, “to take, originally to have”) (compare 1sg. present <i>е́млю</i> (jémlju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>water-retaining</li></ol><p>From <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ём</i> (-jóm) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij), based on a combining form of <i>имать</i> (imatʹ, “to take, originally to have”) (compare 1sg. present <i>е́млю</i> (jémlju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>во́доросль</i> (vódoroslʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoist, to raise, to erect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въдрѫжати</i> (vŭdrǫžati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъdrǫžati</i>, from <i>&#42;drǫgъ</i> (“bar, pole”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoist, to raise, to erect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въдрѫзити</i> (vŭdrǫziti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antarctic Ocean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>herpetology</i>) pond turtle (turtle of the genus <i>Mauremys</i>)</li></ol><p><i>водяно́й</i> (vodjanój, “water, aquatic”) +&lrm; <i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa, “turtle, tortoise”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Australian water dragon (<i>Intellagama lesueurii</i>)</li></ol><p><i>водяно́й</i> (vodjanój, “water”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>я́щерица</i> (jáščerica, “lizard”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watery, watered-down</li><li>watery, wishy-washy</li><li>pale, watery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) water</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) vodyanoy (a water sprite in Slavic mythology)</li></ol><p><i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watercress (<i>Nasturtium officinale</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unskilful driver</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>води́тель</i> (vodítelʹ, “driver”) +&lrm; <i>дя́тел</i> (djátel, “dimwit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to war, to wage war, to be at war</li></ol><p>From <i>вой-</i> (voj-) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojevati</i>, based on the root of <i>во́ин</i> (vóin, “soldier”) (compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojь</i> (“soldier”), Old East Slavic <i>вои</i>&nbsp;pl (voi, “soldiers”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voivode, governor of province, duke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojevoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voivodeship (the jurisdiction of a voivode)</li><li>voivodeship (type of province in Poland)</li></ol><p><i>воево́да</i> (vojevóda) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic, evidently from <i>въ</i> (vŭ) +&lrm; <i>ѥдинъ</i> (jedinŭ) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander, military leader; leader in war, warlord</li></ol><p><i>во́ин</i> (vóin, “a warrior”) +&lrm; <i>нача́льник</i> (načálʹnik, “a superior, a chief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>militarized; paramilitary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military commissar</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>вое́нный комисса́р</i> (vojénnyj komissár, “military commissar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military commissariat, voenkomat (military registration and enlistment office)</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i><b>вое́н</b>ный <b>ком</b>иссари<b>а́т</b></i> (<b>vojén</b>nyj <b>kom</b>issari<b>át</b>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;journalism) war correspondent, military reporter</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>вое́нный корреспонде́нт</i> (vojénnyj korrespondént).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) military democracy</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Militärdemokratie</i> from the works of the classics of Marxism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war economy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air force</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) air force</li></ol><p><i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>возду́шный</i> (vozdúšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navy (sea force)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naval</li></ol><p><i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>морско́й</i> (morskój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>military-political, (<i>relational</i>) military policy</li></ol><p><i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полити́ческий</i> (politíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>military-industrial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) military technology</li></ol><p><i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) military transport</li></ol><p><i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тра́нспортный</i> (tránsportnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martial art (military prowess)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martial law</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(criminal law,&#32;international law) war crime (a punishable offence under international law for violations of the laws of war by any person or persons, military or civilian)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a person liable for military service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner of war</li></ol><p>From <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пле́нный</i> (plénnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) military man, military personnel, serviceman, soldier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) war; military</li><li>martial</li><li>soldier</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) soldier, military man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojьnьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vojьna</i> (“war”) + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vojьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vojь</i> (“soldier”) + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>война́</i> (vojná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;history) war communism</li></ol><p>It is first found in Alexander Bogdanov in the book (1918) <i>Problems of Socialism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warship (type of watercraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war correspondent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military instructor</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>вое́нный руководи́тель</i> (vojénnyj rukovodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Russian system of military trade</li><li>a military shop/store of that system</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>вое́нная торго́вля</i> (vojénnaja torgóvlja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;derogatory) militarists, military clique</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>война́</i> (vojná) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, ringleader</li><li>pack leader, alpha (the dominant animal in a pack)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counselor (camp counselor)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>вожа́тай</i> (vožátaj), under the influence of <i>-атый</i> (-atyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust, desire (strong attraction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въжделение</i> (vŭždelenije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>въжделѣти</i> (vŭždelěti), from <i>въз-</i> (vŭz-) +&lrm; <i>желѣти</i> (želěti). Compare <i>возжела́ть</i> (vozželátʹ). Related to Russian <i>вожделе́ть</i> (voždelétʹ, “to lust, desire strongly”), Russian <i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ, “to wish, to desire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eagerly desired, longed-for</li></ol><p><i>вожделе́ние</i> (voždelénije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driving (of a vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief, chieftain (<i>of a tribe</i>)</li><li>leader (<i>inspiring or commanding a movement</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) captain (<i>leading an army</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вождь</i> (voždĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voďь</i>. Displaced inherited Old East Slavic <i>вожь</i> (vožĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rein <i>(strap or rope for guiding an animal; also figuratively; usually plural)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to milk something; to keep doing something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) reins (<i>now usually plural</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart</li><li>cartload</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vozъ</i></p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ВОЗ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>Всеми́рная организа́ция здравоохране́ния</i> (Vsemírnaja organizácija zdravooxranénija): WHO (World Health Organization)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) upwards, up</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) returning, re-</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>въз-</i> (vŭz-); compare inherited cognate <i>вз-</i> (vz-). However, there is an opinion about independent development, compare the words <i>взойти</i> (vzojti), <i>взобраться</i> (vzobratʹsja), <i>взорвать</i> (vzorvatʹ), which indicate the presence of a second /ъ/ in the prefix, which makes it possible to create a strong position for /ъ/ and transition to /o/. Also compare <i>възогъловие</i> (*възъголовие) in birch bark manuscript №469. Per Zaliznyak, prefix <i>възъ-</i> arose by analogy with the Old East Slavic prefixes like <i>отъ-</i> and <i>подъ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to prohibit, to forbid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възбранити</i> (vŭzbraniti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to prohibit, to forbid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възбранꙗти</i> (vŭzbranjati). <i>возбрани́ть</i> (vozbranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excitable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stimulant</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) causative agent</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) exciter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excite</li><li>to arouse, to rouse, to provoke</li><li>to stimulate</li><li>to incite, to stir up, to instigate</li><li>to raise, to bring, to present</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възбоудити</i> (vŭzbuditi). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become excited, to become aroused, to be turned on</li></ol><p><i>возбуди́ть</i> (vozbudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excite</li><li>to arouse, to rouse, to provoke</li><li>to stimulate</li><li>to incite, to stir up, to instigate</li><li>to raise, to bring, to present</li></ol><p><i>возбуди́ть</i> (vozbudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become excited, to become aroused, to be turned on</li></ol><p><i>возбужда́ть</i> (vozbuždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stimulating, rousing</li><li>exciting, provocative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arousal</li><li>excitement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възбоуждениѥ</i> (vŭzbuždenije). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>excitedly</li></ol><p><i>возбуждённый</i> (vozbuždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excited (experiencing excitement) (<i>person, crowd, etc.; normally animate</i>)</li><li>aroused, horny</li><li>excited (expressing excitement) (<i>speech, state, etc.; normally inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raising, erection</li><li>exponentiation</li></ol><p><i>возвед-</i> (vozved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To glorify, to extol, to exalt.</li></ol><p><i>возвели́чить</i> (vozvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To glorify, to extol, to exalt.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвеличити</i> (vŭzveličiti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-вели́чить</i> (-velíčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to erect</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to exponentiate, to raise to a power</li><li>to derive [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘from something in the past’</small>]</li><li>to cast (an accusation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвести</i> (vŭzvesti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí). Doublet of <i>взвести́</i> (vzvestí), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to announce, to proclaim, to herald</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвѣстити</i> (vŭzvěstiti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-вести́ть</i> (-vestítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to announce, to proclaim, to herald</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвѣщати</i> (vŭzvěštati). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-веща́ть</i> (-veščátʹ), from <i>весть</i> (vestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to erect</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to exponentiate, to raise to a power</li><li>to derive [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘from something in the past’</small>]</li><li>to cast (an accusation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възводити</i> (vŭzvoditi). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ). Doublet of <i>взводи́ть</i> (vzvodítʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>return (act of returning)</li><li>restitution, reimbursement</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>возврати́ть</i> (vozvratítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to give back</li><li>to recover, to get back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвратити</i> (vŭzvratiti, “to return”). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ). Doublet of <i>взвороти́ть</i> (vzvorotítʹ, “to load onto, to dig up, to turn upside down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to come back</li></ol><p><i>возврати́ть</i> (vozvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) reflexive pronoun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) recurrent</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) reflexive</li></ol><p><i>возврати́ть</i> (vozvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) reflexive verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to give back</li><li>to recover, to get back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвращати</i> (vŭzvraštati, “to return”). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, to come back</li></ol><p><i>возвраща́ть</i> (vozvraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>return</li><li>restitution</li></ol><p><i>возврати́ть</i> (vozvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise <i>(to)</i>, to elevate <i>(to)</i></li><li>to elevate, to ennoble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвысити</i> (vŭzvysiti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́сить</i> (-výsitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise</li><li>to surpass</li></ol><p><i>возвы́сить</i> (vozvýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise <i>(to)</i>, to elevate <i>(to)</i></li><li>to elevate, to ennoble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възвышати</i> (vŭzvyšati). By surface analysis, <i>возвы́сить</i> (vozvýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise</li><li>to tower (above)</li><li>to surpass</li></ol><p><i>возвыша́ть</i> (vozvyšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rise, rising</li><li>elevation</li><li>dais</li></ol><p><i>возвы́сить</i> (vozvýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(location elevated with respect to the surroundings) altitude, elevation, eminence, height, highness</li><li>(landform) hill, hummock, upland</li><li>loftiness, nobleness</li></ol><p><i>возвы́шенный</i> (vozvýšennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lofty, sublime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to head, to be at head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to head, to be at head</li></ol><p><i>возгла́вить</i> (vozglávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exclamation, cry, outcry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възгласъ</i> (vŭzglasŭ). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sublimation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sublimate</li></ol><p><i>возгоня́ть</i> (vozgonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignition, flare up</li></ol><p><i>возгора́ть(ся)</i> (vozgorátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to catch fire, to ignite</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flare up, to break out (<i>of a war, quarrel, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be inflamed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with strong feelings, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>возгоре́ть</i> (vozgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to catch fire, to ignite</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flare up, to break out (<i>of a war, quarrel, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be inflamed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with strong feelings, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>возгора́ть</i> (vozgorátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to catch fire, to ignite</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flare up, to break out (<i>of a war, quarrel, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be inflamed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with strong feelings, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to catch fire, to ignite</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flare up, to break out (<i>of a war, quarrel, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be inflamed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with strong feelings, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>возгоре́ть</i> (vozgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to render, to give in recognition [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to repay, to reward, to recompense [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздавати</i> (vŭzdavati). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to render, to give in recognition [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to repay, to reward, to recompense [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздати</i> (vŭzdati). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retribution, requital, punishment, payback, revenge (punishment inflicted in the spirit of moral outrage or personal vengeance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздаꙗниѥ</i> (vŭzdajanije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erect, to raise</li></ol><p><i>воздви́гнуть</i> (vozdvígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To be constructed/erected; to appear/rise.</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) To stand before/in front of someone [<i>with</i> <b>перед</b> (pered, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>воздви́гнуть</i> (vozdvígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erect, to raise</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздвигнѫти</i> (vŭzdvignǫti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to raise (one's hands)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздѣвати</i> (vŭzděvati). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>дева́ть</i> (devátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect, influence (upon, on, over)</li><li>impact</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affect, to act, to impact (to influence or alter) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to till</li><li>to grow</li></ol><p><i>возде́лать</i> (vozdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstinence (the act or practice of abstaining)</li><li>celibacy (the condition or state of being celibate)</li></ol><p>From <i>воздержа́ться</i> (vozderžátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije). Or borrowed directly from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздръжаниѥ</i> (vŭzdrŭžanije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstention (the act of abstaining)</li></ol><p><i>возде́ржанный</i> (vozdéržannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abstemious, temperate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refrain, to abstain, to keep oneself</li><li>to decline, to forbear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въздьржатисѧ</i> (vŭzdĭržatisę). By surface analysis, <i>воздержа́ть</i> (vozderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refrain, to abstain, to keep oneself</li><li>to decline, to forbear</li></ol><p><i>воздержа́ться</i> (vozderžátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to raise (one's hands)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздѣти</i> (vŭzděti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) air</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) air, atmosphere</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) money</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) the veil for church communion vessels</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздоухъ</i> (vŭzduxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzduxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) air raid siren</li></ol><p>Short of <i>возду́шная трево́га</i> (vozdúšnaja trevóga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf blower (motorized garden tool that propels air out of a nozzle to move leaves and other debris)</li></ol><p>From <i>во́здух</i> (vózdux) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air intake <i>(jet fighters, rockets, etc.)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>airtight</li></ol><p><i>во́здух</i> (vózdux) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air purifier (device used to remove contaminants from the air)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aeronautics, aerostatics</li></ol><p><i>во́здух</i> (vózdux) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пла́вание</i> (plávanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(aviation,&#32;military) air force</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airway (flight path used by aeroplanes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) airborne</li></ol><p><i>возду́шный</i> (vozdúšnyj, “aerial”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>деса́нтный</i> (desántnyj, “landing (<i>relational</i>)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aerospace</li></ol><p><i>возду́шный</i> (vozdúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>косми́ческий</i> (kosmíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;electricity,&#32;engineering) air-arc (related to arc welding with the removal of metal by compressed air)</li></ol><p><i>возду́шный</i> (vozdúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дугово́й</i> (dugovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airspace (controlled portion of atmosphere)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aircraft (machine capable of atmospheric flight)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) air; aerial</li><li>(figurative,&#32;relational) aviation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) light, graceful</li></ol><p><i>во́здух</i> (vózdux) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite (flying toy on string)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airlift (the transportation of troops)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air raid (attack on a ground target by military aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airbrake <i>(jet fighter)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airstrike (attack by military aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(automobiles,&#32;tanks,&#32;engines) air cleaner, air filter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balloon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>toy balloon</li><li>hot air balloon, hydrogen balloon etc.</li></ol></ol><p>Literally, “Air sphere”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) admirer, adorer, one who loves another person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appeal (entreaty)</li></ol><p><i>воззва́ть</i> (vozzvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appeal <i>(to someone for)</i></li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>view, opinion, outlook</li></ol><p><i>воззре́ть</i> (vozzrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look (at), to gaze (at)</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare (at), to start staring</li></ol><p><i>воззре́ть</i> (vozzrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convey, to carry (by vehicle), to deliver, to transport</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voziti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to busy oneself, to mess around, to spend time, to take trouble</li><li>to tinker</li></ol><p><i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to lay, to place (something on a certain area for memorial purposes)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to assign (someone with tasks, commands, blames, hopes, or responsibilities)</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beside, by, near</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzdьlě</i>. Compare Polish <i>wedle</i> and Czech <i>vedle</i> from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъdьlě</i>, as well as <i>по́дле</i> (pódle) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podьlě</i>. Syntactically, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) + an element related to <i>длина́</i> (dliná, “length”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to recline (<i>as e.g. in Roman banquets</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възлежати</i> (vŭzležati). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;also&#32;humorous) libation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drinking-bout</li></ol><p><i>возлия́ть</i> (vozlijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) laying, placing (<i>for memorial purposes</i>)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) entrusting, assigning (<i>of tasks, responsibilities, hopes, blame, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>возложи́ть</i> (vozložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to lay, to place (something on a certain area for memorial purposes)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to assign (someone with tasks, commands, blames, hopes, or responsibilities)</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poеtic,&#32;dated) to come to love, to fall in love [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by force of something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възлюбити</i> (vŭzljubiti). Compare native <i>невзлюбить</i> (nevzljubitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beloved</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover, love, sweetheart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retribution, requital, punishment, payback, revenge (punishment inflicted in the spirit of moral outrage or personal vengeance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възмьздиѥ</i> (vŭzmĭzdije). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>мзда</i> (mzda) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reimburse</li><li>to compensate, to indemnify, to atone (to make reparation, compensation, or amends, for an offence or a crime)</li><li>to refund, to repay</li><li>to offset</li><li>to recoup</li><li>to replace</li><li>to redress</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be recovered, to replaced, to repaid or recouped</li></ol><p><i>возмести́ть</i> (vozmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reimburse</li><li>to compensate, to indemnify, to atone (to make reparation, compensation, or amends, for an offence or a crime)</li><li>to refund, to repay</li><li>to offset</li><li>to recoup</li><li>to replace</li><li>to redress</li></ol><p><i>возмести́ть</i> (vozmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be recovered, to be replaced, to be repaid or recouped</li></ol><p><i>возмеща́ть</i> (vozmeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compensation, indemnification, reimbursement, refund</li><li>compensation, indemnity, damages</li></ol><p><i>возмести́ть</i> (vozmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) perhaps, possibly, as... as possible</li></ol><p><i>возмо́жный</i> (vozmóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opportunity, chance, potential (chance for advancement, progress or profit)</li><li>possibility</li></ol><p><i>возмо́жный</i> (vozmóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possible, probable (able but not certain to happen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възможьнъ</i> (vŭzmožĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to be able to, to have the strength to, to be in a position to (do something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възмощи</i> (vŭzmošti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) virile maturity</li></ol><p><i>возмужа́лый</i> (vozmužályj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) mature, grown up (<i>of a man</i>); virile</li></ol><p><i>возмужа́ть</i> (vozmužátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outrageous</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) inciting rebellion</li></ol><p><i>возмути́ть</i> (vozmutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outrage, to anger, to make indignant, to trouble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възмѫтити</i> (vŭzmǫtiti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>мути́ть</i> (mutítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be indignant, to be filled with indignation, to be exasperated, to be outraged</li><li>to rebel</li></ol><p><i>возмути́ть</i> (vozmutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outrage, to anger, to make indignant, to trouble</li><li>to incite to insurrection</li></ol><p><i>возмути́ть</i> (vozmutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be outraged</li><li>to be indignant at/with something, to be filled with indignation, to be exasperated, to be outraged</li><li>to rebel</li></ol><p><i>возмуща́ть</i> (vozmuščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outrage</li><li>indignation, resentment (anger or displeasure felt out of belief that others have engaged in wrongdoing or mistreatment)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) disturbance</li></ol><p><i>возмути́ть</i> (vozmutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indignantly</li></ol><p><i>возмущённый</i> (vozmuščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outraged, perturbed, angry, indignant (<i>experiencing anger or rage towards someone; normally of a person</i>)</li><li>outraged, perturbed, angry, indignant (<i>expressing anger or rage; of a face, look, tone, etc.; normally inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward</li><li>to remunerate</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>награди́ть</i> (nagradítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward</li><li>to remunerate</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>награжда́ть</i> (nagraždátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward, recompense; remuneration; payoff</li></ol><p><i>вознагражда́ть</i> (voznagraždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;humorous) to intend, to make up one's mind, to get it into one's head [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вознаме́риться</i> (voznaméritʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;humorous) to intend, to make up one's mind, to get it into one's head [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>наме́р-</i> (namér-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate, to start hating</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възненавидѣти</i> (vŭznenaviděti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>ненави́деть</i> (nenavídetʹ, “to hate”). Compare Belarusian <i>зненаві́дзець</i> (znjenavídzjecʹ), Ukrainian <i>знена́видіти</i> (znenávydity).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apotheosis, ascension (loosely, release from earthly life, ascension to heaven&#59; death)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възнесеньѥ</i> (vŭznesenĭje). By surface analysis, <i>вознести́</i> (voznestí) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije). Doublet of <i>взнесенье</i> (vznesenʹje) and <i>взнесение</i> (vznesenije).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вознесение)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ascension (holiday celebrating Ascension of Jesus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;in toponyms) Assumption</li></ol><p><i>вознесе́ние</i> (voznesénije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise high, to uplift</li><li>to exalt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възнести</i> (vŭznesti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí). Doublet of <i>взнести́</i> (vznestí), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to praise to the skies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to tower</li><li>(<i>ironical</i>) to become conceited/stuck-up</li></ol><p><i>вознести́</i> (voznestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise, to appear, to emerge, to originate, to spring up</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to object, to protest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възникати</i> (vŭznikati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergence, occurrence, appearance, rise, onset, arising</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възникновениѥ</i> (vŭzniknovenije). By surface analysis, <i>возни́кнуть</i> (vozníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise, to appear, to emerge, to originate, to spring up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възникнѫти</i> (vŭzniknǫti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charioteer, coachman, wagoner (someone who drives a wagon), cart driver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charioteer, coachman, wagoner, (cart) driver</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) charioteer, coachman, wagoner, (cart) driver; charioteer's, coachman's, wagoner's, cart driver's</li></ol><p><i>возни́ца</i> (vozníca) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, adjectival suffix)</p><p><i>возни́ца</i> (vozníca) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, possessive suffix)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Возничий)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Auriga</li></ol><p>From <i>возни́чий</i> (vozníčij, “charioteer”), calque of Latin <i>Aurīga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise high, to uplift</li><li>to exalt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възносити</i> (vŭznositi). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ). Doublet of <i>взноси́ть</i> (vznosítʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to praise to the skies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to tower</li><li>(<i>ironical</i>) to become conceited/stuck-up</li></ol><p><i>возноси́ть</i> (voznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuss, bustle, scurrying, fumbling</li><li>trouble, bother</li><li>noise, racket, romp, roughhousing</li></ol><p><i>вози́ть(ся)</i> (vozítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to prevail [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘over something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>облада́ть</i> (obladátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew, to resume, to recommence, to revive</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>обнови́ть</i> (obnovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recommence</li></ol><p><i>возобнови́ть</i> (vozobnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal; resumption, recommencement</li></ol><p><i>возобновля́ть</i> (vozobnovljátʹ, “to renew”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>renewable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew, to resume, to recommence, to revive</li></ol><p><i>возобнови́ть</i> (vozobnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to think, to imagine, to suspect</li><li>to think too high (of oneself)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възмьнѣти</i> (vŭzmĭněti) or <i>възъмьнѣти</i> (vŭzŭmĭněti). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to clamour, to start to yell</li></ol><p>See <i>вопи́ть</i> (vopítʹ, “to yell”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to cry out</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възъпити</i> (vŭzŭpiti) with levelling of the present stem.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;sometimes ironic) to rejoice, to be happy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въздрадовати сѧ</i> (vŭzdradovati sę). By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>ра́доваться</i> (rádovatʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;formal</i>) to object, to mind, to protest</li></ol><p><i>возрази́ть</i> (vozrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>objection, rejoinder, retort [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘against’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>возрази́ть</i> (vozrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to object, to mind, to protest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzraziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ). Originally meant "to strike back".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age (whole duration of a being)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>възрастъ</i> (vŭzrastŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age of consent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age of consent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growth, increase, increment, rise; augmentation</li></ol><p><i>возраста́ть</i> (vozrastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up</li><li>to increase, to rise</li></ol><p><i>возрасти́</i> (vozrastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ascending, increasing</li><li>progressive, incremental</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up</li><li>to increase, to rise</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age group (demographic grouping based on age)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) age; age-related</li><li>age-specific</li></ol><p><i>во́зраст</i> (vózrast) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>andropause (male equivalent of the menopause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revive, to regenerate, to restore</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-, “re-, returning”) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “give birth to”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revive, to be revived, to be reborn</li><li>to return to life, to be restored to life</li></ol><p><i>возроди́ть</i> (vozrodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revive, to regenerate, to restore</li></ol><p><i>возроди́ть</i> (vozrodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revive, to be revived, to be reborn</li><li>to return to life, to be restored to life</li></ol><p><i>возрожда́ть</i> (vozroždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebirth, renaissance, revival, reconstruction</li></ol><p><i>возроди́ть</i> (vozrodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>воз</i> (voz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce (an effect), to conceive (a wish), to form (intention)</li></ol><p><i>воз-</i> (voz-) +&lrm; <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier, warrior, serviceman, military man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воинъ</i> (voinŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojinъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>military, martial</li></ol><p><i>во́ин</i> (vóin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, bellicose</li></ol><p><i>во́инство</i> (vóinstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) army, host, troops (collective)</li></ol><p><i>во́ин</i> (vóin) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>militant</li></ol><p>From <i>вои́нствовать</i> (voínstvovatʹ, “to militate”), from <i>во́инство</i> (vóinstvo, “army”), from <i>во́ин</i> (vóin, “warrior”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) truly, in truth, indeed, verily</li><li>(lofty,&#32;parenthesis) indeed</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>и́стину</i> (ístinu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>өйлек</i> (öylek, “domestic material”), from <i>өй</i> (öy, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-лек</i> (-lek, “<i>suffix for appurtenances</i>”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) felt</li></ol><p><i>во́йлок</i> (vójlok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war, warfare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воина</i> (voina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojьna</i> (“war”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Civil War</li></ol><p>Literally, “War of the North and South”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Wikimedia jargon</i>) edit war (a dispute over the content of a page on a wiki or other editable work)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>edit war</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>война́</i> (vojná): little war; war game (also sarcastically of politicians' actions)</li></ol><p><i>война́</i> (vojná) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) voice message (in a chat)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) voice chat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>voice (message&#47;chat)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troops, armed forces</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) army, host</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) troops, forces</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воиско</i> (voisko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vojьsko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organic artillery <i>(artillery units that make up a permanent part of a larger nonartillery unit and provide specialized capability to the parent unit, including the соединение, the часть, and подразделение)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) army, military</li></ol><p>From <i>войска́</i> (vojská) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój). Compare Polish <i>wojskowy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to go in(to) (by foot), to get in(to)</li><li>to penetrate into</li><li>to be included in</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to log in</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;literary) vocabulary</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) list (of words), collection (of words) (such as for memorisation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vocābulārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocalism, singing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) vocalisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocalist, singer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vocal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>vocal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transport</i>) station, terminal (<i>with a building for passenger facilities</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>train station, railroad station, railway station, depot (<i>with a building for passenger facilities</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) park, pleasure garden</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Vauxhall</i>, in reference to Vauxhall Gardens in London. The ending was influenced by <i>зал</i> (zal, “hall”). The spelling <i>фоксал</i> (foksal) attested in 1777 is from French <i>facs-hall</i>, from the same English source. The transportation sense derives from an epithet for the Pavlovsk railway station building, which at one time served as both a passenger lounge and a place of entertainment. Doublet of <i>Воксхолл</i> (Voksxoll).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) station</li></ol><p><i>вокза́л</i> (vokzál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>вокза́л</i> (vokzál): (little) train station, railroad station, railway station, (small) depot</li></ol><p><i>вокза́л</i> (vokzál) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walkie-talkie (portable radio)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>walkie-talkie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>round, around, about</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around, about</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox, bullock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flounce</li><li>shuttlecock (a lightweight object used in badminton.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>volant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volapük</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) incomprehensible, garbled language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) moist, damp, wet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlgъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) moist, damp, wet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlglъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>во́лгнуть</i> (vólgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to soak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Volgograd</li></ol><p><i>Волгогра́д</i> (Volgográd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blister <i>(bubble on the skin)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъldyrь</i>, reflected in Belarusian <i>баўды́р</i> (bawdýr) and <i>вовды́рь</i> (vovdýrʹ) (spelled by Nosovič), dialectal Polish <i>bołdur</i> (“smoke ventilation cap on a place of cooking”) and Eastern German hydronymy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>volitional</li><li>strong-willed, resolute, determined</li><li>authoritarian</li></ol><p><i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declaration of will</li></ol><p><i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>изъявле́ние</i> (izʺjavlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>willy nilly, perforce, nolens volens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) volleyball (game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>volleyball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volleyball player, volleyballer</li></ol><p>From <i>волейбо́л</i> (volejból, “volleyball”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>волейболі́ст</i> (volejbolíst), Belarusian <i>валейбалі́ст</i> (valjejbalíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) volleyball</li></ol><p><i>волейбо́л</i> (volejból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of the Volga region</li></ol><p><i>Во́лга</i> (Vólga) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Volga</li></ol><p><i>Во́лга</i> (Vólga) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вълкъ</i> (vŭlkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wilkás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wĺ̥kʷos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) wolf in sheep's clothing (a disguised danger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolfhound</li></ol><p><i>волк</i> (volk, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дав(ить)</i> (dav(itʹ), “to press”), possibly calqued from Turkic, compare Kazakh <i>бөрібасар</i> (börıbasar, “wolfhound, lit. wolf-presser”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf snake (<i>Lycodon</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Lycodon</i>, as <i>волк</i> (volk, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>werewolf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlkolakъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>werewolf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlkolakъ</i>, from <i>&#42;vьlkъ</i> (“wolf”) and <i>&#42;dolkъ</i> (“skin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>волк</i> (volk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave</li><li>wavelength</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) wool of sheep or goats</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вълна</i> (vŭlna, “wave”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlna</i> (“wave”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>вльна</i> (vlĭna), Bulgarian <i>вълна</i> (vǎlna), Czech <i>vlna</i>, Slovak <i>vlna</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>vilni̇̀s</i> (“wave”), Latvian <i>vilnis</i> (“wave”), Sanskrit <i>ऊर्मि</i> (ūrmí, “wave”), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬨𐬌</i> (var<sup><small>ə</small></sup>mi), Baluchi گورم (gwarm), Old High German <i>wella</i>, German <i>Welle</i>, Albanian <i>valë</i> (“wave”), and further with Old Church Slavonic <i>вьлати</i> (vĭlati, “undulate”), German <i>wallen</i> (“undulate”), and Russian <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ), <i>вал</i> (val).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlna</i> (“wool”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wílˀnāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wĺ̥h₁neh₂</i> (“wool”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>вльна</i> (vlĭna), Bulgarian <i>въ́лна</i> (vǎ́lna), Serbo-Croatian <i>ву̏на&#47;vȕna</i>, Czech <i>vlna</i>, Slovak <i>vlna</i> and Polish <i>wełna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at sea</i>) choppiness, roughness</li><li>excitement, tizzy</li><li>(social) unrest, riot, civil commotion</li></ol><p>From <i>волна́</i> (volná, “wave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wavy, corrugated</li></ol><p><i>волна́</i> (volná) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate, to trouble, to disturb, to worry, to alarm, to upset</li><li>to ruffle, to stir</li></ol><p>From <i>волна́</i> (volná, “wave”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry, to be upset, to be agitated</li><li>to ripple, to be rough (<i>waves</i>), to billow, to surge</li></ol><p><i>волнова́ть</i> (volnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>quantum mechanics</i>) wavefunction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wave; undulatory</li></ol><p><i>волна́</i> (volná) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakwater, groyne, pier</li></ol><p>From <i>волна́</i> (volná) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лом</i> (-lom), combining form of <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ, “to break”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakwater</li><li>wave-piercing vessel</li></ol><p>From <i>волна́</i> (volná) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-рез</i> (-rez), combining form of <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exciting, stirring</li><li>perturbing, disturbing, nerve-racking</li><li>thrilling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vol-au-vent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>vol-au-vent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ox</li></ol><p><i>вол</i> (vol) +&lrm; <i>-о́вий</i> (-óvij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxhouse</li></ol><p><i>вол</i> (vol) +&lrm; <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) moisture</li><li>(regional,&#32;dated) liquid fat, liquid fatty broth or gravy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волога</i> (vologa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volga</i>; cognates include Ukrainian <i>воло́га</i> (volóha, “moisture”). Doublet of <i>вла́га</i> (vlága), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влага</i> (vlaga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vologda</li></ol><p><i>Во́логда</i> (Vólogda) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vologda (a city in Russia, administrative centre of Vologda oblast)</li></ol><p><i>Во́логда</i> (Vólogda) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) to possess, to own</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>володѣти</i> (voloděti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volděti</i> (“to rule”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;walˀdā́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂welh₁dʰh₁-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“to rule”). Doublet of <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>володі́ти</i> (volodíty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portage (carrying of boats overland)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lot of fuss and trouble</li><li>red tape</li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) philander, lady's man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fibrous; stringy</li></ol><p><i>волокно́</i> (voloknó) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fibre, filament</li><li>grain <i>(of wood)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volkъnò</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>волокно́</i> (voloknó)</li></ol><p><i>волокно́</i> (voloknó) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volunteer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>volontaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ox-eyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) a hair</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волосъ</i> (volosŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volsъ</i>. Doublet of <i>влас</i> (vlas), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. From <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“hair; wool”).</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वल्श</i> (válśa, “twig, sprout”), Persian گرس (gors, “curled hair”), Northern Kurdish <i>pirç</i> (“hair”) and Lithuanian <i>valai̇̃</i>, as well as with Old Armenian <i>վարս</i> (vars), an Iranian borrowing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hairy</li></ol><p><i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>во́лос</i> (vólos, “hair”)</li></ol><p>From <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(an individual) hair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thick or untidy hair</li></ol><p><i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>во́лос</i> (vólos): (small) hair</li><li>hairspring (of a watch); filament (of a bulb)</li></ol><p><i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) volost board, parish committee (collegial body nominally responsible for the administration of a volost)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>волостно́е правле́ние</i> (volostnóje pravlénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to a volost</li></ol><p><i>во́лость</i> (vólostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) volost, district, county</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волость</i> (volostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volstь</i> (“rule”). Doublet of <i>власть</i> (vlastʹ, “power, authority”), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>во́лос</i> (vólos).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hair; pileous</li></ol><p><i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-яной</i> (-janoj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wolof (ethnicity)</li><li>Wolof (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a member of one of the Romanic peoples, such as the Italians or Romanians.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волохъ</i> (voloxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Vyshny Volochyok</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawplate</li></ol><p><i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag, to trail</li><li>to draw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волочити</i> (voločiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volčiti</i>. Doublet of <i>влачи́ть</i> (vlačítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag, to trail, to be dragged/trailed</li><li>to run <i>(after)</i>, to dangle <i>(after)</i>, to chase <i>(after)</i> (esp. a woman)</li></ol><p><i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag, to haul, to draw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velťi</i>. Doublet of <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magus, mage</li><li>(<i>paganism</i>) volkhv; high priest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъхвъ</i> (vlŭxvŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъснѫти</i> (vlŭsnǫti, “to stutter”), referring to the trances the priests used in their ceremonies.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>волк</i> (volk): wolf</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wolfish, wolf's, lupine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьlčьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>волк</i> (volk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ravenous hunger</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>wilczy apetyt</i>, French <i>faim de loup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>волк</i> (volk)</li></ol><p><i>волк</i> (volk, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>волк</i> (volk): little wolf, wolf cub</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) spinning top (<i>toy</i>)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) little bittern (<i>bird</i>)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;regional) water sprout, epicormic shoot</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;colloquial) peephole in a prison cell door</li></ol><p><i>волк</i> (volk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf cub</li></ol><p><i>волк</i> (volk) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) magic, sorcery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъшьба</i> (vlŭšĭba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъlšьba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic wand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard, sorcerer, magician</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъшьбьникъ</i> (vlŭšĭbĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>волшба́</i> (volšbá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magic, magical, miraculous</li><li>fantastic, fantastical, fanciful</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) enchanting, charming, captivating</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъшьбьнъ</i> (vlŭšĭbĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>волшба́</i> (volšbá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;uncountable</i>) magic (use of supernatural rituals, forces, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влъшьбьство</i> (vlŭšĭbĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>волшба́</i> (volšbá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>вол</i> (vol).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bagpipes (musical wind instrument)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dawdling, delay, procrastination, slacking</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) handgun, firearm</li></ol><p><i>Волы́нь</i> (Volýnʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Volyn or Volhynia</li></ol><p><i>Волы́нь</i> (Volýnʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>волы́нский</i> (volýnskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of Volhynia</li></ol><p><i>Волы́нь</i> (Volýnʹ) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) volvox (<i>Volvox</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Volvox</i>, from Latin <i>volvō</i> (“to roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>freely, freely and easily, in a relaxed manner</li></ol><p><i>вольго́тный</i> (volʹgótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open-air cage, enclosure</li><li>aviary, volary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>volière</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>volō</i> + <i>-ārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) community of people who escaped the harsh conditions of serfdom and settled on the outskirts of Russia, e.g. Cossacks</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) strong-willed person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>freely (in a free manner)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) at ease</li></ol><p><i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) freethinker</li></ol><p>From <i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), a calque of German <i>Freidenker</i>. First attested in 1763.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free-thinking</li></ol><p><i>вольноду́мец</i> (volʹnodúmec) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free thought</li></ol><p>From <i>вольноду́мец</i> (volʹnodúmec) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>working for hire, freelance (<i>especially of a civilian at a military institution</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freelancer (<i>especially of a civilian at a military institution</i>)</li></ol><p><i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>наём</i> (najóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) one-year volunteer, EF</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedman (man who has been released from a condition of slavery)</li></ol><p><i>во́льно</i> (vólʹno) +&lrm; <i>отпусти́ть</i> (otpustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberty; freedom</li><li>deviation from generally accepted rules, norms, traditions</li></ol><p><i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free</li><li>free, available, at liberty [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>unrestricted</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) free-style</li><li>familiar, impudent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) volt</li><li>(<i>circus</i>) circle (by riders in the circus stage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volt-ampere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing chair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voltmeter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tungsten</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wolfram</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) volume (<i>of a book</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) voluntarism (a doctrine that prioritizes the will over emotion or reason)</li><li>(politics,&#32;management) arbitrariness, subjective decision making</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Voluntarismus</i>; compare to English <i>voluntarism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, willpower</li><li>power</li><li>freedom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>волꙗ</i> (volja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voľà</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wáljāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wombat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) there</li><li>off, out (away), outside, away</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>out, get out!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вънъ</i> (vŭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъnъ</i>. Cognate with dialectal <i>во́нки</i> (vónki, “out”), Old Church Slavonic <i>вънъ</i> (vŭnŭ, “out”), Ukrainian <i>во́нка</i> (vónka, “out”), Bulgarian <i>вън</i> (vǎn, “out”) (also <i>въ́нка</i> (vǎ́nka)), Serbo-Croatian <i>ва̏н</i> (“out”), Slovene <i>vèn</i> (“out”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>ven</i> (“out”), Slovak <i>von</i> (“out”), Old Polish <i>wen</i> (“out”), Upper Sorbian <i>won</i> (“out”), Lower Sorbian <i>wen</i> (“out”). See <i>вне</i> (vne) for further discussion.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>won (currency)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>원</i> (won).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust into, to plunge into [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>вонзи́ть</i> (vonzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>вонза́ть</i> (vonzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust into, to plunge into [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>въньзити</i>&nbsp;pf (vŭnĭziti), <i>въньзнѫти</i>&nbsp;impf (vŭnĭznǫti). By surface analysis, <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>-нз-</i> (-nz-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), where the root -нз- is also found in <i>пронзи́ть</i> (pronzítʹ) and is cognate with Polish <i>nizać</i> and further with <i>нож</i> (nož, “knife”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>вонзи́ть</i> (vonzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wonton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stink, stench</li><li>smell, smelliness, reek</li><li>fetidness, fetor</li><li>malodor, malodorousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voňь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stinking, stinky, smelly</li></ol><p><i>воня́ть</i> (vonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ю́чий</i> (-júčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skunk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stinker (a person who stinks)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) stinker (a contemptible person)</li></ol><p><i>воню́чий</i> (vonjúčij, “stinking”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stink; to reek</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to complain, to nag</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вонѧти</i> (vonęti, “to smell”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voňati</i>. Cognates with Czech <i>vonět</i>, Polish <i>wonieć</i>, both meaning “to be fragrant”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imaginary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imaginary friend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;childish) smarty-pants, nose-in-the-air, bighead</li></ol><p><i>вообража́ть</i> (voobražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imagine, to fancy</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to be conceited; to show off</li></ol><p><i>вообрази́ть</i> (voobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imagination, fancy</li></ol><p>From <i>вообрази́ть</i> (voobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije), a calque of German <i>Einbildung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imaginable</li></ol><p><i>вообрази́ть</i> (voobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imagine, to fancy</li></ol><p>From <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ), itself a calque of German <i>einbilden</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>generally, in general, usually</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) always</li><li>approximately, in general terms</li><li>(somewhat&#32;colloquial,&#32;in the negative) at all, absolutely, totally, under any conditions</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crazy</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) as a matter of fact, in fact, actually, generally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>в общем</i> (v obščem)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire; to encourage; to cheer up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inspired, to be enthused</li><li>to be carried away (<i>with an idea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ardour, enthusiasm, fervour; inspiration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire; to encourage; to cheer up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inspired, to be filled with enthusiasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inspired, full of inspiration (<i>experiencing inspiration; of a person</i>)</li><li>inspired, inspirational (<i>expressing inspiration; of a look, face, speech, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm, to equip</li><li>to set against, to instigate</li></ol><p><i>вооружи́ть</i> (vooružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm, to provide, to furnish <i>(oneself)</i></li></ol><p><i>вооружа́ть</i> (vooružátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arming, armament</li><li>arm (weapon)</li><li>equipment, rig, requisites</li></ol><p><i>вооружи́ть</i> (vooružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm, to equip</li><li>to set against, to instigate</li></ol><p>From <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>ору́жие</i> (orúžije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm, to provide, to furnish <i>(oneself)</i></li></ol><p><i>вооружи́ть</i> (vooružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armed forces, army</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armed (equipped, especially with a weapon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(international law,&#32;military) armed conflict (a contested incompatibility that entails the use of armed force between two parties including a territory, the government of a state, or both)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) with one's own eyes</li><li>plainly, clearly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въ очью</i> (vŭ očĭju), consisting of the locative dual of <i>око</i> (oko, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to yell, to scream, to howl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въпити</i> (vŭpiti, “to shriek, yell”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъpìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flagrant, outrageous, egregious, appalling, crying</li></ol><p>Cognate with Bulgarian <i>вопиющ</i> (vopijušt).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>blatant</i> (akin to English <i>bleat</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to appeal (<i>to God for mercy, justice, vengeance, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to cry out, to yell, to scream</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to lament, to wail for the dead</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въпити</i> (vŭpiti) with levelling of the present stem. Ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъpìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incarnate, to embody, to personify</li><li>to realize (a dream, a project, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въплътити</i> (vŭplŭtiti). By surface analysis, <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be incarnated/embodied <i>(in, by)</i></li><li>to ro be personified <i>(by)</i></li><li>to be realized/implemented, to be put into action/practice, to come true</li></ol><p><i>воплоти́ть</i> (voplotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incarnate, to embody, to personify</li><li>to realize (a dream, a project, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>воплоти́ть</i> (voplotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be incarnated/embodied <i>(in, by)</i></li><li>to be personified <i>(by)</i></li><li>to be realized/implemented, to be put into action/practice, to come true</li></ol><p><i>воплоща́ть</i> (voploščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incarnation, embodiment</li></ol><p><i>воплоти́ть</i> (voplotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(also&#32;figurative,&#32;often&#32;ironic) incarnate, in the flesh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, wail, howl, yell, scream (prolonged cry of distress or anguish)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъplь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in spite of, contrary to</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въпрѣкꙑ</i> (vŭprěky), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;реr-kъ</i>, whose first element comes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preh₂-</i> and is thus cognate with English <i>for</i>. Cognates include Bulgarian <i>въ́преки</i> (vǎ́preki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question</li><li>matter, issue</li><li>problem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въпросъ</i> (vŭprosŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вопро́с</i> (voprós)</li></ol><p><i>вопро́с</i> (voprós) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>interrogatively</li></ol><p><i>вопроси́тельный</i> (voprosítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interrogative pronoun (pronoun used in a question)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) question</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) interrogative</li><li>expressing a question, bewilderment or doubt</li></ol><p><i>вопроси́ть</i> (voprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question mark (punctuation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to inquire <i>(of)</i>, to question</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въпросити</i> (vŭprositi). By surface analysis, <i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) question</li></ol><p><i>вопро́с</i> (voprós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>questioner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to inquire <i>(of)</i>, to question</li></ol><p><i>вопроси́ть</i> (voprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) traitor, apostate, outlaw</li><li>vore (sexual fetish)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorъ</i>. Probably related to <i>врать</i> (vratʹ, “to lie”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>vore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief in law (under the USSR, a respected, high-ranking criminal who obeys the Thieves’ Code)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) train oil, whale oil, seal oil, fish oil (liquid oil produced by rendering marine mammal blubber or oily fish)</li><li>(dated,&#32;by extension) blubber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вървонъ</i> (vŭrvonŭ, “huntable marine mammal”), which is conventionally believed to derive from Old East Norse <i>narhval</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush into, to burst, to enter by force, violently or hurriedly, to intrude</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) related to Microsoft Word</li></ol><p><i>Ворд</i> (Vord, “Microsoft Word”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) petty thief</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) young thief</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) to work</li></ol><p>From English <i>work</i> +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coo</li><li>to bill and coo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorbьjь</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Воробей)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Vorobey</li></ol><p>From <i>воробе́й</i> (vorobéj, “sparrow (bird)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>воробе́й</i> (vorobéj)</li></ol><p><i>вороб-</i> (vorob-) +&lrm; <i>-ушек</i> (-ušek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>воробе́й</i> (vorobéj)</li></ol><p><i>вороб-</i> (vorob-) +&lrm; <i>-ышек</i> (-yšek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sparrow</li></ol><p><i>воробе́й</i> (vorobéj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abducted, stolen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, to pilfer</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) female traitor, apostate, outlaw</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thiefly</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor, “thief”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ски</i> (-ski).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thieves'; thievish</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-овско́й</i> (-ovskój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft, stealing, larceny</li></ol><p><i>ворова́ть</i> (vorovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) enemy, foe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ворогъ</i> (vorogŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorgъ</i>. Doublet of <i>враг</i> (vrag), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>во́рог</i> (vórog).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) fortune-telling, sorcery, witchcraft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voržьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ворожи́ть</i> (vorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) fortune-teller, seer, soothsayer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to tell fortunes, to divine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voržiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воронъ</i> (voronŭ, “black”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vornъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;warnás</i>. Related to Russian <i>воро́на</i> (voróna, “crow”), Lithuanian <i>var̃nas</i> (“crow, raven”). Doublet of <i>вран</i> (vran), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crow (bird)</li><li>cuckoo, gawk, gaper, loafer</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ворона</i> (vorona), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorna</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wárˀnāˀ</i>. Related to Lithuanian <i>várna</i> (“crow”), Tocharian B <i>wrauña</i> (“crow”). Also related to Russian <i>во́рон</i> (vóron, “raven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Voronezh; Voronezhian</li></ol><p><i>Воро́неж</i> (Vorónež) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crow; crow's; corvine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воронии</i> (voronii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vornьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>воро́на</i> (voróna) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funnel (utensil used to guide poured liquids)</li><li>crater; shell hole (from a shell or a bomb)</li><li>vortex; a whirlpool or a whirlwind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorna</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;regional) black horse</li></ol><p><i>вороно́й</i> (voronój) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black (<i>of horses</i>)</li><li>jet-black (<i>of human hair</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black horse</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>во́рон</i> (vóron, “raven”) +&lrm; <i>-о́й</i> (-ój); cognate with Polish <i>wrony</i>; ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wornéh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to burn, be black”), same as for Etymology 2. Compare English <i>raven-haired</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) common house martin (a type of swallow)</li><li>(regional,&#32;animate) black-coated horse</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) paddy wagon (locked police car for transportation of prisoners)</li></ol><p><i>вороно́й</i> (voronój, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, “(diminutive)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) ravens; crows</li><li>(collective,&#32;derogatory) greedy or vicious people; enemies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar (of a shirt)</li><li>winch, windlass, wheel and axle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воротъ</i> (vorotŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vortъ</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>вратъ</i> (vratŭ). Related to <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gate</li><li>goal (in many sports, e.g. soccer, an area into which the players attempt to put an object)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorta</i>. Doublet of <i>врата́</i> (vratá), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rule, to control, to manage</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to make sick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring back; to give back</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>воротити</i> (vorotiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vortiti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wártīˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wortéyeti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;wert-</i>. Doublet of <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>вратити</i> (vratiti) (cf. <i>возврати́ть</i> (vozvratítʹ), <i>изврати́ть</i> (izvratítʹ), <i>отврати́ть</i> (otvratítʹ), <i>соврати́ть</i> (sovratítʹ)).</p><p>Cognate to <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ), <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ), <i>воро́чать</i> (voróčatʹ) / <i>вороча́ть</i> (voročátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to return</li></ol><p><i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar</li></ol><p><i>во́рот</i> (vórot) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>воротни́к</i> (vorotník): collar</li></ol><p><i>воротни́к</i> (vorotník) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>воро́та</i> (voróta): (small) gates, wicket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wicket, timber (<i>in a game of cricket</i>)</li></ol><p><i>воро́та</i> (voróta) +&lrm; <i>-ца</i> (-ca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vorarephilia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile, heap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorxъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>во́рох</i> (vórox, “heap, pile”), also <i>воро́ха</i> (voróxa), Belarusian <i>во́рох</i> (vórox, “noise”), Bulgarian <i>врах</i> (vrah, “sheaves intended for threshing”), Polish <i>zawroch</i> (“blizzard, whirlwind”).</p><p>More distantly cognate with Russian Church Slavonic <i>врѣшти</i> (vrěšti, “to hammer”) (1sg. <i>вьрху</i> (vĭrxu)), Bulgarian <i>връха</i> (vrǎha, “to hammer”), Slovene <i>vršíti</i> (“to trample sheaves with cattle”), Latvian <i>vârsms</i> (“grain spread out for threshing, pile of sieved grain”), Latin <i>verrō</i> (“to drag, to sweep”) (infinitive <i>verrere</i>), Ancient Greek <i>ἔρρω</i> (érrhō, “to walk with difficulty, to limp, to trudge”), Old High German <i>wërran</i> (“to confuse”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wers-</i> (“to drag along the ground”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over with a rake (straw, hay)</li><li>to turn over once with a rake (straw, hay)</li></ol><p><i>во́рох</i> (vórox) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, to roll, to shift (something bulky or heavy)</li><li>to move (<i>with some purpose</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, esp. a body part’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work hard, to work like a dog</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to conduct, to handle, to turn over [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘lots of business or capital’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run, to have control of, to boss [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to return, to bring back, to take back</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ворочати</i> (voročati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorťati</i>. Doublet of <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p><p><i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, to toss and turn (<i>of the whole body</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, to roll (<i>of something bulky or heavy</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to return, to come back, to go back</li></ol><p><i>воро́чать</i> (voróčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>вороча́ть</i> (voročátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to turn over</li><li>to turn over with a rake (straw, hay)</li><li>to ruffle, to tousle (hair)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to stir up (unpleasant old memories)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voršiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>во́рох</i> (vórox) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nap, pile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop for napping fabric</li></ol><p><i>ворси́ть</i> (vorsítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nap (fabric) (to form a raised pile on)</li></ol><p><i>ворс</i> (vors) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop for napping fabric</li></ol><p><i>ворсова́ть</i> (vorsovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nap (fabric) (to form a raised pile on)</li></ol><p><i>ворс</i> (vors) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Pskov,&#32;rare) sausage</li></ol><p>From Estonian <i>vorst</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teasel</li></ol><p><i>ворс</i> (vors, “pile”) +&lrm; <i>-янка</i> (-janka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grumbling</li><li>growling, growl</li></ol><p><i>ворча́ть</i> (vorčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to complain, to grouse (at), to snarl (at)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъrčati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>grumpily, grouchily</li></ol><p><i>ворчли́вый</i> (vorčlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grumpy, grouchy</li></ol><p>From <i>ворч(а́ть)</i> (vorč(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grumbler, nag, nagger, grouch</li></ol><p>From <i>ворча́ть</i> (vorčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) thieves</li></ol><p>From <i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) thief</li></ol><p><i>вор</i> (vor) +&lrm; <i>-ю́га</i> (-júga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>воз-</i> (voz-) (<i>used before a voiceless consonant</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) back (to the original place, where one came from); homewards, homeward, home</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въ своѩ си</i> (vŭ svoję si).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eighteen-year period</li><li>eighteenth anniversary, eighteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>восемна́дцать</i> (vosemnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eighteen-year</li><li>eighteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>восемна́дцать</i> (vosemnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octadecagon</li></ol><p><i>восемна́дцать</i> (vosemnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighteen (18)</li></ol><p>From <i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ, “eight”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>18×, eighteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight (8)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осмь</i> (osmĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osmь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;aśtṓni</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty (80)</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ), literally “eight tens”. <b>Де́сят</b> was the Old East Slavic genitive plural, which is required by numbers <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “5”) and up.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-eight (88)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-two (82)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-nine (89)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-one (81)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-five (85)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-seven (87)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-three (83)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-four (84)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-six (86)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight hundred (800)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>8×, eight times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclaim, to cry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскликнѫти</i> (vŭskliknǫti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>кли́кнуть</i> (klíknutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exclamation (loud calling or crying out&#59; outcry)</li></ol><p><i>восклица́ть</i> (vosklicátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exclamatory, interjectional</li></ol><p><i>восклица́ть</i> (vosklicátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exclamation mark, exclamation point (punctuation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclaim, to cry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсклицати</i> (vŭsklicati), from Old Church Slavonic <i>клицати</i> (klicati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klicati</i>. Compare Russian <i>кли́кать</i> (klíkatʹ), Ukrainian <i>кли́кати</i> (klýkaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax refinery</li></ol><p><i>воск</i> (vosk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wax; waxen</li><li>wax-white</li></ol><p><i>воск</i> (vosk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to revive, to resurrect, to rise from the dead</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to revive, to feel reanimated, to rise again</li><li><i>(feelings, images etc.)</i> to revive, to come back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрьсати</i> (vŭskrĭsati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzkrьsati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>крес</i> (kres) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). More at <i>&#42;kresati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resurrection, anastasis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрьсеньѥ</i> (vŭskrĭsenĭje). By surface analysis, <i>воскреса́ть</i> (voskresátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;in toponyms) Resurrection</li></ol><p><i>воскресе́ние</i> (voskresénije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunday</li><li>alternative form of <i>воскресе́ние</i> (voskresénije, “resurrection”)</li></ol><p>From воскресе́ние (voskresénije), воскреше́ние (voskrešénije) (resurrection, of Christ).</p><p><i>День воскреше́ния</i> (Denʹ voskrešénija) (The Day of Resurrection) means a holiday, a nonworking day, and in this meaning it replaced the word <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja) which had been used for Sunday before.</p><p>Compare with Old East Slavic and Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрьсеньѥ</i> (vŭskrĭsenĭje), <i>въскрѣшеньѥ</i> (vŭskrěšenĭje).</p><p>Because of the prefix вос- (vos-) (rather than вс- (vs-)), it was an adoption from Old Church Slavonic (instead of a direct East Slavic adaptation).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise from the dead, to resurrect</li><li>to revive, to resuscitate, to reanimate</li><li>to revive, to recall, to call up, to bring back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрѣсити</i> (vŭskrěsiti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>крес</i> (kres) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). More at <i>&#42;kresati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to revive, to resurrect, to rise from the dead</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to revive, to feel reanimated, to rise again</li><li><i>(feelings, images etc.)</i> to revive, to come back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрьснѫти</i> (vŭskrĭsnǫti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzkrьsnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>крес</i> (kres) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sunday</li></ol><p>From <i>воскре́снуть</i> (voskrésnutʹ, “to resurrect”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); meaning influenced by <i>воскресе́нье</i> (voskresénʹje, “Sunday”, literally “resurrection”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise from the dead</li><li>to revive, to resuscitate, to reanimate</li><li>to revive, to recall, to call up, to bring back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въскрѣшати</i> (vŭskrěšati). By surface analysis, <i>воскреш-</i> (voskreš-) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resurrection; raising from the dead; revival</li></ol><p><i>воскреси́ть</i> (voskresítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflammation (medical condition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспалѣниѥ</i> (vŭspalěnije, “combustion, inflammation”). By surface analysis, <i>воспали́ть</i> (vospalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). The modern meaning is probably a semantic loan from French <i>inflammation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pneumonia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Said when a person is faking being sick.</li></ol><p>Literally, “cunningness inflammation”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflammatory</li></ol><p><i>воспали́ть</i> (vospalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inflame</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become inflamed</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to be inflamed <i>(with, by)</i></li></ol><p><i>воспали́ть</i> (vospalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inflame</li></ol><p><i>воспали́ть</i> (vospalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become inflamed</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to be inflamed <i>(with, by)</i></li></ol><p><i>воспаля́ть</i> (vospaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflamed</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) excited, feverish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing <i>(of)</i>, to glorify, to celebrate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспѣвати</i> (vŭspěvati). By surface analysis, <i>воспе́ть</i> (vospétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing <i>(of)</i>, to glorify, to celebrate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспѣти</i> (vŭspěti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>education, upbringing</li><li>training</li></ol><p><i>воспита́ть</i> (vospitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster child; pupil; alumnus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeding (good manners as characteristic of the aristocracy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-bred, well-mannered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>воспита́ние</i> (vospitánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>educator, (chiefly nursery/kindergarten, orphanage) teacher, mentor; tutor</li></ol><p><i>воспита́ть</i> (vospitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governess, mentor; tutor</li><li>female nursery/kindergarten teacher, governess</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>educational, educative, pedagogic</li></ol><p><i>воспита́тель</i> (vospitátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring up, to rear, to educate</li><li>to educate, to train (someone to be someone)</li><li>to foster, to cultivate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспитати</i> (vŭspitati). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring up, to rear, to educate</li><li>to educate, to train (someone to be someone)</li><li>to foster, to cultivate</li></ol><p><i>воспита́ть</i> (vospitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>replenishment</li><li>filling of gaps</li></ol><p><i>воспо́лнить</i> (vospólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill in, to supply</li><li>to make up <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспльнити</i> (vŭsplĭniti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill in, to supply</li><li>to make up <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>воспо́лнить</i> (vospólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to utilize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘health, good fortune, power, privilege, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзовать</i> (pólʹzovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory, remembrance, recollection, flashback</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) memoirs, reminiscences, memorials</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспоминаньѥ</i> (vŭspominanĭje). By surface analysis, (<i>archaic, literary</i>) <i>воспомина́ть</i> (vospominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije). Doublet of <i>вспомина́ние</i> (vspominánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to follow</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevent, to hinder, to thwart</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>препя́тствовать</i> (prepjátstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) to forbid, to prohibit [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспрѣтити</i> (vŭsprětiti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>прети́ть</i> (pretítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) to forbid, to prohibit [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспрѣщати</i> (vŭsprěštati). By surface analysis, <i>воспрет-</i> (vospret-) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) alternative form of <i>воспринима́ть</i> (vosprinimátʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсприимати</i> (vŭspriimati). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>приима́ть</i> (priimátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>susceptibility</li><li>receptiveness</li></ol><p><i>восприи́мчивый</i> (vospriímčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>susceptible, impressionable, quickly perceptive to</li></ol><p><i>восприима́ть</i> (vospriimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive, to apprehend</li><li>to appreciate, to take in (to be aware of)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>восприима́ть</i> (vospriimátʹ), remodeled after <i>принима́ть</i> (prinimátʹ). Ultimately from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсприимати</i> (vŭspriimati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive, to apprehend</li><li>to appreciate, to take in (to be aware of)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>восприя́ть</i> (vosprijátʹ), remodeled after <i>приня́ть</i> (prinjátʹ). Ultimately from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсприѩти</i> (vŭsprijęti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perception (conscious understanding of something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсприѩтиѥ</i> (vŭsprijętije). By surface analysis, <i>восприя́ть</i> (vosprijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) alternative form of <i>восприня́ть</i> (vosprinjátʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсприѩти</i> (vŭsprijęti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>прия́ть</i> (prijátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproduction</li><li>playback, play</li></ol><p><i>воспроизвед-</i> (vosproizved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproduce</li><li>to recall, to call to mind</li><li>to repeat</li><li><i>(tape recorder, VCR etc.)</i> to play <i>(back)</i></li><li>to reprint</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>произвести́</i> (proizvestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to reproduce (<i>in one's own kind</i>), to multiply</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be produced or created anew, to be re-created, to arise again</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reappear in one's memory, to be reexperienced</li></ol><p><i>воспроизвести́</i> (vosproizvestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproducer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproduce</li><li>to recall, to call to mind</li><li>to repeat</li><li><i>(tape recorder, VCR etc.)</i> to play <i>(back)</i></li><li>to reprint</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>производи́ть</i> (proizvodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to reproduce (<i>in one's own kind</i>), to multiply</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be produced or created anew, to be re-created, to arise again</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reappear in one's memory, to be reexperienced</li></ol><p><i>воспроизводи́ть</i> (vosproizvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproduction (the act of making copies)</li><li>procreation, reproduction (the process by which an organism produces others of its kind)</li></ol><p><i>воспроизводи́ть</i> (vosproizvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose, to object, to resist, to stand up <i>(against)</i>, to set one's face (against)</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>проти́виться</i> (protívitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to jump up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to perk up, to become more lively</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въспрѧнѫти</i> (vŭspręnǫti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>пря́нуть</i> (prjánutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;higher register,&#32;often&#32;ironic&#32;in modern usage) to sit (<i>in state, on a throne</i>)</li></ol><p><i>воссе́д-</i> (vosséd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;higher register,&#32;often&#32;ironic&#32;in modern usage) to sit down (upon), to mount (<i>in state, upon a throne</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;biblical) to praise, to glorify [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въславити</i> (vŭslaviti). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>сла́вить</i> (slávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;biblical) to praise, to glorify [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>воссла́вить</i> (vosslávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reunion</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>соедине́ние</i> (sojedinénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reunite</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reunite</li></ol><p><i>воссоедини́ть</i> (vossojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recreate</li><li>to reconstitute, to reconstruct, to renew</li><li>to recall, to remember</li></ol><p><i>воссозда́ть</i> (vossozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recreation, reconstruction (act of restoring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recreate</li><li>to reconstitute, to reconstruct, to renew</li><li>to recall, to remember</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolt, to rise, to rebel</li></ol><p><i>восста́ть</i> (vosstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, to reestablish, to reinstate</li><li>to recover, to recuperate</li><li>to regain, to retrieve</li><li>to regenerate, to reclaim</li><li>to reconstruct, to rebuild</li><li>to repair, to revive</li><li>to reinstall</li><li>to renew</li></ol><p><i>восстанови́ть</i> (vosstanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehabilitate oneself, to be reinstated</li><li>to be restored, to recover, to restore one's health back to normal</li></ol><p><i>восстана́вливать</i> (vosstanávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebellion, revolt, insurgency (organized resistance against a political authority)</li></ol><p><i>восста́ть</i> (vosstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) restoration; reconstructive</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) reducing</li></ol><p><i>восстанови́ть</i> (vosstanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, to reestablish, to reinstate</li><li>to recover, to recuperate</li><li>to regain, to retrieve</li><li>to regenerate, to reclaim</li><li>to reconstruct, to rebuild</li><li>to repair, to revive</li><li>to reinstall</li><li>to renew</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>-станови́ть</i> (-stanovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehabilitate oneself, to be reinstated</li><li>to be restored, to recover, to restore one's health back to normal</li></ol><p><i>восстанови́ть</i> (vosstanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restoration, renewal (the process of bringing an object back to its original state)</li><li>reinstatement</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) reduction</li></ol><p><i>восстанови́ть</i> (vosstanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolt, to rise, to rebel</li></ol><p>From <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ). Compare <i>встать</i> (vstatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>east</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въстокъ</i> (vŭstokŭ, “rising, east”), displacing the native East Slavic form <i>всток</i> (vstok) (preserved in some dialects); the Old Church Slavonic term is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀνατολή</i> (anatolḗ, “rising, east”). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>ток</i> (tok), formally by surface analysis, deverbal from <i>востека́ть</i> (vostekátʹ), though the verb is very rare and attested later. Root cognate with Bulgarian <i>изток</i> (iztok).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Восток)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the East, the Orient</li></ol><p>From <i>восто́к</i> (vostók, “east”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orientalist</li></ol><p>From <i>восто́к</i> (vostók) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вед</i> (-ved), combining form of <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oriental studies</li></ol><p><i>восток</i> (vostok) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapture, ecstasy, delight</li><li>admiration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въстръгъ</i> (vŭstrŭgŭ), a deverbal of <i>въстръгати</i> (vŭstrŭgati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight, to enrapture, to ravish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въстръгати</i> (vŭstrŭgati), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъrgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be delighted, to be enthusiastic, to be enraptured, to be in raptures, to be in ecstasies</li></ol><p><i>восторга́ть</i> (vostorgátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enthusiastically, rapturously</li></ol><p><i>восто́рженный</i> (vostóržennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enthusiasm; exaltation</li></ol><p><i>восто́рженный</i> (vostóržennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enthusiastic, ecstatic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Russian Church Slavonic <i>&#42;восторженый</i> (&#42;vostorženyj), originally past passive participle of <i>восторгнꙋти</i> (vostorgnuti, “to pull out”); compare <i>отве́рженный</i> (otvéržennyj), originally past passive participle of <i>отве́ргнуть</i> (otvérgnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to triumph over</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>торжествова́ть</i> (toržestvovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Eastern Armenian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Eastern Armenian</li></ol><p><i>восто́чный</i> (vostóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>армя́нский</i> (armjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Eastern European</li></ol><p><i>восто́чный</i> (vostóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) East Slavic (relating to the East Slavs)</li></ol><p><i>восто́чный</i> (vostóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>славя́нский</i> (slavjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>East Timorese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>east, eastern</li><li>oriental</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсточьнъ</i> (vŭstočĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>восто́к</i> (vostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claiming, demanding</li></ol><p><i>востре́бовать</i> (vostrébovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demand, need</li><li>being in demand, importance, relevance</li></ol><p><i>востре́бованный</i> (vostrébovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to claim, to collect (money)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to demand, to require (something to be done)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to summon</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;transitive) to sharpen, to whet</li></ol><p>From <i>во́стрый</i> (vóstryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, swift, agile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a pointed, narrow nose</li></ol><p>From a dialectal variant of <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj, “pointed, sharp”) (compare Belarusian <i>во́стры</i> (vóstry)) + <i>-о-</i> (-o-) + <i>нос</i> (nos) + <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise, glorification, praising, laudation, encomium (category of oratory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise, to eulogize</li></ol><p><i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise, to eulogize</li></ol><p><i>восхвали́ть</i> (vosxvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, delightful, admirable (causing wonder and amazement)</li></ol><p><i>восхити́ть</i> (vosxitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrapture, to delight, to ravish</li><li>(dated,&#32;high-style) to lift up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсхытити</i> (vŭsxytiti, “to fascinate, to snatch away”).</p><p>Possibly an independent borrowing of the same Old Church Slavonic word as above. See also <i>-хи́тить</i> (-xítitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be delighted</li><li>to admire [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to be carried away</li></ol><p><i>восхити́ть</i> (vosxitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrapture, to delight, to ravish</li><li>(dated,&#32;high-style) to lift up</li></ol><p><i>восхити́ть</i> (vosxitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>восхи́тить</i> (vosxítitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be delighted</li><li>to admire [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to be carried away</li></ol><p><i>восхища́ть</i> (vosxiščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiration, delight, rapture, ravishment</li></ol><p><i>восхити́ть</i> (vosxitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>admiringly, with admiration</li></ol><p><i>восхищённый</i> (vosxiščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>admiring, rapturous, ecstatic, delighted (<i>experiencing admiration, delight or rapture; of a person</i>)</li><li>admiring, rapturous, ecstatic, delighted (<i>expressing admiration, delight or rapture; of a face, look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rise</li><li>sunrise</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) east</li><li>Voskhod (Soviet spacecraft)</li><li>Voskhod (Soviet motorcycle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсходъ</i> (vŭsxodŭ). Doublet of <i>всход</i> (vsxod). Сompare <i>восходи́ть</i> (vosxodítʹ) and <i>всходи́ть</i> (vsxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend, to climb, to go up, to mount</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to go back (to a particular time)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсходити</i> (vŭsxoditi). By surface analysis, <i>вос-</i> (vos-) +&lrm; <i>ходить</i> (xoditʹ). Doublet of <i>всходи́ть</i> (vsxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rising, ascending</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascent, rise (act of ascending&#59; motion upwards)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eighth part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective number</i>) eight (in a group together), eight of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) octahedron</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eighty-year period</li><li>eightieth anniversary, eightieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>восьми́десяти</i> (vosʹmídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eighty-year</li><li>eighty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>восьми́десяти</i> (vosʹmídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eightieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eight-day</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eight-year period</li><li>eighth anniversary, eighth birthday</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eight-year</li><li>eight-year-old</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eight-week</li><li>eight weeks old</li></ol><p>From <i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octagon</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>octagonal</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eight-hour</li></ol><p>From во́семь (vósemʹ, “eight”) and часово́й (časovój, “hour (relational)”), the latter itself from <i>час</i> (čas, “hour”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój, “<i>relational suffix</i>”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осмъ</i> (osmŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;òsmъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;aśtmas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;now&#32;rare) one eighth</li></ol><p><i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number eight, eight (e.g. cards), figure of eight</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) eight</li><li>No. 8 bus, tram, etc</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) VAZ-2108 (Lada Samara) 3-door hatchback</li><li>(<i>rowing</i>) eight, octuple (a boat with eight rowers)</li><li>(<i>climbing</i>) figure 8 (belay device)</li><li>(<i>climbing</i>) figure-eight loop</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) wisdom tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Used in place of a pronoun to direct attention towards something; this (is), here (is), that (is), there (is)</li><li>Used before&#59; both before and after&#59; or (less commonly) after a pronoun or adverb to direct attention towards a particular thing being shown, pointed to, indicated, etc.</li><li>Used before a full sentence to describe a scene unfolding before one's eyes.</li><li>Used as a lead-in, often when introducing an example; so, now</li><li>(often with <i>и</i> (i)) Used before a statement to draw attention to a conclusion or result; so, that's why</li><li>Used in set phrases.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used to express emphasis or strong emotion; what a, such a</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as a filler word, usually between statements.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>here, look</li><li>(<i>casual</i>) take it! (<i>when offering something</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ото</i> (oto) or <i>вото</i> (voto), compare with Polish <i>oto</i> and <i>ot</i>, Ukrainian <i>от</i> (ot) and <i>ото</i> (oto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> вот (vot),&lrm; бы (by).</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) used to express a wish, can be used with infinitives, verb in past tense or with noun phrases</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) oh really, is that so, is that how it is (exclamation expressing surprise, usually with a sense of indignation or distrust)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>lo and behold (used to express surprise)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wow, how about that <i>(expression of astonishment)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) just about to; on the point of</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) yes, that's it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in, into)</i>, to drive <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wadmal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vote (formalised choice)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>votum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrimonial estate, patrimony</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) patrimony</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>отьчина</i> (otĭčina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otьčina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>patrimōnium</i> (&lt; <i>pater</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patrimony</li></ol><p><i>во́тчина</i> (vótčina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain, vainly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вътъще</i> (vŭtŭšte).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to ascend the throne</li><li>to set in, to be established</li></ol><p><i>во-</i> (vo-) +&lrm; <i>цари́ть</i> (carítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to ascend the throne</li><li>to set in, to be established</li></ol><p><i>воцари́ться</i> (vocarítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christian church rite for an infant & mother after a successful birth.</li><li>Generally, the rituals, habits, and customs attendant to regular participation in church services.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to the “churching of women” in the Eastern Orthodox Church.</li><li>Generally relating to the rites or customs of the Orthodox Church.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>вошь</i> (vošʹ)</li></ol><p><i>вошь</i> (vošʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>louse (insect)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъšь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wax, to polish with wax</li></ol><p><i>воск</i> (vosk) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voyage, trip (formal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>voyage</i>, further from Latin <i>viāticum</i>, from Latin <i>viāticus</i>, from Latin <i>via</i> + <i>-āticus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;derogatory</i>) warrior, fighter</li><li>fire-eater</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(river)</i> to fall <i>(into)</i>, to flow <i>(into)</i></li><li><i>(a condition)</i> to fall <i>(into)</i>, to lapse <i>(into)</i>, to sink <i>(into)</i></li><li><i>(cheeks, eyes etc.)</i> to sink in, to become hollow/sunken</li></ol><p><i>впад-</i> (vpad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depression, hollow, trough, scoop (area that is lower than its surroundings)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sunken-eyed</li></ol><p>From <i>впа́л(ый)</i> (vpál(yj), “sunken”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having sunken cheeks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sunken (<i>of the cheeks, eyes, or another part of the face or body</i>)</li></ol><p><i>впа(д)-</i> (vpa(d)-) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(river)</i> to fall <i>(into)</i>, to flow <i>(into)</i></li><li><i>(a condition)</i> to fall <i>(into)</i>, to lapse <i>(into)</i>, to sink <i>(into)</i></li><li><i>(cheeks, eyes etc.)</i> to sink in, to become hollow/sunken</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solder in</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to sentence (to), to condemn (to)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пая́ть</i> (pajátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) for the first time, alternative form of <i>впервы́е</i> (vpervýje)</li></ol><p>Similar to <i>впервы́е</i> (vpervýje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for the first time</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>первы́е</i> (pervýje). Compare Polish <i>wpierw</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trying to overtake the opponent or one another; trying to do something ahead of someone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trying to overtake the opponent or one another; trying to do something ahead of someone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trying to overtake the opponent or one another; trying to do something ahead of someone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in front (of), ahead (of), before</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въ переди</i> (vŭ peredi), the latter the locative case of <i>передь</i> (peredĭ), which was displaced by modern перёд (perjód), from <i>передъ</i> (peredŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternately, in turn</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>перемеж(а́ться)</i> (peremež(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ку</i> (-ku)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternately, in turn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pell-mell, in a jumble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in all directions, every which way</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>переме́ш(ивать)</i> (pereméš(ivatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ку</i> (-ku)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;higher register,&#32;transitive) to turn, to fix (<i>especially взгля́д, взор, о́чи</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;informal) to get across (<i>idea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stare, to gaze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;higher register,&#32;transitive) to turn, to fix (<i>especially взгля́д, взор, о́чи</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;informal) to get across (<i>idea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stare, to gaze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) from now on, for the future</li><li>in advance</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>перёд</i> (perjód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impression</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Eindruck</i>, which in turn is a calque of French <i>impression</i>. By surface analysis, <i>впечатли́ть</i> (vpečatlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressionable</li><li>impressive, intense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъpečatьlitelьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>впечатли́ть</i> (vpečatlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impress</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>впечатл-</i> (vpečatl-) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(into)</i>, to pierce, to bite <i>(into)</i>, to drive one's sting <i>(into)</i>, to dig one's fangs <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fix one's eyes <i>(on)</i>, to fix with one's eyes, to fasten one's eyes <i>(upon)</i>, to glue one's eyes <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>впи́ться</i> (vpítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to insert (something to the text that is already written by writing)</li><li>to record (something in a diary or a journal)</li><li>to inscribe</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blend <i>(with)</i>, to fall in <i>(with)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fit <i>(into)</i>, to fit in <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>вписа́ть</i> (vpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing in, filling in (<i>a box in a form</i>)</li><li>a name or other text that has been written in or added</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a place to crash, couch surfing accommodations</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) an overnight house party</li></ol><p><i>вписа́ть</i> (vpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to insert (something to the text that is already written by writing)</li><li>to record (something in a diary or a journal)</li><li>to inscribe</li></ol><p><i>вписа́ть</i> (vpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blend <i>(with)</i>, to fall in <i>(with)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fit <i>(into)</i>, to fit in <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>впи́сывать</i> (vpísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absorbed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soak up, to soak in, to drink in, to absorb, to imbibe, to take in, to sop</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption (act or process of absorbing or sucking in anything)</li></ol><p><i>впи́тывать</i> (vpítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soak up, to soak in, to drink in, to absorb, to imbibe, to take in, to sop</li></ol><p><i>впита́ть</i> (vpitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(into)</i>, to pierce, to bite <i>(into)</i>, to drive one's sting <i>(into)</i>, to dig one's fangs <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fix one's eyes <i>(on)</i>, to fix with one's eyes, to fasten one's eyes <i>(upon)</i>, to glue one's eyes <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shove in, to squeeze in, to insert</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пихну́ть</i> (pixnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>closely, tightly</li><li>seriously, in earnest</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пло́тный</i> (plótnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>up to, right up to, down to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with half-closed eyes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sotto voce (quietly (of speech))</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>го́лоса</i> (gólosa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl in, to crawl inside</li><li>to crawl up, to climb up by crawling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to climb up with difficulty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to penetrate, to creep in (<i>of fear, anxiety, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вползти́</i> (vpolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl in, to crawl inside</li><li>to crawl up, to climb up by crawling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to climb up with difficulty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to penetrate, to creep in (<i>of fear, anxiety, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quite, fully, entirely</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) at half strength</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) halfheartedly, halfway</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>си́лы</i> (síly)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) apropos, to the point, to the purpose, opportunely</li></ol><p>From <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>попа́д</i> (popád), where the second part appears to be an obsolete or nonexistent noun based on <i>попа́сть</i> (popástʹ, “to hit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a hurry, hurriedly, helter-skelter, hastily</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пы́хать</i> (pýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ах</i> (-ax).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fitting just right</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one might as well, one can do little more than</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>по́ру</i> (póru)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>subsequently, afterwards, afterward</li><li>thereafter, after</li><li>at a later date</li><li>in the sequel</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>после́дствии</i> (poslédstvii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the dark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) really, indeed</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вду</i> (právdu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the right</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>henceforth, henceforward, from now on, in future, for the future</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъ</i> (prědŭ, “before, in front of”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bouncing, skipping (e.g. when running or walking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>right (up) against, without a gap, flush, back to back, chockablock</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) with no reserve or surplus; exactly corresponding to something [<i>with</i> finite verb <i>or</i> genitive noun]</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>приты́к</i> (pritýk, “connection between wooden parts at the ends”), related to <i>притыка́ть</i> (pritykátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for future use, in reserve, in store</li><li>to one's benefit</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>прок</i> (prok, “benefit, use”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>however, nevertheless</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>про́чем</i> (próčem), prepositional singular of <i>про́чий</i> (próčij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injection</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) injected drug</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>впры́снуть</i> (vprýsnutʹ), based on the underlying stem found in imperfective <i>впры́скивать</i> (vprýskivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inject</li><li>to spray (liquid, solution, gas, etc.) in small doses inside (something)</li></ol><p><i>впрыск-</i> (vprysk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inject</li><li>to inject once</li><li>to spray (liquid, solution, gas, etc.) in small doses inside (something)</li><li>to spray (a single dose of liquid, solution, gas, etc.) inside (something)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пры́снуть</i> (prýsnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) straight, frankly, like it is</li></ol><p>From <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>пряму́ю</i> (prjamúju), feminine accusative singular of <i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój, “straight, direct”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) really, indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harness (<i>a beast of burden</i>), to yoke (<i>an ox</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-прячь</i> (-prjačʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let in, to admit</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let in, to admit</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain, for nothing (without success)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пусту́ю</i> (pustúju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, foe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врагъ</i> (vragŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorgъ</i>. Compare the inherited doublet <i>во́рог</i> (vórog, “enemy, foe”) (<i>poetic</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;law) enemy of the people (Roman law, French Revolution 1789‒1799 and Soviet Union 1930‒1950)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enmity, hostility, animosity, antagonism (hostile or unfriendly disposition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вражьда</i> (vražĭda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voržьda</i> (the Old East Slavic form is found in the poetic Ukrainian <i>ворожда</i> (vorožda)), from <i>&#42;vorgъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ьda</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>wróżda</i>, Czech <i>vražda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a hostile manner, in an unfriendly manner</li></ol><p><i>вражде́бный</i> (vraždébnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostility, animosity, enmity</li></ol><p><i>вражде́бный</i> (vraždébnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile, inimical</li><li>malevolent (aspiring to cause harm)</li></ol><p><i>вражда́</i> (vraždá) +&lrm; <i>-е́бный</i> (-ébnyj). A Church Slavicism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel <i>(with, with each other)</i>, to be at war, to be at loggerheads <i>(with, with each other)</i></li></ol><p><i>вражда́</i> (vraždá) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enemy, hostile</li></ol><p><i>враг</i> (vrag) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) enemy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;voržьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>враг</i> (vrag) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) all together, simultaneously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) immediately, at once, straight away</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>раз</i> (raz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disconnectedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, uncoordinatedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, uncoordinatedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a waddling manner, shifting one's weight from one leg to the other</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sprawling, with arms and legs stretched out</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>разва́л(ивать)</i> (razvál(ivatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ку</i> (-ku); compare впереме́шку (vpereméšku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>irregularly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, uncoordinatedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(usually with verbs such as <i>идти́</i> (idtí, “to go”)) against, counter (to), at odds (with) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental)]</li><li>(usually with verbs such as <i>идти́</i> (idtí, “to go”)) in spite of, contrary to [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>разре́з</i> (razréz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intelligible</li></ol><p><i>вразуми́ть</i> (vrazumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince, to enlighten</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ра́зум</i> (rázum) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince, to enlighten</li></ol><p><i>вразуми́ть</i> (vrazumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lie, falsehood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lies, nonsense, tall tales; vranyo</li><li>lying</li></ol><p>from <i>врать</i> (vratʹ, “to lie”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unawares, unexpectedly, by surprise</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>расплох</i> (rasplox). The latter is formally related to плохо́й (ploxój), but semantically is closer to поло́х (polóx), which both derive from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in all directions, helter-skelter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow in, to take root, to grow <i>(into)</i></li><li>to root itself <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>врасти́</i> (vrastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow in, to take root, to grow <i>(into)</i></li><li>to root itself <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) gate, gates</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врата</i> (vrata), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorta</i>. Doublet of <i>воро́та</i> (voróta), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goalkeeper</li><li>goaltender</li></ol><p>From <i>врата́</i> (vratá, “gate”) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ), calque of German <i>Torwart</i> (“goalkeeper”, literally “gate guardian”). Compare Belarusian <i>варата́р</i> (varatár), Serbo-Croatian <i>vràtār</i> / вра̀та̄р, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie</li><li>to make a mistake</li><li>to be inaccurate</li><li>to tell tales</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrati</i>, 1sg. <i>&#42;vьrǫ</i> (as a verb, only preserved in Russian and Serbo-Croatian). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to speak”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>varati</i> (“to betray”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;formal) therapist, not surgeon, not hospital nurse</li><li>doc, vet, medic, physician, doctor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врачь</i> (vračĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьračь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>врать</i> (vratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač). Originally meant "witch-doctor, enchanter".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medical</li></ol><p><i>врач</i> (vrač) +&lrm; <i>-е́бный</i> (-ébnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal</li></ol><p><i>врач</i> (vrač) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolve, to rotate, to turn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вращати</i> (vraštati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorťati</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i>, whence English <i>vertex</i>, <i>versatile</i>, <i>worth</i> and <i>-ward</i>. Doublet of <i>воро́чать</i> (voróčatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, to revolve, to rotate <i>(intransitive)</i></li></ol><p><i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotation, revolution (turning around a centre)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;врати́ть</i> (&#42;vratítʹ) + <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harm, damage</li><li>detriment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣдъ</i> (vrědŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verdъ</i>. Doublet of <i>ве́ред</i> (véred), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pest; vermin</li><li>saboteur; wrecker; troublemaker; damage-doer</li><li>harmful computer program; a piece of malware</li></ol><p><i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sabotage, wrecking; act of sabotage</li></ol><p><i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure, to harm, to hurt, to damage, to cause damage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣдити</i> (vrěditi). from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вред</i> (vred) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>harmfully</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spitefully</li></ol><p><i>вре́дный</i> (vrédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmfulness</li><li>unhealthiness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bitchiness (of a character)</li></ol><p><i>вре́дный</i> (vrédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, bad, injurious, detrimental, unhealthy</li><li>spiteful, malicious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣдьнъ</i> (vrědĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verdьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вред</i> (vred) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) harmful computer program, a piece of malware</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>вредоно́сный</i> (vredonósnyj, “harmful”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, detrimental, deleterious (causing damage or harm)</li></ol><p><i>вред</i> (vred) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut in, to fit in, to cut <i>(into)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit, to paste it, to give a smack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a piece of one's mind, to let have it, to give the business</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>вре́зать</i> (vrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run into, to cut into, to collide with</li></ol><p><i>вре́зать</i> (vrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>вреза́ть</i> (vrezátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut in, to fit in, to cut <i>(into)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вре́зать</i> (vrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run into, to cut into, to collide with</li></ol><p><i>вре́зывать</i> (vrézyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at times, from time to time, now and then, every now and then, now and again, sometimes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>temporarily</li></ol><p><i>вре́менный</i> (vrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) time; temporal</li></ol><p><i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>temporary, short term, transient</li><li>makeshift, stopgap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>врѣменьнъ</i> (vrěmenĭnŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣменьнъ</i> (vrěmenĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) favourite/favorite, minion (<i>of a monarch</i>)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) timeserver (someone temporarily and undeservedly raised to a position of authority)</li><li>temporary inhabitant or worker</li></ol><p>From <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-), combining form of <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja, “time”), which also had the now obsolete sense of “fortunate incident”, + <i>-щи́к</i> (-ščík).</p><p>From <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-), combining form of <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja, “time”), + <i>-щик</i> (-ščik). Compare etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja): time</li></ol><p><i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko, “(usually endearing diminutive suffix)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) tense</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>врѣмѧ</i> (vrěmę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vermę</i> &lt; <i>&#42;vertmen</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wertmen</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>веремѧ</i> (veremę). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वर्त्मन्</i> (vártman, “track, course”).</p><p>See also pleophony (CeRC &gt; CeReC) and liquid metathesis (CeRC &gt; CReC, CRēC).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>season</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from time to time, at times, now and then, every now and then, now and again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>time-consuming</li></ol><p><i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja) +&lrm; <i>затра́тный</i> (zatrátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a temporary building, hut, shack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to perspire profusely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьрѣти</i> (vĭrěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrěti</i> (“to boil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acting (temporarily assuming a position) [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘role being filled’</small>]</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>вре́менно исполня́ющий (исполня́ющая) обя́занности</i> (vrémenno ispolnjájuščij (ispolnjájuščaja) objázannosti, “temporarily performing the duties”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>flush, even, level [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>on a par</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the same</li></ol><p>From <i>в</i> (v) + a form of unclear formation related to <i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj, “even, level”) (close to the masculine singular short form <i>ро́вен</i> (róven)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>like, such as, kind of, not unlike, similar to</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>as if, it seems</li></ol><p>Most likely a univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ро́де</i> (róde).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apparently, seemingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kind of, sort of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innate, inborn, indigenous</li></ol><p>Evidently a calque of Latin <i>innātus</i> (“inborn”), compare with English <i>inborn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>asunder, apart</li><li>separately, apart</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>рознь</i> (roznʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>asunder, apart</li><li>separately, apart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Wroclaw</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>wrocławski</i>. By surface analysis, <i>Вро́цлав</i> (Vróclav) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insert [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into a hole cut in something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to switch on, to turn on</li></ol><p><i>вруби́ть</i> (vrubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get the hang of something</li></ol><p><i>вруба́ть</i> (vrubátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insert [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into a hole cut in something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to switch on, to turn on</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get the hang of something</li></ol><p><i>вруби́ть</i> (vrubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hand to hand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p><i>врать</i> (vratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>врун</i> (vrun): liar</li></ol><p><i>врун</i> (vrun) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand over, to deliver, to present</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to entrust</li></ol><p><i>вручи́ть</i> (vručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handing in, delivery; presentation (of a reward, bonus)</li></ol><p><i>вручи́ть</i> (vručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand over, to deliver, to present</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to entrust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by hand, manually</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ручну́ю</i> (ručnúju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p>From <i>вр(ать)</i> (vr(atʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush into, to burst, to enter by force, to enter violently or hurriedly, to intrude</li><li>to dig oneself into</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hardly, scarcely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>I doubt it, (it is) unlikely, I'm afraid not</li><li>hardly, scarcely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>вряд ли</i> (vrjad li)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вз-</i> (vz-) (<i>used before voiceless consonants</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(into)</i>, to thrust <i>(into)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend <i>(on)</i>, to pour <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horseman, equestrian, rider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсадьникъ</i> (vŭsadĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>садить</i> (saditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(into)</i>, to thrust <i>(into)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend <i>(on)</i>, to pour <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>всади́ть</i> (vsadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) true, real</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption, suction (act or process of absorbing or sucking in anything)</li></ol><p><i>вса́сывать</i> (vsásyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck in</li><li>to soak up, to soak in, to absorb</li></ol><p><i>всоса́ть</i> (vsosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>everyone, everybody</li><li>(<i>in the genitive case</i>) used after comparatives of adjectives or adverbs as one way to form the superlative when talking about people; than anyone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>all together!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) all hands on deck! (order)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>all-, omni-, pan-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absolutely everyone, absolutely all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>omniscient</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; (<i>a variant of</i>) <i>ве́дающий</i> (védajuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) great, almighty</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-seeing (having knowledge of all things said or done)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>ви́дящий</i> (vídjaščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>various, of all kinds</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>возмо́жный</i> (vozmóžnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) the Most High, the supreme God</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>вы́шний</i> (výšnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьсегда</i> (vĭsegda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьśegъda</i>. The все part is related to весь meaning "all, every, the whole".</p><p>The -гда ending is also found in когда, see the etymology there. This ending may have derived from the genitive of <i>год</i> (god, “period of time”). See also годъ, тогда.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) customary, accustomed, wonted</li></ol><p><i>всегда́</i> (vsegdá) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with numbers</i>) in total, in all</li><li>(<i>with numbers</i>) only, merely, no more than</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>all the best (good luck)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>only, no more than</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>all the best (good luck)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>all the best (good luck)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in all</li><li>only, a mere</li></ol><p><i>всего́</i> (vsevó) +&lrm; <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>всего́</i> (vsevó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only, no more than, a mere</li></ol><p><i>всего́</i> (vsevó) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everyday, daily, day-to-day, usual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permissiveness, policy of all-permissiveness</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>дозво́ленность</i> (dozvólennostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>know-it-all</li></ol><p>From <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the imperative <i>знай</i> (znaj) of <i>знать</i> (znatʹ, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>omniscient</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>зна́ющий</i> (znájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, accommodation (<i>into a new residence</i>), moving in</li></ol><p><i>всели́ть</i> (vselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>universe, the world</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въселенаꙗ</i> (vŭselenaja, “the inhabited world, ecumene”). Doublet of <i>вселённая</i> (vseljónnaja).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вселенная)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Universe (the specific universe where we all live in)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) universe; universal</li><li>(<i>often&#32;ironic</i>) worldwide, universal</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) ecumenical</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въселеньскъ</i> (vŭselenĭskŭ). By surface analysis, <i>вселе́н(ная)</i> (vselén(naja)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make someone occupy some space; to settle someone or something in</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figurative) to instill, imbue (confidence, fear, etc) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move in, to move <i>(into)</i>, to install oneself <i>(in)</i>, <i>(illegally)</i> to squat <i>(in)</i></li><li>to take root <i>(in)</i>, to become implanted <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>всели́ть</i> (vselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle in (someone for a living), to take in (tenants)</li><li>to instill (hope, horror, etc), to imbue, to inspire [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>всели́ть</i> (vselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move in, to move <i>(into)</i>, to install oneself <i>(in)</i>, <i>(illegally)</i> to squat <i>(in)</i></li><li>to take root <i>(in)</i>, to become implanted <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>вселя́ть</i> (vseljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in all possible ways, in every possible way</li></ol><p><i>всеме́рный</i> (vsemérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all possible, every possible</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>-ме́рный</i> (-mérnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the amount of seven persons</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by hook or by crook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>universally</li></ol><p><i>всеми́рный</i> (vsemírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>global, world-wide, universal</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>ми́р</i> (mír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omnipotence</li></ol><p><i>всемогу́щий</i> (vsemogúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>almighty, all-powerful, omnipotent</li></ol><p>From <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>могу́щий</i> (mogúščij). See also <i>могу́чий</i> (mogúčij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national, nationwide</li><li>public</li></ol><p>Either from <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>наро́дный</i> (naródnyj) or from <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) night service <i>(vespers and matins)</i></li><li>(<i>music</i>) vespers</li></ol><p>Apparently a substantivized feminine of <i>всено́щный</i> (vsenóščnyj), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-night (<i>especially of a religious service or vigil</i>)</li></ol><p>Constructed as if from <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), but the щ indicates influence from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>universal, general</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-encompassing (including everything)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>объе́млющий</i> (obʺjémljuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full armor</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>ору́жие</i> (orúžije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comprehensively, all-encompassingly</li></ol><p><i>всеохва́тный</i> (vseoxvátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-inclusive, all-encompassing</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>охва́т</i> (oxvát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-inclusive (providing all amenities, benefits or perquisites in a single package)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>охва́тывающий</i> (oxvátyvajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) all-devouring</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>пожира́ющий</i> (požirájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-Russian</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in earnest, seriously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worldwide</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>almighty (unlimited in might)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(uncommon,&#32;historical) pan-Soviet (spanning all or most of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;religion) holocaust (offering or sacrifice to a deity that is completely burned to ashes)</li></ol><p>From <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>сожжение</i> (sožženije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-Union (referring to the whole of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comprehensively, thoroughly, holistically</li></ol><p><i>всесторо́нний</i> (vsestorónnij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comprehensive, thorough, overall, detailed, all-round, holistic</li></ol><p>From <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in the expression&#58;</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>услы́шать</i> (uslýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) fully, wholeheartedly, unreservedly, wholly, entirely, completely</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>це́лый</i> (célyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>omnivorous</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) indiscriminate, undiscerning (<i>of tastes, interests</i>)</li></ol><p><i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>-яд</i> (-jad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); calque of Latin <i>omnivorus</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(unexpectedly) to jump (up, onto, out of), to leap (on, into, onto), to get up quickly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rise, to swell (up), to come up (<i>e.g. a pimple on the face, a bump on the head</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вскок-</i> (vskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scramble <i>(up, upon)</i>, to clamber <i>(up, on to)</i></li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>кара́бкаться</i> (karábkatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scramble <i>(up, upon)</i>, to clamber <i>(up, on to)</i></li></ol><p><i>вскара́бкаться</i> (vskarábkatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nurture (an animal); to feed and raise (an animal)</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to raise, to educate (a child, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вскорми́ть</i> (vskormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw up, to toss up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to jerk up (head, hands, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вски́нуть</i> (vskínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) to rear</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attack, to go <i>(for)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>вски́дывать</i> (vskídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw up, to toss up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to jerk up (head, hands, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) to rear</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attack, to go <i>(for)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>вски́нуть</i> (vskínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil up</li></ol><p><i>вскипе́ть</i> (vskipétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to boil</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flare up, to fly into a rage</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disheveled; tousled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>колошма́тить</i> (kološmátitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to shake, to stir</li><li>to stir up, to rouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in passing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>soon</li><li>before long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nurture (an animal); to feed and raise (an animal)</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to raise, to educate (a child, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) soon, shortly</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>ско́рости</i> (skórosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(unexpectedly) to jump (up, onto, out of), to leap (on, into, onto), to get up quickly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rise, to swell (up), to come up (<i>e.g. a pimple on the face, a bump on the head</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shriek, cry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout out loudly</li></ol><p><i>вскри́кнуть</i> (vskríknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout out loudly</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>кри́кнуть</i> (kríknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout out loudly</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open, to unseal</li><li>to reveal, to bring to light, to disclose, to uncover, to expose</li><li>to dissect, to make a postmortem, to perform an autopsy on</li><li>to burst, to cut, to open, to lance</li></ol><p><i>вскрыть</i> (vskrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to light, to be revealed, to be disclosed</li><li><i>(abscess, boil)</i> to burst, to break</li></ol><p><i>вскрыва́ть</i> (vskryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening, unsealing</li><li>autopsy, dissection (a dissection performed on a cadaver)</li></ol><p><i>вскрыть</i> (vskrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open, to unseal</li><li>to reveal, to bring to light, to disclose, to uncover, to expose</li><li>to dissect, to make a postmortem, to perform an autopsy on</li><li>to burst, to cut, to open, to lance</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to one's heart's content</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сласть</i> (slastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>after, right after, behind, following [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after, behind, following</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>след</i> (sled)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as a consequence of</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сле́дствие</i> (slédstvije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blind, blindly</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>слепу́ю</i> (slepúju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aloud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen attentively <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen attentively <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>вслу́шаться</i> (vslúšatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrutinize, to peer, to look carefully</li></ol><p><i>всмотре́ться</i> (vsmotrétʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrutinize, to peer, to look carefully</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>в смы́сле</i> (v smýsle); what do you mean</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>в смы́сле</i> (v smýsle); (I) mean, (you) mean etc.</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>в</i> (v, “in”) +&lrm; <i>смы́сле</i> (smýsle, “sense”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>soft-boiled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) completely broken, smashed (e.g. of a car after a bad accident)</li></ol><p>(<i>soft-boild egg</i>): <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>смя́тка</i> (smjátka) +&lrm; <i>-у</i> (-u), akin to <i>мясти́</i> (mjastí), <i>мяте́ж</i> (mjatéž), <i>му́тный</i> (mútnyj).</p><p>(<i>smashed</i>): From <i>смя́тый</i> (smjátyj), <i>смять</i> (smjatʹ) (<~ <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to stuff, to cram [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сова́ть</i> (sovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to shove [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li><li>to thrust (<i>into one's hands or a pocket</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-со́вывать</i> (-sóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck in</li><li>to soak up, to soak in, to absorb</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flutter out or up (<i>of birds, butterflies, snow, sheets of paper, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bound up, to quickly and easily jump up</li></ol><p><i>вспорхну́ть</i> (vsporxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slit open</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to till (to plow in preparation for sowing)</li><li>to turn up, to plow up, to loosen (<i>soil, snow, etc.</i>), leaving marks on the surface</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to till (to plow in preparation for sowing)</li><li>to turn up, to plow up, to loosen (<i>soil, snow, etc.</i>), leaving marks on the surface</li></ol><p><i>вспаха́ть</i> (vspaxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cry a little, to weep a little [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splash</li><li>bump, burst, flash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash</li><li>to clasp</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>плесну́ть</i> (plesnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise to the surface, to surface</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to appear, to pop up, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>всплыть</i> (vsplytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise to the surface, to surface</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to appear, to pop up, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash</li></ol><p><i>всплеск-</i> (vsplesk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flash (of light or fire)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) northern lights (or a flash or reflection of them)</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) anxiety, turmoil</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) alarm bell announcing a disaster and calling together the people</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>поло́х</i> (polóx), a deverbal of Old East Slavic <i>полошити</i> (pološiti, “to scare”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate, to rouse, to startle, to alarm</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>полоши́ть</i> (pološítʹ, “to frighten, to cause concern”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be thrown into a flutter, to be startled, to take alarm</li></ol><p><i>всполоши́ть</i> (vspološítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recall, to remember, to recollect, to reminisce</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to mind, to be recalled</li></ol><p><i>вспомина́ть</i> (vspominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to recall, to recollect</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to mind, to be recalled</li></ol><p><i>вспо́мнить</i> (vspómnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auxiliary, subsidiary</li></ol><p><i>вспомога́ть</i> (vspomogátʹ, “to help”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auxiliary verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auxiliary language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slit open</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flutter out or up, to flit out or up (<i>of birds, butterflies, snow, sheets of paper, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bound up, to quickly and easily jump up</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>порхну́ть</i> (porxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get sweaty, to perspire</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to get worried, to get nervous, to start worrying</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>поте́ть</i> (potétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away, to frighten away</li></ol><p><i>вспугну́ть</i> (vspugnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away, to frighten away</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пугну́ть</i> (pugnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to become swollen</li></ol><p><i>вспу́хнуть</i> (vspúxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to inflate</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fire up, to flare up, to blaze up</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пыл</i> (pyl) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irascible, irritable, quick-tempered, hot-tempered (easily or quickly angered)</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пы́лкий</i> (pýlkij, “ardent, fervent”) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj) or <i>вспыли́ть</i> (vspylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flash, flare (a sudden, brief light)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вспы́хнуть</i> (vspýxnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash (to briefly illuminate a scene)</li><li>to blaze up, to break out (of fire), to flare up</li><li>to blush, to flush</li></ol><p><i>вспы́хнуть</i> (vspýxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash (to briefly illuminate a scene)</li><li>to blaze up, to break out (of fire), to flare up</li><li>to blush, to flush</li></ol><p>From <i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>пыхну́ть</i> (pyxnútʹ, “to puff”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flash</li><li>outbreak</li></ol><p><i>вспы́хнуть</i> (vspýxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) back (to or in a previous condition or place)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъzъ-</i> (“up, back”) + <i>&#42;pęta</i> (“heel”). Shares the same root as <i>опя́ть</i> (opjátʹ), <i>пя́тка</i> (pjátka), <i>пя́титься</i> (pjátitʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bad, low-quality, cheapo, tasteless, uninteresting, soggy, crappy, shitty</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) intoxicated by drugs</li></ol><p><i>всрать</i> (vsratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj), ultimately from срать (sratʹ, “to shit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up, to get up, to rise</li><li>to rise (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>to arise, to come up (<i>of a question</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>встать</i> (vstatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise with the lark; to rise at cockcrow (to wake up early)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insert, to put in, to paste</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to fit [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вста́вить</i> (vstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting in, fixing; framing; mounting; insertion, inset</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insert, to put in</li></ol><p><i>вста́вить</i> (vstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to fit [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вставля́ть</i> (vstavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) insert, insertion</li><li>plug-in</li><li>false, artificial (<i>of a body part</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вста́в(ить)</i> (vstáv(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a long time ago, in olden times, formerly, in the olden days, of old</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up, to get up, to rise</li><li>to rise (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>to arise, to come up (<i>of a question</i>)</li><li>to come to a stop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>възстати</i> (vŭzstati), from <i>въз-</i> (vŭz-) + <i>стати</i> (stati). By surface analysis, <i>вз- &#47; вс-</i> (vz- &#47; vs-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ). Compare <i>восста́ть</i> (vosstátʹ), with the Church Slavonic version of the prefix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build in</li></ol><p><i>встро́ить</i> (vstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wedged in</li><li>to meddle/intervene in</li><li><i>(figurative)</i> to find oneself in an unpleasant situation</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-стрева́ть</i> (-strevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alarmed, anxious (<i>experiencing alarm or anxiety; of a person</i>)</li><li>offended, resentful (<i>expressing alarm or anxiety; of a look, tone, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb</li><li>to worry, to trouble, to harass</li></ol><p><i>встрево́жить</i> (vstrevóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb</li><li>to worry, to trouble, to harass</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>трево́жить</i> (trevóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be anxious/uneasy/worried <i>(about)</i>, to become anxious <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>встрево́жить</i> (vstrevóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rouse oneself, to liven up</li><li>to startle, to start</li><li>to shake its wings, to open/spread its wings (<i>of a bird</i>)</li><li>to throb, to beat faster (<i>of the heart</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>трепе-</i> (trepe-) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to encounter, to come across</li><li>to meet, to receive, to welcome</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъrětiti</i> (related to <i>&#42;sъrěsti</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to encounter, to come across [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to see each other, to meet, to date</li><li>to be found, to be met with, to occur, to happen</li></ol><p><i>встре́тить</i> (vstrétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, encounter</li><li>date</li><li>reception</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summit (<i>gathering of leaders</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occurrence, frequency</li></ol><p><i>встреча́емый</i> (vstrečájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to encounter, to come across</li><li>to meet, to receive, to welcome</li><li>to celebrate</li></ol><p><i>встре́тить</i> (vstrétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to encounter, to come across [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to see each other, to meet, to date</li><li>to be found, to be met with, to occur, to happen</li></ol><p><i>встреча́ть</i> (vstrečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automobile&#32;slang</i>) oncoming lane of traffic</li><li>(<i>automobile&#32;slang</i>) traffic violation consisting of exiting into the oncoming lane of traffic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) financial audit of the response of contractors of an audited firm</li></ol><p><i>встре́ча</i> (vstréča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oncoming</li><li>counter-, head... (coming from in front, coming from the opposite direction)</li><li>on one’s way</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passer-by</li></ol><p><i>встре́ча</i> (vstréča, “encounter”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>built-in, integrated, embedded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to embed <i>(in)</i>, to integrate <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shake, shaking (<i>transitive</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) emotional shock, shaking up</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) reprimand, scolding</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) emotional uplifting</li></ol><p>From <i>встряхну́ть</i> (vstrjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), but constructed as if from &#42;встрясти́ (compare imperfective <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí), perfective <i>тряхну́ть</i> (trjaxnútʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meddle/intervene in</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-стрять</i> (-strjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li><li>to shake up</li></ol><p><i>встряхну́ть</i> (vstrjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake off, to remove by shaking [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>встря́хивать</i> (vstrjáxivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li><li>to shake up</li></ol><p><i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>тряхну́ть</i> (trjaxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake off, to remove by shaking [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive,&#32;perfective only) to cheer up, to shake off the blues</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to have fun</li></ol><p><i>встряхну́ть</i> (vstrjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>встре́ча</i> (vstréča).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to march into (of the troops)</li><li>to join (to become a member of)</li><li>to begin, to start, to assume</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up for, to stick up for, to take someone's side, to come to the defense of</li></ol><p><i>вступа́ть</i> (vstupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>introductory, opening, inaugural</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) entrance</li></ol><p><i>вступи́ть</i> (vstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to march into (of the troops)</li><li>to join, to enlist</li><li>to begin, to start, to assume</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up for, to stick up for, to take someone's side, to come to the defense of</li></ol><p><i>вступи́ть</i> (vstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entry (act of entering)</li><li>introduction, preamble, prelude</li><li>accession, joining, entry (a coming to)</li></ol><p><i>вступи́ть</i> (vstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) in vain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>въсоуѥ</i> (vŭsuje), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sujь</i> (“futile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to shove [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li><li>to thrust (<i>into one's hands or a pocket</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eating dry cold food without any drinks (such as tea, water, milk, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>всу́чивать</i> (vsúčivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>всучи́ть</i> (vsučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to palm off onto, to foist onto</li></ol><p><i>всучи́ть</i> (vsučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to palm off onto, to foist onto</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сучи́ть</i> (sučítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sob</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sob, to weep</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>всхлип</i> (vsxlip) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobbing, weeping, sobs</li></ol><p><i>всхли́пывать</i> (vsxlípyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sob, to weep</li></ol><p><i>всхли́пнуть</i> (vsxlípnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mount, to go up, to climb, to ascend</li><li>(<i>of a heavenly body</i>) to rise</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) to come up, to sprout</li></ol><p>From <i>вс-</i> (vs-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ). Doublet of <i>восходи́ть</i> (vosxodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young growth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat or scold strongly [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>всы́пать</i> (vsýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) everywhere, anywhere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вьсѭду</i> (vĭsjǫdu), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьśǫdu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mishmash, sundries, whatnot, hodgepodge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>any, every</li><li>all sorts of</li><li>all kinds of</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anybody, anyone, everybody, everyone</li><li>anything</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьśako</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>уся́кий</i> (usjákyj), Belarusian <i>уся́кі</i> (usjáki), Bulgarian <i>все́ки</i> (vséki), Serbo-Croatian <i>сва̏ки</i> / <i>svȁki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(in) everyway (in every way, however possible)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of every kind, of all kinds; various</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>normally in the expression</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>everything</li><li>Used in certain situations where in English "it" or "things" would be used. Has the approximate meaning of "the situation".</li><li>(<i>in the genitive case</i>) used after comparatives of adjectives or adverbs as one way to form the superlative</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) always, all the time, constantly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) still</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) only, merely, specifically, namely</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;predicative) that's it, done, enough</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used to express that one is fed up or has had enough; that's it, , stop it</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used to express the completion&#47;achievement of something or that something is enough&#47;sufficient (and often also the desire to move on to something else); alright, okay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>no worries, not to worry, everything is fine</li></ol><p>Literally, "everything is in order".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all along (duration)</li><li>always, all the time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still, yet, as yet (up to the present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nevertheless, still, yet, however, that said</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>it’s all the same; it doesn’t matter</li><li>anyway; in any case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as good as (almost completely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>all that, everything, whatever, stuff, so on, that kind/sort of thing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absolutely everything</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>still, all the same, nevertheless, for all that <i>(with a tone of regret)</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and again, and yet, however, yet <i>(reinforcing something)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>все</i> (vse).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>W, watt</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>ватт</i> (vatt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly (in secret)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to push, to shove [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to stuff, to cram, to shove [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something or completely, in several movements’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>втолкну́ть</i> (vtolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag <i>(in, into)</i>, to drag <i>(up, on)</i></li></ol><p><i>втаск-</i> (vtask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag <i>(in, into)</i>, to drag <i>(up, on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to punch, to give a smack</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow <i>(into)</i>, to discharge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вте́к-</i> (vték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>те́сный</i> (tésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow <i>(into)</i>, to discharge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze, to cram</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ти́скать</i> (tískatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze <i>(in, into)</i>, to cram <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вти́ск-</i> (vtísk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>вти́скать</i> (vtískatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze <i>(in, into)</i>, to squeeze oneself <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вти́скивать</i> (vtískivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze <i>(in, into)</i>, to cram <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ти́снуть</i> (tísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze <i>(in, into)</i>, to squeeze oneself <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вти́снуть</i> (vtísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) on the sly (slyly, in an inconspicuous manner, so as not to be seen&#59; secretly&#59; stealthily)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quietly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) secretly, quietly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly, quietly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to stuff, to cram, to shove [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something or completely, in several movements’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to push, to shove [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make understand, to get across to (<i>someone</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make understand, to get across to (<i>someone</i>)</li></ol><p><i>втолкова́ть</i> (vtolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrophysics</i>) escape velocity</li></ol><p>Literally, “second cosmic velocity”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invade</li><li>to intrude, to encroach, to break in, to trespass</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>вто́ргнуться</i> (vtórgnutʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invade</li><li>to intrude, to encroach, to break in, to trespass</li><li>to meddle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invasion, intrusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to echo, to repeat (an uttering or sounds)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to second (to agree with someone's opinion or idea)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to play or sing the second part</li></ol><p><i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Compare Polish <i>wtórzyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a second time, for the second time</li></ol><p><i>втори́чный</i> (vtoríčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secondary</li><li>second, repeated</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj). From Old East Slavic <i>&#42;въторица</i> (&#42;vŭtorica) (whence Old East Slavic <i>въторицею</i> (vŭtoriceju), akin to Old Church Slavonic <i>вторицею</i> (vtoriceju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuesday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъtorьnikъ</i>. Related to <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój, “second”). Literally, “second day of the week”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second course (meal)</li><li>secondly, used as <i>во-вторы́х</i> (vo-vtorýx)</li><li>the second (in dates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) second wind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) second person</li><li>assistant to the top person in an organization or government</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) prime minister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addend (term added to another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>second</li><li>upper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>въторъ</i> (vŭtorŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъtorъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wi-tero-</i> (“more apart”), from <i>&#42;wí</i> (“asunder”) + comparative suffix <i>&#42;-teros</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>вівто́рок</i> (vivtórok), Belarusian <i>аўторак</i> (awtórak), Bulgarian <i>втори</i> (vtori), Czech <i>úterý</i>, Polish <i>wtóry</i>, Upper Sorbian wutora, German wieder, English wither, maybe ancient Greek δεύτερος (deúteros). Alternatively further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énteros</i>. See cognate Russian <i>полтора́</i> (poltorá) (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;роlъ vъtora</i>), Lithuanian <i>pusantro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sophomore, second-year student (<i>in a university or college</i>)</li></ol><p><i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a hurry, hurriedly, hastily</li></ol><p>Per Trubachev, from <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>торопи́ть</i> (toropítʹ, “to hurry”) +&lrm; <i>-ях</i> (-jax), an unclear suffix (possibly a prepositional plural suffix?).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secondary, minor, of less importance</li></ol><p><i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сте́пень</i> (stépenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recyclables</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very expensively</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>три</i> (tri) +&lrm; <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>three times as much</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>тро́е</i> (tróje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>three (together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) bush, plug, hub (of a wheel)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>тул</i> (tul, “quiver”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) in vain</li></ol><p><i>в</i> (v) +&lrm; <i>туне</i> (tune), from Old Church Slavonic <i>тоуне</i> (tune), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuňь</i>. Compare with Slovene <i>stunja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telling-off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in, into)</i>, to drive <i>(in, into)</i></li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull (<i>in, into, on, up</i>), to draw (<i>in, into, on, up</i>)</li><li>to take in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to draw (<i>into</i>), to get involved (<i>in</i>)</li></ol><p><i>втяг-</i> (vtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sag, to fall in</li><li>to get used, to get accustomed <i>(to)</i>, to feel at home <i>(with)</i>, to take <i>(to)</i>, to develop a liking <i>(for)</i>, to begin to enjoy</li><li>to get mixed up <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>втя́гивать</i> (vtjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull (<i>in, into, on, up</i>), to draw (<i>in, into, on, up</i>)</li><li>to take in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to draw (<i>into</i>), to get involved (<i>in</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sag, to fall in</li><li>to get used, to get accustomed <i>(to)</i>, to feel at home <i>(with)</i>, to take <i>(to)</i>, to develop a liking <i>(for)</i>, to begin to enjoy</li><li>to get mixed up <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>втяну́ть</i> (vtjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) without seeing the opponent's cards; without showing one's cards</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blindly, in the dark</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>тёмную</i> (tjómnuju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voyeurism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veil, voile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>voile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vuvuzela</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>vuvuzela</i>, from Zulu <i>vuvuzela</i> (“to make a <i>vu vu</i> noise”), from the /vuː/ sound the horns make.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voodoo (Afro-Caribbean religion)</li><li>wudu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>voodoo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>вы́сшее уче́бное заведе́ние</i> (výsšeje učébnoje zavedénije, “higher/highest educational establishment”): university, college, institute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>college student</li></ol><p><i>вуз</i> (vuz) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) university</li></ol><p><i>вуз</i> (vuz) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>уй</i> (uj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volcano</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>vulcano</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>volcanic</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) volcanic, explosive, seething</li></ol><p><i>вулка́н</i> (vulkán) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volcanologist, vulcanologist</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>вулка́н</i> (vulkán) +&lrm; <i>-о́-</i> (-ó-) +&lrm; <i>-лог</i> (-log).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vulva (collectively the external female sexual organs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vulgarize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulgarism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vulgar (obscene)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vulgāris</i>, from <i>vulgus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sarcastic</i>) wonderweapon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wunderwaffe</i> + вафля (waffle).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child prodigy, wunderkind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Wunderkind</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) vampire</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) bloodsucker, sadist (a cruel person)</li></ol><p>Coined by Alexander Pushkin in 1836 in an eponymous poem; an alteration of <i>волкола́к</i> (volkolák) and <i>вуркола́к</i> (vurkolák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to the point of losing consciousness or death (half to death)</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;figuratively,&#32;about intoxication) very strongly, to an extreme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance, entry</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>входи́ть</i> (vxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to go in(to) (by foot), to get in(to)</li><li>to penetrate into</li><li>to be a part of / to be included</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance door</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) entrance</li></ol><p><i>вход</i> (vxod) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entry (<i>to, into</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain, for nothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to grasp, to clutch</li><li>to cling (<i>of a sticky object</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to not let go, to get attached emotionally, to hate to be separated (from)</li><li>to bite (<i>of hunting dogs</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to catch hold <i>(of)</i>, to clutch <i>(at)</i>, to cling <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>вцепи́ться</i> (vcepítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yesterday</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yesterday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьčera</i>, related to Russian <i>ве́чер</i> (véčer, “evening”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yesterday's</li></ol><p>From <i>вчера́</i> (včerá) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the rough, rough draft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in four rows or layers, in four</li><li>(by) four times (<i>in reference to a quantity, not to occurrences</i>)</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>че́тверо</i> (čétvero)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>four together</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>че́тверо</i> (čétvero) +&lrm; <i>-о́м</i> (-óm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read carefully, to understand by reading [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read carefully, to understand by reading [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>вчита́ться</i> (včitátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to empathize</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/be lousy <i>(lice-ridden; alive with lice)</i></li></ol><p><i>вшивый</i> (všivyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lice infestation, lousiness</li></ol><p><i>вши́вый</i> (všívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lousy (infested with lice)</li><li>lousy (remarkably bad)</li></ol><p><i>вошь</i> (vošʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in breadth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>в</i> (v).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate into, to saturate, to impregnate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to penetrate deeply, to pierce</li></ol><p><i>въед-</i> (vʺjed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entry, entrance (act of entering, especially when using any means of transportation)</li><li>entry, entrance (doorway that provides a means of entering a building)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>въезжа́ть</i> (vʺjezžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter (by vehicle), to go in(to) (by vehicle)</li><li>to move into (e.g. a new house)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to understand, to get</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate into, to saturate, to impregnate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to penetrate deeply, to pierce</li></ol><p><i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter (by vehicle), to ride in, to drive in, to go in(to) (by vehicle)</li><li>to move into (e.g. a new house)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to understand, to get</li></ol><p>From <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in real life, not in a dream, openly, really, indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in real life, not in a dream, openly, really, indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to work someone's ass off; to get one's ass in gear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (<i>plural or formal singular</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>вꙑ</i> (vy), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vy</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yúHs</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>यूयम्</i> (yūyam) and English <i>you</i>.</p><p>Use of Вы as a formal singular is attested in East Slavic literature from the 11th-16th century, under influence of Byzantine speech. It started becoming widespread in Russian society during the rule of Peter the Great, under French and German influence, and became ingrained by the 19th century.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вы)</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>вы</i> (vy) - you (polite, sg and pl)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) out, out from (describing an action of moving outside from inside)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) completion or fulfillment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑ-</i> (vy-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vy-</i> (“out”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úd</i>, or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)ud</i> (Derksen), or <i>&#42;ūd-</i> : <i>&#42;ūt-</i> (Černyx). In Slavic, the final consonant was dropped.</p><p>Cognates include English <i>out</i>, German <i>aus</i> (“out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run out (outside)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run out (outside)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>беси́ть</i> (besítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating out, knocking out, dislodging</li></ol><p><i>выбива́ть</i> (vybivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat out, to knock out, to dislodge</li><li>to eliminate or knock someone out of a game</li></ol><p><i>вы́бить</i> (výbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out, to break loose (from), to show (from under), to work loose</li></ol><p><i>выбива́ть</i> (vybivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select, to pick, to pick out</li><li>to elect</li><li>to take out, to pick out, to remove</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to haul in (a net, rope, or hawser), to pull up (an anchor)</li><li>to find (free time, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out</li></ol><p><i>выбира́ть</i> (vybirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat out, to knock out, to dislodge</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out, to break loose (from), to show (from under), to work loose</li></ol><p><i>вы́бить</i> (výbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;rude) bastard (an illegitimate child)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) bastard (a disgusting, bad person&#59; a scoundrel)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>вы́блѧдокъ</i> (výbljadok). Analysable as <i>вы-</i> (vy-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pothole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice (option or decision)</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) election</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́брать</i> (výbratʹ). Compare Czech <i>výbor</i> and Polish <i>wybór</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vyborg (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Вы́борг</i> (Výborg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection, excerption, sampling</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́брать</i> (výbratʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>вы́бор-</i> (výbor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>вы́бер-</i> (výber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elective</li><li>electoral, voting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elected representative, delegate</li></ol><p><i>вы́боры</i> (výbory) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selective</li></ol><p><i>вы́борка</i> (výborka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elector</li></ol><p><i>вы́бор</i> (výbor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chosen, selected, hand-picked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away (discard or dispose of)</li><li>to emit, to eject</li></ol><p><i>вы́бросить</i> (výbrositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump down (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li><li>to throw oneself (<i>somewhere</i>), to be thrown (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>to erupt (<i>of jets of flame, streams of water, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>выбра́сывать</i> (vybrásyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select, to pick out</li><li>to elect</li><li>to take everything out</li><li>to haul in (net), to pull up (anchor)</li><li>to find (time, suitable moment)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out</li></ol><p><i>вы́брать</i> (výbratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave</li></ol><p><i>вы́брить</i> (výbritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>брить</i> (britʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emission, discharge, release, ejection</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́бросить</i> (výbrositʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away (discard or dispose of)</li><li>to emit, to eject</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump down (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li><li>to throw oneself (<i>somewhere</i>), to be thrown (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>to erupt (<i>of jets of flame, streams of water, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́бросить</i> (výbrositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart</li><li>to quit [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz) <small>‘from’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вы́быть</i> (výbytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart</li><li>to quit [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz) <small>‘from’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to get out, to dump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop</li><li>to pour out of</li><li>to drag <i>(through)</i>, to roll <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́валить</i> (vývalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>вы́валять</i> (vývaljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Arkhangelsk,&#32;Siberia) clipping of <i>выва́ливать языќ на плечо́</i> (vyválivatʹ jazyḱ na plečó); to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to dump (one's) tongue”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Volgograd,&#32;sometimes with the verb omitted) to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to dump (one's) tongue on (one's) shoulder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tumble out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour out of</li><li>to become covered <i>(by rolling or dragging)</i></li></ol><p><i>выва́ливать</i> (vyválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to get out, to dump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop</li><li>to pour out of</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Arkhangelsk,&#32;Siberia) clipping of <i>вы́валить языќ на плечо́</i> (vývalitʹ jazyḱ na plečó); to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to dump (one's) tongue”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Volgograd,&#32;sometimes with the verb omitted) to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to dump (one's) tongue on (one's) shoulder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tumble out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour out of</li></ol><p><i>вы́валить</i> (vývalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag <i>(through)</i>, to roll <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered <i>(by rolling or dragging)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́валять</i> (vývaljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, to wangle, to ferret out, to elicit</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conclusion, consequence, eduction, illation, inference</li><li>breeding, raising, nurturing</li><li>removing</li><li>launching</li></ol><p><i>вывед-</i> (vyved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, to wangle, to ferret out, to elicit</li></ol><p><i>вы́ведать</i> (vývedatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out (by vehicle), to remove</li><li>to deliver</li><li>to export</li><li>to bring back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rescue, to save</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to verify, to proofread</li><li>to align, to adjust, to regulate</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verification, proofreading</li><li>alignment, adjustment, regulation</li></ol><p><i>выверить</i> (vyveritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist, to wrench (an arm, a leg, etc.)</li><li>to turn inside out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come unscrewed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wriggle/get out of a difficulty/fix, to find a way out, to manage to escape scot-free</li></ol><p><i>вы́вернуть</i> (vývernutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twist, turn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strange gesture (<i>especially in a dance</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) quirk, idiosyncrasy</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́вернуть</i> (vývernutʹ), based on the underlying stem <i>вы́верт-</i> (vývert-) (compare <i>вы́вертеть</i> (vývertetʹ), <i>вывёртывать</i> (vyvjórtyvatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to verify, to proofread</li><li>to align, to adjust, to regulate</li></ol><p><i>вы́верить</i> (výveritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang out, to post up, to put up</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signboard, sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out, to lead out, to bring out, to move out; to help out (help someone to get off)</li><li>to withdraw, to call off (troops)</li><li>to derive, to conclude, to infer, to deduce</li><li>to remove</li><li>to extirpate, to destroy; to exterminate (pests); to remove (stains, pimples)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to display the results of a program operation</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to save the results of a program operation outside of its RAM</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become extinct</li><li>to go out of use, to fall into disuse</li><li>to come out, to go away (<i>of stains</i>)</li><li>to hatch</li></ol><p><i>вы́вести</i> (vývesti) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove (odor, dampness, etc.) by airing out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to eliminate</li><li>to weather, to erode</li></ol><p><i>вы́ветрить</i> (vývetritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dissipate (<i>due to air; of a smell, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to weather, to erode</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to be forgotten (<i>of thoughts, feelings, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>выве́тривать</i> (vyvétrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove (odor, dampness, etc.) by airing out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to eliminate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weather, to erode</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́трить</i> (-vétritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dissipate (<i>due to air; of a smell, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to weather, to erode</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to be forgotten (<i>of thoughts, feelings, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́ветрить</i> (vývetritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang out, to post up, to put up</li></ol><p><i>вы́весить</i> (vývesitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dislocation (<i>of a joint</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dislocate, to put out, to sprain (a joint)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withdrawal</li><li>conclusion, takeaway, inference, deduction</li><li>derivation</li><li>outlet, leading-out wire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out, to lead out, to bring out, to move out; to help out (help someone to get off)</li><li>to withdraw, to call off (troops)</li><li>to derive, to conclude, to infer, to deduce</li><li>to remove</li><li>to extirpate, to destroy; to exterminate (pests); to remove (stains, pimples)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) to excrete</li><li>(biology,&#32;agriculture) to breed, to selection the new breed or sort</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to display the results of a program operation</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to save the results of a program operation outside of its RAM</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to infuriate, to exasperate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become extinct</li><li>to go out of use, to fall into disuse</li><li>to come out, to go away (<i>of stains</i>)</li><li>to hatch</li></ol><p><i>выводи́ть</i> (vyvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brood, hatch, litter</li></ol><p>From <i>выводи́ть</i> (vyvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), with perfective-verb stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>export, exportation, taking out (by vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out (by vehicle), to remove</li><li>to deliver</li><li>to export</li><li>to bring back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rescue, to save</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bring it, to perform well</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil, to bedraggle</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ); by rule, вы́- attracts the stress in perfective but not imperfective verbs.</p><p>See <i>вы́возить</i> (vývozitʹ) above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag out</li></ol><p><i>вы́волок-</i> (vývolok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telling-off, scolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist, to wrench (an arm, a leg, etc.)</li><li>to turn inside out</li></ol><p><i>вы́воротить</i> (vývorotitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come unscrewed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wriggle/get out of a difficulty/fix, to find a way out, to manage to escape scot-free</li></ol><p><i>вывора́чивать</i> (vyvoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to unscrew</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to twist, to wrench (an arm, a leg, etc.)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to turn inside out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to come unscrewed</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to wriggle/get out of a difficulty/fix, to find a way out, to manage to escape scot-free</li></ol><p><i>вы́воротить</i> (vývorotitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist, to wrench (an arm, a leg, etc.)</li><li>to turn inside out</li></ol><p><i>вы́верт-</i> (vývert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come unscrewed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wriggle/get out of a difficulty/fix, to find a way out, to manage to escape scot-free</li></ol><p><i>вывёртывать</i> (vyvjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gain</li><li>to economize, save</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to iron, to smooth with an iron</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to iron, to smooth with an iron</li></ol><p><i>вы́гладить</i> (výgladitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, to appear, to seem</li><li>to find, to discover, to spy out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ).</p><p>Apparently a calque of German <i>aussehen</i>. Compare Czech <i>vyhledat</i>, Polish <i>wyglądać</i>, and Slovak <i>vyhľadať</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look out, to peep out</li><li>to appear, to emerge, to come into view</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look out, to peep out</li><li>to appear, to emerge, to come into view</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to drive out, to oust (to force someone to leave)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to expel, kick (from an organization or community)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arched, bent, curved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to articulate, to pronounce, to utter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tell off, to reprimand, to lecture</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reserve for oneself</li></ol><p><i>вы́говорить</i> (výgovoritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say/have one's say</li></ol><p><i>выгова́ривать</i> (vygovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproof; reprimand; rebuke, scolding; dressing down, admonition</li><li>pronunciation; accent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to articulate, to pronounce, to utter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reserve for oneself, to stipulate, to set as a condition (<i>in a contract, transaction, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to censure, to reprimand, to rebuke</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say/have one's say</li></ol><p><i>вы́говорить</i> (výgovoritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profit</li><li>advantage, benefit</li></ol><p><i>вы́годить</i> (výgoditʹ, “mark time”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>advantageously (in an advantageous manner)</li><li>it pays (to do something), it's worth</li></ol><p><i>вы́годный</i> (výgodnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profitable, paying, remunerative</li><li>advantageous, favorable</li></ol><p><i>вы́года</i> (výgoda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beneficiary; one who gains from an action</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́год-</i> (výgod-, “benefit, profit”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>получа́тель</i> (polučátelʹ, “receiver”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture (land on which cattle can be kept for feeding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to drive out, to oust (to force someone to leave)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear energy</i>) burnup (<i>of reactor fuel</i>)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) burnout (experience of long-term mental exhaustion)</li></ol><p><i>выгора́ть</i> (vygorátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije). The psychology sense is a semantic loan from English <i>burnout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn down, to burn away, to burn out, to be destroyed by fire, to be gutted</li><li>to fade (<i>in the sun</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pan out</li></ol><p><i>вы́гореть</i> (výgoretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn down, to burn away, to burn out, to be destroyed by fire, to be gutted</li><li>to fade <i>(in the sun)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pan out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shovel out, to rake out, to remove by shoveling or raking</li><li>to row, to move by rowing (<i>of a boat</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́греб-</i> (výgreb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shovel out, to rake out, to remove by shoveling or raking</li><li>to row, to move by rowing (<i>of a boat</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>грести́</i> (grestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to unlade, to unship, to disembark, to detrain</li><li>to upload</li></ol><p><i>вы́грузить</i> (výgruzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to disembark, to unship, to detrain</li></ol><p><i>выгружа́ть</i> (vygružátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to unlade, to unship, to disembark, to detrain</li><li>to upload</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to disembark, to unship, to detrain</li></ol><p><i>вы́грузить</i> (výgruzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unloading, unshipping, disembarkation, detraining</li></ol><p><i>вы́грузить</i> (výgruzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unloader (person who unloads)</li></ol><p><i>вы́грузить</i> (výgruzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (a dog) for a walk</li></ol><p><i>вы́гулять</i> (výguljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (a dog) for a walk</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give out, to pay out, to hand, to issue, to distribute</li><li>to produce</li><li>to deliver up, to give up, to extradite</li><li>to betray, to give away</li><li>to pass off as, to pose as</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑдавати</i> (vydavati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vydavati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry off (to arrange the marriage of someone (typically of a female relative))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protrude</li><li>to stand out</li></ol><p><i>выдава́ть</i> (vydavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out, to press out</li><li><i>(an expression of feeling)</i> to force</li><li><i>(glass etc.)</i> to push out, to press out, to break</li><li>to emboss, to stamp</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out, to press out</li><li><i>(an expression of feeling)</i> to force</li><li><i>(glass etc.)</i> to push out, to press out, to break</li><li>to emboss, to stamp</li></ol><p><i>вы́давить</i> (výdavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give out, to pay out, to hand, to issue, to distribute</li><li>to produce</li><li>to deliver up, to give up, to extradite</li><li>to betray, to give away</li><li>to pass off as, to pose as</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑдати</i> (vydati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vydati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry off (to arrange the marriage of someone (typically of a female relative))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protrude</li><li>to stand out</li></ol><p><i>вы́дать</i> (výdatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery; distribution, issue, issuance; giving out, serving out; payment</li><li>ejection</li><li>outputting</li><li>extradition</li><li>betrayal, disclosure</li><li>output</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) search engine results</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prominent, outstanding, distinguished, remarkable, notable</li><li>protruding, projecting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw out, to move out, to pull out</li><li>to put forward, to advance</li><li>to nominate</li></ol><p><i>вы́двиг-</i> (výdvig-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(forward)</i></li><li><i>(drawer etc)</i> to slide in and out, to move in and out</li><li>to rise, to rise from the ranks, to work one's way up, to be distinguished</li><li>to come up, to advance</li></ol><p><i>выдвига́ть</i> (vydvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) promotee, often an ordinary person promoted to a managerial position</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) nominee</li></ol><p><i>вы́двиг-</i> (výdvig-) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promotion, advancement</li><li>advance, movement forward or to a certain position</li><li>telescoping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawer (open-topped box in a cabinet used for storing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw out, to move out, to pull out</li><li>to put forward, to advance</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(forward)</i></li><li><i>(drawer etc)</i> to slide in and out, to move in and out</li><li>to rise, to rise from the ranks, to work one's way up, to be distinguished</li><li>to come up, to advance</li></ol><p><i>вы́двинуть</i> (výdvinutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expulsion, removal, deportation</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́дворить</i> (výdvoritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evict, to expel, to deport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evict, to expel, to deport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to manufacture</li><li><i>(leather)</i> to dress, to curry</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apportionment</li><li>separation</li><li>isolation</li><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) release</li><li>(<i>physiology</i>) secretion, excretion, discharge</li><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) setting off, emphasis</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) selection, highlighting</li></ol><p><i>вы́делить</i> (výdelitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, to choose, to single out, to select</li><li>to allot, to mark</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to detach, to find, to provide (<i>a military unit</i>)</li><li>to apportion</li><li>to mark out, to distinguish, to emphasize</li><li>(biology,&#32;medicine) to secrete, to excrete, to discharge</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to evolve, to educe, to isolate</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) to release, to produce</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) to select (to choose or highlight a piece of text or other content in order to perform some operation on it)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand out, to be distinguished (by), to be notable (by)</li></ol><p><i>вы́делить</i> (výdelitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacturing, production</li><li>workmanship</li><li>dressing (<i>of leather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́делать</i> (výdelatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to manufacture</li><li><i>(leather)</i> to dress, to curry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be up <i>(to)</i>, to do</li></ol><p><i>вы́делать</i> (výdelatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act out (to express one's feelings through disruptive actions)</li></ol><p><i>выде́лывать</i> (vydélyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, to choose, to single out, to select</li><li>to allot, to mark</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to detach, to find, to provide (<i>a military unit</i>)</li><li>to apportion</li><li>to mark out, to distinguish, to emphasize</li><li>(biology,&#32;medicine) to secrete, to excrete, to discharge</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to evolve, to educe, to isolate</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) to release, to produce</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) to select (to choose or highlight a piece of text or other content in order to perform some operation on it)</li></ol><p><i>вы́делить</i> (výdelitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand out, to be distinguished (by), to be notable (by)</li></ol><p><i>выделя́ть</i> (vydeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to withstand, to bear, to sustain, to endure</li><li>to pass <i>exam</i></li><li>to contain oneself, to control oneself</li><li>to maintain, to keep up, to observe</li><li>to age, to mature, to season</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to withstand, to bear, to sustain, to endure</li><li>to pass <i>exam</i></li><li>to contain oneself, to control oneself</li><li>to maintain, to keep up, to observe</li><li>to age, to mature, to season</li></ol><p><i>вы́держать</i> (výderžatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-control, self-mastery, self-restraint; tenacity; endurance</li><li>extract, excerpt, quotation</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) exposure</li></ol><p><i>вы́держать</i> (výderžatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhalation, outward breath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe out, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose smell, to lose fragrance, to become flat</li><li>to be played out, to be used up, <i>(emotion)</i> to spend itself, <i>(attack)</i> to come to nothing; to fizzle out, to be a wash-out, to peter out, to run out of steam</li></ol><p><i>вы́дохнуть</i> (výdoxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otter (<i>mammal</i>)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) mean, ugly woman</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>выдра</i> (vydra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vydra</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ūdrāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udréh₂</i>, the feminine form of <i>&#42;udrós</i>, whence English <i>otter</i> and Sanskrit <i>उद्र</i> (udra). Doublet of <i>ги́дра</i> (gídra), which was borrowed from Greek.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to train (animals)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to drill (a person)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>дрессирова́ть</i> (dressirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent, to contrive, to devise</li><li>to concoct, to fabricate, to make up</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invention, fiction; fable; fib; tale</li><li>contrivance</li><li>fabrication</li></ol><p><i>вы́думать</i> (výdumatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fabricator, liar</li></ol><p><i>вы́думать</i> (výdumatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent, to contrive, to devise</li><li>to concoct, to fabricate, to make up</li></ol><p><i>вы́думать</i> (výdumatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe out, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose smell, to lose fragrance, to become flat</li><li>to be played out, to be used up, <i>(emotion)</i> to spend itself, <i>(attack)</i> to come to nothing; to fizzle out, to be a wash-out, to peter out, to run out of steam</li></ol><p><i>выдыха́ть</i> (vydyxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li></ol><p><i>вы́дерг-</i> (výderg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to kick ass</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Compare with Belarusian <i>вы́ебаць</i> (výjebacʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to show off (<i>one's manners, judgments, attire, etc.</i>), while manifesting arrogance and disrespect for others</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to kick ass</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away (to erode or corrode gradually), to eat out (eat the inside)</li></ol><p><i>вы́ед-</i> (výjed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure, leaving</li><li>exit, egress; gateway</li><li>exit road</li><li>emigration</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>выезжа́ть</i> (vyjezžátʹ). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>виїзд</i> (vyjizd), Polish <i>wyjazd</i>, Czech <i>výjezd</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exit</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) riding</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eligible to travel abroad</li><li>traveling (associated with going somewhere)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) away</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) used for outings</li></ol><p><i>вы́езд</i> (výjezd) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exit, to go out (by vehicle)</li><li>to break in (a horse)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-, “(<i>indicating action or motion out of an area</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking out, removing; seizure; collection</li><li>hole, groove, cavity, depression</li><li>recess, trough, notch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вы́ебсти</i> (výjebsti)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away (to erode or corrode gradually), to eat out (eat the inside)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>вы́ебсти</i> (výjebsti)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exit, to go out (by vehicle)</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́-</i> (vý-, “(<i>indicating action or motion out of an area</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out, to press out</li><li>to wring (a cloth, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to wring out, to get out</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to press, to lift (a weight)</li><li>to step on, to press on (the clutch, etc.)</li><li>to remove the harvest from</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bide one's time, to wait <i>(for)</i>, to wait till something is over</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out, to burn down, to scorch</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to cauterize</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>survivability</li><li>(biology,&#32;genetics) survival rate</li></ol><p><i>выжива́емый</i> (vyživájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>survival</li></ol><p><i>выжива́ть</i> (vyživátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survive (continue to live)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make someone leave (apartment, work or study place) by making their stay miserable or inconvenient, to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>вы́жить</i> (výžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rogue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out, to burn down, to scorch</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to cauterize</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in expectation, waiting</li></ol><p><i>выжида́тельный</i> (vyžidátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waiting, hesitating, dilatory</li></ol><p><i>выжида́ть</i> (vyžidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bide one's time, to wait <i>(for)</i>, to wait till something is over</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-жида́ть</i> (-židátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out, to press out</li><li>to wring (a cloth, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to wring out, to get out</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to press, to lift (a weight)</li><li>to step on, to press on (the clutch, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove the harvest from</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survive (continue to live)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make someone leave (an apartment, work or study place) by making their stay miserable or inconvenient, to squeeze out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) whip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generated, caused</li><li>aroused, renewed, touched</li><li>called, summoned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to send for</li><li>to challenge, to defy</li><li>to summon</li><li>to arouse, to cause, to evoke, to excite</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to call on the carpet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer to help, to offer one's services</li></ol><p><i>вы́звать</i> (výzvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freeing, release, deliverance (from danger, captivity, servitude, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to liberate (<i>from captivity, bondage, or trouble</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>wyzwolić</i>. By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>воля</i> (volja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to liberate (<i>from captivity, bondage, or trouble</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́зволить</i> (výzvolitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convalescence, recovery (gradual healing)</li></ol><p><i>выздора́вливать</i> (vyzdorávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover, to convalesce (in terms of one's health by overcoming illness or trauma)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to get rid of something that is unpleasant, harmful, or dangerous</li><li>(<i>imperative</i>) get well soon (expressing hope that the listener will recover from illness quickly)</li><li>(imperative,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;sarcastic,&#32;derogatory) cope and seethe; stay mad (used to dismiss or mock someone perceived to be upset or frustrated)</li></ol><p><i>вы́здороветь</i> (výzdorovetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get better, to recover, to convalesce</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>здорове́ть</i> (zdorovétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recovery, convalescence (recovery from a disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call</li><li>shot (critical or insulting comment)</li><li>summons, subpoena</li><li>challenge</li><li>invitation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vyzovъ</i>. Compare <i>вы́звать</i> (výzvatʹ). See <i>&#42;zъvati</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen (fruits or vegetables)</li></ol><p><i>вы́зреть</i> (výzretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling</li><li>evocation</li></ol><p><i>вызыва́ть</i> (vyzyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to send for</li><li>to challenge, to defy</li><li>to summon</li><li>to arouse, to cause, to stimulate, to evoke, to call forth</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer to help, to offer one's services</li></ol><p><i>вызыва́ть</i> (vyzyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>defiantly, provocatively</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brazenly, audaciously, cheekily</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) attracting attention; brightly, catchily</li></ol><p><i>вызыва́ющий</i> (vyzyvájuščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defiant</li><li>provocative, aggressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win</li><li>to win [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘against someone’</small>]</li><li>to benefit, to gain</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win</li><li>to win [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘against someone’</small>]</li><li>to benefit, to gain</li></ol><p><i>вы́играть</i> (výigratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>win, winning</li><li>gain, benefit</li><li>winnings, prize, profit</li></ol><p><i>вы́играть</i> (výigratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš). Congate with Polish <i>wygrana</i>.</p><p>Compare other natively formed Bulgarian <i>печалба</i> (pečalba) (akin to <i>печа́ль</i> (pečálʹ), <i>обеспе́чить</i> (obespéčitʹ), <i>обеспече́ние</i> (obespečénije)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>winning, prize</li><li>advantageous</li></ol><p><i>вы́игрыш</i> (výigryš) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discover, <i>(try to)</i> to find out, to seek out, to hunt <i>(for)</i>, to hunt out, to track down</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discover, <i>(try to)</i> to find out, to seek out, to hunt <i>(for)</i>, to hunt out, to track down</li></ol><p><i>вы́искать</i> (výiskatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>выиграть</i> (vyigratʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exit, to go out</li><li>to appear, to be published</li><li>to spend, to be used up</li><li>to come off, to come out</li><li>to come, to originate</li><li>to leave, to drop out</li><li>to look, to face, to front</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑити</i> (vyiti). By surface analysis, <i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry, to take a husband [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative)]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose self-control, to get furious</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get out of oneself”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become inactive, inoperable, unfit for work (usually of equipment but can also be used for people or animals)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get out of order/array/line”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mince, to bramish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show, to make known</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show, to make known</li></ol><p><i>вы́казать</i> (výkazatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calcine, to fire (e.g. ceramics)</li></ol><p><i>вы́калить</i> (výkalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to puncture, to pierce</li><li>to poke out, to gouge out, to remove by poking or gouging</li><li>to draw (a tattoo, image, etc.) by piercing or poking</li><li>to cut out the middle of</li></ol><p><i>вы́колоть</i> (výkolotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to calcine</li></ol><p><i>вы́калить</i> (výkalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вы́кание</i> (výkanije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to obtain something by cadging or pestering someone</li></ol><p><i>вы́канючить</i> (výkanjučitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig (a hole or depression)</li><li>to dig up, to unearth</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to dig up, to find</li></ol><p><i>вы́копать</i> (výkopatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clamber out (<i>with difficulty</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to get out of a predicament, to overcome adversity</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кара́бкаться</i> (karábkatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clamber out (<i>with difficulty</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to get out of a predicament, to overcome adversity</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кара́бкаться</i> (karábkatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll out, to wheel out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive/come out <i>(swiftly)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll out <i>(of)</i></li><li>to drive/come out <i>(swiftly)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́катить</i> (výkatitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll out, to wheel out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive/come out <i>(swiftly)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́катить</i> (výkatitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll out <i>(of)</i></li><li>to drive/come out <i>(swiftly)</i></li></ol><p><i>выка́тывать</i> (vykátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use the formal form of address, to be on formal terms with someone</li></ol><p><i>вы</i> (vy, “you (polite)”) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate, to cough up</li></ol><p><i>вы́кашлять</i> (výkašljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate, to cough up</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ка́шлять</i> (kášljatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to discard, to dispose of, to chuck out</li><li>to strand</li><li>to have a miscarriage, to miscarry</li><li>to hoist (a flag)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw (a tantrum, a mischief)</li></ol><p><i>вы́кид-</i> (výkid-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miscarriage, abortion</li></ol><p><i>вы́кидывать</i> (výkidyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to discard, to dispose of, to chuck out</li><li>to strand</li><li>to have a miscarriage, to miscarry</li><li>to hoist (a flag)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw (a tantrum, a mischief)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil away</li></ol><p><i>вы́кипеть</i> (výkipetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil away, to evaporate</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ, “to boil”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying out, laying-out, spreading out, spreading-out</li><li>pack, outfit, kit</li><li>computation, calculation</li></ol><p>From <i>выкла́д(ывать)</i> (vyklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), with perfective-verb stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay out, to spread out, to lay, to line, to pave</li><li>to take out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fork out, to cough up, to unbosom oneself (of) (money, truth, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give all one has got <i>(for)</i>, to pay an arm and a leg <i>(for)</i></li><li>to exert oneself, to push oneself to the limit</li></ol><p><i>выкла́дывать</i> (vykládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(electric) switch</li></ol><p><i>выключа́ть</i> (vyključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shut down, to turn off, to disable (to put a mechanism, device or system out of operation)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exclude (to omit from consideration)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>ключ</i> (ključ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>switching off</li><li>shutdown (action of closing a computer)</li><li>cutoff, disconnection</li></ol><p><i>вы́ключить</i> (výključitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shut down, to turn off, to disable (to put a mechanism, device or system out of operation)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exclude (to omit from consideration)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>ключ</i> (ključ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge (to shape metal)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, to pluck out, to take out, to pull out, to extract while digging up, rummaging, delving</li></ol><p><i>вы́ковырять</i> (výkovyrjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, to pluck out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to puncture, to pierce</li><li>to poke out, to gouge out, to remove by poking or gouging</li><li>to draw (a tattoo, image, etc.) by piercing or poking</li><li>to cut out the middle of</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig (a hole or depression)</li><li>to dig up, to unearth</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to dig up, to find</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) to eradicate, to uproot, to root out, to extirpate (to pull up by the roots)</li></ol><p><i>вы́корчевать</i> (výkorčevatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take secretly from somewhere, to steal</li></ol><p><i>вы́крад-</i> (výkrad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (patterns, shapes, etc. in tissue or skin)</li><li>to shape by cutting</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to allocate (a part of an area or room)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to find (a place for something)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to have difficulty finding (the time or money to meet someone)</li></ol><p><i>вы́кроить</i> (výkroitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen as a result of cutting</li><li>to barely happen (<i>about time, money, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>выкра́ивать</i> (vykráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to dye</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take secretly from somewhere, to steal</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>красть</i> (krastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to dye</li><li>to crumble</li></ol><p><i>вы́красить</i> (výkrasitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>вы́крошить</i> (výkrošitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, shout; yell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scream out, to yell</li><li>to call out</li></ol><p><i>вы́крикнуть</i> (výkriknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scream out, to yell</li><li>to call out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кри́кнуть</i> (kríknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (patterns, shapes, etc. in tissue or skin)</li><li>to shape by cutting</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to allocate (a part of an area or room)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to find (a place for something)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to have difficulty finding (the time or money to meet someone)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>крои́ть</i> (kroítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen as a result of cutting</li><li>to barely happen (<i>about time, money, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́кроить</i> (výkroitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting out (<i>of patterns or shapes</i>)</li><li>pattern (template for making clothes)</li></ol><p><i>вы́кроить</i> (výkroitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кроши́ть</i> (krošítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) frills, flourishes, ornaments</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rhetorical flourishes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eccentricies, oddities, idiosyncrasies</li></ol><p>From <i>вы-</i> (vy-, “out”) + <i>круто́й</i> (krutój, “steep, abrupt”) + an unclear suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist off, to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wring out</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ). Compare Polish <i>wykręcić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come unscrewed</li><li>to extricate oneself <i>(from)</i>, to get <i>(out of)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́крутить</i> (výkrutitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist off, to tweak off, to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wring out</li></ol><p><i>вы́крутить</i> (výkrutitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come unscrewed</li><li>to extricate oneself <i>(from)</i>, to get <i>(out of)</i></li></ol><p><i>выкру́чивать</i> (vykrúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ransom</li><li>redemption, redeeming</li><li>repurchase; buying-out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redeem</li><li>to ransom, to buy out</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get, to understand</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to understand the greatness of</li><li>to bath, to bathe, to give a bath</li></ol><p><i>вы́купить</i> (výkupitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redeem</li><li>to ransom, to buy out</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get, to understand</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to understand the greatness of</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke <i>(a cigarette etc)</i></li><li>to smoke out <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to drive out, to get rid <i>(of)</i></li><li>to distil</li></ol><p><i>вы́курить</i> (výkuritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke <i>(a cigarette etc)</i></li><li>to smoke out <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to drive out, to get rid <i>(of)</i></li><li>to distil</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to get out, to fish out</li></ol><p><i>вы́ловить</i> (výlovitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;regional) alternative form of <i>вылеза́ть</i> (vylezátʹ)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;rare) to search around from all sides</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;rare) to visit (many places) by climbing, to climb (many places)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sally, sortie</li><li>excursion, outing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break down (a door, etc.)</li><li>to knock out (a brick, etc.)</li><li>to get by breaking</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to dislocate</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́ломать</i> (výlomatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>вы́ломить</i> (výlomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out, to climb out, to get off</li><li>(of hair) to fall out, to come out</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́лезть</i> (výleztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) or from <i>вы́лезти</i> (výlezti) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out, to climb out, to get off</li><li>(of hair) to fall out, to come out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out, to climb out, to get off</li><li>(of hair) to fall out, to come out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to model, to sculpture, to sculpt, to fashion, to shape, to sculp</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) takeoff</li><li>flight departure</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) sortie</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) crash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly out, to fly (from a place)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to crash</li></ol><p><i>вы́лететь</i> (výletetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly out, to fly (from a place)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to crash</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cure, to heal</li></ol><p><i>вы́лечить</i> (výlečitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be cured, to get well</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>выле́чивать</i> (vyléčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cure <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лечи́ть</i> (lečítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be cured, to get well</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>вы́лечить</i> (výlečitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out (liquid)</li></ol><p><i>вы́лить</i> (výlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run out, to flow out, to pour out</li><li>to spill over <i>(into)</i>, to develop <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вылива́ть</i> (vylivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick clean</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick clean</li></ol><p><i>вы́лизать</i> (výlizatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the spitting image of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out (liquid)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run out, to flow out, to pour out</li><li>to spill over <i>(into)</i>, to develop <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́лить</i> (výlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to get out, to fish out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay out, to spread out, to lay, to line, to pave</li><li>to publish or post a message to a social site</li><li>to take out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fork out, to cough up, to unbosom oneself (of) (money, truth, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give all one has got <i>(for)</i>, to pay an arm and a leg <i>(for)</i></li><li>to exert oneself, to push oneself to the limit</li></ol><p><i>вы́ложить</i> (výložitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break down (a door, etc.)</li><li>to knock out (a brick, etc.)</li><li>to get by breaking</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to dislocate</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>вы́ломать</i> (výlomatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to open (the eyes) wide (<i>so as to stare</i>)</li><li>to pod (to remove peas from their case)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>лупи́ть</i> (lupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hatch (<i>of birds and reptiles</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;low colloquial) to open wide (<i>of the eyes</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to stare wide-eyed [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘at someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вы́лупить</i> (výlupitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to open (the eyes) wide (<i>so as to stare</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́лупить</i> (výlupitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hatch (<i>of birds and reptiles</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;low colloquial) to open wide (<i>of the eyes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вылу́пливать</i> (vylúplivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to open (the eyes) wide (<i>so as to stare</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́лупить</i> (výlupitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hatch (<i>of birds and reptiles</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;low colloquial) to open wide (<i>of the eyes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вылупля́ть</i> (vylupljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pod (to remove peas from their case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pod (to remove peas from their case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear on, to cover by smearing</li><li>to soil, to stain, to pollute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use up by smearing on</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear oneself</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear on, to cover by smearing</li><li>to soil, to stain, to pollute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use up by smearing on</li></ol><p><i>вы́мазать</i> (výmazatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implore; to beg</li></ol><p><i>вы́молить</i> (výmolitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, <i>lure</i></li><li>to wave (someone) away</li><li>to swindle someone or cheat someone out of something, to coax something out of someone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manipulate, to wrap around one's little finger</li></ol><p><i>вы́манить</i> (výmanitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, lure</li><li>to wave (someone) away</li><li>to swindle someone or cheat someone out of something, to coax something out of someone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manipulate, to wrap around one's little finger</li></ol><p>From <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ, “to beckon, to lure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pave</li></ol><p><i>вы́мостить</i> (výmostitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(something to something)</i> to barter <i>(for)</i>, to exchange <i>(for)</i>, to swap <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́менять</i> (výmenjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(something to something)</i> to barter <i>(for)</i>, to exchange <i>(for)</i>, to swap <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die out, to become extinct</li><li><i>(town)</i> to become desolate/deserted/depopulated</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ви́мерти</i> (výmerty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dying; disappeared (in terms of hope)</li><li>extinct</li><li>dormant (for volcanoes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep, <i>(litter etc)</i> to sweep out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep, <i>(litter etc)</i> to sweep out</li><li>to edge, to buttonhole</li></ol><p><i>вымет-</i> (vymet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to spawn”)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to baste”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dying out, extinction</li></ol><p><i>вымира́ть</i> (vymirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die out, to become extinct</li><li><i>(town)</i> to become desolate/deserted/depopulated</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-мира́ть</i> (-mirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extortionist, extortioner, blackmailer (someone who extorts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extortion, blackmail (the practice of extorting money or other property)</li></ol><p><i>вымога́ть</i> (vymogátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extort</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>мог-</i> (mog-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get soaked, to get drenched, to get very wet</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to soak, to steep; to be retted (<i>of fibers</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́мок(нуть)</i> (výmok(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get soaked, to get drenched, to get very wet</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to soak, to steep; to be retted (<i>of fibers</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мо́кнуть</i> (móknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, to utter</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implore; to beg</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moribund</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pave</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мости́ть</i> (mostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pennant, pennon, pendant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>wimpel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash, to wash out, to wash up, to wash away, to eluate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invention, fiction, fantasy, figment</li><li>imagination, fancy</li><li>falsehood, lie, fabrication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash, to wash out, to wash up, to wash away, to eluate</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alias (another name&#59; an assumed name)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imaginary, fictitious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent, to fabricate (by creating falsehood)</li></ol><p><i>вымысли́ть</i> (vymyslítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>udder</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>вымѧ</i> (vymę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vymę</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ówHdʰr̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edge, to buttonhole</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́мет(нуть)</i> (výmet(nutʹ), “to spawn”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or <i>вы́метать</i> (výmetatʹ, “to spawn”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p><p><i>вы́метать</i> (výmetatʹ, “to make (buttonholes)”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(a child, an egg)</i> to carry, to bear, to bring forth, to be pregnant with</li><li><i>(an idea, plans)</i> to nurture, to hatch, to mature</li></ol><p><i>выноси́ть</i> (vynosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronouncement, pronouncing (of a decision, verdict); rendition</li><li>removal</li></ol><p><i>вынес-</i> (vynes-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to take out, to bring out, to remove</li><li>to transfer</li><li>to get, to acquire</li><li>to submit</li><li>to pass, to render, to pronounce (a decision or a verdict)</li><li>to endure, to bear, to stand, to sustain</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to crack up (to make laugh)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;algebra) to factor out (to isolate a common factor from an expression)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to factor out (to omit, to not consider as a factor, to not take into account)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to take something out of the parentheses”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart out, to fly out</li></ol><p><i>вы́нести</i> (výnesti) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out, to pull out, to draw out, to extract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying-out, bearing-out; carryover</li><li>removal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to take out, to bring out, to remove</li><li>to transfer</li><li>to get, to acquire</li><li>to submit</li><li>to pass, to render, to pronounce (a decision or a verdict)</li><li>to endure, to bear, to stand, to sustain</li><li>to carry, to bear, to bring forth, to be pregnant with (a child, an egg)</li><li>to nurture, to hatch, to mature (an idea, plans)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to air one's dirty laundry in public, to wash one's dirty linen in public, to foul one's own nest, to tell tales out of school</li></ol><p>Literally, “to take the trash out of the izba”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart out, to fly out</li></ol><p><i>выноси́ть</i> (vynosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>вы́носить</i> (výnositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endurance, stamina</li><li>durability, sturdiness</li></ol><p><i>выно́сливый</i> (vynóslivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardy, sturdy, endurant</li></ol><p><i>выноси́ть</i> (vynosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to compel, to oblige</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ну́дить</i> (núditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to compel, to oblige</li><li>to extort</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>нуждать</i> (nuždatʹ), variant of <i>нудить</i> (nuditʹ, “to force”). Cognates include Gothic <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (nauþs, “need, compulsion”), Old Norse <i>nauð</i> (“need, distress”) and English <i>need</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency landing, forced landing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a forced or coerced manner</li></ol><p><i>вы́нужденный</i> (výnuždennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced (<i>to do something</i>)</li><li>forced, of necessity (<i>action, confession, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out, to pull out, to draw out, to extract</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come up, to come to the surface, to emerge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up</li></ol><p><i>вы́нырнуть</i> (výnyrnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come up, to come to the surface, to emerge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>нырну́ть</i> (nyrnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>вы́пуск</i> (výpusk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunge</li><li>attack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop out, to fall out (out of something)</li><li>to come out</li><li>(of hair) to fall out, to come out</li><li>to fall (precipitations, sediments)</li></ol><p><i>вы́пад-</i> (výpad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falling out, shedding (e.g. hair, teeth)</li><li>fall (of rain, snow, etc.)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prolapse</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) dropping</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) elision</li></ol><p><i>выпада́ть</i> (vypadátʹ, “to fall out”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije, verbal abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shoot, to fire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blurt out</li></ol><p><i>вы́палить</i> (výpalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shoot, to fire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blurt out</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weed, to weed out, to pull up (weeds)</li></ol><p><i>вы́полоть</i> (výpolotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly out, to flutter out, to flit out (<i>quickly and easily of birds, butterflies, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to flit out</li></ol><p><i>вы́порхнуть</i> (výporxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture (land on which cattle can be kept for feeding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graze, to pasture, to drive cattle to pasture</li></ol><p><i>вы́пасти</i> (výpasti) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop out, to fall out</li><li>to come out</li><li>(of hair) to fall out, to come out</li><li>to fall (precipitations, sediments)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baking (action of to bake)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake (bread) in an oven</li></ol><p><i>вы́пек-</i> (výpek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show off (<i>one's manners, judgments, attire, etc.</i>), while manifesting arrogance and disrespect for others</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show off (<i>one's manners, judgments, attire, etc.</i>), while manifesting arrogance and disrespect for others</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bulge out, to protrude, to stick out</li><li>to push out, to shove out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuck/turn/drive out (<i>of</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baking (action of to bake)</li><li>baked goods, buns and rolls</li></ol><p><i>вы́пек-</i> (výpek-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake (bread) in an oven</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink up</li><li>to drink, to hit the bottle</li></ol><p><i>вы́пить</i> (výpitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) drinking session, party, booze-up</li><li>(collective,&#32;uncountable) drinks, booze</li></ol><p><i>выпива́ть</i> (vypivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) drinks, booze</li><li>(<i>familiar</i>) party, drinking session</li></ol><p><i>вы́пить</i> (výpitʹ) or <i>вы́пивка</i> (výpivka) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alcoholic, drunkard</li></ol><p><i>выпива́ть</i> (vypivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́ха</i> (-óxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw, to saw up, to cut out, to carve</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to remove, to take down</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill off</li></ol><p><i>вы́пилить</i> (výpilitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to break down, to deteriorate from wear and tear</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to quit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to commit suicide, to self-delete, to self-destruct</li></ol><p>From <i>выпи́ливать</i> (vypílivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw, to saw up, to cut out, to carve</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to remove, to take down</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill off</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to break down, to deteriorate from wear and tear</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to quit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to commit suicide, to self-delete, to self-destruct</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́пилить</i> (výpilitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bulge out, to protrude, to stick out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be too prominent/obvious</li><li>to push out, to shove out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuck/turn/drive out (<i>of</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign out</li><li>to subscribe (<i>e.g. to a magazine</i>)</li><li>to write, to write out, to write down</li><li>to copy</li><li>to prescribe</li><li>to discharge (<i>from hospital</i>)</li><li>to take out</li><li>to draw out, to draw</li><li>to draw up</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to document one's exit from (somewhere), to check out of [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive)]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be exhausted creatively</li></ol><p><i>вы́писать</i> (výpisatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing out, copying</li><li>extract, excerpt</li><li>statement</li><li>order, subscription</li><li>discharge</li><li>notice</li></ol><p><i>вы́писать</i> (výpisatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign out</li><li>to subscribe (<i>e.g. to a magazine</i>)</li><li>to write, to write out, to write down</li><li>to copy</li><li>to discharge (<i>from hospital</i>)</li><li>to take out</li><li>to draw out, to draw</li><li>to draw up</li></ol><p><i>вы́писать</i> (výpisatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wheel around, to circle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to document one's exit from (somewhere), to check out of [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive)]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be exhausted creatively</li></ol><p><i>выпи́сывать</i> (vypísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink, to drink up</li><li>to have a drink</li><li>to take a medicine</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smelting</li><li>smelted metal, melt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to obtain by weeping, to get by dint of one's tears</li><li>to sob out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to obtain by weeping, to get by dint of one's tears</li><li>to sob out</li></ol><p><i>вы́плакать</i> (výplakatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payout (an amount of money paid out or an act of paying out)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́платить</i> (výplatitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay, to disburse</li><li>to pay off, to pay in full</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay, to disburse</li><li>to pay off, to pay in full</li></ol><p><i>вы́платить</i> (výplatitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splash (act of splashing, or splashed liquid)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ejected flame, sparks, etc.</li></ol><p>Likely deverbal from <i>вы́плеснуть</i> (výplesnutʹ, “to splash”) (compare imperfective <i>выплёскивать</i> (vypljóskivatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) agony (paroxysm of joy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to splash out, to spill out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>плеска́ть</i> (pleskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to splash out, to spill out</li></ol><p><i>вы́плескать</i> (výpleskatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash/spill out</li><li>to spill out, to eject, to gush <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(emotions)</i> to vent, to give way <i>(to)</i></li><li>to pour out <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>плесну́ть</i> (plesnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to splash out</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to spill out, to spill over</li><li>to erupt (<i>e.g. of flames</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to manifest rapidly (<i>e.g. of feelings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́плеснуть</i> (výplesnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim out (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́плыть</i> (výplytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim out (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>плю́нуть</i> (pljúnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-плёвывать</i> (-pljóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash/spill out</li><li>to spill out, to eject, to gush <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(emotions)</i> to vent, to give way <i>(to)</i></li><li>to pour out <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́плескать</i> (výpleskatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to splash out, to spill out</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to spill out, to spill over</li><li>to erupt (<i>e.g. of flames</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to manifest rapidly (<i>e.g. of feelings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>выплёскивать</i> (vypljóskivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep out, to crawl out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag oneself out</li></ol><p><i>вы́ползти</i> (výpolzti) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep out, to crawl out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag oneself out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fulfillment, realization, implementation, carrying out, execution (<i>action</i>)</li><li>accomplishment, realization (<i>result</i>)</li><li>creation, production</li></ol><p><i>вы́полнить</i> (výpolnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feasible</li></ol><p><i>вы́полнить</i> (výpolnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to implement, to accomplish, to fulfill, to execute, to perform</li><li>to make up, to create</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to implement, to accomplish, to fulfill, to execute, to perform</li><li>to make up, to create</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weed, to weed out, to pull up (weeds)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>поло́ть</i> (polótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flog, to flagellate</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly out, to flutter out, to flit out (<i>quickly and easily of birds, butterflies, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to flit out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>порхну́ть</i> (porxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gut, to eviscerate, to disembowel, to remove the entrails from</li><li>to clean out, to empty the contents of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clean out, to rob</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze information from (someone) (<i>by forcible interrogation, torture, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>потроши́ть</i> (potrošítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten</li><li>to correct, to rectify, to straighten out</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;obsolete) <i>(a document)</i> to get, to obtain</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straightening, correcting</li><li>deportment, bearing</li></ol><p><i>вы́править</i> (výpravitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten</li><li>to correct, to rectify, to straighten out</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;obsolete) <i>(a document)</i> to get, to obtain</li></ol><p><i>вы́править</i> (výpravitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free from</li></ol><p><i>выпра́стывать</i> (vyprástyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (try to) get <i>(out of)</i>, to ask <i>(someone for)</i>, to solicit <i>(someone for, something of)</i>, to beg <i>(someone for)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́просить</i> (výprositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to send packing, to send away, to dismiss</li></ol><p><i>вы́проводить</i> (výprovoditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to send packing, to send away, to dismiss</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (try to) get <i>(out of)</i>, to ask <i>(someone for)</i>, to solicit <i>(someone for, something of)</i>, to beg <i>(someone for)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free from</li></ol><p><i>вы́простать</i> (výprostatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump out, to leap out</li></ol><p><i>вы́прыгнуть</i> (výprygnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump out, to leap out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>прями́ть</i> (prjamítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up straight, to become straight; to erect oneself, to draw oneself up</li></ol><p><i>вы́прямить</i> (výprjamitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten</li><li>(<i>electrical engineering</i>) to rectify</li></ol><p><i>вы́прямить</i> (výprjamitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become straight, to stand erect, to draw oneself up</li></ol><p><i>выпрямля́ть</i> (vyprjamljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convexity (state of being convex)</li></ol><p><i>вы́пуклый</i> (výpuklyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convex (curved or bowed outward like the outside of a bowl or sphere or circle)</li><li>gibbous</li></ol><p>From <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пуклый</i> (puklyj, “arched”), from <i>пук</i> (puk, “bunch, bundle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>issue, output</li><li>emission, discharge</li><li>part, number, installment, edition</li><li>graduates</li><li>omission, cut</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пуск</i> (pusk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let out, to let go, to release, to set free</li><li>to launch, to shoot (a missile, a rocket)</li><li>to deliver, to release (a film)</li><li>to produce, to manufacture, to output, to turn out</li><li>to issue, to publish</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exposed, to stick out</li><li>to be produced</li><li>to be published</li></ol><p><i>выпуска́ть</i> (vypuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graduate (from a university) (one who leaves school)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;automotive) exhaust</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) graduation</li></ol><p><i>вы́пуск</i> (výpusk) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let out, to let go, to release, to set free</li><li>to launch, to shoot (a missile, a rocket)</li><li>to deliver, to release (a film)</li><li>to produce, to manufacture, to output, to turn out</li><li>to issue, to publish</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exposed, to stick out</li><li>to be produced</li><li>to be published</li></ol><p><i>вы́пустить</i> (výpustitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bulge out, to make a bulge <i>(in)</i></li><li>to thrust out</li></ol><p><i>вы́пучить</i> (výpučitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bulge out, to make a bulge <i>(in)</i></li><li>to thrust out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пу́чить</i> (púčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bittern (bird of the family Ardeidae)</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>вопи́ть</i> (vopítʹ), <i>вопль</i> (voplʹ).</p><p>From Dahl: <i>Выпь, а выпь, полно тебе выть! Кабы она не выла, так и не была бы выпь.</i></p><p>Compare typologically other names of the birds akin to <i>бу́хать</i> (búxatʹ), <i>бу́кать</i> (búkatʹ), <i>буча́ть</i> (bučátʹ), <i>бык</i> (byk):</p><p>Further compare Latin <i>būtiō</i>, whence English <i>bittern</i> (a blend of <i>būtiō</i> and <i>taurus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be stretched out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out, to thrust out</li><li>to over-emphasize, to over-stress</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>пя́тить</i> (pjátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out, to thrust out</li><li>to over-emphasize, to over-stress</li></ol><p><i>вы́пятить</i> (výpjatitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manufacture, to produce, to make</li><li>to work out, to draw up, to elaborate</li><li>to form, to cultivate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to earn</li><li>to use up</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to work out</li></ol><p><i>вы́работать</i> (výrabotatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to manufacture, to produce</li><li>to cultivate, to develop, to form (including certain qualities pertaining to a person)</li><li>to exhaust, to use up (by using something to extreme or until it is not functional)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacture, making</li><li>output, production, yield</li><li>(<i>generally plural</i>) (<i>mining</i>) excavation, mine working</li></ol><p><i>вы́работать</i> (výrabotatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoothing, levelling</li><li>alignment, equalization</li><li>justification <i>(of a text)</i></li></ol><p><i>выра́внивать</i> (vyrávnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (make) even, to smooth out, to make level</li><li>to flatten (out)</li><li>to iron out (differences)</li><li>to align, to line up (to adjust or form to a line)</li><li>to equalize</li><li>to justify (e.g. text on a page)</li></ol><p><i>вы́ровнять</i> (výrovnjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express, to convey (meaning)</li></ol><p><i>вы́разить</i> (výrazitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express oneself</li><li>to manifest oneself</li><li>to swear, to use bad / strong language</li></ol><p><i>выража́ть</i> (vyražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of expressing something, mostly one's feelings or thoughts</li><li>face expression</li><li>phrase, sentence</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;computing) expression</li></ol><p><i>вы́разить</i> (výrazitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Compare Polish <i>wyrażenie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) severity</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) distinctness, clarity</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) certainty</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) clear manifestation</li></ol><p><i>вы́раженный</i> (výražennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expressed, pronounced, distinct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exponent, spokesman, mouthpiece</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>expressively</li></ol><p><i>вырази́тельный</i> (vyrazítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expressiveness</li></ol><p><i>вырази́тельный</i> (vyrazítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expressive (effectively conveying feeling)</li><li>articulate (speaking in a clear or effective manner)</li></ol><p><i>вы́разить</i> (výrazitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express, to convey (meaning)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express oneself</li><li>to manifest oneself</li><li>to swear, to use bad / strong language</li></ol><p><i>вы́разить</i> (výrazitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to increase</li><li>to arise, to appear, to rise up, to sprout</li></ol><p><i>вы́расти</i> (výrasti) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to increase</li><li>to arise, to appear, to rise up, to sprout</li><li>to grow up</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to raise, to cultivate</li><li>to breed, to rear, to raise</li><li>to bring up</li><li>to train, to prepare, to form</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raising (e.g. of plants), cultivation</li></ol><p><i>выра́щивать</i> (vyráščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to raise, to cultivate</li><li>to breed, to rear, to raise</li><li>to bring up</li><li>to train, to prepare, to form</li></ol><p><i>вы́растить</i> (výrastitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out</li><li>to tear out</li><li>to snatch out (<i>from someone's hands</i>)</li><li>to pull up (a plant)</li><li>to eradicate, to extirpate, to root out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extort, to wring, to wrest</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to vomit</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break away, to break loose, to escape</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to escape (words, sound, moan, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вы́рвать</i> (výrvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut, cutting</li><li>cutout</li><li>low neck, decollete</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) excision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carve, to engrave</li><li>to cut out, to excise</li><li>to massacre, to slaughter, to butcher</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>вы́резать</i> (výrezatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting out, engraving, carving</li><li>press cutting, press clipping</li><li>undercut, sirloin, fillet, tenderloin</li></ol><p><i>вы́резать</i> (výrezatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carve, to engrave</li><li>to cut out, to excise</li></ol><p><i>вы́резать</i> (výrezatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw every detail <i>(of)</i>, to draw carefully</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come into sight/view, to loom, to appear, to come into the picture</li><li>to become clearer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take shape, to emerge, to develop</li></ol><p><i>вы́рисовать</i> (výrisovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw every detail <i>(of)</i>, to draw carefully</li></ol><p><i>вы́рисовать</i> (výrisovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come into sight/view, to loom, to appear, to come into the picture</li><li>to become clearer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take shape, to emerge, to develop</li></ol><p><i>вырисо́вывать</i> (vyrisóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (make) even, to smooth out, to make level</li><li>to flatten (out)</li><li>to iron out (differences)</li><li>to align, to line up (to adjust or form to a line)</li><li>to equalize</li><li>to justify (e.g. text on a page)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ровня́ть</i> (rovnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to degenerate</li><li>to disappear, to be lost over time</li></ol><p><i>вы́родить</i> (výroditʹ, “to (cause to) degenerate (<i>dated</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) freak, monster, degenerate (a person distinguished by physical deformity and&#47;or reprehensible behavior, especially when compared to their family and the surrounding community)</li><li>black sheep</li></ol><p><i>вы́родиться</i> (výroditʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Compare with Ukrainian <i>ви́родок</i> (výrodok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to degenerate</li><li>to disappear, to be lost over time</li></ol><p><i>вырожда́ть</i> (vyroždátʹ, “to (cause to) degenerate (<i>dated</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>degeneration, degradation</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>рожде́ние</i> (roždénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>degenerate, degenerated (having lost vital, desirable qualities)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) degenerate (qualitatively simpler than typical objects of the same class)</li><li>(<i>quantum mechanics</i>) degenerate (having the same energy level)</li></ol><p><i>вырожда́ться</i> (vyroždátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop, to let fall (out)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-рони́ть</i> (-ronítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protuberance, growth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut down, to fell</li><li>to cut out</li><li><i>(hole, window etc)</i> to make</li><li><i>(ore, coal)</i> to cut, to hew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn off, to switch off, to cut off</li></ol><p><i>вы́рубить</i> (výrubitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut down, to fell</li><li>to cut out</li><li><i>(hole, window etc)</i> to make</li><li><i>(ore, coal)</i> to cut, to hew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn off, to switch off, to cut off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go out, to cut out (about electrical power)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pass out (lose consciousness or fall asleep)</li></ol><p><i>вы́рубить</i> (výrubitʹ) +&lrm; <i>ся</i> (sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting down, felling, deforestation</li><li>glade, cleared space, clearing</li></ol><p><i>вы́рубить</i> (výrubitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear, to curse, to use bad language, to call names</li></ol><p><i>вы́ругать</i> (výrugatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Võru (relating to the city or county of Võru, Estonia)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Võro (language)</li></ol><p><i>Вы́ру</i> (Výru) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;usually&#32;collective) Russians who have shed their Russian identity in favour/favor of another; ex-Russians, turncoats, traitors</li></ol><p>Attested since 2004. From <i>вы-</i> (vy-, “out-”) +&lrm; <i>русь</i> (rusʹ, “Rus”); modeled on <i>вы́крест</i> (výkrest). Literally, “ex-Russian” or “no longer Russian”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rescue, to come to someone's help/aid/assistance</li></ol><p><i>вы́ручить</i> (výručitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rescue, to come to someone's help/aid/assistance</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to gain, to net, to clear</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-ручи́ть</i> (-ručítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain; proceeds, receipts, takings; earnings</li><li>rescue, assistance, aid, relief, help</li></ol><p><i>вы́ручить</i> (výručitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out, to dig up, to unearth, to exhume</li><li>to pull out</li><li>to tear out</li><li>to snatch out <i>(from hands)</i></li><li>to pull up <i>(a plant)</i></li><li>to eradicate, to extirpate, to root out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extort, to wring, to wrest</li></ol><p><i>вы́рыть</i> (výrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break away, to break loose, to escape</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to escape (words, sound, moan, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>вырыва́ть</i> (vyryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out, to dig up, to unearth, to exhume</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set down, to put ashore, to disembark, to land, to put off, to drop off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smash, to break in, to break open</li><li>to transplant, to bed out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alight, to dismount (from on horseback or from a carriage)</li><li>to disembark, to land (from a ship)</li><li>to get off (from a tram or from a train)</li></ol><p><i>вы́садить</i> (výsaditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(aviation,&#32;navy) landing, debarkation, disembarkation</li><li>transplanting</li></ol><p><i>вы́садить</i> (výsaditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set down, to put ashore, to disembark, to land, to put off, to drop off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smash, to break in, to break open</li><li>to transplant, to bed out</li></ol><p><i>вы́садить</i> (výsaditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alight, to dismount (from on horseback or from a carriage)</li><li>to disembark, to land (from a ship)</li><li>to get off (from a tram or from a train)</li></ol><p><i>выса́живать</i> (vysáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck out</li></ol><p><i>вы́сосать</i> (výsosatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to think of something, to make up, to make up out of thin air, to invent (<i>false stories, figures, etc.</i>), to pull out of one's ass (fabricate a claim with no factual basis or evidence)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to suck out of one's finger”. Compare German <i>sich aus den Fingern saugen</i>, Dutch <i>uit de duim zuigen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to highlight</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to highlight</li></ol><p><i>вы́светить</i> (výsvetitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free (<i>from</i>), to let out (<i>of</i>), to disengage (<i>from</i>), to disentangle (<i>from</i>)</li><li>to free up, to release, to make available</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>свобо́да</i> (svobóda) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free from [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive)]</li><li>to be vacant, to be free</li></ol><p><i>вы́свободить</i> (výsvoboditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free (<i>from</i>), to let out (<i>of</i>), to disengage (<i>from</i>), to disentangle (<i>from</i>)</li><li>to free up, to release, to make available</li></ol><p><i>вы́свободить</i> (výsvoboditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free from [<i>with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive)]</li><li>to be vacant, to be free</li></ol><p><i>высвобожда́ть</i> (vysvoboždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release</li><li>disentanglement</li></ol><p><i>вы́свободить</i> (výsvoboditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew, to carve, to sculpt, to cut</li></ol><p><i>вы́сек-</i> (výsek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eviction, resettlement; expulsion; ouster</li></ol><p><i>вы́селить</i> (výselitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evict, to expel</li><li>to evacuate, to resettle</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move out (to vacate one's place of residence)</li></ol><p><i>вы́селить</i> (výselitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ться</i> (selítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement formed by migrants from another one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evict, to expel</li><li>to evacuate, to resettle</li></ol><p><i>вы́селить</i> (výselitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ). By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>селить</i> (selitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move out (to vacate one's place of residence)</li></ol><p><i>выселя́ть</i> (vyseljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). By surface analysis, <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>селя́ться</i> (seljátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something worthless produced by a person; piece of shit</li></ol><p>Related to <i>вы́срать</i> (výsratʹ) or <i>высира́ть</i> (vysirátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew, to carve, to sculpt, to cut</li><li>to whip (<i>as a punishment</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit, remain, stay</li><li>to hatch, incubate, brood</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to hatch out, hatch up</li></ol><p><i>вы́сидеть</i> (výsidetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tower (above), to rise (above)</li></ol><p><i>высь</i> (vysʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, to scrub</li><li>to erase</li></ol><p><i>вы́скоблить</i> (výskoblitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express (oneself), to say, to tell, to pronounce</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express oneself, to express one's opinion</li></ol><p><i>вы́сказать</i> (výskazatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utterance, expression, statement, saying</li></ol><p><i>выска́зывать</i> (vyskázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express (oneself), to say, to tell, to pronounce</li></ol><p><i>вы́сказать</i> (výskazatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express oneself, to express one's opinion</li></ol><p><i>выска́зывать</i> (vyskázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump out, to leap out</li><li>to appear, to come up (of a sore, pimple, etc.)</li><li>to drop out, to fall out</li></ol><p><i>вы́скок-</i> (výskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́скользнуть</i> (výskolʹznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump out, to leap out</li><li>to appear, to come up (of a sore, pimple, etc.)</li><li>to drop out, to fall out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upstart, parvenu, nouveau riche, arriviste</li></ol><p><i>вы́скочить</i> (výskočitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch, to send forward</li><li>to banish, to exile; to deport, to expel</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trail, to follow</li><li>to track down</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>espial; tracing; tracking</li></ol><p><i>высле́живать</i> (vysléživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trail, to follow</li></ol><p><i>вы́следить</i> (výsleditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long and meritorious service</li><li>seniority</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>вы́служить</i> (výslužitʹ), with underlying <i>г</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, to qualify for (<i>through service</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́служить</i> (výslužitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, to qualify for (<i>through service</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to serve (a length of time), to usually in a single institution</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear out, to listen (to the end)</li><li>to auscultate (to practice auscultation)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing, listening</li><li>auscultation</li></ol><p><i>выслу́шивать</i> (vyslúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear out, to listen (to the end)</li><li>to auscultate (to practice auscultation)</li></ol><p><i>вы́слушать</i> (výslušatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow one's nose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look out <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spy out</li></ol><p><i>вы́смотреть</i> (výsmotretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ridicule, to make fun of, to deride, to scoff</li></ol><p><i>вы́смеять</i> (výsmejatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ridicule, to make fun of, to deride, to scoff</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-смея́ть</i> (-smejátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow (one's nose)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>сморка́ть</i> (smorkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow one's nose</li></ol><p><i>вы́сморкать</i> (výsmorkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look out <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spy out</li><li>to detect</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to stick out, to thrust out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-со́вывать</i> (-sóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean out, to thrust oneself forward</li></ol><p><i>высо́вывать</i> (vysóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij): tall</li></ol><p><i>высо́к(ий)</i> (vysók(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tall</li><li>high-altitude</li><li>high, great, intense</li><li>lofty, elevated</li><li>unselfish, noble</li><li>high-ranking</li><li>approving, favorable, good</li><li>high-pitched</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑсокъ</i> (vysokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vysokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>high, aloft, high up</li></ol><p>From <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó) (ending-stressed variant) or <i>-о</i> (-o) (stem-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;meteorology) altocumulus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;meteorology) altostratus</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>слои́стый</i> (sloístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly allergenic</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>аллерге́нный</i> (allergénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) (Your, His, Her, Their) Honour/Honor (form of address)</li></ol><p><i>высоко-</i> (vysoko-) +&lrm; <i>благоро́дие</i> (blagoródije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) high voltage</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вольт</i> (volʹt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alpine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(finance,&#32;of a portfolio) multidiversified; highly diversified.</li></ol><p>From <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij, “tall, high”) +&lrm; <i>диверсифици́ровать</i> (diversificírovatʹ, “to diversify”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-quality (of very good quality)</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ка́чество</i> (káčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly-qualified</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>квалифици́рованный</i> (kvalificírovannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-quality, high-grade</li><li>upscale</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haughtiness, arrogance, superciliousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>arrogantly (in an arrogant manner)</li></ol><p><i>высокоме́рный</i> (vysokomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>haughty, arrogant, supercilious</li></ol><p><i>высокоме́рие</i> (vysokomérije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij): tall</li></ol><p><i>высо́к(ий)</i> (vysók(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Very highly educated.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly paid (in terms of jobs and careers)</li></ol><p>From <i>высоко</i> (vysoko, “highly”) +&lrm; <i>опла́чиваемый</i> (opláčivajemyj, “paid”). Compare Ukrainian <i>високоопла́чуваний</i> (vysokoopláčuvanyj), Belarusian <i>высокаапла́тны</i> (vysokaaplátny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-ranking</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поста́вленный</i> (postávlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Your Excellency; Majesty; Highness</li></ol><p>From <i>высокий</i> (vysokij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>превосходи́тельство</i> (prevosxodítelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Honorific/title uttered before the name of a bishop or metropolitan in the Russian Orthodox Church; “Their Eminence, Their Grace”.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-performance</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) high-productivity; highly productive</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>производи́тельный</i> (proizvodítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high developed</li></ol><p><i>высоко-</i> (vysoko-) +&lrm; <i>ра́звитый</i> (rázvityj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-risk</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>риско́ванный</i> (riskóvannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) high-sulfur; having a high sulfur content.</li></ol><p>From <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij, “high, tall”) +&lrm; <i>серни́стый</i> (sernístyj, “sulfurous”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-speed</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>ско́рость</i> (skórostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-grade, of superior quality</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сорт</i> (sort) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) high temperature</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>температу́ра</i> (temperatúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-tech</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>техноло́гия</i> (texnológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly toxic</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>токси́чный</i> (toksíčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) high-precision</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly artistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-performance</li><li>highly effective</li><li>highly efficient</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to think of something, to make up, to make up out of thin air, to invent (<i>false stories, figures, etc.</i>), to pull out of one's ass (fabricate a claim with no factual basis or evidence)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to suck out of one's finger”. Compare Polish <i>wyssać z palca</i>, Czech <i>vycucat z prstu</i>, German <i>sich aus den Fingern saugen</i>, Dutch <i>uit de duim zuigen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height (distance from bottom to top)</li><li>altitude, elevation</li><li>height (an area of high altitude)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) hill, peak</li></ol><p>From <i>высь</i> (vysʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altitude, elevation, height above sea level</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hillock</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) high-rise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) A pilot specialized for high-altitude flights and missions</li><li>An installer specialized in the construction of high-rise structures</li></ol><p><i>высота́</i> (vysotá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tall, high-rise</li><li>high-altitude</li></ol><p><i>высота́</i> (vysotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altimeter (an apparatus for measuring altitude)</li></ol><p><i>высота</i> (vysota) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мерить</i> (meritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to dry out</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dried-up, dry, shrivelled, wizened, withered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very high, very tall</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Highness</li></ol><p><i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij, “high”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep in, to sleep one's fill</li><li>to get some sleep, to get a good night's sleep, to catch some z's</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bombard with questions</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out (<i>by asking lots of questions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́спросить</i> (výsprositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) high-flown, grandiloquent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>выспрьнъ</i> (vysprĭnŭ), from <i>выспрь</i> (vysprĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вы́спренний</i> (výsprennij): high-flown, grandiloquent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bombard with questions</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out (<i>by asking lots of questions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>спроси́ть</i> (sprosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) to shit out</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;rude) to blurt out, to say something out of place and irrelevant</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;rude) to do something ridiculous or awkward</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;prison slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;derogatory) to give birth to an illegitimate child while one's husband or former cohabitant is serving a sentence in a corrective labour colony</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put forward, to move out, to put outside, to bring forward, to place in front</li><li>to set out, to display, to present</li><li>to flaunt</li><li>to propose, to suggest</li><li>to put down (marks), to mark (as in school)</li><li>to expose</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhibit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show off</li></ol><p><i>вы́ставить</i> (výstavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhibiting, showing</li><li>exposition, display</li></ol><p><i>вы́ставить</i> (výstavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put forward, to move out, to put outside, to bring forward, to place in front</li><li>to set out, to display, to present</li><li>to flaunt</li><li>to propose, to suggest</li><li>to put down (marks), to mark (as in school)</li><li>to expose</li></ol><p><i>вы́ставить</i> (výstavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhibit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show off</li></ol><p><i>выставля́ть</i> (vystavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exhibition, show</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́ставок-</i> (výstavok-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); derived from the underlying stem of <i>вы́ставка</i> (výstavka, “exhibition”) (compare genitive plural <i>вы́ставок</i> (výstavok)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand (for a long time), to remain standing</li><li>to withstand, to bear (difficulty), to resist (an enemy), to survive</li></ol><p><i>вы́стоять</i> (výstojatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash, to launder</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover completely, to cover the whole surface of; to carpet, to pave, etc.</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>стлать</i> (stlatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand (for a long time), to remain standing</li><li>to withstand, to bear (difficulty), to resist (an enemy), to survive</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, to endure</li><li>to gain/achieve through much suffering</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alignment; orientation</li><li>building; constructing; erecting</li></ol><p><i>выстра́ивать</i> (vystráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li><li>to line up, to draw up, to form up</li><li>to arrange</li><li><i>(a structure, a theory)</i> to build, to develop</li></ol><p><i>вы́строить</i> (výstroitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw up, to form, to line up</li></ol><p><i>выстра́ивать</i> (vystráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) artillery round (an artillery shell together with its propellant charge)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rocket-propelled grenade round</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) boomkin, bumkin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to fire</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to say or speak intermittently</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-стрели́ть</i> (-strelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot oneself in the foot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off (hair, fur, grass), to clip out, to shear</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li><li>to line up, to draw up, to form up</li><li>to arrange</li><li><i>(a structure, a theory)</i> to build, to develop</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw up, to form, to line up</li></ol><p><i>вы́строить</i> (výstroitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protrusion, protuberance, prominence, projection (anything that protrudes)</li><li>(<i>cartography</i>) a panhandle</li><li>ledge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to project, to protrude, to jut, to jut out, to come out, to stand out</li><li>to step forth, to step forward</li><li>to set out, to march off</li><li>to appear on the surface</li><li>to strut</li><li>to speak in public, to perform <i>on stage</i></li><li>to advocate for</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pace slowly</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to project, to protrude, to jut, to jut out, to come out, to stand out</li><li>to step forth, to step forward</li><li>to set out, to march off</li><li>to appear on the surface</li><li>to strut</li><li>to perform <i>at the scene</i></li><li>to advocate for</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pace slowly</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, speech, address</li><li>performance</li><li>departure</li></ol><p><i>вы́ступить</i> (výstupitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to stick out, to thrust out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean out, to thrust oneself forward</li></ol><p><i>вы́сунуть</i> (výsunutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry</li><li>to roast, to toast, to parch</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to wear out, to exhaust, to torment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harden (<i>one's heart</i>), to make heartless</li></ol><p><i>вы́сушить</i> (výsušitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry</li></ol><p><i>высу́шивать</i> (vysúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry</li><li>to roast, to toast, to parch</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to wear out, to exhaust, to torment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harden (<i>one's heart</i>), to make heartless</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry</li></ol><p><i>вы́сушить</i> (výsušitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to figure out, to reckon</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to figure out, to reckon</li></ol><p><i>вы́считать</i> (výsčitatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>college, higher school (institution of higher education)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat of higher learning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Supreme Leader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch, to send forward</li><li>to banish, to exile; to deport, to expel</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispatch, sending</li><li>exile, banishment; deportation, expulsion; renvoi</li></ol><p>From <i>высыла́ть</i> (vysylátʹ, “to dispatch”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with stress based on perfective <i>вы́слать</i> (výslatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew out, to pour out, to empty (to distribute objects or pieces of something)</li><li>to pour out, to throng (<i>of a crowd</i>)</li><li>to break out, to come out (<i>of a rash, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pour out, to spill out (<i>of bulk material, objects, or pieces, not liquid</i>)</li><li>to have a good sleep, to sleep one's fill</li></ol><p><i>вы́сыпать</i> (výsypatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>высыпа́ть</i> (vysypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпа́ть</i> (-sypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to dry out</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-сыха́ть</i> (-syxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ether, the realms of fancy, the world of fantasy</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) summit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vysь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push out</li><li>to chuck out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(an oven)</i> to heat</li><li><i>(fat)</i> to melt, <i>(oil)</i> to clarify</li></ol><p><i>вы́топить</i> (výtopitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down, to destroy by trampling</li><li>to clear (e.g. a path) by trampling</li></ol><p><i>вы́топтать</i> (výtoptatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тара́щить</i> (taráščitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out, to drag out, to take out</li></ol><p><i>вы́таск-</i> (výtask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn <i>(with a turning lathe)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sharpen</li></ol><p><i>вы́точить</i> (výtočitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sewing</i>) dart (a fold that is stitched on a garment)</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>тача́ть</i> (tačátʹ, “to stitch”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out; to pull out, to drag out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag (out) (<i>someone against their will</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be up to, to do (foolish things)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be up to, to do (foolish things)</li></ol><p><i>вы́творить</i> (výtvoritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to happen, to occur <i>(something unusual, unexpected or improper)</i></li></ol><p><i>вытворя́ть</i> (vytvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow out, to escape, to run out, to drip out</li><li>to have its source <i>(from)</i>, to flow <i>(from, out of)</i></li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to result <i>(from)</i>, to follow <i>(from)</i>, to ensue</li></ol><p><i>вы́тек-</i> (výtek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to bear, to endure, to suffer</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dislodgment, displacement, exclusion, extrusion, forcing out, pushing out</li><li>ousting</li><li>replacement</li></ol><p><i>вы́теснить</i> (výtesnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oust, to force out, to eject, to supplant, to exclude, to dislodge</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) to displace</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тесни́ть</i> (tesnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oust, to force out, to eject, to supplant, to exclude, to dislodge</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) to displace</li></ol><p><i>вы́теснить</i> (výtesnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leak, to flow out, to run out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;military) veteran</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>вытира́ть</i> (vytirátʹ, “to wipe [an ass]”) +&lrm; <i>ветера́н</i> (veterán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chuck out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(an oven)</i> to heat</li><li><i>(fat)</i> to melt, <i>(oil)</i> to clarify</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down, to destroy by trampling</li><li>to clear (e.g. a path) by trampling</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn <i>(with a turning lathe)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sharpen</li></ol><p><i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “sharpen”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrode, to etch, <i>(spots)</i> to remove</li><li>to exterminate with poison</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to eradicate, to stamp out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вытравля́ть</i> (vytravljátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>вы́травить</i> (výtravitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrode, to etch, <i>(spots)</i> to remove</li><li>to exterminate with poison</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to eradicate, to stamp out</li></ol><p><i>вы́травить</i> (výtravitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunk tank</li></ol><p>From <i>вы́трезвить</i> (výtrezvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake out, to drop, to let fall</li></ol><p><i>вы́тряхнуть</i> (výtrjaxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake out, to drop, to let fall</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тряхну́ть</i> (trjaxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to howl</li><li>to wail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vyti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>howl (protracted, mournful cry of a dog or wolf)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out, to extract, to draw out</li><li>to stretch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hold out, to bear, to endure, to stick</li></ol><p><i>вы́тяг-</i> (výtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to stretch oneself, to extend oneself</li></ol><p><i>вытя́гивать</i> (vytjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretching</li><li>pulling out</li><li>drawing out</li><li>extract (substance extracted from plant or animal tissue)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) stretching out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ventilation hatch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) extractor hood</li></ol><p><i>вы́тяг-</i> (výtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elongated</li><li>outstretched</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull out, to extract, to draw out</li><li>to stretch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hold out, to bear, to endure, to stick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make it, to pull through</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to stretch oneself, to extend oneself</li></ol><p><i>вы́тянуть</i> (výtjanutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to fish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fish out, to coax out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>уди́ть</i> (udítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to fish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fish out, to coax out</li></ol><p><i>вы́удить</i> (výuditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn (to completion, typically something involving rote memorisation)</li></ol><p><i>вы́учить</i> (výučitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to learn, to study; to train for [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘a skill’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘an occupation or role to take on’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to graduate</li></ol><p><i>выу́чивать</i> (vyúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn (to completion, typically something involving rote memorisation)</li><li>to memorize</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to learn, to study; to train for [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘a skill’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘an occupation or role to take on’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>вы́учить</i> (výučitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skill</li><li>(level of) training, teaching</li></ol><p><i>вы́учить</i> (výučitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rear, to bring up</li><li>to nurse, to restore to health</li><li>to go over, to go all over, to go through</li></ol><p><i>вы́ходить</i> (výxoditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch out, to snap out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch out, to snap out</li></ol><p><i>вы́хватить</i> (výxvatitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) exhaust</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) result (beneficial or tangible effect)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>хлоп</i> (xlop, “bang”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exhaust</li></ol><p><i>вы́хлоп</i> (výxlop) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going out, coming out, leaving, departure, withdrawal</li><li>exit, way out</li><li>solution, way out, outlet</li><li>yield, output</li><li>publication, release</li><li>appearance</li><li>outcrop</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>выходи́ть</i> (vyxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native (of), emigrant</li></ol><p>From <i>выходи́ть</i> (vyxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), with perfective-verb stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to come out, to walk out</li><li>to withdraw, to retire</li><li>to appear, to be published, to be issued</li><li>to turn out, to result</li><li>to happen, to arise, to originate</li><li>to spend, to use up, to run out of</li><li>to rear, to bring up</li><li>to nurse, to restore to health</li><li>to go over, to go all over, to go through</li></ol><p>From <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry, to take (a husband) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘a man’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose self-control, to get furious</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get out of oneself”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become inactive, inoperable, unfit for work (usually of equipment but can also be used for people or animals)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get out of order/array/line”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, prank</li><li>freak, escapade, antics</li></ol><p>From <i>выходи́ть</i> (vyxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with perfective-verb stress.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>excessus</i> (whence <i>эксце́сс</i> (ekscéss)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exit</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) holiday, day off; festive</li><li>free (not working), on a day off, off</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) discharge; given on discharge</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day off (a day of vacation)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) weekend</li></ol><p><i>вы́ход</i> (výxod) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day off, day of rest, holiday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>days off</li><li>weekend (break in the working week)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate</li><li>to deprive (a theory, a doctrine etc.) of vivacity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate</li><li>to deprive (a theory, a doctrine etc.) of vivacity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desman</li></ol><p>Connected in an unknown manner with Belarusian <i>хуху́ля</i> (xuxúlja), <i>хахо́ля</i> (xaxólja), Ukrainian <i>хоху́ля</i> (xoxúlja), <i>хуху́ля</i> (xuxúlja), <i>хоху́ль</i> (xoxúlʹ), Polish <i>chochoł</i>, <i>chochuł</i> meaning this animal. The original form is probably oxytone <i>&#42;xoxuľь</i>&nbsp;m.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade</li></ol><p><i>вы́цвет-</i> (výcvet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>вы́черкнуть</i> (výčerknutʹ)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>черка́ть</i> (čerkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross out, to strike out, to cut out, to expunge, to delete</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>черкну́ть</i> (čerknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subtract, to deduct, to take away (to remove or reduce)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deduction, takeout</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) residue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) calculation, computation, calculating, computing, reckoning (the act or process of computing)</li></ol><p><i>вы́числить</i> (výčislitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computer</li><li>calculator</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) computer specialist</li></ol><p><i>вы́числить</i> (výčislitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computational biology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) computer</li><li>computing</li></ol><p><i>вычисли́тель</i> (vyčislítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compute</li><li>to calculate</li><li>to evaluate, to weigh</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to deduce</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ), from <i>число́</i> (čisló).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compute</li><li>to calculate</li><li>to evaluate, to weigh</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to deduce</li></ol><p><i>вы́числить</i> (výčislitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean <i>(out, up, off)</i></li><li>to deseed, to remove the pips from something</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) subtrahend (a number to be subtracted from another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) subtraction (calculation)</li></ol><p><i>вычита́ть</i> (vyčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find (by reading)</li><li>to proofread</li><li>to subtract, to deduct, to take away (to remove or reduce)</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find (by reading)</li><li>to proofread</li></ol><p><i>вы́читать</i> (výčitatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean <i>(out, up, off)</i></li></ol><p><i>вы́чистить</i> (výčistitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross out, to strike out, to cut out, to expunge, to delete</li></ol><p><i>вы́черкнуть</i> (výčerknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pace, to measure by pacing</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>шага́ть</i> (šagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pace, to measure by pacing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to goosestep</li></ol><p><i>вы́шагать</i> (výšagatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>вы́шагнуть</i> (výšagnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to goosestep</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>шагну́ть</i> (šagnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw out, to fling out, to hurl out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuck out, to kick out, to expel</li></ol><p><i>вы́швырнуть</i> (výšvyrnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw out, to fling out, to hurl out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuck out, to kick out, to expel</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>швырну́ть</i> (švyrnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>высоко́</i> (vysokó)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vyšьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in plenty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-mentioned</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>изло́женный</i> (izlóžennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-named, above-mentioned, aforesaid</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>на́званный</i> (názvannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) abovedescribed</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>опи́санный</i> (opísannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-listed</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>перечи́сленный</i> (perečíslennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the aforesaid, the preceding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aforesaid, preceding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of higher rank, superordinate</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́щий</i> (stojáščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-mentioned (mentioned or named before&#59; aforesaid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-mentioned (mentioned or named before&#59; aforesaid)</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>упомя́нутый</i> (upomjánutyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bouncer, gatekeeper</li></ol><p><i>вышиба́ть</i> (vyšibátʹ, “to kick out”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock out, to break in</li><li>to chuck out, to fire out, to kick out</li></ol><p><i>вы́шибить</i> (výšibitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock out, to break in</li><li>to chuck out, to fire out, to kick out</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vyshyvanka (traditional Ukrainian or Belarusian clothing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>вишива́нка</i> (vyšyvánka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective) Ukrainian ultranationalists, adherents excessively proud of Ukrainian culture or history, especially in a jingoistic way.</li></ol><p>From <i>вышива́нка</i> (vyšivánka, “vyshyvanka, traditional Ukrainian clothing”) +&lrm; <i>ва́та</i> (váta, “vatniks, ultranationalist fanatics”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) a Ukrainian ultranationalist; especially one who is seen to behave like a vatnik.</li></ol><p>From вышива́нка (vyšivánka, “Ukrainian traditional clothing”) and ва́тник (vátnik, “quilted jacket, Russian ultranationalist”), in turn derived from Ukrainian <i>вишива́нка</i> (vyšyvánka). Coined in response to the vatnik memes mocking Russian nationalists.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroider</li></ol><p><i>вы́шить</i> (výšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embroidery (ornamentation, fabric)</li></ol><p><i>вышива́ть</i> (vyšivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>embroidered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroider</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li><li>control tower</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) higher education (from <i>высшее образование</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) higher mathematics, advanced maths (from <i>высшая математика</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) capital punishment, death penalty (from <i>высшая мера наказания</i>, supreme measure of punishment)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Высшая школа экономики, a university in Moscow with campuses in other cities in Russia including Saint Petersburg</li></ol><p><i>вы́ше</i> (výše) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feel out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) neck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>вы́ꙗ</i> (výja), from Old East Slavic <i>вꙑ́ꙗ</i> (výja), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вꙑꙗ</i> (vyja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vyja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal, to display</li><li>to discover, to uncover, to bring to light, to identify, to detect</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal itself, to stand exposed, to come to light, to manifest itself</li></ol><p><i>вы́явить</i> (výjavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detection, elicitation</li><li>revelation</li></ol><p><i>вы́явить</i> (výjavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal, to display</li><li>to discover, to uncover, to bring to light, to identify, to detect</li></ol><p><i>вы́явить</i> (výjavitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal itself, to stand exposed, to come to light, to manifest itself</li></ol><p><i>выявля́ть</i> (vyjavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarification, elucidation, finding-out, ascertainment</li></ol><p><i>вы́яснить</i> (výjasnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear up, to elucidate, to clarify, to find out, to ascertain</li></ol><p><i>вы́-</i> (vý-) +&lrm; <i>-ясни́ть</i> (-jasnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out, to come out, to be discovered, to become clear, to come to light</li></ol><p><i>вы́яснить</i> (výjasnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear up, to elucidate, to clarify, to find out, to ascertain</li></ol><p><i>вы́яснить</i> (výjasnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out, to come out, to be discovered, to become clear, to come to light</li></ol><p><i>выясня́ть</i> (vyjasnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) boastful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to act out, to fuck around (to express one's feelings through disruptive actions)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to show off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vietnamese person</li></ol><p><i>Вьетна́м</i> (Vʹjetnám) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flip-flop (footwear)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Asian conical hat</li></ol><p><i>Вьетна́м</i> (Vʹjetnám) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thongs, flip-flops (footwear)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Vietnamese</li></ol><p><i>Вьетна́м</i> (Vʹjetnám) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blizzard, snowstorm</li></ol><p><i>вьять</i> (vʹjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-уга</i> (-uga) (compare dialectal <i>завья́ть</i> (zavʹjátʹ), family name Завьялов). By surface analysis, <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-уга</i> (-uga). Influenced by <i>вить</i> (vitʹ). Alternatively from <i>вить</i> (vitʹ) (compare typologically <i>вихрь</i> (vixrʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bale, load</li><li>saddlebag</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>юкъ</i> (jukŭ, “burden, load”), From a Turkic source, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yük</i> (“load, burden, freight”). Influenced by <i>вить</i> (vitʹ) (since Old East Slavic <i>вити</i> (viti)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loach, weatherfish (fish of the genus <i>Misgurnus</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) bindweed, convolvulus</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fidget, fidgety person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cunning, sneaky, person; dodger</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sycophant, toady</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vijunъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ви́ться</i> (vítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-юн</i> (-jun, “alt form of -ун”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beast of burden, pack animal (animal that carries or pulls heavy loads)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pack, burden</li></ol><p><i>вьюк</i> (vʹjuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curly (<i>hair</i>)</li><li>climbing, twisting (<i>plant</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter В (V), в (v).</li><li>The Roman letter V, v.</li><li>The Roman letter W, w.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Classical Chinese, Wenyan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 文言 (<i>wényán</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astringent (a substance which draws tissue together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astringent (having the effect of drawing tissue together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elm (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vęzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knitter</li><li>binder</li></ol><p><i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tying, binding</li><li>knitting (action or result), looping</li></ol><p><i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaf; bundle; truss; faggot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knitted, crocheted</li></ol><p><i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to bind</li><li>to knit</li><li>to be astringent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vę̄zàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree <i>(with)</i>, to accord <i>(with)</i>, to square <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viscous, glutinous, tenacious, sticky</li><li>oozy, boggy, swampy, marshy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) viscoplastic</li></ol><p><i>вя́зко-</i> (vjázko-) +&lrm; <i>пласти́чный</i> (plastíčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viscosity</li><li>viscidity</li><li>miriness, ooziness, bogginess, marshiness, swampiness</li><li>viscosity, toughness, ductility</li></ol><p><i>вя́зкий</i> (vjázkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>вязнути</i> (vjaznuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vęznǫti</i>. Cognate to <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elm</li></ol><p><i>вяз</i> (vjaz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vyaz (Cyrillic calligraphy)</li><li>ligature</li><li>a type of connected writing, such as Arabic or some type of connected calligraphies</li></ol><p>From <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make noise (<i>e.g. of a pet</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to complain, to whimper</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to blather, to talk nonsense</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to blurt out (to say without thinking)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wékʷ-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak”). Cognate with Slovene <i>vékati</i>, Sanskrit <i>वक्ति</i> (vakti)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make noise (<i>e.g. of a pet; once</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to complain, to whimper (<i>once</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to blather, to talk nonsense (<i>once</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to blurt out (to say without thinking) (<i>once</i>)</li></ol><p><i>вя́кать</i> (vjákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying, dry-curing, jerking</li></ol><p><i>вя́лить</i> (vjálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) dried, dry-cured, sun-dried, jerk</li></ol><p><i>вя́лить</i> (vjálitʹ, “to dry, to jerk”) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jerk (to cure (meat or fish) by cutting into strips and drying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sluggishly, listlessly, inertly</li></ol><p><i>вя́лый</i> (vjályj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sluggishness, slowness, lethargy, inactivity</li></ol><p><i>вя́лый</i> (vjályj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) slowly progressive, sluggish, indolent</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) dragged out, protracted</li></ol><p><i>вя́лый</i> (vjályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>теку́щий</i> (tekúščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>withered, faded</li><li>flabby, flaccid, limp</li><li>sluggish, listless, languid, lethargic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, to shrivel, to fade, to dry up (shrivel, droop, dry up, e.g. of flowers)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vędnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vyatka</li></ol><p><i>Вя́т(ка)</i> (Vját(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringdove, wood pigeon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) more powerful</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>вѧщии</i> (vęštii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vęťьjь</i>. Displaced Old East Slavic <i>вѧчии</i> (vęčii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) fair and warm weather (<i>in the summer</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ведро</i> (vedro), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vedro</i>&nbsp;n, from <i>&#42;vedrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) page layout</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) page proof</li></ol><p><i>верста́ть</i> (verstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oar, paddle, scull</li></ol><p><i>весло́</i> (vesló) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowbane, poison parsnip, water hemlock (any plant of the genus <i>Cicuta</i>)</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>wioch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>ве́шка</i> (véška)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>век</i> (vek).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ве́ра</i> (véra).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ве́тер</i> (véter).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ве́тка</i> (vétka).</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Вѣтка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ве́тка</i> (Vétka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>грамм</i> (gramm, “gram”)</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>hecto- (x 100)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гекто-</i> (gekto-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>госпожа́</i> (gospožá): Mrs., Mistress, Madam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>господи́н</i> (gospodín): Mr., Mister, sir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>L-shaped</li></ol><p><i>г</i> (g) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz, “shape, image”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>год</i> (god, “year”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>го́род</i> (górod, “city, town”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ha, hectare</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гекта́р</i> (gektár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) honk (sound a goose makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outline, periphery, perimeter</li><li>linear dimensions (such as length and width)</li><li>position lamps</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gabarit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gabion</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cavus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;mycology) habit, habitus (general appearance of a plant)</li><li>(<i>sociology</i>) habitus (lifestyle, values, dispositions, and expectations of particular social groups that are acquired through the activities and experiences of everyday life)</li><li>(<i>crystallography</i>) crystal habit</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>habitus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gabonese</li></ol><p><i>Габо́н</i> (Gabón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) woof, arf (sound a dog makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hawaiian</li></ol><p><i>Гава́йи</i> (Gaváji) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hawaiian shirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hawaiian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hawaiian language</li></ol><p><i>Гава́йи</i> (Gaváji) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haven (harbour), port</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>haven</i> or Middle Low German <i>haven</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark (to make a loud noise (dogs))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gavъkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gavotte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gavotte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) punk; fool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eider</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loon, diver (any aquatic bird of the genus <i>Gavia</i>)</li></ol><p>Of onomatopoeic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auk, razorbill (seabird of the family Alcidae)</li></ol><p><i>гага́ра</i> (gagára) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jet (a hard, black form of coal, sometimes used in jewellery)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jet (a hard, black form of coal, sometimes used in jewellery)</li></ol><p><i>гага́т</i> (gagát) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gagauz (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gagauz</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>гагау́з</i> (gagaúz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eider</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reptile or amphibian</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) creep, repulsive person, vile creature, scoundrel, cad, asshole, bastard</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>гадъ</i> (gadŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gadъ</i>. Cognates include German <i>Kot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortune-teller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination, fortune-telling, <i>(by hand)</i> palmistry, chiromancy, <i>(cards)</i> card reading, cartomancy</li><li>guess, guessing, guesswork</li></ol><p><i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortune-teller</li></ol><p><i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell fortunes, to divine</li><li>to conjecture, to guess, to surmise, to figure out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gadati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gadget (any device or machine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gadget</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vermin</li><li>bastard, vile creature, cur <i>(very strong term)</i> (m or f)</li><li>garbage</li></ol><p><i>гад</i> (gad, “creep”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to defecate, to shit, to take a dump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil, to sully, to defile</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to do mischief, to play dirty tricks on</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gàditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гад</i> (gad) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting (repulsive, distasteful)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gadъkъ</i> (“repulsive”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ugly duckling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disgust</li></ol><p><i>гадли́вый</i> (gadlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gadolinium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abomination (something abominable)</li></ol><p><i>га́д(кий)</i> (gád(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viper, adder</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) evil, insidious or treacherous person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spanner, wrench</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas (state of matter)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) natural gas</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gas, gas pedal, throttle</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) wind, gas</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) vodka</li><li>(<i>singular only</i>) gauze, gossamer (<i>fabric</i>)</li><li>guz (<i>obsolete unit of length in Asia</i>) (around two to three feet, depending on the region)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gaz</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χάος</i> (kháos, “chasm, void”).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>plyn</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>пли̑н</i> (i.e. <i>**плын</i>, akin to <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ), note Polish <i>płyn</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>gaze</i> (“gauze”), probably from the Palestinian town of Gaza where the material was believed to originate.</p><p>Borrowed from Persian گز (gaz, “cubit, a length of 24 finger-breadths, or six hands”), whence also Urdu گز (guz, “yard, three feet”), Armenian <i>գազ</i> (gaz), Northern Kurdish <i>gez</i>, Chagatai <i>gez</i>, <i>kez</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ГАЗ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>GAZ (Gorky Automobile Plant)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Го́рьковский автомоби́льный заво́д</i> (Górʹkovskij avtomobílʹnyj zavód, “Gorky Automobile Plant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>газе́та</i> (gazéta, “newspaper”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gazelle</li><li>a minibus, a small van</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) ghazal (a poetic form mostly used for love poetry in Middle Eastern, South, and Central Asian poetry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gazelle</i>, from Arabic غَزَال (ḡazāl).</p><p>Generic capitalization of <i>ГАЗель</i> (GAZelʹ), the name of a series of light commercial vehicles, itself a blend of <i>ГАЗ</i> (GAZ) (the manufacturer) and <i>газе́ль</i> (gazélʹ).</p><p>Borrowed from Arabic غَزَل (ḡazal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newspaper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gazette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>газе́та</i> (gazéta): newspaper</li></ol><p><i>газе́та</i> (gazéta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) newspaper</li></ol><p><i>газе́та</i> (gazéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newspaper seller, newsvendor, newsboy, newspaper boy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) journalist, newsman, pressman</li></ol><p><i>газе́та</i> (gazéta) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>газе́та</i> (gazéta): tabloid</li></ol><p><i>газе́та</i> (gazéta) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ГАЗ</i> (GAZ): a GAZ car</li></ol><p><i>ГАЗ</i> (GAZ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carbonate</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerification, aerating, aeration, carbonation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sparkling water, soda water, carbonated water</li></ol><p><i>газирова́ть</i> (gazirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autoclaved aerated concrete, cellular concrete, porous concrete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas water heater</li></ol><p>From <i>га́зовая</i> (gázovaja) +&lrm; <i>коло́нка</i> (kolónka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>га́зовая</i> (gázovaja) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) worker of gas industry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gasman, gas fitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gas</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gauze</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gazon</i> (“lawn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gastight, gasproof</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lawn</li></ol><p><i>газо́н</i> (gazón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawnmower</li></ol><p><i>газо́н</i> (gazón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>коси́лка</i> (kosílka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A person who mows lawns or grass.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gaseous, gasiform</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas pipeline</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прово́д</i> (provód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas supply</li></ol><p><i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>снабже́ние</i> (snabžénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>га̀зо-</i> (gàzo-) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bandoleer sewed into a military uniform or into a traditional festival dress. Used by Cossacks, Georgians and the nations of North Caucasus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haitian</li></ol><p><i>Гаи́ти</i> (Gaíti, “Haiti”) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Haitian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) traffic cop</li></ol><p><i>ГАИ́</i> (GAÍ) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) small grove</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>гай</i> (haj), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gajь</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>гај</i>/<i>gaj</i>, Slovene <i>gaj</i>, Slovak <i>háj</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Guyanese</li></ol><p><i>Гайа́на</i> (Gajána) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a Ukrainian kind of soldier in 18th century, also used during the Civil War in the beginning of the 20th century</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hajduk (outlaw, highwayman or freedom fighter in the Balkans)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) lackey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>hajdúk</i>, plural of <i>hajdú</i> (“foot-soldier”), through Ukrainian <i>гайду́к</i> (hajdúk) and Polish <i>hajduk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nut (fastener intended to be screwed onto a threaded bolt)</li></ol><p>Per Trubachev and Anikin (although Vasmer expressed skepticism) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gajьka</i> (“something that connects; something that prohibits”), derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gajiti</i> (“to protect”).</p><p>Attested since 17th century.</p><p>Cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>gȃjka</i> (“movable ring; nut”), dialectal Czech <i>hajka</i> (“straw landmark on a pole as a sign prohibiting road use”), Ukrainian <i>га́їти</i> (hájity, “to slow down; to linger”), Czech <i>hájiti</i> (“to protect, care”), Slovak <i>hájit’</i> (“to protect, stand up for”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) sinusitis</li></ol><p>Derived from the name of British surgeon Nathaniel Highmore, who described the maxillary sinus, formerly known as the antrum of Highmore, + <i>-ит</i> (-it).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) iron hook used for lifting equipment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) addition (normally used in <i>с га́ком</i> (s gákom, “a bit more than, plus (after numbers)”))</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) gac fruit (<i>Momordica cochinchinensis</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>haak</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Vietnamese <i>gấc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce <i>г</i> as a voiced velar fricative (regular in Southern Russian dialects)</li></ol><p>From onomatopoeic <i>га</i> (ga) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galaxy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>γαλακτικός</i> (galaktikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>galactic</li></ol><p><i>гала́ктика</i> (galáktika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) <i>see брюква</i></li></ol><p>From <i>голла́ндка</i> (gollándka), from Dutch <i>Holland</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haberdashery, fancy goods</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>courteously, gallantly</li></ol><p><i>гала́нтный</i> (galántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gallant</li></ol><p>French <i>galant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a racket (a lot of noise)</li></ol><p>From <i>га́лда́</i> (gáldá), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;galъda</i>, cognate with Polish <i>gałda</i> (“noise, uproar”). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;galъ</i> (“noise”), cognate with Ukrainian <i>га́лас</i> (hálas), Belarusian <i>га́лас</i> (hálas) and Polish <i>gałuszyć</i> (“to rumble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clatter, uproar, noise, clamour, din</li></ol><p>From <i>галде́ть</i> (galdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) galley (large vessel for war)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) drudgery, hard work</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallery</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Medieval Latin <i>galeria</i>, possibly through Dutch <i>galerij</i> due to the -ея suffix reminiscent of -ij.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any mushroom of the genus <i>Galerina</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Galerina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galette (a type of flat, round cake)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>galette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galilean</li></ol><p><i>Галиле́я</i> (Galiléja) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) gobbledygook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>galimatias</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abalone (edible univalve mollusc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Galician (relating to Galicia in northwest Spain)</li></ol><p><i>Гали́сия</i> (Galísija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) flared breeches</li></ol><p>From the name of French general Gaston de Galliffet, who purportedly invented breeches of such design.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Galich</li></ol><p><i>Га́лич</i> (Gálič) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) jackdaw, daw (bird of the genus <i>Coloeus</i>)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) checkmark</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;galъka</i>, from <i>&#42;galъ</i> (“black, black-colored bird”) + <i>&#42;-ъka</i> (compare Russian <i>-ка</i> (-ka)). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>гал</i> (gal, “jackdaw”), dialectal Macedonian <i>гал</i> (gal, “raven”), archaic Serbo-Croatian <i>га̏о</i> (“black, dirty”) (fem. sg. <i>га̏ла</i>, neut. sg. <i>га̏ло</i>), <i>га̑л</i> (“black color”), Slovene <i>gàl</i> (“bird with black feathers”), dialectal Czech <i>gáł</i> (“dirt; large black peas”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaul (native or inhabitant of the historical region of Gaul, or poetically the modern nation of France) (<i>usually male</i>)</li><li>gall, gall nut</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Gallus</i></p><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>galla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gall midge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallicism (a loanword borrowed from French)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gallicisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallon (a unit of volume used for liquids)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gallon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hallucination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halo (atmospheric phenomenon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) halogen (any element of group 7)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallop</li><li>galop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>га́лка</i> (gálka): (small) jackdaw, daw</li><li>tick, checkmark</li></ol><p>From <i>га́лка</i> (gálka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galosh</li><li>swim fin foot pocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) board, reach, tack, traverse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>hals</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necktie, tie</li><li>(scouts,&#32;pioneers) neckerchief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Halstuch</i> (“neckerchief”) or Dutch <i>halsdoek</i> (“neckerchief”). For the second part of the word doublet of <i>фа́ртук</i> (fártuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galloon, braid, lace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of dumpling with no filling, normally added to soups, halušky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>галу́шка</i> (halúška), from Polish <i>gałuszka</i>, from <i>gałka</i> (“ball, knob; jaw; testicles”), <i>gały</i> (“eyes”). Compare Russian <i>га́лка</i> (gálka, “glass ball”), Belarusian <i>га́лы</i> (hály, “eyes, buttocks”), Slovene <i>gȃłka</i> (“oak gall, nutgall”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Slovak <i>hálka</i> (“ball; top of a tower”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) galbik <i>(from Gogol’s novel </i>Dead Souls (Мёртвые ду́ши)<i>, it refers to a game of chance, probably cards but possibly dice)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>galvanic</li></ol><p>French <i>galvan(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pebble, shingle</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gleǵ-</i> (“round, spherical, stone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gallery (cheap seats in a theater)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) the back row of tables in a school classroom; also the students sitting there</li></ol><p><i>галер(е́я)</i> (galer(éja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) din, row, rumpus, hubbub, racket (loud noise, commotion)</li></ol><p>Either onomatopoeic or an alteration of <i>го́мон</i> (gómon, “hubbub”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(zoology,&#32;relational) Of the Gamasina, certain mites; gamasid.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Gamasina</i> +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hamac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gambian</li></ol><p><i>Га́мбия</i> (Gámbija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) gambit</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) gambit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gambit</i>. Several steps omitting from Late Latin <i>gamba</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>καμπή</i> (kampḗ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂emp-</i> (whence Russian <i>купи́на</i> (kupína), Latin <i>campus</i>), from <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“bend, curve”).</p><p>For Latin <i>gamba</i> (“leg”) relation, compare typologically <i>вступа́ть</i> (vstupátʹ), <i>выступа́ть</i> (vystupátʹ) (akin to <i>ступня́</i> (stupnjá)).</p><p>For Latin <i>gamba</i> (“leg”) from <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“bend, curve”) derivation, compare typologically <i>кривоно́гий</i> (krivonógij) (&lt; <i>криво́й</i> (krivój) + <i>нога́</i> (nogá)), <i>кривору́кий</i> (krivorúkij) (&lt; <i>криво́й</i> (krivój) + <i>рука́</i> (ruká)), also compare <i>криву́лька</i> (krivúlʹka), <i>криву́льки</i>&nbsp;pl (krivúlʹki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hamburger, beefburger, burger</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) hamburger icon, hamburger button (an icon with three horizontal lines, indicating a menu in graphical user interfaces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hamburger</i>, with the choice of Cyrillic transcription for the initial <i>ha</i> influenced by <i>Га́мбург</i> (Gámburg, “Hamburg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hamburg (a city in Germany)</li></ol><p><i>Га́мбург</i> (Gámburg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) gamelan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay <i>gamelan</i>, from Javanese <i>gamelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gamete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gamma (the name of the third letter of the Greek alphabet)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) scale</li><li>range, gamut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ganache</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ganache</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangrene, mortification</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γάγγραινα</i> (gángraina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangster (especially in a foreign context)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gangster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handball <i>(sport)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>handball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handball player, handballer</li></ol><p>From <i>гандбо́л</i> (gandból, “handball”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>гандболі́ст</i> (handbolíst) and Belarusian <i>гандбалі́ст</i> (handbalíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>гондо́н</i> (gondón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>iterative of <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ghanaian, Ghanan</li></ol><p><i>Га́на</i> (Gána) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dumbbell</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anything shaped like a dumbbell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hantel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gansu</li></ol><p>From <i>Ганьсу́</i> (Ganʹsú) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) kaoliang (gaoliang), Chinese sorghum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 高梁／高粱 (<i>gāoliáng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garage</i>. Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warōną</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (whence also Latin <i>vereor</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garage</li></ol><p><i>гара́ж</i> (garáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guarantor, sponsor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;law,&#32;finance) guarantee</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) warranty</li></ol><p><i>гара́нтия</i> (garántija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guaranteeing</li></ol><p><i>гаранти́ровать</i> (garantírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guaranteed, assured, secured</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guarantee, to vouch</li><li>to guarantee, to secure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guarantee, warranty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garantie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>горгу́лья</i> (gorgúlʹja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(of an epee,&#32;saber,&#32;sword) guard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guarde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloakroom, checkroom</li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) wardrobe</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) clothes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garde-robe</i>, from <i>garder</i> (“to keep”) + <i>robe</i> (“robe, dress”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloakroom attendant, coatroom attendant</li></ol><p><i>гардеро́б</i> (garderób) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout, to shout <i>(at)</i>, to bark <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrkati</i>. Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout, to shout <i>(at)</i>, to bark <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>га́ркать</i> (gárkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmonization</li></ol><p><i>гармониз(и́ровать)</i> (garmoniz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to harmonize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>harmonisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) alternative form of <i>гармонизи́ровать</i> (garmonizírovatʹ, “to harmonize”)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>harmoniser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) accordion (a small, portable, keyed wind instrument)</li><li>harmony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Harmonika</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harmonize, to accord (to agree or correspond&#59; to be in harmony) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental)]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garmon player, squeezeboxer, accordionist</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) harmonist</li></ol><p><i>гармо́ния</i> (garmónija) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;mathematics,&#32;physics) harmonic</li><li>harmonious</li></ol><p><i>гармо́н(ия)</i> (garmón(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>harmoniously</li></ol><p><i>гармони́чный</i> (garmoníčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmonious</li></ol><p><i>гармо́ния</i> (garmónija) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>harmonia</i>, through Latin <i>harmonia</i> from Ancient Greek <i>ἁρμονία</i> (harmonía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) garmon player, accordionist, squeezeboxer</li></ol><p><i>гармонь</i> (garmonʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) garmon (a small Russian button accordion)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>гармо́ника</i> (garmónika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гармо́нь</i> (garmónʹ): garmon (a Russian accordion)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) anything that resembles a bellows, e.g., some types of sliding doors</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bendy bus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garrison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garnison</i> or German <i>Garnison</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garrison</li></ol><p><i>гарнизо́н</i> (garnizón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garnish, side dish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garnir</i> (“to furnish, to garnish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) side dish</li></ol><p>From <i>гарни́р</i> (garnír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>set (of items of the same style, e.g. furniture, accessories)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>font, type family</li><li>headset with a microphone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garniture</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harpoon</li></ol><p>First attested in 1772. Borrowed from German <i>Harpune</i>, from Dutch <i>harpoen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;French context</i>) waiter, garçon, boy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>garçon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гарсо́н</i> (garsón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worsted (type of woolen cloth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) worsted</li></ol><p><i>га́рус</i> (gárus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to prance</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>harcować</i>, cognate with Ukrainian <i>гарцювати</i> (harcjuvaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something burnt, char, cinder</li><li>a burnt area of a forest</li><li>(archaic,&#32;Christianity) a committed act of a collective self-immolation (among the Old Believers)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;garь</i> (see the verb <i>&#42;gorěti</i> (“to burn”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slungshot, single-headed meteor hammer (a weapon consisting of a weight attached to a rope, belt, or chain)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) candle snuffer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gasidlo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гаси́ть</i> (gasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damper, suppressor, shock absorber</li><li>quencher, quenching agent, killer, luminescence killer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to extinguish</li><li>to turn off (light)</li><li>to cancel (debt); to make invalid</li><li>to quench</li><li>to slake (lime)</li><li>to kill (a ball)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill, to murder, to waste; to beat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gasìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gascon (a native or inhabitant of Gascony, a region of southwest France)</li></ol><p>From <i>Гаско́нь</i> (Gaskónʹ, “Gascony”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out (of light, fire)</li><li>to die away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гаснꙋти</i> (gasnuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gàsnǫti</i>, perfective of <i>&#42;gasìti</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)gʷes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest worker, migrant worker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gastarbeiter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) guest worker, migrant worker; non-Western immigrant</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>гастарба́йтер</i> (gastarbájter).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gastritis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gastrite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tour, to be on tour, to perform/play on tour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to job-hop</li></ol><p><i>гастро́ль</i> (gastrólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) (concert) tour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gastrolle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on tour, touring</li></ol><p><i>гастро́ль</i> (gastrólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>performer on tour</li><li>guest performer</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;derogatory) frequent job-changer (particularly someone who is dishonest or in search of easy money)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) criminal operating outside of his or her area of residence</li></ol><p><i>гастро́ль</i> (gastrólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deli, grocery, delicatessen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gastronomic</li></ol><p><i>гастроно́мия</i> (gastronómija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) meat and dairy products</li><li>meat and dairy department (<i>of a store</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) gastronomy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gastronomie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gastropod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;biology) gastrulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a causeway through swamps, made from logs or brushwood, a corduroy road</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gatь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>howitzer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>haubica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gun-howitzer (an artillery piece that combines characteristics of a gun and a howitzer, allowing fire both with a flat trajectory or with steep angle of elevation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaultheria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Gaultheria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gauleiter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(historical,&#32;politics) an official in charge of a district of the Nazi Party (after 1933 increasingly adopting state functions)</li><li>(historical,&#32;politics) a chief of a local department of the National Bolshevik party</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) any local or regional official who is considered unpleasant and/or illegitimate</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gauleiter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a stockade (prison)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) detention in a stockade as a punishment</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) a guardhouse with an area for line-up of guards</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) a chief guard of a city or a garrison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hauptwache</i> (“main guardhouse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaucho (cowboy of the South American pampas)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>gaucho</i>, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) gaff rig</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>gaffel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hafnium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>háček; the diacritical mark ˇ</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>háček</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawers, pantaloon, a kind of trousers covering the loin</li><li>loins, hips</li><li>the upper part of the hind-legs in a domestic beast</li><li>the plumage around the legs of a bird of prey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gaťę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extinguishing</li><li><i>(postal stamps)</i> cancellation</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) hydrating, slaking</li></ol><p><i>гаси́ть</i> (gasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hashish</li></ol><p>Arabic حَشِيش (ḥašīš, “grass; hashish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Germany,&#32;Austria,&#32;neologism,&#32;colloquial) a little restaurant or a café (especially in a German-speaking country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gaststätte</i> with a simplified pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) hydrated, slaked (mixed with water to form a chemical compound&#59; especially of lime)</li><li>(<i>philately</i>) cancelled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to soil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) din, row, rumpus, hubbub, racket (loud noise, commotion)</li></ol><p>Polish <i>gwałt</i>, from Middle High German <i>gewalt</i> (“strength, power”) (whence also German <i>Gewalt</i>). Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldą</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardsman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) guard (squad responsible for protecting something)</li><li>guardsman's</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) Guards (<i>in the names of units</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гварде́(ец)</i> (gvardé(jec)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) multiple rocket launcher (Katyusha and similar World War II-era systems)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard (armed forces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>guardia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Guatemalan</li></ol><p><i>Гватема́ла</i> (Gvatɛmála) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Guinean</li></ol><p><i>Гвине́я</i> (Gvinéja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гвоздь</i> (gvozdʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carnation, dianthus</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) cloves (<i>spice</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>гвоздь</i> (gvozdʹ, “nail”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka). Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гвозника</i> (gvoznika), <i>гвоздьника</i> (gvozdĭnika) (both forms used interchangeably in <i>A Journey Beyond the Three Seas</i>, circa 1466–1472).</p><p>Vasmer and Chernykh believe that the Russian term is ultimately a calque of Polish <i>goździk</i>, <i>gwoździk</i> (or rather from their Old Polish antecedent), which in turn was a semantic loan from Middle High German <i>negelkîn</i> or Middle Low German <i>nēgelke</i> (“carnation; clove”, literally “little nail”) (whence modern German <i>Nelke</i>). But Shansky dissents, asserting that the Old East Slavic term was an independent formation referring to the clove bud's nail-like shape.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a nail header (used by a blacksmith in production of iron nails)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carnation, dianthus</li></ol><p><i>гвозди́ка</i> (gvozdíka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat's-paw (small crowbar with a handle at a right angle to a blade with a V-shaped notch, principally used by carpenters to remove nails)</li></ol><p>From <i>гвоздь</i> (gvozdʹ) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nail, tack, peg <i>(construction)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гвоздь</i> (gvozdĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gvozdь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hWh, hectowatt-hour</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гектова́тт-ча́с</i> (gektovátt-čás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hWh, hectowatt-hour</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гектова́тт-ча́с</i> (gektovátt-čás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hWh, hectowatt-hour</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гектова́тт-ча́с</i> (gektovátt-čás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>го́ды</i> (gódy, “years”)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>г.</i> (g.), abbreviation of <i>год</i> (god, “year”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gdańsk, Gdansk</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Гда́ньск</i> (Gdánʹsk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native or inhabitant of city&#58;Gdańsk (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>gdańszczanin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li><li>now (sometimes; occasionally)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>къдѣ</i> (kŭdě), alteration of <i>къде</i> (kŭde) with the locative ending <i>-ѣ</i> (-jě), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere or other <i>(indefinite adverb)</i></li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) anywhere at all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>indefinite pronoun, anywhere, somewhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere</li><li>(by extension,&#32;colloquial) somewhere around, about, approximately (not exactly)</li></ol><p>From <i>где</i> (gde) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>где</i> (gde).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) hegemony</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἡγεμονία</i> (hēgemonía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hedonistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Gehenna</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>г҄еона</i> (gʹjeona), from Ancient Greek <i>γέεννᾰ</i> (géennă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey!</li><li>hi!</li><li>giddyup!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a male homosexual; gay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>hei</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>gay</i>. First attested in 1993.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay bar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gay bar</i>. First attested in 1994.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay marriage (marriage between two people of the same sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay movement</li></ol><p>From <i>гей</i> (gej) +&lrm; <i>движе́ние</i> (dvižénije), calque of English <i>gay movement</i>. First attested in 1993.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay periodical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay club</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gay club</i>. First attested in 1995.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay culture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay parade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gay porn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>alleged</i>) gay propaganda, propaganda of homosexuality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay community</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay festival</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gay aesthetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geyser (boiling spring)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>Geiser</i>, an archaic variant of <i>Geysir</i>, from Icelandic <i>Geysir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) gamer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gamer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gamer, gamers'</li></ol><p><i>ге́ймер</i> (géjmer) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(gaming,&#32;neologism) gameplay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gameplay</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gayish</li></ol><p><i>гей</i> (gej) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). First attested in 1994.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geisha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>芸者<rp>(</rp><rt>げいしゃ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>geisha</i>, “artisan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hecatomb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἑκᾰτόμβη</i> (hekătómbē)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gecko (any lizard of the family Gekkonidae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) hexakosioihexekontahexaphobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) hexene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectare</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hectare</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἑκατόν</i> (hekatón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>hecto-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectowatt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectogram (100 grams)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectoliter, hectolitre</li></ol><p><i>гекто-</i> (gekto-) +&lrm; <i>литр</i> (litr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectometer/hectometre <i>(SI unit of measure)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectopascal</li></ol><p><i>гекто-</i> (gekto-) +&lrm; <i>паска́ль</i> (paskálʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gel</li></ol><p><i>гель</i> (gelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mercedes-Benz G-Class, G-Wagen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Geländewagen</i> (“off-roader”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronunciation spelling of <i>гелендва́ген</i> (gelendvágen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun pitcher (<i>Heliamphora</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Heliamphora</i>. From a compound of Ancient Greek <i>ἥλῐος</i>&nbsp;m (hḗlĭos, “sun”) +&lrm; Latin <i>amphora</i>&nbsp;f (“amphora”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>helium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) + <i>-ium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Mercedes-Benz G-Class, G-Wagen</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Gelendzhik</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>гелендва́ген</i> (gelendvágen) + <i>-и́к</i> (-ík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heliconia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Heliconia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) helion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heliotrope (plant of genus <i>Heliotropium</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Heliotrop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gel (suspension of solid in liquid)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gel</i>. Doublet of <i>желе́</i> (želé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hematogen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) hematoidin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hematologic</li></ol><p><i>гематоло́гия</i> (gematológija, “hematology”) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haematology, hematology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hematuria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) a geminate (<i>consonant</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>gemināta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carved hardstone, an intaglio or cameo</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>gemma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haemoglobin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hémoglobine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) haemoglobin</li></ol><p><i>гемоглоби́н</i> (gemoglobín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>haemorrhagic (<i>UK</i>), hemorrhagic (<i>US</i>)</li></ol><p><i>геморраги́я</i> (gemorragíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemorrhage</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>haemorrhagia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>αἱμορραγία</i> (haimorrhagía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hemorrhoid, hemorrhoids</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) problem, difficulty, pain in the ass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>genetics</i>) gene (unit of heredity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) January</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>генварь</i> (genvarĭ), <i>генуарии</i> (genuarii), from Byzantine Greek <i>Γενάρης</i> (Genárēs), <i>Ἰανουάριος</i> (Ianouários), from Latin <i>Iānuārius</i>, from Latin <i>Iānus</i> (“god in Rome mythology”), from (perhaps) Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ei-</i> (“to go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gender</li></ol><p>English <i>gender</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general director</li><li>chief executive officer, CEO</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>генера́льный дире́ктор</i> (generálʹnyj diréktor, “general director”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genealogical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family tree, genealogical tree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γενεαλογία</i> (genealogía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) genesis, origin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>genesis</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γένεσις</i> (génesis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) general</li><li>(pathology,&#32;colloquial) syphilis</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) syphilitic person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>General</i>, further from Latin <i>generālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) figured bass, thoroughbass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Generalbass</i>; compare Finnish <i>kenraalibasso</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governor general</li></ol><p><i>генера́л</i> (generál) +&lrm; <i>губерна́тор</i> (gubernátor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) lieutenant general</li></ol><p>First attested in 1703. Borrowed from German <i>Generalleutnant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) major general</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>major-generalcy, major-generalship</li></ol><p><i>генера́л-майо́р</i> (generál-majór) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) colonel general</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) generalissimo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>generalissimus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;collective) supreme command, generals</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) higher authorities</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Generalität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>генера́л</i> (generál)</li></ol><p><i>генера́л</i> (generál) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general's</li></ol><p><i>генера́л</i> (generál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the wife (or widow) of a general</li></ol><p><i>генера́л</i> (generál) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation (origination by some process&#59; formation)</li><li>(rare,&#32;formal) generation (single step in the succession of natural descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to generate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>generieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genet (mammal, <i>Genetta</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geneticist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>гене́тика</i> (genétika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>genetically</li></ol><p><i>генети́ческий</i> (genɛtíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genetic, genetical</li></ol><p><i>гене́т(ика)</i> (genɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ingeniously</li></ol><p><i>гениа́льный</i> (geniálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genius (of a person or of an idea)</li></ol><p><i>гениа́льный</i> (geniálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;sarcastic</i>) brilliant, ingenious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a genius (someone possessing extraordinary mental capacity)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) genius (extraordinary mental capacity)</li><li>(animate,&#32;Roman mythology) genius, tutelary deity</li><li>(animate,&#32;literary,&#32;figurative) embodiment, quintessence, spirit</li></ol><p>First attested 1763. Borrowed from German <i>Genius</i> or directly from Latin <i>genius</i>. Displaced the earlier form гениуш, which was borrowed from Polish.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>саморо́док</i> (samoródok), <i>прирождённый</i> (priroždjónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) genitalia (genitals or sex organs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genital</li></ol><p><i>генита́л(ии)</i> (genitál(ii)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gene</li><li>genetic</li></ol><p><i>ген</i> (gen) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genotype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gene pool</li></ol><p><i>ген</i> (gen) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>фонд</i> (fond)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genocide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) General Prosecutor's Office</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>генера́льная прокурату́ра</i> (generálʹnaja prokuratúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attorney general, public prosecutor, prosecutor general</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>генера́л-прокуро́р</i> (generál-prokurór) or <i>генера́лный прокуро́р</i> (generálnyj prokurór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) henry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) abbreviation of <i>генера́льный секрета́рь</i> (generálʹnyj sekretárʹ, “general secretary”)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i><b>ген</b>ера́льный <b>сек</b>рета́рь</i> (<b>gen</b>jerálʹnyj <b>sek</b>retárʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Genoese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general staff, joint staff; general headquarters</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>генера́льный штаб</i> (generálʹnyj štab, “general staff”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>geo- (Earth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geographical</li></ol><p><i>геогра́фия</i> (geográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geodesy, geodetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) geoinformation</li></ol><p><i>геоинформа́ция</i> (geoinformácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geologist (<i>male or female</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geologic, geological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geological survey</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) geological survey team</li></ol><p><i>геоло́гия</i> (geológija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>разве́дка</i> (razvédka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) geological survey</li></ol><p><i>геологоразве́дка</i> (geologorazvédka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geometric, geometrical</li></ol><p><i>геоме́трия</i> (geométrija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) geometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geomorphology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geopolitics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geopolitical</li></ol><p><i>геополи́тика</i> (geopolítika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij); see also <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Georgian era (a period in British history)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Saint George; Saint George's</li></ol><p><i>Гео́ргий</i> (Geórgij) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dahlia</li></ol><p>Literary borrowing from German <i>Georgine</i>, named after Russian professor Georgi (1802).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) alternative form of <i>георги́н</i> (georgín)</li></ol><p>See <i>георги́н</i> (georgín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geostationary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geothermal</li></ol><p>From <i>гѐо-</i> (gèo-) +&lrm; <i>терма́льный</i> (tɛrmálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) geothermy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravitropism, geotropism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geophysicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geophysics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geophysical</li></ol><p><i>геофи́з(ика)</i> (geofíz(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geochemical</li></ol><p><i>геохи́мия</i> (geoxímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheetah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guépard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheetah</li></ol><p><i>гепа́рд</i> (gepárd) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hepatitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gepid</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Gepidae</i>, from Gothic <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;gibiþs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gibidōz</i> (which see for more).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gepidic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gepid</li></ol><p><i>гепи́д</i> (gepíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heraldry (the profession of devising and blazoning arms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heraldist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geranium</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>geranium</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Герань)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) The Герань-1 ("Geranium-1") Iranian-designed military drones, known originally as the Shahed 131.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) The Герань-2 ("Geranium-2") Iranian-designed military drones, known originally as the HESA Shahed 136.</li></ol><p>See гера́нь (geránʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coat of arms</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>гербъ</i> (gerb) (att. 1571), from Old Ruthenian <i>гербъ</i> (herb) (early 16th c.), from Middle Polish <i>herb</i>, from Old Polish <i>herb</i> (13th c.), from Old Czech <i>herb</i>, from <i>erb</i>, from Middle High German <i>erbe</i> (“heritage”), from Old High German <i>erbi</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arbī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbiją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃orbʰ-yo-m</i>. Ultimately from the root <i>&#42;h₃erbʰ-</i> (“to change allegiance, status, ownership”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herbarium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>herbārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herbicide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) strong man</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) oatmeal</li></ol><p>From <i>Геркуле́с</i> (Gerkulés, “Hercules”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Геркулес)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hercules</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Herculēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Herculean</li></ol><p><i>Геркуле́с</i> (Gerkulés, “Hercules”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Геркулесов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hercules's (belonging to Hercules)</li></ol><p><i>Геркуле́с</i> (Gerkulés, “Hercules”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hercules; Herculean</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) oatmeal</li></ol><p><i>Геркуле́с</i> (Gerkulés, “Hercules”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj). The association with oatmeal derives from a popular Soviet-era oatmeal brand, itself derived from the idea that eating oatmeal will make one strong like Hercules.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>герма́нский</i> (germánskij): Germ., German, Germanic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a male member of a Germanic tribe, a Teuton</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) German (<i>male</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Герма́ния</i> (Germánija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Germanization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>germanium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incorrect spelling of Германия</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Германия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Germany (a country in Central Europe, formed in 1949 as West Germany, with its provisional capital Bonn until 1990, when it incorporated East Germany)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Germānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) German woman or girl</li></ol><p><i>Герма́ния</i> (Germánija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Germano-, German (of Germany)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>German</li><li>Germanic</li></ol><p><i>Герма́ния</i> (Germánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermaphrodite (individual or organism having both male and female gonads)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hermaphrodītus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἑρμαφρόδῑτος</i> (Hermaphródītos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sealant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hermetic (not letting gas or water through, waterproof or gasproof)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hermetically sealed, airtight, airproof, watertight, waterproof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermetic door (<i>civil defense</i>)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>гермети́чный затво́р</i> (germetíčnyj zatvór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heroism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heroin (powerful and addictive drug)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heroin</li></ol><p><i>герои́н</i> (geroín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heroinist</li></ol><p><i>герои́н</i> (geroín) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>heroically</li></ol><p><i>герои́ческий</i> (geroíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heroic</li></ol><p><i>геро́й</i> (gerój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hero</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>héros</i>, from Latin <i>hērōs</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs, “demi-god, hero”). Influenced by the displaced earlier <i>иро́й</i> (irój), directly from the Greek. Cognate with English <i>hero</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heroic</li></ol><p><i>геро́й</i> (gerój) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heroism</li></ol><p><i>геро́й</i> (gerój) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gerontologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herpes</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>herpes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>German-speaking countries</i>) Mr, Herr (a title used before the surname)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Herr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gerund</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>gerundium</i>. See <i>ерунда́</i> (jerundá) (a possible doublet).</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>rzeczownik</i>, <i>odsłownik</i>, Slovene <i>glagolnik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hertz (the derived unit of frequency)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hertz</i>, named for the German physicist Heinrich Rudolf Hertz.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hertz (the derived unit of frequency)</li></ol><p>From <i>герц</i> (gerc, “hertz”) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj), from German <i>Hertz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hertz (the derived unit of frequency)</li></ol><p>From <i>герц</i> (gerc, “hertz”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj), from German <i>Hertz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Herzog</i>. The second component of the term is ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i>, whence via Latin <i>dux</i> (akin to <i>dūcō</i>) also <i>дож</i> (dož), <i>ду́че</i> (dúče), <i>дюк</i> (djuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duchy (a dominion or region ruled by a duke or duchess)</li></ol><p>From <i>ге́рцог</i> (gércog) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any extinct bird of the genus <i>Hesperornis</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Hesperornis</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos, “western”) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>ὄρνις</i> (órnis, “bird”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gestapo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gestapo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hetaera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) heterogeneous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>heterogen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heterosexual</li></ol><p>From German <i>heterosexual</i>, most likely through English <i>heterosexual</i>, as Google Books analysis shows the word entered Russian in the 1980s through publications about HIV/AIDS.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heterosexuality</li></ol><p>From <i>гетеросексуа́льный</i> (getɛrosɛksuálʹnyj, “heterosexual”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterosexual (sexually attracted to members of the opposite sex)</li></ol><p><i>гетеросексуа́л</i> (getɛrosɛksuál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hetman (a Cossack headman or general, a leader in various areas in Eastern Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>hetman</i>, from Middle High German <i>häuptmann</i> (“captain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hetman</li></ol><p><i>ге́тман</i> (gétman) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg warmer, gaiter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guêtre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghetto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>ghetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gethsemane</li></ol><p>From <i>Гефсима́н(ия)</i> (Gefsimán(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyacinth</li><li>jacinth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss, death, demise (<i>due to a violent or unnatural cause, e.g. war, accident, or natural disaster</i>)</li><li>destruction, ruin</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sinking, loss (<i>of a ship due to a violent or unnatural cause, e.g. battle, storm, or collision</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) lots of, an immense number of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гꙑбель</i> (gybelĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gybělь</i>, from <i>&#42;gyb-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ělь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disastrous, destructive, ruinous, pernicious</li><li>fatal</li></ol><p><i>ги́бель</i> (gíbelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hibernation (state of inactivity during winter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bending (<i>especially in a technical context</i>)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) bender (device for bending)</li></ol><p>From <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). The term is logically derived from <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ) but has the form of the root found in imperfective derivations in <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flexible, pliable, agile</li><li>adaptable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gybъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flexibility (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>ги́бкий</i> (gíbkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perish, to die</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gybnǫti</i>. Cognates include Russian <i>ги́нуть</i> (gínutʹ), Old Church Slavonic <i>гꙑбнѫти</i> (gybnǫti); Russian <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ) (see <i>загну́ться</i> (zagnútʹsja), <i>(согну́ть) в бара́ний рог</i> ((sognútʹ) v baránij rog)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hybrid, mongrel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) hybridism, hybridness, hybridity</li></ol><p>From <i>гибри́дный</i> (gibrídnyj, “hybrid”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, abstract-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hybrid</li></ol><p><i>гибри́д</i> (gibríd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bender (one who bends)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) traffic cop</li></ol><p><i>ГИБДД</i> (GIBDD) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>giga-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) gigabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>гига-</i> (giga-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) gigabit</li></ol><p>From <i>гига-</i> (giga-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giantess</li></ol><p><i>гига́нт</i> (gigánt) +&lrm; <i>-е́сса</i> (-éssa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giant</li></ol><p><i>гига́нт</i> (gigánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gigaton</li></ol><p>From <i>гига-</i> (giga-) +&lrm; <i>тонна</i> (tonna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hygiene (conditions and practices that promote and preserve health)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hygiene</li><li>hygienic</li></ol><p><i>гигие́на</i> (gigijéna) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hygrometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hygroscopic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide (someone who guides, especially someone hired to show people around a place)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guide</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (via Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any fungus of the genus <i>Hydenellum</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Hydnellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὕδρα</i> (húdra). Distant doublet of <i>вы́дра</i> (výdra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydraulics (engineering science that deals with liquid in motion)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>hydraulicus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὑδραυλικός</i> (hudraulikós, “of a water organ”), from <i>ὕδραυλις</i> (húdraulis, “water organ”), from <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “pipe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>гидравли́ческая</i> (gidravlíčeskaja) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydraulic</li></ol><p><i>гидра́вл(ика)</i> (gidrávl(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry&#32;and&#32;medicine</i>) mercury, Hg, hydrargyrum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>hydrargyrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) hydrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>hydro- (pertaining to water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydroaerodrome</li></ol><p>From <i>гидро-</i> (gidro-) +&lrm; <i>аэродром</i> (aerodrom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrogeology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrogeochemistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immersion suit.</li></ol><p><i>гидро-</i> (gidro-) +&lrm; <i>костю́м</i> (kostjúm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydroxide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrology</li></ol><p>Morphologically <i>гидро-</i> (gidro-, “hydro-”) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija, “-logy”). Compare Belarusian <i>гідрало́гія</i> (hidralóhija), Polish <i>hydrologia</i>, Ukrainian <i>гідроло́гія</i> (hidrolóhija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrometallurgy</li></ol><p>From <i>гидро-</i> (gidro-) +&lrm; <i>металлурги́я</i> (metallurgíja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydrometeorological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydraulic broom</li></ol><p><i>гидро-</i> (gidro-) +&lrm; <i>метла́</i> (metlá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydronym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaplane, hydroplane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaplane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water power plant, hydroelectric power plant</li></ol><p><i>гидро-</i> (gidro-) +&lrm; <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dye transfer</li></ol><p><i>гидроти́пия</i> (gidrotípija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>гѝдро-</i> (gìdro-) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrofoil (<i>wing</i>)</li><li>hydrofoil (<i>vessel</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hydrofoil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personal watercraft, PWC, water scooter, boatercycle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydroelectric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water power plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydroelectric power</li></ol><p>From <i>гѝдро-</i> (gìdro-) +&lrm; <i>энерге́тика</i> (ɛnɛrgétika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hydroelectric power</li></ol><p>From <i>гѝдроэнерге́тика</i> (gìdroɛnɛrgétika) +&lrm; <i>-ический</i> (-ičeskij). By surface analysis, <i>гѝдро-</i> (gìdro-) +&lrm; <i>энергети́ческий</i> (ɛnɛrgetíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyena</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ὕαινα</i> (húaina), which from Ancient Greek <i>ὗς</i> (hûs), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-</i> (whence Latin <i>sūs</i>, English <i>swine</i>, Russian <i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) nonsense, rubbish, baloney, bilge, poppycock</li></ol><p>Compared with Russian <i>гул</i> (gul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guild</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gilde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shell, casing (of a bullet or missile)</li><li>cigarette paper</li><li>cylinder sleeve, bushing sleeve</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hülse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guillotine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guillotine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guillotine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) guillotine</li></ol><p>From <i>гильоти́н(а)</i> (gilʹotín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Himalayan</li></ol><p><i>Гимала́и</i> (Gimalái) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) hymenium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthem</li><li>hymn</li></ol><p>Presumably from Polish <i>hymn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;pre-Revolutionary) gymnasium student, high school student</li></ol><p><i>гимна́зия</i> (gimnázija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gymnasium (<i>in pre-Revolutionary Russia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гимна́зия</i> (gimnázija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high school <i>(US)</i></li><li>grammar school <i>(UK)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gymnast</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>γυμναστής</i> (gumnastḗs, “trainer of athletes”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gymnastics</li><li>physical exercises</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gymnastic</li></ol><p><i>гимна́ст(ика)</i> (gimnást(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) blouse (<i>U.S.</i>), tunic (<i>British</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Gymnopus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Gymnopus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gynaecologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gynecological</li></ol><p><i>гинеколо́гия</i> (ginekológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gynecology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginkgo (either the tree, or its seed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional) to disappear, to vanish, to die</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>ги́бнуть</i> (gíbnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>hyper-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ὑπέρ</i> (hupér, “over”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i> (“over, above”). Doublet of супер-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hyperbola</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) hyperbole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπερβολή</i> (huperbolḗ) via Polish <i>hiperbola</i> or Latin <i>hyperbolē</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to hyperbolise</li></ol><p><i>гипе́рбола</i> (gipérbola) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(geometry,&#32;relational) hyperbolic</li><li>(literature,&#32;philosophy) hyperbolic, exaggerated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyperborean</li></ol><p>From <i>Гиперборе́я</i> (Giperboréja, “Hyperborea”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hyperborea</li></ol><p><i>Гиперборе́я</i> (Giperboréja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypersonic sound</li></ol><p><i>гипер-</i> (giper-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypersonic</li></ol><p><i>гипер-</i> (giper-) +&lrm; <i>звуковой</i> (zvukovoj) or <i>гиперзвук</i> (giperzvuk) +&lrm; <i>-овой</i> (-ovoj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyperinflation</li></ol><p><i>гипер-</i> (giper-) +&lrm; <i>инфляция</i> (infljacija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) hyperlipidaemia</li></ol><p><i>гипер-</i> (giper-) +&lrm; <i>липи́д</i> (lipíd) +&lrm; <i>-еми́я</i> (-emíja), derived from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπέρ</i> (hupér, “over”) + <i>λῐ́πος</i> (lĭ́pos, “animal fat”) + Ancient Greek <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypermarket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hypermarket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hypernova</li></ol><p><i>гипер-</i> (giper-) +&lrm; <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) hypernym (superordinate grouping word)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyperrealism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;Internet) hyperlink (link from one electronic document to another)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>hyperlink</i>, from <i>гипер-</i> (giper-, “hyper-”) +&lrm; <i>ссы́лка</i> (ssýlka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hypertension; hypertensive</li></ol><p><i>гипертони́я</i> (gipertoníja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hypertension</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Hypertonie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypnosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hypnotize</li><li>to mesmerize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypnotism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypnotizer, hypnotist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypnotic</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hypnotizing, mesmerizing</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>hypnot(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) tube lichen, hypogymnia (<i>Hypogymnia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Hypogymnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hypocaust</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hypocaustum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó, “underneath”) + <i>καυστόν</i> (kaustón, “burnt offering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) hypocoristic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hypoxia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyponym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypothesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hypotenuse</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from Ancient Greek <i>ὑποτείνουσα</i> (hupoteínousa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) hypothetically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypothetical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypochlorite</li></ol><p><i>гипо-</i> (gipo-) +&lrm; <i>хлори́т</i> (xlorít)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any ladybug of the genus <i>Hippodamia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Hippodamia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus (mammal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hippopotame</i>. Several steps omitting from Ancient Greek <i>ἱπποπόταμος</i> (hippopótamos), from <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos) + <i>ποταμός</i> (potamós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) gypsum board, drywall</li></ol><p>From the Gyproc brand of drywall.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gypsum, plaster of Paris</li><li>(sculpture,&#32;surgery) plaster cast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;mineralogy) gypsum; gypseous</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) plaster</li></ol><p><i>гипс</i> (gips) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gypsum board, drywall, plasterboard</li></ol><p>From <i>гипс</i> (gips) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>карто́н</i> (kartón). Compare Ukrainian <i>гіпсокарто́н</i> (hipsokartón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Northern Russia,&#32;rare,&#32;dated) buck (adult male deer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Karelian <i>hirvaš</i> or Finnish <i>hirvas</i>, ultimately from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;hirvas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) kettlebell lifter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garland</li><li>Christmas lights thred</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>guirlande</i>, further from or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīraz</i> (whence English <i>wire</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garland</li></ol><p><i>гирля́нда</i> (girljánda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight <i>for measuring</i></li><li>kettlebell, girya</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гыря</i> (gyrja). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ги́ря</i> (hýrja), Belarusian <i>гіря</i> (hirja).</p><p>Vasmer connects it to the root of гвор (gvor, “bladder, tumor”),</p><p>ultimately PIE &#42;gʷreh₂- (“heavy”). The phonology is difficult and may show Iranian influence (Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;gr̥Húš</i>, modern Persian گران (gerân, “heavy”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>histogram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>histology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guitar</li><li>(<i>mechanical engineering</i>) change gear, swing-frame, swingarm, bracket</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) swing-frame gear</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) quadrant</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) adjustment plate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>gitara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guitarist (person playing or performing on the guitar)</li></ol><p><i>гита́ра</i> (gitára) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) guitar</li></ol><p><i>гита́ра</i> (gitára) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hitlerite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hitler; Hitler's, Hitlerite</li></ol><p><i>Ги́тлер</i> (Gítler) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) GIF (an image or video encoded in GIF format)</li></ol><p>From English <i>GIF</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) Any mushroom of the genus <i>Hypholoma</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Hypholoma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) gig, galley.</li></ol><p>From English <i>gig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>глава́</i> (glavá, “chapter”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>глаго́л</i> (glagól, “verb”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Гл.)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliographical abbreviation of <i>Голова́цкий</i> (Golováckij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mostly, mainly</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гла́вным о́бразом</i> (glávnym óbrazom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head, top, summit</li><li>cupola, dome</li><li>chapter, section (<i>of a book</i>)</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) chief (<i>on a shield</i>)</li><li>head, chief (<i>title</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>глава</i> (glava), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>глава</i> (glava).</p><p>Doublet of <i>голова́</i> (golová), the regular East Slavic reflex.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>capitāneus</i> (whence <i>капита́н</i> (kapitán)) &lt; <i>caput</i> (whence also <i>шеф</i> (šef)). Also compare typologically Latin <i>capitulum</i> (whence English <i>chapter</i>), Ancient Greek <i>κεφάλαιον</i> (kephálaion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of household (person responsible for governing a group that lives together, such as a family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader (e.g. of a gang); ringleader; chieftain</li></ol><p><i>глава́</i> (glavá) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head physician, chief doctor</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>гла́вный врач</i> (glávnyj vrač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primacy, supremacy</li><li>prevailing importance</li></ol><p><i>гла́вный</i> (glávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to dominate</li><li>to prevail</li></ol><p><i>главе́нство</i> (glavénstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>central administrative board, central directorate, major committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>гла́вный комите́т</i> (glávnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) commander in chief</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>главнокома́ндующий</i> (glavnokomándujuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) main diagonal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>гла́вный</i> (glávnyj): most important, of paramount importance</li></ol><p><i>гла́вный</i> (glávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the main thing, the essentials</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chiefly, above all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) commander in chief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chief, leading, main, principal, head, central</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>главьнъ</i> (glavĭnŭ), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golvьnъ</i>. Parsable as <i>глава́</i> (glavá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>головно́й</i> (golovnój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main spar (<i>of an airframe wing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) Glavny starshina</li></ol><p>Literally, “chief elder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mainly, chiefly</li><li>mostly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verb</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) word; speech</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>глаголъ</i> (glagolŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golgolъ</i> (“speech, word”). In the sense "verb" introduced by Meletius Smotrytsky as a calque of Latin <i>verbum</i>. Displaced the native Russian <i>&#42;гологол</i> (&#42;gologol), still found in <i>голого́лить</i> (gologólitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verb of motion, motion verb (verb that describes movement on the part of the subject)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;grammar) copula, linking verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;stilted&#32;or&#32;ironic) to say, to speak, to make a speech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glagolitic alphabet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>глаго́л</i> (glagól).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the letter Г in old East Slavic alphabets</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) the Г-shaped gallows</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>verbal</li></ol><p><i>глаго́л</i> (glagól, “verb”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, “adjectival suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) hunger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гладъ</i> (gladŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goldъ</i>. Doublet of <i>го́лод</i> (gólod), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladiator</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>gladiātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folding-bone, folding-stick, bone folder</li></ol><p><i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironing board (a long board on which one can iron)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ironing</li></ol><p><i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironing room</li></ol><p><i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladiolus (plant)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Gladiolus</i>, from Latin <i>gladiolus</i> (“little sword, sword lily”), diminutive of <i>gladius</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to iron, to press (clothes)</li><li>to pet (animals, etc.)</li><li>to stroke, to caress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glàditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well-fed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gladъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>smoothly</li></ol><p><i>гла́дкий</i> (gládkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smoothbore shotgun</li></ol><p><i>гла́дкий</i> (gládkij, “smooth”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol, “barrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoothbore</li></ol><p><i>гла́дкий</i> (gládkij, “smooth”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol, “barrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smooth surface</li><li>satin stitch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li><li>eyesight</li><li>view, opinion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>глазъ</i> (glazŭ, “ball, eye”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glazъ</i> (“ball”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;g(ʰ)el-</i> (“round, spherical, stone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) big-eyed, bug-eyed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sharp-eyed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spotted, splotched, dotted (decorated with bright spots or circles)</li></ol><p><i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stare, to gape, to look, to eye [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘at someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>глаз</i> (glaz)</li></ol><p><i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glazed curd</li></ol><p><i>глазиро́ванный</i> (glaziróvannyj) +&lrm; <i>сыро́к</i> (syrók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>глаз</i> (glaz)</li></ol><p><i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(eye) orbit, eye-pit, eyesocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy) orbital (relating to the eye socket)</li></ol><p>From <i>глазни́ца</i> (glazníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) eyeball (ball of the eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eye; ocular</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nominalized) eye doctor, ophthalmologist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glazьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) eyebag; bag under the eye</li></ol><p>Literally, “eye sack(s)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>глаз</i> (glaz): little eye</li><li>eye spot, globule</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reproductive bud on a vegetable</li><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) <i>(in rural areas)</i> kidney-shaped scion cut from a plant and grafted onto rootstock</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) lentigo or pigmented spot (in the coloration of insects, birds, plants); circle or dot in the design on a fabric</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) eyelet for threading the warp on a loom</li><li>eyehole, peephole, spyhole, or optical device for seeing through a wall or door</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glazъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ability to assess the size of objects or distance between objects by naked eye, the capacity to eyeball</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ, “to measure”). Compare with German <i>Augenmaß</i> from <i>Auge</i> (“eye”) + <i>Maß</i> (“size, extent, measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fried egg; an egg fried sunny side up, over easy</li></ol><p>From <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) syrup <i>(for fruit)</i>, icing <i>(for cakes)</i></li><li>(<i>ceramics</i>) glaze</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Glasur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>глаза́</i> (glazá, “eyes”)</li></ol><p>Has the form of a plural of <i>&#42;глазёнка</i> (&#42;glazjónka), from <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glamour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tonsil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>glande</i>, from Latin <i>glans</i> (“acorn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) voice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гласъ</i> (glasŭ), not exhibiting pleophony as opposed to the inherited doublet <i>го́лос</i> (gólos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, to read</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гласити</i> (glasiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golsiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глас</i> (glas) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Doublet of <i>голоси́ть</i> (golosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vowel (letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>publicly, aloud, loudly</li></ol><p><i>гласный</i> (glasnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public, publicity</li><li>openness</li><li>glasnost (Soviet policy)</li></ol><p>From <i>гла́сный</i> (glásnyj, “public, open”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;phonetics) vowel</li><li>public, open</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) vowel</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) town councillor (member of the city duma)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гласьнъ</i> (glasĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golsьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глас</i> (glas, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). The phonetic sense is a semantic loan from Latin <i>vōcālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) glaucoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herald, spokesman, town crier, mouthpiece (person who used to make proclamations to the common folk, before the development of mass media)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>глашатаи</i> (glašatai, “herald”), itself from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golšatajь</i> (“herald”), a compound of &#42;golšati (“to speak”) and &#42;-ajь (“deverbal suffix”). By surface analysis, <i>гласи́ть</i> (glasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mountain climbing</i>) glacier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology) glycoprotein</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>glycoprotein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>глина</i> (glina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glina</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gléiˀnāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gleh₁y-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;especially&#32;geology) clay</li><li>clayey</li><li>clay-colored</li></ol><p><i>гли́на</i> (glína) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a person that is mixing clay, clayster</li><li>(animate,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) gay, faggot</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) clay mixer</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;slang) offroader, automobile apt to traverse mud</li></ol><p><i>гли́на</i> (glína, “clay”) +&lrm; <i>меси́ть</i> (mesítʹ, “knead”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulled wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>glühend Wein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) clay</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pottery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gliněnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гли́на</i> (glína) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) glidepath</li></ol><p>Derived from French <i>glissade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speedboat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>glisseur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitic worm, helminth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glistъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helminth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glyph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>glyphe</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γλυφή</i> (gluphḗ, “carving”), from <i>γλύφω</i> (glúphō, “I carve, engrave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glycerine, glycerol</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>Glyzerin</i> or <i>Glycerin</i>, from French <i>glycérine</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>гліцери́н</i> (hlicerýn), Belarusian <i>гліцэры́н</i> (hlicerýn) and <i>гліцэры́на</i> (hlicerýna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisteria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>globalisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) globalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>global warming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>global, holistic, world-wide, universal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geography,&#32;cartography) globe (model of the Earth)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1697. Borrowed from Latin <i>globus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw</li><li>to torment, to gnaw at (<i>of feelings</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glodati</i>; cognate with Ukrainian <i>глода́ти</i> (hlodáty), Bulgarian <i>гло́ждя</i> (glóždja), Serbo-Croatian <i>гло̀дати</i>, Slovene <i>glódati</i>, Czech <i>hlodat</i>, Polish <i>głodać</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>hłodać</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>głodaś</i> (“to scratch”). More distantly cognate with Old Norse <i>glata</i> (“to destroy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;zoology) glomerid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Glomeridae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glossary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>глътати</i> (glŭtati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glъtati</i>, cognate to Latin <i>glūtiō</i>, gluttiō, glūtus.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat, gullet</li><li>pharynx</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glъtъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глот-</i> (glot-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow, to gulp</li><li>to take a gulp <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>глота́ть</i> (glotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallow, draught, gulp</li></ol><p><i>глота́ть</i> (glotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>глото́к</i> (glotók): small gulp, small swallow</li></ol><p><i>глото́к</i> (glotók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) glottal (articulated with the glottis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow/to become deaf</li><li>to fade out, to die away, to subside (of a noise)</li><li>to stall (of an engine)</li><li>to become choked with weeds</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glъxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>глубоко́</i> (glubokó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depth</li><li>depth, profoundness</li><li>profundity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glǫbina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глуб(о́кий)</i> (glub(ókij)) +&lrm; <i>-ина́</i> (-iná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backwater, remote / out-of-the-way place, hinterland</li></ol><p><i>глубина́</i> (glubiná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) deep-water</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) remote</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) underlying</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) implicit, hidden</li></ol><p><i>глубина́</i> (glubiná, “depth”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep, profound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glǫbokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deep, deeply</li><li>profoundly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) deep-water; abyssal</li></ol><p><i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profundity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) dear, respected, highly esteemed (used as an honorific)</li></ol><p><i>глубо́ко</i> (glubóko, “deeply”) +&lrm; <i>уважа́емый</i> (uvažájemyj, “respected”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glǫbь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glewbʰ-</i>, whence English <i>cleave</i>, <i>cleft</i> and <i>glyph</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) synonym of <i>глумле́ние</i> (glumlénije)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glumъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to mock, to ridicule, to scorn, to jeer, to scoff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glumiti</i>. Compare Romanian <i>glumi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoff, to scorn, to mock</li></ol><p><i>глуми́ть</i> (glumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mockery, ridicule</li></ol><p><i>глуми́ть</i> (glumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mocking, derisive, jeering</li></ol><p><i>глуми́ть</i> (glumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get dumber, to become more stupid</li></ol><p><i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fool, blockhead, turkey, gander, dummy, dunce, idiot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>foolishly, stupidly</li></ol><p><i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid</li><li>sheepish</li><li>dim-witted</li></ol><p><i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupidity, foolishness, foolery, nonsense (quality of being stupid)</li><li>something stupid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glupostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj, “silly, stupid, foolish; inane”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness, -ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silly, stupid, foolish</li><li>inane, irrational, unreasonable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockhead, dolt, numskull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glupъ</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>глупав</i> (glupav), Serbo-Croatian <i>глу̏пав</i>/<i>glȕpav</i>, Slovak <i>hlúpy</i>, Slovene <i>glup</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Old Norse <i>glópr</i> (“idiot”) (compare Icelandic <i>glópur</i>). According to Erich Berneker and Uhlenbeck the Slavic words were borrowed from a Germanic source, which is contested by Vasmer.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silly fellow, naive kid, silly little thing</li><li>fulmar (seabird, genus <i>Fulmarus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>глуп(ый)</i> (glup(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš).</p><p>(<i>bird</i>): Compare typologically <i>о́луша</i> (óluša), <i>бакла́н</i> (baklán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) western capercaillie</li></ol><p><i>глуха́р(ь)</i> (gluxár(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) western capercaillie, wood grouse (<i>Tetrao urogallus</i>)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) sheltopusik (<i>Pseudopus apodus</i>)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) porcini mushroom (<i>Boletus edulis</i>)</li><li>(animate,&#32;low colloquial) deaf person</li><li>type of large screw with a hexagonal or square head</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;law enforcement&#32;slang) a case not likely to be solved</li></ol><p><i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young western capercaillie</li></ol><p><i>глуха́р(ь)</i> (gluxár(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deafly</li><li>dully, vaguely, in a muffled way (<i>sound</i>)</li><li>voicelessly (<i>consonant</i>)</li><li>out-of-the-way, remotely (<i>place</i>)</li><li>wildly (<i>forest</i>)</li></ol><p><i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat deaf</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) slightly muffled, quiet (<i>of a voice, sound, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deaf</li><li>dull, vague, muffled (<i>sound</i>)</li><li>(<i>phonology</i>) unvoiced, voiceless (<i>consonant</i>)</li><li>out-of-the-way, remote, god-forsaken (<i>place</i>)</li><li>overgrown, wild (<i>forest</i>)</li><li>dead-end</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) deaf person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gluxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick forest</li><li>wilderness, desolate place</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) backwater, podunk</li></ol><p>From <i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój, “remote, desolate”) + an unclear suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deaf-mute (unable to hear or speak)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) deaf-mute person</li></ol><p><i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój, “deaf”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>немо́й</i> (nemój, “mute”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deafness</li></ol><p><i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muffler (part of exhaust pipe)</li><li>silencer (an attachment to a gun)</li></ol><p><i>глуши́ть</i> (glušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stun</li><li>to deafen, to damp</li><li><i>(plants)</i> to choke</li><li><i>(motor, engine etc)</i> to stop</li><li>to stifle, to suppress</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to guzzle</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>глухо́й</i> (gluxój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialect</i>) (collective sense) wild shoots (of plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backwater, boondock, middle of nowhere, hinterland, backwoods</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(solid, hard) block, lump, burr, clod, chunk (such as of rock or ice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glyba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a hallucination, an illusion</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;computing</i>) glitch, bug (problem that needs fixing)</li></ol><p>From <i>галлюцина́ция</i> (galljucinácija, “hallucination”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glucose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) gluon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gluten (cereal protein)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>glūten</i> (whence also English <i>glue</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>clipping of <i>гляди́</i> (gljadí); look (interjection to attract attention)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staring contest (children's game)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look</li><li>to look after, to take care of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ględěti</i>; compare <i>&#42;glę̀dati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look, to gaze at one's reflection [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in a mirror, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be reflected in water</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look like [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look right</li></ol><p><i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>lo and behold!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polish, gloss, lustre, shine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Glanz</i>. Related to inherited глядеть and English glint.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glance, to give a brief look</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glossy</li></ol><p><i>гля́нец</i> (gljánec) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glacé (iced coffee with ice cream)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>glacé</i> (“icy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glaciology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hawthorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glogъ</i>, possibly via Ukrainian <i>глід</i> (hlid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectometer</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хм</i> (xm)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>гектоме́тр</i> (gektométr, “hectometer”).</p><p>Echoic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>H, henry</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>ге́нри</i> (génri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive</li><li>to distill</li><li>to chase, to pursue</li><li>to speed along</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to lie</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to give (<i>used in the imperative to make a forceful demand</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъnati</i>, with present tense conjugation forms from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goniti</i>, related to English <i>fend</i>; <i>defend</i>; <i>offend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) to send someone packing, to run out of town (to forcefully and roughly expel or chase away)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase, to pursue [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger; wrath</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gněvъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring a state of anger</li></ol><p>From <i>гнев</i> (gnev) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). Cognate with Polish <i>gniewać</i>, Slovak <i>hnevať</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry</li></ol><p><i>гнев</i> (gnev) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>angrily, wrathfully</li></ol><p><i>гне́вный</i> (gnévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, irate, wrathful</li></ol><p><i>гнев</i> (gnev) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chestnut (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chestnut-colored horse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnědъ</i>. Cognates include Czech <i>hnědý</i> and Slovak <i>hnedý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nest (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set up and live in a den (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to huddle together, to live in crowded conditions (<i>of people</i>)</li><li>to live in nests, to in large numbers (<i>of insects</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to take root, to be present (<i>of thoughts or feelings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest, aerie</li><li>socket, bezel</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) group of words that are related in some way</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>гнѣздо</i> (gnězdo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnězdo</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;nísdan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nisdós</i> (whence also Latin <i>nīdus</i>, English <i>nest</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nest, aerie</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) socket, bezel</li><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) group of words that are related in some way</li></ol><p>From <i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, to weigh down, to press</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnestì</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnet-</i> (“to press together”), from <i>&#42;gen-</i> (“to compress”). Cognate to English <i>knead</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distressing, depressing, oppressive (<i>of thoughts, feelings, silence, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) nit (louse egg)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) scumbag, rascal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gňida</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotting, putrefaction, decay</li></ol><p><i>гнить</i> (gnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a disease of bees</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rotten, putrid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) damp (<i>of the weather or climate</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) weak, infirm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gňilъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a direction whence a wet and cold wind comes, bringing fog and bad weather. This direction depends on the geolocation (e.g. on the Commander Islands it is the south-west, while in northern European Russia it is the north-west)</li></ol><p>Literally, “rotten corner”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rottenness, rot</li></ol><p><i>гнить</i> (gnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, to go bad, to putrefy, to decompose, to decay</li><li><i>teeth</i> to become carious</li><li><i>water</i> to stagnate</li></ol><p>From Late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gňìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy, to irritate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bully, to terrorize, to persecute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>гноби́ти</i> (hnobýty), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnobiti</i>. According to Pokorny, possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnet-</i>; compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnesti</i> (“to press”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnosis</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>γνῶσις</i> (gnôsis, “knowledge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let rot, to allow to decay</li><li>to rot <i>(someone in bad conditions)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pus</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) manure, dung</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abscess (cavity filled with pus)</li></ol><p><i>гной</i> (gnoj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гнойни́к</i> (gnojník): small abscess (cavity filled with pus)</li></ol><p><i>гнойни́к</i> (gnojník) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purulent</li></ol><p><i>гной</i> (gnoj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnome</li><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) a dwarf</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>gnomus</i>, used by Paracelsus as a synonym for <i>pygmaeus</i> (“pygmy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnome (saying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dwarf, gnome</li></ol><p><i>гном</i> (gnom) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnomon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnoseology (the philosophical study of knowledge)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>гнозис</i> (gnozis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gnostic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gnosticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gnostic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnu, wildebeest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gnu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) mosquitoes, midges, horseflies and other bloodsucking insects</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnusъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) to speak with changed timbre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) nasal (having a sound imparted by means of the nose)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abominably, detestably</li></ol><p><i>гну́сный</i> (gnúsnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infamy, heinousness</li><li>heinous act</li></ol><p><i>гну́сный</i> (gnúsnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abominable, hideous, detestable, odious, vile</li><li>mean, villainous (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnusьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foul thing, dirty thing(s), rot, filth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve</li><li>to drive at, to aim at</li><li>to stubbornly insist on or state one's opinion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bent, to be curved, to bend (intransitive)</li></ol><p><i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disdain, to abhor, to shun, to loathe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гнушати сѧ</i> (gnušati sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnušati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гну́с(ный)</i> (gnús(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó, “nest”)</li></ol><p><i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press, weight</li><li>oppression, yoke</li><li>weight <i>(of)</i>, being oppressed <i>(with / by)</i></li></ol><p>Deverbal noun from <i>гнести́</i> (gnestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гнёздышко</i> (gnjózdyško).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гнев</i> (gnev).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>go (<i>board game</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>碁</i> (ご, go).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoatzin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gobelin, tapestry, arras (wall hanging)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gobelin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goblin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oboe</li></ol><p>From English <i>hautboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) to fast and attend church services in preparation for communion</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to respect, to esteem, to honor highly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gověti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;rare) a member of a punk and rock-music subculture in Russia but who despises other styles and foreign music</li></ol><p>From <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “faeces, shit”) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>govnar</i>, Polish <i>gówniarz</i>, and Macedonian <i>гомнар</i> (gomnar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit</li></ol><p><i>говн(о́)</i> (govn(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (of a person) the lowly character flaw, usually enviousness</li></ol><p><i>говни́стый</i> (govnístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shitty, disgusting, nasty (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>говно́</i> (govnó) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) shit, feces, especially when liquid and in a large amount</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) something of exceptionally poor quality</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) very bad person</li></ol><p><i>говно́</i> (govnó) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;vulgar) shit</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit, shithead, shitass, turd (worthless person)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit, crap, junk (worthless thing)</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;vulgar) bullshit (nonsense)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>говьно</i> (govĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govьno</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gawāˀ</i>, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-воз</i> (-voz), combining form of <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ, “to convey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;literal) a shiteater, a scatophage</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) bastard, an unpleasant person</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) an omnivorous person who does not care about the quality of their food</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) a person with poor artistic taste who does not care about the quality of their favourite/favorite media</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) a dog that rolls and smears its body with feces</li></ol><p><i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”). Compare Ukrainian <i>гівної́д</i> (hivnojíd, “shiteater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;usually in singular) rock music of poor artistic value or low quality.</li></ol><p>From <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “faeces, shit”) +&lrm; <i>рок</i> (rok, “rock music”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) stinker, shithead, bugger, asshole</li></ol><p><i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-юк</i> (-juk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the sound of talking, the sound of voices, the sound of speech</li><li>murmur</li><li>dialect, patois, regionalism</li><li>pronunciation, accent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) talking-shop</li></ol><p><i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) orator, speaker (someone who speaks)</li></ol><p>From <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ, “speak”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, to talk (perfective поговорить)</li><li>to say, to tell (perfective сказать)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govoriti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewH-</i> (“to call, cry”) with a <i>&#42;-b⁽ʰ⁾-</i> enlargement, perhaps connected to the synonymous <i>&#42;ǵʰewH-</i>. Cognate to Polish <i>gaworzyć</i>, Czech <i>hovořit</i>, Ukrainian <i>говорити</i> (hovoryty), Serbo-Croatian <i>гово̀рити</i>, Persian گفتن (goftan, “to say, to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) (<i>expressing something to talk about</i>)</li></ol><p><i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, chatty, loquacious, garrulous</li><li>boisterous, noisy (<i>of a crowd</i>)</li><li>burbling, babbling (<i>of water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) manner of speech</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dialect, accent</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) murmur</li><li>(dated,&#32;animate,&#32;colloquial) chatterbox, windbag</li><li>(dated,&#32;animate,&#32;regional) lawyer</li></ol><p><i>го́вор</i> (góvor) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talker, chatterbox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chatterbox</li></ol><p>From <i>говор(и́ть)</i> (govor(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>го́вор</i> (góvor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talking, speaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beef (meat of a cow or bull)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govędina</i>, from <i>&#42;govędo</i> (“a head of cattle”). By surface analysis, <i>говя́до</i> (govjádo, “a head of cattle”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina, suffix denoting a type of meat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>говя́дина</i> (govjádina, “beef”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;dialectal,&#32;collective,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) domestic bovine animals, cattle (<i>cows, oxen, bulls</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govędo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷew-n̥d-</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷṓws</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beef</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govęďь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>говя́до</i> (govjádo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shitty, crappy, lousy</li></ol><p><i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gogol; Gogol's (related to Nikolai Gogol)</li></ol><p><i>Го́голь</i> (Gógolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Common goldeneye (<i>Bucephala clangula</i>), a kind of duck.</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>гоголь</i> (gogolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gogoľь</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Гоголь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gogol</li></ol><p>From <i>го́голь</i> (gógolʹ, “common goldeneye, <i>Bucephala clangula</i>”), from Old East Slavic <i>гоголь</i> (gogolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gogolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cackle, honk (the sounds a goose makes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) loud laughter, roars of laughter, guffaw</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>гогота́ть</i> (gogotátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cackle (<i>of geese</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roar with laughter</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>годъ</i> (godŭ, “time, period, year, age”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;godъ</i> (“suitable time”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gadás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰodʰo-</i>, from <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i> (“to unite, be associated, be suitable”).</p><p>Cognates include</p><p>Ukrainian <i>годі</i> (hodi, “enough”),</p><p>Bulgarian <i>годе</i> (gode, “ever”) (as in кой-годе (koj-gode) = whoever),</p><p>Czech <i>hod</i>,</p><p>Polish <i>gody</i> (“yearly feast”),</p><p>Upper Sorbian <i>hody</i> (“Christmas”),</p><p>Lower Sorbian <i>gódy</i> (“Christmas”),</p><p>Latvian <i>gadigs</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include</p><p>German <i>Gatte</i> (“husband”), <i>gätlich</i> (“convenient”),</p><p>Dutch <i>gegade</i>, <i>gade</i>, <i>gading</i>,</p><p>Dutch <i>gader</i>, <i>goed</i>,</p><p>German <i>gut</i>, as well as English <i>gather</i>, <i>together</i> and <i>good</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for years; for years on end</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for consumption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>год</i> (god): year</li></ol><p><i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) time</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) year</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;godina</i>. Equivalent to <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to wait</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to delay</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to loiter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;godìti</i> (“to please”). The original meaning is preserved in the derivative <i>угоди́ть</i> (ugodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suit</li><li>to be fit (for), to be of use</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;godìti sę</i>. By surface analysis, <i>годи́ть</i> (godítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) year; year's</li><li>annual, yearly</li></ol><p><i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fitness (of something for some purpose), suitability; validity</li></ol><p>From <i>го́дный</i> (gódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Cognate with Belarusian <i>го́днасць</i> (hódnascʹ), and, more distantly, with English <i>goodness</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, suitable, well-suited, good for, valid, appropriate [<i>with</i> <b>для</b> (dlja, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘for/to something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) nice, legit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>годьныи</i> (godĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;godьnъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i> (“to unite, be associated, suit”). Cognate with English <i>good</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-year-old</li></ol><p><i>годово́й</i> (godovój) +&lrm; <i>-а́лый</i> (-ályj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annual (happening once a year)</li></ol><p><i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anniversary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hodograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>год</i> (god, “year”)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate) contemporary</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) soldier who has served a full year</li></ol><p><i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>год</i> (god).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;hyperbolic) An unusually long time.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goy, gentile, non-Jew.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic,&#32;with <i>ты</i> (ty)) Used as an exclamation, greeting or solemn address&#59; original literal meaning&#58; abundance, peaceful life.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>גוי</i> (goy, “gentile”), from Hebrew <i>גּוֹי</i> (góy, “nation”).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гои</i> (goi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;folklore) be alive, be healthy (greeting or formula of praise)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;slang,&#32;humorous) approving dashing greeting; call for immediate action, also used as a battle cry; come on</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) An interjection allegedly used during the reign of Ivan the Terrible to celebrate the executions.</li></ol><p>Originally a variant spelling of Russian <i>айда</i> (ajda), also compare Ukrainian <i>гайда</i> (hajda). Originally the word was supposed to have stress on the second syllable, but was later reanalyzed as having stress on the initial syllable. Ultimately from a Turkic language; compare Ottoman Turkish هایده (hayde).</p><p>The word is attested in foreign sources since the XVI century.</p><p>First appears in Russian literature around 19th century, possibly a learned borrowing from the term in the foreign source.</p><p>The word was then reborrowed into spoken language with a positive meaning and used in mostly humorous contexts.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) goal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>goal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) absolutely poor (but crafty, resourceful, street-smart)</li></ol><p>Literally, “naked as a battering ram”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Golan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) see брю́ква (brjúkva)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) see бакла́га (baklága)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;sports) goal</li></ol><p><i>гол</i> (gol) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) assist (a pass that leads directly to scoring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golem</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>גולם \ גֹּלֶם</i> (gólem).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bootleg, top of a (high) boot</li></ol><p><i>го́лень</i> (gólenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) shin, shank</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>голѣнь</i> (golěnĭ), <i>голень</i> (golenĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golěnь</i> / <i>&#42;golenь</i> (“shin”), from <i>&#42;golъ</i> (“naked”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loach</li><li>char (any of the several species of fishes of the genus <i>Salvelinus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) sheer, utter</li><li>(<i>argotic</i>) bad</li></ol><p><i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj). Meaning “bad” is later.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;East,&#32;Siberia) loud noise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъlkъ</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>глъч</i> (glǎč) and <i>глъчка</i> (glǎčka, “tumult”), Slovene <i>golk</i>, Slovak <i>hlučný</i> (“noisy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dutchman</li></ol><p><i>Голла́ндия</i> (Gollándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocklestove (masonry heater)</li></ol><p><i>Голла́ндия</i> (Gollándija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dutch</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Dutch language (short for голла́ндский язы́к (gollándskij jazýk))</li></ol><p><i>Голла́ндия</i> (Gollándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a perfect, white, and straight smile</li></ol><p>Literally, Hollywood smile.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hollywood</li></ol><p><i>Голливу́д</i> (Gollivúd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li><li>mind; brains</li><li>head; chief; master</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>голова</i> (golova), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golva</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;galˀwā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelH-</i>. Doublet of <i>глава́</i> (glavá) (Church Slavonicism).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tadpole, polliwog</li></ol><p>From <i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) big-headed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clever, smart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ember, firebrand</li><li>Amur sleeper (<i>Perccottus glenii</i>)</li></ol><p><i>голов(ня́)</i> (golov(njá)) +&lrm; <i>-е́шка</i> (-éška)</p><p><i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-е́шка</i> (-éška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>голова́</i> (golová): small head</li><li>head (of pin, hammer, nail, etc.)</li><li>bulb, clove (onion, garlic, etc.)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) glans</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ellipsis of <i>голо́вка полово́го чле́на</i> (golóvka polovóvo čléna, “glans penis”)</li><li>ellipsis of <i>голо́вка кли́тора</i> (golóvka klítora, “clitoral glans”)</li></ol></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) clitoral glans</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) glans penis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headache, cephalalgia</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) pain in the neck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) head</li><li>main, principal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golvьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) brain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headgear, headdress (anything worn on the head, single item)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) brand (piece of wood)</li><li>smut (range of fungi)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golvьňa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giddiness, dizziness, vertigo</li></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>круже́ние</i> (kružénije, “whirling, spinning”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dizzying, giddying (causing dizziness or a feeling of unsteadiness)</li></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кружи́ть</i> (kružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puzzle, brain-teaser</li></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ, “to break”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>puzzling, intricate, tricky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dressing-down (reprimand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cephalopod (type of mollusk)</li></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>голова́</i> (golová): small head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daredevil</li><li>thug, cutthroat</li></ol><p>From <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-рез</i> (-rez), combining form of <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”). Compare with Macedonian <i>главорез</i> (glavorez) and Ukrainian <i>головорі́з</i> (holovoríz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>голова́</i> (golová): small head</li></ol><p><i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to talk nonsense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golgoliti</i>, from <i>&#42;golgolъ</i>. Related to глаго́л (glagól), глаголать (glagolatʹ), from Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hologram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger</li><li>famine</li><li>starvation</li><li>dearth, shortage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>голодъ</i> (golodŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goldъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>गृध्र</i> (gṛ́dhra, “desiring greedily”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starvation, famishment</li><li>fasting</li></ol><p><i>голода́ть</i> (golodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starve, to go hungry</li><li>to fast, to go without food</li><li>to go on a hunger strike</li></ol><p><i>го́лод</i> (gólod) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry, starving</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) famine, starveling, starvation, hunger</li><li>meagre, scanty, poor</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hungry person, starving person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goldьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́лод</i> (gólod, “hunger”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Cognate with Belarusian <i>гало́дны</i> (halódny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger strike</li><li>starvation; famine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger striker</li></ol><p>From <i>голодо́вка</i> (golodóvka) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a famine with mass deaths, especially one of the Soviet famines in 1921–22, 1932–33, or 1946–47</li><li>Holodomor (Ukrainian famine of 1932–33)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>голодомо́р</i> (holodomór). Compare <i>го́лод</i> (gólod, “hunger”) and <i>мор</i> (mor, “plague, pestilence”), <i>мори́ть</i> (morítʹ, “to kill, to exterminate”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Голодомор)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holodomor (Ukrainian famine of 1932-33)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Голодомо́р</i> (Holodomór). Compare <i>го́лод</i> (gólod, “hunger”) and <i>мор</i> (mor, “plague, pestilence”), <i>мори́ть</i> (morítʹ, “to kill, to exterminate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beggar, ragamuffin</li></ol><p><i>голо-</i> (golo-) +&lrm; <i>дран</i> (dran) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) (prolonged) hunger, starvation</li></ol><p><i>го́лод</i> (gólod) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) nudibranchian, belonging to or characteristic of the Nudibranchia or nudibranchs.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;zoology) nudibranch</li></ol><p>From <i>го́лый</i> (gólyj, “nude, naked”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, vocalic interfix) +&lrm; <i>жа́бра</i> (žábra, “gill(s)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix), literally “naked-gilled”, a calque of translingual <i>Nudibranchia</i>, (from <i>nūdus</i> (“naked, nude”) + <i>branchia</i> (“gill(s)”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice-covered ground, black ice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glaze (layer of ice formed by freezing rain)</li></ol><p><i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лёд</i> (ljod)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golomyanka (either of two species of fish of the family Cottidae, endemic to Lake Baikal in Siberia (<i>Comephorus baicalensis</i> and <i>Comephorus dybowskii</i>))</li></ol><p><i>голо́мя</i> (golómja, “open water”) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golъ</i> (“bare”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bare-handed</li></ol><p><i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>voice, word, remark, opinion</li><li>vote</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>голосъ</i> (golosŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golsъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;galsás</i>. Doublet of <i>глас</i> (glas), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic without pleophony.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud-voiced, vociferous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golsiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfounded, unsubstantiated</li></ol><p>Most likely comes from Polish gołosłowny from the combination goły + słowo.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voting, poll, polling</li><li>hitchhiking <i>(from raising a hand as if voting)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>голосова́ть</i> (golosovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vote (assert a formalised choice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golsovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice mail (system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice leading</li></ol><p><i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>веде́ние</i> (vedénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice message</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) voice; vocal</li></ol><p><i>го́лос</i> (gólos, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) voice message</li></ol><p><i>голосово́е</i> (golosovóje) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocal cords</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́лос</i> (gólos): (high or soft) voice</li></ol><p><i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) deficit, the absence of something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;in the plural,&#32;genitive case,&#32;"голубых кровей") blue blood (noble or aristocratic ancestry)</li><li>household name of the pharmaceutical drug Перфторан (perftoran, perfluorane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azure-winged magpie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dove breeder, pigeon breeder</li></ol><p>From <i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ, “dove, pigeon”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A letter delivered by carrier pigeon.</li></ol><p><i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ, “pigeon, dove”) +&lrm; <i>-гра́мма</i> (-grámma, “-gram”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queer, fag</li></ol><p><i>голубо́й</i> (golubój) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij). Formally a diminutive.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show blue</li><li>to turn blue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn gay <i>(homosexual)</i></li></ol><p><i>голубо́й</i> (golubój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blueness, sky-blue colour/color</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) homosexuality</li></ol><p>From <i>голуб(о́й)</i> (golub(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-изна́</i> (-izná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bog bilberry, bog blueberry, northern bilberry, western blueberry, (<i>Vaccinium uliginosum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>голубо́й</i> (golubój) +&lrm; <i>-ика</i> (-ika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bog bilberry, bog blueberry, northern bilberry, western blueberry</li></ol><p><i>голуби́ка</i> (golubíka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dove (female), small pigeon (female)</li><li>(my) dear, darling, honey</li></ol><p>From <i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ, “dove, pigeon”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bluish</li></ol><p><i>голубо́й</i> (golubój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue-eyed</li></ol><p><i>голубо́й</i> (golubój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light blue, sky blue, azure (contrasts with <i>си́ний</i> (sínij, “dark blue, indigo”))</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ideal</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;derogatory) gay, homosexual (mostly related to male homosexuals, i.e. not lesbian)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;nominalized) gay man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>голѫбъ</i> (golǫbŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golǫbъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ, “pigeon, dove”) +&lrm; <i>-о́й</i> (-ój); the sense refers to the bluish tint of the bird's neck plumage.</p><p>The usage for "homosexual" was derived from a slang word used by gay men during the 1960's, referring to the pigeons in Sverdlov Square in Moscow where gay men used to meet secretly. The usage was also derived from a song in the 1951 film "We are for peace" (Мы за мир), "Fly, pigeons" (Летите, голуби) by Mikhail Matusovsky, where the first lines "fly, pigeons, fly" (Летите, голуби, летите) were used to warn gay men about a coming police or KGB raid.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ): young pigeon, dove</li><li>(<i>familiar&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) (to a man) darling, darl, sweetheart, dear, honey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>голу́бка</i> (golúbka): (my) dear, darling, honey</li></ol><p><i>голу́б(ка)</i> (golúb(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-онька</i> (-onʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>голу́бка</i> (golúbka)</li><li>(<i>endearing</i>) darling, honey, my dear (<i>to a female</i>)</li></ol><p><i>голу́б(ка)</i> (golúb(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golubtsy, cabbage rolls</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>my dear (fellow), my friend; my lad (can be ironical)</li></ol><p><i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ, “dove”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dove, pigeon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golǫbь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squab (meat)</li></ol><p>From <i>голубя́т-</i> (golubját-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>голубёнок</i> (golubjónok, “squab”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dovecote</li></ol><p>From <i>голубя́т-</i> (golubját-) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), the former being the plural stem of <i>голубёнок</i> (golubjónok, “squab”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked, nude, bare</li><li>poor, miserable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelH-</i> (“naked, bald”), whence English <i>callow</i>. Compare Polish <i>goły</i> and German <i>kahl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) the poor</li></ol><p>From a root akin to <i>го́лый</i> (gólyj, “bare, naked”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stark naked (completely unclothed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collectively</i>) the poor, poor people</li><li>poverty</li><li>waste, wasteland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holmium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) golf</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) knee sock, knee high</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>golf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golfer</li></ol><p><i>гольф</i> (golʹf) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female golfer</li></ol><p>From <i>гольфи́ст</i> (golʹfíst) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) golf</li></ol><p><i>гольф</i> (golʹf) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Kursk) branch, bough</li></ol><p>Akin to Old Armenian <i>կողր</i> (kołr, “branch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar, pauper</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the absence of something</li><li>galyak</li></ol><p><i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́лень</i> (gólenʹ): shank</li><li>diminutive of <i>голени́ще</i> (goleníšče): bootleg</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an undressed woman</li></ol><p><i>го́лень</i> (gólenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p><p><i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeopathy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Homeric</li></ol><p><i>Гоме́р</i> (Gomér) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) homo, gay</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>гомосексуали́ст</i> (gomosɛksualíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hominoid (a great tailless ape (including humans and apes))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) shit, usually human</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) a despicable person, shithead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govьno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homo sapiens</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>Homo sapiens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hubbub</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gomonъ</i> (“noise”). Possibly formed from the onomatopoeic <i>гом</i> (gom, “cry, noise”), preserved in some dialects as <i>гам</i> (gam). The Proto-Slavic etymon is possibly related to or, alternatively, borrowed from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamaną</i> (“game, fun, amusement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce a hubbub, to rumble (<i>of a crowd</i>)</li><li>to talk loudly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gomoniti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́мон</i> (gómon) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>го́мон</i> (gómon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) homosexual, faggot, fag, queer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sex between homosexuals, homosex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homosexual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homosexualism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homosexual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homosexuality</li></ol><p>From <i>гомосексуа́льный</i> (gomosɛksuálʹnyj, “homosexual”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homosexual</li></ol><p><i>гомосексуа́л</i> (gomosɛksuál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homophobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homunculus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) globe amaranth (<i>Gomphrena</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Gomphrena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) pursuit, chase</li><li>group of people involved in a chase</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) rut</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) lies</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) grad (one-hundredth of a right angle)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гонъ</i> (gonŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gonъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰónos</i> (compare Ancient Greek <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”)), <i>o</i>-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰen-</i> (whence Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъnàti</i>, 1sg. <i>&#42;ženǫ</i>, whence Russian <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)).</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γονία</i> (gonía, “angle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gonad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gondola (<i>in all senses</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>gondola</i>, from Venetan <i>góndoła</i>, probably from Byzantine Greek <i>κοντούρα</i> (kontoúra, “small tail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) condom, rubber</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) dickhole, an asshole, a disliked person</li></ol><p>Distortion of <i>кондо́м</i> (kondóm), from English <i>condom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Honduran</li></ol><p><i>Гондура́с</i> (Gondurás) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courier, messenger; herald</li><li>Gonets (a Russian civilian low Earth orbit communications satellite system)</li></ol><p><i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>persecuted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Goniolimon</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Goniolimon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) racing, race</li><li>rush</li></ol><p><i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arms race (a competition for military supremacy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race (a contest between people, animals, vehicles, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hong Konger</li></ol><p>From <i>Гонконг</i> (Gonkong, “Hong Kong”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to Hong Kong, Hongkongese.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) honor, dignity</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) arrogance, haughtiness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>honor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fee; honorarium, honorary; royalties</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Honorar</i>, eventually from Latin <i>honōrārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) honorific</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) gonorrhea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) race (competition), racing</li></ol><p><i>го́нка</i> (gónka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shake (type of wooden shingle)</li><li>shingled roof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>горньчаръ</i> (gornĭčarŭ), from <i>гърньчаръ</i> (gŭrnĭčarŭ), from <i>гърньць</i> (gŭrnĭcĭ, “pot”) (whence <i>горнец</i> (gornec, “pot”)). Compare <i>га́рнец</i> (gárnec) (via Polish), <i>горн</i> (gorn), <i>горни́ло</i> (gornílo), <i>горшо́к</i> (goršók) (all akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)).</p><p>For the <i>р</i> falling compare <i>кочега́р</i> (kočegár) (&lt; <i>кочерга́</i> (kočergá)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pottery workshop</li></ol><p><i>гонча́р</i> (gončár) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beagle, hound, foxhound (dog)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trained to chase prey (<i>of dogs</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гон-</i> (gon-) +&lrm; <i>-чий</i> (-čij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racer</li></ol><p><i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pursuit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive</li><li>to distill</li><li>to chase, to pursue</li><li>to speed along</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gaňati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰen-</i> (“to kill”). Cognate with Albanian <i>gjan</i> (“to hunt”), Lithuanian <i>giñti</i>, Old Norse <i>gunnr</i> (“fight”), Latin <i>dēfendō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>θείνω</i> (theínō), Armenian <i>գան</i> (gan, “beating, blows”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase, to pursue [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) go!, hop! encouraging someone to jump, hop, or leap, or exclaimed while jumping or leaping.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>), (<i>archaic</i>) bam; representing a sudden, abrupt motion, e.g. striking, grabbing, etc.</li></ol><p>Probably onomatopoeic; compare Ukrainian <i>гоп</i> (hop), Polish <i>hop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) mugging, street robbery, heist, hold-up, stickup (when a few people, some го́пники (gópniki), approach a victim on the street and take their money and valuables)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, hop, or leap while shouting excitedly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shout <i>«гоп»&#33;</i> («gop»&#33;)</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;obsolete) to loaf around, to loiter</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;obsolete) to sleep rough</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;transitive) to accost, to rob, to mug</li></ol><p><i>гоп</i> (gop) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p><p>Unclear. Attested by Dal in an 1842 manuscript on the Saint Petersburg criminal argot.</p><p>Folk etymology connects the term to the acronym for <i>Городско́е о́бщество призо́ра</i> (Gorodskóje óbščestvo prizóra), the name of a former Saint Petersburg orphanage and workhouse for street children.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free-and-easy gathering; riff-raff crew</li></ol><p><i>гоп</i> (gop) +&lrm; <i>компа́ния</i> (kompánija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gopnik (a member of the Russian urban criminal subculture&#59; a young hoodlum&#59; a member of a street gang)</li><li>a young bully; a rowdy youth; a young tough</li><li>an aggressive, poorly educated and ill-mannered person, even if not so young; a redneck</li></ol><p><i>го́пать</i> (gópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) or <i>гоп</i> (gop) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p><p>Folk etymology connects the term to the acronym for <i>Городско́е о́бщество призо́ра</i> (Gorodskóje óbščestvo prizóra), the name of a former Saint Petersburg orphanage and workhouse for street children.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gopnik</li></ol><p><i>го́пник</i> (gópnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The state of being a gopnik</li></ol><p>гопник (<i>gopnik</i>) + -ество (<i>-estvo</i>)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) the gopniks</li></ol><p>From <i>го́пник</i> (gópnik) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goeppertia (<i>Goeppertia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Goeppertia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li><li>heap, pile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gora</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;garā́ˀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) skilful; capable (<i>of doing something</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorazdъ</i>. Per Vasmer, an ancient borrowing from Gothic, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + <i>&#42;razdō</i> (“voice, sound”); originally "speaking intelligently".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much, far, by far, a lot, considerably, quite [<i>with</i> complement]</li></ol><p><i>гора́зд</i> (gorázd) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hump, hunch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гърбъ</i> (gŭrbŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrbъ</i> (“hump”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work a lot and hard</li><li>to be in a crouched position</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humpbacked, hunchbacked (having a hump on the back)</li><li>humpy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrbatъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) humpback (humpbacked person)</li><li>humpback whale</li></ol><p>From <i>горб</i> (gorb, “hump, hunch”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gorbachev; Gorbachev's (especially referring to Mikhail Gorbachev)</li></ol><p><i>Горбачёв</i> (Gorbačóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hump, bump</li><li>hillock, hummock</li><li>augmentative of <i>горб</i> (gorb, “hump”)</li></ol><p><i>го́рбить</i> (górbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p><p><i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bump (<i>on a nose</i>)</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>горби́на</i> (gorbína, “hump”)</li></ol><p><i>горби́на</i> (gorbína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hunch</li></ol><p><i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hunch over</li></ol><p><i>го́рбить</i> (górbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) humpback (humpbacked person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pink salmon, humpback salmon (<i>Oncorhynchus gorbuscha</i>)</li></ol><p><i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-уша</i> (-uša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crust, heel (end-piece of a loaf of bread)</li></ol><p><i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bump (<i>on wood</i>), half-beam, half-log, slab</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) croaker (type of fish), weakfish</li></ol><p><i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-ыль</i> (-ylʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sciaenidae (a biological family that includes weakfish and yellow croakers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorgonzola</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>gorgonzola</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;inanimate) gargoyle (carved grotesque figure on a spout)</li><li>(fantasy,&#32;animate) gargoyle (winged monster with a body of living stone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, haughty, proud (having excessive pride)</li></ol><p>Presumably from Old East Slavic <i>гърдѣти</i> (gŭrděti, “to be proud”) (also <i>гордѣти</i> (gorděti)) + <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj). Compare <i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj), <i>горди́ться</i> (gordítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proud man</li></ol><p>From <i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be proud [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>proudly</li></ol><p><i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p><i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud</li><li>majestic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrdъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance, pride (act or habit of arrogating, or making undue claims in an overbearing manner)</li></ol><p><i>го́рдый</i> (górdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) grief, distress, sadness</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) trouble</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) misfortune, disaster</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>placed before a word to express one's view that something or someone is not worthy of being called as such; so-called, poor excuse for</li><li>(literary,&#32;obsolete) up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goře</i>. Akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ, “to burn, to be consumed by fire”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>печа́ль</i> (pečálʹ) (akin to <i>печь</i> (pečʹ, “to bake”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) sad sack, double-trouble, blunderer, muddler (ironically about someone who is having trouble or causing trouble to others)</li></ol><p>Literally, “onion tragedy”, i.e. in tears over nothing, usually of a child who frets about nothing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criticaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grieve <i>(for)</i>, to mourn <i>(over, for)</i></li></ol><p><i>го́ре</i> (góre) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burner (of a gas range, heater or light using liquid or gas fuel).</li><li>The nozzle of a welding torch.</li><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) vodka, usually in a Ukrainian context.</li></ol><p><i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>горі́лка</i> (horílka). Akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ, “to burn”), Ukrainian <i>горі́ти</i> (horíty, “to burn”). Similar to Belarusian <i>гарэ́лка</i> (harélka). Note <i>гора́льник</i> (gorálʹnik) (&lt; <i>гора́ть</i> (gorátʹ), a causative of <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)) (compare typologically <i>виноку́р</i> (vinokúr) <+ <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>brandy</i> (whence <i>бре́нди</i> (bréndi)), akin to English <i>bren</i>, <i>burn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race-and-catch (a Russian outdoor game)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt</li></ol><p><i>горе́(ть)</i> (goré(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sculpture</i>) high relief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>haut-relief</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) high relief</li></ol><p><i>горелье́ф</i> (gorelʹjéf) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) poor, miserable creature, wretch</li></ol><p><i>го́ре</i> (góre) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miserable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, combustion</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) enthusiasm</li></ol><p><i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́рница</i> (górnica): (small) room, chamber; cenacle; ben</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sorrowfully, sadly</li></ol><p><i>го́рестный</i> (górestnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorrowful, sad, mournful</li><li>pitiful</li></ol><p><i>го́ресть</i> (górestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow, grief</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gořestь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn (to be consumed by fire)</li><li>to be lit, to be on (<i>of lights</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to burn (<i>with emotion</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to burn, to sting (<i>of a wound</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to redden, to flush (<i>of the cheeks, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to gleam, to shine (<i>of the eyes, the dawn, etc.</i>)</li><li>to rot from heat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be pressing, urgent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorěti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;garḗˀtei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highlander</li><li>knotweed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorьcь</i>, substantivisation of a variant of <i>&#42;gorьkъ</i> with progressive palatalisation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гореча́вка</i> (gorečávka)</li><li>gentianella (<i>Gentianella</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Gentianella</i>, as <i>гореча́вка</i> (gorečávka, “gentian”) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitterness, bitter taste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorьčь</i>, nominalization of <i>&#42;gorьkъ</i> (“bitter”). Compare Kashubian <i>gòrëcz</i> and Polish <i>gorycz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horizon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὁρίζων</i> (horízōn). Doublet of <i>ао́рист</i> (aórist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrophysics</i>) event horizon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horizontal (line)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) rank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>horizontally (in a horizontal direction or position)</li></ol><p><i>горизонта́льный</i> (gorizontálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horizontalness</li></ol><p>From <i>горизонта́льн(ый)</i> (gorizontálʹn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horizontal, level</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>горе́лка</i> (gorélka, “vodka”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>горі́лка</i> (horílka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) gorilla (animal)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hunk, muscleman, beefy guy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) city executive committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>городско́й исполко́м</i> (gorodskój ispolkóm), in turn acronym of <i>городско́й исполни́тельный комите́т</i> (gorodskój ispolnítelʹnyj komitét, “city executive committee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mountainous</li></ol><p><i>гора́</i> (gorá) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) redstart (<i>Phoenicurus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гора́</i> (gorá): hill, hillock</li><li>slide (an item of play equipment that children can climb up and then slide down again)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) steep climb</li><li>(dated,&#32;furniture) china cabinet, display case, vitrine</li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) wall unit</li></ol><p><i>гора́</i> (gorá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city committee, municipality</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>городско́й комите́т</i> (gorodskój komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak very loud or sing at the top of one's lungs</li></ol><p>From <i>го́рло</i> (górlo, “throat”) + an unclear suffix + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtledove, collared dove, Eurasian collared dove</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtledove</li><li>collared dove</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrdlica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat</li><li>gullet</li><li>neck (of a vessel)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гърло</i> (gŭrlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrdlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) throat doctor, laryngologist</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person with a sore throat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) throat singer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth, orifice, neck (<i>e.g. of a sweater</i>)</li></ol><p><i>го́рло</i> (górlo, “throat”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) throat</li><li>guttural, deep-throat</li></ol><p><i>го́рло</i> (górlo) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khrenovina (a condiment made from shredded tomatoes, horseradish, garlic and salt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́рло</i> (górlo)</li><li>neck (the tapered part of a bottle toward the opening)</li></ol><p><i>го́рло</i> (górlo) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxtongue (<i>Picris</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hormone (substance produced by the body that affects physiological activity)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ὁρμῶν</i> (hormôn), present participle of <i>ὁρμάω</i> (hormáō, “to set in motion, urge on”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;physiology) hormone; hormonal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>hormonal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fireplace, hearth</li><li>forge, furnace</li><li>bugle, clarion, horn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrnъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain cavy (<i>Microcavia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>го́рная</i> (górnaja, “mountain, rock”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>сви́нка</i> (svínka, “guinea pig”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) heavenly, celestial</li></ol><p><i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) forging furnace, hearth</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) crucible, a place of severe test or trial</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грънило</i> (grŭnilo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrnidlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) room, chamber; cenacle; ben, ben-room</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) guest room, spare room</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>горьнь</i> (gorĭnĭ) + <i>-ица</i> (-ica); cognate with <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain, heap”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maid, parlourmaid, chambermaid</li></ol><p><i>го́рница</i> (górnica) +&lrm; <i>-ная</i> (-naja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mining and smelting</li></ol><p><i>го́рный</i> (górnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>металлурги́ческий</i> (metallurgíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mining</li></ol><p><i>го́рный</i> (górnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>добыва́ющий</i> (dobyvájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alpine skiing, skiing, ski</li></ol><p><i>го́рный</i> (górnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лы́жи</i> (lýži) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpine skiing (sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ermine (in winter), stoat (in summer)</li><li>ermine fur</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gornostajь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpine skis</li><li>alpine skiing (sport)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mountain</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rock</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mining</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorьnъ</i>; analyzable as <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) burramys (<i>Burramys parvus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>го́рный</i> (górnyj, “mountain, rocky”) +&lrm; <i>куску́с</i> (kuskús, “cuscus”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock crystal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miner (a person who works in a mine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city, town</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a bigger part of a city, including the downtown and inner city areas (as opposed to the suburbs and outskirts)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>го́родъ</i> (górodŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gȏrdъ</i>. Doublet of <i>град</i> (grad), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) Hero City (Soviet award to cities that suffered greatly during World War II)</li></ol><p><i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>геро́й</i> (gerój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk nonsense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorditi</i>, from <i>&#42;gordъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;derogatory</i>) townlet; burg; a (small) town</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>го́род</i> (górod, “city”)</li><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) fortified settlement</li><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) ruins of a fortified settlement</li></ol><p><i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorodki (an ancient Russian folk sport)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>городки́</i> (gorodkí), plural of <i>городо́к</i> (gorodók, “townlet”), diminutive of <i>го́род</i> (górod, “town, city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) governor, mayor of an uyezd city</li></ol><p><i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>городско́й</i> (gorodskój, “city (<i>relational</i>), urban”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Russian Empire) city policeman</li></ol><p><i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>го́род</i> (górod): small city, town</li></ol><p><i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>городо́к</i> (gorodók): (small) town</li></ol><p><i>городо́к</i> (gorodók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) town, city; urban, municipal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gordьskъ</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>го́род</i> (górod).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>townsman, city dweller, urbanite (male resident of a town)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>горожанинъ</i> (gorožaninŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorďaninъ</i>. Doublet of <i>граждани́н</i> (graždanín), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horoscope (astrological forecast)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peas</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) very small fruit, tubers, etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>горохъ</i> (goroxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pea, peas</li><li>pea-green</li></ol><p><i>горо́х</i> (goróx) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>горо́х</i> (goróx): (small) peas</li><li>polka dots</li><li>tare, vetch (the name of any plants in the <i>Vicia</i> genus)</li></ol><p><i>горо́х</i> (goróx) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) hairy vetch (<i>Vicia villosa</i>)</li></ol><p><i>горо́шек</i> (goróšek, “vetch”) +&lrm; <i>мохна́тый</i> (moxnátyj, “hairy”), a calque of translingual <i>Vicia villosa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pea (<i>seed</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) something round and small resembling a pea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of pea soup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) highlander; highland</li></ol><p><i>гора́</i> (gorá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city council, municipal council</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>городско́й сове́т</i> (gorodskój sovét, “municipal council”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handful</li><li>small group</li></ol><p><i>горсть</i> (gorstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>горсть</i> (gorstʹ, “cupped hands, handful, small amount”)</li></ol><p><i>горсть</i> (gorstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow of the hand, cupped hand</li><li>handful, fistful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrstь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) larynx; laryngeal</li><li>guttural</li></ol><p><i>горта́нь</i> (gortánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adam's apple (the lump in the throat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larynx; throat</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>гъртань</i> (gŭrtanĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrtanь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorьčica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij, “bitter”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p><p>For the further relation with <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ), compare typologically <i>полы́нь</i> (polýnʹ) (akin to <i>по́лымя</i> (pólymja), <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard plaster</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) yellow card</li></ol><p><i>горчи́ца</i> (gorčíca) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard pot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mustard</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mustard (color)</li></ol><p><i>горчи́ца</i> (gorčíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;regional) potter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter (one who makes pots and ceramic wares)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) comparative degree of <i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij): more bitter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gořьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot, crock (a rounded ceramic vessel)</li><li>flowerpot</li><li>chamber pot</li><li>bowl cut</li></ol><p>From a diminutive form of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gornъ</i> (“(flower)pot”) (compare <i>&#42;gъrnъ</i>), out of which Macedonian <i>грне</i> (grne, “ceramic pot”) comes. Compare Belarusian <i>гаршчо́к</i> (harščók), Ukrainian <i>го́рщик</i> (hórščyk), <i>горщо́к</i> (horščók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>горшо́к</i> (goršók)</li></ol><p><i>горшо́к</i> (goršók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter (comparative is <i>го́рче</i> (górče))</li><li>woeful, sorrowful (comparative is <i>го́рше</i> (górše) or <i>го́рший</i> (góršij))</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unhappy, hopeless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorьkъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Горький)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Gorky</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Gorky (former name, from 1932 to 1990, of Nizhny Novgorod, a city, the administrative center of Nizhny Novgorod Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>горький</i> (gorʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bitter, bitterly</li><li>poignant, poignantly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bitter! (<i>an old Russian tradition: a shout by wedding guests urging the newlyweds to kiss, thus removing the "bitterness"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitterish</li></ol><p><i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gorky (city in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Maxim Gorky; Gorkian</li></ol><p><i>Го́рький</i> (Górʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) citizen of Gorky</li></ol><p><i>Го́рький</i> (Górʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miserable person, wretch</li></ol><p><i>го́р(е)</i> (gór(e)) +&lrm; <i>-ю́н</i> (-jún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuel, gasoline, petrol (of engines)</li><li>propellant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combustible, inflammable</li></ol><p><i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ю́чий</i> (-júčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nominalized) main course, hot plate/dish</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>горя́чий</i> (gorjáčij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>горячо́</i> (gorjačó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) горячка</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot (having a high temperature)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ardent, passionate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heated (<i>of a dispute</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hot-tempered</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) busy (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) radioactive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>горѧчии</i> (goręčii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goręťь</i>, from <i>&#42;gorěti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij). Doublet of <i>горя́щий</i> (gorjáščij) and <i>горю́чий</i> (gorjúčij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excite</li></ol><p><i>горя́чий</i> (gorjáčij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get angry (and act or talk rashly), to fly into a passion, to lose one's temper</li></ol><p><i>горячи́ть</i> (gorjačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fever (also figuratively); rush</li></ol><p><i>горячи́ть</i> (gorjačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hot temper, hot-headed nature</li><li>fervor, ardor, zeal</li><li>vehemence</li></ol><p><i>горя́чий</i> (gorjáčij) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hotly, warmly</li><li>passionately (in a passionate manner)</li></ol><p><i>горя́чий</i> (gorjáčij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) state-owned mass media</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственное СМИ</i> (gosudárstvennoje SMI, “state mass media”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state machinery</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный аппара́т</i> (gosudárstvennyj apparát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state archive</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный архи́в</i> (gosudárstvennyj arxív, “state archive”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state-owned bank</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный банк</i> (gosudárstvennyj bank, “state bank”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state security</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная безопа́сность</i> (gosudárstvennaja bezopásnostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>national budget, state budget</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный бюдже́т</i> (gosudárstvennyj bjudžét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state power, government power</li><li>organs of state power, as well as their leadership</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная власть</i> (gosudárstvennaja vlastʹ, “state power”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state border</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>госуда́рственная граница</i> (gosudárstvennaja granica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state dacha</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная да́ча</i> (gosudárstvennaja dáča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) state department (in the US)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>госуда́рственный департа́мент</i> (gosudárstvennyj departáment).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political office</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная до́лжность</i> (gosudárstvennaja dólžnostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state revenue</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный дохо́д</i> (gosudárstvennyj doxód).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) State Duma</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Госду́ма</i> (Gosdúma, “State Duma”) +&lrm; <i>ду́ра</i> (dúra, “female fool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>national order, state order (request for some product or service by a government)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный зака́з</i> (gosudárstvennyj zakáz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government procurement</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная заку́пка</i> (gosudárstvennaja zakúpka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high treason, state treason</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>госуда́рственная изме́на</i> (gosudárstvennaja izména).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government committee, state committee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government firm, state-owned company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government agency</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный о́рган</i> (gosudárstvennyj órgan, “state agency”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospitalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hospitalize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>hospitalisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially&#32;military</i>) hospital</li></ol><p>Derived from Old French <i>hospital</i> (compare French <i>hôpital</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hospital</li></ol><p><i>го́спиталь</i> (góspitalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hospodar (a type of leader in Wallachia or Moldova)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gospodařь</i>, ultimately from <i>&#42;gospodь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state support (economic, financial or legal support from the state)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная подде́ржка</i> (gosudárstvennaja poddéržka, “state support”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(possessive,&#32;somewhat&#32;dated) Lord's, God's (<i>now mostly in collocations</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Го́споди</i> (Góspodi)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Господи)</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh my God, oh my Lord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>for God's sake, for Christ's sake, Jesus Christ, dear God, good lord (<i>exclamation of dread, impatience, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Господи Боже)</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>го́споди бо́же</i> (góspodi bóže)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleman (a man of a high rank or position)</li><li>(formal,&#32;term of address) mister, Mr., sir (polite, formal term of address to a man)</li><li>lord, master (the master of a household of servants or a manor)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gospodinъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>господи́н</i> (gospodín).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God's, Lord's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lord, owner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dominancy, dominance, domination, supremacy, rule</li></ol><p><i>Госпо́дь</i> (Gospódʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule, to reign</li><li>to predominate, to prevail</li><li>to command, to dominate, to tower above</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruling</li><li>prevailing, prevalent, predominant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Госпо́дь</i> (Gospódʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gospodь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gástipatis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰóstipotis</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Господь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Lord, God</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gospodь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(formal,&#32;term of address) madam, ma'am, Mrs., Miss (polite, formal term of address for a woman)</li><li>lady, mistress (the mistress of a household of servants or a manor)</li><li>(<i>BDSM</i>) mistress (term of address by a submissive partner to a dominant woman)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gospoďa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state-owned company, state-owned enterprise</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственное предприя́тие</i> (gosudárstvennoje predprijátije, “state company”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) government propaganda, state propaganda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Secretary of State (in the US and other countries)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный секрета́рь</i> (gosudárstvennyj sekretárʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil servant</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный слу́жащий</i> (gosudárstvennyj slúžaščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil service, government service</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>госуда́рственная слу́жба</i> (gosudárstvennaja slúžba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state property</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная со́бственность</i> (gosudárstvennaja sóbstvennostʹ, “state property”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state institution, state agency</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная структу́ра</i> (gosudárstvennaja struktúra, “state institution, state agency”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state secret</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственная та́йна</i> (gosudárstvennaja tájna, “state secret”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) guest</li></ol><p><i>гость</i> (gostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guesthouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hospitable, xenial (cordial and generous towards guests)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospitality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living room</li><li>suite of furniture for a living room</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>гости́ная ко́мната</i> (gostínaja kómnata, literally “guest room”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present, gift (often a sweet food)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hotel, inn</li></ol><p>From <i>гость</i> (gostʹ). Cognates include Belarusian <i>гасці́ніца</i> (hascínica), Ukrainian <i>гости́ниця</i> (hostýnycja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hotel</li></ol><p><i>гости́ница</i> (gostínica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гости́нец</i> (gostínec): little present, gift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;relational) guest</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;relational) trade</li></ol><p><i>гость</i> (gostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay <i>(with)</i>, to be on a visit <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gostiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гость</i> (gostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest, visitor</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) merchant, especially one that trades overseas.</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>гость</i> (gostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gostь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gástis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰóstis</i>. Cognate with English <i>guest</i> and <i>host</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) king, sovereign; king's, sovereign's</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рь</i> (gosudárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statist (supporter of strong state power)</li><li>statesman</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рствен(ный)</i> (gosudárstven(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statehood</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рственный</i> (gosudárstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) state</li><li>national</li><li>(<i>law</i>) public</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рство</i> (gosudárstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statesman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>government</i>) State</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рь</i> (gosudárʹ, “sovereign”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donor state</li></ol><p>First attested in 1989. From <i>госуда́рство</i> (gosudárstvo) +&lrm; <i>до́нор</i> (dónor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member state, participant state</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Your Majesty; madam, ma'am (very respectful)</li></ol><p><i>госуда́рь</i> (gosudárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ыня</i> (-ynja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereign</li><li>Your Majesty</li><li>sire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>государь</i> (gosudarĭ), a later form of <i>господарь</i> (gospodarĭ). The reshaping of the latter has been explained by the influence of the root <i>суд-</i> (sud-, “to judge”) or by a relaxed pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state university</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный университе́т</i> (gosudárstvennyj universitét, “state university”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public services</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственные услу́ги</i> (gosudárstvennyje uslúgi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government agency, state agency</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственное учрежде́ние</i> (gosudárstvennoje učreždénije, “state institution”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state agricultural enterprise</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i><u>гос</u>уда́рственное <u>хоз</u>я́йство</i> (<u>gos</u>udárstvennoje <u>xoz</u>jájstvo, “state farm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government official, state official</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>госуда́рственный чино́вник</i> (gosudárstvennyj činóvnik, “state official”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goth (historical)</li><li>goth (subculture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>го́тский</i> (gótskij): Goth., Gothic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) Gothic style</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Gothic-style works of art</li></ol><p>German <i>Gotik</i> +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gothic</li></ol><p><i>гот</i> (got) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gothicness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>case of drawing instruments</li></ol><p>First attested in 1637. Borrowed from Polish <i>gotowalnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to make ready</li><li>to cook</li><li>to prepare, to train</li><li>to store up, to lay in (stock)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gotoviti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гото́вый</i> (gotóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, to prepare oneself [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li><li>to study, to prepare [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be impending, to be imminent, to be threatening</li></ol><p><i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cooking</li></ol><p><i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparedness, readiness</li><li>willingness</li></ol><p>From <i>гото́вый</i> (gotóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, prepared [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)]</li><li>ready, inclined, willing [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li><li>finished, ready-made</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gotovъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>гато́вы</i> (hatóvy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gothic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gothic (an extinct Germanic language, once spoken by the Goths)</li></ol><p><i>гот</i> (got) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) To ghost, to perform an act of ghosting.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) hornbeam (<i>Carpinus</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) (<i>Carpinus betulus</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grabrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Armenian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>գրաբար</i> (grabar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) digger, shovelman, navvy, sandhog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Graber</i>?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гра́бли</i> (grábli): (small) rake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) graben</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Graben</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robber, burglar (one who robs or steals)</li><li>plunderer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob, to sack, to loot, to plunder</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gràbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rake (garden tool)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grabji</i>. Analyzable as <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ, “to rake”) +&lrm; <i>-ли</i> (-li).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery, open theft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grabežь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gravier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engrave, to etch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>gravieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grave accent ( ˋ )</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) gravitational interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gravitational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) gravity, gravitation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) graviton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engraving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gravure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) hail</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) deluge, hail, shower (overwhelming flow or barrage of something)</li><li>(poetic,&#32;archaic) town, city, used as a common city name suffix (Волгоград, Калининград, Ленинград)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>градъ</i> (gradŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gradъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Lithuanian <i>gruodas</i> (Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;grōda-</i>), Latin <i>grandō</i>, Old Armenian <i>կարկուտ</i> (karkut), Sanskrit <i>ह्रादुनि</i> (hrādúni, “hail”) and possibly with English <i>grind</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>градъ</i> (gradŭ), which is a reflex of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gȏrdъ</i>. Doublet of <i>го́род</i> (górod), which was normally inherited by pleophony. Old Church Slavonic word shows liquid metathesis characteristic of South Slavic area.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>гра́дус</i> (grádus): deg., degree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gradation</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>gradātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) gradient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hailstone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooling tower</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>gradieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>like hail; flying or falling in great profusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) mayor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mayor</li></ol><p><i>градонача́льник</i> (gradonačálʹnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>city-forming (used to describe a production enterprise that employs a significant part or the majority of the working population of a city or town)</li></ol><p><i>град</i> (grad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>образу́ющий</i> (obrazújuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) town planning</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) urban development</li></ol><p><i>град</i> (grad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строи́тельный</i> (stroítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urban planning</li></ol><p>From <i>град</i> (grad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строи́тельство</i> (stroítelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;physics,&#32;meteorology) degree</li><li>grade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>gradus</i>, from the verb <i>gradi</i> (to step), from Proto-Indo-European base <i>*gʰredʰ-</i> (to go). Akin with the native грядущий, грясти.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical thermometer</li></ol><p><i>гра́дус</i> (grádus) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen, national</li><li>(formal,&#32;law enforcement) sir, mister, Mr. (<i>form of address used by a police officer towards a male civilian</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гражданинъ</i> (graždaninŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorďaninъ</i>. Compare native <i>горожа́нин</i> (gorožánin), which was inherited from Old East Slavic. Related to <i>го́род</i> (górod, “city”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>граждани́н</i> (graždanín, “citizen”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(formal,&#32;law enforcement) madam, ma'am, Ms. (form of address used by a police officer towards a female civilian)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) civilian life, civvy street</li></ol><p>From <i>граждани́н</i> (graždanín) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil war</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>American Civil War</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) civil defense (efforts to protect the citizens of a state from military attack)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civil, civic</li><li>civilian (unrelated to armies or ground forces)</li></ol><p><i>гражда́н(ин)</i> (graždán(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil marriage</li><li>(<i>informal&#32;in modern usage</i>) cohabitation (<i>of two lovers</i>), common-law marriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) civil code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the civil script, a modernized version of the Cyrillic script, introduced by Peter I of Russia in 1708</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) civil law (<i>as opposed to criminal law</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гражда́нский</i> (graždánskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civilian (non-military person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil disobedience</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) civil law (body of law dealing with private relations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гражда́нский шрифт</i> (graždánskij šrift).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civic consciousness</li></ol><p><i>гражда́нственный</i> (graždánstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civic, civil</li></ol><p><i>гражда́нство</i> (graždánstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizenship, nationality</li></ol><p><i>граждани́н</i> (graždanín) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) grammar Nazi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grammar Nazi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grammar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>grammatically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grammatical</li></ol><p><i>грамма́т(ика)</i> (grammát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gramophone, record player</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) record player, gramophone</li></ol><p><i>граммофо́н</i> (grammofón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>граммофо́н</i> (grammofón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading and writing (<i>sum of knowledge necessary to be able to read and write</i>)</li><li>document; during medieval times often used to refer to a diplomatic correspondence or a decree issued and promulgated by authorities</li><li>patent</li><li>diploma</li><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;historical</i>) letter</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>γράμματα</i> (grámmata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>educated, literate person, scholar</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>гра́мота</i> (grámota) +&lrm; <i>-е́й</i> (-éj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>correctly, grammatically</li><li>competently, skilfully</li></ol><p><i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literacy</li><li>competence</li></ol><p><i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literate</li><li>grammatical, correct (<i>of texts, i.e. no spelling mistakes</i>)</li><li>competent, skilful</li></ol><p><i>гра́мота</i> (grámota) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phonograph record, gramophone record, vinyl disk</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>граммофо́нная пласти́нка</i> (grammofónnaja plastínka, “gramophone record/disk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain (unit of weight)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>grānum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grand prize</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grand prix</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Гран-при)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grand Prix</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Grand Prix</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate</li><li>garnet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Granat</i>, from Latin <i>pomum granatum</i> (“grainy apple”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) grenade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>granata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pomegranate</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) garnet</li><li>garnet (<i>color</i>), dark red</li></ol><p><i>грана́т</i> (granát) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grenade launcher</li><li>portable rocket launcher, portable missile launcher, bazooka</li></ol><p>From <i>грана́та</i> (granáta, “grenade”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мёт</i> (-mjót), combining form of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grenade launcher</li></ol><p><i>гранатомёт</i> (granatomjót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>grandly</li></ol><p><i>грандио́зный</i> (grandióznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grandiose (large and impressive, in size, scope or extent)</li></ol><p>Per Shansky, probably an adapted borrowing of French <i>grandiose</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Per Vasmer, from either German <i>grandios</i> or Italian <i>grandioso</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>грандіо́зний</i> (hrandióznyj), Belarusian <i>грандыёзны</i> (hrandyjózny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapidary workshop</li></ol><p><i>грани́ть</i> (granítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granite</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>grānum</i>. Further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂nóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) granite; granitic</li><li>stone grey (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>грани́т</i> (granít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to facet</li></ol><p><i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, boundary, bounds, confines, frontier</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>граница</i> (granica) attested in Southern Rus' since 1375 and speculated to be an Old Polish loan, even though there's no scholarly consensus, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;granica</i>. The more common Muscovite term was Middle Russian <i>рубежъ</i> (rubež), but it was to some extent displaced, perhaps under Polish and Ruthenian influence. By surface analysis, <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contiguous, adjoining, abutting, bordering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to border, to be contiguous</li><li>to adjoin, to abut, to neighbour on</li><li>to verge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) proof, galley proof, slip</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) galley</li><li>cutting, faceting (<i>of gemstones or glass</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of solitaire card game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) grant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grant</i>, several steps omitting from Latin <i>crēdō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) grant equivalent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) grant element</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grantor</li></ol><p><i>грант</i> (grant, “grant”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ, “to give”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a recipient of money grants, especially from foreign entities, who advocates the policies of the donors</li></ol><p><i>грант</i> (grant) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grantee, grant receiver, beneficiary</li></ol><p><i>грант</i> (grant, “grant”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>получа́тель</i> (polučátelʹ, “receiver”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granule</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>grānum</i>. Further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂nóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) granulomatosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pellet mill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facet, side (<i>of an object with flat, sharply defined surfaces</i>)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) face (<i>of a polyhedron</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) border, verge, brink, edge</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) bound (<i>upper or lower</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грань</i> (granĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;granь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut, faceted</li></ol><p><i>грани́ть</i> (granítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grappa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>grappa</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burr, guttural pronunciation, uvular pronunciation (of <i>р</i> (r), as in French)</li></ol><p><i>грасси́ровать</i> (grassírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pronounce the <i>р</i> (r) sound uvularly/gutturally, as in French</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grasseyer</i> (“to pronounce "r" uvularly”) + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gratin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gratin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>count, earl (the male ruler of a county)</li><li>(<i>graph theory</i>) graph (set of vertices (or nodes) connected together by edges)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Graf</i>.</p><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>graph</i>, first used in this sense by J. J. Sylvester in 1878.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>column (a vertical body of text)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grapheme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diagram, graph (a chart of the relation between two or three quantities)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) schedule, time-table, program (a list of planned activities with the times at which they should occur)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) graphic artist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) drawing; graphic arts</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) graphics</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) script, character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>graphica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decanter, carafe; water-bottle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>графи́н</i> (grafín, “carafe, decanter”): small carafe, cruet</li></ol><p><i>графи́н</i> (grafín) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graphite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>graphically</li></ol><p><i>графи́ческий</i> (grafíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graphic, graphical</li></ol><p><i>гра́ф(ика)</i> (gráf(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graphologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graphology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) graphomaniac, scribbler</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>графома́ния</i> (grafománija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graphomania</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) pretentious writing style; a text in such style</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>graphomanie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) eye dialect spelling</li><li>(<i>computer slang</i>) graphics</li></ol><p><i>гра́фика</i> (gráfika) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) count; earl</li></ol><p><i>граф</i> (graf) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) county, earldom (the territory ruled by a feudal count or earl)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) countdom, earldom (the noble rank of being a count or earl)</li><li>county, shire (administrative subdivision in Britain, Ireland, or Liberia)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) county (administrative subdivision in the USA or Canada)</li></ol><p><i>граф</i> (graf, “count, earl”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graffiti</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>graffiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gracefully</li></ol><p><i>грацио́зный</i> (gracióznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graceful</li></ol><p>Italian <i>grazioso</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grace, gracefulness</li><li>long-line bra, corselet</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Grace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rook <i>(a black bird whose large flocks signal the arrival of spring)</i></li></ol><p>Originally Russian word formed from the onomatopoeia <i>гра</i> (gra) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač). Cf. <i>ка́ркать</i> (kárkatʹ) and other similar words with onomatopoeic origin, such as Ukrainian <i>грак</i> (hrak, “rook”) and <i>крук</i> (kruk, “raven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>грач</i> (grač).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk&#32;poetic</i>) to caw, to croak (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to laugh loudly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to ridicule noisily, to play jokes on, to fool</li><li>to quarrel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grajati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pectinate, comb-shaped</li><li>pectineal, related to the pecten</li></ol><p><i>гре́бень</i> (grébenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb</li><li>(<i>of a bird</i>) crest, comb</li><li>hackle, hatchel</li><li>ridge, crest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;greby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower, oarsman</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) hay raker</li></ol><p><i>греб-</i> (greb-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>гре́бень</i> (grébenʹ): comb, tooth-comb</li><li>crest (of a bird)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) scallop (mollusc)</li></ol><p><i>гре́бень</i> (grébenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-шо́к</i> (-šók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strickle, scraper</li></ol><p><i>греб-</i> (greb-, “to row, to rake, originally to scrape”) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rowing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grebja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rowing</li></ol><p><i>греб-</i> (greb-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb</li></ol><p><i>гре́бень</i> (grébenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) grevillea (<i>Grevillea</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Grevillea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) grewia (<i>Grewia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Grewia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) gregarine (sporozoan of the subclass Gregarinasina)</li></ol><p>Likely an internationalism from English <i>gregarine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dream <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>грёза</i> (grjóza) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) grader</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) graveled road</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grader</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grapefruit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grapefruit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grapefruit</li></ol><p><i>грейпфру́т</i> (grejpfrút) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greyhound (lean breed of dog used in hunting and racing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>greyhound</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek (by ethnicity)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грькъ</i> (grĭkŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>грькъ</i> (grĭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grьkъ</i>. By surface analysis, back-formation from <i>гре́цкий</i> (gréckij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hot water bottle</li></ol><p><i>греть</i> (gretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thunder (to make a noise like thunder)</li><li>to rattle (to make a rattling noise)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grьměti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rattlesnake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thundering, explosive</li><li>rattling</li></ol><p><i>греме́ть</i> (gremétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain, silkworm eggs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>graine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) grenadier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grenadier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toast (toasted bread) or crouton fried lightly in butter or vegetable oil, until crisp and brown</li><li>French toast</li></ol><p>Compare typologically <i>су́шка</i> (súška) (&lt; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greenlander</li></ol><p><i>Гренла́ндия</i> (Grenlándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toast (toasted bread) or crouton fried lightly in butter or vegetable oil, until crisp and brown</li><li>French toast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to row, to scull</li><li>to rake</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rake in (money)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gretì</i> (“to dig, to scrape, to rake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat (to cause an increase in temperature of an object or space)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i> (“warm”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>θερμός</i> (thermós, “warm”) (whence <i>те́рмос</i> (térmos), <i>термо-</i> (termo-)), Latin <i>formus</i>, <i>furnus</i>, and possibly Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warmaz</i> (whence English <i>warm</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to warm oneself by drinking hot liquid</li><li>to become warm</li></ol><p><i>гре́ть</i> (grétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theology</i>) sin</li><li>fault</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depravity, peccability, peccancy, sin</li></ol><p><i>грехо́вный</i> (grexóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěxovьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-о́вный</i> (-óvnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;Judaism,&#32;Islam) fall of man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>грѣхопадениѥ</i> (grěxopadenije). By surface analysis, <i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>паде́ние</i> (padénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Greek</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грьчьскъ</i> (grĭčĭskŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>грьчьскъ</i> (grĭčĭskŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grьčьskъ</i>. See also <i>гре́ческий</i> (gréčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walnut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гре́цкий</i> (gréckij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>гре́ческий</i> (gréčeskij, “Greek”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>гре́ческий язы́к</i> (gréčeskij jazýk, “Greek language”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>сло́во гре́ческого языка́</i> (slóvo gréčeskovo jazyká, “word of the Greek language”): Greek words, in terms of etymology or philology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Northern Russia,&#32;Saint Petersburg) <i>local variation of гре́чка (gréčka, “buckwheat”)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Greek</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Greek language (short for гре́ческий язы́к (gréčeskij jazýk))</li></ol><p>From <i>грек</i> (grek) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij). See also <i>гре́цкий</i> (gréckij), an older form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>гре́ческий</i> (gréčeskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buckwheat (plant)</li></ol><p>From <i>гре́ча</i> (gréča) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) buckwheat (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buckwheat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buckwheat porridge (<i>breakfast cereal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>гре́ча</i> (gréča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) buckwheat</li></ol><p><i>гре́ча</i> (gréča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) guilty, culpable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin (to commit a sin)</li><li>to contradict, to go against (logic)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěšiti</i>, also analysable as <i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěšьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female sinner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěšьnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěšьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>I must admit, I must confess; I am sorry to say, much as I regret it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peccadillo, trivial offence</li><li>diminutive of <i>грех</i> (grex)</li></ol><p><i>грех</i> (grex) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fungus (a category of eukaryotic organism, having cell walls but no chlorophyll)</li><li>mushroom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gribъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mushroomer, habitual gatherer of mushrooms, mushroom picker, mushroom hunter</li></ol><p><i>гриб</i> (grib) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mycelium, mushroom spawn</li></ol><p><i>гриб(ни́к)</i> (grib(ník)) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p><p><i>грибно́й</i> (gribnój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mushroom</li></ol><p><i>гриб</i> (grib) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>гриб</i> (grib): small mushroom</li><li>fungus (any member of the kingdom Fungi)</li><li>fungal infections, mycosis</li></ol><p><i>гриб</i> (grib) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athlete's foot (fungal infection)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>грибо́к</i> (gribók); small mushroom</li></ol><p><i>грибо́к</i> (gribók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;griva</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ग्रीवा</i> (grīvā́).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ten-copeck coin</li></ol><p>From <i>гри́вна</i> (grívna) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>гри́венник</i> (grívennik)</li></ol><p><i>гри́венник</i> (grívennik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) grivna (unit of currency and weight in medieval Rus)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) ten-copeck coin</li><li>hryvnia, hryvna, grivna (the currency of Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From <i>гри́ва</i> (gríva, “mane, neck”), derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;griva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Gregorian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gregorian calendar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) room for the entourage in a Russian prince's palace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grizzly bear</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grizzly</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grill (barbecue)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup (especially theatrical makeup)</li><li>grease-paint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grimace (a distortion of the countenance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Grimasse</i>, from French <i>grimace</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grimace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up, apply cosmetics to</li></ol><p><i>грим</i> (grim) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make up (to apply facial cosmetics)</li></ol><p><i>грим</i> (grim, “makeup”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ, productive verbal suffix) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja, reflexive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>make-up artist</li></ol><p><i>грим</i> (grim) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prime meridian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilot whale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Faroese <i>grind</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>influenza, flu, grippe, grip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grippe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) influenza; influenzal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>grippös</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture (bird)</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) griffin, gryphon</li><li>fingerboard, neck (the extension of any stringed instrument on which a fingerboard is mounted)</li><li>signature stamp</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>γρύψ</i> (grúps).</p><p>From German <i>Griff</i>.</p><p>From French <i>griffe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slate pencil, stylus for writing on a slate board</li><li>pencil lead</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Griffel</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γραφείον</i> (grapheíon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) griefer</li></ol><p>From English <i>griefer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) griffin (mythical beast having the body of a lion and the wings and head of an eagle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffin</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) grave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grobъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb, sepulchre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coffin</li><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) sepulchral, deathly</li></ol><p><i>гроб</i> (grob) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunder, thunderstorm</li><li>disaster</li><li>danger, menace</li><li>terror</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гроза</i> (groza, “horror”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bunch, cluster, (botany) truss (a group of a number of similar things)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grozdъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li><li>to make threatening gestures</li><li>to threaten, to be about to happen (<i>of something bad</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be in danger of, to face</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groziti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to threaten <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>грози́ть</i> (grozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>threateningly, menacingly</li><li>sternly</li></ol><p><i>гро́зный</i> (gróznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, harsh, cruel</li><li>fearsome, formidable, threatening, menacing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grozьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гроза́</i> (grozá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Грозный)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grozny (the regional capital and largest city in Chechnya, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thunderstorm</li></ol><p><i>гроза́</i> (grozá) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism,&#32;rare) to grok</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grok</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) thunder, thunderbolt</li><li>roar, thunderous sound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass, bulk, pile, heap</li><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) (territorial) hromada, community of various levels in Ukraine (city, town, region, village, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromada</i> (“heap, pile”).</p><p>Sense 2 is a semantic loan from Ukrainian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hulk, huge object</li><li>hulk, giant</li></ol><p><i>грома́да</i> (gromáda) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immensity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>huge, colossal, enormous, immense</li></ol><p><i>грома́да</i> (gromáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) thug, gangster, hoodlum, bully</li></ol><p><i>громи́ть</i> (gromítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raid, to sack, to loot</li><li>to smash up</li><li>to inveigh <i>(against)</i>, to fulminate <i>(against)</i></li><li><i>(an enemy)</i> to smash, to rout, to crush, to pound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud</li><li>famous; widely known and publicised</li><li>pompous, excessively ceremonial</li></ol><p>From <i>гром</i> (grom, “thunder”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>loudly, aloud</li></ol><p><i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loudspeaker</li></ol><p>From <i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij, “loud”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ, “to speak”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”), a calque of English <i>loudspeaker</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>гучнагавары́цель</i> (hučnahavarýcjelʹ), Latvian <i>skaļrunis</i>, Lithuanian <i>garsiakalbis</i>, Ukrainian <i>гучномо́вець</i> (hučnomóvecʹ), formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loudness, noisiness, volume</li></ol><p><i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) thunderer, god of thunder and lightning</li></ol><p>From <i>гром</i> (grom, “thunder”) +&lrm; <i>-вергать</i> (-vergatʹ, “to throw, cast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thunder</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) thunderous, very loud, deafening</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stunning, awe-inspiring</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) destructive</li></ol><p><i>гром</i> (grom) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>громово́й</i> (gromovój)</li></ol><p><i>гром</i> (grom) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud, loud-voiced, public</li></ol><p><i>гром</i> (grom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pile up, to heap up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromozditi</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;gromoditi</i>, from <i>&#42;gromada</i> (“pile, heap”). Related to <i>грома́да</i> (gromáda, “heap, pile, mass”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tower</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to climb <i>(onto)</i>, to struggle up <i>(onto)</i>, to place oneself <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>громозди́ть</i> (gromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulky, cumbersome, cumbrous, unwieldy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning rod</li></ol><p><i>гром</i> (grom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>отво́д</i> (otvód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>гро́мко</i> (grómko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to produce loud sounds</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ledger, account book (a collection of accounting entries consisting of credits and debits)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Großbuch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) Grandmaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>Großmeister</i>. Further from German <i>groß</i> (akin to English <i>great</i>) + Latin <i>magister</i> (see <i>маги́стр</i> (magístr) for more).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grotto</li><li>mainsail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grotte</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>groot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literature,&#32;art) grotesque (genre)</li><li>grotesque ornament</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) grotesque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>grotesque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bang</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bang</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop with a crash/bang, to bang down, to break, to smash</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roar <i>(with laughter)</i></li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) to kill, to murk, to waste</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to ban</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash, to tumble</li></ol><p><i>гро́хнуть</i> (gróxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crash, din, thunder, roar <i>(of weapons)</i>, rumble, roll, rattle</li><li>riddle, screen, sifter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groxotъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash, to roll, to peal, to rumble, to roar, to thunder, to clatter</li></ol><p><i>гро́хот</i> (gróxot) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) half-kopeck coin</li><li>grosz</li><li>groschen</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) penny, cent, farthing, small amount of money, peanuts (pl: <i>гроши́</i> (groší))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>grosz</i>, from German <i>Groschen</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>denarius grossus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not worth a farthing, worthless, two-bit</li></ol><p><i>грош</i> (groš) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) grubber (cultivator for deep loosening of soil)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grubber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>гру́бый</i> (grúbyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>гру́бо</i> (grúbo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to insult, to speak rudely to; to talk back to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(sports,&#32;intransitive,&#32;imperfective only) to break the rules when playing</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to roughen</li></ol><p><i>гру́бый</i> (grúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>churl, boor, cad, chuff, vulgarian, bounder (rude and mean fellow)</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Polish <i>grubian(in)</i>, or from Czech <i>hrubián</i>, from German <i>Grobian</i> (“brute”), influenced by грубый (grubyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coarsely, roughly</li><li>crudely</li></ol><p><i>гру́бый</i> (grúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>roughly speaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat rough, uncouth, rude</li></ol><p><i>гру́бый</i> (grúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roughness, coarseness, crudity, crudeness</li><li>rudeness, rude manner, rough manner</li><li>rude words</li></ol><p><i>гру́бый</i> (grúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncouth, boorish, rude, uncultured</li><li>rough, coarse (<i>of cloth, a surface, etc.</i>)</li><li>rough, hoarse, raspy (<i>of a voice or sound</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rough, approximate, inexact (<i>of work, an estimate, etc.</i>)</li><li>severe, egregious, gross, unacceptable (<i>of an error or miscalculation</i>)</li><li>brute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grǫbъ</i>.</p><p>Cognates include German <i>grob</i>, Lithuanian <i>grubùs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile, heap, mass, clod</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gruda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busty</li></ol><p><i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) November</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грудьнь</i> (grudĭnĭ, “November”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grudьňь</i>. Cognates include Bulgarian <i>груден</i> (gruden, “November”) and Polish <i>grudzień</i> (“December”). Related to Russian гру́да (grúda, “clod”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brisket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) chest (esp. culinary)</li></ol><p><i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angina pectoris (colloquial&#58; intermittent chest pain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest, thorax</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) suckling; nursing infant</li></ol><p><i>грудь</i> (grudʹ, “breast”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) breast; pectoral</li></ol><p><i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest</li><li>breast, bosom</li><li>thorax</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грѫдь</i> (grǫdĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grǫdь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰrendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight, load</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) burden</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) weight, counterweight</li><li>cargo, freight, lading</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grǫzъ</i>, itself possibly deverbal of <i>&#42;gręzti</i> (“to sink”). Compare Ukrainian <i>грузь</i> (hruzʹ) and Polish <i>grąz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;euphemistic) cargo-200 (code word referring to casualties for transportation in the Soviet and modern Russian military)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;euphemistic) (code word referring to injured persons for transportation in the Soviet and modern Russian military)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to puzzle (someone), to make someone feel uncomfortable or burdened</li></ol><p><i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Lactarius mushroom, milk-cap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gruzdь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) plummet, lead, sinker</li></ol><p><i>груз(ить)</i> (gruz(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgian, Kartvelian (person from the country of Georgia)</li></ol><p>Modification of Old East Slavic <i>гурзи</i>&nbsp;pl (gurzi, “Georgians”). Compare also Old East Slavic <i>гурзиискъ</i> (gurziiskŭ, adjective), <i>гурзꙑньскаꙗ землꙗ</i> (gurzynĭskaja zemlja). Ultimately of the same origin as Persian گرج (gorj), as the word <i>Georgia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Georgiophobia, Gruzinophobia, anti-Georgianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Georgian (relating to Georgia, nation south of the Caucasus Mountains)</li></ol><p>From <i>грузи́н</i> (gruzín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (a vehicle)</li><li>to load (cargo)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to talk about something at length in order to confuse, to deceive, make worried</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bother, to annoy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grǫziti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take cargo</li><li>to pile into (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a bulky or unwieldy manner</li></ol><p><i>гру́зный</i> (grúznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaviness, unwieldiness, corpulence</li></ol><p><i>гру́зный</i> (grúznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy, massive, bulky, cumbersome, cumbrous, unwieldy, stout, corpulent</li></ol><p><i>груз</i> (gruz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lorry, truck (motor vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>грузово́й</i> (gruzovój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>грузови́к</i> (gruzovík)</li></ol><p><i>грузови́к</i> (gruzovík) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cargo, shipping</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cargo, freight</li></ol><p><i>груз</i> (gruz) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) truck, lorry (motor vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goods/freight turnover, goods/freight traffic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consignor, shipper, sender of freight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;vehicles) cargo-passenger, cargo and passenger</li></ol><p><i>груз</i> (gruz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пассажи́р</i> (passažír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load-carrying capacity, carrying capacity; tonnage</li></ol><p>From <i>грузоподъёмный</i> (gruzopodʺjómnyj, “load-bearing”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>load-bearing, lifting</li></ol><p><i>груз</i> (gruz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подъёмный</i> (podʺjómnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loader, stevedore, docker, porter</li></ol><p><i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groom (a person who looks after horses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>groom</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soil, ground, bottom</li><li>priming, primer coat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Grund</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ground, earth, soil</li></ol><p><i>грунт</i> (grunt) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grouper (fish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>grouper</i>, from Portuguese <i>garoupa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gruppe</i>. Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kruppaz</i>. Doublet of <i>круп</i> (krup) and <i>крупье́</i> (krupʹjé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) army group (large operational military command that controls several armies&#59; equivalent to U.S. army group)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) group of forces</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood type, blood group (blood classification, lit.&#58; "group of blood")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) gruppetto, turn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>gruppetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to group, to classify</li><li>to collect, to concentrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to group together, to be grouped, to cluster</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to assume the correct posture (<i>preparatory to a jump, somersault, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>группирова́ть</i> (gruppirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coterie, faction; gang</li><li>grouping</li></ol><p><i>группирова́ть</i> (gruppirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa)</li></ol><p><i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) group, team</li></ol><p><i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) group sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) group sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sad, to grieve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grustiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>грусть</i> (grustʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sadly</li></ol><p><i>гру́стный</i> (grústnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad</li></ol><p><i>грусть</i> (grustʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadness (state&#47;emotion)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grustь</i>. Vasmer adduces <i>гру́да</i> (grúda) and/or <i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ) as possible cognates.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) swede (see брюква)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pear (fruit or tree)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) punching bag (boxing equipment of such shape)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gruša</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;kruša</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Груша)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Grusha, of the female given name Аграфе́на (Agraféna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pearlike, pear-shaped</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pear; made of pears</li></ol><p><i>гру́ша</i> (grúša) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гру́ша</i> (grúša): (little) pear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an employee of the GRU</li></ol><p>From <i>ГРУ</i> (GRU) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hernia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gryža</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupturewort (<i>Herniaria</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p><i>гры́жа</i> (grýža) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) hernia, same as <i>гры́жа</i> (grýža)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) swede, same as <i>брю́ква</i> (brjúkva)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fight between animals</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squabble, wrangling, argy-bargy</li></ol><p><i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw</li><li>to nibble</li><li>to crack <i>(nuts)</i></li><li>to bite <i>(fingernails)</i></li><li>to nag at</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gryzti</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>гриза́</i> (grizá), Serbo-Croatian <i>гри̏сти</i>/<i>grȉsti</i>, Slovene <i>gristi</i>, Slovak <i>hrýzť</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Old Armenian <i>կրծեմ</i> (krcem) and probably Gothic <i>𐌺𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (kriustan, “grate, grind”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rodent</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a Georgian person.</li></ol><p><i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ, “to nibble”) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún).</p><p>Sense 2 is not based on the etymology of the Russian word <i>грузин</i> (gruzin, “a Georgian”) but is a xenophobic pun, playing on the phonetic similarity between Russian <i>грузин</i> (gruzin) and "грызун".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gruyere</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gruyère</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) garden bed</li><li>ridge, (mountain) range</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>грѧда</i> (gręda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grędà</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;grindā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed (for plants), seedbed</li></ol><p><i>гряда́</i> (grjadá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the future</li></ol><p>From the present participle <i>гряду́щий</i> (grjadúščij, “approaching”), from <i>грясти́</i> (grjastí, “to be coming, to be approaching”) (<i>грядёт</i> (grjadjót)), from Old East Slavic <i>грѧсти</i> (gręsti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gręsti</i> (“to go”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrHdʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>gradior</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌹𐌳</i> (grid, “step”), English <i>progress</i>, <i>congress</i>, <i>ingress</i>, <i>grade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) future, coming, forthcoming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mud, dirt</li></ol><p><i>грязь</i> (grjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirty bomb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to make dirty, to besmirch, to litter</li><li>to sully, to tarnish</li></ol><p><i>гря́зный</i> (grjáznyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dirtily, dirty</li></ol><p><i>гря́зный</i> (grjáznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirty creature</li><li>dirty fellow</li><li>slut</li></ol><p><i>гря́зный</i> (grjáznyj) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to sink into something sticky or boggy</li><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;literary,&#32;figurative) to wallow in, to get stuck in (<i>something bad</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grę̑znǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirty person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dirty, soiled, dingy, filthy, grimy, grubby</li><li>dirty, low, vile</li><li>muddy</li><li>garbage, slop</li></ol><p><i>грязь</i> (grjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt (uncleanliness)</li><li>mud</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) filth, smut, dirt, schmutz</li><li>pollution</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грѣзъ</i> (grězŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grę̑zь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break out, to burst out, to burst forth</li><li><i>(song, orchestra)</i> to strike up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grędnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to collapse; to crash with a loud noise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;solemn) to come, to begin (<i>of time, period, event</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to go, to come</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>грѧсти</i> (gręsti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gręsti</i> (“to go”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>gradior</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌹𐌳</i> (grid, “step”), English <i>progress</i>, <i>congress</i>, <i>ingress</i>, <i>grade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial) fricking, frigging, darn, dang</li></ol><p>Euphemistic alteration of <i>ёбаный</i> (jóbanyj, “fucking”, a vulgar curse), influenced by the verb <i>грести</i> (gresti, “to row, rake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream, daydream, vision</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>грѣза</i> (grěza), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>грех</i> (grex).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guava (fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>guava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guacamole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guano</li><li>(<i>minced oath</i>) crap, shit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>guano</i>.</p><p>A euphemistic alteration of <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guanhua (language)</li></ol><p>From Mandarin <i>guānhuà</i>&nbsp;/ <i>官话</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Guaraní (language)</li><li>guaraní (currency)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>painting</i>) gouache</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gouache</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>губерния</i> (gubernija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lip</li><li>(<i>chiefly plural</i>) tip of certain instruments (<i>e.g. forceps</i>)</li><li>(botany,&#32;colloquial) polypore</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;zoology) sponge</li><li>(geography,&#32;Northern Russia,&#32;Eastern Russia) bay or inlet of a sea or lake (<i>usually at the mouth of a large river</i>)</li><li>(military,&#32;slang) stockade (<i>prison</i>)</li><li>(military,&#32;slang) detention in a stockade (as a punishment)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) type of judicial district in Russia in the 16th and 17th centuries</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫba</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫba</i>. Probably in origin the same as Etymology 1 (where the sense "lip" developed figuratively from the original meaning "mushroom, sponge").</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;guba</i> (“fold”), action noun of <i>&#42;gъbnǫti</i> (“bend”). Alternatively, per Vasmer, from губа́ (gubá, “lip”), originally "river mouth".</p><p>Shortening of губва́хта (gubváxta), a corruption of гауптва́хта (gauptváxta).</p><p>Per Vasmer, deverbal from <i>губи́ть</i> (gubítʹ, “to destroy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) having prominent lips, lippy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) governor</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>gubernator</i>, from Latin <i>gubernator</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κυβερνήτης</i> (kubernḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gubernatorial, governor's</li></ol><p><i>губерна́тор</i> (gubernátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) province, governorate, gubernia (an administrative subdivision in the Russian empire)</li></ol><p>Probably from a reanalysis of <i>губерна́тор</i> (gubernátor, “governor”), modeled on <i>импе́рия</i> (impérija, “empire”) &lt; <i>импера́тор</i> (imperátor, “emperor”). Compare Polish <i>gubernia</i>, generally described as borrowed from the Russian term; however, Smirnov (1910) believed that the direction of borrowing was the opposite. Compare Medieval Latin <i>gubernium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) guberniya, province (<i>prerevolutionary</i>); provincial</li></ol><p><i>губе́рния</i> (gubérnija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>губе́рнский</i> (gubérnskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baneful, pernicious, ruinous, disastrous, destructive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>гоубител҄ьнъ</i> (gubitelʹĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>губи́ть</i> (gubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wreck, to destroy, to ruin</li><li>to kill (make useless)</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gubiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>губа́</i> (gubá, “lip”)</li><li>sponge</li><li>polypore</li></ol><p><i>губа́</i> (gubá) +&lrm; <i>ка</i> (ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmonica</li></ol><p>Literally, “Garmon of the lips”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lipstick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lip</li><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) labial</li></ol><p><i>губа́</i> (gubá) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lipstick</li></ol><p><i>губно́й</i> (gubnój) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governess</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tutor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Huguenot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to google</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Google, Google's</li></ol><p><i>Гугл</i> (Gugl) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Googlephone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Googlephone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>googol (10<sup>100</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>googolplex (10<sup>10<sup>100</sup></sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buzzing; droning; humming</li><li>honking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buzz; to drone; to hoot, to honk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to party, revel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫsti</i> ("to play a musical instrument"). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>гуді́ти</i> (hudíty), <i>густи́</i> (hustý), Old Polish <i>gąść</i> and Belarusian <i>гусці́</i> (huscí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gujarati (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hooter; choo; toot; siren; honk; whistle (a sound which calls attention to a ship, train, factory, car, etc.)</li><li>gudok (an ancient Russian musical instrument)</li></ol><p><i>гуде́ть</i> (gudétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gudok musician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar (a black, oily, sticky, viscous substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>goudron</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>гудро́н</i> (gudrón)</li></ol><p><i>гудро́н</i> (gudrón) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;puristic) humming, music</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гѫдьба</i> (gǫdĭba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫdьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>гудеть</i> (gudetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба́</i> (-bá). Cognate to Ukrainian <i>гудьба</i> (hudʹba, “humming, music”) and Czech <i>hudba</i> (“music”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tug</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫžь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rump <i>(of a bird)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) butt (of a human being or an animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geoduck, a species of clam (<i>Panopea generosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>geoduck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Guizhou</li></ol><p>From <i>Гуйчжо́у</i> (Gujčžóu) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boom, rumble, rumbling, hum, buzz, din</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gulъ</i>, presumably formed from <i>&#42;-lъ</i> and related to <i>&#42;govorъ</i>. Compare semantically analogous Svan <i>გულ</i> (gul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulab jamun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>गुलाब जामुन</i> (gulāb jāmun), from <i>गुलाब</i> (gulāb, “rosewater”), in turn from Persian گلاب (golâb), from گل (gol, “rose”) + آب (âb, “water”), and <i>जामुन</i> (jāmun, “jambul”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) acronym of <i>Гла́вное управле́ние исправи́тельно-трудовы́х лагере́й</i> (Glávnoje upravlénije ispravítelʹno-trudovýx lageréj, “chief administration of correctional labor camps”): gulag</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ГУЛАГ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) acronym of <i>Гла́вное управле́ние исправи́тельно-трудовы́х лагере́й</i> (Glávnoje upravlénije ispravítelʹno-trudovýx lageréj, “chief administration of correctional labor camps”): gulag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) GULAG, gulag</li></ol><p>From <i>гула́г</i> (gulág) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) GULAG, gulag</li></ol><p>From <i>гула́г</i> (gulág) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stroll, to take a walk (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have fun (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make the sound of a pigeon</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud, booming</li><li>resonant</li></ol><p><i>гул</i> (gul) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>resonantly</li><li>resoundingly, in a booming manner</li></ol><p><i>гу́лкий</i> (gúlkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>very tall person, giant</li></ol><p>From the protagonist Lemuel Gulliver of Jonathan Swift's novel <i>Gulliver's Travels</i>, who found himself a relative giant in a land of tiny people.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affectionate term for a dove</li><li>bump, blister, bruise</li></ol><p>Formed from the onomatopoeia <i>гуль-гу́ль</i> (gulʹ-gúlʹ) + <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p><p>Related to Serbo-Croatian <i>жуљ</i>. Probably related to Icelandic <i>kúla</i> (“sphere, ball, lump, bullet”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kūlǭ</i> (“bump, hole”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket, arugula (<i>Sisymbrium</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p><i>гуля́вший</i> (guljávšij) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reveller</li><li>idler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) outing, party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walk, walking; stroll</li><li>revelry, open-air merrymaking, outdoor fete; (popular) festival</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to go for a walk, to stroll</li><li>to have time-off, to have free time</li><li>to make merry, to enjoy oneself, to carouse</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to party</li></ol><li>to go with, to go out <i>with someone</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat, to "stray from" or be unfaithful to one's spouse or partner</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;about babies) to stay awake</li><li>to let alone, to stop bothering, get lost</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Cognate with Belarusian <i>гуля́ць</i> (huljácʹ), Ukrainian <i>гуля́ти</i> (huljáty) and Old Polish <i>oguł</i> (“wholeness, entirety”), for the latter compare Russian <i>огу́лом</i> (ogúlom, “all at once, indiscriminately; together”). Therefore from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gulъ</i>.</p><p>Potebnja and Sobolevsky consider earlier meaning "to drink (an alcoholic beverage)", and compare Ukrainian <i>гуля́ка</i> (huljáka, “drunkard”), Serbo-Croatian <i>гу́лити</i> (“to booze, drink heavily”). It is also proposed to consider "to rest, to spend time resting" the original meaning, and thus connected to Lithuanian <i>guliù</i>, <i>gulti</i> (“get into bed, lie down”). Also connected to Ukrainian <i>бити в гулю</i> (byty v hulju, “to play with a ball”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goulash</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>gulyás</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) carousing, idle, living a life of leisure</li><li>(attributive,&#32;dated) holiday, party</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) loose, promiscuous, sluttish (<i>usually of a woman</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) free (a commoner not subject to serfdom or bondage)</li></ol><p><i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanism</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>humanismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humanistic, humanitarian</li></ol><p><i>гумани́ст</i> (gumaníst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanitarian</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>humanitarian</i> + <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humanitarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanitarian corridor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, humaneness</li></ol><p><i>гума́нный</i> (gumánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humane</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanoid (a being having the appearance or characteristics of a human)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) threshing-floor, cleared plot of land in farms on which stacks of grain are piled, accompanied by threshing and processing of grain</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) barn, shed for compressed grain</li><li>(<i>minced oath</i>) crap, shit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гꙋмьно</i> (gumĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gumьno</i>.</p><p>A euphemistic alteration of <i>говно́</i> (govnó, “shit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;medicine) bodily fluid; humoral</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>humoral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humus</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) threadbare clothes, rags, tatters</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial,&#32;figurative) (male or female) sloven, slut, slattern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;guňa, &#42;guna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain annoyingly, to bellyache</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk nasally; to talk through one's nose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) nasal (having a sound imparted by means of the nose)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whining, bellyaching</li></ol><p>From <i>гунд(е́ть)</i> (gund(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hun</li></ol><p>Modern learned borrowing from German <i>Hunne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кунг-фу́</i> (kung-fú)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>гу́на</i> (gúna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Siberia</i>) roe deer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>houri</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gourmet (gourmet&#59; a person who appreciates good food)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gourmand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drove, herd, flock</li><li>milled edge (of a coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>hurt</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gurt</i> (“belt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guru</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>गुरु</i> (guru, “venerable, respectable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gurukula (a type of residential school in India with pupils living near the guru)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>गुरुकुल</i> (gurukula).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crowd, throng</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gander (goose)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) pluck (offal from the thoracic part of slaughtered animals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫsakъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hussar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>gusar</i> (“highwayman”), from Italian <i>corsaro</i> (“corsair”), from Medieval Latin <i>cursarius</i> (“pirate”), from Latin <i>cursus</i> (“running”), from <i>currō</i> (“run”), from <i>&#42;ḱers-</i> (“to run”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hussar</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) reckless</li></ol><p><i>гуса́р</i> (gusár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гуса́р</i> (gusár): hussar</li></ol><p><i>гуса́р</i> (gusár) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гуса́к</i> (gusák)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gusli musician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gusli</li></ol><p><i>гу́сли</i> (gúsli) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) caterpillar (larva of a butterfly)</li><li>(mechanical,&#32;inanimate) caterpillar track</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>юсеница</i> (jusenica), Ukrainian <i>гу́сениця</i> (húsenycja), Bulgarian <i>гъсе́ница</i> (gǎsénica), Serbo-Croatian <i>гу̏сеница</i>/gȕsenica/гу̏сјеница/gȕsjenica, Slovene <i>gosẹ̑nica</i>, Czech <i>housenka</i>, Slovak <i>húsenica</i>, Old Polish <i>wąsienica</i>, Polish <i>gąsienica</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>husańca</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>guseńca</i>. Probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫsěnica</i>. The initial <i>г-</i> (g-) is prothetic or by conflation with Common Slavonic <i>гоущерица</i> (gušterica, “lizard”) (Russian <i>я́щерица</i> (jáščerica)), and is not related to <i>гусь</i> (gusʹ, “goose”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>гу́сеница</i> (gúsenica): caterpillar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caterpillar</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) caterpillar track</li></ol><p><i>гу́сеница</i> (gúsenica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anseriform</li></ol><p><i>гусь</i> (gusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a "goose fucker"</li></ol><p>From <i>гусь</i> (gusʹ, “goose”) and <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose bumps, goose pimples, goose flesh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) goose</li></ol><p><i>гусь</i> (gusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hussite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) gusli</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гоусли</i> (gusli), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫsli</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) ghusl (full-body wash taken by Muslims to restore the body to a state of purity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic غُسْل (ḡusl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gusli musician</li></ol><p><i>гу́сли</i> (gúsli) +&lrm; <i>-я́р</i> (-jár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thicken</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to become denser</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫstěti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>густо́й</i> (gustój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thickly, densely</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) not much, not a lot (to go on, to make use of, to help one with something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫstъ</i>. Cognate to Serbo-Croatian <i>gust</i>, Bulgarian <i>гъст</i> (gǎst), Macedonian <i>густ</i> (gust), Polish <i>gęsty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dense (having a viscous consistency)</li><li>thick, viscous (having a viscous consistency&#59; of liquid, fog, smoke, etc.)</li><li>rich, deep, heavy (<i>of a sound or voice</i>)</li><li>dark, deep (<i>of a color</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>густꙑи</i> (gustyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫstъ</i> (“dense”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickness; density</li></ol><p><i>густо́й</i> (gustój) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;ǵʰh₂éns</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫ̑sь</i>. Cognates include English <i>goose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in single file, filing (past), one after the other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose (meat)</li></ol><p>From <i>гуся́т-</i> (gusját-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>гусёнок</i> (gusjónok, “gosling”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>гусь</i> (gusʹ); goose</li><li>a type of Russian friction drum</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) game of the goose (board game)</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) jib (of a crane)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) cyma reversa (moulding)</li></ol><p><i>гусь</i> (gusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gosling</li></ol><p><i>гусь</i> (gusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Hutsul, Hutsulian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sediment, <i>(coffee)</i> grounds, <i>(beer)</i> lees, <i>(soup)</i> the body</li><li>thicket</li><li>thick, centre, middle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>гуща</i> (gušča), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫšča</i>, from <i>&#42;gǫstъ</i> (“dense”) + <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>густо́й</i> (gustój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>гу́сто</i> (gústo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>घी</i> (ghī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hz, hertz</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>герц</i> (gerc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Г, г.</li><li>The Roman letter G, g, also called же.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) a member of the Soviet or Russian state security such a KGB, FSB, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>госуда́рственная безопа́сность</i> (gosudárstvennaja bezopásnostʹ), <i>госбезопа́сность</i> (gosbezopásnostʹ), <i>ГБ</i> (GB) - pronounced гэ-бэ́ (gɛ-bɛ́).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;collective,&#32;politics) members of the Soviet or Russian state security (Cheka, NKVD, KGB, FSB, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>ГБ</i> (GB, “state security”) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá). <i>ГБ</i> (GB), short for <i>госуда́рственная безопа́сность</i> (gosudárstvennaja bezopásnostʹ) is pronounced гэ-бэ́ (gɛ-bɛ́).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) GDR, DDR, East Germany</li></ol><p><i>ГДР</i> (GDR) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) "hekanye", the non-standard pronunciation of letter <i>Г</i> (G), <i>г</i> (g) as [ɣ] or [ɦ] and [x] or [h] in devoiced positions, instead of the standard [ɡ] and [k] in devoiced positions as heard in Southern Russian (the European part, south from Voronezh), Ukrainian and Belarusian, also commonly by Russians or Russian speakers in Ukraine and Belarus.</li><li>(phonology,&#32;rare) "gekanye", the standard and common pronunciation of letter <i>Г</i> (G), <i>г</i> (g) as [ɡ] and [k] in devoiced positions. This is the feature of the standard Russian and the most common pronunciation in Northern and Central dialects and spread in most parts of Eastern Russian - Urals, Siberia and the Far East.</li></ol><p><i>гэ́кать</i> (hɛ́katʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p><p><i>гэ́кать</i> (gɛ́katʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak using the voiced velar fricative [ɣ] instead of the voiced velar plosive [g], which is usual for Ukrainian, Belarusian and Southern Russian speakers</li></ol><p><i>г</i> (g) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>下駄<rp>(</rp><rt>げた</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>geta</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blunt-nosed viper (<i>Macrovipera lebetina</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian گرزه (gorza).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religious slur</i>) giaour (infidel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كاور (gâvur), from Persian گاور (gâvor).</p><p>Replaced earlier (attested since 15th century) <i>гаур</i> (gaur) in 18th century, possibly under influence of Western European languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gyoza</li></ol><p>Japanese <i>餃子</i> (ぎょうざ, gyōza)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) girlfriend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>girlfriend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>d, deci- (0.1)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>деци-</i> (deci-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>d'Artagnan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dr., doctor (PhD or MD)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>до́ктор</i> (dóktor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>дом</i> (dom, “house, building”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>дере́вня</i> (derévnja, “hamlet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>древневерхненеме́цкий</i> (drevneverxneneméckij, “Old High German”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>древневерхненеме́цкий</i> (drevneverxneneméckij, “Old High German”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yes</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yes (used to confirm that the person being addressed is listening)</li><li>right, isn't it (used at the end of an affirmative sentence, turning what was said into a question, asking for confirmation)</li><li>really (used to express surprise or ask for confirmation)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "yes"</li><li>an affirmation in support of a cause (as used in slogans or appeals)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let, may [<i>with</i> finite verb]</li><li>Used as an emphasiser, usually at the start of a sentence or phrase. In many cases, emphasises impatience or irritation, but in others may simply emphasise surprise or an observation.</li><li>Used as an emphasiser before an imperative verb to express urgency.</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) and</li><li>but</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>da, deca- (x 10)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>да</i> (da), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;da</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>дека-</i> (deka-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;swear word,&#32;vulgar) for fuck's sake!, oh fuck! (expression of anger or disappointment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) right, isn't it (used at the end of an affirmative sentence, turning what was said into a question, asking for confirmation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>long live!, viva! (<i>for a singular person or thing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sarcastic</i>) yeah right, get off with you! (disbelief)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exclamation used to express annoyance or irony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey (<i>expressing pleasant surprise</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (oh) yes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) yes, milord</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) yes, milady</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>да</i> (da) +&lrm; <i>-с</i> (-s), which is an abbreviated form of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ)/<i>суда́рыня</i> (sudárynja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The English letter W, w.</li></ol><p>From English <i>double-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;non-standard colloquial) in order to, in order that, so that</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>да бꙑ</i> (da by). By surface analysis, <i>да</i> (da) +&lrm; <i>бы</i> (by).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let's [<i>with</i> imperfective infinitive]</li><li>to let, come on, c'mon [<i>with</i> perfective first-person plural future]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) goodbye, see you</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>to let [<i>with</i> perfective first-person plural future]</li><li>let us, let's [<i>with</i> imperfective infinitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;derogatory) skank, slut, whore (a female who has sex with many males)</li></ol><p><i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) davallia (<i>Davallia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Davallia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Davallia canariensis</i>, a species of fern from Macaronesia and Iberia.</li></ol><p><i>дава́ллия</i> (davállija, “<i>Davallia</i>”) +&lrm; <i>кана́рский</i> (kanárskij, “Canarian, relating to the Canary Islands”), a calque of translingual <i>Davallia canariensis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>to let, to allow</li><li>to hit, to strike, to clip</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to put out, to consent to sex</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dāvàti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>дава́ти</i> (daváty, “to give”), Belarusian <i>дава́ць</i> (davácʹ, “to give”), Bulgarian <i>давам</i> (davam, “I give”) (imperfective), Serbo-Croatian <i>да́вати</i>, <i>dávati</i>, Polish <i>dawać</i> (“to give”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend, to loan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give someone to understand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to tip [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘amount of money’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘recepient’</small>] (to give a small monetary gratuity to a service worker or servant)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to give [some money] for tea”. Compare Chinese <i>茶錢</i>&nbsp;/ <i>茶钱</i> (cháqián, “tip, gratuity; bribe”) and Burmese <i>လက်ဖက်ရည်ဖိုး</i> (lakhpak-ranyhpui&#58;, “bribe”), as well as French <i>pourboire</i> (“tip, gratuity”) and German <i>Trinkgeld</i> (“tip, gratuity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give one's word</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let oneself, to allow oneself (to be caught, to be cheated, etc.)</li><li>to come easily</li></ol><p><i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lately, recently</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>давѣ</i> (davě, “recently”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;davě</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) recent</li></ol><p>From <i>да́веча</i> (dáveča) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij) or Old East Slavic <i>давѣ</i> (davě) + <i>-eшний</i> (-ešnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winepress</li></ol><p><i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh, to lie heavy</li><li>to crush</li><li>to press, to squeeze</li><li>to suppress, to stifle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill, to destroy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put pressure on, to pressurize, to pressure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>давити</i> (daviti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dāvìti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke (be unable to breathe because of obstruction of the windpipe)</li></ol><p><i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throng, jam, crush</li></ol><p><i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pressure (physics: amount of force divided by area)</li></ol><p><i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for quite a while</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long ago</li></ol><p>From <i>давно́</i> (davnó) +&lrm; <i>-е́нько</i> (-énʹko, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, ancient, age-old, distant, bygone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;davьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) longstanding, old</li></ol><p><i>да́вний</i> (dávnij) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long ago</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>да́вний</i> (dávnij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó). Compare Czech <i>dávno</i> and Polish <i>dawno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remoteness (in time), antiquity; long standing; time limitation</li></ol><p><i>да́вний</i> (dávnij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long ago</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) once upon a time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Dagestani (native of Dagestan)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>дагеста́нец</i> (dagestánec) or <i>дагеста́нка</i> (dagestánka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daguerreotype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daguerreotype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dagestani (native of Dagestan)</li></ol><p>From <i>Дагеста́н</i> (Dagestán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dagestan; Dagestani</li></ol><p><i>Дагеста́н</i> (Dagestán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dadaist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;da že</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) really, is that so</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> да́же (dáže),&lrm; так (tak).</li></ol><p>Literally, “even so”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>when addressing one, familiar person; followed by an inflected perfective verb form [with the optional subject in between]</i>) the more insistent form of давай: to let</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>diver</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) diving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>diving</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digest (magazine of abridged articles)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>digest</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) dyke, dike</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dike</i>, <i>dyke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) daimyo (Japanese feudal lord)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>大名</i> (daimyō), from Middle Chinese <i>大名</i> (dàj-mjieng, “excellent one”), from <i>大</i> (“great”) + <i>名</i> (mjieng, “name”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>так</i> (tak)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dacian (member of the ancient Dacian people)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dako</i>.</p><p>From Latin <i>Dacus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dactyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>декали́тр</i> (dɛkalítr, “decaliter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalai Lama</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>далёкий</i> (daljókij)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>далеко́</i> (dalekó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dalieba (Chinese bread based on Russian rye bread, generally available in northeastern China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>大列巴</i> (dàlièbā), from <i>大</i> (dà, “big”) + <i>列巴</i> (lièbā, “bread”, Harbin dialect), the latter from Russian <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) alternative form of <i>да́льше</i> (dálʹše): farther, further; furthermore; then, next, afterwards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дале</i> (dale, “far, farther, further”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dalje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far, far off, far away</li><li>a long way</li></ol><p>From <i>далёкий</i> (daljókij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far far away, very far</li></ol><p><i>далеко́</i> (dalekó) +&lrm; <i>далеко́</i> (dalekó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;obsolete,&#32;poetic) far off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dahlia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatian</li></ol><p><i>далма́т-</i> (dalmát-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dalь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) Amur softshell turtle (<i>Pelodiscus maackii</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дальневосто́чный</i> (dalʹnevostóčnyj, “Far-Eastern”) +&lrm; <i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa, “turtle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a resident of Russian Far East</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Far East; Far Eastern</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Russian Far East</li></ol><p>From <i>да́льний</i> (dálʹnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>восто́к</i> (vostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare <i>Да́льний Восто́к</i> (Dálʹnij Vostók, “Far East”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>future, what has happened or will happen subsequently, later on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>further, subsequent (following in time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far, distant</li><li>distant (<i>of ancestry</i>)</li><li>long</li><li>long-term (<i>of securities</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) long-range (<i>of weapons, munitions, weapon fire</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transport) long-range, long-haul</li></ol><p><i>да́льний</i> (dálʹnij, “far”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj, “beating”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long-haul truck driver</li></ol><p>From <i>дальнобо́йный</i> (dalʹnobójnyj, “long-range”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik). Literally, “long-range guy”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-sighted, forward-looking, prudent</li></ol><p><i>да́льний</i> (dálʹnij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rangefinder</li></ol><p><i>да́льний</i> (dálʹnij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мер</i> (-mer), combining form of <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ, “to measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance, remoteness, range</li></ol><p><i>да́льний</i> (dálʹnij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daltonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>далёкий</i> (daljókij): further</li><li>comparative degree of <i>далеко́</i> (dalekó): further</li><li>furthermore</li><li>then, next, afterwards</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far, distant, remote</li><li>long way off, wide (of)</li><li>strange (to)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smart, clever</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dalekъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady</li><li>dance partner</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) queen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dame</i>, from Latin <i>domina</i> (“lady”), from <i>domus</i> (“house”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i>, from root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dem-</i> (“to build”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Damascene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dam, levee, dike</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>dam</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(draughts,&#32;checkers) king</li></ol><p><i>да́ма</i> (dáma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Damocles', of Damocles</li></ol><p><i>Дамо́кл</i> (Damókl) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword of Damocles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>да́ма</i> (dáma): dame, damsel, young lady, young woman</li></ol><p><i>да́ма</i> (dáma) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dumpster diving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dumpster diving</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lady's, ladies'</li></ol><p><i>да́ма</i> (dáma) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladies and gentlemen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Dane (member of a Germanic tribe)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) dan</li></ol><p>From Korean <i>단</i> (dan) and/or Japanese <i>段</i> (dan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Afar (a region in Ethiopia, a people residing in this region and their language)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afar (language of the Afar people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tributary <i>(nation, state, etc.)</i></li></ol><p><i>дань</i> (danʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a given</li></ol><p><i>да́нный</i> (dánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>data, facts, information</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given, present, this</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dentist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dentiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contribution, tribute</li><li>tributary</li><li>homage, tribute</li><li>toll</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>дань</i> (danĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;danь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dṓˀnis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taoism (Chinese philosophy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift, present</li><li>talent, aptitude</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>даръ</i> (darŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;darъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Darwinism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Darwinist, Darwinian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Darwinian</li></ol><p><i>Да́рвин</i> (Dárvin) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Dargin, a Dargwa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kumyk <i>дарги</i> (dargi, “Dargwa”) + <i>-и́нец</i> (-ínec), ultimately from Dargwa <i>дарган, дарккван</i> (dargan, darkkvan, the Dargwa autonym). Probably cognate with Dargwa <i>дарг</i> (darg, “inner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dargin, Dargwa</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dargwa, the Dargin language</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kumyk <i>дарги</i> (dargi, “Dargwa”) + <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij), ultimately from Dargwa <i>дарган, дарккван</i> (dargan, darkkvan, the Dargwa autonym). Probably cognate with Dargwa <i>дарг</i> (darg, “inner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donation</li></ol><p><i>дари́ть</i> (darítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dari</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gifter, grantor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to make a present, to present, to donate</li><li>to favour/favor, to bestow, to award</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dariti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дар</i> (dar) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free (of charge), gratuitous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) sponge, sponger, parasite, scrounger</li></ol><p><i>дармово́й</i> (darmovój, “free, gratuitous”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitism (about people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darmstadtium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slightly less common</i>) alternative form of <i>здаро́ва</i> (zdaróva)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift, talent</li><li>bestowal</li></ol><p><i>дарова́ть</i> (darovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to grant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;darovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дар</i> (dar) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gratis, for free, for nothing</li><li>in vain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ciborium (a receptacle used in the Eucharist)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дар</i> (dar).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dastarkhān</li></ol><p><i>дастарха́н</i> (dastarxán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date (point in time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date of birth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) dative (denoting the case of noun which expresses indirect object)</li></ol><p>From <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>δοτική</i> (dotikḗ, “related to giving”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dating (determining the date of something, e.g. a historical manuscript)</li><li>dating (noting the date of writing or executing a given document)</li></ol><p><i>дати́ровать</i> (datírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) to date (to note the date of writing or executing) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a document’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with a specific date’</small>]</li><li>to date (a historical manuscript, etc.) (to determine the date of something)</li></ol><p><i>да́та</i> (dáta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to date (back) to, to date from [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a specific date in the past’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дати́ровать</i> (datírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dating (determining the date of something, e.g. a historical manuscript)</li><li>dating (noting the date of writing or executing a given document)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danish language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Danish</li></ol><p>From older <i>да́цкий</i> (dáckij), already in 1602, from <i>дак</i> (dak) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), after Medieval Latin <i>Dacus</i> (“Dane”), <i>Dacia</i> (“Denmark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Dane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dane (person from Denmark or of Danish descent)</li></ol><p><i>Да́тска</i> (Dátska) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin). Already in 1630.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transducer, sensing unit, sending unit, gage, sensor</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>переда́тчик</i> (peredátčik, “transmitter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>(<i>followed by an infinitive [with the optional direct object in between, in the dative]</i>) to let, to allow</li><li>to organize, to create</li><li>to hit, to strike, to clip, to give it to someone</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to estimate someone's age from their appearance</li><li>(slang,&#32;figurative) to consent to sex</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dṓˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dédeh₃ti</i> (“to give”), whence English <i>donate</i>, <i>date</i> and <i>data</i>.</p><p>Also cognate with Sanskrit <i>ददाति</i> (dadāti), Sanskrit <i>दान</i> (dāna), Persian دادن and Latin <i>dō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give someone to understand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to circuit, to make a long detour around smth.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to tip [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘amount of money’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘recepient’</small>] (to give a small monetary gratuity to a service worker or servant)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to give [some money] for tea”. Compare Chinese <i>茶錢</i>&nbsp;/ <i>茶钱</i> (cháqián, “tip, gratuity; bribe”) and Burmese <i>လက်ဖက်ရည်ဖိုး</i> (lakhpak-ranyhpui&#58;, “bribe”), as well as French <i>pourboire</i> (“tip, gratuity”) and German <i>Trinkgeld</i> (“tip, gratuity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give one's word</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let oneself, to allow oneself (to be caught, to be cheated, etc.)</li><li>to come easily</li></ol><p><i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare Polish <i>dać się</i>, Italian <i>darsi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone with Down syndrome</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;vernacular,&#32;offensive,&#32;disparaging or ironic) downie, mongoloid, retard, spaz, spastic</li></ol><p>From <i>Да́ун</i> (Dáun), from <i>синдро́м Да́уна</i> (sindróm Dáuna), from the surname of British physician John Langdon Down, who described the syndrome in the 1860s.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Даун)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Down, County Down (a county of Northern Ireland, United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Down</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) downsizing (<i>reducing engine's capacity at same power or increasing engine's power without increasing capacity</i>)</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) shrinkflation</li><li>(<i>business</i>) downsizing (<i>mass layoff in the restructuring of a company</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>downsizing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a monastic university in Tibetan Buddhism, as well as a temple complex, especially one that is located in the Russian Far East</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolian <i>дацан</i> (dacan), itself borrowed from Tibetan <i>གྲྭ་ཚང།</i> (grwa tshang).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) dazibao, a wall-mounted newspaper etc., as a popular form of communication in China.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese 大字報／大字报 (<i>dàzìbào</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dacha, villa, country house</li><li>allotment; a (small) plot of land in the outskirts of Russian cities usually used by city dwellers for growing fruit and vegetables</li><li>giving</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;daťa</i>, from <i>&#42;dati</i> (whence Russian <i>дать</i> (datʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>да́ча</i> (dáča): (little) dacha</li></ol><p>From <i>да́ч(а)</i> (dáč(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summer resident; owner of a dacha</li></ol><p><i>да́ча</i> (dáča) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dacha</li><li>suburban (<i>of means of transport; i.e. taking passengers to dachas</i>)</li><li>summer (time when people go to dachas)</li></ol><p><i>да́ча</i> (dáča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Dashnak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>դաշնակ</i> (dašnak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) of or pertaining to Dashnaks</li></ol><p><i>дашна́к</i> (dašnák) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let there be...!, we want...!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;dated,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belgorod,&#32;Kursk,&#32;Smolensk,&#32;Voronezh,&#32;Southern Russia) to care; to look after</li></ol><p>From Ukrainian <i>дба́ти</i> (dbáty) and/or Belarusian <i>дбаць</i> (dbacʹ), from Old Ruthenian <i>дба́ти</i> (dbáti), possibly from Old Polish <i>dbać</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъbati</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>dbać</i>, Czech <i>dbát</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two (2)</li><li><i>(mark)</i> two <i>(out of five)</i>, poor; D mark, D grade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дъва</i> (dŭva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъ̏va</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;duwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i> (“two”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) icosahedron</li></ol><p><i>двадцать</i> (dvadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twenty-two-year period</li><li>twenty-second anniversary, twenty-second birthday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twenty-year period</li><li>twentieth anniversary, twentieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) twenty-year</li><li>twenty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>двадца́ть</i> (dvadcátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icosihenagon</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icosipentagon, 25-sided polygon</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icosagon</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) icositetrahedron</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icositetragon</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) 24 hours</li></ol><p>From the genitive of два́дцать четы́ре (dvádcatʹ četýre, “twenty-four”) and часово́й (časovój, “hour (relational)”), the latter itself from <i>час</i> (čas, “hour”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój, “<i>relational suffix</i>”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twenty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twenty roubles, dollars, etc.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (tram, bus, etc.) number twenty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty (20)</li></ol><p>From <i>два</i> (dva) +&lrm; <i>-дцать</i> (-dcatʹ), or "twice ten". This was an Old East Slavic dual form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-eight (28)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-two (22)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-nine (29)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-one (21)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twenty-first</li></ol><p><i>два́дцать</i> (dvádcatʹ, “twenty”) + <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj, “first”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-five (25)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-seven (27)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-three (23)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-four (24)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-six (26)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>20×, 20 times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>twice, on two occasions</li><li>2×, twice as much</li></ol><p><i>два</i> (dva) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) Great Feasts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) one of twelve</li></ol><p><i>двана́десять</i> (dvanádesjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) twelve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) A member of the online Dvach community.</li></ol><p>From <i>Двач</i> (Dvač) +&lrm; <i>-ер</i> (-er). Compare English <i>4channer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) two pair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) dodecahedron</li></ol><p><i>двенадцать</i> (dvenadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twelve-year period</li><li>twelfth anniversary, twelfth birthday</li></ol><p><i>двена́дцать</i> (dvenádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) twelve-year</li><li>twelve-year-old</li></ol><p><i>двена́дцать</i> (dvenádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) duodenum (first part of the small intestine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typically used in the compound term двенадцатиперстная кишка (duodenum)</li></ol><p>From двенадцать ("twelve") + перст ("finger") + -ный (adjective-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dodecagon</li></ol><p><i>двена́дцать</i> (dvenádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twelfth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelve (12)</li></ol><p>From <i>две</i>&nbsp;f (dve, “two”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>12×, twelve times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ): (a small) door</li></ol><p><i>дверь</i> (dverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) door</li></ol><p>From <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ): door</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>двьрь</i> (dvĭrĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvьrь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dwírs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwṓr</i>, whence English <i>door</i>, Lithuanian <i>durys</i>, Latin <i>forum</i>, Sanskrit <i>द्वार</i> (dvā́ra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two hundred (200)</li></ol><p>An Old East Slavic dual form from the Old Church Slavic dual <i>двѣстѣ</i> (dvěstě).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engine, motor</li></ol><p><i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internal combustion engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impellent, motive (causing motion)</li></ol><p><i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html>:/ˈdʲvʲiɡətʲ/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to set going</li><li>to move, to incite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvigati</i> (“to raise, lift, move up”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to move</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement, motion, stirring (also social and political)</li><li>traffic</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) light gymnastics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being moved, pushed (by); being motivated (by)</li></ol><p>Originally it was the present passive participle of the obsolete verb дви́жить, which explains the irregular spelling (instead of the expected spelling *дви́жемый).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propeller, propulsion device, driver, mover (something which propels)</li></ol><p><i>движе́ние</i> (dvižénije) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (small) engine, motor</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) engine</li><li>slider, cursor, arm, movable indicator, runner</li></ol><p><i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) movement (usually social), increased activity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) driving force, powerhouse, moving spirit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moving, driving, motive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to set going</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit, to cosh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvignǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to go crazy, to lose one's wits</li></ol><p><i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of Northern Dvina region</li></ol><p><i>Двина́</i> (Dviná) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine) (<i>non-standard</i>) duel</li></ol><p>Adaptation of Ukrainian <i>двобій</i> (dvobij) which, along with many other cognates, is eventually descended from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъva</i> (“two”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;bojь</i> (“fight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two (in a group together), two of them</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъvojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dual faith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarchy</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-faced</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bigamy</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje, “two”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>жена́</i> (žená, “wife”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo, “-ity”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doublethink</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a sign of the cross made with two fingers</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>перст</i> (perst) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colon <i>(punctuation)</i></li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>то́ч(ка)</i> (tóč(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil with only the lowest mark "two" (F) in his record.</li><li>(by extension) anyone who is a bad performer</li></ol><p>From <i>дво́йка</i> (dvójka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). The USSR and now the Russian Federation and other ex-Soviet republics used/use a five-point academic grading system where <i>два</i> (dva, “two, number 2”) denotes hardly any knowledge, below average, the first level of failing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any female who is a bad performer</li></ol><p><i>дво́йка</i> (dvójka) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to divide in two, to bifurcate</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) to plow a second time, to replow</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biquinary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;programming,&#32;computer engineering) binary, binomial</li></ol><p><i>дво́иц(а)</i> (dvóic(a), “pair; couple”) [ц → ч] + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two</li><li><i>(mark)</i> two <i>(out of five)</i>, poor; D mark, D grade</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) two, deuce</li><li>No. 2 (bus, tram, etc.)</li><li>(<i>rowing</i>) double, pair (a boat with two rowers)</li><li>two-piece suit</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>look-alike, double (a person resembling or standing for another)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъvojьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double</li><li>binary</li><li>dual</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twins</li></ol><p><i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one of two twins</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duality; duplicity; ambivalence</li></ol><p><i>дво́йственный</i> (dvójstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dual; double; twofold (consisting of two parts, or a pair of something)</li><li>double; dual; biune (exhibiting duality&#59; manifesting in two forms or ways)</li><li>bipartite (having two parties)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) dual (pertaining to a grammatical number in certain languages that refers to two of something)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) two-faced, double-faced; duplicitous (marked by deliberate deceptiveness)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ambivalent; ambiguous (expressing two contradictory feelings, beliefs, motivations, or meanings)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) dual polyhedron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yard</li><li>courtyard</li><li>homestead</li><li>court</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) royal family and its closest associates</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дворъ</i> (dvorŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvòrъ</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>forum</i> and Sanskrit <i>द्वार</i> (dvāra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dvorьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>двор</i> (dvor) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butler</li></ol><p><i>дворе́ц</i> (dvoréc) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>двор</i> (dvor): (a small) courtyard</li></ol><p><i>двор</i> (dvor) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>janitor</li><li>street cleaner</li><li>wiper, windshield wiper, windscreen wiper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) domestic servants</li></ol><p><i>двор</i> (dvor) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cur, mutt, mongrel</li></ol><p>From <i>дво́рный</i> (dvórnyj, “pertaining to yard”) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>дворня́га</i> (dvornjága): cur, mutt, mongrel</li></ol><p><i>дворня́га</i> (dvornjága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) court, yard</li><li>(relational,&#32;historical) house serf; menial</li></ol><p><i>двор</i> (dvor) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) palace</li></ol><p><i>дворе́ц</i> (dvoréc) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noble, nobleman (a man of noble rank)</li><li>armiger (person entitled to bear a coat of arms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дворяни́н</i> (dvorjanín).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gentry, nobleman</li><li>nobleman's</li></ol><p><i>дворян(и́н)</i> (dvorjan(ín)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) nobility; the nobles; gentry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(proscribed,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>двою́родный</i> (dvojúrodnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>denotes a relative in the same generation as the head noun but one degree greater in separation</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>дъвою родꙋ</i> (dŭvoju rodu, “of two generations”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). <i>дъвою</i> (dŭvoju) is a genitive dual form, from which <i>-ю-</i> (-ju-) was extracted and used analogously in <i>трою́родный</i> (trojúrodnyj), <i>четверою́родный</i> (četverojúrodnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first cousin <i>(uncle's or aunt's son)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandnephew, great-nephew <i>(nephew's son)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great uncle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first cousin once removed <i>(great uncle's son; different from двоюродный племянник)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first cousin once removed <i>(uncle's grandson; different from двоюродный дядя)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandnephew <i>(nephew's grandson)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(mark)</i> two <i>(out of five)</i>, D grade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double, twofold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>двоꙗкыи</i> (dvojakyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;d(ъ)vojakъ</i>, from <i>&#42;d(ъ)vojь</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-akъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two ways, in two forms, twofold</li></ol><p><i>двоя́кий</i> (dvojákij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubleness</li></ol><p><i>двоя́кий</i> (dvojákij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>bi-, di-, duo- (two, twice, double)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rowing</i>) A rowboat (or canoe or kayak) with two rows, meant to be used by two people.</li></ol><p>From <i>дву-</i> (dvu-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>весло́</i> (vesló, “oar”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “-er”, nominal suffix), literally “double-oarer”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) biaspectual (of verbs having two grammatical aspects)</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-headed, double-headed, bicephalous</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>глава́</i> (glavá) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-humped</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bactrian camel (<i>Camelus bactrianus</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “two-humped camel”. Compare <i>одного́рбый верблю́д</i> (odnogórbyj verbljúd, “dromedary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) dihedron</li></ol><p>From <i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “two-side-er”. Compare Ukrainian <i>двогра́нник</i> (dvohránnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) twenty-copeck coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two doors</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) having two parts</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) dicotyledonous</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>до́ля</i> (dólja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-house</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) dioecious</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) biune (two-in-one&#59; both two and one at the same time)</li></ol><p>From <i>дву-</i> (dvu-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj, “unified”). Compare Ukrainian <i>двоєди́ний</i> (dvojedýnyj), Belarusian <i>двуадзі́ны</i> (dvuadzíny), Polish <i>dwujedyny</i> and Bulgarian <i>двуеди́нен</i> (dvuedínen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two teeth</li><li>two-pronged</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-wheeled horse-drawn carriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twofold, double repeated, reiterated</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>крат</i> (krat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) a dipteran</li><li>(entomology,&#32;plural only) the order Diptera</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ое</i> (-oje, neuter noun-forming suffix), literally “two-winger; a two-wing”; calque of Ancient Greek <i>δίπτερος</i> (dípteros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dipteral, dipterous, two-winged</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-edged</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>ле́звие</i> (lézvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with two faces</li><li>double-faced, duplicitous, two-faced, (given to or marked by deliberate deceptiveness in behavior or speech)</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>лик</i> (lik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two leaves</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duplicity (intentional deceptiveness)</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-faced, two-faced, duplicitous (given to or marked by deliberate deceptiveness in behavior or speech)</li></ol><p><i>двули́чие</i> (dvulíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) birefringence</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>преломле́ние</i> (prelomlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-dimensional</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) bivariate</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) Great Feasts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-legged; bipedal</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;dated) dioxide</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>о́кись</i> (ókisʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbon dioxide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) A sign of the cross made with two fingers.</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>перст</i> (perst) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bisexual, androgynous</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-handed, bimanous</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) two-handled joiner's or carving tool</li></ol><p><i>двуру́ч(ный)</i> (dvurúč(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-handled</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>ру́ч(ка)</i> (rúč(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) double-dealer, turncoat</li></ol><p>Originally <i>&#42;двуру́чник</i> (&#42;dvurúčnik), from <i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) disyllabic</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>слог</i> (slog) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ambiguously (in an ambiguous manner)</li><li>obliquely</li></ol><p><i>двусмы́сленный</i> (dvusmýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambiguity</li><li>ambiguous expression, double entendre</li></ol><p><i>двусмы́сленный</i> (dvusmýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambiguous, equivocal</li><li>indecent, suggestive, risque</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>смы́сл(ить)</i> (smýsl(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>double bed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>double-barrelled shotgun</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate) prostitute</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate) chick, girl</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) female breasts, tits, boobs</li></ol><p><i>двуство́ль(ный)</i> (dvustvólʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-barrelled</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bivalve (of mollusc)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) French door, double doors</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bicuspid (of heart valve)</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>ство́рка</i> (stvórka) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-sided, two-sided</li><li>bilateral, bipartite, two-way</li><li>(<i>clothing</i>) reversible</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(construction,&#32;mechanical engineering) I-beam, H-beam</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>тавр</i> (tavr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>bi-, di-, duo- (two, twice, double)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two versts</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>верста́</i> (verstá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-year (relating to a period lasting for two years)</li></ol><p>From <i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-headed, double-headed</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two doors</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two inch</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>дюйм</i> (djujm) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-wheeled horse-drawn carriage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-wheeled</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-room</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-copeck coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-year period</li><li>second anniversary, second birthday</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-year</li><li>two-year-old</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) biennial</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-liter</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>литр</i> (litr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>двуме́рный</i> (dvumérnyj)</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double, for two</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-month (lasting two months)</li><li>two-month-old</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-meter</li><li>two-meter long</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-week (lasting two weeks)</li><li>two-week-old</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-chamber, bicameral</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>пала́та</i> (paláta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-party, bipartisan (involving two political parties)</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>па́ртия</i> (pártija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-hundredth, 200th</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military slang</i>) corpse, dead body, KIA</li></ol><p><i>двухсо́т</i> (dvuxsót) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p><p>From <i>груз 200</i> (gruz 200).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>double-barrelled shotgun</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-barrelled</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) in double measure</li><li>(relational,&#32;engineering) two-stroke</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-stroke engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) two-volume work</li></ol><p><i>двухто́м(ный)</i> (dvuxtóm(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-thousandth, bimillenary (ordinal form of 2000)</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) two-figured</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>фигу́ра</i> (figúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two hours</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) two-storey building</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) two-story</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) double-decker</li></ol><p><i>двух-</i> (dvux-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bilingualism</li></ol><p><i>двуязы́чный</i> (dvujazýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bilingual</li></ol><p><i>дву-</i> (dvu-) +&lrm; <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>децигра́мм</i> (decigrámm, “decigram”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) says (<i>used informally to mark reported speech</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>дѣеть</i> (dějetĭ, “to say”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>de facto (in fact or in practice)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>de facto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>de jure (by right)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>de jure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deadaptation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deactivate, to disable (to put something out of action)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>deactiv(ate)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) deanonymization</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>деанонимиза́ция</i> (deanonimizácija), itself borrowed from English <i>deanonymization</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дебелъ</i> (debelŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;debelъ</i>. Compare native dialectal <i>дебёлый</i> (debjólyj) and Belarusian <i>дзябёлы</i> (dzjabjóly).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>accounting</i>) debit (an entry in the left hand column of an account or a sum of money taken out of a bank account)</li><li>debt</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>dēbitum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) debit</li></ol><p><i>де́бет</i> (débet) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moron, imbecile, idiot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>débile</i>, from Latin <i>debilis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) moronism, imbecility</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idiotism (<i>about something very idiotic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>деби́л</i> (debíl) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moron, imbecile, idiot</li></ol><p><i>дебил</i> (debil) +&lrm; <i>-оид</i> (-oid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moron, imbecile, idiot</li></ol><p><i>дебил</i> (debil) +&lrm; <i>-ус</i> (-us).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asinine, moronic</li></ol><p><i>деби́л</i> (debíl) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) debtor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēbitor</i> (“debtor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accounts receivable (total monetary amount)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;finance) debit; debtor's; receivable</li></ol><p><i>дебито́р</i> (debitór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debauch (act of debauchery)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>débauche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rowdy, debauchee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>débaucheur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickets, jungle, wilderness, wilds, bush</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) maze, labyrinth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дъбрь</i> (dŭbrĭ) : <i>дьбрь</i> (dĭbrĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьbrь</i> (“valley, ravine”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dubris</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰubr-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰewbʰ-</i> (“deep”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debut</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) opening</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>début</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debutant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>débutant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to début, to make one's début</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>debütieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) or <i>дебю́т</i> (debjút) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) debut album</li></ol><p><i>дебю́т</i> (debjút) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) debut</li></ol><p><i>дебю́т</i> (debjút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) maiden, maid, virgin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děva</i>. Via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> cognate with <i>де́ти</i> (déti), <i>дитя́</i> (ditjá), <i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ)) Latin <i>fēmina</i>, <i>fētus</i>, <i>fīlius</i>, and many more.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Дева)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Virgo (constellation of the zodiac containing the bright binary star Spica, in the shape of a set of a virgin)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Virgo (zodiac sign for the virgin, covering August 23 — September 22)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>device (equipment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>device</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) devaluation (depreciation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Devalvation</i>, from French <i>dévaluation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Devanagari</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>देवनागरी</i> (devánāgarī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place</li><li>to do with</li><li>to leave, to mislay</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>деть</i> (detʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>, whence English <i>do</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to get (to another place)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put oneself, to go away</li></ol><p><i>дева́ть</i> (devátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother-in-law <i>(husband’s brother)</i>. His wife is one’s невестка.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děverь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dayh₂wḗr</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>dieveris</i>, Sanskrit <i>देवृ</i> (devṛ́), Armenian <i>տեգր</i> (tegr), Hindi <i>देवर</i> (devar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deviation</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) drift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>devise</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>dīvidō</i>. Doublet of <i>дефи́с</i> (defís), <i>дивиде́нд</i> (dividénd), and <i>диви́зия</i> (divízija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damsel, maid, maiden, girl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děvica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girlhood, maidenhood (period of life of a girl before marriage)</li></ol><p><i>деви́ца</i> (devíca) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>girlish</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) maiden; maidenly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) virgin, unblemished</li></ol><p>From <i>де́вица</i> (dévica, “(<i>obsolete or poetic</i>) maiden”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij); also stressed as <i>деви́ца</i> (devíca) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a marriage rite on a night before the wedding</li><li>bachelorette party, hen party, hen night</li></ol><p><i>деви́ца</i> (devíca) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maiden name (married woman's original last name)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;low colloquial,&#32;derogatory) wench, broad</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whore, tart, strumpet</li></ol><p><i>де́ва</i> (déva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Czech <i>děvka</i> — <i>dívka</i>, Polish <i>dziwka</i> — <i>dziewka</i> distinction.</p><p>Compare typologically Czech <i>nevěstka</i> (&lt; <i>nevěsta</i> + <i>-ka</i>, whence also <i>nevěstinec</i>, a natively formed term for “brothel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, little girl (<i>before the age of puberty</i>)</li></ol><p><i>де́ва</i> (déva) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, teenage girl (<i>at the age of puberty</i>)</li></ol><p><i>де́вочка</i> (dévočka) +&lrm; <i>подро́сток</i> (podróstok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) virginal membrane, hymen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virgin (<i>male</i>)</li></ol><p><i>де́вственный</i> (dévstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virginity, chastity</li></ol><p><i>де́вственный</i> (dévstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virginal (being or resembling virgin)</li></ol><p><i>де́вство</i> (dévstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) maidenhood</li></ol><p><i>де́ва</i> (déva, “maiden”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, lass (<i>from the age of puberty to womanhood</i>)</li><li>miss (informal but common form of address to a young woman&#59; may also apply to middle-aged women working in the service industry, e.g., sales)</li><li>girlfriend</li></ol><p><i>де́ва</i> (déva) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) girls</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>дівча́та</i> (divčáta), the plural of Ukrainian <i>дівча́</i> (divčá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) girl</li><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;derogatory</i>) gal, girl, inexperienced woman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) girlie, lassie</li></ol><p><i>девч(о́нка)</i> (devč(ónka)) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety (90)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>девѧносъто</i> (devęnosŭto), attested since 12th c., of unclear origin. With no parallels outside East Slavic—other Slavic branches point to <i>&#42;devętь desętъ</i> (literally “nine tens”)—likely to be a late dialectal innovation (even more so considering that the higher decades are less frequent and more prone to innovations). Many explanations have been proposed and recently compared by Anikin, who cautiously supports <i>*devę(t)no sъto</i> 'nine-related hundred'.</p><p>Other hypotheses include Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥(d)ḱomt</i> (literally “ninth ten”), from <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i> (whence <i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ)) and <i>&#42;déḱm̥t</i> (whence <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ)), with the ending influenced by <i>съто</i> (sŭto, “hundred”). Compare Latin <i>nōnāgintā</i> and Ancient Greek <i>ἐνενήκοντα</i> (enenḗkonta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-eight (98)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-two (92)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-nine (99)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-one (91)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-five (95)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-seven (97)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-three (93)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-four (94)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-six (96)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ninety-year period</li><li>ninetieth anniversary, ninetieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>девяно́сто</i> (devjanósto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ninety-year</li><li>ninety-year-old</li></ol><p><i>девяно́сто</i> (devjanósto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninetieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inula, elecampane (<i>Inula</i> spp., especially <i>Inula helenium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;devęsilъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ninth part, one ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine (in a group together), nine of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) enneahedron</li></ol><p><i>девять</i> (devjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nine-year period</li><li>ninth anniversary, ninth birthday</li></ol><p><i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nine-year</li><li>nine-year-old</li></ol><p><i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nine-banded armadillo (<i>Dasypus novemcinctus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>девятипо́ясный</i> (devjatipójasnyj, “nine-belted”) +&lrm; <i>бронено́сец</i> (bronenósec, “armadillo”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enneagon</li></ol><p><i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nine-storey building</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(digit)</i> nine</li><li>group of nine</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) nine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) No 9 bus (tram, etc)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) top corner of the goal</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Nickname of the VAZ-2109, a 5-door hatchback produced in the USSR since 1987.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nineteen-year period</li><li>nineteenth anniversary, nineteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>девятна́дцать</i> (devjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nineteen-year</li><li>nineteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>девятна́дцать</i> (devjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enneadecagon</li></ol><p><i>девятна́дцать</i> (devjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nineteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nineteen (19)</li></ol><p>From <i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ, “nine”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>19×, nineteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>девѧтъ</i> (devętŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;devę̃tъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dewintas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine (9)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>девѧть</i> (devętĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;devętь</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine hundred (900)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>9×, nine times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>degenerate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēgenerātus</i> (“degenerated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>degeneration (mostly in biological contexts)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) degeneracy</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) devolution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēgenerātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to degenerate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>degenerieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>degradation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to degrade, to degenerate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>dégrader</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar works</li></ol><p><i>дегтя́рь</i> (degtjárʹ, “tar worker”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expert taster (<i>e.g. of wine</i>), degustator</li></ol><p>Derived from New Latin <i>dēgustātor</i>, or formed analogously from <i>дегуста́ция</i> (degustácija, dɛgustácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tasting (<i>e.g. of wine</i>), degustation</li></ol><p><i>дегуст(и́ровать)</i> (degust(írovatʹ), dɛgust(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>dégustation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste (<i>e.g. wine</i>)</li></ol><p>French <i>déguster</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), possibly through German <i>degustieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandfather</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) old man</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) forefathers</li><li>(<i>military&#32;slang</i>) soldier who is approaching demobilization</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dědъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deadline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>deadline</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grandfather; grandfather's</li></ol><p><i>дед</i> (ded) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;military) dedovshchina, mobbing, hazing, bullying (<i>in the army</i>)</li></ol><p>From the army slang <i>дед</i> (ded, “old-timer”) +&lrm; <i>-овщина</i> (-ovščina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;rare) elderly; grandfather's, grandparents'</li></ol><p><i>дед</i> (ded, “grandfather”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deduction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;endearingly) grandfather, granddad, pops</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;endearingly) old man</li></ol><p><i>дед</i> (ded) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) alternative form of <i>диду́х</i> (didúx, “didukh”)</li></ol><p>Calque of Ukrainian <i>діду́х</i> (didúx). By surface analysis, <i>дед</i> (ded, “grandfather”) +&lrm; <i>-ух</i> (-ux, augmentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дед</i> (ded)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>grandfather</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) old man</li></ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) top of the house, ninety in the lotto game</li></ol><p><i>дед</i> (ded) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grandfather's</li></ol><p><i>де́душка</i> (déduška) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>дееприча́стие</i> (dejepričástije, “adverbial participle, gerund, verbal adverb”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adverbial participle, gerund, verbal adverb</li></ol><p><i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>прича́стие</i> (pričástije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present adverbial participle, present gerund</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) past adverbial participle, past gerund</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capacity</li><li>efficiency, vitality</li><li>(<i>law</i>) competence, legal capacity</li></ol><p><i>дееспосо́бный</i> (dejesposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective</li><li>able</li><li>(<i>law</i>) competent</li></ol><p><i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kneading trough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děža</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰoyǵʰyeh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to knead (clay)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deja vu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>déjà-vu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be on duty, to keep vigil, to keep watch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on duty</li><li>standing</li><li>open extra hours (<i>of a shop</i>)</li><li>on call</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person on duty/on call</li></ol><p>Univerbation of French <i>de jour</i> (“for a day”) with <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duty, watch</li></ol><p><i>дежу́р(ный)</i> (dežúr(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disinformation</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>дезинформа́ция</i> (dezinformácija, dɛzinformácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(formal,&#32;officialese) to disavow</li></ol><p>From French <i>désavouer</i>, likely through German <i>desavouieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maladaptation (<i>esp. of social behaviors</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maladaptive (<i>esp. of social behaviors</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociology</i>) to make (someone) maladapted; to isolate, to disturb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decontaminate (to neutralize or remove dangerous substances)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>désactiver</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deserter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>déserteur</i> or German <i>Deserteur</i>. First attested in 1710.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to desert, to defect</li></ol><p>Possibly German <i>desertieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) desertion</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) debt evasion</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dereliction of duty</li></ol><p><i>дезерти́р</i> (dezertír) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decomposition, disintegration, disintegration, disruption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disinfection</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>désinfection</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дез-</i> (dez-) +&lrm; <i>инфе́кция</i> (infékcija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disinfect</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>desinfizieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). By surface analysis, <i>дез-</i> (dez-) +&lrm; <i>инфици́ровать</i> (inficírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disinformation</li></ol><p><i>дез-</i> (dez-) +&lrm; <i>информация</i> (informacija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deodorant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) deoxyribonucleic acid, DNA</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deoxyribonucleic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disorient (to cause to lose orientation or direction)</li><li>to misinform, to lead astray</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) deism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deinterlacing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>deinterlacing</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) deist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>efficacy, efficiency</li></ol><p><i>де́йственный</i> (déjstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective</li></ol><p><i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>action, activity</li><li>effect, efficacy</li><li>influence, impact</li><li>(military,&#32;engineering,&#32;mathematics) operation</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) act</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣиствиѥ</i> (děistvije). By surface analysis, <i>де́йство</i> (déjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html>:/dʲɪ(j)stˈvʲitʲɪlʲnə/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, actually, truly, indeed</li></ol><p><i>действи́тельный</i> (dejstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present participle active</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) past participle active</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>real number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality, actuality</li><li>validity (of a document)</li></ol><p><i>действи́тельный</i> (dejstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>real, actual, valid</li><li>(<i>law</i>) current</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) active</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) real</li></ol><p><i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active voice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) action</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣиство</i> (děistvo). By surface analysis, <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do an action or activity</li><li>to affect, to have an effect on</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣиствовати</i> (děistvovati). By surface analysis, <i>де́йство</i> (déjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p><p>Same as above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agent (active power or cause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dramatis personae</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>active, functioning, in force</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active volcano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) deuterium (isotope of hydrogen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>дека́брь</i> (dekábrʹ) or <i>дека́бря</i> (dekábrja, “December”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sounding board</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>deca- (x10)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Decembrist (sympathizer of the Decembrist revolt)</li></ol><p><i>декабрь</i> (dekabrʹ, “December”) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>December</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>декѧбрь</i> (dekębrĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>декѧбр҄ь</i> (dekębrʹĭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>Δεκέμβριος</i> (Dekémbrios). Ultimately from Latin <i>december</i>, from <i>decem</i> + Latin <i>-ber</i>, from <i>-bris</i>, an adjectival suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) December</li></ol><p><i>дека́брь</i> (dekábrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period of ten days</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decadence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décadence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decadent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décadent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decadence</li></ol><p>From <i>декаде́нт</i> (dekadént) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) decadal (relating to a decade, a period of ten days or ten years)</li></ol><p>From <i>дека́да</i> (dekáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decaliter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dean (senior official in college or university)</li><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) decane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dekan</i>, from Late Latin <i>decānus</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Decan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dean's office</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dekanat</i>, from <i>Dechanat</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>decanatus</i>, from <i>decanus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decanter (vessel or receptacle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decant (to slowly pour so as not to disturb the sediment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décanter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decathlon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) deckle, tympan (a device in a papermaking machine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Deckel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recitation, declamation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) false pathos, phrasemongering</li></ol><p><i>деклам(и́ровать)</i> (deklam(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declaim, to recite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declaration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proclaim, to declare, to lay down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>déclassé</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>décolletage, décolleté</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décolleté</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decommunization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>décor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decorativeness, decorativity, ornamentality</li><li>picturesqueness</li></ol><p><i>декорати́вный</i> (dekoratívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decorative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décoratif</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decorator</li></ol><p>From <i>декор(и́ровать)</i> (dekor(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor), or borrowed from English <i>decorator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decoration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decorate</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>dekorieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) decorum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décorum</i>, from Latin <i>decōrus</i> (“proper, decent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decree, edict</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>декре́тный о́тпуск</i> (dekrétnyj ótpusk); maternity leave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēcrētum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) decree</li></ol><p><i>декре́т</i> (dekrét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) parental leave, maternity leave, family leave</li></ol><p>From the Decree of the All-Russian Central Executive Committee of December 22, 1917 (January 4, 1918) "On insurance in Case of Illness".</p><p>Literally, “decree leave”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adz, adze (cutting tool, used in railway business)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dechsel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doing, making</li></ol><p><i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The one who does something; doer, maker, acting agent.</li></ol><p>From <i>делать</i> (delatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to do</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dělati</i> (“to do”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) to look (as if) surprised or puzzled. (Lit., "to make large eyes".)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pretend, to affect (to make a false display of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a mountain out of a molehill</li></ol><p>Literally, “to make an elephant of a fly”, from a Latin proverb "elephantem ex musca facere", recorded in w:Paraphrases of Erasmus (1523). See w:Make a mountain out of a molehill.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to set the tone</li></ol><p>Literally, “to make weather”, an approximate calque of French <i>faire la pluie et le beau temps</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shop (to visit shops)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propose (to ask for one's hand in marriage) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to put a brave face on it, to grin and bear it</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>faire bonne mine à mauvais jeu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to honour/honor, to give credit</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>faire honneur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, to get, to grow, to turn</li><li>to happen (with, to), to be going on</li></ol><p><i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delegate, deputy, representative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēlēgātus</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>дэлега́т</i> (deljehát), Czech <i>delegát</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>delegat</i>, Polish <i>delegat</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>делѐга̄т</i>, Slovak <i>delegát</i>, Slovene <i>delegat</i>, Ukrainian <i>делега́т</i> (delehát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delegation (a group of delegates)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1768. Borrowed from Polish <i>delegacja</i>, from French <i>délégation</i>, from Latin <i>delegatio</i>, from <i>delego</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delegation</li></ol><p><i>делеги́ровать</i> (delegírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to send as a delegate</li><li>(<i>also&#32;software</i>) to delegate</li></ol><p>French <i>déléguer</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, partition</li><li>point <i>(on a scale)</i></li></ol><p><i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;derogatory</i>) businessman, moneymaker, operator</li></ol><p><i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delicacy; dainty; delicatessen; tidbit; gourmet food</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>délicatesse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>politely, tactfully, nicefully</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) delicately</li></ol><p><i>делика́тный</i> (delikátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tact, tactfulness, delicacy</li></ol><p><i>делика́тный</i> (delikátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polite, considerate, tactful</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) delicate (<i>of a subject</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) delicate, fragile</li></ol><p>From German <i>delikat</i> or French <i>delicat</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) dividend (а number that is to be divided by another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divisible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delirium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) divisor (a number that another is to be divided by)</li></ol><p><i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide</li><li>to share, to exchange</li><li>to confide, to tell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děliti</i> (“to divide”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;doyl-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰayl-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰoyl-</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>dǣlan</i> (English <i>deal</i>) and Lithuanian <i>dailyti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be divided</li><li>to be divisible (only imperfective)</li><li>to share (perfective: подели́ться (podelítʹsja))</li></ol><p><i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>дела́</i> (delá): affairs, matters, things</li></ol><p><i>дела́</i> (delá) +&lrm; <i>-и́шки</i> (-íški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affair, matter, concern</li><li>work, business</li><li>art, science</li><li>deed, act, action</li><li>(<i>law</i>) case, investigation</li><li>(<i>law enforcement</i>) file, dossier</li><li>cause</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dělo</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>, whence English <i>do</i>, <i>deed</i>, <i>deem</i>, <i>doom</i>, and via Latin, <i>fact</i> and <i>affair</i>.</p><p>Other cognates include, via Ancient Greek, English <i>theme</i>, <i>thesis</i>, Sanskrit <i>दधाति</i> (dadhāti, “to put”) Sanskrit <i>धातृ</i> (dhātṛ, “creator”) and <i>धातु</i> (dhātu, “layer, element, root”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>busily</li></ol><p><i>делови́тый</i> (delovítyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business-like character, business-like manners</li></ol><p><i>делови́тый</i> (delovítyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>businesslike</li></ol><p><i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-ови́тый</i> (-ovítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) business, work, working</li><li>businesslike</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flippant, uppity</li></ol><p><i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filing clerk; secretary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clerical work, office work, records management (management of official documents)</li></ol><p><i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>произво́дство</i> (proizvódstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) delulu (delusional)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>delulu</i>, a clipping with reduplication of <i>delusional</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>efficient, businesslike</li><li>sensible</li></ol><p><i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delta (the Greek letter Δ&#47;δ (d))</li><li>delta (<i>of a river</i>)</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;sciences) delta (change in a quantity)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deltoid muscle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δέλτα</i> (délta), whereas the sense of a deltoid is a clipping of <i>дельтови́дная мы́шца</i> (dɛlʹtovídnaja mýšca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hang glider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>deltaplane</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolphin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Delphin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;humorous&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>де́ло</i> (délo)</li></ol><p><i>де́ло</i> (délo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plot of land for cultivation or cutting down</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) share, portion</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) share of total work allocated to someone</li></ol><p>From <i>деля́на</i> (deljána, “plot of land for cultivation”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ, “to divide”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharing (out), distribution</li></ol><p><i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) division, distribution</li></ol><p><i>делёж</i> (deljóž) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demagogue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demagogy, demagogism, demagoguery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demarcation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>démarche</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>démarche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dematerialization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;informal) demob</li><li>(animate,&#32;informal) demobee</li></ol><p>From <i>демобилиза́ция</i> (demobilizácija, dɛmobilizácija), since the 1950s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of fabric made of cotton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) <i>демикото́н</i> (a type of fabric made of cotton)</li></ol><p><i>демикото́н</i> (demikotón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demilitarization/demilitarisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>диминути́в</i> (diminutív)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demiurge</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Демиург)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Demiurge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demobilization</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>démobilisation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demobilize (<i>e.g. troops</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to discharge</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>démobiliser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be demobilized, to stop military service as a result of demobilization</li></ol><p><i>демобилизова́ть</i> (demobilizovátʹ, dɛmobilizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demogeron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demographic</li></ol><p><i>демогра́фия</i> (demográfija, “demography”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demography</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democrat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democratization</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) structural changes in the Russian Army in 1917, which, despite their original goals, have resulted in dissolution of this army</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to democratize/to democratise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democracy</li><li>democratism</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>démocratisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>democratically (in a democratic way)</li></ol><p><i>демократи́ческ(ий)</i> (demokratíčesk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>democratic</li></ol><p><i>демокра́тия</i> (demokrátija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democratic socialism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democratic centralism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, unpretentious, humble; like or in the manner of the common man</li><li>affordable or accessible for the ordinary person</li><li>egalitarian</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>démocrat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democracy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>democratia</i> and Ancient Greek <i>δημοκρατία</i> (dēmokratía). First attested in 1704 as <i>димократия</i>, mimicking the Greek pronunciation.</p><p>The current form "демократия" most likely stems from German <i>Demokratie</i>. Had it been borrowed from French or Polish, it would have yielded "демокрация".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>демокра́тия</i> (demokrátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) demon (evil spirit)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) daemon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demonization (the act of demonizing or something demonized)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to demonize (to describe or represent as evil or diabolic, usually falsely)</li></ol><p>From a European language, maybe German <i>dämonisieren</i>, + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomastics</i>) demonym</li></ol><p>Internationalism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demonic</li></ol><p><i>де́мон</i> (démon) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demonstrator (a person involved in a demonstration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an emphatic manner</li></ol><p><i>демонстрати́вный</i> (demonstratívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demonstrative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) demonstration (public display, political protest, or show of military force)</li><li>demonstrative</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) diversionary action</li></ol><p><i>демонстра́ция</i> (demonstrácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demonstration (show, display)</li><li>demonstration (march, protest)</li><li>demonstration (show of military force)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) mass procession</li><li>(<i>military</i>) diversionary action</li></ol><p><i>демонстр(и́ровать)</i> (demonstr(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demonstrate, to exhibit, to show</li></ol><p>From German <i>demonstrieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dismantling, disassembling, dismantlement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>démontage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismantle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disband, to abolish</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>démonter</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). By surface analysis, <i>де-</i> (de-) +&lrm; <i>монти́ровать</i> (montírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>де́мон</i> (démon); little demon</li></ol><p><i>де́мон</i> (démon) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) demoralization, corruption of morals</li><li>(<i>military</i>) demoralization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demoralize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) dumping (selling goods at less than their normal price, especially in the export market)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dumping</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) the state of mind of a rabid radical democrat</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) rabid radical democrats</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>демокра́тия</i> (demokrátija, “democracy”) +&lrm; <i>шизофрени́я</i> (šizofreníja, “schizophrenia”). Used when the convictions of the radical democrats are compared to schizophrenia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>denazification</li><li>(politics,&#32;neologism,&#32;euphemistic) to act of attacking military structures and personnel in Ukraine.</li></ol><p>For the sense of the 2022 invasion of Ukraine, derived from accusations against the Ukrainian government of being a Nazi government due to the usage of the Azov Battalion into its armed forces, which used fascistic symbols.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denazify (remove Nazi or fascistic influences or activities)</li><li>(politics,&#32;neologism,&#32;euphemistic) to attack military structures and personnel in Ukraine.</li><li>(neologism,&#32;colloquial,&#32;slang,&#32;Ukraine) to attack Russian soldiers participating in the 2022 invasion of Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>денацифика́ция</i> (denacifikácija) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>денацифікува́ти</i> (denacyfikuváty), Belarusian <i>дэнацыфікава́ць</i> (denacyfikavácʹ), Polish <i>denazyfikować</i>.</p><p>For the sense of the 2022 invasion of Ukraine, derived from accusations against the Ukrainian government of being a Nazi government due to the usage of the Azov Battalion into its armed forces, which used fascistic symbols. Later repurposed by Russian-speaking Ukrainians and supporters of Ukraine in mockery towards Russian nationalist rhetoric.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandy, a man very concerned about his clothes and his appearance.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dandy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arboretum (place of many varieties of trees)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) dendrite</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) dendrite</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>δενδρίτης</i> (dendrítēs, “of or pertaining to a tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) dendrology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dendrochronology</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron) + <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) half-copeck coin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>деньга́</i> (denʹgá, “money”)</li></ol><p><i>де́ньга</i> (dénʹga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi)</li></ol><p><i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monetary, pecuniary (relating to money)</li></ol><p><i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Denisovan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box stall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) dawn</li><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) morning star</li></ol><p>From <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>де́нно и но́щно</i> (dénno i nóščno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) day and night (all the time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;relational) day, daytime</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ден(ь)</i> (den(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;anatomy,&#32;medicine,&#32;dentistry) dental</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>dental</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>denuclearization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) valet, batman, aide-de-camp (a man's personal male attendant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day, daytime</li><li>afternoon, early evening</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дьнь</i> (dĭnĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьnь</i> (“day”). Compare Belarusian <i>дзень</i> (dzjenʹ), Ukrainian <i>день</i> (denʹ), Bulgarian <i>ден</i> (den), Sanskrit <i>दिन</i> (dina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) birthday</li></ol><p>Literally, “jam day”. First attested in 1998. From a scene in <i><i>Малы́ш и Ка́рлсон</i> (Malýš i Kárlson, “Little Brother and Karlsson”)</i>, a Russian cartoon adaptation of <i>Karlsson-on-the-Roof</i> by Astrid Lindgren, where Karlsson is wishing the boy a happy birthday while eating jam but, since the Russian <i>варе́нье</i> (varénʹje, “jam”) rhymes with <i>рожде́ние</i> (roždénije, “birth”), he confuses the words and wishes him a happy <i>jam</i> day instead.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>All Hallows' Day (Christian feast day)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: День всех святых)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>All Saints' Day (feast day)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>до́брый день</i> (dóbryj denʹ, “good day, good afternoon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from day to day, day after day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>independence day (any of various festivals celebrated across the world about a country becoming independent from foreign rule)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all the livelong day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(numismatics,&#32;historical) denga (an old Russian coin)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) money, dough</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) coin, note</li></ol><p>Compare Bashkir <i>тәңкә</i> (təñkə, “ruble, silver coin”), and see there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, cf. Kazakh <i>теңге</i> (teñge), Bashkir <i>тәңкә</i> (təñkə) perhaps from the Russian дань. First used in the singular form <i>де́ньга, деньга́</i> (dénʹga, denʹgá, “ancient coin”), preserved in modern Russian as <i>деньга́</i> (denʹgá, “money, dough (<i>colloquial</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi) money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi, “money”): (<i>colloquial</i>) (big) money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>де́ньги</i> (dénʹgi, “money”): money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>де́нежки</i> (dénežki, “money (diminutive)”): (<i>nonstandard</i>) money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>де́нежки</i> (dénežki, “money (diminutive)”): (<i>nonstandard</i>) money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>день</i> (denʹ): day</li></ol><p><i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>денёк</i> (denjók): day</li></ol><p><i>денёк</i> (denjók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deoccupation (cessation of occupation (occupying) of a place)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to deoccupy (to remove an occupation from (a place))</li></ol><p>From <i>де-</i> (de-) +&lrm; <i>оккупи́ровать</i> (okkupírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department; board</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>diplomacy</i>) dispatch</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) telegram</li><li><i>(patry of post sendings)</i> dispatch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Depesche</i>, from French <i>dépêche</i>. Further from Latin <i>dis-</i> + Latin <i>impedicō</i> (whence also <i>импи́чмент</i> (impíčment)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transport</i>) depot, station, warehouse (company or facility that provides maintenance, repair and parking for rolling stock, usually rail)</li><li>station, firehouse, fire station (facility that accommodates fire trucks and special equipment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the 18th century from French <i>dépôt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deposit (money placed in an account)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depositor</li><li>deposit account, savings account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deportation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;formal</i>) to deport (to evict, especially from a country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>depressiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) depression, slump, decline</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) depression, dejection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy, assemblyman, delegate</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>dēputātus</i>. Attested since the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) deputy, delegate</li></ol><p><i>депута́т</i> (deputát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;neologism) deputinization</li></ol><p><i>де-</i> (de-) +&lrm; <i>путиниза́ция</i> (putinizácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>дере́вня</i> (derévnja, “hamlet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dörbet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merlin (<i>Falco columbarius</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dervish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish درویش (derviş), from Classical Persian درویش (darvēš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corncrake, crake</li><li>nail puller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corncrake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lignification (turning to wood)</li></ol><p><i>деревене́ть</i> (derevenétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stiffen</li><li>to grow numb</li><li>to lignify, to become wood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;obsolete) wood; wooden</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;деревѣнъ</i> (&#42;derevěnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dervěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>country, rural, rustic; (<i>relational</i>) village</li></ol><p><i>дере́вня</i> (derévnja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yokel, bumpkin, hick, redneck (unsophisticated or rural person)</li></ol><p>From <i>дереве́нский</i> (derevénskij) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дере́вня</i> (derévnja): (a small) village</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village, hamlet</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) the countryside, the rural population</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;both feminine and masculine with animacy) yokel, bumpkin</li><li>(collective,&#32;dialectal) a tree</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a place which is not a wild field</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a rural community</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a bunch of fallen trees</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дере́внꙗ</i> (derévnja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrvьňa</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latvian <i>druva</i> (“cornfield, plough-land”) and Lithuanian <i>dirvà</i> (“field, land”), also Lithuanian <i>dirvónas</i> (“fallow field, set-aside land”). Baltic borrowing (with later semantic change from plough-land to homestead and then to hamlet) is likely considering the lack of cognates in other Slavic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li><li>wood (only <i>singular</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дерево</i> (derevo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dervo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i>, whence English <i>tree</i>. Doublet of <i>дре́во</i> (drévo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodworking shop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joiner, woodworker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woodworking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woodworking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felling machine, tree-felling machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дере́вня</i> (derévnja): (a small) village</li></ol><p><i>дере́в(ня)</i> (derév(nja)) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>де́рево</i> (dérevo): sapling, small tree</li></ol><p><i>де́рево</i> (dérevo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>де́рево</i> (dérevo): sapling, small tree</li></ol><p><i>де́рево</i> (dérevo) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lignify, to become wood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ligneous, woody</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common tormentil (a low, rosaceous herb of Europe, <i>Potentilla erecta</i>)</li></ol><p><i>де́рево</i> (dérevo) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodiness</li></ol><p><i>деревя́нный</i> (derevjánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wood; wooden</li><li>woody, ligneous</li></ol><p>First attested in year 1615. From <i>деревяный</i> (derevjanyj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Secondary form is also attested in Old Czech <i>dřevěnný</i> (beside <i>dřěvěný</i>, <i>dřěvený</i>, <i>drěvený</i>?, <i>dřevený</i>) and dial. Polish <i>drzewianny</i> (beside obs. <i>drzewiany</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) wooden coat (coffin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>деревя́нный</i> (derevjánnyj)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>деревꙗнъ</i> (derevjanŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dervěnъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derwēno-</i> / <i>&#42;derwoyno-</i>, from <i>&#42;deru-</i> (see <i>&#42;dóru</i>). By surface analysis, <i>де́рево</i> (dérevo, “tree, wood”) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj, “made from”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of wood</li><li>stump, wooden leg</li></ol><p><i>де́рево</i> (dérevo, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boxthorn, wolfberry (genus <i>Lycium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dereza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, power (a powerful country)</li><li>globus cruciger, orb (a globe with a cross used as a symbol of royal power)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1344. Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дьржава</i> (dĭržava), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьržava</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva, “strength; estate; state (government), power”), Belarusian <i>дзяржа́ва</i> (dzjaržáva, “state (government)”), Old Church Slavonic <i>дрьжава</i> (drĭžava), <i>дръжава</i> (drŭžava), Bulgarian <i>държа́ва</i> (dǎržáva, “state (government)”), Macedonian <i>држава</i> (država, “state (government)”), Serbo-Croatian <i>др̀жава</i> (“possession; state (government)”), Slovene <i>držȃva</i> (“composition, content; state, power; possession”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>država</i> (“possession; power, state”), Slovak <i>dŕžava</i> (“state, power”), Polish <i>dzierżawa</i> (“lease; (<i>archaic</i>) possession”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sovereign, holding supreme power</li><li>majestic</li></ol><p><i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) holder</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) holder</li></ol><p><i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, to keep, to support</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>держати</i> (deržati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьržati</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>firmus</i> and English <i>dare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get by the balls</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep someone on a short leash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep an eye peeled, to be on the alert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang tough; to hold one's head high</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang tough; to hold one's head high</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold on</li><li>to hold (by, to), to stick (to)</li><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;formal</i>) to bear/conduct/comport oneself, to behave</li><li>to keep, to stick (to)</li><li>to hold out, to stand firm; to hold one's ground</li><li>to restrain oneself, to bear up</li><li>to last</li></ol><p><i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Jerusalem thorn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, to venture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrzati</i>. Cognate with English <i>dare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be insolent, to sass (to talk back)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brazen, impudent, impertinent, insolent, cheeky, pert</li><li>bold, daring, audacious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дрьзъкъ</i> (drĭzŭkŭ), as <i>&#42;дёрзкий</i> (&#42;djórzkij) would be expected, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrzъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>де́рзый</i> (dérzyj) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insolently, brazenly, impudently</li><li>boldly, audaciously</li></ol><p><i>де́рзкий</i> (dérzkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, to venture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrznǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudence, impertinence, insolence, rudeness, cheek</li><li>daring, boldness, audacity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дрьзость</i> (drĭzostĭ), as <i>&#42;дёрзость</i> (&#42;djórzostʹ) would be expected. By surface analysis, <i>де́рзый</i> (dérzyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>derivative (financial instrument whose value depends on the valuation of an underlying instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leatherette (type of artificial leather)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dermatine</i>, apparently from Ancient Greek <i>δερμάτινος</i> (dermátinos, “made of leather”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dermatitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit, crap (excrement)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) shit, crap (stuff, things)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) scumbag, shithead (a bad person)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) shit, any recreational drug</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;derьmо</i>. Related to <i>драть</i> (dratʹ, “to tear off”). Sense of a recreational drug is a semantic loan from English <i>shit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;rude) crappy, shitty, lousy</li></ol><p><i>дерьмо́</i> (derʹmó, “shit”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) democracy</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>дерьмо́</i> (derʹmó, “crap, shit”) +&lrm; <i>демокра́тия</i> (demokrátija, “democracy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistle thrush (<i>Turdus viscivorus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot (a shot of an alcoholic drink)</li><li>to scoop up, to hog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landing <i>(of troops)</i></li><li>troops landed, landing party</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>descente</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landing, dropping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paratrooper, commando</li></ol><p>From <i>деса́нт</i> (desánt) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;relational) airborne assault</li></ol><p><i>деса́нтный</i> (desántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>штурмовой</i> (šturmovoj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) landing</li></ol><p><i>деса́нт</i> (desánt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dessert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dessert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dessert wine, sweet wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dessert</li></ol><p><i>десе́рт</i> (desért) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;agriculture</i>) desiccant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>A particle introducing reported speech; (he/she) says, (they) say</li><li>A particle introducing the explanation of someone's thoughts, gestures, or behavior; meaning, like, as if saying</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>дѣ́етъ</i> (dě́jet) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum <i>(soft tissue around the teeth)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>дѧсна́</i> (djasná), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dęsna</i>, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃dónts</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>), (<i>figurative</i>) right hand</li><li>(<i>hydrology</i>), (<i>historical</i>), (<i>dated</i>) tributary of a water body flowing in from the right side relative to its stream direction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desovietization</li></ol><p><i>де-</i> (de-) +&lrm; <i>советиза́ция</i> (sovetizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despotism, tyranny</li></ol><p>First attested in 1768. Borrowed from French <i>despotisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despotism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destabilization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) destalinization</li></ol><p><i>де-</i> (de-) +&lrm; <i>сталиниза́ция</i> (stalinizácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>destruktiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenth (one of ten equal parts of a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenfold</li><li>consisting of ten of anything</li><li>denary</li></ol><p><b>десятеричный</b> &larr; десятеро (<i>collective</i> ten) + -ичн- &larr; десять (ten)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective number</i>) ten (in a group together), ten of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decathlete</li></ol><p>From <i>десятибо́рье</i> (desjatibórʹje, “decathlon”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>athletics</i>) decathlon</li></ol><p>From <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>боро́ть(ся)</i> (borótʹ(sja), “to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) decahedron</li></ol><p><i>десять</i> (desjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ten-year period, decade</li><li>tenth anniversary, tenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ten-year secondary school</li></ol><p><i>десятиле́тие</i> (desjatilétije) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ten-year; decennial</li><li>ten-year-old</li></ol><p><i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dessiatina (old Russian unit of land measurement, approximately 2.7 acres)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) tithe</li></ol><p><i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>десятери́чное</i> (desjateríčnoje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myriagon</li></ol><p><i>де́сять ты́сяч</i> (désjatʹ týsjač) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decagon</li></ol><p><i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ten (about numeral, card, bank note, bus number etc.)</li><li>top ten</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) ten</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a sentence of ten years in prison</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ten, set of ten</li><li>tens, ten's place</li><li>decade</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dozens of, many</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>десѧтъ</i> (desętŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;desętъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;deśimtás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱm̥tós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten (10)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>десѧть</i> (desętĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;desętь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;déśimt</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déḱm̥t</i>, whence English <i>ten</i> and <i>deci-</i>. Russian <i>-дцать</i> (-dcať) is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dḱom̥t</i> lately influenced by Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;desętь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ten Commandments</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten thousand (10,000)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>10×, ten times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specification in detail</li></ol><p><i>детализ(и́ровать)</i> (detaliz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detailed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to specify in detail</li></ol><p>German <i>detalisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detail, nicety</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) minutiae</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) part, component</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>détail</i>, from <i>détailler</i> (“to set apart”). Further from Latin <i>de-</i> + Latin <i>tāliō</i>. Attested since the middle of 19th century.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>подро́бность</i> (podróbnostʹ) (akin to <i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in detail, minutely</li></ol><p><i>дета́льный</i> (detálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detailed, minute</li></ol><p><i>дета́ль</i> (detálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) children, kids</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>дітвора</i> (ditvora) during XIX century, which was derived from Old East Slavic <i>дѣтъва</i> (dětŭva) (compare derivation of мошкара (moškara)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dětъva</i>. Compare Polish <i>dziatwa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>де́тский дом</i> (détskij dom, “children's home”): children's home, orphanage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detective</li><li>detective story (<i>book or film</i>), whodunit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) detective</li></ol><p><i>детекти́в</i> (dɛtɛktív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detector</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>detector</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) determinant</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) determinant, determining factor</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>determinant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) determiner (class of words)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>déterminatif</i>, from Latin <i>dētermināre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) husky / hefty / stalwart lad / chap, big fellow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>де́ти</i> (déti): (cute little) children, kids</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-и́шки</i> (-íški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) child, offspring</li><li>(figurative,&#32;inanimate) work, creation, brainchild</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče). Compare Belarusian <i>дзе́цішча</i> (dzjécišča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby, honey (term of endearment)</li><li>baby, child (endearingly or ironically)</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childful (having any children, not childless)</li></ol><p>Backformation from бездетный.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detonator (device used to detonate an explosive device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) childbearing, procreation</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рожде́ние</i> (roždénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>де́тка</i> (détka): child, kid</li></ol><p><i>де́тка</i> (détka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>LGBTQ</i>) detransition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>detransition</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Detroit</li></ol><p><i>Детро́йт</i> (Detrójt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>де́тский сад</i> (détskij sad, “kindergarten”): kindergarten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child's room, children's room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playground</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) child; child's, children's</li><li>childish</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) baby</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>children's home, orphanage (a public institution for the care and protection of children whose parents have died or can no longer care for them)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindergarten</li></ol><p><i>де́тский</i> (détskij, “children's”) +&lrm; <i>сад</i> (sad, “garden”), calque of German <i>Kindergarten</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood</li></ol><p><i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place</li><li>to do with</li><li>to leave, to mislay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>, whence English <i>do</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to vanish, to get lost</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to escape, to get away</li></ol><p><i>деть</i> (detʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young one, youngster</li><li>baby, young child</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young of an animal</li><li>baby animal, cub</li></ol><p><i>дет-</i> (det-) +&lrm; <i>-ёныш</i> (-jónyš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defect, blemish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective, handicapped</li></ol><p><i>дефект</i> (defekt) +&lrm; <i>-ивный</i> (-ivnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective</li></ol><p><i>дефе́кт</i> (defékt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defenestration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>dēfenestrātiōnem</i>, Latin <i>dē</i> (“from; out”) + <i>fenestra</i> (“window”) + <i>-ātiō</i> (suffix indicating an action or process).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defile (thin canyon)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) defiling, marching, parade</li><li>fashion parade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>definition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyphen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Divis</i> (whence also Polish <i>dywiz</i>), from Latin <i>dīvīsus</i>, from Latin <i>dīvidō</i>. Doublet of <i>деви́з</i> (devíz), <i>дивиде́нд</i> (dividénd), and <i>диви́зия</i> (divízija). Compare Slovene <i>deljaj</i> (“hyphen”) (akin to Russian <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)).</p><p>On the other hand compare Czech <i>spojovník</i> (“hyphen”), Serbo-Croatian <i>спо́јница</i> (akin to Russian <i>пая́ть</i> (pajátʹ), <i>спайка</i> (spajka)), Slovene <i>vezaj</i> (“hyphen”) (akin to Russian <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ), <i>свя́зывать</i> (svjázyvatʹ), <i>свя́зка</i> (svjázka)), Polish <i>łącznik</i>, Belarusian <i>злучо́к</i> (zlučók) (akin to Russian <i>отлучи́ть</i> (otlučítʹ), <i>разлу́чить</i> (razlúčitʹ), <i>cлучи́ть</i> (clučítʹ), <i>слу́чка</i> (slúčka)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deficit</li><li>deficiency, shortage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprofitable, showing a loss</li><li>scarce, hard-to-get, in short supply</li></ol><p><i>дефици́т</i> (deficít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defoliant (an agent used to defoliate plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) default</li><li>(electronics,&#32;computing) default</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) default</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>default</i>. First attested in 1998.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deformation</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>deformatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deforming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deform</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>déformer</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Central Asian or Iranian peasant</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Persian دهقان (dehqân, “landowner”) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin), cf. Tajik <i>деҳқон</i> (dehqon), Uzbek <i>dehqon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dechlorinate, to remove chlorine from something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decentralization</li></ol><p><i>децентрализ(ова́ть)</i> (dɛcentraliz(ovátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decentralize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>décentraliser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>deci- (10-1)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>decimus</i> (“tenth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decibel (a common measure of sound intensity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decigram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decilitre (a unit of volume, decilitre)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>decillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>33</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decimetre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>décimètre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get cheaper</li></ol><p><i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheapness</li></ol><p>From <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дёшево</i> (djóševo)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decipher, to decode, to decrypt</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>dechiffrieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decipher, to decode, to decrypt</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the base of German <i>dechiffrieren</i> + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), by analogy with <i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decipherer</li></ol><p><i>дешифров(а́ть)</i> (dešifrov(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;usually about low-quality goods) the cheapest, made of cheap materials or with cheap processes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj, “cheap”)</li></ol><p><i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) low price</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a cheap, worthless thing; cheapie</li></ol><p>From <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheap, empty, worthless</li><li>cheap, inexpensive, low (of a price)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дешевъ</i> (deševŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;deševъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>de-escalation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deed, act, action, exploit (heroic or extraordinary deed)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dějanьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doer, actor, agent (one who acts&#59; a doer)</li><li>(<i>famous</i>) figure, personality</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dějateľь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>famous female personality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activity</li><li>work</li></ol><p><i>де́ятельный</i> (déjatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>active, energetic</li></ol><p><i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to do; to accomplish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѣꙗти</i> (dějati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dějati</i>, an extension of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děti</i> (“to do, to say”) (whence <i>деть</i> (detʹ)). Cognate with Belarusian <i>дзе́яць</i> (dzjéjacʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) jazz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) jazz band (group of musicians who play jazz music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jazz band</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jazz</li></ol><p><i>джаз</i> (džaz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jacuzzi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jacuzzi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Armenia,&#32;familiar) an affectionate term of address, dear, darling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>ջան</i> (ǰan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>junk food (food with little or no nutritional value)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>junk food</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cezve, Turkish coffee pot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جزوه (cezve).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> J, j</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) J-pop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>J-pop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goitered gazelle, black-tailed gazelle (<i>Gazella subgutturosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed via a Turkic language from Persian جیران (jêrân), from Middle Mongol (ǰērän, “antelope”), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yẹgren</i> (“yellow, chestnut”).</p><p>Compare Azerbaijani <i>ceyran</i> (“gazelle”), Kyrgyz <i>жейрен</i> (jeyren, “goitered gazelle”), Turkish <i>ceylan</i> (“antelope, gazelle”), Uzbek <i>jayron</i> (“goitered gazelle”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>дзерен</i> (dzeren, “dzeren, Mongolian gazelle”), from Kalmyk <i>зеерң</i> (zeerñ, “antelope”) or Mongolian <i>зээр</i> (zeer, “gazelle”), from Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;jexeren</i>, commonly derived from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yẹgren</i> (“yellow, chestnut”), whence also Kazakh <i>жайран</i> (jairan, “dzeren, Mongolian gazelle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackpot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jackpot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackfruit (fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jackfruit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jam (<i>food</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jam</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jam (food)</li></ol><p><i>джем</i> (džem) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jumper (woolen sweater or pullover)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jumper</i> with some phonetic changes. See also шу́ба (šúba), зипу́н (zipún).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) djent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gentleman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;historical&#32;or&#32;ironic) pirate, thief, robber, bandit, fraud</li></ol><p>Literally, “gentleman of fortune”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gentleman</li></ol><p><i>джентльме́н</i> (džentlʹmɛ́n) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>джири́д</i> (džiríd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) jerky (lean meat cured and preserved by cutting into thin strips and air-drying in the sun)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jerky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jersey, functional shirt</li><li>jersey (type of fabric)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jersey</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) jet lag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jetlag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) jet pack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jetpack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> G, g</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Djiboutian</li></ol><p><i>Джибу́ти</i> (Džibúti) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Azerbaijan</i>) cheater, cheat, one who cheats in card or board games (especially one who insists that s&#47;he didn't cheat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>cığal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>джида́</i> (džidá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Caucasus, Central Asia</i>) dzhigit (djigit, jigit) (a skilled horseman in the Caucasus and Central Asia).</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Cf. Karakalpak <i>jigit</i>, Kazakh <i>жігіт</i> (jıgıt), Kyrgyz <i>жигит</i> (jigit), Karachay-Balkar <i>джигит</i> (cigit), Southern Altai <i>јиит</i> (ǰiit), <i>јигит</i> (ǰigit), Shor <i>чиңет</i> (čiŋet), Khakas <i>чиит</i> (çiit) etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>джири́д</i> (džiríd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oleaster, silverberry (<i>Elaeagnus</i> genus and species, especially <i>Elaeagnus angustifolia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>жийда</i> (jiyda) / <i>jiyda</i>, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yidge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>джи́зья</i> (džízʹja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) jizya</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic جِزْيَة (jizya),</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gym (sporting facility specialized for lifting weights and exercise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gym</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>gin</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>iūniperus</i>.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>jałowcówka</i> (&lt; <i>jałowiec</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jinn, jinni, genie <i>(any of a class of spirits from Islamic mythology)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic جِنّ (jinn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) denim</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jeanswear, denimwear</li><li>(copywriting,&#32;ironic) advertisement content of low quality, containing a lot of cliches</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>джи́нсы</i> (džínsy): jeans</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jeans</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) denim</li></ol><p>From <i>джи́нсы</i> (džínsy) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) or from <i>джи́нсы</i> (džínsy) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeans</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jeans</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jeep</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jeep, SUV</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jeep</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jereed</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish جرید (cirid) (Turkish <i>cirit</i>), from Arabic جَرِيد (jarīd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jujitsu (method of self-defence established in Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>柔術</i> (jūjutsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;Islam) jihad (holy war undertaken by Muslims)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic جِهَاد (jihād, “struggle; effort”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jogging (form of exercise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jogging</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jogging (form of exercise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jogging</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;video games) joystick</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>joystick</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) joker</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) A trick-taking card game popular in Russia and other countries of the former USSR.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>joker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jockstrap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jockstrap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joule (derived unit of energy, work and heat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>joule</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) Friday prayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic جُمْعَة (jumʕa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jungle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jungle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially&#32;Central Asia</i>) Chinese chive</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Mandarin <i>韭菜</i> (jiǔcài), via Kazakh <i>жұсай</i> (jūsai), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) initialism of <i>дома́шнее зада́ние</i> (domášneje zadánije); hw, homework</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ДЗ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) initialism of <i>дома́шнее зада́ние</i> (domášneje zadánije); hw, homework</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(business,&#32;historical) zaibatsu (a large business conglomerate founded under the Empire of Japan, generally controlled by a single family or individual)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>財閥<rp>(</rp><rt>ざいばつ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>zaibatsu</i>). Doublet of <i>чебо́ль</i> (čebólʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zelkova tree</li><li>zelkova wood</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ძელქვა</i> (ʒelkva), from <i>ძელი</i> (ʒeli, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>ქვა</i> (kva, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Caucasian zelkova (tree or wood), <i>Zelkova carpinifolia</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) keyaki, Japanese zelkova (tree or wood), <i>Zelkova serrata</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zen Buddhism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeta (the Greek letter Ζ (Z)&#47;ζ (z))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ζῆτᾰ</i> (zêtă). Doublet of <i>зи́та</i> (zíta) and <i>зэд</i> (zɛd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dzongkha (<i>language</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) judo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>柔<rp>(</rp><rt>じゅう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby><ruby>道<rp>(</rp><rt>どう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>jūdō</i>, literally “the soft way”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> D, d</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>di- (two, twice, double)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) diabetes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagnosis (identification of the nature and cause of an illness)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>διάγνωσις</i> (diágnōsis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagnostician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagnostician</li></ol><p><i>диагно́ст</i> (diagnóst) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagnostics, diagnosis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διαγνωστικός</i> (diagnōstikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to diagnose</li></ol><p><i>диагно́ст(ика)</i> (diagnóst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diagnostic</li></ol><p>From <i>диагно́стика</i> (diagnóstika, “diagnostics, diagnosis”) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij, “-ish, -ic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>line</i>) diagonal</li><li>(<i>cloth</i>) diagonal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagram, graph, chart</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διάγραμμα</i> (diágramma). Compare Ukrainian <i>діагра́ма</i> (diahráma), Belarusian <i>дыягра́ма</i> (dyjahráma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diadem, crown, tiara; a royal headdress</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>διάδημα</i> (diádēma, “a decorative wreath-shaped headdress”), from Ancient Greek <i>διαδέω</i> (diadéō, “to encircle, bind, wrap around”) + <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deacon</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>διάκονος</i> (diákonos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dialect</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>диале́кт</i> (dialékt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) dialect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dialectics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dialektik</i>, from Late Latin <i>dialectica</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>διαλεκτική</i> (dialektikḗ, “the art of argument through interactive questioning and answering”), from <i>διαλεκτικός</i> (dialektikós, “relating to dialogue”), from <i>διαλέγομαι</i> (dialégomai, “to participate in a dialogue”), from <i>διά</i> (diá, “through, across”) + <i>λέγειν</i> (légein, “to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dialectical</li></ol><p><i>диале́кт(ика)</i> (dialékt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Marxism,&#32;philosophy) dialectical materialism</li></ol><p>Literally, “dialectical materialism”, a calque of German <i>dialektischer Materialismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dialect; dialectal</li></ol><p><i>диале́кт</i> (dialékt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dialectological</li></ol><p>From <i>диалектоло́гия</i> (dialektológija, “dialectology”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “-ical”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dialectology</li></ol><p>From <i>диале́кт</i> (dialékt, “dialect”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija, “-logy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dialogue/dialog</li></ol><p>Derived from Old French <i>dialoge</i>, from Late Latin <i>dialogus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>διάλογος</i> (diálogos, “conversation, discourse”), from <i>διά</i> (diá, “through, inter”) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech, oration, discourse”), from <i>διαλέγομαι</i> (dialégomai, “to converse”), from <i>διά</i> (diá) + <i>λέγειν</i> (légein, “to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) diamond</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) name of one of small fonts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Marxism,&#32;philosophy) dialectical materialism</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>диалекти́ческий материали́зм</i> (dialektíčeskij materialízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) diameter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διάμετρος</i> (diámetros).</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>średnica</i>, Czech <i>průměr</i>, Slovene <i>premer</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>промјер</i>, Macedonian <i>пречник</i> (prečnik), Serbo-Croatian <i>пречник</i> (Russian <i>попере́чник</i> (poperéčnik)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diametrically</li></ol><p><i>диаметра́льный</i> (diametrálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antipodal, antipodean, diametrically opposed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>range; (radio) band; gamut</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>διαπασῶν</i> (diapasôn), that is <i>διά</i> (diá, “through”) + <i>πασῶν</i> (pasôn, “all”) (<i>χορδῶν</i> (khordôn, “notes”)), "through all (notes)".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>UK</i>) diarrhoea, (<i>US</i>) diarrhea</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>διάρροια</i> (diárrhoia, “through-flowing”), from <i>διά</i> (diá, “through”) + <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “flow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) diarsenide (any arsenide containing two atoms of arsenic per molecule)</li></ol><p><i>ди-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>арсени́д</i> (arseníd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarchy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diaspora (dispersion of a group of people)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) diaspore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) twinspur (<i>Diascia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Diascia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) diaphragm</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) diaphragm shutter</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) stop (<i>of the shutter</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) diaphragm (<i>female condom</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>διάφραγμα</i> (diáphragma, “partition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diaphragm</li></ol><p><i>диафра́гм(а)</i> (diafrágm(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mythology,&#32;folklore) demon, harbinger of misfortune</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;divъ</i> (“evil spirit”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diva</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>diva</i> (“diva, goddess”), from Latin <i>dīva</i> (“goddess”), feminine of <i>dīvus</i> (“divine, divine one; notably a deified mortal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>furniture</i>) sofa, couch, divan</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) divan, diwan (the council of state in a Muslim country)</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) divan, diwan (a collection of poems)</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish دیوان (dîvân), from Classical Persian دیوان (dīwān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed settee, daybed, settee bed, sofa-bed</li></ol><p>From <i>дива́н</i> (diván) +&lrm; <i>крова́ть</i> (krovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic</i>) armchair generals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sofa, couch, divan</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;ironic) armchair (giving unqualified, uninformed advice or opinions)</li></ol><p><i>дива́н</i> (diván) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic</i>) armchair critic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дива́н</i> (diván): (a small) sofa, couch, settee, divan</li></ol><p><i>дива́н</i> (diván) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saboteur (especially foreign), wrecker</li></ol><p>From <i>диве́рсия</i> (divérsija, “subversion”) +&lrm; <i>-ант</i> (-ant, agent suffix), with the first element borrowed ultimately from Latin <i>dīversiōnem</i>, via one or several western intermediaries (cf. Polish <i>dywersja</i>, French <i>diversion</i>), and with the second element borrowed from French <i>-ant</i>. No such word as <i>&#42;diversant</i> exists in French.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sabotage, subversion</li><li>subversive</li></ol><p><i>диве́рсия</i> (divérsija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diversification (act of diversifying)</li></ol><p>German <i>Diversifikat(ion)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sabotage (сommitting an explosion, arson or other actions not related to the conduct of combat, aimed at destroying or damaging factories, fortresses, transport infrastructure facilities and other objects important for defense)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1706. Ultimately from Latin <i>dīversiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>диве́рсия</i> (divérsija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divertissement, variety show, light entertainment</li><li>(<i>music</i>) divertimento</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>divertissement</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dividend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) battalion (<i>in artillery or cavalry</i>)</li><li>(aviation,&#32;nautical) squadron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Division</i> or Polish <i>dywizjon</i>, from French <i>division</i>, from Latin <i>dīvīsiō</i>. Doublet of <i>диви́зия</i> (divízija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) division; divisional</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) cavalry battalion</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) artillery battalion</li><li>(relational,&#32;aviation) squadron</li></ol><p><i>диви́зия</i> (divízija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p><p><i>дивизио́н</i> (divizión) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) division</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>dywizja</i>, from French <i>division</i>, from Latin <i>dīvīsiō</i>. Doublet of <i>дивизио́н</i> (divizión).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to surprise</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ди́во</i> (dívo) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;diviti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to marvel <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>диви́ть</i> (divítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>marvellously, wonderfully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marvelous, wonderful, delightful, lovely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;divьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wonder, miracle, marvel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dȋvo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to marvel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Cossack) girl (<i>usually in the Ukrainian or dialectal (Kuban or Don river) contexts</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ді́вчина</i> (dívčyna) or <i>дівчи́на</i> (divčýna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digamma (the archaic Ancient Greek letter Ϝ&#47;ϝ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δίγαμμα</i> (dígamma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) digraph (two-character sequence)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) digraph (a pair of letters, especially a pair representing a single phoneme)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>didact</li></ol><p>Ancient Greek <i>διδακτός</i> (didaktós)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>didactician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>didactics</li></ol><p>Derived via Western European languages from Ancient Greek <i>διδακτικός</i> (didaktikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>didactic</li></ol><p>From <i>дида́ктика</i> (didáktika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) DJ</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>DJ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) DJ set; a set used by a disk jockey.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digital, information technology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>digital</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>didukh (a Ukrainian decoration made from sheaves of wheat tied up with colorful ribbons, which is used in rituals around Christmas and Epiphany, and traditionally believed to contain ancestor spirits)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>діду́х</i> (didúx), from <i>дід</i> (did, “grandfather”) + <i>-ух</i> (-ux, augmentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sharp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dièse</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>δίεσις</i> (díesis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diet; dietary</li></ol><p><i>дие́та</i> (dijéta, diéta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>design, style</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>design</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designer (person who designs&#59; male or female)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>designer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) designer, designer's</li></ol><p><i>диза́йнер</i> (dizájner) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) to design</li></ol><p><i>диза́йн</i> (dizájn) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diesel fuel</li><li>diesel engine</li><li>diesel motor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Diesel</i>, named after inventor&#32;Rudolf Diesel.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diesel</li></ol><p><i>ди́зель</i> (dízelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) dysentery</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Dysenterie</i>; ultimately from Latin <i>dysenteria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>δυσεντερία</i> (dusentería).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually in reference to Bulgaria</i>) threshing sledge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>дика́ня</i> (dikánja), from a variant of Ancient Greek <i>τυκάνη</i> (tukánē, “threshing sledge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heathen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savage</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) shy person, unsociable person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) non-official holiday-maker (without a tour group or a prepaid hotel)</li></ol><p><i>ди́кий</i> (díkij) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wild, savage, untamed</li><li>uncivilized</li><li>odd, bizarre</li><li>outrageous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wildly</li><li>with a wild look</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) terribly, awfully, badly</li></ol><p><i>ди́кий</i> (díkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porcupine</li></ol><p>From <i>ди́кий</i> (díkij, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz, “looking”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) porcupine</li></ol><p><i>дикобра́з</i> (dikobráz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something surprising, unprecedented or unheard of; rarity, curiosity, an unusual or outlandish object</li></ol><p><i>ди́кий</i> (díkij) +&lrm; <i>-о́вина</i> (-óvina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>дико́вина</i> (dikóvina): (small) wonder, a strange or unusual thing or phenomenon, gimmick</li></ol><p><i>дико́вина</i> (dikóvina) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bizarre, strange, peculiar, unusual, out of the way</li></ol><p><i>дико́вина</i> (dikóvina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wildness, savagery</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) shyness, unsociableness, lack of sociability</li></ol><p><i>ди́кий</i> (díkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictation (an activity in school where the teacher reads a passage aloud and the students write it down)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictatorship</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>dictatura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;and&#32;figurative</i>) to dictate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictation, dictating (action)</li></ol><p><i>диктова́ть</i> (diktovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchor, announcer, speaker anchorman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictation machine, dictaphone</li></ol><p>Transcription with akanye of English <i>dictaphone</i> (perhaps influenced by микрофон); genericized trademark dating from 1907.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberian spruce grouse, <i>Falcipennis falcipennis</i></li></ol><p><i>ди́к(ий)</i> (dík(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dictiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(logic,&#32;rhetoric) dilemma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>dilemma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>δίλημμα</i> (dílēmma, “double proposition”), from <i>δι-</i> (di-) + <i>λῆμμα</i> (lêmma, “premise, proposition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dealer (<i>of automobiles, drugs, cards, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) dealer, broker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dealer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dealer (<i>of automobiles, drugs, cards, securities, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ди́лер</i> (díler) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smatterer, dilettante</li><li>amateur, dabbler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dilettant</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>dēlectō</i> (whence also English <i>delight</i>). Further from Latin <i>dē-</i> + Latin <i>laciō</i> (the ultimate source of Russian <i>делика́тный</i> (delikátnyj), Russian <i>деликате́с</i> (delikatés)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amateur, amateurish, dilettante (showing a lack of professionalism, experience or talent)</li></ol><p><i>дилета́нт</i> (diletánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage-coach, diligence (horse-drawn coach to transport passengers and mail)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>diligence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stage-coach, diligence</li></ol><p><i>дилижа́нс</i> (diližáns) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) dimer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) diminutive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyne</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Дина)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Dina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;general) dynamism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dynamisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loudspeaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynamics</li><li>movement, action</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynamite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dynamite</li></ol><p><i>динами́т</i> (dinamít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dynamic</li></ol><p><i>дина́м(ика)</i> (dinám(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dynamically</li></ol><p><i>динами́чный</i> (dinamíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynamism, energy</li></ol><p><i>динами́чный</i> (dinamíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dynamic</li></ol><p><i>дина́м(ика)</i> (dinám(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynamo</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) woman or girl who flirts without offering (promised) sex; cocktease</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynamo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) dinar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dingo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dinosaur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Dinosauria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>δεινός</i> (deinós, “scary, awful”) + <i>σαῦρος</i> (saûros, “lizard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ding dong (onomatopoeia for the sound of a doorbell)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) diode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>diode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any fern in the genus <i>Diplazium</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Diplazium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diploma</li></ol><p>From French <i>diplôme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person who is awarded with academic diploma; prizewinner, laureate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diplomat (person who is accredited to represent a government)</li><li>briefcase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>diplomate</i>. The sense “briefcase” arose from the custom of diplomats to carry briefcases.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diplomatic</li></ol><p><i>диплома́тия</i> (diplomátija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diplomacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student working on graduation thesis</li></ol><p><i>дипло́м</i> (diplóm) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diploma, certificate of degree</li></ol><p><i>дипло́м</i> (diplóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diplomatic mission</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>дипломати́ческое представи́тельство</i> (diplomatíčeskoje predstavítelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diplomatic officer, diplomatic worker</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>дипломати́ческий сотру́дник</i> (diplomatíčeskij sotrúdnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>directive (instruction or guideline that indicates how to perform an action or reach a goal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>directive</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manager, director, principal, headmaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Direktor</i>. From Late Latin <i>dīrēctor</i>, from Latin <i>dīrēctus</i>, from Latin <i>dīrigō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) directory, folder</li><li>(historical,&#32;politics) directory, directorate, directoire (a form of government with a collegial executive&#59; most commonly, of the French revolutionary Directoire, or of various short-lived regional governments existing after the break-up of the Russian Empire and during the Russian Civil War)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>directoire</i>.</p><p>In the computing sense, a semantic loan from English <i>directory</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>directorial, managerial; director's, manager's</li></ol><p><i>дире́ктор</i> (diréktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head's wife</li></ol><p>From <i>дире́ктор</i> (diréktor) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) headmistress</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) directrix</li><li>(<i>military</i>) line of fire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>directrice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>directorate, board of directors</li><li>directorate, management</li><li>director's office, manager's office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airship, dirigible, blimp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dirigeable</i>. Some steps omitting, from Latin <i>dīrigō</i> (whence also Russian <i>дирижёр</i> (dirižór), <i>дирижи́ровать</i> (dirižírovatʹ); also <i>дире́ктор</i> (diréktor), <i>директи́ва</i> (direktíva) (&lt; p.p.p. <i>dīrēctus</i>)) + Latin <i>-abilis</i>, i.e. directable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(orchestra, choir)</i> to conduct</li><li>(<i>deprecative</i>) to orchestrate</li></ol><p>From French <i>diriger</i> + <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) conductor of a musical ensemble, bandmaster</li></ol><p>Probably an inner-Russian formation based on <i>дирижи́ровать</i> (dirižírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór). The usual corresponding French term is <i>dirigeant</i>, not the quite rare or marginal <i>dirigeur</i>. Some steps omitting, from Latin <i>dīrigō</i> (whence also <i>дирижа́бль</i> (dirižáblʹ)); also note p.p.p. <i>dīrēctus</i> (whence <i>дире́ктор</i> (diréktor) and many others).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirham</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic دِرْهَم (dirham).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disaccharide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disbalance, imbalance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disharmony</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) inconsistency</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Disharmonie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disk, disc</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) discus, platter</li><li>cartridge-drum</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) CD, compact disc</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δίσκος</i> (dískos, “a circular plate suited for hurling”), from <i>δικείν</i> (dikeín, “to hurl, to launch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disc jockey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>disc jockey</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disqualification</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Disqualifikation</i> or English <i>disqualification</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disqualify</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>disqualifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) floppy disk</li></ol><p>From English <i>diskette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disclaimer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>disclaimer</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disco (<i>type of music</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a discotheque with a bar, a discobar</li></ol><p><i>ди́ско</i> (dísko) +&lrm; <i>бар</i> (bar)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A discus thrower.</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>δισκοβόλος</i> (diskobólos, “discus thrower”), from <i>δίσκος</i> (dískos, “discus”) + <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “throw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) disk drive</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>disk drive</i>.</p><p><i>диск</i> (disk, “disk”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discomfort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discount</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discounting</li></ol><p>From <i>дисконти́ровать</i> (diskontírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) discount</li></ol><p><i>диско́нт</i> (diskónt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record library</li><li>event where records are played</li><li>youth evening with music and dance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discredit, discrediting; defamation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discredit, to compromise, to disgrace</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>diskreditieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>discretely</li></ol><p><i>дискре́тный</i> (diskrétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>discrete</li><li>discontinuous, quantified, sampled</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>diskret</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discrimination (<i>distinct treatment on the basis of prejudice</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discourse</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>discursus</i>. First attested in 1709; for the second time borrowed from English <i>discourse</i> in 1970s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) discussion</li><li>controversial, debatable</li></ol><p><i>диску́ссия</i> (diskússija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discussion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>discussio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discussant</li><li>panellist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss, to debate (participate in a discussion or debate)</li></ol><p>From German <i>diskutieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>discutere</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location, disposition</li><li>displacement, dislocation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to deploy, to station</li></ol><p>From German <i>dislozieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to be deployed, to be stationed</li></ol><p><i>дислоци́ровать</i> (dislocírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Disneyan</li></ol><p><i>Дисне́й</i> (Disnéj) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispensary (institution that dispenses medical supplies and advice)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dispensaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>average adjuster, claims adjuster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dispacheur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;physics) dispersion; dispersive</li><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics) variance</li></ol><p><i>диспе́рсия</i> (dispérsija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry,&#32;materials science) dispersion</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;statistics) variance</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>dispersiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispatcher, controller, traffic superintendent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dispatcher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dispatch</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) traffic control</li></ol><p><i>диспе́тчер</i> (dispétčer) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>display (electronic screen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>display</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) disposition (<i>of troops, etc.</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) written marching orders (<i>in the Russian army during the 18th and 19th centuries</i>)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) disposition</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>dispositiō</i> (“arrangement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dysprosium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disproportion</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from New Latin <i>disprōportiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispute, debate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to debate (mostly used in scholarly settings)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissection</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dissectiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissertation, thesis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>dysertacja</i> or German <i>Dissertation</i>, from Latin <i>dissertātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissident; maverick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dissident</li></ol><p><i>диссиде́нт</i> (dissidént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) dissimilation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;linguistics,&#32;figurative) dissonance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dissonance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>distal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) remote control (device for appliances such as a TV); remote</li><li>(neologism,&#32;colloquial) distance learning</li><li>(neologism,&#32;colloquial) remote work due to self-isolation or quarantine</li></ol><p><i>дистанцио́нный</i> (distanciónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance learning (education obtained remotely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remote control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remote-controlled</li><li>(relational,&#32;literary) distance</li></ol><p>From <i>диста́нция</i> (distáncija, “distance”) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to distance [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>диста́нция</i> (distáncija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to distance oneself, to move away [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дистанци́ровать</i> (distancírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance (amount of space between two points)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>distance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) distillation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>distīllātiō</i>. Shansky posits an adapted borrowing of German <i>Destillation</i> +&lrm; <i>-я́ция</i> (-jácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>distribūtiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>дистрибью́тор</i> (distribʹjútor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>distributor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) distributor (intermediary between manufacturer and reseller)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>distributor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dystrophy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) + <i>τροφή</i> (trophḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dysphoria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δυσφορία</i> (dusphoría, “excessive pain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dysfunction (failure to function)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dysfunktion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discipline</li><li>branch of science</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disciplinary</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>disciplinary</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discipline, self-discipline</li></ol><p><i>дисциплини́рованный</i> (disciplinírovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disciplined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discipline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>disziplinieren</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дисципли́на</i> (disciplína) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) child, baby, kid (also used ironically or in similes)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѣтѧ</i> (dětę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dětę</i>. The <i>и</i> of the first syllable in the singular is from дити́на (ditína), nowadays written дети́на (detína) by analogy with the accented де́ти (déti), with regular distant assimilation <i>ѣ</i>…<i>и́</i> &gt; <i>и</i>…<i>и́</i>. An alternative formulation by Šakhmatov (<i>ѣ</i> &gt; <i>и</i> before a soft consonant) explains these forms more directly. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i>, whence also <i>де́ва</i> (déva), <i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ), Latin <i>fētus</i>, <i>fīlius</i>, <i>fēcundus</i>, <i>fēmina</i>, <i>fēlīx</i>, and many more.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>сосуно́к</i> (sosunók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indigo child</li></ol><p>дитя (ditja) + индиго (indigo), by analogy with Indigo child</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>дитя́</i> (ditjá)</li></ol><p><i>дитя́</i> (ditjá) +&lrm; <i>-ё</i> (-jó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little baby, young child</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dithyramb, eulogy (a solemn choral song in honor of the god Dionysus)</li><li>exaggerated praise or accolade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διθύραμβος</i> (dithúrambos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diffraction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diphtheria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) diphthong</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>δῐ́φθογγος</i> (dĭ́phthongos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defamation, libel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;automobiles) differential</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Differential</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) differential</li></ol><p><i>дифференциа́л</i> (differenciál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>differentiation, differentiating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to differentiate, to distinguish</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to differentiate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>differenzieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) diffusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run wild</li><li>to become wild, to grow wild</li><li>to become shy or unsociable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venison, game</li></ol><p>From <i>дичь</i> (dičʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina). Cognate with Belarusian <i>дзічы́на</i> (dzičýna), Ukrainian <i>дичи́на</i> (dyčýna). Compare also Polish <i>dziczyzna</i> (“wildmeat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game (wild animals or birds hunted for food)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wilderness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, bosh, rubbish, rot</li></ol><p>From <i>ди́к(ий)</i> (dík(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>дзіч</i>&nbsp;f (dzič, “game; backwoods”), Polish <i>dzicz</i>&nbsp;f (“wilderness; backwater; mob”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dielectric (electrically insulating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) diethyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) palm (<i>of the hand</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>длань</i> (dlanĭ). Doublet of <i>ладо́нь</i> (ladónʹ), the inherited Old East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;text messaging,&#32;vulgar) abbreviation of <i>долбоёб</i> (dolbojób, “bastard, dipshit”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьlina</i>. Compare the verb <i>длить</i> (dlitʹ, “to delay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothing and shoes</i>) too long, longer than necessary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дли́нен</i> (dlínen).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>длина́</i> (dliná, “length”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>дли́нно</i> (dlínno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>дли́нно</i> (dlínno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a long time</li><li>at length, too verbosely</li></ol><p><i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-bearded</li></ol><p>From <i>дли́нн(ый)</i> (dlínn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бород(а́)</i> (borod(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a long blade</li></ol><p>From <i>длинн(ый)</i> (dlinn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>клин(о́)к</i> (klin(ó)k) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-legged (having long legs)</li></ol><p><i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-skirted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-armed</li></ol><p><i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lengthiness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) long-flowered; having long flowers</li></ol><p>From <i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj, “long, lengthy”) +&lrm; <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix). Often used as a calque of Latin <i>longiflōrus</i> for species' names translated from their binomial name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-necked</li></ol><p><i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ше́я</i> (šéja) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li><li>lengthy</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) tall, lanky (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>длина́</i> (dliná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very long</li></ol><p><i>дли́нный</i> (dlínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ю́щий</i> (-júščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duration, time length</li></ol><p><i>дли́тельный</i> (dlítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long, protracted, lengthy</li></ol><p><i>длить</i> (dlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to delay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to last (of time duration)</li></ol><p><i>длить</i> (dlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьľa</i>, <i>&#42;děľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dmitriy, Dmitriy's</li></ol><p><i>Дми́трий</i> (Dmítrij) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dmitrov (city in Russia, near Moscow)</li></ol><p><i>Дми́тров</i> (Dmítrov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orderly; person on duty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diary</li><li>journal, datebook, daybook</li><li>school record book</li></ol><p>Calqued from French <i>journal</i> in the 18th century. By surface analysis, <i>дневно́й</i> (dnevnój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diary</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) journal, datebook</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) notebook, school record book</li></ol><p><i>дневни́к</i> (dnevník) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) day, daytime</li></ol><p><i>днев-</i> (dnev-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dnipropetrovsk;</li></ol><p><i>Днепропетро́вск</i> (Dnepropetróvsk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) now, nowadays, today</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дьньсь</i> (dĭnĭsĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьnьsь</i> (“today”), from <i>&#42;dьnь</i> (“day”) + <i>&#42;sь</i> (“this”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) life</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom, bilge (of a vessel)</li><li>augmentative of <i>дно</i> (dno): bottom</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;youth slang,&#32;ironic) something awkward, embarrassing, or disgusting</li></ol><p><i>дно</i> (dno) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor, bed (hard surface at the bottom of a body of water)</li><li>bottom surface of a container</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bottom of society</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дъно</i> (dŭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъno</i> (“bottom”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dubnas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰubnos</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰewb-</i> (“deep”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) birthday</li><li>birthday party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>дню́ха</i> (dnjúxa)</li></ol><p><i>дню́ха</i> (dnjúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by day, during the day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>day and night (all the time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li><li>up to, till, until</li><li>with respect to, concerning, regarding</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(musical note)</i> C</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;do</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i>, whence English <i>to</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>BC (before Christ); BCE (before the Common Era)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>до Рождества́ Христо́ва</i> (do Roždestvá Xristóva, “before Christ's birth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>musical note</i>) C flat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>see you (later), goodbye (farewell)</li></ol><p>Literally, “till the meeting”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>musical note</i>) C sharp (C#)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>until when</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>до на́шей э́ры</i> (do nášej éry): B.C., BCE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>до на́шей э́ры</i> (do nášej éry): B.C., BCE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Before the Common Era (BCE), before Christ (BC)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) completely, totally, to the bone</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) every single thing, absolutely everything</li></ol><p>Literally, “to the [last] thread”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>until further notice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;in expressions) don't give a fuck, don't give a shit, don't care</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) too fucking much</li></ol><p>Literally, “to the cunt”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a.m. (before noon)</li><li>ante meridiem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for the time being, in the meantime, temporarily, until the (right) time comes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) until one is blue in the face, for a long time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>goodbye, see you later</li></ol><p>Literally, “until next time”. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>довиждане</i> (doviždane). Compare parallel and more distant cognate French <i>au revoir</i>, Italian <i>arrivederci</i>, related to Latin <i>videō</i> (+ Latin <i>ad-</i> + Latin <i>re-</i>). Also compare Portuguese <i>até mais ver</i>, Spanish <i>hasta la vista</i>, also related to Latin <i>videō</i> (and probably also + Latin <i>ad</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>до свида́ния</i> (do svidánija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still, so far, yet, as yet, hitherto, up to now, thus far</li><li>till now</li><li>before now</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as yet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>see you soon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>see you soon (goodbye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> до (do),&lrm; смерть (smertʹ).</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to (one's) death</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) utterly, extremely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>until, till</li><li>so long as, as long as, as (while)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so long as, as long as (while)</li><li>unless and until</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>while</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before, till</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>till one drop; till one is quite exhausted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) many, a lot of, much</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “dick”). Literally, “to the dick”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) up to</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) completely (describing a completion of an action to its end)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;used with reflexive verbs) and get in trouble, and get what's coming to one (describing actions, often risky or excessive, which result in negative consequences)</li><li>(<i>used with adjectives and nouns</i>) pre-, ante- (earlier in time)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;do</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrue</li></ol><p><i>доба́вить</i> (dobávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additive (substance altering another substance)</li><li>amendment</li><li>second helping</li><li>refill</li></ol><p><i>доба́вить</i> (dobávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition, addendum, add-on, supplement</li></ol><p><i>доба́вить</i> (dobávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add</li></ol><p><i>доба́вить</i> (dobávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrue</li></ol><p><i>добавля́ть</i> (dobavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>additionally, addedly (in addition)</li></ol><p><i>доба́вочный</i> (dobávočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>additional, extra</li><li>supplementary</li><li>accessory</li></ol><p><i>доба́вка</i> (dobávka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to reach</li><li>to finish running</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run into trouble</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to reach</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to white heat</li><li>till something is spotlessly clean or white</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dobermann</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dobermann, Dobermann pinscher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill off, to finish off</li><li>to crush completely, to destroy completely</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish</li><li>to beat [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to a particular state’</small>]</li><li>to finish chiming (of a clock)</li></ol><p><i>доби́ть</i> (dobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (try to) achieve, to attain, to get, to reach [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to strive [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li><li>to find out [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘about something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>добива́ть</i> (dobivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get (to), to reach</li></ol><p><i>добира́ть</i> (dobirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill off, to finish off</li><li>to crush completely, to destroy completely</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish</li><li>to beat [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to a particular state’</small>]</li><li>to finish chiming (of a clock)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (try to) achieve, to attain, to get, to reach [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to find out [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘about something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>доби́ть</i> (dobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valiant, gallant, valorous, heroic, brave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>доблѥстьнъ</i> (dobljestĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobľestьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>до́блесть</i> (dóblestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valour, prowess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>доблѥсть</i> (dobljestĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobľestь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get (to), to reach</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plod, to trudge (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>добред-</i> (dobred-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj): kindest, most kind, very kind, too kind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj, “good, kind”)</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plod, to trudge (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become kind</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gain weight, to get fat</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good (that which is ethical or moral, the opposite of evil)</li><li>goods, property</li><li>stuff</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) green light, consent, permission, the OK</li><li>the name of the letter д in the ancient Slavonic or old Russian alphabet</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>okay</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>добръ</i> (dobrŭ), in turn from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dòbrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>welcome!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volunteer (one who voluntarily offers himself for service&#59; voluntary soldier&#59; one who acts without legal obligation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>voluntarily (in a voluntary manner)</li></ol><p><i>доброво́льный</i> (dobrovólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voluntary, of one's free will</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>доброво́льный</i> (dobrovólʹnyj) in XVIII, from Old Polish <i>dobrowolny</i>. By surface analysis, <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) volunteer</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) voluntary</li></ol><p><i>доброво́лец</i> (dobrovólec) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;sometimes&#32;humorous) good (whatever) time of day, good morning/afternoon/evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bon voyage!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virtue (excellence in morals)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>добродѣтѣль</i> (dobrodětělĭ), a calque from Ancient Greek <i>εὐεργεσία</i> (euergesía)). By surface analysis, <i>добро-</i> (dobro-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virtuous, righteous</li></ol><p><i>доброде́тель</i> (dobrodételʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mansuetude, amiability, geniality, good nature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>genially</li></ol><p><i>доброду́шный</i> (dobrodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good-natured</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>добродꙋшьнꙑи</i> (dobrodušĭnyi), from <i>добро</i> (dobro, “good”) + <i>дꙋша</i> (duša, “soul”) + <i>-ьнꙑи</i> (-ĭnyi), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὔψυχος</i> (eúpsukhos). Also, compare with Macedonian <i>добродушен</i> (dobrodušen), Serbo-Croatian <i>добродушан</i>, Czech <i>dobrodušný</i>, Polish <i>dobroduszny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-wisher</li></ol><p>From <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ, “wish”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>benevolently</li></ol><p><i>доброжела́тельный</i> (dobroželátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benevolence, kindness, goodwill</li></ol><p><i>доброжела́тельный</i> (dobroželátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>benevolent, well-wishing</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good night</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) benign</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ка́чество</i> (káčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respectable, honest</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поря́дочный</i> (porjádošnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>honestly</li><li>(<i>law</i>) in good faith</li></ol><p><i>добросо́вестный</i> (dobrosóvestnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conscientiousness, bona fides</li><li>scrupulosity</li></ol><p><i>добросо́вестный</i> (dobrosóvestnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscientious, fair</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>со́весть</i> (sóvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neighbourly, good-neighbourly (showing the qualities of a friendly and helpful neighbour)</li></ol><p>From <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сосе́д</i> (soséd, “neighbour”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Ukrainian <i>добросусі́дський</i> (dobrosusídsʹkyj), Belarusian <i>добрасусе́дскі</i> (dobrasusjédski), Polish <i>dobrosąsiedzki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(good-)neighbourliness; (good)-neighbourly relations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindness, goodness</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>доброта</i> (dobrota), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobrota</i>. By surface analysis, <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>durable, high-quality</li></ol><p><i>доброта́</i> (dobrotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-wisher</li><li>volunteer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good</li><li>benevolent, kind, kindly, good-hearted, kindhearted</li><li>pure, honest</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) good, solid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>добръ</i> (dobrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobrъ</i>.</p><p>Via PIE related to Latin <i>faber</i> (“artisan, craftsman, architect, creator, maker, artificer, forger, smith”), whence <i>фа́брика</i> (fábrika, “factory, plant, mill”).</p><p>Also related to Russian <i>до́ба</i> (dóba, “time”), Russian <i>сдо́ба</i> (sdóba, “shortening, cakes, buns”), Russian <i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj, “comfortable, convenient”), Russian <i>до́блесть</i> (dóblestʹ, “valour, prowess”), Lithuanian <i>dabà</i> (“habit, character”), English <i>daft</i>, English <i>deft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good day, good afternoon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>добрꙑи дьнь</i> (dobryi dĭnĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dòbrъ dь̏nь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kind, good-hearted man, good-natured person, good soul</li></ol><p><i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>добря́к</i> (dobrják): kind, good-hearted man, good-natured person, good soul</li></ol><p><i>добря́к</i> (dobrják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed in waking up, <i>(manage to)</i> to wake up</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>getting, procuring</li><li>extraction, mining</li></ol><p><i>добыва́ть</i> (dobyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain, to get, to procure</li><li>to extract, to mine, to quarry</li><li>to bag <i>hunt</i></li></ol><p><i>добы́ть</i> (dobýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extractive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who extracts resources (miner, hunter, fisherman, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) breadwinner, (good) earner (someone who is able to make good money)</li></ol><p><i>добы́ча</i> (dobýča) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain, to get, to procure</li><li>to extract, to mine, to quarry</li><li>to bag <i>hunt</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobyti</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extraction, mining, quarrying</li><li>booty, spoil, plunder (plunder taken from an enemy in time of war, or seized by piracy)</li><li>prey, catch (something which is captured or caught)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dobyťa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finishing, bringing to (something)</li><li>reduction</li></ol><p><i>довед-</i> (doved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>checkers</i>) king, kinged piece</li></ol><p>Related to <i>довести</i> (dovesti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take <i>(to)</i>, to carry <i>(as far as, to)</i>, to bring <i>(to, as far as)</i></li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) letter of attorney, power of attorney</li><li>proxy</li></ol><p><i>дове́ренный</i> (dovérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trusted, authorized</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proxy</li><li>agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trust (confidence in or reliance on some person or quality)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>верие</i> (verije), from the verb <i>верить</i> (veritʹ, “to trust, believe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>confidentially, in confidence</li></ol><p><i>довери́тельный</i> (doverítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secrecy, privacy, confidentiality</li></ol><p><i>довери́тельный</i> (doverítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confidential, secret</li><li>trusting, confiding</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) warrant, power of attorney; trust, confidence</li></ol><p><i>дове́рить</i> (dovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entrust (to give someone or something to someone for safekeeping) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to entrust (to tell someone a secret) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘with a secret’</small>]</li><li>to entrust, to charge, to delegate [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘with doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust <i>(in)</i>, to confide <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>дове́рить</i> (dovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the top, to the brim</li></ol><p><i>до́</i> (dó) +&lrm; <i>ве́рху</i> (vérxu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trustingly (in a trusting manner)</li></ol><p><i>дове́рчивый</i> (dovérčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trustfulness</li><li>credulity</li><li>gullibility (quality of being easily deceived)</li></ol><p><i>дове́рчивый</i> (dovérčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>credulous, gullible (excessively ready to believe things)</li></ol><p><i>дове́рить</i> (dovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completion, consummation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to trust, to have confidence in [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to entrust (to give someone or something to someone for safekeeping) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to entrust (to tell someone a secret) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘with a secret’</small>]</li><li>to entrust, to charge, to delegate [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘with doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дове́рить</i> (dovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ), cognates include Ukrainian <i>довіря́ти</i> (dovirjáty), Belarusian <i>давяра́ць</i> (davjarácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust <i>(in)</i>, to confide <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>доверя́ть</i> (doverjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) makeweight</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any piece added to something to achieve a desired weight</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to take, to accompany (<i>to</i>), to bring (<i>into</i>)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to scald</li><li>to drive, to reduce, to bring, to carry</li><li>to inform</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to have occasion, to have a chance</li></ol><p><i>довести́</i> (dovestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;rare) sufficiently, enough</li></ol><p>Possibly from Old Church Slavonic <i>довълъ</i> (dovŭlŭ) or Old East Slavic <i>довлѣ</i> (dovlě). Compare <i>довле́ть</i> (dovlétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) satisfy, fulfill</li></ol><p><i>до́вле</i> (dóvle) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) to satisfy, to be sufficient</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘над + whom?’</small>] to hover over, to weigh on, to hang over someone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>довьлѣти</i> (dovĭlěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dovьlěti</i>. The second sense appeared in the late 19th century and is not connected with the original; it arose by association with дави́ть (davítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument, reason</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>доводи́ть</i> (dovodítʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>до́від</i> (dóvid), Belarusian <i>до́вад</i> (dóvad), Czech <i>důvod</i>, Polish <i>dowód</i>, and Slovak <i>dôvod</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, to lead, to take, to accompany (<i>to</i>), to bring (<i>into</i>)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to scald</li><li>to drive, to reduce, to bring, to carry</li><li>to inform</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have an occasion (<i>to</i>), to happen (<i>to</i>)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be related to</li></ol><p><i>доводи́ть</i> (dovodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finishing</li><li>tuning, refinement, tweaking</li></ol><p><i>доводи́ть</i> (dovodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antebellum, prewar</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-, “pre-”) +&lrm; <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj, “military, war”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take <i>(to)</i>, to carry <i>(as far as, to)</i>, to bring <i>(to, as far as)</i></li><li>to finish conveying</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficient</li><li>quite, rather, pretty, fairly</li><li>contentedly</li></ol><p><i>дово́льный</i> (dovólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rather, somewhat, to a fair extent</li></ol><p><i>дово́льно</i> (dovólʹno) +&lrm; <i>таки́</i> (takí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>content, satisfied, pleased</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ration, allowance</li><li>contentment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contentment, satisfaction</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prosperity, comfortable circumstances</li></ol><p><i>дово́ль(ный)</i> (dovólʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be content/satisfied <i>(with)</i>, to content oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to draw allowances/supplies</li></ol><p><i>дово́льство</i> (dovólʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first-aid</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>врач</i> (vrač) +&lrm; <i>-е́бный</i> (-ébnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) to fuck around and find out</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-, prefix indicating actions which result in negative consequences) +&lrm; <i>выёбываться</i> (vyjóbyvatʹsja, “to fuck around, to show off”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Dane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess (to reach an unqualified conclusion)</li><li>to surmise, to suspect, to conjecture</li><li>to cotton on, to glom on</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guess, conjecture, surmise; (plural) guesswork</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick-witted, clever</li></ol><p><i>догада́ть(ся)</i> (dogadátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess (to reach an unqualified conclusion)</li><li>to surmise, to suspect, to conjecture</li></ol><p><i>догада́ться</i> (dogadátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogma (authoritative principle, belief or statement of opinion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctrine; tenet; dogma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogmatist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;religion&#32;or&#32;general) dogmatic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>dogmat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up, to overtake</li><li>to drive to, to bring to</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish speaking/saying/telling</li></ol><p><i>договори́ть</i> (dogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree (mutually), to arrange (about, for), to come to an agreement / understanding (about)</li><li>to come (to), to talk (to the point of)</li><li>to negotiate (about) (<i>no perfective</i>)</li></ol><p><i>догова́ривать</i> (dogovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, contract, pact, treaty</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>говор</i> (govor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish speaking/saying/telling</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree (mutually), to arrange (about, for), to come to an agreement / understanding (about)</li><li>to come (to), to talk (to the point of)</li></ol><p><i>договори́ть</i> (dogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) treaty, contract; contractual</li></ol><p><i>догово́р</i> (dogovór) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports&#32;slang</i>) fixed match (a match with a fraudulently prearranged outcome)</li><li>(<i>politics&#32;slang</i>) secret deal, unwritten agreement</li><li>(politics&#32;slang,&#32;by extension) a deal, an agreement in general (<i>especially a peace deal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>договорно́й</i> (dogovornój) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement (an understanding to follow a course of conduct)</li></ol><p><i>договорённый</i> (dogovorjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(stark)</i> naked</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>голый</i> (golyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) tag (children's game)</li></ol><p>From <i>догоня́ть</i> (dogonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-л</i> (-l) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up, to overtake</li><li>to drive to, to bring to</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn down, to burn out, to burn low</li></ol><p><i>догоре́ть</i> (dogorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn down, to burn out, to burn low</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog hunter (a person who is engaged in the extermination of stray dogs)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dog hunter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dodecahedron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish doing, to complete, to bring a task to the end</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish doing, to complete, to bring a task to the end</li></ol><p><i>доде́лать</i> (dodélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>duodecillion (10<sup>39</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dojo (training facility, usually led by one or more sensei&#59; a hall or room used for such training)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dojo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a male homosexual</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a weak or effeminate man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dodo (<i>Raphus cucullatus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;nautical) pre-dreadnought battleship</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>дредно́ут</i> (drednóut); partial calque of English <i>pre-dreadnought</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over/out/through <i>(to the end)</i>, to do some more thinking <i>(about)</i></li><li>to think up what is missing, to complete</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think up, to come to the idea, to hit</li><li>to go as far as</li></ol><p><i>доду́мать</i> (dodúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over/out/through <i>(to the end)</i>, to do some more thinking <i>(about)</i></li><li>to think up what is missing, to complete</li></ol><p><i>доду́мать</i> (dodúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think up, to come to the idea, to hit</li></ol><p><i>доду́мывать</i> (dodúmyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck to, to fuck until (to reach a certain state or come to a certain result by having sex or performing a sexual act / sexual acts)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to find fault (with something or someone)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to fuck with (to pester, harass or demand from) [(<i>also</i>) <i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to finish eating, to eat up</li></ol><p><i>дое́д-</i> (dojéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to arrive (by horse or vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to finish eating, to eat up</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to arrive (by horse or vehicle)</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) doge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>doge</i>, from Italian <i>doge</i>. Further from Latin <i>dux</i> (akin to <i>dūcō</i>), whence also <i>ду́че</i> (dúče) and <i>дюк</i> (djuk). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i>, whence via German also <i>цуг</i> (cug) and the second component of <i>ге́рцог</i> (gércog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait until, to (manage to) wait for a long time</li><li>to end (by)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puffball <i>(mushroom)</i></li><li>raincoat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rain</li></ol><p><i>дождь</i> (doždʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthworm (animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>дождь</i> (doždʹ)</li></ol><p><i>дождь</i> (doždʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to rain, to be raining</li></ol><p><i>дождь</i> (doždʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>дождь</i> (doždʹ, “rain, tinsel”)</li></ol><p><i>до́ждик</i> (dóždik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rainy</li></ol><p><i>дождь</i> (doždʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дъждь</i> (dŭždĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъždžь</i>, with spelling from Church Slavonic.</p><p>Doublet of <i>дожж</i> (dožž, “rain (<i>obsolete</i>)”), from <i>дъжжь</i> (dŭžžĭ), from the same Proto-Slavic etymon.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live so long</li></ol><p><i>дожи́ть</i> (dožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to await, to wait (un)till, to (manage to) wait for a long time</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-жида́ть</i> (-židátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used upon observing something undesirable to express exasperation or dissatisfaction that we've reached the point of things like that supposedly happening in this day and age, similar to the meanings of "look at what we've come to", "the world's gone crazy" etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, to remain alive, to survive <i>(until)</i>, to live to see</li><li>to find oneself in a disgraceful condition</li><li>to stay, to spend <i>(the rest of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use up the last of one's money</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dose, draught</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refuel (a plane)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>запра́вить</i> (zaprávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to refuel (<i>of a plane</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дозапра́вить</i> (dozaprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refueling</li></ol><p><i>дозапра́вить</i> (dozaprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refuel (a plane)</li></ol><p><i>дозапра́вить</i> (dozaprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to refuel (<i>of a plane</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дозаправля́ть</i> (dozapravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispenser</li></ol><p>Derived from French <i>dose</i> + <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(try to)</i> get on the phone, to get through <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>дозвони́ться</i> (dozvonítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) permission</li></ol><p><i>дозво́лить</i> (dozvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permissibility</li></ol><p><i>дозво́ленный</i> (dozvólennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allowed; permitted; legal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allowable, permissible</li></ol><p><i>дозво́лить</i> (dozvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to permit, to allow, to authorize</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>dozwolić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to permit, to allow, to authorize</li></ol><p><i>дозво́лить</i> (dozvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(try to)</i> get on the phone, to get through <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dose, to dispense medication to</li><li>to establish the dose of</li><li>to divide into doses</li><li>to meter, to ration</li></ol><p>From German <i>dosieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dosing</li><li>dosage</li></ol><p>From <i>дози́ровать</i> (dozírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;military) investigator, interrogator</li></ol><p><i>дознава́(ться)</i> (doznavá(tʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, to inquire <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>дозна́ться</i> (doznátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry, inquest, investigation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, to inquire <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrol, round</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>дозьръти</i> (dozĭrŭti, “to observe”), further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> (“to see, to look at”). Related to зреть (zretʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patrol</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrol person, watchman</li></ol><p><i>дозо́р</i> (dozór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen completely (<i>of fruit</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to mature (<i>of a design, manufacturing process, performance, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дозре́ть</i> (dozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen completely (<i>of fruit</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to mature (<i>of a design, manufacturing process, performance, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish playing</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish playing</li></ol><p><i>доигра́ть</i> (doigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try to find out</li></ol><p><i>дои́скивать</i> (doískivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prehistoric (relating to the epoch before written records)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>истори́ческий</i> (istoríčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk (to express milk from a mammal)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to give a handjob</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>доити</i> (doiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dojiti</i> (“to give milk, milk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give milk</li></ol><p><i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milking</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) milker, breast of a human female</li></ol><p><i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to, to come to, to arrive at, to reach</li><li>to come down to</li><li><i>(prices)</i> to rise, to run up to, to amount to</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to ejaculate, to cum</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dock</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ДОК)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>woodworking</i>) acronym of <i>деревообрабатывающий комбинат</i> (derevoobrabatyvajuščij kombinat, “woodworking plant”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) expert, authority, pundit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demonstrative, evidentiary, evidential, evincive, probative, conclusive</li></ol><p><i>доказа́ть</i> (dokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evidence, proof, justification</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) proof</li></ol><p><i>доказа́ть</i> (dokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove, to demonstrate</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substantiation, proof, proving, showing</li></ol><p><i>дока́зывать</i> (dokázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove, to demonstrate</li></ol><p><i>доказа́ть</i> (dokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig down <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to <i>(try to)</i> find out, to discover, to hunt out, to arrive, to get to the bottom <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>дока́пывать</i> (dokápyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). See <i>докопа́ться</i> (dokopátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(to)</i>, to go rolling <i>(to)</i></li><li>to reach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(sounds)</i> to roll, to thunder, to boom</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to come down <i>(to)</i>, to sink <i>(into)</i>, to end up</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ться</i> (katítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(to)</i>, to go rolling <i>(to)</i></li><li>to reach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(sounds)</i> to roll, to thunder, to boom</li></ol><p><i>докати́ться</i> (dokatítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report, paper</li><li>lecture</li><li>announcement</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>докла́дывать</i> (dokládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>докладна́я запи́ска</i> (dokladnája zapíska, “memorandum, report”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) report</li></ol><p><i>докла́д</i> (doklád) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lecturer; speaker, reporter; rapporteur</li></ol><p><i>докла́д(ывать)</i> (doklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, to announce (to notify formally)</li><li>to add more, to put more (e.g. into a portion)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pre-COVID</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) how long</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) as long as, until</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ruin, to finish off, to end, to destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>конать</i> (konatʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konati</i>. Compare Polish <i>dokonać</i>, Czech <i>dokonat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat up, to drink up</li><li>to conclude</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill off, to finish off, to destroy completely</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig down <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to <i>(try to)</i> find out, to discover, to hunt out, to arrive, to get to the bottom <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>докопа́ть</i> (dokopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). From <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ, “to dig”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pre-COVID</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precrisis</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>кри́зис</i> (krízis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctor (PhD or MD)</li><li>physician</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>doctor</i>, via German <i>Doktor</i> or Polish <i>doktor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the highest degree in higher education in Russia, based on the quality of the researcher's (professor's) work as a whole</li></ol><p>Literally, “doctor of sciences”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) Doctor of Sciences course</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>доктора́нт</i> (doktoránt) +&lrm; <i>-у́ра</i> (-úra). Compare Ukrainian <i>докторанту́ра</i> (doktorantúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) doctor</li></ol><p><i>до́ктор</i> (dóktor) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doctor's</li><li>doctoral</li></ol><p><i>до́ктор</i> (dóktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctrine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>doctrina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how far; up to where, until where</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>куда́</i> (kudá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>document, paper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) documentary</li></ol><p><i>документа́льный</i> (dokumentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>documentarily</li></ol><p><i>документа́льный</i> (dokumentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>documentary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>documentary film</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>documentation (something transposed from a thought to a document)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) documentation (documents that explain the operation of a particular software program)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>documentatio</i>, reinforced by English <i>documentation</i> in the computing sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to document</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>document workflow, document circulation (workflow of documents in an organization, from inception to disposal)</li></ol><p><i>докуме́нт</i> (dokumént) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>оборо́т</i> (oborót)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>докуме́нт</i> (dokumént).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy in addition to what has been previously purchased</li></ol><p><i>докупи́ть</i> (dokupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy in addition to what has been previously purchased</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish smoking</li></ol><p><i>докури́ть</i> (dokurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish smoking</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annoy, to bother, to pester, to importune (to disturb or irritate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tiresome, annoying, bothersome, importunate, pesky</li></ol><p><i>докуча́ть</i> (dokučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bothersome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dale, vale</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolъ</i>. Cognate with English <i>dale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to peck once, to hammer once</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drink</li></ol><p><i>долба́ть</i> (dolbátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьlbati</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>довба́ти</i> (dovbáty), Polish <i>dłubać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peck (e.g. of birds), to gouge a hole in (to make a dent or a hole by constantly hitting the surface)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock, to hit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to constantly repeat, to remind about something</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to memorize by constantly repeating</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to scold, to criticize</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to destroy by using sloppily</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use drugs</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to fuck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьlbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thump, to pound, to bang</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to be pounded, to fuck, to pound one another</li></ol><p><i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to kiss (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuckery; stupidity, foolishness</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuckery; meaninglessness, absurdity, uselessness</li></ol><p><i>долбоёб</i> (dolbojób, “dumbfuck”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;paganism) Slavic neopaganism.</li></ol><p>From <i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ, “to knock, to fuck up”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́ва</i> (sláva, “glory, glorification; (general) opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p><p>A pun on долбоёб (dolbojób, “idiot”) and правосла́вие (pravoslávije, “Orthodoxy”), as the latter was used by some Slavic neopagan groups as the name for their religion. The word may also be derived from "Dobroslav" (Добросла́в (Dobrosláv), the chosen religious name for Alexey Dobrovolsky, a notable neo-Nazi and leader of a neopagan nationalist group in Russia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) dickhead, fuckwit, dumbass, dipshit, motherfucker (stupid person)</li></ol><p>From <i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>довбойо́б</i> (dovbojób), Belarusian <i>даўбаёб</i> (dawbajób).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>долбоёб</i> (dolbojób)</li></ol><p><i>долбоёб</i> (dolbojób, “dumbfuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) stupidity, dumbfuckery, foolishness</li></ol><p><i>долбоёб</i> (dolbojób, “dumbfuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debt</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) duty, obligation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъlgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long (having great duration)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьlgъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dl̥h₁gʰós</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दीर्घ</i> (dīrgha, “"long"”), Latin <i>longus</i>, English <i>long</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a long time; for a long time</li></ol><p>From <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o). Compare Czech <i>dlouho</i> and Polish <i>długo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rather a long time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longevity, durability</li></ol><p><i>долгове́чный</i> (dolgovéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>durable, long-lasting</li><li>long-lived</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) debt</li></ol><p><i>долг</i> (dolg) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-term, of long duration, lasting</li><li>(<i>military</i>) permanent</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lanky, gangling</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) + Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vęzъ</i> (“neck”) (cognate with Czech <i>vaz</i> (“neck”), dialectal Russian <i>вязы</i> (vjazy, “neck”)) + <i>-ый</i> (-yj). Akin to <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>даўгавя́зы</i> (dawhavjázy).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ше́я</i> (šéja) (akin to <i>шить</i> (šitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a long mane</li></ol><p>From <i>долг(ий)</i> (dolg(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гри́в(а)</i> (grív(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-awaited</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij, “long”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жда́ть</i> (ždátʹ, “to await”) +&lrm; <i>-нный</i> (-nnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>very-long-lived person, centenarian (<i>approximately; normally refers to someone 90 years and older</i>)</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longevity (the quality of being long-lasting, especially of life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cranefly</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij, “long”) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), literally “long-legger”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) weevil (herbivorous beetle)</li></ol><p><i>долгий</i> (dolgij, “long”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-skirted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarsier</li></ol><p>From <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij, “long”) + genitive plural of <i>пята́</i> (pjatá, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-armed</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-dated, long-range, long-term</li></ol><p>From <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Probably a calque of German <i>langfristig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) lingering construction, unfinished construction, long-term construction</li></ol><p><i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij, “long”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geology,&#32;geography) longitude (angular distance)</li><li>length (of the day)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) phonemic length</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дългота</i> (dŭlgota, “longitude, duration; length”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьlgota</i> (“length”), from <i>&#42;dьlgъ</i> (“long”) + <i>&#42;-ota</i> (“-ness”). By surface analysis, <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-suffering</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>длъготрьпѣниѥ</i> (dlŭgotrĭpěnije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) longitude, longitudinal</li></ol><p>From <i>долгота́</i> (dolgotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk on and on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) share, portion</li><li>lengthwise, longitudinal</li></ol><p><i>до́ля</i> (dólja) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p><p><i>до́л(гий)</i> (dól(gij)) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) comparative degree of <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) comparative degree of <i>до́лго</i> (dólgo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(to, as far as)</i>, <i>(thrown object, projectile etc)</i> to reach</li><li>to reach</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(to, as far as)</i>, <i>(thrown object, projectile etc)</i> to reach</li><li>to reach</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;rare,&#32;dated) to lend, to borrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) a must, something that must be done, duty</li></ol><p><i>до́лжен</i> (dólžen) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to have to, to be obliged</li></ol><p><i>до́лженство</i> (dólženstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debtor, debitor</li></ol><p><i>долг</i> (dolg) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) it must be, probably</li></ol><p><i>до́лжный</i> (dólžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used as a parenthetical to express a speaker's opinion that something is certain, analogously to English "must".</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is due</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office, duty, position (job)</li></ol><p><i>до́лжность</i> (dólžnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office, post, appointment, position</li></ol><p><i>до́лжный</i> (dólžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>due; required</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъlžьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>долг</i> (dolg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>долг</i> (dolg)</li></ol><p><i>долг</i> (dolg) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add, pour some more</li><li>to fill</li></ol><p><i>доли́ть</i> (dolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valley</li><li>vale, dale</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolina</i>, from <i>&#42;dolъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any snake of the genus <i>Dolichophis</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Dolichophis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) dollar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dollar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dollar</li></ol><p><i>до́ллар</i> (dóllar) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, to announce (to notify formally)</li><li>to add more, to put more (e.g. into a portion)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down</li><li>away, down [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> (<i>sometimes</i>) genitive <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>доло́вь~доло́ви</i> (dolóvĭ~dolóvi) (with loss of intervocalic /v/), the dative singular of Old East Slavic <i>долъ</i> (dolŭ, “bottom, pit”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolъ</i>. A form доло́вь (dolóvʹ) survives in dialectal usage. Compare Belarusian <i>дало́ў</i> (dalów), Ukrainian <i>долíв</i> (dolív), Old Czech <i>dolov</i> (modern dolů), Slovak <i>dolu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) palm (<i>of the hand</i>)</li><li>(agriculture,&#32;dialectal) threshing-floor</li></ol><p>Metathesis of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolnь</i>; displaced with metathetic <i>ладо́нь</i> (ladónʹ). Doublet of <i>длань</i> (dlanʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>доло́ня</i> (dolónja) or <i>доло́нь</i> (dolónʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chisel</li><li>boring bit, drill bit, gouge, rock-drill</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>долото</i> (doloto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolto</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dálbtan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelbʰ-</i>. Related to Russian <i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ, “to peck, to gouge a hole in, to knock, to hit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>долото́</i> (dolotó)</li></ol><p><i>долото́</i> (dolotó) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slice (of a fruit)</li><li>diminutive of <i>до́ля</i> (dólja): lobule; clove; segment</li></ol><p><i>до́ля</i> (dólja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) dolmen (tomb consisting of a capstone supported by two or more upright stones)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetry,&#32;inanimate) dolnik; a form of verse intermediate between purely accentual and accentual-syllabic, where stressed syllables are separated by one or two unstressed syllables in free variation</li><li>(historical,&#32;animate) a metayer, a sharecropper (<i>in medieval Russia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>до́ля</i> (dólja, “part, share”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>до́лго</i> (dólgo)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьľьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shareholder, investor (<i>most commonly of an investor in shared-equity construction</i>)</li><li>(<i>film</i>) dolly grip</li></ol><p><i>до́ля</i> (dólja, “part, share”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p><p>From English <i>dolly</i> +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, portion, share</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fate, lot</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe (<i>of the brain</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>долꙗ</i> (dolja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;doľa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dáljāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to divide”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house, building (a residential building)</li><li>house, home (<i>as part of a name of an establishment</i>)</li><li>plot (in some street addresses&#58; a group of buildings sharing the same street number)</li><li>home</li><li>dynasty, noble house</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>домъ</i> (domŭ, “house; home”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dȍmъ</i> (“house; home”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;damús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i> (“home; house”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brothel</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>maison de tolérance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at home</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish grinding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premarxist</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>марксист</i> (marksist) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;school) homework</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;school) home reading</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) house arrest</li></ol><p><i>дома́шний</i> (domášnij) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(any type of) domesticated animal; companion animal; pet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homework</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close relatives</li><li>domesticated animals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>home, household, house</li><li>private</li><li>domestic; (<i>relational</i>) family</li><li>home-made, homespun</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (own) folks, family</li></ol><p>From <i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house arrest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth (<i>as a symbol for home or family life</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homework</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) dombra (long-necked lute of Turkic or Central Asian origin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>домбыра</i> (dombyra), from Persian دنب بره (donb-e barre, literally “lamb's tail”). Related to Ottoman Turkish طنبوره (tanbura/tambura), Turkish <i>tambura</i>, Romanian <i>tambură</i>, Arabic طُنْبُور (ṭunbūr), etc.</p><p>Doublet of <i>до́мра</i> (dómra) and <i>танбу́р</i> (tanbúr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dombra player</li></ol><p><i>домбра́</i> (dombrá) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dombra (type of long-necked lute)</li></ol><p><i>домбра́</i> (dombrá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to suspect, to understand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to suspect, to understand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domain (<i>in various senses</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>domaine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blast furnace</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) domain</li></ol><p><i>до́мна</i> (dómna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>доме́н</i> (domén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дом</i> (dom); a little house</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) dominant (tone of a musical scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dominican (referring to the Dominican Republic or its inhabitants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domination, dominance; prevalence</li></ol><p><i>домини́ровать</i> (dominírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevail, to predominate</li><li>to command, to dominate</li><li>to finish mining (to sow or plant mines)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>dominieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>мини́ровать</i> (minírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dominant</li><li>prevailing, predominant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dominoes</li><li>domino (masquerade costume or the mask)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a (small) house</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško, “(pejorative suffix)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>дом</i> (dom, “house”)</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>домово́й комите́т</i> (domovój komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jack (device for raising and supporting a heavy object)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>dommekracht</i>. For the first part of the word doublet of <i>дюйм</i> (djujm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blast furnace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) coffin</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeowner, landlord</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>владе́лец</i> (vladélec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>домо́вый</i> (domóvyj, “house (<i>relational</i>)”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) domovoy, a house spirit or sprite</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) house</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harassment</li><li>solicitation</li></ol><p><i>домога́ть(ся)</i> (domogátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doggedly pursue, to persistently seek or strive for [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>мог-</i> (mog-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homemade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>home, homewards, to the house</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>домови</i> (domovi), <i>домовь</i> (domovĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;domovi</i>, the u-stem dative singular of &#42;domъ (“home”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дом</i> (dom): (small) house</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish grinding</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homebred, home-grown, homebrewed</li><li>crude, primitive</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рост</i> (rost) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stay-at-home, homebody</li></ol><p><i>домо-</i> (domo-, “house”) + a verbal noun derived from the root of <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ, “to sit”). Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;domasědъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house building, house construction</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) A 16th century set of household rules and instructions about religion, obedience to the tsar and God, and cuisine</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) Patriarchal tyranny.</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom, “home”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building management, house management</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) office of a building manager</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>управле́ние</i> (upravlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door phone, interphone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeowner, householder</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́ин</i> (xozjáin, “owner”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housewife</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́йка</i> (xozjájka), literally "mistress of the house".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of household</li></ol><p><i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ча́до</i> (čádo) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domra (round-bodied lute of East Slavic origin with either three or four strings)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, from Persian تنبور (tanbur). Compare Kazakh <i>домбыра</i> (dombyra), Uzbek <i>doʻmbira</i>, Bashkir <i>думбыра</i> (dumbıra).</p><p>Doublet of <i>домбра́</i> (dombrá) and <i>танбу́р</i> (tanbúr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) housekeeper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дом</i> (dom).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsubstantiated supposition, guesswork, speculation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>don (Spanish or Italian title)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>dong, bong (sound of a large bell ringing)</li><li>(Chechnya,&#32;colloquial) so, anyway, well, yeah (<i>used as a filler word or for emphasis</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>don</i> and Italian <i>don</i>.</p><p>Onomatopoeic</p><p>Borrowed from Chechen <i>дӏон</i> (dˀon), abbreviated form of Chechen <i>дуй хьуна</i> (duj ḥʳuna).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Дон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Don (a river, the fifth-longest in Europe, in Tula, Lipetsk, Voronezh, Volgograd and Rostov Oblasts, Russia, flowing 1160 miles to the Sea of Azov)</li><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Don</i></li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>Донъ</i> (Donŭ), perhaps from Avestan <i>𐬛𐬁𐬥𐬎</i> (dānu, “river”), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;dānu</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dáHnu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₂nu</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>Дін</i> (Din), Ancient Greek <i>Τάναϊς</i> (Tánaïs), Ossetian <i>дон</i> (don, “river”).</p><p>Probably from the common Celtic river name Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Dānu</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i>, found in many other river names such as <i>Danube</i>, and <i>Dniester</i>. Connected with the Welsh river goddess Dôn, whose name is from the same origin, though it could have been influenced by Welsh <i>dawn</i> (“gift”), Irish <i>dán</i> (“gift, offering”), Latin <i>dōnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) donation (voluntary gift or contribution for a specific cause)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>donate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;Internet slang) to donate</li></ol><p>From English <i>donate</i> +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish carrying</li><li>to wear out</li><li>to carry (a child) to full term (<i>in a pregnancy</i>)</li></ol><p><i>доноси́ть</i> (donosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Donbassian, relative to Donbass</li></ol><p><i>Донба́сс</i> (Donbáss) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donburi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>丼</i> (どんぶり, donburi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) dong (the currency of Vietnam)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Vietnamese <i>đồng</i> (Hán tự: <i>銅</i>&nbsp;/ <i>铜</i> (tóng, “copper”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) totally, utterly, completely</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>нельзя́</i> (nelʹzjá), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report, message; dispatch</li></ol><p><i>донес-</i> (dones-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry (to, as far as), to bring (to), to deliver</li><li>to report, to announce, to inform</li><li>to denounce (to), to inform (on, against), to squeal on</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get / be worn out (of clothes, shoes)</li><li>to reach one's ears, to be heard; to be carried by the wind</li></ol><p><i>донести́</i> (donestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Donets (name of several rivers in Eastern Europe)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Donetsk (<i>city in Ukraine</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Доне́ц</i> (Donéc) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) or <i>Доне́ц(к)</i> (Donéc(k)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dungeon, keep, donjon (the main tower of a motte or castle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>donjon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the (very) bottom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harass, to bother</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quixotic (possessing or acting with the desire to do noble and romantic deeds, without thought of realism and practicality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bottom</li></ol><p><i>дно</i> (dno) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donor (one who donates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) donor</li></ol><p><i>до́нор</i> (dónor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>denunciation (of), information (against)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) informer, snitch</li></ol><p><i>доноси́ть</i> (donosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reporting, snitching, betrayal</li></ol><p><i>доноси́тель</i> (donosítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry (<i>to, as far as</i>), to bring (<i>to</i>), to deliver</li><li>to report, to announce, to inform</li><li>to denounce (<i>to</i>), to inform (<i>on, against</i>), to squeal on</li><li>to finish carrying</li><li>to wear out</li><li>to carry (a child) to full term (<i>in a pregnancy</i>)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get / be worn out (of clothes, shoes)</li><li>to reach one's ears, to be heard; to be carried by the wind</li></ol><p><i>доноси́ть</i> (donosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) informer, snitch, ear</li></ol><p><i>доноси́ть</i> (donosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Don</li></ol><p><i>Дон</i> (Don) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) hitherto</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ны́не</i> (nýne)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дно</i> (dno): bottom</li></ol><p><i>дно</i> (dno) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harass, to bother</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a priori, pre-experiential</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish singing</li></ol><p><i>допе́ть</i> (dopétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>finished singing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish singing</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overprinting (printing in addition)</li><li>reprint (of printed arts); run-on</li></ol><p><i>допеча́тать</i> (dopečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink up, to finish by drinking</li></ol><p><i>допи́ть</i> (dopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stimulant, performance-enhancing drug, dope (any illicit drug)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>doping</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish <i>(writing)</i></li><li>to write <i>(to)</i></li><li>to write in addition, to add</li><li>to finish <i>(painting)</i>, to paint in addition</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish <i>(writing)</i></li><li>to write <i>(to)</i></li><li>to write in addition, to add</li><li>to finish <i>(painting)</i>, to paint in addition</li></ol><p><i>дописа́ть</i> (dopisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink up, to finish by drinking</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additional payment, extra charge, surcharge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay extra</li></ol><p><i>доплати́ть</i> (doplatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim as far as</li></ol><p><i>доплы́ть</i> (doplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim as far as</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for sure</li></ol><p><i>допо́длинный</i> (dopódlinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) authentic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) true</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>по́длинный</i> (pódlinnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>until late, until all hours</li></ol><p><i>до</i> (do) +&lrm; <i>по́здно</i> (pózdno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl, to creep <i>(to, as far as)</i></li></ol><p><i>доползти́</i> (dopolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl, to creep <i>(to, as far as)</i></li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brimful</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>полна́</i> (polná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition, supplement, addendum</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) object, complement</li></ol><p><i>допо́лнить</i> (dopólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>additionally, in addition, extra (by way of addition)</li></ol><p><i>дополни́тельный</i> (dopolnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>additional, supplementary</li><li>extra</li></ol><p><i>допо́лнить</i> (dopólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supplement, to add, to amplify, to supply, to enlarge, to expand, to complete, to fill up</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fuller, to be replenished</li></ol><p><i>допо́лнить</i> (dopólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supplement, to add, to amplify, to supply, to enlarge, to expand, to complete, to fill up</li></ol><p><i>допо́лнить</i> (dopólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fuller, to be replenished</li></ol><p><i>дополня́ть</i> (dopolnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antediluvian (extremely ancient)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;ironic) antiquated, old-fashioned, outdated, obsolete</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пото́п</i> (potóp) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); literally, “before the flood”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interrogate</li></ol><p><i>допроси́ть</i> (doprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interrogation (act of interrogating or questioning)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interrogate</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to jump to, to reach by jumping [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>access, admission, admittance</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) tolerance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admit</li><li>to accept, to permit, to allow, to tolerate</li><li>to assume</li><li>to let happen</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be accepted, to be allowed, to be permitted</li></ol><p><i>допуска́ть</i> (dopuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let's assume, let's say, suppose (for example)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>acceptably</li></ol><p><i>допусти́мый</i> (dopustímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admissibility, permissibility</li></ol><p><i>допусти́мый</i> (dopustímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permissible, acceptable</li><li>justifiable</li><li><i>(mathematics)</i> admissible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admit</li><li>to permit, to allow, to tolerate</li><li>to assume</li><li>to let happen</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admission; assumption</li><li>permission, allowance</li></ol><p><i>допусти́ть</i> (dopustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, to elicit</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>пыта́ться</i> (pytátʹsja). Compare Polish <i>dopytać</i> and Slovak <i>dopýtať</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try to find out, to keep asking <i>(about)</i>, to try to elicit</li></ol><p><i>допыта́ться</i> (dopytátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ). Compare Polish <i>dopytywać</i> and Slovak <i>dopytovať</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work <i>(till; until)</i></li><li>to finish off, to complete</li><li>to improve, to work further <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>дорабо́тать</i> (dorabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work <i>(till; until)</i></li><li>to finish off, to complete</li><li>to improve, to work further <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modification, rework, additional work, reworking</li></ol><p><i>дорабо́тать</i> (dorabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gilt-head bream (<i>Sparus aurata</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Spanish <i>dorada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gilt-head bream (<i>Sparus aurata</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Spanish <i>dorada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dorama, Japanese television drama serials</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese ドラマ (<i>dorama</i>), from English <i>drama</i>. Doublet of <i>дра́ма</i> (dráma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow (to; as high as), to reach [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘some level’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be old enough [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘for something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>до того́, что́бы</b> (do tovó, štóby, + infinitive) <i>or</i> <b>что́бы</b> (štóby, + infinitive) <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to rise (to), to attain the level (of) [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘some level’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дорасти́</i> (dorastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow (to; as high as), to reach [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘some level’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be old enough [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘for something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>до того́, что́бы</b> (do tovó, štóby, + infinitive) <i>or</i> <b>что́бы</b> (štóby, + infinitive) <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to rise (to), to attain the level (of) [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘some level’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out completely (shoes, clothes, etc.)</li><li>to finish plucking, to pick the last of (flowers)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pounce on, to fall greedily on, to throw oneself at [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘food, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause oneself trouble through frivolous behavior</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to walk up to in rags [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дорва́ть</i> (dorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prerevolutionary</li><li>specifically, before the October Revolution</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) + German <i>Revolution</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road</li><li>trip, journey</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) way, direction, path to follow, route</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dorga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>expensively, dearly (in an expensive manner, also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dearness (being expensive)</li></ol><p>From <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, precious</li><li>expensive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dorgъ</i>. Doublet of <i>драго́й</i> (dragój), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-priced, expensive</li></ol><p><i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́ящий</i> (stójaščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) my dear, darling (a term of address for both men and women)</li></ol><p>From <i>дорог(о́й)</i> (dorog(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump, chubby, corpulent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дородьныи</i> (dorodĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dorodьnъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>дорі́дний</i> (dorídnyj, “productive; tall, large; natural, innate”), Polish <i>dorodny</i> (“healthy; beautiful; mature”). Meanings such as "healthy", "beautiful", "good", "productive", etc. can be found dialectally in Russian. Per ESSJa, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;do-</i> + <i>&#42;rodъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>; compare Russian <i>до-</i> (do-), <i>род</i> (rod), <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise in price, to go up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>до́рого</i> (dórogo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to value, to prize</li><li>to take care of, to value</li><li>to not to waste, to use wisely</li></ol><p><i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>доро́га</i> (doróga): small road, path</li><li>alley, walkway, footpath, lane, track, trail</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) track, lane</li><li>(music,&#32;computing) track</li><li>runner, rug</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) runway</li><li>channel</li><li>strip, stripe, trace</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) line (<i>of a powdery drug</i>)</li></ol><p><i>доро́га</i> (doróga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) road transport</li></ol><p><i>доро́жный</i> (doróžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тра́нспортный</i> (tránsportnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) road, travelling</li></ol><p><i>доро́га</i> (doróga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traffic sign, road sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roadside bomb (explosive device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveller's cheque (reprinted cheque for a fixed amount)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) dormitory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out completely (shoes, clothes, etc.)</li><li>to finish plucking, to pick the last of (flowers)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish digging</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>доры́ть</i> (dorýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pounce on, to fall greedily on, to throw oneself at [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘food, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause oneself trouble through frivolous behavior</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to walk up to in rags [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to dig to the point of finding [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дорыва́ть</i> (doryvátʹ, “to wear out, to finish plucking”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>дорыва́ть</i> (doryvátʹ, “to finish digging”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish digging</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig to the point of finding [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>доры́ть</i> (dorýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annoyance, nuisance, vexation</li><li>disappointment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dosada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, to annoy, to bother</li><li>to finish planting</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>peevishly, with annoyance</li></ol><p><i>доса́дливый</i> (dosádlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peevish, expressing annoyance</li></ol><p><i>досад(и́ть)</i> (dosad(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>annoyingly, distressingly</li></ol><p><i>доса́дный</i> (dosádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annoying, vexatious, disappointing, deplorable, regrettable, troublesome</li><li>distressed, vexed (expressing distress or vexation) (<i>of tears, a look, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>доса́да</i> (dosáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be annoyed [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>доса́да</i> (dosáda) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, to annoy, to bother</li></ol><p><i>досади́ть</i> (dosadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) goodbye, see you later</li></ol><p><i>до свид(а́ния)</i> (do svid(ánija)) +&lrm; <i>-о́с</i> (-ós)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) goodbye, see you later</li></ol><p><i>до свида́ния</i> (do svidánija) +&lrm; <i>-у́ли</i> (-úli, plural of <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hitherto</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>доселѣ</i> (doselě), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;doselě</i>. Cognates include Slovak <i>dosiaľ</i>, Ukrainian <i>до́сіль</i> (dósilʹ), Serbo-Croatian <i>dosle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board, plank</li><li>blackboard, chalkboard, whiteboard</li><li>plate, panel (a flat metal or stone object of uniform thickness)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) snowboard, surfboard, skateboard</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a flat-chested woman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъska</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diskuz</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>discus</i>. See there for details. Doublet of <i>диск</i> (disk, “disc”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noticeboard, bulletin board</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish telling (a story), to finish saying (a thought, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish telling (a story), to finish saying (a thought, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>досказа́ть</i> (doskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorough</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>doskonały</i> (“perfect, excellent”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send the remainder <i>(of)</i>, to send on, to send in addition</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to seat, to chamber</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ). Compare Czech <i>doslat</i>, Kashubian <i>dosłac</i>, Polish <i>dosłać</i>, and Silesian <i>dosłać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>verbatim, literally, word for word</li></ol><p><i>досло́вный</i> (doslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literalness, verbatimness (the quality of being close to the original&#59; usually of repeated or translated text)</li></ol><p><i>досло́вный</i> (doslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literal; verbatim</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay in service, to stay employed [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘until some time period’</small>]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to serve out (some time period)</li></ol><p><i>дослужи́ть</i> (doslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to achieve (a position or rank) through service, to be promoted [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to some position or rank’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дослу́живать</i> (doslúživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay in service, to stay employed [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘until some time period’</small>]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to serve out (some time period)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to achieve (a position or rank) through service, to be promoted [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to some position or rank’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дослужи́ть</i> (doslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to the whole/rest <i>(of)</i>, to hear through/out, to sit out</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch (something until some event happens)</li><li>to finish watching</li></ol><p><i>досмотре́ть</i> (dosmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervision; examination, inspection; screening</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>досмотре́ть</i> (dosmotrétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch (something until some event happens)</li><li>to finish watching</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pre-Soviet</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) armor (protective layer over a body)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>доспѣти</i> (dospěti), itself eventually from Proto-Slavic &#42;do and &#42;spěti.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ahead of schedule or time; before the appointed time</li></ol><p><i>досро́чный</i> (dosróčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early, ahead of schedule, preterm, premature</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to touch</li><li>to take, to go fetch, to take out</li><li>to get, to procure, to obtain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy someone badly</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to suffice</li></ol><p><i>доста́ть</i> (dostátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall to one's share; to fall to one's lot</li></ol><p><i>достава́ть</i> (dostavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to convey, to supply, to furnish</li><li>to procure, to cause, to give</li><li>to give, to provide, to afford</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery, shipping</li></ol><p><i>доста́вить</i> (dostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to convey, to supply, to furnish</li><li>to procure, to cause, to give</li><li>to give, to provide, to afford</li></ol><p><i>доста́вить</i> (dostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) delivery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliveryman</li></ol><p><i>доста́вить</i> (dostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliverywoman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosperity, (good) fortune; sufficiency</li><li>welfare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>достатъкъ</i> (dostatŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dostatъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>доста́ть</i> (dostátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficiently</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rather, fairly, pretty, quite</li></ol><p><i>доста́точный</i> (dostátočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adequacy, sufficiency</li></ol><p><i>доста́точный</i> (dostátočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sufficient</li></ol><p><i>доста́ток</i> (dostátok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to touch</li><li>to take, to go fetch, to take out</li><li>to get, to procure, to obtain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy someone badly</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to suffice</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall to someone's share; to fall to someone's lot</li></ol><p><i>доста́ть</i> (dostátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to arrive at [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to attain, to achieve [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a limit, achievement, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to amount to, to come to [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a quantity’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дости́гнуть</i> (dostígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>achieved, reached</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to arrive at [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to attain, to achieve [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a limit, achievement, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to amount to, to come to [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a quantity’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́гнуть</i> (-stígnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attainment, achievement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reachable, accessible</li><li>attainable, achievable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, to arrive at [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to attain, to achieve [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a limit, achievement, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to amount to, to come to [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a quantity’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-стичь</i> (-stičʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reliably</li><li>for certain, for sure</li></ol><p><i>достове́рный</i> (dostovérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authenticity (the quality of being genuine or not corrupted from the original)</li></ol><p><i>достове́рный</i> (dostovérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authentic (<i>of a document</i>)</li><li>reliable, trustworthy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>достоиновѣрьнъ</i> (dostoinověrĭnŭ), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀξιόπιστος</i> (axiópistos). By surface analysis, <i>досто-</i> (dosto-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рный</i> (vérnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>positive quality</li><li>dignity, self-respect</li><li>denomination, value (<i>of a banknote</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) rank, title</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>достоиньство</i> (dostoinĭstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suitably, adequately, in an appropriate/adequate/proper manner</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) with dignity</li></ol><p><i>досто́йный</i> (dostójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy, decent</li><li>well-deserved; stately</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>достоинъ</i> (dostoinŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dostojьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>memorable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attraction (something remarkable or worthy of taking note)</li><li>attraction, tourist attraction, landmark, sight, place of interest (notable building or place with historical, cultural or geographical significance)</li><li>(animate,&#32;figuratively) celebrity, attraction</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) sexually attractive part of the body</li></ol><p><i>достопримеча́тельный</i> (dostoprimečátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noteworthy, remarkable, sightseeing, tourist, touristy</li></ol><p><i>досто́йный</i> (dostójnyj, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>примеча́ть</i> (primečátʹ, “to notice, to take note”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;formal) dear, respected, highly esteemed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) property, possessions</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) asset, heritage, treasure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish building</li></ol><p><i>достро́ить</i> (dostróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish cutting (hair, fur, grass, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish cutting (hair, fur, grass, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish building</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>access, admittance, admission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accessibility; availability; moderateness (of prices)</li></ol><p><i>досту́пный</i> (dostúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accessible, available</li><li>simple, easily understood, intelligible, comprehensible</li><li>approachable, popular</li><li>moderate, reasonable</li><li>affordable</li></ol><p><i>до́ступ</i> (dóstup) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free time, leisure, spare time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dosǫgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) free time, leisure, spare time</li></ol><p><i>досу́г</i> (dosúg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leisurely; idle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) caused by idleness, empty; idle</li><li>capable (able and efficient)</li></ol><p><i>досу́г</i> (dosúg) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish counting</li><li>to count to, to count up to</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be found when counting (<i>usually used in the negative, with the meaning "to be missing when counting"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>досчита́ть</i> (dosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish counting</li><li>to count up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>досчита́ть</i> (dosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be found when counting (<i>usually used in the negative, with the meaning "to be missing when counting"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>досчи́тывать</i> (dosčítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send the remainder <i>(of)</i>, to send on, to send in addition</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to seat, to chamber</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ). Compare Kashubian <i>doséłac</i>, Polish <i>dosyłać</i>, and Silesian <i>dosyłać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to the full</li></ol><p><i>до</i> (do) +&lrm; <i>сы́та</i> (sýta), old short genitive of <i>сы́тый</i> (sýtyj), compare Polish <i>do syta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dossier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dossier</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>dorsum</i>. (whence also English <i>endorse</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (to) here, (up to) here</li></ol><p><i>до</i> (do) +&lrm; <i>сюда́</i> (sjudá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reach</li></ol><p><i>досяга́емый</i> (dosjagájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reachable, attainable, accessible, approachable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to reach, to get</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to attain (a post or position)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>сяга́ть</i> (sjagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to reach, to get</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to attain (a post or position)</li></ol><p><i>досяга́ть</i> (dosjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish carrying, to finish dragging, to transfer (<i>everything</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out (clothes, shoes)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drag [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish carrying, to finish dragging, to transfer (everything)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out (clothes, shoes)</li></ol><p><i>дотаск</i> (dotask) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>дотаска́ть</i> (dotaskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(state) subsidy, grant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drag [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>until dark</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>темно́</i> (temnó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) dothideomycete</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Dothideomycetes.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Dothideomycetes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the ground</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>до</i> (do) +&lrm; <i>тла</i> (tla), genitive case of the obsolete noun <i>тло</i> (tlo, “bottom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earlier, already (prior to some time)</li><li>until that temporal point, thereunto</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>дото́шный</i> (dotóšnyj): meticulousness</li></ol><p><i>дото́шный</i> (dotóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) meticulous, pernickety, pedantic; inquisitively, insistently and meticulously or hypercritically going into every detail</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>то́шный</i> (tóšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (make physical contact with) [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>дотро́г</i> (dotróg) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to touch (make physical contact with) [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (make physical contact with) [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>тро́нуть</i> (trónutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw <i>(up to, as far as)</i>, to drag <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to haul <i>(up to; as far as)</i></li><li>to stretch out <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to extend <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to bring <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hold out <i>(till)</i>, to last out <i>(till)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to while away one's time <i>(till, until)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live <i>(till)</i>, to live to see, to last out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to improve, to put right, to brush up, to edit, to touch up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to delay, to protract, to drag one's feet</li></ol><p><i>дотяг-</i> (dotjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach <i>(to)</i>, to touch</li><li><i>(time)</i> to drag by <i>(until)</i></li></ol><p><i>дотя́гивать</i> (dotjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw <i>(up to, as far as)</i>, to drag <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to haul <i>(up to; as far as)</i></li><li>to stretch out <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to extend <i>(to; as far as)</i>, to bring <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hold out <i>(till)</i>, to last out <i>(till)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to while away one's time <i>(till, until)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live <i>(till)</i>, to live to see, to last out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to improve, to put right, to brush up, to edit, to touch up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to delay, to protract, to drag one's feet</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach <i>(to)</i>, to touch</li><li><i>(time)</i> to drag by <i>(until)</i></li></ol><p><i>дотяну́ть</i> (dotjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>doula</i>, itself derived from Greek <i>δούλα</i> (doúla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dopamine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dauphin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dauphin</i>. Doublet of <i>дельфи́н</i> (delʹfín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberian fur coat, wolfskin</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Доха)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doha (the capital city of Qatar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) skinny, gaunt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sickly, weakly</li></ol><p><i>до́х(нуть)</i> (dóx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carrion (dead flesh&#59; carcasses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) to croak (to die)</li><li>to take a breath, to breathe</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to blow (of a wind), to have a (single) blow</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the same source as <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ) below.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>income, revenue (net gain before subtracting the loss)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;doxodъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to, to arrive at, to reach (<i>usually by foot</i>)</li><li>(<i>by&#32;extension</i>) to finally or eventually get to, to achieve some state or condition</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to become understood (by someone) (e.g. about a joke or fraud)</li><li>to come down to</li><li><i>(prices)</i> to rise, to run up to, to amount to</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to ejaculate, to cum</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profitability</li></ol><p><i>дохо́дный</i> (doxódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profitable, paying, lucrative, remunerative</li></ol><p><i>дохо́д</i> (doxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, straightforwardly, in an easy-to-understand way, understandably, simply, intelligibly</li></ol><p><i>дохо́дчивый</i> (doxódčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, straightforward, easy to understand, understandable, simple, intelligible</li></ol><p><i>доходи́ть</i> (doxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) alternative form of <i>до хуя́</i> (do xujá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>docent, reader, associate professor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dozent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precivilized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”)</li><li>diminutive of <i>до́чка</i> (dóčka, “daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daughter's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) daughter; filial</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) daughter, branch; affiliated, subsidiary</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъťerьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>до́чер-</i> (dóčer-) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish reading, to read further (must be used with the preposition <i>до</i> (do))</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish reading, to read further (must be used with the preposition до (do))</li></ol><p><i>дочита́ть</i> (dočitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ): (little) daughter</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) girl, honey (term of address for a young woman)</li><li>(business,&#32;informal,&#32;inanimate) subsidiary, daughter company</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъkťьka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”)</li><li>diminutive of <i>до́чка</i> (dóčka, “daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ, “daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰugh₂tḗr</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дъчи</i> (dŭči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъ̏ťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preschool child (boy)</li></ol><p><i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>шко́ла</i> (škóla) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pre-school</li></ol><p>From <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>шко́льный</i> (škólʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preschooler, little boy under school age</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cunning (sly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of boards, planks</li></ol><p><i>доска́</i> (doská) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>доска́</i> (doská): small plank / board, tablet</li></ol><p><i>доска́</i> (doská) +&lrm; <i>-ечка</i> (-ečka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milker, dairyman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milkmaid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to bother, to pester, to harass or to find fault</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>день рождения</i> (denʹ roždenija, “birthday”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drag, dredge, seagoing dredge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precious metal</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i><b>драг</b>оце́нный <b>мета́лл</b></i> (<b>drag</b>océnnyj <b>metáll</b>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) precious metal</li></ol><p><i>драгмета́лл</i> (dragmetáll) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dear</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) expensive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>драгъ</i> (dragŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dorgъ</i>. Doublet of <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) dragoman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gem, jewel; (plural) jewelry</li><li>precious thing / possession; (plural) valuables</li></ol><p><i>драгоце́нный</i> (dragocénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precious</li></ol><p>From <i>драго́й</i> (dragój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>це́нный</i> (cénnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gem, jewel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragée (a sweet or confection, originally as used to administer drugs, medicine, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>dragée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teasing rhyme, taunting ditty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) teaser (a short film or quote meant to draw an audience to a film or show)</li></ol><p><i>дразни́ть</i> (draznítʹ, “to tease”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease, to pester</li><li>to whet (appetite), to stimulate (curiosity)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drazniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nautical) to burnish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scrub clean</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold, to chew out</li></ol><p>Dutch <i>draaien</i> (“to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) drive (self-motivation)</li><li>drive (in golf, baseball, etc.)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) (disk) drive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drive</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) driver, an interface between hardware and software</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scuffle, fight, tussle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragon (mythical creature)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>δράκων</i> (drákōn). Doublet of <i>драгу́н</i> (dragún).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(diminutive,&#32;endearing) a small dragon; a dwaggy.</li></ol><p><i>драко́н</i> (drakón, “dragon”) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draculin (glycoprotein)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) dram (currency of Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>դրամ</i> (dram).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) drum machine (an electronic musical instrument that can be programmed to create percussion sounds, drum beats, and patterns)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drum machine</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>дра̀м-маши́на</i> (dràm-mašýna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drama</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>drāma</i> (potentially via German <i>Drama</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>δρᾶμα</i> (drâma, “an act, a theatrical act, a play”), from <i>δράω</i> (dráō, “to act, to take action, to achieve”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dramatic effect</li><li>tension</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drama; dramatic, theatrical</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>dramat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dramatically</li></ol><p><i>драмати́чный</i> (dramatíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dramatic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>dramat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playwright, dramatist, dramaturge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dramaturgic (relating to the art of dramatic composition for the stage)</li></ol><p><i>драматурги́я</i> (dramaturgíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dramaturgy (the art of dramatic composition for the stage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>драматический кружок</i> (dramatičeskij kružok, “theatrical troupe”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) A decrepit vehicle of any sort; a clunker or piece of junk</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) A name for any unspecified piece of machinery; a thingamajig</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>dryndula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick potato pancake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>дра́нік</i> (dránik), related to the Russian verb <i>драть</i> (dratʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shake (type of wooden shingle)</li><li>shingled roof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ragged, tattered</li><li>scraggly-coated, mangy (<i>of an animal that has lost patches of fur</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tore up, gashed (having many wounds)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lousy, rotten (terrible)</li></ol><p><i>драть</i> (dratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick woolen cloth</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fleeing, skedaddling, runner (as in “to do a runner”)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) marijuana</li><li>(<i>military</i>) acronym of <i>да́льний разве́дывательный авиацио́нный полк</i> (dálʹnij razvédyvatelʹnyj aviaciónnyj polk, “long-range reconnaissance aviation regiment”)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1787. Borrowed from French <i>drap</i>.</p><p>Deverbal from <i>дра́пать</i> (drápatʹ, “to skedaddle, to hightail”), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drapati</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Romani <i>drab</i> (“medicine”), attested no later than 1981.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skedaddle, to hightail, to flee</li></ol><p>Presumably, with the original sense being “to tear, to scratch”, ultimately descends from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drapati</i>; compare to Czech <i>drápat</i> and Polish <i>drapać</i>; compare to <i>дать дёру</i> (datʹ djóru), <i>удира́ть</i> (udirátʹ), <i>рвану́ть</i> (rvanútʹ), <i>рвать ко́гти</i> (rvatʹ kógti) for other examples of mutation of “to tear” into “to flee”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, to tear up, to tear to pieces</li><li>to strip off, to tear off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to charge, to fleece, to rip off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill (<i>of wild animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause a stinging/burning pain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run away, to escape, to take flight</li><li>to flog, to whip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fuck, to screw, to shag</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дра́ти</i> (dráti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьràti</i> (“to tear, to flay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial) to beat the hell out of (someone) (lit.&#58; to fleece like Sidor's (Isidore's) (she-)goat), to punish severely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to scuffle</li><li>to struggle</li><li>to tear <i>(intransitive)</i></li></ol><p><i>драть</i> (dratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drachma</li><li>drachm, dram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pugnacious</li></ol><p><i>дра́ка</i> (dráka) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighter, brawler</li></ol><p>From <i>драч-</i> (drač-) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún); compare dialectal <i>дра́ча</i> (dráča, “fight, scuffle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) junk, trash, rubbish</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) nonsense, rubbish</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, probably related to <i>дре́безг</i> (drébezg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tinkling, sound of broken glass</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shard, fragment, smithereen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drebězgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jingle, to tinkle</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>дре́безг</i> (drébezg, “jingle, tinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ, stative suffix). The root is from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drebězgъ</i>, cognate with Lithuanian <i>drebė̃znos</i>&nbsp;pl, <i>drebė̃nos</i>&nbsp;pl (“remnants”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dreb-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrebʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raspy, rattletrap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood, wood material, wood substance</li><li>lignin</li><li>timber</li><li>wood pulp, wood cellulose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree frog</li><li>leopard moth (<i>Zeuzera pyrina</i>)</li></ol><p><i>древе́с(ный)</i> (drevés(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laminated-wood</li></ol><p><i>древе́сный</i> (drevésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>слои́стый</i> (sloístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodiness</li></ol><p><i>древе́сный</i> (drevésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) charcoal</li></ol><p><i>древе́сный</i> (drevésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pyroligneous-acid</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pyrolignite</li></ol><p><i>древесноу́ксусный</i> (drevesnoúksusnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pyroligneous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wood; woody, ligneous</li><li>fibrous</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) tree; arboreal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) wood pulp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charcoal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaft, pole, staff</li></ol><p>From <i>дре́во</i> (drévo, “tree, wood”) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko, “diminutive suffix”) (stem-stressed variant) or <i>дре́во</i> (drévo) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Drevlian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) ancient (Relating to antiquity as a historical period)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) old (denoting an ancestral form of a language)</li></ol><p>From <i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij, “ancient”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-Saxon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old English (language of the Anglo-Saxons)</li></ol><p>From <i>дрѐвне-</i> (drèvne-) +&lrm; <i>англи́йский</i> (anglíjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old High German</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old High German</li></ol><p>From <i>дрѐвне-</i> (drèvne-, “ancient”) +&lrm; <i>вѐрхненеме́цкий</i> (vèrxneneméckij, “High German”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ancient Greek</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ancient Greek (language), (short for дрѐвнегре́ческий язы́к (drèvnegréčeskij jazýk))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Biblical Hebrew</li><li>pertaining to ancient inhabitants of Judaea or their culture</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Biblical Hebrew, Classical Hebrew</li></ol><p><i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>евре́йский</i> (jevréjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ancient Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>Дре́вний Еги́пет</i> (Drévnij Jegípet, “Ancient Egypt”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old Icelandic (a historical language under the West Scandinavian language subfamily)</li></ol><p>From <i>дрѐвне-</i> (drèvne-, “old”) +&lrm; <i>исла́ндский</i> (islándskij, “Icelandic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) world's oldest profession</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Novgorodian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Novgorodian (dialect)</li></ol><p><i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>новгоро́дский</i> (novgoródskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ancient Roman</li></ol><p>From <i>дрѐвне-</i> (drèvne-) +&lrm; <i>ри́мский</i> (rímskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old East Slavic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old East Slavic, Old Russian (language), (short for дрѐвнеру́сский язы́к (drèvnerússkij jazýk))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Norse (of or pertaining to ancient Scandinavia, its inhabitants, or their language or culture)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(elliptically for <i>дрѐвнескандина́вский язы́к</i>) Old Norse (common ancestor-language of Danish, Faroese, Icelandic, Swedish, and Norwegian)</li></ol><p><i>дрѐвне-</i> (drèvne-, “old”) +&lrm; <i>скандина́вский</i> (skandinávskij, “Scandinavian”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the ancients</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ancient</li><li>antique, very old</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drevьňь</i>, a yo-stem variant of <i>&#42;drevьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquity <i>(time)</i></li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) antiquities, virtu</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drevьnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дре́вний</i> (drévnij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) tree</li><li>tree (in the expressions <i>родосло́вное дре́во</i> (rodoslóvnoje drévo, “family tree”), <i>генеалоги́ческое дре́во</i> (genealogíčeskoje drévo, “family tree”), <i>дре́во жи́зни</i> (drévo žízni, “tree of life”))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>дрѣво</i> (drěvo). Doublet of <i>де́рево</i> (dérevo), the inherited Old East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feller, felling machine</li><li>tree uprooter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodiness</li></ol><p><i>древови́дный</i> (drevovídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>treelike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodworker, cabinetmaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Xylophaga</i> <i>(genus of marine, wood-boring bivalves)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dendrometry <i>(the measuring of the diameter of standing trees)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planting of trees</li><li>tree plantation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>treelike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing timber, stand of trees</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borer (carpenter moth, Cossidae)</li><li>borer <i>(mollusk)</i></li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) borer</li></ol><p><i>дре́во</i> (drévo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulp grinder <i>(paper)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood plastics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;nautical) dreadnought</li><li>(<i>music</i>) Dreadnought guitar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dreadnought</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) drift (act or motion of drifting)</li><li>drift (movement of something carried by a current)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>drijven</i> or one of its forms.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quail, to funk, to show the white feather, to show cowardice</li></ol><p><i>дрейф</i> (drejf) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drift (to move slowly, pushed by currents of water, air, etc)</li></ol><p><i>дрейф</i> (drejf) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Jewish,&#32;uncountable) dreck, shit, crap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>דרעק</i> (drek), ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrakjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;dated,&#32;also plural) clubs, poles, stakes, usually employed as a weapon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drьkolь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drill (tool)</li><li>PBK-500U Drel (Russian inertial and GLONASS-guided cluster glide bomb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doze, to slumber, to drowse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drowsiness</li></ol><p><i>дрема́ть</i> (dremátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dense, thick, impenetrable (<i>of forests or scrubland</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) backwoods (<i>of people or places</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) backward, ignorant</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) utter, hopeless, impenetrable, abysmal (<i>of negative personal traits</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>дрема́ть</i> (dremátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij); literally, “sleeping, slumbering”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(construction,&#32;medicine) drainage</li><li>drainage system</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) drainage tube</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>drainage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to train (animals)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drill (a person)</li></ol><p>French <i>dresser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>training (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) skin-pass rolling, temper rolling</li></ol><p><i>дрессирова́ть</i> (dressirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animal) trainer, tamer</li></ol><p><i>дрессирова́ть</i> (dressirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dreamcore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dreamcore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarrhea, shits</li><li>bullcrap, junk, worthless product</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>дрисня́</i> (drisnjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have diarrhea, to shart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dristati</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>drístati</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>дрискати</i> (driskati) and more distantly English <i>dirt</i>, Norwegian <i>dritt</i> (“excrement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;nautical) drift (difference between the diameter of a bolt and a hole)</li><li>(<i>specialist</i>) drifting of the value zero on an instrument</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) invertebrate drift (method of movement of certain aquatic animals in a river flow downstream)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) drifting (driving technique)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drift</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;automotive) to drift (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p>From <i>дрифт</i> (drift) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;automotive) to drift (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>дрифт</i> (drift) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) diarrhea</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) wimp, pussy, a weak or a sick person</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a scrawny person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crusher, grinder, breaker, mincer</li></ol><p><i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ, “to crush”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li><li>to smash, to shatter</li><li>to fragment, to split up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drobiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up into small parts, to be crushed</li><li>to break (<i>of waves</i>)</li><li>to be refracted (<i>of rays of light</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figurative) to disintegrate</li></ol><p><i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crushing, breaking up, splitting</li><li>fragmentation, subdivision</li></ol><p><i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fractional (relating to a fraction)</li><li>staccato (with each sound or note sharply detached or separated from the others)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drobьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shotgun</li></ol><p><i>дробь</i> (drobʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) fraction, broken number</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) small-shot, pellets, shot</li><li>(<i>music</i>) drum roll</li><li>(<i>symbol</i>) slash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drobь</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (gadraban, “to chisel, to hew out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firewood</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) wood (low-quality wooden object)</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) drivers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъrva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>дрова́</i> (drová)</li></ol><p><i>дрова́</i> (drová) +&lrm; <i>-и́шки</i> (-íški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(peasant's) wood sledge</li></ol><p><i>дрова́</i> (drová) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firewood cutting, wood cutting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodchopper</li><li>log splitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wood-splitting, wood-chopping</li></ol><p><i>дровоко́л</i> (drovokól) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) wood-cutter <i>(UK)</i>, lumberjack <i>(US)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) woodcutter <i>(UK)</i>, lumberjack <i>(US)</i></li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) longhorn beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A longhorn beetle of the genus <i>Macrodontia</i>, noted for their large proportions and the males' mandibles.</li></ol><p>The term is a calque of translingual <i>Macrodontia</i> (literally “large-toothed”), from <i>дровосе́к</i> (drovosék, “lumberjack, longhorn beetle”) +&lrm; <i>большезу́б</i> (bolʹšezúb, “bigtooth”, noun, respective adjective большезу́бый (bolʹšezúbyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) woodshed</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) firewood merchant</li></ol><p><i>дровя(но́й)</i> (drovja(nój)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wood, firewood</li></ol><p><i>дрова́</i> (drová) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rail or perch in a cart, connecting the front axle to the rear one</li></ol><p>From <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ, “to pull (on a wagon), tug”). Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;droga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cold, to become frozen, to feel chilly; to freeze, to numb (by coldness), to shiver</li><li>to shiver, to shake, to tremble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaking, trembling</li><li>shake, tremor</li></ol><p><i>дрожа́ть</i> (drožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shiver, to shake, to tremble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drъžati</i>; cognate with Polish <i>drżeć</i> and Lower Sorbian <i>rdžaś</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trembling <i>(with)</i>, shivering <i>(with)</i>, shivery</li><li><i>(sound)</i> tremulous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yeast, barm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yeast, barm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дрожди</i> (droždi), <i>дрождиꙗ</i> (droždija), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;droždžьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дро́ги</i> (drógi): droshky (open horse-drawn carriage, especially in Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trembling, shiver, shivering</li><li>vibration</li><li>ripples</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drъžь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrush (bird)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;history) a male member of the Drozdovsky Division of the White Army of South Russia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drozdъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;trasdás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trósdos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Drozdovsky (especially Mikhail Drozdovsky)</li></ol><p><i>Дроздо́в(ский)</i> (Drozdóv(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) drosophila</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Drosophila</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dromedary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drone (unmanned aircraft, or, <i>sensu lato</i>, unmanned vehicle in general)</li><li>(regional,&#32;collective) (wooden) shingles, shakes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drone</i>.</p><p>Compare <i>дра́нка</i> (dránka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kamikaze drone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) drone operator</li></ol><p>From <i>дрон</i> (dron, “drone”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drone (unmanned vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>дрон</i> (dron) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) the most energetic and memorable part of an electronic music composition</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) a small batch of exclusive products that are going on sale</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) game items, money, etc. that drop from a monster or a player's character after they die</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to stop doing something</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to publish</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) get rid of game items (resources, weapons, ammunition, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) throttle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Drossel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) wire</li><li>dart</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>drut</i>.</p><p>Unclear, possibly from Old Norse <i>darraðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin, dart (throwing spear)</li><li>(games,&#32;sports) dart (<i>used in the game of darts</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great bustard (<i>a species of bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dropъty</i>, whose first part is probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dreh₂-</i> (“run”) and the other from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъta</i> (“bird”), which is probably based on Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;put-</i> (“a young, a child, a little animal”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p><p>Compare Czech <i>drop</i>, Polish <i>drop</i>. Cognate with German <i>Trappe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) wanker, masturbator</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) an obsessive fan of something</li></ol><p><i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ер</i> (-er)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wanker, masturbator</li></ol><p><i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ, “to wank, to jerk off”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) to jerk off, to wank, to beat off (to masturbate)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) to tease, to humiliate, to punish; to train to exhaustion</li><li>(slang,&#32;video games) to perfect a skill</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;rare) to pat, to cherish, to pamper</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;rare) to raise, to swell, to irritate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dročiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) a wank (UK), a jerk off (US) (an instance of masturbation)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) a hassle</li></ol><p><i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) masturbator</li></ol><p><i>дроч(и́ть)</i> (droč(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close friend</li><li>(<i>in pairs</i>) other, another</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drugъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;draugás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrewgʰ-</i>.</p><p>Originally the predicative (short) form of <i>друго́й</i> (drugój, “another”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one another, each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>one another, each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to one another, to each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one after another</li><li>in single file</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to one another, to each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>against one another, against each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one from another</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>toward each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>face to face; vis-à-vis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with one another, with each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at one another</li><li>from one another</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>other, another, different</li><li>else, next, second</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дроугъ</i> (drugŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drȗgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>друг</i> (drug).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship (condition of being friends)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;družьba</i>, from <i>&#42;drugъ</i> (“friend, companion”) + <i>&#42;-ьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendliness (the quality of being friendly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>friendly (in a friendly manner)</li></ol><p><i>дружелю́бный</i> (druželjúbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly, amiable (warm, approachable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amiably, in a friendly manner</li></ol><p><i>дру́жеский</i> (drúžeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly</li></ol><p><i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>friendly (in a friendly manner)</li></ol><p><i>дру́жественный</i> (drúžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amiable, friendly</li></ol><p>From <i>дру́жество</i> (drúžestvo, “friendship”) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly fire (weapons fire coming from allied or friendly forces, as opposed to fire coming from enemy forces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) friendship</li></ol><p><i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodyguard, retinue</li><li>militia</li><li>troop, brigade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;družina</i> (“team, company”), from <i>&#42;drugъ</i> (“friend, companion”) + <i>&#42;-ina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>USSR, Russia</i>) police helper; vigilante</li><li>(historical,&#32;Russian Empire) militiaman (a civilian called upon to fight in time of need)</li><li>(historical,&#32;medieval Russia) retainer, fighting man, guard (a member of a medieval prince’s armed retinue, a professional soldier)</li></ol><p><i>дружи́на</i> (družína) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be friends (with), to be on friendly terms (with)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;družiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) old chap, old boy, old fellow</li></ol><p><i>друг</i> (drug, “friend”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>best man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дру́жный</i> (drúžnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>дру́жно</i> (drúžno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amicably, in a friendly manner</li><li>simultaneously, hand in hand, (everybody) together, in a concerted effort</li></ol><p><i>дру́жный</i> (drúžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly, amicable, personable</li><li>harmonious, concurrent, unanimous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;družьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;drugъ</i> (“friend, companion”) + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buddy, pal (friend or casual acquaintance)</li></ol><p><i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friends-comrades (male or female)</li></ol><p>From <i>друг</i> (drug), <i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>druid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>druid</i>, French <i>druide</i>, from Old French, via Latin <i>Druidae</i>, from Gaulish <i>&#42;druwits</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;druwits</i> (literally either “oak-knower” or “firm knower, great sage”), from either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”) or <i>&#42;drew-</i> (“solid, firm, hard”) and <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see, to have knowledge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) cudgel</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuckpole, tallywhacker, truncheon</li></ol><p>Supposed by Moscow circle philologers Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drynъ</i>, as also found in a dialectal Ukrainian <i>дрін</i> (drin); were it so, it would probably be borrowed from a descendant of Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dráwnam</i>, however the word is not even attested before the 20th century, but entered argots, internet corners and political discourse from Soviet-era regiolects, so can be presumed to be an expressive formation from <i>драть</i> (dratʹ), like in the 18th already was formed <i>дрянь</i> (drjanʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰruHs-ne-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰrewHs-</i> (“to fall down, break off”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreusaną</i> (“to fall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drag queen (person, usually male, who dresses up in women's clothing and makeup, typically in an exaggerated fashion and for public performance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>drag queen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, to hit, to screw up</li><li>to bash, to criticize severely, to punish</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have sex with</li><li>to steamroll, to outplay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drǫčiti</i>, the soft "р" is probably of expressive origin, compare <i>дрюк</i> (drjuk) and <i>друк</i> (druk), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drǫkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дрюк</i> (drjuk) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Cognate with Russian <i>удручи́ть</i> (udručítʹ), Old Church Slavonic <i>оудрѫчити</i> (udrǫčiti), Polish <i>dręczyć</i>. Doublet of <i>дручи́ть</i> (dručítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) sex, banging</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate) mistress, prostitute, slut</li></ol><p><i>дрю́чить</i> (drjúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flabby, flaccid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) weak-willed, spineless</li></ol><p>Compare <i>дря́бнуть</i> (drjábnutʹ, “to become flabby”), <i>дрябь</i>&nbsp;f (drjabʹ, “flabbiness”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dręb-</i>. Per Vasmer, quoting Mikloshich, related to Lithuanian <i>dri̇̀bti</i> (“to trickle, to drip with thick drops”) (1sg. <i>drimbù</i>), <i>drabnùs</i> (“fat”), <i>dramblỹs</i> (“thick-chested; elephant”). Apparently also related to Ancient Greek <i>θρόμβος</i> (thrómbos, “clot”), <i>θρομβεῖον</i> (thrombeîon, “lump”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squabbles, small quarrels</li><li>petty troubles, worries</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, perhaps ultimately from imitative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrēu-</i> (“to drone”), ultimately related to Sanskrit <i>ध्रणति</i> (dhráṇati, “to sound”), Old Irish <i>drésacht</i> (“crackling, noise”), German <i>trensen</i> (“to make a prolonged moo”), Dutch <i>drenzen</i> (“to moan”), Ancient Greek <i>θρῆνος</i> (thrênos, “lamentation for the deceased”), Gothic <i>𐌳𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (drunjus, “sound”), English <i>drone</i>, Old Prussian <i>droanse</i> (“corncrake”). Per Vasmer, the singular is <i>дрязг</i> (drjazg) (and Ushakov notes a rare genitive plural <i>дря́згов</i> (drjázgov)); the cognates given above suggest a Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dręzgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lousy, crappy</li></ol><p><i>дрянь</i> (drjanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rubbish, trash</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;animate) rotter, skunk, dirty dog</li></ol><p>First attested in 1731. From <i>дрань</i> (dranʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьranь</i>, from <i>&#42;dьranъ</i>, past passive participle of <i>&#42;dьrati</i> (“to tear, flay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decrepit, senile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дряхлъ</i> (drjaxlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dręx(ъ)lъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slumber</li><li>dormancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) dualism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) dualist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) duathlon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oak (<i>Quercus</i>) (tree and wood)</li><li>tan, bark of an oak or other tree used to obtain tannic acid</li><li>(<i>rаre</i>) boat made from oak boards or a single oak-tree trunk</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) stupid man, blockhead, numskull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dǫbъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) extreme cold; very cold weather</li></ol><p><i>дубе́ть</i> (dubétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p><p>Compare typologically <i>коле́ть</i> (kolétʹ), <i>околе́ть</i> (okolétʹ) (akin to <i>кол</i> (kol)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tannery</li></ol><p><i>дуби́ть</i> (dubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club (weapon)</li><li>fool</li></ol><p>From <i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>ornus</i> (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃s-</i>, whence also <i>я́сень</i> (jásenʹ)). Also compare Ancient Greek <i>δόρυ</i> (dóru) (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>балда́</i> (baldá), <i>оря́сина</i> (orjásina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дуби́на</i> (dubína): bludgeon, cudgel; truncheon, club, baton</li></ol><p><i>дуби́на</i> (dubína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tan (leather, hides)</li></ol><p><i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tanning (<i>of leather or hides</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дуби́ть</i> (dubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tanning (<i>of leather or hides</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дуби́ть</i> (dubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duplicate (an identical copy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubling, duplication</li><li>dubbing (movies)</li></ol><p><i>дубли́ровать</i> (dublírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to duplicate</li><li>to dub (a film)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubloon <i>(former gold coin of Spain and Latin America, Switzerland, and Italy)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>doublon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>film</i>) take</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) reserve</li><li>double</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>double</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter W, w.</li></ol><p>From French <i>double vé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>film</i>) dubbing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>doublage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin coat (with the skin outside)</li></ol><p><i>дублённый</i> (dubljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understudy</li><li>backup (person)</li><li>(<i>film</i>) dubber</li><li>stunt double</li><li>frontage road</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) substitution (substitute item, workalike)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>doubleur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of Dubna</li></ol><p><i>Дубна́</i> (Dubná) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Dubna (name of various cities and rivers in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Дубна́</i> (Dubná) +&lrm; <i>-е́нский</i> (-énskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dubnium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oakwood, oak forest or grove</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) extreme cold</li></ol><p>From <i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p><p>From <i>дубе́ть</i> (dubétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dialectal&#32;or&#32;Middle Russian) alternative form of <i>дубо́вый</i> (dubóvyj, “oak”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѫ́бовъ</i> (dǫ́bovŭ). By surface analysis, <i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-овой</i> (-ovoj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oak; oaken, oaky</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѫ́бовъ</i> (dǫ́bovŭ). By surface analysis, <i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oaklet, oakling, young oak</li></ol><p><i>дуб</i> (dub) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oakwood, oak forest, oak grove</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dǫbrava</i>, <i>&#42;dǫbrova</i> related to <i>&#42;dǫbъ</i> (“oak”) + an unclear suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дуб</i> (dub).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arc, arch</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) arc</li><li>bow, curve, rib</li><li>shaft bow (<i>of a harness</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>дуга</i> (duga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dǫga</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dangāˀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;technology) arc (<i>lamps, lighting, or welding</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дуга́</i> (dugá) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arched, bow-shaped</li></ol><p><i>дуга́</i> (dugá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) pipe, bagpipes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pipe, fife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a fife’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drone on, to harp on one string</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie</li></ol><p><i>дуда́</i> (dudá, “fife”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fife, pipe (small shrill pipe)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) vape</li></ol><p>From <i>дуда́</i> (dudá, “fife, pipe”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Miklosich and Berneker considered this Slavic word to be borrowed from Turkic, but Vasmer and Brückner believe that the close sound rendition of these onomatopoetic words is a "mere chance". Cognate with Lithuanian <i>daudýtė</i> (“panpipe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ду́дка</i> (dúdka, “fife”)</li></ol><p><i>ду́дка</i> (dúdka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) duduk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>դուդուկ</i> (duduk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дуга́</i> (dugá)</li><li>side <i>(of spectacles)</i></li><li>handle, bail (<i>of bucket, kettle, etc.</i>)</li><li><i>(croquet)</i> hoop</li></ol><p><i>дуга́</i> (dugá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ducat</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>ducātus</i>, from <i>dux</i> (akin to <i>dūcō</i>), whence also <i>дож</i> (dož), <i>ду́че</i> (dúče), <i>дюк</i> (djuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzle (of a firearm)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) barrel (of a firearm)</li></ol><p><i>ду(ть)</i> (du(tʹ), “to blow”) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) small pear</li><li>fig sign (a rude gesture in which the hand makes a fist and the thumb is stuck between the index and middle fingers); indicates "nothing for you", "nuts to you"</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>гду́ля</i> (gdúlja) and <i>гду́ня</i> (gdúnja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъduňa</i>, from Latin <i>cydōnia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Κυδωνία</i> (Kudōnía, “quince-tree”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>фи́га</i> (fíga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, meditation</li><li>duma (<i>Russian legislature</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duma</i> (“thought, care, council”), Origin disputed. Either from Germanic, possibly Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmaz</i> (“judicial decision, act, thought”), Gothic <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐍃</i> (dōms, “judgment, act, thought”), <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan, “to judge”), <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼</i> (dōm, “council, thought”). Also compare modern German <i>-tum</i>, English <i>-dom</i>, <i>doom</i>. Or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke”), akin to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duti</i> (“to blow, to inflate”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dymъ</i> (“smoke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) a device supposedly capable of self-learning</li></ol><p><i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li><li>to believe, to suppose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dumati</i>, origin disputed. Either a loan from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmijaną</i> (“to judge, think”) (whence English <i>deem</i>) either as a direct loan or ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke”), akin to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duti</i> (“to blow, to inflate”), <i>&#42;dymъ</i> (“smoke”). For the latter compare <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul, emotion”), occasionally also “thought, mind”. Also compare <i>надме́нный</i> (nadménnyj, “supercilious, haughty, arrogant”) as another meaning change to a mental characteristic from the same root.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be minding one's own business, to be in one's own world (not paying attention to outside stimuli)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to think about one's own (thing/business)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem, to appear</li></ol><p><i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of the duma (the lower house of the Federal Assembly, or the Russian national parliament)</li></ol><p><i>ду́ма</i> (dúma) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ду́ма</i> (dúma, “thought”): (<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) thought</li></ol><p><i>ду́ма</i> (dúma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) of the Boyars' Council</li></ol><p><i>ду́ма</i> (dúma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) duma</li></ol><p><i>ду́ма</i> (dúma) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) doomscrolling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>doomscrolling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dungan (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dungan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waft, whiff, puff, breath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to rush, to hasten, to dash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dunǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>duodecillion (10<sup>39</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Southern Russia) ass, arse (the buttocks of a person or animal)</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>dupa</i> (“arse”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dupa</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) great snipe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hollowed (<i>of wood</i>)</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) decayed, rotten (<i>of teeth</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дупло́</i> (dupló) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree hollow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dental cavity</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) cunt, pudendum muliebre</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duplo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imbecile, fool, simpleton, idiot <i>(stupid woman with poor judgment)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) fool, simpleton, idiot (stupid person with poor judgment)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) durak (a simple card game in which the last player holding cards in their hand is the loser)</li></ol><p>From <i>дурь</i> (durʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid; blasted (<i>used as an intensifier</i>)</li></ol><p><i>дура́к</i> (durák) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;derogatory) imbecile, fool, simpleton, idiot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool (someone)</li></ol><p><i>дура́к</i> (durák) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fool about, to play the fool</li></ol><p><i>дура́чить</i> (duráčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дура́к</i> (durák): fool, idiot, imbecile</li></ol><p><i>дура́к</i> (durák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>playful, mischievous, naughty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) madhouse, bedlam, funny farm</li><li>(informal,&#32;colloquial) chaos, bedlam</li></ol><p>Possibly from дом дураков <i>(house of fools)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) fool, idiot, imbecile (person with poor judgement or little intelligence)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ду́рень</i> (dúrenʹ). By surface analysis, <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>дурь</i> (durʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>durian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>durian</i>, from Malay <i>durian</i>, from Proto-Malayic <i>&#42;duri-an</i>, from Proto-Malayo-Polynesian <i>&#42;duʀi-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to frolic, to play pranks, to be naughty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave like a fool</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obstinate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duriti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mental hospital</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mess; muddle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>datura</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) narcotic, drug</li></ol><p>A Russian formation based on <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój). An alternative hypothesis linking the word to Turkish <i>derman</i> (“medicine”) is rejected.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intoxicate, to stupefy, to cloud the consciousness</li></ol><p><i>дурма́н</i> (durmán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notoriety</li><li>ill repute, disrepute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ду́рно</i> (dúrno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ду́рно</i> (dúrno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become less attractive; to become ugly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly (in a bad manner)</li></ol><p><i>дурно́й</i> (durnój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish, stupid</li><li>bad</li><li>ugly</li><li>reprehensible</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;durьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дурь</i> (durʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giddiness, nausea</li><li>unbeautifulness</li></ol><p><i>дурно́й</i> (durnój) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupidity, folly</li></ol><p><i>дур(но́й)</i> (dur(nój)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colander (a bowl-shaped kitchen utensil with holes in it used for draining food such as pasta)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Durchschlag</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>cedník</i>, Macedonian <i>цедалка</i> (cedalka), Polish <i>cedzak</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>цедило</i>, Slovene <i>cedilo</i> (akin to <i>цеди́ть</i> (cedítʹ), i.e. <i>**цеди́ло</i>, <i>**це́дник</i>, <i>**цежа́к</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) folly, foolishness, nonsense</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) marijuana; grass, pot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;durь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) female fool, idiot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) preparatory powder of the insecticide DDT (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>dust</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to be drafty</li><li>to go quickly, to scamper</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke marijuana</li></ol><p>From Late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dǫti</i> from Early Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъm-ti</i>, from the root of <i>&#42;dymъ</i> (“smoke”). Cognate to Russian <i>дым</i> (dym) and English <i>damp</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get angry [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pout, to sulk</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to play passionately [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a game’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ду́ть</i> (dútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compressed gas (for metal smelting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit</li><li>mind</li><li>courage</li><li>ghost</li><li>breath</li><li>scent</li><li>rebel (especially in Afghanistan; from душма́н (dušmán))</li><li>(military,&#32;slang) new recruit, rookie who is sometimes abused by the longer serving soldiers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dukhan (a small restaurant in the Caucasus)</li></ol><p>Compare Georgian <i>დუქანი</i> (dukani, “shop, store”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfume</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clergy, priesthood</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Polish <i>duchowieństwo</i>, a calque of German <i>Geistlichkeit</i>. By surface analysis, <i>духо́вный</i> (duxóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven (chamber used for baking or heating)</li></ol><p><i>духово́й</i> (duxovój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) father confessor, chaplain, spiritual father</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>духовьникъ</i> (duxovĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duxovьnikъ</i>. Compare Old Czech <i>duchovník</i>, Ukrainian <i>духівни́к</i> (duxivnýk), Belarusian <i>духо́ўнік</i> (duxównik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>spiritually</li></ol><p><i>духо́вный</i> (duxóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirituality</li></ol><p><i>духо́вный</i> (duxóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li><li>ecclesiastical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air rifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;music) wind; brass</li><li>heat-operated</li><li>pneumatic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duxovъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дух</i> (dux) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind instrument</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brass band (a group of musicians who play brass instruments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sultriness, closeness, stuffiness, sultry / stuffy air</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) duce (nickname for Mussolini)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) an authoritarian leader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>duce</i> (“leader”). Further from Latin <i>dux</i> (akin to <i>dūcō</i>), whence also <i>дож</i> (dož) and <i>дюк</i> (djuk). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i>, whence also the second component of <i>ге́рцог</i> (gércog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shower (device for bathing)</li><li>shower (<i>instance of using the shower</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>douche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soul</li><li>spirit</li><li>darling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>доуша</i> (duša, “soul”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duša</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shower room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychic pain, mental anguish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mentally</li><li>sincerely</li></ol><p><i>душе́вный</i> (dušévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mentally sick, insane, mad, crazy, lunatic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insane person, mentally sick person</li></ol><p><i>душе́вный</i> (dušévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>больно́й</i> (bolʹnój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sincerity, heartfulness</li></ol><p><i>душе́вный</i> (dušévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mental, psychic</li><li>sincere, heartful</li><li>soulful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duševьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shower</li></ol><p><i>душ</i> (duš) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) murderer</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) villain; an evil person</li></ol><p><i>душе-</i> (duše-) +&lrm; <i>губ</i> (gub)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>душа</i> (duša): soul</li><li>an affectionate, familiar term of address</li></ol><p><i>душ(а́)</i> (duš(á), “soul”) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legal representative, executor <i>(of someone's will)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heartbreaking, heartrending</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deliberately tragic</li></ol><p><i>душе-</i> (duše-) +&lrm; <i>раздира́ющий</i> (razdirájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) soul-saving</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) emphatically instructive, edifying</li></ol><p><i>душа́</i> (dušá, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спаси́тельный</i> (spasítelʹnyj, “saving”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ду́шка</i> (dúška) pleasant person, dear, love (a term of friendly address)</li></ol><p>From <i>ду́шка</i> (dúška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sentimental, heart-wrenching</li></ol><p><i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>щипа́ть</i> (ščipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragrant, sweet-scented</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allspice</li></ol><p>Literally, “fragrant pepper”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) strangler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smother, to stifle, to strangle, to throttle</li><li>to oppress, to stifle, to repress, to suppress</li><li>to choke, to suffocate</li><li>to perfume, to scent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dušiti</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dušiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>духи</i> (duxi) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oregano (<i>Origanum</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasant person, dear, love, nice fellow, nice woman</li></ol><p><i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Turkmenistan,&#32;Afghanistan) mujahid, enemy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دشمان (düşman), from Persian دشمن (došman).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) killjoy, bore, pedant (someone who is unpleasant to talk to due to being excessively negative, boring or pedantic)</li></ol><p><i>душни́ть</i> (dušnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be a killjoy, to be a bore, to be pedantic (to act excessively negatively, boringly or pedantically)</li></ol><p><i>ду́шный</i> (dúšnyj, “stuffy”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stuffily (in a poorly-ventilated and close manner)</li></ol><p><i>ду́шный</i> (dúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat stuffy (poorly-ventilated)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat fussy</li></ol><p><i>ду́шный</i> (dúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stuffy (poorly-ventilated&#59; close)</li><li>(slang,&#32;neologism,&#32;sarcastic) fussy, nitty, pedantic; boring (of a person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dušьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>дух</i> (dux) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whiff of decay</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) taint</li></ol><p><i>дух</i> (dux) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>душа́</i> (dušá, “soul”)</li></ol><p><i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duel (combat between two persons, often over a matter of honor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>duel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) duet, duo</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from German <i>Duett</i>, from Italian <i>duetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dharma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>धर्म</i> (dhárma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Church Slavonic,&#32;obsolete&#32;or&#32;ironic) daughter</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰugh₂tḗr</td></tr></table></p><p>Borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>дъщи</i> (dŭšti). Doublet of <i>дочь</i> (dočʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rack (torturing device)</li></ol><p><i>ды́бать</i> (dýbatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Ukraine</i>) to totter, to walk slowly and clumsily forward especially on back feet</li><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;slang) to pinch, to obtain in a nefarious fashion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dybati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(standing) on end</li></ol><p>Apparently the instrumental case of a no-longer-existent word; compare <i>на дыбы́</i> (na dybý, “on its hind legs (<i>of a horse</i>)”), and see <i>-ом</i> (-om).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>на дыбы́</i> (na dybý)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) well, so, then (<i>used as a generic lead-in or expresses emphasis</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) or what?, right? (<i>asks for affirmation</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) of course, you bet (<i>expresses certainty</i>)</li></ol><p>Dialectal form of <i>так</i> (tak), from Old East Slavic <i>тако</i> (tako), <i>такъ</i> (takŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tako</i> (adverb), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;takъ</i> (adjective).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beanpole (a tall, thin person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke</li><li>(historical,&#32;obsolete,&#32;taxation) taxable household unit in medieval Rus</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰuh₂mós</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee smoker</li></ol><p><i>дым</i> (dym) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) smokescreen</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>дымова́я заве́са</i> (dymovája zavésa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, to emit smoke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dymiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, to emit smoke, to fume</li></ol><p><i>дыми́ть</i> (dymítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haze, mist</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) dimity</li></ol><p>From <i>дым</i> (dym, “smoke, fume”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>From Ottoman Turkish دیمی (dimi) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Influenced by <i>ды́мка</i> (dýmka, “haze, mist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoky</li></ol><p><i>дым</i> (dym) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) smokescreen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) smoke</li></ol><p><i>дым</i> (dym) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smoke grenade</li></ol><p><i>дымов(о́й)</i> (dymov(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small stream of smoke</li></ol><p>From <i>дым</i> (dym) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flue</li></ol><p><i>дым</i> (dym) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoky</li><li>tinted, smoke-colored</li></ol><p><i>ды́мка</i> (dýmka) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дым</i> (dym).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) melon</li></ol><p><i>ды́ня</i> (dýnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дꙑнꙗ</i> (dynja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dyňa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κυδώνιον (μῆλον)</i> (kudṓnion (mêlon), “quince”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>пе́рсик</i> (pérsik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) backwater, out-of-the-way place</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dyra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дыра́</i> (dyrá): small, often inaccurately made hole</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) vagina, pussy</li></ol><p><i>дыра́</i> (dyrá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a kind of Priestless Old Believer (in particular, the one who prays through a special hole in the wall)</li></ol><p>From <i>дыра</i> (dyra) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole punch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ды́рка</i> (dýrka): small, often inaccurately made hole</li></ol><p><i>ды́рка</i> (dýrka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;sciences) hole; p-type</li></ol><p><i>ды́рка</i> (dýrka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>containing many holes, holey</li></ol><p><i>дыра́</i> (dyrá) +&lrm; <i>-явый</i> (-javyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) breath, exhalation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) upper part of the abdomen, epigastrium</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dyxъ</i>, deverbal from <i>&#42;dyxati</i> (compare Russian <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ)). Cognate with literary Bulgarian <i>дих</i> (dih, “breath”), dialectal Macedonian <i>дих</i> (dih, “sigh, breath”), Serbo-Croatian <i>dȋh</i> (“sigh, breath”), Slovak <i>dych</i> (“sigh, breath”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blowhole (<i>in a marine animal</i>)</li><li>blowhole (<i>in the ground</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) spiracle</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) stigma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath</li><li>breathing, respiration</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ, “to breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije, verbal abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>дыха́ние</i> (dyxánije, “breath, breathing, respiration”)</li></ol><p><i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нье</i> (-ánʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respiratory</li></ol><p><i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) to breathe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dyxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe, to respire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dyšati</i> (“to breathe”), from earlier <i>&#42;dyxěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be on one's last legs, to be at death's door (to be or seem about to die or fail, about a person, building, company etc.)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to breathe onto the frankincense”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to breathe</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to get on well, to live</li></ol><p><i>дыша́ть</i> (dyšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>breathing, respiring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawbar, shaft (<i>attached to the front axle of a trailer; historically, the central shaft used to harness two animals to a wagon</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>dyszel</i>, from Middle Low German <i>dîsel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) devil, insidious person</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>dammit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>диꙗволъ</i> (dijavolŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos), from <i>διαβάλλω</i> (diabállō, “I slander”, from <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “I throw”)).</p><p>For the meaning development, compare Russian <i>кида́ла</i> (kidála), <i>кида́лово</i> (kidálovo) related to <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Дьявол)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Devil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satanist, devil worshipper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil-worship</li></ol><p><i>дья́вол</i> (dʹjávol, “devil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>покло́нничество</i> (poklónničestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>devilishly (in a devilish manner)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;as an intensifier) as hell (very, extremely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devil's</li><li>diabolical, fiendish, devilish</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;as an intensifier) devil's own, hellish, hell of a</li></ol><p><i>дья́вол</i> (dʹjávol) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little devil, imp</li><li>hyperactive child, mischievous child</li></ol><p><i>дья́вол</i> (dʹjávol, “devil”) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok, “the young of an animal; -let, -ling”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) dyak, clerk, official in Russia in the 14th-17th centuries</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) dyak, a low-ranked clergyman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>диꙗкъ</i> (dijakŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>διάκος</i> (diákos). Compare another meaning development from the source: Czech <i>žák</i> (“pupil; disciple”). Akin to <i>дья́кон</i> (dʹjákon).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>clēricus</i> (whence <i>клерк</i> (klerk)), from Ancient Greek <i>κληρικός</i> (klērikós), whence <i>кли́рик</i> (klírik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deacon</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>διάκονος</i> (diákonos).</p><p>For the <i>дья́кон</i> (dʹjákon)—<i>дьяк</i> (dʹjak) relation, compare typologically <i>кли́рик</i> (klírik), <i>клерк</i> (klerk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deacon's wife</li></ol><p><i>дья́кон</i> (dʹjákon) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) office of a dyak, clerk, official in Russia in 14-17 centuries</li></ol><p><i>дьяк</i> (dʹjak) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) dyak, clerk, official in Russia in 14-17 centuries</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) dyak, a low-ranked clergyman</li></ol><p><i>дьяк</i> (dʹjak) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Д, д.</li><li>The Roman letter D, d.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang&#32;or&#32;BDSM</i>) daddy (a dominant male partner)</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) a sugar daddy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>daddy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) dowel, pin, key</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dübel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dugong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) very</li></ol><p><i>дю́жий</i> (djúžij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e). Compare Ukrainian <i>дуже</i> (duže, “very”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) very strong</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) hefty, stout</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dužь</i>. Vasmer suggests a variant of dialectal <i>ду́жий</i> (dúžij, “strong”), cognate with Ukrainian <i>ду́жий</i> (dúžyj, “powerful, strong”), Belarusian <i>ду́жы</i> (dúžy, “powerful, strong”), Polish <i>duży</i> (“big, strong”), <i>dużo</i> (“a lot”), and more distantly with Lithuanian <i>daũg</i> (“a lot”), Latvian <i>daũdz</i> (“a lot”), <i>padūgt</i> (“to have the opportunity”), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (dugan, “to suit”), German <i>taugen</i> (“to suit”), <i>Tugend</i> (“virtue”), Ancient Greek <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō, “to encounter, to come upon”), Old Irish <i>dúal</i> (“suitable”). Others see a relation with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dęg-</i>, as in dialectal <i>дя́гнуть</i> (djágnutʹ, “to recover, to improve”).</p><p>Noncognate with Russian <i>недю́жинный</i> (nedjúžinnyj) (which is derived from <i>дю́жина</i> (djúžina), which is via PIE componentwise cognate with <i>двена́дцать</i> (dvenádcatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dozen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>douzaine</i> (“twelve”), from Latin <i>duodecim</i> (“twelve”), from <i>duo</i> (“two”) + <i>decem</i> (“ten”) (via PIE componentwise cognate with <i>двена́дцать</i> (dvenádcatʹ)). Influenced by <i>дю́жий</i> (djúžij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordinary, nothing special</li></ol><p><i>дю́жина</i> (djúžina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>duim</i>, cognate with English <i>thumb</i>. Doublet of <i>домкра́т</i> (domkrát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) inch</li><li>one-inch-long, one-inch-wide, one-inch-thick, one-inch-high</li></ol><p><i>дюйм</i> (djujm) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dune (a ridge or hill of sand piled up by the wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Düne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duralumin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Düsseldorf</li></ol><p><i>Дю́ссельдорф</i> (Djússelʹdorf) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angelica (<i>Angelica archangelica</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dęgyľь</i> or <i>&#42;dęgľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дя́дя</i> (djádja): uncle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gaffer, guv</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дя́дя</i> (djádja, “uncle”): (<i>colloquial</i>) man, fellow, guy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncle's</li><li>avuncular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) man, guy; gaffer, guv</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>дя́дя</i> (djádja): uncle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) man, uncle (an older man&#59; also as a term of address)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) the man, boss (authority figure)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>дѣдѩ</i> (dědję), baby-talk assimilation of Old East Slavic <i>дѣдъ</i> (dědŭ). Displaced Russian <i>уй</i> (uj) and <i>стрый</i> (stryj), the common Slavic terms for maternal and paternal uncle, respectively.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodpecker (e.g., <i>Dendrocopos major</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) informer, whistle-blower</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) dimwit, blockhead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dętelъ</i>. Cognate with regional Bulgarian <i>детел</i> (detel), Serbo-Croatian <i>дѐтао</i>, Slovene <i>detel</i>, Slovak <i>ďateľ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дя́тел</i> (djátel).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar, pitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;degъtь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;degut(j)as</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰegʷʰ-</i>. Cognate with Slovak <i>decht</i>.</p><p>Likely a Baltic borrowing (cf. Lithuanian <i>degùtas</i> : <i>dègti</i>; Latvian <i>deguts</i> (“birch tar”) : <i>degt</i>). Absent in the South Slavic languages. Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žeťi</i> (whence Russian <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ).</p><p>Non-Slavic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>τέφρα</i> (téphra), Ancient Greek <i>θεπτανός</i> (theptanós), Latin <i>foveō</i>, Latin <i>febris</i> (whence Russian <i>февра́ль</i> (fevrálʹ), English <i>fever</i>, Russian <i>фебри́льный</i> (febrílʹnyj)), Sanskrit <i>दहति</i> (dahati, “burn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twitchy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fidgety, jumpy, antsy</li></ol><p>Adjectivised passive past participle of <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, to tug</li><li>to pull out</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to twitch, to throb, to twinge</li><li>to move sharply, to jerk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disturb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrgati</i> (“to pull, to comb (flax)?”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twitch</li><li><i>(image)</i> to jitter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fret, to fidget</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to scurry, to scamper</li></ol><p><i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornel, dogwood (<i>Cornus</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dernъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turf, sod, divot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, to tug</li><li>to pull out</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to twitch, to throb, to twinge</li><li>to move sharply, to jerk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disturb</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drink, to knock back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set out, to go out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twitch</li><li><i>(image)</i> to jitter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fret, to fidget</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to scurry, to scamper</li></ol><p><i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cheaply (in a cheap manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>диа́кон</i> (diákon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>де́ва</i> (déva).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>де́душка</i> (déduška).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>дед</i> (ded).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>де́ло</i> (délo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Е</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>E</i>/<i>e</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) yeah!, yo!, oh yeah!</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Е)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Е</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>E</i>/<i>e</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>его величество</i> (jevo veličestvo, “His Majesty”)</li><li>initialism of <i>её величество</i> (jejó veličestvo, “Her Majesty”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging,&#32;vulgar) censored spelling of <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ)</li></ol><p>Shortening of еба́ть (jebátʹ, “to fuck, to have sex”). The "5" part is from пять (pjatʹ, “five”), due to the word's similar sound to the second syllable of еба́ть.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuckability, fuckableness (the abstract quality of being desirable as a sexual partner, sexual attractiveness)</li></ol><p><i>еба́бельный</i> (jebábelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuckable</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) + suffix based on French <i>-able</i> (as in <i>респекта́бельный</i> (respektábelʹnyj), <i>комфорта́бельный</i> (komfortábelʹnyj)) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) stupid thing</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) "foreign agent" mark, prescribed to be used for members of Russian opposition</li></ol><p>from Chuvash <i>япала</i> (jap̬ala) IPA [jabaʲla] thing</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar) a stupid person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) face</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) mouth</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) mouth, piehole</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) face, mug</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) nose</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;rare) penis, dick, cock</li></ol><p><i>еба́ло</i> (jebálo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) face</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) head</li></ol><p><i>еба́ло</i> (jebálo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) oddball, weirdo, fool, moron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;vulgar) something incomprehensible</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;vulgar) hassle</li><li>(animate,&#32;vulgar) fool, simpleton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) stupid, idiotic, moronic, crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ёбаный</i> (jóbanyj)</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;transitive) to fuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;with the word <i>мозг</i> (mozg, “brain”)) to get on someone's nerves, to be a pain in the arse/ass, to annoy, to blow someone's mind, to mindfuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;interrogative or negative) to concern, to bother, to be of interest</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to treat with contempt or indifference, to not care</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to kick ass</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;interrogative or negative) to know</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) expressing surprise, holy shit, what the fuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck you!; fuck off!</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) very, a lot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jebàti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;jebtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃yébʰeti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃yebʰ-</i>. Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>οἴφω</i> (oíphō), Sanskrit <i>यभति</i> (yabhati).</p><p>Compare Czech <i>jebat</i>, Polish <i>jebać</i>, Ukrainian <i>їба́ти</i> (jibáty), Serbo-Croatian <i>јебати</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (mutually, together)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to tinker with, to mess with, to screw with</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck oneself</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do something intensively - to hit, to beat, to strike; to knock; to work (hard), to work one's ass off; to move, to walk (a long distance); to play an instrument; to shake; to fall (of rain or snow) (replaces a number of verbs)</li></ol><p>Derived from еба́ть (jebátʹ, “to fuck”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to drink (alcoholic beverage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;colloquial) bumfuck nowhere, Hicksville</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucker, motherfucker, fuck (contemptible person, usually male)</li></ol><p><i>ебло́</i> (jebló) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) stupid, bad</li></ol><p><i>ебла́н</i> (jeblán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) augmentative of <i>ебло́</i> (jebló)</li></ol><p><i>ебло́</i> (jebló) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) face, mug</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) mouth, piehole</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;vulgar) the act of fucking, fuck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jèbľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) an act of sexual intercourse</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) nonsense, rubbish</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something long and boring; tedious or troublesome task</li></ol><p><i>еб(а́ть)</i> (jeb(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a long and boring, tedious task</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something boring</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá). Compare with Ukrainian <i>їботня́</i> (jibotnjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to beat</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;vulgar) to work (hard)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;vulgar) to go</li></ol><p>Derived from еба́ть (jebátʹ, “to fuck”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;transitive) to fuck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ети</i> (jeti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jetì</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (mutually, together)</li></ol><p><i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) cold (low temperature), freezing weather, frost</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be cold, to feel cold</li></ol><p><i>ебу́н</i> (jebún) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (mutually, together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) the capability to have sex frequently or for a long time</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shittiness</li></ol><p><i>ебу́чий</i> (jebúčij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking (capable of having or willing to have sex frequently or for a long time)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) fucking (used as an emphasiser)</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;humorous) used to exclaim peeve, dismay or anger as well as awe or astonishment</li></ol><p>Literally, “fucked mother”; <i>ебёна</i> (jebjóna, “fucked”) + <i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucked</li></ol><p><i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gospel (first section of New Testament)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good message”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evangelist, evangelist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evangelical; (<i>relational</i>) gospel</li></ol><p><i>ева́нгелие</i> (jevángelije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(fandom slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;derogatory&#32;or&#32;humorous) obsessed with the anime or manga Neon Genesis Evangelion.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Е́ва</i> (Jéva) +&lrm; <i>ебану́тый</i> (jebanútyj, “fucking stupid, fucking crazy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eugenics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>eugenics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) eugenia (<i>Eugenia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Eugenia</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Евгения)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Yevgeniya, from Ancient Greek, equivalent to English Eugenia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Εὐγενία</i> (Eugenía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eunuch</li></ol><p>Ultematily from Ancient Greek <i>εὐνοῦχος</i> (eunoûkhos)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>еговый</i> (jegovyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) his</li></ol><p>First attested as <i>евымъ</i> (jevym, n.Isg) in 1661.02.25 in letter from Иван Гебдон (Івашко Гебдонъ) to Ю. И. Ромодановский.</p><p>Compare <i>их</i> (ix), <i>ихий</i> (ixij) and Czech <i>jí</i>, <i>její</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>егойный</i> (jegojnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>егоный</i> (jegonyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yevpatoria</li></ol><p>From <i>Евпато́рия</i> (Jevpatórija, “Yevpatoria”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian</li></ol><p><i>евр-</i> (jevr-) +&lrm; <i>А́зия</i> (Ázija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jew, Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἑβραῖος</i> (Hebraîos), from Aramaic <i>עִבְרָי</i> (ʿiḇrāy), from Hebrew <i>עִבְרִי</i> ('ivrí, ʿiḇrī), from <i>עבר</i> ('éver, ʿéḇer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jewish</li><li>Hebrew</li><li>Yiddish</li></ol><p><i>евре́й</i> (jevréj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Judaeo-Spanish</li></ol><p>From <i>евре́йский</i> (jevréjskij, “Jewish, Hebrew”) +&lrm; <i>испа́нский</i> (ispánskij, “Spanish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>евре́йский</i> (jevréjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jewishness</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) the Jews; Jewry</li></ol><p><i>евре́й</i> (jevréj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>е́вро</i> (jévro); euro</li></ol><p><i>е́вро</i> (jévro) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>euro (€, currency)</li></ol></html>:/ˈjevrə/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Euro- (related to Europe)</li></ol><p>Related to French <i>euro-</i>, from <i>Евро́па</i> (Jevrópa, “Europe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>европеец</i> (jevropejec, “European”) +&lrm; <i>гей</i> (gej, “gay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European (person)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) westerner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Europeanisation (assimilation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>European</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) European jaguar (<i>Panthera gombaszoegensis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij, “European”) +&lrm; <i>ягуа́р</i> (jaguár, “jaguar”). Compare English <i>European jaguar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>europium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building renovation using modern Western European materials</li></ol><p><i>Евро́па</i> (Jevrópa) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ремо́нт</i> (remónt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Euroscepticism</li></ol><p><i>евро-</i> (jevro-) +&lrm; <i>скептици́зм</i> (skepticízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurocentrism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eustachian tube</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acai (South American palms of the genus <i>Euterpe</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) mugwort, wormwood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>евшанъ</i> (jevšanŭ) (attested 1201), from a Turkic language; compare to Ottoman Turkish یاوشان, Turkish <i>yavşan</i>, Turkmen <i>ýowşan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gamekeeper</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) hunter</li><li>(<i>military</i>) jaeger, chasseur (a type of light infantryman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Jäger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><i>Еги́пет</i> (Jegípet) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptology (the study of ancient Egypt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) his</li><li>(<i>possessive</i>) its</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fidgety person, fidget</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) of him</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) his</li></ol><p>Per Russian language dictionary (1897), <i>ево́йный</i> (jevójnyj) is from <i>ево́ный</i> (jevónyj, “his”) with 'й' from <i>е́йный</i> (jéjnyj).</p><p>Compare Old Polish <i>jegojski</i> and Russian <i>ево́й</i> (jevój) and <i>свой</i> (svoj), <i>твой</i> (tvoj), <i>мой</i> (moj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) of him</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) his</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, <i>ево́ный</i> (jevónyj) is from <i>его́</i> (jevó, “of him; his”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, “adjective”).</p><p>Compare Belarusian <i>ягоны</i> (jahóny, “his”) and Old Polish <i>jegojski</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>еди́нственное число́</i> (jedínstvennoje čisló, “singular number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>еди́нственное число́</i> (jedínstvennoje čisló, “singular number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li><li>meal</li><li>eating</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěda</i>, from <i>&#42;(j)ěsti</i> (“to eat”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ḗˀstei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i> (“to eat”). Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἔδω</i> (édō) (> <i>ἔδεσμα</i> (édesma)), Latin <i>edō</i> (> <i>epulum</i>, <i>ēsca</i>; see also <i>prandium</i>), Sanskrit <i>अत्ति</i> (átti), Lithuanian <i>ė́sti</i> (“to eat (of animals), to swallow, to gobble”), Hittite <i>𒂊𒀉𒈪</i> (eidmi, “I eat”), Old English <i>etan</i> (English <i>eat</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, barely</li><li>scarcely</li><li>just, barely, as soon as</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edъva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, scarcely, barely (used to express doubt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost</li><li>perhaps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just barely, only just, only slightly</li></ol><p><i>едва́</i> (jedvá) +&lrm; <i>едва́</i> (jedvá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>uni- (one)</li></ol><p>From <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj, “united; unified; one; single”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥдинъ</i> (jedinŭ). Doublet of <i>оди́н</i> (odín, “one”) and <i>одно-</i> (odno-, “uni-”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unity</li><li>union</li><li>solidarity</li><li>alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>един-</i> (jedin-) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) one, unity (neutral element in multiplication)</li><li>digit</li><li>unit</li><li><i>(mark)</i> one <i>(out of five)</i>, poor; F mark, F grade</li><li>number one (e.g. bus, tram, trolleybus, etc.)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (a) few</li></ol><p>From <i>един-</i> (jedin-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single</li><li>solitary, isolated</li></ol><p><i>едини́ца</i> (jediníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>uni- (one)</li></ol><p>From <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj, “united; unified; one; single”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥдинъ</i> (jedinŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) monotheism</li></ol><p>From <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(single) combat, duel</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) co-religionist</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) representative of the edinoverie</li></ol><p><i>единове́рие</i> (jedinovérije, “common belief”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-time</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) simultaneous</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unanimously</li></ol><p><i>единогла́сный</i> (jedinoglásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unanimous</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) autocracy</li></ol><p><i>единодержа́в(ие)</i> (jedinoderžáv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unanimity</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unanimously</li></ol><p><i>единоду́шный</i> (jedinodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unanimous</li></ol><p><i>единоду́шие</i> (jedinodúšije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once, on one occasion (<i>only</i>)</li><li>1×, one time as much</li></ol><p><i>еди́но-</i> (jedíno-) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tied by blood kinship, of the same tribe or clan</li><li>born of the same father but different mother, half- (<i>of brothers and sisters</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) connected by common origin (<i>of nations or states</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Literally, “of one blood”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half brother <i>(paternal)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alone, single-handedly</li></ol><p><i>единоли́чный</i> (jedinolíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sole, independent</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unanimity</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>like-minded person; associate; accomplice, adherent</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>мы́шлен(ный)</i> (mýšlen(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniformity</li></ol><p>From <i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uniform (consistent)</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) unicorn</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sea unicorn, narwhal</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) licorne (a type of 18th and 19th century muzzle-loading gun-howitzer)</li></ol><p>From <i>едино-</i> (jedino-, “uni-”) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog, “horn”).</p><p>The artillery sense stems from the unicorn on the coat of arms of count P. I. Shuvalov, who introduced this type of artillery piece in the Russian army.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) the only (begotten) (of the only child in a family)</li></ol><p><i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang) member of the United Russia party</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>Еди́ная Росси́я</i> (Jedínaja Rossíja, “United Russia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uterine sister, maternal half sister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the same mother but a different father, half- (<i>of brothers and sisters</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>едино-</i> (jedino-) +&lrm; <i>утро́ба</i> (utróba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Literally, “of one womb”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half brother <i>(maternal)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>solely, only, just</li></ol><p><i>еди́нственный</i> (jedínstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) singular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniqueness, singularity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) uniqueness, exceptionality, incomparability, peerlessness</li></ol><p><i>еди́нственный</i> (jedínstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sole, only, unique</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unique, exceptional, unparalleled</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) singular</li></ol><p><i>еди́нство</i> (jedínstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of a kind (unique specimen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unity, unanimity, oneness</li></ol><p>From <i>един-</i> (jedin-) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united, unified, common, indivisible</li><li>one, single, sole</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥдинъ</i> (jedinŭ) + <i>-ый</i> (-yj). By surface analysis, <i>един-</i> (jedin-) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj). Related to Russian <i>оди́н</i> (odín, “one”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caustic</li><li>sarcastic, biting (<i>of words</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ědъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caustic potash, potassium hydroxide, potassa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eater (person who eats, usually of a specified type, cf. "fast eater", "picky eater", etc.)</li><li>(<i>officialese</i>) person to feed, mouth to feed</li></ol><p><i>ед-</i> (jed-) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) hillock, hummock, ridge, high riverbank</li><li>(Northern Russia,&#32;collective) forest wilderness</li><li>(Siberia,&#32;collective) yedoma (a type of permafrost in the Pleistocene-age with an ice content of 50–90&#37; by volume)</li><li>(<i>Siberia</i>) a yedoma terrace or hillock</li></ol><p>Derived from Finnic, perhaps with an original meaning of “distant or high land”; compare to Karelian <i>edäh</i> (“far away”) + Karelian <i>mua</i> (“land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>once a... (occurring once in a given time period)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>иже</i> (iže) (whence Russian <i>и́же</i> (íže)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jь že</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually singular</i>) bramble, blackberry bush</li><li>clipping of <i>ежеви́ка си́зая</i> (ježevíka sízaja, “dewberry”)</li><li>(<i>usually singular</i>) berries of blackberry or dewberry shrub</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;eževika</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ёж</i> (jož, “hedgehog”) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka). See another variant Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ežina</i>, whence <i>ожи́на</i> (ožína), Czech <i>ježiník</i>, Polish <i>jeżyna</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>јежиница</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket of blackberry</li></ol><p><i>ежеви́ка</i> (ježevíka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blackberry</li></ol><p><i>ежеви́ка</i> (ježevíka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annual, yearbook (annual publication)</li></ol><p><i>ежегодный</i> (ježegodnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>annually, yearly (once every year)</li><li>per annum, p.a.</li></ol><p><i>ежего́дный</i> (ježegódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annual (happening once a year)</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diary</li><li>calendar, organizer, agenda</li><li>daily (newspaper)</li></ol><p><i>ежедне́вный</i> (ježednévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>дневный</i> (dnevnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily (every day)</li></ol><p><i>ежедне́вный</i> (ježednévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily (occurring every day)</li><li>everyday (appropriate for ordinary use, rather than for special occasions)</li></ol><p>From <i>еже</i> (ježe) +&lrm; <i>дневный</i> (dnevnyj), second element from <i>днев-</i> (dnev-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quarterly (happening every quarter year)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) if, in case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>jeżeli</i>, probably via Old Ruthenian <i>ежели</i> (eželi),</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>monthly</li></ol><p><i>ежеме́сячный</i> (ježemésjačnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monthly (occurring every month)</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(happening) every minute (at intervals of a minute)</li></ol><p><i>ежемину́тный</i> (ježeminútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occurring once a minute</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) incessant, constant</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>мину́та</i> (minúta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weekly (magazine, journal)</li></ol><p><i>еженеде́льный</i> (ježenedélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>неде́льный</i> (nedélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>weekly (every week)</li></ol><p><i>еженеде́льный</i> (ježenedélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weekly (happening once a week or every week)</li></ol><p>From <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>неде́льный</i> (nedélʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nightly (happening once a night)</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily, everyday</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>су́тки</i> (sútki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) <i>or</i> <i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>су́точный</i> (sútočnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hourly (at intervals of an hour)</li></ol><p><i>ежеча́сный</i> (ježečásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hourly (happening once an hour)</li></ol><p><i>еже-</i> (ježe-) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Yezhovshchina, or Yezhov Era; Great Purge</li></ol><p><i>Ежо́в</i> (Ježóv) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina). The term came from the surname​ of the People's Commissar for Internal Affairs of the USSR Nikolay Yezhov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) iron fist, mailed fist, iron hand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hedgehog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;eževъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ёж</i> (jož) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedgehog pup; hoglet</li></ol><p><i>ёж</i> (jož) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ride, riding</li><li>drive, driving</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ězda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonstandard form of <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ězditi</i> with change of suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (by horse or vehicle), to ride, to drive</li><li>to come, to visit</li><li>to travel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ězditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do something that is clearly not needed</li></ol><p>Literally, “to go to Tula with your own samovar”; compare bring owls to Athens and carry coals to Newcastle. The phrase derives from the fact that Tula is well known for its samovars. The phrase was coined by Chekhov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rider (someone who can ride or is riding a horse or other animal)</li><li>driver (someone who can drive or is driving a vehicle)</li></ol><p>From <i>езда́</i> (jezdá) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go by vehicle, to travel (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yazidi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yazidi, relating to Yazidism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) language (especially an online nonstandard language, in particular Padonkaffsky jargon)</li></ol><p>Deliberate misspelling of <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>really!; really and truly!; honest to God!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yekaterinburg (<i>large city in Russia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Екатеринбу́рг</i> (Jekaterinbúrg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Catherine; Catherine's</li></ol><p><i>Екатери́на</i> (Jekaterína) +&lrm; <i>-инский</i> (-inskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce unstressed <i>e</i> as such rather than as <i>i</i> (as characteristic of some Central and Northern Russian dialects)</li></ol><p><i>е</i> (je) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) litany, suffrage (one of a number of prayer petitions that make up most of the worship in the Orthodox Church)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>ектенїѧ</i> (ekteniję), from Ancient Greek <i>ἐκτένεια</i> (ekténeia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Kazakhstan</i>) title of a head of state in Kazakhstan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>елбасы</i> (elbasy).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Елбасы)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Nursultan Nazarbayev, former president of Kazakhstan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>Елбасы</i> (Elbasy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a penis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian یلدا (yaldâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, scarcely, barely</li><li>slightly</li><li>with (great) difficulty</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ѥлѣ</i> (jelě), ѥль (jelĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;le</i>, <i>&#42;lě</i> <i>(see *ale)</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>єле</i> (jele), dialectal <i>ле</i> (le), Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥлѣ живъ</i> (jelě živŭ) (translates Ancient Greek ἡμιθανής (hēmithanḗs)), Old Church Slavonic <i>лѣ</i> (lě), Serbo-Croatian <i>ле</i>, <i>ље̏</i> (“negative intensifier”), Bulgarian <i>еле</i> (ele, “finally”), dated Slovene <i>lė́</i> (“only, just”), Czech <i>le</i> (“and, but, however”), Old Polish <i>le</i> (“only”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, scarcely, barely</li><li>slightly</li><li>with (great) difficulty</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>е́ле</i> (jéle).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spruce, fir</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edlovъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ель</i> (jelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy oil, chrism</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) balm, unction</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥлѣи</i> (jelěi), from Byzantine Greek <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Elysian, paradisiacal</li></ol><p>From <i>Эли́сий</i> (Elísij, “Elysium”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). <i>Эли́сий</i> (Elísij) is a less common form of <i>Эли́зий</i> (Elízij), also <i>Эли́зиум</i> (Elízium). The former term was borrowed directly from Ancient Greek <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion, “Elysian”), while the latter two terms came via Latin, in turn from the same Greek word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spruce, fir</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edlovъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ель</i> (jelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fidget</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move randomly and constantly (<i>of one's fingers, elbows, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move around in different directions [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, e.g. a toothbrush in one's mouth, a bow on violin's strings, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>елоза́</i> (jelozá, “fidgety person”) + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), of unclear further provenance.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spruce</li><li>fir-tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edlь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Schrenk's spruce (<i>Picea schrenkiana</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ель</i>&nbsp;f (jelʹ, “spruce; fir”) +&lrm; <i>Шре́нка</i> (Šrénka, “of Schrenk”, genitive proper noun), a calque of translingual <i>Picea schrenkiana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fir-grove, fir-plantation, fir / spruce forest</li></ol><p><i>ель</i> (jelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yeltsin (former leader of Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Е́льцин</i> (Jélʹcin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Yelets</li></ol><p><i>Еле́ц</i> (Jeléc) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>име́йл</i> (imɛ́jl)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>email</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raccoon (mammal)</li><li>raccoon fur, coonskin</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) genet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Genette</i>, from French <i>genette</i>, with unclear -<i>о</i>-. The original meaning was “genet”. Doublet of генета.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) raccoon</li></ol><p><i>ено́т</i> (jenót) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eparch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) diocese; diocesal, eparchial</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>епа́рхия</i> (jepárxija) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diocese, bishopric</li><li>domain, sphere of competence, area of responsibility</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>єпархиꙗ</i> (eparxija), borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπαρχία</i> (eparkhía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishop</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἐπίσκοπος</i> (epískopos, “overseer, supervisor”). Doublet of <i>би́скуп</i> (bískup) and <i>эписко́п</i> (episkóp).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) penance, punishment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) voluntary renunciation (of something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιτιμία</i> (epitimía, “punishment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) epitrachelion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>ἐπιτραχήλιον</i> (epitrakhḗlion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) letter "ъ", now called твёрдый знак (tvjórdyj znak)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥръ</i> (jerŭ).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ЕР)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) initialism of <i>Еди́ная Росси́я</i> (Jedínaja Rossíja, “United Russia”), a Russian political party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jumble</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) eralash</li><li>Yeralash (Soviet and Russian children's comedy TV show)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic. Compare Tatar <i>аралаш</i> (aralaş), Kazakh <i>аралас</i> (aralas). Ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;āra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yerevan</li></ol><p><i>Ерева́н</i> (Jereván) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obstinate and angry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) heresy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, rot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>αἵρεσις</i> (haíresis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heretic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>еретикъ</i> (eretikŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>αἱρετικός</i> (hairetikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creek, brook, a secondary or intermittent river-channel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic. Per Vasmer, from <i>&#42;yarïk</i> (“crack, fracture”), compare Turkish <i>yarık</i>, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yār-</i> (“to split”).</p><p>However, more likely from <i>&#42;ayïrïk</i> (“fork, crossroad, creek, brook”), particularly compare ablauted Tatar <i>әйерек</i> (äyerek), ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;adïr-</i> (“to tear apart”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yarmulke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>יאַרמלקע</i> (yarmlke), from Polish <i>jarmułka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to tousle, to dishevel, to ruffle</li></ol><p>From <i>еро́ха</i> (jeróxa) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), from <i>ёрш</i> (jorš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) a light cavalry unit in the Russian army of the 16th and 17th century, used as a vanguard and mobile picket force</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chagatai (jortağul, “mounted raiding party”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) nonsense, rubbish</li><li>trifle</li></ol><p>Possibly from Latin <i>gerundium</i> (whence also <i>геру́ндий</i> (gerúndij)) (rejected by Chernykh). German <i>hier und da</i> proposed as an etymon in Leskov's story is dismissed by Anikin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act foolishly or to talk nonsense</li></ol><p><i>ерунда́</i> (jerundá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piddly, trifling</li></ol><p><i>ерунда́</i> (jerundá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Name of the letter Ы (Y), ы (y), normally called ы (y).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥрꙑ</i> (jery).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) letter "ь", now called мя́гкий знак (mjáxkij znak)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥрь</i> (jerĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) Cossack captain, esaul</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic language, ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>札撒兀勒</i> (zhásāwùlè /⁠⁠ǰasa'ul⁠/). Compare Ukrainian <i>осавул</i> (osavul, “Cossack captain”). Distantly related to <i>яса́к</i> (jasák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if, in case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>єсли</i> (jesli) around XVI, from Old Polish <i>jestli, jesli</i>. By surface analysis, <i>есть</i> (jestʹ) +&lrm; <i>ли</i> (li).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if (only)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;joking,&#32;ironic) clipping of <i>е́сли бы, да кабы́, да во рту́ росли́ грибы́, тогда́ бы был не ро́т, а це́лый огоро́д</i> (jésli by, da kabý, da vo rtú roslí gribý, togdá by byl ne rót, a célyj ogoród)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>provided, provided that, providing, as long as (if, assuming)</li><li>sobeit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard) a nonstandard or dated spelling of е́сли бы (jésli by)</li></ol></html>:/jɪsˈtʲesːnə/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>naturally</li></ol><p><i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) natural science; natural scientific</li></ol><p><i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нау́чный</i> (naúčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naturalness</li></ol><p><i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural (relating to nature)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>естьствьнъ</i> (estĭstvĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>естество́</i> (jestestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>evolutionary theory</i>) natural selection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature, essence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>естьство</i> (estĭstvo), which is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>οὐσία</i> (ousía). By surface analysis, <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “there is”) +&lrm; <i>-ество́</i> (-estvó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural science</li><li>natural history</li></ol><p><i>естество́</i> (jestestvó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зна́ние</i> (znánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural scientist</li></ol><p><i>естество́</i> (jestestvó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>испыта́ть</i> (ispytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ебсти́сь</i> (jebstísʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li><li>there is, there are, exists/exist (expresses availability, existence or presence)</li><li>am/is/are located (at) (usually omitted)</li><li>used to express possession</li><li>am, is, are (used with any person or thing in any number, usually omitted but used for emphasis)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yes!, hooray!</li><li>okay, right, roger</li><li>(<i>military</i>) yessir!</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) aye-aye!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěsti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ēˀstei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁édti</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>есть</i> (jestĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;estь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;esti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i>, verb form of <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>yes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>ебсти́сь</i> (jebstísʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) thaler, Joachimsthaler (former Russian name for the Bohemian silver coin)</li></ol><p><i>Ефи́м</i> (Jefím) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), from a colloquial confusion of the names <i>Ефи́м</i> (Jefím) and <i>Иоаки́м</i> (Ioakím, “Joachim”), referring to German <i>Joachimsthaler</i> and to the image of Saint Joachim on the obverse of the coin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Yefinhar (now Pliushchivka)</li></ol><p><i>Ефинга́р</i> (Jefingár) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Yefinhar (now Pliushchivka)</li></ol><p><i>Ефинга́р</i> (Jefingár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ефре́йтор</i> (jefréjtor, “(military rank) lance corporal”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) lance corporal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Gefreiter</i> with the dialectal realization /j/ for word-initial ‹g› found in many Central German dialects including large parts of historical Prussia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (by horse or vehicle), to ride, to drive</li><li>to travel</li><li>to move from a place, to slide off</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echidna (any of the four species of small spined monotremes)</li><li>red-bellied black snake</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) viper, snake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ехидьна</i> (jexidĭna, “venomous snake”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥхидьна</i> (jexidĭna), from Ancient Greek <i>ἔχιδνα</i> (ékhidna, “viper, snake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>maliciously</li><li>mockingly, sarcastically</li></ol><p><i>ехи́дный</i> (jexídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malicious, spiteful, insidious, venomous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ехи́д(на)</i> (jexíd(na)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malice, spite</li></ol><p><i>ехи́д(ный)</i> (jexíd(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet, more</li><li>else</li><li>still</li><li>even (used with comparatives or to express frustration)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ešče</i> (“still, yet”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁esk(ʷ)e(h₁)</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>яшчэ</i> (jašče), Ukrainian <i>ще</i> (šče), Polish <i>jeszcze</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ещё (ješčó),&lrm; бы (by).</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) all that is left is... (<i>indicating that it would be worse if...</i>)</li><li>if only (<i>indicating impossibility and possibly a wish or irony</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>of course, sure, to be sure</li><li>(<i>sarcastic</i>) I'll say, absolutely not</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not yet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not yet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>nonsense, fat chance (strong statement of disagreement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;highly&#32;formal</i>) alternative form of <i>ея̈</i> (jejǫ́)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>еѣ</i> (jejě), <i>ѥѣ</i> (jejě), further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jeję̇</i>. Spelling comes from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥѩ</i> (jeję).</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥѩ</i> (jeję), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jeję̇</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>её</i> (jejó):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>possessive</i>) her, its</li><li>genitive of <i>она́</i> (oná)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) her, its</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>еѣ</i> (jejě), <i>ѥѣ</i> (jejě), further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jeję̇</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>же</i> (že)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>же́нский род</i> (žénskij rod, “feminine gender”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>же́нская убо́рная</i> (žénskaja ubórnaja, “ladies' room, women's toilet”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ж)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>же́нская убо́рная</i> (žénskaja ubórnaja, “women's restroom”): ladies' room, women's toilet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(onomatopoeia,&#32;imitative) buzz, bzz (sound a bee or fly makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>же́нский род</i> (žénskij rod, “feminine gender”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>желе́зная доро́га</i> (želéznaja doróga, “RR, railway, railroad”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>железнодоро́жный</i> (železnodoróžnyj, “RR, railway, railroad (<i>relational</i>)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad</li><li>(<i>regional&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) frog</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tonsillitis, quinsy</li><li>(slang,&#32;computing) Java (<i>programming language</i>)</li><li>(slang,&#32;computing) Adobe Photoshop</li><li>(slang,&#32;computing) a photoshopped image, a shoop (see also фотожаба)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žaba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) toad</li></ol><p><i>жа́ба</i> (žába) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jabot, ruff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jabot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sensu stricto</i>) Any snake of the genus <i>Xenodon</i>.</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;rare) A French person.</li></ol><p><i>жа́ба</i> (žába, “frog”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”).</p><p>Sense 2 refers to a stereotype depicting French people eating frogs. Compare with English <i>frogeater</i> and Polish <i>żabojad</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gill</li></ol><p>Unknown. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>жа́бри</i> (žábry), Belarusian <i>же́бры</i> (žjébry), Bulgarian <i>жа́бри</i> (žábri), Czech <i>žábra</i>, Slovak <i>žiabra</i>, but a Proto-Slavic form hasn't been reconstructed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gill, gills</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bleach</li></ol><p><i>жаве́ль</i> (žavélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleach</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>javel</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Жавель)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Javel (a neighbourhood of Paris, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Javel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lark, skylark</li></ol><p><i>жа́воронок</i> (žávoronok) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lark, skylark</li><li>early bird, morning person, someone who is always up with the lark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to thirst (for), to crave (for)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жѧдати</i> (žędati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žę̄dàti</i> (“to wish, desire”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gend-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰedʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheapskate, greedy guts, hog (someone who doesn't give freely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be greedy</li></ol><p><i>жа́д(ный)</i> (žád(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greedily, avidly</li></ol><p><i>жа́дный</i> (žádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed, avarice</li></ol><p><i>жа́дный</i> (žádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greedy</li><li>avaricious, covetous [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘of/for something’</small>]</li><li>selfish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жадьнъ</i> (žadĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žędьnъ</i> (“avid, greedy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li><li>craving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жѩжда</i> (žjęžda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žęďa</i>, from <i>&#42;žędati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ja</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>жажа</i> (žaža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to thirst (for), to crave (for), to hunger (for)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жѧждати</i> (žęždati). By surface analysis, <i>жажда</i> (žažda) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>жада́ти</i> (žadáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirsty, avid, desirous, ambitious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jacket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jaquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zhaleika (Russian wind instrument)</li></ol><p>From <i>жалея́</i> (žalejá, “zhaleika; pipe”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix), from <i>жале́ть</i> (žalétʹ, “to pity, feel sorry”) + <i>-ея</i> (-eja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel sorry, to be sorry, to be sad (about something), to regret</li><li>to pity, to commiserate</li><li>to save, to spare (e.g. time, energy)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) big zhaleika</li></ol><p>From <i>жале́ть</i> (žalétʹ, “to pity, feel sorry”) + <i>-ея</i> (-eja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of insects</i>) to sting</li><li>(<i>of snakes</i>) to bite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žędliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitiable</li><li>miserable, pitiful, wretched</li><li>pathetic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žalъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;žalь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pitiably, miserably, wretchedly</li><li>negligibly</li><li>plaintively</li><li>to feel sorry for</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>sorry, it is a pity</li><li>unfortunately</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little stinger, sting</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>жа́ло</i> (žálo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinger</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) forked tongue <i>(of a venomous snake)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žędlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complaint</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жалоба</i> (žaloba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žaloba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>plaintively, dolefully, sorrowfully</li></ol><p><i>жа́лобный</i> (žálobnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plaintive, doleful, sorrowful</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>жа́лоба</i> (žáloba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remuneration, salary</li></ol><p><i>жа́ловать</i> (žálovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) granted; appointed (<i>of a title, estate, position, etc.; not hereditary</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) bestowed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) pay, salary; wages; stipend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grant, to bestow, to confer, to reward, to award (<i>stilted style</i>)</li><li>to favour/favor, to regard with favour/favor; to like</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to visit, to come to see (<i>stilted style or jocular</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>жалъ</i> (žal) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain, to lament [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘(about) something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compassionate</li><li>sad, touching, pitiful</li></ol><p><i>жа́лость</i> (žálostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitiful</li><li>pathetic</li></ol><p>From <i>жа́лость</i> (žálostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Cognate with Belarusian <i>жа́ласны</i> (žálasny), Ukrainian <i>жа́лісний</i> (žálisnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compassion</li><li>pity</li></ol><p><i>жа́л(кий)</i> (žál(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blind, jalousie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jalousie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gendarme</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gendarme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gendarmerie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gendarmerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gendarme</li></ol><p><i>жанда́рм</i> (žandárm) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>genre</i>. Doublet of <i>ге́ндер</i> (génder).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) genre</li></ol><p><i>жанр</i> (žanr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li><li>ardour</li><li>fever, high temperature</li><li>embers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žārъ</i>, perhaps together with <i>жара́</i> (žará) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰēr-</i> (“heat”, root noun), from <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i> (“heat, hot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;mythology) firebird, phoenix (<i>in Slavic mythology</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tempting, hard-to-achieve desire</li></ol><p><i>жар</i> (žar) +&lrm; <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat, hot weather, hot spell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žārā̀</i>, perhaps together with <i>жар</i> (žar) for Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰēr-</i> (“heat”, root noun), from <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i> (“heat, hot”). Cognate with Hindi <i>गर्म</i> (garm) and Hindi <i>गर्मी</i> (garmī), Albanian <i>zjarr</i>, Old Armenian <i>ջեր</i> (ǰer), Latin <i>fornāx</i>, Old Norse <i>varmr</i>, Old Prussian <i>goro</i> (“hearth; stove”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jargon</li><li>slang, cant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jargon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying</li><li>roasting</li></ol><p><i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fried, roasted (cooked by frying or roasting)</li></ol><p><i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying</li><li>roasting</li></ol><p><i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енье</i> (-enʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roast, to fry, to broil, to grill</li><li>to burn, to scorch (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>to do something intensively</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>жа́рити</i> (žáriti), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žàriti</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>жа́рити</i> (žáryty), Belarusian <i>жа́рыць</i> (žárycʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fry, to roast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sunbathe, to bask in the sun</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swelter, to be in a hot place</li></ol><p><i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot, make one feel hot (such as of the surrounding air or the sun)</li><li>having a hot climate, southern, tropical</li><li>fervent, warm, passionate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žarъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hotly</li></ol><p><i>жа́ркий</i> (žárkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roast (meat), bubble and squeak; stew; stir-fry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brazier (a portable heater)</li></ol><p><i>жар</i> (žar) +&lrm; <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>жар</i> (žar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>жа́ркий</i> (žárkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>жа́рко</i> (žárko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) jasmine (genus <i>Jasminum</i>)</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) jasmine flowers (esp. used for tea, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jasmine</li></ol><p><i>жасми́н</i> (žasmín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jasmine</li></ol><p><i>жасми́н</i> (žasmín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest (process of harvesting, gathering the ripened crop, harvest yield)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жѧтва</i> (žętva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žętva</i>, from <i>&#42;žęti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tva</i>. By surface analysis, <i>жать</i> (žatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to squeeze</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to benchpress, to lift</li><li>to pinch, to hurt, to be tight</li><li>to press out, to squeeze out</li><li>to oppress, to draw near</li><li>to reap, to crop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žęti</i>; cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жѧти</i> (žęti), 1sg. <i>жьмѫ</i> (žĭmǫ).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žęti</i>; cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жѧти</i> (žęti), 1sg. <i>жьнѭ</i> (žĭnjǫ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to huddle up</li><li>to draw close <i>(to)</i>, to huddle up <i>(to)</i>, to cuddle up <i>(to)</i>, to nestle up <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crowd together</li><li>to shrink <i>(up)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be stingy, to stint, to pinch and save</li></ol><p><i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a large) can; pitcher (<i>especially for kvass, wine, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьbanъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Czech <i>džbán</i>, Polish <i>dzban</i> and Slovak <i>džbán</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Mandibulata.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Mandibulata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rumination, chewing</li><li>cud</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chewing-gum</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>жва́ка</i> (žváka, “chewing”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), itself from <i>жева́ть</i> (ževátʹ, “to chew”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plait, braid, wisp</li><li>tourniquet (a tightly compressed bandage used to stop bleeding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burning, smarting</li></ol><p><i>жг-</i> (žg-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wait for (to stay where one is or delay action) [<i>with</i> genitive; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wait (to stay where one is or delay action)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wait for (to eagerly expect something) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to expect, to wait (to believe that something will happen or count on something happening) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;transitive,&#32;in third person only) to await (to be in store for, to be ready for) [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьdati</i> (“to wait, to await”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;g(e)id-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰeydʰ-</i>. Compare Old High German <i>gīt</i> (“avarice”) and Old Prussian <i>gēide</i> (“they are waiting”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awaiter, someone who waits</li><li>(neologism,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) an enemy sympathizer; a secretly disloyal civilian waiting to be liberated by the other side's armed forces (<i>used in both Russia and Ukraine, and applied to civilians on either side of the Russian-Ukrainian war</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>contrasting</i>) and, but</li><li>on the other hand, whereas, as for, as to</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Emphasises identity: very, same</li><li>With expressions of time and order&#58; emphasises promptitude: very, right, immediately, without delay</li><li>Marks an objection by pointing to the rationale behind said objection: after all, but</li><li>Especially in questions&#58; expresses consequence of or reaction to what was said before: then, so</li><li>In questions and imperatives&#58; imparts a note of insistence and urgency: ever, on earth, for goodness' sake</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zhe: The name of the Cyrillic-script letter <b>Ж</b>.</li><li>gee: The name of the Latin-script letter <b>G</b>.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>же</i> (že), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;že</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ge</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰi</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>že</i>, Ancient Greek <i>γε</i> (ge), Sanskrit <i>हि</i> (hi), Ukrainian <i>ж</i> (ž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chewing gum (flavoured&#47;flavored preparation for chewing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chewing; masticatory, manducatory</li></ol><p><i>жева́ть</i> (ževátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew, to munch</li><li>to ruminate, to masticate</li><li>to repeat with tiresome monotony, to repeat something monotonously</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьvati</i> (“to chew”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵyewh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;proscribed) alternative spelling of <i>жва́чка</i> (žváčka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a device for the ignition of gunpowder in hunting cartridges</li></ol><p>From Joseph-Marin Gévelot (1786-1843) who invented the primer mercury fulminate and organized manufacturing of shotshell primers.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, rod, wand</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жьзлъ</i> (žĭzlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žezlъ</i>. Doublet of native dialectal <i>жезе́л</i> (žezél).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something desirable, something one would like to have</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desired, wished for</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, desire</li><li>wanting, volition, tending</li></ol><p><i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desired, desirable, welcome, sought-after</li></ol><p><i>жела́ние</i> (želánije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirable (suitable to be desired)</li></ol><p><i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gelatine (edible jelly)</li></ol><p>From French <i>gélatine</i>, itself from Italian <i>gelatina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish, to desire</li><li>to covet</li><li>to wish, to bear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘what’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who wishes, one who desires</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaw muscle</li><li>tumour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tortoise; turtle</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a kind of earth tortoise with a thick shell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьlvъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jelly</li><li>stock or fish broth brought to a gelatinous condition, aspic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gelée</i>. Doublet of <i>гель</i> (gelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želza</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰelǵʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>գեղձ-ք</i> (gełj-kʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ferrous (containing iron)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gland; glandular</li></ol><p><i>желе́зо</i> (želézo) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p><p><i>железа́</i> (železá) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a piece of iron or unidentified metal</li><li>glandule, glandula</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) railway</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) chemin de fer</li></ol><p><i>желе́зо</i> (želézo) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) railway, railroad <i>(track or system)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironwort (<i>Sideritis</i> gen. et spp.)</li><li>face mite (<i>Demodex</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p><i>желе́зный</i> (želéznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>railway station (place where trains stop)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>railwayman; railroadman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) railroad, railway, rail</li></ol><p><i>желе́зный</i> (želéznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>доро́га</i> (doróga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>train station, railroad station, railway station, depot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literal&#32;and&#32;figurative) iron</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) ferrous</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) ferric</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ironclad (certain, reliable, unbreakable)</li></ol><p><i>желе́зо</i> (želézo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron curtain (impenetrable barrier)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron</li><li>any iron-like metal</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) hardware</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>желѣзо</i> (želězo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želězo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reinforced concrete</li></ol><p>Partial calque of German <i>Eisenbeton</i>, attested since 1899. By surface analysis, <i>желе́зо</i> (želézo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бето́н</i> (betón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reinforced concrete, ferroconcrete</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) iron-hard</li></ol><p><i>железобето́н</i> (železobetón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>желе́зка</i> (želézka), (<i>low colloquial</i>) a piece of iron or another metal</li></ol><p>From <i>желе́зо</i> (želézo, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-я́ка</i> (-jáka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jelly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black woodpecker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьlna</i> (“green woodpecker”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gilˀnāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>жёлоб</i> (žólob); small trench or gutter</li><li>groove</li></ol><p><i>жёлоб</i> (žólob) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become yellow</li><li>to show yellow, to be seen, to be visible</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”), whence English <i>gild</i> and <i>glow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellowness</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Dolichophis caspius</i>, the Caspian whipsnake.</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo, “belly”), literally “yellow-belly”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow-bellied</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>брю́хо</i> (brjúxo) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellowish grey</li></ol><p><i>желтова́т(ый)</i> (želtovát(yj), “yellowish”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “grey”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellowish</li><li>sallow</li><li>lurid</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>харза́</i> (xarzá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow-throated</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yolk, vitellus</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellowmouth</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) yellow-beaked, immature, inexperienced</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaundice</li></ol><p>From <i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”), whence possibly English <i>jaundice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clouded yellow (<i>Colias</i>)</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška, diminutive)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turmeric (herb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) stomach (alimentary organ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želǫdъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gastrointestinal</li></ol><p><i>желу́дочный</i> (želúdočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кише́чный</i> (kišéčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stomach, gastric, stomachic</li></ol><p><i>желу́док</i> (želúdok, “stomach”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gall; bilious</li><li>acrimonious (sharp and harsh)</li></ol><p><i>желчь</i> (želčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bile</li><li>gall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зълчь</i> (zŭlčĭ) (rarer <i>жълчь</i> (žŭlčĭ)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьlčь</i> (“bile”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źulʔ-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰlh₃-</i>. Related to <i>&#42;zelenъ</i> and was under the influence of <i>&#42;žьltъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>желе́зная доро́га</i> (želéznaja doróga).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>желе́зница</i> (želéznica).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>железнодоро́жный</i> (železnodoróžnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>желе́зный</i> (želéznyj).</li></ol><p><i>желѣ́зо</i> (želě́zo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>желе́зо</i> (želézo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Samogitian; relating to the Samogitians</li></ol><p>From <i>жема́йт</i> (žemájt, “Samogitian; inhabitant of Samogitia”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) someone prone to flirtatious antics and mannerisms</li></ol><p><i>-жимать</i> (-žimatʹ, “to squeeze”) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act cutesy or mannered</li></ol><p><i>жема́н</i> (žemán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave unnaturally coy or flirtatious</li></ol><p><i>жема́н</i> (žemán) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in cutesy, mannered, prudish, demure or prissy manner</li></ol><p><i>жема́нный</i> (žemánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cutesy, mannered, prudish, demure, prissy</li></ol><p><i>жема́н</i> (žemán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pearl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жьмьчюгъ</i> (žĭmĭčjugŭ) (birchbark letter No. 809, 12th c.), from more common <i>жьньчюгъ</i> (žĭnĭčjugŭ) (since 1161), from Bulgar <i>&#42;ǯinǯü</i> (whence also Chuvash <i>ӗнчӗ</i> (ĕnč̬ĕ) and Hungarian <i>gyöngy</i>) with auslaut akin to <i>&#42;ǯinǯüɣä</i> (dat. sg.), from Proto-Common Turkic <i>&#42;yinǯü</i> (whence also Old Turkic <i>𐰖𐰅𐰨𐰇</i> (yénčü), Kazakh <i>інжу</i> (ınju), Turkish <i>inci</i> etc.), from Middle Chinese <i>珍珠</i> (trin tsyu), <i>真珠</i> (tsyin tsyu, “true pearls”), whence also Mandarin <i>珍珠</i> (zhēnzhū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pearl (individual)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) gem, pearl</li></ol><p><i>же́мчуг</i> (žémčug) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pearl</li><li>pearly</li></ol><p><i>же́мчуг</i> (žémčug) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;poetic) woman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жена</i> (žena), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žena</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;génāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i>, whence English <i>queen</i> and <i>gyno-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>married, having a wife</li><li>(by extension,&#32;of a couple) married, having a spouse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Geneva; Genevan</li></ol><p><i>Жене́ва</i> (Ženéva) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wife's</li></ol><p><i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry off (to arrange the marriage of someone (typically of a male relative))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage (especially from a man's perspective)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>женитва</i> (ženitva).</p><p>Akin to Czech <i>ženitba</i>, Bulgarian <i>женитба</i> (ženitba), Macedonian <i>женидба</i> (ženidba)</p><p>Serbo-Croatian <i>женидба</i>, Kashubian <i>Żenba</i>, Slovak <i>ženba</i>.</p><p>Probably connected with Sanskrit <i>जनित्व</i> (janitva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get married, to take as a wife [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘(to) a woman’</small>]</li><li>(<i>io</i>) to get married, to marry (<i>of a couple</i>)</li></ol><p><i>жени́ть</i> (ženítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridegroom; fiancé</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ženixъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>жени́х</i> (ženíx).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misogynist (hater of women)</li></ol><p><i>жена́</i> (žená, “woman (archaic)”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ненави́стник</i> (nenavístnik, “hater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misogyny (hatred or contempt for women)</li></ol><p><i>женоненави́стник</i> (ženonenavístnik, “misogynist”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>womanlike, effeminate</li></ol><p><i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>female</li><li>woman's, women's</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) feminine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ženьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) feminine gender</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>femininity</li></ol><p><i>же́нственный</i> (žénstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>womanly, feminine (having the characteristics of a woman)</li></ol><p>From <i>женство</i> (ženstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>жена́</i> (žená, “wife”): wife</li></ol><p><i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) Madam, lady, woman (rather crude but common form of address)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ženьščina</i>, from <i>&#42;ženьskъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ina</i>, from <i>&#42;žena</i> + <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>же́нский</i> (žénskij) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginseng</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese 人參／人参 (<i>rénshēn</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perch; pole; ricker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жьрдь</i> (žĭrdĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrdь</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰerdʰ-</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>żerdź</i>, Slovak <i>žrď</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a piece of metal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žerbьjь</i>. Doublet of <i>жре́бий</i> (žrébij), which was borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stallion, stud horse</li><li>stud (sexually active and attractive man)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žerbьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;žerbę</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷrebʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жрѣбѧ</i> (žrěbę), Bulgarian <i>жребе́ц</i> (žrebéc). Compare Ancient Greek <i>βρέφος</i> (bréphos, “baby, foetus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draw, drawing, sortition (procedure by which the result of a lottery is determined)</li><li>toss-up, toss</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>жре́бий</i> (žrébij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foal, colt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žerbę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>жерло́</i> (žerló)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жерело</i> (žerelo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žerdlo</i> (“mouth, gorge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) asp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) crater, orifice, vent</li><li>narrow and deep aperture</li><li>(<i>military</i>) muzzle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жьрло</i> (žĭrlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrdlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sacrifice</li><li>victim</li><li>casualty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жрьтва</i> (žrĭtva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrtva</i> (“sacrifice”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;girʔ-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷrH-</i>. The inherited form would be <i>&#42;жёртва</i> (&#42;žórtva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar</li><li>credenza, credence table</li><li>prothesis (liturgy)</li></ol><p>From <i>же́ртвен(ный)</i> (žértven(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Жертвенник)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ara (constellation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-sacrifice, sacrifice</li></ol><p><i>же́ртвенный</i> (žértvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacrificial</li></ol><p><i>же́ртва</i> (žértva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to donate</li><li>to sacrifice, to give, to offer [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>же́ртва</i> (žértva) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering, sacrifice, oblation</li></ol><p><i>же́ртва</i> (žértva) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>приноше́ние</i> (prinošénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gesture (communicative motion of the limbs or body)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) gesture (act or a remark that serves as a formality or as a sign of attitude)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>geste</i>, from Latin <i>gestus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gesticulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gesticulation</li></ol><p><i>жестикул(и́ровать)</i> (žestikul(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-я́ция</i> (-jácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cruel</li><li>brutal, savage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жестокъ</i> (žestokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žestokъ</i>. Cognate to <i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hard, harshly, sorely, violently, foully, sore, sharply</li></ol><p><i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brutality</li><li>cruelty</li></ol><p><i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin (plate), sheet metal</li><li>frozen soil</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used in surprise, or denoting something horrific.</li></ol><p>Probably a Turkic or Mongolic borrowing. Compare Kazakh <i>жез</i> (jez, “brass”), Tatar <i>җиз</i> (ciz, “brass”), Mongolian <i>зэс</i> (zes, “copper, brass”). Remodeled under the influence of <i>жесть</i> (žestʹ, “frozen soil”).</p><p>Related to <i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin, can (airtight container)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tin</li></ol><p><i>жесть</i> (žestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>token; coin (e.g. in a casino), chip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jeton</i>. Further from Latin <i>iectō</i> (&lt; Latin <i>iaciō</i>, akin to <i>iaceō</i>), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)yeh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn (to cause something to be consumed by fire&#59; to cause burns or a burning sensation in something)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to torment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жечи</i> (žeči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žeťi</i> (“to burn”) <</p><p><i>&#42;džetti</i> &lt; <i>&#42;džegti</i> &lt; <i>&#42;degti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;degtéi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰégʷʰeti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Rzeszów</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Же́шув</i> (Žéšuv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning</li><li>burning sensation</li></ol><p><i>жг-</i> (žg-) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Roman letter J, j</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;Northeast Caucasus) Used as an intensifier filler phrase&#59; sometimes translated to really, indeed.</li></ol><p>Often thought to come from <i>есть же</i> (jestʹ že).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) manpower, personnel</li></ol><p>Literally, “live force”. Compare Ukrainian <i>жива́ си́ла</i> (žyvá sýla), Belarusian <i>жыва́я сі́ла</i> (žyvája síla), Polish <i>siła żywa</i>, Bulgarian <i>жи́ва си́ла</i> (žíva síla). Compare also French <i>forces vives</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>живо́й</i> (živój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>жи́во</i> (žívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>живе́е</i> (živéje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>live bait <i>(small fish)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invigorating, strengthening life forces</li></ol><p><i>живи́ть</i> (živítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) to invigorate, to enliven, to revitalize</li></ol><p><i>живо́й</i> (živój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sap, galipot</li><li>sapwood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;živica</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷh₃i-u-</i> (“to live”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>կիւ</i> (kiw).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small living creatures collectively</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) poultry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small cattle; sheep and goats</li></ol><p>From <i>живо́й</i> (živój, “living”) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vividly, strikingly, keenly</li><li>with animation</li><li>quickly, promptly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>make haste!, be quick!</li></ol><p><i>живо́й</i> (živój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knackery</li></ol><p><i>живой</i> (živoj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living, live, alive</li><li>lively, vivid, vivacious, animated</li><li>quick, nimble, brisk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;živъ</i>. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>जीव</i> (jīva), English <i>quick</i> and Latin <i>vīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature corner (an indoor area where people can observe and care for small pets and houseplants, e.g. in a corner of a classroom or office)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to picture, to describe vividly, to paint a vivid picture (of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painter</li></ol><p><i>жи́вопись</i> (žívopisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a picturesque manner</li><li>vividly</li></ol><p><i>живопи́сный</i> (živopísnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>picturesque, scenic</li><li>vivid</li></ol><p><i>жи́вопись</i> (žívopisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painting</li><li>pictures, paintings</li></ol><p><i>живо́й</i> (živój, “alive”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пис</i> (-pis) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), from a combining form of <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”); cf. Ancient Greek <i>ζωγραφία</i> (zōgraphía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viviparous (giving birth to live babies (as opposed to by laying eggs))</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>viviparus</i>, from <i>живо́й</i> (živój, “alive”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>родя́щий</i> (rodjáščij), present active imperfective participle of <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “to give birth”). In modern Russian <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) is only used as a perfective verb and doesn't have the present tense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vivipary</li></ol><p><i>живо́й</i> (živój, “alive”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рожде́ние</i> (roždénije, “birth”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) live fish</li></ol><p><i>живо́й</i> (živój, “live”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vivacity, liveliness, animation, vividness, alacrity, sprightliness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;živostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>живо́й</i> (živój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly, stomach</li><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) life</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>животъ</i> (životŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;životъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>живо́т</i> (živót)</li></ol><p><i>живо́т</i> (živót) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) companion animal, pet</li><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) livestock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stockbreeder, livestock breeder</li></ol><p>From <i>живо́тно-</i> (živótno-) + deverbal from <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead, to drive, to breed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattle breeding, animal husbandry, stock-raising</li></ol><p><i>животново́д</i> (životnovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) animal, brute, beast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>животьноѥ, животьно</i> (životĭnoje, životĭno), the nominative neuter singular of Old Church Slavonic <i>животьнъ</i> (životĭnŭ). See <i>живо́тный</i> (živótnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>animal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brutal, bestial</li></ol><p><i>живо́т</i> (živót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fauna, animal world (animals considered as a group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitality, survivability, viability, survival capability</li><li>durability</li></ol><p><i>живу́чий</i> (živúčij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viable, of great vitality, tenacious (<i>in life</i>)</li><li>enduring, lasting</li></ol><p><i>жив-</i> (živ-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lively person</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sperm cell, spermatozoon</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong artery throbbing <i>(usually on the temple)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) live (as it happens)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alive</li></ol><p>Constructed as if the instrumental case of &#42;живьё.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) gigue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lighter (device used for creating fire)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>giga</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>зажига́лка</i> (zažigálka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(criminal slang,&#32;originally&#32;derogatory) thief in law</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) scoundrel; swindler; fraudster; rogue; cheat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gigolo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhiguli-brand car (internal Russian name of the Lada)</li></ol><p>Named after the Zhiguli Mountains, from Proto-Slavic &#42;žigъ (“burn mark, brand”) ie "land cleared by burning". Compare Schwyz and Brentwood.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Жигули)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountains on the right bank of the Volga</li><li>name of various villages in Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zhiguli, Lada (type of car)</li></ol><p><i>жигули́</i> (žigulí, “Zhiguli, Lada”) +&lrm; <i>-ёвский</i> (-jóvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>жигули́</i> (žigulí, “Zhiguli-brand car”)</li></ol><p><i>жигули́</i> (žigulí) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Jew, yid, kike</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) niggard; miser</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жидъ</i> (židŭ, “Jew”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;židъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>жи́дкий</i> (žídkij)</li></ol><p><i>жи́дкий</i> (žídkij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liquid, fluid (existing in the physical state of a liquid)</li><li>watery, weak, thin</li><li>sparse, scanty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;židъkъ</i> (“liquid, watery”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluid</li><li>liquid</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>жи́дкий</i> (žídkij, “liquid”) +&lrm; <i>тело</i> (telo, “body”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liquid crystal</li></ol><p><i>жи́дк(ий)</i> (žídk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кристалли́ческий</i> (kristallíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) liquidness</li><li>liquid, fluid (substance)</li><li>(<i>only in compounds</i>) body fluid, biofluid</li></ol><p>First attested in 16th century. From <i>жи́дкий</i> (žídkij, “liquid, watery, thin”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness, -ity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a Judeo-Banderite: a Ukrainian ultranationalist of Jewish descent.</li></ol><p><i>жид</i> (žid, “a Jewish person, a kike”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>банде́ровец</i> (bandérovec, “a Banderite”). This word was originally coined in 2006 by Ukrainian users, as a mockery of Russian ultranationalist views against their country, along with фофу́дья (fofúdʹja). Later both used unironically by Russian nationalists, and as a self-deprecatory term by some Ukrainians to mock Russian nationalists.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(now&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) Jewry</li></ol><p>From <i>жид</i> (žid) +&lrm; <i>-овщина</i> (-ovščina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) kike, Jew, diminutive of <i>жид</i> (žid)</li><li>(slang,&#32;by extension) niggard, miser; greedy person</li></ol><p>From <i>жид</i> (žid) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;now&#32;often&#32;humorous) a participant of the Judeo-Masonic conspiracy</li></ol><p><i>жид</i> (žid) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>масон</i> (mason)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>offensive</i>) Judeo-Masonic</li></ol><p>From жидомасо́н (židomasón), itself made of <i>жид</i> (žid) +&lrm; <i>масон</i> (mason).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) broth, liquid part of a soup</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) liquid for vapes and electronic cigarettes</li><li>slush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>жи́дкий</i> (žídkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>жи́дко</i> (žídko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;usually as an interjection) a relatable situation (a life situation that the speaker has personally experienced)</li></ol><p>A clipping derived one way or another from <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ, “life”). If directly, then cf. <i>винда́</i> (vindá) &lt; <i>Ви́ндоуз</i> (Víndouz) for the conversion to a class 1 feminine noun. Semantically, however, it corresponds better to <i>жи́зненный</i> (žíznennyj, “relatable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) vital activity</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) life activity</li></ol><p><i>жизнеде́ятельный</i> (žiznedéjatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>active, lively, energetic</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) vital</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>де́ятельный</i> (déjatelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of life</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sensuality, love of carnal pleasures</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>authentically, realistically, truthfully, in a lifelike way</li><li>vitally</li></ol><p><i>жи́зненный</i> (žíznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitality, vital power</li><li>authenticity, credibility, truthfulness</li></ol><p><i>жи́зненный</i> (žíznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vital (relating to or characteristic of life), life</li><li>true to life, lifelike</li><li>credible, authentic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жизньнъ</i> (žiznĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elixir of life</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life support</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>обеспе́че́ние</i> (obespéčénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biography, (description of) life</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>описа́ние</i> (opisánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerful, buoyant</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ра́дость</i> (rádostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viability; vital capacity, vitality; survival</li></ol><p><i>жизнеспосо́бный</i> (žiznesposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viable</li></ol><p><i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li><li>practice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жизнь</i> (žiznĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žiznь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-знь</i> (-znʹ). Compare Old Polish <i>żyźń</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a long hinge mounting used on doors, especially in old houses in Russia and some other countries</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a seal ring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) once upon a time</li></ol><p>A pluperfect construction using жил (žil, “lived”) and был (byl, “had, was”), which is nonstandard in the current language. Compare Ukrainian <i>жили-були</i> (žyly-buly).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sinew, tendon</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vein</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) vein</li><li>strand</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) miser, niggard, skinflint</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žila</i>.</p><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vest, waistcoat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>gilet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vest</li></ol><p><i>жиле́т</i> (žilét) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodger, tenant, roomer; inmate</li></ol><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ле́ц</i> (-léc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinewy, stringy</li><li>sinewy</li></ol><p><i>жи́ла</i> (žíla) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to appropriate, to welch, to snatch (<i>to claim or use something as property especially when it belongs to someone else</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be greedy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female tenant</li></ol><p><i>жили</i> (žili) +&lrm; <i>-ца</i> (-ca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling, lodging, lodgings</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жилище</i> (žilište). By surface analysis, <i>жить</i> (žitʹ, “to live, to reside”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́ще</i> (-líšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>housing and communal</li></ol><p><i>жили́щно-</i> (žilíščno-) +&lrm; <i>коммуна́льный</i> (kommunálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) housing</li></ol><p><i>жили́ще</i> (žilíšče) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(diminutive)</i> veinlet</li><li><i>(leaf, wing, marble & figurative)</i> vein</li></ol><p><i>жи́ла</i> (žíla) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>residential (relating to a place of personal residence)</li><li>habitable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žilъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лой</i> (-loj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor space; dwelling or living space</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) housing</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>жила́я пло́щадь</i> (žilája plóščadʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housing, accommodation, dwelling, domicile</li><li>habitation (an inhabited place)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>жить</i> (žitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;weightlifting) a press, an exercise in which weight is forced away from the body by extension of the arms or legs.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) honeysuckle</li></ol><p><i>жи́молость</i> (žímolostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeysuckle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Lonicera nummulariifolia</i></li></ol><p><i>жи́молость</i> (žímolostʹ, “honeysuckle”) +&lrm; <i>монетоли́стный</i> (monetolístnyj, “coin-leaved”), calque of translingual <i>Lonicera nummulariifolia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat or oil from the body of an animal</li><li>grease</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жиръ</i> (žirŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žirъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giraffe</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Жираф)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Giraffe, Camelopardalis (a northern constellation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) giraffe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) giraffe</li></ol><p><i>жира́ф</i> (žiráf) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) fatso</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fat</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(organic chemistry,&#32;biochemistry) fatty acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) much, a lot of, plenty of</li></ol><p><i>жи́рный</i> (žírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) grease</li><li>greasy</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) fleshy</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rich</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) bold</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lardbucket, fatso</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lipoma</li><li>fat-lamp</li><li>talc</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fat; fatty, adipose</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adipose fin (a soft, fleshy fin found on the back of a fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>жир</i> (žir): small fat deposit in a tissue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reserve, stock, surplus</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Girondist</li></ol><p>French <i>Girondiste</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Girondist, Girondistic</li></ol><p>From <i>жиронди́ст</i> (žirondíst, “Girondist”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southern smooth snake (<i>Coronella girondica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>жиро́ндский</i> (žiróndskij, “of Gironde”) +&lrm; <i>медя́нка</i> (medjánka, “grass snake”), a calque of translingual <i>Coronella girondica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Of Gironde; Girondese.</li></ol><p>From <i>Жиро́нда</i> (Žirónda, “Gironde”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat-soluble</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>раствори́мый</i> (rastvorímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>жиртре́ст</i> (žirtrést)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;derogatory) fat or obese person; fatso</li></ol><p><i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>трест</i> (trest).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>жир</i> (žir).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worldly, of life</li><li>not unusual, everyday, ordinary</li></ol><p>From <i>житьё</i> (žitʹjó, “life”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant, resident, occupant, dweller</li></ol><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ, “to live, reside”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residence</li></ol><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to live, to reside, to dwell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;ecclesiastic,&#32;dated) life, existence</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;ecclesiastic) life, biography</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>житиѥ</i> (žitije, “life”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žitьje</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žiti</i> (“to live”). Doublet of <i>житьё</i> (žitʹjó), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granary</li><li>breadbasket, food bowl</li></ol><p><i>жи́то</i> (žíto, “grain”) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>grānārium</i> (whence English <i>garner</i>, <i>granary</i>) (&lt; Latin <i>grānum</i> (whence English <i>grain</i>, Russian <i>гра́нула</i> (gránula))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain (any unground grain, such as rye, wheat, buckwheat, millet, barley, maize, oats)</li><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;regional,&#32;Belarus&#32;or&#32;Ukraine) rye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žito</i> (“grain, corn”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;géiˀta</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃tom</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li><li>to reside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žiti</i> (compare Belarusian <i>жыць</i> (žycʹ), Ukrainian <i>жи́ти</i> (žýty), Old Church Slavonic <i>жити</i> (žiti)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeih₃w-</i> (“to live”). Cognate to Lithuanian <i>gyventi</i>, Sanskrit <i>जीवति</i> (jīvati, “lives”), Latin <i>vīvō</i>, and English <i>quick</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) (for life) to be like, (for life) to go (used to describe conditions of life or existence)</li></ol><p><i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) life</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) existence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žitьje</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žiti</i> (“to live”). Doublet of <i>житие́</i> (žitijé), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) life</li></ol><p><i>житьё</i> (žitʹjó) +&lrm; <i>бытьё</i> (bytʹjó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) schlub</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) greedy, avaricious person</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) any large, burly man, often a bodybuilder</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a person who does not participate in a criminal enterprise</li></ol><p>From Yiddish <i>זשלאָב</i> (zhlob, “goyish bumpkin, redneck”), derived from Polish <i>żłób</i> (“manger, furrow”), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žȇlbъ</i>. Doublet of <i>жёлоб</i> (žólob, “trench, trough”), an inherited form.</p><p>Originated in klezmer musician cant <i>(Klezmer-loshn)</i>. The nature of the semantic shift from "manger, crib" to "boorish goy" remains unclear. By the year 1869 however, it had become common enough to be included in a Russian-Yiddish dictionary published in Zhytomyr. Jewish immigrants brought the word to the United States, where it is best known under the form <i>schlub</i>. In the Russosphere, the word <i>жлоб</i> spread seemingly through criminal slang, which is considerably influenced by the Odessan dialect.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial,&#32;Ukraine) the palm with the fingers bent so that they could scoop up, grab or hold something</li><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) handful</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>жме́ня</i> (žménja).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>гра́бка</i> (grábka) (usually <i>гра́бки</i>&nbsp;pl (grábki), akin to <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ), <i>грести́</i> (grestí), <i>граба́стать</i> (grabástatʹ), also note <i>гра́бли</i>&nbsp;pl (grábli), <i>загребу́щий</i> (zagrebúščij)),</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheapskate, miser, greedy person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squint, to narrow one's eyes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьžuriti</i> via metathesis. Cognate to Russian <i>мига́ть</i> (migátʹ), Russian <i>миг</i> (mig), Russian <i>мгнове́ние</i> (mgnovénije). Possibly influenced by <i>жму</i> (žmu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw up one's eye(s), to narrow one's eyes, to blink (e.g. under the bright sunlight)</li><li>to shut one's eyes tightly (especially in the perfective form)</li></ol><p><i>жму́рить</i> (žmúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blind man's buff (children's game)</li></ol><p>From <i>жму́рить</i> (žmúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also plural</i>) oil cake</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also plural) pomace (pulpy residue after juice has been squeezed from fruit)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) raw opium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reaper, harvester, harvest-man</li></ol><p><i>жн-</i> (žn-, “to reap”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field of wheat, rye or other grains</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) stubble (short stalks left in a field after a harvest)</li><li>eddish (aftergrowth of grass after mowing)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) harvest, reaping crops</li><li>harvest time</li></ol><p>From <i>жни́во</i> (žnívo, “harvest”) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó), from <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) stubble</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) the act of harvesting, reaping</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) the time of harvest</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) the result of harvest, crop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow and blue (the colors of the Ukrainian flag)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) Ukrainian (relating to Ukrainian authorities, the Ukrainian national team, etc.)</li><li>(politics,&#32;figuratively,&#32;colloquial) Ukrainian nationalist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>жо́вто-блаки́тний</i> (žóvto-blakýtnyj, “yellow-blue”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jockey (one who rides racehorses competitively)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>jockey</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jockey</li></ol><p><i>жоке́й</i> (žokéj, “jockey”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to juggle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juggler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jongleur</i>. Further from Latin <i>ioculor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) ass, arse (the buttocks of a person or animal)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figurative) middle of nowhere, Bumfuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figurative) shit (a difficult situation)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;figurative,&#32;derogatory) annoying person; shithead, asshole</li></ol><p>Uncertain, presumed to derive from a Proto-Slavic form which cannot be easily reconstructed. According to Vasmer, two probable hypotheses are:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) having a big ass</li></ol><p><i>жопа</i> (žopa) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be unwilling to give (someone something)</li></ol><p><i>жо́п(а)</i> (žóp(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ass (the buttocks of a person)</li></ol><p><i>жо́п(а)</i> (žóp(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) asslicker</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) sycophant</li></ol><p><i>жо́п(а)</i> (žóp(a)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лиз(а́ть)</i> (liz(átʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mobile telephony,&#32;slang,&#32;humorous) GPRS</li></ol><p>From <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “an ass”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-рез</i> (-rez), becoming something like "ass-cutter". Phono-semantic matching of English <i>GPRS</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) person with a very ugly face, ass face</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) a ZAZ-965/ZAZ-968 vehicle</li></ol><p>From <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “ass”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ро́жа</i> (róža, “face”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p><p>Sense 2 is a play on запоро́жец (zaporóžec), referring to the car's appearance.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) faggot, bitch boy, bottom (a male homosexual who takes a passive role in sex)</li><li>overly greedy person</li><li>(<i>by extension, a general pejorative</i>) asshole, dickhead, shithead</li></ol><p>From <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “ass”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeding period <i>(of fish)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) voracious appetite, munchies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;slang) edgy</li></ol><p>Deliberate misspelling of <i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) food, chow, grub</li></ol><p>From <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to eat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to devour, to gobble (eat greedily)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;of an animal) to gobble</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;low colloquial) to guzzle <i>(alcohol)</i></li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to guzzle <i>(fuel or power)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;of an insect) to sting, to bite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жьрати</i> (žĭrati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrati</i>, related to English <i>devour</i> and <i>voracity</i>.</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>же́рти</i> (žérty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lot</li><li>fate, destiny</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жрѣбии</i> (žrěbii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žerbьjь</i>. Doublet of <i>же́ребей</i> (žérebej), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heathen priest, pagan priest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;žьrti</i> + <i>&#42;-ьcь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;girˀ-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷerH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) prostitute</li></ol><p>Literally, “priestess of love”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) chewing gum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) ground beetle (Carabidae)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жужелица</i> (žuželica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žuželica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hum; buzz; drone</li></ol><p><i>жужжа́ть</i> (žužžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hum, to buzz, to drone, to whirr</li><li>(figurative,&#32;often&#32;derogatory) to say something</li><li>(<i>figurative&#32;derogatory</i>) to fatigue someone by saying too much or by repetition of the same thing (прожужжать все уши, все уши прожужжал)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žužati</i>. Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zhuz, horde, a Kazakh clan or tribal division</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>жүз</i> (jüz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beetle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rogue, crook</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žukъ</i>. Per Vasmer (and others) likely related to <i>жужжа́ть</i> (žužžátʹ), <i>жу́желица</i> (žúželica). Per Chernykh, however, likely related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;govędo</i> (denied by Anikin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinoceros beetle (<i>Oryctes nasicornis</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stag beetle, <i>Lucanus cervus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petty thief, pilferer, filcher, swindler</li><li>cheat, cardsharp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swindle, to cheat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindle, fraud, cheating, racket</li></ol><p><i>жу́льнич(ать)</i> (žúlʹnič(atʹ), “to cheat, to swindle”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) swindlers, frauds</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zhupan (a style of jerkin in Poland and Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>giuppone</i>, from Arabic جُبَّة (jubba). Doublet of <i>зипу́н</i> (zipún), <i>шу́ба</i> (šúba), and <i>ю́бка</i> (júbka). Also doublet of <i>дже́мпер</i> (džémper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) brimstone</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) bogeyman, bête noire (something frightening or unpleasant, or something used to frighten)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>жоупелъ</i> (župelŭ) (alternative form: <i>зюпелъ</i> (zjupelŭ)), from Old High German <i>swebal</i> or <i>sweval</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweblaz</i>. An alternative etymology, which explains the presence of <i>оу</i>, derives <i>жоупелъ</i> (župelŭ) from Latin <i>sulpur</i>, possibly via a Rhaeto-Romance or other unknown intermediary.</p><p>The figurative sense derives from a remark by a character in Ostrovsky's <i>Difficult Days</i> (1863).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crane (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p><i>жура́вль</i> (žurávlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) crane (bird)</li><li>(animate,&#32;figuratively) pie in the sky, jam tomorrow</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) sweep, swape, shadoof (a traditional crane-like device with a lever arm used to lift buckets of water from a well)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;Ukraine) a type of folk dance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жеравль</i> (žeravlĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žeravľь</i>. Cognates include Belarusian <i>жураве́ль</i> (žuravjélʹ) and Ukrainian <i>жураве́ль</i> (žuravélʹ).</p><p>The figurative sense is a reference to the proverb <i>не сули́ журавля́ в не́бе, дай сини́цу в ру́ки</i> (ne sulí žuravljá v nébe, daj sinícu v rúki).</p><p>For <i>жура́вина</i> (žurávina), <i>журави́ка</i> (žuravíka, “cranberry”), compare Polish <i>żurawina</i>, Latvian <i>dzērvene</i>, German <i>Kranbeere</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>tranebær</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) pie in the sky, jam tomorrow</li></ol><p>From the proverb <i>не сули́ журавля́ в не́бе, дай сини́цу в ру́ки</i> (ne sulí žuravljá v nébe, daj sinícu v rúki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby crane</li></ol><p><i>жура́вль</i> (žurávlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold slightly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žuriti</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>жури́ти</i> (žurýty, “to sadden”), Belarusian <i>жури́цца</i> (žurícca, “to grieve”), Serbo-Croatian <i>жу́рити</i> (“to hurry (<i>intransitive</i>)”), Slovene <i>žúriti</i> (“to force, to rush, to hurry (<i>transitive</i>)”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>). Per Vasmer, related to Serbo-Croatian <i>гу́рати</i> (“to push”) (1sg. <i>гу̑ра̄м</i>), Slovene <i>gúrati</i> (“to wear out, to torment”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), and distantly to Sanskrit <i>घोर</i> (ghorá, “scary, terrible, rash”), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (gaurs, “indignant, saddened”), <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaurjan, “to sadden”), Old High German <i>gōrag</i> (“miserable”), Old Irish <i>gúre</i> (“diseased state”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magazine, periodical, journal</li><li>diary</li><li>log, logbook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>journal</i> (which also calqued into <i>дневни́к</i> (dnevník)). Further from Latin <i>diurnālis</i>, from <i>diurnus</i>, close akin to <i>diurnum</i> (whence French <i>jour</i>, whence <i>дежу́рный</i> (dežúrnyj)). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (whence <i>день</i> (denʹ), <i>ди́во</i> (dívo)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journalist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>journaliste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>journalistic</li></ol><p><i>журнали́ст</i> (žurnalíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) magazine, journal</li></ol><p><i>журна́л</i> (žurnál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>журна́л</i> (žurnál)</li></ol><p><i>журна́л</i> (žurnál) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murmur, gurgling, babble (of water)</li></ol><p><i>журча́ть</i> (žurčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of water</i>) to babble, to purl, to murmur</li></ol><p>Of imitative origin</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) horror film</li></ol><p><i>жуть</i> (žutʹ, “horror”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horrifying, horrible, inducing a sense of horror</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) horrible, bad, of poor quality</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) extreme, super (<i>as an intensifier</i>)</li></ol><p><i>жуть</i> (žutʹ, “dismay, dread, horror”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frighteningly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) terribly, extremely (<i>as an intensifier</i>)</li></ol><p><i>жу́ткий</i> (žútkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat horrifying, somewhat creepy</li></ol><p><i>жу́ткий</i> (žútkij) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dismay, dread, horror</li></ol><p>Backformation from <i>жуткий</i> (žutkij), from an earlier form *жудъкый, which, in its turn, is related to <i>жуда́</i> (žudá, “need, want; ordeal, trouble; dread”). Further etymology unclear. Either of expressive origin or, if unlikely, a loan from Samogitian <i>žiadùs</i> (“harsh”) &lt; <i>žudyti</i> (“to slay”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰed-</i> (“take, seize, grasp”), the only cognates in either case being Belarusian <i>жуда́</i> (žudá, “dread”) and <i>жу́дасны</i> (žúdasny, “dreadful”) and, in the latter, also Old English <i>ġietan</i>, English <i>get</i>, Ancient Greek <i>χᾰνδᾰ́νω</i> (khăndắnō, “to hold, contain”), Latin <i>hedera</i> (“ivy”). It may also be akin to Serbo-Croatian <i>žud</i> (“desire”), <i>žudjeti</i>, stemming from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьlděti</i>. Similarity to Bulgarian <i>жуда</i> (žuda) incidental.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>жук</i> (žuk, “beetle”)</li></ol><p><i>жук</i> (žuk, “beetle”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>жук</i> (žuk) (small) beetle, bug (insect)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) home-made electrical fuse</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bug, wiretap (an electronic intercept device)</li></ol><p><i>жук</i> (žuk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>же</i> (že, “the Cyrillic letter Ж, ж; the Roman letter G, g, also called гэ”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ЖЭК</i> (ŽEK)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ЖЭК)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housing maintenance office</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>жили́щно-эксплуатацио́нная конто́ра</i> (žilíščno-ekspluataciónnaja kontóra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renminbi, Chinese currency</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 人民幣／人民币 (<i>rénmínbì</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jury (group of judges in a competition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>jury</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trough, trench</li><li>gutter, chute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жолобъ</i> (žolobŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želbъ</i>, , from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelbh-</i>, <i>&#42;gelebh-</i>. Cognate with Church Slavic <i>жлѣбъ</i> (žlěbŭ), Ukrainian <i>жо́лоб</i> (žólob), Belarusian <i>жо́лаб</i> (žólab), Bulgarian <i>жлеб</i> (žleb), Serbo-Croatian <i>жлије̑б</i>, <i>ждлије̑б</i>, <i>жле̑б</i>, Slovene <i>žlȇb</i>, Old Czech <i>žleb</i>, Slovak <i>žľаb</i>, <i>žleb</i>, Polish <i>żłób</i> (genitive <i>żłobu</i>), Upper Sorbian <i>złob</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>złob</i>. Further connections difficult; per Vasmer, most likely is with Old Norse <i>golf</i> (“cavity in a vessel, floor”). Doublet of <i>жлоб</i> (žlob, “schlub”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) yellow fever</li></ol><p>Literally, “yellow fever”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lime, yellow-green</li></ol><p><i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj, “green”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow-red or consisting of yellow and red colours individually</li></ol><p><i>жёлт(ый)</i> (žólt(yj), “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow</li><li>(publishing,&#32;journalism) of doubtful accuracy (<i>for mass media</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) deathly sick (<i>for person</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>жьлтъ</i> (žĭltŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьltъ</i> (“yellow”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gilˀtás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”), whence English <i>yellow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow light (traffic light between the green light and the red light)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acorn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želǫdь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gel-</i>, <i>&#42;gil-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьlčь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wife</li><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus) wife; woman</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>женъка</i> (ženŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ženъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>жена́</i> (žená, “wife”): wife</li></ol><p><i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millstone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьrny</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;gírˀnūˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷr̥h₂-n-uH-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ग्रावन्</i> (grāvan), Old Armenian <i>երկան</i> (erkan) and Old English <i>cweorn</i> (English <i>quern</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, stiff, tough, rigid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>жестъкъ</i> (žestŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žestъkъ</i>. Cognate to Old Church Slavonic <i>жестъ</i> (žestŭ, “hard”), <i>жестѣти</i> (žestěti, “to harden”). Also related to <i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij, “cruel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) hard drive</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>hard disk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stiffly, rigidly</li></ol><p><i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rigidity, inflexibility</li><li>hardness</li><li>inclemency</li><li>acerbity</li><li>harshness, asperity</li></ol><p><i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>жёсткий</i> (žóstkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>жёстко</i> (žóstko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of motion</i>) to</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) during (close to в течение (v tečenije))</li><li>(of time,&#32;with <i>до</i> (do)) time before an event</li><li>for; to; by; after; over</li><li>instead of</li><li>(<i>of voting</i>) in favor of</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) about</li><li>(<i>used in expressions</i>)</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> instrumental]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of location</i>) behind; beyond; on the other side of</li><li>at; while</li><li>owing to</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;za</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abroad, overseas (as a location)</li></ol><p>Literally, “beyond the hill”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) cheers! (toast when drinking)</li></ol><p>Literally, “for your health”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> за (za),&lrm; верста́ (verstá).</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) from/at/to a great distance; (from) miles away</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>less, minus, deducting, with the deduction (of); except for, apart from</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind someone's back</li><li>ample, in plenty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>cheers (toast when drinking, generic but less common)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>with the exception of, except, apart from</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>set phrase</i>) faute de mieux</li></ol><p>A calque of French faute de mieux.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>recently, lately, of late</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind bars; in the slammer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abroad (as a location)</li></ol><p>Literally, “beyond the border”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) cheers! (toast when drinking)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to your health”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(used in folk tales&#32;or&#32;figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) very far away, beyond the ends of the earth</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) in the middle of nowhere, too far away from cities and convenient infrastructure, somewhere hard to get to</li></ol><p>Literally, “thrice nine lands away”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) behind, beyond</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) to begin doing (describing an action of commencing)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) in advance (describing an action of doing something ahead of time)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) perfectivizes verbs</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) to the finish, to the end (describing a completed result)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action of covering something, of being covered, or hiding behind something</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an intensified or extreme action or an action of doing something up to the point that an object becomes junk</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action that implies the existence of barriers, obstacles, or hindrance</li><li>(<i>with a reflexive verb</i>) to do with abandonment</li><li>(<i>used with nouns, in place names</i>) trans-</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;za</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to abuse (physically, psychologically, financially or otherwise)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>абью́зить</i> (abʹjúzitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) to aggro (to cause (a non-player character) to become aggressive towards the player's character)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;by extension) to provoke; to deliberately anger; to pick a fight</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>а́грить</i> (ágritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to get angry</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>а́гриться</i> (ágritʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start clapping/applauding, to burst into applause</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>аплоди́ровать</i> (aplodírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) to archive</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>архиви́ровать</i> (arxivírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fun, amusement, entertainment</li><li>pastime</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to amuse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabaviti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to divert, to entertain (to entertain or occupy in a pleasant manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse oneself <i>(with)</i>, to have fun, to make oneself merry</li></ol><p><i>забавля́ть</i> (zabavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amusingly, in an amusing manner</li></ol><p><i>заба́вный</i> (zabávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amusing, funny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabavьnъ</i>. Compare typologically <i>заня́тный</i> (zanjátnyj), <i>захва́тывающий</i> (zaxvátyvajuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) banned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to ban</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ба́нить</i> (bánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to drum</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бараба́нить</i> (barabánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go on strike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strike, walkout</li></ol><p><i>забастова́ть</i> (zabastovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) strike, walkout</li></ol><p><i>забасто́вка</i> (zabastóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgetting (act of forgetting)</li><li>oblivion, neglect, obscurity, disregard (state of being forgotten)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>забъвениѥ</i> (zabŭvenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) heat</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>забе́гать</i> (zabégatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eatery, eating house, snack bar (usually of low quality)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a place (e.g. a business, shop) with a low reputation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start running</li><li>to become shifty (of a look)</li><li>to get [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>] by running or as if by running</li><li>to run ahead (in anticipation), to run before someone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to visit, to drop in [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>] by running or as if by running</li><li>to run ahead (in anticipation), to run before someone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to visit, to drop in [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to whiten</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>беле́ть</i> (belétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zabelia, <i>Zabelia</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Zabelia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get pregnant</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бере́менеть</i> (berémenetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to worry <i>(about)</i>, to begin to feel anxious/uneasy <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>беспоко́иться</i> (bespokóitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in (nail), to hammer in, to nail up</li><li>to score (a goal)</li><li>to beat up</li><li>to slaughter</li><li>to block up, to stop up, to cram, to obstruct</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outdo, to surpass</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ignore; to give up on some (previous) intention</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to reserve</li></ol><p><i>заби́ть</i> (zabítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;euphemistic) to not care about, to not be bothered about, to leave alone, to ignore</li><li>(youth slang,&#32;euphemistic) to be done/finished with</li></ol><p>Literally, “to hammer/drive/screw a bolt (onto)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get clogged or blocked with</li><li>to hide, to get (into a corner)</li></ol><p><i>забива́ть</i> (zabivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to injure</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>обижа́ть</i> (obižátʹ, “to offend someone”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bandage</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бинтова́ть</i> (bintovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bandage</li></ol><p><i>забинтова́ть</i> (zabintovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ыва</i> (-yva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to seize, to collect; to capture, to take possession</li><li>to collect, to pick up</li><li>to arrest</li><li>to pick up (to collect a passenger)</li><li>to bear, to turn off, to aside</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb (into), to creep (into), to get (into)</li><li>to get/move into a remote place</li></ol><p><i>забира́ть</i> (zabirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>downtrodden, brow-beaten</li><li>plugged, clogged, locked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in (nail), to hammer in, to nail up</li><li>to score (a goal)</li><li>to beat up</li><li>to slaughter</li><li>to block up, to stop up, to cram, to obstruct</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outdo, to surpass</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ignore, to neglect, to blow off something the actor is supposed to do</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to stop doing something especially because of one's couldn't care less attitude</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to give up on one's (previous) intention</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to reserve, to earmark</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;euphemistic) to not care about something, to not be bothered about, to leave alone, to ignore</li><li>(youth slang,&#32;euphemistic) to be done/finished with</li></ol><p>Literally, “to hammer/drive/screw a bolt (onto)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to arrange to meet somewhere, to set a meeting place</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get clogged or blocked with</li><li>to hide, to get (into a corner)</li><li>to begin to beat/throb</li></ol><p><i>заби́ть</i> (zabítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bully</li><li>squabbler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>zabijaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in due time, beforehand, in advance, ahead of time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to find it necessary, to consider it proper [<i>with</i> infinitive], to do something</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>благо́й</i> (blagój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рассуди́ть</i> (rassudítʹ), with unexpected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (seem, deem) necessary or proper [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заблагорассу́дить</i> (zablagorassúditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shine/glitter/glow</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>блесте́ть</i> (blestétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to bleat</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бле́ять</i> (bléjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block</li><li>to blockade</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>блоки́ровать</i> (blokírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose one's way, to go astray, to get lost</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>блуди́ть</i> (bludítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to lose one's way in broad daylight</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get lost in three pines”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) lost, stray</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) misguided, dissolute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic; has the form of a past active participle. Compare <i>заблуди́ться</i> (zabludítʹsja, “to get lost”), <i>заблужда́ться</i> (zabluždátʹsja, “to err, to be mistaken”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to err, to be mistaken</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>блужда́ть</i> (bluždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delusion (state of being deluded or misled); misbelief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter (of cattle)</li><li>(pit-)face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morbidity, incidence (rate); sickness rate</li></ol><p><i>заболева́ть</i> (zabolevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease, sickness, illness</li></ol><p><i>заболева́ть</i> (zabolevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall ill (to become ill)</li><li>to begin to hurt, to become hurt (<i>of a body part</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заболе́ть</i> (zabolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall ill (to become ill)</li><li>to begin to hurt, to become hurt (<i>of a body part</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapwood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) intake</li><li>collection</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>забра́ть</i> (zabrátʹ, “to take, to collect; to fence in”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong, pungent <i>(of liquor, tobacco, etc)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) risqué, racy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start muttering, to start mumbling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бормота́ть</i> (bormotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) visor (part of a knight's helmet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>забороло</i> (zaborolo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabordlo</i>. Doublet of <i>забра́ло</i> (zabrálo), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>забо́р</i> (zabór, “fence”)</li></ol><p><i>забо́р</i> (zabór) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care</li><li>concern, anxiety, worry, trouble</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>зобота</i> (zobota), from Proto-Slavic, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gab-</i> (“to watch, show”). Cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kapēn</i> (“to watch, to look”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kōpijaną</i> (“to look after, care for”) (whence English <i>keep</i>). Also compare Ukrainian <i>забо́та</i> (zabóta), Slovene <i>zóbati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry, to be a cause for concern <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>забо́та</i> (zabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care, to take care, to look after</li><li>to worry, to be anxious, to trouble</li></ol><p><i>забо́тить</i> (zabótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thoughtfully, considerately</li></ol><p><i>забо́тливый</i> (zabótlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtfulness, considerateness</li><li>diligence, carefulness</li></ol><p><i>забо́тливый</i> (zabótlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtful, caring, considerate, solicitous</li></ol><p><i>забо́тить</i> (zabótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to fear, to become afraid [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>боя́ться</i> (bojátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject, to consider defective</li><li>to condemn</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бракова́ть</i> (brakovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visor (part of a knight's helmet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Church Slavonic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabordlo</i>. Doublet of <i>заборо́ло</i> (zaborólo), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to hurl <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>to send</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take <i>(to a place)</i></li><li>to put, to mislay</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to give up, to drop</li><li>to pelt <i>(with)</i></li><li>to shower <i>(with)</i></li><li>to fill <i>(with)</i>, to fill up <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>забро́сить</i> (zabrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>заброса́ть</i> (zabrosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to seize, to collect; to capture, to take possession</li><li>to collect, to pick up</li><li>to arrest</li><li>to pick up (to collect a passenger)</li><li>to bear, to turn off, to turn aside</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>забрати</i> (zabraty), Belarusian <i>забра́ць</i> (zabrácʹ), Polish <i>zabrać</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb (into), to creep (into), to get (into)</li><li>to get/move into a remote place</li></ol><p><i>забра́ть</i> (zabrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stray, to wander</li></ol><p><i>забред-</i> (zabred-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to begin to dawn</li><li>to begin to flicker faintly</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бре́зжить</i> (brézžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stray, to wander</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to book, to reserve</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>брони́ровать</i> (bronírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pelt <i>(with)</i></li><li>to shower <i>(with)</i></li><li>to fill <i>(with)</i>, to fill up <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to hurl <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>to send</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take <i>(to a place)</i></li><li>to put, to mislay</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to give up, to drop</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned (forsaken, deserted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) abandoned building, abandoned structure</li></ol><p><i>забро́шенный</i> (zabróšennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to splatter, to begin to splash</li><li>to sprinkle (someone with liquid)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spray (someone with liquid)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бры́згать</i> (brýzgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle (someone with liquid)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spray (someone with liquid)</li></ol><p><i>забры́згать</i> (zabrýzgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reckless, brave, riotous</li><li>dissolute, debaucherous</li><li>meager, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>бубён</i> (bubjón, “diamonds (<i>genitive plural; playing cards</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Diamonds (<i>бу́бны</i> (búbny)) was the lowest ranking card suit in medieval Russia. It was customary for the prison inmates to have it on the back of their clothes. The term was used as an informal term for <i>prisoner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous&#32;or&#32;derogatory) abroad, overseas, foreign countries</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-, “beyond”) +&lrm; <i>буго́р</i> (bugór, “hill”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje), from the expressions <i>за бугро́м</i> (za bugróm) and <i>за буго́р</i> (za bugór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to start drinking alcohol, to take up booze</li><li>to start thumping, to start banging</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forget</li><li>to leave behind</li></ol><p><i>забы́ть</i> (zabýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doze, to drop off</li><li>to become oblivious, to lose contact with reality</li><li>to forget oneself, to cross the line of what is allowed or to lose control over oneself</li></ol><p><i>забыва́ть</i> (zabyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgetfulness</li></ol><p><i>забы́вчивый</i> (zabývčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgetful, oblivious (liable to forget)</li></ol><p><i>забыва́ть</i> (zabyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgotten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forget</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to leave, to forget to bring, to forget to take</li><li>to let it go, to let it slip</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zabỳti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doze, to drop off</li><li>to become oblivious, to lose contact with reality</li><li>to forget oneself, to cross the line of what is allowed or to lose control over oneself</li></ol><p><i>забы́ть</i> (zabýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unconsciousness, swoon</li><li>semiconsciousness, half-conscious state</li><li>dissociation (a state of detachment from reality)</li></ol><p>From <i>забы́ть</i> (zabýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manager; chief, head; director</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>заве́дующий</i> (zavédujuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>заве́дующий</i> (zavédujuščij, “manager, chief”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>заво́д</i> (zavód, “plant, factory”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>заводско́й</i> (zavodskój, “of plant or factory”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstruction, blockage, abatis</li><li>steep slope</li><li>backlog (an accumulation of unfinished work)</li><li>rubble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heap up, to bury</li><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>завали́ть</i> (zavalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie down</li><li>to tumble down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blow <i>(into)</i>, to blow in, to stroll <i>(into)</i>, to stroll in; to dive <i>(into)</i>, to dive in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to flop</li></ol><p><i>зава́ливать</i> (zaválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen embankment (to protect a house from freezing during winter)</li></ol><p><i>зава́л</i> (zavál) +&lrm; <i>-инка</i> (-inka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heap up, to bury</li><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie down</li><li>to tumble down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blow <i>(into)</i>, to blow in, to stroll <i>(into)</i>, to stroll in; to dive <i>(into)</i>, to dive in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to flop</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>):</li></ol><p><i>завали́ть</i> (zavalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brew, to infuse, to steep, <i>(tea, coffee etc)</i> to make</li><li>to pour boiling water <i>(over)</i>, to scald</li><li>to weld up, to close <i>(up)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start</li></ol><p><i>завари́ть</i> (zavarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brew, to infuse, to steep, <i>(tea, coffee etc)</i> to make</li><li>to pour boiling water <i>(over)</i>, to scald</li><li>to weld up, to close <i>(up)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (varítʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>завари́ти</i> (zavarýty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brew (to make tea, coffee or other drinks by mixing tea leaves or coffee beans with water)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of trouble, etc.) to happen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the process of brewing</li><li>tea (<i>dried tea leaves for brewing</i>)</li><li>tea (<i>brewed tea leaves</i>)</li><li>the process of welding</li></ol><p><i>завари́ть</i> (zavarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) teapot</li></ol><p><i>завари́ть</i> (zavarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choux pastry (<i>dough</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “preboiled dough”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) preboiled, boiled</li><li>brewing, steeping (<i>of tea preparation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>завари́ть</i> (zavarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teapot</li></ol><p>Literally, “brewing kettle”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steeping, brewing (<i>of tea preparation</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) teapot</li></ol><p><i>зава́рка</i> (zavárka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teapot</li></ol><p>Literally, “brewing kettle”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brawl, battle, bustle, commotion</li><li>a porridge made of flour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>institution, establishment</li></ol><p><i>завед-</i> (zaved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conduct, directorship</li></ol><p><i>заве́довать</i> (zavédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in charge, to be the head/chief, to head, to manage</li><li>to operate (in terms of a factory or office-based environment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deliberately, intentionally, knowingly</li><li>obviously</li></ol><p><i>заве́домый</i> (zavédomyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obvious, notorious</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домый</i> (védomyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>director, chief, head, manager</li><li>governor (in schools or hospitals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take <i>(to a place)</i>, to drop off <i>(at a place)</i></li><li>to supply, to deliver</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlist, to recruit, to hire</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вербова́ть</i> (verbovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assurance; pledge</li></ol><p><i>заве́рить</i> (zavéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to squeal</li><li>to begin to chirp</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вереща́ть</i> (vereščátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure</li><li>to attest, to authenticate, to witness, to certify</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap, to roll</li><li>to screw tight, to tighten (a nut)</li><li>to tuck up, to roll up</li><li>to turn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(takes preposition в)</i> to drop in, to call (at a place)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn down, to reject</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to deny <i>sb.</i> entry/access</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend back, to fold back, to be rolled back</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend up, to turn up, to curl up</li></ol><p><i>заверну́ть</i> (zavernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to twirl/whirl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn someone's head</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to turn/spin/whirl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start moving, to stir <i>(oneself)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have one's hands full, to be in a whirl</li></ol><p><i>заверте́ть</i> (zavertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to complete, to accomplish</li><li>to conclude, to crown</li></ol><p><i>заверши́ть</i> (zaveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to end, to come to an end</li></ol><p><i>заверша́ть</i> (zaveršátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final, concluding, closing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completion (making complete&#59; conclusion)</li></ol><p><i>заверши́ть</i> (zaveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Compare Ukrainian <i>заве́ршення</i>&nbsp;n (zavéršennja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to complete, to accomplish</li><li>to conclude, to crown</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>верши́ть</i> (veršítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to end, to come to an end</li></ol><p><i>заверши́ть</i> (zaveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness</li></ol><p><i>завершённый</i> (zaveršónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure</li><li>to attest, to authenticate, to witness, to certify</li></ol><p><i>заве́рить</i> (zavéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>заве́са</i> (zavésa, “curtain, screen; (<i>figuratively</i>) veil”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) screen</li><li>curtain, screen</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Завеса)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the Curtain forces of Soviet Russia in 1918</li></ol><p>From <i>заве́са</i> (zavésa, “curtain”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close (e.g. a window) by hanging something over it</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obscure, to veil (<i>of a cloud of smoke, the fog, the rain, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to weigh</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, to bring, to lead</li><li>to get, to procure, to acquire, to buy</li><li>to establish, to set up, to found</li><li>to start</li><li><i>(a habit, friendship)</i> to form, to contract</li><li>to start <i>(a motor)</i>; to wind up <i>(a clock)</i>; to set <i>(an alarm clock)</i></li><li>to get someone worked up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be acquired [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>get</i> or <i>acquire</i> with the object of <i>у</i> (u) as the subject)</li><li>to infest [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘(in) somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to be wound up (<i>of a mechanism or clock</i>); to start (<i>of a car or motor</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get worked up, to be turned on</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вести́сь</i> (vestísʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>заве́са</i> (zavésa, “curtain, screen; (<i>figuratively</i>) veil”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legacy</li><li>precept, maxim</li><li>(biblical,&#32;law) covenant, testament</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zavětъ</i>, from <i>&#42;větъ</i> (“talk, agreement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cherished, lifelong, fondest (<i>dream, desire</i>)</li><li>hidden, secret</li></ol><p><i>заве́т</i> (zavét, “precept, covenant, (<i>obsolete</i>) vow”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a wall, a window, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by hanging with something in large numbers’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close (e.g. a window) by hanging something over it</li><li>to cover [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a wall, a window, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by hanging with something in large numbers’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заве́сить</i> (zavésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>заве́шать</i> (zavéšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will (formal document)</li></ol><p><i>завеща́ть</i> (zaveščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) testator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bequeath, to leave, to will</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>завѣщати</i> (zavěštati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zavěťati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inveterate, confirmed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>zawzięty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to vibrate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вибри́ровать</i> (vibrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(hair)</i> curling, waving</li><li><i>(technical)</i> twisting, curling, winding, coiling, convolution, folding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frizz, to curl, to wave <i>(especially of hair)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to frizz, to curl, to wave <i>(especially of hair)</i></li></ol><p><i>завива́ть</i> (zavivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waving, curling</li><li>wave, hairdo</li></ol><p>From <i>завива́ть</i> (zavivátʹ, “to wave, curl, fizzle”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, to catch sight of, to notice (from afar)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enviably</li></ol><p><i>зави́дный</i> (zavídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enviable</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy, to be envious of [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>за-</i> (za-, “behind”) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ, “to see”).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>invideō</i> (“to envy”), <i>invidia</i> (whence English <i>envy</i>) (cognate via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal, to scream, to set up a howl</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>визжа́ть</i> (vizžátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start wagging</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>виля́ть</i> (viljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) false, absurd, foolish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie (to give false information)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie (to give false information)</li></ol><p><i>завира́ть</i> (zavirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to go viral</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вируси́ться</i> (virusítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accentor (bird of the Prunella genus)</li><li>diminutive of <i>завиру́ха</i> (zavirúxa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hover <i>(above)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to hang</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be taken up <i>(with)</i>, to get a bee in one's bonnet <i>(about)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang out, to hang (to spend some time somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>зави́снуть</i> (zavísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depend (on), to rely (on) [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive)]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dependence</li><li>addiction</li></ol><p><i>зави́симый</i> (zavísimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dependent</li><li>subordinate</li><li>addicted (being dependent on something)</li></ol><p><i>зави́сеть</i> (zavísetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hover <i>(above)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to hang</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be taken up <i>(with)</i>, to get a bee in one's bonnet <i>(about)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang out, to hang (to spend some time somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>envious</li></ol><p><i>за́висть</i> (závistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envier, envious person</li></ol><p><i>за́висть</i> (závistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zavistь</i>. Cognate to Latin <i>invidia</i> (“envy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curl; swirl; spiral</li><li>cartouche</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) helix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringlet, lock (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li>flourish, twirl, whorl, curl</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) rhetorical flourish (artsy, complex figure of speech)</li></ol><p>From <i>зави́ть</i> (zavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška). Appearance of -т- is unexpected but also found in other derivatives of the same verb.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbulence, eddy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take possession of, to seize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to capture [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>завладе́ть</i> (zavladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take possession of, to seize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to capture [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allure, to lure, to entice (to attempt to draw)</li></ol><p><i>завле́к-</i> (zavlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allure, to lure, to entice (to attempt to draw)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>factory, plant</li><li>breeding farm (also in terms of husbandry)</li><li>winding mechanism</li><li>winding up</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заводи́ть</i> (zavodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subcontractor (contractor hired by contractor, a factory, plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>заво́д</i> (zavód, “factory”)</li></ol><p><i>заво́д</i> (zavód) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, to bring, to lead</li><li>to get, to procure, to acquire, to buy</li><li>to establish, to set up, to found</li><li>to start</li><li><i>(a habit, friendship)</i> to form, to contract</li><li>to start <i>(a motor)</i>, to wind up <i>(clock)</i></li><li>to get someone worked up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be acquired [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>get</i> or <i>acquire</i> with the object of <i>у</i> (u) as the subject)</li><li>to infest [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘(in) somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to be wound up (<i>of a mechanism or clock</i>); to start (<i>of a car or motor</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get worked up, to be turned on</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>води́ться</i> (vodítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) clockwork, (<i>relational</i>) wind-up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lively, quick-moving, full of pep</li></ol><p><i>заво́д</i> (zavód) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>за́водь</i> (závodʹ); a small creek or backwater.</li></ol><p><i>за́водь</i> (závodʹ, “creek, backwater”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) factory, plant</li></ol><p><i>заво́д</i> (zavód) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creek, backwater</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conquest</li><li>winning</li></ol><p><i>завоева́ть</i> (zavojevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conqueror; winner</li></ol><p><i>завоева́ть</i> (zavojevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, to win</li><li>to gain</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>воева́ть</i> (vojevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of delivering, usually of something heavy, unwieldy, or in large quantities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (<i>to a place</i>), to drop off (<i>at a place</i>)</li><li>to supply, to deliver</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to carry (<i>by vehicle</i>), to begin to transport</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to dirty, to contaminate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get dirty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to mess around</li></ol><p><i>завози́ть</i> (zavozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ться</i> (vozítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat-bottomed boat used to ferry cargo and people across the Don and Volga rivers until the 19th century</li></ol><p><i>завози́ть</i> (zavozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to cloud (the sun, the sky, one's eyes with tears, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>заволо́к-</i> (zavolók-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to overcast, to become cloudy, to become dark</li></ol><p><i>завола́кивать</i> (zavolákivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(water surface)</i> to become agitated</li><li>to begin to worry/fret</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>волнова́ться</i> (volnovátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to cloud (the sun, the sky, one's eyes with tears, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to overcast, to become cloudy, to become dark</li></ol><p><i>заволо́чь</i> (zavolóčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yell, to scream</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вопи́ть</i> (vopítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast a spell <i>(over)</i></li><li>to charm, to bewitch</li></ol><p><i>заворожи́ть</i> (zavorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw tight, to tighten</li><li>to tuck up, to roll up</li><li>to wrap up <i>(in)</i></li><li>to turn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in <i>(at a place)</i>, to call <i>(at a place)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin, to come on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to say, to utter, to let loose</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run, to manage</li></ol><p><i>завороти́ть</i> (zavorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wrap oneself up</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to bend back, to fold back, to be rolled back</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to bend up, to turn up, to curl up</li></ol><p><i>завора́чивать</i> (zavoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast a spell <i>(over)</i></li><li>to charm, to bewitch</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ворожи́ть</i> (vorožítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bewitched, enchanted, spellbound (<i>of a person; experiencing being enchanted or bewitched</i>)</li><li>bewitched, enchanted, spellbound (<i>of a look, one's eyes, etc.; expressing being enchanted or bewitched</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck up, to roll up</li><li>to wrap up <i>(in)</i></li><li>to turn</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to bend back, to fold back, to be rolled back</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to bend up, to turn up, to curl up</li></ol><p><i>завороти́ть</i> (zavorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to turn, to roll or shift (something bulky)</li><li>to start to move (<i>with some purpose</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, esp. a body part’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>воро́чать</i> (voróčatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to toss and turn (<i>of the whole body</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to start to turn, to start to roll (<i>of something heavy or bulky</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заворо́чать</i> (zavoróčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to grumble</li><li>to begin to growl</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ворча́ть</i> (vorčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard-won</li><li>conquered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, to win</li><li>to gain</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>воева́ть</i> (vojevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie (to give false information) excessively</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>врать</i> (vratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) always</li></ol><p><i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>всегда́</i> (vsegdá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habitué, regular (client, visitor)</li></ol><p>From <i>завсегда́</i> (zavsegdá, “always”) +&lrm; <i>-атай</i> (-ataj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>завътра</i> (zavŭtra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;za utra</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>у́тро</i> (útro).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakfast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zajutrъkъ</i> or Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zautrъkъ</i>, the same origin as Russian <i>завтра</i> (zavtra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have breakfast, to breakfast, to lunch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow's</li></ol><p>From <i>за́втра</i> (závtra) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literally) to veil</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to veil (make less distinct)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вуаль</i> (vualʹ, “veil”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>director / head of studies (at school)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>заве́дующий уче́бной ча́стью</i> (zavédujuščij učébnoj částʹju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logistics manager, superintendent, steward</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>заве́дующий хозя́йством</i> (zavédujuščij xozjájstvom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) howling, howl</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) wailing, wail</li></ol><p><i>завыва́ть</i> (zavyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to howl</li></ol><p><i>завы́ть</i> (zavýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overstate, to overrate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́сить</i> (-výsitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(begin to)</i> howl, to raise a howl</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>выть</i> (vytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overstate, to overrate</li></ol><p><i>завы́сить</i> (zavýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to fasten</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to start, to strike up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quit (a bad habit or activity)</li><li>to stick <i>(in)</i>, to sink <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get stuck <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p><p><i>завя́знуть</i> (zavjáznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get tied up [tied into a knot]</li><li>to set in, to begin, to start</li><li><i>(fruit)</i> to set</li></ol><p><i>завяза́ть</i> (zavjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string, lace <i>(to tie)</i></li><li>plot <i>(of a play or novel)</i></li><li>start, outset</li></ol><p><i>завяза́ть</i> (zavjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in)</i>, to sink <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get stuck <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вя́знуть</i> (vjáznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to fasten</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to start, to strike up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quit (a bad habit or activity)</li></ol><p><i>завяза́ть</i> (zavjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get tied up (tied into a knot)</li><li>to set in, to begin, to start</li><li><i>(fruit)</i> to set</li></ol><p><i>завя́зывать</i> (zavjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ovary (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, to shrivel, to fade, to dry up (shrivel, droop, dry up, usually of flowers or plants)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>вя́нуть</i> (vjánutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wilt, to wither, to fade, to languish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap, to roll</li><li>to screw tight, to tighten (a nut)</li><li>to tuck up, to roll up</li></ol><p><i>заверт-</i> (zavert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend back, to fold back, to be rolled back</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend up, to turn up, to curl up</li></ol><p><i>завёртывать</i> (zavjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>заве́т</i> (zavét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(riddle)</i> to set, to offer</li><li>to think <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make plans, to plan ahead</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to pollute</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>га́дить</i> (gáditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riddle, conundrum, puzzle</li><li>mystery, enigma</li></ol><p><i>загада́ть</i> (zagadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Polish <i>zagadka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mysteriously, enigmatically</li></ol><p><i>зага́дочный</i> (zagádočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscrutability, mystery</li></ol><p><i>зага́дочный</i> (zagádočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enigmatic, mysterious</li><li>obscure (difficult to understand)</li><li>riddlesome</li></ol><p>From <i>зага́дка</i> (zagádka, “riddle, enigma”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from <i>за-</i> (za-) + <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ, “to divine, to foretell”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(riddle)</i> to set, to offer</li><li>to think <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make plans, to plan ahead</li></ol><p><i>загада́ть</i> (zagadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to pollute</li></ol><p><i>зага́дить</i> (zagáditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to make a racket (a lot of noise)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>галде́ть</i> (galdétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suntan, tan (darkened coloration of the skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to extinguish</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гаси́ть</i> (gasítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to soil</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гва́здать</i> (gvázdatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) snag, trouble, hitch, rub, impediment, catch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn up, to turn down, to bend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exaggerate, to pull someone's leg</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;perfective) to say, to let loose</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to curl up; to curl down (<i>of the edge of something</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to kick the bucket, to bite the dust, to croak, to die</li></ol><p><i>загиба́ть</i> (zagibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hypnotize</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гипнотизи́ровать</i> (gipnotizírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title, heading</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital letter (upper-case letter A B C (Latin) or А Б В (Cyrillic), etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title page</li><li>main page</li><li>homepage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headword (a word used as the title of a section)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) title, heading</li><li>capital (<i>of a letter</i>), uppercase</li></ol><p><i>загла́вие</i> (zaglávije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to swallow (<i>of fish</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заглота́ть</i> (zaglotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muddiness</li></ol><p>From <i>заглинизированный</i> (zaglinizirovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to swallow (<i>of fish</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>глота́ть</i> (glotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(sound)</i> to die away, to abate, to fade out, to subside</li><li>to grow wild, to turn into a wilderness, to run/go to seed, to decay, to grow lifeless</li><li><i>(feeling, interest etc)</i> to subside, to decline</li><li><i>(engine)</i> to fail, to cut out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гло́хнуть</i> (glóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to deafen, to damp, to drown, to jam</li><li><i>(pain etc)</i> to alleviate, to soothe, to dull</li><li><i>(plants)</i> to choke <i>(up)</i></li><li>to suppress, to stifle, to smother</li><li>to stop</li></ol><p><i>заглуши́ть</i> (zaglušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to deafen, to damp, to drown, to jam</li><li><i>(pain etc)</i> to alleviate, to soothe, to dull</li><li><i>(plants)</i> to choke <i>(up)</i></li><li>to suppress, to stifle, to smother</li><li>to stop</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>глуши́ть</i> (glušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choke, plug, stopper, terminator, faceplate, stub, dummy.</li></ol><p><i>заглуш(ить)</i> (zagluš(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight for sore eyes, beauty (someone or something that is beautiful to look at)</li></ol><p><i>загляде́ть(ся)</i> (zagljadétʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>загляде́ние</i> (zagljadénije)</li></ol><p><i>загляде́ть</i> (zagljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glance (at), to peep (in), to look (into), to have a look (at)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in (on), to look in (on), to call (on)</li></ol><p><i>загляд-</i> (zagljad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glance (at), to peep (in), to look (into), to have a look (at)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in (on), to look in (on), to call (on)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive into</li><li>to hammer into</li><li>to herd into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sell, to flog</li><li>to exhaust, to fatigue; to drive to exhaustion, to drive too hard (a horse)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, to decay</li></ol><p><i>загни́ть</i> (zagnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ива</i> (-iva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, curled, crooked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn up, to turn down, to bend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exaggerate, to pull someone's leg</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to say, to let loose</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to curl up; to curl down (<i>of the edge of something</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to kick the bucket, to bite the dust, to croak, to die</li></ol><p><i>загну́ть</i> (zagnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to talk</li><li>to tire (someone) with one's talk, to talk someone's ear off</li><li>to exorcise, to cast a spell</li></ol><p><i>заговори́ть</i> (zagovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be carried away by a conversation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk nonsense, to ramble in speech</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wander, to make slips of the tongue</li></ol><p><i>загова́ривать</i> (zagovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) meat-fare day, cheese-fare day (the day before a fast, and thus the last day when food of animal origin may be eaten&#59; traditionally, a day of feasting and eating rich food)</li></ol><p>From <i>загове́ть</i> (zagovétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енье</i> (-enʹje), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gověti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conspiracy (act of working in secret to obtain some goal)</li><li>spell, incantation (a special sequence of words with a magic effect)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>го́вор</i> (góvor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to speak</li><li>to talk someone's ear off (tire someone out by one's talk)</li><li>to exorcise, to bewitch, to charm, to cast a spell, to enchant</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk excessively (<i>within a group of people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заговори́ть</i> (zagovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conspirator, plotter</li></ol><p><i>за́говор</i> (zágovor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) flourish, squiggle, whorl, curl</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) object of this shape</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) unexpected turn of events, plot twist</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) knob, thick end of a stick</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;гогу́ля</i> (&#42;gogúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina); compare Polish <i>gogółka</i> (“fruit seed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in advance, previously, in good time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heading; header; title; headline; caption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to wail</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>голоси́ть</i> (golosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herding, driving in, rounding up</li><li>enclosure</li><li>pen, cattle pen</li><li>corral</li><li>paddock</li><li>yard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beater (a person who drives game towards shooters in a hunting party, often working in a group)</li></ol><p><i>заго́н</i> (zagón) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive into</li><li>to hammer into</li><li>to herd into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sell, to flog</li><li>to exhaust, to fatigue; to drive to exhaustion, to drive too hard (a horse)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose, to shut in, to fence in</li><li>to bar, to block up, to obstruct, to barricade</li></ol><p><i>загороди́ть</i> (zagorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tan, to suntan, to sunbathe</li><li>to acquire a tan</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to idle, to loaf</li></ol><p><i>загоре́ть</i> (zagorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch fire</li><li>to break out</li><li>to be eager <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to feel an urge</li></ol><p><i>загора́ть</i> (zagorátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sunburnt, tanned; (strongly) brown, bronzed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire a tan</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch fire</li><li>to light up, to turn on, to come on (of a light source)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to break out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be eager <i>(for)</i>, to light up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;impersonal) to feel an urge</li></ol><p><i>загоре́ть</i> (zagorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose, to shut in, to fence in</li><li>to bar, to block up, to obstruct, to barricade</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>городи́ть</i> (gorodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fence</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) enclosure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) partition</li></ol><p><i>загороди́ть</i> (zagorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) country; rural</li><li>out-of-town</li><li>suburban</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to store up, to lay in (<i>stock</i>)</li><li>to prepare</li><li>to procure</li><li>to harvest</li></ol><p><i>загото́вить</i> (zagotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) zagotzerno (Bureau of Grain Procurement)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>управле́ние по загото́вкам зерна́</i> (upravlénije po zagotóvkam zerná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to store up, to lay in (<i>stock</i>)</li><li>to prepare</li><li>to procure</li><li>to harvest</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procurement</li><li>laying-in, stocking</li><li>workpiece, half-finished product</li><li>placeholder</li></ol><p><i>загото́вить</i> (zagotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to store up, to lay in (<i>stock</i>)</li><li>to prepare</li><li>to procure</li><li>to harvest</li></ol><p><i>загото́вить</i> (zagotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) To ghost, to perform an act of ghosting.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(action)</i> blocking, obstruction</li><li>barrier, obstruction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) a foreign country or countries, or any territory outside homeland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, from abroad</li></ol><p><i>заграни́ца</i> (zagraníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passport (for traveling)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>заграни́чный па́спорт</i> (zagraníčnyj pásport). Literally, “passport for abroad”, or more loosely translated as “foreign passport”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rake together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rake in (money)</li><li>to bank (the embers of a fire in a furnace)</li></ol><p><i>загреб-</i> (zagreb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zagrebian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to thunder</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be fired, to get into prison</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-, “to begin doing”) +&lrm; <i>греме́ть</i> (gremétʹ, “to rattle, to thunder”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rake together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rake in (money)</li><li>to bank (the embers of a fire in a furnace)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>грести́</i> (grestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nape, back of the neck, scruff of the neck; withers (of a horse)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гри́ва</i> (gríva) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok); compare with подборо́док (podboródok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afterlife (life after death)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) afterlife</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гроб</i> (grob, “coffin; grave”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Literally, “behind/after the grave”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block up, to jam</li></ol><p><i>загромозди́ть</i> (zagromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to roar/rumble, to begin to thunder</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>грохота́ть</i> (groxotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (something on an automobile or a vessel)</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) to charge, to feed (provide a machine or an electronic device with fuel or electricity)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to boot, to load</li></ol><p><i>загрузи́ть</i> (zagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) to be booted, to be loaded</li></ol><p><i>загружа́ть</i> (zagružátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workload</li><li>congestion (<i>of traffic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>загру́женный</i> (zagrúžennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>загру́женность</i> (zagrúžennostʹ, “workload; traffic congestion”)</li></ol><p><i>загружённый</i> (zagružónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to feed</li><li>to engage, to occupy</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to boot, to load, to download</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bend someone's ear, to ramble on</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) to be booted, to be loaded</li></ol><p><i>загрузи́ть</i> (zagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loading (of objects or cargos)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) loading, bootstrap (of computer systems)</li><li>download</li></ol><p><i>загрузи́ть</i> (zagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loader (a worker or machine that loads, or a computer program that prepares other programs for execution)</li></ol><p><i>загрузи́ть</i> (zagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start being sad, to start grieving</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>грусти́ть</i> (grustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soiling, pollution, contamination</li></ol><p>From <i>загрязня́ть</i> (zagrjaznjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to make dirty, to besmirch, to litter</li><li>to sully, to tarnish</li><li>to pollute</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>грязни́ть</i> (grjaznítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to make dirty, to besmirch, to litter</li><li>to pollute</li></ol><p><i>загрязни́ть</i> (zagrjaznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ЗАГС</i> (ZAGS)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ЗАГС)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>отде́л за́писей а́ктов гра́жданского состоя́ния</i> (otdél zápisej áktov gráždanskovo sostojánija): civil registry (office), civil registration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to waste, to ruin, to destroy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>губи́ть</i> (gubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to google</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гу́глить</i> (gúglitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to drone/buzz/hum</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гуде́ть</i> (gudétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spree</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>загуля́ть</i> (zaguljátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to go on a bender, to paint the town red, to binge-drink</li></ol><p><i>загуля́ть</i> (zaguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to go on a bender, to paint the town red, to binge-drink</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend too much time strolling/walking</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend too much time having fun (especially drinking, fornicating, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>загуля́ть</i> (zaguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thicken</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>густе́ть</i> (gustétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back, rear</li><li>bottom, buttocks, nates, posterior, seat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set, to assign <i>(an exercise, a lesson)</i></li><li>to ask <i>(a question)</i></li><li>to pose <i>(a problem)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to throw <i>(a dinner, a banquet)</i></li><li>to give, to administer <i>(a punishment)</i></li><li>to preset, to specify, to assign</li></ol><p><i>зада́ть</i> (zadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>of period, time</i> to have</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed, to be a success</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give oneself airs, to put on airs</li></ol><p><i>задава́ть</i> (zadavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush <i>(to death)</i></li><li><i>(car)</i> to run over, to knock down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to suppress, to crack down <i>(on)</i></li><li>to oppress, to exhaust</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush <i>(to death)</i></li><li><i>(car)</i> to run over, to knock down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to suppress, to crack down <i>(on)</i></li><li>to oppress, to exhaust</li></ol><p><i>задави́ть</i> (zadavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assignment, task</li><li>setting (<i>of parameters, etc., as a process</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) task, mission, assignment</li></ol><p><i>зада́ть</i> (zadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predetermined, given, established</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for a song, for nothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for free (without paying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potential (talent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deposit</li><li>advance payment</li><li>earnest (money)</li><li>key money</li><li>handsel</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) beginning</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set, to assign (an exercise or lesson)</li><li>to ask (a question)</li><li>to pose (a problem)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to throw (a dinner or banquet)</li><li>to give, to administer (a punishment)</li><li>to preset, to specify, to assign</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>of period, time</i> to have</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed, to be a success</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give oneself airs, to put on airs</li></ol><p><i>зада́ть</i> (zadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) problem</li><li>task, problem</li><li>object, objective, aim, goal, mission, end</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зада́ча</i> (zadáča): (small) task, problem</li></ol><p><i>зада́ча</i> (zadáča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of problems <i>(a book that contains mathematical problems)</i></li></ol><p><i>задача</i> (zadača) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start moving</li><li>to push (into), to slide (into) (e.g. curtains, drawer, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ)</p><p><i>задви́г-</i> (zadvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, to close, to slide</li><li>to be slidable, to be movable</li><li>to begin to move, to stir</li></ol><p><i>задвига́ть</i> (zadvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>задви́гать</i> (zadvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(shutting)</i> bolt</li><li>door bolt</li><li>window catch</li><li>damper <i>(of a chimney)</i></li><li>slide valve</li></ol><p><i>задви́г-</i> (zadvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push (into), to slide (into) (e.g. curtains, drawer, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, to close, to slide</li></ol><p><i>задви́нуть</i> (zadvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backyard; boondocks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military slang</i>) to kill in action (<i>of enemy soldiers</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>двухсо́т-</i> (dvuxsót-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to brush <i>(against)</i></li><li>to be caught, to hit, to strike</li><li><i>(in a conversation, narration)</i> to touch <i>(upon)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to affect, to offend, to hurt</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to affect</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mislay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start working/operating, to spring into action</li><li>to bring into play, to set in motion</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствовать</i> (déjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reserve, stock, margin</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) backlog</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) + a deverbal noun derived from <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up</li><li>to seal</li><li>to caulk</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up</li><li>to seal</li><li>to caulk</li></ol><p><i>заде́лать</i> (zadélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apprehension (arrest), detention (custody)</li></ol><p><i>задержа́ть</i> (zaderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrested, detained</li><li>delayed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrestee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold up, detain, to hold back, to stop</li><li>to delay, to check</li><li>to arrest, to detain</li><li>to slow down, to retard, to delay, to hamper</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to linger, to stay (too long)</li><li>to be late, to be delayed</li><li>to linger (on, over)</li></ol><p><i>задержа́ть</i> (zaderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detain, to hold back, to stop</li><li>to delay, to check</li><li>to arrest, to detain</li><li>to slow down, to retard, to delay, to hamper</li></ol><p><i>задержа́ть</i> (zaderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to linger, to stay too long</li><li>to be late, to be delayed</li><li>to linger (on, over)</li></ol><p><i>заде́рживать</i> (zadérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detention, stoppage</li><li>delay, retardation</li></ol><p><i>задержа́ть</i> (zaderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to brush <i>(against)</i></li><li>to be caught, to hit, to strike</li><li><i>(in a conversation, narration)</i> to touch <i>(upon)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to affect, to offend, to hurt</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to affect</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) to design</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>диза́йнить</i> (dizájnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>external back part of a shoe covering the heel</li><li>backstrap, back cut of meat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to split (skin, fingernails)</li><li>to lift, to pull up (head, nose, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy badly</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cocky; quarrelsome; cheeky; blusterous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) velar (Articulated at the velum or soft palate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rear, back, hind</li></ol><p><i>зад</i> (zad) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindbrain</li><li>rhombencephalon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taillight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back part of a shoe, above the heel</li><li>backdrop, backcloth</li></ol><p><i>зад</i> (zad) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to win someone's favour by flatter, bribe etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зад</i> (zad)</li><li>tailgate, back</li></ol><p><i>зад</i> (zad) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to tire, pester, to exasperate, to piss off, to bore</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>долба́ть</i> (dolbátʹ). A euphemism for заеба́ть (zajebátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long before, well in advance</li></ol><p><i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>до́лго</i> (dólgo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to borrow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become indebted to, to owe</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>должа́ть</i> (dolžátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liabilities, debt, indebtedness, arrears (state or condition of owing something to another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back, backward (in a backward direction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back to front</li><li>backward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;Internet slang) to donate</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дона́тить</i> (donátitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fervour, ardour, pep, vigour</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>деру́</i> (derú). Compare <i>раздо́р</i> (razdór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ardent</li></ol><p><i>задо́р</i> (zadór) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscientific ideas about history and linguistics</li><li>pseudoscience</li></ol><p><i>Задо́рнов</i> (Zadórnov) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina), after the satirist Mikhail Zadornov, who made pseudoscientific statements on television.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fervent, zealous, ardent</li><li>cocky, arrogant</li><li>provocative, perky</li></ol><p><i>задо́р</i> (zadór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to choke, to strangle, to suffocate</li><li>to gasp</li><li>to pant</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to split (skin, fingernails)</li><li>to lift, to pull up (head, nose, etc.)</li><li>to tear to pieces, to kill (of a predator)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to pester, to exasperate, to piss off</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ). Since it is also used as a euphemism for ебать (jebatʹ), sense 4 is a common euphemism for заебать (zajebatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doze off, to get drowsy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дрема́ть</i> (dremátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) seedy, crummy, dilapidated, unattractive, shabby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to dirty or wear out (usually clothes)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дри́пать</i> (drípatʹ), with <i>дри́пать</i> (drípatʹ) ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dripa</i> (“rag, tatter, cloth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to tremble, to begin to shiver</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дрожа́ть</i> (drožátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) nerd (no-lifer)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) nerd (good student)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) geek</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) expert</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) physically weak male</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) gamer</li></ol><p>From <i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) to play computer games too much</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) to be a loser</li></ol><p><i>задро́т</i> (zadrót) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to begin to masturbate</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to exhaust, tire out, wear out, wear down</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow out (a candle, fire, etc.)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to put (a blast furnace) into action</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to gust</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to blow, penetrating [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to carry by wafting</li></ol><p><i>заду́ть</i> (zadútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devise</li><li>to think <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, to meditate, to reflect</li><li>to wonder, to muse</li><li>to begin to think, to be engrossed in thoughts</li><li>to hesitate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plan, idea</li></ol><p><i>заду́мать</i> (zadúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pensively</li></ol><p><i>заду́мчивый</i> (zadúmčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtfulness; reverie</li></ol><p><i>заду́мчивый</i> (zadúmčivyj, “pensive, thoughtful”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pensive, thoughtful (having the appearance of thinking)</li></ol><p><i>заду́мать</i> (zadúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plan, to conceive, to intend, to have the intention</li><li>to think <i>(offering to guess)</i></li></ol><p><i>заду́мать</i> (zadúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, to meditate, to reflect</li><li>to wonder, to muse</li><li>to begin to think, to be engrossed in thoughts</li><li>to hesitate</li></ol><p><i>заду́мывать</i> (zadúmyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to begin to blow (<i>of the wind, etc.</i>)</li><li>to blow out (a candle, fire, etc.)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to put (a blast furnace) into action</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimate</li><li>sincere, heartfelt</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>душе́вный</i> (dušévnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smother, to stifle, to strangle, to throttle</li><li>to oppress, to stifle, to repress, to suppress</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>души́ть</i> (dušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to begin to emit smoke</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to begin to smoke cigarettes</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fill with smoke</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cover with soot</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дыми́ть</i> (dymítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to choke, to strangle, to suffocate</li><li>to gasp</li><li>to pant</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>задохну́ться</i> (zadoxnútʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start breathing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дыша́ть</i> (dyšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to tug at [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to torment (a horse) through repeatedly tugging the reins</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harass or torment through repeated demands</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to twitch</li></ol><p><i>задёргать</i> (zadjórgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to pull, to shut (a curtain)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to torment (a horse) through repeatedly tugging the reins</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harass or torment through repeated demands</li></ol><p><i>задёрг-</i> (zadjórg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(curtain)</i> to draw, to pull, to shut</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) very good, wonderful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to wear out (someone) by fucking</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (someone) to death (also figuratively)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to pester, to exasperate, to piss off</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ).</p><p>From <i>заести́</i> (zajestí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literally,&#32;vulgar) to get exhausted from having sex</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be fed up to the back teeth (to become very tired or annoyed while doing something physically hard or boring)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) + <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) + <i>ся</i> (sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to score (a goal)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ебе́нить</i> (jebénitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fucking great, damned good</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fucking excellent!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to wear out (someone) by fucking</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (someone) to death (also figuratively)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to pester, to exasperate, to piss off</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be cold, to feel cold</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ебу́н</i> (jebún) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jam, to stick (e.g. of equipment or zippers) (<i>also impersonal</i>)</li><li>to eat after a drink of alcohol or medicine</li><li>to kill by eating to death</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to torment</li></ol><p><i>заед-</i> (zajed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a short) visit, call (by a vehicle)</li><li>lap, round, heat (sports)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call in on the way or casually</li><li>to drive into, to go into (of a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jaded, worn-out (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hackneyed, cliched, trite (<i>of a phrase or word</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visiting</li><li>touring</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visitor, person passing through</li></ol><p><i>зае́зд</i> (zajézd) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to fidget, to begin to move restlessly</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ело́зить</i> (jelózitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>заебсти́</i> (zajebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jam, to stick (e.g. of equipment or zippers) (<i>also impersonal</i>)</li><li>to eat after a drink of alcohol or medicine</li><li>to kill by eating to death</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to torment</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call in on the way</li><li>to drive into</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry, to roast, to broil, to grill</li></ol><p><i>зажа́рить</i> (zažáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fry or roast to completion</li></ol><p><i>зажа́ривать</i> (zažárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry, to roast, to broil, to grill</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fry or roast to completion</li></ol><p><i>зажа́рить</i> (zažáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to squeeze, to clutch</li><li>to stop up, to block up, to plug up</li><li>to suppress, to stifle, to restrict</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to withhold, to let be giving back something borrowed or otherwise rightfully belonging to someone else</li><li>to start to reap, to start to harvest</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wait very long, to get tired from waiting</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set fire, to light, to kindle, to inflame, to ignite, to turn on (headlights)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch fire</li><li>to light up, <i>(electical lights)</i> to turn on, to go on</li><li><i>(eyes, face etc)</i> to light up <i>(with)</i>, to kindle <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(feeling, desire etc)</i> to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to be overcome <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get enthusiastic <i>(about, over)</i>, to get carried away <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>заже́чь</i> (zažéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal, to close up, to skin over (of wounds)</li></ol><p><i>зажи́ть</i> (zažítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>being alive</li></ol><p>First attested in 1741. From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жи́во</i> (žívo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighter (fire making device)</li></ol><p><i>зажига́ть</i> (zažigátʹ, “to ignite, to set fire”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting up, firing, igniting</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) ignition</li></ol><p><i>зажига́ть</i> (zažigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incendiary</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) inflammatory</li></ol><p><i>зажига́ть</i> (zažigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set fire, to light, to kindle, to inflame, to ignite, to turn on (headlights)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch fire</li><li>to light up, <i>(electical lights)</i> to turn on, to go on</li><li><i>(eyes, face etc)</i> to light up <i>(with)</i>, to kindle <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(feeling, desire etc)</i> to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to be overcome <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get enthusiastic <i>(about, over)</i>, to get carried away <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>зажига́ть</i> (zažigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to appropriate, to welch, to snatch (<i>to claim or use something as property especially when it belongs to someone else</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to appropriate, to welch, to snatch (<i>to claim or use something as property especially when it belongs to someone else</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clamp, clutch, clip, terminal</li><li>clampdown, suppression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to squeeze, to clutch</li><li>to stop up, to block up, to plug up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to suppress, to stifle, to restrict</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to reap, to start to harvest</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prosperous, affluent, well-to-do</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal, to close up, to skin over (of wounds)</li><li>to begin to live (<i>only perfective</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw up one's eyes, to close one's eyes tight</li></ol><p><i>зажму́ривать</i> (zažmúrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw up or shut one's eyes, to screw up one's eyelids</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill someone</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жму́рить</i> (žmúritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw up one's eyes, to close one's eyes tight</li></ol><p><i>зажму́рить</i> (zažmúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be unwilling to give (someone something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жо́п(а)</i> (žóp(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow-covered pool of water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude) to become picky with food, having had too much to eat</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude,&#32;figuratively) to start taking things for granted, to become greedy, picky or self-obsessed due to having a life of abundance, being coddled, having good living/working/etc. conditions</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ, “to eat, to gobble, to guzzle”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zazaki (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to invite <i>(persistently)</i>, to call in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shout (to someone) to come in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to ring/jingle, to ring out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>звене́ть</i> (zvenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start ringing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to sound/resound</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>звуча́ть</i> (zvučátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inattentive, to be absent-minded</li><li>to gape, to gawk</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground (electrical conductor connected to point of zero potential or electrical point of zero potential)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bizarre world, world where everything is absurd</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>зе́ркало</i> (zérkalo) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). Compare English <i>looking glass</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snake, to meander</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>змеи́ться</i> (zmeítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give oneself airs, to be conceited</li></ol><p>Cf знавать.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) love attraction</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) the object of such attraction, a sweetheart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зазно́ба</i> (zaznóba)</li></ol><p><i>зазно́ба</i> (zaznóba) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gap, clearance, spacing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) + deverbal from <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in expression</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за́зрить</i> (zázritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in vain, for nothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jagged</li><li>notched, serrated</li><li>learned by rote (learned without understanding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notch, jag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barker, tout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to invite <i>(persistently)</i>, to call in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shout (to someone) to come in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to play</li><li>to begin to sparkle</li><li>to look to advantage, to stand out</li><li><i>(a record, tape recorder etc.)</i> to spoil/wear out by playing</li><li>to run down, to exhaust, to overuse, to overwear <i>(by repetition)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appropriate, to help oneself <i>(to)</i>, to hook</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flirting, flirtation, footsie</li><li>approach, playing to the gallery (movements to gain favor&#59; advances)</li></ol><p><i>заи́грывать</i> (zaígryvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flirt <i>(with)</i></li><li>to make advances <i>(to)</i></li><li><i>(a record, tape recorder etc.)</i> to spoil/wear out by playing</li><li>to run down, to exhaust, to overuse, to overwear <i>(by repetition)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appropriate, to help oneself <i>(to)</i>, to hook</li></ol><p><i>заигра́ть</i> (zaigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stutterer</li><li>stammerer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stammer, to stutter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hint <i>(at)</i>, <i>(just)</i> to mention, to touch <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ика́ть</i> (ikátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stammer, to stutter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hint <i>(at)</i>, <i>(just)</i> to mention, to touch <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>икну́ть</i> (iknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to get hold of</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creditor, lender</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borrowing, taking, adoption</li><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) loanword</li></ol><p><i>заи́мствовать</i> (zaímstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adopted, borrowed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to borrow, to take (from)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered with frost</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>и́ндеветь</i> (índevetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an interested manner</li></ol><p><i>заинтересо́ванный</i> (zainteresóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concernment, interest, commitment; motives</li></ol><p><i>заинтересо́ванный</i> (zainteresóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interested (experiencing interest, taking an interest in) (<i>of a person</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li><li>interested (expressing interest) (<i>of a look, glance, tone, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (start to) interest, to concern</li><li>to motivate (someone)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>интересова́ть</i> (interesovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become interested, to take an interest [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заинтересова́ть</i> (zainteresovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become interested, to take an interest [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заинтересо́вывать</i> (zainteresóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intrigue, to arouse interest in (someone)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>интригова́ть</i> (intrigovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>за́яц</i> (zájac); (little) hare</li><li>Used as a term of endearment, usually for a child or lover.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curry favor with, to ingratiate oneself with, to flatter, to suck up to [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>за́яц</i> (zájac)</li><li>darling, baby (especially of a child)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zajьka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (to see), to come in (to see, etc.), to call, to drop in</li><li>to pick up, to fetch</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to call at, to enter (a port)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to get, to advance</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to pass, to draw out</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to approach</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to outflank</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to turn, to disappear, to go behind</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) to set</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be well received</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be beside oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to become/grow numb</li></ol><p><i>зайти́</i> (zajtí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually with ехать (jexatʹ)</i>) without paying, for free</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare (meat)</li></ol><p>From <i>зайча́т-</i> (zajčát-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>зайчо́нок</i> (zajčónok, “leveret”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>за́яц</i> (zájac): bunny, hare; darling, sweetheart, sunny</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) reflection of a lightbeam</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;colloquial) Belarusian ruble</li></ol><p><i>зайц-</i> (zajc-) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leveret</li></ol><p><i>за́йка</i> (zájka) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enslave, to put into bonded labour</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кабала́</i> (kabalá) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offscreen</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кадр</i> (kadr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), from <i>за ка́дром</i> (za kádrom, “offscreen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order (request for some product or service)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заказа́ть</i> (zakazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to place an order</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) to forbid, to prohibit, to ban</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to order the murder of</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made to order; made to measure (<i>of clothes and shoes</i>)</li><li>registered</li><li>protected, reserved</li></ol><p><i>зака́з</i> (zakáz) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) material (e.g. articles, documentaries) in the mass media made on order</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) assassination, contract murder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>customer, client (who orders goods or services)</li></ol><p><i>заказа́ть</i> (zakazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to place an order</li></ol><p><i>заказа́ть</i> (zakazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to call the tune, to call the shots</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, to temper</li></ol><p><i>закали́ть</i> (zakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become tempered, to harden (<i>of metal</i>)</li><li>to become strong and healthy</li><li>to become hardy [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>зака́ливать</i> (zakálivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to temper (to heat-treat)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to temper, to harden, to inure</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кали́ть</i> (kalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become tempered, to harden (<i>of metal</i>)</li><li>to become strong and healthy</li><li>to become hardy [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>закали́ть</i> (zakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempering, hardening</li><li>training (for endurance)</li></ol><p><i>закали́ть</i> (zakalítʹ, “to temper, to harden”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill by stabbing</li><li>to slaughter (an animal)</li><li>to attach with a pin</li></ol><p><i>заколо́ть</i> (zakolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to temper (to heat-treat)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to temper, to harden, to inure</li></ol><p><i>закали́ть</i> (zakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become strong and healthy</li><li>to become hardy [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>to become tempered, to harden (<i>of metal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>закаля́ть</i> (zakaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>physically strong, hardy, not sick</li><li>possessing endurance, able to endure unfavorable conditions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to end, to finish, to conclude, to complete</li></ol><p><i>зако́нчить</i> (zakónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end, to be over</li></ol><p><i>зака́нчивать</i> (zakánčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spot, to smudge</li><li><i>(medicine)</i> to put drops <i>(in)</i></li><li>to start dripping</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ка́пать</i> (kápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury, to dig <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fill up <i>(with earth)</i></li><li>to spot, to smudge</li><li><i>(medicine)</i> to put drops <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>закопа́ть</i> (zakopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>зака́пать</i> (zakápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfeed; to bring harm by overfeeding</li></ol><p><i>закорми́ть</i> (zakormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunset</li><li>decline</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) west</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up</li><li>to seal, to close</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll (into, under, etc.), to wheel (into, under, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to cause; to create (a scandal, a scene, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(under)</i>, to roll <i>(behind)</i></li><li><i>(celestial body)</i> to set</li><li>to wane, to decline</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go off <i>(to)</i></li><li>to go into a fit <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>закати́ть</i> (zakatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sunset</li></ol><p><i>зака́т</i> (zakát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up</li><li>to seal, to close</li><li>to roll (into, under, etc.), to wheel (into, under, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) ; to cause; to create (a scandal, a scene, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>заката́ть</i> (zakatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>закати́ть</i> (zakatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(under)</i>, to roll <i>(behind)</i></li><li><i>(celestial body)</i> to set</li><li>to wane, to decline</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go off <i>(to)</i></li><li>to go into a fit <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>зака́тывать</i> (zakátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to swing/rock/sway</li><li>to rock to sleep</li><li>to pump <i>(into)</i>, to pump in, to inject <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to upload</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to swing/rock/sway</li></ol><p><i>закача́ть</i> (zakačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumping</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) download</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a fit of coughing</li></ol><p><i>зака́шляться</i> (zakášljatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a fit of coughing</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ка́шлять</i> (kášljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ferment, to leaven</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ква́сить</i> (kvásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaven, ferment, barm</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mold</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stuff (<i>referring to a person's quality</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заква́сить</i> (zakvásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ferment, to leaven</li></ol><p><i>заква́сить</i> (zakvásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to nod <i>(one's head)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кива́ть</i> (kivátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pelt <i>(with)</i></li><li>to shower <i>(with)</i></li><li>to fill <i>(with)</i>, to fill up <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) oddity, strangeness, peculiarity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to hurl <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>to toss, to throw, to tilt</li><li>to pelt <i>(with)</i></li><li>to shower <i>(with)</i></li><li>to fill <i>(with)</i>, to fill up <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>заки́д-</i> (zakíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>закида́ть</i> (zakidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to put out feelers, to investigate, to ask around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to hurl <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>to toss, to throw, to tilt</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to put out feelers, to investigate, to ask around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to boil, to be on the boil, to simmer, to bubble</li></ol><p><i>закипе́ть</i> (zakipétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to boil, to be on the boil, to simmer, to bubble</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sour, turn sour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grow rusty/passive/indifferent, to vegetate</li></ol><p><i>заки́снуть</i> (zakísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pawning, mortgaging</li><li>pledge (something pawned)</li><li>stake, wager, bet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookmark (strip used to mark a place in a book)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) bookmark (of a web browser)</li><li>(slang,&#32;recreational drugs) drug cache (to be collected by a client)</li></ol><p>From <i>закла́д(ывать)</i> (zaklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>The computing sense is a semantic loan from English <i>bookmark</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to lay, to place</li><li>to lay the foundation <i>(of)</i>, to lay</li><li>to heap <i>(with)</i>, to pile <i>(with)</i>, to block up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to pawn, to pledge, to mortgage</li><li>to harness, to put a horse, to get ready a carriage</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to betray, to give up <i>(to police)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stabbing to death</li><li>sacrifice (action or object)</li></ol><p><i>закла́ть</i> (zaklátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to stab to death</li><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) to sacrifice by stabbing</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>заклати</i> (zaklati, “to stab”); doublet of the inherited East Slavic word <i>заколо́ть</i> (zakolótʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue up, to stick up, to tape</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to plaster with</li></ol><p><i>закле́ить</i> (zakléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue up, to stick up, to tape</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to plaster with</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stamp, to brand</li><li>to stigmatize</li><li>to label, e.g. to label someone a racist</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>клейми́ть</i> (klejmítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incantation, spell</li></ol><p><i>заклина́ть</i> (zaklinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch, to charm, to cast a spell (over)</li><li>to beseech, to conjure, to adjure, to entreat, to implore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to jam</li><li>to wedge</li></ol><p>From <i>закли́нить</i> (zaklínitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>заклини́ть</i> (zaklinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to jam</li><li>to wedge</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-кли́ни́ть</i> (-klínítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conclude, to close, to end</li><li>to conclude, to infer</li><li>to conclude</li><li>to enclose, to put</li><li>to confine, to imprison, to incarcerate</li></ol><p><i>заключи́ть</i> (zaključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conclude (with, in), to end (with)</li><li>to be concluded, to be signed</li><li>to consist, to be, to lie [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заключа́ть</i> (zaključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confinement, detention, imprisonment</li><li>conclusion (of), concluding (of); signing</li><li>conclusion, inference, resolution</li></ol><p>From <i>заключи́ть</i> (zaključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final, last, ultimate</li><li>closing</li><li>conclusive, concluding</li></ol><p><i>заключи́ть</i> (zaključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conclude, to close, to end</li><li>to conclude, to infer</li><li>to conclude</li><li>to enclose, to put [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li><li>to confine, to imprison, to incarcerate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incarcerated, imprisoned</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner (person incarcerated in a prison)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch, to charm, to cast a spell (over)</li><li>to beseech, conjure, to adjure, to entreat, to implore</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>клясть</i> (kljastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>implacable, sworn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sworn enemy, archenemy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rivet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shackle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shackle</li></ol><p><i>закова́ть</i> (zakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intricate, complicated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to code, to encode, to encrypt</li><li>to give hypnotic/suggestive treatment <i>(for alcoholism, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>коди́ровать</i> (kodírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hammer in</li><li>to nail, to fasten with nails, to board up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to earn, to knock up, to knock down, to pull down</li></ol><p><i>заколоти́ть</i> (zakolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bewitched, enchanted, spellbound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enchant, to bewitch, to cast a spell <i>(on)</i></li><li>to charm, to spellbind, to fascinate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>колдова́ть</i> (koldovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enchant, to bewitch, to cast a spell <i>(on)</i></li><li>to charm, to spellbind, to fascinate</li></ol><p><i>заколдова́ть</i> (zakoldovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to begin to shake, to begin to rock/sway</li><li>(slang,&#32;rude) to torment, to pester, to strongly annoy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>колеба́ть</i> (kolebátʹ)</p><p>Same as above; but in this sense, a euphemism for заеба́ть (zajebátʹ). See also задолба́ть (zadolbátʹ) and задра́ть (zadrátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start swaying, oscillating</li><li>to start doubting</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become unstable</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) to become tired and irritated from repeated unsuccessful attempts at something</li></ol><p><i>заколеба́ть</i> (zakolebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrette (a clasp or clip for gathering and holding the hair)</li></ol><p><i>заколо́ть</i> (zakolótʹ, “to attach with a pin”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hammer in</li><li>to nail, to fasten with nails, to board up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to earn, to knock up, to knock down, to pull down</li><li>to begin to beat, to strike</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill by stabbing</li><li>to slaughter (an animal)</li><li>to attach with a pin</li></ol><p>East Slavic word inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zakolti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ). Doublet of <i>закла́ть</i> (zaklátʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) law</li><li>rule</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zakonъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sod's law, Murphy's law</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) legal expert, jurist</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strict observer of the law</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) thief in law</li><li>(dated,&#32;inanimate) legal code</li></ol><p><i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>legally, lawfully (as permitted by law)</li></ol><p><i>зако́нный</i> (zakónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legality, lawfulness, legitimacy</li><li>rule of law</li></ol><p><i>зако́нный</i> (zakónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawful, legal (conforming to or recognised by law or rules)</li></ol><p>From <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawmaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>legislatively</li></ol><p><i>законода́тельный</i> (zakonodátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legislative</li></ol><p><i>законода́тель</i> (zakonodátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legislation</li></ol><p><i>законода́тель</i> (zakonodátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regularity, pattern, rule; appropriateness; objective laws</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Gesetzmäßigkeit</i>. By surface analysis, <i>закономе́рный</i> (zakonomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). First attested in 1900, strongly associated with jurisdiction and later extending into general usage.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regular, appropriate, natural, in accordance with the laws (e.g. of nature)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>gesetzmäßig</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рный</i> (mérnyj). Known at least since the mid-19th century, per Anikin initially strongly associated with jurisdiction and later extending into general usage.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caulk, to calk</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>конопа́тить</i> (konopátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>law-abiding</li></ol><p><i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>послу́шный</i> (poslúšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bill (draft of a law)</li></ol><p><i>зако́н</i> (zakón, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прое́кт</i> (proékt, “project”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawmaking</li></ol><p><i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тво́рчество</i> (tvórčestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve, to can (food)</li><li>to preserve (<i>through special treatment, e.g. wood, a body, archaeological items, etc.</i>)</li><li>to temporarily suspend (an activity)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>консерви́ровать</i> (konservírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness</li><li>virtuosity, state of being accomplished (referring to a person)</li></ol><p><i>зако́нченный</i> (zakónčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complete</li><li>accomplished</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end</li><li>to complete (work, lessons, etc.)</li><li>to conclude</li><li>to close (put an end to)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end, to be over</li></ol><p><i>зако́нчить</i> (zakónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>зако́н</i> (zakón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury, to dig <i>(into)</i></li><li>to fill up <i>(with earth)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to smoke</li><li>to smoke, to cure by smoking</li><li>to blacken with smoke, to make sooty</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>копти́ть</i> (koptítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) a dug-up ground, exploratory dig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfeed; to bring harm by overfeeding</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squiggle, twirl, curlicue, flourish, hook</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) snag, hitch</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) trick</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>корю́ка</i> (korjúka, “hook”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Per Vasmer, <i>корю́ка</i> (korjúka) is expressively derived from <i>крюк</i> (krjuk, “hook”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be rigid or inflexible</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>косный</i> (kosnyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alleyway, (narrow) lane; nook; backstreet</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ко-</i> (ko-) +&lrm; <i>у́лица</i> (úlica) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Akin to Russian <i>зау́лок</i> (zaúlok), Polish <i>zaułek</i>, and with another prefix to Russian <i>переу́лок</i> (pereúlok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become numb from the cold</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кочене́ть</i> (kočenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint over, to paint out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to blush, to start to redden (of a person etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>красне́ть</i> (krasnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) fixer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to secure, to fix, to attach, to mount, to anchor</li><li>to consolidate, to strengthen</li><li>to allot</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consolidate one's grip <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>закрепи́ть</i> (zakrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastening, attaching; strengthening; securing</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) fixing</li></ol><p><i>закрепи́ть</i> (zakrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to secure, to fix, to attach, to mount, to anchor</li><li>to consolidate, to strengthen</li><li>to allot</li></ol><p><i>закрепи́ть</i> (zakrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consolidate one's grip <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>закрепля́ть</i> (zakrepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shout, to cry out, to give a shout</li><li>to shout out, to cry out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain bin</li></ol><p><i>за́-</i> (zá-) +&lrm; <i>кром</i> (krom, “kremlin, fortress”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to be round (e.g. on the end)</li><li>to be made round (e.g. on the end), to be made rounded off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to round off (to begin to be done with some action, to prepare an end on some task)</li></ol><p><i>закругля́ть</i> (zakrugljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Related to <i>круг</i> (krug, “a circle”). The colloquial sense (used at least since the late 1950-s) may be a calque from some other language: compare English <i>round off</i> and Swedish <i>avrunda</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rounded, roundish, orbed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to turn/whirl, to send whirling</li><li>to make dizzy, to sweep off one's feet</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кружи́ть</i> (kružítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to whirl/spin, to begin to go round</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be run off one's feet, to be in a whirl</li></ol><p><i>закружи́ть</i> (zakružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twirl, to twist, to wind around</li><li><i>(tap etc)</i> to turn off, <i>(nut etc)</i> to turn tight, to tighten <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to become twisted</li><li>to become screwed up</li><li>to begin to turn</li><li>to be tied up with one's affairs, to be bound up in one's affairs</li></ol><p><i>закрути́ть</i> (zakrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tourniquet</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) twist</li></ol><p><i>закрути́ть</i> (zakrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twirl, to twist, to wind round</li><li><i>(tap etc)</i> to turn off, <i>(nut etc)</i> to turn tight, to tighten <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>закрути́ть</i> (zakrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to become twisted</li><li>to become screwed up</li></ol><p><i>закру́чивать</i> (zakrúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to hide</li><li>to close, to shut</li></ol><p>From <i>закры́ть</i> (zakrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>закрыва́ць</i> (zakryvácʹ), Ukrainian <i>закрива́ти</i> (zakryváty), Polish <i>zakrywać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to close, to shut</li></ol><p><i>закрыва́ть</i> (zakryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) flap</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>closing, shutting</li><li>close, end, finish</li></ol><p><i>закры́ть</i> (zakrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of being closed, closedness (<i>e.g. of a society</i>)</li></ol><p><i>закры́тый</i> (zakrýtyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>closed</li><li>secret</li><li>boarding (<i>school</i>)</li><li>high-necked (<i>dress</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to hide</li><li>to close, to shut</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to lock up, put away (put in jail)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>закры́ць</i> (zakrýcʹ), Ukrainian <i>закри́ти</i> (zakrýty), Polish <i>zakryć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to close, to shut</li></ol><p><i>закры́ть</i> (zakrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>backstage, behind-the-scenes</li><li>offstage</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кули́са</i> (kulísa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, to buy in, to buy up</li><li>to lay in a stock <i>(of)</i></li><li>to bathe <i>(till/until)</i></li></ol><p><i>закупи́ть</i> (zakupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stock up (on), to buy a bunch (of), to buy a (whole) load (of) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to bathe (oneself) <i>(till/until)</i>, to bathe for too long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, to purchase (a large amount)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stock up (on), to buy a bunch (of), to buy a (whole) load (of) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>закупи́ть</i> (zakupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purchase, buying, procurement (usually in bulk or large quantities)</li></ol><p><i>закупи́ть</i> (zakupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plug, to cork, to clog</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to block</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to strand</li></ol><p><i>заку́порить</i> (zakúporitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plug, to cork, to clog</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to block</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to strand</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ку́порить</i> (kúporitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockage, clot</li><li>occlusion, vascular occlusion, thrombosis</li></ol><p><i>заку́пор(ить)</i> (zakúpor(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) purchasing</li></ol><p><i>заку́пка</i> (zakúpka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light <i>(up)</i> <i>(a cigarette [pipe])</i></li></ol><p><i>закури́ть</i> (zakurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start smoking, to light up</li><li>to become a smoker, to take up smoking</li><li>to smoke something through (e.g. make a room full of (cigarette) smoke)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li><li>to have a snack, to get a bite, to eat</li><li><i>(eating)</i> to take <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snack, bite</li><li>hors d'oeuvres, appetizer</li></ol><p>From <i>закуси́ть</i> (zakusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) snack, bite (to eat)</li></ol><p><i>закуси́ть</i> (zakusítʹ) or <i>заку́ска</i> (zakúska) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>заку́ска</i> (zakúska): (small) snack, hors d'oeuvres, appetizer, zakuska</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snack bar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li><li>to have a snack, to get a bite, to eat</li><li>(<i>eating</i>) to take <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>закуси́ть</i> (zakusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to wrap up, to tuck <i>(in bed)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ку́тать</i> (kútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle/wrap oneself up</li></ol><p><i>заку́тать</i> (zakútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nook (hidden or secluded spot)</li><li>(a little) corner, nook</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) diminutive of <i>заку́т</i> (zakút)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫtъ</i>. Compare Polish <i>zakątek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to wrap up, to tuck <i>(in bed)</i></li></ol><p><i>заку́тать</i> (zakútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle/wrap oneself up</li></ol><p><i>заку́тывать</i> (zakútyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) zakat (almsgiving, one of the five pillars of Islam)</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish زكات (zeḱat), from Arabic زَكَاة (zakāh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hall</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) living room</li><li>clipping of <i>тренажёрный зал</i> (trenažórnyj zal, “gym”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Saal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waiting room, lounge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hall, large room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to keep talking <i>(about)</i>, to harp <i>(on/about)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly in the negative) to go right, to work out</li></ol><p><i>зала́дить</i> (zaláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break <i>(by bending)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заломи́ть</i> (zalomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to patch, to mend by patching</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лата́ть</i> (latátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to bark, to give tongue</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ла́ять</i> (lájatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedding, hibernation</li><li>becoming ingrained</li><li>underlying, location (<i>at the bottom</i>)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) bedding (location of rocks, ores, etc. in the earth's crust)</li></ol><p><i>залега́ть</i> (zalegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) to lie, to bed, to be deposited, to be bedded</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-лега́ть</i> (-legátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn to ice, to freeze</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ледене́ть</i> (ledenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down for too long; to be in bed for too long</li><li>to lay unused or unsold</li><li>to spoil from being stored for too long (<i>e.g. of agricultural products</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) deposit, accumulation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>залега́ть</i> (zalegátʹ, “to lie, to be deposited”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb up</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to get into, to scramble</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>зале́зть</i> (zaléztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb up (finished action)</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to get into, to scramble</li><li>to meddle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close up <i>(with)</i>, to stick up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to glue up <i>(with)</i>, to paste up, to paste over <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close up <i>(with)</i>, to stick up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to glue up <i>(with)</i>, to paste up, to paste over <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal/mete out <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>залепи́ть</i> (zalepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>to fly <i>(as high as)</i>, to go up <i>(as far as)</i>, to fly <i>(as far as)</i>, to fly <i>(behind, beyond)</i></li><li>to land <i>(somewhere)</i> on the way, to make a stopover <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get into a fix, to get into a mess, to get something one doesn't want <i>(e.g. contract a sexually transmitted disease or become pregnant)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to fly</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>to fly <i>(as high as)</i>, to go up <i>(as far as)</i>, to fly <i>(as far as)</i>, to fly <i>(behind, beyond)</i></li><li>to land <i>(somewhere)</i> on the way, to make a stopover <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get into a fix, to get into a mess, to get something one doesn't want <i>(i.e. contract a sexually transmitted disease or become pregnant)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie (down)</li><li>to lie in hiding [in wait]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to drop flat</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulf</li><li>bay</li></ol><p>From the vb. <i>лить</i> (litʹ, “to pour, shed, cast”), ultimately from PIE <i>&#42;h₂leybʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>lībō</i> (“to sprinkle, spill”) and Ancient Greek <i>ἀλείφω</i> (aleíphō, “to anoint”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood (in terms of bodies of water)</li><li>to fill up (with liquid)</li><li>to pour on, to spill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie, tell lies</li><li>to upload, to put up</li></ol><p><i>зали́ть</i> (zalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink underwater</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to laugh loudly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filling <i>(with)</i>, pouring <i>(with)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>floodable; flooded</li><li>used for filling</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) jellied; cooked in jelly</li><li>(linguistics,&#32;phonetics) sonorous</li></ol><p><i>залив-</i> (zaliv-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick one's wounds (to withdraw temporarily while recovering from a defeat&#59; to recuperate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) addictive, addicting, engrossing, satisfying</li></ol><p><i>залипа́ть</i> (zalipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become sticky; to get covered in a sticky substance</li><li>to get stuck (<i>e.g. of a gummed up mechanism</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to freeze in rapt attention or deep thought</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be engrossed in; to be obsessed with; to be (<i>figuratively</i>) addicted to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a subject’</small> <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional <i>or</i> accusative) <small>‘a gadget, a platform, social media’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>зали́пнуть</i> (zalípnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become sticky; to get covered in a sticky substance</li><li>to stick together</li><li>to get stuck</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to freeze in rapt attention or deep thought</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be engrossed in; to be obsessed with; to be (<i>figuratively</i>) addicted to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a subject’</small> <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional <i>or</i> accusative) <small>‘a gadget, a platform, social media’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ли́пнуть</i> (lípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood (in terms of bodies of water)</li><li>to fill up (with liquid)</li><li>to pour on, to spill</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) to upload</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink underwater</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to laugh loudly</li></ol><p><i>зали́ть</i> (zalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>зали́ть</i> (zalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pawning, pawn</li><li>pledge, deposit, security, bail</li><li>collateral</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) voice (a particular mode of inflecting or conjugating verbs, or a particular form of a verb)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) virgin land (land that has never been plowed)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) abandoned land</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zalogъ</i>.</p><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>διάθεσις</i> (diáthesis, “disposition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pledge, deposit, security</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) collateral</li><li>(relational,&#32;grammar) voice</li><li>(relational,&#32;dialectal) virgin land</li><li>(relational,&#32;dialectal) abandoned land</li></ol><p><i>зало́г</i> (zalóg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stuffy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to lay, to place</li><li>to lay the foundation <i>(of)</i>, to lay</li><li>to heap <i>(with)</i>, to pile <i>(with)</i>, to block up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to pawn, to pledge, to mortgage</li><li>to harness, to put a horse, to get ready a carriage</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to betray, to give up <i>(to police)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostage</li></ol><p><i>заложи́ть</i> (založítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), related to <i>зало́г</i> (zalóg, “pledge, deposit, security”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crease (<i>in paper or film</i>)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) prepared black-spined herring (<i>Alosa kessleri</i>)</li><li>blockage in a river (<i>caused by logs, ice floes, etc.</i>)</li><li>the accumulation of ice floes on the shoals near the sea coast</li><li>a type of witchcraft performed by tying grains together</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заломи́ть</i> (zalomítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvo, volley</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Salve</i>, from Latin <i>salve</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) dickhead, bell-end (body part)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) an unpleasant person, bell-end, dickhead</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) nothing, fuck all</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>залупи́ть</i> (zalupítʹ), <i>за-</i> (za-) + <i>лупи́ть</i> (lupítʹ), cf. <i>облупи́ть</i> (oblupítʹ), <i>облу́пленный</i> (oblúplennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) quarrelsome</li></ol><p><i>залу́па</i> (zalúpa) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to pick a quarrel</li></ol><p><i>залу́па</i> (zalúpa) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>задира́ться</i> (zadirátʹsja), <i>задира́ть</i> (zadirátʹ), <i>зади́ра</i> (zadíra), <i>вздо́рный</i> (vzdórnyj) (akin to <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) quarrelsome</li></ol><p><i>залу́па</i> (zalúpa) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bald patch above the side of the forehead and the temple, high temples</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare in an admiring fashion [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘at something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>любова́ться</i> (ljubovátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slap, to splotch, to daub (paint on something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ля́пать</i> (ljápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slap, to splotch, to daub (paint on something)</li></ol><p><i>заля́пать</i> (zaljápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down for too long; to be in bed for too long</li><li>to lay unused or unsold</li><li>to spoil from being stored for too long (<i>e.g. of agricultural products</i>)</li></ol><p><i>залежа́ться</i> (zaležátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy, assistant</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>замести́тель</i> (zamestítelʹ, “deputy, assistant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>замести́тель</i> (zamestítelʹ, “substitute, deputy”)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) abbreviation of <i>замыка́тель</i> (zamykátelʹ, “(electrical) switch; contactor; locking mechanism”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint over</li><li>to stick with putty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to daub over, to make dirty, to stain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to efface, to erase</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putty, mastic</li><li><i>(colloquial)</i> correction fluid</li></ol><p><i>зама́зать</i> (zamázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint over</li><li>to stick with putty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to daub over, to make dirty, to stain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to efface, to slur over</li></ol><p><i>зама́зать</i> (zamázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal, to keep secret, to cover up, to slur over, to veil, to hush up, to suppress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enticement</li></ol><p><i>зама́нивать</i> (zamánivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, to lure, to allure, to decoy</li></ol><p><i>замани́ть</i> (zamanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, to lure, to allure, to decoy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tempting, alluring, beguiling (having the power to allure)</li><li>attractive</li></ol><p><i>замани́ть</i> (zamanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil, to dirty, to pollute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defame, to disgrace</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мара́ть</i> (marátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) dirty person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marinate, to pickle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>маринова́ть</i> (marinovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil, to dirty, to pollute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defame, to disgrace</li></ol><p><i>замара́ть</i> (zamarátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>masked</li><li>disguised, hidden, secret</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mask, to disguise</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to camouflage</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>маскирова́ть</i> (maskirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on a mask</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to camouflage oneself</li><li>to dress up (wear a costume) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘as someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>замаскирова́ть</i> (zamaskirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, to fold up</li><li>to twist <i>(round)</i>, to wind <i>(round)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out, to tire out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to withhold, to refuse to give, to appropriate, to help oneself, to hook</li></ol><p><i>замота́ть</i> (zamotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) cast</li><li>(<i>fencing</i>) moulinet</li><li>(<i>soccer</i>) shooting feint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to wave, to flap</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>маха́ть</i> (maxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten <i>(someone with something)</i>, to raise <i>(threateningly)</i> <i>(something at someone)</i></li><li>to venture, to make a bold push <i>(for)</i>, to make a stab <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>замахну́ться</i> (zamaxnútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten <i>(someone with something)</i>, to raise <i>(threateningly)</i> <i>(something at someone)</i></li><li>to venture, to make a bold push <i>(for)</i>, to make a stab <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soaking, presoak, steeping, maceration (immersing an object in water and allowing it to become saturated, e.g. in order to soften or clean it)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) knocking off, murdering</li></ol><p><i>зама́чивать</i> (zamáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet</li><li>to soak, to steep</li></ol><p><i>замочи́ть</i> (zamočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) habit, manners</li><li>the male hemp plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to loom, to appear in the distance</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мая́чить</i> (majáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Zambian</li></ol><p><i>За́мбия</i> (Zámbija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy chief, deputy head</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель главы́</i> (zamestítelʹ glavý).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy director, deputy manager</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель дире́ктора</i> (zamestítelʹ diréktora).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowing down, deceleration; delay</li></ol><p><i>заме́длить</i> (zamédlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, delayed</li><li>slow-motion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slow down, to decelerate</li><li>to hold back, to delay, to retard</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ме́длить</i> (médlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become slower, to slow down</li><li>to be delayed, to be drawn out</li></ol><p><i>заме́длить</i> (zamédlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slow down, to decelerate</li><li>to hold back, to delay, to retard</li></ol><p><i>заме́длить</i> (zamédlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become slower, to slow down</li><li>to be delayed, to be drawn out</li></ol><p><i>замедля́ть</i> (zamedljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start flickering, to start flashing, to appear briefly</li><li>to start to quickly follow in sequence</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мелька́ть</i> (melʹkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>replacement, substitution</li><li>substitute</li><li>(<i>law</i>) commutation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaměna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitute (product)</li></ol><p><i>замени́ть</i> (zamenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to replace, to substitute</li><li>to commute</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to replace, to substitute</li><li>to commute</li></ol><p><i>замени́ть</i> (zamenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measurement, metering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand motionless, to freeze</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to come to a standstill</li><li>to die down (of a sound)</li><li>to sink (of the heart)</li><li>to falter (of the voice)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezing</li><li>freezing to death</li></ol><p><i>замерза́ть</i> (zamerzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) to freeze</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to freeze, to congeal</li><li>to freeze to death</li></ol><p><i>замёрзнуть</i> (zamjórznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as if dead, in a dead faint, unconscious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure</li></ol><p><i>заме́рить</i> (zaméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mash (a mass of mixed ingredients)</li><li>fight (a physical confrontation between people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead, to puddle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>меси́ть</i> (mesítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep <i>(up)</i></li><li><i>(with snow, sand etc.)</i> to cover, to drift <i>(against)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;passive voice,&#32;only in third person) to seize, to pinch, to run in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover one's tracks</li></ol><p>Literally, “sweep up (one's) tracks”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitute, replacement</li><li>deputy, assistant, vice-</li></ol><p><i>замести́ть</i> (zamestítʹ, “to deputize for, replace, substitute for”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substitute <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;nonstandard,&#32;proscribed) instead of</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto, “place”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep <i>(up)</i></li><li><i>(with snow, sand etc.)</i> to cover, to drift <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to seize, to pinch, to run in, to arrest</li><li>to baste, to tack, to sew up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shower, to bombard</li><li>to start throwing</li></ol><p><i>замести́</i> (zamestí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to baste”)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw, to cast”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover one's tracks</li></ol><p>Literally, “sweep up (one's) tracks”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to rush about</li><li>to begin to toss</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ться</i> (metátʹsja)</p><p><i>замета́ть</i> (zametátʹ, “to cover, to sweep, to seize”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>замета́ть</i> (zametátʹ, “to baste, to sew up”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>замета́ть</i> (zametátʹ, “to throw, to start throwing”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notice, to remark, to note, to observe</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manifest, to appear</li></ol><p><i>заме́тить</i> (zamétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>note (memorandum)</li><li>a short article, item</li></ol><p><i>заме́тить</i> (zamétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>заме́тный</i> (zamétnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>заме́тно</i> (zamétno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>noticeably, perceptibly, visibly (capable of being seen or noticed)</li><li>markedly, conspicuously</li><li>outstandingly</li></ol><p><i>заме́тный</i> (zamétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noticeable, perceptible, visible (capable of being seen or noticed)</li><li>marked, conspicuous</li><li>outstanding</li></ol><p><i>заме́тить</i> (zamétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remark, observation, criticism</li><li>reproof, rebuke, reprimand, talking-to, telling-off</li></ol><p><i>замеча́ть</i> (zamečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>remarkably</li></ol><p><i>замеча́тельный</i> (zamečátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remarkable, outstanding, marvellous, wonderful</li><li>notable</li></ol><p><i>замеча́ть</i> (zamečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) Famous equations consisting of limits; (<i>term is common among Russian-speakers</i>).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notice, to remark, to note, to observe</li></ol><p><i>заме́тить</i> (zamétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manifest, to appear</li></ol><p><i>замеча́ть</i> (zamečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>involved in, implicated in, entangled in (a crime etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perplexity (quality of being confused or puzzled)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mix together, to form a liquid solution, suspension or emulsion</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to involve in, to entangle in (a crime, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get involved (mixed up) <i>(in)</i>, to entangle <i>(in)</i></li><li>to mix, to knead</li></ol><p><i>замеша́ть</i> (zamešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>замеси́ть</i> (zamesítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tarry, to loiter, to linger, to be late</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ме́шкать</i> (méškatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act <i>(for sb)</i>, to deputize <i>(for sb)</i>, to substitute <i>(for sb)</i>, to replace <i>(sb)</i></li><li>to substitute <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to replace</li></ol><p><i>замести́ть</i> (zamestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitution; replacement; displacement</li></ol><p><i>замести́ть</i> (zamestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to blink</li><li>to begin to wink</li><li>to begin to twinkle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мига́ть</i> (migátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, to rumple, to crumple</li><li>to hush up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-мина́ть</i> (-minátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to mine (to lay mines in)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to defecate somewhere</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мини́ровать</i> (minírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hitch, (short) delay</li><li>hesitation (in speech)</li><li>cool down exercise</li></ol><p><i>замина́ть</i> (zaminátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dying down, fading</li></ol><p><i>замира́ть</i> (zamirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand motionless, to freeze</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to come to a standstill</li><li>to die down (of a sound)</li><li>to sink (of the heart)</li><li>to falter (of the voice)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pacify, to reconcile</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsociability; reticence</li><li>insularity</li><li>completeness (of a circuit)</li></ol><p><i>за́мкнутый</i> (zámknutyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reserved</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tight-lipped</li><li>secluded, isolated</li><li>exclusive</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;electricity) closed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vicious circle (a situation in which the solution to one problem creates a chain of problems)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close (by connecting)</li><li>to surround</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) to create a closed circuit, to short-circuit</li><li>to lock</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-мкнуть</i> (-mknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock, to be locked</li><li>to be closed, to close <i>(up)</i></li><li>to become/be sullen/withdrawn</li></ol><p><i>замкну́ть</i> (zamknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy minister; vice-minister, undersecretary</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель мини́стра</i> (zamestítelʹ minístra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy head, deputy chief</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель нача́льника</i> (zamestítelʹ načálʹnika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(voice)</i> sepulchral, hollow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castle</li><li>lock (fastener that opens with a key, or part of a firearm)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) keystone</li><li>(<i>jewelry</i>) clasp, fastener</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>zamek</i>, which is a calque via Czech <i>zámek</i> of Middle High German <i>sloz</i> (“lock, keep”), which, in turn, is a calque of Latin <i>clūsa</i> (“lock, fort, fortification”). Attested from the 17th century. Related to замыкать (zamykatʹ) (замкну́ть (zamknútʹ)). But note also Old Novgorodian <i>ꙁамъке</i> (zamŭke, “lock”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zamъkъ</i>, deverbal from <i>&#42;zamъknǫti</i> (whence Russian <i>замкну́ть</i> (zamknútʹ)). Doublet of <i>за́мок</i> (zámok), a borrowing from Polish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to find no one at home</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>pocałować klamkę</i>; compare also French <i>trouver porte close</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get soaked</li><li>to stop leaking (<i>due to swelling from moisture; of wooden vessels</i>)</li></ol><p><i>замо́кнуть</i> (zamóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get soaked</li><li>to stop leaking (<i>due to swelling from moisture; of wooden vessels</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мо́кнуть</i> (móknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become / fall silent, to stop / cease (speaking / talking), to break off</li></ol><p><i>замо́лкнуть</i> (zamólknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become / fall silent, to stop / cease (speaking / talking), to break off</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лкнуть</i> (mólknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become / fall silent, to stop / cease (speaking / talking), to break off</li><li>to conceal, to keep secret, to cover up, to slur over, to veil, to hush up, to suppress</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass death of fish with lack of oxygen or contaminated water</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>замори́ть</i> (zamorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezing, icing, gelation, frost <i>(process)</i></li></ol><p><i>замора́живать</i> (zamoráživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to freeze</li><li>(meteorology,&#32;rare,&#32;of&#32;weather,&#32;impersonally) to freeze</li></ol><p><i>заморо́зить</i> (zamorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to mislead, to fool, to confuse</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to distract, to divert attention (with conversations, requests, remarks, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>заморо́чить</i> (zamoróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bother (with), to go through the trouble (of getting) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get hung up (on), to get wrapped up (about), to worry (about), to obsess (over), to overthink, to beat oneself up (over) [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>по по́воду</b> (po póvodu, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>насчёт</b> (nasčót, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional)]</li></ol><p><i>замора́чивать</i> (zamoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to blink, to begin to wink</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>морга́ть</i> (morgátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tire out, to overwork, to underfeed, to give short rations</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мори́ть</i> (morítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a snack, take a bite, pick a mouthful, stay one's stomach, still one's hunger, stop the rumbling</li></ol><p>Literally, “to kill a little worm”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to freeze</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(morning/night) frost, light frost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) overnight freeze, cold snap <i>(usually in late spring or early autumn, when days are warm)</i></li></ol><p><i>заморо́зить</i> (zamorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mislead, to fool, to confuse</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>моро́чить</i> (moróčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to get tired while doing something</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to take care of something in advance, and also to get carried away with something</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bother (with), to go through the trouble (of getting) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get hung up (on), to get wrapped up (about), to worry (about), to obsess (over), to overthink, to beat oneself up (over) [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>по по́воду</b> (po póvodu, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>насчёт</b> (nasčót, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional)]</li></ol><p><i>заморо́чить</i> (zamoróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) unclear situation, (unforeseen) trouble; hurdle; difficulty</li></ol><p><i>заморо́чить</i> (zamoróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) overseas, foreign</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мо́ре</i> (móre) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) starveling; scallywag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starving, emaciated</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) exhausted, worn-out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, to fold up</li><li>to twist <i>(round)</i>, to wind <i>(round)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out, to tire out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to withhold, to refuse to give, to appropriate, to help oneself, to hook</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tie up, to bind up</li><li>to shake, to begin to shake</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;religious slur,&#32;neologism) niqabi or hijabi, (usually female) raghead</li></ol><p><i>замота́ть</i> (zamotátʹ, “to roll up, to fold”) +&lrm; <i>-ашка</i> (-aška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>замо́к</i> (zamók, “lock”)</li></ol><p><i>замо́к</i> (zamók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet</li><li>to soak, to steep</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to wet, to duppy, to do in, to bump off, to polish off, to take for a ride (to murder)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keyhole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lock</li></ol><p><i>замо́к</i> (zamók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;Soviet Union) deputy commander for political affairs</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель команди́ра по полити́ческой рабо́те</i> (zamestítelʹ komandíra po politíčeskoj rabóte).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice chairman</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>заместитель председателя</i> (zamestitelʹ predsedatelja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deputy chairman, vice-chairman</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>замести́тель председа́теля</i> (zamestítelʹ predsedátelja, “deputy chairman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>married (<i>to a man only</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>за</i> (za, “behind”) +&lrm; <i>муж</i> (muž, “husband”), literally “behind the husband”. The form <i>муж</i> (muž) is used instead of the modern accusative case, <i>мужа</i> (muža), because <i>за́муж</i> (zámuž) fossilized well before the masculine, animate accusative case had been suppleted by the genitive.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>married (<i>to a husband only</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за́ мужем</i> (zá mužem)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage (especially from a woman's perspective)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>married (<i>to a man</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за́муж</i> (zámuž) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal (a hole or opening with masonry)</li><li>to immure (to cloister someone behind walls, or to hide something inside of a wall)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to prevent (someone) from freely communicating or moving</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мурова́ть</i> (murovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal (a hole or opening with masonry)</li><li>to immure (to cloister someone behind walls, or to hide something inside of a wall)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to prevent (someone) from freely communicating or moving</li></ol><p><i>замурова́ть</i> (zamurovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, to make turbid</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мути́ть</i> (mutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>заму́чить</i> (zamúčitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>му́чать</i> (múčatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>заму́читься</i> (zamúčitʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>заму́чать</i> (zamúčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torment</li></ol><p><i>заму́чить</i> (zamúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torture, to torment</li><li>to tire out, to wear out, to bore, to weary</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agonize, to be exhausted, to distress oneself, to be fed up, to be worn out</li></ol><p><i>заму́чить</i> (zamúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suede, chamois leather</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>zamsz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) suede</li><li>suedelike</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) soft</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) insinuating</li></ol><p><i>за́мша</i> (zámša) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mossy, moss-grown</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) moss-grown, old-fashioned; set in one's ways</li></ol><p><i>замше́ть</i> (zamšétʹ, “to become mossy”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become mossy, to become covered or overgrown with moss</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мох</i> (mox) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locking, closing; closure</li></ol><p><i>замыка́ть</i> (zamykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock</li><li>to close (by connecting)</li><li>to go last</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to torment or exhaust by bossing around</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start mooing; to start moaning</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мы́кать</i> (mýkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock, to be locked</li><li>to be closed, to close <i>(up)</i></li><li>to become/be sullen/withdrawn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be tormented or exhausted by being bossed around</li></ol><p><i>замыка́ть</i> (zamykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>замы́кать</i> (zamýkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intention, plan, design, scheme</li><li>conception, idea</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to think up, to imagine, to conceive, to plan</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intricate, ingenious, complicated, complex</li></ol><p><i>замы́слить</i> (zamýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start mooing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start mumbling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мыча́ть</i> (myčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to think up, to imagine, to conceive, to plan</li></ol><p><i>замы́слить</i> (zamýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>audio</i>) to mute (silence a soundtrack)</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to mute (disable a user's ability to communicate or be seen in a feed)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) + a derivation from English <i>mute</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, to rumple, to crumple</li><li>to hush up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мя́ть</i> (mjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to falter, to become confused, to stop short <i>(in confusion)</i></li></ol><p><i>замя́ть</i> (zamjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) to freeze</li><li>to freeze, to congeal</li><li>to be cold, to freeze, to feel cold</li><li>to freeze to death</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>мёрзнуть</i> (mjórznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) casting (<i>of a fishing net</i>)</li><li>(local,&#32;dialect) a pile of something swept together</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) folding leather top (<i>e.g., of a carriage</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baste, to tack, to sew up</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to shower, to bombard</li></ol><p><i>замета́ть</i> (zametátʹ, “to sew up, to baste”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>замета́ть</i> (zametátʹ, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain (in a theater that hides the stage from view)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>наве́с</i> (navés, “awning, shed”). See also <i>заве́са</i> (zavésa, “curtain, veil”) and <i>за́навесь</i> (zánavesʹ, “(window) curtain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curtain</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>наве́сить</i> (navésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain <i>(piece of cloth acting as a barrier)</i></li></ol><p><i>за́навес</i> (zánaves) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(window) curtain</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) (theater) curtain</li></ol><p>From <i>занаве́сить</i> (zanavésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), with expected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curtain</li></ol><p><i>занаве́сить</i> (zanavésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) stash (criminal or otherwise)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nest egg</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) spike shoved into a scale to reduce weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dirty by wearing a long time</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to carry, to bring (somewhere) (<i>in passing; repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>заноси́ть</i> (zanosítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>заноси́ть</i> (zanosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get dirty and frayed from being worn a long time</li></ol><p><i>зана́шивать</i> (zanášivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zangid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Zangid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall ill</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start getting nervous</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>не́рвничать</i> (nérvničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to carry (<i>in</i>)</li><li>to note down, to enter (data, records)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to skid</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to become covered (<i>with</i>) (e.g. snow, dust, etc.)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;by extension,&#32;colloquial) to get carried away (say something excessive in excitement) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘who’</small>]</li><li>to lift, to raise (an arm, a leg, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put on airs, to become arrogant, to become conceited</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get carried away, to get swept up (<i>especially in thoughts or dreams</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exaggerate, to shade the truth</li><li>to begin to lay eggs (<i>of birds</i>)</li></ol><p><i>занести́</i> (zanestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>нести́сь</i> (nestísʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understate, to put too low, to underestimate</li></ol><p><i>зани́зить</i> (zanízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understatement (<i>of a quantity</i>), underestimation</li></ol><p><i>зани́зить</i> (zanízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understate, to put too low, to underestimate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-ни́зить</i> (-nízitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>entertaining, diverting, amusing</li></ol><p><i>занима́ть</i> (zanimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to borrow</li><li>to occupy, to take up</li><li>to employ, to busy</li><li>to reserve, to secure, to keep</li><li>to interest, to engross</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to learn [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to be occupied with, to engage in [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>занима́ть</i> (zanimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew, freshly, again (usually in a different way to before)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splinter <i>(stuck in a body)</i></li><li>thorn in one's side, nag</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>нозить</i> (nozitʹ), further, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nьziti</i>. Cognate with <i>вонзи́ть</i> (vonzítʹ), <i>пронзи́ть</i> (pronzítʹ) and <i>нож</i> (nož).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drift, drifting snow</li><li><i>car</i> skidding, sideslip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to carry (somewhere) (<i>in passing</i>)</li><li>to note down, to enter (data, records)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause to skid</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause to become covered (<i>with</i>) (e.g. snow, dust, etc.)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;by extension,&#32;colloquial) to get carried away (say something excessive in excitement) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘who’</small>]</li><li>to lift, to raise (arm, leg, etc.)</li><li>to dirty by wearing a long time</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put on airs, to become arrogant, to become conceited</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get carried away, to get swept up (<i>especially in thoughts or dreams</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exaggerate, to shade the truth</li><li>to get dirty and frayed from being worn a long time</li></ol><p><i>заноси́ть</i> (zanosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance, insolence, self-conceit, presumption</li></ol><p><i>зано́счивый</i> (zanósčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, presumptuous, haughty</li></ol><p><i>заноси́ть</i> (zanosítʹ, “to lift (one's nose?)”) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay the night, to spend the night, to stay overnight</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ночева́ть</i> (nočevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn, worn out, threadbare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bore, spoilsport, pain in the ass</li><li>nerd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to number, to enumerate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>нумерова́ть</i> (numerovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (begin to) ache/hurt</li><li>to (begin to) whine</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ныть</i> (nytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occupation, work, business (activity or task with which one occupies oneself)</li><li>exercise</li><li>lesson, lecture</li><li>(<i>military</i>) capture</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заня́ть</i> (zanjátʹ), probably calque of French <i>occupation</i>. Cf. Polish <i>zajęcie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) interesting, entertaining, amusing</li></ol><p><i>заня́ть</i> (zanjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busy (doing a great deal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>employment (rate, status), occupancy</li><li>being busy, pressure of work</li></ol><p><i>за́нятый</i> (zánjatyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busy (doing a great deal)</li><li>occupied, taken, filled (unavailable for use)</li><li>busy, engaged (of a telephone line)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to borrow</li><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) to lend (standard: одолжи́ть)</li><li>to occupy, to take up</li><li>to employ, to busy</li><li>to reserve, to secure, to keep</li><li>to interest, to engross</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to learn</li><li>to be occupied with, to engage in</li></ol><p><i>заня́ть</i> (zanjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) beyond the clouds; transcendental; empyreal; sky-high</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) beyond the clouds, exorbitant, stratospheric, sky-high</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>о́блако</i> (óblako) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in concert, as one</li><li>simultaneously, at the same time, on one's way, while one is at it</li></ol><p><i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>одно́</i> (odnó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transoceanic</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>океа́н</i> (okeán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to shout, to yell, to bawl, to scream</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to start to plow/plough</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infraspinate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ость</i> (ostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to stress, to emphasize</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>остри́ть</i> (ostrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to stress, to emphasize</li></ol><p><i>заостри́ть</i> (zaostrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp, pointed</li><li>acute, pointed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without seeing, sight unseen</li><li>(<i>law</i>) by default, in absentia</li><li><i>(education)</i> by correspondence, extramurally</li></ol><p><i>зао́чный</i> (zaóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance learning (education obtained remotely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(done or said) in someone's absence; (<i>relational</i>) absentee</li><li>by correspondence, remote (e.g. of education), extramural</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>око</i> (oko) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Attested at least since the mid-16th.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>west</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>западъ</i> (zapadŭ, “sunset”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zapadъ</i>, from <i>&#42;pasti</i>. Compare Latin <i>occidens</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Запад)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the West</li></ol><p>From <i>за́пад</i> (západ, “west”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall back, to fall behind, to sink down</li><li>to sink deeply</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to take <i>(to)</i>, to fall <i>(for)</i></li><li>to start to fall</li></ol><p><i>запад-</i> (zapad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) beneath one's dignity, unworthy, shameful</li></ol><p>From <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>па́дло</i> (pádlo), with unexpected stress shift perhaps by analogy with the common slang suffix <i>-ло́</i> (-ló).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) western gull (<i>Larus occidentalis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj, “western”) +&lrm; <i>ча́йка</i> (čájka, “gull”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Westernism</li></ol><p>From <i>за́падный</i> (západnyj, “Western”) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm, “-ism”). The term was introduced by a Soviet philosopher Alexander Zinoviev in 1995.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Westernizer</li></ol><p><i>за́пад</i> (západ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), since the 1840s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Westernism, a 19th-century intellectual ideology which saw Russia's development as dependent upon the adoption of Western European technology and liberal government.</li></ol><p><i>за́пад</i> (západ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo), since the 1840s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>западноевропе́йский</i> (zapadnojevropéjskij)</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>West Asian (person from West Asia)</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>азиа́т</i> (aziát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>West Asian</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>азиа́тский</i> (aziátskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) western American (of the western United States)</li></ol><p>From <i>за́падный</i> (západnyj, “western”) +&lrm; <i>америка́нский</i> (amerikánskij, “American”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Western Armenian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Western Armenian</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>армя́нский</i> (armjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(history,&#32;geopolitics) West German (<i>relating to West Germany, the former state</i>)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) West Germanic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>за́падный</i> (západnyj, “west, western”) +&lrm; <i>герма́нский</i> (germánskij, “German, Germanic”). Compare <i>За́падная Герма́ния</i> (Západnaja Germánija, “West Germany”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Western European</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) West Slavic (relating to the West Slavs)</li></ol><p><i>за́падный</i> (západnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>славя́нский</i> (slavjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Westcentrism</li></ol><p>From <i>западный</i> (zapadnyj, “west”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>центр</i> (centr, “center”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Western</li><li>West</li></ol><p><i>за́пад</i> (západ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap, snare</li></ol><p><i>запа́сть</i> (zapástʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja). Parallel to Polish <i>zapadnia</i>. Akin to Czech <i>past</i> (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pastь</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>Falle</i> (&lt; <i>fallen</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be late, to be behind schedule</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to lag</li></ol><p><i>запозда́ть</i> (zapozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuse, primer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ardor, enthusiasm</li><li>heaves (<i>disease of horses and other domesticated animals</i>)</li><li>spoilage of grain still on the stalk due to drought</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) heat (<i>dangerous situation where failure may lead to the participants getting caught</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>запали́ть</i> (zapalítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to kindle, to set on fire</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drive (a horse) too hard</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to water (an overheated horse)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to throw [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a stone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>запали́ть</i> (zapalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to kindle, to set on fire</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drive (a horse) too hard</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to water (an overheated horse)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;perfective only,&#32;intransitive) to begin to shoot</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to throw [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a stone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignition</li></ol><p><i>запалить</i> (zapalitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quick temper, vehemence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irascible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) to forget</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>па́мятовать</i> (pámjatovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soak in boiling water (instant noodle, tea etc.)</li><li>to wear out (a horse)</li><li>to wear out by beating with a banny venik</li></ol><p><i>запа́рить</i> (zapáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soak in boiling water (instant noodle, tea etc.)</li><li>to wear out (a horse)</li><li>to wear out by beating with a banny venik</li><li>to pester, to annoy</li><li>to beging to glide</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пари́ть</i> (parítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steam, steaming (serving to steam)</li><li>steamed (prepared by steaming)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;youth slang) problematic, difficult, a lot of work</li></ol><p><i>запари́ть</i> (zaparítʹ, “to steam (leaves etc.)”) +&lrm; <i>ный</i> (nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reserve, store, supply, stock, stockpile, inventory, log, margin</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>запасти́</i> (zapastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automobile</i>) range (<i>usually full tank, also the available distance with the remaining fuel left in the fuel tank</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to store up, to lay in a stock <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>запасти́</i> (zapastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide oneself <i>(with)</i>, to stock up, to lay in a supply <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>запаса́ть</i> (zapasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) spare tyre</li></ol><p>From <i>запасной</i> (zapasnoj, “spare (adj.)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storeroom (often specifically in a museum)</li><li>(military,&#32;colloquial) reservist</li></ol><p><i>запасти́</i> (zapastí) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p><p><i>запа́с</i> (zapás) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spare, reserve</li><li>auxiliary (supplementary or subsidiary)</li></ol><p><i>запа́с</i> (zapás) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) alternate aerodrome, alternate airport, alternate airfield (an aerodrome to which a flight must proceed if landing at the intended destination becomes impossible or inadvisable)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) plan B, backup option</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>запасно́й</i> (zapasnój)</li></ol><p><i>запа́с</i> (zapás) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to store up, to lay in a stock <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пасти́</i> (pastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide oneself <i>(with)</i>, to stock up, to lay in a supply <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>запасти́</i> (zapastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall back, to fall behind, to sink down</li><li>to sink deeply</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to take <i>(to)</i>, to fall <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to patent, to take out a patent <i>(for, on)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>патентова́ть</i> (patentovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, odor, scent (sensation)</li><li>wrap over (lapel, tails, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пах-</i> (pax-)</p><p>From <i>запа́хивать</i> (zapáxivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough</li><li>to plough in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough</li><li>to plough in</li><li>to wrap/draw tighter/closer, to close</li></ol><p><i>запаха́ть</i> (zapaxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>запахну́ть</i> (zapaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to smell, to emit a smell</li><li>to wrap/draw tighter/closer, to close</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>па́хнуть</i> (páxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to stain</li><li>to daub</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>па́чкать</i> (páčkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragrant</li></ol><p><i>за́пах</i> (zápax) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precentor, leading singer (e.g. in a choir)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who starts something (and others follow)</li></ol><p><i>запева́ть</i> (zapevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set/give the tune, to lead the singing/choir</li></ol><p><i>запе́ть</i> (zapétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casserole; baked pudding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) casserole; baked pudding</li></ol><p><i>запека́нка</i> (zapekánka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>запе́к-</i> (zapék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li><i>(blood)</i> to clot, to coagulate</li><li><i>(lips)</i> to become parched/cracked</li></ol><p><i>запека́ть</i> (zapekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock</li><li>to block up, to bar, to blockade</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock <i>(oneself)</i> up</li></ol><p><i>запере́ть</i> (zaperétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start singing, to sing out</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ). Compare Czech <i>zapět</i>, Kashubian <i>zapiôc</i>, Silesian <i>zapiŏć</i>, and Polish <i>zapiać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal, to seal up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imprint <i>(in)</i>, to impress <i>(in)</i>, to engrave <i>(in)</i></li><li>to depict, to portray, to photograph</li></ol><p><i>запечатле́ть</i> (zapečatlétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imprinted (<i>in one's memory, heart, soul, etc.</i>)</li><li>to be expressed, to be reflected (<i>on one's face, a photograph, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запечатлева́ть</i> (zapečatlevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imprint <i>(in)</i>, to impress <i>(in)</i>, to engrave <i>(in)</i></li><li>to depict, to portray, to photograph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>запечатьлѣти</i> (zapečatĭlěti). By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-печатле́ть</i> (-pečatlétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imprinted (<i>in one's memory, heart, soul, etc.</i>)</li><li>to be expressed, to be reflected (<i>on one's face, a photograph, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запечатле́ть</i> (zapečatlétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal up</li></ol><p><i>запеча́тать</i> (zapečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li><i>(blood)</i> to clot, to coagulate</li><li><i>(lips)</i> to become parched/cracked</li></ol><p><i>запе́чь</i> (zapéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash down <i>(with)</i>, to take <i>(after/with)</i></li><li>to take to drink, to go on a drinking bout/spree</li></ol><p><i>запи́ть</i> (zapítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a chaser (when drinking alcohol, any liquid that contains fewer degrees and is used to wash down the previously consumed one)</li></ol><p><i>запива́ть</i> (zapivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-он</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to talk excessively</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lie (to give false information) excessively</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start beeping (making a short squeak)</li><li>to bleep out inappropriate words (most often expletive words) by using a bleep censor over them (usually a 1000 Hz tone sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bleep censor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bleep out inappropriate words (most often expletive words) by using a bleep censor over them (usually a 1000 Hz tone sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;colloquial) a guitar solo (usually the bass guitar or the electric one) or a fast violin solo</li><li>(Internet,&#32;slang) reparation, creation (e.g. of a website)</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>запилить</i> (zapilitʹ), a form of <i>пилить</i> (pilitʹ, “to saw”).</p><p>(<i>Internet slang</i>): Also from <i>запилить</i> (zapilitʹ), but used in a context of a 2006 meme video, where a man is asking to <i>saw back</i> his door, which was sawed by the police forces.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble, to halt</li><li>to stammer, to falter, to stop short</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пина́ть</i> (pinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stumbling (in speech or when walking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock</li><li>to block up, to bar, to blockade</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock <i>(oneself)</i> up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to deny, to disavow, to refuse to admit one's guilt</li></ol><p><i>запира́ть</i> (zapirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write down, to enter (to set something down in writing)</li><li>to record (to make a record of information&#59; to make an audio or video recording of)</li><li>to enroll, to register (to enlist (someone) or make (someone) a member of)</li><li>(computing,&#32;rare) to save (to write a file to a disk)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to burn (to write a file to an optical disc)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register for</li><li>to enroll</li><li>to sign up for</li></ol><p><i>записа́ть</i> (zapisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>note, memo (memorandum)</li></ol><p><i>записа́ть</i> (zapisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notebook, pocketbook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) note; intended for notes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inveterate, out-and-out</li></ol><p>From <i>за́пись</i> (zápisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>запи́ска</i> (zapíska)</li></ol><p><i>запи́ска</i> (zapíska) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write down, to enter (to set something down in writing)</li><li>to record (to make a record of information&#59; to make an audio or video recording of)</li><li>to enroll, to register (to enlist (someone) or make (someone) a member of)</li><li>(computing,&#32;rare) to save (to write a file to a disk)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to burn (to write a file to an optical disc)</li></ol><p><i>записа́ть</i> (zapisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register for</li><li>to enroll</li><li>to sign up for</li></ol><p><i>запи́сывать</i> (zapísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record, recording (<i>e.g. sound</i>)</li><li>entry (<i>e.g. in a log, a journal, a database or a document</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) official act, record, deed, registration</li></ol><p><i>записа́ть</i> (zapisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash down <i>(with)</i>, to take <i>(after/with)</i>, to chase <i>(with)</i></li><li>to go on a drinking bout/spree</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пи́ть</i> (pítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push <i>(in, into)</i>, to cram <i>(in, into)</i>, to stuff <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пиха́ть</i> (pixátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push <i>(in, into)</i>, to cram <i>(in, into)</i>, to stuff <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>запихну́ть</i> (zapixnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>запиха́ть</i> (zapixátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push <i>(in, into)</i>, to cram <i>(in, into)</i>, to stuff <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пихну́ть</i> (pixnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tear-stained, in tears</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start crying, weeping</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plan, to project, to schedule</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>плани́ровать</i> (planírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit on</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mouldy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mold, to become moldy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пле́сневеть</i> (plésnevetʹ, “to mold”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to braid</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to intertwine</li></ol><p><i>заплести́</i> (zaplestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to braid</li></ol><p><i>заплет-</i> (zaplet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to be slurred (<i>of speech</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to be wobbly, to totter (<i>of one's legs</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to intertwine</li></ol><p><i>заплета́ть</i> (zapletátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(a tooth)</i> to stop, to fill</li><li>to seal</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пломбировать</i> (plombirovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swim</li><li>a sail, a row (<i>a trip in a boat</i>)</li><li>(swimming,&#32;rowing,&#32;sailing) a race, an event</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заплы́ть</i> (zaplýtʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim (<i>somewhere far away</i>)</li><li>to swim or sail [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to be bloated, to swollen</li></ol><p><i>заплы́ть</i> (zaplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim (<i>somewhere far away</i>)</li><li>to swim or sail [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to be bloated, to swollen</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to dance</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пляса́ть</i> (pljasátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit on</li></ol><p><i>заплева́ть</i> (zaplevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble, to halt</li><li>to stammer, to falter, to stop short</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пну́ть</i> (pnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature reserve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>protected</li><li>secret</li></ol><p><i>за́поведь</i> (zápovedʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandment</li><li>precept, rule</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>заповѣдь</i> (zapovědĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zapovědь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>за́поведь</i> (zápovedʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to suspect</li></ol><p><i>заподо́зрить</i> (zapodózritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspect</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>подозре́ть</i> (podozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belated, late, tardy</li><li>overdue</li><li>later</li><li>backward, back</li><li>dilatory</li><li>last, latter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lateness, tardiness</li></ol><p><i>запозда́ть</i> (zapozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be late, to be behind schedule</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to lag</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-позда́ть</i> (-pozdátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be late</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>по́здно</i> (pózdno, “late”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binge, bout, drinking bout (recurring drunkenness due to a pathological craving for alcoholic drink; a period of continuous drunkenness)</li><li>booze</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) binge (without stopping, in immoderate quantities; with unbridled enthusiasm)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zapojь</i>. Compare <i>запи́ть</i> (zapítʹ). See <i>&#42;piti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl into</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl into</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filling, filling up, filling in (e.g. forms)</li></ol><p><i>запо́лнить</i> (zapólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occupancy, used up capacity (how full something is)</li></ol><p><i>запо́лненный</i> (zapólnennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filler, aggregate, placeholder</li></ol><p><i>запо́лнить</i> (zapólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill (something to the end)</li><li>to fill in, to fill out (a form or a document)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-, “to the finish, to the end”, prefix) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “full, complete”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up, to be filled up</li></ol><p><i>запо́лнить</i> (zapólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill (something to the end)</li><li>to fill in, to fill out (a form or a document)</li></ol><p><i>запо́лнить</i> (zapólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up, to be filled up</li></ol><p><i>заполня́ть</i> (zapolnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to take prisoner, to take captive</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to captivate, to charm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill up (a space, a place)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>полони́ть</i> (polonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to take prisoner, to take captive</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to captivate, to charm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill up (a space, a place)</li></ol><p><i>заполони́ть</i> (zapolonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) restless, fussy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anxious, worried, agitated</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>полоши́ть</i> (pološítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare <i>поло́х</i> (polóx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get hold <i>(of)</i>, to pick up, to get oneself</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>получи́ть</i> (polučítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polar, Arctic (<i>referring specifically to location beyond the Arctic Circle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Заполя́рье</i> (Zapoljárʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memorization, memorizing</li></ol><p><i>запомина́ть</i> (zapominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to keep in mind</li><li>to memorize</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to remember (to be remembered), to stick to one's memory, to remain in one's memory</li></ol><p><i>запомина́ть</i> (zapominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to keep in mind</li><li>to memorize</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to remember (to be remembered), to stick to one's memory, to remain in one's memory</li></ol><p><i>запо́мнить</i> (zapómnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apron, pinafore; protective cloth worn over clothing</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) ceremonial apron, especially the apron worn by Freemasons</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-, “over, covering”) + <i>пон</i> (pon, “stretched cloth, panel of fabric”), thus literally “a cloth placed over (something)." According to Pokorny, the latter is ultimately Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to weave”); see Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pęti</i> (“to stretch, tile”).</p><p>Related to Bulgarian <i>по́нява</i> (pónjava), Serbo-Croatian <i>поњава</i> (“bedsheet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cufflink (button used to hold a sleeve cuff together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>latch, lock</li><li>constipation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;impersonal</i>) to dust, to cover lightly, to cover with a thin layer (<i>of snow or dust</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запороши́ть</i> (zaporošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Zaporozhye cossack</li><li>Zaporozhets (ZAZ-965, ZAZ-966, or ZAZ-968 automobile)</li></ol><p><i>Запоро́жье</i> (Zaporóžʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Zaporizhzhian (of, from or relating to the Ukrainian city or oblast of Zaporizhzhia (formerly Zaporozhye))</li></ol><p><i>Запоро́жье</i> (Zaporóžʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;impersonal</i>) to dust, to cover lightly, to cover with a thin layer (<i>of snow or dust</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to fall lightly (<i>of snow</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пороши́ть</i> (porošítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to flutter</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>порха́ть</i> (porxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to post</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>по́стить</i> (póstitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steam up, to mist up</li></ol><p><i>запоте́ть</i> (zapotétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steam up, to mist up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>поте́ть</i> (potétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;dated) alternative form of <i>потре́бно</i> (potrébno)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>потре́бно</i> (potrébno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck in</li><li>to fill</li><li>to feed, to insert</li><li>to dress <i>(with)</i>, to season <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) seasoning, dressing</li><li>filling (with fuel)</li><li>gas station, petrol station</li></ol><p><i>запра́вить</i> (zaprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck in</li><li>to fill</li><li>to feed, to insert</li><li>to dress <i>(with)</i>, to season <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run, to rule, to boss, to call the shots</li></ol><p><i>запра́вить</i> (zaprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;slang) to pretend, to bullshit, to fake</li><li>to pretend to be a bad cardplayer in order to attract a less-experienced player and eventually win over him</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) refilling, refueling (<i>e.g. gasoline, ink</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запра́вка</i> (zaprávka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire <i>(about, after)</i>, to request</li><li>to request information <i>(from)</i>, to inquire <i>(of)</i></li><li>to charge <i>(price)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to call, to request</li></ol><p><i>запроси́ть</i> (zaprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exorbitant, outrageous</li><li>(<i>radio</i>) beyond-cutoff</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>преде́л</i> (predél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prohibition, interdiction, ban</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative name of Instagram</li></ol><p>From <i>запре́т</i> (zaprét) +&lrm; <i>Инстагра́м</i> (Instagrám), ending in -гра́м, similar to the Instagram and Telegram social media names in Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid, to prohibit [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>запрѣтити</i> (zaprětiti, “to forbid”). By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>прети́ть</i> (pretítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be subject to a ban</li></ol><p><i>запрети́ть</i> (zapretítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forbidden</li></ol><p><i>запре́т</i> (zaprét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid, to prohibit [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>запрѣщати</i> (zaprěštati). By surface analysis, <i>запрет-</i> (zapret-) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be prohibited, to be forbidden, to be banned</li></ol><p><i>запреща́ть</i> (zapreščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ban, banning, prohibition</li></ol><p>From <i>запрети́ть</i> (zapretítʹ, “to forbid, ban”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a prohibited substance or item</li></ol><p><i>запрещённый</i> (zapreščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forbidden, illicit, prohibited</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to notice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to wail, to begin to weep, to begin to lament</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>причита́ть</i> (pričitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to program</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>программи́ровать</i> (programmírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw back</li></ol><p><i>запроки́д-</i> (zaprokíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw back</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry</li><li>request, demand</li><li>query <i>(computer)</i></li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) demands, requirements, claims, interests, pretensions</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>запроси́ть</i> (zaprosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire <i>(about, after)</i>, to request</li><li>to request information <i>(from)</i>, to inquire <i>(of)</i></li><li>to charge <i>(price)</i></li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to call, to request</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily, just like that (without observance of generally accepted formalities; without ceremony, without hesitation)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily (without effort, without work)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>про́сто</i> (prósto)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protest <i>(against)</i>, to make a protest <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>протестова́ть</i> (protestovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dam, weir</li><li>(dammed) pond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dam, to dam up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flood, to fill completely</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пруди́ть</i> (prudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>запру́живать</i> (zaprúživatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>запруди́ть</i> (zaprudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dam, to dam up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flood, to fill completely</li></ol><p><i>запруди́ть</i> (zaprudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to jump, <i>(heart)</i> to (begin to) thump/throb</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гать</i> (prýgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump (<i>onto/into</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запры́гнуть</i> (zaprýgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump (<i>onto/into</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harness (<i>a beast of burden</i>), to yoke (<i>an ox</i>)</li></ol><p><i>запря́г-</i> (zaprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to conceal</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to conceal</li></ol><p><i>запря́тать</i> (zaprjátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harness (<i>a beast of burden</i>), to yoke (<i>an ox</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-прячь</i> (-prjačʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intimidate, to cow, to browbeat</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bullying, intimidation</li></ol><p><i>запу́гивать</i> (zapúgivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intimidate, to cow, to browbeat</li></ol><p><i>запуга́ть</i> (zapugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to deceive, to mislead, to bullshit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>startup (act)</li><li>launch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to launch (a missile, rocket, a probe, etc.)</li><li>to launch; to start (operation or manufacturing of something)</li><li>to let in</li><li>to neglect, to fail to take care of, to let something decline or decay; to abandon</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desolation; (state of) neglect</li><li>state of disrepair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to launch (a missile, rocket, a probe, etc.)</li><li>to launch; to start (operation or manufacturing of something)</li><li>to let in</li><li>to neglect, to fail to take care of, to let something decline or decay; to abandon</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tangled</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) intricate, confused</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entangle, to tangle</li><li>to tangle, to confuse, to complicate, to muddle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get entangled/enmeshed <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tangled/entangled/enmeshed</li><li>to become more confused/complicated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be misled, to get confused, to get mixed up</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get entangled/enmeshed <i>(in, by)</i></li></ol><p><i>запу́тать</i> (zapútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entangle, to tangle</li><li>to tangle, to confuse, to complicate, to muddle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get entangled/enmeshed <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>запу́тать</i> (zapútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tangled/entangled/enmeshed</li><li>to become more confused/complicated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be misled, to get confused, to get mixed up</li></ol><p><i>запу́тывать</i> (zapútyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned, neglected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spare parts, spares</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>запасны́е ча́сти</i> (zapasnýje části).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spare part</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>запа́сная часть</i> (zapásnaja častʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover with dust, to make dusty</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пыли́ть</i> (pylítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered in dust, to get dusty</li></ol><p><i>запыли́ть</i> (zapylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be out of breath from exertion; to be breathless</li><li>to pant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) wrist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zapęstьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пясть</i> (pjastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comma (punctuation)</li><li>decimal point</li><li>hitch, fix</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ая</i> (-aja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pęti</i> (“to stretch, to tie/fix together”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stain, to soil</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to disgrace, to defame</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) to touch or hit (<i>in games of tag or lapta</i>)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) to mark, to stamp, to brand</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>пятна́ть</i> (pjatnátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earning (money)</li></ol><p><i>зараба́тывать</i> (zarabátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to earn</li></ol><p><i>зарабо́тать</i> (zarabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a living, to bring home the bacon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to earn</li><li>to start to work</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a living, to bring home the bacon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wages, salary, pay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wages, salary; earned</li></ol><p>From <i>зарабо́тать</i> (zarabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with unexpected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earnings, income; wages, pay</li></ol><p><i>зарабо́тать</i> (zarabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Compare Polish <i>zarobek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infect</li><li>to contaminate</li></ol><p><i>зарази́ть</i> (zarazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to contract, to be infected <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(emotions, mood, ideas etc)</i> to be infected/contaminated <i>(with, by)</i></li></ol><p><i>заража́ть</i> (zaražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infection; contamination</li></ol><p><i>зарази́ть</i> (zarazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infected</li><li>contaminated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) infection, contagion, pest</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) scumbag, bastard</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>damn it</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaraza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) infectious, contagious</li></ol><p><i>зарази́ть</i> (zarazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infect</li><li>to contaminate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaraziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ, “to strike”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to contract, to be infected <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(emotions, mood, ideas etc)</i> to be infected/contaminated <i>(with, by)</i></li></ol><p><i>зарази́ть</i> (zarazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infectious, contagious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person suffering from a contagious disease</li></ol><p><i>зара́за</i> (zaráza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beforehand, in advance, in good time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be overgrown, to be covered with vegetation, to hair, to fur, etc.</li><li>to heal, to skin over (of a wound)</li></ol><p><i>зарасти́</i> (zarastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be overgrown, to be covered with vegetation, to hair, to fur, etc.</li><li>to heal, to skin over (of a wound)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go too far, to go to extremes, to overdo things</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рва́ться</i> (rvátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to blush, to start to redden (<i>of a person or the dawn, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рдеть</i> (rdetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to blush, to start to redden (<i>of a person or the dawn, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зарде́ть</i> (zardétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) August</li></ol><p>Etymology 1</p><p>Related to <i>září</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to roar/bellow</li><li>to begin to cry/howl/blubber</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>реве́ть</i> (revétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blaze, glow, gleam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) synonym of <i>зарегистри́ровать</i> (zaregistrírovatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ре́гать</i> (régatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register, to log, to record</li><li>to enroll</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>регистри́ровать</i> (registrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to register one's marriage</li></ol><p><i>зарегистри́ровать</i> (zaregistrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stab (to death), to knife, to kill, to carve up; to butcher, to stick</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to axe, to kill (a part in a play, a bill, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>заре́зать</i> (zarézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to book, to reserve</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>резерви́ровать</i> (rezervírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to prohibit</li><li>to force to renounce or forswear</li></ol><p><i>заре́к-</i> (zarék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renounce, to forswear, to swear off</li></ol><p><i>зарека́ть</i> (zarekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove to be, to turn out (a particular way)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рекомендова́ть</i> (rekomendovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal by way of retouching; to polish or edit (an image) by way of retouching.</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ретушь</i> (retušʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>situated beyond a river</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to prohibit</li><li>to force to renounce or forswear</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renounce, to forswear, to swear off</li></ol><p><i>заре́чь</i> (zaréčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal with a grate or with bars (e.g. a window)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>решети́ть</i> (rešetítʹ) (end-stressed) or from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>реше́тить</i> (rešétitʹ) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal with a grate or with bars (e.g. a window)</li></ol><p>From <i>зарешети́ть</i> (zarešetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) (end-stressed) or from <i>зареше́тить</i> (zarešétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rust</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ржа́веть</i> (ržávetʹ) or <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ржаве́ть</i> (ržavétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neigh</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to laugh</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ржать</i> (ržatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) sarin (neurotoxin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sarin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sketching, drawing (act)</li><li>sketch, drawing (result)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) short story or essay about someone or something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat lightning</li><li>Zarnitsa (Soviet massive children's war game)</li></ol><p><i>заря́</i> (zarjá) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) migrant worker from Ukraine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>заробітчанин</i> (zarobitčanyn), from Ukrainian <i>заробіток</i> (zarobitok, “wages”), which is cognate with <i>за́работок</i> (zárabotok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engender, to rouse, to give rise <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be conceived</li><li>to arise, to be born</li></ol><p><i>зароди́ть</i> (zarodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embryo, foetus</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) germ</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engender, to rouse, to give rise <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>зароди́ть</i> (zarodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be conceived</li><li>to arise, to be born</li></ol><p><i>зарожда́ть</i> (zaroždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>germination, conceiving, initiation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn pink</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negative oath or vow (solemn promise not to do something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zarokъ</i>. Deverbal from early Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zarekti</i> (later <i>&#42;zareťi</i>), with Indo-European ablaut typical of nouns. Compare <i>заре́чь</i> (zaréčʹ, “to prohibit, to force to renounce”), <i>заре́чься</i> (zaréčʹsja, “to renounce, to forswear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undergrowth, brushwood, thicket</li></ol><p><i>зарос-</i> (zaros-) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overgrown, weedy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wages, salary, pay; paycheck</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>за́работная пла́та</i> (zárabotnaja pláta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill with a sabre <i>[axe, etc]</i></li><li>to notch, to make an incision <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to hew, to make a cut <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to ruin, to turn down, to say no <i>(to)</i>, to shoot down <i>(in flames)</i></li></ol><p><i>заруби́ть</i> (zarubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign (from a different country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abroad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill with a sabre <i>[axe, etc]</i></li><li>to notch, to make an incision <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to hew, to make a cut <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to ruin, to turn down, to say no <i>(to)</i>, to shoot down <i>(in flames)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incision, notch (v-shaped cut)</li></ol><p><i>заруби́ть</i> (zarubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antique embroidered and jeweled cuff of a woman’s dress.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure, to enlist</li></ol><p><i>заручи́ться</i> (zaručítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure, to enlist</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-ручи́ть</i> (-ručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li><li>to fill up (<i>with earth</i>), to make (a ditch, pit, etc.) level with the ground</li></ol><p><i>зары́ть</i> (zarýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide one's light under a bushel (to conceal one's positive qualities or talents, especially due to modesty or shyness); to let one's talent go to waste</li></ol><p>From the Parable of the Talents in the Bible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury oneself, to dig in</li><li>to go too far, to go to extremes, to overdo things</li></ol><p><i>зарыва́ть</i> (zaryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to sob/blubber, to burst out sobbing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рыда́ть</i> (rydátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li><li>to fill up (<i>with earth</i>), to make (a ditch, pit, etc.) level with the ground</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide one's light under a bushel (to conceal one's positive qualities or talents, especially due to modesty or shyness); to let one's talent go to waste</li></ol><p>From the Parable of the Talents in the Bible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury the hatchet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury oneself, to dig in</li></ol><p><i>зары́ть</i> (zarýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to roar/growl</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>рыча́ть</i> (ryčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawn, daybreak</li><li>dusk, nightfall</li><li>(<i>military</i>) reveille</li><li>(<i>military</i>) tattoo, retreat</li><li>outset, start</li><li>Zarya (module of the International Space Station)</li><li>Zarya (type of Soviet directional mediumwave broadcasting antenna)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zořa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charge, a load of explosive</li><li>shot, shell</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) charge (the quantity of electricity)</li><li>electrically charged body</li><li>conserved quantum number</li><li>store, fund, supply</li><li>charging up</li><li>snow squall</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>зарядить</i> (zarjaditʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (a weapon)</li><li>to charge (a battery, accumulator, etc.)</li><li>to inspire, to imbue</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to keep repeating (an action) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> infinitive]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to fall nonstop (<i>of rain</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical exercise</li><li>the filling up of something; a charge (i.e. a battery)</li><li>charger; charging device</li></ol><p><i>заряди́ть</i> (zarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charger; charging device</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) charging; charge (e.g. battery)</li></ol><p><i>заря́д</i> (zarjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomic number (number of protons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;electricity) charge</li></ol><p><i>заря́д</i> (zarjád) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) charger; charging device</li></ol><p><i>заряжа́ть</i> (zarjažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load <i>(weapon)</i></li><li>to charge <i>(battery)</i></li><li>to inspire, to imbue</li></ol><p><i>заряди́ть</i> (zarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loaded, live (<i>gun, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) charged</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robin (<i>Erithacus rubecula</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambush</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>засади́ть</i> (zasadítʹ). Compare Polish <i>zasada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shut <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shut <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>засади́ть</i> (zasadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit down <i>(to)</i>, to settle down <i>(to)</i>, to set <i>(to)</i>, to bury oneself <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle, to retire, to stay</li></ol><p><i>заса́живать</i> (zasáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); pairing with <i>засе́сть</i>&nbsp;pf (zaséstʹ) corresponds to pairing between <i>сади́ться</i>&nbsp;impf (sadítʹsja) and <i>сесть</i>&nbsp;pf (sestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greasy, grease-stained</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt, to pickle, to corn</li><li>to grease, to smear, to make greasy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grease, to smear, to make greasy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>са́лить</i> (sálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to suck on (something)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to start to hurt</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to suck in, to absorb</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to draw in, to suck in (<i>of a routine, an environment, etc.</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to gulp, to chug</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to induce, to bring under control</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to fascinate, to mesmerize, to captivate</li></ol><p><i>засоса́ть</i> (zasosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wind</i>) to freshen, to become stronger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start shining, to start flashing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сверка́ть</i> (sverkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shine</li><li>to light</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a smack <i>(on)</i></li><li>to expose to light, to overexpose</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light up</li><li>to be ruined/spoiled by <i>(exposure to)</i> light, to be overexposed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to let oneself be noticed/spotted, to give oneself away, to come into the spotlight</li></ol><p><i>засвети́ть</i> (zasvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before dark (before night begins to fall)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>светло́</i> (svetló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expose to light, to overexpose</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>свечение</i> (svečenije) +&lrm; <i>-в</i> (-v) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ruined/spoiled by <i>(exposure to)</i> light, to be overexposed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to let oneself be noticed/spotted, to give oneself away, to come into the spotlight</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>свечение</i> (svečenije) +&lrm; <i>-в</i> (-v) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to witness, to testify</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>свиде́тельствовать</i> (svidételʹstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to whistle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow (a field)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strew lavishly</li></ol><p><i>засе́ять</i> (zaséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>session, meeting (meeting of a body to conduct business)</li></ol><p><i>заседа́ть</i> (zasedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assessor</li></ol><p><i>заседа́ть</i> (zasedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to be in session</li><li>to take part in (attend) a conference/meeting</li><li>to get stuck, to lodge (<i>in</i>) (bullet, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>засе́д-</i> (zaséd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow (a field)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strew lavishly</li></ol><p><i>засе́ять</i> (zaséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abatis, barricade of felled trees</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notch</li><li>to determine by intersection</li><li>to locate, to pinpoint</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spot, to trace, to nail, to catch in the act</li><li>to flog/whip to death</li></ol><p><i>засе́к-</i> (zasék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to classify as secret, to place on the secret list, to restrict, to hush up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to admit to secret work, to give access to secret documents, to clear for security, to give clearance to</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>секре́т</i> (sekrét) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to classify as secret, to place on the secret list, to restrict, to hush up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to admit to secret work, to give access to secret documents, to clear for security</li></ol><p><i>засекре́тить</i> (zasekrétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settling, settlement, colonization</li><li>occupancy (<i>of a building</i>)</li></ol><p><i>засели́ть</i> (zaselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occupy, to settle <i>(in)</i>, to move <i>(into)</i>, to populate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to take up one's residence</li></ol><p><i>засели́ть</i> (zaselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occupy, to settle <i>(in)</i>, to move <i>(into)</i>, to populate</li></ol><p><i>засели́ть</i> (zaselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>populous, highly populated (<i>of a city, location, etc.</i>)</li><li>(fully) occupied (<i>of a house</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit down <i>(to)</i>, to settle down <i>(to)</i>, to set <i>(to)</i>, to bury oneself <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle, to retire, to stay</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stick <i>(in)</i>, to stick fast <i>(in)</i>, to lodge <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zasěsti</i>; compare Serbo-Croatian <i>zàsjesti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notch, cut, mark</li></ol><p><i>засе́к-</i> (zasék-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) abatis</li></ol><p><i>засе́ка</i> (zaséka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notch</li><li>to plot on a map</li><li>to detect, to locate, to pinpoint</li><li>to determine by intersection</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spot, to trace, to nail, to catch in the act</li><li>to whip to death, to flog to death</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow (a field)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strew lavishly</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit down (for a long time), to stay (for a long time)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare with Belarusian <i>засядзецца</i> (zasjadzjecca), Ukrainian <i>засидітися</i> (zasyditysja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit down (for a long time), to stay (for a long time)</li></ol><p><i>засиде́ться</i> (zasidétʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmful domination (of someone or something somewhere), stranglehold</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) power, force</li></ol><p>First attested in 1876 (<i>The Well-Meant Speeches</i> by M. Saltykov-Shchedrin). From dialectal <i>засилить</i> (zasilitʹ, “to noose (a bird); to tie to”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje), from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>си́лить</i> (sílitʹ, “to net; to force”), from <i>си́ла</i> (síla). Compare with Belarusian <i>засі́лле</i> (zasíllje), Ukrainian <i>заси́лля</i> (zasýllja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;humorous) then, whereupon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to shit (something through, like clothing or a carpet), to shit all over (something)</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) to dirty, to spoil, to trash; to shit all over</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) to mess up, to ruin; to shit all over</li></ol><p><i>засра́ть</i> (zasrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-сира́ть</i> (-sirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shine</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to jump, to leap; break into a gallop (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>to jump somewhere far away and call in there</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, to spring [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘behind a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, to spring [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break into [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in, to stop by [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump somewhere far away and drop in there</li></ol><p><i>заскок-</i> (zaskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>заскака́ть</i> (zaskakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bounce, jump, hop</li><li>quick visit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) oddity, eccentricity, peculiar habit</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;politics) deviation, (extreme) position</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lapse (<i>e.g. in one's memory, reason, or attention</i>), absent-mindedness, blackout</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>заска́кивать</i> (zaskákivatʹ) / <i>заскочи́ть</i> (zaskočítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start sliding, to start slipping</li><li>to start gliding</li><li>to start floating</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>скользи́ть</i> (skolʹzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardened; corny</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) callous; insensitive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, to spring [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘behind a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, to spring [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break into [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in, to stop by [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to creak</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>скрипе́ть</i> (skripétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to whine</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>скули́ть</i> (skulítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel sad/miserable, to have a fit of the blues, to be blue, to feel bored</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>скуча́ть</i> (skučátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li><li>to dispatch/send to the wrong address</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screen, cover</li><li>block, wall, barrier</li><li>(<i>military</i>) covering force</li><li>oven door</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaslonъ</i>, from <i>&#42;za-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;slonъ</i> (“offset, cornice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screen, to shield</li><li>to cover, to hide</li><li>to overshadow, to take the place <i>(of)</i>, to push into the background</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-слони́ть</i> (-slonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flap, valve, shutter, screen</li></ol><p><i>заслони́ть</i> (zaslonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screen, to shield</li><li>to cover, to hide</li><li>to overshadow, to take the place <i>(of)</i>, to push into the background</li></ol><p><i>заслони́ть</i> (zaslonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merit, desert</li><li>service</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zasluga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deservedly, justifiably</li><li>fairly, equitably</li></ol><p><i>заслу́женный</i> (zaslúžennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deserved, well-deserved, well-earned</li><li>celebrated, distinguished</li><li>honoured/honored (<i>part of a title</i>)</li><li>equitable, fair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deserve</li><li>to earn</li><li>to merit</li></ol><p><i>заслужи́ть</i> (zaslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deserve</li><li>to earn</li><li>to merit</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>заслу́женный</i> (zaslúžennyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear out (something spoken publicly)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to listen to (something) too many times</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become absorbed, be engrossed listening</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear out (something spoken publicly)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to listen to (something) too many times</li></ol><p><i>заслу́шать</i> (zaslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become absorbed, be engrossed listening</li></ol><p><i>заслу́шивать</i> (zaslúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to hear</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hear (something in the distance)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to hear about (something through rumors) [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smell (something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh, to start laughing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>смея́ться</i> (smejátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snow-covered, snowbound, snowed up, covered with deep snow</li></ol><p>Has the form of a past passive participle of <i>заснежи́ть</i> (zasnežítʹ), although the current past passive participle of this verb is <i>заснежённый</i> (zasnežónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to cover with snow</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>снежи́ть</i> (snežítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>засне́женный</i> (zasnéžennyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to photograph, to take a picture of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to film</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>снима́ть</i> (snimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall asleep</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to photograph, to take a picture of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to film</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>снять</i> (snjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) crossbar, bolt <i>(locking mechanism)</i></li><li>bar, bolt <i>(locking mechanism)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put in, to slip, to butt in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сова́ть</i> (sovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put (in)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to poke, to stick in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt, to pickle, to corn</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>соли́ть</i> (solítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start doubting</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сомнева́ться</i> (somnevátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to sniff/snuffle, to breathe heavily, to snore</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сопе́ть</i> (sopétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockage, clog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block, to obstruct, to clog, to inquinate</li><li>to contaminate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сори́ть</i> (sorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block, to obstruct, to clog</li><li>to contaminate</li><li>to cause constipation</li></ol><p><i>засори́ть</i> (zasorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hickey, love bite (swelling on the skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to suck on (something)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to start to hurt</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to suck in, to absorb</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to draw in, to suck in (<i>of a routine, an environment, etc.</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to gulp, to chug</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to induce, to bring under control</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to fascinate, to mesmerize, to captivate</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleepy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to forget something after sleeping</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smother (a baby) in one’s sleep (rare, dated, colloquial)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have pins and needles in a body part during sleep</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to hurry, to begin to hasten</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to argue, to get into an argument [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>спо́рить</i> (spóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) scallywag, rascal</li></ol><p><i>засра́ть</i> (zasrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нец</i> (-ánec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moderately&#32;vulgar</i>) to shit (something through, like clothing or a carpet), to shit all over (something)</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) to dirty, to spoil, to trash; to shit all over</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) to mess up, to ruin; to shit all over</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) gate, turnpike</li><li>outpost</li><li>picket, security detachment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to catch, to (manage to) meet (e.g. someone at home or at work)</li></ol><p><i>заста́ть</i> (zastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compel, to force, to make</li><li>to fill, to cram, to jam, to clutter</li><li>to block up, to close off</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headpiece, headband</li><li>signature tune, theme song</li><li>opening sequence, intro</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) splash screen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compel, to force, to make</li><li>to fill, to cram, to jam, to clutter</li><li>to block up, to close off</li></ol><p>Imperfective of <i>заста́вить</i> (zastávitʹ), from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ). The sense “to compel” is possibly a calque of German <i>anhalten</i>.</p><p>Imperfective of <i>заста́вить</i> (zastávitʹ), from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand too long without moving</li><li>to linger, to stay for a while in one place</li><li>to stagnate (<i>of air, water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>застоя́ться</i> (zastojátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chronic</li><li>inveterate, ingrained</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-е́лый</i> (-élyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to catch, to (manage to) meet (e.g. someone at home or at work)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buckle up, to zip up, to button up, to fasten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glaze (to install glass in)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стекли́ть</i> (steklítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glaze (to install glass in)</li></ol><p><i>застекли́ть</i> (zasteklítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) back room</li><li>torture chamber</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the plural) confines</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стена́</i> (stená) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shyly (in a shy manner)</li></ol><p><i>засте́нчивый</i> (zasténčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shyness, timidity</li></ol><p><i>засте́нчивый</i> (zasténčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shy, coy, timid, reserved</li></ol><p><i>засте́нок</i> (zasténok) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to overtake</li></ol><p><i>засти́гнуть</i> (zastígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to overtake</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́гнуть</i> (-stígnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to cloud, to screen, to hide from view</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-стила́ть</i> (-stilátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wash out (a stain)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to destroy by washing</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wash out (a stain)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to destroy by washing</li></ol><p><i>застира́ть</i> (zastirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to overtake</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́чь</i> (-stíčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to cloud, to screen, to hide from view</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стлать</i> (stlatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stagnation, standstill, deadlock</li><li>depression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stagnant</li></ol><p><i>засто́й</i> (zastój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast (at a table), tableful</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) table; occurring at the table</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start moaning</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стона́ть</i> (stonátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to stop, to bring to a halt/standstill (to make something stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li></ol><p><i>засто́порить</i> (zastóporitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to falter, to come to a halt, to face a standstill (to stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li></ol><p><i>засто́поривать</i> (zastóporivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to stop, to bring to a halt/standstill (to make something stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) to catch red-handed, to catch in the act</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) to rob</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сто́порить</i> (stóporitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to falter, to come to a halt, to face a standstill (to stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li></ol><p><i>засто́порить</i> (zastóporitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stagnant</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́лый</i> (stojályj, “stagnant”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand too long without moving</li><li>to linger, to stay for a while in one place</li><li>to stagnate (<i>of air, water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build (a building on a site), to construct (something by filling a plot of land with many buildings)</li></ol><p><i>застро́ить</i> (zastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become built-up, to become filled with buildings</li></ol><p><i>застра́ивать</i> (zastráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure (to provide for compensation if some risk occurs)</li><li>to guard against</li><li>to stand by (in sports)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure (to provide for compensation if some risk occurs)</li></ol><p><i>застрахова́ть</i> (zastraxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stuck</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-стрева́ть</i> (-strevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to chirr</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стрекота́ть</i> (strekotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to gun down</li></ol><p><i>застрели́ть</i> (zastrelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to gun down</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-стрели́ть</i> (-strelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot oneself</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) what a blast!, it's a screamer!, it's enough to drive you mad!, I'll be damned!</li></ol><p><i>застрели́ть</i> (zastrelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) eaves</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) eavestrough</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стреха́</i> (strexá), with unexpected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to develop <i>(a site)</i>, to build <i>(on)</i>, to erect buildings (on)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become built-up, to become filled with buildings</li></ol><p><i>застро́ить</i> (zastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building up, housing, site development (construction)</li></ol><p><i>застро́ить</i> (zastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder, developer</li></ol><p><i>застро́ить</i> (zastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stuck</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-стрять</i> (-strjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start knocking</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to catch someone doing something illegal or disallowed, to catch red-handed</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drown out (someone's speech) by knocking</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to catch red-handed</li><li>(<i>childish</i>) to tag (<i>in hide and seek</i>)</li></ol><p><i>засту́кать</i> (zastúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spade</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) overstep, (<i>tennis</i>) foot fault</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>заступа́ть</i> (zastupátʹ), from spades designed to transmit the power of a foot’s tread.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the place (of something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercede (for), to plead (for), to take the part (of), to stand up (for), to stick up (for)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the place (of something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercede <i>(for)</i>, to plead <i>(for)</i>, to take the part <i>(of)</i>, to stand up <i>(for)</i>, to stick up <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defender, intercessor</li><li>protector, patron</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>заступи́ть</i> (zastupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>застъпник</i> (zastǎpnik, “intercessor, mediator”) (which corresponds Russian <i>посре́дник</i> (posrédnik)). Compare typologically Latin <i>intercessor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, defense (of someone); standing up (for someone); patronage; advocacy, intercession (on someone's behalf)</li></ol><p><i>засту́пник</i> (zastúpnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to knock, to rap, etc.</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to harden, to solidify</li><li>to cool down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to freeze, to feel cold; to catch cold</li><li>to stiffen, to freeze, to be / become / stand stockstill</li></ol><p>From <i>засты́ть</i> (zastýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ) or from <i>засты́нуть</i> (zastýnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solidified, hard</li><li>frozen</li><li>immobilized, immobile, motionless, stiff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel ashamed, to blush with shame, to become confused</li></ol><p><i>застыди́ть</i> (zastydítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>засты́ть</i> (zastýtʹ)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сты́нуть</i> (stýnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to harden, to solidify</li><li>to cool down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to freeze, to feel cold; to catch cold</li><li>to stiffen, to freeze, to be / become / stand stockstill</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>стыть</i> (stytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buckle up, to zip up, to button up, to fasten</li></ol><p><i>застегну́ть</i> (zastegnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastener, clasp, buckle</li></ol><p><i>застег(ну́ть)</i> (zasteg(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zipper, zip fastener (a fastener used in clothing, bags)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to bustle <i>(about)</i>, to start fussing</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>суети́ться</i> (sujetítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (in)</li><li>to poke, to stick in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drought</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drought-tolerant</li></ol><p><i>за́суха</i> (zásuxa) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>усто́йчивый</i> (ustójčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, to tuck up</li></ol><p><i>засучи́ть</i> (zasučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, to tuck up</li><li>to begin to twist/spin</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сучи́ть</i> (sučítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to dry <i>(transitive)</i></li></ol><p><i>засуши́ть</i> (zasušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry up, to dry</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, droughty, arid (lacking rain)</li></ol><p><i>засуши́ть</i> (zasušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to include, to take into account</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to include, to take into account</li></ol><p><i>засчита́ть</i> (zasčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li><li>to dispatch/send to the wrong address</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up</li><li>to cover, to bury under</li><li>to engulf</li><li>to pour into</li><li>to begin to pour, to strew (see <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ))</li><li>to bombard with, to heap upon, to load with</li><li>to fill</li><li>to cover</li><li>to overwhelm</li><li>to assail, to shower, to besiege</li><li>to lavish on</li><li>to fall asleep, to drop off to sleep</li><li>(rare,&#32;dated,&#32;colloquial) to smother a baby in one’s sleep</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпа́ть</i> (-sypátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filling up, pouring in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something poured onto or added to food</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) assistant miller (employee who pours grain for grinding, pours flour into bags, etc.)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) refiller, loader, backfiller (<i>of ore or coal into a furnace</i>)</li></ol><p><i>засы́пать</i> (zasýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-сыха́ть</i> (-syxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with bated breath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal</li><li>to harbour</li></ol><p><i>затаи́ть</i> (zataítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to keep a low profile</li></ol><p><i>зата́ивать</i> (zatáivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal</li><li>to harbour</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to keep a low profile</li></ol><p><i>затаи́ть</i> (zataítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) anacrusis (an unstressed note (or notes) before the first strong beat of a phrase)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push <i>(into)</i>, to shove <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>затолка́ть</i> (zatolkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down, to trample under foot</li></ol><p><i>затопта́ть</i> (zatoptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to chatter, to start to babble</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тарато́рить</i> (taratóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to drag in</li></ol><p><i>затаск-</i> (zatask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li></ol><p><i>заточи́ть</i> (zatočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to drag in</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret, hidden</li><li>repressed, suppressed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harden</li></ol><p><i>затверде́ть</i> (zatverdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harden</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тверде́ть</i> (tverdétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to memorize through repetition</li><li>to begin repeating, to begin to harp on</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тверди́ть</i> (tverdítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt, bar, latch (<i>for locking a door, window, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) gate, valve</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) shutter</li><li>(<i>plumbing</i>) trap</li><li>(<i>firearms</i>) breechblock</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) gate (<i>of a field-effect transistor</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zatvorъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;verti</i>. By surface analysis, deverbal from <i>затворя́ть, затвори́ть</i> (zatvorjátʹ, zatvorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, to shut</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to mix (dough) with water and yeast</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-твори́ть</i> (-tvorítʹ)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(door etc)</i> to close, to shut</li><li><i>(person)</i> to lock/shut/close oneself in</li><li>(<i>church</i>) to be in retreat, to go into retreat, to withdraw into seclusion/solitude</li></ol><p><i>затвори́ть</i> (zatvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermit, recluse, anchorite</li></ol><p><i>затвори́ть</i> (zatvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solitary</li><li>isolated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, to shut</li></ol><p><i>затвори́ть</i> (zatvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(door etc)</i> to close, to shut</li><li><i>(person)</i> to lock/shut/close oneself in</li><li>(<i>church</i>) to be in retreat, to go into retreat, to withdraw into seclusion/solitude</li></ol><p><i>затворя́ть</i> (zatvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to undertake, to start</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to decide (to do something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to venture</li></ol><p><i>зате́ять</i> (zatéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to start, to arise</li></ol><p><i>затева́ть</i> (zatevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fancy, intricate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prone to fabrications; inventive</li></ol><p>From <i>зате́я</i> (zatéja) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj), or from <i>зате́ять</i> (zatéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventor</li><li>organizer of a massive entertaining event</li></ol><p><i>зате́ять</i> (zatéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow <i>(in)</i>, to pour <i>(into)</i>, to leak <i>(into)</i></li><li>to swell, to swell up</li><li>to be numb, to be asleep</li></ol><p><i>зате́к-</i> (zaték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then, thereupon, after that</li><li>for that purpose, that’s why</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>те́м</i> (tém).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkening</li><li>(<i>military</i>) blackout</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blackout device</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) obscuring, obfuscation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dullness, lack of clarity (<i>of thoughts or consciousness</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) dark spot on an X-ray indicating organ death</li></ol><p><i>затемни́ть</i> (zatemnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, to black out</li><li>to obscure</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>темни́ть</i> (temnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, to black out</li><li>to obscure</li></ol><p><i>затемни́ть</i> (zatemnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shade, to shield from light</li><li>to block, to obscure (a light source)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тени́ть</i> (tenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shade, to shield from light</li><li>to block, to obscure (a light source)</li></ol><p><i>затени́ть</i> (zatenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to light</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erase, to wipe off</li><li>to paint over, to overwrite</li><li>to hold fast, to trap</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to ruin the career of (someone) by scheming</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out through constant use</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to begin to rub</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mislay, to lose</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to vanish, to be lost</li></ol><p><i>затеря́ть</i> (zaterjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut narrower, to sharpen, to hone (a log, board, stake, etc.)</li><li>to notch</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>теса́ть</i> (tesátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to worm one's way [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>затеса́ть</i> (zatesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow <i>(in)</i>, to pour <i>(into)</i>, to leak <i>(into)</i></li><li>to swell, to swell up</li><li>to go numb, to fall asleep</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enterprise, undertaking</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) piece of fun, fancy</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>зате́ять</i> (zatéjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to undertake, to start</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to decide (to do something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to venture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tějati</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>заце́яць</i> (zacjéjacʹ, “to undertake”) and (with a different prefix) Ukrainian <i>ви́тіяти</i> (výtijaty, “to undertake”). Per Vasmer, the Proto-Slavic etymon is possibly related to Latvian <i>tievêt</i> (“to strive for”), <i>tĩtît</i> (“to be stubborn, to be annoyed”) (1sg. <i>tĩtĩju</i>), <i>tītinât</i> (“to tease, to annoy”), Lithuanian <i>titinoti</i> (“to brag”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to start, to arise</li></ol><p><i>зате́ять</i> (zatéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erase, to wipe off</li><li>to paint over, to overwrite</li><li>to hold fast, to trap</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to ruin the career of (someone) by scheming</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out through constant use</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to begin to rub</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become silent, to become quiet, to stop speaking, to stop crying</li><li>to die away, to die off</li><li>to calm down, to abate, to subside, to lull</li></ol><p><i>зати́хнуть</i> (zatíxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become silent, to become quiet, to stop speaking, to stop crying</li><li>to die away, to die off</li><li>to calm down, to abate, to subside, to lull</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ти́хнуть</i> (tíxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lull, calm</li><li>quiet conditions, slack (on the market)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ти́х(ий)</i> (tíx(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up, to plug, to cork</li><li>to stick, to thrust</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be stopped, to be corked</li><li>to shut up (stop talking)</li></ol><p><i>заткну́ть</i> (zatknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to make invisible, to eclipse</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obscure, to make unclear (<i>e.g. thoughts</i>)</li><li>to outshine</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to eclipse, to surpass, to outdo, to outshine</li></ol><p><i>затми́ть</i> (zatmítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) eclipse (passage of a planetary object between others)</li><li>blackout, mental derangement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to make invisible, to eclipse</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obscure, to make unclear (<i>e.g. thoughts</i>)</li><li>to outshine</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to eclipse, to surpass, to outdo, to outshine</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тмить</i> (tmitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) however, on the other hand</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>за</i> (za, “for”) +&lrm; <i>то</i> (to, “that”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ЗАТО)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i>закрытое административно-территориальное образование</i> (zakrytoje administrativno-territorialʹnoje obrazovanije, literally “closed administrative territorial formation”): closed cities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push <i>(into)</i>, to shove <i>(into)</i></li><li>to jostle</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push <i>(into)</i>, to shove <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be carried away by a conversation</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backwater</li><li>boatyard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zatopnъ</i>. By surface analysis, deverbal from <i>затону́ть</i> (zatonútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тону́ть</i> (tonútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to stomp/stamp [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘one's feet’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>то́пать</i> (tópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood, to inundate, to submerge</li><li>to sink</li><li>to light <i>(a stove)</i>, to light/kindle the fire <i>(in a stove)</i></li><li>to begin to heat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flooding</li><li>sinking</li></ol><p><i>затопи́ть</i> (zatopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood, to inundate, to submerge</li><li>to sink</li></ol><p><i>затопи́ть</i> (zatopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down, to trample under foot</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congestion, obstruction, blockage, jam, traffic jam</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zatorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brake</li><li>to hinder, to impede, to slow down</li></ol><p><i>затормози́ть</i> (zatormozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brake</li><li>to hinder, to impede, to slow down</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тормози́ть</i> (tormozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to hurry, to start to rush</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>торопи́ться</i> (toropítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to be sad</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to be bored</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to miss, to start to yearn for [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to plaintively express longing for [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тоскова́ть</i> (toskovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to incarcerate, to imprison</li></ol><p><i>заточи́ть</i> (zatočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incarceration, captivity</li></ol><p><i>заточи́ть</i> (zatočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to incarcerate, to imprison</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “sharpen”)</p><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “exude”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharpening (a tool, a knife, a pencil), grinding (an edge on a blade)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a self-made stabbing weapon; shiv, shank</li></ol><p><i>заточи́ть</i> (zatočítʹ, “to sharpen”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool grinder, sharpener, tool dresser, grinderman</li></ol><p><i>заточи́ть</i> (zatočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt down, to bring to bay</li><li>to badger, to intimidate, to make someone's life miserable, to leave no breathing space</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acquired through hunting; trapped (<i>of an animal</i>)</li><li>hounded, harassed, bullied, persecuted (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to affect</li><li>to affect, to wound</li><li>to touch <i>(upon)</i>, to deal <i>(with)</i></li><li>to affect, to infringe</li></ol><p><i>затро́г-</i> (zatróg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) happening during the meal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) insignificant</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) everyday, worn out</li><li>(relational,&#32;obsolete) <i>трапе́з</i> (trapéz, “now obsolete type of cheap cloth”)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тра́пеза</i> (trápeza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>From <i>затрапе́з</i> (zatrapéz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from surname <i>Затрапе́зный</i> (Zatrapéznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expenditure</li><li>cost</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тра́та</i> (tráta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cost-based</li><li>loss-making, running at a loss</li></ol><p><i>затра́та</i> (zatráta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li></ol><p><i>затра́тить</i> (zatrátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to formally request or demand (<i>e.g. a document, someone's appearance, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to tremble</li><li>to begin to palpitate</li><li>to begin to shiver</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>трепета́ть</i> (trepetátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to crack/crackle, <i>(begin to)</i> to tear apart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start chattering</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>треща́ть</i> (treščátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slap in the face, box on the ear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>затра́гивать</i> (zatrágivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>затро́г-</i> (zatróg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to affect</li><li>to affect, to wound</li><li>to touch <i>(upon)</i>, to deal <i>(with)</i></li><li>to affect, to infringe</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тро́нуть</i> (trónutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difficulty, trouble, impediment, encumbrance; obstruction</li></ol><p><i>затрудни́ть</i> (zatrudnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cumbersome, hard, straitened or awkward (situation)</li></ol><p><i>затрудни́ть</i> (zatrudnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hamper, to impede, to complicate</li><li>to give/cause someone trouble, to make difficulties <i>(for)</i>, to embarrass</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have/find difficulty <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>затрудни́ть</i> (zatrudnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hamper, to impede, to complicate</li><li>to give/cause someone trouble, to make difficulties <i>(for)</i>, to embarrass</li></ol><p><i>затрудни́ть</i> (zatrudnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have/find difficulty <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>затрудня́ть</i> (zatrudnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult, laborious, labored (done with effort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shake</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to shake/tremble</li></ol><p><i>затрясти́</i> (zatrjastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to dim, to cloud, to fog</li></ol><p><i>затума́нить</i> (zatumánitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to dim, to cloud, to fog</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тума́нить</i> (tumánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dull, to blunt</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose a sharp edge; to become dull</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harass or torment through repeated demands</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to hang out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>туси́ть</i> (tusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out (of light, fire)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to die down, to diminish, to stop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out (of light, fire)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to die down, to diminish, to stop</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to go bad, to spoil (of food etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ту́хнуть</i> (túxnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>musty, smelling of dampness and rot</li><li>stale, spoiled from long storage</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) conventional, rigid, resistant to change</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zatъxlъ</i>.</p><p>Per Vasmer, a variant of <i>за́дхлый</i> (zádxlyj). By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>дох(ну́ть)</i> (dox(nútʹ), “to breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj). Also related to <i>ту́хлый</i> (túxlyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up, to plug, to cork</li><li>to stick, to thrust</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-тыка́ть</i> (-tykátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be stopped, to be corked</li><li>to shut up (stop talking)</li></ol><p><i>затыка́ть</i> (zatykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nape, back of the head, occiput</li><li>neck</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тыл</i> (tyl) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>заты́лок</i> (zatýlok)</li></ol><p><i>заты́лок</i> (zatýlok, “nape, neck”) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cervical, occipital</li></ol><p><i>заты́лок</i> (zatýlok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plug, spigot</li></ol><p><i>затыка́ть</i> (zatykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tightening</li><li>delaying, protraction</li></ol><p><i>затя́гивать</i> (zatjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten</li><li>to cover, to close</li><li>to procrastinate, to delay, to drag out</li><li>to pull <i>(into)</i></li><li>to drag <i>(into)</i>, to suck <i>(into)</i>, to suck <i>(down)</i>, to engulf, to swallow up</li><li>to swallow up, to absorb, to engross</li><li>to start singing, to begin to harp <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>затяг-</i> (zatjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tightened, to jam</li><li>to grow overcast</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to skin over, to heal (<i>of wounds</i>)</li><li>to be delayed, to be dragged out</li><li>to inhale (cigarette, cigar, smoking pipe)</li></ol><p><i>затя́гивать</i> (zatjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>addictive, captivating (causing or tending to cause addiction&#59; habit-forming)</li><li>bogging</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puff, inhalation (cigarette)</li><li>drawing tight, tightening</li><li>tie-bar, baulk, straining beam, string piece</li></ol><p><i>затяг-</i> (zatjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lengthy, protracted</li></ol><p><i>затя́ж(ка)</i> (zatjáž(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten</li><li>to cover, to close</li><li>to procrastinate, to delay, to drag out</li><li>to pull <i>(into)</i></li><li>to drag <i>(into)</i>, to suck <i>(into)</i>, to suck <i>(down)</i>, to engulf, to swallow up</li><li>to swallow up, to absorb, to engross</li><li>to start singing, to begin to harp <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to tighten one's belt (to be more frugal&#59; to make difficult economic savings due to a lowering of expected income)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tightened, to jam</li><li>to grow overcast</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to skin over, to heal (<i>of wounds</i>)</li><li>to be delayed, to be dragged out</li><li>to inhale (cigarette, cigar, smoking pipe)</li></ol><p><i>затяну́ть</i> (zatjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut narrower, to sharpen, to hone (a log, board, stake, etc.)</li><li>to notch</li></ol><p><i>затеса́ть</i> (zatesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to worm one's way [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>затёсывать</i> (zatjósyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make narrower, to make tighter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make narrower, to make tighter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start smiling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>улыба́ться</i> (ulybátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) in an excessively complicated or unclear manner</li></ol><p><i>зау́мный</i> (zaúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) excessively complicated or unclear</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;countable) bollocks, nonsense (that which is silly, illogical, and lacks any meaning, reason, or value&#59; that which does not make sense)</li><li>(historical,&#32;poetry,&#32;uncountable) Zaum (linguistic experiments in sound symbolism and language creation of Russian Cubo-Futurist poets such as Velimir Khlebnikov and Aleksei Kruchenykh)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>зау́мный</i> (zaúmnyj). Coined by Russian poet, artist, and theorist Aleksei Kruchyonykh.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mournful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordinary, run-of-the-mill, commonplace, mediocre</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare <i>рядово́й</i> (rjadovój, “average, ordinary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agnail, hangnail</li><li>wire-edge, burr</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agnail, hangnail</li><li>wire-edge, burr</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matins</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>у́тро</i> (útro) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), with irregular vowel epenthesis; see <i>у́треня</i> (útrenja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to memorize</li><li>to learn by heart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaučiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix, to set, to settle, to state</li><li>to record</li><li>to fix <i>(a photograph)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>фикси́ровать</i> (fiksírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flex one's muscles</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flex (flaunt one's superiority or show off)</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>фле́ксить</i> (fléksitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show off; to swank</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to boast; to brag; to put on airs</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to aggressively push or promote (something) in an artificial manner in order to generate forced hype, especially online</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>форси́ть</i> (forsítʹ).</p><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>фо́рсить</i> (fórsitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, to ruin, to spoil, to deprave, etc.</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) make a prisoner an <i>опу́щенный</i> (opúščennyj, “bitch, punk”) by raping him</li></ol><p><i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>форшма́чить</i> (foršmáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) to friend, to befriend (to add as a friend)</li></ol><p>Prefix <i>за-</i> (za-) + English <i>friend</i> + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in, to go see (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>заходи́ть</i> (zaxodítʹ)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pick up, to fetch (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>заходи́ть</i> (zaxodítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>заходи́ть</i> (zaxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to mope/sulk</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хандр(а́)</i> (xandr(á)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seizure, capture, taking, take, takeover</li><li>usurpation</li><li>pincher</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) clench, clinch, clutch, hold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, to grip, to grab, to clench, to collar</li><li>to seize, to capture, to occupy, to usurp</li><li>to absorb, to captivate, to possess, to carry away, to thrill, to engross, to enthral</li><li>to catch, to snatch, to take (away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take along</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invader, aggressor, occupier; usurper</li></ol><p><i>захвати́ть</i> (zaxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, to grip, to grab, to clench, to collar</li><li>to seize, to capture, to occupy, to usurp</li><li>to absorb, to captivate, to possess, to carry away, to thrill, to engross, to enthral</li><li>to catch, to snatch, to take (away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take along</li><li>to fascinate</li></ol><p><i>захвати́ть</i> (zaxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intriguing, exciting, breathtaking, thrilling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become sick, to fall ill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to contract, to come down with [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘an illness’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хвора́ть</i> (xvorátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start giggling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хихи́кать</i> (xixíkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke, to swallow the wrong way</li></ol><p><i>захлебну́ть</i> (zaxlebnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow over, to overflow (<i>of a wave</i>)</li><li>to lash (<i>around</i>)</li><li>to sweep over, to overwhelm</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хлестну́ть</i> (xlestnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to sob</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хли́пать</i> (xlípatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to clap</li><li>to interrupt with an applause</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam, to bang (a door shut)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пнуть</i> (xlópnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam to, to be slammed, to close with a bang</li></ol><p><i>захло́пнуть</i> (zaxlópnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam, to bang (a door shut)</li><li>to interrupt with an applause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam to, to be slammed, to close with a bang</li></ol><p><i>захло́пывать</i> (zaxlópyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke, to swallow the wrong way</li></ol><p><i>захлёбывать</i> (zaxljóbyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) overwhelming (with something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow over, to overflow (<i>of a wave</i>)</li><li>to lash (<i>around</i>)</li><li>to sweep over, to overwhelm</li><li>to lash mercilessly</li></ol><p>From <i>захлестну́ть</i> (zaxlestnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>захлеста́ть</i> (zaxlestátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become intoxicated, to drunk, to tipsy or high</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to become exhilarated</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хмеле́ть</i> (xmelétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to whimper</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хныкать</i> (xnykatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recess, set, setting, sundown</li><li>stopping, calling <i>(a ship)</i>, approach <i>(an airplane)</i></li><li>attempt, trial</li><li>sunset</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) west</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zaxodъ</i>. By surface analysis, deverbal from <i>заходи́ть</i> (zaxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (to see), to come in (to see, etc.), to call, to drop in</li><li>to pick up, to fetch</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to call at, to enter (a port)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to get, to advance</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to pass, to draw out</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to approach</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to outflank</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to turn, to disappear, to go behind</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) to set</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to walk oneself off one's feet (imperfective only)</li><li>to be beside oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to become/grow numb</li></ol><p><i>заходи́ть</i> (zaxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backwater, hinterland (a remote place; somewhere that remains unaffected by new events, progresses, ideas, etc.)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; has been compared with the prefix <i>за-</i> (za-) and Old Church Slavonic <i>халѫга</i> (xalǫga, “enclosure, fenced area”) and Slovene <i>halọ́ga</i> (“thicket, seaweed”), but in that case one would expect <i>&#42;захалу́жье</i> (&#42;zaxalúžʹje). Derivation influenced by <i>холосто́й</i> (xolostój, “idle, unmarried”) has also been proposed, but is considered improbable, as well as comparison with <i>холо́п</i> (xolóp) and <i>холу́й</i> (xolúj) is likewise unsatisfactory. Kalima attempts to derive <i>захолу́стье</i> (zaxolústʹje) from <i>&#42;захоло́стье</i> (&#42;zaxolóstʹje) from <i>хо́лост</i> (xólost) but this is phonetically problematic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial, burying; burial place</li></ol><p><i>захорони́ть</i> (zaxoronítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хорони́ть</i> (xoronítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to want, to begin to desire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to want, to desire, to feel like (doing/having/etc.)</li></ol><p><i>захоте́ть</i> (zaxotétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh loudly, to laugh heartily, to roar with laughter, to guffaw</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хохота́ть</i> (xoxotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to snore</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>храпе́ть</i> (xrapétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to wheeze</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to speak hoarsely</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хрипе́ть</i> (xripétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start grunting (of pigs)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хрю́кать</i> (xrjúkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shabby, scrubby; rundown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to hit (someone)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to kill (someone)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to impose a punishment</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do, to attach (something)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>хуя́рить</i> (xujáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start blossoming, to start blooming</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start blossoming, to start blooming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to check out; also sometimes to dig, to rate, to feel (in the sense "to like, to enjoy")</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) train surfer, a person who illegally rides on a train by hanging on its exterior part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hook, to grapple, to fasten</li><li>to catch (accidentally)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ); compare <i>цепля́ть</i> (cepljátʹ), constructed as if derived from &#42;цепи́ть.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch hold <i>(of)</i></li><li>to catch <i>(at)</i>, to grab <i>(at)</i>, to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to seize <i>(on)</i>, to fasten <i>(onto)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>зацепи́ть</i> (zacepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hook, peg</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) snag, hitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) influence, pull</li></ol><p><i>зацепи́ть</i> (zacepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hook, to grapple, to fasten</li><li>to catch (accidentally)</li></ol><p><i>зацепи́ть</i> (zacepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch hold <i>(of)</i></li><li>to catch <i>(at)</i>, to grab <i>(at)</i>, to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to seize <i>(on)</i>, to fasten <i>(onto)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>зацепля́ть</i> (zacepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to repeat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start talking or speaking about one thing repeatedly, to get hung up on something</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to get into an infinite loop</li></ol><p>From <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>цикл</i> (cikl) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enchanted, bewitched</li><li>spellbound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch, to captivate, charm, to enchant, to hex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch, to captivate, charm, to enchant, to hex</li></ol><p><i>зачарова́ть</i> (začarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken</li><li>to begin to clatter/batter/chug</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk at a great speed, to spout words</li><li>to become a frequent visitor <i>(to a place)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>части́ть</i> (častítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often (frequently, many times)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conception (initiation of an embryonic animal life)</li></ol><p><i>зача́ть</i> (začátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embryo</li><li>vestige, rudiment</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) beginning, early stages</li></ol><p><i>зача́тие</i> (začátije) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;začęti</i>, also see Old Church Slavonic <i>зачѧти</i> (začęti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wilt, to wither, to waste away</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ча́хнуть</i> (čáxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) why, what for (for what purpose, to what end, with what goal)</li><li>(<i>indicative</i>) why, what for (for what purpose, to what end, with what goal)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>за чем</i> (za čem), from the preposition <i>за</i> (za) + <i>чем</i> (čem), instrumental singular of <i>что</i> (što, “what”). This was originally marked as Saint Petersburg dialect, while in Moscow <i>зачто́</i> (zaštó) was used as late as the 18th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for some (unknown) reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross out, to strike out, to strike off, to strike through</li><li>to efface, to reduce to nothing, to cancel out</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>черкну́ть</i> (čerknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to scoop, to spoon up/out, to ladle out</li><li>to take in, to ship <i>(water)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>черпну́ть</i> (čerpnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to scoop, to spoon up/out, to ladle out</li><li>to take in, to ship <i>(water)</i></li></ol><p><i>зачерпну́ть</i> (začerpnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb to a certain direction or position</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to begin to scratch (an itch)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to credit, to accept as a credit (e.g. money towards a debt)</li><li>to pass, to give a passing grade to (e.g. coursework, a dissertation)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be accepted (<i>e.g. for a debt</i>), to be credited (<i>towards</i>), to count (<i>towards</i>)</li></ol><p><i>заче́сть</i> (začéstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) beginning</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) introduction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) founder, initiator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceive</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>-чина́ть</i> (-činátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instigator, ringleader</li></ol><p><i>зачина́ть</i> (začinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enrolment, admission</li></ol><p><i>зачи́слить</i> (začíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrol, to enlist, to engage, to take in (students), to hire, to take on</li><li>to record, to enter, to include</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrol, to enlist, to engage, to take in (students), to hire, to take on</li><li>to record, to enter, to include</li></ol><p><i>зачи́слить</i> (začíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleaning, clearing</li><li>trimming</li><li>(house to house) search</li><li>(<i>military</i>) clearing operation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read out</li><li>to start reading</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out by reading too much</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail to return a borrowed book</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get carried away reading, to read too long</li></ol><p><i>зачита́ть</i> (začitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read out</li><li>to start reading</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out by reading too much</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail to return a borrowed book</li><li>to credit, to accept as a credit (e.g. money towards a debt)</li><li>to pass, to give a passing grade to (e.g. coursework, a dissertation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get carried away reading, to read too long</li><li>to be accepted (<i>e.g. for a debt</i>), to be credited (<i>towards</i>), to count (<i>towards</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зачи́тывать</i> (začítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to say "chur"</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>чура́ться</i> (čurátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross out, to strike out, to strike off, to strike through</li><li>to efface, to reduce to nothing, to cancel out</li></ol><p><i>зачеркну́ть</i> (začerknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb to a certain direction or position</li></ol><p><i>зачеса́ть</i> (začesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test, pretest, examination</li><li>(finance,&#32;trading) setoff, offset</li><li>pass (of a task, test)</li><li>reckoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to walk</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шага́ть</i> (šagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to start to shake, to start to sway</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шата́ть</i> (šatátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to start to shake, to start to sway</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to start to lose influence or significance</li></ol><p><i>зашата́ть</i> (zašatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start rustling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шебурша́ть</i> (šeburšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to stir/move</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шевели́ть</i> (ševelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to stir/budge</li></ol><p><i>зашевели́ть</i> (zaševelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to whisper</li><li>to whisper a spell <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise, to hurt</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to drink hard, to hit the bottle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive) to make money</li></ol><p><i>зашиби́ть</i> (zašibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise, to hurt</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to drink hard, to hit the bottle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive) to make money</li></ol><p><i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew up, to stitch up</li><li>to suture</li></ol><p><i>заши́ть</i> (zašítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to hiss</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шипе́ть</i> (šipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew up, to stitch up</li><li>to suture</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encode, to encrypt</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encode, to encrypt</li></ol><p><i>зашифрова́ть</i> (zašifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to go through the roof, to go ham</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шкала́</i> (škalá) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) shameful act, act of disrespect</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>зашква́рить</i> (zaškváritʹ, “to defile (criminal slang)”, literally “to fry until cracklings form”), related to <i>шква́рка</i> (škvárka, “crackling”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lace up</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шнур</i> (šnur) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lace up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrow-mindedness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blinkered (wearing blinkers or blinders)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) blinkered (having tunnel vision&#59; unable to see what is happening around one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to close using a curtain</li></ol><p><i>зашто́рить</i> (zaštóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to close using a curtain</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>што́ра</i> (štóra) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(begin to)</i> make a noise, to become noisy</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шуме́ть</i> (šumétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start rustling</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>шурша́ть</i> (šuršátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beging to chirp</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ще́бет</i> (ščébet) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch; to jam; to squeeze</li><li>to start to pain, to start to hurt (<i>also morally, figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>щеми́ть</i> (ščemítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch; to jam; to squeeze</li><li>to start to pain, to start to hurt (<i>also morally, figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>защеми́ть</i> (zaščemítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defense, protection, advocacy</li><li>aegis, shield, armour, shelter, cover</li><li>protection</li><li>defense, guard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend, to guard, to protect</li><li>to speak in support (of), to stand up for, to support, to vindicate, to advocate</li><li><i>in a court of law</i> to defend, to plead for</li><li><i>thesis, diploma</i> to uphold, to defend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>защитити</i> (zaštititi). By surface analysis, <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>щит</i> (ščit) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend oneself, to protect oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defend one's thesis</li></ol><p><i>защити́ть</i> (zaščitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defender, protector</li><li>(<i>Criminal law</i>) defence counsel</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) full-back</li></ol><p><i>защити́ть</i> (zaščitítʹ, “to defend”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>protective</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) safety</li></ol><p><i>защити́ть</i> (zaščitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protective skin cream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend, to guard, to protect</li><li>to speak in support (of), to stand up for, to support, to vindicate, to advocate</li><li>to defend, to plead for (<i>in a court of law</i>)</li><li>to uphold, to defend (a thesis, diploma or dissertation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>защищати</i> (zaštištati). By surface analysis, <i>защити́ть</i> (zaščitítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend oneself, to protect oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defend one's thesis</li></ol><p><i>защища́ть</i> (zaščiščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protectability</li></ol><p><i>защищённый</i> (zaščiščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock mechanism</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bolt, latch, catch</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>за-</i> (za-) + a deverbal form of <i>щёлкать</i> (ščólkatʹ, “to click”) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to crack, to snap, to click, etc. [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a whip, one's heels, one's fingers, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to begin to click (to begin to make a clicking sound)</li><li>to begin to flick (someone)</li><li>to begin to warble (<i>of a bird</i>)</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>щёлкать</i> (ščólkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth&#32;slang</i>) to use</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ю́зать</i> (júzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;childish,&#32;endearing) hare, bunny</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;endearing) baby, honey, sweetie (a term of affection for a lover or a child)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>за́яц</i> (zájac, “hare”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicant (one who applies)</li><li>declarant (a person who makes a formal declaration or statement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, to announce</li><li>to say (of a high-rank person or ironically), to claim, to state</li><li>to apply (to submit oneself as a candidate)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to file [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a complaint’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘to authorities or police’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to appear, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>заяви́ть</i> (zajavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>application, claim, request, demand</li></ol><p><i>заяви́ть</i> (zajavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declaration, statement</li><li>petition, application</li></ol><p><i>заяви́ть</i> (zajavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, to announce</li><li>to say (of a high-rank person or ironically), to claim, to state</li><li>to apply (to submit oneself as a candidate)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to file [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a complaint’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘to authorities or police’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>заяви́ть</i> (zajavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to appear, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>заявля́ть</i> (zajavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inveterate, confirmed</li><li>avid, enthusiastic</li><li>true, genuine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>zajadły</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare, jackrabbit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one who uses public transportation without buying a ticket, fare dodger, stowaway</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zajęcь</i>, <i>&#42;zajьcь</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Finnish <i>jänis</i> (“hare; stowaway”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>заяц</i> (zajac, “hare”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hare; hare's</li></ol><p><i>за́яц</i> (zájac) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) harelip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to wear out (someone) by fucking</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (someone) to death (also figuratively)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to pester, to exasperate, to piss off</li></ol><p>From <i>заеба́ть</i> (zajebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) loan</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) borrowed, not one's own</li></ol><p><i>заём</i> (zajóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borrower, debtor</li></ol><p><i>заём</i> (zajóm) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to begin to fidget</li></ol><p><i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>ёрзать</i> (jórzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сби́тень</i> (sbítenʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title, rank, status (indicating qualifications, official recognition, etc.)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rank</li></ol><p><i>звать</i> (zvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invited</li><li>with invited guests</li></ol><p><i>звать</i> (zvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ный</i> (-ányj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) vocative (relating to the case used to express the person to which one is calling)</li></ol><p>From <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ, “to call”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective—forming suffix), a calque of Latin <i>vōcātīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call</li><li>to invite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зъвати</i> (zŭvati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъvati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be called</li></ol><p><i>зва́ть</i> (zvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) star (celestial body)</li><li>star (celebrity)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) star (geometric shape)</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) spider</li><li>Zvezda (module of the International Space Station)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>звѣзда</i> (zvězda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gvězda</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źwaisdā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwoysdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) Star of David</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asterisk (instrument with radiating arms placed over the diskos used during the Eucharist)</li></ol><p>From <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;science fiction</i>) spacefarer (one who travels in space)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) astrologer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring, to jingle, to clink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvьněti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>link (element of a chain)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) part, branch; team, squad (smallest unit in a hierarchy or sequence)</li><li>(aviation,&#32;military) flight, squad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvenò</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>link collector <i>(jet fighter)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ringing, clinking, tinkling, jingling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fly into a rage</li><li>to become a beast, to become brutalized</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvěrěti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menagerie; zoo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>animal, feral</li><li>brutal, savage, wild, feral</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvěrinъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hunter, especially one who hunts marine mammals</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) St. John's wort, a plant of the genus <i>Hypericum</i>.</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;anim (zverʹ, “animal; mammal”) +&lrm; <i>бой</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;inan (boj, “fighting, combat”)</p><p>Uncertain. Proposed to be altered by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beastlike</li></ol><p>From <i>звер(ь)</i> (zver(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>monstrously, beastly, cruelly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awfully, terribly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brutal, bestial, atrocious, savage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) terrific, tremendous</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atrocity, brutality (extremely cruel act)</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ): little animal, beastie</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal, beast</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brute</li><li>(<i>sciences</i>) mammal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>звѣрь</i> (zvěrĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvěrь</i>. Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr) and Latin <i>ferus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) wild animals</li><li>(collective,&#32;figurative) rascals; wild, violent people</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ё</i> (-jó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ): little animal, beastie</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ю́шка</i> (-júška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ): small beast</li></ol><p><i>зверь</i> (zverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little animal, baby animal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring, jingle, chime (sound)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvonъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell tower</li></ol><p><i>звона́рь</i> (zvonárʹ, “bellringer”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell ringer / bell-ringer / bellringer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scandal-monger, gossip</li></ol><p>From <i>звон</i> (zvon) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call (to contact by telephone) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to call [<i>with</i> locative adverb <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>to cause to ring [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a bell, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ring (to make a ringing sound) (<i>a bell, a telephone, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>звонити</i> (zvoniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvoniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sonorous, clear, soniferous</li><li>resonant</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) voiced</li></ol><p><i>звон</i> (zvon) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>resoundingly</li><li>clearly</li></ol><p><i>зво́нкий</i> (zvónkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell (signaling device such as a bicycle bell)</li><li>bell (an audible signal such as a school bell)</li><li>ring, call (a telephone call or telephone conversation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>звоно́к</i> (zvonók): small bell (device for making a ringing sound)</li></ol><p><i>звоно́к</i> (zvonók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvǫkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) sound wave (longitudinal wave of pressure transmitted through any plastic material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound card</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sound, audio</li></ol><p><i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound recording, audio recording (process or result)</li></ol><p><i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>за́пись</i> (zápisʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soundproofing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soundproof</li></ol><p>From <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onomatopoeia</li></ol><p><i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подража́ние</i> (podražánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickup, sound receiver (electronic device for detecting sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, sounding; phonation</li></ol><p><i>звуча́ть</i> (zvučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound</li></ol><p><i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sounding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>loudly, sonorously</li></ol><p><i>зву́чный</i> (zvúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resounding, sonorous</li></ol><p><i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>plink, bang (a short, high-pitched percussive or metallic sound)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zvękъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tinkle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>звꙗкати</i> (zvjakati). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>звꙗкати</i> (zvjakati), Slovene <i>zvékati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tinkle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a buzz, to give a tinkle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stars and Stripes</li><li>(informal,&#32;figuratively) American, Yankee</li></ol><p><i>звёздный</i> (zvjózdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полоса́тый</i> (polosátyj), calque of English <i>Stars and Stripes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) star; stellar (relating to stars)</li></ol><p><i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) stellar wind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá): small star</li><li>star, asterisk (a symbol resembling a small star)</li><li>chain-wheel, sprocket, star wheel</li><li>(<i>exercise</i>) jumping jack</li></ol><p><i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gvězda</i> + diminutive suffix <i>-очка</i> (-očka) (from diminutive infixes <i>-к-</i> (-k-) + <i>-к-</i> (-k-)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>звёздочка</i> (zvjózdočka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building, edifice, structure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>зьданиѥ</i> (zĭdanije). By surface analysis, <i>здать</i> (zdatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) yo, hey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сьдесь</i> (sĭdesĭ), from Old East Slavic <i>сьде</i> (sĭde) + Old East Slavic <i>сь</i> (sĭ). Cognate with Slovene <i>zdè</i>, Czech <i>zde</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>сьде</i> (sĭde).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) local, relating to this place (the place where the speaker is currently located)</li></ol><p><i>зде(сь)</i> (zde(sʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet, to salute, to say hello/hi</li></ol><p><i>здоро́во</i> (zdoróvo) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very strong, muscular (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) huge, enormous</li></ol><p><i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become healthier or stronger</li></ol><p><i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) great, very well, awesomely</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very strongly, to a very great extent</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) great!, well done</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hi!; hello!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy, sound</li><li>strong, robust</li><li>wholesome, salubrious</li><li>big, large</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъdorvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>здра́вый</i> (zdrávyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>health</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъdorvьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). Doublet of <i>здра́вие</i> (zdrávije), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) healthy, robust person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) husky (big man or animal)</li></ol><p><i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj, “healthy”): (<i>colloquial</i>) healthy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) health</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съдравьѥ</i> (sŭdravĭje), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъdorvьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>здра́вый</i> (zdrávyj) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Doublet of <i>здоро́вье</i> (zdoróvʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toast (salutation while drinking alcohol)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanatorium, health resort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reasonably; soundly; sensibly</li></ol><p><i>здра́вый</i> (zdrávyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasonableness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sane, sensible (mentally sound, possessing a rational mind)</li></ol><p><i>здра́во</i> (zdrávo, “soundly”) +&lrm; <i>мы́слящий</i> (mýsljaščij, “thinking”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public health (service), health protection, health care</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be well, to prosper, to thrive</li></ol><p><i>здра́вый</i> (zdrávyj, “healthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) hello</li></ol><p>From imperative form of <i>здравствовать</i> (zdravstvovatʹ, “to be well, to prosper”), from adjective <i>здравый</i> (zdravyj) borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съдравъ</i> (sŭdravŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъdorvъ</i> of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;humorous) exclamation of surprise or displeasure.</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;humorous) exclamation of displeasure towards someone seen as stupid.</li></ol><p>Literally, "Hello, asshole, Happy New Year to you!"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal&#32;or&#32;plural form</i>) hello</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensible, sound, sane, reasonable</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) healthy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съдравъ</i> (sŭdravŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъdorvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj), the inherited Old East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common sense (ordinary understanding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>здра́вие</i> (zdrávije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hi, hello, howdy, abbreviated form of <i>здра́вствуйте</i> (zdrávstvujte)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Excuse you! <i>(expression of surprise and often disagreement with what someone has just said)</i></li></ol><p>Corruption of <i>здра́вствуйте</i> (zdrávstvujte).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>здра́сте</i> (zdráste)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>здесь</i> (zdesʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) zebra</li><li>zebra crossing (a pedestrian crosswalk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Western European language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zebu, <i>Bos taurus indicus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat, gullet, pharynx</li><li><i>(technical)</i> span <i>(of a wrench)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zěvъ</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, gaper, gawker, spectator; person who is primarily busy looking at something for fun, and without a particular purpose</li></ol><p><i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ, “to gape”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare <i>раззя́ва</i> (razzjáva) (from the same root).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>gaper</i> (a distant cognate via PIE).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to gape</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss (a train, a stop, a chance, an opportunity, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zěvati</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>зіва́ти</i> (ziváty), Russian Church Slavonic <i>зѣвати</i> (zěvati), Serbo-Croatian <i>зиjѐвати</i> (first singular <i>зи̏jева̑м</i>), Slovene <i>zévati</i>, Czech <i>zívat</i>, Polish <i>ziewać</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>zywać</i>. Also cognate with <i>зия́ть</i> (zijátʹ) as well as Lithuanian <i>žióvauti</i> and Latvian <i>žàvât</i>, as well as Old High German <i>giwēn</i>, <i>gewōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn (once)</li></ol><p><i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну-</i> (-nu-)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yawn</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) temporary loss of vigilance (<i>usually leading to negative results</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a fit of) yawning</li></ol><p><i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>зэк</i> (zɛk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>зе́лено</i> (zéleno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, turn green</li><li>to show green</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>teal</li></ol><p><i>зеленова́тый</i> (zelenovátyj, “greenish”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>голубо́й</i> (golubój, “light blue”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greenish grey</li></ol><p><i>зеленова́т(ый)</i> (zelenovát(yj), “greenish”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “grey”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greenish</li></ol><p><i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corkwing (fish, <i>Symphodus</i>)</li><li>blue damselfish (fish, <i>Chromis chromis</i>)</li><li>greenfinch (European greenfinch, <i>Chloris chloris</i>)</li><li>man on horseback, yellow knight, saddle-shaped tricholoma (mushroom, <i>Tricholoma equestre</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) greenery, verdure (vegetation)</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) greens (leaves and green stems of plants eaten as vegetables or in salads)</li><li>green (<i>color or pigment</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dollars, greenbacks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young shoots of winter crops</li></ol><p>Compare <i>зе́лень</i> (zélenʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) very, exceedingly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dzělo</i>. It could also be a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potion (a small portion or dose of a liquid which is medicinal, poisonous, or magical)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) gunpowder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zelьje</i> (“vegetation, herbs”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj, adjective)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brilliant green (dye and antiseptic)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) thickets used to hide from opponents</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) state certificate attesting to the right to real estate</li><li>(<i>agriculture slang</i>) green fodder for cattle</li></ol><p><i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Green army militiaman (member of one of the Green army peasant militias during the Russian Civil War of 1918 to 1922)</li></ol><p>From <i>зелё̀но-</i> (zeljòno-, “green”) +&lrm; <i>арме́ец</i> (arméjec, “infantryman”), as a pun on the <i>кра̀сноарме́йцы</i> (kràsnoarméjcy, “red infantrymen”) of the Red Army and the <i>белогварде́йцы</i> (belogvardéjcy, “white guardsmen”) of the White Army, in reference to the vast forests of rural Russia in which they operated.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zielona Góra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>green</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) inexperienced, raw</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) sickly, deathly pale (of a face)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) verdant, lush with vegetation (of a place)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green, environmentalist (a member of a green party)</li><li>greenhorn, new recruit</li><li>(slang,&#32;inanimate) greenback, U.S. dollar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zelenъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i>; whence English <i>yellow</i>, <i>gold</i>, <i>glow</i> and <i>chlorine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime (a green citrus fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green onion, spring onion, scallion (<i>Allium fistulosum</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green light (literal and idiomatic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green tea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) land; agricultural</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landowner</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>владе́лец</i> (vladélec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landownership, landholding</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>владе́ние</i> (vladénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer, tiller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farming, agriculture, husbandry, agronomy (the art or science of cultivating the ground)</li><li>Zemledeliye (a Russian remote minelaying system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) farming</li></ol><p><i>земледе́л(ие)</i> (zemledél(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>земледе́лие</i> (zemledélije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navvy, digger (a laborer engaged in excavation)</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-, combining interfix) +&lrm; <i>коп(а́ть)</i> (kop(átʹ), “to dig”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land use</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзование</i> (pólʹzovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explorer, discoverer (of unknown lands)</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>проходи́ть</i> (proxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrew (mammal)</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “earth, soil”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-рой</i> (-roj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on a combining form of <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ, “to dig”) (compare 1sg. present <i>ро́ю</i> (róju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthquake</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>трясе́ние</i> (trjasénije, “shaking, trembling”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>земля́</i> (zemljá): soil; land</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth</li><li>land</li><li>ground, soil</li><li>(poetic,&#32;dated,&#32;countable) country, land</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) state, <i>Land</i> (first-level administrative division of Germany)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>землꙗ</i> (zemlja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zemľa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źémē</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>क्षम्</i> (kṣam), Avestan <i>𐬰𐬀𐬨</i> (zam), Latin <i>humus</i>, the source of English <i>human</i>; Ancient Greek <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn), Persian زمین (zamin).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Земля)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Earth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) land ahoy</li></ol><p>Literally, “the land is seen”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Judaism,&#32;Christianity) the Promised Land</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) dream (hope or wish)</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) heaven, utopia (place where everything is perfect)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compatriot, countryman</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) strawberry, the genus <i>Fragaria</i></li><li>wild strawberry (<i>Fragaria vesca</i>)</li></ol><p><i>земляно́й</i> (zemljanój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka) from Russian <i>земля́</i> (zemljá). Akin to Polish <i>poziomka</i>, Russian <i>па́земка</i> (pázemka), Serbo-Croatian <i>поземљуша</i>, Pannonian Rusyn <i>жемна ягода</i> (žemna jahoda).</p><p>Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþōbazją</i> (whence German <i>Erdbeere</i>, Danish <i>jordbær</i>, Old English <i>eorþberġe</i> (<i>earthberry</i>) and others).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terran, Earthling</li></ol><p><i>Земля́</i> (Zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) strawberry</li></ol><p><i>земляни́ка</i> (zemljaníka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dugout, earth-house, (mud) hut, zemlyanka</li><li>a female Earthling (a woman or girl from Earth)</li></ol><p><i>земляно́й</i> (zemljanój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>земля́н(ин)</i> (zemlján(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) earth</li><li>earthen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zemľanъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aardwolf (the mammal species Proteles cristatus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peanut</li><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) tiger nut, chufa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthworm (animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) swede, rutabaga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a men's society organized by home region in late 19th century Russia</li></ol><p><i>земля́к</i> (zemlják) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>земля́к</i> (zemlják)</li></ol><p><i>земля́к</i> (zemlják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphibian</li></ol><p>Neuter of земново́дный (zemnovódnyj) (from земно́й (zemnój) + во́дный (vódnyj)) (живо́тное&nbsp;n (živótnoje)) - "earthly-watery (animal)"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amphibious (capable of functioning on land or in water)</li></ol><p><i>земно́й</i> (zemnój, “terrestrial”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́дный</i> (vódnyj, “aquatic”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) earth; terrestrial</li><li>earthly</li><li>earthy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zemьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) people's</li><li>(relational,&#32;historical) zemstvo</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) land</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>зем-</i> (zem-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) zemstvo</li></ol><p><i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zenith, vertex, culmination, meridian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>zénith</i>. Several steps omitting from Arabic سمت, from Latin <i>sēmita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;military) anti-aircraft gun</li></ol><p><i>зени́т(ный)</i> (zenít(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zenith; zenithal</li><li>(<i>military</i>) antiaircraft / anti-aircraft, surface-to-air (of artillery or missiles&#58; intended for surface-to-air fire)</li></ol><p>From <i>зени́т</i> (zenít, “zenith”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>The military sense refers to the fact that antiaircraft guns are designed to fire with a very high angle of elevation (“towards the zenith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) anti-aircraft missile system, (surface-to-air missile system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) operator, or a crew member of a surface-to-air anti-aircraft missile system</li></ol><p><i>зени́т(ка)</i> (zenít(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;poetic) pupil (<i>of the eye</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zěnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple of someone's eye (favourite&#47;favorite, a particular preference, or a loved one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) core drill, end reamer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Senker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>zepto- (10−21)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirror, looking glass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrkadlo</i>, from <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> (whence Old Church Slavonic <i>зьрѣти</i> (zĭrěti, “to look at”)) + a suffix originating in Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tlom</i>. Probably a calque of Latin <i>speculum</i> (whence Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spēgl</i>, whence German <i>Spiegel</i> and many others). Compare Kashubian <i>szpédżel</i> and <i>zdrzadło</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Bulgarian <i>огледало</i> (ogledalo), Serbo-Croatian <i>огледало</i> (<&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ględati</i>, akin to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ględěti</i>, whence <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reflecting, mirror-like</li><li>very smooth</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mirror</li></ol><p><i>зе́ркало</i> (zérkalo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зе́ркало</i> (zérkalo): (a small) mirror</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>зерни́стый</i> (zernístyj)</li><li>grain, grit (a linear texture of a material or surface)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grainy, granular</li></ol><p><i>зерно́</i> (zernó) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain, cereal</li><li>seed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrno</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂nóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain carrier (truck or ship that transports grain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain cart</li><li>grain truck</li></ol><p><i>зерново́з</i> (zernovóz) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grain, cereal</li></ol><p><i>зерно́</i> (zernó) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain forage; grain feed</li></ol><p><i>зерно́</i> (zernó) +&lrm; <i>фура́ж</i> (furáž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grain forage; grain feed</li></ol><p><i>зернофура́ж</i> (zernofuráž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granary</li></ol><p><i>зерно</i> (zerno) +&lrm; <i>храни́лище</i> (xranílišče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granulating (jewellery)</li><li>old gambling game of dice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) zero</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>zéro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;folkloric) mirror or similar reflective surface (e.g. of a river)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) mirror armor (a type of cuirass)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>зрьцало</i> (zrĭcalo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrcadlo</i>. Compare Polish <i>zwierciadło</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>зр̀цало</i> / Serbo-Croatian <i>zr̀calo</i>. Doublet of native <i>зе́ркало</i> (zérkalo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter Z, z.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;politics) a supporter of the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>Z</i> +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) zettabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>зетта-</i> (zɛtta-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) zettabit</li></ol><p>From <i>зетта-</i> (zɛtta-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zephyr</li><li>marshmallow, or a similar Eastern European confectionery</li></ol><p>From French <i>zéphyr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Hitlergruß, Nazi salute</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;politics) The symbol Z used by supporters of the Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>From German <i>Sieg</i>, as in "Sieg Heil!".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to give a Nazi salute (once)</li></ol><p><i>зи́га</i> (zíga) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zigzag</li></ol><p>From French <i>zigzag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to give a Nazi salute (repeatedly/customarily)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;slang) to support or publicly express far-right or Chauvinist ideas</li></ol><p><i>зи́га</i> (zíga) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ). Compare with Ukrainian <i>зіґува́ти</i> (ziguváty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zygote (fertilized egg cell)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creative</li></ol><p><i>зи́ждить</i> (zížditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to build, to create</li><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to establish</li><li>(literary,&#32;dated,&#32;rhetorical) to base on</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Church Slavonic; compare Old Church Slavonic <i>зьдати</i> (zĭdati) (1sg. <i>зиждѫ</i> (ziždǫ)), and see Russian <i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ) for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to be based / founded (on), to rest (on)</li></ol><p><i>зи́ждить</i> (zížditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ziziphora (<i>Ziziphora</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ziziphora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зима</i> (zima), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zima</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źeimā́ˀ</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéyōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Zimbabwean</li></ol><p><i>Зимба́бве</i> (Zimbábvɛ) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) winter</li><li>wintry, hibernal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zimьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зима́</i> (zimá) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice road; a seasonal winter road over frozen terrain and/or frozen bodies of water</li></ol><p><i>зима́</i> (zimá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hibernation (state of inactivity during winter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to winter, to spend winter</li><li>to hibernate</li></ol><p>From <i>зима́</i> (zimá) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wintering, spending the winter; hibernation</li></ol><p><i>зимова́ть</i> (zimovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winterer</li></ol><p><i>зимова́ть</i> (zimovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wintering</li><li>winter hut</li></ol><p><i>зимова́ть</i> (zimovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in winter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingfisher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;Central Asia,&#32;Ottoman Turkey,&#32;Iran,&#32;Afghanistan,&#32;Iran,&#32;Pakistan&#32;or&#32;North Caucasus) (<i>historical</i>) zindan (an improvised cell consisting of a hole in the ground covered with a metal grille)</li><li>(Chechnya,&#32;Ukraine) any improvised earth prison during the two Chechen wars, and during the Russo-Ukrainian War in the territories occupied by Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian زِنْدَان (zindān) and/or Ottoman Turkish زندان (zindan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ziploc bag (resealable plastic bag)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zip fastener</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>zipper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) zipun (medieval Russian type of peasant upper garment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Venetan <i>zipón</i>, possibly via Greek <i>ζιπούνι</i> (zipoúni), since the 16th century. Ultimately from Arabic جُبَّة (jubba, “long garment”). Doublet of <i>жупа́н</i> (župán), <i>шу́ба</i> (šúba), and <i>ю́бка</i> (júbka). Also doublet of <i>дже́мпер</i> (džémper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cumin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zirvak (a garnish for pilaf is made from meat, onions, carrots and other vegetables)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Uzbek <i>zirvak</i> or Tajik <i>зирвак</i> (zirvak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeta (the modern Greek letter Ζ (Z)&#47;ζ (z))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>ζήτα</i> (zíta). Doublet of <i>дзе́та</i> (dzéta) and <i>зэд</i> (zɛd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) hiatus (syllable break between two vowels)</li></ol><p><i>зия́ть</i> (zijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to gape, to yawn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zijati</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>žióti</i> (“to open (the mouth) wide”) (first singular <i>žióju</i>) <i>žióvauti</i> (“to yawn”), Latvian <i>žàvât</i> (“to yawn”), Ancient Greek <i>χαίνω</i> (khaínō, “to yawn”), <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn”), Latin <i>hiāre</i> (“to yawn”), Sanskrit <i>विहायस्</i> (vihāyas, “airspace”). See also <i>yawn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) herb, grass, cereal, graminoid <i>(type of grass)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>злакъ</i> (zlakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zolkъ</i>. The hypothetical East Slavic inherited form <i>&#42;золокъ</i> (&#42;zolokŭ) is unattested.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) jewel beetle, buprestid</li></ol><p><i>зла́то</i> (zláto, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) <i>Chrysobothris</i>-genus beetle.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>зла́тка</i> (zlátka, “jewel beetle”) +&lrm; <i>ямчатокры́лый</i> (jamčatokrýlyj, “hole-winged”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) gold</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>злато</i> (zlato), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zolto</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) gold, golden</li></ol><p><i>зла́то</i> (zláto) +&lrm; <i>-ой</i> (-oj). Doublet of <i>зло́тый</i> (zlótyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden-feathered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) golden-horned; gilded-horned (having golden or gilded horns)</li></ol><p>From <i>злато</i> (zlato, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, possessive adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;now&#32;ironic) eloquent orator, gold-tongued orator, master of rhetoric</li></ol><p>From <i>злато́й</i> (zlatój, “golden”) +&lrm; <i>уста́</i> (ustá, “mouth”). Ultimately a calque of Ancient Greek <i>χρυσόστομος</i> (khrusóstomos), perhaps through Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke to anger</li></ol><p>From <i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry</li></ol><p><i>злить</i> (zlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, bad, wrong</li><li>harm</li><li>malice, rage, anger, grudge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зъло</i> (zŭlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitterness (with regards to an animate being)</li><li>spite, malice</li><li>rage, anger</li><li>grudge</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>зълоба</i> (zŭloba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъloba</i>, from <i>&#42;zъlъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-oba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-оба</i> (-oba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>maliciously, maleficently, malevolently</li><li>angrily, hatefully</li></ol><p><i>зло́бный</i> (zlóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitterful, spiteful, malicious, malignant, bitter, wicked</li></ol><p><i>зло́ба</i> (zlóba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>topical (of current importance)</li></ol><p>From <i>зло́ба</i> (zlóba, “fury”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>днев-</i> (dnev-, “day”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), based on the expression <i>зло́ба дня́</i> (zlóba dnjá, “something of acute current importance”, literally “the fury of the day”), using the combining form of <i>день</i> (denʹ) (compare e.g. <i>двухдне́вный</i> (dvuxdnévnyj, “lasting two days”), <i>дневно́й</i> (dnevnój, “day (<i>relational</i>)”)). A reference to Matthew 6:34, "Sufficient unto the day <i>is</i> the evil thereof", meaning that there is enough trouble today (so one should not worry about tomorrow).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be / feel angry, to bear malice</li></ol><p><i>зло́ба</i> (zlóba) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ominously (in an ominous manner)</li></ol><p><i>злове́щий</i> (zlovéščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ominous, ill-boding, sinister</li></ol><p>From <i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́щий</i> (véščij, “prophetic, oracular”). Compare Bulgarian <i>злове́щ</i> (zlovéšt), Polish <i>złowieszczy</i>, Belarusian <i>злаве́сны</i> (zlavjésny), Ukrainian <i>злові́сний</i> (zlovísnyj), Czech <i>zlověstný</i>, Slovak <i>zlovestný</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stench, foul smell</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вонь</i> (vonʹ, “stench”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fetid, smelly, foul-smelling</li></ol><p>From <i>злово́ние</i> (zlovónije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from <i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) + <i>вонь</i> (vonʹ, “stench”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) harmful computer program, a piece of malware</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>зловре́дный</i> (zlovrédnyj, “harmful, noxious, pernicious”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, pernicious (causing damage or harm)</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вред</i> (vred, “harm”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malefactor, evildoer, villain, miscreant, scoundrel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъlodějь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-дей</i> (-dej), combining form of <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ, “to do”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdeed, villainy, evil deed</li></ol><p><i>злоде́й</i> (zlodéj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdeed, outrage, villainy, atrocity</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дея́ние</i> (dejánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wicked, evil, ill-natured</li><li>malicious, malevolent, spiteful</li><li>angry, enraged, "mad"</li><li>rabid, irate, venomous</li><li>fierce, severe, bitter</li><li>ill, harmful, evil, bad</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pungent, stinging, painfully spicy</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) malignant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъlъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źulas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) malignant (tending to cause death)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) malignant, malevolent</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) immoral</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ка́чество</i> (káčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mishap, misfortune, misadventure</li></ol><p><i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>(при)ключе́ние</i> ((pri)ključénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) dolose, malevolent, perfidious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>зълокъзньнъ</i> (zŭlokŭznĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>зло-</i> (zlo-) +&lrm; <i>козни</i> (kozni) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) malicious, ill-intentioned</li></ol><p><i>зл(о)</i> (zl(o), “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>наме́ренный</i> (namérennyj, “intentional”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vindictive, rancorous, unforgiving, likely to hold a grudge</li></ol><p>From Middle Russian <i>злопамятной</i> (zlopamjatnoj), from Old East Slavic <i>злопомнити</i> (zlopomniti, “to remember evil”). Likely from an unattested Old Church Slavonic form calquing Ancient Greek <i>μνησίκακος</i> (mnēsíkakos). By surface analysis, <i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ, “memory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-starred, ill-fated</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>злополоучьнъ</i> (zlopolučĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>получи́ть</i> (polučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil willer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gloatingly</li><li>spitefully, malevolently</li></ol><p><i>злора́дный</i> (zlorádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloating</li><li>spiteful, malevolent</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schadenfreude, epicaricacy (delight in someone else's misfortune)</li></ol><p>From <i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo), a liberal calque of German <i>Schadenfreude</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gloat, to feel schadenfreude/epicaricacy</li></ol><p><i>злора́дство</i> (zlorádstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slander, scandal, backbiting (false and malicious talk, gossip)</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slander, to traduce, to backbite (to make defamatory statements about another)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъlosloviti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malicious</li><li>fraudulent</li><li>persistent (<i>offender</i>)</li></ol><p><i>злость</i> (zlostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spite, malice, rage, anger, grudge (a strong feeling of displeasure, hostility or antagonism towards someone or something)</li></ol><p><i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Compare with Ukrainian <i>злість</i> (zlistʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ill-starred, ill-fated</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сча́стье</i> (sčástʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) zloty (currency of Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>złoty</i>. Doublet of <i>золото́й</i> (zolotój) and <i>злато́й</i> (zlatój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plot with evil intent, to intend something bad, to wish someone harm</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>умы́сел</i> (umýsel, “design, intention”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malefactor, plotter, evildoer</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>умы́шлен(ный)</i> (umýšlen(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abuse, to misuse, to overdo</li></ol><p><i>зло</i> (zlo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>употреби́ть</i> (upotrebítʹ, “to use”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abuse, misuse</li><li>excessive usage</li></ol><p><i>злоупотреби́ть</i> (zloupotrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abuse, to misuse</li><li>to make excessive use</li></ol><p><i>злоупотреби́ть</i> (zloupotrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) evil woman; she-devil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) a vicious or angry person</li></ol><p><i>злой</i> (zloj) +&lrm; <i>-ю́ка</i> (-júka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics,&#32;neologism) "zmahar" or "zmagar", oppositionist, someone who opposes Lukashenka regime in Belarus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>змага́р</i> (zmahár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) coil (pipe)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) serpentine, ophite</li></ol><p><i>змея́</i> (zmejá) +&lrm; <i>-еви́к</i> (-evík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragonhead (<i>Dracocephalum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>змея́</i> (zmejá, “snake”) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), a calque of translingual <i>Dracocephalum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short-toed snake eagle (<i>Circaetus gallicus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>змея</i> (zmeja) +&lrm; <i>яд</i> (jad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snake; serpentine</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) serpentine, venomous, viperine</li></ol><p><i>змея́</i> (zmejá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snake, to meander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;mythology&#32;or&#32;biblical) serpent (snake)</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) serpent, dragon (legendary serpentine creature)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) snake (crafty, wily, evil man)</li><li>kite (flying toy)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zmьjь</i>. Doublet of <i>змий</i> (zmij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p><p>For the “kite” sense, compare New Latin <i>draco volans</i> (literally “flying dragon”), German <i>Drachen</i> (literally “dragon”), French <i>cerf-volant</i> &lt; Old French <i>serpe volant</i> (literally “flying serpent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) little snake</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) spiral or helical object, such as the condenser of a distillation apparatus</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>змея́</i> (zmejá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake, serpent</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) crafty, sneaky person, snake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zmьja</i>. See also <i>змей</i> (zmej).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby snake, young snake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snakelet (a baby snake)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) angry, offensive, harmful person, presumably young or small in size</li></ol><p><i>зме-</i> (zme-) +&lrm; <i>-ёныш</i> (-jónyš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) dragon</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) serpent</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) insidious, cunning, evil man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>змии</i> (zmii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zmь̀jь</i>. Doublet of <i>змей</i> (zmej), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>зме́йка</i> (zméjka).</li></ol><p><i>змѣя́</i> (změjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>змея́</i> (zmejá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually negative) to experience (joy, grief, defeat, rest, etc.) (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>знать</i> (znatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) y'know</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erudite</li></ol><p>From <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the imperative <i>знай</i> (znaj) of <i>знать</i> (znatʹ, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, mark</li><li>symbol</li><li>omen</li><li>badge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>знакъ</i> (znakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znakъ</i>, from <i>&#42;znati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-kъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ampersand (the symbol &amp;)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>№, the number sign.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punctuation mark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics) sign</li><li>(relational,&#32;computing) character</li><li>token, emblematic, symbolic, indicative</li></ol><p><i>знак</i> (znak) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pal; acquaintance</li></ol><p><i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce, to acquaint, to familiarize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to make the acquaintance, to make someone’s acquaintance, to get acquainted</li><li>to familiarize oneself, to go into</li><li>to visit, to see</li></ol><p><i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquaintance, connection, contact</li><li>familiarity</li></ol><p><i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>familiar</li><li>acquainted (with)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) acquaintance, friend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) denominator (the number or expression written below the line in a fraction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>significant, remarkable, noteworthy, portentous, momentous</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) categorematic, significant</li></ol><p><i>знаменова́ть</i> (znamenovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, portent, omen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znamenьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame</li><li>celebrity, star (person)</li></ol><p><i>знамени́тый</i> (znamenítyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famous, renowned, celebrated, noted, notorious, distinguished, eminent, illustrious</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znamenitъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signify, to be a sign/symbol <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>зна́мен-</i> (známen-) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dated form of <i>знамено́сец</i> (znamenósec)</li></ol><p>From <i>знамен-</i> (znamen-, “flag”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to bear, to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard-bearer, banner-bearer, gonfalonier</li></ol><p>From <i>знаменоно́сец</i> (znamenonósec) by haplology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard, banner, flag, colors, ensign</li><li>Znamya (Russian space solar mirror)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znamę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) standard, flag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge, erudition</li></ol><p>From <i>знать</i> (znatʹ, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a noble way</li><li>in an outstanding, distinguished way</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) great</li></ol><p><i>зна́тный</i> (znátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble</li><li>outstanding, distinguished</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) good, excellent</li></ol><p><i>знать</i> (znatʹ, “nobility”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>nōbilis</i> (&lt; <i>nōscō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognoscente, connoisseur, pundit, adept, maven, expert</li></ol><p><i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know: to be familiar with or accustomed to a person, place, or thing</li><li>to know: to be in possession of a specific fact or datum</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) nobility, noble people</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) it seems, then</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>знати</i> (znati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źnōˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵnéh₃t</i> (“to know, get to know”), from <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i>. Cognates include Polish <i>znać</i>, English <i>know</i>, <i>kenning</i>, <i>canny</i>, German <i>kennen</i> and Sanskrit <i>ज्ञा</i> (jñā, “to know”).</p><p>For the collective noun sense of "nobility, aristocratic people", itself derived from the verb знать.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quack, witch doctor, sangoma, shaman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znaxařь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>significance, meaning, sense</li><li>significance, importance, consequence, weight</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;physics,&#32;computing) value</li></ol><p><i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meaningfulness</li><li>significance, importance</li></ol><p><i>зна́чимый</i> (znáčimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>significant, meaningful, important</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) meaningful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>so, then, well, therefore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>A particle introducing an explanation, meaning roughly&#58; So, this is the way it is/was/will be.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>significantly</li><li>considerably, substantially</li></ol><p><i>значи́тельный</i> (značítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>importance, significance</li></ol><p><i>значи́тельный</i> (značítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>considerable, sizeable, strong, substantial, large</li><li>consequential, important, major, significant</li><li>meaningful, suggestive</li></ol><p><i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mean, to signify</li><li>to mean, to be of importance, to matter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;značiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>знак</i> (znak) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be mentioned, to appear</li><li>to be listed, to be registered</li></ol><p><i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>знак</i> (znak), a small sign, symbol</li><li>badge (distinctive mark)</li><li>insignia</li></ol><p><i>знак</i> (znak) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned, knowledgeable, informed, knowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to hit with a fever, chills, or shivers</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) to chill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znobiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heatwave, heat, swelter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;znojь</i>. Cognate with Slovak <i>znoj</i> (“heat, sweat”), Polish <i>znój</i> (“heat”). An ancient stem in the form of -ju- is assumed. Compare dialectal Russian <i>знеть</i> (znetʹ), <i>зне́ять</i> (znéjatʹ, “to rot, to moulder, to incandesce”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scorching, hot, sultry</li><li>passionate, hot</li></ol><p><i>зной</i> (znoj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) crop, craw</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) goitre/goiter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зобь</i> (zobĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zobь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call, summons (words calling someone somewhere)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) invitation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zovъ</i>. Compare <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ). See <i>&#42;zъvati</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zodiac</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ζῳδιακός</i> (zōidiakós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;stilted) architecture (art and science of designing buildings and other structures)</li></ol><p><i>зо́дчий</i> (zódčij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;stilted) architect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зьдъчии</i> (zĭdŭčii, “potter, builder, bricklayer, mason”), from <i>зьдъ</i> (zĭdŭ, “stone wall”), <i>зьдати</i> (zĭdati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьdati</i> (“to build”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberia form of <i>цо́кор</i> (cókor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zola</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister-in-law (husband's sister)</li></ol><p>Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>зълъва</i> (zŭlŭva), Serbo-Croatian <i>zaova</i>, Polish <i>zełwa</i>, <i>zołwa</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъly</i>, genitive <i>&#42;zъlъve</i>, which is cognate to Latin <i>glōs</i>. See there for details.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) gland, neck cancer</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) fish liver</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) fish gallbladder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>желоза</i> (želoza), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želza</i>, with distant assimilation <i>ж</i>…<i>з</i> &gt; <i>з</i>…<i>з</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>за́лоза</i> (záloza), Belarusian <i>зало́за</i> (zalóza). Compare железа́ (železá), from Old East Slavic <i>железа</i> (železa), with different treatment of the liquid diphthong.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeunesse dorée, bright young things (children of the rich and powerful, accustomed to a life of ease)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>jeunesse dorée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golden mean (position of balance between excess and deficiency)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>золоти́стый</i> (zolotístyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>золоти́сто</i> (zolotísto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>золоти́стый</i> (zolotístyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>золоти́сто</i> (zolotísto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>light golden, gold yellow</li></ol><p><i>золоти́стый</i> (zolotístyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden brown (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goldish, resembling gold in color or shine</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gild</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto)</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) zolotnik (an old Russian unit of weight, equal to 1&#47;96 of a pound or approximately 4.266 grams)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;figuratively) a scruple, an ounce (a very small quantity of something)</li><li>(animate,&#32;obsolete) prospector</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;engineering) piston, slide</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;engineering) piston valve</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold (metal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>золото</i> (zoloto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zolto</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰolh₃tom</i> (o-grade), from <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”). Doublet of <i>зла́то</i> (zláto), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>злато</i> (zlato).</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>золото</i> (zoloto), Belarusian <i>золата</i> (zólata), Bulgarian and Macedonian <i>злато</i> (zlato), Czech, Serbo-Croatian, Slovene, and Slovak <i>zlato</i>, Polish, Kashubian, Lower Sorbian, and Upper Sorbian <i>złoto</i>, Polabian <i>zlåtă</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gold and currency</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>валю́та</i> (valjúta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldmining (industry)</li></ol><p>From <i>золото</i> (zoloto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>добыча</i> (dobyča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold digger, gold prospector</li></ol><p>From <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ), a calque of German <i>Goldsucher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gold; golden</li><li>gold, golden (<i>color</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dear, precious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold coin</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-о́й</i> (-ój). Doublet of <i>зло́тый</i> (zlótyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldmining (industry)</li></ol><p>From <i>золото</i> (zoloto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>промышленность</i> (promyšlennostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto, “gold”)</li><li>darling, honey (form of address)</li></ol><p><i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gilded, gilt</li></ol><p><i>золоти́ть</i> (zolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) zombie</li></ol><p>From <i>зо́мби</i> (zómbi, “zombie”) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zombie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>zombie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to brainwash, to make viewers/readers fool by a massive propaganda in the media, to affect their will as if they were zombies</li></ol><p><i>зо́мби</i> (zómbi, “zombie”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), literally “to zombify”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An idiot box.</li></ol><p>From <i>зо́мби</i> (zómbi, “zombie”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>я́щик</i> (jáščik, “box”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zone</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) prison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>zona</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ζώνη</i> (zṓnē, “girdle, belt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zone; zonal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>zonal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) rock opera</li></ol><p>Borrowing from the German <i>Song</i> (“piece of pop music”), ultimately from English <i>song</i>, and Italian <i>opera</i>. Coined to describe <i>Orpheus and Eurydice</i>, since the term <i>рок</i> (rok, “rock music”) was prohibited by the Soviet Ministry of Culture.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>probe (something which penetrates something else)</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) probe (instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sonde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>probing, reconnaissance, sounding</li></ol><p><i>зонди́ровать</i> (zondírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>research</i>) to probe, to search, to sound</li><li>to probe, to find out beforehand</li></ol><p>From German <i>sondieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umbrella</li><li>sunshade, parasol</li><li>canopy, awning</li><li>cupola</li><li>hood (of furnace)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>зо́нтик</i> (zóntik), as if the latter had ended with diminutive <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umbrella</li><li>sunshade</li><li>canopy</li><li>cupola</li><li>hood (of a furnace)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) umbel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>zonnedek</i> (“cover from sun”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зо́нтик</i> (zóntik): (small) umbrella</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>zoo- (pertaining to animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>zoological</li></ol><p><i>зооло́гия</i> (zoológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pet shop, pet store</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoomorphism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoonosis (interspecies disease transmitted to humans by animals)</li></ol><p>Medical term, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon, “animal”) +&lrm; <i>νόσος</i> (nósos, “disease”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoo</li></ol><p>Compare to English <i>zoopark</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зоо-</i> (zoo-) +&lrm; <i>парк</i> (park).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(zoology,&#32;psychology) zoopsychologist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>zoopsychologue</i>, or derived from зоопсихоло́гия (zoopsixológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoophile</li></ol><p>From the Neo-Classical compound zoophile, composed of Ancient Greek ζῷον (zōîon, “animal”) and φίλος (phílos, “friend, but <b>sexually attracted to</b> when used in post-Classical compounds”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoophilia, bestiality (sexual attraction to animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zoochory</li></ol><p>From <i>зоо-</i> (zoo-) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to move, spread”) +&lrm; <i>-и́я</i> (-íja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-eyed</li><li>vigilant, watchful</li><li>Zorki (Soviet 35mm rangefinder camera)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zorъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зр(еть)</i> (zr(etʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vigilantly, watchfully</li></ol><p><i>зо́ркий</i> (zórkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vigilance, watchfulness</li></ol><p><i>зо́ркий</i> (zórkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoroastrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoroastrianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Zoroastrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) look, sight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zorkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>зрак</i> (zrak), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>заря́</i> (zarjá): dawn</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) lychnis, fireballs</li></ol><p><i>заря́</i> (zarjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) zostera (<i>Zostera</i>-genus plants)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Zostera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) zopherid</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Zopheridae</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Zopheridae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>telegram</i>) comma (punctuation)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>запята́я</i> (zapjatája).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) look, glance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>зракъ</i> (zrakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zorkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>зо́рок</i> (zórok), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil (<i>of the eye</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зрак</i> (zrak) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) abbreviation of <i>зенитный ракетный дивизион</i> (zenitnyj raketnyj divizion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectacle, show (something exhibited to view)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrělišče</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ, “to watch, to look at”) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spectacle, show</li><li>impressive, grandiose</li></ol><p><i>зре́лище</i> (zrélišče) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ripeness, maturity</li></ol><p><i>зре́лый</i> (zrélyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mature</li><li>ripe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrělъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight, vision</li></ol><p><i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen, to mature</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to watch, to look at</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> (“to ripen”).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зьрѣти</i> (zĭrěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> (“to see, to look at”). Cognate to Lithuanian <i>žiūrėti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p><i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectator, onlooker, viewer</li><li>audience</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьriteľь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ, “to watch, look at”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visual (related to of affecting the vision)</li></ol><p><i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditorium (the space where the audience is located)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optic nerve (nerve)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) audience, viewer/viewers, spectator/spectators</li></ol><p><i>зри́тель</i> (zrítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in vain, to no avail, for nothing</li><li>mistakenly, making a mistake (used to indicate someone ought not to do&#47;think&#47;etc. something, or to express the sentiment that it's too bad that they do)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>&#42;зьрѧ</i> (&#42;zĭrę), adverbial participle of зреть, originally “idly looking”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seeing (able to see, not blind)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seeing person, non-blind person (someone able to see)</li></ol><p><i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>зре́лый</i> (zrélyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth</li><li><i>(technical)</i> tooth, cog, dent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зѫбъ</i> (zǫbŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zǫbъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źámbas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵómbʰos</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Old Church Slavonic <i>зѫбъ</i> (zǫbŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large-toothed, with large teeth, toothy</li><li>sharp-tongued, biting, sarcastic</li></ol><p><i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) wolffish, catfish <i>(Anarhichadidae)</i></li><li>(<i>Far East</i>) rainbow smelt, <i>Osmerus mordax</i></li></ol><p><i>зубатый</i> (zubatyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technology</i>) tooth, cog</li><li>jag</li><li>merlon, battlement (any of the projections between the embrasures of a battlement)</li></ol><p><i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc). Compare German <i>Zahnrad</i>, Dutch <i>tandwiel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(metal) chisel, point-tool (tool consisting of a slim oblong block of metal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothache</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dental floss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothpaste</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothbrush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tooth; dental</li></ol><p><i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dental therapist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зуб</i> (zub, “tooth”)</li><li>clove (of garlic, etc.)</li><li>coal cutter pick</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>зуб</i> (zub, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothpick (stick for removing food residue from the area between the teeth)</li></ol><p><i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bison (European bison), wisent</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diehard, ace, pro</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) reactionary (<i>comparison of supporters of a return to the old order with obsolete forms of life, i.e. endangered bison</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zǫbrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) crammer, rote memorizer (someone who studies by rote memorization)</li></ol><p>From <i>зубри́ть</i> (zubrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la). Compare Ukrainian <i>зубри́ло</i> (zubrýlo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make notches (in something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to learn by rote</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cram (for an exam), to hit the books</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetgrass, bison grass (any grass of the <i>Hierochloe</i> genus)</li><li>zubrowka</li></ol><p><i>зубр</i> (zubr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attributive</i>) bison, wisent; bison-like</li></ol><p><i>зубр</i> (zubr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toothed, cogged</li><li>notched, indented</li></ol><p>From <i>зубе́ц</i> (zubéc) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj) (ending-stressed) or from <i>зубе́ц</i> (zubéc) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>зуб</i> (zub)</li><li>sprocket</li><li>denticle</li><li>clove <i>(of garlic)</i></li></ol><p><i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>itch (sensation felt on an area of the skin that causes a person or animal to want to scratch said area)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zudъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>itch mite</li></ol><p><i>зуде́ть</i> (zudétʹ, “to itch”) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to itch</li><li>to hum, to buzz (to make a droning sound)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to nag</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>зуд</i> (zud) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p><p>Onomatopoeic. See <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zulu (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buzzer (device)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Summer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) zurna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زورنا (zurna), from Persian زرنا (zornâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to cause to sway</li><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;poetic,&#32;transitive) to rock, to shake</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to cause to hesitate, to sow doubt in</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>зыбь</i> (zybʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to sway</li><li>(literary&#32;or&#32;dated,&#32;intransitive) to rock, to shake</li></ol><p><i>зы́бить</i> (zýbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging cradle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsteady</li><li>changeable, unstable, vacillating</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zybъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quicksand (wet sand that things readily sink in, often found near rivers or coasts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsteady, shaky, shifting</li></ol><p><i>зы́бать</i> (zýbatʹ, “to shake, to ripple, to fluctuate”) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ripple, swell (surface wave in the absence of wind)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wave (<i>generally on the sea</i>)</li><li>unstable surface, quagmire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>зꙑбь</i> (zybĭ, “unsteady place, quagmire”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zybь</i>. Derivable synchronically as <i>зы́б(кий)</i> (zýb(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak brusquely</li></ol><p><i>зык</i> (zyk, “loud sound, scream”) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Komi (person)</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, perhaps named for a small tribe of Komis who met the Russians, possibly related to the Uralic origin of Northern Khanty <i>сӑран</i> (săran, “Komi”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zyrian</li></ol><p><i>зыря́н(ин)</i> (zyrján(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud, stentorian</li></ol><p>From <i>зык</i> (zyk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Cyrillic letter <i>З</i> (Z), <i>з</i> (z)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(Latin letter)</i> Z, z</li></ol><p>From English <i>zed</i>. Doublet of <i>дзе́та</i> (dzéta) and <i>зи́та</i> (zíta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) convict, inmate</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>заключённый каналоармеец</i> (zaključónnyj kanaloarmejec), via the initialism З-К. <i>Заключённый</i> is the adjective "prisoner". <i>Каналоармеец</i>, "member of the canal army", was coined in an analogy with the words <i>красноармеец</i> meaning "member of the Red Army" or <i>трудоармеец</i> (member of a labor army). In 1932, when Anastas Mikoyan visited the construction of the White Sea – Baltic Canal, Lazar Kogan told him "Comrade Mikoyan, how shall we call them? (…) I thought up the word: 'kanaloarmeyets'. What do you think?" Mikoyan approved it."</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter Z, z.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) south</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) souther (south wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>zuid</i> (“south”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) southwest</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) southwester (<i>wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зюйд</i> (zjujd) +&lrm; <i>вест</i> (vest)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) southeast</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) southeaster (<i>wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>зюйд</i> (zjujd) +&lrm; <i>ост</i> (ost)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sou'wester, southwester, cape ann (waterproof hat worn by sailors)</li><li>sou'wester, southwester (rainjacket worn by sailors)</li></ol><p><i>зюйд-ве́ст</i> (zjujd-vést) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), a calque of Dutch <i>zuidwester</i> or English <i>sou'wester</i>. The term derives from the observation that in the North Sea region, southwesterly wind often brings heavy rain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sensitive to cold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) against the cold</li></ol><p><i>зя́бкий</i> (zjábkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaffinch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zęba</i> (whence also Polish <i>zięba</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>зеба</i>). Compare typologically Macedonian <i>снегар</i> (snegar) (&lt; <i>снег</i> (sneg) &lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sněgъ</i>).</p><p>Also compare typologically <i>снеги́рь</i> (snegírʹ) (Serbo-Croatian <i>зимница</i> <&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zima</i>). Also see <i>зиморо́док</i> (zimoródok) (Czech <i>ledňáček</i> <&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ledъ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>зя́блик</i> (zjáblik).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cold, to freeze, to feel cold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zębnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land ploughed in autumn (for spring sowing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zębiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) mouth, when the person talks too much, too loud, or in an inappropriate way; mouth when it is wide open</li></ol><p>Related to <i>зиять</i> (zijatʹ, “to yawn, gape, open the mouth”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son-in-law</li><li>brother-in-law (the husband of one’s sister or sister-in-law)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zętь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>зерно́</i> (zernó): (small) kernel (single seed of corn or wheat); granule</li></ol><p><i>зерно́</i> (zernó) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>И</i>.</li><li>Yi (an ethnic group of southwestern China)</li><li>Yi (the Tibeto-Burman languages used by the Yi)</li><li>Yi (the script used to write the languages)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and</li><li>as well as, both... and...</li><li>as well</li><li>even</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Used as an emphasiser, including in a few set phrases.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>To emphasise the truth of a verb</li><li>To emphasise a contrast</li><li>To emphasise a parenthetical remark</li><li>As other emphasis or in set phrases</li></ol></ol><p>From the early Cyrillic letter iže, from Greek <i>Η</i> (I, eta).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;i</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ey</i>, an early locative singular determiner, formed from the root <i>&#42;h₁e-</i>, <i>&#42;h₁o-</i>. Compare Greek <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”), Gothic <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei, “and, so that, be it”).</p><p>From Mandarin <i>彝</i> (yí).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: И)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>И</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>I</i>/<i>i</i>.</li></ol><p>From the early Cyrillic letter iže, from Greek <i>Η</i> (I, eta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>and others</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Й</i>/<i>й</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Й</i>/<i>й</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Й</i>/<i>й</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Й</i>/<i>й</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>и про́чее</i> (i próčeje, “and others, and so on, etc.”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>и про́чие</i> (i próčije, “and others, and so on, etc.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>etc., and so forth, and so on</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>и тому́ подо́бное</i> (i tomú podóbnoje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>etc., and so forth, and so on</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>и тому́ подо́бное</i> (i tomú podóbnoje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as it is (already); anyway; already</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>et cetera, and so forth, and so on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and even then (and even in that case)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as an emphatic particle.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in response to someone) (and) that's true</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;in response to someone) (and) that's true</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>emphatic</i>) period!, and nothing else!, and nothing less!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) hee-haw (sound a donkey makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) whinny, neigh (sound a horse makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>исполня́ющий обя́занности</i> (ispolnjájuščij objázannosti): acting (temporarily assuming the duties or authority of another person when they are unable to do their job)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: И.О.)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>исполня́ющий обя́занности</i> (ispolnjájuščij objázannosti): acting (temporarily assuming the duties or authority of another person when they are unable to do their job)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibadite</li></ol><p>From Arabic إِبَاض (ʔibāḍ, “[Abd Allah b.] Ibad”) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Iberian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ibis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἶβις</i> (îbis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) for, because, as, since</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ибо</i> (ibo). By surface analysis, univerbation of <i>и</i> (i) +&lrm; <i>бо</i> (bo), which is a calque from Ancient Greek καὶ γάρ (commonly used in Ancient Greek since the conjunction γάρ (gár, “because”) cannot appear first in a sentence). Compare или (ili) for similar derivation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>и́ва</i> (íva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jь̀va</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;éiˀwāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eyHwéh₂</i> (“yew”).</p><p>Slavic cognates include Bulgarian <i>и́ва</i> (íva), Macedonian <i>ива</i> (iva), Polish <i>iwa</i> and Ukrainian <i>і́ва</i> (íva). More distant cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ὄᾱ</i> (óā), Icelandic <i>ýr</i>, Dutch <i>ijf</i>, German <i>Eibe</i>, English <i>yew</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rose-bay, willowherb, fireweed</li><li>willowherb tea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ива́н</i> (Iván, “Ivan”, here used as a placeholder for a name of a common Russian man, compare to English <i>Jack</i>) +&lrm; <i>чай</i> (čaj, “tea”); the intended sense is thus along the lines of “commoner's tea” or “peasant's tea”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ivanovo (city in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ivanov; Ivanov's (referring to anyone named Ivanov)</li></ol><p><i>Ива́ново</i> (Ivánovo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) when referring to Ivanovo; <i>Ива́нов</i> (Ivánov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) when referring to Ivanov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sardine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>鰯</i> (iwashi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow bed, osier bed</li><li>willow branches, osier branches</li></ol><p>From <i>ива</i> (iva) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Salicaceae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>и́вовый</i> (ívovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oriole</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьvьlga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>и́волг</i> (ívolg)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Hebrew language, especially Modern Hebrew, Ivrit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עִבְרִית</i> (ivrít).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needle</li><li>thorn, prickle</li><li>quill, spine, bristle</li><li>Igla (Soviet spacecraft docking system)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьgъla</i>. Akin to Russian <i>и́го</i> (ígo). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yewg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prickly, covered with needles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>alternative medicine</i>) acupuncture</li></ol><p><i>игла́</i> (iglá, “needle”) +&lrm; <i>рефлѐксотерапи́я</i> (reflèksoterapíja, “reflexotherapy”), literally “needle reflexotherapy”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acupuncture (insertion of needles for remedial purposes)</li></ol><p><i>игла́</i> (iglá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ука́лывание</i> (ukályvanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>igloo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) ignoring (not responding)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) ignore mode (mode in which a user is muted, hiding their content)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ignore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignoring</li></ol><p><i>игнори́ровать</i> (ignorírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ignore, to disregard (to deliberately pay no attention to)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>ignorieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ignore (to not respond)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to ignore (to mute a user, hiding their content)</li></ol><p><i>игно́р</i> (ignór) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) ignorer (someone who ignores)</li></ol><p><i>игно́р</i> (ignór) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) yoke; slavery, oppression</li><li>(archaic,&#32;rare) yoke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьgo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yugóm</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yewg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) whinny, neigh (imitates the sound of a horse neighing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>игла́</i> (iglá): needle</li></ol><p><i>игла́</i> (iglá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>иго́лка</i> (igólka), diminutive of <i>игла́</i> (iglá)</li></ol><p><i>иго́лка</i> (igólka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) needle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gambling, playing</li></ol><p><i>игра́</i> (igrá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game</li><li>a sport which is played (<i>also</i> <i>спорти́вная игра́</i> (sportívnaja igrá))</li><li><i>(action)</i>: play in the following senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Activity for amusement only, especially among the young.</li><li>Similar activity, in young animals, as they explore their environment and learn new skills.</li><li>(<i>ethology</i>) "Repeated, incompletely functional behavior differing from more serious versions..., and initiated voluntarily when... in a low-stress setting."</li><li>An individual's performance in a sport or game.</li></ol><li>acting, performance</li><li>role-playing (<i>also</i> <i>ролева́я игра́</i> (rolevája igrá))</li><li>playing <i>(a musical instrument)</i></li><li>movement of gas bubbles in a sparkling drink</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) glitter, sparkle, radiance (of precious stones)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>игра</i> (igra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьgra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pun, wordplay</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>gra słów</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playing card (one of usually 52 rectangular pieces of card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>playing</li></ol><p>From <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ, “to play”) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to play</li><li>to play [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a sport or game’</small>]</li><li>to play [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘a musical instrument’</small>]</li><li>to take advantage of [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘someone's emotions, feelings, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to play, to perform [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a song, a composer, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to act, to play [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a role, a character in a film or play, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to deal with (something) in a careless, irresponsible or contemptuous way [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to play, to pretend to be or do (<i>often as a children's game</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to play for a team, or as a character or faction in a game [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative)]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to flit, flicker or shimmer [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘on/in something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьgrati</i>. Cognate to Serbo-Croatian <i>ȉgrati</i> and Czech <i>hrát</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to play with fire (put oneself in a precarious situation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) <i>(esp. of children)</i> to play <i>(around)</i></li></ol><p><i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily; with ease; playfully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>player</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter Y, y.</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) Yankee (<i>Y</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>i grec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) player (of a musical instrument)</li></ol><p>From <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ, “to play”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>playful (liking or prone to play)</li></ol><p><i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparkling (<i>of wine</i>)</li></ol><p><i>игра́</i> (igrá) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merrymaking</li></ol><p><i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) game, play</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) (movie, theater) acting</li><li>playing</li></ol><p><i>игра́</i> (igrá) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>player</li><li>gambler</li></ol><p>First attested in the first half of 18th century. From <i>игра́</i> (igrá, “game”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ludomaniac (someone suffering from ludomania, or gaming addiction)</li></ol><p><i>игра́</i> (igrá) +&lrm; <i>-ман</i> (-man)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ludomania</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) toy</li><li>toy (not real)</li><li>miniature</li></ol><p><i>игру́шка</i> (igrúška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toy</li><li>plaything</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) video game</li></ol><p>From <i>игра́</i> (igrá) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iguana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>iguana</i>, from Taíno <i>&#42;iwana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Father Superior, hegumen, abbot</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic, from Old Church Slavonic <i>игоумєнъ</i> (igumenŭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>ἡγούμενος</i> (hēgoúmenos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ideal (a perfect standard of beauty, intellect, etc.)</li><li>(<i>algebra</i>) ideal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to idealize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idealism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idealist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idealistic</li></ol><p><i>идеали́ст</i> (idealíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ideally (in various senses)</li></ol><p><i>идеа́льный</i> (ideálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ideal, perfect (being optimal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ideological, ideal</li><li>high-principled (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>иде́я</i> (idéja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) descriptor, identifier, handle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to identify</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>identifizieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>identically</li></ol><p><i>иденти́чный</i> (idɛntíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identity (the sameness some individuals share to make up the same kind or universal)</li></ol><p><i>иденти́чный</i> (idɛntíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>identical</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>ident(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ideogram, ideograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) ideographic</li></ol><p><i>идеогра́фия</i> (ideográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) ideography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ideologue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ideologically</li></ol><p><i>идеологи́ческий</i> (ideologíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ideological</li></ol><p>From <i>идеоло́гия</i> (ideológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ideology (doctrine, body of ideas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idea, thought, notion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἰδέα</i> (idéa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck off!, go fuck yourself! (indicates the speaker's feeling of displeasure)</li></ol><p>Literally, “go to the dick/cock”. Compare Polish <i>idź w chuj</i> and Ukrainian <i>іди́ на́ хуй</i> (idý ná xuj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idyl, idyll</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īdyllium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>εἰδύλλιον</i> (eidúllion), diminutive of <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form, shape”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiolect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lect, language variety, idiom (a generic term for a manner of speaking shared among a group of people&#59; encompasses languages, dialects, sociolects, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idiomatic (relating to or conforming to the mode of expression characteristic of a language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiom, idiomatic expression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiom, idiomatic expression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idiosyncratic</li></ol><p><i>идиосинкрази́я</i> (idiosinkrazíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiosyncrasy (way of behaving or thinking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiot</li></ol><p>First attested in 1803. Borrowed from Latin <i>idiōta</i> via French <i>idiot</i> or German <i>Idiot</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἰδιώτης</i> (idiṓtēs, “a private citizen, one who has no professional knowledge, layman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiocy, imbecility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idiotic</li></ol><p><i>идио́т</i> (idiót) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idiotic</li></ol><p><i>идио́т</i> (idiót) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiophone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yiddish (language)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>אידיש</i> (idish), alternative form of <i>ייִדיש</i> (yidish).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ido (a constructed language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idol</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) callous person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>идолъ</i> (idolŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>εἴδωλον</i> (eídōlon, “image, idol”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;religion) idol worshipper, idolater, iconophile</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;offensive) heretic, infidel (<i>used as a term of abuse</i>)</li></ol><p><i>и́дол</i> (ídol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́с</i> (-bés), calque of Ancient Greek <i>εἰδωλομανής</i> (eidōlomanḗs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;archaic,&#32;religion) idolatry</li></ol><p>From <i>и́дол</i> (ídol, “idol”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́сие</i> (-bésije, “mania, madness”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal sacrifice (to idols)</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>идоложе́ртвенный</i> (idoložértvennyj, “sacrificial (to idols)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacrificial (to idols)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) idolatrous</li></ol><p><i>идол</i> (idol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>же́ртвенный</i> (žértvennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolater</li></ol><p>From <i>и́дол</i> (ídol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>покло́нник</i> (poklónnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolatry</li></ol><p><i>и́дол</i> (ídol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>покло́нничество</i> (poklónničestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolatry</li></ol><p><i>и́дол</i> (ídol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>покло́н</i> (poklón) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to walk</li><li>(<i>of precipitation</i>) to fall</li><li>to function, to work (of clocks and watches)</li><li>to suit, to become (of wearing clothes)</li><li>(<i>used in expressions</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ити</i> (iti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьti</i> (“to go”). The infinitive spelled with <i>д</i> (d) is attested since the 14th century, under influence of <i>иду́</i> (idú), <i>идёшь</i> (idjóšʹ), etc.</p><p>Past tense <i>шёл</i> (šol) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьdlъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to back down, to back out, to backtrack, to backpedal, to reverse course, to fold, to compromise, to go back on one's word</li></ol><p>Literally, “to go onto the reverse (path, way etc.)”. Alteration of <i>идти́ на попя́тный</i> (idtí na popjátnyj). Overtook <i>идти́ на попя́тный</i> (idtí na popjátnyj) in usage in the second half of the 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;less common) alternative form of <i>идти́ на попя́тную</i> (idtí na popjátnuju)</li></ol><p><i>На попя́тный</i> (Na popjátnyj) is an ellipsis of <i>на попя́тный двор</i> (na popjátnyj dvor), which sees use from the 1820s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ides</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īdūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>come on</li><li>let's go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesuit (member of the Society of Jesus)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) guileful, crafty person</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ecclesiastical Latin <i>Iēsūīta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesuitism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) insidious female person</li></ol><p><i>иезуи́т</i> (ijezuít) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) yen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>円</i> (en, “yen, circle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hierarch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hierarchical</li></ol><p><i>иера́рхия</i> (ijerárxija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hierarchy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἱεραρχία</i> (hierarkhía, “rule of a high priest”), from <i>ἱεράρχης</i> (hierárkhēs, “high priest”), from <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) + <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) priest of the second rank in the Orthodox church</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hieroglyph</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) undecipherable handwriting, inscrutable symbol</li><li>Chinese character; hanzi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hieroglyphic</li></ol><p><i>иеро́глиф</i> (ijeróglif) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hieromonk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>ἱερομόναχος</i> (hieromónakhos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Jerusalem</li></ol><p><i>Иерусали́м</i> (Ijerusalím) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dependant</li></ol><p><i>иждиве́ние</i> (iždivénije) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material support</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) money, funds</li></ol><p>From <i>иждиви́ть</i> (iždivítʹ, “to spend”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>иждити</i> (ižditi), derived from &#42;из-жити. See <i>из-</i> (iz-), <i>жить</i> (žitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dependency (state of dependence&#59; inability to exercise initiative&#59; lack of self-reliance)</li></ol><p><i>иждиве́нец</i> (iždivénec) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;ecclesiastic&#32;or&#32;literary) which; et cetera</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>izhe, Church Slavonic name of the Cyrillic letter и</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>иже</i> (iže), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jь že</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>иже</i> (iže).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>izhitsa (obsolete Cyrillic letter Ѵ)</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ingrian, Izhorian (person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ижеръ</i> (ižerŭ), borrowed from Ingrian <i>inkeroin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from</li><li>out of</li><li>of, through</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;eǵʰs</i>, <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;idiomatic) in a quick-and-dirty manner, crudely, from materials available at hand</li></ol><p>Literally, “from shit and sticks”; vulgar alteration of <i>из спи́чек и желуде́й</i> (iz spíček i želudéj, “from matchsticks and acorns”), a common phrase in Soviet children's books providing instructions for making toys by hand from found materials.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>year in, year out; from year to year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;derogatory</i>) from rags to riches; from zero to hero</li></ol><p>Literally, “from dirt to princedom”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up and down, back and forth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>out from</li><li>When added to a verb, it may form a verb with a sense of completeness, abundance or exhaustion.</li><li>When added to a verb, it may form a verb with a sense of destruction, ruined due to use.</li><li>When added to nouns, especially when they have the suffix -ье, it indicates location of an object.</li><li>When added to adjectives for colours&#47;colors, an adverb is formed indicating nuance.</li><li>When added to nouns, it may form an adverb indicating spatial or temporal properties. Usually in archaic or highly formal style.</li><li>When added to adverbs, it can form an adverb with a sense of direction for an action.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьz-</i>; see <i>&#42;jьz</i> for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from (behind)</li><li>(away) from (from a place where one was occupied)</li><li>because of, due to, over</li></ol><p>From <i>из</i> (iz, “from”) +&lrm; <i>за</i> (za, “behind”). Compare Polish <i>zza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore (in a colloquial context)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from under</li><li><i>As a designation of former use</i>, from</li></ol><p>Compare <i>испо́д</i> (ispód).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under duress, by sheer compulsion; forced</li></ol><p>Literally, “from under a stick”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ezafe, izafet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish اضافت (izâfet), from Classical Persian اِضَافَت (izāfat) / اِضَافَه (izāfa), from Arabic إِضَافَة (ʔiḍāfa). Doublet of <i>идáфа</i> (idáfa), borrowed directly from Arabic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>izba, (peasant's) log hut, cottage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>изъба, истъба</i> (izŭba, istŭba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьstъba</i>, which may be related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stubu</i> (“room, sitting room, oven”) (whence ultimately English <i>stove</i>) or to French <i>étuve</i> (“heated room, bathroom”), itself from Latin <i>&#42;extupāre</i>.</p><p><br/>The phonetic transition /стъб/→/зб/ is associated with the simplification of the combination /стъб/→/сб/ after the fall of the reduced vowels and voicing /сб/→/зб/, compare transition /сд/→/зд/ in <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) from <i>съдоро́въ</i> (sŭdoróvŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village library, village reading room</li></ol><p><i>изба́</i> (izbá) +&lrm; <i>чита́льня</i> (čitálʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redeemer, rescuer, savior</li></ol><p><i>изба́вить</i> (izbávitʹ, “to redeem, rescue, save”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rid, to save, to relieve [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>избавити</i> (izbaviti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid of; to rid oneself (of), to escape from, to dispose of [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>изба́вить</i> (izbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cure</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) salvation</li></ol><p><i>изба́вить</i> (izbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rid, to save, to relieve [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>изба́вить</i> (izbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid of; to rid oneself (of), to escape from, to dispose of [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>избавля́ть</i> (izbavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-indulgence, pamperedness</li></ol><p><i>избало́ванн(ый)</i> (izbalóvann(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spoiled, pampered (<i>of a child, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spoil</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>балова́ть</i> (balovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become spoiled</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>баловать</i> (balovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spoil</li></ol><p><i>избалова́ть</i> (izbalovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become spoiled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, to refrain from [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>to keep off, to steer clear of, to shun, to evade, to elude [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to escape [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from trouble, misfortune, punishment, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to run (many places), to run about, to run all over</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>избѣгати</i> (izběgati). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, to refrain from [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>to keep off, to steer clear of, to shun, to evade, to elude [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to escape [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from trouble, misfortune, punishment, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>избѣгнѫти</i> (izběgnǫti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́гнуть</i> (-bégnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avoidance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, to refrain from [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>to keep off, to steer clear of, to shun, to evade, to elude [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to escape [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from trouble, misfortune, punishment, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>избѣжати</i> (izběžati). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, to thrash (unmercifully)</li></ol><p><i>изби́ть</i> (izbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating, massacre</li><li>beating, battery</li></ol><p><i>изби́ть</i> (izbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voter, elector</li></ol><p><i>избира́ть</i> (izbirátʹ, “to choose, elect”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electoral</li><li>selective</li></ol><p><i>избира́ть</i> (izbirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to elect</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be elected</li></ol><p><i>избира́ть</i> (izbirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election committee, election commission</li></ol><p>From избира́тельная коми́ссия (izbirátelʹnaja komíssija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beaten, mauled, battered</li><li>well-trodden (<i>road, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) trite, hackneyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, to thrash (unmercifully)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>избити</i> (izbiti, “to slaughter”), equivalent to <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be/become exhausted</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to furrow</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to leave ruts</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to travel around, to gallivant (by car, train or ship, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election (choice of a leader or representatives)</li><li>selection</li></ol><p><i>избра́ть</i> (izbrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the chosen one; (the) elect</li><li>(one's) darling, sweetheart</li></ol><p>From the same source as the verb <i>избрать</i> (izbratʹ, “to choose, elect”). More at Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-nikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) darling, sweetheart, betrothed (referring to a female)</li></ol><p><i>избра́н(ник)</i> (izbrán(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) chosenness</li></ol><p><i>и́збранный</i> (ízbrannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chosen, selected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to elect</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>изба́</i> (izbá): izba, (peasant's) log hut, cottage</li></ol><p><i>изба́</i> (izbá) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>избы́ть</i> (izbýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance, plenty</li><li>excess, surplus, redundancy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>избꙑтъкъ</i> (izbytŭkŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>избꙑти</i> (izbyti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redundancy, superfluousness</li></ol><p><i>избы́точный</i> (izbýtočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surplus, redundant</li></ol><p><i>избы́ток</i> (izbýtok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>изба́</i> (izbá): log hut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sculpture, statue; graven image; sculptured figure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sculpt</li><li>(figurative,&#32;humorous) to produce, to make something</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изваꙗти</i> (izvajati). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>вая́ть</i> (vajátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to learn from personal experience, to come to know</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to experience, to feel (a mental state)</li><li>to know, to comprehend</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to learn from personal experience</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to experience, to feel (a mental state)</li><li>to know, to comprehend</li></ol><p><i>изве́дать</i> (izvédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster, cruel person, fiend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgorge; to excrete</li></ol><p><i>изве́ргнуть</i> (izvérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgorge; to excrete</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eruption</li><li>ejection, expulsion, excretion</li><li><i>words, offences</i> outpouring, torrent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sharply</li><li>to twist deftly (<i>to accomplish or avoid something</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to find a way out of a predicament, to be resourceful</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to try to get rid of something unpleasant</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to use cunning or trickery, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use up, to spend</li><li>to exterminate</li><li>to exhaust</li><li>to torment</li><li>to vex, to exasperate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>news, tidings</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>весть</i> (vestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify, to give notice</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to announce, to advertise (to give public notice of&#59; to announce publicly)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-вести́ть</i> (-vestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lime, quicklime</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) limestone</li><li>paper-white, pale</li></ol><p><i>извёстка</i> (izvjóstka) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj): most familiar; most famous</li></ol><p><i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reputation, fame, repute, notoriety, renown</li><li>publicity</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) celebrity, prominent figure, notability</li></ol><p><i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known, familiar</li><li>well-known, renowned, famous</li><li>notorious</li><li>certain</li></ol><p><i>извести́ть</i> (izvestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limestone, chalkstone</li></ol><p><i>и́звесть</i> (ízvestʹ, “lime”) +&lrm; <i>-ня́к</i> (-nják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime (calcium oxide, quicklime)</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ἄσβεστος</i> (ásbestos). Cognate with Russian <i>асбе́ст</i> (asbést, “asbestos”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) information <i>(against)</i>, report, slander, calumny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzvětъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become decrepit</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ветша́ть</i> (vetšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal, everlasting</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́чный</i> (véčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify, to give notice</li><li>to announce, to advertise</li></ol><p><i>извести́ть</i> (izvestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notice, notification, announcement, advertisement (public notice)</li></ol><p><i>извести́ть</i> (izvestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, twist, meander, curve</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) unexpected turn of thought or mood, unexpected development</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>извива́ться</i> (izvivátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coil, to wriggle</li><li><i>(river, road etc)</i> to twist, to wind, to meander</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ви́ться</i> (vítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, twist, convolution, curve</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gyrus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meandering, winding, twisty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) complex, confusing</li></ol><p><i>изви́л(ина)</i> (izvíl(ina)) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pardon, apology, excuse, regret</li></ol><p><i>извини́ть</i> (izvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse, to pardon, to forgive [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>вини́ть</i> (vinítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apologize (to make an apology or defense) [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>извини́ть</i> (izvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse, to pardon, to forgive [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>извини́ть</i> (izvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apologize (to make an apology or defense) [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>извиня́ть</i> (izvinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to extract, to elicit, to draw out</li></ol><p><i>извле́к-</i> (izvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to come out, to be pulled out, to be removed</li></ol><p><i>извлека́ть</i> (izvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extract, extracting (process)</li></ol><p><i>извле́к-</i> (izvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to extract, to elicit, to draw out</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>извлѣщи</i> (izvlěšti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to come out, to be pulled out, to be removed</li></ol><p><i>извле́чь</i> (izvléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the outside, from outside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) recension</li><li>(<i>art</i>) style of Orthodox Christian icons of the medieval era</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wasting (by using up all the resources)</li><li>consumption</li></ol><p>Presumably the same as etymology 2, but with unclear sense development.</p><p>Deverbal from <i>изводи́ть</i> (izvodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use up, to spend</li><li>to exterminate</li><li>to exhaust</li><li>to torment</li><li>to vex, to exasperate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrier's trade, carrier (by trade), carrying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coachman, cabman, carter, wagoner, carman</li></ol><p><i>изво́з</i> (izvóz) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) will, pleasure, volition</li></ol><p>From <i>изво́лить</i> (izvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;ironic) to desire, to wish</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to kindly, to please be good enough [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to if you wish, to all right, to with pleasure</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sharply</li><li>to twist deftly (<i>to accomplish or avoid something</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to find a way out of a predicament, to be resourceful</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to try to get rid of something unpleasant</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to use cunning or trickery, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) turning sharply and deftly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sharp, deft turn</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) unexpected turn of thought</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) bend</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) trick, subterfuge</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>извороти́ться</i> (izvorotítʹsja) (dated); compare the corresponding imperfective <i>извора́чиваться</i> (izvoráčivatʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resourcefulness</li><li>cunning, cleverness, shiftiness</li><li>elusiveness (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изворо́тливый</i> (izvorótlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resourceful</li><li>cunning, clever, shifty</li><li>elusive (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изворо́т</i> (izvorót) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pervert</li><li>to distort, to give false account</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>извратити</i> (izvratiti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ, “to turn”), Doublet of <i>извороти́ть</i> (izvorotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pervert</li><li>to distort, to give false account</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>извращати</i> (izvraštati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzvorťati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pervert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>извращьньць</i> (izvraštĭnĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzvorťьnьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>изврат</i> (izvrat) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perversion, perversity</li><li>distortion</li></ol><p><i>изврати́ть</i> (izvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;humorous</i>) pervert</li></ol><p><i>извращ(а́ть)</i> (izvrašč(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́га</i> (-úga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distorted</li><li>perverted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sharply</li><li>to twist deftly (<i>to accomplish or avoid something</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to find a way out of a predicament, to be resourceful</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to try to get rid of something unpleasant</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to use cunning or trickery, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>изверт-</i> (izvert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime mortar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lime (calcium oxide, quicklime)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lime-based whitewash</li></ol><p>From <i>и́звесть</i> (ízvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>изве́стно</i> (izvéstno).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to soil</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>гва́здать</i> (gvázdatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, curve, turn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve, to crook</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve</li></ol><p><i>изгиба́ть</i> (izgibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erase, to efface, to blot out; to smooth out, to destroying traces of someone or something in the process</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to weaken, to make imperceptible (feelings, impressions, memories, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erase, to efface, to blot out; to smooth out, to destroying traces of someone or something in the process</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to weaken, to make imperceptible (feelings, impressions, memories, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>изгла́дить</i> (izgláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile, deportation</li><li>expulsion, ejection</li><li>banishment, proscription</li><li>expelling</li><li>ostracism</li><li>eviction</li><li>excommunication</li><li>extrusion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzgъnanьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>изгна́ть</i> (izgnátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile, outcast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to banish, to exile</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outcast, outlaw, castaway, derelict</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>изгои</i> (izgoi). Asynchronically analyzable as <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>го́ить</i> (góitʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gojiti</i> (“to heal, feed”), the latter being a causative form of <i>жить</i> (žitʹ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of the bed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изголода́ться</i> (izgolodátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>голода́ть</i> (golodátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starve</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be starving, to feel an urgent need [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>изголода́ть</i> (izgolodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exile, to banish</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) to exorcise</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence (barrier)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to make, to manufacture</li></ol><p><i>изгото́вить</i> (izgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>изготовля́ться</i> (izgotovljátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>изгота́вливать</i> (izgotávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacturer, producer, maker</li></ol><p><i>изгото́вить</i> (izgotóvitʹ, “to produce, make, manufacture”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to make, to manufacture</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, to place oneself in readiness</li></ol><p><i>изгото́вить</i> (izgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production, manufacture, making</li></ol><p><i>изгото́вить</i> (izgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to make, to manufacture</li></ol><p><i>изгото́вить</i> (izgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>издание</i> (izdanije)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>издательство</i> (izdatelʹstvo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish, to print</li><li>to edit</li><li>to issue, to promulgate, to enact</li><li>to utter, to emit, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>изда́ть</i> (izdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be published, to be printed</li><li>to be edited</li><li>to be issued, to be promulgated, to be enacted</li><li>to be uttered, to be emitted, to be exhaled</li></ol><p><i>издава́ть</i> (izdavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long since; since long ago</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from afar</li><li>afar off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from a distance, from afar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>издалека́</i> (izdaleká) from afar, afar off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publication</li><li>issue</li><li>promulgation</li><li>edition</li></ol><p><i>изда́ть</i> (izdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije). Root and suffix cognate with Czech <i>vydání</i>, Polish <i>wydanie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publisher (one who publishes, especially books)</li></ol><p><i>изда́ть</i> (izdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>publishing</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) publisher</li></ol><p>From <i>изда́тель</i> (izdátelʹ, “publisher”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publishing house, publisher</li></ol><p><i>издатель</i> (izdatelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish, to print</li><li>to edit</li><li>to issue, to promulgate, to enact</li><li>to utter, to emit, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be published, to be printed</li><li>to be edited</li><li>to be issued, to be promulgated, to be enacted</li><li>to be uttered, to be emitted, to be exhaled</li></ol><p><i>изда́ть</i> (izdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mocking, scornful</li></ol><p><i>издева́ть(ся)</i> (izdevátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельский</i> (-telʹskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abuse, mockery, bullying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jeer, to scoff, to taunt</li><li>to maltreat, to torture, to bully</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>дева́ть</i> (devátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(manufactured) article, product, (plural) goods, wares</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) corvee, statute labor, gavel work (unpaid labor due to a feudal lord)</li></ol><p><i>изде́лье</i> (izdélʹje, “corvee”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to spend, to expend, to waste</li></ol><p>From <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ). Compare Czech <i>zdržet</i> and Polish <i>zdzierżyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to spend, to expend, to waste</li></ol><p><i>издержа́ть</i> (izderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually plural</i>) expenses, costs</li></ol><p>From <i>издержа́ть</i> (izderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-к</i> (-k); since the 16th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rend, to tear (<i>badly</i>)</li><li>to scratch all over</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharecropper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharecropping</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>до́ля</i> (dólja, “share”) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>дуба́сить</i> (dubásitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sneer, taunt, jeer; mockery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>издева́ться</i> (izdevátʹsja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bassia prostrata</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>изен</i> (izen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roast, to fry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill by burning, to burn at the stake</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to burn, to roast (one's shoulders from the sun, shoes from a fire, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>изжа́рить</i> (izžáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roast, to fry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill by burning, to burn at the stake</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to burn, to roast (one's shoulders from the sun, shoes from a fire, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fry or roast to completion</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to overheat (<i>from too long in the sun, the oven, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изжа́рить</i> (izžáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid <i>(of)</i>, to overcome, to eliminate</li></ol><p><i>изжи́ть</i> (izžítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid of, to overcome, to eliminate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heartburn, pyrosis</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzgaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) crumpled, wrinkled, chewed-up</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) hackneyed, trite, well-worn (<i>of a topic, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cold, to freeze, to feel cold</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>зя́бнуть</i> (zjábnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easy</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily, easy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>easy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expound, to explain, to state, to set forth, to relate, to retell</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cure, treatment (action)</li></ol><p><i>излечи́ть</i> (izlečítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cure, to heal, to remedy, to restore</li></ol><p><i>излечи́ть</i> (izlečítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover <i>(from)</i>, recuperate <i>(from)</i>, get over</li><li>to rid oneself <i>(of)</i>, get over</li></ol><p><i>изле́чивать</i> (izléčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curable</li></ol><p><i>излеч(и́ть)</i> (izleč(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cure, to heal, to remedy, to restore, to sanitate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>лечи́ть</i> (lečítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pour out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pour out, to vent (emotions, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>изли́ть</i> (izlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill out, to pour out, to flow out</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to radiate (<i>of light, sound, or smell</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to manifest, to appear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in a specified way; of feelings, moods, or experiences’</small>]</li><li>to pour one's heart out</li></ol><p><i>излива́ть</i> (izlivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pour out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pour out, to vent (emotions, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill out, to pour out, to flow out</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to radiate (<i>of light, sound, or smell</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to manifest, to appear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in a specified way; of feelings, moods, or experiences’</small>]</li><li>to pour one's heart out</li></ol><p><i>изли́ть</i> (izlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surplus, excess</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excess, excessiveness, superfluity; glut</li></ol><p><i>изли́ш(ний)</i> (izlíš(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unnecessarily, excessively (often due to inappropriateness)</li></ol><p><i>изли́шний</i> (izlíšnij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnecessary, excessive (often due to inappropriateness)</li></ol><p>Related to Old Church Slavonic <i>лихъ</i> (lixŭ, “superfluous, excessive, bad”) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lixъ</i> (“remaining, unnecessary, superfluous, bad”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) outpouring (<i>of emotion</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изли́ть</i> (izlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch (by chasing)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>изловчи́ться</i> (izlovčítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ло́вкий</i> (lóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statement (presentation of opinion or position)</li><li>essay (a short piece of writing on a particular subject)</li></ol><p><i>изложи́ть</i> (izložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expound, to explain, to state, to set forth, to relate, to retell</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fracture, break</li><li>sharp bend</li><li>twist, turn, zigzag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to radiate</li></ol><p><i>излучи́ть</i> (izlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiation, emanation</li></ol><p><i>излучи́ть</i> (izlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, curve, bow (bend in a rod or planar surface)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to radiate</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). See also <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>favorite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme extent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make dirty, to smear</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make dirty, to smear</li></ol><p><i>изма́зать</i> (izmázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exhaust, to make extremely tired</li></ol><p><i>измота́ть</i> (izmotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be exhausted</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ма́ять</i> (májatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble up, to chop, to crush, to pound, to grind</li><li>to become small/smaller</li><li>to become petty, to degenerate</li></ol><p><i>измельчи́ть</i> (izmelʹčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>мельча́ть</i> (melʹčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attrition; breakage; breaking; chipping; chopping; crush; fining; reducing</li></ol><p><i>измельчи́ть</i> (izmelʹčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble up, to chop, to crush, to pound, to grind</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>мельчи́ть</i> (melʹčítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrayal, treason</li><li>adultery, unfaithfulness, faithlessness</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>измѣна</i> (izměna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzměna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change, modification, alteration</li><li>inflection</li></ol><p><i>измени́ть</i> (izmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, to alter, to modify, to edit</li><li>to vary</li><li>to betray, to become unfaithful, to cheat on [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to break, to violate <i>(an oath, promise)</i></li><li>to fail <i>(memory)</i></li><li>to desert</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) + Old East Slavic <i>мѣнити</i> (měniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>измени́ть</i> (izmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traitor, betrayer</li></ol><p><i>изме́на</i> (izména) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>changeableness, mutability; changeability</li></ol><p><i>изме́нчивый</i> (izménčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>changeable, inconstant, unsteady, fickle, volatile</li></ol><p><i>измени́ть</i> (izmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, to alter, to modify, to edit</li><li>to vary</li><li>to betray, to become unfaithful, to cheat on [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to break, to violate (an oath, promise)</li><li>to fail (one's memory, hearing, sight, strength, etc.)</li><li>to desert</li></ol><p><i>измени́ть</i> (izmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изменя́ть</i> (izmenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measurement, measuring, gauging</li><li>dimension, size</li><li>survey</li><li>sounding, fathoming</li><li>determination</li></ol><p><i>изме́рить</i> (izméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measurable</li></ol><p><i>изме́рить</i> (izméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measuring</li></ol><p><i>изме́рить</i> (izméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure (ascertain the quantity of a unit)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat in measuring, to give short measure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изме́рить</i> (izméritʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изме́риться</i> (izméritʹsja)</li><li>to measure <i>(a stated size)</i>, to amount <i>(to)</i>, to be as large [long, deep, etc] <i>(as)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from childhood, since childhood, since youth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haggardness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>haggard, peaked</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>измъжденъ</i> (izmŭždenŭ), past passive participle of <i>измъждити</i> (izmŭžditi). Related to <i>мозжи́ть</i> (mozžítʹ, “to ache dully but persistently”), <i>мо́зглый</i> (mózglyj, “dank; frail”), dialectal <i>мо́згнуть</i> (mózgnutʹ, “to spoil, to perish”), <i>мзга</i> (mzga, “wet weather”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rime, hoarfrost, glaze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drizzle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exhaust, to make extremely tired</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изму́чить</i> (izmúčitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>му́чать</i> (múčatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изму́читься</i> (izmúčitʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>изму́чать</i> (izmúčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exhausted, worn out, tired out (<i>experiencing exhaustion; of a person</i>)</li><li>exhausted, worn out, tired out (<i>expressing exhaustion; of a face, look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li><li>to be exhausted</li></ol><p><i>изму́чивать</i> (izmúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weary, to tire out, to wear out, to exhaust</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) + <i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ, “to torment, torture, wear out”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li><li>to become exhausted</li></ol><p><i>изму́чить</i> (izmúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to invent, to fabricate, to forge</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to contrive, to devise</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabrication, invention</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) lie, fabrication</li></ol><p><i>измы́слить</i> (izmýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to invent, to fabricate, to forge</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to contrive, to devise</li></ol><p><i>измы́слить</i> (izmýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to crumple</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/become rumpled/crumpled</li></ol><p><i>измя́ть</i> (izmjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underside, inner side, wrong side, reverse side (of clothing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rape (<i>the crime of forcing another person to submit to sexual intercourse against their will</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изнаси́ловать</i> (iznasílovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rape, to violate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>наси́ловать</i> (nasílovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initially, originally, at first, first (at the beginning)</li></ol><p><i>изнача́льный</i> (iznačálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initial, primordial, pristine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wear out (clothing, footwear, machinery, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>износи́ть</i> (iznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pampered, spoiled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exhausted <i>(by)</i>, to break down <i>(with)</i>, to grow faint <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>изнемо́г-</i> (iznemóg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhaustion, weariness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>completely exhausted, extremely tired, worn out (<i>of a person; experiencing complete exhaustion</i>)</li><li>bleary, fatigued (<i>of a look, one's eyes, etc.; expressing complete exhaustion</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exhausted <i>(by)</i>, to break down <i>(with)</i>, to grow faint <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to carry out, bring forth</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to bring, deliver</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to report, proclaim, declare</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to say, pronounce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изнести</i> (iznesti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, extermination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wear, deterioration</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) rape</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>изнаси́лование</i> (iznasílovanije, “rape”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wear out (clothing, footwear, machinery, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy-duty, wear-resistant (resistant to wear)</li></ol><p>From <i>изно́с</i> (iznós) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance to wear</li></ol><p><i>изно̀состо́йкий</i> (iznòsostójkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn-out, threadbare, shabby</li><li>depreciated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wearisome, wasting, exhausting</li></ol><p><i>изнури́ть</i> (iznurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out, to exhaust, to fatigue</li><li>to overwork, to overdrive</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>нури́ть</i> (nurítʹ, “to hang (one's head sadly)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out, to exhaust, to fatigue</li><li>to overwork, to overdrive</li></ol><p><i>изнури́ть</i> (iznurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn out, exhausted, fatigued (<i>experiencing exhaustion or fatigue; of a person</i>)</li><li>worn out, exhausted, fatigued (<i>expressing exhaustion or fatigue; of a look, face, tone, appearance, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from within, from the inside, from inside, out of</li><li>on the inside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pine away, to pine for, to languish</li><li>to be bored to death</li></ol><p><i>изны́ть</i> (iznýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pine away, to pine for, to languish</li><li>to be bored to death</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ныть</i> (nytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>из</i> (iz): from, out of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as hard as one can, with all one's might/strength, struggling, doing one's best, going all out</li></ol><p>Literally, “from (using) all of one's strength” (<i>си́ла</i> (síla, “strength”) is pluralised when not referring to a particular strength).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from day to day, day after day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>из-</i> (iz-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) isobar (nuclide)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isobar <i>line of constant pressure</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance, plenty, profusion, multitude, wealth, plethora</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>оби́лие</i> (obílije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abound, to bristle (to be plentiful)</li><li>to teem</li><li>to flow</li><li>to roll in</li><li>to be replete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plentiful, rich, rife, abundant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant, plentiful, affluent</li></ol><p><i>изоби́лие</i> (izobílije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unmask</li><li>to betray (to indicate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unmasker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unmask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depict, to picture, to portray</li><li>to describe; to represent, to paint</li><li>to imitate</li></ol><p><i>изобрази́ть</i> (izobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to appear</li></ol><p><i>изобража́ть</i> (izobražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image, picture, illustration, figure</li></ol><p><i>изобрази́ть</i> (izobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visual arts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graphic, figurative</li></ol><p><i>изобрази́ть</i> (izobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depict, to picture, to portray</li><li>to describe; to represent, to paint</li><li>to imitate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzobraziti</i>. Derived from <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz, “shape, image, figure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to appear</li></ol><p><i>изобрази́ть</i> (izobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent</li><li>to devise, to contrive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изобрѣсти</i> (izobrěsti), which is probably a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εξευρίσκω</i> (exeurískō, “to invent”). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>обрести́</i> (obrestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reinvent the wheel</li></ol><p>Literally, “to invent the bicycle”. Compare German <i>das Rad neu erfinden</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventor</li></ol><p><i>изобрета́ть</i> (izobretátʹ, “to invent”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventiveness, ingenuity, resourcefulness</li></ol><p><i>изобрета́тельный</i> (izobretátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inventive</li></ol><p><i>изобрета́ть</i> (izobretátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent (design a new process or mechanism)</li><li>to devise, to contrive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изобрѣтати</i> (izobrětati), which is probably a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εξευρίσκω</i> (exeurískō, “to invent”). By surface analysis, <i>изобрет-</i> (izobret-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reinvent the wheel</li></ol><p>Literally, “to invent the bicycle”. Compare German <i>das Rad neu erfinden</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invention</li></ol><p>From <i>изобрет-</i> (izobret-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije) or borrowed directly from Old Church Slavonic <i>изобрѣтениѥ</i> (izobrětenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isohypse (a line on a map connecting points of both equal height and equal barometric pressure, such as an isobar on a constant height chart)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, curved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve, to crook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve</li></ol><p><i>изогну́ть</i> (izognútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rend, to tear (<i>badly</i>)</li><li>to scratch all over</li></ol><p><i>изо-</i> (izo-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be drained of, to shed a lot of (blood, tears, etc.)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to expire, to come to an end</li></ol><p><i>изо-</i> (izo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>isolated, separate, solitary</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) insulated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to isolate <i>(from)</i>, to quarantine</li><li>to insulate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>isolieren</i>, German <i>isolieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) (per Shansky, however, French <i>isoler</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>īnsula</i> (whence also <i>и́нсула</i> (ínsula), <i>инсули́н</i> (insulín)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) isolate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) insulator</li><li>isolation ward, infirmary</li><li>jail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isolationism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isolation</li><li>insulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) isomer</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) + <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part, share, portion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) isomerism</li></ol><p><i>изоме́р</i> (izomér) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>isomeric</li></ol><p><i>изоме́р</i> (izomér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) isopod</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>isopod</i>, translingual <i>Isopoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rip up, to tear up, to tear to pieces</li><li>to tear in many places</li></ol><p><i>изо-</i> (izo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isotherm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) isotope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cultivate, to refine, to make perfect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изощрити</i> (izoštriti, “to sharpen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cultivate, to refine, to make perfect</li></ol><p><i>изощри́ть</i> (izoščrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sophistication, refinement</li><li>intricacy, convolutedness</li></ol><p><i>изощрённый</i> (izoščrjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sophisticated, clever, subtle, ingenious (<i>of a person; in general</i>)</li><li>sophisticated, clever, ingenious [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in some action’</small>] (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>sophisticated, clever, subtle, ingenious (<i>of an idea, one's mind, etc.</i>)</li><li>highly sensitive, keen (<i>of hearing, vision or another sense</i>)</li><li>refined, sophisticated (<i>of tastes; sometimes to an excessive degree</i>)</li><li>convoluted, tricky, ingenious (<i>of a lie, trick, scheme, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceramic tile</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc), with unexplained vowel shift in the root. Per Preobrazhensky (quoted by Trubachev), the root is rather <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ, “to strike”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Israeli</li><li>Israelite</li></ol><p><i>Изра́иль</i> (Izráilʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Israeli</li><li>Israelite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure (<i>gravely or in many places</i>)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ра́нить</i> (ránitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li><li>to consume, to use up</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>расхо́довать</i> (rasxódovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend, to lay out money</li><li>to be used <i>(up)</i>, to be spent/consumed</li></ol><p><i>израсхо́довать</i> (izrasxódovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rarely, seldom</li><li>from time to time, occasionally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut into many pieces</li><li>to use up by cutting into pieces</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut up (<i>with a knife or dagger, making many wounds</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to break up in many places, to make rugged or uneven (e.g. a coastline, terrain, one's face)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>изре́зывать</i> (izrézyvatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>изре́зать</i> (izrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut into many pieces</li><li>to use up by cutting into pieces</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut up (<i>with a knife or dagger, making many wounds</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to break up in many places, to make rugged or uneven (e.g. a coastline, terrain, one's face)</li></ol><p><i>изре́зать</i> (izrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;archaic) to utter, to speak solemnly / pompously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mouth, to say</li></ol><p><i>изрек-</i> (izrek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apophthegm, dictum, saying</li></ol><p><i>изре́к-</i> (izrék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;archaic) to utter, to speak solemnly / pompously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mouth, to say</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzreťi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to cover completely in holes, to pierce in many places, to riddle</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>решети́ть</i> (rešetítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to cover completely in holes, to pierce in many places, to riddle</li></ol><p><i>изрешети́ть</i> (izrešetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to rifle through, to search through (all of something)</li><li>to rip up, to tear up, to tear to pieces</li><li>to tear in many places</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>изры́ть</i> (izrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to belch, to disgorge, to spew out</li></ol><p><i>изры́гнуть</i> (izrýgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to rifle through, to search through (all of something)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fairly, considerably, very</li></ol><p><i>изря́дный</i> (izrjádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>considerable, sizeable, fair</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) good</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religious) fanatic</li><li>outcast</li><li>beast, fiend, sadist, brute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maim, to mutilate</li></ol><p><i>изуве́чить</i> (izuvéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maim, to mutilate</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>уве́чить</i> (uvéčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>изуми́тельный</i> (izumítelʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>изуми́тельно</i> (izumítelʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amazingly, marvellously, lovely</li></ol><p><i>изуми́тельный</i> (izumítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, stupendous, wonderful</li></ol><p><i>изуми́ть</i> (izumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amaze, to astound, to astonish, to strike (to fill with surprise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изоумѣти</i> (izuměti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Originally meant "to make mad".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be amazed, to be astonished, to be surprised, to marvel (at)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изоумѣти сѧ</i> (izuměti sę). By surface analysis, <i>изуми́ть</i> (izumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amazement, astonishment, awe</li></ol><p><i>изуми́ть</i> (izumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amaze, to astound, to astonish, to strike (to fill with surprise)</li></ol><p><i>изуми́ть</i> (izumítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be amazed, to be astonished, to be surprised, to marvel (at)</li></ol><p><i>изумля́ть</i> (izumljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a wondering manner</li><li>in an amazed, astonished, surprised manner</li></ol><p><i>изумлённый</i> (izumljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazed, astonished, surprised, dumbfounded (<i>experiencing amazement, astonishment or surprise; of a person</i>)</li><li>amazed, astonished, surprised, dumbfounded (<i>expressing amazement, astonishment or surprise; of a look, face, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emerald</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian زمرد (zomorrod) (per Burlak also the source of <i>зиморо́док</i> (zimoródok)). Doublet of <i>смарагд</i> (smaragd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) emerald</li><li>emerald (color)</li></ol><p><i>изумру́д</i> (izumrúd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure, to deform, to mar</li><li>to mutilate, to maim, to cripple</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>уро́довать</i> (uródovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to learn (<i>implying that an individual or a group is studying or learning something with an in-depth approach</i>)</li><li>to familiarize oneself with, to master</li><li>to analyse, to look into, to scrutinize</li></ol><p><i>изучи́ть</i> (izučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>studying (<i>of a particular subject</i>), exploration</li></ol><p><i>изучи́ть</i> (izučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-known, familiar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn, to study</li><li>to research, to explore</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzučiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>из</i> (iz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to destroy by eating (<i>e.g. of moths, ants</i>)</li><li>to corrode, to eat into (<i>of acid</i>)</li></ol><p><i>изъе́д-</i> (izʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to travel all over</li><li>to ruin (e.g. a road) by riding on for too long</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to travel all over</li><li>to ruin (e.g. a road) by riding on for too long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn out, overused (<i>of a road, field, etc.; from excessive use</i>)</li><li>jaded, hackneyed, worn out (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to destroy by eating (<i>e.g. of moths, ants</i>)</li><li>to corrode, to eat into (<i>of acid</i>)</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to travel all over</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indicative mood (indicative mood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indicative (relating to the indicative mood)</li></ol><p><i>изъяви́ть</i> (izʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to express</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reveal</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to express</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reveal</li></ol><p><i>изъяви́ть</i> (izʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defect, fault, stain, loss, flaw</li></ol><p>From Persian زیان (ziyân, “damage, injury”), influenced by <i>изъя́ть</i> (izʺjátʹ, “withdraw, remove”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to express, to state</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>-ясни́ть</i> (-jasnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to express oneself, to explain one's thoughts</li></ol><p><i>изъясни́ть</i> (izʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to express, to state</li></ol><p><i>изъясни́ть</i> (izʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to express oneself, to explain one's thoughts</li></ol><p><i>изъясня́ть</i> (izʺjasnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withdrawal, removal, immobilization <i>(of currency)</i></li><li>exception</li></ol><p><i>изъя́ть</i> (izʺjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, to withdraw</li><li>to take away, to confiscate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изѧти</i> (izęti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzъjęti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ). Cognate with Polish <i>zdjąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confiscation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, to withdraw</li><li>to take away, to confiscate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изимати</i> (izimati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzimati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>ыма́ть</i> (ymátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) extravagance, outlandishness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigation, research, (mining) prospect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Prepositional case of <b>изысканный</b></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refinement, elegance, grace, daintiness</li></ol><p><i>изы́сканный</i> (izýskannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>refined, elegant</li><li>choice, exquisite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to find, to discover through diligent searching</li></ol><p><i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ). Cognate with Polish <i>zyskać</i> (“to gain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to find, to discover through diligent searching</li></ol><p><i>изыска́ть</i> (izyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raisin</li></ol><p>A Turkic loan word. Cognate with Uyghur ئۈزۈم (üzüm, “grape”), Turkish <i>üzüm</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Изюм)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Izium (a city in Kharkiv Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From <i>изю́м</i> (izjúm, “raisin”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>изю́м</i> (izjúm): (an individual) raisin</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) salt, zest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) raisin</li></ol><p><i>изю́м</i> (izjúm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finesse, refinedness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elegantly, fine, smart</li></ol><p><i>изя́щный</i> (izjáščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elegance</li></ol><p><i>изя́щн(ый)</i> (izjáščn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fine, refined</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>изѧщьнъ</i> (izęštĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzętjьnъ</i>. Compare Czech <i>vzácný</i> (“precious”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Й</i>/<i>й</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiccup</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce unstressed <i>е</i> (je) as <i>и</i> (i), to the Standard Russian pronunciation, to rather than as <i>е</i> (je)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьkati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>и</i> (i, onomatopoeic) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ).</p><p><i>и</i> (i) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ikebana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>生け花</i> (いけばな, ikebana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiccup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) icon, usually referring to picturesque images of saints canonized in Eastern Orthodoxy or Catholicism.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>εικόνα</i> (eikóna), from Ancient Greek <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ико́на</i> (ikóna): (a small) icon (religious painting)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) icon (small picture)</li></ol><p><i>ико́на</i> (ikóna) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iconography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) iconoclasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iconograph, icon painter</li></ol><p><i>и́конопись</i> (íkonopisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon painting</li></ol><p>From <i>ико́на</i> (ikóna) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), with an unexpected stress shift onto the initial syllable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iconodulism</li></ol><p>From <i>ико́на</i> (ikóna) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>почита́ние</i> (počitánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iconostasis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from some European language; compare German <i>Ikonostase</i>, French <i>iconostase</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>икра́</i> (ikrá): caviar</li></ol><p>From <i>и́кра</i> (íkra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caviar bowl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caviar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icosahedron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiccup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only singular</i>) roe, spawn (<i>of fish</i>), also small eggs of frogs or other amphibia</li><li>(<i>only singular</i>) caviar (<i>as food</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;only singular) paste (dish of mushrooms or vegetables minced into small particles)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) calf of the leg, (<i>musculus gastrocnemius</i>) gastrocnemius muscle</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) drifting ice floe (<i>especially on a river</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>икра</i> (ikra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьkrā</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ikrā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yékʷr̥</i>. Compare the semantic development of English <i>roe</i>; however, this is not an etymological cognate.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caviar</li></ol><p><i>икр(а́)</i> (ikr(á)) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter X, x.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ikebana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>生け花</i> (いけばな, ikebana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silt, clay, mud, ooze, sludge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>илъ</i> (ilŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьlъ</i>. Compare Czech <i>jíl</i>, Polish <i>ił</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or</li><li>or else</li><li>either</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>или</i> (ili). By surface analysis, univerbation of <i>и</i> (i) +&lrm; <i>ли</i> (li).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Или)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ili (a river in Kazakhstan and China)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) or what</li></ol><p>Literally, “or how”. Compare Polish <i>czy jak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) illative case (grammatical case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illusion (anything that seems to be something that it is not)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illusory</li></ol><p>Probably an adapted borrowing of French <i>illusoire</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Latin <i>illūsōrius</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>ілюзо́рний</i> (iljuzórnyj), Belarusian <i>ілюзо́рны</i> (iljuzórny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porthole, viewport, (circular) window (e.g. on an airplane), bullseye</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illumination</li></ol><p><i>иллюмин(и́ровать)</i> (illjumin(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illuminate</li><li>to color (a painting)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>illuminieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illustration, picture</li><li>example</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illustrated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illustrate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>illustrieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ил</i> (il).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) <i>a shortened version of</i> <b>и́ли (íli)</b>: or, or else, either</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elm, wych elm (<i>Ulmus glabra</i>) (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьlьmъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ilkhan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>named after (usually not translated, see <i>и́мя</i> (ímja))</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>и́мени</i> (ímeni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) imago (final developmental stage of an insect after undergoing metamorphosis)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) imago (idealized concept of a loved one)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>imāgō</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>dospělec</i> (akin to <i>спе́лый</i> (spélyj)). Compare typologically a coordinate term <i>личи́нка</i> (ličínka) (akin to <i>лик</i> (lik), <i>о́блик</i> (óblik), <i>обли́чье</i> (oblíčʹje)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) imam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic إِمَام (ʔimām).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;ironic) to take, to grasp, to catch, to get, to acquire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьmati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) something OP (overpowered, providing a player or team with too great an advantage in a way that disrupts the balance of the game)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) a large, cast-iron bowl in which lunch is cooked in a field</li><li>imba (household or dwelling among the Shona people of Zimbabwe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>imba</i>, a clipping of <i>imbalanced</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>im-</i> (further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;né</i>) + Latin <i>bilanx</i> (whence also Russian <i>бала́нс</i> (baláns)).</p><p>Borrowed from Shona <i>imbá</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) unbalanced; imbalanced (lacking game balance, such that gameplay is unfair in some way)</li></ol><p>From English <i>imbalance</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare <i>имбо́вый</i> (imbóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imbecile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ginger; made of ginger</li></ol><p>From <i>имби́рь</i> (imbírʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginger</li></ol><p>First attested as <i>инби́рь</i> (inbírʹ). Borrowed from Polish <i>imbir</i>, from older German <i>Imber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;video games) OP (overpowered, providing a player or team with too great an advantage in a way that disrupts the balance of the game)</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) awesome; amazing</li></ol><p>From <i>имба́</i> (imbá, “something OP”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj), from English <i>imba</i>, a clipping of <i>imbalanced</i>. Compare <i>имбала́нсный</i> (imbalánsnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>email (system, message)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>email</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>named after (usually not translated, see <i>и́мя</i> (ímja))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estate, manor, domain, property</li></ol><p><i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthday boy/man (someone celebrating their birthday)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) name-day boy/man (someone celebrating their name day, i.e. the day of the saint who they are named after)</li></ol><p><i>имени́ны</i> (imeníny) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) name-day woman or girl (woman or girl celebrating her name day, i.e. the day of the saint who she is named after)</li></ol><p><i>имени́н(ник)</i> (imenín(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) name day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name day</li><li>(<i>popularly</i>) birthday</li></ol><p>From <i>имен-</i> (imen-) (stem of <i>и́мя</i> (ímja, “name”)) + the plural form of <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) nominative</li></ol><p>From <i>имен-</i> (imen-, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj), formed by analogy to other cases such as <i>роди́тельный</i> (rodítelʹnyj, “genitive”) that end in <i>-и́тельный</i> (-ítelʹnyj) (the corresponding verb is actually <i>именова́ть</i> (imenovátʹ, “to name”)). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὀνομαστική</i> (onomastikḗ, “for naming”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distinguished, eminent</li></ol><p><i>имен-</i> (imen-) +&lrm; <i>-и́тый</i> (-ítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) predicative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used to emphasise, clarify or confirm the meaning or identity of the following word or what comes later and sometimes can be rather difficult to translate into English. Often translated with a word order involving "it was &#91;this&#93; that", "&#91;this&#93; is what", "&#91;this&#93; is the one that" etc., otherwise "exactly", "precisely", "just", "specifically", etc. or not translated.</li><li>(usually as <i>а <b>и́менно</b></i> (a <b>ímenno</b>)) namely, specifically, which is/are, to wit; used to specify something or to enumerate a list.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exactly, that's right, right, precisely, indeed; used to emphasise agreement or confirmation.</li></ol><p><i>именно́й</i> (imennój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) name; nominal, nominative</li><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) noun</li></ol><p><i>имен-</i> (imen-, “noun”) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>named (having a name)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>именовати</i> (imenovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьmenovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>имен-</i> (imen-, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be called</li></ol><p><i>именова́ть</i> (imenovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have, to possess</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;colloquial) to fuck, to have</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to have to; to be obliged (to do something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьměti</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>мець</i> (mjecʹ), Bulgarian <i>имам</i> (imam), Ukrainian <i>ма́ти</i> (máty), Polish <i>mieć</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>имати</i>/<i>imati</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>имѣти</i> (iměti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mean (to signify)</li><li>to bear in mind, to keep in mind, to consider, to note</li></ol><p>Literally, “to have in view”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be, to be present, to be available, to there is / there are</li></ol><p><i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image (characteristic as perceived by others)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>image</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imageboard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>imageboard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitation, mimicking (act of imitating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic, to imitate</li><li>to simulate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>imitieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immigrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) immigration; immigrational</li></ol><p><i>иммигра́ция</i> (immigrácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immigration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to immigrate (to move in from another country or area)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to immunize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>immūnitās, immūnitātem</i> (“immunity”, literally “freedom or exemption from taxes or public service”), from <i>in-</i> (negative prefix) + <i>mūnus</i> (“duty”) + -is + <i>-tās</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immune</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>immūnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunoglobulin</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>immunoglobulin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunodeficiency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) immunodiagnosis</li></ol><p><i>иммуно-</i> (immuno-) +&lrm; <i>диагностика</i> (diagnostika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>иммуноло́гия</i> (immunológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immunological</li></ol><p>From <i>иммуноло́гия</i> (immunológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunology</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>Immunolog(ie)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immunotherapy (the treatment of disease by adjusting the body's immune response)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) immunoassay</li></ol><p><i>имму́нный</i> (immúnnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ферме́нт</i> (fermént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impala</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>imperātor</i>. The feminine form is influenced by the oblique stem (compare the ablative singular <i>imperātrīce</i>) of Latin <i>imperātrīx</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>emperor's, empress's; imperial</li></ol><p><i>импера́тор</i> (imperátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperialist</li><li>someone who lives in an empire; a subject or inhabitant under the rule of an empire</li><li>(politics,&#32;neologism,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) a supporter of Russian imperialism.</li></ol><p>From <i>импе́рия</i> (impérija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperialism (the policy of extending power, by force)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperialist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperialist</li></ol><p><i>империали́ст</i> (imperialíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>imperium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;slang,&#32;politics) the flag of the Russian Empire during the reign of Tsar Alexander II, nowadays associated with far-right groups.</li></ol><p><i>импе́рия</i> (impérija, “imperial, related to the Russian Empire”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperial</li></ol><p><i>импе́рия</i> (impérija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperfect, imperfect tense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) impetigo (contagious bacterial skin disease)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>impetīgō</i>, from <i>impetĕre</i> (literally “to rush upon, assail, attack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impeachment (the act of impeaching a public official)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>impeachment</i>. Further from Latin <i>impedicō</i> (whence also <i>депе́ша</i> (depéša)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>implementation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) implication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>imposingly, impressively, strikingly</li></ol><p><i>импоза́нтный</i> (impozántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imposing, grand, spectacular, distinguished-looking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impress (someone), to make an impression (on someone), to appeal <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>imponieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). Further from Latin <i>impōnō</i> (p.p.p. <i>impositus</i>). Compare Polish <i>imponować</i> (direct borrowing from Latin), also compare Serbo-Croatian <i>импонирати</i>. Further from <i>in-</i> + Latin <i>pōnō</i> (p.p.p. <i>positus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>import</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to import (to bring in from a foreign country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imported, import</li></ol><p><i>и́мпорт</i> (ímport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>import substitution, import substitution industrialization</li></ol><p><i>и́мпорт</i> (ímport) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>замеще́ние</i> (zameščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>importer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>importer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impotent (man)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impotent</li></ol><p><i>импоте́нт</i> (impotént, impotɛ́nt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impotence</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>impotentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impresario (a manager or producer in the entertainment industry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>impresario</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impressionism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impressionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improviser</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improvisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improvised, impromptu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improvise, to extemporize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>improvisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urge, sudden desire</li><li>stimulus, impetus</li><li>action potential, spike train</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) momentum</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) impulse (of force), the integral of force over time</li><li>wave packet</li><li>pulse, surge, short electric signal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impulsive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>impulsiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) impulse</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pulse; pulsed</li></ol><p><i>и́мпульс</i> (ímpulʹs) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) property, material</li></ol><p><i>иму́щество</i> (imúščestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property, belongings</li></ol><p><i>иму́щий</i> (imúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, wealthy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>имушть</i> (imuštĭ), present active participle of <i>имѣти</i> (iměti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;hedge) IMHO (in my humble opinion)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) opinion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>IMHO</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>им</i> (im):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>instrumental of <i>онъ</i> (on)</li><li>instrumental of <i>оно́</i> (onó)</li><li>dative of <i>они́</i> (oní)</li><li>dative of <i>онѣ́</i> (oně́)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personal name, first name, Christian name, given name (for inanimate things, see <i>назва́ние</i> (nazvánije))</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) noun (broad sense)</li><li>renown, reputation</li><li>name (for inanimate things without intention to animate them)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>имѧ</i> (imę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьmę</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;inˀmen</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁nómn̥</i>.</p><p>Cognates include English <i>name</i> and <i>noun</i>, Old Prussian <i>emmens</i>, Sanskrit <i>नामन्</i> (nāman), Persian نام (nâm), Ancient Greek <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma), Latin <i>nōmen</i>.</p><p>Neologism sense: calque of English <i>name</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name and patronymic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) common noun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) adjective</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>nomen adiectivum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) proper noun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) noun, substantive</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>nomen substantivum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numeral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) name and patronymic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;relational) name day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) name day</li></ol><p>From <i>имян-</i> (imjan-) (stem of <i>и́мя</i> (ímja, “name”)) + the plural form of <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) onomatodoxy</li></ol><p><i>и́мя</i> (ímja, “name”) +&lrm; <i>сла́вить</i> (slávitʹ, “glorify”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism,&#32;humorous) Instagram, a social media website</li></ol><p>From <i>Инстагра́м</i> (Instagrám, “Instagram, a social media website”) +&lrm; <i>сто</i> (sto, “a hundred”), with "ста" read as "сто" or "100". Ultimately from English <i>Instagram</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissent, dissidence</li><li>heterodoxy</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>инако</i> (inako) (from <i>&#42;jьnako</i> (“otherwise, differently”)) + <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to inactivate, to block the activity of, to reduce the effectiveness of</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>inaktivieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inauguration (act of inaugurating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently, in a different way; otherwise</li><li>otherwise, else, or else</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьnako</i>. Comparative degree of either Old East Slavic <i>инакъ</i> (inakŭ), Old East Slavic <i>инако</i> (inako) or Old Church Slavonic <i>инакъ</i> (inakŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in other words (stated or interpreted another way)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allen key (a hex head wrench)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>INBUS</i>, abbreviation of <i>Innensechskantschraube Bauer &amp; Schaurte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Allen wrench, Allen key</li></ol><p><i>и́нбус</i> (ínbus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allen key (a hex head wrench)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) invasive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a disabled person, an invalid</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) an invalid (<i>a retired soldier unfit for active duty because of age or injury</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>invalide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;colloquial) vehicle designed for disabled people</li></ol><p><i>инвали́д</i> (invalíd) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disability, invalidism</li></ol><p><i>инвали́дный</i> (invalídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) disabled, handicapped (<i>of or for use by a disabled person</i>); disabled person's; invalid's</li></ol><p><i>инвали́д</i> (invalíd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventory, stocktaking; inventory taking, inventory control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventory, stock</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the 18th century from German <i>Inventar</i> (“inventory”), from Latin <i>inventārium</i>; suffix influenced by <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) invention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inversion</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>inversiō</i> (“inversion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investment, investing</li></ol><p><i>инвести́ровать</i> (investírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) investment, investing</li></ol><p><i>инвести́ция</i> (investícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Investition</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invoice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>invoice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to inhibit (a chemical reaction)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>inhibieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhibitor, retarder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;paganism) Ynglism, an esoteric neopagan sect from Russia.</li></ol><p><i>инглинг</i> (ingling, “Yngling, a Scandinavian royal dynasty”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm).</p><p>In Ynglist belief, the name refers to a fiery primordial radiance known as <i>Инглия</i> (Inglija) emanated by the supreme god <i>Рамха</i> (Ramxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingredient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ingush (<i>male</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ingush <i>Ангушт</i> (Angušt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ingush</li></ol><p><i>ингу́ш</i> (ingúš) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered with frost</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>и́ней</i> (ínej, “hoarfrost”) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), with epenthetic д. Compare archaic/dialectal <i>иневеть</i> (inevetʹ). Possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;inьjevěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian, Native American</li></ol><p>From English <i>Indian</i>. Applied to inhabitants of the Americas due to an early misconception that the Americas were the eastern end of Asia / the Indies.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indian (relating to the aboriginal people of the Americas)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian summer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>index</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indexation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to index (to arrange an index)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indie pop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>indie pop</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) individual</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>indīviduum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>individualism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>individually (separately, independently)</li></ol><p><i>индивидуа́льный</i> (individuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>individuality</li></ol><p><i>индивидуа́льный</i> (individuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>individual (relating to a single person or thing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>individual</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>individuum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indigo (<i>dye</i>)</li><li>indigo (<i>color/colour</i>)</li><li>indigo plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) indigo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian, Hindu</li></ol><p><i>И́ндия</i> (Índija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indian, Hindu, East Indian (relating to India or the East Indians)</li></ol><p><i>И́ндия</i> (Índija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian elephant (Elephas maximus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indicative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indicator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indifferently</li></ol><p><i>индиффере́нтный</i> (indifferéntnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indifferent</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;about an oxide) neutral (showing neither basic nor acidic properties)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>indifferēns</i>, probably via Polish <i>indyferentny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Indo-European (person), a member of the old Indo-European people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indo-European</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Indo-Iranian</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>индо-</i> (indo-, “Indo-”) +&lrm; <i>ира́нский</i> (iránskij, “Iranian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indonesian (person from Indonesia)</li></ol><p><i>Индоне́зия</i> (Indonɛ́zija, Indonézija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indonesian</li></ol><p><i>Индоне́зия</i> (Indonɛ́zija, Indonézija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) a fabulous beast in Russian mythology, the “father of all animals”</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps an alteration of <i>единоро́г</i> (jedinoróg, “unicorn”); alternatively, perhaps from the Slavic transliteration of an animal name (Ancient Greek <i>ἔνυδρις</i> (énudris, “otter”) or <i>ὕδρωψ</i> (húdrōps, “water beast, aurochs”)) in the <i>Physiologus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hinduism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hindouisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) inductive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>induction, inductance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Catholicism</i>) indulgence, remission (<i>of sins</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) leniency, pardon, forgiveness for any improper act</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>indulgentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hindu (<i>male</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) Indian (<i>a man from India</i>)</li><li>(programming&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) junior coder, code monkey (an inexperienced or unqualified software developer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hindu</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) Indian (relating to India or (East) Indians)</li></ol><p><i>инду́с</i> (indús) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industrialization</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>industrialisation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>industrial (relating to industry)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>industria</i> from <i>industrius</i> (“active, assiduous, diligent, industrious”) from Old Latin <i>indu</i>, archaic variant of <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>struō</i> (“to arrange, build, compose, construct”) from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;strowō</i> (“to pile up, construct”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strew-</i> (“to spread, strew”). Compare English <i>industry</i>, French <i>industrie</i>, German <i>Industrie</i>, Italian <i>industria</i>, Portuguese <i>indústria</i>, Spanish <i>industria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to induce</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>induzieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male turkey (bird), turkeycock, tom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>indicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turkey <i>(meat)</i></li></ol><p><i>индюша́та</i> (indjušáta, “young turkeys”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina, suffix denoting a type of meat)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turkey-poult, young turkey</li></ol><p><i>индю́к</i> (indjúk) +&lrm; <i>-онок</i> (-onok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarfrost, frost, rime</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) white hair</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьnьjь</i> / <i>&#42;jьnьje</i> / <i>&#42;jьnьja</i> (“hoar-frost, rime”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sluggishness, inactivity, inertia, passivity, inertness</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) inertia</li></ol><p><i>ине́ртный</i> (inɛ́rtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inert</li><li>slow, lazy, passive</li></ol><p>From English <i>inert</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or from French <i>inerte</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Ultimately from Latin <i>iners</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) inert gas, noble gas (the elements of group 18 of the periodic table)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inertial</li></ol><p><i>ине́рция</i> (inɛ́rcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) inertia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the Internet</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>интерне́т</i> (intɛrnɛ́t, “internet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injector (any of various devices that are used to inject something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engineer (person qualified or professionally engaged in engineering)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ingenieur</i>, from French <i>ingénieur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engineering</li></ol><p><i>инжене́р</i> (inženér) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engineering and technical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) engineers, engineering</li></ol><p><i>инжене́р</i> (inženér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig</li></ol><p>From Turkic (compare Turkish <i>incir</i>), from Persian انجیر (anjir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initial (the first letter of a word or a name)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to initialize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) anlaut, onset, initial, first part of a syllable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>initiative</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>initiō</i>, from Latin <i>initium</i>, from Latin <i>ineō</i> (<i>in-</i> + <i>eō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initiative, having/taking the initiative, full of initiative</li></ol><p><i>инициати́ва</i> (iniciatíva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiator (one who initiates)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>initiateur</i>, from Late Latin <i>initiātor</i>, from <i>initiō</i>, from Latin <i>initium</i>, from <i>ineō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>initiātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to initiate, to institute</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>initiieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money collector, collection agent, cash messenger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>incassare</i> (“to collect cash, put in a box”). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>capsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquisitor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inquisition (tribunal of the Roman Catholic Church)</li><li>Inquisition (harsh or rigorous interrogation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scumbag, rascal</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>инко́гнито</i> (inkógnito, “incognito”) +&lrm; <i>гни́да</i> (gnída, “nit; scumbag, rascal”). Perhaps the form was first used in the story “Inkognida” (1927) by Vasily Lebedev-Kumach. However, the form is used by him as the surname of the auditor with hyperbole and satire. The etymology was proposed by Margarita S. Maleeva in <i>Leksicheskaya i sintaksicheskaya obʺektivaciya znaniya v slovoobrazovatel'nom kontekste</i> (1983: 50).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incognito</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incognito</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>incognito</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incorporation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to incorporate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incrustation, inlay (process or result)</li><li>embellishment (<i>in a work of art or literature, or in a speech</i>)</li></ol><p>Derivable synchronically as <i>инкрусти́ровать</i> (inkrustírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inlaying, incrustation</li></ol><p><i>инкрусти́ровать</i> (inkrustírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incrust, to inlay</li></ol><p>From German <i>inkrustieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), or directly from French <i>incruster</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incubus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatcher, incubator, nursery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incubation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>incubātiō</i>. Also derivable synchronically as <i>инкуби́ровать</i> (inkubírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incubate</li></ol><p>From German <i>inkubieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), or directly from French <i>incuber</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;zoology) to innervate (to supply (an organ or part of the body) with nerves)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) innovation</li><li>innovative, ground-breaking</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>innovatio</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innovation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>innowacja</i>, from Late Latin <i>innovatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign agent</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>иностра́нный аге́нт</i> (inostránnyj agént).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) another form of existence, being</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бытие́</i> (bytijé)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) adherent of a different faith, infidel (a non-Christian in the context of the Russian Empire)</li></ol><p>From <i>инове́рный</i> (inovérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sometimes, at times</li><li>now and then</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>иногда</i> (inogda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьnogъda</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-гда</i> (-gda); see <i>когда́</i> (kogdá) for the origin of the last element.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonresident (<i>with respect to a given city</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-resident (<i>with respect to a given city</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) non-Cossack peasant (<i>in non-Cossack regions of Russia before 1917</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) stranger, foreigner (person in a foreign land)</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) foreign</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зем(ля́)</i> (zem(ljá)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>other, another, different</li><li>some, many a</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>инъ</i> (inŭ) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jь̀nъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;iˀnas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁inos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sometimes, at times</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) monk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>инокъ</i> (inokŭ); distantly cognate with Latin <i>ūnicus</i> (“sole, only”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌷𐌰</i> (ainaha, “only”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foreign-made car</li></ol><p>From <i>иностра́нная ма́рка</i> (inostránnaja márka); since the 1980s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) Any mushroom of the genus <i>Inonotus</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Inonotus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extraterrestrial</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плане́та</i> (planéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alien, extraterrestrial</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плане́та</i> (planéta, “planet”) +&lrm; <i>-я́нин</i> (-jánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) person belonging to a different tribe or nation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>иноплємєньникъ</i> (inoplemenĭnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a different tribe or nation, foreign</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пле́мен-</i> (plémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;strictly) A representative of a special class of Russian subjects to whom different legislation applied by virtue of their ethnicity; These were essentially Jews and certain indigenous peoples of the Caucasus, the Caspian basin, Central Asia, Siberia and the Arctic.</li><li>(historical,&#32;informal) Any non-East Slavic subject of the Russian Empire; can be translated as "alien, foreigner" or also "aboriginal", depending on context.</li></ol><p>From <i>иноро́дный</i> (inoródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, alien</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allegory</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀλληγορία</i> (allēgoría, “allegory”, literally “other-speaking”), from <i>ино́й</i> (inój, “other”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ние</i> (skazánije, “story”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreigner</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>иностра́нкой</i> (inostránkoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amble, pace (a lateral gait of a horse)</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foreign-language</li></ol><p><i>ино́й</i> (inój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) insight (in a general sense)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) insight (sudden understanding or "grasping" of a solution to a problem, first studied by Gestalt psychology)</li><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) insight (ability to recognise one's own mental illness, typically absent in psychoses but present in neuroses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>insight</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insecticide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insinuation (a suggestion or intimation by distant allusion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>controller, inspector, surveyor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspection</li><li>organisation intended to carry out inspections</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Instagrammer</li></ol><p><i>инстагра́м</i> (instagrám, “Instagram”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik, “doer”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) installation</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) (process of) installation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>installation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) instance</li><li>(<i>military</i>) authority</li><li>(official) channels, hierarchy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instinct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instinct of self-preservation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>instinctively</li></ol><p><i>инстинкти́вный</i> (instinktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>instinctive</li><li>involuntary, unconscious</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>instinktiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>institute</li><li>institution</li><li>college, school (in a university)</li><li>girl’s boarding school</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) finishing school for daughters of nobility</li><li>(idiomatic,&#32;chiefly in the negative) high society, a place for delicate and refined people</li></ol><p>Literally, “institute of noble maidens”. Calque of French <i>pensionnat de jeunes filles</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) institute</li></ol><p><i>институ́т</i> (institút) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>institutionalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>institutional</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>institutionell</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>institution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefing, instruction, instructing; training</li></ol><p><i>инструкти́ровать</i> (instruktírovatʹ, “to instruct”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to instruct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coach, instructor, trainer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction (act of instructing or that which instructs, or with which one is instructed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool (mechanical device intended to make a task easier)</li><li>instrument</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) instrumental</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) tool, toolmaking</li><li>(relational,&#32;computing) tool</li></ol><p><i>инструме́нт</i> (instrumént) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruments, tools (collectively), instrumentarium, tooling, toolkit, toolset</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>īnstrūmentārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insulin (polypeptide hormone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) stroke, apoplexy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt (a literary work) for theater, to film or television; to stage (a literary work)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>inszenieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), from German <i>in Szene</i> (“in scene”). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staging; direction; dramatization; performance</li><li>pretence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) integral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics) integral</li><li>(electronics,&#32;computing) integrated</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) integral, whole</li></ol><p><i>интегра́л</i> (intɛgrál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) integration</li></ol><p><i>интегра́ция</i> (intɛgrácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>integration (act or process of making whole or entire)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) integration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to integrate</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to integrate</li></ol><p>From German <i>integrieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to integrate, to acculture (familiarize oneself with a new culture)</li></ol><p><i>интегри́ровать</i> (intɛgrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain, intellect, intelligence, mentality, nous, pate, reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intellectual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) intellectual property</li></ol><p>From <i>интеллектуа́льная</i> (intellektuálʹnaja) + <i>со́бственность</i> (sóbstvennostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intellectual (belonging to, or performed by, the intellect&#59; mental or cognitive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intellectual (intelligent person, interested in intellectual matters)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligence, intellectuality</li></ol><p><i>интеллиге́нтный</i> (intelligéntnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cultured, civilized, refined, educated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) intellectual, intelligentsia</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) intellectualist (characteristic of the supposed doubt and pessimism of intellectuals)</li></ol><p><i>интеллиге́нт</i> (intelligént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) intelligentsia (the intellectual elite of a society, particularly in Marxist doctrine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>inteligencja</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>intellegentia</i> (“intelligence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) abbreviation of <i>интеллиге́нт</i> (intelligént); an intelligent person; an intellectual.</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>интеллиге́нт</i> (intelligént, “intellectual; intelligent person”), borrowed from English <i>intelligent</i>. Somewhat similar in construction to English <i>intel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) quartermaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>intendant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) quartermaster's, service corps</li></ol><p><i>интендант</i> (intendant) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) quartermaster service, intendance, commissariat</li></ol><p><i>интендант</i> (intendant) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>intensively</li><li>vigorously</li></ol><p><i>интенси́вный</i> (intɛnsívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intensity, tenseness</li></ol><p><i>интенси́вный</i> (intɛnsívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intensive, intense</li><li>vigorous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intensification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>inter- (among, between)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interactive (acting with each other)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interval, space (a distance in space or time)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) interval</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intervention <i>(military)</i>, invasion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) intervocalic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>интер-</i> (inter-) +&lrm; <i>вока́льный</i> (vokálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interview</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>interview</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interviewer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>interviewer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interest [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Polish <i>interes</i> or German <i>Interesse</i>. In any case, ultimately from Latin <i>interesse</i> (“to make a difference, to concern, to matter”). First recorded in 1703, during the reign of Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>интере́сный</i> (interésnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>интере́сно</i> (interésno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>интере́сный</i> (interésnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>interestingly, in an interesting manner</li></ol><p><i>интере́сный</i> (interésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interesting</li><li>handsome, attractive</li></ol><p><i>интере́с</i> (interés) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interest, to concern</li></ol><p><i>интере́с</i> (interés) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be interested [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>to ask about something, to enquire</li></ol><p><i>интересова́ть</i> (interesovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) interconsonantal</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>интер-</i> (inter-) +&lrm; <i>консона́нтный</i> (konsonántnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Interlingua (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boarding school</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) international (transnational organization of political parties of similar ideology)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Интернациона́л</i> (Intɛrnacionál)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Интернационал)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Internationale (the traditional hymn of the groups of proletarians) (<i>also lower case</i>)</li><li>international (international association of workers) (<i>also lower case</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Internationale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) internationalism</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) internationalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internationalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>international</li><li>internationalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Internet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Internet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) meme (media, usually humorous, which is copied and circulated online with slight adaptations, such as basic pictures, video templates, etc)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Internet meme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) a collection of information resources posted on the Internet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>website</li></ol><p><i>интерне́т</i> (intɛrnɛ́t) +&lrm; <i>сайт</i> (sajt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expositor</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) interpreter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpretation, understanding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret, to expound, to construe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intersexuality (physical features of both sexes)</li></ol><p><i>интерсексуа́льный</i> (intɛrsɛksuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intersexual, intersex</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>intersexual</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) intertrigo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>intertrīgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) interface</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>interface</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;psychology,&#32;linguistics) interference</li></ol><p>From <i>интерфере́нция</i> (interferéncija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;psychology,&#32;linguistics) interference</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>interference</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interfere</li></ol><p>From German <i>interferieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interferometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interferometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interferon</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>interferon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interior (of a building, room)</li></ol><p>From French <i>intérieur</i>, itself from Latin <i>interior</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) close, intimate relationship</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intimacy (in a sexual or non-sexual sense)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anything intimate</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>инти́мный</i> (intímnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intimacy (feeling of closeness and openness towards someone else&#59; especially, sexual)</li></ol><p><i>инти́мный</i> (intímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimate (sexually)</li><li>intimate (romantically close)</li></ol><p>From French <i>intime</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intifada</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اِنْتِفَاضَة (intifāḍa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) intonational construction for dealing with Russian colloquial speeches (often abbreviated as <i>ИК</i>, from ИК-1 to ИК-7)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intonation, prosodic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;music) intonation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intranasal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>intranasal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intrigue, plot (<i>also in literature</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) scheming</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) amour, gallantry, love affair</li></ol><p>Derived from French <i>intrigue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) schemer, conspirator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>intrigant</i> (after the form <i>интрига́нт</i> (intrigánt) from German <i>Intrigant</i>).</p><p>Compare also Polish <i>intrygant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to plot, to scheme</li><li>to intrigue, to interest greatly</li></ol><p><i>интри́га</i> (intríga) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) introversion, a personality orientation towards the self and mental abstraction; behavior expressing such orientation.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ecology</i>) introduce (new species to an environment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>instinctively, intuitively, on a hunch, by intuition</li></ol><p><i>интуити́вный</i> (intuitívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intuitive (automatic, without requiring conscious thought)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intuition (immediate cognition without the use of rational processes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign tourist</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>иностра́нный тури́ст</i> (inostránnyj turíst, “foreign tourist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inuit</li></ol><p>From Inuktitut <i>ᐃᓄᐃᑦ</i> (inoit, “the people”), singular <i>ᐃᓄᒃ</i> (inok, “person”), from Proto-Inuit <i>&#42;inuɣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Inuit</li></ol><p><i>инуи́т</i> (inuít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inuktitut (language)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Inuktitut <i>ᐃᓄᒃᑎᑐᑦ</i> (inoktitot, “like the Inuit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) information</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>информа́ция</i> (informácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infantile, childish</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>infantile</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) infarct</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īnfarctus</i>, tu-stem derivation of <i>īnfarciō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infectious</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>Infektion</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infinitive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) infection, contagion, contamination</li></ol><p><i>инфици́ровать</i> (inficírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infect</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>infizieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(social media,&#32;marketing) influencer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>influencer</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;economics) inflation; inflationary</li></ol><p><i>инфля́ция</i> (infljácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflation (increase in prices)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>īnflātiō</i>, through the intermediate of another language such as Polish <i>inflacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informativeness, informativity, information value</li></ol><p><i>информати́вный</i> (informatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>informative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>informativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informatisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informatics</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) computer science</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>information and analytical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) information technology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) information; informational</li></ol><p><i>информа́ция</i> (informácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>information</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>īnfōrmātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>information bureau/centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informing</li></ol><p><i>информи́ровать</i> (informírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness (the state or quality of being aware of something)</li></ol><p><i>информи́рованный</i> (informírovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify, to advertise (to give public notice of&#59; to announce publicly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;offensive) marketing fraudster, business couch, personal growth coach, someone who provides untrue or unreliable information on the internet</li></ol><p><i>инфо</i> (info) +&lrm; <i>цыган</i> (cygan). Literally, “info-Gypsy”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>infra- (below, beneath, inferior)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infrared (also abbreviated as <i>ИК</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>инфра-</i> (infra-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infrastructure (an underlying base or foundation especially for an organization or system)</li></ol><p>From <i>инфра-</i> (infra-) +&lrm; <i>структу́ра</i> (struktúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infusorium (a minute aquatic creature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) incel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>incel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>incestus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incident (event causing interruption or crisis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) inshallah (God willing)</li></ol><p>A transcription of Arabic إِن شَاء اَللّٰه (ʔin šāʔ allāh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) inshallah (God willing)</li></ol><p>A transcription of the Arabic إِن شَاء اَللّٰه (ʔin šāʔ allāh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) injective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injection</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>iniectiō, iniectiōnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in other words</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 陰／阴 (<i>yīn</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yin-yang (a circular symbol consisting of a white and black droplet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 陰陽／阴阳 (<i>yīnyáng</i>), from Old Chinese.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;education) faculty or department of foreign languages</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;less common) foreign language (as a subject)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>иностра́нный язы́к</i> (inostránnyj jazýk, “foreign language”) or <i>иностра́нные языки́</i> (inostránnyje jazykí, “foreign languages”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>йод</i> (jod, “iodine”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>iode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dated form of <i>йо́дный</i> (jódnyj, “iodine”)</li></ol><p>From <i>иод</i> (iod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) iodometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) ion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἰόν</i> (ión, “going”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ion</li><li>ionic</li></ol><p><i>ио́н</i> (ión) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ionosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;obsolete) ionophoresis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jordanian</li></ol><p><i>Иорда́ния</i> (Iordánija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jordanian</li></ol><p><i>Иорда́ния</i> (Iordánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Josephite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) iota (the Greek letter Ι&#47;ι)</li><li>iota (small amount)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἰῶτα</i> (iôta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) ypakoë</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѵпакои́</i> (üpakoí), from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπακοή</i> (hupakoḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a local leader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὕπαρχος</i> (húparkhos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hypatos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὕπατος</i> (húpatos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subdeacon</li></ol><p>From <i>ипо-</i> (ipo-) +&lrm; <i>диа́кон</i> (diákon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) subdeacon</li></ol><p>From <i>ипо-</i> (ipo-) +&lrm; <i>дья́кон</i> (dʹjákon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>иподиа́кон</i> (ipodiákon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) hypocrite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѵпокри́тъ</i> (üpokrítŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ῠ̔ποκρῐτής</i> (hŭpokrĭtḗs, “actor, hypocrite”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theology</i>) hypostasis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπόστασις</i> (hupóstasis, “sediment, foundation; substance, existence, essence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypothec, mortgage</li></ol><p>From French <i>hypothèque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mortgage; hypothecary</li></ol><p><i>ипоте́ка</i> (ipotéka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypochondria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippodrome; racetrack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upsilon (Υ, υ&#59; the twentieth letter of Classical and Modern Greek&#59; the twenty-second letter of Old and Ancient Greek)</li><li>Y, y (25th letter of the German alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὖ ψιλόν</i> (û psilón, “simple Υ”) and German <i>Ypsilon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upsilon particle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>и́псилон</i> (ípsilon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iraqi</li></ol><p><i>Ира́к</i> (Irák) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Iraqi</li></ol><p><i>Ира́к</i> (Irák) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iranian (person from Iran or of Iranian descent)</li></ol><p><i>Ира́н</i> (Irán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Iranian</li></ol><p><i>Ира́н</i> (Irán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow leopard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠢᠷᠪᠢᠰ</i> (irbis), modern <i>ирвэс</i> (irves), from Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;irbis</i>, from Proto-Common Turkic <i>&#42;irbiš</i> whence within the distribution of the animal Uzbek <i>ilvirs</i>, Uyghur يىلپىز (yilpiz), Kyrgyz <i>илбирс</i> (ilbirs), Kazakh <i>ілбіс</i> (ılbıs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snow leopard</li></ol><p><i>и́рбис</i> (írbis) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juneberry, mespilus, shadbush (<i>Amelanchier</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cotoneaster (<i>Cotoneaster</i>)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>&#42;ïrgaï</i>.</p><p>Compare Kazakh <i>ырғай</i> (yrğai, “juneberry”), Mongolian <i>яргай</i> (jargaj, “dogwood, cornel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) iridium; iridic</li></ol><p><i>ири́дий</i> (irídij) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iridium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iris (flower)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) iris of the eye</li><li>toffee, fudge <i>(candy)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) German iris (<i>Iris</i> × <i>germanica</i>)</li></ol><p><i>и́рис</i> (íris, “iris”) +&lrm; <i>герма́нский</i> (germánskij, “German”), literally “German iris”, calque of translingual <i>Iris × germanica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Iberian iris (<i>Iris iberica</i>)</li></ol><p><i>и́рис</i> (íris, “iris”) +&lrm; <i>ибери́йский</i> (iberíjskij, “Iberian”), a calque of translingual <i>Iris iberica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Iris tigridia</i></li></ol><p><i>и́рис</i> (íris, “iris”) +&lrm; <i>тигро́вый</i> (tigróvyj, “tiger”, relational adjective), a calque of translingual <i>Iris tigridia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toffee</li><li>butterscotch</li></ol><p><i>ири́с</i> (irís) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) toffee, toffee's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) butterscotch, butterscotch's</li></ol><p><i>ири́ска</i> (iríska) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;relational) iris flower, iris flower's</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;relational) eye iris, eye iris's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) toffee, toffee's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) butterscotch, butterscotch's</li></ol><p><i>и́рис</i> (íris) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p><p><i>ири́с</i> (irís) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Irkutsk, of Irkutsk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Irkutsk</li></ol><p><i>Ирку́тск</i> (Irkútsk) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>ирла́ндский</i> (irlándskij): Ir., Irish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irishman</li></ol><p><i>Ирла́ндия</i> (Irlándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Irish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irish (language)</li></ol><p><i>Ирла́ндия</i> (Irlándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tyrant, villain, monster</li></ol><p>From <i>И́род</i> (Írod, “Herod”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ирод)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Herod</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>Иродъ</i> (Irodŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡρῴδης</i> (Hērōídēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iroquois, Iroquese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Iroquois</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak ironically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ironically (In an ironic manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ironic, ironical</li></ol><p><i>иро́ния</i> (irónija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ironically</li></ol><p><i>ирони́чный</i> (ironíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ironic</li></ol><p><i>иро́ния</i> (irónija) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) irony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īrōnīa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) irrationalism</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>irrationalis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexplicable</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) irrational</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>irratiōnālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irredentism (an annexation doctrine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>irredentismo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irrigation (The act or process of irrigating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irrumatio</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) tanned sheepskin or goatskin</li></ol><p>Via Polish <i>ircha</i> (“chamois leather”), from Middle High German <i>irch</i>, <i>еrсh</i> (“thin white tanned leather”), from Latin <i>hircus</i> (“goat”). Compare Ukrainian <i>ірха</i> (irxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>из-</i> (iz-)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Hesychasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) suit, action</li><li>complaint, claim</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distort, to disfigure</li></ol><p><i>искази́ть</i> (iskazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distortion, corruption (visual, auditory, etc.)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) corruption (of a word or phrase or its interpretation)</li><li>perversion (of justice, etc.)</li><li>misrepresentation, bias, falsification, twisting, contortion (of the truth, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>искази́ть</i> (iskazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contorted (<i>with rage</i>), drawn, strained (<i>about one's face</i>)</li><li>distorted (<i>meaning, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distort, to disfigure</li><li>to misrepresent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исказити</i> (iskaziti). By surface analysis, <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>кази́ть</i> (kazítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutilate, to cripple, to maim</li><li>to destroy, to ruin, to mutilate</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>кале́чить</i> (kaléčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>search</li></ol><p><i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iscariot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Ἰσκαριώτης</i> (Iskariṓtēs, “Iscariot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) seeker</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;technical) detector</li></ol><p><i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look for, to seek [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to sue (for)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьskati</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>и́скам</i> (ískam, “I want”), Sanskrit <i>इच्छति</i> (icchati, “to desire”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ѝскати</i>, <i>ìskati</i> (“to look for”), Lithuanian <i>ieškoti</i> (“to seek”), Old English <i>āscian</i> (whence English <i>ask</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be looking for a needle in a haystack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclude</li><li>to expel</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to eliminate</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>exclure</i> or German <i>ausschließen</i>, ultimately a calque of Latin <i>exclūdō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>исключи́ть</i> (isključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>except</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exception</li><li>expulsion, exclusion</li></ol><p><i>исключи́ть</i> (isključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exceptionally, exclusively, extraordinarily</li><li>solely, only</li></ol><p><i>исключи́тельный</i> (isključítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exceptionality</li><li>exclusiveness; singleness</li></ol><p><i>исключи́тельный</i> (isključítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceptional</li><li>exclusive</li><li>extraordinary, excellent</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>exclusif</i>, ultimately a calque of Latin <i>exclūsīvus</i>. By surface analysis, <i>исключи́ть</i> (isključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Doublet of <i>эксклюзи́вный</i> (ekskljuzívnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclude</li><li>to expel</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to eliminate</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>exclure</i> or German <i>ausschließen</i>, ultimately a calque of Latin <i>exclūdō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to damage, to ruin</li><li>to disfigure, to injure</li><li>to pervert</li><li>to distort (words, thoughts, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>кове́ркать</i> (kovérkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to damage, to ruin</li><li>to disfigure, to injure</li><li>to pervert</li><li>to distort (words, thoughts, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>искове́ркать</i> (iskovérkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;law) action, suit, lawsuit</li></ol><p><i>иск</i> (isk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) desired, wanted, sought-after</li></ol><p><i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́мый</i> (-ómyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) since olden times, from of old, from time immemorial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искони</i> (iskoni), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьz koni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aboriginal, native</li><li>primordial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исконьнъ</i> (iskonĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>искони́</i> (iskoní) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fossil</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) resources, minerals</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something or someone obsolete, old-fashioned, a "dinosaur"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fossilized</li><li>old-fashioned</li></ol><p><i>ископа́ть</i> (iskopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eradication, extirpation, extermination</li></ol><p><i>искорени́ть</i> (iskorenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extirpate</li><li>to eradicate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extirpate</li><li>to eradicate</li></ol><p><i>искорени́ть</i> (iskorenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>и́скра</i> (ískra): sparkle, sparklet</li></ol><p>From <i>и́скра</i> (ískra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) or from <i>искра́</i> (iskrá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend strongly, to warp; to wreck by warping</li></ol><p><i>искорёжить</i> (iskorjóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend strongly, to warp; to wreck by warping</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>корёжить</i> (korjóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>askance, asquint (of a look or glance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spark, sparkle</li><li>spangle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьskra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sincerely</li></ol><p><i>и́скренний</i> (ískrennij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sincere</li><li>frank, candid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzkrьňь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>и́скренне</i> (ískrenne, “sincerely”)</li></ol><p><i>и́скренний</i> (ískrennij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sincerity, candour, frankness</li></ol><p><i>и́скренний</i> (ískrennij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to crook, to curve</li><li>to distort</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>криви́ть</i> (krivítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distortion, warp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to crook, to curve</li><li>to distort</li></ol><p><i>искриви́ть</i> (iskrivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>и́скра&#47;искра́</i> (ískra&#47;iskrá); sparkle, twinkle</li></ol><p><i>и́скра&#47;искра́</i> (ískra&#47;iskrá) +&lrm; <i>-и́нка&#47;-инка</i> (-ínka&#47;-inka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spark</li><li>to sparkle, to effervesce, to fizz</li><li>to scintillate</li><li>to glisten</li><li>to glister</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьskriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>и́скра</i> (ískra) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (stem-stressed variant) or <i>и́скра</i> (ískra) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scintillating, sparkling</li></ol><p><i>искра́</i> (iskrá, “spark”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, to give a bath <i>(transitive)</i></li><li>to expiate, to atone, to redeem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, to take a bath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expiate, to atone, to redeem</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atonement, expiation, redemption</li></ol><p><i>искупи́ть</i> (iskupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, test</li><li>temptation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>искусъ</i> (iskusŭ), which was borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искусъ</i> (iskusŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite <i>(badly, all over)</i></li><li>to sting <i>(badly, all over)</i></li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seducer</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) the serpent of Eden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to experience, to acquire knowledge of</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to test, to tempt</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to tempt, to entice, to seduce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искоусити</i> (iskusiti). By surface analysis, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ably, skillfully (with great ability)</li></ol><p><i>иску́сный</i> (iskúsnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skillful, skilled, crafty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искоусьнъ</i> (iskusĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>искуси́ть</i> (iskusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>artificially</li></ol><p><i>иску́сственный</i> (iskússtvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial respiration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial insemination (introduction of semen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificiality (state of being man-made, not natural)</li><li>artificiality, fakeness</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) unnaturalness, insincerity</li></ol><p><i>иску́сственный</i> (iskússtvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artificial, man-made</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) false (in terms of teeth)</li><li>imitation, fake</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) unnatural, insincere, contrived</li></ol><p>From <i>иску́сство</i> (iskússtvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial intelligence (intelligence exhibited by an artificial entity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial selection (intervention by humans in the breeding of plants or animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art</li><li>skill, craftsmanship, craft</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искусьство</i> (iskusĭstvo), which was either inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzkusьstvo</i>, or an inner formation from <i>искоусъ</i> (iskusŭ, “test, experiment”) + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arts critic, arts specialist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>искусствове́дение</i> (iskusstvovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>science of art, art science.</li></ol><p><i>-о-</i> (-o-, “art”) +&lrm; <i>иску́сство</i> (iskússtvo, “knowledge, competence”) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije), calque of German <i>Kunstwissenschaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to experience, to acquire knowledge of</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to test, to tempt</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to tempt, to entice, to seduce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>искоушати</i> (iskušati). By surface analysis, <i>искуси́ть</i> (iskusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temptation</li></ol><p><i>искуси́ть</i> (iskusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-versed, experienced [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic إِسْلَام (ʔislām, “submission”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islamization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islamism (Islamic fundamentalism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islamophobia (the fear or hatred of Islam or Muslims)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Islamic</li></ol><p><i>исла́м</i> (islám) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Icelander (a person from Iceland or of Icelandic descent)</li></ol><p><i>Исла́ндия</i> (Islándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Icelandic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Icelandic (language)</li></ol><p><i>Исла́ндия</i> (Islándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) ison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>ίσον</i> (íson), from Ancient Greek <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dirty</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>па́кость</i> (pákostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spaniard, a Spanish person (person from Spain)</li></ol><p><i>Испа́ния</i> (Ispánija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;obsolete) Spanish influenza</li></ol><p><i>Испа́ния</i> (Ispánija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>по-испа́нски</i> (po-ispánski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Spanish</li></ol><p><i>Испа́ния</i> (Ispánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evaporation, vaporization, volatilization</li><li>exhalation</li><li>vapor, vapors, damp, fumes</li></ol><p><i>испари́ть</i> (isparítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perspiration, sweat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to evaporate</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пари́ть</i> (parítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evaporate, to vaporize, to turn into vapor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to vanish into thin air</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) to walk; to be stolen</li></ol><p><i>испари́ть</i> (isparítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to evaporate</li></ol><p><i>испари́ть</i> (isparítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evaporate, to vaporize, to turn into vapor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to vanish into thin air</li></ol><p><i>испаря́ть</i> (isparjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to stain</li><li>to daub</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>па́чкать</i> (páčkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cinder, to reduce to ashes, to incinerate</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пе́пел</i> (pépel) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cinder, to reduce to ashes, to incinerate</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пе́пел</i> (pépel) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li>to scorch <i>(said of the sun)</i></li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li></ol><p><i>испе́чь</i> (ispéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to speckle (<i>often with many colors</i>)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to mark up with notes</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to speckle (<i>often with many colors</i>)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to mark up with notes</li></ol><p><i>испещри́ть</i> (ispeščrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a little, some of something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to drink up, to drink [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to drink in, to suffer [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘sorrow, loss, bitterness, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>испи́ть</i> (ispítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to saw off, to saw completely (everything or many things)</li><li>to wear out by sawing</li></ol><p><i>испили́ть</i> (ispilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to saw off, to saw completely (everything or many things)</li><li>to wear out by sawing</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover with writing</li><li><i>(pen, pencil)</i> to use up</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover with writing</li><li><i>(pen, pencil)</i> to use up</li></ol><p><i>исписа́ть</i> (ispisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a little, some of something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to drink up, to drink [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to drink in, to suffer [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘sorrow, loss, bitterness, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confessional (room)</li></ol><p><i>испове́дать</i> (ispovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confession (profession of one's faith), creed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to confess (one's sins)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hear the confession</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пове́дать</i> (povédatʹ). Compare Polish <i>spowiadać</i>, Czech <i>zpovídat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess <i>(one's sins)</i></li><li>to confess, to unbosom oneself</li></ol><p><i>испове́дать</i> (ispovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confessor, confesser</li></ol><p><i>испове́д(аться)</i> (ispovéd(atʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess, to hear the confession</li><li>to profess <i>(faith, religion)</i>, to worship</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пове́довать</i> (povédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess <i>(one's sins)</i></li><li>to confess, to unbosom oneself</li></ol><p><i>испове́довать</i> (ispovédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) confession</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исповѣдь</i> (ispovědĭ) or <i>испове́дать</i> (ispovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>и́споведь</i> (íspovedʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, to ruin, to spoil, to deprave, etc.</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пога́нить</i> (pogánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) wrong side</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>исподъ</i> (ispodŭ, “bottom”). By surface analysis, <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>под</i> (pod). Compare <i>и́з-под</i> (íz-pod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little by little, gradually, by degrees</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from earlier <i>и́сподоволь</i> (íspodovolʹ), from <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дово́ль(но)</i> (dovólʹ(no)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frowningly</li></ol><p>From <i>и́з-под</i> (íz-pod) +&lrm; <i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ья</i> (-ʹja). Compare Polish <i>spode łba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) underwear, lingerie</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) something hidden, mystery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stealthily, on the quiet, on the sly</li></ol><p>From <i>из-под</i> (iz-pod) +&lrm; <i>тишка́</i> (tišká), genitive singular of <i>тишо́к</i> (tišók), preserved only in <i>тишко́м</i> (tiškóm, “silently, covertly”) and related to <i>тишина́</i> (tišiná, “silence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) giant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Russian Church Slavonic <i>исполинъ</i> (ispolinŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьspolinъ</i>; compare Old Church Slavonic <i>споловъ</i> (spolovŭ), Old Polish <i>stolim</i>, <i>stolin</i>, Kashubian <i>stolem</i>, <i>stolëm</i>, <i>stolim</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gigantic</li></ol><p><i>исполи́н</i> (ispolín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>executive committee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>execution, fulfilment [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of a duty, task, etc.’</small>]</li><li>performance [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘by someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) full</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>executor</li><li>doer, performer</li><li>contractor</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ, “to perform”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscientious (working thoroughly, executing orders precisely and fast)</li><li>executive (designed for execution)</li><li>obedient</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>executive</li><li>industrious, careful, thorough, efficient</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельский</i> (-telʹskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to fulfill, to execute</li><li>to perform, to play</li><li>to fill, to imbue</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come true, to be fulfilled</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to turn (of age)</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, to fulfill, to execute</li><li>to perform, to play</li><li>to fill, to imbue</li></ol><p><i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come true, to be fulfilled</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to turn (of age)</li></ol><p><i>исполня́ть</i> (ispolnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acting (temporarily assuming the duties or authority)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, usage</li><li>utilization</li></ol><p><i>испо́льзовать</i> (ispólʹzovatʹ, “to use”) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to apply</li><li>to utilize, to exploit, to exercise</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзовать</i> (pólʹzovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>using</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenant farmer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenant farming</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol, “half”) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spoil, to ruin, to mar, to damage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to corrupt, to spoil, to deflorate (virgins)</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deteriorate, to worsen, to become worse, to go bad</li><li>to become corrupt/demoralized</li><li>to get spoilt, to rot, to decay</li></ol><p><i>испо́ртить</i> (ispórtitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faulty, damaged</li><li>bad</li><li>evil</li><li>rotten, spoiled</li><li>decayed (<i>of teeth</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>correctable</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>correctional</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to improve, to repair</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to amend, to better, to improve (to become better)</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correction, improvement, reform (act of correcting or a substitution for an error or mistake)</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corrected, reformed, revised</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to mend, to patch</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to amend, to better, to improve (to become better)</li></ol><p><i>исправля́ть</i> (ispravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ispravnic</li></ol><p>From obsolete <i>исправля́ть</i> (ispravljátʹ, “to carry out duties”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in good order</li><li>industriously, punctiliously, meticulously</li></ol><p><i>испра́вный</i> (isprávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serviceability, soundness, functioning condition (<i>of a machine</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) diligence, studiousness, industriousness</li></ol><p><i>испра́вный</i> (isprávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>functioning, working (<i>of a machine</i>)</li><li>in good condition</li><li>hardworking</li></ol><p><i>испра́вить</i> (isprávitʹ, “to repair, to correct”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excrement (animal solid waste, usually plural)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to empty, free</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to wipe out</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to excrete</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>испраздьнити</i> (isprazdĭniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzporzdьniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defecate (to empty the bowels of feces)</li></ol><p><i>испражни́ть</i> (ispražnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) to defecate (to empty the bowels of feces)</li></ol><p>Imperfective of <i>испражни́ться</i> (ispražnítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to request or ask something of someone with the aim of receiving something.</li></ol><p>From <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>просить</i> (prositʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try out, to test, to check (the quality of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to taste (something for quality)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try out, to experience (a lot of something)</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>про́бовать</i> (próbovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fright, alarm (sudden surprise with fear or terror)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>испуга́ть</i> (ispugátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frightened (in a frightened manner)</li></ol><p><i>испу́ганный</i> (ispúgannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightened, scared, afraid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frightened (of), to be startled (of), to take fright (at); to shy (at) [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to be afraid [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emit, to let out, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emit, to let out, to exhale</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up the ghost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, test</li><li>examination</li><li>ordeal, trials and tribulations</li></ol><p><i>испыта́ть</i> (ispytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tried, tested, proven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tester (a person who tests products or methods)</li></ol><p><i>испыта́ть</i> (ispytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) test</li><li>probationary</li></ol><p><i>испыта́ть</i> (ispytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to test</li><li>to experience, to undergo, to feel</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>пыта́ть</i> (pytátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) test; under test, being tested</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test subject</li></ol><p>Constructed as if the present passive participle of a nonexistent verb &#42;испытова́ть; compare <i>испыта́ть</i>&nbsp;pf (ispytátʹ, “to test”), <i>испы́тывать</i>&nbsp;impf (ispýtyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to test, to assay</li><li>to experience, to undergo, to feel</li></ol><p><i>испыта́ть</i> (ispytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out, to carve out (of stone)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to excise</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of wrinkles, cracks etc.) to cut through (across a surface), to cross</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>-сека́ть</i> (-sekátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out, to carve out (of stone)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to excise</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of wrinkles, cracks etc.) to cut through (across a surface), to cross</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) issid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Issidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bluish-black</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>research, study</li><li>exploration, investigation</li><li>analysis</li><li>research paper, study</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>иссле́довать</i> (isslédovatʹ, “to explore, examine”), which in turn comes from the noun <i>след</i> (sled, “track, trail, mark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>examined, explored</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>researcher</li></ol><p><i>иссле́довать</i> (isslédovatʹ, “to research”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) research</li></ol><p><i>иссле́довать</i> (isslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельский</i> (-telʹskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to research, to study in detail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyssop (any of several aromatic bushy herbs, of the genus <i>Hyssopus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὕσσωπος</i> (hússōpos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>иссо́п</i> (issóp).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>since long ago</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) ecstasy, delirium, frenzy, craze, rage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исступлениѥ</i> (isstuplenije), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἔκστασις</i> (ékstasis, “ecstasy”, literally “out of step”), from <i>ис-</i> (is-) (compare Russian <i>ис-</i> (is-, “out”)) and <i>ступлениѥ</i> (stuplenije) (compare Russian <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ, “to step”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frantic; frenzied; berserk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to wither</li><li>to exhaust, to wear up</li></ol><p><i>иссуши́ть</i> (issušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dehydration, insiccation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to wither</li><li>to exhaust, to wear up</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run dry, to dry up</li><li>to run out, to disappear, to fail, to run low, to run short</li></ol><p><i>исся́кнуть</i> (issjáknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run dry, to dry up</li><li>to run out, to disappear, to fail, to run low, to run short</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>ся́кнуть</i> (sjáknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoke (a furnace)</li><li>to consume, to expend (firewood etc.) for heating</li><li>to melt (all of something)</li></ol><p><i>истопи́ть</i> (istopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt, to thaw</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the establishment (the political elite, the ruling circles)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>establishment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to flow <i>(out of)</i></li><li>to shed, to pour forth</li><li>to elapse, to run out, to expire, to fall due</li></ol><p><i>исте́к-</i> (isték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the past, the previous (<i>day, year, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rip to shreds, to tear to pieces</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to torment, to afflict</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>терза́ть</i> (terzátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hysteric man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hysterics</li></ol><p>Borrowed probably from French <i>hystérique</i>, from Latin <i>hysterica passiō</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὑστέρα</i> (hustéra), feminine of <i>ὕστερος</i> (hústeros), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udero-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be given to hysterics, to throw a tantrum, to have a fit</li></ol><p>Derived from the noun истерия, from the ancient Greek ὑστέρα for uterus (ancient medicine explained psychological disorders through problems with the uterus), further from the Russian истерика — a formal borrowing from the Latin hysterica (passiō) "sickness of the nerves," from which is derived the German Hysterie for hysteria.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hysterical</li></ol><p><i>исте́р(ика)</i> (istér(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hysterical</li></ol><p><i>истери́я</i> (isteríja) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hysteria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) plaintiff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>истьць</i> (istĭcĭ). Compare Bulgarian <i>исте́ц</i> (istéc), Polish <i>iściec</i> (“legal owner”). Per Sobolevsky (Lectures 133; quoted in Vasmer), derived from Old East Slavic <i>исто</i> (isto, “capital”) + <i>-ьць</i> (-ĭcĭ) (whence Russian <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)); original meaning "owner". Cognate with <i>и́стый</i> (ístyj, “real, true”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expiration, expiry</li><li>outflow; efflux; fluxion</li></ol><p><i>исте́к-</i> (isték-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to flow (<i>out of</i>)</li><li>to shed, to pour forth</li><li>to elapse, to run out, to expire, to fall due</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li><li>verity, reality</li><li>truism</li><li>(religion,&#32;mathematics,&#32;logic) truth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>истина</i> (istina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьstina</i>, from <i>&#42;jistъ</i>. Compare Latvian <i>īstens</i> (“real, true, genuine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly (in accordance with the facts)</li></ol><p><i>и́стинный</i> (ístinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fact, sooth, truth, verity, validity</li></ol><p><i>и́стинный</i> (ístinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>veritable, true</li><li>genuine</li><li>actual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>истиньнъ</i> (istinĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>и́стина</i> (ístina, “truth”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top quark, truth quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrasion (act of abrading)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, to decay, to putrefy, to moulder</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn to ashes</li></ol><p><i>истле́ть</i> (istlétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay, to wither, to rot, to putrefy, to moulder</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn to ashes</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>тлеть</i> (tletʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fervently, ardently</li></ol><p><i>и́стовый</i> (ístovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fervent, ardent</li></ol><p><i>и́стый</i> (ístyj) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>source (of a river)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) origins</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>исте́чь</i> (istéčʹ) (compare stem <i>истек-</i> (istek-)), with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpretation; construing; illumination</li></ol><p><i>истолкова́ть</i> (istolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret, to construe, to comment <i>(upon)</i>, to explain</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret, to construe, to comment <i>(upon)</i>, to explain</li></ol><p><i>истолкова́ть</i> (istolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, to crush, to grind (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li></ol><p>From <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>languor, lassitude, pleasant relaxation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>истоми́ть</i> (istomítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, to tire</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>томи́ть</i> (tomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, to tire</li></ol><p><i>истоми́ть</i> (istomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoke (a furnace)</li><li>to consume, to expend (firewood etc.) for heating</li><li>to melt (all of something)</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoker</li></ol><p><i>истопи́ть</i> (istopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník). Root cognate with Czech <i>topič</i>.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>palacz</i> (akin to <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)), Slovak <i>kurič</i>, Slovene <i>kurjač</i> (akin to <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ), also note Latvian <i>kurinātājs</i>, Lithuanian <i>kūrikas</i>).</p><p>Note the French counterpart <i>chauffeur</i>, from which many descendants arise, including <i>шофёр</i> (šofjór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>исто́рия</i> (istórija): story</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>historian</li></ol><p><i>исто́рия</i> (istórija) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>historiographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>historiography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>historically (in a historic manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>historic</li><li>historical</li></ol><p><i>исто́рия</i> (istórija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>history</li><li>story</li><li>affair, thing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>histoire</i>, remodelled with an awareness of Latin <i>historia</i> or Ancient Greek <i>ἱστορία</i> (historía), from Ancient Greek <i>ἵστωρ</i> (hístōr), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéydtōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art history (study of the history of the visual arts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shed, <i>(fragrance)</i> to exhale</li></ol><p><i>источи́ть</i> (istočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (water source)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) source</li></ol><p>From <i>исто́к</i> (istók) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piercingly loudly</li></ol><p><i>исто́шный</i> (istóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piercingly loud, desperate-sounding (<i>of a voice, a scream, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Originally <i>исто́чный</i> (istóčnyj), from <i>источи́ть</i> (istočítʹ, “to spill, to pour out generously”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, to deplete, to wear out</li></ol><p><i>истощи́ть</i> (istoščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhaustion; emaciation</li></ol><p><i>истощи́ть</i> (istoščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, to deplete, to wear out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend, to waste</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destroyer, exterminator, eradicator</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) fighter plane</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) fighter pilot</li><li>(nautical,&#32;obsolete) destroyer (warship)</li></ol><p>From <i>истреби́ть</i> (istrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ). The nautical sense, attested early 20th century, was a semantic loan from English <i>destroyer</i>; the aviation sense, attested in the 1910s and by far the predominant one in modern usage, evolved from the nautical sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) fighter-bomber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructive</li><li>(relational,&#32;aviation,&#32;military) fighter (<i>plane, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>истреби́ть</i> (istrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annihilate, to destroy, to eradicate, to exterminate (to reduce to nothing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>истрѣбити</i> (istrěbiti). Morphologically, <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>тре́ба</i> (tréba) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction</li><li>extermination</li></ol><p>Deverbative of <i>истреби́ть</i> (istrebítʹ, “to exterminate”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annihilate, to destroy, to eradicate, to exterminate (to reduce to nothing)</li></ol><p><i>истреби́ть</i> (istrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear to rags, to fray, to tear</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frayed/torn, to be worn out</li></ol><p><i>истрепа́ть</i> (istrepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear to rags, to fray, to tear</li></ol><p><i>истрепа́ть</i> (istrepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frayed/torn, to be worn out</li></ol><p><i>истрёпывать</i> (istrjópyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idol, statue</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sedentary, insensate person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>истуканъ</i> (istukanŭ), past passive participle of <i>истукати</i> (istukati, “to carve”); compare <i>ту́кать</i> (túkatʹ, “to hit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) real, true, authentic</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) zealous, devoted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>истꙑи</i> (istyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jistъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) (from) behind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torture, abuse (physical maltreatment)</li></ol><p><i>истяза́ть</i> (istjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torturer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>истѧѕати</i> (istędzati). By surface analysis, <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>-тяза́ть</i> (-tjazátʹ) (akin to <i>тяга́ть</i> (tjagátʹ), via PIE akin to <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ) (akin to <i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij), <i>мягчи́ть</i> (mjaxčítʹ)), Latin <i>torqueō</i>, <i>tortus</i> (whence English <i>torture</i>, <i>torment</i>) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terkʷ-</i> (“to turn”)), Latin <i>cruciō</i> (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to turn, to bend”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go; to walk / to stroll all over</li><li>to wear out (one's legs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outcome, issue</li><li>Exodus</li><li>outlet</li><li>way out (from a situation)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>исходи́ть</i> (isxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective&#32;only</i>) to issue (from), to come (from), to originate (from), to emanate (from)</li><li>(<i>imperfective&#32;only</i>) to proceed (from), to base oneself (on))</li><li>to be drained of, to shed a lot of (blood, tears, etc.)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to expire, to come to an end</li><li>to go; to walk / to stroll all over</li><li>to wear out (one's legs)</li></ol><p>From <i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>original, initial, starting</li></ol><p><i>исхо́д</i> (isxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from; based on; upon</li></ol><p>From <i>исходя</i> (isxodja) +&lrm; <i>из</i> (iz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>emaciated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become emaciated</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>худо́й</i> (xudój) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch all over</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch all over</li></ol><p><i>исцара́пать</i> (iscarápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cure; healing; recovery</li></ol><p><i>исцели́ть</i> (iscelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to heal, to cure</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>цели́ть</i> (celítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to heal, to cure</li></ol><p><i>исцели́ть</i> (iscelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;derogatory) offspring, progeny, brood (usually in the expression <i>исча́дие а́да</i> (isčádije áda, “brood of hell”)</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>ча́до</i> (čádo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to vanish</li><li>to wear off</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исчезати</i> (isčezati) or <i>ищезати</i> (ištezati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьzčezati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disappearance, vanishing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исчезновениѥ</i> (isčeznovenije) or <i>ищезновениѥ</i> (išteznovenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to vanish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>исчезнѫти</i> (isčeznǫti), from Old Church Slavonic <i>чезнѫти</i> (čeznǫti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čeznǫti</i>. The inherited form would be <i>&#42;исчёзнуть</i> (&#42;isčóznutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>че́рпать</i> (čérpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be confined/reduced <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>исче́рпать</i> (isčérpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust</li></ol><p><i>исче́рпать</i> (isčérpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be confined/reduced <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>исче́рпывать</i> (isčérpyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exhaustive, thorough, comprehensive, holistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation; calculus; numeration</li></ol><p><i>исчи́слить</i> (isčíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calculable, computable</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) countable</li></ol><p><i>исчи́слить</i> (isčíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to estimate</li></ol><p><i>ис-</i> (is-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>count noun (noun used to refer to things that can be counted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>countable (freely used with numbers and the definite article)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to estimate</li></ol><p><i>исчи́слить</i> (isčíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amount <i>(to)</i>, to come <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>исчисля́ть</i> (isčisljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>ещё</i> (ješčó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eta (the modern Greek letter Η&#47;η)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>ήτα</i> (íta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so, now, right, okay (used at or near the beginning of a sentence, which is a logical conclusion from the previous one&#59; corresponds in meaning to the terms 'thus', 'as a result', 'consequently')</li></ol><p><i>и</i> (i, “and”) +&lrm; <i>так</i> (tak, “so”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italian (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Italian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) wop (person of Italian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iteration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sum, total</li><li>result</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>итого́</i> (itovó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>total, in total, in all</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>и</i> (i) +&lrm; <i>того́</i> (tovó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>total, resultant</li><li>final, closing, concluding</li></ol><p><i>ито́г</i> (itóg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ytterbium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) alternative form of <i>идти́</i> (idtí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yttrium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judaism</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>Ἰουδαϊσμός</i> (Ioudaïsmós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Judaean</li><li>Judaist, Jew (a follower of Judaism)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰουδαῖος</i> (Ioudaîos), ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (Yəhūḏā(h), “Judah”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jewish</li></ol><p><i>иуде́й</i> (iudéj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ifrit (kind of djinn mentioned in the Qur'an)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عِفْرِيت (ʕifrīt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) their</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jixъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics&#32;jargon,&#32;neologism) a meme, reflecting constant denial of presence of Russians who take part in hybrid wars in other countries, especially in Ukraine's Crimea and Donbass</li></ol><p>From <i>их</i> (ix, “genitive of <i>они́</i> (oní)”) +&lrm; <i>там</i> (tam, “there”) +&lrm; <i>нет</i> (net, “there is/are not; no”). Literally, “they are not there”, written as one word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) ichthyosis</li></ol><p>Possibly from French <i>ichtyose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ichthyosaur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ichthyologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ichthyology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ichthyologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ikhshid (title of rulers of Ferghana and Sogdia in Transoxiana)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ikhshidid</li></ol><p><i>Ихшиди́д</i> (Ixšidíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>их</i> (ix):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>possessive</i>) their</li><li>genitive/accusative of <i>они́</i> (oní)</li><li>genitive/accusative of <i>онѣ́</i> (oně́)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) donkey, ass</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) stupid person</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) stubborn person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>иша́къ</i> (išák), <i>ише́къ</i> (išék), attested in 1567 and 1623. Further borrowed from Turkic languages, probably from Kipchak. Compare Tatar <i>ишәк</i> (işäk), Bashkir <i>ишәк</i> (işək), also Turkish <i>eşek</i>, Crimean Tatar <i>eşek</i>, Azerbaijani <i>eşşək</i>), ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;eĺčgek</i>, which is related to Proto-Tungusic <i>&#42;eyiken</i> and Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;elǰigen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ishan</li></ol><p>From Persian ایشان (išân, “they”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ischemia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sevan trout (<i>Salmo ischchan</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>իշխան</i> (išxan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) see!; (just) look, listen; there; oh; now</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>вишь</i> (višʹ), in turn contraction of <i>ви́дишь</i> (vídišʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bloodhound</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) sleuth, detective, undercover cop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>looking for, searching</li><li>zetetic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ию́ль</i> (ijúlʹ) or <i>ию́ля</i> (ijúlja, “July”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>July</li></ol><p>From Byzantine Greek <i>Ἰούλιος</i> (Ioúlios), from Latin <i>iūlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) July</li></ol><p><i>ию́ль</i> (ijúlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ию́нь</i> (ijúnʹ) or <i>ию́ня</i> (ijúnja, “June”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>June</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>июнии</i> (ijunii), from Byzantine Greek <i>Ἰούνιος</i> (Ioúnios), from Latin <i>iūnius</i>, from <i>Iūnō</i> (“Juno”), from (perhaps) Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂yuHn̥ḱós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂óyu</i> (“vital force, youthful vigor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) June</li></ol><p><i>ию́нь</i> (ijúnʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yemeni</li></ol><p><i>Йе́мен</i> (Jémɛn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yeti</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>yeti</i>, from Tibetan <i>གཡའ་དྲེད</i> (g.ya' dred, “rock bear”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>и</i> (i, “Yi”) (ethnic group, language, script etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yo-yo (toy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fucking hell!</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>ёбанный в рот</i> (jóbannyj v rot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yogi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>योगी</i> (yogī), with rebracketing of <i>йо́ги</i> (jógi) as the nominative plural.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoga</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>योग</i> (yóga, “yoga”, literally “yoking; union”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yogic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoghurt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یوغورت (<i>yoğurt</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yogurt</li></ol><p>From <i>йо́гурт</i> (jógurt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iodine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>iode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) iodide</li></ol><p>From <i>йод</i> (jod, “iodine”) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id, “-ide”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) iodide</li></ol><p>From <i>йоди́д</i> (jodíd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) iodism</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>iodism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) iodine</li></ol><p>From <i>йод</i> (jod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iodine deficiency</li></ol><p><i>йод</i> (jod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дефици́т</i> (deficít)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iodoform</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoik (traditional Sami style of singing)</li><li>yoik (song in this style)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Sami language; compare Skolt Sami <i>juõigg</i> and Northern Sami <i>juoigat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>yocto- (10<sup>−24</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yorkshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Йо́ркшир</i> (Jórkšir) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) or from <i>Йоркши́р</i> (Jorkšír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yorkshire terrier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yoruba (a sub-Saharan people)</li><li>Yoruba (a person of the Yoruba tribe)</li><li>Yoruba (a sub-Saharan language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter J, j.</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) yod (palatal approximant).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iota (the Greek letter Ι&#47;ι)</li><li>iota (small amount)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἰῶτα</i> (iôta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) iotation</li></ol><p>From <i>йоти́ровать</i> (jotírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) iotation</li></ol><p>From <i>йоти́ровать</i> (jotírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to iotate</li></ol><p><i>йот</i> (jot) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) yottabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>йотта-</i> (jotta-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) yottabit</li></ol><p>From <i>йотта-</i> (jotta-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ётун</i> (jótun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;humorous) dick (euphemism for хуй)</li><li>errative for юг</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj) in reverse.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to, toward</li><li>by, for (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>against (<i>change of position</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>k, kilo-</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъ(n)</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>кило-</i> (kilo-).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: К)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) Kt (knight)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>конь</i> (konʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at your service (way of introduction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuck away; far away (far away, in an unspecified direction, so as not to have anything more to do with a given person or thing)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuck away; completely, fully, totally, utterly (used to express determination to carry out one's intentions)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to the fucked mother”, where <i>ебе́не</i> (instead of the expected <i>ебёной</i> (jebjónoj)) is a fossilized dative case from Old East Slavic, otherwise not used in modern Russian except of this phrase. Compare also <i>к едрене фене</i> (k jedrene fene).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to date, as yet (up to the present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for example, for instance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by the way, incidentally, side note (in connection with what has just been said, in addition to what has already been said)</li><li>in a word, so to speak, just saying, if I/we may say so, dare I/we say, it must be said, I have to say (used to introduce and highlight the expressivity of a summary or description of something already talked about)</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> к (k),&lrm; сло́ву (slóvu).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unfortunately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fortunately (it is fortunate that)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides, at that, in addition</li></ol><p>Literally, “to that also”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> к (k),&lrm; чему́ (čemú).</li><li>to what end, what for</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka): kopek, copeck, kopeck <i>(singular or plural)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>кинофильм</i> (kinofilʹm)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Cyrillic letter К, к</li><li>the Latin letter K, k</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: КА)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Red Army</i> (“USSR”); abbreviation of <i>Кра́сная а́рмия</i> (Krásnaja ármija, “Red Army”)</li><li><i>space ship</i>, <i>satellite</i>; abbreviation of <i>космический аппарат</i> (kosmičeskij apparat, “spacecraft”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tavern, pub, bar</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) watering hole, dive, joint</li><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) pumpkin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Low German <i>Kabacke</i> (“hut, hovel”).</p><p>A Turkic borrowing, compare to Azerbaijani <i>qabaq</i> (“pumpkin”), Tatar <i>кабак</i> (qabaq, “pumpkin”), Turkish <i>kabak</i> (“zucchini, squash”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) promissory note, leading to debt bondage if unpaid</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) debt bondage</li><li>enslavement, servitude, bondage</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) kabbalah</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кабала</i> (kabala, “document, deed, debt issue”), from Turkish <i>kabal</i> (“certain quantity, tasked labour, one day labour”).</p><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>קַבָּלָה</i> (“receiving/tradition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boar, wild boar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boar, male pig</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) lardass, oinker (a fat man)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language; compare Tatar <i>kaban</i>, Azerbaijani <i>qaban</i>, Kazakh <i>қабан</i> (qaban).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boar, wild boar</li></ol><p><i>каба́н</i> (kabán) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musk deer (mammal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kabardian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabaret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cabaret</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tavern</li></ol><p><i>каба́к</i> (kabák) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable marrow paste</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vegetable marrow, squash, zucchini</li></ol><p><i>кабачо́к</i> (kabačók) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>каба́к</i> (kabák); a (small) tavern, pub, bar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small restaurant</li><li>courgette, vegetable marrow, zucchini (an edible variety of <i>Cucurbita pepo</i> squash)</li></ol><p><i>каба́к</i> (kabák, “tavern, pub, bar”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, diminutive suffix), from Low German <i>Kabacke</i>.</p><p><i>каба́к</i> (kabák, “pumpkin”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, diminutive suffix), from Turkic; compare Azerbaijani <i>qabaq</i> (“pumpkin”), Tatar <i>кабак</i> (qabaq, “pumpkin”), Turkish <i>kabak</i> (“vegetable marrow; squash in general”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kabel</i> or Dutch <i>kabel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cable</li></ol><p><i>ка́бель</i> (kábelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cable (heavy rope or chain as used for mooring)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) unit of length, equal to 0.1 of a nautical mile (185.2 metres)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kabeltouw</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) capstan, reel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cabestan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabin, cubicle, booth</li><li>elevator car</li><li>cab (of a truck/lorry)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) cockpit</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) face, head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office (building or room), private office, home office</li><li>study (room)</li><li>examination room</li><li>massage parlor</li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) cabinet</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) cabinet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kabinett</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>кабине́т</i> (kabinét): small office; small cabinet</li></ol><p><i>кабине́т</i> (kabinét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) study (room)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) examination room</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) theoretical, abstract, far from practical life</li></ol><p><i>кабине́т</i> (kabinét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>каби́на</i> (kabína): (small) cabin, booth, cubicle</li></ol><p><i>каби́на</i> (kabína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heel (part of shoe)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) an IZh-2715 small truck</li><li>(<i>draughts/checkers</i>) a tactical combination</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) submissive husband</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koblǫkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>каблу́к</i> (kablúk): (small) heel (of a shoe)</li></ol><p><i>каблу́к</i> (kablúk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabriolet, convertible (an automobile with a retractable top)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kabuki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>歌舞伎</i> (かぶき, kabuki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) if, if only</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ka</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;by</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>каб</i> (kab), dialectal Ukrainian <i>коби</i> (koby) and коб (kob), Carpathian Rusyn <i>кеби</i> (keby).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kava (an intoxicating beverage made from the kava plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kawaiiness</li></ol><p>From <i>кава́йный</i> (kavájnyj, “kawaii”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kawaii</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>可愛い</i>, <i>かわいい</i> (kawaii, “lovable, adorable; cute”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) cavalier (<i>in medieval Western Europe</i>)</li><li>knight (a person on whom knighthood has been conferred)</li><li>a person decorated with an order</li><li>male dance partner</li><li>suitor, boyfriend (male partner in a romantic relationship)</li></ol><p>Attested 1698. Borrowed from Polish <i>kawaler</i> or German <i>Kavalier</i>, from French <i>cavalier</i>, from Italian <i>cavaliere</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) cavalryman, horseman from a prestigious cavalry regiment (the Russian Empire, 18-early 20th centuries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cavalier de la garde</i> or <i>chevalier-garde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;relational) cavalry</li></ol><p><i>кавале́рия</i> (kavalérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavalryman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kavallerist</i>; composed as if from <i>кавале́рия</i> (kavalérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavalry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavalcade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>cavalcata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medley, mess, mishmash</li><li>hodgepodge (a dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, compare Kazakh <i>қуырдақ</i> (quyrdaq), ultimately derived from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kagur-</i> (“to parch, to roast”). Sense&nbsp;2 is the original one. For the development of the sense&nbsp;1, compare <i>каша</i> (kaša, “porridge, mess”), <i>винегрет</i> (vinegret, “vinegret, mess”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) cover version (a version of a song that is a new performance or rerecording of that song by a different artist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cover</i>; clipping of <i>кавер-ве́рсия</i> (kaver-vérsija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>по́лость</i> (pólostʹ)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cavus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kawi (an ancient writing system)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कवि</i> (kavi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavitation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasian (native of the Caucasus region)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Caucasian Shepherd Dog</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Caucasian War veteran</li></ol><p>From <i>Кавка́з</i> (Kavkáz) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasologist</li></ol><p><i>Кавказ</i> (Kavkaz, “Caucasus”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вед</i> (ved)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Caucasian (related to the Caucasus)</li></ol><p><i>Кавка́з</i> (Kavkáz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Albanian (of an extinct Northeast Caucasian language)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Albanian (an extinct Northeast Caucasian language), short for кавка́зско-алба́нский язы́к (kavkázsko-albánskij jazýk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quotation mark (either [«◌»] or [„◌“])</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>кавы́ка</i> (kavýka, “breve, hook”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kavyka</i>. According to Chernykh, likely related to dialectal <i>ка́выш</i> (kávyš, “duckling”), <i>ка́вка</i> (kávka, “frog”), <i>кавы́кать</i> (kavýkatʹ, “to hobble”), suggesting the sense development “webbed footprint” → “a hook-like diacritic” → “quotation mark”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) khagan (khan of khans&#59; equal in status to an emperor)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>каганъ</i> (kaganŭ), from a Turkic language, compare Old Turkic <i>𐰴𐰍𐰣</i> (qǧn¹ /⁠qaɣan⁠/).</p><p>Doublet of <i>хан</i> (xan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khaganate</li></ol><p><i>каган</i> (kagan) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) clamp (compact pile of agricultural produce used for temporary storage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial&#32;or&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) an employee of the KGB, agent of the KGB, KGB operative</li></ol><p>From <i>КГБ</i> (KGB) (phonetically: <i>ка-гэ-бэ́</i> (ka-gɛ-bɛ́)) + <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an employee of the KGB</li></ol><p>From <i>КГБ</i> (KGB) (phonetically: <i>ка-гэ-бэ́</i> (ka-gɛ-bɛ́)) + <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) inventory</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) cadastre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cadastre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literary) inventory</li><li>(relational,&#32;literary) cadastre; cadastral</li></ol><p><i>када́стр</i> (kadástr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) cadet</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Constitutional Democrat</li></ol><p>From French <i>cadet</i>.</p><p>From <i>к.-д.</i> (k.-d.), an abbreviation of the party name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cadet</li><li>(relational,&#32;historical) Constitutional Democrat</li></ol><p><i>каде́т</i> (kadét) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cadet corps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Qajar</li></ol><p><i>Каджа́р</i> (Kadžár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>automobile of Cadillac brand</li></ol><p>From English <i>Cadillac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censer, thurible (religious ornamental container for burning incense)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кадило</i> (kadilo, “incense”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kadidlo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кади́ть</i> (kadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kadidlьnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn incense</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flatter, to curry favor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кадити</i> (kaditi), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kadìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pail, wooden tub, vat</li></ol><p>From <i>кадь</i> (kadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Ancient Greek <i>κᾰ́δος</i> (kắdos, “pail, jar, cask”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cadmium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́дка</i> (kádka): a (small) wooden tub</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>still, shot, frame</li><li>scene, shot (from a movie)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) cadre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cadre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;military) cadre, personnel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) team, crew, staff</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) girl, chick, girlfriend, lady friend (a woman as an object of desire or as a partner in an unmarried romantic relationship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cadre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to pick up (<i>a romantic partner</i>), to hit on, to flirt with, to court</li></ol><p><i>ка́дра</i> (kádra, “girl”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;military) regular officer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) member of a company's personnel, staff worker</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) human resources officer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) member of the permanent staff</li></ol><p><i>ка́дровый</i> (kádrovyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) personnel, human resources</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cadre</li><li>(<i>military</i>) regular</li><li>(relational,&#32;engineering) frame</li></ol><p><i>ка́дры</i> (kádry) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personnel, cadre</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) human resources, HR, personnel department</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cadre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caduceus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cādūceum</i>, <i>cādūceus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Adam's apple</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing: compare Uyghur قاتتىق (qattiq, “hard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily, day-to-day, everyday, diurnal</li></ol><p><i>ка́ждый</i> (káždyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>every, each</li><li>either (of two)</li><li>everybody, everyone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кажьдꙑи</i> (kažĭdyi), from Post Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kažьdъ</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъžьdo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>every time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apparently, it seems</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) maybe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) it seems, I think, I guess, it's as if</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apparent, seeming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cossack, cossack</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Kazakh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>козакъ</i> (kozakŭ). Doublet of <i>каза́х</i> (kazáx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) apparently, seemingly</li><li>it would seem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kazan (a type of cauldron or cooking pot used in Central Asian cuisine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language, compare to Azerbaijani <i>qazan</i>, Kazakh <i>қазан</i> (qazan), Turkish <i>kazan</i>, Uzbek <i>qozon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A romantic and attractive man who is popular among women.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>casanova</i>, named after Italian&#32;adventurer&#32;Giacomo Casanova&nbsp;(1725–1798).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kazan (city in Russia&#59; the capital of Tatarstan)</li></ol><p><i>Каза́нь</i> (Kazánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) barrack, barracks</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Italian <i>caserma</i> (“barracks”), via Polish <i>kazarma</i> or older German <i>Kasarme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barracks</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) rude, vulgar, pejorative</li></ol><p><i>каза́рма</i> (kazárma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to show</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to tell</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to indicate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kāzàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem, to appear, to look</li></ol><p><i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kazakh <i>(person)</i></li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>қазақ</i> (qazaq), from Old Turkic <i>𐰴𐰔𐰍𐰸</i> (*qazǧaq, “profiteer”), from <i>𐰴𐰔𐰍𐰣𐰢𐰸</i> (qazǧanmaq, “to acquire”), from <i>𐰴𐰔𐰢𐰸</i> (qazmaq, “to dig out”), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kaŕ-</i>. Doublet of <i>каза́к</i> (kazák, “Cossack”). Russian [x] possibly reflects the Kazakh allaphony of the phoneme /q/ to [χ], i.e. қазақ /qazaq/ being realised as [qazaχ].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kazakh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kazakhstan</li></ol><p><i>Казахста́н</i> (Kazaxstán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cossack, cossack</li></ol><p><i>каза́к</i> (kazák) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Cossacks (collectively)</li></ol><p><i>каза́к</i> (kazák) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cossack</li></ol><p><i>каза́к</i> (kazák) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kazachok, kozachok (dance)</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>каза́к</i> (kazák): (young, little) Cossack (<i>can also be condescending</i>)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) boy servant</li></ol><p><i>каза́к</i> (kazák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cossack boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) casemate</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a prison cell for political prisoners in the pre-revolutionary Russia</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;somewhat&#32;dated) prison, jail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>casemate</i>, Italian <i>casamatta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casino</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>casino</i> via French.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) to spoil, to ruin, to deform, to mar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kazìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury, exchequer</li><li>fisc</li><li>(<i>firearms</i>) breech (the part of a breechloader)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Kypchak Turkic source, compare Kipchak <i>qazna</i> (“treasury”) (Codex Cumanicus, <i>Ave, porta paradisi</i>, verse 75), further origin uncertain but probably Iranian, possibly ultimately from Old Median <i>&#42;ganǰam</i> (“treasure”).</p><p>Directly attested since 1389 (treaty of Dmitry Donskoy with Vladimir the Bold) while its derivative <i>казначе́й</i> (kaznačéj) is used already in the last will of Ivan II of Moscow circa 1358.</p><p>Formally close cognates include Nogai <i>казна</i> (kazna), Kazakh <i>қазына</i> (qazyna), Bashkir <i>ҡаҙна</i> (qaźna), Ossetian <i>хъӕзна</i> (qæzna), Chechen <i>хазна</i> (xazna, “treasure, treasury”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ха̏зна</i> (“treasury; cash register”), Bulgarian <i>хазна</i> (hazna, “treasure; cash register”), Romanian <i>hazna</i> (“treasury; septic tank”), Turkish <i>hazne</i> (“treasury”), Arabic خَزْنَة (ḵazna, “[a] safe”).</p><p>Also compare Ottoman Turkish خزینه (hazîne), Turkish and Crimean Tatar <i>hazine</i>, Tatar <i>хәзинә</i> (xäzinä, “treasure, treasury”), Persian خزینه (xazine), Arabic خَزِينَة (ḵazīna, “treasury”), Ancient Greek <i>γάζα</i> (gáza), Middle Persian (ganǰ), Persian گنج (ganj, “treasure”), Sogdian (γznyʼ /⁠γaznyā⁠/, “treasury”), Parthian <i>𐫃𐫉𐫗𐫁𐫡</i> (gznbr /⁠gaznβar⁠/, “treasurer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer</li></ol><p>Related to <i>казна́</i> (kazná). From a Turkic source, cognates include Tatar <i>казначы</i> (qaznaçı), Kazakh <i>қазынашы</i> (qazynaşy), Kyrgyz <i>казыначы</i> (kazınacı), Uzbek <i>xazinachi</i> / <i>gʻaznachi</i>, Uyghur خەزىنىچى (xezinichi), Turkmen <i>hazynaçy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury, exchequer</li></ol><p><i>казначе́й</i> (kaznačéj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to execute, to put to death</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to torture, to scourge, to castigate, to punish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kazniti</i>. Related to <i>казнь</i> (kaznʹ) and to <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>execution (putting someone to death)</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) torture, harsh punishment, retribution</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) plague (<i>divine retribution</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaznь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;uncommon,&#32;neologism) the Kazakh-speaking community on Twitter.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>каза́хский</i> (kazáxskij, “Kazakh”) +&lrm; <i>Тви́ттер</i> (Tvítter, “Twitter”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;philosophy) casuistry</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) sophistry, casuistry, chicanery (deliberately misleading arguments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incident, extraordinary occurrence, case, casus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cāsus</i> (&lt; <i>cadō</i> + <i>-tus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casus belli (an act seen as justifying or causing a war&#59; act of war)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cāsus</i> (“case”) + <i>bellī</i> (“of war”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>casuistic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unusual, bizarre, curious</li></ol><p><i>ка́зус</i> (kázus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>казённый</i> (kazjónnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) state, government</li><li>official, public</li><li>formal, perfunctory</li><li>commonplace</li><li>(<i>military</i>) breech (of a rifle)</li></ol><p>From <i>казна́</i> (kazná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); derived as if <i>казна́</i> (kazná) had a reducible stem, with hypothetical unreduced genitive plural <i>&#42;казён</i> (&#42;kazjón) (in fact, the noun has no genitive plural).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) A house in governmental property</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) prison, big house</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;nautical) caïque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>caïque</i>. Doublet of <i>каю́к</i> (kajúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrayer, murderer</li><li>urchin, rascal</li></ol><p>From <i>Ка́ин</i> (Káin, “biblical character — son of <i>Adam and Eve</i>”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Каин)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Cain (son of Adam and Eve)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Каинъ</i> (Kainŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Κάϊν</i> (Káïn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kaiser or kaiser (a German or Austrian emperor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kaiser</i>. Doublet of <i>ке́сарь</i> (késarʹ), <i>царь</i> (carʹ), <i>це́зарь</i> (cézarʹ), and <i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kaiser, kaiser</li></ol><p>From <i>ка́йзер</i> (kájzer) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, hem, selvage</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaymak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قایماق (kaymak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caiman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guillemot, willock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>billiards</i>) kaisa (variant of the game billiards played chiefly in Finland and Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>kaisa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kif, high (euphoria or pleasant stupor caused by a recreational drug)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bliss, pleasant idleness</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic كَيْف (kayf, “pleasure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bool; to chill out, to bliss out, to be high (from drugs)</li></ol><p><i>кайф</i> (kajf) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) very well</li></ol><p><i>кайфо́вый</i> (kajfóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) pleasurable, pleasant, mood-elevating (providing a high, providing pleasure)</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;figuratively) cool, great (very good)</li></ol><p><i>кайф</i> (kajf) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a verb or short-form adjective, which can be implied and omitted</i>) how (in what way, in what manner) (<i>translated as "what" with some verbs</i>)</li><li>(<i>with an adverb or short-form adjective</i>) how (to what extent&#47;degree)</li><li>(exclamative,&#32;with an adverb or short-form adjective) how (to a significant extent&#47;degree) (used to indicate surprise, delight or horror)</li><li>(concessive,&#32;with <i>ни</i> (ni) and a short-form adjective, adverb or verb) as, however, however much</li><li>(<i>between same-tense verbs</i>) as soon as, when</li><li>(<i>after a phrase about passed time and before a verbal phrase</i>) since (the last time something happened)</li><li>(in the following construction with <i>так</i> (tak) and <i>и</i> (i)) both (<i>or not translated</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;with a conjugated perfective verb) suddenly, all of a sudden (in the past)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>like (in the same manner as &#47; a similar manner to); occasionally <i>as</i></li><li>as (in the capacity&#47;role of)</li><li>as, since (because, due to the fact that)</li><li>(when beginning a clause after <i>так (же)</i> (tak (že)) + a short-form adjective or adverb) as (introducing an object or scenario of comparison)</li><li>(when beginning a clause after a verb or short-form adjective + <i>так (же)</i> (tak (že))) as (introducing an object or scenario of comparison&#59; can also be "how" &#43; pronoun &#43; verb in this case, or even just "how", if a verb and pronoun already exist)</li><li>Used to connect a verb of perception (see, hear etc.) with a verb phrase describing an action being perceived. Usually not translated, but can sometimes be translated as "how". Sometimes, <i>что</i> (što) can be used in place of <i>как</i> (kak), but this has a different meaning and is usually translated as "that".</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;between a duplicated noun) Used to emphasise the insignificance of something, that something isn't special.</li><li>(<i>with a negative or interrogative</i>) except, besides, other than</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>како</i> (kako), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kako</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as if, as though</li><li>like, as</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kind of, quasi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>like, kind of, sort of</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as if</li><li>how... would</li><li>as... would</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that; lest (<i>implying emotional negativity towards an event that would happen in a possible future or that already happened in the past</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>as if, not likely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>no matter what, whatsoever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) precisely</li></ol><p>Literally, “like at a chemist's”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how long (for what duration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as is</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without pause, ad nauseam, dull as dishwater, monotonously</li></ol><p>Literally, “like a started car”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) so they say</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>it is impossible!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as much as possible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as soon as possible (as soon as is possible)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as soon as possible (as soon as is possible)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unfortunately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) as if nothing had happened</li><li>(<i>simile</i>) as if it were the most natural thing in the world</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>any way one slices it, no matter how one looks at it</li></ol><p>Literally, “however you turn it”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more than ever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) (words) falling on deaf ears (<i>said in irritation</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “like peas (bouncing) off the wall”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as usual (as is usually the case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial) without any problems; well, swimmingly, like on wheels (used mostly with идти (idti), течь (tečʹ))</li></ol><p>Literally, “like on oil”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a rule (in general&#59; most often)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just, exactly</li><li>as it happens/happened (by coincidence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>like a duck takes to water</li></ol><p>Literally, “like a fish in water”. Compare French <i>comme un poisson dans l'eau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well, thoroughly, properly</li></ol><p>Literally, “as one should”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as soon as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ad lib, at will, as it pleases [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘(to) someone’</small>]</li><li>arbitrarily, randomly (without any order or purpose)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somehow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somehow, somehow or other, anyhow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sometime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nevertheless, after all, for (despite) all that</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somehow, one way or another</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to some extent, somewhat, rather</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) once in the past, at some point</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) how, in what way, in what manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) poo, poop, crap</li><li>(<i>childish</i>) something bad</li><li>kaka (parrot of New Zealand)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaka</i>.</p><p>Borrowed via English from Māori <i>kākā</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockatoo (a bird)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeGerman <i>Kakadu</i>bor.Russian <i>какаду</i></p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kakadu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocoa, cacao</li><li>hot chocolate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cacao</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) to poop, to crap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kakati</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kakka-</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>kakati</i>, <i>kakiti</i>, Italian <i>cacare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) diminutive of <i>кака́шка</i> (kakáška); piece of poo, crap</li></ol><p>From <i>кака́шка</i> (kakáška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) poopy</li><li>piece of shit, turd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how, in what way, in what manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what (referring to what comprises something)</li><li>like what, how (referring to what something is like)</li><li>(<i>exclamative</i>) what (a) (used to indicate an intense manifestation of something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;formal</i>) synonym of <i>како́й</i> (kakój)<i> in the main determiner and pronoun senses.</i></li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;formal</i>) synonym of <i>кото́рый</i> (kotóryj)<i> in the interrogative and relative pronoun senses.</i></li></ol><p><i>как</i> (kak) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(out of all instances of a noun) what</li><li>(out of a specific set of instances of a noun) what, which</li><li>what kind/type/sort of</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;uncommon) some, some kind(s) of</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(out of all instances of a noun) what, what one</li><li>(out of a specific set of instances of a noun) what, what one, which, which one</li><li>what kind/type/sort</li><li>like what, how</li><li>(<i>exclamative</i>) what (a) (used to indicate an intense manifestation of something)</li><li>(concessive,&#32;with <i>ни</i> (ni)) whatever/whoever/however/whatever like, no matter what/who/how/what like</li><li>used to link a relative clause, usually after <i>тако́й</i> (takój), <i>тако́в</i> (takóv), <i>тот</i> (tot) etc.&#59; may be translated as "that" or "which", or may not be translated</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a long-form adjective</i>) how (to what extent&#47;degree)</li><li>(<i>exclamative</i>) how (to a significant extent&#47;degree) (used to indicate surprise, delight or horror)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>какъ</i> (kakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kakъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kōkos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, any</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, any</li><li>some (no more than); (only) about</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) at least some sort of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, a/an (certain, unspecified or unknown)</li><li>a kind of, a sort of; something like</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cacophony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cactus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κάκτος</i> (káktos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cactus</li></ol><p><i>ка́ктус</i> (káktus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cactus wren (bird of the genus <i>Campylorhynchus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ка́ктусовый</i> (káktusovyj, “of cactuses”) +&lrm; <i>крапи́вник</i> (krapívnik, “wren”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement, feces, poo</li><li>cal, calorie</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>калъ</i> (kalŭ, “mud, muddy puddle, scum, sewage”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kalъ</i> (“mud, swamp”).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>кало́рия</i> (kalórija, “calorie”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>калеба́с</i> (kalebás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>калеба́с</i> (kalebás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>калеба́с</i> (kalebás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) kalam</li><li>alternative form of <i>каля́м</i> (kaljám, “qalam, reed pen”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كلام (kalām).</p><p>Borrowed from Arabic قلم (qalam), derived from Ancient Greek <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) A pun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>calembour</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea otter (<i>Enhydra lutris</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from Koryak <i>кала́га (кола́ха)</i> (kalága (koláxa), “beast”) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) calanthe (<i>Calanthe</i>)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from translingual <i>Calanthe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) kalanchoe (<i>Kalanchoe</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Kalanchoe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchtower</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine) tall person, beanpole</li></ol><p>Borrowed no later than the 18th century from a Turkic language, where кала meant "fortress" and -ча is a diminutive suffix, with epenthetic -н- (cf. <i>каранда́ш</i> (karandáš)). Compare Tatar <i>кала</i> (qala, “city, town”), Kazakh <i>қала</i> (qala, “city, town”), Azerbaijani <i>qala</i> (“castle”), Turkish <i>kale</i> (“fort”). Ultimately from Arabic قَلْعَة (qalʕa, “fortress”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Calathea</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Calathea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kalach (<i>traditional East Slavic bread shaped like a padlock or a wheel</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>кола́ч</i> (koláč) (through akanye), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolačь</i>, a derivation of <i>&#42;kolo</i> (“circle, wheel”). By surface analysis, <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač). Cognate to Ukrainian <i>кола́ч</i> (koláč), Bulgarian <i>кола́ч</i> (koláč), Serbo-Croatian <i>kòlač</i>, Slovak <i>koláč</i>, Czech <i>koláč</i>, Polish <i>kołacz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кала́ч</i> (kaláč)</li><li>(<i>used in the expression</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кала́ч</i> (kaláč) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;weaponry) Kalashnikov AK-47</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalashnikov (a particular model of Russian assault rifle)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Калашников)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Kalashnikov</li></ol><p>Possessive of <i>кала́шник</i> (kalášnik), i.e. literally, “belonging to (son of) the 'kalach-man'”, where <i>кала́ч</i> (kaláč) is the Russian word for a specific type of twisted white bread. From <i>кола́ч</i> (koláč) (through akanye), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolačь</i>, a derivation of <i>&#42;kolo</i> (“circle, wheel”). Cognate to Ukrainian and Bulgarian <i>кола́ч</i> (koláč), Serbo-Croatian <i>kòlač</i>, Slovak and Czech <i>koláč</i>, Polish <i>kołacz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) the Lithuanian language</li></ol><p>From Lithuanian <i>kalba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a member of the Banu Kalb tribe of Arabs in Mesopotamia</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a Central Asian person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كَلْب (kalb, “a dog”), originally referring to the Banu Kalb tribe.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kale</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кале)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calais (a city, port, commune, and subprefecture of Pas-de-Calais department, Hauts-de-France, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Calais</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calabash tree (<i>Crescentia cujete</i>)</li><li>calabash vine (<i>Lagenaria siceraria</i>)</li><li>a vessel made from the fruit of a plant denoted by this word</li><li>a vessel from which the mate beverage is properly intaken independently from its material</li><li>a musical instrument made from the fruit of a plant denoted by this word worked into a resonating body</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>calebasse</i>, <i>calabasse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>калеба́с</i> (kalebás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaleidoscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kaleidoscopic</li></ol><p><i>калейдоскоп</i> (kalejdoskop) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cripple (person who has severe impairment in his physical abilities&#59; has some negative connotation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قالیق (qalyq).</p><p>Older sources derived from Persian کالک (kâlak, “hideous”) via Ottoman Turkish كالك (kālak).</p><p>The word <i>кали́ка</i> (kalíka, “wanderer”) may be related.</p><p>For the source of borrowing, compare Serbo-Croatian <i>сакат</i>, Macedonian <i>сакат</i> (sakat) (whence <i>сакатник</i> (sakatnik)) (&lt; Ottoman Turkish سقط).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>календа́рь</i> (kalendárʹ)</li></ol><p><i>календа́рь</i> (kalendárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) calendar</li><li>calendric</li></ol><p><i>календа́рь</i> (kalendárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calendar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kalendarz</i>, from Latin <i>kalendārium</i>; compare English <i>calendar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) calendula, marigold (<i>Calendula</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Calendula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Calenduleae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>кале́ндуловый</i> (kaléndulovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) calendula, marigold; calendular</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;relational) Of the tribe Calenduleae.</li></ol><p>From <i>кале́ндула</i> (kaléndula, “calendula”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incandescence</li></ol><p><i>кали́ть</i> (kalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maim, to cripple, to mutilate</li><li>to pervert, to twist, to warp</li></ol><p><i>кале́ка</i> (kaléka) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) potash</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кали)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Hinduism</i>) Kali (Hindu goddess)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calibre (diameter of the bore of a firearm)</li><li>go/no go gauge, plug gauge (tool)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) swede, rutabaga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) swede, rutabaga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potassium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>kalium</i>, from German <i>Kalium</i>, from German <i>Kali</i> (“potash”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) glowing, incandescent</li></ol><p><i>кали́ть</i> (kalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guelder rose, snowball tree, <i>Viburnum opulus</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kalina</i>, further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kalъ</i> (whence Bulgarian <i>кал</i> (kal), Czech <i>kal</i>, Russian <i>кал</i> (kal)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kaliningrad</li></ol><p><i>Калинингра́д</i> (Kaliningrád) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кали́на</i> (kalína)</li><li>Kalinka (Russian folk-style song)</li></ol><p><i>кали́на</i> (kalína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) <i>Viburnum</i></li></ol><p><i>кали́на</i> (kalína) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) money bag, burse, purse</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) a small pastry with potato or cabbage filling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language, compare Kazakh <i>қалта</i> (qalta, “pouch, pocket”), from Persian خلته (xalta), خلیته (xalīta), from Arabic خَرِيطَة (ḵarīṭa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small door in a fence; wicket gate</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) a small pie</li><li>(<i>anime&#32;slang</i>) Steins;Gate</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, most likely from <i>&#42;колита</i> (&#42;kolita, “door”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) with akanye, where <i>&#42;колита</i> (&#42;kolita) comes from <i>кол</i> (kol, “stake, peg”) and its original meaning was "equipped with pegs".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incandesce, to heat</li><li>to roast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaliti</i> (“to temper, case-harden”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caliph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>californium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Californian</li></ol><p><i>Калифо́рния</i> (Kalifórnija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu stricto) The Caliciaceae.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu lato) The Caliciales.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>калициевый</i> (kalicijevyj, “caliciaceous, of <i>Calicium</i>”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Black Sea turbot, kalkan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calligraphy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>καλλιγραφία</i> (kalligraphía, “pretty writing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) calliphorid, blowfly</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Calliphoridae</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Calliphoridae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kalmyk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kalmyk</li></ol><p>From <i>калмы́к</i> (kalmýk) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orange lichen, <i>Caloplaca</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Caloplaca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calorie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>calorie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galosh</li><li>swim fin foot pocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaluga, great Siberian sturgeon (type of sturgeon&#58; <i>Huso dauricus</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably of Finnic origin (cf. Finnish <i>kala</i> (“fish”)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Калуга)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kaluga (a city, the administrative center of Kaluga Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>лу́жа</i> (lúža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Kaluga</li></ol><p><i>Калу́га</i> (Kalúga) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kaluga (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Калу́га</i> (Kalúga) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bride price</li><li>graft, gift for unlawful service, palm-oil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>калым</i> (qalım, “bride price”), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kalïŋ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) tracing paper</li><li>tracing <i>(a reproduction on such paper)</i></li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) calque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>calque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculator (electronic device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>calmar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) long underwear, long johns, that is worn either under normal clothing in cold weather, or as a night dress.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>caleçon</i>. Further from Latin <i>calceus</i> from Latin <i>calx</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) calceolaria (<i>Calceolaria</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Calceolaria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calcium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>calcium</i>, from Latin <i>calx</i> (“chalk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) calcite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hookah, narghile, shisha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian قلیان (qalyân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hookah</li></ol><p><i>калья́н</i> (kalʹján) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to converse, to talk</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;somewhat&#32;derogatory) to draw (pictures), to paint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qalam, reed pen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic قَلَم (qalam), derived from Ancient Greek <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-hot, tempered (of iron), made from tempered steel</li><li>roasted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camembert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>camembert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flounder, plaice, sole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>kampela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pleuronectidae (a biological family that includes flounders almost always with eyes on their right side of the body)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Pleuronectiformes (a biological order that includes flounders, soles, turbot, plaice, and halibut)</li></ol><p><i>ка́мбала</i> (kámbala) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cambodian (a person from Cambodia or of Cambodian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cambodian</li></ol><p><i>Камбо́джа</i> (Kambódža) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to combed wool</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kammwolle</i> (“combed wool”), from <i>Kamm</i> (“comb”) +&lrm; <i>Wolle</i> (“wool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) camwhore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to camwhore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gum</li></ol><p><i>каме́дь</i> (kamédʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum (resin)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) comedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>комидь</i> (komidĭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>κομμίδιον</i> (kommídion), ultimately from Egyptian <i>qmy</i> (“anointing oil”), <i>qmyt</i> (“acanthus resin, gum”); compare English <i>gum</i>.</p><p>Distortion of <i>коме́дия</i> (komédija), from Ancient Greek <i>κωμωδία</i> (kōmōdía); see English <i>comedy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) camellia</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) strumpet, prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small hearth or fireplace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn to stone, to petrify</li><li><i>(with fear, shock etc)</i> to petrify, to be petrified</li><li>to harden, to become embittered</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stony, rocky</li></ol><p><i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stone</li><li>lifeless, stone-cold</li><li>hard, immovable, stony</li></ol><p><i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) Stone Age</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windowless cell with stone walls, stone dead end</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(bituminous) coal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarrier</li></ol><p>From <i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лом</i> (-lom), combining form of <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ, “to break”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone quarry</li></ol><p><i>каменоло́м</i> (kamenolóm) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonemason</li></ol><p><i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>теса́ть</i> (tesátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bricklayer, mason</li></ol><p><i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone, rock</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) calculus, stone</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) weight</li><li>cliff</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) processor</li><li>(<i>horology</i>) jewel (analog watch)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>камень</i> (kamenĭ), accusative of <i>камꙑ</i> (kamy), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kamy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stumbling block</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cameo (brief appearance in a movie)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1990s. Borrowed from English <i>cameo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) camera</li><li>chamber, cell, compartment</li><li>prison cell, ward</li><li>room, office</li><li>inner tube (of a tire)</li><li>bladder (of a ball)</li><li>barrel (of a pump)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>camera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surveillance camera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combustion chamber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>left-luggage office (a place at a railway station or airport where luggage may be left for a small charge)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamberlain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kammerherr</i> or Dutch <i>kamerheer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) body servant, valet</li></ol><p>First attested in 1710. Borrowed from German <i>Kammerdiener</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abigail (lady's waiting-maid)</li></ol><p><i>камериста</i> (kamerista) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chamber</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) camera</li><li>chambered</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) small-scale</li></ol><p><i>ка́мера</i> (kámera) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tuning fork (fork-shaped object which emits a tone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kammerton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cameroonian</li></ol><p><i>Камеру́н</i> (Kamerún) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́мень</i> (kámenʹ): stone, rock, pebble</li></ol><p><i>кам-</i> (kam-) +&lrm; <i>-ешек</i> (-ešek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cameo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) camisole (man's jacket)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Italian <i>camiciola</i> via <i>Kamisol</i> or <i>camisole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kamikaze</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>神風</i> (かみかぜ, kamikaze).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kalimavkion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth, fireplace</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kamin</i>, from Greek κάμινος (furnace).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming out of the closet (self-disclosure of sexual orientation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coming out</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ками́н</i> (kamín): (small) hearth, fireplace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damask (fabric)</li></ol><p>From Turkic; compare Tatar <i>камка</i> (qamqa), Kazakh <i>қамқа</i> (qamqa, “silk fabric, similar to satin”). Ultimately from Persian کمخا (kamxâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform a shamanistic ritual</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic verb <i>&#42;qamla-</i> (“to perform a shamanistic ritual”), affixed by -а́ть.</p><p>Turkic source details</p><p>The Turkic source verb <i>&#42;qamla-</i> (“to perform a shamanistic ritual”) is derived from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kām</i> (“shaman, sorcerer”) + action affix -la-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kamleika, a Chukchi or Aleut robe made from sea mammal mostly sea otter intestine, which was light and waterproof.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saxifrage (<i>Saxifraga</i> sp.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) come on, c'mon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>come on</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>closet, a small room</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>komórka</i> (“very small room”). Further from Latin <i>camara</i>, <i>camera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) sporadic effort</li></ol><p><i>кампа́ния</i> (kampánija, “campaign”) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>campaign (series of operations undertaken to achieve a set goal)</li><li>crusade</li><li>(<i>military</i>) sequence of military operations pursuing some goal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kampania</i>, itself from French <i>campagne</i>, from Italian <i>campagna</i>, from Latin <i>campānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>campus (grounds or property of a school, etc)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>campus</i> (“field”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>before the Soviet Union&#32;or&#32;in&#32;neo-Nazism</i>) comrade, colleague, mate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kamerad</i> and French <i>camarade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dated form of <i>хамса́</i> (xamsá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camouflage (disguise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>camouflage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) camouflage</li></ol><p><i>камуфля́ж</i> (kamufljáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ка́мешек</i> (kámešek, “stone, rock, pebble”)</li></ol><p><i>кам-</i> (kam-) +&lrm; <i>-ушек</i> (-ušek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camphor</li><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol><p>Possibly via Italian <i>canfora</i>, from Latin <i>camphora</i>, from Arabic كَافُور (kāfūr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ка́мвхора</i> (kámvxora)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ка́мвхорить</i> (kámvxoritʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamchadal (a native person of Kamchatka, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kamchatkan (of, from or relating to Kamchatka Krai, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) damask</li></ol><p><i>камка́</i> (kamká) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj) or <i>камча́</i> (kamčá) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) clubrush (<i>Scirpus spp.</i>)</li><li>(botany,&#32;colloquial) bulrush; any of various tall, narrow-leaved plants growing near water, such as cattail, cane, rush, reed</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Compare Turkish <i>kamış</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moorhen</li></ol><p><i>камы́ш</i> (kamýš) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>仮名</i> (kana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, gutter, drain, trench</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kanał</i>, from Italian <i>canale</i>, from Latin <i>canalis</i>, from <i>canna</i>, from <i>κάννα</i> (kánna). Doublet of <i>кана́л</i> (kanál).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кана́ва</i> (kanáva): groove</li></ol><p><i>кана́ва</i> (kanáva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canadian <i>(person)</i></li></ol><p><i>Кана́да</i> (Kanáda) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Canadian</li></ol><p><i>Кана́да</i> (Kanáda) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>channel, canal, pipe</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) channel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>canalis</i>. Doublet of <i>кана́ва</i> (kanáva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sewage</li></ol><p><i>канализа́ция</i> (kanalizácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewerage, drainage; canalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;humorous) rogue, rascal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>canaglia</i>. Further from Latin <i>canis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) settee, canapé (<i>a small, elegant sofa</i>)</li><li>canapé (<i>a small piece of toast topped with some savory food</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>canapé</i>. Further from Ancient Greek <i>κωνωπεῖον</i> (kōnōpeîon) (via Latin <i>cōnōpēum</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Central Asia</i>) a large bag in which cotton is collected</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>қанар</i> (qanar) (Uzbek <i>qanor</i>), from Persian کنار (kanâr, “side, edge, border”) apparently because the sack was to the side of man, or the sacks were at the border of the field, during reaping.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Canarian (of, from or relating to the Canary Islands, Spain)</li></ol><p>From <i>Кана́ры</i> (Kanáry, “Canary Islands”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, cable</li></ol><p>Comparing the Turkism <i>ча́лить</i> (čálitʹ, “to bind, to moor”) this may be derived from Ottoman Turkish قنب (kınnap, “twine, cord”), though the end consonant shift is unaccounted; maybe confused with the common Turkic قنات (kanat, “wing”). Alternatively, possibly borrowed from Greek <i>κανάτι</i> (kanáti), from Byzantine Greek <i>καννάτα</i> (kannáta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Abutilon</i>.</li></ol><p><i>кана́т</i> (kanát, “rope, cable”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thick rope, cable</li></ol><p><i>кана́т</i> (kanát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tightrope walker, funambulist</li></ol><p>From <i>кана́т</i> (kanát) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Compare Ukrainian <i>канатохо́дець</i> (kanatoxódecʹ), Belarusian <i>канатахо́дзец</i> (kanataxódzjec).</p><p>Compare Ancient Greek <i>ἀκροβάτης</i> (akrobátēs) (<i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros) + <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs)), whence <i>акроба́т</i> (akrobát); Latin <i>fūnambulus</i> (<i>fūnis</i> + <i>ambulō</i>). Also compare Bulgarian <i>въжеиграч</i> (vǎžeigrač), Polish <i>linoskoczek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to go, to leave</li><li>(slang,&#32;with negation) to be fit, to be appropriate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canvas (<i>for embroidery</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) outline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>canevas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackles, manacles, gyves, fetters, irons (paired wrist or ankle restraints)</li></ol><p>Borrowed via Turkic from Arabic قَيْدَان (qaydān), dual form of قَيْد (qayd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candela (unit of luminous intensity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candelabrum, girandole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>candélabre</i>, from Latin <i>candēlābrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kanji</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>漢字</i> (kanji).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candidate (person running in an election)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) ellipsis of <i>кандида́т нау́к</i> (kandidát naúk, “Candidate of Sciences”) (a degree similar to a Ph.D.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kandydat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Candidate of Sciences (possessor of a Candidate of Sciences degree)</li><li>Candidate of Sciences (first of two doctoral level scientific degrees in Russia and some of the Commonwealth of Independent States)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Candidate of Sciences (first of two doctoral level scientific degrees in the USSR and Czechoslovakia)</li></ol><p>Literally, “candidate of sciences”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>candidate's (especially referring to a Ph.D. candidate)</li></ol><p><i>кандида́т</i> (kandidát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candidature, candidacy, nomination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) a vigil lamp; a cresset, usually hanging and richly decorated, containing a floating wick fed by oil, which is placed before icons and altars</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>канъдило</i> (kanŭdilo), from Ancient Greek <i>κανδήλη</i> (kandḗlē), from Latin <i>candela</i> (“candle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candoluminescence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cane Corso (Italian dog breed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>cane corso</i> (literally “Corsican dog”), with adjective <i>corso</i> being more likely derived from Latin <i>cohors</i> (“farmyard; enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(school, university, parliament) vacation <i>(U.S.)</i>, holiday <i>(UK)</i></li></ol><p>From the Latin <i>Canicula</i>, meaning "small dog", an alternative name of the star Sirius, which from the 24th of July to the 24th of August rises and sets at the same time as the sun, thus concerning this summer period. See <i>dog days</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jerrycan</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>canistrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>jewelry</i>) thin metallic gold or silver thread, used for embroidery or decoration</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) protracted, tedious affair, waste of time</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cannetille</i>, from Spanish <i>cañutillo</i>, diminutive of <i>cañuto</i>, from Mozarabic, from Latin <i>canna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κάννα</i> (kánna), from Akkadian <i>𒄀</i> (qanû), from Sumerian <i>𒄀𒈾</i> (gi.na).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of sailcloth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) канифас (A kind of sailcloth)</li></ol><p><i>канифа́с</i> (kanifás) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) канифас (A kind of sailcloth)</li></ol><p><i>канифа́с</i> (kanifás) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosin, colophony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kolophonium</i>, from Latin <i>colophonium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κολοφωνία</i> (kolophōnía), from <i>Κολοφων</i> (Kolophōn, “town in Lydia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rosin, colophony</li></ol><p><i>канифо́ль</i> (kanifólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cancan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any asteraceous plant of the genus <i>Cancrinia</i>; a cancrinia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cancrinia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kannada (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannibal (an organism which eats others of its own species)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannibalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canoeist, canoer</li></ol><p>From <i>кано́э</i> (kanóe) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>канонъ</i> (kanonŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>канонъ</i> (kanonŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>κανών</i> (kanṓn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannonade</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from French <i>cannonade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gunboat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) canonization</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) canonicalization</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>canonizātiō</i>. Also derivable synchronically as <i>канониз(и́ровать)</i> (kanoniz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;also&#32;religious) to canonize</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>kanonisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>canonical</li></ol><p><i>кано́н</i> (kanón) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canoe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge, cant, brim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kante</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cantaloupe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cantilena</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canton (administrative division)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>canton</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кантон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canton (former name of Guangzhou, city in China)</li><li>Canton (former name of Guangdong, province in China)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Portuguese <i>Cantão</i>, from Mandarin <i>廣東</i>&nbsp;/ <i>广东</i> (Guǎngdōng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) Cantonese person</li></ol><p>From <i>Канто́н</i> (Kantón) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) cantonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cantonese</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cantonese</li></ol><p><i>Канто́н</i> (Kantón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) country music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>country</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eve</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κανών</i> (kanṓn). Originally meant "Christian holiday". Doublet of <i>кано́н</i> (kanón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kanuri (Nilo-Saharan language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to sink</li><li>to disappear, to vanish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kapnǫti</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́пнуть</i> (kápnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to fall into oblivion</li></ol><p>ка́нуть (kánutʹ) is a rare synonym of the verb to sink, Ле́та (Léta) is the river Lethe from the Greek mythology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office</li><li>chancellery</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) office supplies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office</li><li>clerical</li><li>bureaucratic, dry</li></ol><p><i>канцеля́рия</i> (kanceljárija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carcinogen, carcinogenic substance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chancellor (title of various high-ranking politicians or academic officials, including heads of government in Germany and Austria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kanzler</i>, from Late Latin <i>cancellarius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stationery products</li></ol><p><i>канц-</i> (kanc-) +&lrm; <i>това́ры</i> (továry)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canyon</li><li>(<i>Israel</i>) indoor shopping mall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>cañón</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>קַנְיוֹן</i> (kanyón, “shopping mall”). Normally only used in Israeli diaspora.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buzzard (any bird from the genus <i>Buteo</i>, or, <i>sensu lato</i>, from the subfamily Buteoninae)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kanjukъ</i> / <i>&#42;kanjakъ</i>, from <i>&#42;kaňa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kōnyā</i> or <i>&#42;kan-</i> (“to sing”), whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanô</i> (“rooster, cock”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanjō</i> (“hen”) (whence English <i>hen</i>), Latin <i>cicōnia</i> (“stork”), Latin <i>canō</i> (“to sing”), Welsh <i>canu</i>, Ancient Greek <i>καναχέω</i> (kanakhéō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pester with requests, to moan, to grumble</li></ol><p><i>каню́к</i> (kanjúk, “buzzard”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaolin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) kaolinite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cape</li><li>nodule, woodknob, burl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) gumshield, mouthguard</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) cope, cape, hooded cloak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kappe</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kapa</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>cappa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;18th–early 19th century,&#32;sometimes&#32;21st century&#32;tech&#32;slang) capable; qualified</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>capable</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drip, to drop, to spill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeal, to inform, to dob</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kapati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choir of singers (from the 18th century, mixed ensemble of singers and musicians)</li><li>concert hall for chamber music</li><li>chapel</li><li><i>idiomatic</i>; see <i>а капе́лла</i> (a kapɛ́lla)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Italian <i>cappella</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaplain (a member of the clergy officially assigned to an institution, group, private chapel, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) scorifier, capsule (small porous thick-walled cup made of pressed bone ash, used for purifying gold from oxidizable metals)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kapelle</i>, probably from New Latin <i>cupella</i>, diminutive of Latin <i>cupa</i> (“cup”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usher (person who escorts people to their seats in a theatre, an opera, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kapelldiener</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́пля</i> (káplja): small drop, droplet</li><li>a bit, a grain, a little</li></ol><p><i>ка́пля</i> (káplja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapelmaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Austrian German <i>Kapellmeister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) drip, dropper, dropping bottle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to capers (the plant).</li><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) capparaceous, relating to the caper family (Capparaceae).</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) Any plant of the family Capparaceae.</li></ol><p>From <i>ка́перс</i> (kápers, “caper”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, relational adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) More common spelling of <i>копе́ц</i> (kopéc); end, death; bad or crazy thing or situation</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) Exclamation expressing the noting of something bad or crazy. man, (US) jeez, gosh, damn</li></ol><p>Pronunciation spelling of <i>копе́ц</i> (kopéc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capybara</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capillary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital, asset (money, wealth, etc.)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>capitālis</i>, from <i>caput</i> + <i>-ālis</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>скот</i> (skot) (<&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skattaz</i> (“cattle, cows; owndom, wealth, goods; hoard, treasure, money”)), <i>поголо́вье</i> (pogolóvʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>капита́л</i> (kapitál): (little) capital, asset (money, wealth, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>капита́л</i> (kapitál) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capitalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capitalism (socio-economic formation, based on private ownership rights)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kapitalismus</i>, in the first half of the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capitalist (one whose wealth (capital) is invested in business ventures in order to return a profit)</li><li>capitalist (supporter of capitalism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capitalist; capitalistic (supporting or endorsing capitalism)</li></ol><p><i>капитали́ст</i> (kapitalíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investment (a placement of capital in expectation of deriving income or profit from its use)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>main, fundamental, major, large</li><li>(economics,&#32;finance,&#32;relational) capital</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) expensive; (by extension) excellent, magnificent</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) uppercase, capitalised</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain, commander, master, skipper, team leader</li><li>(<i>military</i>) captain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kapitan</i>. Further from Latin <i>capitāneus</i>, from Latin <i>caput</i> (whence also <i>шеф</i> (šef)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>глава́</i> (glavá), <i>голова́</i> (golová), <i>глава́рь</i> (glavárʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) captain; captain's</li></ol><p><i>капита́н</i> (kapitán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) bridge, pilothouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain's wife or widow</li></ol><p>From <i>капита́н</i> (kapitán) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital (architecture)</li><li>(<i>typing</i>) small caps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to capitulate, to surrender</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to give in</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>kapitulieren</i> (“to capitulate”), from Latin <i>capitulare</i>, from <i>capitulum</i>, diminutive form of <i>caput</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kauput-</i>, <i>&#42;káput</i> (“head”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capitulation (surrender to an enemy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the Petrovian epoch from Polish <i>kapitulacja</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>capitulatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pagan temple</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>капище</i> (kapište, “idol; pagan temple”), from <i>капь</i> (kapĭ, “image”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap, bear trap, leg trap (device designed to catch or kill animals)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic. Compare Crimean Tatar <i>qapqan</i> and Turkish <i>kapan</i> (“trap, snare”). Cognate with Georgian <i>ხაფანგი</i> (xapangi, “trap”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupcake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cupcake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquid medicine in the form of drops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kaplica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop</li><li>bit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>каплꙗ</i> (kaplja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kapľa</i>, from <i>&#42;kapati</i> + <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) a drop in the bucket</li></ol><p>Literally, “a drop in the sea”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drip, to drop, to spill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kapnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) kapo (a prisoner in a Nazi concentration camp who was given food and privileges in return for supervising other prisoners doing forced labor)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a modern prisoner who cooperates with prison authorities in supervising fellow prisoners</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kapo</i>, derived from Italian <i>capo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hood, bonnet</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cappa</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>капо́т</i> (kapót), <i>капу́т</i> (kapút), <i>капуци́н</i> (kapucín), <i>капучи́но</i> (kapučíno), <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón), <i>ке́пка</i> (képka), and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) housecoat</li><li>hood, bonnet, cowl (of a car)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>capote</i>. Further from Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón), <i>капу́т</i> (kapút), <i>ке́пка</i> (képka), and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nose over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capoeira (martial art)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kappa (the Greek letter Κ&#47;κ)</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) mouthguard, aligner</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) mouthguard, gumshield</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) kappa (<i>in Japanese mythology</i>)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>κάππα</i> (káppa).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kappe</i>, ultimately from Late Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́па</i> (kápa) and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>河童</i> (kappa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cappadocian (a native or inhabitant of Cappadocia)</li></ol><p><i>Каппадо́кия</i> (Kappadókija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cappadocian (relating to Cappadocia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Каппадо́кий-</i> (Kappadókij-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Maoism,&#32;chiefly&#32;historical,&#32;derogatory) capitalist roadism</li></ol><p>By analogy with <i>ка̀ппути́ст</i> (kàpputíst, “capitalist roader”), a calque of Chinese <i>走資派</i>&nbsp;/ <i>走资派</i> (zǒuzīpài, literally “one who takes the capitalist road”). By surface analysis, <i>кап&#91;италисти́ческий&#93;</i> (kap&#91;italistíčeskij&#93;, “capitalist”) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “road”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm, “-ism”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Maoism,&#32;chiefly&#32;historical,&#32;derogatory) capitalist roader</li></ol><p>From <i>кап&#91;италисти́ческий&#93;</i> (kap&#91;italistíčeskij&#93;, “capitalist”) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “road”) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist, “-ist”), as a calque of Chinese <i>走資派</i>&nbsp;/ <i>走资派</i> (zǒuzīpài, literally “one who takes the capitalist road”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;Maoism,&#32;chiefly&#32;historical,&#32;derogatory) capitalist roadist (relating to capitalist roaders or their alleged political beliefs, capitalist roadism)</li></ol><p>From <i>ка̀ппути́ст</i> (kàpputíst, “capitalist roader”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix), from <i>кап&#91;италисти́ческий&#93;</i> (kap&#91;italistíčeskij&#93;, “capitalist”) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “road”) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist, “-ist”), a calque of Chinese <i>走資派</i>&nbsp;/ <i>走资派</i> (zǒuzīpài, literally “one who takes the capitalist road”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) corporal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whim, caprice, fancy, fit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>caprice</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>caper</i>. Doublet of <i>шевро́н</i> (ševrón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be capricious, to play up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(inanimate objects)</i> to give trouble</li></ol><p><i>капри́з</i> (kapríz) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capricious, fickle, fretful</li><li>uncertain</li></ol><p><i>капри́з</i> (kapríz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Lonicera caprifolium</i>, a species of honeysuckle.</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>caprifolium</i>. The limitation to this particular species of honeysuckle is influenced by translingual <i>Lonicera caprifolium</i>, the taxonomic name with that specific epithet.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polycaprolactam</li></ol><p>Following the Soviet subjugation of the industries of Germany, where the compound was invented in 1938 by Paul Schlack, and the reestablishment of production and trade, as a shorthand from <i>капролакта́м</i> (kaprolaktám).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sagger, saggar (a box or casing of clay used to protect delicate ceramics during firing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physiology,&#32;pharmacy) capsule</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>capsula</i> from Latin <i>capsa</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́псюль</i> (kápsjulʹ), <i>ка́сса</i> (kássa), <i>кассе́та</i> (kasséta), and <i>шасси́</i> (šassí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>percussion cap, fulminate cap, detonator, primer (metal cap on muzzleloading firearms)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>capsule</i>, from Latin <i>capsula</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́псула</i> (kápsula).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) keeper of a warehouse or storehouse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>capitaine d'armes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) keeper of a warehouse or storehouse</li></ol><p><i>капт(ена́рмус)</i> (kapt(ɛnármus)) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) warehouse, storehouse</li></ol><p><i>каптёр</i> (kaptjór) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) cabbage</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;slang) money, in particular United States dollars.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>капуста</i> (kapusta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acai (South American palms of the genus <i>Euterpe</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated,&#32;inanimate) cabbage patch</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) cabbage worm</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cabbage pie</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) kapustnik (<i>series of skits or songs, with lyrics changed to refer to the guests of honor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>капу́ста</i> (kapústa) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cabbage</li></ol><p><i>капу́ста</i> (kapústa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruin, destruction, end</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>kaputt</i>. Further from Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>капо́т</i> (kapót), <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón), <i>ке́пка</i> (képka), and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Capuchin</li><li>capuchin monkey (<i>Cebus</i> gen. and spp.)</li><li>nasturtium (<i>Tropaeolum</i> gen. and spp.)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cappa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hood</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>capuchon</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>капо́т</i> (kapót), <i>капу́т</i> (kapút), <i>капуци́н</i> (kapucín), <i>капучи́но</i> (kapučíno), <i>ке́пка</i> (képka), and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power barrow, dummy car</li><li>forklift, abbreviation for автока́р (avtokár), электрока́р (elektrokár)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) A cirque — a half-open steep-sided hollow at the head of a valley or on a mountainside, formed by glacial erosion.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>caw (cry of a crow or raven)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>car</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penalty, punishment, retribution</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kara</i> (“punishment”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kor-</i>. Cognate with Old Irish <i>caire</i> (“blame, disapproval”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Karabakh</li></ol><p><i>Караба́х</i> (Karabáx) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) carabid; ground beetle.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Carabidae</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́да</i> (-ída).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbine (short-barreled rifle)</li><li>carabiner (metal link with a gate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Karabin</i> (obsolete variant of <i>Karabiner</i>) or French <i>carabine</i>. First attested in the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clamber</li><li>to scramble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>korovai (large, single-piece round or rectangular loaf of bread, elaborately decorated and served at a wedding)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>короваи</i> (korovai), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korvajь</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>корова́й</i> (korováj), Bulgarian <i>крава́й</i> (kraváj), Serbo-Croatian <i>кра̀ва̄ј</i> (“type of cake fed to the bride and groom”) (genitive <i>крава́ја</i>), Slovene <i>kravȃj</i>, <i>krávaj</i>. Per Vasmer, probably related to <i>коро́ва</i> (koróva, “cow, also dialectally bride”); originally thought to have been a wedding gift that was intended to induce fertility.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caravan, convoy</li></ol><p>Probably from Persian کاروان. Attested since the 1st quarter of 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caravanserai</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian کاروانسرای (kârevânsarây).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caravan</li></ol><p><i>карава́н</i> (karaván) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Crimean Karaite (adherent of Karaism, a sect of Judaism)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Karaim (Kipchak Turkic language traditionally spoken in the Crimea and elsewhere, whose speakers are&#47;were Karaites)</li></ol><p><i>караи́мы</i> (karaímy) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caracal</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language. First attested in 1788.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuttlefish (any of various squidlike mollusks)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) clumsy or ugly woman</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic &#42;korkъ (“limb”) with the suffixes -атый (-atyj, “<i>-provided</i>”) and -ица (-ica, “<i>feminine suffix</i>”), thus roughly meaning <i>the legged one</i>. The form корока́тица (korokática) would thus be the etymologically correct one, but the most used one is карака́тица (karakática), influenced by кара- (kara-, “(from Turkish) black”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qara Khitai (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) karakul sheepskin</li></ol><p><i>кара́куль</i> (karákulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unclear, clumsily or carelessly written letter</li><li>doodle (randomly, whimsically winding line)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the plural) scribble, scrawl, illegible letter or handwriting</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) curved tree or twig</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) tongs for pulling wood from a smelting furnace</li><li>(archaic,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) set of four sharp iron spikes shaped so that one always points up, thrown under the feet of the enemy's cavalry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starfruit, carambola (fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannon, carom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caramelization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caramel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>caramel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caramel</li></ol><p><i>караме́ль</i> (karamélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pencil</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) pencil marks, pencil drawing</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) stick (a small, thin cylinder of some material)</li></ol><p>In Russian from the 16–17th centuries. Borrowing from a Turkic language. Compare Turkish <i>karataş</i> (“black slate”), composed of <i>kara</i> (“black”) + <i>taş</i> (“stone”), Tatar <i>кара таш</i> (qara taş, “graphite, black lead”). The origin of epenthetic <i>-н-</i> (-n-) is unclear. Compare <i>каланча́</i> (kalančá) for the same epenthesis.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow pencil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>каранда́ш</i> (karandáš, “pencil”)</li></ol><p><i>каранда́ш</i> (karandáš) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pencil</li></ol><p><i>каранда́ш</i> (karandáš) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarantine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) quarantine</li></ol><p><i>каранти́н</i> (karantín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karaoke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>カラオケ</i> (karaoke), from <i>空</i> (から, kara, “empty”) + <i>オケ</i> (オーケストラ, oke, “orchestra”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carapace (hard protective covering)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) tot, kid, child, toddler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crucian carp (<i>Carassius carassius</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;karasь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carat (jewelry unit of mass)</li><li>karat (unit of fineness of gold)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Ancient Greek <i>κεράτιον</i> (kerátion), from <i>κέρας</i> (kéras). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head, top; horn”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>гран</i> (gran) (&lt; Latin <i>grānum</i>, via PIE cognate with <i>зерно́</i> (zernó)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>空手</i>, from <i>唐手</i> (karate), from Okinawan <i>唐手</i> (tūdī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punisher; chastener</li></ol><p><i>кара́ть</i> (karátʹ, “to punish”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>punitive</li></ol><p><i>кара́ть</i> (karátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karateka</li></ol><p><i>карате́</i> (karaté) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish, to chastise, to inflict penalty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;karati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>空手</i>, from <i>唐手</i> (karate), from Okinawan <i>唐手</i> (tūdī, “empty hand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard; watch</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>help!</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing; compare Tatar <i>каравыл</i> (qarawıl), Kazakh <i>қарауыл</i> (qarauyl). Ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>ᠬᠠᠷᠠᠭᠤᠯ</i> (qaraɣul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to keep watch <i>(over)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to watch <i>(for)</i>, to lie in wait <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>карау́л</i> (karaúl) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sentry, guard</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sentry, guard</li></ol><p><i>карау́л</i> (karaúl) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardroom</li></ol><p><i>карау́л</i> (karaúl) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchman, guard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Karachay-Balkar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) all fours</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) carbide, acetylide</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) calcium carbide</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>carbide</i>, pronounced as if a French word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) calcium carbide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) carbonado</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carbonnade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbonate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) carborane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carburetor (a device in an internal combustion engine)</li></ol><p>From French <i>carburateur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Astrakhan) crow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hag, harridan</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. Compare Turkish <i>karga</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>striga</i>, <i>strix</i> (&lt; Ancient Greek <i>στρίξ</i> (stríx)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;special) cargo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardamom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardan, universal joint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardigan (type of sweater)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cardigan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardinal <i>(official in Catholic Church)</i></li><li>(<i>bird</i>) cardinal <i>(Cardinalis)</i></li><li>White Cloud Mountain minnow <i>(Tanichthys)</i></li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) cardinality</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cardinālis</i> (per Vasmer via German <i>Kardinal</i> or French <i>cardinal</i>), further from Latin <i>cardō</i> (whence also, via French and German, <i>шарни́р</i> (šarnír)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>radically, drastically, completely</li></ol><p><i>кардина́льный</i> (kardinálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardinal number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cardinal</li><li>fundamental</li><li>drastic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cardiogram</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>cardiogramme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardiologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) square</li><li><i>(as an adjective)</i> square</li><li><i>(women's haircut)</i> bob</li><li>(<i>poker</i>) four of a kind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carré</i> (“square”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kareli, Karelian (male)</li></ol><p>From Swedish <i>karel</i>, <i>kareler</i>, from Old Swedish <i>karel</i>, <i>karelar</i>, formed from the toponym Medieval Latin <i>Carelia</i> via a Finnic endonym derived from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;karja</i>. Compare Polish <i>Karel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Karelo-Finnish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Karelian</li></ol><p><i>каре́л</i> (karél) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) carriage, coach (an enclosed horse-drawn vehicle for transporting people)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) ellipsis of <i>каре́та ско́рой по́мощи</i> (karéta skóroj pómošči, “ambulance”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kareta</i>, from Italian <i>carretta</i>, from Latin <i>carrus</i> + <i>-ittus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;formal) ambulance (emergency vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartwright</li></ol><p><i>каре́та</i> (karéta) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carriage, coach</li></ol><p>From <i>ка́рет</i> (káret) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>From <i>каре́та</i> (karéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seriema (New World terrestrial bird of family Cariamidae)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of the scientific name <i>Cariama</i>, of Tupian origin (from Old Tupi <i>saria</i> (“crest”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carib</li></ol><p>From or cognate with English <i>Carib</i>, from Kalinago <i>karifuna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Caribbean</li></ol><p><i>кари́б</i> (karíb) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caribou</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caries</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>eye color</i>) brown, hazel</li><li>(<i>horse color</i>) dark-chestnut</li></ol><p>Possibly from Bulgar <i>&#42;kar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caricature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caricaturist, cartoonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carignan (grape variety)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hibiscus tea, carcade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كَرْكَدِيه (karkadīh, “carcade”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frame, framework, skeleton, case, wireframe, airframe</li><li>hackberry (<i>Celtis</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carcasse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caw</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to prophesy ill, to be a prophet of doom</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caw</li></ol><p><i>ка́ркать</i> (kárkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf (<i>various senses</i>), midget, gnome, pygmy, manikin, elf</li><li>(<i>Magic: The Gathering</i>) a gnome</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>karel</i>, <i>karlík</i> or Polish <i>karlę</i>, <i>karzeł</i>, <i>karlik</i>, all from Old High German <i>karal</i>, whence Middle High German <i>karl</i> (“little boy”) and German <i>Kerl</i> (“young man”). Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf planet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dwarf; dwarfish, diminutive, pygmean</li></ol><p><i>ка́рлик</i> (kárlik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>कर्म</i> (kárma) form of कर्मन् (kárman, “act, action, performance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pocket</li><li>a road lay-by</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) clipboard</li></ol><p>Uncertain, a distorted word, with the <i>р</i> (r) presumably excrescent in compliance with Russian phonotactics, earlier <i>гама́н</i> (gamán, “purse, wallet”) which is still current usually in the extension <i>гамане́ць</i> (hamanécʹ) in Ukrainian; probably a Turkic borrowing, possibly with the Ukrainian and Southern Russian value /ɣ/ for <i>г</i> (g) specifically from (the early 18th-century version of) Chuvash <i>хаман</i> (haman, literally “durable”), also applied to fabrics and also to a skin bag, which would ultimately derive from Persian خام (xâm, “raw, crude”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickpocket, cutpurse</li></ol><p><i>карма́н</i> (karmán) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pocket watch (watch)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pocket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) puppet</li></ol><p><i>карма́н</i> (karmán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickpocket, cutpurse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>карма́н</i> (karmán)</li></ol><p><i>карма́н</i> (karmán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickpocket, cutpurse</li></ol><p><i>карма́н</i> (karmán) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>карма́н</i> (karmán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carmine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>karmic</li></ol><p><i>ка́рма</i> (kárma) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carnival</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carnaval</i>, from Italian <i>carnevale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carnival</li></ol><p><i>карнава́л</i> (karnavál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karnay (a long trumpet or horn made of brass or copper, used as a traditional instrument in Uzbekistan and other parts of Central Asia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornice</li><li>eaves</li><li>curtain rod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Karnies</i>. Further from Ancient Greek <i>κορωνίς</i> (korōnís) (via Spanish <i>cornisa</i>), i.e. <i>криву́лина</i> (krivúlina). Further from Ancient Greek <i>κορωνός</i> (korōnós), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to turn, to bend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cornice</li></ol><p><i>карни́з</i> (karníz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karoshi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>過労死</i> (かろうし, karōshi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) carotin, carotene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>): <i>Cyprinus carpio</i>, common carp (<i>species</i>)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>): common carp (meat)</li></ol><p>A Germanic borrowing, from Old High German <i>karpo, karpho</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Carpathian (of or relating to the Carpathians)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) carp (fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyprinidae (a biological family that includes carps)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) <i>Cyprinus carpio</i></li></ol><p><i>карп</i> (karp) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Cyprinodontiformes.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Cypriniformes (a biological order that includes carps, minnows, and loaches)</li></ol><p><i>карп</i> (karp) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tamil <i>கறி</i> (kaṟi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>karst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A go-cart</li></ol><p>From the American brand <i>Go-Kart</i>, presumably from English <i>cart</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carte blanche, blank check, proxy in blank (unlimited discretionary power to act&#59; unrestricted authority)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carte blanche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>map</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) chart</li><li>card</li><li>playing card</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) menu</li><li>patch (of asphalt on a road)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>karta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mispronounce some sounds, especially /r/ and, less commonly, /l/</li></ol><p>Related to Polish <i>kartać</i> (“to incite, to instigate”), Ukrainian <i>карта́ти</i> (kartáty, “to scold, to reproach”), Bulgarian <i>къ́ртя</i> (kǎ́rtja, “I break”), Russian <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij, “short”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(about pronunciation of the sound of <i>р</i> (r) or, less commonly, <i>л</i> (l)) incorrectly, with a burr, gutturally, uvularly</li></ol><p><i>карта́вый</i> (kartávyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burr, guttural pronunciation, uvular pronunciation, incorrect pronunciation (of the sound of <i>р</i> (r) or, less commonly, <i>л</i> (l))</li></ol><p><i>карта́вый</i> (kartávyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>about a person</i>) incorrectly pronouncing the sound of <i>р</i> (r) or, less commonly, <i>л</i> (l)</li><li>(about pronunciation of the sound of <i>р</i> (r) or <i>л</i> (l)) incorrectly pronounced</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who incorrectly pronounces the sound of <i>р</i> (r) or <i>л</i> (l)</li></ol><p><i>карта́вить</i> (kartávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crankcase</li></ol><p>From the English <i>Carter</i> from the brand name <i>Carter gear-case</i>, named for British mechanical engineer John Harrison Carter. Compare also Dutch <i>carter</i>, Italian <i>carter</i>, Estonian <i>karter</i> and many others.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Картер)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Carter</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>Carter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) mitrailleuse</li></ol><p><i>карте́чь</i> (kartéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grapeshot; canister shot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kartätsche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picture (representation of visible reality produced by drawing, etc.)</li><li>painting, canvas</li><li>movie, film</li><li>picture, image (as produced in transient fashion by technology, e.g. a monitor)</li><li>scene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>cartina</i> (“fine paper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>карти́на</i> (kartína): a small picture, photograph or illustration; icon</li></ol><p><i>карти́на</i> (kartína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art gallery (space where works of art are placed on display)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>expressively</li><li>picturesquely, in a picturesque manner, with external beauty</li></ol><p><i>карти́нный</i> (kartínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) picture; pictorial</li><li>expressive</li><li>picturesque</li></ol><p><i>карти́на</i> (kartína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>ка́рты</i> (kárty, “cards”): cards, card game</li></ol><p><i>ка́рты</i> (kárty) +&lrm; <i>-ишки</i> (-iški).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cartographic</li></ol><p><i>картогра́фия</i> (kartográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartography</li></ol><p>From French <i>cartographie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χάρτης</i> (khártēs, “map”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardboard, paperboard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carton</i> or from German <i>Karton</i>, both borrowed from Italian <i>cartone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardboard box</li><li>cardboard</li></ol><p><i>карто́н</i> (kartón) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cardboard</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unnatural</li></ol><p><i>карто́н</i> (kartón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>file cabinet, document cabinet, card index</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) potato</li></ol><p><i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potato masher</li></ol><p><i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potato peeler (device)</li></ol><p><i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) an individual potato</li></ol><p><i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) potatoes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kartoffel</i>. The forms ending in <i>-а</i> (-a) are mediated by Polish <i>kartofel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(UK, Ireland, Australia, New Zealand) chips, (US, Canada) French fries</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mashed potatoes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) potato</li></ol><p><i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) potato</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>card</li><li>small photograph</li></ol><p><i>ка́рта</i> (kárta) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>card game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) card</li></ol><p><i>ка́рточ(ка)</i> (kártoč(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>карто́шка</i> (kartóška), diminutive of <i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ)</li></ol><p><i>карто́шка</i> (kartóška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) potato</li></ol><p>From <i>карто́ха</i> (kartóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive ending), the former a dialectal variant of <i>карто́фель</i> (kartófelʹ, “potato”). Recorded since the middle of the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>UK, Ireland, Australia, New Zealand</i>) chips, (<i>US, Canada</i>) French fries</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartridge (vessel which contains the ink for a computer printer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cartridge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of a peaked cap with high cap band; fiddler cap, kashket</li><li>(by extension,&#32;modern usage) newsboy cap, flat cap</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) paper bag for various loose materials</li><li>(military,&#32;historical) powder bag</li></ol><p>According to Shansky, from earlier forms <i>карпуз</i> (karpuz), <i>карпуц</i> (karpuc), from Dutch <i>karpoets</i> (“traveling hat”), and distinct from Etymology 2 (see below). The term originally referred to military cloth or fur hats; the “peaked cap” sense developed in mid-19th century.</p><p>According to Vasmer, from Middle Low German or Low German <i>kartûse, kardûse</i>, from French <i>cartouche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>карту́з</i> (kartúz)</li></ol><p><i>карту́з</i> (kartúz) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartouche</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cartouche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cards, card game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>card player</li><li>card gambler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merry-go-round, carousel</li><li>whirligig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Carthaginian</li></ol><p><i>Карфаге́н</i> (Karfagén) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lockup; punishment cell; sweatbox, isolation ward</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>carcer</i> via German <i>Karzer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(oncology,&#32;pathology) carcinoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full gallop</li><li>open-pit mine; quarry; sandpit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>carrière</i>. Further from Latin <i>carrāria</i>, from Latin <i>carrus</i>.</p><p>Several steps omitting from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;quadrāria</i>, from Latin <i>quadrāre</i> (“to make four-corned, square; cut into a square”), from Latin <i>quadrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>career</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>carriera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>careerism, self-advancement</li></ol><p>From <i>карье́ра</i> (karʹjéra) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>deprecative</i>) careerist; social climber</li></ol><p>From <i>карье́ра</i> (karʹjéra) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) career</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) full gallop</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) open-pit mine</li></ol><p><i>карье́ра</i> (karʹjéra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>карье́р</i> (karʹjér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) concerning, regarding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch, touching, contact</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) tangent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>concerning, regarding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial,&#32;animate) affectionate address to a male</li><li>iris (plant) (<i>Iris</i>)</li><li>male swallow (bird)</li></ol><p>From <i>коса́тый</i> (kosátyj, “having pigtails (<i>коси́чки</i> (kosíčki))”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Original meaning was "male swallow".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallow, barn swallow (<i>Hirundo rustica</i>)</li><li>(<i>endearing</i>) darling</li></ol><p>From <i>коса́тый</i> (kosátyj, “having a pigtail”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>коса́</i> (kosá) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to concern, to have to do with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>касатисѧ</i> (kasatisę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kasati</i> + reflexive <i>&#42;sę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li><li>hard hat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>casque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cascade</li><li>(<i>radio</i>) stage</li><li>(<i>operetta</i>) song and dance number</li><li>(<i>circus</i>) stunt sequence</li><li>(<i>figure skating</i>) jump combination</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cascade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>specialist</i>) to cascade (to arrange in a stepped series)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>каска́д</i> (kaskád) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), though compare German <i>kaskadieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cascade</li><li>(relational,&#32;theater) variety <i>(show)</i>, music hall</li></ol><p><i>каска́д</i> (kaskád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stunt performer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cascadeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat cap as well as a newsboy cap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>casquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) hull insurance (insurance for automobiles, ships, aircraft, etc., against damage, theft or unauthorized use. It does not cover cargo, third-party liability, etc.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>casco</i> (“hull”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Caspian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caspius</i> (“Caspian”) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pay desk, cash desk, checkout</li><li>ticket window, box office</li><li>cash</li><li>bank, fund</li><li>cash register</li><li><i>(polygraphy)</i> case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>cassa</i>, from Latin <i>capsa</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́псула</i> (kápsula), <i>ка́псюль</i> (kápsjulʹ), <i>кассе́та</i> (kasséta), and <i>шасси́</i> (šassí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;law) appeal, cassation</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>Kassation</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). By surface analysis, <i>касса́ция</i> (kassácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cassette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cashier, teller</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>Kassierer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cash desk, cash register</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cash</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) box office</li><li>successful in box-office terms</li></ol><p><i>ка́сса</i> (kássa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kaste</i> or French <i>caste</i>, from Portuguese <i>casta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) castanet (a single handheld percussion instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>castañeta</i> (“diminutive form of castaña”). Ultimately from Latin <i>castanea</i> (also the ultimate source of Russian <i>кашта́н</i> (kaštán)), from Ancient Greek <i>καστάνεια</i> (kastáneia, “chestnut”), from Ancient Greek <i>κάστανα</i> (kástana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brass knuckles (weapon which reinforces the fist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>casse-tête</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Castilian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casting (selection of performers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>casting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) casting (selection of performers)</li></ol><p><i>ка́стинг</i> (kásting) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caste</li></ol><p><i>ка́ста</i> (kásta) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castrato</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emasculation</li></ol><p><i>кастри́ровать</i> (kastrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate (remove the testicles of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кастрю́ля</i> (kastrjúlja) (small) pan, saucepan, pot</li></ol><p><i>кастрю́ля</i> (kastrjúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stockpot, saucepan</li></ol><p><i>кастрю́ля</i> (kastrjúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stockpot, saucepan, pan, pot, casserole (a deep cooking vessel with one or two handles and a lid)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kastrol</i>, variant of <i>Kasserolle</i>, from French <i>casserole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) executioner</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cat</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) khat</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) cut (livejournal cut, <lj-cut>, which allows you to hide all or part of your entry behind a link)</li><li>gat (a traditional Korean hat made of horsehair, once worn by married gentlemen)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;katъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kat</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Arabic قات (qāt).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>cut</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>갓</i> (gat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) cutscene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) katabasia <i>(an orthodox hymn)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) confusion, row, muddle, mess</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek καταβασία (κατάβασις), "descent", from καταβαίνω, "descend"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;geology) catagenesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>katakana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>片仮名</i> (katakana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cataclysm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) catacomb</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>catacumbae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games&#32;slang</i>) cardsharp</li><li>(slang,&#32;by extension) cheater, scoundrel</li></ol><p>From <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la); originally from criminal cant.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joint, jug (prison)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lockup (prison cell)</li></ol><p>From <i>такела́ж</i> (takeláž, “tackle, rigging”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with metathesis.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catalan (language)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Catalan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catalyst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>catalytic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>catalyt(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrow, gurney (a stretcher having wheeled legs)</li></ol><p><i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ, “to roll”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catalog, catalogue (a systematic list of names, books, pictures, etc.)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) folder, directory</li></ol><p>From French <i>catalogue</i>, itself derived from Ancient Greek <i>κατάλογος</i> (katálogos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catalogue, to catalog</li></ol><p>From <i>катало́г</i> (katalóg, “catalog, catalogue”) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catalan, Catalonian (native or inhabitant of Catalonia) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catalan nationalism, Catalanism</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Катало́ния</i> (Katalónija) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Catalan</li></ol><p><i>Каталония</i> (Katalonija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catamaran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>katana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>刀</i> (katana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll, rolling (the act of rolling)</li><li>driving, drive, riding, ride (a vehicle, bicycle, horse, etc, swing, especially for pleasure)</li><li>skating (на конька́х), skiing (на лы́жах), boating (на ло́дке), sliding (с го́рки), horseriding (верхо́м)</li></ol><p><i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) felt boot</li></ol><p><i>ка́таный</i> (kátanyj) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catapult (medieval siege-warfare device)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) catapult (device for launching an airplane)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) ejection seat mechanism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>catapulta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catapult</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to eject from an airplane</li></ol><p><i>катапу́льта</i> (katapúlʹta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ejector seat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ejectable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) catarrh</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) Cathar</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Катар)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qatar (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cataract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catharsis (in drama, a release of emotional tension)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Qatari</li></ol><p><i>Ката́р</i> (Katár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perestroika (a program of political and economic reform carried out in the Soviet Union in the 1980s)</li></ol><p>катастро́фа (katastrófa, “catastrophe”) + перестро́йка (perestrójka, “rebuilding”). Coined around the 1980s by Alexander Zinoviev.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catastrophe, disaster, calamity</li><li>accident, crash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>catastrophically</li></ol><p><i>катастрофи́ческий</i> (katastrofíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>catastrophic (relating to a catastrophe)</li><li>catastrophic, disastrous, ruinous</li></ol><p><i>катастро́фа</i> (katastrófa) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) catatonia (a severe psychiatric condition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (a trolley), to take (someone) for a drive</li><li>to ride (on a bicycle, on a swing, etc.)</li><li>to roll (to cause to revolve)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ката́ти</i> (katáti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;katati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ride <i>in a vehicle</i></li><li>to roll <i>(abstract verb), see</i> <i>кати́ться</i> (katítʹsja)</li><li>to ride <i>on skis, skates, bicycle, or snowboard</i></li></ol><p><i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catafalque, catafalco</li><li>hearse, bier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>catafalque</i>, from Italian <i>catafalco</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;catafalicum</i> (whence ultimately also <i>эшафо́т</i> (ešafót) (a doublet), English <i>scaffold</i>), probably derived from Ancient Greek <i>κατά</i> (katá, “down”) + Latin <i>fala</i> (“scaffolding, wooden siege tower”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) cataphor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retroreflector, cat's eye</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κατά</i> (katá, “downwards”) + <i>φωτός</i> (phōtós, “light”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>categorically</li><li>flatly, strongly, out of hand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>categorical, <i>(refusal, denial etc)</i> flat</li></ol><p><i>катего́рия</i> (kategórija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>categoricity, categoricalness</li></ol><p><i>категори́чный</i> (kategoríčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>categorical, flat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>category</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socialism of the chair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kathedersozialismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) catenoid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) motorboat, boat (with an inboard engine)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cutter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cutter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minesweeper</li></ol><p><i>ка́тер</i> (káter) +&lrm; <i>тра́льщик</i> (trálʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>ка́тер</i> (káter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) soldier in a motorboat</li></ol><p><i>ка́тер</i> (káter) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cutter, launch</li></ol><p><i>ка́тер</i> (káter) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́тер</i> (káter)</li></ol><p><i>ка́тер</i> (káter) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter building</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter builder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́тер</i> (káter)</li></ol><p><i>катеро́к</i> (katerók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) leg, cathetus (side in a triangle)</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>κάθετος</i> (káthetos) (via Latin <i>cathetus</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>odvěsna</i> (i.e. <i>отве́сная</i> (otvésnaja)), Polish <i>przyprostokątna</i> (i.e. **припростоку́тная).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catheter</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>καθετήρ</i> (kathetḗr) (via Latin <i>cathetēr</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>cévka</i>, Polish <i>cewnik</i> (i.e. **цевник, akin to <i>це́вка</i> (cévka), <i>цевни́ца</i> (cevníca), <i>цевьё</i> (cevʹjó)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) catechism</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>κατήχησις</i> (katḗkhēsis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>кафи́зма</i> (kafízma, “kathisma”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology) cathine (psychoactive alkaloid derived from the khat plant)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>Ка́тин</i> (Kátin)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>cathine</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Катин)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Katya's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) cation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>кати́зма</i> (katízma), a rare form of <i>кафи́зма</i> (kafízma, “kathisma”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, to push <i>(some object with wheels)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive <i>(quickly)</i></li><li><i>(a car etc.)</i> to bowl along</li><li>to fit, to work</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>катити</i> (katiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to tear</li><li>to sweep, to move, to flow, to stream, to roll</li></ol><p><i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games&#32;slang</i>) a video game match</li></ol><p>Uncertain. By surface analysis, <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ, “to roll”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Either a semantic loan from English <i>let's roll</i> or derived from criminal slang (compare <i>ката́ла</i> (katála, “cardsharp”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Katowice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>katowicki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) cathode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cathodoluminescence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skating rink, ice rink</li><li>steamroller, road roller, roller (<i>compactor-type engineering vehicle</i>)</li><li>mangle</li></ol><p>From <i>ката́ться</i> (katátʹsja, “to skate”) and <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ, “to roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman Catholic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Catholicism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Catholic</li></ol><p><i>като́л(ик)</i> (katól(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catholicism</li></ol><p><i>като́лик</i> (katólik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo); see also <i>-и́чество</i> (-íčestvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>type of punishment</i>) penal servitude, hard labour</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) misery, hard life</li></ol><p>From Byzantine Greek <i>κάτεργον</i> (kátergon, “galley; penal labor”), from Ancient Greek <i>κάτεργος</i> (kátergos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) convict sentenced to hard labor in a penal colony</li></ol><p><i>ка́торга</i> (kátorga) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) convict sentenced to hard labor in a penal colony</li></ol><p><i>ка́торга</i> (kátorga) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>penal, (<i>relational</i>) convict</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unbearable, very hard, backbreaking</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) convict sentenced to hard labor in a penal colony</li></ol><p><i>ка́торга</i> (kátorga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crambe</li><li>spiny dogfish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quatrain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>quatrain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(textile)</i> spool, reel, bobbin</li><li><i>(electric)</i> coil, bobbin</li><li><i>(cinema)</i> roll</li></ol><p><i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Katyusha (vehicle-mounted rocket launcher)</li></ol><p>From <i>Катю́ша</i> (Katjúša), diminutive of <i>Ка́тя</i> (Kátja, “Katya”), in turn diminutive of <i>Екатери́на</i> (Jekaterína, “Katherine”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Катюша)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Katyusha, of the female given names Ка́тя (Kátja), Екатери́на (Jekaterína), or Катери́на (Katerína)</li><li>a Russian folk song composed in 1938.</li><li>a type of artillery rocket launcher consisting of an array of rockets mounted on a truck, originated in the Soviet Union, named after the song.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) turd (piece of solid feces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) caudillo (a military dictator, especially in Latin American)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>caudillo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caudofoveate, a mollusc of the class Caudofoveata.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Caudofoveata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bay (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caoutchouc (natural rubber)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>caoutchouc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rubber</li></ol><p><i>каучу́к</i> (kaučúk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>café</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>café</i>, from Italian <i>caffè</i>, from Ottoman Turkish قهوه (kahve), from Arabic قَهْوَة (qahwa). Doublet of <i>ко́фе</i> (kófe).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department/school/college in a university or institute; chair.</li><li>pulpit, rostrum, dais</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>καθέδρα</i> (kathédra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;church) cathedral</li><li>(sub)department <i>(of an institute or university)</i></li></ol><p><i>ка́федра</i> (káfedra) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) ceramic tiles, Dutch tiles, glazed tiles (<i>used for walls or flooring</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kachel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glazed tile</li></ol><p><i>ка́фель</i> (káfelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>café (coffee shop)</li><li>cafeteria (a type of restaurant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кафе́</i> (kafɛ́); café (usually small)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) kathisma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>каѳисма</i> (kaθisma), from Byzantine Greek <i>κάθισμα</i> (káthisma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>кафи́зма</i> (kafízma, “kathisma”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kafkaesque</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaftan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قفتان (kaftan), from Persian خفتان.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kakheti or Kakhetians</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kakhetian wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of clothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) katsap, a Russian person, Russian, Russki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>каца́п</i> (kacáp).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) katsaps, Russkis (especially from the Ukrainian perspective)</li></ol><p><i>каца́п</i> (kacáp) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Katskar man (<i>from a community who lives along the Katka River in Yaroslavl Oblast</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seesaw, teeter-totter</li><li>rocking chair, rocker</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) jack pump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gym, workout room</li></ol><p><i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ, “to rock, to swing”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocking, swinging, oscillation, swaying, <i>(of a pendulum)</i> swing</li><li>pumping</li></ol><p><i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to swing, to shake, to wobble; to dandle</li><li>to pump</li><li>to toss, to roll, to pitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lift up, to chair (to toss someone up, like a group of fans their champion)</li><li>to download</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to train/pump muscle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>in the expression</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kačati</i>, iterative-durative for <i>&#42;kotiti</i> (“to roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rock, to swing</li><li>to stagger, to lurch, to reel</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to oscillate, to swing</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to pump iron</li></ol><p><i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swing (<i>hanging seat</i>)</li><li>seesaw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jerked fish</li><li>a soft type of yukola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with quality, of high quality</li><li>qualitatively</li></ol><p><i>ка́чественный</i> (káčestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>qualitative</li><li>high-quality, high-grade</li></ol><p><i>ка́чество</i> (káčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quality</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>качьство</i> (kačĭstvo), from <i>как</i> (kak). Compare Latin <i>quālitās</i> from <i>quālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) rolling, pitching</li><li>(regional,&#32;Kuban) duck</li></ol><p>From <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to swing, to shake, to wobble; to dandle (once)</li><li>to pump (once)</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;impersonal</i>) to toss, to roll, to pitch (once)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lift up, to chair (to toss someone up, like a group of fans their champion)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to train/pump muscle (once)</li></ol><p>Possibly <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) (or derived the other way around).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rock, to swing</li><li>to stagger, to lurch, to reel</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to oscillate, to swing</li></ol><p><i>качну́ть</i> (kačnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a person with excessive muscle mass; a muscleman; a bodybuilder</li></ol><p><i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porridge, gruel</li><li>mash, mush</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) muddle, mess, confusion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>каша</i> (kaša), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaša</i>. Cognates include Slovak <i>kaša</i> and Serbo-Croatian <i>kaša</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cachalot, sperm whale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cachalot</i>, from Portuguese <i>cachalote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person who prepares kasha</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaš(ь)ľь</i> (“cough”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kaʔs(u)lio-</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeh₂s-</i> (whence also English <i>whoost</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cashmere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cashmere</li></ol><p><i>кашеми́р</i> (kašemír) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>porridge-like, pasty</li></ol><p><i>ка́ша</i> (káša) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semiliquid mass, slurry</li><li>gruel, liquid porridge</li><li>endearing form of <i>ка́ша</i> (káša)</li></ol><p><i>ка́ша</i> (káša) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p><p><i>ка́ша</i> (káša) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ка́ша</i> (káša, “porridge”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) white clover</li></ol><p><i>ка́ша</i> (káša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>каске́т</i> (kaskét)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kaszkiet</i>, from French <i>casquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough</li></ol><p><i>ка́шлять</i> (kášljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) or <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kašьľati</i> (“to cough”), from <i>&#42;kaš(ь)ľь</i> (“cough”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kāˀsulia</i>, <i>&#42;kāˀslia</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeh₂s-</i> (whence also English <i>whoost</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kashmiri (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kashmiri</li></ol><p><i>кашми́ри</i> (kašmíri) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarf, muffler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cache-nez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornamental flowerpot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cache-pot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chestnut</li><li>chestnut tree</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from Polish <i>kasztan</i>, itself from Latin <i>castanea</i> (also the ultimate source of Russian <i>кастанье́та</i> (kastanʹjéta)), from Ancient Greek <i>καστάνεια</i> (kastáneia), from <i>κᾰ́στᾰνᾰ</i> (kắstănă).</p><p>Attested since the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chestnut</li><li>chestnut-colored, maroon</li></ol><p><i>кашта́н</i> (kaštán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kashubian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;nautical) caique</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kiayguk</i> (“boat, oar”). Compare Azerbaijani <i>qayıq</i> (“boat”), Crimean Tatar <i>qayıq</i> (“boat”), Kyrgyz <i>кайык</i> (kayık, “boat”), Southern Altai <i>кайык</i> (kayïk, “oar”), Turkmen <i>kayık</i> (“boat”), Uzbek <i>qayiq</i> (“boat”). Doublet of <i>каи́к</i> (kaík).</p><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) wardroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cabin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kajüte</i> (whence also Czech <i>kajuta</i>, Polish <i>kajuta</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kayak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Inuktitut <i>ᖃᔭᖅ</i> (qayaq, “man's boat”) (Inuvialuktun), from Proto-Eskimo <i>&#42;qayaʁ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;regional) tit (any bird of the family Paridae)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Komi-Permyak <i>кай</i> (kaj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repent (to feel sorrow or regret for what one has done or omitted to do)</li><li>to confess (to admit to the truth, particularly in the context of sins or crimes committed)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>каꙗтисѧ</i> (kajatisę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kàjati</i> + reflexive <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare Ukrainian <i>ка́ятися</i> (kájatysja), Belarusian <i>ка́яцца</i> (kájacca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кафи́зма</i> (kafízma, “kathisma”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кафи́сма</i> (kafísma), an archaic form of <i>кафи́зма</i> (kafízma, “kathisma”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kV, kilovolt</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>килово́льт</i> (kilovólʹt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apartment, flat</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>croak, ribbit: an imitation of the sound made by a frog.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) rectangular cuboid</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding</i>) quad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Quader</i> and clipping of <i>квадри́цепс</i> (kvadríceps).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>socionics</i>) quadra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quadrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>square</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>quadrātum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>квадра́т</i> (kvadrát): (small) square</li></ol><p><i>квадра́т</i> (kvadrát) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>square</li></ol><p><i>квадра́т</i> (kvadrát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quadrature, squareness, squaring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>quadrillion (1,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>15</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) <i>quasar</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>quasar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pseudo-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) quasi-state</li></ol><p><i>квази-</i> (kvazi-) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рство</i> (gosudárstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) quasi-state</li></ol><p><i>квази-</i> (kvazi-) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рство</i> (gosudárstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quasi-satellite</li></ol><p><i>квази-</i> (kvazi-) +&lrm; <i>спу́тник</i> (spútnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>croak, ribbit: an imitation of the sound made by a frog.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to croak, to give a croak</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quaker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Quaker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) qualification, qualifying</li></ol><p><i>квалифика́ция</i> (kvalifikácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qualification</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Qualifikation</i>, from French <i>qualification</i>, from Middle French <i>qualification</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>quālificātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>qualified, skilled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to qualify</li><li>to qualify <i>(as)</i>, to categorize <i>(as)</i>, to consider <i>(as; to be)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>qualifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) quantum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quantum mechanics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>quantum mechanics</i>) quantum number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) quantum</li></ol><p><i>квант</i> (kvant) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) fourth</li><li>quart (a unit of liquid capacity)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) fourth position in fencing and the corresponding parry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarter (<i>of a year</i>)</li><li>city block</li><li>quarter, district (<i>of a city; in various expressions</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Quartal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quarterly (<i>in reference to a quarter of a year</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) city block</li><li>(relational,&#32;historical) urban ward (<i>in Russia until 1862</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ward policeman (<i>in Russia until 1862</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кварта́л</i> (kvartál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) quartet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apartment, flat</li><li>(<i>military</i>) quarters (accommodation, particularly for troops)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>quārtārius</i>, via German <i>Quartier</i> or Dutch <i>kwartier</i> or, perhaps, Old Polish <i>kwartera</i> (modern <i>kwatera</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodger, tenant, occupant</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) husband (<i>spoken by his wife</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tenant of a communal apartment</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;prison slang) short-term convict (<i>usually up to two years</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra) +&lrm; <i>-а́нт</i> (-ánt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra): (small) flat, apartment</li></ol><p><i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) apartment, flat</li><li>(<i>military</i>) billeting</li></ol><p><i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, to reside, to dwell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenant (<i>of an apartment</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>съёмщик</i> (sʺjómščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landlord, apartment/flat owner</li></ol><p><i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra, “apartment”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, “owner”) +&lrm; <i>хозя́ин</i> (xozjáin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rent</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>кварти́рная пла́та</i> (kvartírnaja pláta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) quartz</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Quarz</i>, from a West Slavic language, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvьrdъ</i> (“hard”). Doublet of <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quartzite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Quarzit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кварц</i> (kvarc).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kvass (a type of fermented no- or low-alcohol beverage, made from bread, often flavored with fruit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>квасъ</i> (kvasŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kvasъ</i> (“leaven, fermented drink”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>cāseus</i> whence English <i>cheese</i>, and Sanskrit <i>क्वथ्</i> (kvath, “to boil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make sour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to booze</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to thrash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kvasiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>квас</i> (kvas) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kvass</li></ol><p><i>квас</i> (kvas) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alum (a double sulfate)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) sheepskin pickling solution</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>квасцы</i> (kvascy, “alum”), first attested in a 1470s manuscript, presumed to derive from a term for a sheepskin (or other hide) pickle more often called квасы which may be made from flour, similarly to kvass drink, and salt, with or without alum</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>квас</i> (kvas).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin (or other hide) pickling solution made from fermented flour similarly to the kvass drink, but with addition of salt and optional alum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) quaternion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;relational) quaternion</li></ol><p><i>кватернио́н</i> (kvatɛrnión) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>quattuordecillion (10<sup>45</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauerkraut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fermented, sour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>вверх</i> (vverx)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>кве́рху</i> (kvérxu)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upward, up (toward an upper level)</li></ol><p><i>к</i> (k, “towards”) +&lrm; <i>верх</i> (verx, “the top”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adventure game (video game genre)</li><li>quest (journey or effort in pursuit of a goal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>quest</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) quilling</li></ol><p>From English <i>quilling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>quindecillion (10<sup>48</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) quintet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>quintillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>18</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quintessence</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>quīnta essentia</i> (“fifth essence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receipt, ticket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kwitantie</i>, see there for more. Compare Italian <i>quietanza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) receipt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>квито́к</i> (kvytók), derived from Polish <i>kwit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) quodlibet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>quodlibet</i>, from Latin <i>quod libet</i> (“that which is pleasing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quorum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>quōrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quota</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>quota</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hen</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sitting duck, dumb cluck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quenya (constructed language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kg, kilogram</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>килогра́мм</i> (kilográmm, “kilogram”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kebab (skewered dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كَبَاب (kabāb) via Turkic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowling alley, skittle alley</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) bowling (game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kegelbahn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skittle, bowling pin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sports,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) plimsoll, sneaker, Chuck (kind of running shoes with a flat thin sole, similar to classic Chuck Taylor All-Stars design)</li></ol><p>From Keds.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sports,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) plimsoll, sneaker, Chuck (kind of running shoes with a flat thin sole, similar to classic Chuck Taylor All-Stars design)</li></ol><p>From Keds.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cedar</li><li><i>Pinus sibirica</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cedrus</i>; ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to gather cedar nuts</li></ol><p>From <i>кедр</i> (kedr) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cedar</li></ol><p><i>кедр</i> (kedr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кедровый)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kedrovy (a city in Tomsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine nut (edible seeds of evergreen pine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) KDE</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) K-pop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>K-pop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A K-pop fan.</li></ol><p>From <i>кей-по́п</i> (kej-póp) +&lrm; <i>-ер</i> (-er).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefcase</li><li>hard-sided case (<i>e.g. for a musical instrument</i>)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;business) case (instance or event as a topic of study)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>case</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catering (the business of providing food and related services)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>catering</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;mining) cake (solid obtained by filtering or drying a suspension or leaching solution)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) kek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cake</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>kek</i>, ultimately from Korean <i>ㅋㅋ</i> (kk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) sponge cake, sweet bread</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate,&#32;rare) dude, guy</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;inanimate) filter-avoidance spelling of <i>секс</i> (seks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Keks</i>, from English <i>cakes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lay brother</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;Central Asia,&#32;chiefly&#32;Uzbekistan) bride</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>kelin</i> (“bride”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kelvin (SI temperature unit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Germany,&#32;Poland&#32;or&#32;Ukraine) waiter</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) servant (in a hotel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kellner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trowel (mason's tool)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kielce</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) cell (room in a monastery for sleeping one person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>келиꙗ</i> (kelija), from Byzantine Greek <i>κελλία</i> (kellía), plural of <i>κελλίον</i> (kellíon, “garret”), diminutive of <i>κέλλα</i> (kélla), from Latin <i>cella</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to doze, to slumber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cambridge</li></ol><p>From <i>Ке́мбридж</i> (Kémbridž) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Ukrainian <i>ке́мбриджський</i> (kémbrydžsʹkyj), Belarusian <i>ке́мбрыджскі</i> (kjémbrydžski).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kemerovo (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Ке́мерово</i> (Kémerovo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camping ground, campground, campsite, camp</li><li>camping</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>camping</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kangaroo</li></ol><p><i>кенгур(у́)</i> (kengur(ú)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kangaroo</li></ol><p><i>кенгур(у́)</i> (kengur(ú)) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kangaroo (a member of the Macropodidae family of large marsupials with strong hind legs for hopping, native to Australia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kangaroo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kangaroo joey</li></ol><p><i>кенгуру́</i> (kengurú) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kenyan</li></ol><p><i>Ке́ния</i> (Kénija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cenotaph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fam, homie, friend</li><li>dude, guy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>קענט</i> (kent), <i>באַקענט</i> (bakent, “familiar, well-known”); see <i>קענען</i> (kenen, “to know”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кент)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kent (a county of England)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Kent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) centaur (a mythical creature, half man, half horse)</li></ol><p>From the Ancient Greek <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kepi (cap with a flat circular top)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>képi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft, billed hat (usually for a male)</li><li>peaked cap</li></ol><p>From <i>ке́пи</i> (képi) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from French <i>képi</i>, from German <i>Käppi</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>капо́т</i> (kapót), <i>капу́т</i> (kapút), <i>капуци́н</i> (kapucín), <i>капучи́но</i> (kapučíno), <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón), and <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ке́пка</i> (képka): (small) cap</li></ol><p><i>ке́пка</i> (képka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expanded clay aggregate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) expanded clay aggregate</li></ol><p><i>керамзи́т</i> (keramzít) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceramics</li><li>earthenware</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κεραμικός</i> (keramikós, “potter's”), from <i>κέραμος</i> (kéramos, “potter's clay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ceramic</li></ol><p><i>кера́м(ика)</i> (kerám(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) Old Believer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) female Old Believer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) churl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) (a cylindrical sample of rock obtained by drilling)core</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kern</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punch, center punch (device for creating holes in thin material)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Körner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kerogas, Primus stove</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kerosene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kerosene</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to booze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil-stove, kerosene stove</li><li>heater that runs on kerosene</li></ol><p><i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kerosene</li></ol><p><i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kerosene lamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kerosene</li></ol><p><i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín)</li></ol><p><i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kerosene man (someone who sells kerosene)</li></ol><p><i>кероси́н</i> (kerosín) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kerch (a city in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Керчь</i> (Kerčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quesadilla (Mexican dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>quesadilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;colloquial) cup, particularly piyāla</li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>кесе</i> (kese) +&lrm; <i>-айка</i> (-ajka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;archaic) Caesarean, Caesar's</li></ol><p><i>ке́сарь</i> (késarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesarean section (incision in the womb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;archaic) Caesar (title of a Roman or Byzantine emperor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кесарь</i> (kesarĭ), from Koine Greek <i>Καῖσαρ</i> (Kaîsar), itself a borrowing from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́йзер</i> (kájzer), <i>царь</i> (carʹ), <i>це́зарь</i> (cézarʹ), and <i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;colloquial) cup, particularly piyāla</li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>кесе</i> (kese) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chum salmon, Siberian salmon (<i>Oncorhynchus keta</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Nanai <i>кета</i> (kjeta), <i>кэта</i> (keta); attested 1755.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) ketone (organic chemicals with the &gt;CO functional group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ket (relating to the Ket people)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ket (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixture of tomato ketchup and mayonnaise</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>ке́тчуп</i> (kétčup, “ketchup”) +&lrm; <i>майоне́з</i> (majonéz, “mayonnaise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tomato sauce</i>) ketchup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ketchup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mullet</li><li>grey mullet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>κέφαλος</i> (kéfalos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kefir (a yogurt-like beverage made from fermented cows' milk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed into Russian from a language of the Caucasus; compare Mingrelian <i>ქიფური</i> (kipuri), Ossetian <i>къӕпы</i> (k’æpy), Karachay-Balkar <i>гыпы</i> (gıpı). The ultimate origin is unknown. Relation with a potential Kipchak substrate <i>&#42;köpür-</i>, akin to Turkish <i>köpürmek</i> (“to foam”), is unknown.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kefir</li></ol><p><i>кефи́р</i> (kefír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Quechua (tribe)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) Quechua (member of the tribe)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Quechua (language)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Quechuan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) cash-back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cashback</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cashew</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Portuguese <i>caju</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>cyber- (the Internet or cyberspace)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) cyberattack (an attack over a computer network)</li></ol><p><i>кибер-</i> (kiber-, “cyber-”) +&lrm; <i>ата́ка</i> (atáka, “attack”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyberwar, cyberwarfare</li></ol><p><i>кибер-</i> (kiber-, “cyber-”) +&lrm; <i>война́</i> (vojná, “war”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyberneticist, cybernetician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cybernetics</li></ol><p>Adaptation of English <i>cybernetics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cybernetic</li></ol><p><i>киберне́т(ика)</i> (kibernɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyberpunk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cyberpunk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cybercrime (an act or occurrence of cybercrime)</li></ol><p><i>кибер-</i> (kiber-, “cyber-”) +&lrm; <i>преступле́ние</i> (prestuplénije, “crime”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cybercrime (crime committed using computer networks)</li></ol><p><i>кибер-</i> (kiber-, “cyber-”) +&lrm; <i>престу́пность</i> (prestúpnostʹ, “crime”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyberspace</li></ol><p><i>кибер-</i> (kiber-, “cyber-”) +&lrm; <i>простра́нство</i> (prostránstvo, “space”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cybersecurity</i>) cyberstalking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cyberstalking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kibitka (horse-drawn carriage)</li><li>yurt, kibitka (nomad's dwelling of some ethnic groups such as the Kalmyks and Kyrgyz)</li><li>telega or sleigh with a (usually rounded) cover over the passenger seats</li></ol><p>Attested since the middle of the 17th century. Related to <i>кибита́</i> (kibitá, “arched hoop, half of a hoop of a large fish-trap”), obsolete (17th c.) <i>киби́ть</i> (kibítʹ, “arc of the bow”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>киби́тка</i> (kybýtka), Belarusian <i>кібі́тка</i> (kibítka).</p><p>Borrowed from Turkic: compare Tatar <i>кибет</i> (kibet), Bashkir <i>кибет</i> (kibet), Kipchak (kebit), <small>[script needed]</small> (kibit), <small>[script needed]</small> (kabit, “stall, shop”), Karakhanid (käbit, “inn”).</p><p>Probably ultimately from Sogdian (qpyδ /⁠kəpēδ⁠/, “stall”) (akin to Persian کلبه (kolbe); see it for more), though Arabic قُبَّة (qubba, “dome”) too is often mentioned as another possible ultimate source.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kibitka</li></ol><p><i>киби́тка</i> (kibítka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyborg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kibbutz</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>קבוץ</i> (kibútz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod, to beckon</li><li>to motion, to point, to allude</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyvati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) shako</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiwi (fruit)</li><li>kiwi (bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod, to beckon</li><li>to motion, to point, to allude</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nod</li></ol><p><i>кива́ть</i> (kivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) con man</li></ol><p><i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ, “to swindle”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p><p>For the meaning development, compare <i>дья́вол</i> (dʹjávol) (ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>δια-</i> (dia-) + <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “I throw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swindle, fraud, scam</li></ol><p><i>кида́(ть)</i> (kidá(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-во</i> (-vo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khitan (person)</li></ol><p>From <i>кида́нь</i> (kidánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to scam, to steal from</li><li>(slang,&#32;Internet) to send</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to abandon, to ditch</li></ol><p><i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khitan (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Khitan <i>𘱿𘲫</i> (&#42;qid ún). Doublet of <i>Кита́й</i> (Kitáj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khitan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khitan (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>кида́нь</i> (kidánʹ, “Khitan”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “-ish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to fling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to abandon, to leave behind <i>(someone)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat, to swindle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kydati</i>. Cognate with English <i>shoot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw oneself, to fling oneself</li><li>to dash, to rush</li></ol><p><i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyivan (person)</li></ol><p><i>Ки́ев</i> (Kíjev) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kyiv/Kiev; Kyivan/Kievan, Kyivite/Kievite.</li></ol><p><i>Ки́ев</i> (Kíjev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) The "Kyiv regime", the government of Ukraine as an enemy of Russia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coho salmon (<i>Oncorhynchus kisutch</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornel, dogwood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kïŕïl</i> (“red”). Thoroughly replaced the native Slavic word (<i>дёрен</i> (djóren) from <i>&#42;dernъ</i>) early as also with Belarusian <i>кізі́л</i> (kizíl), while Ukrainian <i>дере́н</i> (derén) is still more common than <i>кизи́л</i> (kyzýl). Compare Ottoman Turkish قزلجق and Turkish <i>kızılcık</i> (cornelian cherry).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotoneaster (<i>Cotoneaster</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кизи́л</i> (kizíl, “cornel”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare the meaning development in Armenian <i>չմենի</i> (čʻmeni, “cornel; cotoneaster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) stick</li><li>cue (straight tapering rod used to impel the balls in games such as billiards, snooker, and pool)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to kick (a user from a chat, group, channel, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kick</i> + <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) kickboxing (sport like boxing but allowing strikes with the feet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kickboxing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) kikimora (a female house spirit in the Slavic mythology)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) ugly woman or girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to kick (a user from a chat, group, channel, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kick</i> + <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kikuyu (member of Bantu people)</li><li>Kikuyu (Bantu language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) clubroot</li><li>(rare,&#32;pathology) hernia</li><li>(<i>games</i>) kila (a traditional Russian game)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) keel</li></ol><p><i>киль</i> (kilʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евой</i> (-evoj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Orenburg,&#32;Belgorod,&#32;Caucasus) rug, carpet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>kilim</i>, Ottoman Turkish كلیم (kilim), from Classical Persian گلیم (gilīm), from Ancient Greek <i>κάλυμμα</i> (kálumma, “covering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contract killer, hitman, paid assassin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>killer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kilogram, kilo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>kilo-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) kilobyte</li></ol><p>From <i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) kilobit</li></ol><p>From <i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilowatt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilovolt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilovolt-ampere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilogram</li></ol><p><i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>грамм</i> (gramm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilocalorie</li></ol><p>Related to English <i>kilocalorie</i>, French <i>kilocalorie</i>, and Polish <i>kilokaloria</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>кало́рия</i> (kalórija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilometer/kilometre <i>(SI unit of measure)</i></li></ol><p><i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilometer per hour, km/h</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilometrage (distance travelled in kilometres)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>kilométrage</i>, equivalent to <i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr) +&lrm; <i>-аж</i> (-až).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-kilometer-long</li></ol><p><i>киломе́тр</i> (kilométr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiloton</li></ol><p><i>кило-</i> (kilo-) +&lrm; <i>тонна</i> (tonna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilt (traditional Scottish garment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>kilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keel, back</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) fin, tailfin, fin surface, keel</li><li>keel bone (bird’s sternum)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kiel</i> or Low German <i>Kiel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wake <i>(path left behind a ship on the surface of the water)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kielwater</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprat, brisling (any of various small marine fish in the family Clupeidae)</li></ol><p>From Estonian <i>kilu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kimono</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>着物<rp>(</rp><rt>きもの</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>kimono</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>ки́мрский</i> (kímrskij): Welsh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kimchi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>김치</i> (gimchi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kimchi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>김치</i> (gimchi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) the smallest unit of speech production, articulatory distinguishing feature</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>κίνημα</i> (kínēma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinematics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinematography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filmmaker, cinematographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) film, screen, motion picture, movie; cinematographic</li></ol><p><i>кинематогра́фия</i> (kinematográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinema, cinematography, filmmaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кинемато́граф</i> (kinematógraf).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) kinetic energy (energy from motion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kinetic (relating to motion)</li></ol><p><i>кине́т(ика)</i> (kinɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dagger, poniard (a stabbing weapon)</li><li>Kh-47M2 Kinzhal (Russian nuclear-capable hypersonic aero-ballistic air-to-surface missile)</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. Compare Nogai <i>кынжал</i> (kınjal), Kumyk <i>хынжал</i> (xınjal), Crimean Tatar <i>къанджер</i>, <i>ханджер</i>, <i>гъанджер</i>, Tatar <i>хәнҗәр</i> (xäncär), Yakut <i>кынчаал</i> (kïnčaal). Related to Persian خنجر (xanjar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кинжа́л</i> (kinžál).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coriander <i>(especially leaves)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ქინძი</i> (kinʒi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie, motion picture, film</li><li>movies, cinema, the pictures</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>кинемато́граф</i> (kinematógraf, “cinematography”) or German <i>Kino</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>cinema; film; movie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quinoa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie actor</li></ol><p><i>кино́-</i> (kinó-) +&lrm; <i>актёр</i> (aktjór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film camera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinnabar (mineral)</li><li>vermilion (pigment, color)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κιννάβαρι</i> (kinnábari), from perhaps Arabic زِنْجَفْر (zinjafr); cognate with English <i>cinnabar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newsreel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie theater, cinema</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>зал</i> (zal)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie star</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie camera</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ка́мера</i> (kámera)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion picture</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>картина</i> (kartina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movie, film</li><li>reel of film (containing a copy of a movie)</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́нта</i> (lénta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(film,&#32;colloquial) movie fan; cinephile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>projectionist</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>меха́ник</i> (mexánik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>film</i>) cinematic/film montage</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-, “film”) +&lrm; <i>монта́ж</i> (montáž, “montage”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinematic opera (as a genre of film)</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>о́пера</i> (ópera)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cameraman, cameraperson (male or female)</li></ol><p><i>кино́-</i> (kinó-) +&lrm; <i>опера́тор</i> (operátor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film <i>(for film projector)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film projector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film rental and screening</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прока́т</i> (prokát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film director</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>режиссёр</i> (režissjór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film studio</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filming, shooting (<i>of film</i>), cinematography</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>съёмка</i> (sʺjómka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) filming, shooting</li></ol><p><i>киносъёмка</i> (kinosʺjómka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinema, movie theatre</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>теа́тр</i> (teátr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film festival</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion picture, film, picture, movie</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>фильм</i> (filʹm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newsreel</li></ol><p><i>кино-</i> (kino-) +&lrm; <i>хро́ника</i> (xrónika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кино́</i> (kinó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) filmmaker</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;low colloquial) cinema; movie; cinematography</li></ol><p><i>кино́</i> (kinó) +&lrm; <i>-шный</i> (-šnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location or site of an abandoned permanent Sami people settlement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami <i>кӣннт</i> (kīnnt, “location of permanent human settlement”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to fling</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to leave (someone)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to text, to send (someone) an SMS message</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to transfer (money) (<i>from one bank account to another</i>)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) to rob</li><li>(informal,&#32;originally&#32;criminal slang) to scam, to cheat, to swindle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kydnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;slang,&#32;euphemistic) to fuck</li></ol><p>Literally, “to throw a stick”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw oneself, to fling oneself</li><li>to dash, to rush</li></ol><p><i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kinyarwanda (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiosk, stall, booth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>kiosque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stallholder, newsagent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>kiosquier</i>, <i>кио́ск</i> (kiósk) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon case, kiot</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>κιβωτός</i> (kibōtós) (via Old Church Slavonic <i>кивотъ</i> (kivotŭ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile, stack (usually of papers)</li><li>bale, pack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cypress</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κυπάρισσος</i> (kupárissos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cypress</li></ol><p><i>кипари́с</i> (kiparís) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil, to seethe</li><li>to boil, to rage, to seethe, to burn (in terms of emotions)</li><li>to be in full swing (in terms of action)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kypě̀ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwep-</i>, whence English <i>cupidity</i>, <i>covet</i> and <i>hope</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) noise; quarrel</li><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;slang</i>) excitement, commotion</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) house search, police raid</li></ol><p>From <i>хи́пес</i> (xípes, “a special kind of theft with the assistance of an attractive prostitute; prostitute, stealing from the client”), from Yiddish <i>חופּות</i> (khupes), the plural of <i>חופּה</i> (khupe, “canopy used in a wedding ceremony”) (from Hebrew <i>חופה</i> (khupá)), whence also slang <i>хи́па</i> (xípa, “female genitalia”). Likely influenced by <i>кипеть</i> (kipetʹ, “to boil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot (person from Cyprus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot (of, from or relating to Cyprus)</li></ol><p><i>Кипр</i> (Kipr) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кыпча́к</i> (kypčák)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil (causative of <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ) (<i>causative inchoative and causative continuative</i>))</li></ol><p>Derived from the same root as <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ, “to boil”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kypě̀ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwep-</i>, whence English <i>covet</i> and <i>hope</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get angry or agitated; to lose one's temper</li></ol><p><i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>specifically</i>) hot water served as a hot beverage</li><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial) water that was boiled and then cooled to room temperature, as for example to make nonpotable tapwater be potable.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a hot-tempered person</li></ol><p><i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiling</li></ol><p><i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiled</li></ol><p><i>кипяти́ть</i> (kipjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuirass (piece of defensive armor, covering the body from the neck to the girdle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyz (person)</li></ol><p>From Kyrgyz <i>кыргыз</i> (kırgız)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kyrgyz</li></ol><p><i>Кирги́зия</i> (Kirgízija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubberized canvas (<i>commonly used in jungle boots</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) products made from rubberized canvas, especially boots</li></ol><p>Modification of older <i>керза</i> (kerza), borrowed from English <i>kersey</i> (“type of rough woolen cloth”). Popularly etymologized as an abbreviation of <i>ки́ровский заво́д</i> (kírovskij zavód, “Kirov factory”), where it supposedly was first made.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boot made of rubberized canvas</li></ol><p><i>ки́рза</i> (kírza) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač) or <i>кирза́</i> (kirzá) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rubberized canvas</li></ol><p>From <i>ки́рза</i> (kírza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) (stem-stressed version) or from <i>кирза́</i> (kirzá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) (suffix-stressed version).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gilbertese language</li><li>Kiribati people</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кирибати)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kiribati (a country consisting mostly of the Gilbert Islands archipelago, located in Micronesia in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrillization (putting text into the Cyrillic alphabet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrillic alphabet</li></ol><p><i>Кири́лл</i> (Kiríll) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cyrillic</li></ol><p><i>кири́лл(ица)</i> (kiríll(ica)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickaxe</li><li>Lutheran church</li></ol><p>Unclear. Likely from Turkic, compare: Turkish <i>kürek</i>, Azerbaijani <i>kərki</i> (“adze”) (whence Tat <i>kerki</i>). Compare also Ancient Greek <i>κερκίς</i> (kerkís).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kirche</i>. Doublet of <i>церковь</i> (cerkovʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pickaxe, pick hammer, pick</li></ol><p><i>кирка́</i> (kirká) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kirov</li></ol><p><i>Ки́ров</i> (Kírov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable&#32;and&#32;uncountable</i>) brick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) no entry traffic sign</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. Compare Kazakh <i>кірпіш</i> (kırpış), Turkish <i>kerpiç</i>, Bashkir <i>кирбес</i> (kirbes), Azerbaijani <i>kərpic</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кирпи́ч</i> (kirpíč)</li></ol><p><i>кирпи́ч</i> (kirpíč) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brick</li><li>brick-red</li></ol><p><i>кирпи́ч</i> (kirpíč) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lutheran church</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kirche</i>. Doublet of <i>церковь</i> (cerkovʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Protestantism</i>) parish community</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kirchspiel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>here kitty kitty (a call to make a cat come closer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;endearing) kitty, pussy, cat</li><li>(<i>affectionate term of address</i>) dear, sweetie</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) girl, chick</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) kitty, pussy (female genitals)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) triacetone triperoxide, TATP, TCAP (primary explosive)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;dated) purse, moneybag, drawstring bag</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>трипе́рекись</i> (tripérekisʹ, “triperoxide”) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p><p>From a Turkic language, compare to Turkish <i>kese</i>, Azerbaijani <i>kisə</i>, from Persian کیسه (kise).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) muslin</li></ol><p><i>кисея́</i> (kisejá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kisel, kissel (a popular jellied dessert made by cooking berry juice or other fruit juice with milk, flour and oatmeal, thickened with starch)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) weak-willed person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyselь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tobacco pouch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;endearing) kitty, cat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) pussy (female genitalia)</li></ol><p><i>ки́са</i> (kísa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). The meaning of female genitalia is a post-Soviet semantic loan from English <i>pussy</i>, comparatively obscure to the English word, more similar to the use of English <i>kitty</i> in the same sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dispiritedly, in a dejected manner</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a sour or displeased manner</li></ol><p><i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sour-milk, fermented-milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxygen</li></ol><p>From <i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj, “acidic”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-род</i> (-rod), combining form of <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “to give birth”); calque of French <i>oxygène</i>. Compare <i>водоро́д</i> (vodoród), <i>углеро́д</i> (ugleród).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oxygen</li><li>oxygenous</li></ol><p><i>кислоро́д</i> (kisloród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) acid</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) LSD</li></ol><p><i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj, “sour”) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá). Introduced by Lomonosov in chemistry.</p><p>The "LSD" sense is a semantic loan from English <i>acid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acidity (quality or state of being acid)</li></ol><p><i>кисло́тный</i> (kislótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;relational) acid, acidic</li></ol><p><i>кислота́</i> (kislotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acid rain (unusually acidic rain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acidogenic (that generates acid or acidity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sour</li><li>fermented, sour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dispirited, disheartened, crestfallen, down in the dumps</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) displeased, dissatisfied</li><li>(relational,&#32;chemistry) acid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kys(ь)lъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour fruit</li><li>sour fruit drink, citric drink</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kismet, fate, destiny</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قسمت (kısmet), from Arabic قِسْمَة (qisma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to feel depressed, to mope <i>(around)</i>, to languish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kysnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кис(лый)</i> (kis(lyj)) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cyst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy) hand; carpal</li></ol><p><i>кисть</i> (kistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) flail, bludgeon (a weapon with a striking head attached to a handle by a chain or rope)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кисть</i> (kistʹ): (small) brush; tassel</li></ol><p><i>кисть</i> (kistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brush, paintbrush (<i>instrument for applying paint</i>)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hand</li><li>tassel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kystь</i>. Related to Polish <i>kiść</i> (“a bunch (of fruit, flowers, or other things)”), and to <i>ки́та</i> (kíta, “bunch, bouquet)”). See also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skauba-</i> (“sheaf”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ки́са</i> (kísa); kitty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as a term of endearment.</li></ol><p><i>ки́са</i> (kísa) +&lrm; <i>-у́ня</i> (-únja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whale</li><li>(<i>Russian Far East</i>) a male Chinese person</li><li>(<i>university slang</i>) the Chinese language</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>китъ</i> (kitŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>κῆτος</i> (kêtos).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>Китай</i> (Kitaj) or <i>китаец</i> (kitajec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>кита́йский</i> (kitájskij, “Chinese”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) bunch, bouquet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyta</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кита)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kita (one of 23 special wards of Tokyo prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>北</i> (きた, Kita).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) The Qur'an</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كتاب</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinology, Chinese studies</li></ol><p><i>Кита́й</i> (Kitáj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Chinese person</li></ol><p>From <i>Кита́й</i> (Kitáj, “China”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinicization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sinicize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinology, Chinese studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;inanimate) a kind of cloth from China</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>китая́нка</i> (kitajánka)</li><li>(regional,&#32;inanimate) Chinese food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) it's all Greek to me; double Dutch</li></ol><p>Literally, “a Chinese letter”. From an official letter obtained from the Chinese Emperor by Ivan Petlin's 1618–1619 expedition, which remained untranslated in Russia for decades.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chopsticks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chinese</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) fake, forged, counterfeit, shoddy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the Chinese language (short for кита́йский язы́к (kitájskij jazýk))</li></ol><p><i>Кита́й</i> (Kitáj, “China”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “-ese”)</p><p>Sense 2 comes from the popular notion of Chinese manufactured goods having poor quality in the 1990s and often being counterfeit copies of renowned Western brands.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chinese boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Chinese person, chink, chinaman</li></ol><p><i>Китай</i> (Kitaj, “China”) +&lrm; <i>-ёза</i> (-józa, “a little fool”). The suffix is also from French <i>-euse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of a military uniform</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kittel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) a mythical creature in the East Slavic mythology similar to centaur</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>китоврасъ</i> (kitovrasŭ), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) whale, cetacean</li></ol><p><i>кит</i> (kit, “whale”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoebill</li></ol><p><i>кит</i> (kit, “whale”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глав</i> (glav, “head”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whaler, whaleman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кит</i> (kit).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whale calf, baby whale</li></ol><p><i>кит</i> (kit) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) clink, bin, jail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kittchen</i> during World War II German occupation, then gulag language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to put on airs; to boast, to brag</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кычити</i> (kyčiti), <i>кычити ся</i> (kyčiti sja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyčiti</i>, derivative of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conceited, arrogant, haughty</li></ol><p><i>кичи́ть(ся)</i> (kičítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiche</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>quiche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infest, to overrun</li><li>to swarm, to pullulate</li><li>to be infested</li><li>to abound, to be abundant</li><li>to crawl with</li></ol><p>Possibly inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyšěti</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>киші́ти</i> (kyšíty), Belarusian <i>кішэ́ць</i> (kišécʹ). Related to Lithuanian <i>kušė́ti</i> (“to move”) and to Latvian <i>kustēt</i> and <i>kušņāt</i> (“to move”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gut flora, intestinal flora (microorganisms that live in the digestive tracts of animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowels, intestines</li></ol><p><i>кишка́</i> (kišká) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) intestine; intestinal</li></ol><p><i>кишка́</i> (kišká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gut, intestine, bowel</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;plural only) giblets, pluck</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;plural only) insides, contents, spares</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) hose (for watering)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;rare) tunnel, tunnel-like room</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyšьka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li><li>gut eater</li></ol><p><i>кишка</i> (kiška, “gut”) +&lrm; <i>глот</i> (glot, “eater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qishlaq, kishlak (a rural settlement of nomadic people)</li><li>(Central Asia,&#32;Afghanistan,&#32;etc.) village, hamlet, a small settlement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, cognates include Turkish <i>kışlak</i>, Azerbaijani <i>qışlaq</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallet (small maul)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kijanka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) keyboard</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>клавиату́ра</i> (klaviatúra) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harpsichord</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>clavecin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keyboard (<i>of a computer, a typewriter, or a piano</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klaviatur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) clavichord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keyboard instrument (piano, harpsichord, clavichord, etc.)</li><li>piano arrangement of an opera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klavier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dated form of <i>кла́виша</i> (kláviša)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key (on a keyboard, typewriter, musical instrument, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>klawisz</i>, from Latin <i>clāvis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) Any fungus of the genus <i>Clavulinopsis</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Clavulinopsis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure, hoard</li></ol><p>Related to <i>класть</i> (klastʹ). See there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cemetery, graveyard</li></ol><p><i>клад-</i> (klad-) +&lrm; <i>-бище</i> (-bišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cemetery, graveyard</li></ol><p><i>кла́дбище</i> (kládbišče) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) fount, treasury, storehouse (abundant, inexhaustible source of something, e.g. knowledge)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кладѧѕь</i> (kladędzĭ, “well”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòldędzь</i>; compare the inherited doublet <i>коло́дезь</i> (kolódezʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>меч-кладене́ц</i> (meč-kladenéc, “a mythical sword with special powers”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>&#42;кладѣньць</i> (&#42;kladěnĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koldenьсь</i> (“stabber, sword of stabbing type”). Related to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kladiwos</i> (“stabber, sword of stabbing type”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolti</i> (“to stub”). Native East Slavic *колоденьць &gt; Russian: *колоденец.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying (construction), placing</li><li>masonry, brickwork, walling</li><li>laying <i>(of eggs)</i>, hatch, clutch <i>(of eggs)</i></li></ol><p><i>клад-</i> (klad-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantry, storeroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кладовая</i> (kladovaja): (little) storeroom</li></ol><p>From <i>кладов(а́я)</i> (kladov(ája)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pantry, storeroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) designed for storing something</li><li>(relational,&#32;dated) load, cargo</li></ol><p><i>кладь</i> (kladʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storekeeper, warehouseman, stockman, storeman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warehousewoman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure-hunter</li></ol><p><i>клад</i> (klad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>иска́тель</i> (iskátelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>klaxon, horn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>klaxon</i> or <i>Klaxon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clan (group having common ancestor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clannish</li></ol><p><i>клан</i> (klan) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (to, before), to greet (to bend oneself as a gesture of respect or deference)</li><li>to give/send regards to someone</li><li>to cringe (before); to humiliate oneself (before); to humbly beg</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>кла́нять</i> (klánjatʹ, “to make bow”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klaňati</i>. Iterative of <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ), with unexpected stress shift and root vowel lengthening.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valve</li><li>vent, stop (<i>of a musical instrument</i>)</li><li>flap (<i>of clothes</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klappen</i>, plural of <i>Klappe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compressor valve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) clarinet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>clarinette</i>, from Italian <i>clarinetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarinetist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кларне́т</i> (klarnét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) ear of corn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>класъ</i> (klasŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolsъ</i>. Doublet of <i>ко́лос</i> (kólos), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>class</li><li>(sociology,&#32;economics) class</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) class</li><li>(education,&#32;school) grade (<i>United States</i>); form (<i>British</i>) (in primary and secondary school)</li><li>classroom</li><li>(programming,&#32;object-oriented programming) class</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) great!</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>classis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classic (an author of a classical work)</li><li>classicist</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) classic (a scholar of ancient Greek and Latin)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>classicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classic (lasting work, classical music)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) systematic list for classifying/designating/grouping objects</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) sizer, classifier (machine for classifying ore, coal, etc. by size)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) classifier (specialist in classification)</li></ol><p>From or in imitation of Latin; compare Italian <i>classificatore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to classify, to class (to identify or divide into classes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classicism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>classicisme</i> or German <i>Klassizismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>classical (literature, art, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>кла́сс(ика)</i> (kláss(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackboard (a surface that can be written upon with chalk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) classroom (room in a school)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cool, awesome, fantastic</li></ol><p><i>кла́ссный</i> (klássnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) class (in education)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awesome, super, top-class, cool, neat, nifty</li></ol><p><i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form master; homeroom teacher; teacher in charge of a class; class teacher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>class struggle (struggle between classes)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Klassenkampf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) class (social grouping, based on job, wealth, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cluster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cluster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to lay, to lay down, to deposit</li><li>to apply</li><li>to spend</li><li>to erect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klasti</i>; compare Old Church Slavonic <i>класти</i> (klasti) (first-person singular <i>кладѫ</i> (kladǫ)), Ukrainian <i>класти</i> (klasty), Belarusian <i>класці</i> (klasci), Serbo-Croatian <i>кла̏сти</i> (first singular <i>кла́де̄м</i>), Slovene <i>klásti</i> (first singular <i>kládem</i>), Czech <i>klást</i> (first singular <i>kladu</i>), Slovak <i>klаsť</i>, Polish <i>kłaść</i> (first singular <i>kładę</i>). More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>klóti</i> (“to lay, to cover”) (first singular <i>klóju</i>), Latvian <i>klāt</i> (“to cover”) (first singular <i>klāju</i>), Gothic <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hlaþan, “to load”), English <i>lade</i>, Old High German <i>hladan</i> (“to load”), German <i>laden</i> (“to load”). Not cognate with English <i>load</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claustrophobia</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>claustrophobia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clack, a clatter, a click, a tap</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clacking, clattering, clicking, tapping</li></ol><p><i>кла́цать</i> (klácatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clack, to clatter, to click, to tap</li></ol><p><i>клац</i> (klac) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peck, to pick (for birds)</li><li>to bite (in terms of biting a baited hook or other lure)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľьvati</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>кълва́</i> (kǎlvá), Belarusian <i>клюва́ць</i> (kljuvácʹ), Old Church Slavonic <i>клюıѫ</i> (kljuıǫ), Slovene <i>kljuváti</i>, Polish <i>kluć</i>, Ukrainian <i>клюва́ти</i> (kljuváty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod off, to be drowsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clover</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>клеверъ</i> (klever), itself borrowed in 16th or 17th c. from Middle Low German <i>klēver</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaibrā</i>. Adopted by botanists, displaced local names like Петров крест and дятловина; кашка and трилистник are still occasionally used.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slander, calumny, aspersion, libel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>клевета</i> (kleveta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kleveta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slander, to calumniate, to libel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>клеветати</i> (klevetati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klevetati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slanderer, calumniator</li></ol><p><i>клевета́ть</i> (klevetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanderous, libelous</li></ol><p>From <i>клеветн-</i> (klevetn-, “slanderer”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) minion, accomplice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>клеврѣтъ</i> (klevrětŭ), from Vulgar Latin <i>collivertus</i>, from Latin <i>collibertus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue, to paste, to gum</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to pick up <i>(a woman)</i></li></ol><p><i>клей</i> (klej, “glue”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to glue</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to go well (<i>usually in the negative</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to seduce, to lure</li></ol><p><i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klějь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sticky, tenacious (clinging to an object or surface; adhesive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gluten (cereal protein)</li></ol><p><i>кле́йкий</i> (kléjkij, “sticky”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenacity</li></ol><p><i>кле́йкий</i> (kléjkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stamp, to brand</li><li>to stigmatize</li></ol><p><i>клеймо́</i> (klejmó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brand, stamp, identification mark</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stain, taint as in tainted reputation</li><li>stigma</li></ol><p>Dialectally also <i>клейно́</i> (klejnó); cognate with Ukrainian <i>клеймо́</i> (klejmó), <i>клейно́</i> (klejnó) and Belarusian <i>кляймо́</i> (kljajmó), <i>кляйно́</i> (kljajnó). Per Vasmer, borrowed from unattested Old High German <i>&#42;kleim</i> (“glue, clay, mortar”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaim</i>.</p><p>(Only Old High German <i>kleimen</i> (“to smear, to oil”) is attested, but the vowel sequence -ей- is most easily derived from Old High German or Old Norse among the Germanic languages, and Vasmer specifically denies a derivation from Old Norse <i>kleima</i>.)</p><p>Compare Old English <i>clām</i> (“clay, mud”) (whence dialectal English <i>cloam</i> (“clay”)), Middle Low German <i>klēm</i> (“glue, mortar, plaster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat paste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kleister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cleistocactus (<i>Cleistocactus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cleistocactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sugarhouse (cabin or shack where sap collected from sugar maples is boiled into maple syrup)</li></ol><p><i>клён</i> (kljon) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) maple</li></ol><p><i>клён</i> (kljon) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rivet</li><li>to connect metal parts, to bending edges and flattening them</li><li>to hammer straight (a blade or iron strip), making it thinner and sharper</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to make, to manufacture</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander, to malign</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klepati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clepsydra (a water clock)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κλεψύδρα</i> (klepsúdra), from combining form of <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”) + <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kleptocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kleptomaniac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) kleptomania</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clerk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>clerc</i>, itself derived from Latin <i>clericus</i>. Attested since the 18th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cage, coop, cell</li><li>square, check <i>(checkered pattern)</i></li><li>(<i>biology</i>) cell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>клѣтъка</i> (klětŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klětъka</i>, from <i>&#42;klětь</i> / <i>&#42;klětъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka): (small) cell, cellule</li></ol><p><i>кле́тка</i> (klétka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cage, coop</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cell; cellular</li></ol><p><i>кле́тка</i> (klétka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>клеть</i> (kletʹ, “closet, storeroom, pantry”)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>клеть</i> (kletʹ, “closet, storeroom, pantry”)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) small, tight, living quarters</li></ol><p><i>клеть</i> (kletʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nutrition</i>) dietary fibre</li><li>(botany,&#32;engineering) cellulose</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cellular tissue</li></ol><p>From <i>кле́тчатый</i> (klétčatyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>checked</li><li>chequered, checkered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) the log walls framing and enclosing a room; cribbing</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) closet, larder, storeroom (a type of unheated storage room or small storage building in a traditional Russian architecture)</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) cage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klětь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw, nipper, pincers</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) hand</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;клѣщьня</i> (&#42;klěščʹnja), from <i>кле́щи</i> (kléšči) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tick, mite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klěščь</i>. Cognate with <i>кле́щи</i> (kléšči).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castor oil plant, castor bean tree, palmcrist</li></ol><p><i>клещ</i> (klešč) +&lrm; <i>-еви́на</i> (-evína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tick, mite</li></ol><p><i>клещ</i> (klešč) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pincers, nippers, tongs</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klěšči</i> (singular <i>&#42;klěšča</i>). Cognate with <i>клещ</i> (klešč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oilcloth, oilskin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oilcloth, oilskin</li></ol><p><i>клеёнка</i> (klejónka) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) jib sail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kluiver</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>client, customer</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) target of a killing, target of extortion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klient</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>customercentric</li></ol><p><i>клие́нт</i> (klijént, “client”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ориенти́рованный</i> (orijentírovannyj, “oriented”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) client, customer</li><li>(relational,&#32;computing) client</li></ol><p><i>клие́нт</i> (klijént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clientele</li></ol><p>From <i>клие́нт</i> (klijént) +&lrm; <i>-у́ра</i> (-úra). Compare Ukrainian <i>клієнту́ра</i> (klijentúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enema (injection of fluid into the rectum)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>klysma</i> or German <i>Klysma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shout, scream</li><li>mouseclick, click</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klikъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>click</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clique (small, exclusive group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to call (someone)</li><li>(computing,&#32;Internet) to click (to press the button on a mouse when using a computer)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klìkati</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>click</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;marketing) clickbait</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clickbait</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to call (<i>someone</i>)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to cry out</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to click (<i>a mouse or button</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klìknǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кли́кать</i> (klíkatʹ, “to call”) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p><p><i>кли́кать</i> (klíkatʹ, “to click”) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) click consonant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clicks</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whooper swan</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bait, lure <i>(animal left on the leash to lure predators into a trap)</i></li></ol><p><i>клик</i> (klik) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) nickname</li></ol><p>From <i>клик-</i> (klik-) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa), derived from <i>кли́чка</i> (klíčka, “nickname”) but using the underlying stem <i>клик-</i> (klik-), as found in <i>кли́кать</i> (klíkatʹ, “to call”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climacteric, menopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climate strike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>climatic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) climate</li></ol><p><i>кли́мат</i> (klímat) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climatotherapy</li></ol><p>From <i>кли́мат</i> (klímat) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лече́ние</i> (lečénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climatology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climatotherapy</li></ol><p>From <i>кли́мат</i> (klímat) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>терапи́я</i> (terapíja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedge</li><li>V formation (<i>of birds, aircraft, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) flying wedge</li><li>(agriculture,&#32;countable&#32;or&#32;collective) field(s) or an area of a field sown with one crop</li><li>(<i>cloth</i>) gusset</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) quoin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jam, jam-up (blockage of a mechanism)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stupor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klinъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clinic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clinical</li></ol><p><i>кли́н(ика)</i> (klín(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clinker (a very hard brick used for paving)</li><li>clinker (slag or ash produced by intense heat in a furnace)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klinker</i>, from Dutch <i>klinker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blade (<i>of a sword or dagger</i>)</li><li>bladed (having a sword-like or dagger-like blade)</li></ol><p><i>клино́к</i> (klinók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wedge-shaped</li></ol><p><i>клин</i> (klin) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blade (sharp-edged part of a sword, sabre, or dagger)</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) blade, sword, sabre, dagger</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) sabre-armed rider, cavalryman; swordsman (<i>as a counting unit of military strength</i>)</li></ol><p><i>клин</i> (klin) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be pivotal, to be caught in a cleft stick, to be in dire straits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuneiform (ancient writing system)</li></ol><p><i>клин</i> (klin, “wedge”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пис(ат)ь</i> (pis(at)ʹ, “to write”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>клино́к</i> (klinók)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) clinch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clinch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Klin</li></ol><p><i>Клин</i> (Klin) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music video</li><li>film clip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clip</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) clipper ship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clipper</i> or Dutch <i>klipper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) cleric</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>клирикъ</i> (klirikŭ), Ancient Greek <i>κληρῐκός</i> (klērĭkós) or <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearing (the process of completing and settling transactions centrally)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clearing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;architecture) choir, kliros (section of a church dedicated to the choir)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros). Connected by folk etymology with <i>кры́лос</i> (krýlos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choir brother</li></ol><p><i>кли́рос</i> (klíros) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enema, clyster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) clitoris</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>narratology</i>) cliffhanger (an ending or stopping point calculated to leave a story unresolved, in order to create suspense)</li></ol><p>From English <i>cliffhanger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call; appeal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name (<i>of an animal</i>)</li><li>nickname, alias</li></ol><p><i>кли́кать</i> (klíkatʹ, “to call”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cliché</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cliché</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cesspool, sink, sewer</li><li>foul place</li><li>cloaca</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleman's club</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>klobuk (monastic headgear)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;клобукъ</i> (&#42;klobukŭ), an early borrowing from a Turkic language. Cognate to <i>колпа́к</i> (kolpák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water closet, toilet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>water closet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuft, flock</li><li>(plural only "кло́чья") shred, rag, tatter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klakъ</i> (“tuft, clump”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gel-</i> (“to curl up, form into a ball”), see also Old High German <i>klakka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bubble, to boil</li><li><i>(emotions)</i> to boil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klokotati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloning</li></ol><p><i>клони́ровать</i> (klonírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline, to bend</li><li>to drive <i>(at)</i>, to get <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kloniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow, to bend</li><li>to tend <i>(to)</i>, to be aimed <i>(at)</i></li><li>to come close</li></ol><p><i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bedbug</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bug</li><li>(familiar,&#32;humorous) small child, person of short stature, squirt, shorty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klоръ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A damsel bug, a nabid.</li></ol><p><i>кло́п</i> (klóp, “bug”) +&lrm; <i>охо́тник</i> (oxótnik, “hunter”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clown</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) irresponsible person, who states unfounded claims, often thoughtless or careless</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>clown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clownery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) clown</li></ol><p><i>кло́ун</i> (klóun) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisp, scrap</li></ol><p>Diminutive from <i>клок</i> (klok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French hobo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>clochard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloister (a covered walk with an open colonnade on one side)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud, puff (<i>of smoke, etc.</i>)</li><li>a dense swarm (<i>of insects or other animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) lump, ball</li><li>club (association of members)</li><li>clubhouse</li><li>nightclub</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>клубъ</i> (klubŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klǫbъ</i>; cognate with Ukrainian <i>клуб</i> (klub), Czech <i>kloub</i>, Polish <i>kłąb</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Club</i>, from English <i>club</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klǫbьnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise clouds</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>клуб</i> (klub) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curl, to wreathe, to swirl</li></ol><p><i>клуби́ть</i> (klubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) tuberous, tuberal, tubery</li></ol><p>From <i>клу́бень</i> (klúbenʹ, “tuber”) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edible tuber, tuber plant used for food</li></ol><p><i>клу́бень</i> (klúbenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>плод</i> (plod)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strawberries (garden strawberry <i>Fragaria</i> × <i>ananassa</i>, green strawberry <i>Fragaria viridis</i>, or musk strawberry <i>Fragaria moschata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>клу́бень</i> (klúbenʹ, “tuber”) + -и́ка.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>клубни́ка</i> (klubníka), strawberry</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) erotica, porn</li></ol><p><i>клубни́ка</i> (klubníka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) strawberry</li></ol><p><i>клубни́ка</i> (klubníka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) club</li></ol><p><i>клуб</i> (klub) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball; clew (of thread)</li><li>glome, glomus</li></ol><p><i>клуб</i> (klub) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>клубо́к</i> (klubók, “ball”)</li></ol><p><i>клубо́к</i> (klubók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ball, clew (of thread)</li></ol><p><i>клубо́к</i> (klubók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) clusia (<i>Clusia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Clusia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flowerbed</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>klomb</i>. Possibly derived from English <i>clump</i> (“small group of trees or plants”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canine tooth</li><li>fang</li><li>tusk</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) dick, penis</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlykъ</i>. Cognate with <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ).</p><p>Compary typologically <i>би́вень</i> (bívenʹ) (&lt; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>клык</i> (klyk); fang, tusk, canine (tooth)</li></ol><p><i>клык</i> (klyk) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beak, bill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľuvъ</i>, which is cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľunъ</i>, whence Bulgarian <i>клюн</i> (kljun), Serbo-Croatian <i>кљун</i>, Slovene <i>kljun</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking stick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľuka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cranberry (plant)</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) cranberries (berries)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) ellipsis of <i>разве́систая клю́ква</i> (razvésistaja kljúkva); klyukva; ignorant stereotypes and tall tales, especially in Western depictions of Russia and Russians</li></ol><p>Of Proto-Slavic origin, the exact etymology is uncertain. A number of hypotheses have been proposed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cranberry bog</li><li>(animate,&#32;criminal slang) a thief stealing from church goers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cranberry</li></ol><p><i>клю́ква</i> (kljúkva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drink made by straining vodka through boiled cranberries</li><li><i>diminutive</i> of клюква (cranberry)</li></ol><p><i>клю́ква</i> (kljúkva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peck, to pick <i>(a bird)</i></li><li>to bite <i>(to bite a baited hook or other lure)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) foal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>клюсѧ</i> (kljusę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľusę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key</li><li>wrench, spanner, screw wrench</li><li>clue, key</li><li>(<i>music</i>) clef, key</li><li>radical (<i>in Chinese characters</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;usually with <i>в</i> (v)) way, vein (about the tone, character or manner of something)</li><li>spring, source (of water)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľučь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kljaukjas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klewk-yo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;kle(h₂)uk-</i>, an extended form of <i>&#42;(s)kleh₂w-</i> (“hook, key, peg”), whence English <i>clef</i> and <i>close</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keyword</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>key, crucial</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) spring (source of water)</li></ol><p><i>ключ</i> (ključ, “key, spring (source of water)”) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ключ</i> (ključ): (small) key</li></ol><p><i>ключ</i> (ključ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) clavicle, collar-bone</li></ol><p>Ultimately a calque of Latin <i>clavicula</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ключ</i> (ključ) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) housekeeper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hockey stick</li><li>golf club (implement)</li></ol><p>From <i>клюка́</i> (kljuká).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blot (a blemish, spot or stain made by a coloured&#47;colored substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Klecks</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beg (from someone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>klęczeć</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klęčati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gag (a device to restrain speech)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse, to damn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klęti</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>клѧти</i> (klęti), Ukrainian <i>клясти</i> (kljasty), Bulgarian <i>кълна</i> (kǎlna), Serbo-Croatian <i>клети</i>, Czech <i>klít</i>, Polish <i>kląć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vow, to swear (to make a vow)</li></ol><p><i>клясть</i> (kljastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath, vow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>клꙗтва</i> (kljatva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klętva</i> (“a type of oath”), per Brückner derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kloniti</i> (“to incline, bend”) due to touching the ground with a hand during this type of oath.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sacramentally</li><li>juratorily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) perjury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jade, nag (old or worn-out horse)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) hag, jade (old, ugly woman)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klęča, &#42;klęčь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peck</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>клева́ть</i> (klevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>клёвый</i> (kljóvyj): (<i>slang</i>) better, cleverest</li><li>comparative degree of <i>клёво</i> (kljóvo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cool, neat, well</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>cool!, fantastic!</li></ol><p><i>клёвый</i> (kljóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cool, excellent, awesome</li></ol><p>First recorded in 1820 by A. A. Uspensky in the Fenya argot of Vladimir with the meaning 'noble' (provided <i>клювый</i> (kljuvyj) in the "Suzdal dialect" by Pallas in 1787 is a misprint or typo instead of *кліовый). From the second half of the 19th century, it also appears in thieves' argot.</p><p>According to Preobrazhensky's suggestion, supported by Vasmer, it comes from the verb <i>клевать</i> (klevatʹ) (further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kľьvati</i>, from which originated, among others: Old Church Slavonic <i>кльвати</i> (klĭvati), perfective <i>клюнѫти</i> (kljunǫti), Ukrainian <i>клювати</i> (kljuvaty), Belarusian <i>клюваць</i> (kljuvacʹ), Bulgarian <i>кълва́</i> (kǎlvá, “I peck”), Serbo-Croatian <i>кљу̏jем</i>, <i>кљу̀вати</i>, Slovene <i>kljuváti</i>, <i>kljújem</i>, Old Czech <i>kl᾽uju</i>, <i>klvati</i>, Czech <i>klít</i> (from <i>&#42;kljuti</i>), Polish <i>kluć</i>, <i>klwać</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>kluwać sо</i> (“tease one another”), Lower Sorbian <i>kluju</i> (“I peel”), modern <i>kluwaś</i>). In this case, it may come from fishermen's jargon, with the original meaning '(good) pecking'.</p><p>According to an alternative hypothesis by Studinsky, supported by Bondaletov, from Greek kλέος 'glory, honor' (a noticeable number of Greek borrowings of not entirely clear origin are known in the Fenya argot). According to Priyomysheva, difficulties with the entry of the word into the literary language may testify in favour/favor of this version.</p><p>Cf. Dalya: <i>клюжий</i> (kljužij) (from <i>клюдь</i> (kljudʹ)), <i>клювый</i> (kljuvyj) 'good, good-looking, handsome, stately, prominent'.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scream, screech (of certain birds of prey)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) scream, cry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klekotъ</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klekati</i> (whence Old East Slavic <i>клекати</i> (klekati), 1sg. <i>клечꙋ</i> (kleču)). Probably onomatopoeic in origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;klenъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossbill (any of various finches of the genus <i>Loxia</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of dumpling with no filling, normally added to soups, kluski</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell-bottoms (pants that are flared from the knee downwards)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cloche</i> (“bell”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>клеть</i> (kletʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>клещ</i> (klešč).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>km, kilometer</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>киломе́тр</i> (kilométr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>km/h, kilometers per hour</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>киломе́тров в час</i> (kilométrov v čas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards the outside, towards the outer side of something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;inanimate) bollard</li><li>(historical,&#32;animate) knight, servant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Knecht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъňiga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) closed book, enigma, mystery</li></ol><p>Literally "a book behind seven seals", the expression stems from the biblical mention of a book sealed with seven seals in Revelation. See: Seven Seals</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookcrossing</li></ol><p>First attested in 2005. Blend of <i>кни́га</i> (kníga, “book”) +&lrm; <i>круговоро́т</i> (krugovorót, “circulation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliophile, book lover</li></ol><p><i>кни́га</i> (kníga) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люб(ить)</i> (ljub(itʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookseller</li></ol><p><i>кни́г(а)</i> (kníg(a)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>торгов(а́ть)</i> (torgov(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookselling</li></ol><p><i>кни́г(а)</i> (kníg(a)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>торго́вля</i> (torgóvlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bookselling</li></ol><p><i>кни́г(а)</i> (kníg(a)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>торг</i> (torg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliophile, book lover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кни́жка</i> (knížka): (small) book, booklet</li><li>diminutive of <i>кни́га</i> (kníga): (small) book, booklet</li></ol><p><i>кни́жка</i> (knížka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booklet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъňižica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>кни́га</i> (kníga, “book”)</li></ol><p><i>кни́га</i> (kníga, “book”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ща</i> (-íšča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book, booklet</li><li>notebook, passport</li><li>omasum, psalterium, the third compartment of the stomach of a ruminant</li></ol><p><i>кни́га</i> (kníga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bibliophile, book lover</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bookseller</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bookish person, person with only book knowledge</li><li>(biblical,&#32;archaic) scribe (expert in sacred texts)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;inanimate) wallet</li></ol><p><i>кни́га</i> (kníga) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) book</li><li>literary, bookish (appropriate to literature rather than everyday writing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кънижьнъ</i> (kŭnižĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>кни́га</i> (kníga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookshop, bookstore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookworm (any of various insects that infest books)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bookworm (an avid book reader)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>кни́га</i> (kníga)</li><li>pejorative of <i>кни́жка</i> (knížka)</li></ol><p><i>кни́га</i> (kníga) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>downward, down</li></ol><p><i>к</i> (k) +&lrm; <i>ни́зу</i> (nízu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtsey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Knickschen</i>, diminutive of <i>Knicks</i> (“curtsey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) cnoidal</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>cnoidal</i>, by analogy with <i>синусоида́льный</i> (sinusoidálʹnyj, “sinusoidal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>button (a mechanical device meant to be pressed with a finger)</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) button (an on-screen control that can be selected as an activator of an attached function)</li><li>drawing pin, thumbtack</li><li>press button, press stud, snap fastener</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Knopf</i>, with <i>-ка</i> (-ka) suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кно́пка</i> (knópka): (small) button (to press)</li></ol><p><i>кно́пка</i> (knópka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) button, snap fastener</li></ol><p><i>кно́пка</i> (knópka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip, knout, scourge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кнутъ</i> (knutŭ), from Old Norse <i>knútr</i> (“knot”). The original meaning was “knotty whip”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot and stick</li></ol><p>Literally, “whip and gingerbread”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>princess (the prince’s consort), duchess</li><li>daughter</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) bride in old wedding rituals</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъnęgyni</i>; compare Ukrainian <i>княги́ня</i> (knjahýnja), Serbo-Croatian <i>кнѐгиња</i>, Slovene <i>knegínja</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>). By surface analysis, <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja). <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ) is derived from Early Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuningu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningaz</i>, with з by the application of Slavic progressive palatalization, which was blocked by the following &#42;y in <i>&#42;kъnęgyni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) arctic raspberries, nagoonberries</li></ol><p>From <i>кня́жий</i> (knjážij, “princely”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prince, duke; prince's, duke's; princely</li></ol><p>From <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij), based on the original stem <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ); compare feminine <i>княги́ня</i> (knjagínja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>principality (a dominion or region ruled by a prince or princess)</li><li>duchy (a dominion or region ruled by a duke or duchess)</li></ol><p>From <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo), based on the original stem <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ); compare feminine <i>княги́ня</i> (knjagínja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule (a principality), to reign</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кънѧжити</i> (kŭnęžiti), equivalent to <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), based on the original stem <i>княг-</i> (knjag-) of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ); compare feminine <i>княги́ня</i> (knjagínja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young prince (the son of a prince); a duke's son</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>кнѧжичъ</i> (knęžičŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъnęžiťь</i> (“son of a prince”). By surface analysis, <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ич</i> (-ič).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince; duke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъnędzь</i>, borrowed from Germanic, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningaz</i>. The feminine form <i>княги́ня</i> (knjagínja, “princess”) has preserved the original, Germanic <i>g</i> sound.</p><p>Doublet of <i>ксёндз</i> (ksjondz), a borrowing from Polish, and <i>ко́нунг</i> (kónung).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ): princeling</li><li>(<i>bird</i>) azure tit</li></ol><p><i>князь</i> (knjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>к</i> (k): to, towards; by, for (a specified time); etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъ(n)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>co- (together, mutually, jointly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coagulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coagulate, coagulum (mass formed by means of coagulation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>koala (tree-dwelling marsupial that resembles a small bear)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female koala</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coalition (group of organizations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby koala, joey</li></ol><p><i>коа́ла</i> (koála) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coati</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobalt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobaltite (mineral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cobalt</li></ol><p><i>ко́бальт</i> (kóbalʹt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male dog</li><li>male wolf, fox, or bear</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving,&#32;of a man) stud, bed-hopper</li></ol><p>Related to Polish <i>przekobiały</i>. Originally referred to animal skin that has cross (пер-ко-) white (белый) lines. An archaic phrase: "Чёрного кобеля не отмоешь добела" meant that black dog (or another animal) with cross-white lines on its skin can't be washed to whiteness. Related to adjective Russian <i>перкобелый</i> (perkobelyj). Formant perko- "cross" works analogically with any other color.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merlin (<i>Falco columbarius</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kobza (old Ukrainian music instrument, a kind of lute)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ко́бза</i> (kóbza), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kopuŕ</i> via a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kobza player</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>кобза́р</i> (kobzár), influenced by -арь (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobra</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hand</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) synonym of <i>коза́</i> (kozá, “fire basket with burning fatwood in it, used for night fishing”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>cobra</i>, maybe via English or German.</p><p>Borrowed from Karelian <i>kopra</i> (“hollow of the hand”) or Veps <i>kobr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cobra</li></ol><p><i>ко́бра</i> (kóbra) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holster</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Compare Turkish <i>kubur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red-footed falcon</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́б(ец)</i> (kób(ec), “small hawk, Pernis apivorus (European honey buzzard)”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobьcь</i>. Per Vasmer, probably cognate with English <i>hawk</i>, Old High German <i>habuh</i> (“hawk”), Old Norse <i>haukr</i> (“hawk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mare</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) A fat woman.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobyla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filly (young female horse)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) workhorse (dependable hard worker)</li><li>locust, grasshopper</li><li>(<i>music</i>) bridge (<i>of a stringed instrument</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кобꙑлъка</i> (kobylŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobylъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кобы́ла</i> (kobýla) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>For the semantic evolution “(small) horse” &gt; “bridge of a stringed instrument”, compare to French <i>chevalet</i> and Portuguese <i>cavalete</i>, and further afield, Persian خرک (xarak) and Japanese <i>駒</i> (こま, koma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) augury, specifically through the flight of birds, ornithomancy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кобь</i> (kobĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobь</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>коба</i> (koba), <i>прокоба</i> (prokoba, “ill fate, portent”), Serbo-Croatian <i>коб</i>/<i>kob</i> (“fate”). Non-Slavic cognates include Old Norse <i>happ</i> (“good luck”), Old Irish <i>cob</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to be obstinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) smith, blacksmith</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovaľь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Коваль)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Koval, from Ukrainian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forged, hammered</li><li>coated with iron</li><li>shod, shoed (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>iron-heeled, hobnailed (<i>of a boot</i>)</li><li>terse (<i>of a style or rhyme</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guileful, insidious, perfidious, crafty, treacherous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>коварьнъ</i> (kovarĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovarьnъ</i>. Related to <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ), <i>ко́зни</i> (kózni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craft, craftiness, guile, perfidy, treachery, insidiousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metalworking</i>) to forge</li><li>(<i>farriery</i>) to shoe (<i>horses</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowboy</li></ol><p>Phonetic transcription of English <i>cowboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowboy hat (also ковбойская шляпа)</li><li>cowboy shirt, checkered shirt</li></ol><p><i>ковбо́й</i> (kovbój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cowboy</li></ol><p><i>ковбо́й</i> (kovbój) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mispronounce words and sounds</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;familiar) to break, to destroy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to recite one's opponent's speech with mistakes; to distort another's speech</li></ol><p><i>ко-</i> (ko-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>COVID</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>COVID-19 vaccination passport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;relational) COVID-19</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metalworking</i>) forging</li><li>smithery</li><li>(<i>farriery</i>) shoeing</li></ol><p>From <i>ковать</i> (kovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) malleable, ductile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loaf, a korovai bread</li></ol><p>The origin of this word is disputed. May be related to Finnish <i>kaura</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>кврга</i>, Turkish <i>gevrek</i> or Norwegian <i>kavring</i>. Known from written sources from the 12th century. Has cognates as e.g. Bulgarian <i>ковриг</i> (kovrig) and Romanian <i>covrig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey cake, gingerbread</li></ol><p><i>коври́га</i> (kovríga, “loaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ковёр</i> (kovjór): small rug, small carpet</li></ol><p><i>ковёр</i> (kovjór) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rug, carpet</li></ol><p><i>ковёр</i> (kovjór) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ark</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ковьчегъ</i> (kovĭčegŭ) from Middle Mongol <i>ᠬᠠᠤᠷᠴᠠᠭ</i> (qaurčag) presumably through a Turkic language. Compare Mongolian <i>хуурцаг</i> (xuurcag).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ark of the Covenant (the sacred container, a gold-plated wooden chest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoop, dipper, ladle</li><li>bucket <i>(of an excavator)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ковшь</i> (kovšĭ), borrowed from Lithuanian <i>káušas</i> (“ladle, dipper, big spoon”), derived from Lithuanian <i>káuti</i> (“hew, chop out”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;káuˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kowh₂-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ковш</i> (kovš): small ladle</li></ol><p><i>ковш</i> (kovš) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) intrigues, machinations</li></ol><p>From <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feathergrass (Any of various perennial hermaphroditic grasses of the genus <i>Stipa</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovylь</i>, <i>&#42;kovylъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hobble, to dodder, to toddle, to stump</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps a Finnic borrowing, compare Finnish <i>kävellä</i> (“to walk”), or a slangy term from Northwest Semitic related to Classical Syriac <i>ܟܒܠܐ</i> (kaḇlā, keḇlā, “clog, fetter”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>kovìtlati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;colloquial) poker, instrument to prick</li><li>(animate,&#32;slang) bulldagger</li></ol><p><i>ковыря́ть</i> (kovyrjátʹ, “to poke”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peck <i>(at)</i>, to pick <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tinker</li><li>to dig</li></ol><p>Native prefix derivative (compare <i>кове́ркать</i> (kovérkatʹ)) from <i>виря́ть</i> (virjátʹ, “to make holes, to make shoes”), with a change from и to ы as in <i>ковыля́ть</i> (kovyljátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rummage <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tinker, to mess about/around</li><li>to dig</li><li>to poke around</li><li>to pick one's nose</li></ol><p><i>ковыря́ть</i> (kovyrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rug, carpet</li></ol><p>Usually explained as an early Turkic borrowing (&lt; Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;köbüŕ</i>). Compare Slovak and Czech <i>koberec</i>, Polish <i>kobierzec</i>, archaic Bulgarian <i>губер</i> (guber).</p><p>Mikkola, quoted by Vasmer and Karl Brugmann, derives the Russian word from Old Norse <i>kǫgurr</i> (“quilt”). This derivation is difficult for phonetic and semantic reasons and fails to explain the Czech and Bulgarian cognates.</p><p>Unrelated to English <i>cover</i> or French <i>couvert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(aviation,&#32;fantasy,&#32;fiction) flying carpet, magic carpet (carpet capable of magical flight)</li></ol><p>From <i>ковёр</i> (kovjór) +&lrm; <i>самолёт</i> (samoljót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>circus</i>) entr'acte performer (typically, during a carpet change)</li></ol><p><i>ковёр</i> (kovjór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Pskov</i>) alternative form of <i>коза́</i> (kozá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>когда</i> (kogda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kogъda</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koda</i>, from which the following words also were derived: Old Church Slavonic <i>къгда</i> (kŭgda), Bulgarian <i>кога</i> (koga), Polish <i>kiedy</i>, <i>gdy</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>када</i> / <i>kada</i>, Slovene <i>kdaj</i>, Czech <i>kdy</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>gdy</i>. Originally the word was probably *koda; compare Lithuanian <i>kada</i> (“when”), Latvian <i>kad</i>, Old Indic and Sanskrit <i>कदा</i> (kadā), Avestan <i>kadā</i>. From *koda came *kъdа, possibly under the influence of *kъdе, kъtо. On the other hand, one may perceive in <i>когда́</i> (kogdá) the old instrumental singular *godō of the word <i>год</i> (god) (the old meaning of which was “time, period”) with the interrogative particle ко-, making a genitive singular form *kogo goda (which time?). Thus the -гда ending may have derived from the genitive of <i>год</i> (god, “period of time”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite adverb)</i> (at) some time or other, one day; ever (legalese, as intensifier)</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) ever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite adverb)</i> sometime (or other), one day</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) ever <i>(in the past)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once (upon a time), formerly; <i>constructions with</i> used to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coherence</li></ol><p>From <i>когере́нтн(ый)</i> (kogeréntn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coherer (a detector of radio waves used in very early radio receivers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognitive science</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cognitive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cohort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) diminutive of <i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ): small claw</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pincer, chela, insect claw</li></ol><p><i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw, talon</li><li>clutch, climbing iron</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kogъtь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keg-</i>, <i>&#42;keng-</i> (“peg; hook”). Compare Belarusian <i>ко́каць</i> (kókacʹ), Ukrainian <i>коготь</i> (kohotʹ), <i>кіготь</i> (kihotʹ), <i>когіть</i> (kohitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any dragonfly of the genus <i>Onychogomphus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ) +&lrm; <i>де́д</i> (déd) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scratching post</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>то́чка</i> (tóčka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ, “claw, talon”)</li></ol><p><i>ко́готь</i> (kógotʹ, “claw, talon”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morse code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>codeine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) codeine</li></ol><p><i>кодеи́н</i> (kodeín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) code</li><li>code <i>(of conduct)</i></li><li>codex (early manuscript book)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;electronics) coder, encoder (a device, program, or algorithm used to encode a signal)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;often&#32;derogatory) coder, code monkey (a computer programmer, usually an uninspired one who follows provided specifications or designs)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coder</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) encoding</li><li>(<i>psychotherapy</i>) coding</li></ol><p><i>коди́ровать</i> (kodírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to code, to encode, to encrypt</li><li>to give hypnotic/suggestive treatment <i>(for alcoholism, etc)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kodieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) or <i>код</i> (kod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coding</li><li>encoding</li></ol><p><i>коди́ровать</i> (kodírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;linguistics) to codify</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) gang (mostly minors, antisocial kids)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) code</li></ol><p><i>код</i> (kod) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(expresses indefiniteness) some <i>(implies that one knows who or what, but is deliberately refraining from naming who or what)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere, here and there, in some places</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anyhow, somehow, haphazardly, hit-or-miss, hurry-scurry, hugger-mugger, rough-and-ready, slapdash</li><li>with difficulty, with great difficulty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, any</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>off and on, now and then</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite pronoun</i>) a certain someone <i>(implies that one knows who it is, but is deliberately refraining from naming who)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here and there, (in)to some place(s), somewhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from somewhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>somebody's, someone's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>a certain something <i>(implies that one knows what it is, but is deliberately refraining from naming what)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a koekchuch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Itelmen.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to (hospital) beds</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́йка</i> (kójka, “hospital bed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin</li><li>leather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koža</i>. By surface analysis, <i>коза́</i> (kozá) +&lrm; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p><p>Compare typologicalyy <i>мех</i> (mex), <i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moysós</i>); <i>овчи́на</i> (ovčína) (<~ <i>овца́</i> (ovcá)); <i>лоси́ны</i> (losíny) (&lt; <i>лось</i> (losʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serotine (any of several small bats of the genus <i>Eptesicus</i>)</li><li>leather coat, leather jacket</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “leather, skin”). By surface analysis, <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an). Cognate with Belarusian <i>кажа́н</i> (kažán), Ukrainian <i>кажа́н</i> (kažán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) type of leather jacket common during the Russian Civil War</li></ol><p><i>ко́жаный</i> (kóžanyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leather</li></ol><p><i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leather manufacturing</li></ol><p><i>коже́вня</i> (kožévnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tannery</li></ol><p><i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-е́вня</i> (-évnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sensu stricto</i>) Any beetle of the genus <i>Dermestes</i>.</li><li>(<i>sensu lato</i>) Any beetle of the family Dermestidae, a skin beetle.</li></ol><p><i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”), a calque of translingual <i>Dermestes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skin-like, having a skin-like surface</li></ol><p><i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža): (thin) skin, pellicle, film; peel, rind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) skin; dermal, cutaneous</li></ol><p><i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rind, peel, skin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin coat</li><li>casing, housing, jacket <i>of a gun</i>, paddle-box, bonnet, boot, box, cabinet, cage, case, cover, encasement, hood, mantle, sheath, shell, shroud, shrouding</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kožuxъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “leather, skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ух</i> (-ux).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat, she-goat</li><li>(<i>fishing</i>) a fire basket with burning fatwood in it, used for fishing at night</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;informal) stupid and/or annoying woman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete spelling of <i>каза́к</i> (kazák): Cossack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tragus</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pessulus</li></ol><p><i>козёл</i> (kozjól, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild mountain goat, alpine ibex</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Козерог)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Capricorn (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a goat)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Capricorn (zodiac sign for the goat, covering December 22 – January 19)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) goat; goat’s</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kozьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>коза́</i> (kozá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gozinaki</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>козина́ки</i> (kozináki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gozinaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>გოზინაყი</i> (gozinaq̇i).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>козёл</i> (kozjól): kid, young goat, little goat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) he-goat</li></ol><p><i>козёл</i> (kozjól) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>козёл</i> (kozjól, “goat”)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) goat</li></ol><p>From <i>козёл</i> (kozjól) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče) (suffix-stressed) or <i>козёл</i> (kozjól) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) goatskin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) goat-legged</li></ol><p><i>козёл</i> (kozjól) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coach box, box (<i>on a horse-drawn vehicle</i>)</li><li>trestle, saw block, sawhorse</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) hayrack</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>kozły</i>, the plural of <i>kozioł</i> (“goat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chevon, goatmeat</li><li>cabrito, kid (meat of a young goat)</li></ol><p>From <i>козля́т-</i> (kozlját-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>козлёнок</i> (kozljónok, “kid, young goat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kid, young goat</li></ol><p><i>козёл</i> (kozjól) +&lrm; <i>-онок</i> (-onok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intrigues, machinations</li></ol><p>Plural of кознь (koznʹ, “intrigue, plot, machination”), further, borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>къзнь</i> (kŭznĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъznь</i>, from <i>&#42;kovati</i> (“to forge”), whence also Old Church Slavonic <i>ковати</i> (kovati, “to forge, to plot”) and Russian <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ, “to forge”). Akin to <i>кова́рный</i> (kovárnyj), <i>кова́рство</i> (kovárstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dorhawk, nightjar</li></ol><p>From <i>коза́</i> (kozá, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ, “to milk”), a calque of translingual <i>Caprimulgus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goatherd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing</i>) diminutive of <i>коза́</i> (kozá): a kid, a little goat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trump, trump card (playing card of that suit)</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) ace up one's sleeve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trump</li></ol><p><i>ко́зырь</i> (kózyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trump (to play a trump card)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to salute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flaunt [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>козыря́ть</i> (kozyrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trump</li></ol><p><i>ко́зырь</i> (kózyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trump</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) main argument, used in the right time to gain advantage</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) unrevealed or hidden advantage</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) high standing collar</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) or (<i>regional</i>) canopy over a horse-drawn cart, sleigh</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) or (<i>regional</i>) peak, visor</li></ol><p>Attested first from the year 1731, borrowed from Polish <i>kozyr</i>, also <i>kozer</i>, variants of <i>kozyra</i>, <i>kozera</i> (“trump; card-sharper; card-game; struggle, calamity”), a dialectal variant – already attested in the 16<sup>th</sup> century in the sense “trump” – of <i>kostera</i>, <i>kostyra</i> (“card sharper”), from <i>kość</i> (“die”), also <i>kozyrnik</i>, <i>kozernik</i> (“card-shark; fullam-player”).</p><p>Compare as another cardgame term that has passed into Russian from Polish: <i>шу́лер</i> (šúler, “card-sharp”).</p><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>козырь</i> (kozyrʹ) (att. 1641), <i>козирь</i> (kozirʹ). Origin unclear, but suggested to be connected to коза.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trump (to play a trump card)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to salute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flaunt [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ко́зырь</i> (kózyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cap) peak, visor</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) canopy, awning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>козырекъ</i> (kozyrek) (att. 1630), from Middle Russian <i>козырь</i> (kozyrʹ), further origin unclear. By surface analysis, <i>ко́зырь</i> (kózyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók). Akin to regional (Bialostok) Polish <i>kozyrek</i>, but that is quite likely a Russian borrowing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rollup, rollie (hand-rolled cigarette)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) adder, viper</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) booger, boogie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) bug, insect</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) little child, baby, toddler</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) booger, boogie</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>коза</i> (koza) +&lrm; <i>-яв</i> (-jav) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat, he-goat, billygoat</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) idiot, blockhead; jerk, bastard, ass</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;very&#32;offensive) an inmate who is an informer and collaborates with authorities</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;very&#32;offensive) homosexual (<i>usually passive</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) game of dominoes</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) buck, a leather-covered frame used for gymnastic vaulting</li><li>(<i>military</i>) stack of arms (<i>e.g. rifles</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>козьлъ</i> (kozĭlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kozьlъ</i> (“he-goat”), from <i>&#42;koza</i> (“she-goat”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьlъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scapegoat</li></ol><p>Interpretation of Biblical Hebrew <i>עֲזָאזֵל</i> (“azazél”) (Leviticus 16:8, 10, 26); rendered as <i>козёл для отпуще́ния</i> (kozjól dlja otpuščénija) in Lev. 16:26 of the Russian Synodal Bible, and as <i>козелъ, ѿпꙋщеный во ѿпꙋщенїе</i> (kozelŭ, otŭpuštenyj vo otŭpuštenije) in the Elizabeth Bible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coitus (sexual interaction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>coitus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) how, which, who, what</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>къи</i> (kŷi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъjь</i> (“who, what, which”), from <i>&#42;kъ</i> (“who”) + <i>&#42;-jь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kas</i> (“who”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“who”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) bunk, hammock</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>by extension</i>) berth in a passenger train</li></ol><li>(<i>healthcare</i>) hospital bed</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) cot, bed; any sleeping furniture for use by one person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kooi</i> or Middle Low German <i>kōje</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>cavea</i>. In sense of a normal bed when used in informal speech compare Swedish <i>krypa till kojs</i> and Norwegian Nynorsk <i>krypa til køys</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coyote</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship's cook</li><li>quiff (<i>hair style</i>)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) spinner (fairing over the hub of a propeller or turbofan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kok</i> (“cook”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>coq</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>coque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) coca</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ко́ка-ко́ла</i> (Kóka-kóla)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кока-кола)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coca-Cola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocaine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocainist</li></ol><p>From <i>кокаи́н</i> (kokaín) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p><i>кокаи́н</i> (kokaín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocainist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocaine addict</li></ol><p><i>кокаи́н</i> (kokaín) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cocarde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coquette (flirtatious woman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>flirtatiously</li><li>winsomely, fetchingly</li><li>skittishly</li></ol><p><i>коке́тливый</i> (kokétlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flirtatious, coquettish (having a tendency to flirt often)</li><li>winsome</li><li>smart, fetching</li><li>skittish</li></ol><p><i>коке́т(ка)</i> (kokét(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coquet, to flirt</li><li>to pose, to show off, to flaunt</li></ol><p><i>коке́тка</i> (kokétka) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coquetry</li></ol><p>From <i>коке́тка</i> (kokétka) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) metal, mold, chill mold (collapsible mold for casting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coquille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whooping cough</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coqueluche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bump off, to kill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cocon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) lasiocampid</li></ol><p><i>ко́кон</i> (kókon, “cocoon”) +&lrm; <i>прясть</i> (prjastʹ, “to spin”, verb), literally “cocoon-spinner”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut (tree&#59; nut)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kokos</i>, from Portuguese <i>cocos</i>, plural of <i>coco</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut palm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coconut</li></ol><p><i>коко́с</i> (kokós) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut (fruit of coco palm)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocotte, courtesan, promiscuous woman, prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kokoshnik (a traditional Russian headdress worn by women and girls to accompany the sarafan)</li></ol><p>From the obsolete (now dialectal) word <i>ко́кошь</i> (kókošʹ, “hen, rooster”) + <i>-ник</i> (-nik), from Old East Slavic <i>кокошь</i> (kokošĭ), due to its resemblance to the crest of the bird.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coke (solid residue from roasting coal in a coke oven)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Koks</i>, from English <i>coke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coke (solid residue from roasting coal in a coke oven)</li></ol><p><i>кокс</i> (koks) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocktail</li><li>milkshake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cocktail</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Molotov cocktail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kokutai (the national essence and character of Japan viewed as conforming to Shinto-Confucian ideals, with the emperor as its ethical, spiritual, cultural and political center)</li><li>kokutai (the ideology centered on the principle of kokutai)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>国体<rp>(</rp><rt>こくたい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>kokutai</i>, literally “national character”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) coccinellid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Coccinellidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake, spicket, pale</li><li>one (<i>on a 5-point scale</i>), F (worst possible mark&#47;grade in school)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Kelly's eye, one in the lotto game</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>количество</i> (količestvo)</li></ol><p>From количество.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) Coca-Cola</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) cola</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cola</i>, from a Niger-Congo language.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кола)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kola (river on the Kola Peninsula, Russia)</li><li>Kola (town on the Kola Peninsula, Russia)</li><li>Kola railway station</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) Kola Peninsula</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Кола</i> (Kola), from Finnish <i>Kuola</i>, also <i>Kuolla</i>, from Kildin Sami <i>Куэлнэгк</i> (Kuelnegk); compare Northern Sami <i>Guoládat</i>, Skolt Sami <i>Kuâlõk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1956 spelling of <i>кала́ч</i> (kaláč).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retort, bottle</li><li>envelope, flask <i>(of a lamp)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kolben</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) Erlenmeyer flask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sausage</li><li>large sausage (edible uncooked)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) penis</li></ol><p>From the Old East Slavic <i>колбаса</i> (kolbasa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlbasa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something intensively - to hit, to beat, to strike; to knock; to work (hard), to work one's ass off; to move, to walk (a long distance); to play an instrument; to shake; to fall (of rain or snow) (<i>replaces a number of verbs</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>колбаса́</i> (kolbasá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>колбаса́</i> (kolbasá): (small) sausage, wurst</li></ol><p><i>колбаса́</i> (kolbasá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sausage shop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) The emigration of people from ex-USSR countries for purely material reasons, especially in the 1980s and 1990s.</li></ol><p>Literally, “sausage emigration”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sausage</li></ol><p><i>колбаса́</i> (kolbasá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laboratory flask</li></ol><p><i>ко́лба</i> (kólba) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cone <i>(photosensitive cell in retina)</i></li></ol><p>Diminutive from колба</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantyhose, tights</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>kalhotky</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>caliga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) whipstaff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kolderstok</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pothole, rut, bump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conjure, to practise witchcraft</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to concoct</li></ol><p>Vasmer derives this word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call”), cf. Latvian <i>kalada</i> (“noise, quarrel”). He rejects connections to Hungarian <i>koldulni</i> (“to beg”) and Ancient Greek <i>Χαλδαῖος</i> (Khaldaîos, “Chaldean”), otherwise surfacing in Russian as <i>халде́й</i> (xaldéj, “Chaldean; soothsayer”), although the latter is very widespread, e.g. Ge'ez <i>ከላዴዎን</i> (käladewon, “magician”), and the Russian word family is not even attested before the 16th century, occasional Ukrainian relatives being borrowed from Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>witch's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) witchcraft</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) magic, magical</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mysterious</li></ol><p><i>колд-</i> (kold-) +&lrm; <i>-овско́й</i> (-ovskój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic, sorcery, witchcraft</li></ol><p><i>колдова́ть</i> (koldovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard, sorcerer, magician, warlock</li></ol><p><i>колдова́ть</i> (koldovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oscillation, vibration</li><li>fluctuation, variation</li><li>hesitation, vacillation, wavering</li></ol><p><i>колеба́ть</i> (kolebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to shake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колѣбати</i> (kolěbati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolěbati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oscillate, to vibrate, to vacillate, to sway</li><li>to fluctuate, to vary</li><li>to hesitate, to waver</li></ol><p><i>колеба́ть</i> (kolebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crankshaft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коле́но</i> (koléno): knee</li></ol><p><i>коле́но</i> (koléno) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) calico (rough cotton cloth)</li><li>(<i>textiles</i>) buckram (coarse muslin stiffened with starch, size, or glue, used in bookbinding or as backing for garments)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thing, business</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from French <i>calencar</i>, ultimately from Persian. Alternatively, although phonologically less likely, perhaps borrowed from French <i>calicot</i>. Ushakov hypothesizes that the term is a blend of the two French borrowings.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) calico (rough cotton cloth)</li></ol><p><i>коленко́р</i> (kolenkór) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) knee</li></ol><p><i>коле́но</i> (koléno, “knee”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) knee</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) lap</li><li>bend <i>(in a river, etc.)</i></li><li>(<i>music</i>) figure, part, passage, turn <i>(in music or dance)</i></li><li>generation, branch</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) tribe</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) joint, elbow, crank <i>(pipes, tubing, rods)</i></li><li>(<i>botany</i>) joint, node</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колѣно</i> (kolěno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolěno</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>коліно</i> (kolino), Bulgarian <i>коляно</i> (koljano), Slovene <i>koléno</i>, Czech <i>koleno</i>, Polish <i>kolano</i>; compare Lithuanian <i>kelis</i> (“knee”), Ancient Greek <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “member of the body”), which may be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to bend, crooked”). Or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn, wheel”), according to Pokorny.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genuflection, kneeling</li></ol><p><i>коле́но</i> (koléno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>преклоне́ние</i> (preklonénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genuflected, kneeling, on one's knees</li></ol><p>From <i>колено</i> (koleno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>преклонённый</i> (preklonjónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>коле́но</i> (koléno):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>joint, link, segment</li><li>bend</li><li>(<i>music</i>) figure, motive</li><li>(<i>dance</i>) turn, move</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) caper, trick</li></ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) acute bend (<i>e.g. in the facial nerve in the medial wall of the middle ear</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) articulated (consisting of one or more joints or bends)</li></ol><p><i>коле́но</i> (koléno, “joint, bend”) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crankshaft (a rotating shaft that drives a crank)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) coleopter, coleopteran</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κολεόπτερος</i> (koleópteros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coleopterology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any casebearer moth of the genus <i>Coleophora</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Coleophora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>painting</i>) colour/color</li><li>(<i>veterinary medicine</i>) staggers <i>(of horses)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kolor</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>ко́лір</i> (kólir), Belarusian <i>ко́лер</i> (kóljer).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Koller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detour</li><li>to drive around (<i>a lot or in different directions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheelwright</li></ol><p><i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Колесник)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an occupational surname, Kolesnik</li></ol><p>From <i>колесник</i> (kolesnik, “wheelwright”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol><p><i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheel</li><li>(<i>acrobatics</i>) cartwheel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колеса</i> (kolesa), plural of <i>коло</i> (kolo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolo</i> (oblique: <i>&#42;koles-</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷekʷlom</i> (“circle”), <i>&#42;kʷékʷlos</i> (“circle”) whence English <i>wheel</i> and <i>cycle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) coleus (<i>Coleus</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Coleus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakfast cereal (as in O's). For example, шоколадные колечки</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кольцо́</i> (kolʹcó)</li></ol><p><i>кольцо́</i> (kolʹcó) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) rut (<i>mark of wheels on the ground</i>)</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) gauge (<i>the distance between the rails of a line of railway track</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolěja</i>. Related to <i>&#42;kolo</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>коловоз</i> (kolovoz), Czech <i>kolej</i>, Polish <i>koleina</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>колотраг</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Galician <i>rodeira</i>, <i>rodal</i>, <i>rodada</i>, <i>rilleira</i>, Spanish <i>rodera</i> (<… Latin <i>rota</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) if (supposing that)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) when</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolě</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hummingbird</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>colibri</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coliving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coliving</i>. Since 2019?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) colic (severe pains that grip the abdomen or the disease that causes such pains)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) what number, which (<i>in a numbered series</i>), how manyth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коликъ</i> (kolikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolikъ</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>коликъ</i> (kolikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) cardinal number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quantitative</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) cardinal</li></ol><p><i>коли́чество</i> (kolíčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quantity, number, amount</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>количьство</i> (količĭstvo), from <i>колико</i> (koliko) +&lrm; <i>-ьство</i> (-ĭstvo), which is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ποσότης</i> (posótēs). Compare Latin <i>quantitas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caustic, biting, mordant, cutting, acrimonious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>poignantly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collaborator (person who cooperates traitorously with an enemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) collaborationism (the act of collaborating, especially with an enemy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>collaborationnisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collaborator (person who cooperates traitorously with an enemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collaboration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>collaboration</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>collage</i>. Doublet of <i>коллаге́н</i> (kollagén), <i>колло́ид</i> (kollóid), and <i>протоко́л</i> (protokól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;astronomy,&#32;figurative) collapse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kollaps</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colleague (fellow member of a profession)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>collega</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joint, collective</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kollegial</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) collegium, government department</li><li>college (group sharing common purposes or goals)</li><li>board, committee</li><li>session, sitting (<i>of a board or committee</i>)</li><li>association (professional body)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>collēgium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>college</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>college</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) collegiate (relating to a collegium)</li></ol><p><i>колле́гия</i> (kollégija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) collegiate secretary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) collegiate councillor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collective, group</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>collēctīvus</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>сбо́рище</i> (sbórišče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collectivization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collectivism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>collectively (in a collective manner)</li></ol><p><i>коллекти́вный</i> (kollektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>collective, joint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electrical engineering</i>) collector, commutator</li><li>distributing centre for libraries</li><li>manifold <i>(gas or liquid leading channel)</i></li><li>debt collector</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>collector</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collector (e.g. of stamps, books, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collecting</li></ol><p><i>коллекциони́ровать</i> (kollekcionírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect <i>(hobby)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) collision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colloid</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>коллаге́н</i> (kollagén), <i>колла́ж</i> (kolláž), and <i>протоко́л</i> (protokól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A midterm exam conducted mainly by a university.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>colloquium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) kolobok (a round loaf)</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) a plump and round person of a short height; anyone resembling a character in the well-known East Slavic fairy-tale Kolobok</li></ol><p>Most likely from <i>ко́лоб</i> (kólob, “a ball, a lump (dialectal)”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, “little thing”); then inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колобъ</i> (kolobŭ), possibly, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolbъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander (to move without purpose or specified destination)</li><li>to make a noise or to make mischief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, loafer (idle person)</li><li>fidgety person, fidget</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hand) brace, crank, auger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolovortъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>калаўро́т</i> (kalawrót) and Ukrainian <i>коло́ворот</i> (kolóvorot). Doublet of <i>коловра́т</i> (kolovrát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) bit brace</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) whirl, swirl</li><li>(paganism,&#32;neologism) swastika or kolovrat; specifically, a neo-pagan symbol with eight legs similar to swastika</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>коловратъ</i> (kolovratŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolovortъ</i>. Doublet of inherited <i>коловоро́т</i> (kolovorót).</p><p>Revived by neo-pagan leader Alexey Dobrovolsky in the 1990s in reference to the symbol he made up.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotifer</li></ol><p><i>коловра́т</i> (kolovrát, “whirl, swirl”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), a calque of translingual <i>Rotifera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) changeableness, inconstancy</li></ol><p>From <i>коловра́т</i> (kolovrát) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block, log</li><li>trough</li><li>pack, deck of playing cards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колода</i> (koloda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) well</li></ol><p><i>коло́дезь</i> (kolódezʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) well</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колодязь</i> (kolodjazĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koldędzь</i>. Early borrowing from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaldingaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaldiǭ</i>, whence Old Norse <i>kelda</i> (“source”), Danish <i>kilde</i> (“source”), Finnish <i>kaltio</i> (“source”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>кла́дезь</i> (kládezʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well (hole sunk into the ground)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koldьcь</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;koldędzь</i> (whence <i>коло́дезь</i> (kolódezʹ)) with change of suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slab</li><li>last, boot-tree (tool for footwear)</li><li>shoe</li><li>ribbon</li><li>wire terminal</li><li>yoke</li><li>(<i>mechanical engineering</i>) brake shoe</li></ol><p><i>коло́да</i> (kolóda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove (mostly near a plain)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) pin (of a string instrument)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tuner, machine head (of a string instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell</li><li>bell-shaped curve (line in a graph that smoothly rises then falls)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolkolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bell-shaped</li></ol><p><i>ко́локол</i> (kólokol) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bell</li></ol><p><i>ко́локол</i> (kólokol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell tower, belfry</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́локол</i> (kólokol) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell, handbell</li><li>bluebell, bellflower, campanula</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) glockenspiel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) RCA connector</li></ol><p><i>ко́локол</i> (kólokol) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cart grease</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́ло</i> (kólo, “wheel (archaic)”) + <i>мазь</i> (mazʹ, “ointment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kolomna (city in Russia, near Moscow)</li></ol><p><i>Коло́мна</i> (Kolómna) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonialism (colonial domination policy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colonial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonist, colonizer</li></ol><p>From <i>колониз(и́ровать)</i> (koloniz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonization (process of establishing a colony)</li><li>colonization, seizure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to colonise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonist, settler (someone who settles in a new location, especially one who makes a previously uninhabited place his home)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colony, settlement</li><li>corrective colony, penal colony, detention centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коло́нна</i> (kolónna)</li><li>column (<i>of figures, print, etc.</i>)</li><li>a device for dispensing liquids or gases, usually cylindrical in shape</li><li>gas water heater</li><li>loudspeaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>column, pillar (upright supporting beam)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) column of troops</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>columna</i>; cf. French <i>colonne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonnade (a series of columns)</li></ol><p>From French <i>colonnade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>columned</li></ol><p><i>коло́нна</i> (kolónna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberian weasel</li><li>kolinsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Colorado beetle.</li><li>(<i>offensive</i>) Pro-Russian separatist in Donetsk/Luhansk in Eastern Ukraine.</li><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) a Russian ultranationalist.</li></ol><p>For the sense of "Russian nationalist", it is from the similarity of the beetle's colours to the Ribbon of Saint George, which is nowadays regarded as a symbol of Russian nationalism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potato beetle, Colorado beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) coloratura</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coloratura</li></ol><p>From <i>колорату́р(а)</i> (koloratúr(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colouring/coloring, colouration/coloration; flavour/flavor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colorful, picturesque, vivid</li></ol><p><i>колори́т</i> (kolorít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear (fruiting body of a grain plant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kȏlsъ</i>. Related to <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in the ear, to form ears</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolsiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ко́лос</i> (kólos) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ко́лос</i> (kólos, “ear (<i>of corn, etc.</i>)”)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) spikelet</li></ol><p><i>ко́лос</i> (kólos) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colossus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colossal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock, to strike, to bang</li><li>to drum</li><li>to beat, to thrash</li><li>to break</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to shake, to make someone shudder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kōltìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat <i>(against)</i>, to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat</li></ol><p><i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;possibly&#32;dialectal) chill, fever, shivering</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;possibly&#32;dialectal) extreme cold</li></ol><p>Most likely from <i>колотить</i> (kolotitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden hammer, mallet, beetle</li><li>rattle, clapper, watchman's stick</li><li>hit, slap</li></ol><p><i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave, to break <i>(sugar)</i>, to crack <i>(nuts)</i>, to chop <i>(firewood)</i></li><li>to stab, to thrust</li><li>to kill, to slaughter</li><li>to prick, to sting</li><li>to have a stitch, to feel a prick or a stab</li><li>to taunt</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to inject</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòlti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chip, to break, to crack</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to spill the beans, to spill</li><li>to prick, to sting</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to shoot <i>(up)</i> <i>(inject drugs)</i>, to get a fix, to get a shot</li></ol><p><i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high-crowned cap: cook cap, calpack, kalpak, fool's cap, nightcap etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колпакъ</i> (kolpakŭ), derived from a Turkic source. Compare Ukrainian <i>ковпа́к</i> (kovpák) or <i>ковпи́к</i> (kovpýk).</p><p>Compare Bashkir <i>ҡалпаҡ</i> (qalpaq), Tatar <i>калфак</i> (qalfaq), Kazakh <i>қалпақ</i> (qalpaq), Uzbek <i>qalpoq</i>, Turkish <i>kalpak</i> (“cap”) etc. Ultimately from Middle Persian <i>kwlʾp̄k'</i> (kulāfak, “little cap; calyx”), diminutive of <i>kwlʾp̄</i> (kulāf, “cap, bonnet”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bell-shaped, bell-type</li></ol><p><i>колпа́к</i> (kolpák) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>колпа́к</i> (kolpák): small high-crowned cap: cook cap, kalpak, fool's cap, nightcap, etc.</li><li>cap, curfew, capsule, hood</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) diaphragm (contraceptive)</li><li>(<i>mycology</i>) early morel</li></ol><p><i>колпа́к</i> (kolpák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any snake of the genus <i>Kolpophis</i>, the only species sometimes considered valid being <i>Kolpophis annandalei</i> (syn. <i>Hydrophis annandalei</i>).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Kolpophis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>densely matted hair, Polish plait</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>каўтун</i> (kawtun), Ukrainian <i>ковтун</i> (kovtun), and Polish <i>kołtun</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>columbarium (a building for public storage of cinerary urns containing cremated remains)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>columbārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Colombian</li><li>Columbian</li></ol><p><i>Колу́мбия</i> (Kolúmbija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splitting maul, splitting axe; froe</li></ol><p><i>кол(оть)</i> (kol(otʹ), “to chop, split”) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) kolkhoz, collective farm</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) crowd</li><li>unprofessional alteration, repair or construction</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>коллекти́вное хозя́йство</i> (kollektívnoje xozjájstvo, “collective farm, household”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collective farmer, kolkhoznik, redneck</li></ol><p>From <i>колхо́з</i> (kolxóz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kolkhoz</li></ol><p><i>колхо́з</i> (kolxóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of hat used in Alexander Kolchak's army</li></ol><p><i>Колча́к</i> (Kolčák) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(folk,&#32;history) Kolchakovshchina, or Kolchak Era (the White Guard regime in Siberia, the Far East and the Urals, 1918-1920)</li></ol><p><i>Колча́к</i> (Kolčák) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina). From the surname of the White Guard Admiral Alexander Kolchak.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>колчан</i> (qolçan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) sulfide or arsenide of a metal (<i>especially about different pyrites</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Anatolian. Compare Hebrew <i>כדכד</i>, Old Armenian <i>կարկեհան</i> (karkehan), Latin <i>chalcēdōnius</i>, Ancient Greek <i>χαλκηδών</i> (khalkēdṓn), Arabic كركند. Compare also French <i>calcédoine</i> and English <i>chalcedony</i>. Doublet of <i>халцедон</i> (xalcedon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cradle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolybělь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>колеба́ть</i> (kolebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lullaby (a soothing song to lull children to sleep)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>колыбе́льная пе́сня</i> (kolybélʹnaja pésnja, “cradle song”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cradle</li><li>(relational,&#32;figurative) infant; infant's, infantile</li></ol><p><i>колыбе́ль</i> (kolybélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart, wagon, carriage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jalopy (old, dilapidated car)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>колымо́гъ</i> (kolymógŭ), <i>колимо́гъ</i> (kolimógŭ), <i>колыма́гъ</i> (kolymágŭ), <i>колима́гъ</i> (kolimágŭ), <i>колъма́гъ</i> (kolŭmágŭ), <i>колима́га</i> (kolimága, “wagon, cart; tabernacle, tent”), a Mongolic borrowing seemingly identical to the name of the Kalmyks named after their ancestors’ wagon forts or covered wagons which fell into disuse after the Mongol invasions.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;slang) homemade four-beam gasoline-powered light made from a tin can</li></ol><p><i>Колыма́</i> (Kolymá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kolyma</li></ol><p><i>Колыма́</i> (Kolymá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of Kolyma (region)</li></ol><p><i>Колыма́</i> (Kolymá) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rhythmically sway, to rock gently</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to excite, to emotionally move</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolyxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to heave, to sway, to wave, to flutter (<i>of flags</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flicker (<i>of a flame</i>)</li></ol><p><i>колыха́ть</i> (kolyxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rhythmically sway, to rock gently</li></ol><p><i>колыха́ть</i> (kolyxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heave, to sway, to wave, to flutter (of flags)</li><li>to flicker (of a flame)</li></ol><p><i>колыхну́ть</i> (kolyxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peg, prop, stake</li></ol><p><i>кол</i> (kol) +&lrm; <i>-ышек</i> (-yšek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ко́ли</i> (kóli)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>collier</i>. Several steps omitting from Late Latin <i>collāre</i> &lt; Latin <i>collāris</i> &lt; Latin <i>collum</i> + <i>-āris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick, to sting</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to have a stitch, to feel a prick or a stab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>вагинопла́стика</i> (vaginoplástika)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kohlrabi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kola (<i>peninsula, river or town</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Ко́ла</i> (Kóla) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colt revolver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annular</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) ring; circular</li></ol><p><i>кольцо́</i> (kolʹcó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) ring, annulus</li><li>hoop</li><li>webring</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) roundabout (a road junction at which traffic streams circularly around a central island)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>кольце</i> (kolĭce), a diminutive from <i>коло</i>&nbsp;n (kolo, “wheel, circle”) +&lrm; <i>-ьце</i> (-ĭce, suffix forming diminutives of neuters), the former from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kȍlo</i> (“wheel”), whence Russian <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó, “wheel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain mail, hauberk</li></ol><p>From <i>кольцо́</i> (kolʹcó) +&lrm; <i>-уга</i> (-uga), or possibly borrowed from Polish <i>kolczuga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbed wire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prickliness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandbur (<i>Cenchrus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>колю́чий</i> (koljúčij, “thorny”) +&lrm; <i>щети́на</i> (ščetína, “bristle, stubble”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorny, spiny, prickly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) biting, cutting</li></ol><p><i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ю́чий</i> (-júčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prickle, prick, thorn</li></ol><p><i>колю́чий</i> (koljúčij) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stickleback, tittlebat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) an extinct Christmas folk tradition, featuring people going around singing Christmas carols and receiving gifts</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;countable) Christmas Eve, Christmas or the Twelve Days of Christmas</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolęda</i>, from Latin <i>kalendae</i>. Doublet of <i>кале́нды</i> (kaléndy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas carol</li></ol><p><i>коляда́</i> (koljadá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby carriage, stroller, perambulator, pram</li><li>carriage (and horse), barouche</li><li>wheelchair</li><li>sidecar, buddy seat</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolo</i>.</p><p>According to Vasmer, this word was documented in Old Russian since 1695 as каслика. It is cognate with Polish <i>kolasa</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>коло</i> (kolo, “a wheel”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó)</li><li>small wheel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wheel</li><li>wheeled</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolesьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>коле́но</i> (koléno).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lump, clod</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;komъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, order</li><li>team, crew, party</li><li>(<i>military</i>) detachment</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) crew, ship's company</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) team</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commande</i> or German <i>Kommando</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) army commander</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>кома́ндующий а́рмией</i> (komándujuščij ármijej).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) commander, commanding officer</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) boss, chief, Cap (term of address to a man)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commandeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send on an official/business trip, to send on a mission [an assignment]</li></ol><p>Borrowed around 1600 from German <i>kommandieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), from French <i>commander</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кома́нда</i> (kománda) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business trip, assignment, mission</li></ol><p><i>командирова́ть</i> (komandirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) business trip</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) assignment (position, job)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) man on a business trip or assignment</li></ol><p><i>командиро́вка</i> (komandiróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tanks</i>) commander’s cupola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>officer's, commander's</li></ol><p><i>команди́р</i> (komandír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) command</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) team</li><li>commanding, preeminent, dominant</li></ol><p><i>кома́нда</i> (kománda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, commanding (action)</li></ol><p><i>кома́ндовать</i> (komándovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to give commands, to be in command (of) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, e.g. a fleet or army’</small>]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to command, to give orders to [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) to boss around, to order around, to dictate to [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to command, to hold a commanding (<i>i.e. high</i>) position [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘over something, e.g. a city or plain’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knight commander, commander; commodore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Comanche</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comanche language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosquito</li><li>gnat</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) hollow punch, punch press</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) nibbling machine, blank-cutting machine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;komarъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mosquito</li></ol><p><i>кома́р</i> (komár) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) food processor, processor</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) coal cutter-loader, coal-plough machine</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) combine, combine harvester, harvester, picker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>combine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) battalion commander</li><li>(<i>military</i>) battery commander</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>команди́р батальо́на</i> (komandír batalʹóna) or <i>команди́р батаре́и</i> (komandír bataréi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combatant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>combattant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) kombed (one of the Committees of Poor Peasants)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>комите́т бедноты́</i> (komitét bednotý).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combine (enterprise)</li><li>industrial complex</li><li>center/centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) schemer</li><li>(<i>computer science</i>) combiner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combination</li><li>scheme, system</li><li>(sports,&#32;politics) manoeuvre</li><li>slip (women’s undergarment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slip (women's undergarment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overalls</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>combinaison</i> (“overalls”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combined, combination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to combine, to arrange</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scheme, to contrive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kombinieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) combretum (<i>Combretum</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Combretum</i>, from Latin <i>combrētum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) divisional commander</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>команди́р диви́зии</i> (komandír divízii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) comedy; comic</li></ol><p><i>коме́дия</i> (komédija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comedy dramatist, comedy playwright</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comedy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt, base</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;komъ</i>. Related to <i>ком</i> (kom) and <i>комо́лый</i> (komólyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandant <i>(of a fortress or town)</i></li><li>superintendent <i>(of a building)</i></li></ol><p>From Early New High German <i>Kommendant</i>. First recorded in 1700 under Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commandant's, superintendent's</li></ol><p><i>коменда́нт</i> (komendánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curfew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandant's wife</li></ol><p><i>коменда́нт</i> (komendánt) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandant's office</li></ol><p>From German <i>Kommandantur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κομήτης</i> (komḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) comet</li></ol><p><i>коме́та</i> (kométa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a Komi, a Zyrian (a member of a Finno-Ugric people who live in the northwest Urals)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) Komi (the Finno-Ugric language spoken by these people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Komi-Zyrian <i>коми</i> (komi).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Коми)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>Респу́блика Ко́ми</i> (Respúblika Kómi, “Komi Republic”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Komi (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comic element</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comedian, comic actor</li><li>humorist, wit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comic, comic book, comic strip (a cartoon story)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>comics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comme il faut (properly&#59; in keeping with etiquette or social standards)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>comme il faut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) commissar</li><li>commissioner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commissariat (any public administrative agency headed by a commissar, комиссар)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agent, broker, factor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) commission shop (type of secondhand store where people who sell goods to the store receive no money until the item is bought, at which point the store owner takes a commission)</li></ol><p>From <i>комиссио́н(ный)</i> (komissión(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from an ellipsis of <i>комиссио́нный магази́н</i> (komissiónnyj magazín, “commission store”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commission; brokerage (fee charged)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) commission</li></ol><p><i>коми́ссия</i> (komíssija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chores</li><li>commission, committee</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>commissiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>committee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>comité</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comic</li><li>comical, funny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumple</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash off, to race through</li></ol><p><i>комо́к</i> (komók) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang) comment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>comment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comment</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>commentārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commentator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commentation, commenting, annotation (process of writing a comment or commentary)</li></ol><p><i>комменти́ровать</i> (kommentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comment (various senses)</li><li>to comment, to comment out (computer code)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kommentieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchant, businessman, trader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commerçant</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>commercium</i> (whence <i>комме́рция</i> (kommércija)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>коммерса́нт</i> (kommersánt, “merchant, businessman, trader”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commercialization</li></ol><p><i>коммерциализ(и́ровать)</i> (kommercializ(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commercialize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>commercialize</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commerce</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>commercium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commercial</li></ol><p><i>комме́рция</i> (kommércija) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>travelling salesman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commis-voyageur</i>. Cognates include Polish <i>komiwojażer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>programming</i>) commit (<i>to a version control system</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>commit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Commiphora</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Commiphora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commune</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commune</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kommunalka</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) utility bills, (payments for) utilities (пла́та за коммуна́льные услу́ги)</li></ol><p><i>коммуна́ль(ный)</i> (kommunálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>communal, public, municipal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker in communal-housing services</li></ol><p><i>коммуна́ль(ный)</i> (kommunálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Communard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;politics) communism</li></ol><p>From French <i>communisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>communicative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kommunikativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) communication, communications</li></ol><p><i>коммуника́ция</i> (kommunikácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communication</li><li>communication line</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) utility lines</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>communicatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communist (person who follows a communist or Marxist-Leninist philosophy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>communiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;philosophy) communist</li></ol><p><i>коммуни́ст</i> (kommuníst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) communist, commie</li></ol><p><i>коммуни́зм</i> (kommunízm) +&lrm; <i>-яка</i> (-jaka). Compare with Ukrainian <i>комуня́ка</i> (komunjáka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;computing) commutative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kommutativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>algebra</i>) commutator</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) commutator, switchboard</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) manual exchange, switchboard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communiqué</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>communiqué</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>комната</i> (komnata), from Latin <i>camīnāta</i>, probably through Old High German <i>kemināta</i> as evidenced by the initial stress. Cognate with English <i>chimney</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata): (small) room</li></ol><p><i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Middle Russian</i>) housemaid</li></ol><p>Literally, “room woman”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housefly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room temperature (normal temperature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) room</li><li>indoor (situated in, or designed to be used in, or carried on within the interior of a building)</li></ol><p><i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata): tiny little room</li></ol><p><i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata, “room”)</li></ol><p><i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest of drawers, bureau, dresser, commode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>commode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lump, wad</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) spasm in the throat or chest</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) commission shop (type of secondhand store where people who sell goods to the store receive no money until the item is bought, at which point the store owner takes a commission)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) camouflage suit</li></ol><p><i>ком</i> (kom) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p><p>From <i>ком(иссио́нный)</i> (kom(issiónnyj)) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), from an ellipsis of <i>комиссио́нный магази́н</i> (komissiónnyj magazín, “commission store”).</p><p>From <i>кам(уфля́жный)</i> (kam(ufljážnyj)) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), from an ellipsis of <i>камуфля́жный костю́м</i> (kamufljážnyj kostjúm, “camouflage suit”). Spelling influenced by other meanings of <i>комо́к</i> (komók) (unstressed а and о are pronounced the same).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hornless (without horns)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;komolъ</i> (“hornless, mutilated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comorian</li></ol><p><i>Комо́ры</i> (Komóry) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Comorian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Comorian (language)</li></ol><p><i>Комо́ры</i> (Komóry) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>комо́к</i> (komók): (small) wad, ball, nubbin, lump, blob</li></ol><p><i>комо́к</i> (komók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) computer, box</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>компью́тер</i> (kompʹjútɛr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) compact disc (CD)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>compact disc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(general,&#32;mathematics) compactness</li></ol><p><i>компа́ктный</i> (kompáktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compact, compacted, solid</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kompakt</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sociable, companionable</li><li>gregarious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) company (only in company names)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>compagnie</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>company</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>company <i>(a group of acquaintances)</i></li><li>company, corporation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kompania</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>compānia</i>, from Latin <i>compāniō</i> (<i>con-</i> + <i>pānis</i> + <i>-ō</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>однока́шник</i> (odnokášnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, partner (a member of a business or law partnership)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) comparative degree, comparative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Communist party</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>коммунисти́ческая па́ртия</i> (kommunistíčeskaja pártija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kompass</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Компас)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyxis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compensating, compensatory</li></ol><p><i>компенса́ция</i> (kompensácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compensation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>compēnsātiō</i>; cf. Polish <i>kompensacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reimbursement, amends</li></ol><p><i>компенси́ровать</i> (kompensírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compensate</li><li>to atone</li><li>to reimburse, to recoup</li><li>to indemnify, to recompense</li><li>to equilibrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competence (the quality or state of being competent for a general role)</li></ol><p><i>компете́нтный</i> (kompeténtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>competent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competence, competency</li><li>line</li><li>terms of reference, jurisdiction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>competentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compilation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compile</li><li>(transitive,&#32;programming) to compile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>kompilieren</i> or French <i>compiler</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) compiler</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;computing) compiler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complex (e.g., a collection of buildings)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) complex</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) insecurity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) complex number (number of the form <i>a + bi</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>composite, combined</li><li>integrated</li><li>comprehensive</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) complex</li></ol><p>Formed as if from <i>ко́мплекс</i> (kómpleks) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), but (especially in the mathematical sense) probably borrowed directly from English, French or German.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be insecure (about something)</li></ol><p><i>комплекс</i> (kompleks) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>set, suit, suite, complement</li><li>kit, outfit</li><li>series</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitting</li></ol><p><i>комплектова́ть</i> (komplektovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering, completing, batching</li><li>staffing, recruiting</li></ol><p><i>комплектова́ть</i> (komplektovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete, to replenish</li><li>to staff, to man, to bring up to strength</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>компле́кт</i> (komplékt) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be completed, to form a complete unit [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>комплектова́ть</i> (komplektovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>components, parts</li></ol><p>Substantivized plural of <i>комплекту́ющий</i> (komplektújuščij), present active participle of <i>комплектова́ть</i> (komplektovátʹ, “to complete”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>build, physique</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>complexiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compliment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composite (structural material that gains its strength from a combination of complementary materials)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composer (author of music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>compositor</i> (“arranger”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) composition</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) compositional</li></ol><p><i>компози́ция</i> (kompozícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composition</li><li>arrangement, layout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>compositiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>component, constituent (smaller, self-contained part of larger entity)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>compōnō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arranging, putting together, grouping</li><li>layout, design</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ticket punch; ticket stamping machine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>composteur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composting</li></ol><p><i>компости́ровать</i> (kompostírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punch (tickets to validate)</li><li>to compost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy, to molest; to nag persistently</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kompot (a sweet beverage made from boiled fruits or berries)</li><li>compote (a dessert made from fruit cooked in syrup)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) hodgepodge, mishmash (a mixture of miscellaneous, often incompatible things)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kompott</i> or directly from French <i>compote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kompot</li></ol><p><i>компо́т</i> (kompót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compress (cloth used to dress or apply pressure to wounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compressor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) compressor</li></ol><p><i>компре́ссор</i> (kompréssor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kompromat; information damaging a targeted person's reputation; compromising or discrediting evidence, dirt</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>компромети́рующий материа́л</i> (komprometírujuščij materiál), a KGB slang term firstly used during Stalin's era.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compromising</li></ol><p><i>компромети́ровать</i> (komprometírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compromise</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>compromettre</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compromising; likely to undermine one’s reputation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compromise, trade-off</li></ol><p>First attested in 1718. Ultimately from Latin <i>comprōmissum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) compromise</li></ol><p><i>компроми́сс</i> (kompromíss) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computer</li><li>(informal,&#32;computing) desktop or laptop computer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>computer</i>. Further from Latin <i>computō</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>комп'ю́тер</i> (kompʺjúter).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computerization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computer game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) computer</li></ol><p><i>компью́тер</i> (kompʹjútɛr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;colloquial) computer specialist/expert, (computer) techie, programmer</li></ol><p><i>компью́тер</i> (kompʹjútɛr) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) syllabic abbreviation of <i>Коммунисти́ческий сою́з молодёжи</i> (Kommunistíčeskij sojúz molodjóži, “Communist Union of Youth”): Komsomol, Young Communist League</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of the Komsomol, Komsomol member</li><li>member of the Young Communist League, Young Communist Leaguer</li><li>name of the nuclear submarine K-278 of the Russian Navy accidented in 1989</li></ol><p><i>комсомо́л</i> (komsomól) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Komsomol</li></ol><p><i>комсомо́л</i> (komsomól) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) Komsomol organizer (grassroots organizer of Komsomol meetings)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>комсомо́льский организа́тор</i> (komsomólʹskij organizátor, “Komsomol organizer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfort (contentment, ease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfort, amenity, comfortableness (something that makes life easier or more pleasant)</li></ol><p><i>комфорта́бельный</i> (komfortábelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comfortable</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>confortable</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). The word may be borrowed from either French or English; the accent matches French, but the spelling matches English.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comfortably</li></ol><p><i>комфо́ртный</i> (komfórtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfortableness, comfortability, comfort</li></ol><p><i>комфо́ртный</i> (komfórtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comfortable</li></ol><p><i>комфо́рт</i> (komfórt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) community</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>community</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a male Komi (member of an indigenous Finno-Ugric people of Russia)</li></ol><p><i>ко́ми</i> (kómi) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak), possibly formed by analogy to Russian <i>пермя́к</i> (permják), or a blend of <i>ко́ми</i> (kómi) +&lrm; <i>пермя́к</i> (permják), which is plausible because of the existence of a related ethnicity of Komi-Permyaks.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>gambling</i> bank, kitty</li><li>game, round</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>конъ</i> (konŭ, “limit”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konъ</i> (“beginning; end”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conveyor, production line</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) conveyor (a method of interrogation in which investigators conduct the questioning in round-the-clock shifts, in order to induce sleep deprivation in the subject)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) pipe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>conveyor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) conveyor</li></ol><p>From <i>конве́йер</i> (konvéjjer) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Convention <i>(elected bodies with extraordinary legislative powers)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) convention (formal agreement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>конве́нция</i> (konvéncija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) converb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Konverb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envelope (for mailing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>couvert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) to convert (currency, etc.)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>konvertieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort, guardsman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escort, to convoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort, convoy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>convoy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) convoy, escort</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort (person who escorts and guards someone, e.g. a convict)</li></ol><p><i>конво́й</i> (konvój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) convulsion (intense muscular contraction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conglomerate (a cluster of heterogeneous things)</li><li>(<i>business</i>) conglomerate (corporation formed by the combination of several smaller corporations)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) conglomerate (rock consisting of gravel or pebbles embedded in a matrix)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>conglomérat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Congolese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congress (legislative body)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>congressus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congressman (member of congress)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>congressman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>congruous, congruent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) synonym of <i>кондиционе́р</i> (kondicionér, “air conditioner”)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>кондиционе́р</i> (kondicionér) (suffixed with <i>-ей</i> (-ej), further by surface analysis, Latin <i>cond(īcō)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ей</i> (-ej)), looks like as if with the suffix <i>-де́й</i> (-déj) like in <i>лиходе́й</i> (lixodéj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) condensate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) Bose-Einstein condensate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) capacitor</li></ol><p>From German <i>Kondensator</i>, from Latin <i>condēnsō</i> (“to thicken”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condensation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>condensatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confectioner, pastry cook</li><li>confectionery owner</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) small restaurant owner</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from German <i>Konditer</i>, popular variant of <i>Konditor</i>, from Latin <i>condītor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetshop, confectioner's, candy store (shop selling predominantly confectionery)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) confectionary (relating to, or of the nature of confections or their production)</li></ol><p><i>конди́тер</i> (kondíter) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air conditioner</li><li>hair conditioner (Any of many cosmetic products that aim to improve the condition of the hair, especially after washing.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>(air) conditioner</i>. Further from Latin <i>condiciō</i> (whence <i>конди́ция</i> (kondícija)), from Latin <i>condīcō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air conditioning (the state of temperature and humidity produced by an air conditioner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, condition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>condition</i> or German <i>Kondition</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(carpentry,&#32;of the wood of coniferous trees) solid, sturdy, with few knots</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a tree</i>) having solid, sturdy wood</li><li>(<i>of a forest</i>) consisting of trees with solid, sturdy wood</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) primitive, old-fashioned</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) of the yellowish-red colour/color of freshly felled pine</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>конда</i> (konda), from earlier <i>ко́нга</i> (kónga), from Finnish <i>honka</i> (“large, straight pine tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condom (flexible sleeve worn on the penis)</li></ol><p>From English <i>condom</i>. The pronunciation as кондо́м instead of ко́ндом could be an influence by German <i>Kondom</i>.</p><p>Became more common after 1990 through coverage of the HIV/AIDS pandemic, though attested from at least 1886 in medical literature; it became popularly mispronounced as гондо́н (gondón), now vulgar.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) condor (bird)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) condor (coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>cóndor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) stroke (CVA)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) sudden death</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conductor</li><li>railroad conductor, guard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuff</li><li>a leather or corduroy lining of the sleeve at the wrist</li><li>standing collar</li><li>peak of a military cap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stud farm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end</li><li>ending, close, termination</li><li>death</li><li>(<i>familiar</i>) distance, way</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;colloquial) male sex organ</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) rope</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) borough (<i>in medieval Novgorod</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коньць</i> (konĭcĭ, “end; edge, border”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьcь</i> (“end”), from <i>&#42;konъ</i> (“beginning; end”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;čęti</i> (“to begin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end of the world</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>of course, certainly</li></ol><p><i>коне́чный</i> (konéčnyj, “final”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limb, extremity, member</li><li>finiteness</li></ol><p><i>коне́чный</i> (konéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Compare Czech <i>konečnost</i> and Polish <i>konieczność</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final, eventual, ultimate</li><li>finite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коньчьнꙑи</i> (konĭčĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьčьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>конь</i> (konʹ): (little) horse, horsy</li></ol><p><i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsemeat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cognac</li></ol><p><i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) conical flask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conic, conical</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cōnicus</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij). Compare Ukrainian <i>коні́чний</i> (koníčnyj), Belarusian <i>кані́чны</i> (kaníčny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) horsecar, a tram drawn by horses</li></ol><p>From <i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Konkani</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concordat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concourse (a large open space in a station or terminal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) concretization, detailing, specification (process of making something more specific), clarification</li></ol><p><i>конкретиз(и́ровать)</i> (konkretiz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concretize, to specify, to make (more) specific</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>konkretisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) specifics</li></ol><p><i>конкре́т(ный)</i> (konkrét(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>concretely, specifically</li></ol><p><i>конкре́тный</i> (konkrétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concrete noun (noun that denotes a tangible thing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concreteness</li></ol><p>From <i>конкре́тный</i> (konkrétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concrete, specific, particular, perceivable, real</li><li>given</li></ol><p>From French <i>concret</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concrete verb (type of verbs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competitor, rival (person against whom one is competing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Konkurrent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>competitive</li></ol><p>From <i>конкуре́нт</i> (konkurént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competitiveness</li></ol><p><i>конкурентоспосо́бный</i> (konkurentosposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>competitive</li></ol><p><i>конкуре́нт</i> (konkurént) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competition (for a limited resource)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>concurrentia</i>, through German <i>Konkurrenz</i> or Polish <i>konkurencja</i>.</p><p>Attested since the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competing</li></ol><p><i>конкури́ровать</i> (konkurírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compete, to rival</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contest; competition</li></ol><p>Judging by the stress borrowed via Polish <i>konkurs</i>, from Latin <i>concursus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>competitive</li></ol><p><i>ко́нкурс</i> (kónkurs, “competition”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conlang, constructed language</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>conlang</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavalry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьnica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>конный</i> (konnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horse</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cavalry; mounted</li><li>horse-drawn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mounted policeman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farrier, horseleech</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) quack, bad doctor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse thief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caulk, to calk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cannabis, hemp</li></ol><p>Dialectally vocalized also коно́пля, which is probably the original stress of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konopľa</i> (“hemp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cannabis</li></ol><p><i>конопля́</i> (konopljá) +&lrm; <i>-я́ный</i> (-jányj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cannabis</li></ol><p><i>конопля́</i> (konopljá) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swampy, boggy, impassable area</li><li>hinterland, backwoods, province</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) plantain</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) knotweed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коното́пъ</i> (konotópŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konotòpъ</i> (“swamp in which the horse drowned”). Cognate with dialectal Belarusian <i>канато́п</i> (kanatóp, “swamp, bog, marsh”).</p><p>Possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konotòpъ</i> (“plants trampled by horses”). Cognate with dialectal Ukrainian <i>коното́п</i> (konotóp, “knotweed; red clover”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Конотоп)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Konotop (a village in Oryol Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Konotop (a city in Sumy Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>Коното́пъ</i> (Konotóp), from Old East Slavic <i>коното́пъ</i> (konotópŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konotòpъ</i> (“swamp in which the horse drowned”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>коното́п</i> (konotóp).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) conophytum (<i>Conophytum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Conophytum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consulting</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>consulting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) consulting</li></ol><p><i>конса́лтинг</i> (konsálting) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>generally uncountable</i>) consensus</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>cōnsēnsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conservative</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) nonsurgical</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>konservativ</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conservatism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conservative; Tory (UK)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conservatory, conservatoire (music academy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>conservatorio</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>консервато́рія</i> (konservatórija), Belarusian <i>кансервато́рыя</i> (kansjervatóryja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conservation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>canned (preserved by canning)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve, to can (food)</li><li>to preserve (<i>through special treatment, e.g. wood, a body, archaeological items, etc.</i>)</li><li>to temporarily suspend (an activity)</li></ol><p>From <i>консе́рвы</i> (konsérvy) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ) and/or borrowed from German <i>konservieren</i> (“to can”) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>can, tin (a tin-plate canister for preserved foods)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tinned food, canned food</li></ol><p><i>консе́рвы</i> (konsérvy) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>can opener (device used to open tin cans)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canned / tinned / potted goods, canned food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consignee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(make-up)</i> concealer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) meeting or consultation among several doctors</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) conference of professionals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cōnsilium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consistence, consistency</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>cōnsistentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horse, equine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьskъ</i>. Equivalent to <i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolidation, union</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funding, consolidation</li></ol><p><i>консолиди́ровать</i> (konsolidírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consolidated</li><li>stable, fixed, permanent (<i>revenue</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consolidate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>konsolidieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); also see <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>console</li><li>console, corbel</li><li>cantilever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) consonant</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>cōnsonāns</i> (genitive <i>cōnsonāntis</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abjad (writing system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consonant</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) consonantal</li></ol><p><i>консона́нт</i> (konsonánt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consortium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>consortium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summary, synopsis, abstract (an abridgement or summary)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>conspectus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conspiratorial, secret, underground, clandestine</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>konspirativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conspirator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conspiracy, secrecy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>conspiratio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Constantinople</li></ol><p><i>Константино́поль</i> (Konstantinópolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascertaining</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to state</li><li>to establish (a fact)</li></ol><p>From French <i>constater</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), German <i>konstatieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>constable</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constitutional (relating to the (legal or political) constitution)</li></ol><p><i>конститу́ция</i> (konstitúcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) constitution</li><li>constitution, physique</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constructing, designing</li></ol><p><i>конструи́ровать</i> (konstruírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to construct, to build, to structure, to design, to form</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>konstruieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>constructively, structurally</li><li>constructively, in a constructive way</li></ol><p><i>конструкти́вный</i> (konstruktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constructive (helpful)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) construction, constructive</li></ol><p>From <i>конструкти́в</i> (konstruktív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), ultimately from Latin <i>cōnstructīvus</i>, from Latin <i>cōnstructus</i> (“constructed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designer, design engineer, constructor</li><li>(<i>children's games</i>) construction set, assembly kit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Konstrukteur</i> or French <i>constructeur</i>, from Late Latin <i>cōnstructor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) designer, constructor</li></ol><p><i>констру́ктор</i> (konstrúktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, structure, design</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consul (official residing in major foreign towns to represent and protect the interests of the merchants and citizens of their country)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) consul (either of the two heads of government and state of the Roman Republic or the equivalent nominal post under the Roman and Byzantine Empires)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) consul (any of the three heads of government and state of France between 1799 and 1804)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) consul, consulate (<i>in any meaning</i>); consular</li></ol><p><i>ко́нсул</i> (kónsul) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consulate</li></ol><p><i>ко́нсул</i> (kónsul) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consultant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advisory, consultative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>consultatif</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consultation, advice</li><li>tutorial</li><li>consulting room, advisory board, advice bureau, guidance centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) consulting, counseling, advising, advisement, consultation</li></ol><p><i>консульти́ровать</i> (konsulʹtírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to act as a consultant for [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone, e.g. a client’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to consult [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone, e.g. a lawyer’</small>]</li></ol><p>From German <i>konsultieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone, e.g. a lawyer’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>консульти́ровать</i> (konsulʹtírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concierge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>concierge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contact</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>contāctus</i>, through French <i>contact</i> or German <i>Kontakt</i>. Attested since the early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in contact [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from German <i>kontaktieren</i>. By surface analysis, <i>конта́кт</i> (kontákt) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contact lens (thin lens placed directly on the eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) contact</li><li>in direct contact</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easy to establish contact with</li><li>helping to establish connections (of a political or commercial nature)</li></ol><p><i>конта́кт</i> (kontákt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>container</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) container</li></ol><p><i>конте́йнер</i> (kontɛ́jner) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>context</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet,&#32;media) content</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>content</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contingent; quota (of troops)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) continent, mainland (large expanse of land)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continental</li><li>mainland</li><li>landlocked</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kontinental</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continuum</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>continuum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office, bureau</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) suspicious company</li><li>(dated,&#32;euphemistic) the KGB</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) KGB successors in the Post-Soviet countries: the FSB, SBU etc.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kontor</i> around 1720, from Middle Low German <i>kontôr</i>, <i>komtôr</i>, <i>komptor</i> (“commercial branch; writing room; counting desk/table”), from Middle Dutch <i>contoor</i>, <i>contoir</i>, <i>comptoir</i>, from Middle French <i>contoir</i>, <i>comptoir</i>, from <i>conter</i>, <i>compter</i> (“to count”) + <i>-oir</i> (instrument suffix), from Latin <i>computāre</i> and <i>-tōrium</i>.</p><p>Sense 'KGB' originated from a pun playing on another rare meaning of the abbreviation, namely <i>контора глубокого бурения</i> (kontora glubokovo burenija, literally “deep-boring field office”), in the 1960s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high sloped desk</li><li>diminutive of <i>конто́ра</i> (kontóra, “bureau, office (part of an organization)”)</li></ol><p><i>конто́ра</i> (kontóra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account book (a book in which accounts are kept)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office, bureau</li></ol><p><i>конто́ра</i> (kontóra) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>counter- (in opposition to)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) lock, safety</li><li>rear (<i>of military ranks; written with a dash</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>contre-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) counter admiral (a navy rank equivalent to rear admiral in English-speaking countries)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterrevolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;derogatory,&#32;often&#32;collective,&#32;politics) counterrevolutionary, counterrevolutionaries; counterrevolution (<i>especially in reference to the 1917 Russian Revolution</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (a small) school test</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>контрреволю́ция</i> (kontrrevoljúcija).</p><p>Clipping of <i>контро́льная</i> (kontrólʹnaja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smuggling (an act of smuggling)</li><li>contraband (smuggled goods)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>contrabbando</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smuggler (someone who smuggles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contraband</li></ol><p><i>контраба́нда</i> (kontrabánda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) double bass, contrabass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a contract party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contract, agreement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kontrakt</i> or Polish <i>kontrakt</i>, from Latin <i>contractus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contractor</li><li>(<i>military</i>) soldier under a contract (<i>as opposed to a drafted conscript</i>)</li></ol><p><i>контра́кт</i> (kontrákt) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) contract; contractual</li></ol><p><i>контра́кт</i> (kontrákt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form a contract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) counterpoint</li></ol><p>Probable, borrowing from German <i>Kontrapunkt</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>contrapunctum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to contrast</li></ol><p><i>контра́ст</i> (kontrást) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contrasting</li></ol><p><i>контра́ст</i> (kontrást) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter-attack (an attack made in response to an attack by the opponents)</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-, “counter-”) +&lrm; <i>ата́ка</i> (atáka, “attack”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrabassoon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economy,&#32;finance) contributor</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>contributor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contribution, indemnity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>counterintuitive</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>интуити́вный</i> (intuitívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterculture</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>countercultural</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>культу́рный</i> (kulʹtúrnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;politics,&#32;slang,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) (a Ukrainian) counter-offensive</li></ol><p>First attested in <i>c.</i> 2022, but in common usage only as of 2023. Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>контрна́ступ</i> (kontrnástup).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter-offensive</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>наступле́ние</i> (nastuplénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monitoring, controlling</li></ol><p><i>контроли́ровать</i> (kontrolírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control, to check</li><li>to monitor, to supervise</li><li>to rule, to superintend (<i>no perfective form</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>controlled</li><li>controllable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>controller (device or system)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>controller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>control</li><li>checkup, checking, inspection, monitoring, supervision, verification</li><li>blank test, standard</li><li>institution or organization that carries out such checks, inspections or monitoring</li><li>area at the entrance to a theater where tickets are checked or collected</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) ticket-collectors, inspectors</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>contrôle</i>. Further from Latin <i>contra-</i> + Latin <i>rotula</i>, from Latin <i>rota</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>контро́льная рабо́та</i> (kontrólʹnaja rabóta): a (small) test (at school)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (small) test (at school)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) control</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) check, verification, inspection</li></ol><p><i>контро́ль</i> (kontrólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checklist (list of things to be verified)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checklist (list of things to be verified)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspector</li><li>ticket collector</li></ol><p>Ultimately from French <i>contrôleur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) counterexample</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>приме́р</i> (primér)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterintelligence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterintelligence officer</li></ol><p><i>контрразве́д(ка)</i> (kontrrazvéd(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterrevolutionary, counterrevolutionist</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>революционе́р</i> (revoljucionér)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>counterrevolutionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterrevolution</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>револю́ция</i> (revoljúcija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterstrategy</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>страте́гия</i> (stratégija); compare to English <i>counterstrategy</i>, French <i>contre-stratégie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter-terrorism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) counterterrorism; counterterrorist</li></ol><p><i>контр-</i> (kontr-) +&lrm; <i>террори́ст</i> (terroríst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often impersonal</i>) to bruise, to contuse</li></ol><p><i>конту́зия</i> (kontúzija) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) contusion, blunt trauma</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) shell concussion; traumatic brain injury, especially when caused by a shock wave</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) post-concussion syndrome</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>contūsiō</i>, likely via French <i>contusion</i> or German <i>Kontusion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contour, outline</li><li>circuit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>contour</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Germanic king</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>konungr</i>. Doublet of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ) and <i>ксёндз</i> (ksjondz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kennel, doghouse</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) small in size, often dirty, unsightly housing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konura</i>. Compare Mongolian <i>хонуурь</i> (xonuurʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>конура́</i> (konurá): (small) kennel, doghouse; (<i>figurative</i>) small in size, often dirty, unsightly housing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cone</li></ol><p><i>ко́нус</i> (kónus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) group chat</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) web conference, telecon, videocon</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>конфере́нция</i> (konferéncija) + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a confederate (a member or supporter of a confederacy or confederation)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a Confederate</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>cōnfoederātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female confederate (a female member or supporter of a confederacy or confederation)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a female Confederate</li><li>rogatywka (four-pointed Polish army cap)</li><li>krakuska (soft, four-pointed cap traditionally worn in Lesser Poland)</li></ol><p><i>конфедера́т</i> (konfederát) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>The headwear sense refers to the use of four-pointed caps by soldiers of the Bar Confederation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confederation, confederacy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cōnfoederātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sweet (sugary confection)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Konfekt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sweet (sugary confection)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Konfekt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master of ceremonies</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>conférencier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conference hall, auditorium, meeting hall, lecture hall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Konferenzsaal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conference</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>conférence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faith, religion, creed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet (sugary confection), bonbon, sweetmeat, candy</li></ol><p>First borrowed from German <i>Konfekt</i> in the form of <i>конфе́кта</i> (konfékta) in the 16th century, ultimately from Latin <i>cōnfectus</i>. Later influenced by or borrowed again from Italian <i>confetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>конфе́та</i> (konféta): candy; something nice</li></ol><p><i>конфе́та</i> (konféta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bowl for sweets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sweet (sugary confection), bonbon, candy</li></ol><p><i>конфе́та</i> (konféta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confetti (small pieces of colored paper generally thrown about at festive occasions)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>confetti</i>, plural for <i>confetto</i>, via French <i>confetti</i>, which affected the word stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>configuration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) confidant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confidentiality</li></ol><p><i>конфиденциа́льный</i> (konfidɛnciálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confidential, private (meant to be kept secret within a certain circle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confiscation, sequestration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confiscate, to seize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Latin <i>confiscāre</i> + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of jam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>confiture</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conflict (clash or disagreement)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>conflictus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) conflict</li><li>contentious, quarrelsome, disputatious</li></ol><p><i>конфли́кт</i> (konflíkt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conflict <i>(with)</i>, to clash <i>(with)</i>, to come up <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>конфли́кт</i> (konflíkt) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>конфо́рка</i> (konfórka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring, burner (of a stove)</li><li>crown, top ring (of a samovar)</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>komfoor</i>, from Picard <i>cauffoir</i> equal to French <i>chauffoir</i>, from Latin <i>calefacere</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) conformation (arrangement of atoms in a molecule)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>cōnfōrmātiō</i> (“conformation, shape, form”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conformism</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>conformisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conformist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confrontation</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>confrontātiō</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>конфронта́ція</i> (konfrontácija), Belarusian <i>канфранта́цыя</i> (kanfrantácyja), Polish <i>konfrontacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discomfiture; embarrassing situation</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cōnfūsus</i>, from Latin <i>cōnfundō</i> (whence also English <i>confound</i>), from Latin <i>con-</i> + Latin <i>fundō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disconcert, to discomfit, to embarrass, to put in an awkward or embarrassing position</li></ol><p><i>конфу́з</i> (konfúz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disconcerted, to be embarrassed, to get into an embarrassing situation</li></ol><p><i>конфу́зить</i> (konfúzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Confucianism (philosophy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terminal, last, final</li></ol><p><i>коне́ц</i> (konéc) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentrated product</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) concentrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) concentration (<i>only with reference to a concentration camp</i>)</li></ol><p><i>концентра́ция</i> (koncentrácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentration</li></ol><p><i>концентри́ровать</i> (koncentrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concentrated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, (<i>military</i>) to mass</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>konzentrieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to amass, to get together, to mass</li><li>to concentrate <i>(on)</i>, to focus <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>концентри́ровать</i> (koncentrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concept</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) conceptual</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>conceptuālis</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concept, idea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>conceptio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concern (business group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Konzern</i> or English <i>concern</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concert, recital</li><li>(<i>music</i>) concerto</li><li>quarrel accompanied by shouting</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the 17th century from Italian <i>concerto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) concert</li></ol><p><i>конце́рт</i> (koncért) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) concession</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>concession</i>, possibly through German <i>Konzession</i>; ultimately from Latin <i>concessiō</i> (“granting, permission”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentration camp</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>концентрацио́нный ла́герь</i> (koncentraciónnyj lágerʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ending (e.g. of a story, film, email address)</li></ol><p><i>конц-</i> (konc-) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) sperm, cum, jizz, spunk</li></ol><p>From <i>конча́ть</i> (končátʹ, “to ejaculate, to cum (slang)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end</li><li>to graduate from</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ejaculate, to cum <i>(of a man)</i>; to orgasm <i>(of a woman)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill; to waste</li><li>to finish; to deplete</li></ol><p><i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end</li></ol><p>From <i>конча́ть</i> (končátʹ) and <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) completely resolved, finished, final, decisive, decided, ultimate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) finished, done, done for, gone, hopeless, washed-up, a lost cause (no longer capable, exhausted or having lost abilities) (<i>often translated to other negative adjectives such as crazy, miserable, stinking, batshit, wicked, base, douchey</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tip, point, cusp (extreme top of something)</li><li>diminutive of <i>коне́ц</i> (konéc, “end, extremity”)</li></ol><p><i>коне́ц</i> (konéc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) death, demise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьčina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end</li><li>to graduate from</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ejaculate, to cum <i>(of a man)</i>; to orgasm <i>(of a woman)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill; to waste</li><li>to finish; to deplete</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konьčiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>коне́ц</i> (konéc) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Cognates include Belarusian <i>ко́нчыць</i> (kónčycʹ), Ukrainian <i>кінчи́ти</i> (kinčýty), Polish <i>kończyć</i>, Czech <i>končit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end</li></ol><p><i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) conjunctivitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conjuncture, state of affairs</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Medieval Latin <i>coniunctūra</i>, likely through Polish <i>koniunktura</i> or German <i>Konjunktur</i>. Used since the times of Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-term, cyclical</li><li>opportunistic, time-serving, mercenary-minded, advantage-seeking</li></ol><p><i>конъюнкту́ра</i> (konʺjunktúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(logic,&#32;mathematics) conjunction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>coniūnctiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse; male horse</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) steed</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) horse</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) knight (Russian abbreviation: К)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skater</li></ol><p><i>коньк-</i> (konʹk-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>беж-</i> (bež-) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cognac</i>, from <i>Cognac</i>, a city in France.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cognac</li></ol><p><i>конья́к</i> (konʹják) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(diminutive,&#32;endearing) brandy, cognac</li></ol><p><i>конья́к</i> (konʹják) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groom, stableman, hostler (person at an inn who looks after horses)</li><li>European buzzard (<i>Buteo buteo</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>конюхъ</i> (konjuxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koňuxъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ух</i> (-ux).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stable, stabling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koňušьna</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ко́нюх</i> (kónjux) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>конь</i> (konʹ): (small) horse</li></ol><p><i>кон(ь)</i> (kon(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>конь</i> (konʹ): (little) horse, horsy</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) skate (ice skate or roller skate)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) hobby, fad, soapbox, favourite/favorite subject</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) pipit (bird)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) <i>(roof)</i> ridge, finial</li></ol><p><i>конь</i> (konʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) cooperative, co-op</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cooperative</li></ol><p><i>кооперати́в</i> (kooperatív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooperative worker</li></ol><p><i>коопер(и́ровать)</i> (kooper(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooperation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to cooperate</li><li>to attract to participate in a cooperative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kooperieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooption, assignment of an (elected) office to a person by higher authorities without an election</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cooptātiō</i> (“cooption”), perhaps through French <i>cooptation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;cartography) coordinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coordinator (one who coordinates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coordination, coordinating</li></ol><p><i>координа́ция</i> (koordinácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coordination, dispatching, managing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coordinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;foreign context) cop (police officer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka): kopek, copeck, kopeck <i>(singular or plural)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) sixty pieces of something (e.g. eggs, cucumbers, sheaves of hay or rye, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copal <i>(resin)</i></li><li>copal varnish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li><li>to dig up, to dig out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>копати</i> (kopati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kop-</i>, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kep-</i> (“to strike, beat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rummage <i>(in)</i>, to delve <i>(into)</i>, to poke <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dawdle</li></ol><p><i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka): kopeyka, kopeck, copeck; penny</li></ol><p><i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worth one penny</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) inexpensive, cheap</li><li>low-paid</li></ol><p><i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kopek, kopeck; also copeck (one 100th of ruble, the monetary unit of Russia).</li></ol><p>From <i>копе́йный</i> (kopéjnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), in the combination <i>"копе́йная деньга́"</i> ("kopéjnaja denʹgá"), from <i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó, “spear”) (from the image of a rider with a spear on the coins minted by Moscow after the capture of Novgorod in 1478).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spear (related to or characteristic of a spear)</li></ol><p><i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copernicium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) end, death; bad or crazy thing or situation</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) end, limit</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) boundary mark, boundary pit; hole in the woods that marks something</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) pit for storing vegetables in winter; small pit for temporary needs</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) well (hole for obtaining water)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) small mound or knoll; kurgan</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) worker who digs up or puts manure on a cart</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) owl</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) kite (bird)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) red-footed falcon</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) steering oar, helm</li></ol><p><i>копа́</i> (kopá, “heap”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc, masculine noun-forming suffix)</p><p>Unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piggy bank, money box (any shape)</li></ol><p><i>копи́ть</i> (kopítʹ, “to accumulate, to save up”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbon paper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) copying</li></ol><p><i>копи́ровать</i> (kopírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photocopier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copying</li></ol><p><i>копи́ровать</i> (kopírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to copy, to make a copy</li><li>to imitate, to mimic, to copy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kopieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass</li><li>to save up (money)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to save up, conserve</li><li>to hide, to conceal (feelings, knowledge, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>копити</i> (kopiti, “to amass, to gather, e.g. people for an army”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopiti</i> (“to stack, to pile, e.g. hay”), from <i>&#42;kopa</i> (“heap, pile”), from <i>&#42;kopati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kop-</i>, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kep-</i> (“to strike, beat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather</li></ol><p><i>копи́ть</i> (kopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copy, duplicate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digging, lifting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shock, stook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a dig, to dig <i>(up)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to penetrate, to see <i>(into)</i>, to plumb, to go to the bottom <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot, (black) smoke, fume</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopъtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swarm (of insects)</li><li>to potter, to putter about (of people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>koppa, qoppa (the early Greek letter Ϙ&#47;ϙ or Ϟ&#47;ϟ)</li><li>koppa (the early Cyrillic letter Ҁ&#47;ҁ, used as a numeric symbol)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ϙόππα</i> (qóppa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copra</li></ol><p>From Portuguese <i>copra</i>, from Malayalam <i>കൊപ്ര</i> (kopra), from Sanskrit <i>कपालः</i> (kapālaḥ, “skull”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) coprolalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copropraxia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scatophilia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κόπρος</i> (kópros) and Ancient Greek <i>φιλία</i> (philía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(primitive)</i> oil lamp, wick lamp</li></ol><p><i>копти́ть</i> (koptítʹ, “to emit smoke”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoking shed (<i>a shed or room where meat is cured by smoking</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>копти́ть</i> (koptítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blacken, to soot</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give off soot and smoke (<i>e.g. of a burning lamp or candle</i>)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to smoke, to cure (<i>meat or fish</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopъtiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;grammar) copula, linking verb</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;statistics) connective, copula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>copula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;literary) to copulate (to engage in sexual intercourse)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;agriculture) union, conjunction</li></ol><p>From French <i>copulation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copulation, breeding (the cominɡ toɡether of male and female in sexual intercourse&#59; copulation of animals)</li></ol><p>From French <i>copulation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a slow person (someone who dawdles, someone who acts slowly)</li></ol><p>From <i>копа́ться</i> (kopátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoking (food preservation or cooking method)</li></ol><p><i>копти́ть</i> (koptítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) coccyx; tailbone (vertebrae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoked, smoke-dried</li></ol><p><i>копти́ть</i> (koptítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plain wooden hoe</li><li>shoe last</li><li>short bar inserted into sled runners and employed to support the body</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopylъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelwort (<i>Asarum europaeum</i>, the genus <i>Asarum</i> and also other species in it)</li></ol><p>Altered from earlier <i>копы́тник</i> (kopýtnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>now&#32;dialectal</i>) hazelwort (<i>Asarum europaeum</i>, the genus <i>Asarum</i> and also other species in it)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;farriery) knife to work on hooves, hoof trimmer, hoof nipper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>копытникъ</i> (kopytnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopytъnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>копы́то</i> (kopýto) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoof (foot of an animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopyto</i>, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱoph₂ós</i>.</p><p>For the relation with <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ), compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>ὄρυξ</i> (órux).</p><p>For the relation with <i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó), compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>ὁπλή</i> (hoplḗ), <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>копы́то</i> (kopýto)</li></ol><p><i>копы́то</i> (kopýto) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ore mine, pit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Spear-headed; having a head that is pointed or spear-shaped.</li></ol><p><i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó, “spear, lance, javelin”) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spear, lance, javelin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>копие</i> (kopije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopьje</i> (see details of derivation there), from <i>&#42;kopati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kop-</i>, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kep-</i> (“to strike, beat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile driver</li><li>headframe</li></ol><p><i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>копе́йка</i> (kopéjka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fitness&#32;slang</i>) core</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>core</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark (of trees)</li><li>crust (of Earth or other planet)</li><li>cortex (of brain)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) joke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кора</i> (kora, “bark”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kora</i> (“bark”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipbuilder; shipwright</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ship; naval</li></ol><p><i>кора́бль</i> (koráblʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship's cat</li></ol><p><i>корабе́льный</i> (korabélʹnyj, “ship”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>ко́т</i> (kót, “cat”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipworm</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>shipworm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipwreck <i>(event)</i></li></ol><p><i>кора́бль</i> (koráblʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>круше́ние</i> (krušénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кора́бль</i> (koráblʹ): (small) ship; toy-ship</li></ol><p><i>кора́бль</i> (koráblʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship, liner</li><li>(<i>formal</i>) warship</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) airliner, (<i>large</i>) aircraft (<i>e.g. large passenger airplane, military transport, or bomber</i>)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) nave</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) matchbox of marijuana</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) a community of the Khlyst movement members</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>корабль</i> (korablĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korabľь</i>, see more there.</p><p>The marijuana sense is by consonance with <i>коробо́к</i> (korobók, “small box”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coral</li><li>coral (color)</li></ol><p><i>кора́лл</i> (koráll) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coral snake (<i>Micrurus</i> and related genera)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>кора́лловый</i> (korállovyj, “coral, coralline”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>а́спид</i> (áspid, “asp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) cord</li></ol><p>From Old French <i>corde</i> (“rope”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corps de ballet, chorus line, ballet personnel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>corps de ballet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cordon (<i>troops or guards deployed in a line to encircle or protect an area</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) border, frontier</li><li>(<i>military</i>) outpost, pickets, guards detachment</li><li>ranger station (<i>in a nature preserve</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kordon</i>, from French <i>cordon</i> (“cord”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Korean person</li></ol><p><i>Коре́я</i> (Koréja) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hallyu, the Korean Wave</li></ol><p>Calque of Korean <i>한류(韓流)</i> (Hallyu, “Korean current”), from <i>корейская</i> (korejskaja, “Korean”) +&lrm; <i>волна</i> (volna, “wave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Korean</li></ol><p><i>Коре́я</i> (Koréja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>:/kɐˈrʲelːə/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) cockatiel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German Korella, zoological name used in 18th–19th century ornithology for various parrots of the Australasian region, ultimately from scientific Latin Corella, genus name applied to some cockatoos and parrots.</p><p>The Latin genus name is of uncertain origin; it may be derived from a geographical or proper name rather than from a classical Latin root.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stocky, thickset, stumpy</li><li>with strong roots (<i>of a tree</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>korenizatsiya, korenization</li></ol><p>From <i>коренизировать</i> (korenizirovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ация</i> (-acija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be rooted in, to have its source in [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something/someone, e.g. an idea, a person, hard work, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to lurk deeply in (<i>e.g. of feelings</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something/someone, e.g. a person, one's heart, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indigenous, native, aboriginal</li><li>fundamental, radical, basic</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in modern context</i>) a native inhabitant (a person who was born and grew up in a particular place, as contrasted with newcomers)</li><li>aborigine, native, person of aboriginal descent (a member of the original ethnic group inhabiting a land, as contrasted with foreign settlers, colonizers, or descendants thereof)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>molar (tooth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;anatomy,&#32;linguistics,&#32;figuratively) root</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;arithmetic) root, radical</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kory</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root vegetables</li></ol><p>nominative/accusative plural of <i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ), only when referring to root vegetables.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) crony, pal, buddy, dawg (close or old friend)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) accomplice (in a crime)</li></ol><p>Probably a shortening of корешо́к (korešók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ): rootlet</li><li>spine, back (of a book)</li><li>counterfoil</li><li>(<i>banking</i>) a bundle of 100 banknotes / bills of the same face value</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ко́реш</i> (kóreš): male friend; buddy, pal, mate, crony</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-шо́к</i> (-šók)</p><p><i>ко́реш</i> (kóreš) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) a type of large, unleavened flatbread</li><li>cake base, cake layer (a flat piece of sponge or butter cake, used as a component of a layer cake or other dessert)</li><li>pie shell</li></ol><p>First attested in the 19th century, borrowed from Ukrainian <i>корж</i> (korž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>korzhik, cookie, flat dry shortbread / shortcake</li><li>diminutive of <i>корж</i> (korž)</li></ol><p><i>корж</i> (korž) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket (container)</li><li>wastebasket</li><li>(<i>basketball</i>) hoop</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) (<i>as described below</i>)</li></ol><p>There is no satisfactory etymology for this East Slavic isogloss. Usually compared to Latvian <i>kur̃zа</i> (“bark box for berries, basket of twigs”), <i>kurzulis</i> (“a type of sleigh”). Possibly borrowed from an unknown Dnieper Baltic dialect. Partially replaced короб.</p><p>A Norse origin (cf. Swedish <i>korg</i> and similar forms) is dismissed by Vasmer.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>корзи́на</i> (korzína): (small) basket</li></ol><p><i>корзи́на</i> (korzína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>корзи́на</i> (korzína, “basket”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>корзи́нка</i> (korzínka, “small basket”)</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) corbicula, pollen basket</li><li>a basket-shaped shortcrust tart with cream, fruit, or other sweet filling</li></ol><p><i>корзи́нка</i> (korzínka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) basket</li></ol><p><i>корзи́нка</i> (korzínka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basketmaker</li></ol><p><i>корзи́на</i> (korzína) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) coat with fur trimming</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrzьno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coriander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corridor, hallway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Korridor</i> or French <i>corridor</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>currō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) corridor</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bellboy</li></ol><p><i>коридо́р</i> (koridór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коридо́р</i> (koridór)</li></ol><p><i>коридо́р</i> (koridór) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corynebacterium</li></ol><p>Partial calque of translingual <i>Corynebacterium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corinthian order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corinthian order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corinthian</li></ol><p><i>Кори́нф</i> (Korínf) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach <i>(with)</i>, to upbraid <i>(with, for)</i></li><li>to cast in someone's teeth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korìti</i>. Compare Old East Slavic <i>кор</i> (kor, “swearword, offence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear physics</i>) corium (lavalike mixture of fissile material created in a nuclear reactor's core during a nuclear meltdown)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>corium</i>, from <i>core</i> +&lrm; <i>-ium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coryphaeus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinnamon (spice)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cinnamon (tree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>корица</i> (korica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korica</i>, diminutive of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kora</i> (“tree bark”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>кори́ця</i> (korýcja), Belarusian <i>кары́ца</i> (karýca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brown</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) ultra-nationalistic, fascist, far right-wing, jingoist</li></ol><p>From <i>кори́ца</i> (koríca, “cinnamon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brown sugar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinnamon (a tree of the genus <i>Cinnamomum</i>&#59; most commonly, <i>Cinnamomum verum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кори́ца</i> (koríca, “cinnamon (spice)”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinnamon (tree)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cinnamon</li></ol><p><i>кори́ца</i> (koríca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crust, scab</li><li>rind, peel</li></ol><p><i>кора́</i> (korá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feed, fodder, food for animals</li><li>the act of feeding</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кърмъ</i> (kŭrmŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrmъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stern (rear end of a ship or other vessel)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadwinner, supporter (of the family)</li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wet nurse</li></ol><p><i>корми́л(ец)</i> (kormíl(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) place where one feeds or eats</li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helm, rudder</li></ol><p><i>корма́</i> (kormá) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed</li><li>to keep, to maintain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrmiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, to eat</li><li>to live <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeding</li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stern, aft</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) forage, feed, fodder</li></ol><p><i>корма́</i> (kormá) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p><p><i>корм</i> (korm) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cormophyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeding trough</li><li>feeding rack, manger</li><li>sinecure, cushy number</li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nautical) helmsman (he who is responsible for steering a ship)</li><li>(stilted,&#32;figuratively) helmsman, leader (of a country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кръмьчии</i> (krŭmĭčii). By surface analysis, <i>корма́</i> (kormá) +&lrm; <i>-чий</i> (-čij); the helm or rudder was usually in the stern of the ship. Compare <i>корми́ло</i> (kormílo, “helm, rudder”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>корм</i> (korm).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feeding</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) place where wild animals, pack horses, etc. are fed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) food</li></ol><p><i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёжка</i> (-jóžka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) cornflakes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>corn flakes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhizome (a horizontal underground stem of some plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) root</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edible root, root vegetable</li></ol><p><i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>плод</i> (plod)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical,&#32;animate) cornet</li><li>(music,&#32;inanimate) cornet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gherkin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cornichon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) cornflakes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cornflakes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, chest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коробъ</i> (korobŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korbъ</i> (“basket”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peddler, hawker</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́роб</i> (kórob, “basket”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warp</li><li>to grate, to jar</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to repulse, to disgust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korbiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerb-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to turn”). Likely related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫpъ</i> (“concise, compact”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, case</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) gearbox</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коробъка</i> (korobŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korbъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ко́роб</i> (kórob) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmission assembly, gear box, gearbox, transmission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmission assembly, gear box, gearbox, transmission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>коро́бкой</i> (koróbkoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small box</li></ol><p><i>ко́роб</i> (kórob) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small box</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) boll, capsule, fruitcase</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) pocket, sandwich</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́бка</i> (koróbka) (к → оч) + <i>-к</i> (-k).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ко́роб</i> (kórob).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;informal) cow; a fat, clumsy or dull woman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korva</i>; related to English <i>horn</i> and <i>hart</i> (“male red deer”), Welsh <i>carw</i> (“deer”) and Latin <i>cervus</i> (“deer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cow; bovine</li></ol><p><i>коро́ва</i> (koróva) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коро́ва</i> (koróva)</li></ol><p><i>коро́ва</i> (koróva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowshed, cowhouse, byre</li></ol><p><i>коро́ва</i> (koróva) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) bovine spongiform encephalopathy, mad cow disease</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feltwort, mullein (<i>Verbascum</i> spp.)</li><li>cow manure</li></ol><p><i>коро́ва</i> (koróva) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коро́ва</i> (koróva): (small) cow</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́в(а)</i> (koróv(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark beetle</li></ol><p><i>кора́</i> (korá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>korokke (a Japanese dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>コロッケ</i> (korokke).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chess,&#32;informal) queen (Russian abbreviation: Ф)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>królowa</i>. Remodeled after <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) prince, king's son</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>королевичь</i> (korolevičĭ); by surface analysis, <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-евич</i> (-evič, “son of”); cognate with Polish <i>królewicz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) princess, king's daughter</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) queen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>королевьна</i> (korolevĭna); by surface analysis, <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-е́вна</i> (-évna, “daughter of”); cognate with Polish <i>królewna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>royal (relating to a monarch or their family)</li></ol><p><i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вский</i> (-évskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingdom</li><li>monarchy, kingship</li></ol><p><i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вство</i> (-évstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) king (Russian abbreviation: Кр)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) king</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>король</i> (korolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldcrest, kinglet (<i>Regulus</i>)</li><li>blood orange</li><li>a variety of Japanese persimmon (<i>Diospyros kaki</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́ль</i> (korólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying pole</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>коро́мисло</i> (korómyslo), Belarusian <i>каро́мысла</i> (karómysla), каро́мысел (karómysjel), Polish <i>koromysło</i>. Related to <i>корма́</i> (kormá, “stern, aft”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carrying pole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown (royal, imperial or princely headdress)</li><li>monarchy</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) corona</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) crown (prize, title)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) <i>SARS-CoV-2</i> (“virus”) &#47; <i>2019-nCoV</i>, or <i>COVID-19</i> (“disease”) (Short for <i>коронавирус</i> (koronavirus).)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>corōna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;politics) covidiocy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>virology</i>) coronavirus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coronavirus</i>, superficially equivalent to <i>коро́на</i> (koróna, “crown”) +&lrm; <i>ви́рус</i> (vírus, “virus”), though Russian compounding would yield короновирус (koronovirus) due to sandhi rules. The latter word is still encountered in some contexts, though it is proscribed and considered poor usage.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coronavirus</li></ol><p><i>коронави́рус</i> (koronavírus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Coronaviridae</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coronation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown <i>(of a tooth)</i></li></ol><p><i>коро́на</i> (koróna) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crown</li><li>crowning, signature, best, greatest</li></ol><p><i>коро́на</i> (koróna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purulent scabs from scabies</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) crust</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) oddity, eccentricity (<i>in behavior or character</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korsta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corncrake, crake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korstělь</i>. Cognate to Bulgarian <i>кра́стел</i> (krástel), Czech <i>chřástal</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>крастѣль</i> (krastělĭ) and Polish <i>chruściel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to while away, to pass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij, “short”)</li></ol><p><i>коро́т(кий)</i> (korót(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short, brief</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) intimate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kortъkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shortly, briefly, in brief</li><li>short, close (length of cut)</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o), with stress shift in the adverb paralleling the short neuter adjective.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short circuit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a short blade, short-bladed</li></ol><p><i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>клино́к</i> (klinók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-armed</li></ol><p><i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too short, shorter than necessary (<i>of clothing and shoes</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shorty, short person</li></ol><p><i>коро́т(кий)</i> (korót(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ы́шка</i> (-ýška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ко́ротко</i> (kórotko)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) in other words, long story short (as an introductory word)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) so, anyway (as an introductory word)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(potentially&#32;impolite,&#32;colloquial) right; signpost word to change the subject in a discussion or discourse</li><li>(<i>impolite&#32;when opponent has finished their speech</i>) a request to repeat statement in layman's terms; without fuss or needless words</li><li>(<i>impolite&#32;when opponent has not finished their speech</i>) a request to shorten speech drastically; to conclude immediately</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>коро́че говоря́</i> (koróče govorjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in brief, in short</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) right; signpost word to change the subject in a discussion or discourse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́рка</i> (kórka): (thin) crust; bark; incrustation</li><li>diminutive of <i>кора́</i> (korá): (thin) crust; bark; incrustation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diploma, certificate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) book cover</li></ol><p><i>ко́рка</i> (kórka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work hard/meticulously/thoroughly (on something), to pore over, to plug away</li></ol><p>Related to <i>корпа́ть</i> (korpátʹ, “to mend clothes, to patch”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrpati</i> (“to patch”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrpa</i> (“a patch, a cloth”), via semantic shift: <i>to patch</i> → <i>to work</i> → <i>to work hard, to pore over</i>. Related to Ukrainian <i>ко́рпати</i> (kórpaty, “to dig”), Serbo-Croatian <i>krpati</i> (“to patch, to mend”), Bulgarian <i>къ́рпа</i> (kǎ́rpa, “a towel”), Serbo-Croatian <i>krpa</i> (“a rag, a dishcloth”), Lithuanian <i>karpýti</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;colloquial) blanket, counterpane</li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>көрпе</i> (körpe) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corporate, corporative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) corporation, company</li><li>association, organization (group of people with a common interest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat, obese, corpulent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpulence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) body, trunk</li><li>frame, case, chassis, body (the hard exterior housing or frame of some device or equipment)</li><li>hull (<i>of a ship or armored vehicle</i>)</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) package (<i>of an integrated circuit</i>)</li><li>building (in a group of similar buildings belonging to the same institution or housing development)</li><li>center, ward (an institutional building, or wing of a building, which is dedicated to a specific purpose)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) corpus</li><li>(military,&#32;politics) corps</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>corpus</i> (“body”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbine casing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) corpus, base, etc. see <i>ко́рпус</i> (kórpus)</li></ol><p><i>ко́рпус</i> (kórpus) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correction, amendment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to adjust</li><li>to proofread</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>correct</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correction, correcting</li></ol><p><i>корректи́ровать</i> (korrektírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) correctly, properly</li><li>consistently, validly</li><li>politely, courteously</li></ol><p><i>корре́ктный</i> (korréktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correctness</li></ol><p><i>корре́ктный</i> (korréktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) correct, proper</li><li>consistent, valid</li><li>polite, courteous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>korrekt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proofreader</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Korrektor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) proofreader</li></ol><p><i>корре́ктор</i> (korréktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proofreading</li><li>proof, printer's proof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correction (the act of correcting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary&#32;or&#32;mathematics,&#32;intransitive&#32;or&#32;rare,&#32;transitive) to correlate [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>korrelieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) correlation</li><li>correlative</li></ol><p><i>корреля́ция</i> (korreljácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correlation (measure of relationship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reporter, journalist</li><li>correspondent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correspondence, mail (parcels and letters)</li><li>correspondence (postal or other written communications)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spanish-style bullfighting, corrida</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>corrida</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corrosion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruptness</li></ol><p><i>коррумпи́рованный</i> (korrumpírovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) corrupt, corrupted</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>corrumpere</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, to bribe</li></ol><p>Likely derived from German <i>korrumpieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a corrupt official</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) corruption</li><li>corrupt</li></ol><p><i>корру́пция</i> (korrúpcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) corruption</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>corruptio</i>. Cf. Polish <i>korupcja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodice (<i>a kind of under waist stiffened with whalebone, etc., worn especially by women; a corset</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>corsage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a corsac fox</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a Central Asian person; especially one perceived to be a backwards yokel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;karsak</i> (“corsak fox”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corsair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>corsaro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corset (woman's garment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>corset</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tennis court, court</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>court</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procession, cortege, motorcade</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;set theory) tuple</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cortège</i>, from Italian <i>corteggio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) dirk</li></ol><p>Attested early 18th century in correspondence regarding Russia's purchase of weapons from Western Europe. The etymology is uncertain; there are several hypotheses.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cortical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haunches</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corundum</li></ol><p>From Tamil <i>குருந்தம்</i> (kuruntam, “ruby, sapphire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>korfball (team ballgame in which one scores by throwing the ball into a basket)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>korfbal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>naval</i>) the top part of the stempost</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) stub, stump of a tree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cramp, spasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uproot, to root out, to extirpate (to pull up by the roots)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrčevati</i>, related to <i>корч</i> (korč, “stub, stump of a tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>to bend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play, to pretend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrčiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ко́рча</i> (kórča) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to writhe, to squirm</li></ol><p><i>ко́рчить</i> (kórčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) tavern, inn where spirits are sold (in Ukraine or Belarus before the Russian Revolution)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrčьma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite <i>(bird)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mercenary, selfish, self-interested, greedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koristьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, profit; self-interest, greed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kȍrystь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>log washer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trough</li></ol><p>From <i>коры́то</i> (korýto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing tub, trough</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) old boat, old ship, old tub, old car</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koryto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measles</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kóry</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>кір</i> (kir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smelt (a fish from the Osmeridae family&#59; most commonly, of species <i>Osmerus eperlanus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Finnic language; compare to Finnish <i>kuore</i>, Karelian <i>kuoreh</i>, Veps <i>koreh</i>, Livvi <i>kuorʹoi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) smelt (a fish from the Osmeridae family&#59; most commonly, of species <i>Osmerus eperlanus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ко́рюшка</i> (kórjuška) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gnarled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clumsy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pockmarked</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crooked</li></ol><p><i>кора́</i> (korá) +&lrm; <i>-я́вый</i> (-jávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Koryak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ethnic Korean in Russia and other post-Soviet states; <i>Koryo-saram</i> (<i>used as an autonym</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>고려 사람</i> (goryeo saram), from <i>고려(高麗)</i> (Goryeo) + <i>사람</i> (saram, “person”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to warp</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to writhe (in pain)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to make indignant, to irritate</li></ol><p>Unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braid, plait, tress, pigtail, queue (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li>scythe</li><li>spit, sandbank, bank, bar (a narrow, low-lying landform jutting into a body of water)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) braid (<i>of strands</i>)</li><li>Xhosa</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коса</i> (kosa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosa</i>.</p><p>From the Xhosa name of the language <i>isiXhosa</i>.</p><p>&nbsp;<b>коса (язык)</b> on Russian&nbsp;Wikipedia</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) someone who mows, a mower</li><li>(slang,&#32;inanimate) a grand, 1000 rubles</li></ol><p>From <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killer whale, orca (<i>Orcinus orca</i>)</li><li>falcated duck (<i>Anas falcata</i>)</li><li>naked catfish, bagrid catfish (Bagridae spp., Siluriformes).</li><li>obsolete spelling of <i>каса́тка</i> (kasátka); barn swallow</li></ol><p>From <i>коса́т</i> (kosát, “oryx”) (<i>dated</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). By surface analysis, <i>коса́</i> (kosá, “scythe”) +&lrm; <i>-а́тка</i> (-átka). A calque of Latin <i>orca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slash ("&#47;" character)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) death</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) reported speech, indirect speech (a form of speech used to express what another has said)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indirectly</li></ol><p><i>ко́свенный</i> (kósvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oblique (not direct in descent&#59; not following the line of father and son&#59; collateral)</li><li>indirect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>косвенъ</i> (kosvenŭ), from the equivalent of Russian <i>косо́й</i> (kosój) + <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosvenъ</i> (“indirect”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblique case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mower (machine for mowing grass or grain)</li></ol><p>From <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>trigonometry</i>) cosine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mow, to cut</li><li>to mow down, to wipe out, to decimate</li><li>to twist, to slant (eyes, mouth)</li><li>to squint, to look asquint</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to evade, to elude, to dodge</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to pretend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kositi</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>коси́ти</i> (kosýty), Belarusian <i>касі́ць</i> (kasícʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look sideways</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to look with an unfavorable eye (<i>at</i>), to look askance (<i>at</i>)</li><li>to become slanted, to deviate from a straight line, to become crooked</li></ol><p><i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to slant, to squint”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to mow”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braid, plait</li><li>tuft (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коса́</i> (kosá): (small) plait, pigtail</li></ol><p><i>коса́</i> (kosá) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy</li></ol><p><i>ко́смы</i> (kósmy) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmetic (any substances applied to enhance the external color or texture of the skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cosmetic</li><li>cosmetic, superficial, skin-deep</li></ol><p><i>космет-</i> (kosmet-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cosmetic bag</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) female cosmetician, cosmetologist, beautician</li></ol><p><i>косме́тика</i> (kosmétika) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmetologist, beautician (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space station</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) space, cosmos; cosmic</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) extremely big, extremely high</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spacecraft, spaceship, space vehicle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronautics</i>) space food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outer space (region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>science fiction</i>) space marine</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́смос</i> (kósmos, “space”) +&lrm; <i>деса́нт</i> (desánt, “landing troops”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmodrome, spaceport</li></ol><p>First attested in 1963. Formed from <i>ко́смос</i> (kósmos) (from Ancient Greek <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos)) and -дро́м, modelled on <i>аэродро́м</i> (aerodróm) (from Ancient Greek <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos); see also <i>ипподро́м</i> (ippodróm), <i>велодро́м</i> (velodróm)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmologist</li></ol><p>From <i>космо-</i> (kosmo-) + <i>-лог</i> (-log).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmology (study of the physical universe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmonaut (the Russian or Soviet equivalent of an astronaut)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) a law enforcement officer in protective uniforms without identification marks</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “universe”) + <i>ναύτης</i> (naútēs, “sailor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmonautics/astronautics, space exploration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmopolitan, cosmopolite</li><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;euphemistic) Jew</li></ol><p>Via French <i>cosmopolite</i> from Latin <i>cosmopolītēs</i> from Koine Greek <i>κοσμοπολίτης</i> (kosmopolítēs, “citizen of the world”), from Ancient Greek <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “world”) + <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs, “citizen”).</p><p>(<i>slur</i>): Used since the Soviet 1948 anti-cosmopolitan campaign, from the propaganda trope безродный космополит (bezrodnyj kosmopolit, “rootless cosmopolitan”): "rootless" being used to refer to those who were not "Soviet" or "communist" enough. This term was used to collectively accuse Jewish dissidents and artists of treason against the Soviet Union, or punish anyone who critised Stalin. Ultimately, the slur originated in the 19th century in Tsarist Russia by the writer Vissarion Belinsky, to condemn writers who were seen as not "Russian" enough.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmopolitanism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cosmopolitisme</i> from Latin <i>cosmopolītēs, cosmopolītismus</i> from Koine Greek <i>κοσμοπολίτης</i> (kosmopolítēs, “citizen of the world”), from Ancient Greek <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “world”) + <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs, “citizen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space, cosmos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “universe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosmocentrism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mane; disheveled locks; matted hair, mop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosmъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethargy, inertia, resistance to change</li></ol><p><i>ко́сный</i> (kósnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (make physical contact with)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to concern, to have to do with</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to affect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>коснѫти сѧ</i> (kosnǫti sę), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosnǫti sę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) sedentary, lazy, slow</li><li>rigid, resistant to change</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>късьнъ</i> (kŭsĭnŭ, “slow, late”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъsьnъ</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>къ́сно</i> (kǎ́sno, “late”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ка̏сно</i> (“late”), Slovene <i>kəsȃn</i> (“careless, slow, late”) (feminine <i>kəsnà</i>), also <i>kəsə̀n</i>. Further cognate with Lithuanian <i>kùšlas</i> (“frail, wretched”) (also <i>kušlùs</i>), Latvian <i>kusls</i> (“weak, small and tender”), Latvian <i>kust</i> (“to tire”) (1sg. <i>kùstu</i>, 1sg. past <i>kusu</i>), Old Prussian <i>ucka-kuslaisin</i> (“the weakest”, acc. sg.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slantwise, obliquely, aslant, askew, asquint</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, lopsided</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become lop-sided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kosovorotka</li></ol><p>From <i>косо́й</i> (kosój, “skewed”) +&lrm; <i>во́рот</i> (vórot, “collar”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strabismus</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cross-eyed</li></ol><p><i>кос(о́й)</i> (kos(ój), “twisted”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “an eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hillside</li><li>slope</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanting, oblique, skewed (inclined at an angle to the plane&#59; not straight, not sheer)</li><li>twisted, wry, crooked</li><li>to the side, not centered</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) distrustful, suspicious, unfriendly (gaze, glance, etc.)</li><li>strabismic, suffering from strabismus, cross-eyed, squinting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cross-eyed person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hare</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) drunk (police slang)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosъ</i>. Per Vasmer, derived from <i>коса́</i> (kosá, “plait, braid”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ко́сий</i> (kósyj), Serbo-Croatian <i>ко̑с</i>, Czech <i>kosý</i>, Polish <i>kosy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wry-mouthed, crooked-mouthed</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bunglesome, butterfingered, club-handed</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oblique-angled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosplayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cosplayer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cosplay</li></ol><p>From <i>коспле́й</i> (kospléj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosplay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cosplay</i>, from Japanese <i>コスプレ</i> (kosupure), from English <i>costume play</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cosplay</li></ol><p>From <i>коспле́й</i> (kosplɛ́j) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosplayer</li></ol><p>From <i>коспле́й</i> (kospléj) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Costa Rican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spleenwort (<i>Asplenium</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>костёр</i> (kostjór, “campfire, bonfire”)</li></ol><p><i>костёр</i> (kostjór) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gambling game that uses dice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bony; osseous</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold, to berate, to lambast, to chide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Death, the Grim Reaper</li></ol><p>From <i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ля́вая</i> (-ljávaja); compare to Polish <i>kostucha</i>. Literally, “the bony one”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bony, gaunt (of a body&#58; very thin, with visible bones under the skin)</li><li>bony (of food&#58; full of bones)</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ля́вый</i> (-ljávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ossuary (a place where the dead are buried)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bone; osseous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kostьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marrow, bone marrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bone-charring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кость</i> (kostʹ): (little) bone</li><li>kernel, stone, seed (stone of certain fruits, such as peaches or plums)</li><li>counting bead (abacus)</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg of beef</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kostroma</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) resident of Kostroma (city and oblast in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Кострома́</i> (Kostromá) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crutch</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) rail spike</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) tail skid</li><li>(<i>programming&#32;slang</i>) kludge, workaround, hack</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ль</i> (-ýlʹ). Compare Polish <i>kostur</i>, <i>koścień</i>.</p><p>For the word formation with suffix <i>-ы́ль</i> (-ýlʹ) compare <i>горбы́ль</i> (gorbýlʹ) from <i>горб</i> (gorb). Also compare <i>моты́ль</i> (motýlʹ), <i>ковы́ль</i> (kovýlʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone</li><li>(<i>games</i>) dice, die</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кость</i> (kostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kostь</i>. Compare Latin <i>costa</i>, whence English <i>coast</i> and English <i>costa</i> (“rib-like structure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suit (suit of clothes, men’s suit or women’s suit)</li><li>costume</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>costume</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>consuetudo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>костю́м</i> (kostjúm): suit</li></ol><p><i>костю́м</i> (kostjúm) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skeleton; bones</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) backbone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone bramble</li></ol><p><i>костяно́й</i> (kostjanój) +&lrm; <i>-ика</i> (-ika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stone bramble</li></ol><p><i>костяни́ка</i> (kostjaníka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bone</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) knuckle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abacus bead</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small ivory, bone, or plastic object, boardgame piece</li></ol><p><i>кость</i> (kostʹ, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman Catholic church (<i>in Poland or the Baltic states</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kościół</i> (“church”), from Czech <i>kostel</i>, from Old High German <i>kastel</i>, from Latin <i>castellum</i> (“castle, fort”), because medieval churches were fortified. Compare Ukrainian <i>костьо́л</i> (kostʹól) / <i>косте́л</i> (kostél) and Belarusian <i>касцёл</i> (kascjól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>campfire, bonfire, wood fire</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) chock</li><li>brome grass (<i>Bromus</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chess grass, rye brome (<i>Bromus secalinus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kostrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roe deer</li><li>(regional,&#32;agriculture) a type of sokha or Russian ard equipped with a coulter and a one-sided mouldboard; an intermediate development between a sokha and a modern plough.</li><li>(regional,&#32;agriculture) a short-handled scythe</li></ol><p>&nbsp;<b>косули</b> on Russian&nbsp;Wikipedia</p><p>From the earlier form <i>козу́ля</i> (kozúlja), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koza</i> (“goat”), referring to the similarity in build and eye shape. Compare to the derivation of Latin <i>capreolus</i> (“a small roe deer, roebuck”) from Latin <i>capra</i> (“she-goat”).</p><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój, “slanted”) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triangular headscarf (<i>traditionally of calico</i>), kerchief, babushka</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) Klondike (solitaire)</li></ol><p><i>косы́ня</i> (kosýnja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>косо́й</i> (kosój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коса́</i> (kosá): (small) braid, plait (or hair)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>construction</i>) jamb, cant, cheek, doorpost</li><li>a V-shaped flock (<i>of flying birds</i>)</li><li>shoal, school (<i>of fish</i>)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a horse herd with one stallion</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) joint, a marijuana cigarette</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) fault, mistake</li></ol><p><i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кося́к</i> (kosják)</li></ol><p><i>кося́к</i> (kosják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomcat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>котъ</i> (kotŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotъ</i>.</p><p>Should be separated from <i>коти́ться</i> (kotítʹsja) (related to <i>око́т</i> (okót), <i>catulus</i>), the connection with this word is secondary.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Schrödinger's cat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig in a poke</li></ol><p>Literally, “cat in a sack”. Calque of French <i>(acheter) chat en poche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ivorian (relating to Côte d'Ivoire or Ivory Coast, the Ivorian people or their language)</li></ol><p><i>Кот-д’Ивуа́р</i> (Kot-d’Ivuár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ко̀т-д’ивуа́рский</i> (kòt-d’ivuárskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) cotangent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>complementi tangens</i> (literally “tangent of the complement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kotatsu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>炬燵</i> (こたつ, kotatsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ivorian (resident or citizen of Ivory Coast).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dixie (a large iron pot, used in the army), kettle, pot</li><li>bowler hat, derby</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) bean, upper storey, dome (one's brain or head)</li></ol><p><i>котёл</i> (kotjól) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiler room, boiler house</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brazier, coppersmith</li></ol><p>From котёл (kotjól, “cauldron, boiler”) and -щик (-ščik, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кот</i> (kot): kitty, little male cat</li><li>fur seal (animal)</li><li>sealskin, seal (fur)</li><li>darling, dear, sweet person</li><li><i>(usually in plural)</i> filter-avoidance spelling of <i>нарко́тик</i> (narkótik)</li></ol><p><i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotillion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cotillon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) cotinga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) to quote, to set the exchange price of (goods, securities or currency)</li></ol><p>French <i>coter</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) to be valued (<i>of goods, securities or currency</i>)</li><li>(finance,&#32;intransitive) to circulate on an exchange, to be listed</li><li>to be acclaimed, to be highly rated</li></ol><p><i>коти́ровать</i> (kotírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(price) quotation; listing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>кот</i> (kot, “tomcat”)</li></ol><p>From <i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meat patty,</li><li>cutlet, pork chop</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bomb, a large sum of money</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>côtelette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>котле́та</i> (kotléta): small meat patty, small cutlet</li></ol><p><i>котле́та</i> (kotléta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation pit, foundation ditch, trench</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) hollow, kettle</li></ol><p><i>котёл</i> (kotjól, “cauldron”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nepeta (any plant of the genus <i>Nepeta</i>)</li><li>ground-ivy (<i>Glechoma hederacea</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кот</i> (kot)</li></ol><p><i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat café</li></ol><p>First attested in 2011. From <i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кафе́</i> (kafɛ́).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) knapsack, bindle</li></ol><p><i>кото́ма</i> (kotóma, “knapsack”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>catlike</li></ol><p><i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative pronoun</i>) what, which (among a specific series)</li><li>(<i>relative pronoun</i>) which, that, who, whom</li><li>(indefinite pronoun,&#32;often about time) not the first, more than one</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>которꙑи</i> (kotoryi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotorъjь</i>, <i>&#42;koterъjь</i>, definite of <i>&#42;&#42;kotorъ</i>, <i>&#42;&#42;koterъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷóteros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitten therapy</li></ol><p>First attested in 2001. From <i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>терапи́я</i> (terapíja, “therapy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detached house, single-family house (a detached, typically two-floor suburban or exurban house with an attached plot of land)</li><li>cottage (<i>in British or historical context</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cottage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of shoes, made of leather and fur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) cat</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>кот</i> (kot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>кот</i> (kot)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cauldron</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) boiler (closed vessel for generating steam or heating water)</li><li>shared supply of food</li><li>(<i>military</i>) A very large encirclement; pocket, entrapment</li><li>(card games,&#32;slang) pot (money available to be won in a card hand)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotьlъ</i>, from Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (katils, “kettle”), from Latin <i>catillus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitten</li></ol><p><i>кот</i> (kot) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>котёнок</i> (kotjónok)</li></ol><p><i>котёнок</i> (kotjónok) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kowtow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 叩頭／叩头 (<i>kòutóu</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;rare) coach, trainer, mentor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffee (in the form of a beverage)</li><li>coffee (in the form of beans)</li></ol><p>First attested in late 17th or early 18th century. Borrowed from a West Germanic language, either English <i>coffee</i>, German <i>Koffee</i>, or directly from Dutch <i>koffie</i>, Ultimately from Italian <i>caffè</i>, from Arabic قَهْوَة (qahwa). Doublet of <i>кафе́</i> (kafé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffeemaker (kitchen apparatus used to brew and filter coffee)</li><li>percolator, coffee machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caffeine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;humorous) coffee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffee pot</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́фе</i> (kófe, “coffee”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink coffee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coffee</li><li>coffee-colored</li></ol><p><i>кофе́й-</i> (koféj-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) coffeehouse, coffee shop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffeehouse</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́фе</i> (kófe) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), with й appearing as in <i>кофе́йный</i> (koféjnyj), <i>кофе́йник</i> (koféjnik); see <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A coffee vending machine.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffee grinder</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́фе</i> (kófe) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́фе</i> (kófe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) coffee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk, chest</li><li>equipment bag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coffre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jersey, cardigan (garment knitted from wool, worn over the upper body)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́фта</i> (kófta): (usually women's) knitted jacket, cardigan</li></ol><p><i>ко́фта</i> (kófta) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>koch <i>(boat of Pomors)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of cabbage</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) (human) head, noggin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kočanъ</i>.</p><p>(<i>head</i>): Compare typologically <i>ре́па</i> (répa), <i>ты́ква</i> (týkva), <i>ты́ковка</i> (týkovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) head of cabbage</li></ol><p><i>коча́н</i> (kočán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead a nomad's life</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) to migrate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кочевати</i> (kočevati), a Turkic borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;köč-</i>. Contains the suffix <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ). Compare also Belarusian <i>качаваць</i> (kačavacʹ), Ukrainian <i>кочувати</i> (kočuvaty), Czech <i>kočovat</i>, Slovak <i>kočovať</i>, Polish <i>koczować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nomad</li></ol><p><i>кочева́ть</i> (kočevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nomadic</li></ol><p>From <i>коч-</i> (koč-) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój); see <i>кочева́ть</i> (kočevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obstinate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pose, to put on airs</li></ol><p>From <i>кочевря́га</i> (kočevrjága, “poser, affected person”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Per Vasmer, possibly from <i>ко-</i> (ko-, “who”) +&lrm; <i>че-</i> (če-, “what”) +&lrm; <i>врать</i> (vratʹ, “to lie, to tell lies”). Compare <i>кочема́зый</i> (kočemázyj, “dirty, grimy; tanned, scorched”), possibly with the same prefixes + a derivative of <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ, “to smear; to soil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoker</li></ol><p>Produced by dissimilation of earlier <i>&#42;кочерга́р</i> (&#42;kočergár), from <i>кочерга́</i> (kočergá). For the <i>р</i> falling compare <i>гонча́р</i> (gončár) &lt; <i>гърньчаръ</i> (gŭrnĭčarŭ).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>stoker</i> (“a person who stokes; a poker”).</p><p>Note the French counterpart <i>chauffeur</i>, from which many descendants arise, including <i>шофёр</i> (šofjór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become numb from the cold</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́чень</i> (kóčenʹ) (variant of <i>коча́н</i> (kočán)) + <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>коле́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (kolétʹ), <i>околе́ть</i>&nbsp;pf (okolétʹ) (&lt; <i>кол</i> (kol)), <i>цепене́ть</i> (cepenétʹ) (akin to <i>цеп</i> (cep)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poker (metal rod for adjusting burning logs or coals in a fire)</li><li>(<i>automotive&#32;slang</i>) steering-wheel lock, club lock</li><li>(<i>automotive&#32;slang</i>) stickshift, manual gear lever</li><li>(<i>automotive&#32;slang</i>) column-mounted shifter</li></ol><p>Per ESSJa, inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kočerьga&#47;&#42;kočьrga</i>.</p><p>According to Vasmer, inherited from a Proto-Slavic form, probably with the original sense of “stump, stalk”; compare Ukrainian <i>кочерга́</i> (kočerhá), <i>кучу́рга</i> (kučúrga), Polish <i>koczarga</i>, Belarusian <i>качарга́</i> (kačarhá), Russian <i>коко́ра</i> (kokóra), <i>коча́н</i> (kočán). Alternatively (but less likely), perhaps a Turkic borrowing; compare Tatar <i>күчәргә</i> (küçärgä, “to move”), Southern Altai <i>кӧчӱр</i> (köčür, “to relocate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) rooster, cock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кочьтъ</i> (kočĭtŭ), ultimately from a suffixal form of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kokotъ</i>; compare Belarusian <i>ко́чат</i> (kóčat), Polish <i>koczot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>коча́н</i> (kočán): head of cabbage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hummock, tussock, mound, hillock</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>колча</i> (kolča) with the Lukerya effect.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Kuban,&#32;Astrakhan) (tribal) ram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) camp of Zaporozhian cossacks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepfold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;košara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;obsolete) cat skinner, cat catcher</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) catlover, cat person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cat shelter, cat colony</li></ol><p><i>ко́шка</i> (kóška) +&lrm; <i>-а́т(ый)</i> (-át(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cat; cat’s, feline, catlike</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́шка</i> (kóška) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), with unexpected stressed -а́-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat café</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) burse, bag, purse (especially woven one)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;košelь</i> from <i>&#42;košь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallet, purse, billfold</li></ol><p>From <i>коше́ль</i> (košélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) kosher</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>כּשר</i> (kosher), from Hebrew <i>כָּשֵׁר</i> (kashér).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ко́шка</i> (kóška): kitty; little cat; pussy-cat</li></ol><p>From <i>ко́шка</i> (kóška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cat-o'-nine-tails</li><li>(technical,&#32;inanimate) grapnel, drag</li><li>(technical,&#32;inanimate) grapple fork</li><li>(cranes,&#32;inanimate) car, trolley, carriage</li><li>(geology,&#32;inanimate) spit, bar</li><li>(climbing,&#32;in the plural,&#32;inanimate) crampons, climbing irons, climbing grapplers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>ко́шка</i> (kóška), from Old East Slavic <i>ко́шька</i> (kóšĭka), from unattested <i>&#42;ко́чька</i> (&#42;kóčĭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòťьka</i>, from <i>&#42;kòťь</i>, from <i>&#42;kòtъ</i>. Cognate with Old Ruthenian <i>ко́шка</i> (kóška), Ukrainian <i>кі́шка</i> (kíška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large felt mat</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language. Compare Azerbaijani <i>qoşmaq</i>, Turkish <i>koşmak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightmare</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) disaster, horror (something extremely unpleasant)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>it's terrible!</li></ol><p>Connected to French <i>cauchemar</i>, from Middle French <i>cauchemare</i>, from Old French <i>cauquemare</i>. First element from Latin <i>calcare</i> (“to trample, tread on”); second element from Middle Dutch <i>mare</i> (“phantom, spirit, nightmare”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marǭ</i> (“nightmare, incubus”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”). See <i>cauchemar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to frighten, to make someone's life nightmarish</li></ol><p>From <i>кошмар</i> (košmar) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) (literally, "to nightmare").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nightmarish</li><li>dreadful</li></ol><p><i>кошма́р</i> (košmár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ration, allowance, funds</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) basket, woven bag</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>), (<i>slang</i>) bag, hag (referring to an ugly woman)</li></ol><p>From a no-longer extant word cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>кошь</i> (košĭ, “basket”) + <i>-ёлка</i> (-jólka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blasphemous, sacrilegious</li></ol><p><i>кощу́нство</i> (koščúnstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemy, sacrilege (desecration, profanation, misuse or violation of something sacred)</li></ol><p>From <i>кощу́н</i> (koščún) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to profane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) coenzyme</li></ol><p><i>ко-</i> (ko-) +&lrm; <i>энзи́м</i> (enzím)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coenzymatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coenzymatic</li></ol><p><i>коэнзи́м</i> (koenzím) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coefficient, factor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspect ratio (the ratio of width to height in a flat surface or 2-dimensional abstract construction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspect ratio (the ratio of an object's longest dimension to its next-longest dimension)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>красноарме́ец</i> (krasnoarméjec, “(military rank) Red Army man”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krabbô</i>, cognates include Dutch <i>krab</i>, German <i>Krabbe</i>, English <i>crab</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) crocket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Krabbe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab stick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab sticks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crab</li></ol><p><i>краб</i> (krab, “crab”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) crabber (a person who catches crabs)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) crabber (a boat used for catching crabs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crabber</li></ol><p>From <i>краболо́в</i> (krabolóv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stolen</li></ol><p><i>крад-</i> (krad-) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>local historian, specialist in local lore</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>краеве́дение</i> (krajevédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>local lore, local history</li></ol><p>From <i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) local lore</li></ol><p><i>краеве́д(ение)</i> (krajevéd(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) krai; regional</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) edge, border, frontier, boundary</li><li>marginal</li></ol><p><i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very important, paramount, key</li></ol><p>From <i>край</i> (kraj, “corner”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “corner”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); meaning "very important" from relational use in <i>краеуго́льный ка́мень</i> (krajeugólʹnyj kámenʹ, “cornerstone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornerstone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>край</i> (kraj): edge</li></ol><p><i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ешек</i> (-ešek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft, larceny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kraďa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(prosody,&#32;phonetics) crasis (the contraction of a vowel or diphthong at the end of a word with a vowel or diphthong at the start of the following word)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κρᾶσις</i> (krâsis, “mixture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>krajina (<i>used in the names of regions, a krajina is one of the regions inhabited mostly by Serbs in the former Yugoslavia</i>)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) outskirts, periphery, edge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krajina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, edge, brink, brim, fringe</li><li>side, rim</li><li>land, country</li><li>krai, territory (in Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krajь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extremely, very, too (much)</li></ol><p><i>кра́йний</i> (krájnij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outermost</li><li>extreme, utmost</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;colloquial) last, most recent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scapegoat, fall guy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krajьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing (sports&#58; person in such position)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extremity, extreme measure</li></ol><p><i>кра́йний</i> (krájnij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) foreskin</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>кра́йний</i> (krájnij, “outermost”) + <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ, “flesh”). Compare to Ukrainian <i>кра́йня плоть</i> (krájnja plotʹ) and Belarusian <i>кра́йняя плоць</i> (krájnjaja plocʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cracovian, Cracow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>krakowski</i>. By surface analysis, <i>Кра́ков</i> (Krákov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cracovian (native or inhabitant of Krakow) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Krakowianin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) mojibake (illegible, corrupt text, usually caused by mismatched encoding&#59; a very common problem with non-Roman scripts and fonts)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) weird looking animal, bizarre fairy-tale creature</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. The first part <i>крако-</i> may be related to <i>крокодил</i> (krokodil, “crocodile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mojibake</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;rare,&#32;card games) king</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>král</i>. Doublet of <i>король</i> (korolʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;card games) queen</li><li>(<i>low colloquial&#32;or&#32;folklore</i>) beauty, beautiful woman; someone's female lover/girlfriend</li></ol><p>Possibly from Polish <i>krala</i> (“queen (card games)”), from Czech <i>král</i> (“king”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>кра́ля</i> (králja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sedition, revolt</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something prohibited, forbidden (e.g. a thought, speech)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>крамола</i> (kramola), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kormola</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>коромола</i> (koromola).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) seditious, subversive, rebellious, mutinous</li></ol><p><i>крамо́ла</i> (kramóla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumpet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crumpet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) valve (a ball valve, a plug valve, or similar rotating device controlling flow through a pipe)</li><li>faucet, tap, spigot</li><li>(<i>machine</i>) crane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kraan</i> or from Low German <i>kran</i>. Cognate to German <i>Kran</i> (“crane”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cranial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кран</i> (kran): (small) valve, tap</li></ol><p><i>кран</i> (kran) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craniologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) craniopharyngioma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crane driver</li></ol><p><i>кран</i> (kran) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specks</li><li>speckled coloring (<i>e.g. on playing cards</i>)</li><li>alternative spelling of <i>крапп</i> (krapp)</li></ol><p>Ablaut to <i>кропи́ть</i> (kropítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nettle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopriva</i>, <i>&#42;kropiva</i>. This is an instance in which the vowel reduction <i>о</i> (o) &rarr; <i>а</i> (a) is reflected in spelling: the expected spelling is <i>кропи́ва</i> (kropíva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) wren</li></ol><p><i>крапи́ва</i> (krapíva, “nettle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nettle</li></ol><p><i>крапи́ва</i> (krapíva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speckle, speck, fleck, spot, dot</li></ol><p><i>крап</i> (krap) +&lrm; <i>-инка</i> (-inka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) madder</li><li>maroon</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) specks</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) speckled coloring</li></ol><p>From <i>крап</i> (krap, “madder”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj); also found as <i>крапп</i> (krapp). Borrowed from German <i>Krapp</i>.</p><p>From <i>крап</i> (krap, “specks”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maroon beret. A member of or the headdress worn by the members of the MVD Spetsnaz units of the USSR.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madder (<i>Rubia tinctorum</i>) and the pigment from it</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Krapp</i>, itself from Middle Dutch <i>crappe</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krappô</i> (“hook”), a reference to the plant’s hook-shaped thorns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speckled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) beauty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>краса</i> (krasa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krasa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) beauty, beautiful woman</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) good boy, good girl, attaboy, attagirl (said, often somewhat ironically, as a form of praise or to mean "well done", usually about a male)</li></ol><p>Suffixed derivative of <i>краса́</i> (krasá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beauty, a handsome man or beautiful object</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any beautiful object referred to with the feminine gender</li></ol><p><i>краса́в(ец)</i> (krasáv(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunk, beauty (beautiful male)</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) attaboy, well done (male, used for general encouragement)</li></ol><p><i>краса́вец</i> (krasávec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>краси́во</i> (krasívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj): most beautiful</li></ol><p><i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj); pretty</li></ol><p><i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>краси́во</i> (krasívo); prettily, nicely, beautifully</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>краси́во</i> (krasívo); pretty, nice, beautiful</li></ol><p><i>краси́во</i> (krasívo) +&lrm; <i>-енько</i> (-enʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beautifully</li></ol><p><i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li></ol><p><i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful, handsome</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) fine, admirable, noble</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>краса́</i> (krasá) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>краси́вий</i> (krasývyj), Belarusian <i>красі́вы</i> (krasívy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyeworks</li></ol><p><i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyer (one whose occupation is to dye)</li></ol><p><i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dye (stuff), colorant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to color, to paint, to dye</li><li>to adorn, to beautify, to decorate, to embellish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>красити</i> (krasiti) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krasiti</i>; the original sense was “to decorate”, as in Slavic cognates like Old Church Slavonic <i>красити</i> (krasiti), Polish <i>krasić</i>, Czech <i>krášlit</i>, Bulgarian <i>крася́</i> (krasjá), Macedonian <i>краси</i> (krasi).</p><p>Compare sense development to <i>кра́ска</i> (kráska, “paint”), original sense hypothesized to be “blush” or “decoration”, and to <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”), original sense “beautiful, good”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be covered or saturated with paint</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be wet with paint, to be freshly painted</li><li>to put on makeup, to wear makeup</li></ol><p><i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paint, dye, ink</li><li>colors</li><li>blush, flush (complexion)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>кра́ска</i> (kráska), from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;кра́съка</i> (&#42;krásŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kràsъka</i>, from <i>&#42;krāsà</i>.</p><p><i>краса́</i> (krasá, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), first attested in the 15th century; original sense is hypothesized to be “blush” or “decoration”.</p><p>Compare sense development to <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”), original sense “beautiful, good”, and to <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ, “to color”), original sense “to decorate”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;slang) five thousand ruble banknote</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red carpet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) socialist, communist</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) socialism, communism</li></ol><p>The word combination кра́сная (krásnaja) and зара́за (zaráza). Literal translation: The red scumbag, bastard / infection.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red cabbage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redcurrant (<i>Ribes rubrum</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>break line, indented line</li><li>rubric (a heading in a book highlighted in red)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj): red, reddish</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redden, to blush</li><li>to show red, to appear red</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear red, to show red</li></ol><p><i>красне́ть</i> (krasnétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of red and white colours individually</li></ol><p><i>кра́сн(ый)</i> (krásn(yj), “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auburn (reddish-brown)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) communo-fascist, communo-nationalist (of a syncretic political ideology combining communist ideas with nationalism or fascism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amaranth (color&#47;dye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Red Army man (soldier in the Red Army of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>Кра́сная а́рмия</i> (Krásnaja ármija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). By surface analysis, <i>красно-</i> (krasno-, “red”) +&lrm; <i>арме́ец</i> (arméjec, “infantryman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Red Army</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Krasnoarmeysk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>а́рмия</i> (ármija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) when referring to the Red Army; from <i>Красноарме́й(ск)</i> (Krasnoarméj(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) when referring to Krasnoarmeysk.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windbag, phrasemonger, rhetorician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) red guard (soldier in the Red Army of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>красно-</i> (krasno-, “red”) +&lrm; <i>гварде́ец</i> (gvardéjec, “guardsman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-eyed, having red or reddened eyes</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computer jargon,&#32;nominalized) user of the computer operating system Linux</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-breasted</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́сн(ый)</i> (krásn(yj), “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>груд(ь)</i> (grud(ʹ), “breast”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Krasnodar</li></ol><p><i>Краснода́р</i> (Krasnodár) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Krasnodar</li></ol><p><i>Краснода́р</i> (Krasnodár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redshift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) communist; socialist</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) communist; socialist</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “ass”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Red Star</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá, “star”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Red Indian, redskin (relating to persons of American Indian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ceanothus</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, nominative suffix), literally “red-rooter”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red cabbage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having red legs or feet</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́сн(ый)</i> (krásn(yj), “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ног(а́)</i> (nog(á), “leg or foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rednosed</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́сн(ый)</i> (krásn(yj), “red”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudd; redeye</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>перо́</i> (peró) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Bulgarian <i>червеноперка</i> (červenoperka), Macedonian <i>црвеноперка</i> (crvenoperka) (i.e. **червонопёрка), Slovene <i>rdečeperka</i> (akin to <i>рдеть</i> (rdetʹ)).</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>Rotfeder</i>, English <i>rudd</i> (akin to <i>rud</i>, <i>ruddy</i>, <i>ruddock</i>, <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>), Irish <i>ruán</i>; Turkish <i>kızılkanat</i> (<i>kızıl</i> + <i>kanat</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eloquently</li><li>expressively</li><li>conspicuously</li></ol><p><i>красноречи́вый</i> (krasnorečívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eloquent</li><li>expressive</li><li>telltale, conspicuous</li></ol><p><i>красноре́чие</i> (krasnoréčije) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eloquence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redness</li></ol><p><i>кра́сн(ый)</i> (krásn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salix acutifolia (Siberian violet-willow, long-leaved violet willow or sharp-leaf willow)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in&#32;compounds</i>) red-necked, having a red neck</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”) +&lrm; <i>ше́я</i> (šéja, “neck”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-cheeked</li></ol><p><i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>щека́</i> (ščeká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Krasnoyarsk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Krasnoyarsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Красноя́р(ск)</i> (Krasnojár(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubella</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red</li><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) beautiful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(history,&#32;politics) a Red; a member of the Red Guard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>красьнъ</i> (krasĭnŭ), <i>красьнꙑи</i> (krasĭnyi, “beautiful, good”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krasьnъ</i> (“beautiful”). The sense “red” is unique to Russian and did not develop in other Slavic descendants of <i>&#42;krasьnъ</i>; first attested in 1515 in Muscovite diplomatic correspondence, it serves as the primary sense of the word in the modern Russian language.</p><p>For sense development, compare <i>кра́ска</i> (kráska, “paint”), original sense hypothesized to be “blush” or “decoration”, and to <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ, “to color”), original sense “to decorate”. Compare this relation to Turkish <i>al</i> (“red”) which in old times also used to refer to paint in general.</p><p>Also compare Votic <i>kauniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chili, red pepper, paprika (spicy fresh or dried fruit of capsicum)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red light (a warning light, especially as a traffic signal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) red terror</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) The "red corner", a household shrine for images of Orthodox saints.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to stand out</li><li>to show off (beauty, looks, cloth, etc)</li><li>to enjoy looking at oneself (ex. in front of the mirror)</li></ol><p><i>красо-</i> (kraso-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty (quality of pleasing appearance)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>красота</i> (krasota), equivalent to <i>кра́с(ный)</i> (krás(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A caterpillar hunter, a beetle of the genus <i>Calosoma</i>.</li></ol><p><i>крас(ивый)</i> (kras(ivyj), “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тел(о)</i> (tel(o), “body”); calque of translingual <i>Calosoma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty (beautiful female)</li></ol><p><i>красота́</i> (krasotá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) paint; dye (<i>for clothing</i>)</li><li>colourful/colorful, coloured/colored</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) expressive, illustrative</li></ol><p><i>кра́ска</i> (kráska) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krasti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stalk, to prowl, to slink</li></ol><p><i>красть</i> (krastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) time, occurrence, -fold</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кратъ</i> (kratŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kortъ</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>-кра̄т</i> (e.g. <i>два́кра̄т</i> (“twice”)), Slovene <i>kràt</i> (gen. <i>kráta</i>), Czech <i>-krát</i> (e.g. <i>dvakrát</i> (“twice”)), Slovak <i>krát</i>, Polish <i>-kroć</i> (e.g. <i>dwakroć</i> (“twice”)), Upper Sorbian <i>-kroć</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>-krot</i>. More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>kar̃tas</i> (“time, occurrence”) (plural <i>kartai̇̃</i>), <i>kartà</i> (“layer, row”) (acc. <i>kar̃tą</i>), Latvian <i>kā̀rtа</i> (“layer, row”), Sanskrit <i>सकृत्</i> (sakṛ́t, “once”), <i>कृत्वस्</i>&nbsp;pl (kṛ́tvas, “times, occurrences”), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬐𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬝</i> (hakər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>t̰, “once”). Vasmer suggests that these terms are cognate with Lithuanian <i>kir̃sti</i> (“to chop”) (1sg. <i>kertù</i>), and notes the parallelism with Russian <i>раз</i> (raz) and <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”), and Lithuanian <i>sỹkis</i> (“time, occurrence”) and Russian <i>секу́</i> (sekú, “I cut, I chop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crater</li><li>krater (an ancient Greek vessel for mixing water with wine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breve (the diacritical mark ˘)</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́ткая</i> (krátkaja, “short”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short form (of an adjective)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brief</li><li>short</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кратъкъ</i> (kratŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kortъkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>коро́ткий</i> (korótkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>briefly</li></ol><p><i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short duration, brevity, shortness, short-term nature</li></ol><p><i>кратковре́менный</i> (kratkovrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brief, short-term, momentary, transitory, short-lived</li></ol><p><i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-term</li></ol><p><i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brevity, conciseness, shortness</li></ol><p>From <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) multiple</li></ol><p><i>крат</i> (krat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(finance,&#32;neologism) crowdfunding (pooling of money for a common goal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crowdfunding</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) krautrock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>krautrock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure, crash, ruin, bankruptcy</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) name-day woman or girl (woman or girl celebrating her name day, i.e. the day of the saint who she is named after)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Krach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) starch, amylum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>krochmal</i>, from German <i>Kraftmehl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starch</li></ol><p><i>крахма́л</i> (kraxmál) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) starch</li><li>starched</li></ol><p><i>крахма́л</i> (kraxmál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) tern</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) crush (person someone is in love with)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) crash (sudden malfunction in a computer program)</li><li>clipping of <i>краш-тест</i> (kraš-tɛ́st); crash test</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crush</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>crash</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) crash test</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the 1990s from English <i>crash test</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>краси́во</i> (krasívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winterfat (<i>Krascheninnikovia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Krascheninnikovia</i>, itself from <i>Крашени́нников</i> (Krašenínnikov, “Krasheninnikov”, surname).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Easter egg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painted, colored, dyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing slang</i>) to crash (a program)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crash</i> + <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crust (a large end-piece of a loaf of bread)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) creative</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) artistic</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) designed to stimulate one's imagination</li></ol><p>English <i>creative</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation, protégé</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrimp, prawn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crevette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shrimp, prawn</li></ol><p><i>креве́тка</i> (krevétka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loan</li><li>credit (<i>in the wider sense, not specifically in reference to credits and debits</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) credit, credibility</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) credit (<i>specifically in reference to credits and debits in double-entry accounting</i>)</li></ol><p>First appearance in 1703; original meaning, "authority". Per Vasmer, probably borrowed from German <i>Kredit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) credit card</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;finance) any bank card that can be used to pay for goods and services</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) banknote, bill</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) credit provider</li></ol><p><i>креди́т</i> (kredít) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>credit card (plastic card, with a magnetic strip)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) credit</li></ol><p><i>креди́т</i> (kredít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crediting, bank loans</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creditor, mortgagee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accounts payable (money that is owed by a party, and is counted by that party as a debit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) creditor</li><li>accounts payable</li></ol><p><i>кредито́р</i> (kreditór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>credo, creed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cruiser (naval vessel detached from the fleet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kruiser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cracker (salty or savory biscuit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cracker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(engineering,&#32;uncountable) cracking, thermal decomposition</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) installation for cracking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cracking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cream, creme</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crème</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaving cream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face cream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe polish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand lotion, hand cream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freckle remover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suntan lotion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation cream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crème brûlée</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crème brûlée</i> (literally “burnt cream”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crematorium, crematory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Krematorium</i>, from New Latin <i>crematorium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кре́млядь</i> (krémljadʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) flint</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) heart of stone, heart of flint, hard-hearted person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kremy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cremation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) Kremlebot, a Kremlin bot, a person hired to make pro-Kremlin comments on social networks or suspected in doing so.</li></ol><p><i>Кремль</i> (Kremlʹ, “Kremlin”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>бот</i> (bot, “bot”)</p><p>(compounding suffix -о/-е)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kremlin (a central fortress in medieval Russian cities)</li><li>(<i>capitalized</i>) The Kremlin (in Moscow)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кремль</i> (kremlĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kremľь</i>, probably related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kroma</i> (“edge”) (referring to the fortifications).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кремль)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Kremlin (in Moscow).</li><li>The government of Russia.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective) A term used to describe officials serving the needs of the Kremlin, not the people, and doing so blindly and inconsiderately.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Кремль</i> (Kremlʹ, “Kremlin”) +&lrm; <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore (vulgar)”). First used by the rock group Телеви́зор (Televízor) (i. e. "TV set") in a song called <i>«Заколоти́те подва́л»</i> («Zakolotíte podvál») (<i>Board up the basement</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kremlin</li></ol><p><i>Кремль</i> (Kremlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёвский</i> (-jóvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) silica, silicon dioxide</li></ol><p><i>креме́нь</i> (kreménʹ, “flint”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-зём</i> (-zjóm, combining form of <i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “earth, ground, soil”))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silicon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silicon</li></ol><p>From <i>креме́нь</i> (kreménʹ, “flint”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij). Calque of Latin <i>silicium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cream</li><li>cream-colored</li></ol><p><i>крем</i> (krem) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) list, heel, careen, roll, tilt</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) bank, roll</li><li>(<i>general</i>) list, roll, tilt</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>крени́ть</i> (krenítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretzel, krendel</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) guy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Krengel</i>, variant of <i>Kringel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>кре́ндель</i> (kréndelʹ); pretzel, krendel</li></ol><p><i>кре́ндель</i> (kréndelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>кренделёк</i> (krendeljók); small krendel</li></ol><p><i>кренделёк</i> (krendeljók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tilt, to tip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>krengen</i> (“to careen”, verb), from Middle Dutch <i>crengen</i>, causative of <i>cringen</i>. See also <i>кренгова́ть</i> (krengovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tilt, to tip, to list</li></ol><p><i>крени́ть</i> (krenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compression wood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Creole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Créole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Creole, creole</li></ol><p>From <i>крео́л</i> (kreól) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crepe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crêpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to secure in place, to timber, to support, to prop</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to hitch, to lash, to make fast</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to constipate, to render costive</li><li>to strengthen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krěpiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain oneself, to take hold of oneself</li><li>to stand firm</li></ol><p><i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, firm, solid</li><li>robust, sound, sturdy, vigorous</li><li>potent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krěpъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) tough nut to crack (challenging problem to solve)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, solidly, fast, strong, hard</li></ol><p><i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-headed</li></ol><p><i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij, “firm”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лоб</i> (lob, “forehead”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastening, strengthening</li><li>attachment (device attached to a piece of equipment or a tool)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) lashing, furling</li></ol><p><i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortified wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow stronger</li><li>to get firmly established</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serfdom</li></ol><p><i>крепостно́й</i> (krepostnój) +&lrm; <i>-и́чество</i> (-íčestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) serf</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) fortress</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serf</li></ol><p>From <i>кре́пость</i> (krépostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кре́пость</i> (krépostʹ, “fortress”): small fort</li></ol><p><i>кре́пость</i> (krépostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ца́</i> (-cá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, stoutness</li><li>firmness, body, hardness</li><li>hardiness, fastness, tenacity</li><li>heartiness</li><li>stronghold, fortress, citadel, bastion, presidio</li><li>(<i>law</i>) deed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>крѣпость</i> (krěpostĭ, “strength”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krěpostь</i>, from <i>&#42;krěpъ</i> (“strong”) + <i>&#42;-ostь</i>.</p><p>Senses related to fortification attested since 16th-17th centuries.</p><p>Possibly influenced by German <i>Festung</i> (“fortress”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, to grow stronger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krěpъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>кре́пко</i> (krépko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sturdy child</li><li>robust but small man</li></ol><p><i>кре́п(кий)</i> (krép(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ш</i> (-ýš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) armchair</li></ol><p><i>кре́сло</i> (kréslo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) June</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armchair, easy chair</li><li>theater stall</li><li>responsible position, post</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krěslo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocking chair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheelchair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cress</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cress</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cross</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;animate) thief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>крьстъ</i> (krĭstŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krьstъ</i>. Doublet of <i>Христо́с</i> (Xristós) and <i>крёст</i> (krjost), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>crosswise, criss-cross</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrum</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clubs (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♣)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>крест</i> (krest): (small) cross</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tic-tac-toe, noughts and crosses</li></ol><p>Literally, “little crosses and zeroes”; compare English <i>noughts and crosses</i>, English <i>X's and O's</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baptismal, of or related to baptism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>christening (any instance of someone's or something's being christened)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>chrzciny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptizer, baptist</li></ol><p><i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baptize, to christen</li><li>to be a godfather, to be a sponsor</li><li>to cross, to make the sign of the cross on (someone)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krьstiti</i>. Compare кстить (kstitʹ), with different treatment of the reduced vowel.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be baptized, to be christened</li><li>to cross oneself</li></ol><p><i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godson</li></ol><p><i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cross</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crosspiece</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) frog</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest, “cross”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cross</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) crusade (military expedition undertaken by the Christians of Europe in the 11th to 13th centuries)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) clubs (card game)</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest, “cross”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p><p><i>кре́сти</i> (krésti, “clubs”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crusade (military expedition undertaken by the Christians of Europe in the 11th to 13th centuries to reconquer the Levant from the Muslims)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) crusader</li><li><i>Anisoplia agricola</i>, a species of shining leaf chafer, a type of beetle.</li></ol><p><i>крест</i> (krest, “cross”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) peasant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>крьстьꙗнинъ</i> (krĭstĭjaninŭ, “Christian; human, person; tax-paying villager”), <i>крьстьꙗнъ</i> (krĭstĭjanŭ, “Christian; peasant, tiller; tax-paying villager”), from Latin <i>christiānus</i> (“Christian”). For semantic shift to "peasant", compare English <i>cretin</i>, Russian <i>крети́н</i> (kretín), also from Latin christiānus, through French. Doublet of <i>крети́н</i> (kretín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) farm; farmer’s</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) peasant</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) country</li></ol><p><i>крестья́н-</i> (krestʹján-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) peasantry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) cretin, idiot (inconsiderate or mean spirited person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crétin</i>. Further from Latin <i>Chrīstiānus</i>. Doublet of <i>крестья́нин</i> (krestʹjánin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gyrfalcon (<i>Falco rusticolus</i>)</li><li>Krechet (Soviet space suit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krečetъ</i>, perhaps of imitative Proto-Indo-European origin (see Ancient Greek <i>κρέξ</i> (kréx, “corncrake”)). Compare Ancient Greek <i>κέρκνος</i> (kérknos, “a Hierax or an Alektryon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) crescendo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>crescendo</i>, from Latin <i>crēscendum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptism (<i>Christian sacrament with water</i>)</li><li><i>(Church holiday)</i> Epiphany</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>крьщение</i> (krĭščenije), equivalent to <i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curve</li></ol><p>Substantivized feminine of <i>криво́й</i> (krivój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetical</i>) falsehood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кривьда</i> (krivĭda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krivьda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curvature</li><li>crookedness</li></ol><p>From <i>криво́й</i> (krivój) +&lrm; <i>-изна́</i> (-izná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to distort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become crooked/bent/lop-sided</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a face</li></ol><p><i>криви́ть</i> (krivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face-maker (one who makes a face)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кривля́нье</i> (krivljánʹje, “affectation, grimacing”)</li></ol><p><i>кривля́(ться)</i> (krivljá(tʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affectation, grimacing</li></ol><p>From <i>кривля́(ться)</i> (krivljá(tʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-нье</i> (-nʹje). Compare Ukrainian <i>кривля́ння</i> (kryvljánnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grimace, to make faces</li><li>to give oneself airs, to be affected</li></ol><p><i>криви́ть</i> (krivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>crookedly</li><li>awry, askew</li></ol><p><i>криво́й</i> (krivój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, lopsided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, curved, wry, bent</li><li>wrong, false, unfair</li><li>one-eyed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krivъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bent-toed gecko, bow-fingered gecko (<i>Cyrtodactylus</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Cyrtodactylus</i>, as <i>кривопа́лый</i> (krivopályj, “crooked-fingered”) +&lrm; <i>гекко́н</i> (gekkón, “gecko”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bunglesome, butterfingered, club-handed</li></ol><p><i>криво́й</i> (krivój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false speculations, gossips</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crank (an arm attached perpendicularly to a rotating shaft)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Krummzapfen</i> att. since 1820s, from <i>криво́й</i> (krivój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>шип</i> (šip). Replaced indigenous Russian <i>мотыль</i> (motylʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) crisis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>crise</i>. Doublet of <i>кри́зис</i> (krízis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crisis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>crisis</i>. Doublet of <i>криз</i> (kriz). Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krey-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>overproduction, crisis of overproduction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crisis</li></ol><p><i>кри́зис</i> (krízis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry (a shout or scream), outcry, scream, shout, shouting, yell</li><li>creek <i>(in English and American creek names)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krȋkъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>creek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cricket (sport)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cricket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud</li><li>clamorous</li><li>garish, flashy</li></ol><p><i>крик</i> (krik) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, to shout (at) (semelfactive)</li><li>to scream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bawler; shouter; screamer, crier (someone who shouts or likes to shout, screams, yells or cries)</li><li>loudmouth; clamo(u)rer; squaller (one who talks too much or too loudly, especially in a boastful or self-important manner, someone who makes noise)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) propagandist; someone spreading negative and sometimes false information</li></ol><p>From <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>krill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>kriel</i>, <i>krill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crime, criminal case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to criminalize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forensic science, criminalistics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) criminals, the criminal world, organized crime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>criminal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) lily</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κρίνον</i> (krínon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;youth slang) cringe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cringe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to cringe (to feel strong embarrassment or disgust)</li></ol><p><i>кринж</i> (krinž) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to cringe (to feel strong embarrassment or disgust)</li></ol><p><i>кринж</i> (krinž) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>кринжевать</i> (krinževatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>кринж</i> (krinž) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) cringily, in a cringey manner</li></ol><p><i>кринжо́вый</i> (krinžóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cringe, cringey</li></ol><p><i>кринж</i> (krinž) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any mushroom of the genus <i>Crinipellis</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Crinipellis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cryogenics</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κρύος</i> (krúos) + <i>γένος</i> (génos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cryogenic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creepy (producing an uneasy fearful sensation)</li><li>creepy (causing discomfort or repulsion due to strange, eccentric or sexually inappropriate behavior)</li></ol><p>From English <i>creepy</i> +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crypt (burial place)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) clipping of <i>криптовалю́та</i> (kriptovaljúta): crypto, cryptocurrency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>crypto-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>κρυπτός</i> (kruptós, “hidden, secret”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cryptocurrency</li></ol><p>From <i>крипто-</i> (kripto-) +&lrm; <i>валю́та</i> (valjúta), a calque of English <i>cryptocurrency</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) cryptovirus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cryptovirus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cryptographer, cryptographist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cryptography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>krypton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>science fiction</i>) a (male) Kryptonian (a native of Krypton)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) krypton</li></ol><p><i>крипто́н</i> (kriptón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cryptocurrencies,&#32;business) A cryptocurrency startup.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crypto startup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kris</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) crystal (a solid composed of an array of atoms with a periodic structure)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) chip, silicon wafer (integrated circuit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kristall</i> or French <i>cristal</i>, from Latin <i>crystallum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κρύσταλλος</i> (krústallos). Doublet of <i>хруста́ль</i> (xrustálʹ), which entered the Russian language via Byzantine Greek.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crystallization</li></ol><p><i>кристаллиз(ова́ть)</i> (kristalliz(ovátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) to crystallize</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to clarify, to define</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cristalliser</i> or English <i>crystallize</i> + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), or constructed directly as <i>криста́лл</i> (kristáll) +&lrm; <i>-изова́ть</i> (-izovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crystalline</li></ol><p><i>криста́лл</i> (kristáll) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crystal</li></ol><p><i>криста́лл</i> (kristáll) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criterion, guideline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>critic</li><li>anatomist</li><li>animadverter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criticism</li><li>critique</li></ol><p>Via French <i>critique</i> from Latin <i>criticus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κριτικός</i> (kritikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to criticize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>critical philosophy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>critically (crucially&#59; extremely seriously&#59; in a criticizing manner)</li></ol><p><i>крити́ческий</i> (kritíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>critical</li></ol><p><i>кри́т(ика)</i> (krít(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>critically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Crete; Cretan</li></ol><p><i>Крит</i> (Krit) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan</li></ol><p><i>Крит</i> (Krit) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, to shout, to scream, to yell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kričati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>крик</i> (krik) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ, stative).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;about appearance, colour/color) conspicuous, garish, flashy, tawdry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Krishnaist (one who practises Krishnaism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter, home</li><li>roof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кровъ</i> (krovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krovъ</i> (“roof”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to stain with blood</li></ol><p><i>крова́вый</i> (krovávyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloody</li><li>sanguinary, murderous</li><li>blood-stained</li><li>blood-red</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъvavъ</i>, from <i>&#42;kry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>крова́ть</i> (krovátʹ): small bed; child's bed, crib</li></ol><p><i>крова́ть</i> (krovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bed</li></ol><p><i>крова́ть</i> (krovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Byzantine Greek <i>κρεβάτιον</i> (krebátion), from Ancient Greek <i>κράββατος</i> (krábbatos). See also Modern Greek <i>κρεβάτι</i> (kreváti), Serbo-Croatian <i>krevet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) roofing</li></ol><p>From <i>кро́вля</i> (króvlja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofer (craftsman who lays or repairs roofs)</li></ol><p><i>кро́вля</i> (króvlja) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) circulatory system; circulatory</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) blood vessel (any vessel in which blood is carried)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofing, roof covering, rooftop (the outer surface of a roof)</li><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) roof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krovlja</i> (<i>&#42;-ja</i> is suffix), correlative with <i>&#42;kroviti</i>, <i>&#42;kryti</i> (“to cover, hide”), <i>&#42;krovъ</i> (“roof”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative (someone connected by blood)</li><li>someone connected by blood feud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blood</li><li><i>(horses)</i> thoroughbred, pureblooded, pedigree</li><li>vital</li><li>deadly, grievous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъvьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodthirsty (eager to resort to violence)</li></ol><p>From <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жа́дный</i> (žádnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haemorrhage (heavy loss of blood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood circulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hemostatic, styptic</li><li>astringent (having the effect of drawing tissue together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bloodsucker (e.g. insect, vampire)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a cruel person (used also as an insult)</li></ol><p>From <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ, “to drink”) +&lrm; <i>-ца</i> (-ca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bruise</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подтёк</i> (podtjók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bloodshed (shedding or spilling of blood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloody, sanguinary</li></ol><p><i>кровопроли́тие</i> (krovoprolítije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bloodletting</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) mass murder</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) war</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ, “to release”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incest (sexual relations between close relatives)</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>смеше́ние</i> (smešénije, “mixing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) blood supply (volume of blood flowing to part of the body over a particular time period)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleeding (the flow or loss of blood from a damaged blood vessel)</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>тече́ние</i> (tečénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bleeding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bleed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) diminutive of <i>кро́вь</i> (króvʹ)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;endearing) darling, my dear (<i>to a close family member</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кро́вь</i> (króvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) blood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>крꙑ</i> (kry), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kry</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;krū́ˀs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krewh₂-</i> (“blood of a wound”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>cruor</i> (“blood(shed)”), Lithuanian <i>kraujas</i>, Welsh <i>crau</i>, English <i>raw</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at all costs, no matter what</li></ol><p>Literally, “blood out of the nose”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood sausage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blood</li></ol><p><i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (fabric to specific lengths and shapes to make clothes)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krojìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut, style <i>(of dress)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li><li>alligator clip</li><li>(<i>military</i>) "Clam Shell", low-altitude search and acquisition radar (76N6)</li><li>(military,&#32;aviation) Mi-24 "Hind" helicopter gunship</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) desomorphine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ugly person</li></ol><p>From Middle Russian <i>крокодилъ</i> (krokodil).</p><p>Sense 5 refers to the drug's damaging effects on the skin, possibly related to the chemical name of the precursor α-chlorocodide.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crocodile</li></ol><p><i>крокоди́л</i> (krokodíl, “crocodile”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crocodile's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) crocodile tears</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crocodile</li></ol><p><i>крокоди́л</i> (krokodíl) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>кракозябра</i> (krakozjabra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>crocus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κρόκος</i> (krókos), of Semitic extraction.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbit (<i>of unspecified gender</i>) (long-eared mammal)</li><li>male rabbit</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;figuratively) father of many children</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>królik</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cunīculus</i> (an <i>-ulus</i> or <i>-culus</i> diminutive), via Germanic where by folk etymology interpreted as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningaz</i> (whence <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ)) related. Compare <i>королёк</i> (koroljók).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кролик)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rabbit (from Winnie-the-Pooh)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rabbit</li></ol><p><i>кро́лик</i> (królik, “rabbit”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>swimming</i>) crawl, front crawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buck rabbit (male rabbit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crawl</i>.</p><p>Back-formation from <i>кро́лик</i> (królik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbit (meat)</li></ol><p>From <i>крольча́т-</i> (krolʹčát-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>крольчо́нок</i> (krolʹčónok, “rabbit kitten”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;figuratively) mother of many children</li></ol><p>From <i>крольк-</i> (krolʹk-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa), using the stem form found in e.g. <i>крольчо́нок</i> (krolʹčónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby rabbit, rabbit kitten</li></ol><p><i>кро́лик</i> (królik) +&lrm; <i>-онок</i> (-onok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>except, besides</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кромѣ</i> (kromě, “outside”), old locative case of <i>крома́</i> (kromá, “partition”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kroma</i> (“edge, boundary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in addition (also&#59; as well)</li><li>besides</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitch-black, impenetrable (<i>darkness</i>)</li><li>sheer, absolute, utter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кромѣщьнъ, кромѣшьнъ</i> (kroměštĭnŭ, kroměšĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>кро́ме</i> (króme) +&lrm; <i>-е́шный</i> (-éšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge, ridge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kromъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shred, to hack up, to cut up, to hackshred</li></ol><p>From <i>крома́</i> (kromá) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ), interfixed with -с-. Compare кромши́ть (kromšítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown (of a tree)</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) crown, koruna, krona, krone, kroon, króna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown prince</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kronprinz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) curlew (<i>Numenius</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kronschnepfe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall bracket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kragstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) aspergill, aspergillum</li></ol><p><i>кропи́ть</i> (kropítʹ, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle, asperse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kropiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>painstakingly; with a lot of patience and determination</li></ol><p><i>кропотли́вый</i> (kropotlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painstaking and slow, meticulous</li><li>laborious, minute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kropotьlivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>кропота́ть</i> (kropotátʹ, “to grumble; to fuss, to bustle”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;dated) loom (usually with some work on it)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krosno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) cross country</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) clipping of <i>кроссо́вка</i> (krossóvka, “athletic shoe, crep, sneaker”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cross</i> or <i>cross country race</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossword</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crossword</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cross-dresser</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cross-dresser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athletic shoe, tennis shoe, trainer, crep, sneaker</li></ol><p><i>кросс</i> (kross) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossosoma, <i>Crossosoma</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Crossosoma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (burrowing insectivore)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, meek, mild</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кротъкъ</i> (krotŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krotъkъ</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>кро̀так</i> / <i>kròtak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gently, meekly</li></ol><p><i>кро́ткий</i> (krótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole hole</li><li>(<i>relativity</i>) a wormhole</li></ol><p><i>крото́вый</i> (krotóvyj, “mole (<i>relational</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>нора́</i> (norá, “burrow”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>molehill</li></ol><p><i>крот</i> (krot, “mole”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вина</i> (-óvina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mole</li></ol><p><i>крот</i> (krot) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meekness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кротость</i> (krotostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krotostь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb</li><li>little child</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>кръха</i> (krŭxa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъxa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;krušā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krows-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very small, tiny, minute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кръхътьнъ</i> (krŭxŭtĭnŭ), from <i>кръхъть</i> (krŭxŭtĭ, “crumb, speck”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъxa</i>. See <i>кро́ха</i> (króxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hash (chopped food, especially meat and potatoes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) medley (mixture of miscellaneous things)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tiny, wee, diminutive</li></ol><p><i>кро́шка</i> (króška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut into small pieces, to chop, to crumble</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hack to pieces</li><li>to drop something like breadcrumbs on the ground</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъšiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble</li></ol><p><i>крошить</i> (krošitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb, bit (a small, broken-off piece of some solid material, e.g. bread)</li><li>(baking,&#32;collective) streusel</li><li>(engineering,&#32;collective) fine crushed rock (<i>with 2–5 mm grains</i>)</li><li>(engineering,&#32;uncountable) crushing, pulverization</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>кро́ха</i> (króxa): baby, little one</li></ol><p><i>кро́ха</i> (króxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>croissant (a flaky roll or pastry in a form of a crescent)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>croissant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle, ring</li><li>sphere, range</li><li>orbit</li><li>average</li><li>slice</li><li>(<i>motor racing</i>) lap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small fortune, a bundle (a large amount of money)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj, “round”)</li><li>chubby, plump</li></ol><p><i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make round, to round</li></ol><p><i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become round</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to appear (<i>of round or rounded objects</i>)</li></ol><p><i>кругли́ть</i> (kruglítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toadhead agama (<i>Phrynocephalus</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj, “round”) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “-er”), literally “round-header”. Compare Belarusian <i>круглагало́ўка</i> (kruhlahalówka) and Ukrainian <i>круглоголо́вка</i> (kruhloholóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round-faced</li></ol><p><i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around the clock (all the time)</li></ol><p><i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>су́точно</i> (sútočno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>around-the-clock, round-the-clock (all day and night)</li></ol><p><i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>су́тки</i> (sútki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) around the clock, all day (all the time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round, circular</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) perfect, complete</li><li>chubby</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫglъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>крѫглъ</i> (krǫglŭ), Ukrainian <i>кру́глий</i> (krúhlyj), Bulgarian <i>кръ́гъл</i> (krǎ́gǎl), Serbo-Croatian <i>окру́гао</i>, Slovene <i>krógǝl</i>, Czech <i>okrouhlý</i>, Polish <i>krągły</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;colloquial) circular movement, whirlwind, gyre</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) cycle, whirlwind</li></ol><p>From <i>круг</i> (krug, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-верт</i> (-vert) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), based on a combining form of <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>circular</li></ol><p><i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circulation, rotation, cycle</li><li>whirl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫgovortъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horizon, look, purview, scope, sight</li></ol><p><i>круг</i> (krug) + <i>зор</i> (zor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>around, round</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>around</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military&#32;command</i>) about turn, about face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round-the-world</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lace bug (Tingidae)</li></ol><p>From <i>кру́жево</i> (krúževo, “lace”) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i>&nbsp;f (-níca, “-er, -ist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lace</li><li>lacy</li></ol><p><i>кру́жево</i> (krúževo) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lace</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) interlacing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>круживо</i> (kruživo), By surface analysis, <i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-иво</i> (-ivo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кру́жево</i> (krúževo, “lace”)</li></ol><p><i>кру́жево</i> (krúževo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going round, whirling, spinning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, to whirl, to spin, to wheel around</li><li>to circle, to go round, to make circles</li><li>to wander</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫžiti</i> or <i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin, to whirl, to go round, to circle</li></ol><p><i>кружи́ть</i> (kružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mug, tankard, noggin</li><li>poor box, charity box</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>кружька, кружка</i> (kružʹka, kružka), from Old Polish <i>kruż, krużyk</i> with gender change (compare <i>фиа́лка</i> (fiálka)), from Middle High German <i>krūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roundabout</li></ol><p><i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(small) circle, ring</li><li>circle, study group, interest group</li><li>kroužek</li></ol><p><i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кружо́к</i> (kružók), diminutive of <i>круг</i> (krug): (small) circle</li></ol><p><i>кружо́к</i> (kružók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruzeiro (the monetary unit of Brazil from 1942 to 1986 and 1990 to 1993)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>cruzeiro</i> (literally “large cross”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruise</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cruise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) croup</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) croup, crupper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>croup</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>croupe</i>. Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kruppaz</i>. Doublet of <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa) and <i>крупье́</i> (krupʹjé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cereals, grits, groats</li><li>sleet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krupa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krowpeh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain</li><li>a grain <i>(of)</i>, a little</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>кру́пный</i> (krúpnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>krupnik (Belarusian, Polish and Ukrainian soup)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into large pieces</li><li>using strong language</li><li>with large strokes</li><li>with large strides</li></ol><p><i>кру́пный</i> (krúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oversized, large</li></ol><p><i>крупн-</i> (krupn-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>габари́т</i> (gabarít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) large-calibre</li></ol><p><i>кру́пный</i> (krúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кали́бр</i> (kalíbr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) macrophyllous, large-leaved.</li></ol><p><i>кру́пный</i> (krúpnyj, “large”) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large-scale</li></ol><p><i>кру́пный</i> (krúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>масшта́б</i> (masštáb) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high grasses</li><li>a unique type of Kamchatka flora, plant giantism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coarse, coarse-grained</li><li>big, massive; numerous; large-scale</li><li>important, prominent, great</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krupьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>круп(а)</i> (krup(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(photography or cinematography)</i> close-up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine wheat flour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>croupier (person who collects bets and pays out winnings at a gambling table)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>croupier</i>. Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kruppaz</i> + the suffix derived from Latin <i>-ārius</i>. Doublet of <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa) and <i>круп</i> (krup).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) groats</li></ol><p><i>крупа́</i> (krupá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist once, to twirl once</li></ol><p><i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steepness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) toughness, coolness, awesomeness</li></ol><p>From <i>крутой</i> (krutoj, “steep”) + <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to twirl, to roll up</li><li>to turn</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive) to whirl, to swirl</li><li>to go out, to have an affair</li><li>to trick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫtiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, to spin, to gyrate</li></ol><p><i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steeply</li><li>abruptly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cool, smart, awesome</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cool, awesome</li></ol><p><i>круто́й</i> (krutój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep</li><li>abrupt, sharp</li><li>drastic</li><li>(<i>of food</i>) thick</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) tough; strong</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) important</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cool, excellent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crouton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>croûton</i>. Further from Latin <i>crusta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a steep place, a cliff</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) Expresses joy over something; cool, awesome, nice, sick</li></ol><p><i>круто́й</i> (krutój) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cliff, precipice, steep descent</li></ol><p><i>круто́й</i> (krutój) +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>круто́й</i> (krutój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>кру́то</i> (krúto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(folk poetic,&#32;dialectal,&#32;poetic) sorrow; woe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accident, crash, wreck</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) shipwreck, shipwrecking, sinking (an event in which a ship sinks or runs aground)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) collapse, loss, ruin</li></ol><p><i>круши́ть</i> (krušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;puristic) various metal ore, metal</li></ol><p>From late 17th c. Middle Russian <i>крушецъ, кружецъ</i> (krušec, kružec, “metallic ore; metal, product of its smelting; mine”), from Old Ruthenian <i>крушецъ</i> (krušecʹ, “ore, useful mineral; metal, product of ore smelting”) (since 1566), from Middle Polish <i>kruszec</i>, Polish <i>kruszcz</i>, from Old Polish <i>kruszec</i> (“ore, useful mineral, especially rock salt”) (since 1472; cf. Polish <i>kruch</i> (“unit of measurement of salt; salt stone”)), from Old Czech <i>krušec</i> (“lump of a useful mineral, especially rock salt”), <i>krušek</i>, from <i>kruch</i> (“piece, chunk”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kruxъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kraušas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krows-o-s</i>, ultimately from <i>&#42;krews-</i> (“to freeze, become hard”).</p><p>Promoted by purists as an alternative to <i>металл</i> (metall) but fell out of use by 1830s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to shatter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krušiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gooseberry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gooseberries, gooseberry bush</li></ol><p>From the non-attested Polish <i>&#42;krzyżewnik</i>. Related to <i>крыж</i> (kryž, “cross”) and probably cognate to <i>Krausbeere</i> (“lingonberry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Pteropodidae.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruise missile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samara (winged indehiscent fruit of trees such as the ash, elm or maple)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) man's loose cloak with a cape</li><li>firefish <i>(Pterois)</i></li></ol><p><i>крыла́т(ый)</i> (krylát(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a winged word; a popular expression or an idiom originating from a quotation or a literary or historical source</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>geflügeltes Wort</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔπεα πτερόεντα</i> (épea pteróenta), often used by Homer.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>winged</li></ol><p><i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>крыльцо́</i> (krylʹcó): (small) porch</li></ol><p><i>крыльцо́</i> (krylʹcó) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(aviation,&#32;engineering) a pair of wings (<i>viewed as a single aerodynamic entity</i>)</li></ol><li>sail (<i>of a windmill</i>)</li><li>fender, wing (<i>panel of a car that encloses the wheel area</i>)</li><li>fender, mudguard (<i>cover over a bicycle or motorcycle wheel</i>)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) wing, faction</li><li>(bodybuilding,&#32;slang,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) a lat or particularly a widely sculpted latissimus dorsi muscle.</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) fixed-wing drone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kridlo</i>. <i>Ы</i> probably appeared by the influence of <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wing-shaped</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>крыло́</i> (kryló): (small) wing, winglet</li><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) alula</li></ol><p><i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wing</li></ol><p><i>кры́ль(ев)</i> (krýlʹ(jev), “wing”, genitive plural) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porch, stoop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kridlьce</i> from <i>&#42;kridlo</i> (“wing”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьce</i> (“diminutive suffix”). By surface analysis, <i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory) "Krymnashism" (support of the Russian annexation of Crimea)</li></ol><p><i>Крым</i> (Krym, “Crimea”) +&lrm; <i>наш</i> (naš, “ours”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm, “-ism”), from the meme <i>крымна́ш</i> (krymnáš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Crimean (referring to the Crimea region)</li></ol><p><i>Крым</i> (Krym) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Crimean Tatar</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crimean Tatar (a Turkic language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crimean</li></ol><p><i>Крым</i> (Krym) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rat</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) an unimportant or unpleasant person</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;prison slang) a thief who steals from his fellows or informs against them; a rat</li><li>(<i>video games</i>) camper</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Hypotheses include:</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>кри́са</i> (krýsa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rat</li></ol><p><i>кры́са</i> (krýsa) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ratcatcher (person who catches rats)</li></ol><p>From <i>кры́са</i> (krýsa) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rattrap (device used to catch rats)</li><li>rat dog, ratter (dog trained to hunt out rats)</li></ol><p>From <i>кры́са</i> (krýsa) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a baby rat, a young rat</li></ol><p><i>кры́са</i> (krýsa) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered, sheltered, with a roof, with an awning, <i>(stadium, pool)</i> indoor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to roof (a house); to thatch, to tile</li><li>to coat (<i>with paint</i>)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to cover, to trump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold, to rail (<i>at</i>), to swear (<i>at</i>)</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to mount (an animal to mate)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kryti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to be, to be concealed</li></ol><p><i>кры́ть</i> (krýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) house, dwelling place</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) head, mind</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) someone who provides protection or cover; the protection that is provided</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;крꙑша</i> (&#42;kryša), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kryša</i>, from <i>&#42;kryti</i> (“to cover, hide”) + <i>&#42;-ša</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;-sja</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) to engage in protection racket schemes, to provide protection in exchange for money; to extort protection money in an ordered and agreed manner</li><li>(<i>more generally</i>) to provide (legal, political, lobbying etc.) protection to unlawful or shady activity</li></ol><p><i>кры́ша</i> (krýša) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кры́шка</i> (krýška): small lid, small cap</li></ol><p><i>кры́шка</i> (krýška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, lid</li><li>(as a predicative,&#32;colloquial) death, ruin, end</li></ol><p><i>кры́ша</i> (krýša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crack (a type of cocaine)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) crack (program or procedure designed to circumvent restrictions on software)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>crack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hook</li><li>detour</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gers-</i> (“to twist, bend, turn”), see also Old Saxon <i>kerian</i>, Old Norse <i>keyra</i> (“to whip”), also Ancient Greek <i>γέρδιος</i> (gérdios, “weaver”).</p><p>According to Vasmer, the word is borrowed from Old Norse <i>krókr</i>, itself probably from the same Indo-European root.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to writhe (in pain)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bend</li></ol><p><i>крюк</i> (krjuk) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pettifogger</li></ol><p>From <i>крюч(о́)к</i> (krjuč(ó)k) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твор(и́ть)</i> (tvor(ítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pettifogger</li></ol><p>From <i>крюч(о́)к</i> (krjuč(ó)k) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твор(и́ть)</i> (tvor(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crochet work</li><li>chicanery, overelaboration, machination, shenanigans</li></ol><p><i>крючо́к</i> (krjučók) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hook</li><li>fish-hook</li><li>crochet-needle</li><li>buttonhook, clasper</li></ol><p><i>крюк</i> (krjuk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) onomatopoeia, quack (the sound a duck makes)</li></ol><p>Of imitative origin</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) quack (the sound a duck makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) straight, in a row, in succession, running</li></ol><p><i>к</i> (k) +&lrm; <i>ря́ду</i> (rjádu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low ridge</li><li>block of wood, log, stump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong, stocky (and often tightfisted) man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kręžь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quack; to emit a noise like a duck</li><li>to grunt, to hem</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to crack <i>(a program)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild duck, mallard</li></ol><p>From <i>кря́кать</i> (krjákatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) cracked (computer program, software)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quack; to emit a noise like a duck</li><li>to grunt, to hem</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to crack <i>(a program)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die</li></ol><p><i>кря</i> (krja) +&lrm; <i>-ну-</i> (-nu-) +&lrm; <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan, to moan (e.g. from exertion); to puff, to pant</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>кря́кать</i> (krjákatʹ, “to quack, to grunt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) cross, crossroad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krьstъ</i>. Compare крест (krest), a Church Slavic borrowing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddaughter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godmother</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) god... (referring to relation through baptism)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) godfather (a mafia leader)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) godfather (a mafia leader)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кре́пко</i> (krépko).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) xanthine (group of alkaloids, including caffeine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) xanthorrhoea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Xanthorrhoea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xenon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) xenon</li></ol><p><i>ксено́н</i> (ksenón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xenophobe</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>-фоб</i> (-fob).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xenophobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photocopying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photocopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) photocopier, xerox (machine which reproduces documents by photographing the original over a glass plate and printing duplicates)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) photocopy (copy made using a photocopier)</li></ol><p>From the brand name <i>Ксе́рокс</i> (Kséroks, “Xerox”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xi (Greek letter Ξ&#47;ξ)</li><li>ksi (early Cyrillic letter Ѯ&#47;ѯ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ξεῖ</i> (xeî), <i>ξῖ</i> (xî).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) identity card, ID</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) any document</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) false document</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a slip or letter passed secretly from cell to cell, from prison to prison or to the outside world</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>כְּתִיבָה</i> (kăṯiḇá, “writing”) via Yiddish <i>כּתיבֿה</i> (ksive), from Hebrew <i>כָּתַב</i> (kaṯáḇ, “write”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xylograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) xylophone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>xylophone</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”) + <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xoanon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>кста́ти</i> (kstáti); btw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>кста́ти</i> (kstáti); btw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) by the way</li><li>as a matter of fact, for the record, incidentally</li><li>(often in the form <i>о́чень <b>кста́ти</b></i> (óčenʹ <b>kstáti</b>)) useful, helpful, handy, welcome, convenient</li><li>at the right time, with good timing, in the nick of time, opportunely</li><li>to the point, apropos, relevant</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) decently, appropriately, properly</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>к</i> (k) +&lrm; <i>ста́ти</i> (státi), dative singular of <i>стать</i> (statʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polish Catholic priest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>ksiądz</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъnędzь</i>. Doublet of <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ) and <i>конунг</i> (konung).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>churchwarden</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κτήτωρ</i> (ktḗtōr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) who</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) who</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>къто</i> (kŭto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъto</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> anybody, anyone, somebody, someone <i>(implies one does not care who, or does not have any particular person in mind)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> anybody, anyone, somebody or someone <i>(implies one does not care who, or does not have any particular person in mind)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite&#32;pronoun</i>) somebody, someone <i>(implies that one has a certain person in mind, but does not know exactly who)</i></li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>кто́</i> (któ) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robber fly, assassin fly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>Q</i>/<i>q</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) cuckoo (sound a cuckoo makes)</li><li>(<i>familiar</i>) hey</li><li>(<i>familiar</i>) gotcha; a reaction during hide and seek or peekaboo game</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) an insane person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) cube (shape with six square sides)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) cube (number raised to the third power)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cubic meter (cubic metre)</li><li>boiler, still, large tank</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) vat dye, specifically alkaline solution of indigo prepared for dyeing</li><li>indigo (<i>plant</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed in the early 18th century from German <i>Kubus</i> or directly from Latin <i>cubus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κύβος</i> (kúbos, “die, cube”).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кубъ</i> (kubŭ, “drinking vessel”), first attested in the 14th century, further origin uncertain.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>куб</i> (kub, “wooden dish”), Polish <i>kubek</i> (“bowl, cup”), Lower Sorbian <i>kub</i> (“bowl, cup”), also <i>kubk</i>.</p><p>Per Vasmer, likely related to Sanskrit <i>कुम्भ</i> (kumbha, “pot”), Avestan <i>𐬑𐬎𐬨𐬠𐬀</i> (xumba, “pot”), Ancient Greek <i>κύμβος</i> (kúmbos, “vessel”), <i>κύβη</i> (kúbē, “cup”), also <i>κύμβη</i> (kúmbē).</p><p>However Trubachev notes that there are no signs of a nasal vowel and reconstructs Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kubъ</i>, thus rejecting a possible link to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kumbʰo-</i>, <i>&#42;kumbʰéh₂-</i> (“vessel”) and proposing a connection to <i>&#42;koubʰ-</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>ку́бовый краси́тель</i> (kúbovyj krasítelʹ, “vat dye”), from <i>ку́бовый</i> (kúbovyj, “related to dye vatting”), from <i>куб</i> (kub, “large tank, vat”), see above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang) a person from the Kuban region of southern Russia, stereotyped as backwards or deceitful.</li></ol><p><i>Куба́нь</i> (Kubánʹ, “the Kuban region of southern Russia”) +&lrm; <i>гумано́ид</i> (gumanóid, “humanoid”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kuban (river and region in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Куба́нь</i> (Kubánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubic capacity, cubic content, cubature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubism (an artistic movement characterized by the depiction of natural forms as geometric structures of planes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block</li><li>cube</li><li>brick</li><li>toy block</li><li>ice cube</li><li>dice</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cubic centimeter (cubic centimetre)</li></ol><p><i>куб</i> (kub) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rubik's cube</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cuban (a person from Cuba)</li></ol><p><i>Ку́ба</i> (Kúba, “Cuba”) +&lrm; <i>-и́нец</i> (-ínec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cuban</li></ol><p><i>Ку́ба</i> (Kúba) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cubic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cube</li></ol><p><i>куб</i> (kub) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) distilling, distillation boiler &lsqb;from 18th c.&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) vat dye &lsqb;from 17th c.&rsqb;</li><li>indigo (<i>color</i>), dark blue &lsqb;from 17th c.&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cubic meter, cubic centimeter, cc (<i>as a suffix in a compound adjective where the first part is a number</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>куб</i> (kub, “large tank, still, vat”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), first attested in late 17th century.</p><p>From <i>куб</i> (kub, “cube”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), probably after the middle of 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goblet, beaker, bowl (a large drinking vessel, usually having a stem and a foot, and often a lid)</li><li>cup (a trophy in the shape of an oversized cup)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кубъкъ</i> (kubŭkŭ, “drinking vessel”) First attested in the 14th century. By surface analysis, <i>куб</i> (kub, “boiler, large tank”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubic metre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sleeping quarters, sleeping compartment</li><li>(nautical,&#32;historical) berth deck</li><li>(<i>military</i>) barracks room, cubicle</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) room <i>(in a dormitory, bunkhouse etc.)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>koebrug</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sledgehammer (kind of hammer)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>кувалда</i> (kuvalda), attested since 1655.</p><p>In spite of phonetical and semantical differences, most likely with metathesis from Old Ruthenian <i>кова́дло</i> (kovádlo, “anvil”) (since 1635), whence also Ukrainian <i>кова́дло</i> (kovádlo), Belarusian <i>кава́дла</i> (kavádla) and, notably, Belarusian <i>кува́дла</i> (kuvádla), from Middle Polish <i>kowadło</i> (in modern sense since 16th c.), from Old Czech <i>kovadlo</i> (since 14th c.; whence Czech <i>kovadlina</i>), from <i>kovat</i> (“to forge”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kovati</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i>. Belarusian <i>кува́лда</i> (kuválda, “sledgehammer”) may either be a late Russian borrowing or a descendant of an unattested Old Ruthenian source. Cognate with <i>кий</i> (kij), <i>кия́нка</i> (kijánka) (via <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moltiti</i> (whence <i>молоти́ть</i> (molotítʹ)) &lt; <i>&#42;moltъ</i> (whence <i>мо́лот</i> (mólot)); Latin <i>malleō</i> &lt; <i>malleus</i> (the opposite derivation (verb <- noun), further, however, from the verb Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (whence <i>&#42;melti</i> &gt; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ); Latin <i>molō</i>)).</p><p>Historically also explained as a derivative of <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ, “to fell, to knock down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kuwaiti</li></ol><p><i>Куве́йт</i> (Kuvéjt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jug, pitcher</li></ol><p>From late Old East Slavic <i>кувшинъ</i> (kuvšinŭ), <i>кушинъ</i> (kušinŭ), interpreted as Lithuanian <i>&#42;kaušinas</i> derived from Lithuanian <i>káušas</i> (“ladle, dipper, big spoon”) whence also <i>ковш</i> (kovš), formed from <i>káuti</i> (“hew, chop out”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;káuˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kowh₂-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>каўшы́н</i> (kawšýn), <i>куўшы́н</i> (kuwšýn), <i>кушы́н</i> (kušýn), <i>кукшы́н</i> (kukšýn), Ukrainian <i>куші́н</i> (kušín), <i>куши́н</i> (kušýn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water lily</li></ol><p><i>кувши́н</i> (kuvšín) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Nymphaeaceae</li></ol><p>Plural of кувши́нковый (kuvšínkovyj, “nymphaeaceous”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) nymphaeaceous</li></ol><p>From <i>кувши́нка</i> (kuvšínka, “nymphaea”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overturn</li></ol><p>Apparently related to <i>кувы́рдать</i> (kuvýrdatʹ, “to throw a stick”), <i>кувы́рдаться</i> (kuvýrdatʹsja, “to tumble”). Per ESSJa (Trubachev), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuvьrtati</i>, formed from a prefix <i>&#42;ku-</i> + the root of <i>&#42;vьrtěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to somersault</li></ol><p><i>кувырка́ть</i> (kuvyrkátʹ, “to overturn”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to somersault</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tumble down</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>кувырка́ться</i> (kuvyrkátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кувыркну́ться</i> (kuvyrknútʹsja)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>кувырка́ться</i> (kuvyrkátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) somersault</li></ol><p>Possibly deverbal from <i>кувырка́ть</i> (kuvyrkátʹ, “to overturn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) cluck (sound a chicken makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(to) where?</li><li>why?</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in a comparison) much (more), a lot (more)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кѫда</i> (kǫda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhither, to somewhere, somewhere to</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) to anywhere, anywhere to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anywhither, anywhere to, somewhere to</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) anywhere to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to somewhere, somewhere to <i>(indefinite adverb)</i>, somewhither</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) cluck (sound a chicken makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cackle, to cluck (like a hen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tow, sliver</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>кѫдѣль</i> (kǫdělĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫdělь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sorcery, fortune-telling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кудесъ</i> (kudesŭ, “magic, sorcery”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kudo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to practise witchcraft</li><li>to play pranks, to make mischief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard, mage</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) miracle worker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кудесникъ</i> (kudesnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to force; to persuade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кудити</i> (kuditi, “to decry; to insult; to spoil”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kùditi</i>, from <i>&#42;kudъ</i> / <i>&#42;kudь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curls</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫdrь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having curls</li><li>curly (of smoke, water, hair)</li><li>budding, lush (of vegetation)</li><li>exquisitely or overly decorated, artsy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кудрꙗвꙑи</i> (kudrjavyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫdřavъ</i>, from <i>&#42;kǫdrь</i> + <i>&#42;-avъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) ringlet of hair, curl, lock (of hair)</li></ol><p><i>ку́дри</i> (kúdri) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;humorous) alternative form of <i>куда́</i> (kudá)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>куди́</i> (kudý), Belarusian <i>куды́</i> (kudý), Polish <i>kędy</i>, Czech <i>kudy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male cousin (any male cousin however distant)</li><li>distant male blood relative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cousin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cousinia (<i>Cousinia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cousinia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smith, blacksmith</li><li>farrier</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>коузньць</i> (kuznĭcĭ), from <i>коузнь</i> (kuznĭ) + <i>-ьць</i> (-ĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuznьcь</i>. Distantly related to <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ), <i>кова́ль</i> (koválʹ) and the equivalent forms in other Slavic languages, such as Ukrainian <i>кова́ль</i> (koválʹ), Polish <i>kowal</i>, Czech <i>kovář</i>, Slovak <i>kováč</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>kòvāč</i>, Cyrillic ко̀ва̄ч.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>кузне́чный</i> (kuznéčnyj)</li></ol><p><i>кузне́ц</i> (kuznéc) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush-cricket</li></ol><p>From <i>кузне́ц</i> (kuznéc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blacksmith's</li></ol><p><i>кузне́ц</i> (kuznéc) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smithy, blacksmith's shop, forge</li><li>farriery</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) foundry, forge, factory, powerhouse, incubator (a place for developing or training some valuable quality&#59; a source of trained specialists)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кузница</i> (kuznica), from <i>кузнь</i> (kuznĭ, “forging”) + <i>-ица</i> (-ica); compare Polish <i>kuźnica</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ku-</i> (“to forge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li><li>body (largest or most important part of anything (e.g. car bodywork))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>kyzau̯</i> (“basket”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Kuzma's mother (part of the Russian idiomatic expression "to show Kuzka's mother to someone")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kuybyshev (anyone with this name, most notably Valerian Kuybyshev, a Russian Bolshevik revolutionary)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kuybyshev (former, Soviet name of Samara, large city in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kuybyshev, Kuybyshevo, Kuybyshevsky (any of various settlements with these names in Russia and other former Soviet states)</li></ol><p><i>Ку́йбышев</i> (Kújbyšev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kookaburra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crow (to make the sound of a rooster)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) cock-a-doodle-doo (sound a rooster makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>HTTP cookie (packet of information sent by a server to a World Wide Web browser and then returned by the browser each time it accesses that server)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cookie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rude gesture in which the hand makes a fist and the thumb is stuck between the index and middle fingers; indicates nothing for you, nuts to you</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doll</li><li>puppet</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) fake money</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кукла</i> (kukla), from Byzantine Greek <i>κούκλα</i> (koúkla), from Latin <i>cuculla</i>, from Latin <i>cucullus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puppeteer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolly, little doll</li><li>pretty child, cute kid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(cry)</i> cuckoo</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to be left high and dry, to stay out in the cold</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to waste one's time waiting</li></ol><p><i>ку-ку</i> (ku-ku) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;sometimes&#32;puristic) cringe</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>cringe</i>. Back-formation from <i>куко́житься</i> (kukóžitʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cringe, to shrivel, to curdle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupa (an insect in its development stage)</li><li>diminutive of <i>ку́кла</i> (kúkla): small doll</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) an attractive woman or girl, dolly</li></ol><p><i>ку́кла</i> (kúkla) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockle, corncockle (<i>Agrostemma githago</i>)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) koukoulion (type of monastic headdress)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫkoľь</i>, <i>&#42;kǫkolъ</i>.</p><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>cucullus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) doll</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) puppet</li></ol><p><i>ку́кла</i> (kúkla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kukri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Nepali <i>खुकुरी</i> (khukurī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Cucujiformia.</li></ol><p>Partial calque of translingual <i>Cucujiformia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn, maize, Indian corn</li></ol><p>A <i>wanderword</i> of Balkan origin. Probably entered Russian from Ukrainian <i>кукуру́(д)за</i> (kukurú(d)za), Serbo-Croatian <i>куку̀руз</i>, or Romanian <i>cucuruz</i> - see there for further history.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornflakes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) corn</li></ol><p><i>кукуру́за</i> (kukurúza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>куку́шка</i> (kukúška, “cuckoo”)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) noggin, conk, biscuit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoo</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) woman who left her newborn baby</li><li>(railroad,&#32;slang) cuckoo (a term used for the old «Ку» series locomotives)</li><li>(railroad,&#32;slang) cuckoo (a term for a suburban light-rail with several cars)</li><li>(military,&#32;slang) sniper whose firing position is located in a tree</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic; from <i>ку-ку́</i> (ku-kú) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška). See Latin <i>cucūlus</i>, English <i>cuckoo</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cuckoo's</li></ol><p><i>кукушка</i> (kukuška) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haircap moss (Polytrichaceae spp.)</li></ol><p>Literally, “cuckoo flax”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heath spotted orchid (<i>Dactylorhiza maculata</i>)</li><li>lesser butterfly-orchid (<i>Platanthera bifolia</i>)</li><li>purple-bract iris, of species <i>Iris ruthenica</i></li><li>any plant of genus <i>Orchis</i></li><li>twayblade, plants of genera <i>Neottia</i> and <i>Liparis</i></li><li>quaking grass, of genus <i>Briza</i></li></ol><p>Literally, “cuckoo’s little teardrops”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kulaga (East Slavic dish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fist</li><li>(<i>military</i>) concentrated force</li><li>(<i>mechanical</i>) cam</li><li>(historical,&#32;usually&#32;derogatory) kulak (a prosperous peasant in the Russian Empire or the Soviet Union, who owned land and could hire workers)</li></ol><p>Probably from Turkic, with the same meaning, from <i>&#42;kul</i> (“hand”). Compare Bashkir <i>ҡул</i> (qul, “arm, hand”), Tatar <i>кул</i> (qul, “arm, hand”) and Turkish <i>kol</i> (“arm”). The sense of “wealthy farmer” is from the expression <i>держа́ть в кулаке́</i> (deržátʹ v kulaké, “to keep in dependence”) or developed figuratively as “fist” → “tightfisted person”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cam</li></ol><p><i>кулачо́к</i> (kulačók) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fist</li></ol><p><i>кула́к</i> (kulák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кула́к</i> (kulák)</li><li>cam, lobe</li></ol><p><i>кула́к</i> (kulák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) cooldown (period in a game during which a player cannot use an item or ability after it has been used)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cooldown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coulibiac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coulibiac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of porridge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) coolie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>coolie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandpiper, snipe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kulikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an expert in culinary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(art of) cooking</li><li>cookery, gastronomy, delicatessen, cuisine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>culīnāria</i>, feminine of <i>culīnārius</i> (“culinary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>culinary</li></ol><p>From <i>кулинари́я</i> (kulinaríja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or from <i>кулина́рия</i> (kulinárija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) link</li><li>slide <i>(of a trombone)</i></li><li>(theater,&#32;in the plural) wings, side scenes, slips, coulisses</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coulisse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kulich (Easter cake)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kulich</li></ol><p><i>кули́ч</i> (kulíč) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pendant</li><li>coulomb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coulant</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>coulomb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) lobbies, couloirs, corridors, (by extension) environment, surroundings</li><li>used in the expression</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>couloirs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack</li><li>(archaic,&#32;dialectal) a cowl or hood</li><li>(archaic,&#32;dialectal) a bundle of straw or twigs</li><li>(archaic,&#32;dialectal) a cod end of a trawling net</li></ol><p>Germanic borrowings in the 16th century:</p><p>Identical at least in a dressing-sense to German <i>Kugel</i> (“cowl”), together with Ukrainian <i>куль</i> (kulʹ), Belarusian <i>куль</i> (kulʹ), Polish <i>kul</i>, all with meanings partially or wholly identical with the Russian, compare the extension of a meaning “hood covering anything” in German <i>Schlappen</i>, and:</p><p>Also identifiable with German <i>Keule</i> and <i>Kugel</i>, note in particular Old Norse <i>kýll</i>, <i>kýl</i> (“bag”) (whence modern Icelandic <i>kýla</i> (“to stuff, to fill, to bag”)), and generally used borrowings Lithuanian <i>kūlỹs</i> (“bundle”), Latvian <i>kūlis</i> (“bundle”), and:</p><p>The cod-end of a fishing-net, on the other hand, can be understood as (the related) German <i>Kuhle</i>, Dutch <i>kuil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climax, culmination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cult, the social movement and practices associated with the admiration or worship of something</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cultus</i> (per Shansky via German or French).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cult of personality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cultivation, cultivating; culturing</li></ol><p><i>культиви́ровать</i> (kulʹtivírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to cultivate, to raise</li><li>to cultivate, to develop</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kultivieren</i> + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>religious</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cult</li></ol><p><i>культ</i> (kulʹt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cultural worker, organizer of cultural activities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culture</li><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) culture</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) culture, cultivation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cultūra</i>. Further from Latin <i>cultus</i>, p.p.p. of <i>colō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷelh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cultural</li><li>(relational,&#32;bacteriology) culture</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>kulturell</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodybuilding</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>culturisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodybuilder</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>culturiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;sarcastic) diminutive of <i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>culturally</li><li>in a cultivated or educated manner</li></ol><p><i>культу́рный</i> (kulʹtúrnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cultural-historical, cultural and historic</li></ol><p><i>культу́рный</i> (kulʹtúrnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>истори́ческий</i> (istoríčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culture, standard of culture</li></ol><p><i>культу́рный</i> (kulʹtúrnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cultural</li><li><i>(person)</i> cultured, cultivated, civilized</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) cultured, cultivated</li></ol><p><i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra, “culture”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culture shock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stump (remains of a limb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>куль</i> (kulʹ)</li><li>paper or plastic bag</li></ol><p><i>куль</i> (kulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trap</li><li>a stone crush trap</li><li>a bear trap</li></ol><p>From Komi-Zyrian</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кулёк</i> (kuljók)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather of one's child</li><li>father of one's godchild</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a friend in high places, one's benefactor</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) security officer, detective</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) man, fellow (form of address to a middle-aged or elderly man)</li><li>(<i>geomorphology</i>) erg, sand sea (<i>in Central Asia</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kumъ</i>, back-formation from <i>&#42;kuma</i> (“godmother”), from Balkan Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;cómmater</i>, from Latin <i>compater</i> (“godfather”).</p><p>Borrowed from Turkic; compare to Kazakh <i>құм</i> (qūm), Kyrgyz <i>кум</i> (kum), Uzbek <i>qum</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Кум)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qom (a city and province of Iran)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian قم (qum).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) form of address to a middle-aged woman</li></ol><p>From Latin commater.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rubus nessensis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coumarin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red calico</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) red calico</li></ol><p><i>кума́ч</i> (kumáč) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) to think, to ponder, to ruminate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have knowledge in some affair; to understand</li></ol><p>From <i>ку-</i> (ku-) +&lrm; <i>ме́кать</i> (mékatʹ) (see <i>смека́ть</i> (smekátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brown trout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Sami language (compare Northern Sami <i>guvžá</i> and Kildin Sami <i>куввч</i> (kuvvč)), likely via Northern Finnish dialects (kumsi) or Karelian <i>kumša</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paganism</i>) idol, graven image</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) beloved person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>коумиръ</i> (kumirŭ), whose origin is uncertain. Perhaps a Turkic or Semitic borrowing: compare Classical Syriac <i>ܟܘܡܪܐ</i> (kūmrāʾ, “priest”), whence Old Armenian <i>քուրմ</i> (kʻurm, “priest”). Has also been connected to Ossetian <i>гуымиры</i> (g°ymiry, “giant”) and Georgian <i>გმირი</i> (gmiri, “hero”) and together with them derived from the name of Cimmerians.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joss house</li></ol><p><i>куми́р</i> (kumír) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kumquat (fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) godfather’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relationship of godparents</li><li>nepotism, cronyism</li></ol><p>From <i>кум</i> (kum) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>koumiss</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, such as Tatar <i>qımız</i> (“fermented milk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) koumiss</li></ol><p><i>кумы́с</i> (kumýs) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кунг-фу́</i> (kung-fú)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kung fu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 功夫 (<i>gōngfu</i>) via English <i>kung fu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beniseed, sesame</li><li>gingelli</li></ol><p>First attested in 1780, other forms <i>кунчук</i> (kunčuk) in 1809, <i>кунчуг</i> (kunčug) in 1805, <i>кунчут</i> (kunčut) in 1795 and <i>кунзют</i> (kunzjut) in 1740. Borrowed from Kipchak <i>&#42;künǰüt</i> &lt; <i>&#42;künǰit</i> (whence Tatar <i>көнжет</i> (könjet), <i>көнҗет</i> (köncet), Kyrgyz <i>күнжүт</i> (künjüt), Kazakh <i>күнжіт</i> (künjıt)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sesame</li></ol><p><i>кунжу́т</i> (kunžút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cunnilingus</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>куннили́нгус</i> (kunnilíngus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) marten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marten</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kunica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cunnilingus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кунг-фу́</i> (kung-fú)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ruse; trick; prank</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kunststück</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) group, heap, pile, clump</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>купа</i> (kupa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kupa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually women's) swimsuit, bathing suit, swimwear</li></ol><p>From <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ, “to bathe, give a bath”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bathing</li></ol><p><i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathrobe (terrycloth robe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathhouse</li></ol><p><i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathing</li></ol><p><i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>купа́ние</i> (kupánije)</li></ol><p><i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нье</i> (-ánʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, to give a bath (<i>transitive only, to bathe someone; use купа́ться (kupátʹsja) (reflexive, intransitive) to bathe oneself</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>купати</i> (kupati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫpati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, to take a bath, to have a bath</li><li>to swim (to immerse oneself, or part of the body, in water for pleasure or refreshment)</li></ol><p><i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) compartment, couchette</li><li>coupé, coupe (a sporty automobile with two doors)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coupé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) compartment (on a train)</li></ol><p><i>купе́</i> (kupé) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) font</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кѫпѣль</i> (kǫpělĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫpělь</i>. Related to <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) merchant, trader</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) buyer, purchaser</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kupьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) merchant; merchant's</li></ol><p><i>купе́ц</i> (kupéc) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) merchants, merchantry</li></ol><p>From <i>купе́ц</i> (kupéc) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo), with unexpected iotation; modeled after <i>купе́ческий</i> (kupéčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>it's what is used to buy: деньги.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(only in expression неопалимая купина)</i> the burning bush</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>купина</i> (kupina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫpina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, to purchase [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kupiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verse, couplet, stanza (of a song or poem)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) satirical songs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>couplet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>купле́т</i> (kuplét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purchase, buying</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kupja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) purchase and sale as qualified in Civil Code of Russia</li></ol><p>Antonymous compound of <i>купля</i> (kuplja) +&lrm; <i>продажа</i> (prodaža). See Azerbaijani <i>alğı-satqı</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupola, dome</li><li>umbrella (the main body of a jellyfish, excluding the tentacles)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Kuppel</i> or French <i>coupole</i>, from Italian <i>cupola</i> (“dome, vault”), from Latin <i>cūpula, cuppula</i> (“a cupped shape; a tub, cask”), from Latin <i>cūpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cupola, dome</li></ol><p><i>ку́пол</i> (kúpol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coupon, voucher</li><li>coupon, official and colloquial name of currencies of several ex-USSR states</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coupon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to plug, to cork</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to block</li><li>to seal firmly</li></ol><p>From <i>ку́пор</i> (kúpor, “corkscrew”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ); <i>ку́пор</i> (kúpor) is from Middle Low German <i>kuper</i> (“cooper”), from <i>kupe</i> (“barrel”), from Latin <i>cupa</i> (“barrel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vitriol (sulphate)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>купоросъ</i> (kuporos) (att. since 1582), from a Western European source like Middle French <i>couperose</i> (Russia didn't trade directly with France in the 16th century, making some intermediary likely), from Old French <i>coperose</i> (whence English <i>copperas</i>), from Latin <i>cuprum</i> (“copper”) and <i>rosa</i> (“rose”), likely calquing Ancient Greek <i>χάλκανθον</i> (khálkanthon, “solution of copper sulfate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vitriol (sulphate)</li></ol><p><i>купоро́с</i> (kuporós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vitriol (sulphate)</li></ol><p><i>купоро́с</i> (kuporós) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) deed of purchase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banknote</li><li>cut, deletion (<i>in a video or audio record, film, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>coupure</i> (“a cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Kupiansk</li></ol><p><i>Ку́пян(ск)</i> (Kúpjan(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ку́пянский</i> (kúpjanskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>now&#32;rare</i>) rooster, cock</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) attention which one pays to someone, gloze</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in diplomatic affairs, accolading, formal address</li><li>to a woman, wooing, cajoling</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>куръ</i> (kurŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kurъ</i>. The normal word for the rooster in Old Russian, displaced from the 17<sup>th</sup> century by <i>пѣту́хъ</i> (pětúx).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>cour</i>, and constructions with <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ), <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) and the like from French <i>faire la cour</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) chicken, hen</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) chicken meat</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) blizzard, snowstorm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whole dried apricots (without the pit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) boldness, spirit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>courage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiming clock, chimes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>courante</i>. Sense development: “music” → “chimes” → “chiming clock”. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>currō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang) an Estonian person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Estonian <i>kurat</i> (meaning "a devil", a commonly-used vulgarity in Estonian).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curator; trustee; supervisor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whore, prostitute</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bitch, slut</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare) bastard</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;dated) fuck!, shit! bollocks!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>курва</i> (kurva, “prostitute, whore”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kury</i> (“whore”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) whore’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) curvularia (<i>Curvularia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Curvularia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrow, kurgan, tumulus (ancient burial mound)</li><li>barrow, hillock (landform)</li></ol><p>From Turkic; compare Turkish <i>kurgan</i> (“fortress”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Курган)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kurgan (a city, the administrative center of Kurgan Oblast in Siberia, Russia); originally called Tsaryovo Gorodishche (Царёво Городище) (1662–1782)</li></ol><p>From <i>курга́н</i> (kurgán, “burial mound”), from Turkic (compare Turkish <i>kurgan</i> (“fortress”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kurgan</li></ol><p><i>курга́н</i> (kurgán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kurgan (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Курга́н</i> (Kurgán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dock-tailed, bobtail, shorthaired <i>(of an animal)</i></li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) too tight, too short <i>(of clothing)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;кърногузъ</i> (&#42;kŭrnoguzŭ), from <i>&#42;кърнъ</i> (&#42;kŭrnŭ, “cut, chopped”) +&lrm; <i>гуз</i> (guz, “buttocks, rump”), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrnъ</i> + <i>&#42;gǫzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kurd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kurdish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kurdish (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>курд</i> (kurd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail fat, fat tail (<i>of a sheep</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;kudruk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kurdyuk, tail fat of a sheep</li></ol><p><i>курдю́к</i> (kurdjúk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tobacco, baccy; cigarettes, something to smoke; (smell of) cigarette smoke</li><li>fumigant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoking (verbal noun, the smoking of tobacco)</li><li>incense</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a Zaporozhian Cossack village or township</li><li>(historical,&#32;Ukraine) a military unit, e.g. a battalion-size unit in the Zaporozhian Cossack Army</li><li>(Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine) a Cossack village house</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) shack, hut (a simple or temporary shelter)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>курі́нь</i> (kurínʹ), from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hoarfrost, rime</li></ol><p>Probably, from a Finno-Ugric language. According to another version, the word is derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rъďa</i> (“rust”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) place for smoking, smoking room, smoking area</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ, “to smoke”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kuril Islands</li></ol><p><i>Кури́лы</i> (Kuríly) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Kurilsk or Severo-Kurilsk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoker</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chicken</li><li>limited, narrow-minded</li></ol><p><i>кур</i> (kur) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curation</li></ol><p><i>кури́ровать</i> (kurírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be in charge, to curate, to supervise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) smoking</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke (tobacco etc.)</li><li>to burn, to fumigate</li><li>to distil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кꙋрити</i> (kuriti, “smoke, emit smoke”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuriti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, to emit smoke, to fume</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only)</li></ol><p><i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken (a bird of species <i>Gallus gallus</i>)</li><li>hen (female chicken)</li><li>chicken (flesh of a chicken used as food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kurica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ку́ра</i> (kúra) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) miser, moneygrubber, penny pincher, cheapskate</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) kurkul (a prosperous peasant in the Russian Empire or the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>курку́ль</i> (kurkúlʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turmeric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Courland</li></ol><p><i>Курля́ндия</i> (Kurljándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kurmanji</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Northern Kurdish <i>kurmancî</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pug-nosed, snub-nosed</li><li><i>(nose)</i> turned-up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кърноносъ</i> (kŭrnonosŭ, “with a cut nose”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrnonosъ</i>. Compare корнать (kornatʹ), корноухий (kornouxij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Spilotes pullatus</i>; the chicken snake.</li></ol><p><i>кур</i> (kur, “rooster/chicken”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “-eater”), literally “rooster/chicken-eater”. Compare English <i>chicken snake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cock, cocking piece (hammer of a firearm trigger mechanism)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;proscribed) trigger (a finger-operated lever used to fire a gun)</li></ol><p><i>кур</i> (kur) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partridge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;куропътъка</i> (&#42;kuropŭtŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuropъtъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resort, holiday resort, health resort</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kurort</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) resort</li></ol><p><i>куро́рт</i> (kurórt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ку́рица</i> (kúrica): (small) hen</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ку́ра</i> (kúra): (small) hen</li><li>female of any fowl (quail, pheasant, chicken, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>ку́ра</i> (kúra) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>school</i>) course, year (in college, university)</li><li>(aviation,&#32;nautical) course, vector</li><li>exchange rate</li><li>line, policy</li><li>See в курсе (v kurse)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>cursus</i>. Cognate with German <i>Kurs</i>, French <i>cours</i>, French <i>course</i>, English <i>course</i> etc. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cadet (a student at a military school who is training to be an officer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>italic (typeface whose letters slant to the right)</li></ol><p>Derived from Medieval Latin <i>cursīvus</i>, from Latin <i>cursus</i>, from Latin <i>currō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow a regular route (<i>of transportation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nonstandard,&#32;dated) girl student</li></ol><p><i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kursk</li></ol><p><i>Кур-</i> (Kur-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) course</li></ol><p><i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cursor (part of scientific instruments that indicates position)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>class, training course</li><li>cram school</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtesan</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cohors</i> (whence also Russian <i>кого́рта</i> (kogórta)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jacket (a short coat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>kurtka</i>, from Latin <i>curtus</i> (“short”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ку́ртка</i> (kúrtka): (small) jacket, coat</li></ol><p><i>ку́ртка</i> (kúrtka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jacket (a short coat)</li></ol><p><i>ку́ртка</i> (kúrtka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kurultai</li></ol><p>From a Mongolic language. Compare Mongolian <i>хуралдай</i> (xuraldaj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curly (<i>hair</i>)</li><li>curly-haired, curly-headed</li><li>(<i>ironical</i>) <i>(style)</i> florid, flowery, frilly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) kurchatovium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>кур</i> (kur).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courier (person who delivers messages)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>courier</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>currō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) courier</li></ol><p><i>курье́р</i> (kurʹjér) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) express train</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curio; funny or interesting thing or incident</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cūriōsus</i>, via German <i>kurios</i> or French <i>curieux</i> in the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>entertaining; amusing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>curieux</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Kursk</li></ol><p><i>Курск</i> (Kursk) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken (meat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken coop</li></ol><p>From <i>кура</i> (kura) +&lrm; <i>-ятник</i> (-jatnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoker (a person who smokes)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoking</li></ol><p>From <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ, “to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, bit, morsel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫsъ</i> (“piece”), from <i>&#42;kǫsati</i> (“to bite”). Cognate with Slovak <i>kus</i>, Slovene <i>kos</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>кус</i>/<i>kus</i>, Bulgarian <i>къс</i> (kǎs). According to Vasmer, perhaps also cognate with Greek <i>κνώδων</i> (knódon), Sanskrit <i>खादति</i> (khādati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li><li>to sting</li></ol><p>Synchronically, possibly derived from <i>кус</i> (kus). Diachronically, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫsati</i>, which may be derived from the Proto-Slavic ancestor of <i>кус</i> (kus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite, to be given to biting</li><li>to bite one another</li><li><i>(cloth)</i> to itch, to be itchy</li></ol><p><i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biting (<i>e.g. of a dog</i>)</li><li>itchy (<i>of coarse cloth</i>)</li><li>prohibitive, stiff (<i>of prices</i>)</li></ol><p><i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́чий</i> (-áčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to eat</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to bite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫsiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuscus (a type of marsupial from New Guinea)</li><li>couscous, a spicy dish that originated in northern Africa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic كُسْكُس (kuskus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece</li><li>bit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) grand (one thousand of some currency)</li></ol><p><i>кус</i> (kus) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar&#32;slang</i>) piece of shit (a bad thing or person)</li></ol><p>Probably calque of English <i>piece of shit</i> (borrowed at the end of the 20th century after the dissolution of the Soviet Union, with the rising influence of American popular culture in Russia), to denote the same idea that <i>ку́ча</i> (kúča) is traditionally used for, but usually only literally (never of a thing or person).</p><p>The calque nature of the idiom is obvious to a native speaker since <i>кусо́к</i> (kusók, “piece”) presupposes <i>це́лое</i> (céloje, “the whole”) in Russian. For example, <i>кусо́к пирога</i> (kusók piroga, “a piece of a pie”). While the English idiom <i>piece</i> is used in the sense of <i>an item belonging to the class of similar items</i>, the meaning of Russian <i>кусо́к</i> (kusók) is closer to that of the English word <i>part</i>.</p><p>Since the source of the calque is transparent, the synonymous combination <i>кусо́к говна</i> (kusók govna) is possible as well, but the usage of both phrases is restricted even in colloquial speech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>кусо́к</i> (kusók): piece, bit, morsel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush, shrub</li><li>(<i>oil industry</i>) wellsite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kustъ</i>. Cognates include Belarusian <i>куст</i> (kust), Ukrainian <i>кущ</i> (kušč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bushes, shrubs, shrubbery</li><li>bush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bush, bushes</li></ol><p><i>куста́рник</i> (kustárnik, “bush”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artisanal, handcrafted</li><li>imperfect, primitive</li></ol><p><i>куста́рь</i> (kustárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artisan, handcraftsman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>kunster</i> (“connoisseur of the arts”), also <i>kunstener</i>; compare German <i>Künstler</i> (“artist”). Influenced by <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>куст</i> (kust): (small) bush, shrub</li></ol><p><i>куст</i> (kust) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bush, shrub</li></ol><p><i>куст</i> (kust) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;Tatarstan) dick, cock</li><li>(<i>very&#32;rare</i>) door latch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>кутак</i> (qutaq).</p><p>Etymology unknown, probably the same as above, as “a rod” can as well encompass the meaning of a male member as the thought of a “bar” locking a thing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle up <i>(in)</i>, to wrap up <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫtati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle/wrap oneself up</li></ol><p><i>ку́тать</i> (kútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) commotion, hubbub</li></ol><p>Derived from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuticle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reveller, bacchanal, hellbender</li><li>flaneur</li></ol><p><i>кути́ть</i> (kutítʹ, “to carouse”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carouse, to revel (make merry)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kutiti</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>кутити</i> (kutiti), Czech <i>kutit</i> and possibly also Polish <i>skutek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lockup, jail, quod</li></ol><p>From the Russian family name <i>Куту́зов</i> (Kutúzov) (after chief of police Павел Васильевич Голенищев-Кутузов).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kutuzov; Kutuzov's (Russian surname, especially referring to Mikhail Kutuzov, Field Marshal of the Russian Empire, or things named after him)</li></ol><p><i>Куту́зов</i> (Kutúzov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couturier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>couturier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunken revelry, drinking bout, bender</li></ol><p><i>кути́ть</i> (kutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female cook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitchen</li><li>suite of furniture for a kitchen</li><li>cuisine</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) behind-the-scenes dealings or operation</li></ol><p>Since the 18th century. Borrowed from Polish <i>kuchnia</i>. Further from Latin <i>coquīna</i>, further from Latin <i>coquō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>. Displaced Russian <i>пова́рня</i> (povárnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kitchen</li></ol><p><i>ку́хня</i> (kúxnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ку́хня</i> (kúxnja): (small) kitchen, kitchenette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of woman's clothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dock-tailed, short-tailed</li><li>short (in terms of stature, length, size, clothing size)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kucъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap, pile</li><li>a lot (a large amount)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) heap</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pile, steaming pile (<i>of excrement</i>)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a hut made of human-height poles covered with dirt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuča</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>куча</i> (kuča), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫťa</i>. Doublet of <i>ку́ща</i> (kúšča), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free-for-all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cumulonimbus</li></ol><p><i>кучево́й</i> (kučevój) +&lrm; <i>дождево́й</i> (doždevój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;meteorology) cumulus</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) heap, pile</li></ol><p><i>ку́ча</i> (kúča) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coachman (one who drives a coach)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Kutscher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coachman (one who drives a coach)</li></ol><p><i>ку́чер</i> (kúčer) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coachman (one who drives a coach)</li></ol><p><i>ку́чер</i> (kúčer) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curly, frizzy, curly-headed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kučeravъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ку́ча</i> (kúča): small heap</li></ol><p><i>ку́ча</i> (kúča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cluster (to form a cluster or group), to gather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belt, girdle, sash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кушакъ</i> (kušakŭ), from Kipchak or Old Anatolian Turkish; equivalent to Crimean Tatar <i>кушак</i> or Turkish <i>kuşak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Kushan (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kushan</li></ol><p>From <i>куша́н</i> (kušán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish, meal, food</li></ol><p>From <i>ку́шать</i> (kúšatʹ, “to eat, have, take”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a guest, woman or child</i>) to eat, to have <i>(a meal)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>кушати</i> (kušati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kušati</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kausjan, “to taste, to sample”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaise longue; a small, backless sofa or couch</li><li>massage table</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>couchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chaise longue, a small, backless sofa or couch, massage table</li></ol><p><i>куше́тка</i> (kušétka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foliage, thicket</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) tent, hut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>кѫща</i> (kǫšta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫťa</i>. Doublet of <i>ку́ча</i> (kúča), the inherited East Slavic form still found in dialects.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ahem (exclamation expressing indecision, thought or irony)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Khmer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial,&#32;rare) shit, fuck</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial,&#32;rare) fuck, shit, damn</li></ol><p>Coined by Belarusian writer Olga Gromyko, as a euphemistic alteration of <i>хер</i> (xer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kshatriya (member of the warrior caste in ancient India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>क्षत्रिय</i> (kṣatriya, “protector of gentle people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kshatriya (member of the warrior caste in ancient India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>क्षत्रिय</i> (kṣatriya, “protector of gentle people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>к</i> (k).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ǃXóõ (Khoisan language of Botswana and Namibia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from ǃXóõ <i>ǃKhong</i>, with the digraph ⟨къ⟩ representing the alveolar click /ǃ/.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) sound a swan makes</li></ol><p>Of imitative origin</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Qizilbash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kipchak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source. The Kipchaks themselves derived their name as meaning "hollow tree" (Karakhanid <i>kuv</i> <i>ağaç</i>, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;ïgač</i> (“tree”)). Compare Chagatay <small>[script needed]</small> (Qipčaq), Turkish <i>Kıpçak</i>. More at Kipchaks.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кирги́з</i> (kirgíz): Kyrgyz (person, preferred form in Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kyrgyz <i>кыргыз</i> (kırgız).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>кирги́зский</i> (kirgízskij): Kyrgyz (person, preferred form in Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>shoo! (especially to a cat)</li></ol><p>Probably from a Turkic language.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>a kysz</i>, German <i>ksch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) kyopolou (Bulgarian and Turkish eggplant dish)</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from Bulgarian <i>кьо́поолу</i> (kjópoolu), from Turkish <i>köpoğlu</i> (literally “son of a bitch”); the semantic evolution of the term is unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> Q, q</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chianti (Tuscan red wine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>chianti</i>, after <i>Chianti</i>, its area of production. Through Latin from the Etruscan <i>𐌂𐌋𐌀𐌍𐌕𐌄</i> (clante).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kyat (currency of Myanmar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Burmese <i>ကျပ်</i> (kyap).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> K, k</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kendo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>剣道</i> (けんどう, kendō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>molecular biology</i>) cap (<i>of an RNA molecule</i>)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) interest rate cap</li><li>(nautical,&#32;informal) Cap, cap'n</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) Captain Obvious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cap</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>Cap</i> or <i>cap'n</i>, short for <i>captain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) cache (fast temporary storage for data)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) cash (money in the form of notes&#47;bills and coins)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cache</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>cash</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>кешбэк</i> (kešbɛk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Roman letter Q, q.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch (<i>along a road</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) cunette, cuvette</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) moat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cuvette</i>. Further from Latin <i>cūpa</i> + <i>-ittus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religious slur</i>) kafir (disbeliever)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كافر (ḱafir), from Arabic كَافِر (kāfir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cologne</li></ol><p><i>Кёльн</i> (Kjolʹn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>winter sports</i>) curling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>curling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curler (sportsman who plays curling)</li></ol><p>From <i>кёрлинг</i> (kjórling) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>литр</i> (litr, “liter”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij, “lightweight”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Л)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) abbreviation of <i>ладья́</i> (ladʹjá, “R (rook, castle)”)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) abbreviation of <i>лату́нь</i> (latúnʹ, “brass”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>ла́мпа</i> (lámpa, “lamp”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>лебёдка</i> (lebjódka, “winch”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>steam locomotives</i>) abbreviation of <i>Лебедянский</i> (Lebedjanskij, “Lebedyanskiy”)</li><li>(<i>submarine series</i>) abbreviation of <i>«Ле́нинец»</i> («Léninec», “K-137 Leninets (Yankee Navaga)”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj, “left”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>ле́тний</i> (létnij, “summer”) (<i>diesel oil marking</i>)</li><li>(<i>shipping</i>) abbreviation of <i>легкове́сный</i> (lexkovésnyj, “lightweight”) (<i>ship class</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>легкорастворимый</i> (lexkorastvorimyj, “highly soluble”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>publishing</i>) abbreviation of <i>лист</i> (list, “sheet, leaf, page”)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>лицо́</i> (licó, “person of a verb”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Л.)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bibliography</i>) abbreviation of <i>Ленингра́д</i> (Leningrád, “Leningrad”) (now Saint Petersburg)</li><li>(<i>bibliography</i>) abbreviation of <i>Ло́ндон</i> (Lóndon, “London”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>maps</i>) abbreviation of <i>лес</i> (les, “forest”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>литерату́ра</i> (literatúra, “literature”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>лошади́ная си́ла</i> (lošadínaja síla): HP, horsepower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>лошади́ная си́ла</i> (lošadínaja síla): HP, horsepower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) lean-to, shed</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) barn, granary, warehouse</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) tree stand, machan</li><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) corner store, bodega</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps borrowed from Komi-Zyrian <i>лобос</i> (lobos), although the direction of borrowing is unclear. Alternatively, possibly of Slavic origin and related to <i>лаба́зник</i> (labáznik, “dropwort”) and <i>лабазина</i> (labazina, “twig, switch”), although as noted by Shansky, this hypothesis would require an improbable semantic evolution.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) corn-dealer</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) dropwort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) corn-dealer's wife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barn, granary, warehouse</li></ol><p><i>лаба́з</i> (labáz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a Lithuanian.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Lithuanian <i>labas</i> (“hello”), as in the greetings labas rytas (“good morning”) and labas vakaras (“good evening”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>labialized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to labialize (make a sound labial)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labyrinth, maze</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>λαβύρινθος</i> (labúrinthos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) labyrinth</li></ol><p><i>лабири́нт</i> (labirínt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laboratory technician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laboratory</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>labōrātōrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laboratory</li></ol><p><i>лаборато́рия</i> (laboratórija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labrador retriever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a Latvian.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latvian <i>labus</i>, accusative plural of Latvian <i>labs</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lava</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) bench</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>lava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lava lamp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lava lamp</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) lavender</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lavender</li></ol><p><i>лава́нда</i> (lavánda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lavender</li></ol><p><i>лава́нда</i> (lavánda) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lavash (a soft, thin flatbread made with flour, water, yeast, and salt, baked in a tandoor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>լավաշ</i> (lavaš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lavash</li></ol><p><i>лава́ш</i> (laváš) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>лавэ́</i> (lavɛ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avalanche</li><li>landslide</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lawine</i>. Further from Latin <i>lābīna</i>, from <i>lābor</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>о́ползень</i> (ópolzenʹ) (&lt; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to tack (to turn the bow through the wind)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to manoeuvre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>laveren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;dated) bench</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;obsolete) plank, floorboard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ла́вица</i> (lávica), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lavà</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench</li><li>a small shop, store</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lavà</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically the <i>банк</i> (bank, “bank”)—<i>ба́нка</i> (bánka, “bench”) (<i>банке́тка</i> (bankétka), <i>банке́т</i> (bankét)) relation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to try to catch, to trap (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ла́вка</i> (lávka): (small) bench; (small) shop/store, kiosk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gang, clique; shady concern</li></ol><p><i>ла́вка</i> (lávka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) petty trader, small shopkeeper</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) greedy bourgeois philistine</li></ol><p><i>ла́вка</i> (lávka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laurel, bay tree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) lavra, (a large male) monastery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherry laurel</li></ol><p><i>лавр</i> (lavr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ви́шня</i> (víšnja), Calque of Latin <i>laurocerasus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laurel</li></ol><p><i>лавр</i> (lavr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) or <i>лавр</i> (lavr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) money, dough</li></ol><p>From Romani <i>love</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>lova</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(technical,&#32;often preceded by <i>временно́й</i> (vremennój, “time”)) lag (delay)</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) lag (delay in the functioning of a computer program)</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) synonym of <i>ла́га</i> (lága); a crossbeam supporting floor decking or a roof base (ceiling)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) chip log</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Lochium Funis (obsolete constellation proposed in Johann Bode's <i>Uranographia</i> in 1801)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) board (side of a ship)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) row or layer of water barrels</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lag</i>.</p><p>Deverbal from <i>лага́ть</i> (lagátʹ, “to lay”).</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>log</i> (“chip log”).</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>laag</i> (“row of guns along one side of a ship”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>construction</i>) a crossbeam supporting floor decking or a roof base (ceiling)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>лага́ть</i> (lagátʹ, “to lay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) to lag</li></ol><p><i>лаг</i> (lag, “lag”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lagati</i>, iterative of <i>&#42;ložiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lager (<i>type of beer</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) camp</li></ol><p><i>ла́герь</i> (lágerʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camp, camp-out</li><li>camp, party, faction</li><li>labour camp, prison camp</li></ol><p>Later borrowing from German <i>Lager</i> (“camp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laghman (Central Asian noodle dish)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Chinese <i>拉麵</i>&nbsp;/ <i>拉面</i> (lāmiàn), via Kazakh <i>лағман</i> (lağman), etc. Doublet of <i>ра́мэн</i> (rámɛn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lagoon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lagoon</li></ol><p><i>лагу́н(а)</i> (lagún(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmony, concord</li><li>manner, way</li><li>(<i>music</i>) mode; harmony, tonality</li><li>(<i>music</i>) fret</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ladъ</i>, of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) darling, sweetheart</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ладый</i> (ladyj, “dear”), but of disputed origin. Possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lada</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂d-</i> (“to love; beloved, wife”), if cognate with Tocharian B <i>lāre</i> (“beloved”, adjective) (&lt; <i>&#42;leh₂d-ro-</i>) and Lycian <i>𐊍𐊀𐊅𐊀</i> (lada, “wife”). Alternatively, simply a substantization of the root of <i>лад</i> (lad, “harmony”) and <i>ладить</i> (laditʹ, “to be on good terms”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лада)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lada</li><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Lada</li><li>Lada (a Russian car)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labdanum, frankincense (a perfume often used in the rites of various religions)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ладанъ</i> (ladanŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>λάδανον</i> (ládanon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rockrose, cistus (<i>Cistus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ла́дан</i> (ládan, “labdanum, frankincense”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Cistaceae</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>ла́данниковый</i> (ládannikovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) labdanum, frankincense</li></ol><p><i>ла́дан</i> (ládan) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ла́дан</i> (ládan).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ladino</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ladino <i>לאדינו</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get on <i>(with)</i>, to agree <i>(with)</i>, to be on good terms <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare, to make ready, to fix, to tune</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to plan</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repeat, to keep saying, to drone <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;laditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лад</i> (lad, “harmony, concord”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work well, to advance</li></ol><p><i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>well, all right, okay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well-built, well-formed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) comfortable, fitting nicely</li></ol><p><i>лад</i> (lad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ladogan (inhabitant of Staraya Ladoga or of the district surrounding Lake Ladoga)</li></ol><p><i>Ла́дога</i> (Ládoga) +&lrm; <i>-а́нин</i> (-ánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ladoga; Ladogan (referring to Lake Ladoga or Staraya Ladoga)</li></ol><p><i>Ла́дога</i> (Ládoga) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm (<i>of the hand</i>)</li></ol><p>Metathesis of <i>Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolnь</i></i> (Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dolnʹ</i>); the expected form <i>доло́нь</i> (dolónʹ) occurs dialectally. Doublet of <i>длань</i> (dlanʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ладо́нь</i> (ladónʹ)</li></ol><p>A diminutive of <i>ладо́нь</i> (ladónʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ладо́ни</i> (ladóni): palms of the hands</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ладо́нь</i> (ladónʹ), diminutive of <i>ладо́ша</i> (ladóša): (small) palm of the hand</li></ol><p><i>ладо́ша</i> (ladóša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ла́да</i> (láda)</li></ol><p><i>ла́да</i> (láda) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clapping game, similar to pattycake</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) alternative form of <i>лады́</i> (ladý); okay, alright</li></ol><p><i>лад(о́нь)</i> (lad(ónʹ), “palm (of the hand)”) +&lrm; <i>-ушки</i> (-uški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) alternative form of <i>ла́дно</i> (ládno); okay, alright</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) boat, longship (a type of medieval vessel propelled by oars and a sail)</li><li>vase or other vessel in the form of a boat</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) rook, castle (Russian abbreviation: Л)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oldī</i>, cognate with modern Russian ло́дка (lódka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>l'agio</i>, from Italian <i>aggio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crap, something unpleasant or poor-quality</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, rubbish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) extreme easiness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deception, lie, fraud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manhole, trapdoor</li><li>Laz</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lazъ</i>. Per Vasmer and ESSJa, most likely deverbal from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làziti</i> (compare Russian <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>climbing</li></ol><p><i>ла́зать</i> (lázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lasagna (pasta)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>lasagna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infirmary, sickbay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrow hole</li><li>loophole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laser (device producing beam of light)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laser pointer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laser</li></ol><p><i>ла́зер</i> (lázɛr, lázer) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb, to clamber</li><li>to creep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làziti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) An ant of the genus <i>Lasius</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Lasius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cerulean, azure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lazurite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lazurite</li></ol><p><i>лазури́т</i> (lazurít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>azure, sky-blue, cerulean</li></ol><p><i>лазу́рь</i> (lazúrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>azure (colour&#47;color)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>lazur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) scout, spy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark (of a dog or other animal), barking, yelp</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>ла́ять</i> (lájatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;nautical,&#32;regional) a large boat, sometimes with a deck, with one or two masts</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) vehicle (usually a car or a bicycle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>laiva</i>, from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;laiva</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flawją</i>, from pre-Germanic <i>&#42;plowyom</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) like</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>like</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laika (<i>lit.</i>, barker), a native Russian breed of dog, a rather small hunting dog of Russia’s northern forests, similar to a husky</li><li>kidskin, kid leather</li></ol><p><i>лай</i> (laj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to like (<i>on social networks</i>)</li></ol><p>English <i>like</i> +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) augmentative of <i>лайк</i> (lajk, “like”)</li></ol><p>From <i>лайк</i> (lajk, “like”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to like (<i>on social networks</i>)</li></ol><p>English <i>like</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime (a green citrus fruit)</li><li>lime colour/color</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime (a green citrus fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Key lime</li><li>lime (color)</li></ol><p><i>лайм</i> (lajm) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>liner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literature</i>) a light novel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>light novel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>varnish</li><li>lacquer, lac, lake</li><li>polish, nail polish</li><li>Lak, a member of the Lak people of Dagestan.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lack</i> or Dutch <i>lak</i> or French <i>laque</i>, from Classical Persian لاک (lāk), from Sanskrit <i>लाक्षा</i> (lākṣā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lap (to take liquid into the mouth with the tongue)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink greedily or in large amounts (usually alcohol)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лакати</i> (lakati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lokati</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>làkti</i> (“to lap”), Sanskrit <i>लाकयति</i> (lākayati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;historical) Lacedaemonian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lackey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>lacayo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) footman's quarters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) footman, lackey</li></ol><p><i>лаке́й</i> (lakéj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lak person (male)</li></ol><p>From <i>лак</i> (lak) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lacquering</li></ol><p><i>лакирова́ть</i> (lakirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacquered</li><li>gleaming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to varnish, to lacquer</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>lackieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), or from <i>лак</i> (lak) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litmus (dyestuff from lichen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litmus paper (paper containing a water-soluble mixture of different dyes extracted from certain lichens)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) litmus</li></ol><p><i>ла́кмус</i> (lákmus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacquered</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lacquer, varnish</li></ol><p><i>лак</i> (lak) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to treat someone to something delicious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regale oneself, to treat oneself to, to feast on</li></ol><p><i>ла́комить</i> (lákomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gourmand</li><li>gourmet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dainty, tidbit; delicacy</li></ol><p><i>ла́комый</i> (lákomyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dainty, tasty</li><li>fond (of); partial (to) (especially food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olkomъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak in a laconic, brief, terse manner</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>λακωνίζω</i> (lakōnízō). By surface analysis, <i>Лако́ния</i> (Lakónija) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>laconic, terse, pithy, concise</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>lacon(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquorice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lakritze</i>, from Latin <i>liquiritia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γλυκύρριζα</i> (glukúrrhiza, “sweet root”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liquorice</li></ol><p><i>лакри́ца</i> (lakríca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) lacrosse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laksa (a spicy noodle soup or stew from Indonesia or Malaysia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lactose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) luxury</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>luxury</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;poetic) ruby (type of gem)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;relational) ruby (type of gem)</li></ol><p><i>лал</i> (lal) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>llama (South American mammal of the camel family, <i>Llama glama</i>)</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) lama</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) lama, Sumerian beneficent protective female deity</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>llama</i>, from Quechua <i>llama</i>.</p><p>From Tibetan <i>བླ་མ</i> (bla ma).</p><p>From Sumerian <i>𒀭𒆗</i> (dlammař [AN.KAL]).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лама)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lama (a tributary of the Shosha River in Moscow and Tver Oblasts, Russia)</li><li>Lama (a river in Krasnoyarsk Krai, in the southern Taymyr Peninsula, Russia)</li><li>Lama (a lake in Krasnoyarsk Krai, in the southern Taymyr Peninsula, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manatee, sea-cow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lamantin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lambada (Brazilian dance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>lambada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A flat-faced longhorn beetle, a lamiine.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Lamiinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laminaria, <i>Laminaria</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Laminaria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laminate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp</li><li>torch</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) vacuum tube</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) lampad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lampad</li></ol><p><i>лампа́да</i> (lampáda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A colored cloth stripe sewn along the outer seam of uniform trousers, lampasse</li><li>lampas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial,&#32;military slang) military general</li></ol><p>From <i>лампа́с</i> (lampás, “lampasse cloth in uniforms”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lampion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lamp</li><li>(relational,&#32;electronics) tube, valve (relating to vacuum tubes)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) mellow, heartful, sincere</li></ol><p><i>ла́мпа</i> (lámpa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj). Internet slang usage stems from the audiophile community and the supposedly smooth, mellow sound produced by traditional vacuum tube audio equipment.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ла́мпа</i> (lámpa)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) light bulb</li></ol><p><i>ла́мпа</i> (lámpa) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) łan (medieval Polish measurement of field area)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) field, cornfield, arable land</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) alternative form of <i>лян</i> (ljan); tael (Chinese unit of weight and currency)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;in quick speech) contraction of <i>ла́дно</i> (ládno); okay, alright</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>łan</i>, Old Czech <i>lán</i>, from Middle High German <i>lēhen</i>, from Old High German <i>lēhan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laihną</i>.</p><p>Derived from Chinese <i>兩</i>&nbsp;/ <i>两</i> (liǎng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lombard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiny lobster, rock lobster</li></ol><p>From French <i>langouste</i>, from Latin <i>locusta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landgrave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Landgraf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lansquenet (German mercenary)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Landsknecht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;dated) swede (<i>Brassica napus</i>, syn. <i>Brassica napus</i> subsp. <i>rapifera</i>)</li><li>(regional,&#32;dated) rutabaga (<i>the plant and the vegetable</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear; see <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landscape (portion of land or territory which the eye can comprehend in a single view)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Landschaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) landscape</li></ol><p><i>ландша́фт</i> (landšáft) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lily of the valley (<i>Convallaria majalis</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Polish <i>łanie uszko</i> (“doe ear”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lily of the valley</li></ol><p><i>ла́ндыш</i> (lándyš) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cheek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ланита</i> (lanita), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olnita</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>ланита</i> (lanita). Non-Slavic cognates include Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (aleina), Latin <i>ulna</i> (“elbow”), Sanskrit <i>अणि</i> (aṇi, “the point of a needle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doe, female deer</li></ol><p><i>лань</i> (lanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lanolin (greasy yellow substance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lancers (a type of mid-19th century quadrille)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>quadrille des lanciers</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lanthanum</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lanthanum</li></ol><p><i>ланта́н</i> (lantán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lancet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) chain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>łańcuch</i>, from an unattested compound of Middle High German <i>lanne</i> (“chain”) and Middle High German <i>zug</i> (“tug, pull”), the former being from Old High German <i>lanna</i> (“metal sheet, platelet; chain”), from Vulgar Latin <i>lanna</i> from Latin <i>lāmina</i> (“sheet especially of metal”), the latter from the well-known Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tugiz</i> (“tug, pull”). Ukrainian <i>ланцю́г</i> (lancjúh) and Belarusian <i>ланцу́г</i> (lancúh) are from the same source.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;English-speaking countries or environment</i>) lunch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lunch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lunch</li></ol><p>From <i>ланч</i> (lanč) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallow deer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laotian, Lao (person)</li></ol><p><i>Лао́с</i> (Laós) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Laotian</li></ol><p><i>Лао́с</i> (Laós) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laotian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) human's hand or foot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lapa</i>, according to Vasmer, cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōfô</i> (“the palm or hollow of the hand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to touch, to paw, to grope</li></ol><p><i>ла́па</i> (lápa) +&lrm; <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a lapidary manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ла́па</i> (lápa); a (small) paw</li><li>leg, limb, foot (<i>of insects or small animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) claw, grip, clutch, pawl, tang</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) curly quote (one of „ “ in Russian typography)</li></ol><p><i>ла́па</i> (lápa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lapti</li></ol><p><i>ла́поть</i> (lápotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bast shoe, bast sandal, lapti</li><li>an outdated, cumbersome, awkward thing</li><li>ignorant, uneducated person, dropout (who might wear cheap, bast shoes)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lapъtь</i>, <i>&#42;lapъtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>ла́па</i> (lápa)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>ла́пка</i> (lápka)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) cutie pie, baby, darling, lassie (endearing word for women or children)</li></ol><p><i>ла́пка</i> (lápka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapserdak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>לאַפּסערדאַק</i> (lapserdak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) lapta (Russian bat and ball game)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) noodle, noodles</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) telephone wire (wires), especially not twisted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лапша</i> (lapša), from Tatar or Uyghur. Compare dialectal <i>локша́</i> (lokšá) or <i>лохша́</i> (loxšá). Cognates with Ukrainian <i>ло́кшина</i> (lókšyna), <i>лапша́</i> (lapšá), Belarusian <i>ло́кшына</i> (lókšyna). Ultimately an Iranian word found in Modern Persian as لخشک (laxšak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instant noodle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) noodle, noodles</li></ol><p><i>лапша́</i> (lapšá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) alternative form of <i>ля́рва</i> (ljárva); a spirit of the underworld, an evil ghost</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) larva (<i>of an insect</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>larva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewelry box</li><li>small chest</li></ol><p><i>ларь</i> (larʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) lari (currency of Georgia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ლარი</i> (lari).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laryngologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) chest, wooden box</li><li>stall, kiosk</li><li>trough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ларь</i> (larĭ), from Old Norse <i>lárr</i> (“chest, drawer”) (compare Swedish <i>lår</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kiosk, stall (a vendor's booth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiosk, stall (a vendor's booth)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) diminutive of <i>ларь</i> (larʹ): a small wooden chest or box</li></ol><p><i>ларь</i> (larʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vendor (in a kiosk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kiosk, stall (a vendor's booth)</li></ol><p><i>ларёк</i> (larjók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caress, endearment</li><li>petting</li><li>weasel (mammal, <i>Mustela nivalis</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làska</i> (“caress”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lasica</i> ~ <i>&#42;laska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocorism (a term of endearment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive, caressing</li></ol><p><i>ласка́ть</i> (laskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress (touch, kiss or stroke lovingly)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làskati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress, to fondle each other</li></ol><p><i>ласка́ть</i> (laskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>affectionately, tenderly</li><li>blandly</li><li>benignly</li><li>grandmotherly, gently</li></ol><p><i>ла́сковый</i> (láskovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affectionate</li><li>tender, gentle</li><li>gentle, soft, caressing</li><li>mild, pleasant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;laskavъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ла́ска</i> (láska) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lasso (a long rope with a sliding loop)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lasso</i>, from Spanish <i>lazo</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>laqueus</i>. Doublet of <i>ла́цкан</i> (láckan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) flipper (wide flat, limb adapted for swimming)</li><li>flipper (wide, flat, paddle-like rubber covering for the foot, used in swimming)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ласт</i> (last, “flipper”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eraser (rubber implement for erasing pencil marks)</li><li>lasting (a type of fabric)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>гуммила́стик</i> (gummilástik), from New Latin <i>gummi elasticum</i>; compare Turkish <i>lastik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gusset <i>(in the underpart of the sleeve)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallow (bird)</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) horizontal stand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lassie, dearie (an endearing term of address to a woman or girl)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lastъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) lat or lats (currency of Latvia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latvian <i>lats</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>лати́нский</i> (latínskij): Lat., Latin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(construction,&#32;regional) lath</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) batten (<i>for a sail</i>)</li><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) patch (<i>of cloth</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) yellow badge (yellow Star of David-shaped patch required to be worn by Jews in Nazi-controlled territories)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>łata</i>, from Middle High German <i>latte</i>; cognate with English <i>lath</i>, German <i>Latte</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lata</i>, <i>&#42;olta</i> (“rag, cloth shred”); cognate with Bulgarian <i>латица</i> (latica), Czech <i>lata</i>, Macedonian <i>алтица</i> (altica), Polish <i>łata</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>ла̏тица</i> (“petal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latakian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to patch, to mend by patching</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latvian (relating to the country Latvia as a state)</li></ol><p><i>Ла́твия</i> (Látvija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>latex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) latex</li></ol><p><i>ла́текс</i> (látɛks) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>latent, hidden</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>latent</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); ultimately from Latin <i>latēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;stilted,&#32;medicine) lateral</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>laterālis</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coelacanth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latinization (act or process of Latinizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Latinism, a Latin loanword</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latinist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Latinist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman alphabet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latin American, Latino</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Latin (<i>of the language</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) Roman Catholic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) synonym of <i>латы́нь</i> (latýnʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) Roman Catholicism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Latin (a person native to the ancient region of Latium)</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) Roman Catholic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) patch</li><li>(Saint Petersburg,&#32;Northern Russia) casserole, braiser, oval-shaped Dutch oven (<i>oval-shaped cookware for baking, braising, roasting, or stewing</i>)</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) casserole (<i>food</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;latъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ла́та</i> (láta, “patch”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;laty</i>; cognate with Bulgarian <i>ла́твица</i> (látvica, “jug”), Slovene <i>látva</i> (“saucer”), Old Polish <i>ɫatka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somebody who is hurrying without need</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>latte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>latte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brass</li><li>brass-colored, brass-yellow</li></ol><p><i>лату́нь</i> (latúnʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brass</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>latten</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) metal cuirass</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) plate armor</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) corset</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>łaty</i> or independently formed as a lemmatized plural of <i>ла́та</i> (láta, “patch”); in either case, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lata</i>, <i>&#42;olta</i> (“rag, cloth shred”). First attested 16th century. Sense development: “leather armor with small metal plates (‘patches’) sown onto it for additional protection” → “cuirass of metal plate”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latin</li></ol><p>Via Ukrainian <i>лати́нь</i> (latýnʹ) from Old East Slavic <i>латинъ</i> (latinŭ), from Latin <i>latinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latvian, Lett</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latvian <i>latvis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latvian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latvian (language)</li></ol><p><i>латы́ш</i> (latýš) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laureate, prizewinner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) benefit; luck; enjoyment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَلَف (ʕalaf, “fodder”), from Old East Slavic <i>алафа</i> (alafa) or <i>олафа</i> (olafa). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>лахва́</i> (laxvá) and Belarusian <i>лахва́</i> (laxvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sort of wine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of long and thin stemware for drinking alcoholic beverages</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lahmacun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkish <i>lahmacun</i>. The forms <i>лахмаджу́</i> (laxmadžú), <i>лахмаджо́</i> (laxmadžó) and <i>ламаджо́</i> (lamadžó) are from Armenian <i>լահմաջու</i> (lahmaǰu) and <i>լահմաջո</i> (lahmaǰo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a seal</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) skin of a marine mammal</li></ol><p>Probably from Finno-Ugric origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lätzchen</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>laqueus</i>. Doublet of <i>лассо́</i> (lassó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lak person (female)</li></ol><p>From <i>лак</i> (lak) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лак</i> (lak)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hovel, shack, hut, shanty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic. Compare Chagatai (alačuɣ) and Tatar <i>алачык</i> (alaçıq), Kyrgyz <i>алачык</i> (alacık), Crimean Tatar <i>alaçıq</i>, Azerbaijani <i>alaçıq</i> (“yurt”), meaning "felt tent", "shelter of branches"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лачу́га</i> (lačúga, “shack”): (small) shack</li></ol><p><i>лачу́га</i> (lačúga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark (like a dog)</li><li>to scold, to rebuke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лаꙗти</i> (lajati, “to bark, to scold”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làjati</i> (“to bark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to argue, to fight</li></ol><p><i>ла́ять</i> (lájatʹ, “to bark”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to tell lies</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъgati</i> (compare Serbo-Croatian <i>ла̀гати</i>/<i>làgati</i>, Bulgarian <i>лъ́жа</i> (lǎ́ža)), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewgʰ-</i>, whence also English <i>lie</i>, German <i>lügen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p>From <i>лгать</i> (lgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лгун</i> (lgun, “liar”): liar</li></ol><p><i>лгун</i> (lgun) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orach, saltbush (<i>Atriplex</i>)</li><li>goosefoot (<i>Chenopodium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lebeda</i> or <i>&#42;elbeda</i>. Doublet of <i>лобода́</i> (lobodá), <i>лабуда́</i> (labudá), <i>бала́нда</i> (balánda), <i>ала́бута</i> (alábuta), and <i>ала́бота</i> (alábota).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swan; swan's</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) snow-white and buxom (<i>of a neck or bosom</i>)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) graceful, majestic</li></ol><p><i>ле́бедь</i> (lébedʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li><li>(figurative,&#32;historical) one-hundred- or two-hundred-rouble note</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;elbedь</i>, <i>&#42;elbędь</i>, <i>&#42;olbǫdь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;albʰós</i> (“white”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лебедь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Lebed</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Cygnus</li></ol><p>From <i>ле́бедь</i> (lébedʹ, “swan (bird)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cygnet (young of a swan)</li></ol><p><i>ле́бедь</i> (lébedʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fawn (upon), to cringe (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) pen (female swan)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) winch</li></ol><p><i>ле́бедь</i> (lébedʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>лебёдкой</i> (lebjódkoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) lev (former currency of Bulgaria)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьvъ</i>, likely a borrowing from a Germanic language, ultimately from Latin <i>leo</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>лев</i> (lev), a variant of <i>лъв</i> (lǎv, “lion”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лев)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lev, of popular usage, equivalent to English Leo</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Leo (constellation of the zodiac containing the bright star Regulus, in the shape of a lion)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Leo (zodiac sign for the lion, covering July 23 – August 22)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) ultraleftist</li><li>illicit dealer, moonshiner</li><li>moonshiner driver, gypsy cab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Levantine</li></ol><p><i>Лева́нт</i> (Levánt) +&lrm; <i>-ийский</i> (-ijskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Levantine person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>леванти́йский</i> (levantíjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>left wing political orientation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become politically (more) leftist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leviathan (mythical creature)</li><li>leviathan (something large)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Λευιάθαν</i> (Leuiáthan). Doublet of <i>Елеафам</i> (Jeleafam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>left wing political orientation</li><li>ostensibly left wing political orientation that is imagined or feigned; showy sociopolitical views that are purported to be leftist; ostentatious, affected left-wing radicalism; pretentious sham pseudo-leftism</li></ol><p><i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj) +&lrm; <i>-изна́</i> (-izná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>levitation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stock-gillyflower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>left (direction): only in <i>сле́ва</i> (sléva), <i>вле́во</i> (vlévo), <i>нале́во</i> (nalévo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) left bank</li></ol><p>From <i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj) +&lrm; <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left-handed</li></ol><p><i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj, “left”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italian greyhound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lefty, left-hander, left-handed person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left, left-hand</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) port</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) left-wing, leftist</li><li>counterfeit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) wrong, strange, fake, impertinent, unrelated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nominalized,&#32;politics) leftist, one who believes in the political left</li></ol><p>From <i>лево́й</i> (levój) (accent alignment – from <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj) – in pairs closely related in meaning) from Old East Slavic <i>лѣвъ</i> (lěvŭ, “left, wrong, evil”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěvъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pointer, setter (breed of dog)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointer, setter (dog)</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) cop, copper (policeman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to legalize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>légaliser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>legally (as permitted by law)</li></ol><p><i>лега́льный</i> (legálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawful, legal (conforming to or recognised by law or rules)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leggings</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>leggings</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legend</li><li>(<i>espionage</i>) cover, cover story</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>legenda</i>, akin to <i>legendus</i>, future passive participle (gerundive) of <i>legō</i> (“pick out, select”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legendary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ле́ггинсы</i> (légginsy, “leggings”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) legionary, legionnaire</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>legionarius</i>, likely through German <i>Legionär</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legitimacy</li></ol><p><i>легити́мный</i> (legitímnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legitimate, legally sound</li></ol><p>Adaptation of French <i>légitime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lightly</li><li>easily, slightly</li><li>luckily</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьgъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(track and field) athlete (a participant in a group of sporting activities which includes track and field, road running, cross country running and racewalking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) track and field</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>credulous, gullible (excessively ready to believe things)</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lightweight</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) passenger (of a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vehicles</i>) passenger car (road vehicle that carries passengers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) passenger car</li></ol><p><i>легков(о́й)</i> (lexkov(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) passenger car (type of automobile)</li></ol><p><i>легково́й</i> (lexkovój) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessly, flippantly</li></ol><p><i>легкомы́сленный</i> (lexkomýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessness</li></ol><p><i>легкомы́сленный</i> (lexkomýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtless, flippant</li><li>frivolous, flighty</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frivolity, light-mindedness, carelessness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;childish) something extremely easy, easy peasy</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily digestible</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>усвоя́емый</i> (usvojájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>легко́</i> (lexkó): very slightly, very lightly</li></ol><p><i>лег(ко́)</i> (leg(kó)) +&lrm; <i>-о́нько</i> (-ónʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to lessen, to abate</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;vernacular)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ле́гче</i> (léxče) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>легко́</i> (lexkó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn to ice, to freeze</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to freeze, to feel very cold</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to grow torpid, to become rigid, to freeze (from a fright, horror, surprise, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-ене́ть</i> (-enétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit-drop, sugar candy, lollypop</li></ol><p><i>ледяно́й</i> (ledjanój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze, to chill</li></ol><p><i>ледяно́й</i> (ledjanój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lollipop</li></ol><p><i>ледене́ц</i> (ledenéc) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady (<i>usually in British context; title of a woman of breeding and authority</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lady</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glacier</li><li>icehouse, icebox</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Possibly a calque of French <i>glacier</i>, similarly composed of <i>glace</i> and <i>-ier</i>.</p><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glacier; glacial</li></ol><p><i>ледни́к</i> (ledník) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice age</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with ice, having plenty of ice</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-ови́тый</i> (-ovítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ice</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod, “ice”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>лёд</i> (ljod, “ice”): thin layer of ice</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icebreaker</li></ol><p>From <i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кол</i> (kol), combining form of <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ, “to split, to break”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice axe, piolet</li></ol><p>From <i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) ledoyom (intermontane depressions which might get completely filled by glaciers from the surrounding mountains at the maxima of glaciation, creating esker-like depressions under the ice)</li></ol><p>From <i>лед-</i> (led-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ём</i> (-jóm), combining form of <i>имать</i> (imatʹ, “to take, originally to have”) (compare 1sg. present <i>е́млю</i> (jémlju)). Formed by analogy with <i>водоём</i> (vodojóm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ice</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) icy (<i>of a glance, a voice, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;leděnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice sheet</li></ol><p><i>ледяно́й</i> (ledjanój) + <i>щит</i> (ščit)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) jackstay (taut wire, both ends of which are fixed to the ship structures)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>leier</i>, from <i>leiden</i> (“to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lounger, sunbed, reclining beach chair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lying down</li></ol><p><i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeping ledge above a Russian chimney stove</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sleeping place or couch</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) niche in the stone wall of a crypt, where the deceased is placed</li></ol><p><i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нка</i> (-ánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie (to be in a horizontal position)</li><li>to be situated</li><li>to rest, to be incumbent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ležati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, recumbent</li><li>sleeping</li></ol><p><i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́чий</i> (-áčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) speed bump</li></ol><p>Literally, “lying [down] policeman”. Compare with <i>sleeping policeman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) ground, rookery, bed, lair (of animals)</li><li>rookery (a place where criminals congregate)</li></ol><p><i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-бище</i> (-bišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lazybones, idler, slacker, loafer (procrastinating or lazy person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blade (sharp-edged part of a cutting tool)</li><li>edge (<i>of a sword, a knife, etc.</i>)</li><li>razor blade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лезъ</i> (lezŭ, “point of a blade”). Further etymology is unclear. Trubachyov proposes that the term came from honey hunting lexicon and is related to <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ, “to climb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lezgi (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lezginka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lezgi (a member of an ethnic group living predominantly in southern Dagestan and northeastern Azerbaijan)</li><li>Lezgi, Lezgian (the Northeast Caucasian language spoken by the Lezgins)</li></ol><p><i>лезги́н</i> (lezgín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb (up, on to), to scramble</li><li>to clamber, to crawl, to scramble (through, into, under)</li><li>to get (into); to thrust the hand (into)</li><li>to thrust oneself (upon); to intrude (upon)</li><li>to meddle (in, with)</li><li>to fit (into)</li><li>to fall out, to come out (of hair or fur)</li><li>to stick out, to be conspicuous</li><li>to slip out of position</li><li>to come on, to do something despite obstacles or objections</li><li>to try to climb, to try to reach a better position in life</li><li>to come to pieces (of cloth or leather)</li><li><i>used in idioms:</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣзти</i> (lězti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lězti</i> (“to crawl, climb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) leu (currency of Moldova and Romania)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romanian <i>leu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>court coachman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Leibkutscher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>label</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>label</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Labourist, a supporter of the Labour Party of the United Kingdom.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Labourist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering can</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) bail</li><li>Leika (brand of camera)</li></ol><p><i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leukemia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Leukämie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leukaemia/leukemia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Leukose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) plaster, band-aid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Leukoplast</i>, the ending influenced by <i>пла́стырь</i> (plástyrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white blood cell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Leipzig</li></ol><p><i>Ле́йпциг</i> (Léjpcig) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) lieutenant (military rank)</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from German <i>Leutenant</i> (archaic form of <i>Leutnant</i>), from French <i>lieutenant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) leitmotif (melodic theme in an opera)</li><li>leitmotif (a recurring theme)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Leitmotiv</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) lek (national unit of currency in Albania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a French curve</li><li>(engineering,&#32;construction) a template, a reference pattern (<i>of a curved shape</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a mold, a former (<i>for bending wood</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a caliper, a gauge</li></ol><p>Attested 1717. Inherited from Old East Slavic, from Old East Slavic <i>лѧкати</i> (lękati, “to bend, to make crooked”), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lękъ</i> (“bent”). Cognate with Belarusian <i>ляка́ла</i> (ljakála). For sense development, compare Polish <i>krzywik</i> (“French curve”) &lt; Polish <i>krzywić</i> (“to bend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medicinal</li></ol><p><i>лека́рство</i> (lekárstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine, drug (substance which promotes healing)</li></ol><p><i>ле́карь</i> (lékarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) doctor, medico, physician (feminine form "ле́карша" is usually considered colloquial)</li><li>a healer, a remedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěkařь</i>.</p><p>Was the official name of the profession in Russian Empire.</p></html>:/lʲɪkˈpom/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) doctor's assistant</li></ol><p>Compound formed from <i>лек-(аря)</i> (lek-(arja), “doctor”) +&lrm; <i>-пом(ощник)</i> (-pom(oščnik), “assistant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) lexeme</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) token</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lexicon, vocabulary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lexicographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lexicographic</li></ol><p><i>лексикогра́фия</i> (leksikográfija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lexicography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lexicologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) lexicology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lexicon, dictionary</li><li>word-stock, vocabulary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lexical</li></ol><p><i>ле́кс(ика)</i> (léks(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lecturer, reader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lēctor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lecture, conference</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>lēctiō, lēctiōnem</i> (“a reading”), from <i>legō</i> (“to read; to gather”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress, to pamper</li><li>to care for, to tend, to look after</li><li>to cherish, to foster</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lelějati</i>. Cognate to Bulgarian <i>лелѐя</i> (lelèja), Ukrainian <i>лелі́яти</i> (lelíjaty) and possibly also Sanskrit <i>ललति</i> (lalati, “to play”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ploughshare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lemexъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) lemma</li></ol><p>From the Ancient Greek <i>λῆμμᾰ</i> (lêmmă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) lemniscate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lemur</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>lemurēs</i> (“spirits of the dead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fief, fee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lehen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) lend-lease</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lend-lease</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lazybones, loafer, idler, slacker, sluggard, <i>female:</i> <i>лени́вица</i> (lenívica)</li><li>sloth (mammal)</li></ol><p><i>лени́вый</i> (lenívyj, “lazy”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lazily (in a lazy manner)</li></ol><p>From <i>ленивый</i> (lenivyj), from Old East Slavic <i>лѣнивꙑи</i> (lěnivyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěnivъ</i> (“lazy, sluggish”), from <i>&#42;lěniti</i> + <i>&#42;ivъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lěniti</i>, from <i>&#42;lěnь</i> / <i>&#42;lěnjь</i> (“laziness”) or <i>&#42;lěnъ</i> (“lazy, slow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lazy (unwilling to work)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣнивꙑи</i> (lěnivyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěnivъ</i> (“lazy, sluggish”), from <i>&#42;lěniti</i> + <i>&#42;-ivъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lěniti</i>, from <i>&#42;lěnь</i> / <i>&#42;lěnjь</i> (“laziness”) or <i>&#42;lěnъ</i> (“lazy, slow”). By surface analysis, <i>лень</i> (lenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resident of Leningrad (now Saint Petersburg)</li></ol><p><i>Ленингра́д</i> (Leningrád) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Leningrad</li><li>Leningradsky (Soviet cake)</li></ol><p><i>Ленингра́д</i> (Leningrád) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leninist</li></ol><p><i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leninism</li></ol><p><i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang) mass demolition of monuments to Vladimir Lenin</li></ol><p><i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin, “Lenin”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́д(ать)</i> (pád(atʹ), “to fall”), modelled on <i>снегопа́д</i> (snegopád, “snowfall”), <i>водопа́д</i> (vodopád, “waterfall”) or <i>листопа́д</i> (listopád, “fall of leaves”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Leninist, Lenin's</li></ol><p>From <i>Ле́нин</i> (Lénin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Ukrainian <i>ле́нінський</i> (léninsʹkyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be lazy</li></ol><p><i>-лени́ть</i> (-lenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laziness, indolence, idleness, sloth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěnostь</i>, based on <i>&#42;lěnъ</i> (“lazy”); compare Old East Slavic <i>лѣнꙑи</i> (lěnyi), Belarusian <i>ляны́</i> (ljaný), Bulgarian <i>лен</i> (len), Serbo-Croatian <i>ли̏jен</i>, Czech <i>líný</i>, etc. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;лено́й</i> (&#42;lenój, “lazy”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), based on a no-longer existing adjectival form of <i>лень</i> (lenʹ, “laziness”), replaced in modern Russian with <i>лени́вый</i> (lenívyj) (but dialectal <i>лено́й</i> (lenój) is still found).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Correlated in meaning with the nouns <i>Ле́на</i> (Léna), <i>Ленск</i> (Lensk).</li></ol><p>From <i>Ле́на</i> (Léna) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ribbon, band</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) tape (adhesive, recording, measuring, etc.)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) belt</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) anything that winds or stretches in a ribbon-like form</li><li>film, movie</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) feed</li></ol><p>From dialectal German <i>Linte</i> (“ribbon”). Compare Dutch <i>lint</i> (“ribbon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Möbius strip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat needlefish (<i>Ablennes hians</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лентови́дный</i> (lentovídnyj, “ribbon-like”) +&lrm; <i>сарга́н</i> (sargán, “garfish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ле́нта</i> (lénta): (small) ribbon; tape</li></ol><p><i>ле́нта</i> (lénta) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band saw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ribbon, band, tape</li></ol><p><i>ле́нта</i> (lénta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lazybones, loafer, idler, slacker, sluggard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лень</i> (lenʹ, “lazyness”)</li></ol><p><i>лень</i> (lenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ца́</i> (-cá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laziness, idleness, indolence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Leontyev (surname of various notable Russians and Soviets)</li></ol><p><i>Лео́нтьев</i> (Leóntʹjev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard (a large wild cat with a spotted coat, <i>Panthera pardus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female leopard, leopardess</li></ol><p>From <i>леопа́рд</i> (leopárd) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leopard (a large wild cat with a spotted coat, <i>Panthera pardus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>леопа́рд</i> (leopárd) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) petal</li></ol><p><i>лепесто́к</i> (lepestók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) petal (one of the component parts of the corolla of a flower)</li><li>lobe (an identifiable segment of an antenna radiation pattern)</li><li>PFM-1 mine (Soviet and Russian scatterable high explosive anti-personnel land mine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babble, prattle (inarticulate or senseless talk)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lepetъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble</li><li>to prattle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) lepidosiren, lungfish</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>lepidosiren</i> or French <i>lépidosirène</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lepiota (<i>Lepiota</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Lepiota</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to model, to sculpt, to fashion, to shape</li><li>to build, to make (cells, a nest, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stick on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slap, to smack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to babble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěpiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cling, to be attached (<i>to something steep or sloping</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modelling (creating a model)</li></ol><p><i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) relief (<i>usually in plaster</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лепно́й</i> (lepnój) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modelled, moulded</li></ol><p><i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) beauty (<i>quality of pleasing appearance</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leprechaun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>leprechaun</i>, from Irish <i>leipreachán</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lepton (coin)</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) mite, two cents, (small) contribution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>λεπτόν</i> (leptón), neuter form of <i>λεπτός</i> (leptós, “small”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) lepton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic,&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;regional): superlative degree of <i>ле́пый</i> (lépyj):</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěpьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;ironic) beautiful</li><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;ironic) good; proper; useful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣпъ</i> (lěpŭ, “beautiful”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěpъ</i> (“beautiful”). Akin to Russian <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ). Compare typologically Latin <i>fōrmōsus</i>, <i>fōrmō</i> (&lt; <i>fōrma</i>). Also compare the second element of Greek <i>όμορφος</i> (ómorfos) (&lt; Ancient Greek <i>εὔμορφος</i> (eúmorphos) &lt; <i>εὖ</i> (eû) + <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ)) (for the first one see <i>хоро́шенький</i> (xoróšenʹkij), Latin <i>bellus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who makes (or sells) flatbreads</li></ol><p><i>лепёшка</i> (lepjóška) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flatbread</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) something thin, flat, and round; a pancake (<i>shape</i>)</li><li>tablet, lozenge</li></ol><p><i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lermontov (anyone with this surname, or the town of this name)</li></ol><p><i>Ле́рмонтов</i> (Lérmontov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest, woods</li><li>(only <i>singular</i>) lumber, timber</li><li>(only <i>plural</i>) леса́ scaffolding, scaffold trestle, falsework</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) much, many, a lot of <i>something</i> (especially high, eminent)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣсъ</i> (lěsŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěsъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing line</li><li>scaffolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lesbian</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>лесбия́нка</i> (lesbijánka). Compare English <i>lesbie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lesbian (between two women&#59; relating to female homosexuality)</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>lesb(isch)</i> (“lesbian”) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesbian</li></ol><p>Adaptation of a Western language lesbian (itself eventually tracing back to Ancient Greek Λέσβος (Lésbos, “Lesbos”), the native island of Sappho, a poetess associated with female homosexuality in the Western literary canon) with the Russian feminizing suffix -ка (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lesbian (between two women&#59; relating to female homosexuality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) lesbian</li></ol><p>From <i>лесбиянка</i> (lesbijanka) +&lrm; <i>-уха</i> (-uxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ле́стница</i> (léstnica): (short) flight of stairs; (short) ladder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodiness</li></ol><p><i>леси́стый</i> (lesístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woody, wooded, timbered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing line</li><li>monofilament line, used in string trimmers</li></ol><p>Diminutive from леса.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ranger, forest ranger, woodsman</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild apple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forestry</li><li>forest district</li></ol><p>From <i>лесни́к</i> (lesník) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodsman, forest ranger, chief forester</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) woodsman's, forest ranger's</li></ol><p><i>лесни́к</i> (lesník) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, adjectival suffix)</p><p><i>лесни́к</i> (lesník) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, possessive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest, wood</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lumber (<i>American</i>), timber (<i>British</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelnut</li><li>hazel (tree &#47; shrub)</li><li>hazel (color)</li><li>nitwit, nit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest fire</li></ol><p><i>лесно́й</i> (lesnój, “forest, woodland”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>пожа́р</i> (požár, “fire”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dendrology, forestry (the study of trees)</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forester, forest cultivator</li><li>silviculturist</li></ol><p>From <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вод</i> (vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forestry; silvicultural</li></ol><p><i>лесово́дство</i> (lesovódstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forestry</li><li>silviculture</li></ol><p><i>лесово́д</i> (lesovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forestry</li></ol><p><i>лесово́д(ство)</i> (lesovód(stvo)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>log transporter, timber carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>воз</i> (voz), combining form of <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ, “to convey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reforestation</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>возвраще́ние</i> (vozvraščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reforestation</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>возобновле́ние</i> (vozobnovlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>лесно́й</i> (lesnój, “wood, forest (<i>relational</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumber mill (<i>American</i>), timber mill (<i>British</i>), sawmill</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заво́д</i> (zavód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lumbering, logging, timber-cutting, timber-felling</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>загото́вить</i> (zagotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumbering, logging, timber cutting, timber felling</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>загото́вка</i> (zagotóvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest protection</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>защи́та</i> (zaščíta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest-protection</li></ol><p><i>лесозащи́та</i> (lesozaščíta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest engineering</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>инжене́рия</i> (inženérija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove, small wood</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>forestry</i>) log-roller</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>through the forest</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumber</li><li>timber <i>(U.S.)</i></li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>материа́л</i> (materiál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest-amelioration</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мелиорати́вный</i> (melioratívnyj, “(<i>relational</i>) amelioration, land reclamation”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest amelioration</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мелиора́ция</i> (meliorácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afforestation, forest planting</li><li>forested tract, wood</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>насажде́ние</i> (nasaždénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timber-working</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>обраба́тывающий</i> (obrabátyvajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest-protection</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>охране́ние</i> (oxranénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest-park</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>парк</i> (park)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest-park</li></ol><p><i>лесопа́рк</i> (lesopárk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawmill</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) saw, sawing</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawmill</li></ol><p>From <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пил</i> (-pil) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), based on a combining form of <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ, “to saw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest-tree nursery</li><li>nursery garden</li><li>arboretum</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пито́мник</i> (pitómnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree felling</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пова́л</i> (povál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windbreak, shelterbelt (a belt of trees or shrubs planted to mitigate wind erosion)</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полоса́</i> (polosá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest management, forest use, forest exploitation</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзование</i> (pólʹzovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest plantations, forest plantings</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поса́дки</i> (posádki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forest-planting</li></ol><p><i>лесопоса́дки</i> (lesoposádki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumberman, lumber dealer (<i>United States</i>), timber merchant (<i>British</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лесопромы́шлен(ный)</i> (lesopromýšlen(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) timber industry</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest exploitation area</li><li>timber exploitation area</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>разрабо́тка</i> (razrabótka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest and vegetation</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>расти́тельный</i> (rastítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumberman, lumberjack, logger, woodcutter</li></ol><p>From <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-руб</i> (-rub), combining form of <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ, “to chop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearing</li><li>wood to be felled</li><li>wood-cutting area, cutting area</li></ol><p>From <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-сек</i> (-sek) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), based on a combining form of <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ, “to chop, to hew”) (compare 1sg. present <i>секу́</i> (sekú)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber-rafting</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сплав</i> (splav)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) timber rafting</li></ol><p><i>лесоспла́в</i> (lesospláv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber slide, timber chute</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спуск</i> (spusk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest-steppe</li></ol><p><i>лесосте́пь</i> (lesostépʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest-steppe</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>степь</i> (stepʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumber kiln</li><li>timber-drying plant, timber dryer</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>суши́лка</i> (sušílka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>log hauler</li><li>log conveyer</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest technology, forestry technology</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij, “technical, engineering”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest tundra</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ту́ндра</i> (túndra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forest tundra</li></ol><p><i>лесоту́ндра</i> (lesotúndra) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forestry</li><li>forest management, forest regulation</li></ol><p>лес + устройство (from строй, строить)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wood-chemical</li></ol><p><i>лесохи́мия</i> (lesoxímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood chemistry; the chemistry of timber.</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les, “forest, timber”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, “chemistry”) +&lrm; <i>хи́мия</i> (xímija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forestry</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́йство</i> (xozjájstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber-export</li></ol><p><i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>э́кспорт</i> (éksport)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) logging enterprise, timber enterprise</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>лесопромы́шленное хозя́йство</i> (lesopromýšlennoje xozjájstvo, “logging industry enterprise”), or of <i>лесно́е промы́шленное хозя́йство</i> (lesnóje promýšlennoje xozjájstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;Christianity) synonym of <i>ле́стница</i> (léstnica)</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>лѣствица</i> (lěstvica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěstva</i>, from <i>&#42;lězti</i> (“to climb”) + <i>&#42;-tv-</i>. Doublet of <i>ле́стница</i> (léstnica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stair, staircase</li><li>ladder, step</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣстница</i> (lěstnica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěstva</i>, from <i>&#42;lězti</i> (“to climb”) + <i>&#42;-tv-</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) staircase, ladder</li></ol><p><i>ле́стница</i> (léstnica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complimentary</li></ol><p><i>лесть</i> (lestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flattery, adulation, cajolery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>льсть</i> (lĭstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьstь</i>. According to Vasmer, a link with German <i>List</i> is possible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forestry</li><li>forestry farm</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>лесно́е хозя́йство</i> (lesnóje xozjájstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lethal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>letal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flying, made to fly</li></ol><p><i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aircraft (machine capable of atmospheric flight)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lětati</i>, frequentative from <i>&#42;letěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flying saucer</li><li>frisbee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly, to be flying</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>летѣти</i> (letěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;letěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) summer</li><li>summery, summerlike</li></ol><p><i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) summer</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) old (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aestivation/estivation (state of inactivity and metabolic depression during summer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summer</li><li>pl: years, age, summers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѣто</i> (lěto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěto</i> (“year”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee entrance <i>(in a hive)</i></li><li>entryway in a nest, hive, nestbox or other structure inhabited by flying creatures</li></ol><p><i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in summer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) chronicler, annalist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) chronicle</li></ol><p>Per Shansky and Zhuravlyov, calque of Ancient Greek <i>χρονογράφος</i> (khronográphos, “chronicler, annalist”, literally “time-writer”), from <i>ле́то</i> (léto, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annals, chronicle, record, fasti (a relation of events)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>летопи́сец</i> (letopísec, “chronicler, annalist”) (per Shansky and Zhuravlev). For the initial stress, compare <i>жи́вопись</i> (žívopisʹ), <i>на́дпись</i> (nádpisʹ), <i>по́дпись</i> (pódpisʹ), <i>за́пись</i> (zápisʹ), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bat</li></ol><p>Literally, “flying mouse”. Compare English <i>flying mouse</i> and English <i>flittermouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flying fish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flying</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) shifting</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) volatile</li></ol><p><i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lechitic (of or pertaining to the language group including Polish, Kashubian and Polabian)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clinic, hospital (medical facility)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medical</li><li>medicinal, healing, therapeutic, curative</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>лече́ние</i> (lečénije) + <i>-е́бный</i> (-ébnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treatment, cure</li></ol><p><i>лечи́ть</i> (lečítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heal, to treat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěčiti</i>. Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēkijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take treatment, to receive treatment undergo a cure (<i>of a patient</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cure (<i>of a disease</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to heal (<i>of wounds, burns</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лечи́ть</i> (lečítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lecsó</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>lecsó</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down</li><li>to fall, to cover (<i>of snow etc. on the ground</i>)</li><li>to flow, to adhere (<i>of paint being applied to a surface</i>)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to heave (to move in a certain direction or into a certain situation)</li><li>(computing,&#32;informal) to fall over, to die</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лечи</i> (leči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;leťi</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;légtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;légʰeti</i>, from <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) leshy (a male woodland spirit in Slavic mythology who protects wild animals and forests)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěšь</i>, from <i>&#42;lěsъ</i> (“forest, woods”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьjь</i> (possessive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bream</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) slap in the face, box on the ear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;leščь</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>leszcz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лещ</i> (lešč): little bream</li></ol><p><i>лещ</i> (lešč) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazel (tree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pseudo-, false</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pseudofolk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscience</li></ol><p><i>лж-</i> (lž-) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>нау́ка</i> (naúka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoprophet, false prophet</li></ol><p>From <i>ложь</i> (ložʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>проро́к</i> (prorók), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ψευδοπροφήτης</i> (pseudoprophḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) perjury</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>лъжесъвѣдѣтельство</i> (lŭžesŭvědětelĭstvo), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ψευδομαρτυρία</i> (pseudomarturía). By surface analysis, <i>лже-</i> (lže-) +&lrm; <i>свиде́тельство</i> (svidételʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscorpion</li></ol><p>From <i>лже-</i> (lže-) +&lrm; <i>скорпио́н</i> (skorpión).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p><i>лгать</i> (lgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceitfulness, mendacity, dishonesty</li></ol><p><i>лжи́вый</i> (lžívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, deceitful, mendacious</li><li>false, feigned</li></ol><p><i>ложь</i> (ložʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(interrogative particle,&#32;placed after the first word in a sentence or phrase) Do/does/did...? Is/are/was/were...?</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>whether, if</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;li</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liana, creeper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>liane</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liana</li></ol><p><i>лиа́на</i> (liána) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) liberal</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>līberālis</i> (“befitting a freeman”), from <i>līber</i> (“free”). Doublet of <i>ливре́я</i> (livréja). Akin to Russian <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;philosophy,&#32;politics) liberalism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>libéralisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) acting in a too liberal or too tolerant manner</li></ol><p>From <i>либера́льничать</i> (liberálʹničatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>либера́льничание</i> (liberálʹničanije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play or to act the liberal, to show (excessive) tolerance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liberal</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>līberālis</i> (“befitting a freeman”), from <i>līber</i> (“free”). Doublet of <i>ливре́я</i> (livréja). Akin to Russian <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) libtard, liberal</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>либера́л</i> (liberál, “liberal”) +&lrm; <i>педера́ст</i> (pederást, pedɛrást, “pederast”). Used when expressing aversion to neoliberalism and/or homosexuality.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) liberal</li></ol><p>From <i>либера́л</i> (liberál), likely modeled after пидора́ха (pidoráxa), a derogatory term for a Russian patriot who is perceived as chauvinistic and hateful.</p><p>The suffix -а́ха (-áxa) was an old-fashioned derogatory suffix, perhaps derived from -шка (-ška); see also дурёха (durjóxa, “a foolish woman”), Илю́ха (Iljúxa, “little Elijah”) (from Илья), and Танюха (Tanjuxa, “little Tatiana”) (from Татьяна) as examples of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Liberian</li></ol><p><i>Либе́рия</i> (Libérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;politics,&#32;economics) libertarianism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>libertarianism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libido (sexual urges or drives)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>libīdō</i>. Via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i> cognate with <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ), <i>любо́вь</i> (ljubóvʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>either... or</li><li>or, either that or</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>либо</i> (libo), from <i>ли</i> (li) + <i>бо</i> (bo). Compare Ukrainian <i>либо́нь</i> (lybónʹ) from Old East Slavic <i>либо нѣ</i> (libo ně).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) libration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lībrātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) librettist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>librettiste</i>. By surface analysis, <i>либре́тто</i> (librétto, “libretto”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libretto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>libretto</i>, diminutive of <i>libro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebanese</li></ol><p><i>Лива́н</i> (Liván) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lebanese</li></ol><p><i>Лива́н</i> (Liván) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour heavily and unexpectedly (of rain)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour</li></ol><p><i>лива́ть</i> (livátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to leave</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) iterative of <i>лить</i> (litʹ); to pour many times</li></ol><p>Derived no later than 2010 from English <i>leave</i> + <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). Doublet of <i>лепить</i> (lepitʹ).</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shower, downpour, heavy rain, cloud-burst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>livermorium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libyan (a person from Libya or of Libyan descent)</li></ol><p><i>Ли́вия</i> (Lívija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lybian</li></ol><p><i>Ли́вия</i> (Lívija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>livistona (<i>Livistona</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Livistona</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to leave</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour heavily and unexpectedly (of rain)</li></ol><p><i>ли́вать</i> (lívatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p><p><i>лива́ть</i> (livátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) storm sewer, surface water drain, storm drain</li></ol><p><i>ли́вень</i> (lívenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liveried</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>livery (in bourgeois houses and courts, the uniforms of a special cut and a certain color for waiters, porters, coachmen and other servants)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) livery (ceremonial dress of knights and courtiers indicating allegiance to a particular overlord)</li><li>livery (proprietary coloring of a vehicle or airplane indicating its service and ownership by a particular company)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>livrée</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>līber</i>. Doublet of <i>либера́льный</i> (liberálʹnyj). Akin to Russian <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(union)</i> league</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) league</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tie, slur, bind</li><li><i>(unit of distance)</i> league</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>ligō</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>лье</i> (lʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ligament</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(typography,&#32;medicine,&#32;music) ligature</li><li>alloy, base metal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lignite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>leader</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leadership</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) lead (precedence; advance position)</li></ol><p><i>ли́дер</i> (líder) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in the lead, to be a leader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) A "leaderman", which is a type of navigator who helps guide an airplane or helicopter during its descent and whose presence on board may sometimes be legally required</li></ol><p><i>лидер</i> (lider) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лизати</i> (lizati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;līzàti</i> (“to lick”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyǵʰ-</i> (“to lick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick oneself</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lick (together with someone, licking each other)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to kiss (together with someone, kissing each other)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to curry favour/curry favor with, to suck up to [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lease, leasing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>leasing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lease</li></ol><p><i>ли́зинг</i> (lízing) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulator, lickspittle (sycophant)</li></ol><p>From <i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ, “to lick”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-блюд</i> (-bljud), combining form of <i>блю́до</i> (bljúdo, “dish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toadyism, sycophancy</li></ol><p><i>лизоблю́д</i> (lizobljúd) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) face</li><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) image or representation of a face on an icon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;likъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) Likbez (campaign of eradication of illiteracy in Soviet Russia and to Soviet Union in the 1920s and 1930s)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grounding (act of giving a basic education in a particular subject)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ликвида́ция безгра́мотности</i> (likvidácija bezgrámotnosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquidator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eliminating</li><li>deletion</li><li>dissolution</li><li>liquidation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eliminate, to abolish</li><li>to dissolve</li><li>to stamp out, to do away with, to destroy, to kill (off)</li><li>to liquidate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquidity</li></ol><p><i>ликви́дный</i> (likvídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) liquid</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>liquidus</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exultation, jubilation; triumph</li></ol><p><i>ликова́ть</i> (likovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice <i>(at, over)</i>, to triumph <i>(over)</i>, to exult <i>(at, in)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ликовати</i> (likovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;likovati</i>, from <i>&#42;likъ</i> (“round dance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) lycopodium (<i>Lycopodium</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Lycopodium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exultant, jubilant, triumphant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liqueur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>liqueur</i>, from Latin <i>liquor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liqueur</li></ol><p><i>ликёр</i> (likjór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liquor, alcohol</li></ol><p><i>ликёр</i> (likjór) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́дочный</i> (vódočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) resembling a lily, white</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lilliputian</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dwarf</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) someone insignificant</li></ol><p>From English <i>lilliputian</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lily</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) fleur-de-lis</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лилия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Liliya, equivalent to English Lily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lilac, violet</li><li>violet, purple</li></ol><p>Shaped after German <i>lila</i> (“lilac-coloured/colored”) or French <i>lilas</i> (“lilac plant and colour/color”) + <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), from Arabic لِيلَك (līlak, “lilac”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj), <i>фиоле́товый</i> (fiolétovyj). Also note <i>цвет</i> (cvet), <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wide estuary</li><li>brackish lagoon</li></ol><p>Via Turkic (compare Turkish <i>liman</i>, Crimean Tatar <i>liman</i>) from Byzantine Greek <i>λιμένιον</i> (liménion), from Ancient Greek <i>λιμήν</i> (limḗn).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лиман)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyman (a lake in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li><li>Lyman (a city in Donetsk Oblast, in eastern Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) estuary, firth, coastal salt lake</li></ol><p><i>лима́н</i> (limán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lyman (<i>city in Ukraine</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Limansky, Limanskaya (referring to anyone with this name, e.g. Maria Limanskaya, famous Russian soldier during World War II)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Limansky, Limanskaya, Limanskoye, Lymanske (referring to any of several settlements with this name in Russia and Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Лима́н</i> (Limán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Limburgish</li><li>Limburg, Limburger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limit, quota</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>limite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limnobiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lemon (citrus fruit)</li><li>lemon tree, wood</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) million</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lemonade</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) soda pop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>limonade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lemonade</li></ol><p><i>лимона́д</i> (limonád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pineapple, hand grenade</li></ol><p><i>лимо́н</i> (limón, “lemon”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;familiar,&#32;ironic) to be or act gentle, tender, nice or cutesy</li></ol><p><i>лимо́н</i> (limón, “lemon”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ). Coined by Russian novelist Fyodor Dostoyevsky in the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lemon</li><li>lemon, lemon-yellow</li></ol><p><i>лимо́н</i> (limón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limousine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>limousine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lymph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) lymphatic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>lymphat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>therapeutic</i>) lymphatic massage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;therapeutic) lymphatic massage</li></ol><p><i>лимфодрена́ж</i> (limfodrenáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(oncology,&#32;pathology) lymphoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lymphocyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lingala (Bantu language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguistics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>linguistic (relating to human language)</li></ol><p><i>лингви́ст(ика)</i> (lingvíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geography,&#32;linguistics) language geography (branch of human geography that studies the geographic distribution of language(s) or its constituent elements)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>лингвисти́ческая геогра́фия</i> (lingvistíčeskaja geográfija). By surface analysis, <i>лингво-</i> (lingvo-) +&lrm; <i>геогра́фия</i> (geográfija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguonym (the name of a language; a glossonym)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler (for measuring or drawing straight lines)</li><li>line <i>(for writing)</i></li><li><i>(polygraphy)</i> rule</li><li>line <i>(formation)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) linear algebra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>linearly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) line; linear</li><li>(<i>military</i>) of the line</li></ol><p><i>ли́ния</i> (línija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) battleship (20th century, dreadnoughts and later designs)</li><li>(historical,&#32;nautical) ship of the line, battleship (Age of Sail)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) battlecruiser</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lens (object focusing or defocusing the light passing through it)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Linse</i> in 1870s (earlier attested once in 1847 with the sense 'lentiform shape'), from Middle High German <i>linse</i> (“lentil”), from Old High German <i>linsa</i>, likely a borrowing from an unidentified source.</p><p>Compare Latin <i>lēns</i>, Lithuanian <i>lęšis</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>лѧща</i> (lęšta) sounding too similar for a coincidence, however different enough to prohibit reconstruction of a common PIE protoform.</p><p>If ultimately a non-IE loanword, locating the source is virtually impossible because cultivation of lentil was widespread in the region since the Neolithic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fresnel lens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line (path through two or more points, or figuratively)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) line (old Russian unit of length equal to 1&#47;10 of an inch, or 2.54 mm)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a building housing a row of adjacent trading stalls; a cloth hall</li><li>type of street or one side of a street</li><li>(<i>video games</i>) lane (in MOBA games, a particular path on the map that may be traversed by enemy characters)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>linia</i> or German <i>Linie</i>, from Latin <i>līnea</i> (“line, thread”), from <i>līnum</i> (“flax”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;līno-</i>. From the same Indo-European root derives <i>лён</i> (ljon, “flax”) (&lt; <i>&#42;lьnъ</i> (“flax”)).</p><p>The MOBA sense is a mistaken semantic loan from English <i>line</i>, caused by Russian-speaking gamers confusing English <i>lane</i> with the more familiar English term <i>line</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly line (system of workers and machinery in a series)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;Internet) link, hyperlink</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>link</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) battleship (20th century, dreadnought and later designs)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>лине́йный кора́бль</i> (linéjnyj koráblʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lined (having lines)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linoleum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linotype machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linotypist</li></ol><p><i>линотип</i> (linotip) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lynch (to subject to lynching, to physically deal with someone objectionable)</li></ol><p>From English <i>lynch</i> +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tench</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) line</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;linь</i>, further etymology uncertain. Possibly a suffixal derivation from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;linjati</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>lijn</i> (literally “thin rope”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moult (the process of shedding or losing a covering of fur, feathers or skin, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded (that has lost some of its former color)</li></ol><p><i>линя́(ть)</i> (linjá(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade, to lose color, to run</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shed, to cast (skin, fur, feathers), to moult</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to bolt, to run away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;liňati</i>. Cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>лињати</i>, Czech <i>línat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Ecdysozoa.</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of <i>линя́ющий</i> (linjájuščij, “molting”), from <i>линя́ть</i> (linjátʹ, “to molt”). Calque of translingual <i>Ecdysozoa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime tree, linden (<i>tree, wood, or flower</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) forgery, fake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>липа</i> (lipa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lipa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;léiˀpāˀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) July</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lipa</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ли́па</i> (lípa) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), referring to linden trees which bloom at this time of year. Cognates include Polish <i>lipiec</i> (“July”), Ukrainian <i>липень</i> (lypenʹ), Belarusian <i>ліпень</i> (lipjenʹ), and Serbo-Croatian <i>lipanj</i> (“June”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) July</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lipa</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ли́па</i> (lípa) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), referring to linden trees which bloom at this time of year. Cognates include Polish <i>lipiec</i> (“July”), Ukrainian <i>липень</i> (lypenʹ), Belarusian <i>ліпень</i> (lipjenʹ), and Serbo-Croatian <i>lipanj</i> (“June”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lipetsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Ли́пецк</i> (Lípeck) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) lipid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ли́па</i> (lípa): young linden tree</li></ol><p><i>ли́па</i> (lípa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adhesive, sticky</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lipъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) linden, lime</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) limewood</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sham, faked, forged</li></ol><p><i>ли́па</i> (lípa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flowers of the linden and a medicinal drink of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something adhesive, sticky</li><li>hook-and-loop fastener</li><li>(automotive,&#32;colloquial) a kind of winter tire</li><li>flypaper</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Lappula</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bore, obsessive person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyre (a stringed musical instrument)</li><li>hurdy-gurdy</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) lira</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>λύρα</i> (lúra).</p><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>lira</i>, from Latin <i>libra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyricist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyric poetry</li><li>lyricism</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sentimental talk, gas, hot air</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lyrical</li></ol><p><i>ли́р(ика)</i> (lír(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male fox (<i>Vulpes vulpes</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лисъ</i> (lisŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lisъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) fox (<i>Vulpes vulpes</i>)</li><li>vixen, female fox</li><li>fox fur</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a foxy fellow/girl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lisa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) studding sail, studsail, stunsail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>lijzeil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fox</li><li>vulpine, foxlike</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) clever, cunning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lisьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лиса́</i> (lisá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox (<i>Vulpes vulpes</i>)</li><li>vixen (female fox)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lisica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>лиса́</i> (lisá, “fox”): little fox, foxie; female fox cub, fox kit, fox pup</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) chanterelle (a mushroom of the <i>Cantharellus</i> genus)</li></ol><p><i>лиса́</i> (lisá) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka). Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lisičьka</i> (whence also Slovene <i>lisička</i>). Compare Czech <i>liška</i>, Macedonian <i>лисичарка</i> (lisičarka) (&lt; <i>&#42;lisa</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically (another animal diminutives) Polish <i>kurka</i>, Slovak <i>kuriatko</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;kura</i>), Latvian <i>gailene</i> (feminine diminutive of <i>gailis</i>). Also compare Dutch <i>hanenkam</i> (&lt; <b><i>haan</i></b> + <i>-en-</i> + <i>kam</i>). Also compare Lithuanian <i>voveraitė</i> (diminutive of <i>voverė</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf (flat green organ of a plant)</li><li>sheet (thin, flat, inexpansible solid object)</li><li>sheet, leaf, page (of a printed material, usually large and unbound)</li><li>certificate, deed, list</li><li>baking tray, sheet pan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;listъ</i> (“leaf”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Möbius strip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leaf through, to flip through (a book, manuscript, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skim, to browse (a book, manuscript, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foliage</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cherry, maidenhead</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ва́</i> (-vá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larch forest</li><li>alternative form of <i>ли́ственница</i> (lístvennica, “larch”)</li><li>a wisp of sessile leaves</li><li>(<i>imageboard slang</i>) synonym of <i>де́вственник</i> (dévstvennik, “male virgin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larch</li></ol><p><i>листва́</i> (listvá) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deciduous</li><li>leafy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лист</i> (list): leaf, leaflet</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaflet</li><li>flyer, flysheet</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka) or <i>листово́й</i> (listovój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Ukrainian <i>листі́вка</i> (lystívka), Belarusian <i>лісто́ўка</i> (listówka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leaf</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) sheet</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) leaf roller</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Tortricidae</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ, “to turn, to twist”, verb) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) leaf eater; something that eats leaves</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) leaf beetle (Chrysomelidae)</li></ol><p><i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “-eater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лист</i> (list): small leaf, small sheet (of paper)</li><li>slip, sheet, paper</li><li>wall newspaper, document, poster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn fall of the leaves</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) October</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;listopadъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пад</i> (-pad), combining form of <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ, “to fall”) (compare 1sg. present <i>паду́</i> (padú)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>листо́к</i> (listók), diminutive of <i>лист</i> (list): (small) leaf, leaflet</li></ol><p><i>листо́к</i> (listók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>лист</i> (list).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>лис</i> (lis).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox cub</li></ol><p><i>лис</i> (lis) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) litas (currency of Lithuania)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Lithuanian <i>litas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) kettledrum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) timpani, kettledrums</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Baltic tribes around the Neman and Daugava river basins, predecessors of modern Lithuanians</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) downpour (heavy rain)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;collective) foundry workers at a factory</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>литъва</i> (litŭva, “Aukštaitians, Lithuanians”), from Old Lithuanian <i>Lietuvà</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;litva</i> (“downpour”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Литва)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lithuania (a country in northeastern Europe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Литъва</i> (Litŭva, “area around Kaunas, modern South Eastern Lithuania; <i>(by extension)</i> Great Principality of Lithuania”), from earlier Old East Slavic <i>литъва</i> (litŭva, “Aukštaitians; <i>(by extension)</i> Lithuanians”), akin to <i>Lietuvà</i> (“Lithuania”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Litvin, a native of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania (or its former territory), but not necessarily an ethnic Lithuanian; especially a Belorussian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) casting, founding (of metal)</li></ol><p><i>литьё</i> (litʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type (block with a raised letter or character on its surface)</li><li>(dated,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;Ukraine) letter (of an alphabet)</li><li>A prepaid mark on a postal envelope</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>littera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literary man, man of letters, writer, litterateur</li></ol><p>Cognates include Ukrainian <i>літера́тор</i> (literátor), Belarusian <i>літара́тар</i> (litarátar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literature</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>litteratura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literary</li></ol><p><i>литерату́ра</i> (literatúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghostwriter</li></ol><p>From <i>литерату́рный</i> (literatúrnyj, “literary”) +&lrm; <i>негр</i> (negr, “black person, (<i>figuratively</i>) slave, wage slave”, see usage notes).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociolinguistics</i>) standard language (standardized idiom)</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) literary language (language of literature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literary critic, specialist in study of literature</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>литературове́дение</i> (literaturovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literary science</li></ol><p><i>литерату́ра</i> (literatúra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lithium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>lithium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) + <i>-ium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literary institute, specifically the Maxim Gorky Literature Institute in Moscow</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>литерату́рный институ́т</i> (literatúrnyj institút, “literary institute”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) lity, name for several prayers in Eastern Orthodoxy:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a special prayer for the dead, performed during a funeral or during the commemoration of the deceased</li><li>part of the nightly vigil, following the pleading of the litany</li><li>a public prayer, performed at the time of a disaster or in memory of a disaster</li></ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic) a solemn prayer or song</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lithuanian (person from Lithuania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lithuanian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lithuanian language</li></ol><p><i>Литва́</i> (Litvá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cast</li><li>full-muscled, muscular, strapping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>herpetology</i>) Australasian treefrog (<i>Litoria</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Litoria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) lithostrotian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Lithostrotia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) lithosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) litotes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>litote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>school&#32;slang</i>) literature</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>литерату́ра</i> (literatúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litreage, capacity, volume in litres/liters</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) engine displacement (<i>in litres/liters</i>)</li></ol><p>From French <i>litrage</i>. By surface analysis, <i>литр</i> (litr) +&lrm; <i>-аж</i> (-až).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liter</li></ol><p><i>литр</i> (litr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liturgical</li></ol><p>From <i>литурги́я</i> (liturgíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liturgy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>lītūrgia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>λειτουργῐ́ᾱ</i> (leitourgĭ́ā) (<&lt; <i>λαός</i> (laós) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon)). Compare Ukrainian <i>літургі́я</i> (liturhíja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour, to spill</li><li>to shed</li><li>to cast, to mould, to found</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;liti</i> (“to pour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ramble or pad out speech without saying much useful; to waffle</li></ol><p>Literally, “to pour water.”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow, to pour</li><li>to pour on oneself</li><li>to spread</li><li>to sound</li></ol><p><i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) founding, casting, moulding</li><li>pouring</li></ol><p><i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>lijf</i> (“corsage”). Doublet of <i>лейб-</i> (lejb-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a person from Livonia (a historical region in the Baltic)/Governorate of Livonia of the Russian Empire, Livonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Лифля́нд(ия)</i> (Lifljánd(ija), “Livonia”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elevator, lift</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lift</i>, specifically British English. Doublet of <i>люфт</i> (ljuft).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lift, elevator</li></ol><p><i>лифт</i> (lift) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brassiere, bra</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) diminutive of <i>лиф</i> (lif); a (small) bodice</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) load bearing vest</li></ol><p><i>лиф</i> (lif, “bodice”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik, “diminutive suffix”). Doublet of <i>лейб-</i> (lejb-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) daredevil, brave man</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) roadhog; a reckless driver</li></ol><p><i>лихо́й</i> (lixój) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) interest, profit (<i>still used in expressions</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lixva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) evil, ill, misfortune, trouble</li><li>An odd number, see the Chetno i licho game.</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dashingly, jauntily</li></ol><p><i>лихо́й</i> (lixój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) evildoer, villain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>лиходѣй</i> (lixoděj), from <i>лихо</i> (lixo, “bad”) + <i>дѣꙗти</i> (dějati, “to do”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lixodějь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ли́хо</i> (líxo, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-дей</i> (-dej, “doer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) corruption, bribery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, hard, sinister</li><li>valiant, intrepid, bold, daring</li><li>dashing, gallant</li><li>nimble</li><li>smart</li><li>criminal, delinquent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лихъ</i> (lixŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lixъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fever, to burn with fever</li><li>to cause fever</li><li>to ague (to strike with an ague, or with a cold fit)</li></ol><p><i>ли́хо</i> (líxo, “evil, misfortune”) +&lrm; <i>ра́дить</i> (ráditʹ, “to wish”) (related to <i>рад</i> (rad))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) fever (higher than normal body temperature)</li><li>rush, mania</li></ol><p><i>лихора́дить</i> (lixoráditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>feverishly</li></ol><p><i>лихора́дочный</i> (lixorádočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feverish, febrile</li></ol><p><i>лихора́дка</i> (lixorádka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravery, daring</li></ol><p><i>лихо́й</i> (lixój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Liechtenstein</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) lighter, stevedore barge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>lichter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) face</li></ol><p><i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) actor</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) pretender, faker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лицедѣи</i> (liceděi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;licedějь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-дей</i> (-dej), combining form of <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ, “to do”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) acting, performance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hypocrisy</li></ol><p>From <i>лицеде́й</i> (licedéj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;ironic) to behold (to see in close proximity)</li></ol><p><i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyceum, lycée</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lyzeum</i>, from Latin <i>lyceum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;licemĕrъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лицо</i> (lico, “face”) +&lrm; <i>мерить</i> (meritʹ, “to measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy, dissimulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be hypocritical, to masquerade [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘before someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hypocritically</li></ol><p><i>лицеме́рный</i> (licemérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypocritical</li></ol><p><i>лицеме́р</i> (licemér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licensor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licensee</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>licentiatus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) licensing</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) licence, licensed</li></ol><p><i>лице́нзия</i> (licénzija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licensing/licencing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licence/license</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) partial, biased, prejudiced</li></ol><p><i>лицеприя́тие</i> (liceprijátije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) face</li><li>countenance</li><li>front</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) person, individual, individuality</li><li>(grammar,&#32;animate) person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asylum seeker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>face to face [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) leecher (a user who only downloads files but never uploads for sharing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>leecher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lychee (Fruits)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>荔枝</i> (lìzhī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лицо́</i> (licó): face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) mask</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) pretense, disguise</li><li>scutcheon, keyplate</li></ol><p><i>лик</i> (lik) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larva, grub, maggot</li></ol><p>Calque of New Latin <i>lārva</i> (“larva”), from Latin <i>lārva</i> (“mask”). From <i>личи́на</i> (ličína, “mask”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) cuckooshrike</li></ol><p><i>личи́нка</i> (ličínka, “larva”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “-eater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>larval</li></ol><p><i>личи́нка</i> (ličínka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;neologism) private messaging, personal messaging, PMs</li><li>(<i>business</i>) personal protection</li><li>(sports,&#32;slang) personal standings, personal rankings</li></ol><p><i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj, “personal”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, feminine noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personal property</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>personally, in person</li></ol><p><i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj, “personal”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personal pronoun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) face; facial</li></ol><p><i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) personality</li><li>personal</li></ol><p><i>ли́чность</i> (líčnostʹ, “person, personality”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personality</li><li>person, individual</li><li>identity</li></ol><p>From <i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ad hominem (personal attack)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>personal (relating to a particular person)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) personal</li></ol><p>From <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) personnel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lichenology</i>) lichen</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) herpes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lišajь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lichen (symbiotic organism)</li><li>consortium</li></ol><p><i>лиша́й</i> (lišáj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive, to rob, to bereave</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to devest, to forjudge, to dispossess</li></ol><p><i>лиши́ть</i> (lišítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be deprived of, to lose</li></ol><p><i>лиша́ть</i> (lišátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) excess, surplus</li></ol><p><i>ли́ш(ний)</i> (líš(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) lishenets</li></ol><p>From <i>лиша́ть</i> (lišátʹ, “to deprive, to disenfranchise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deprivation</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) privations, hardship</li></ol><p><i>лиши́ть</i> (lišítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive, to rob, to bereave</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to devest, to forjudge, to dispossess</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lišiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leykʷ-</i>, whence English <i>lend</i> and <i>loan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be deprived of, to lose</li></ol><p><i>лиши́ть</i> (lišítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surplus; something superfluous</li><li>something spare, reserve, vacant</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something unwanted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extra, spare (reserve, backup, free, unused)</li><li>excess, unnecessary, superfluous, redundant, too much, surplus (more than what is necessary and&#47;or desirable)</li><li>yet another, yet (an) additional (in addition to what was thought to be all)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>лихо́й</i> (lixój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) surplus, excess</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ли́ш(ний)</i> (líš(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók); compare also Old East Slavic <i>лише</i> (liše, “more, except, only”) (original meaning "remaining").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only, merely, just</li><li>as soon as</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>лише</i> (liše), originally comparative form of <i>лихъ</i> (lixŭ, “bad”). Compare with Ukrainian <i>лише́</i> (lyšé), <i>лишень</i> (lyšenʹ) and <i>лиш</i> (lyš), Carpathian Rusyn <i>лиш</i> (lyš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>if only, provided that, so long as</li></ol><p>From <i>лишь</i> (lišʹ, “only, just”) + <i>бы</i> (by, “so that, if”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as soon as, just as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devoid, lacking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumen</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>лю́мен</i> (ljúmen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forehead</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) an adult person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъbъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lubʰo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flathead grey mullet (<i>Mugil cephalus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) lobby (group of people who try to lobby)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lobby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobbying</li></ol><p><i>лобби́ровать</i> (lobbírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lobby <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p>From English <i>lobby</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ) or from <i>ло́бби</i> (lóbbi) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobbyist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobelia (<i>Lobelia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Lobelia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) kissing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to kiss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lobъzati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fretsaw, jigsaw (a saw with fine teeth and a narrow blade)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Laubsäge</i> (whence also Polish <i>laubzega</i>), from German <i>Laub</i> + German <i>Säge</i> (whence Bulgarian <i>джа́га</i> (džága), cognate with English <i>saw</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowpea (<i>Vigna unguiculata</i>)</li><li>hyacinth bean (<i>Lablab purpureus</i>)</li></ol><p>1920s, from Kazakh <i>лобия</i> (lobiä), from Persian لوبیا (lôbiyâ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pubic louse (<i>Pthirus pubis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лобко́вая</i> (lobkóvaja) +&lrm; <i>вошь</i> (vošʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pubis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pubic hair (collectively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pubic</li></ol><p><i>лобо́к</i> (lobók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) frontal lobe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy) forehead; frontal</li></ol><p><i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frontal, head-on</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) front</li></ol><p><i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) mons pubis</li></ol><p><i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobotomy (surgical operation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idler, lazybones</li></ol><p>From <i>лоб</i> (lob, “forehead”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-тряс</i> (-trjas), combining form of <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí, “to shake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobster</li><li>the meat of lobsters or spiny lobsters</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lobster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to kiss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catching, hunting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lovъ</i>, cognates include Czech <i>lov</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womanizer, lady-killer, skirt-chaser</li></ol><p>From a character of Samuel Richardson's novel Clarissa, or the History of a Young Lady sir Robert Lovelace.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li><li>hunter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lovьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to try to catch, to trap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lovìti</i> (“to (try to) catch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fish in troubled waters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get caught (<i>in a net or snare</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dodger, trickster, tricky dick, wheeler-dealer</li></ol><p>From <i>ло́вкий</i> (lóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adroit, dexterous, deft</li><li>cunning, smart, crafty, never at a loss</li><li>comfortable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lovъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>skillfully, artfully, knowingly</li><li>neatly</li><li>cunningly, shrewdly, slick</li><li>trippingly</li><li>nattily</li></ol><p><i>ло́вкий</i> (lóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agility, adroitness, dexterity, deftness</li></ol><p><i>ло́вкий</i> (lóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catching, hunting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hunting</li><li>serving as a snare/trap</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) huntsman, master of hounds</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чий</i> (-čij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dodger, trickster, tricky dick</li></ol><p><i>ловчи́ть</i> (lovčítʹ, “to dodge”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show resourcefulness or cunning, to act deftly, to manage one's affairs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;logъ</i>. Related to English <i>lochia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logarithm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>λογική</i> (logikḗ, “logic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) login (user's identification)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>login</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logistician</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) logicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logistician, logistics specialist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) logistics (civil planning)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>logistic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) logistics</li></ol><p><i>логи́ст(ика)</i> (logíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>logically</li></ol><p><i>логи́ческий</i> (logíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>logical</li></ol><p><i>ло́г(ика)</i> (lóg(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>logically</li></ol><p><i>логи́чный</i> (logíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>logical</li></ol><p><i>ло́гика</i> (lógika) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logo, logotype (symbol or emblem that acts as a trademark or a means of identification of an entity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den, lair (home of certain animals, also figurative)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech therapist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;religion) logos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>λόγος</i> (lógos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logo, logotype (symbol or emblem that acts as a trademark or a means of identification of an entity)</li><li>logotype (a single type combining two or more letters&#59; a ligature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recessed balcony</li><li>loggia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>loggia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lodz</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Лодзь</i> (Lodzʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat, dinghy, gig, yawl (a small vessel, often propelled by oars or paddles)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лодъка</i> (lodŭka), diminutive of <i>лодья</i> (lodĭja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oldī</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ло́дка</i> (lódka): small boat</li></ol><p><i>ло́дка</i> (lódka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boater, boatman</li></ol><p><i>ло́дка</i> (lódka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankle (joint between foot and leg)</li></ol><p>From <i>лодыга</i> (lodyga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oldyga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, slacker, loafer (procrastinating or lazy person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Low German <i>lodder</i> (“lack-all, wretch, someone who does not tend to himself”), cognate to Old English <i>loddere</i> (“poorling”). Ukrainian <i>ле́дар</i> (lédar), Czech <i>lotr</i>, Polish <i>łotr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) box (<i>compartment to sit in</i>)</li><li>(<i>Freemasonry</i>) lodge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>loge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lojban.</li></ol><p>From Lojban <i>lojban.</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shallow ravine, depression</li></ol><p><i>лож(и́ться)</i> (lož(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-би́на</i> (-bína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small hollow, small groove</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ложби́на</i> (ložbína, “shallow ravine, depression”)</li></ol><p><i>ложби́на</i> (ložbína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) bed, couch</li><li>riverbed; channel</li><li>gunstock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ложе</i> (lože), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lože</i>. Cognate to <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ло́жка</i> (lóžka): little spoon; teaspoon</li></ol><p><i>ло́жка</i> (lóžka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) spoonwort (<i>Cochlearia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ло́жка</i> (lóžka, “spoon”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spoon</li></ol><p><i>ло́жка</i> (lóžka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(nonstandard,&#32;proscribed) to put, to lay (down)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ложити</i> (ložiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ložiti</i> (“to lay”). Cognate with English <i>lay</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down</li><li>to fall, to cover (<i>of snow, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoon</li><li>spoonful</li><li>shoehorn</li><li>spoon (musical instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false alarm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>falsely (in a false manner)</li></ol><p><i>ло́жный</i> (lóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pseudo-, false</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>falsely negative</li></ol><p><i>ло́жный</i> (lóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>отрица́тельный</i> (otricátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>falsely positive</li></ol><p><i>ло́жный</i> (lóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>положи́тельный</i> (položítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscorpion</li></ol><p>From <i>ложно-</i> (ložno-) +&lrm; <i>скорпио́н</i> (skorpión).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false (untrue, not factual, wrong)</li><li>fallacious (deceptive or misleading, mistaken)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъžьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ложь</i> (ložʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false friend (a word in a foreign language that bears a deceptive resemblance to a word in one's own language, but has a different meaning)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>faux ami</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive flounder (<i>Paralichthys olivaceus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лог</i> (log): small ravine</li></ol><p><i>лог</i> (log) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie, falsehood</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) false</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лъжь</i> (lŭžĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъžь</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>lyġe</i>, English <i>lie</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lugiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine</li><li>rod, switch (thin, flexible stick)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow bush</li></ol><p>From <i>лоза</i> (loza) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slogan, watchword</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Losung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>localization (act of localizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to locate, to allocate, to position</li><li>to localize, to contain</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to localize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>localiser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>local</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locator, radar set</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lockout (opposite of a strike)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lockout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biwa, loquat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) lockdown (confinement of people as a security measure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lockdown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locomobile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) locomobile</li></ol><p><i>локомоби́ль</i> (lokomobílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locomotive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>locomotif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) locomotive</li></ol><p><i>локомоти́в</i> (lokomotív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock, curl, ringlet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Locken</i>, plural for <i>Locke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ло́коть</i> (lókotʹ): (small) elbow</li></ol><p><i>ло́коть</i> (lókotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) elbow</li><li>cubit, ell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ȍlkъtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ulna, elbow bone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elbow; ulnar, anconal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cubit; cubital</li></ol><p><i>ло́коть</i> (lókotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie, falsehood</li><li>failure; futility</li><li>hopeless affair, no-win affair</li><li>emptiness in the soul</li></ol><p>From <i>локша́</i> (lokšá), dialectal form of <i>лапша́</i> (lapšá, “noodles”). Compare the expression <i>ве́шать лапшу́ на́ уши</i> (véšatʹ lapšú ná uši, “to tell falsehoods”, literally “to hang noodles upon one’s ears”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve in a prison unfairly (because of a judicial mistake)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lolita (sexually alluring young girl)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) jailbait</li></ol><p>From the title character, Dolores “<i>Lolita</i>” Haze, of Vladimir Nabokov's 1955 novel <i>Lolita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;anglicism) lolcat (<i>an internet meme, an image macro of a cat or a kitten with a humorous caption. The caption often contains deliberately gross grammatical and orthographic errors</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>lolcat</i>, from <i>лол</i> (lol) +&lrm; <i>кот</i> (kot, “cat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digging bar, crow, (large) crowbar</li><li>scrap, waste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lomъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poser, affected person</li><li>affected / mincing / simpering woman or girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken</li></ol><p><i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to quarry (stone)</li><li>to rack, to cause to ache</li><li>to cause (someone) to lose spirit or will</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lamati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to break up, to fracture, to split</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break down (of a device, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to collapse, to fall apart</li><li>to crack, to break (of the voice)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pose, to mince, to pretend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obstinate, to cause difficulties, to refuse to agree</li></ol><p><i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pawn shop</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>Lombardia</i> (“Lombardy”). In the Medieval period Lombardy was known for its banks and usury.</p><p>Compare natively formed Bulgarian <i>заложна къща</i> (založna kǎšta), Czech <i>zastavárna</i>, Macedonian <i>заложилница</i> (založilnica), Serbo-Croatian <i>залагаоница</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) card table</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лом</i> (lom): (small) digging bar</li></ol><p><i>лом</i> (lom) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to break</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to charge forward, to break through</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to ache</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lomiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(third person only)</i> to be <i>(near to)</i> breaking <i>(with)</i>, to burst <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to force one's way <i>(to, into)</i>, to come in crowds <i>(to)</i>, to flock <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) withdrawal symptoms (as with drug addicts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brittle, friable (able to break or snap easily under stress or pressure)</li><li>fragile, breakable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lomъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лом-</i> (lom-) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij). Cognate with Czech <i>lámavý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draft horse, carthorse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clematis (<i>Clematis</i>)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ломиніс</i> (lomynis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dull ache</li></ol><p><i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>dolor</i>, <i>doleō</i> (akin to <i>dolō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunk, chunk, slice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) Londoner</li></ol><p><i>Ло́ндон</i> (Lóndon) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) London</li></ol><p><i>Ло́ндон</i> (Lóndon) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longeron, spar, stringer, stiffener <i>(airframe)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>longeron</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) last year</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>лони</i> (loni), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olni</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ol-ney</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) last year</li></ol><p>From <i>лони́</i> (loní) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ). Compare днесь (dnesʹ), вчерась (včerasʹ), летось (letosʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) lap, bosom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lono</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) fall (<i>rope</i>)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;now often considered pejorative) Lapp, Laplander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blade, propeller blade, swim fin blade</li><li>vane, fan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spade, shovel</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) electric guitar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) punchline</li><li>(<i>baking</i>) peel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lopata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shovel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to slog through, to crank through, to work through (to process, do or implement in large quantities)</li></ol><p><i>лопа́та</i> (lopáta) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лопа́та</i> (lopáta); small shovel, small spade</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) shoulder blade, scapula</li><li>spatula</li></ol><p>From <i>лопа́та</i> (lopáta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лопа́тка</i> (lopátka); small shovel, small spade; small spatula</li></ol><p><i>лопа́тка</i> (lopátka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to devour, to gobble</li><li>alternative form of <i>ло́паться</i> (lópatʹsja)</li><li>to cause to burst</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lap-eh₂-ti</i> (“to lick, lap up”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pop, to burst (to break from internal pressure)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crash, to go bankrupt (e.g. of a company, of an idea)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be depleted (of patience)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to express one's opinion harshly</li></ol><p><i>ло́пать</i> (lópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(ergative,&#32;transitive,&#32;intransitive) to pop, to burst (to break from internal pressure)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ergative,&#32;intransitive) to crash, to go bankrupt (e.g. of a company, of an idea)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ergative,&#32;intransitive) to be depleted (of patience)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to express one's opinion harshly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to mumble; to speak unclearly</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;botany) burdock</li><li>(animate,&#32;informal) simpleton</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lopuxъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lopъ</i> (“leaf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-uxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;colloquial) abbreviation of <i>ларингооторино́лог</i> (laringootorinólog, “otorhinolaryngologist”)</li><li>(<i>fandom slang</i>) lore (backstory created around a fictional universe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord (British aristocrat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lord</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocotte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lorgnette (an opera glass with a handle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lorgnette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) tight pants made of moose skin, buckskins</li><li>leggings</li></ol><p>From <i>лось</i> (losʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) (<~ <i>коза́</i> (kozá)); <i>мех</i> (mex), <i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moysós</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elk, moose</li></ol><p><i>лось</i> (losʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gloss, lustre, polish</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) dell, hollow, lowland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъskъ</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag, shred, scrap</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) scraps of cloth or leather in the textile and leather industries</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loskutъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rag, shred, scrap</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) patchwork</li></ol><p>From <i>лоску́т</i> (loskút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), possibly with stress shift as if from <i>ло́скут</i> (lóskut).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лоску́т</i> (loskút)</li></ol><p><i>лоску́т</i> (loskút) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be glossy, to shine</li></ol><p>From <i>ло́сный</i> (lósnyj, “shining”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from <i>лоск</i> (losk, “gloss, luster”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widelip orchid (<i>Liparis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>лоск</i> (losk, “sheen, gloss”) +&lrm; <i>-ня́к</i> (-nják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salmoniformes (a biological order that includes salmon, trout, chars, freshwater whitefishes, and graylings&#59; has only one biological family under it, the Salmonidae)</li></ol><p><i>лосо́сь</i> (losósʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) salmon</li></ol><p><i>лосо́сь</i> (losósʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salmon (meat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salmon <i>(the fish and the seafood)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lososь</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lahs</i>, Icelandic <i>lax</i>, German <i>Lachs</i>, Yiddish <i>לאַקס</i> (laks), English <i>lox</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salmonidae (a biological family that includes salmons and graylings)</li><li>Salmoninae (a subfamily within Salmonidae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) salmon</li></ol><p><i>лосо́сь</i> (losósʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moose <i>(U.S., Canada)</i>, elk <i>(British)</i> (an animal belonging to the <i>Alces</i> genus)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лось</i> (losĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olsь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁el-</i> (“deer”), whence English <i>elk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup remover</li><li>lotion</li><li>aftershave, shaving lotion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lotion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) plummet, sounding line</li><li>lot (old Russian unit of mass equal to 12.8 grams)</li><li>(finance,&#32;auctions) lot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lot</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>lot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lotharingian</li><li>Lorrainer, Lorrainian</li></ol><p>From <i>Лотарингия</i> (Lotaringija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lottery (scheme for the distribution of prizes by lot or chance)</li></ol><p>Likely from Dutch <i>loterij</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotto (game of chance similar to bingo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tray, pan; chute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lotokъ</i>, <i>&#42;lotъkъ</i>, <i>&#42;lotoka</i>, <i>&#42;lotъka</i>, further etymology uncertain, in East Slavic only, Polish <i>łotok</i> being borrowed from there, as well as Lithuanian <i>latãkas</i> and Latvian <i>lataka</i>, <i>lataga</i>, possibly suffixed and ablauting (arbitrarily) to <i>&#42;laty</i>, otherwise related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lattō</i>, or Old Irish <i>lathach</i> (“mire, puddle”), Old Norse <i>leðja</i> (“mud”), Latin <i>latex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>λωτός</i> (lōtós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lotus</li></ol><p><i>ло́тос</i> (lótos) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hasty</li></ol><p><i>лото́ха</i> (lotóxa, “chatterbox, gossip; bustler”) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) low-cost carrier, budget airline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>low-cost</i> + <i>-ер</i> (-er).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawrencium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oleaster, silverberry (<i>Elaeagnus</i>)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) male Atlantic salmon</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) sucker, mark, someone who is easy to swindle or con</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) loser</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) stiff, simpleton, dummy</li><li>(cant,&#32;obsolete) peasant, muzhik</li></ol><p>Recorded in Astrakhan and among Greben Cossacks. Discernably, a transferred meaning of Kalmyk <i>лох</i> (lox, “aloe vera”), which relates through the Kalmyk home in Dzungaria to Chinese <i>蘆薈</i>／<i>芦荟</i> (<i>lúhuì</i>, “aloe vera”), see it for more.</p><p>From a Finnic language, compare Veps <i>lohi</i>, Finnish <i>lohi</i>, Estonian <i>lõhe</i>.</p><p>From the Ofenya argot of travelling peddlers; possibly an alteration of <i>лопу́х</i> (lopúx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лоха́нь</i> (loxánʹ, “washtub, pan”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to make a mistake, to blunder</li></ol><p><i>лох</i> (lox, “dummy, loser, sucker”) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washtub, pan</li><li>(nautical,&#32;figuratively,&#32;derogatory) tub (an old, slow vessel)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лоханꙗ</i> (loxanja), apparently via a Kipchak form from Ancient Greek <i>λακάνη</i> (lakánē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tousle, to dishevel, to ruffle</li></ol><p><i>лохма́тый</i> (loxmátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>shaggy-haired, dishevelled, tousled</li></ol><p>Related to <i>лохмо́тья</i> (loxmótʹja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rags; tatters; duds; frazzles</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loxmotьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лохмо́т</i> (loxmót, “shred”) +&lrm; <i>-ья</i> (-ʹja), from <i>ло́хма</i> (lóxma, “tuft”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disheveled locks; matted hair, mop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a Ukrainian, khokhol</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>лох</i> (lox, “simpleton, dummy”) +&lrm; <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “khokhol, Ukrainian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) loser, simpleton</li></ol><p><i>лох</i> (lox) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) pilot (specialist who navigates ships in dangerous areas, ports, etc.)</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pilot fish (a fish, <i>Naucrates ductor</i>, that often follows large vessels and sharks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>loodsman</i>. Compare English <i>lodesman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsepower</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>horsepower</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horse, equine</li></ol><p><i>ло́шадь</i> (lóšadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ло́шадь</i> (lóšadʹ): horsey, small horse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лошадь</i> (lošadĭ), from <i>лоша</i> (loša), a Turkic borrowing; compare Tatar <i>алаша</i> (alaşa, “pack horse”), Chuvash <i>лаша</i> (laš̬a, “horse”), Kazakh <i>алаша</i> (alaşa, “a kind of camel”), Crimean Tatar <i>алаша</i> (“horse”), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ло́шадь</i> (lóšadʹ): small horse</li></ol><p><i>ло́шадь</i> (lóšadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinny (offspring of male horse and female donkey)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Turkic. See Russian <i>ло́шадь</i> (lóšadʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow, depression, dell, glen, ravine</li></ol><p>From <i>лоск</i> (losk, “dell, hollow, lowland (<i>dialectal</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>loyally (often politically)</li><li>non-judgementally, neutrally, tolerantly</li></ol><p><i>лоя́льный</i> (lojálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loyalty, allegiance</li><li>benevolence, good intentions</li><li>accommodativeness</li><li>lenience, laxity, indulgence, permissiveness</li></ol><p><i>лоя́льный</i> (lojálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loyal (usually politically)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) non-judgemental, neutral, tolerant</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>loyal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) bestie, best friend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) bestie, best friend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inner bark of a tree, bast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lubъ</i> (“bast”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lowbʰ-ó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i> (“to peel, cut off, harm”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>lùpti</i> (“to peel, to shell”), Latvian <i>luba</i> (“narrow strip of wood”), Latin <i>liber</i> (“book, inner bark of a tree”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laubą</i> (whence English <i>leaf</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of bast</li><li>bast product</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) splint</li><li>type of wooden board for printing lubok images</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) lubok (woodcut or engraving of folk art)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) lubok literature (popular literature in prerevolutionary Russia, similar to chapbooks)</li></ol><p><i>луб</i> (lub) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bast</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lubok</li></ol><p><i>лубо́к</i> (lubók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lubyanka</li></ol><p><i>Лубя́нка</i> (Lubjánka) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Luhansk/Lugansk (city in Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From <i>Луга́н(ск)</i> (Lugán(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) A certain mythological creature in Slavic folklore.</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) A plant of the genus <i>Deschampsia</i>; a deschampsia, hair grass or tussock grass.</li><li>(<i>mycology</i>) A fairy ring champignon; a mushroom of the species <i>Marasmius oreades</i>.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>луг</i> (lug, “field”) +&lrm; <i>-овик</i> (-ovik). Compare <i>Луговики́</i> (Lugovikí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meadow</li></ol><p><i>луг</i> (lug) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Луговой)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Lugovoy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tin, to blanch, to tin-plate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puddle, pool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;luža</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;lugja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawn, grass-plot; (forest) glade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лу́жа</i> (lúža): (a small) puddle, pool</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лужица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lusatia (a traditionally Slavic-speaking cultural region in Central Europe, in modern Germany, Poland and the Czech Republic)</li><li>Luzhitsa (a small river in Leningrad Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Upper Sorbian <i>Łužica</i> or Lower Sorbian <i>Łužyca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sorbian, Lusatian, Wendish</li></ol><p><i>Лу́жица</i> (Lúžica, “Lusatia”) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sorb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>луг</i> (lug, “meadow”)</li></ol><p><i>луг</i> (lug) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a decrement on the ground of a playing field as for billiards or golf whereinto the game’s ball is to be transferred, pocket</li></ol><p>From French <i>blouse</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Луза)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Luza (a river in Kirov Oblast, Vologda Oblast, and the Komi Republic, Russia)</li><li>Luza (a town in Luzsky district, Kirov Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Komi-Zyrian <i>Луз</i> (Luz), itself from Kildin Sami <i>лӯсс</i> (lūss, “salmon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) failure, loser</li></ol><p>First attested in 1990. Borrowed from English <i>loser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) louis d'or (17th and 18th century French gold coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>louis d'or</i>. Compare to <i>наполеондо́р</i> (napoleondór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;collectively) onion, onions</li><li>bow (weapon used for shooting arrows)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) appearance, clothing style, look</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lukъ</i>, borrowed from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laukaz</i> (compare German <i>Lauch</i>, English <i>leek</i>, Old Norse <i>laukr</i> whence Danish <i>løg</i> and Swedish <i>lök</i>). Cognates include Polabian <i>lauk</i>, Bulgarian <i>лук</i> (luk) and Slovene and Serbo-Croatian <i>luk</i>.</p><p>from Old East Slavic <i>лукъ</i> (lukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;lankás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lenk-</i> (“to bend”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>lankas</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>look</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue globe onion (<i>Allium caeruleum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>лук</i> (luk, “onion”) +&lrm; <i>голубо́й</i> (golubój, “sky-blue”), a calque of translingual <i>Allium caeruleum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leek (vegetable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend <i>(in a river or road)</i></li><li>pommel <i>(of a saddle)</i></li><li>saddlebow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫka</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Лука)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Luka, equivalent to English Luke or Lucas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be crafty, cunning, to sly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>craftily, cunningly</li></ol><p><i>лука́вый</i> (lukávyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slyness, craftiness, archness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лукавьство</i> (lukavĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫkavьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лука́вить</i> (lukávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sly, crafty, cunning</li><li>arch, playful (knowing, clever, mischievous)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Evil One</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫkavъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lukashism (the political and economic system created by Alexander Lukashenko &#47; Aliaksandr Lukašenka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;obsolete) a basket with vertical sides, typically made of bast</li><li>(regional,&#32;obsolete) a basketful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лукъно</i> (lukŭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫkъno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion</li><li>(botany,&#32;anatomy) bulb</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;architecture) onion-shaped dome of a Russian church</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) old-fashioned pocket watch with thick convex glass and convex backside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulbous</li><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) onion</li></ol><p><i>лу́ковица</i> (lúkovica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>diminutive of</i> луковица</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) onion</li></ol><p><i>лук</i> (luk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small basket with vertical sides, typically made of bast</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>лукно́</i> (luknó) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko). Inherited from Old East Slavic, from a diminutive of <i>лукъно</i> (lukŭno, “basket”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǫkъno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon (the Moon, the satellite of planet Earth)</li><li>moon (any substantially sized natural satellite of a planet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>луна</i> (luna, “moon”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;luna</i> (“moon”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Луна)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Moon (the only natural satellite of the Earth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusement park</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Luna Park</i>, possibly via Italian <i>luna park</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sleepwalker, somnambulist</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) lunatic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lunatiker</i> (“sleepwalker”), from Latin <i>lūnāticus</i>, from <i>lūna</i>. Compare Macedonian <i>месечар</i> (mesečar), Serbo-Croatian <i>ме̏сеча̄р</i>, related to Macedonian <i>месечина</i> (mesečina), Serbo-Croatian <i>ме̏се̄ц</i>, Russian <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) alveolus</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) indentation</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) socket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) lunar eclipse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) moon; lunar</li><li>moonlit</li></ol><p><i>луна́</i> (luná, “moon”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonlight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) menstruation</li></ol><p>From луна "moon".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lunodrome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonquake</li></ol><p><i>Луна́</i> (Luná, “Moon”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>трясе́ние</i> (trjasénije, “shaking, trembling”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunokhod, lunar rover</li></ol><p>From <i>луна́</i> (luná) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod), combining form of <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to walk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lunokhod</li></ol><p><i>лунохо́д</i> (lunoxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) harrier, ring-tail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnifying glass, magnifier (instrument)</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;algebra) loop</li></ol><p>From French <i>loupe</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Macedonian <i>зголемувач</i> (zgolemuvač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goggle-eyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel, to bark</li><li>to thrash, to flog, to spank, to beat, to batter</li><li>to beat, to drub, to clobber</li><li>to knock, to hit, to zap, to shoot <i>(at)</i>, to fire <i>(on)</i>, to bomb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fleece (someone) (of a certain amount)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лупити</i> (lupiti, “to rob”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lupiti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;laupī́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewp-</i> (“to peel, to break off”). Compare Albanian <i>labë</i>, German <i>Laub</i>, Latin <i>liber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goggle-eyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ray, beam</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) radius bone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лучь</i> (lučĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lučь</i> (“ray; torch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lowkis</i>, which is derived from <i>&#42;lewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ray, beam; radial</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) radiation</li></ol><p><i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radiant, effulgent, beaming</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>заря́</i> (zarjá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>луч</i> (luč)</li></ol><p><i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fatwood splinter, chip</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a small torch made of it, a rushlight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лучи́на</i> (lučína)</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) fly</li></ol><p><i>лучи́на</i> (lučína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) radiant, glowing</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) radiant (arising from radiation)</li><li>radially diverging, diverging from a point</li></ol><p><i>луч</i> (luč) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to allow</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) to hunt (fish, etc.) at night with a lit torch</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to unite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lučìti</i> (“to find? to throw? to reach? (<i>reflexive</i>) to happen?”), from an Indo-European root <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“bright”); compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lũčь</i> (“ray, torch”). The meaning "to hunt at night with a torch" may have a separate etymology as a denominative verb derived from <i>&#42;lũčь</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lǭčìti</i> (“to separate”). For the meaning "to snare, to entice" in some prefixed verbs, compare cognate Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lęťi</i> (“to bend, to set traps”) &lt; <i>*lękti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to shine (<i>of light or a light source</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to gleam with emotion (<i>of eyes</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to radiate (<i>of emotions on one's face or in one's eyes</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to radiate outwards (<i>e.g. of wrinkles around the eyes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archer, bowman</li></ol><p>From <i>лук</i> (luk, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лук</i> (luk): onion</li><li>diminutive of <i>лук</i> (luk): bow (weapon)</li></ol><p><i>лук</i> (luk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>хорошо́</i> (xorošó)</li><li>rather, would better (do something) (often used with verbs in the past tense with subjunctive <i>бы</i> (by))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lučьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something better</li><li>the best</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij): best</li><li>comparative degree of <i>хороший</i> (xorošij): better</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lučьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) to smile insolently</li><li>(Smolensk,&#32;Tver,&#32;Novgorod) to smile, to laugh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ski (one of a pair of long flat runners designed for gliding over snow)</li><li>runner (on a sleigh or sled)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lyža</i>, if existing in Proto-Slavic at all. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ли́жа</i> (lýža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) skiing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skier</li></ol><p><i>лы́жа</i> (lýža) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ski</li></ol><p><i>лы́жа</i> (lýža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ski track, trail left by a pair of skis, or one artificially created for the purpose of skiing on</li></ol><p><i>лы́жа</i> (lýža, “ski”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bast (plant fiber)</li><li>yarn, rope, or geotextile made of bast fiber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лꙑко</i> (lyko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lyko</i> (“bast”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;lúnˀka</i> (“bast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become bald</li></ol><p><i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bald spot, bald patch; bald head</li></ol><p>From <i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milled, sawed flattened surface on a shaft</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Eurasian coot, common coot, Australian coot (bird)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lysъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baldish</li></ol><p><i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldness</li></ol><p><i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greenland seal</li></ol><p><i>лысый</i> (lysyj) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian coot, common coot, Australian coot (bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald, bald-headed</li><li>of tyres: whose surface is worn away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lysъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>лысы</i> (lysy), Czech <i>lysý</i>, Polish <i>łysy</i>, Ukrainian <i>лисий</i> (lysyj). Akin to Old Armenian <i>լոյս</i> (loys, “light”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (whence also <i>луна́</i> (luná), <i>луч</i> (luč)).</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>φᾰλᾰκρός</i> (phălăkrós) (akin to <i>φάλαρος</i> (phálaros)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;humorous) a blog posting about one’s daily life</li></ol><p>Recyrillization of <i>lytdybr</i>, from the letters you would get when typing <i>дневни́к</i> (dnevník, “journal, diary”) while using the Latin QWERTY keyboard layout instead of the Cyrillic ЙЦУКЕН layout.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лꙑчьнꙑи</i> (lyčĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lyčьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lyko</i> (“bast”) + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лы́ко</i> (lýko) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ли</i> (li)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ли</i> (li)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) lion's share</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lion's</li></ol><p><i>лев</i> (lev) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lviv, Lvov (city in Ukraine)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lvov (Russian surname, e.g. of Alexei Lvov, Russian composer)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Lvovo, Lvovsky, Lvovskoye (name of several settlements in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Львов</i> (Lʹvov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion cub</li></ol><p><i>лев</i> (lev) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privilege, benefit, exemption, discount</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>льгота</i> (lĭgota), from the root of <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij, “easy”) + <i>-ота</i> (-ota). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>вільго́та</i> (vilʹhóta) and Belarusian <i>ільго́та</i> (ilʹhóta); compare also Polish <i>ulga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beneficiary</li></ol><p><i>льго́та</i> (lʹgóta) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preferential, privileged</li></ol><p><i>льго́та</i> (lʹgóta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice floe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>льди́на</i> (lʹdína): small ice floe</li></ol><p><i>льди́на</i> (lʹdína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy, ice-covered</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ice-cold, freezing cold</li></ol><p><i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) icy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>league (distance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lieue</i>. Further from Latin <i>leuga</i>. Doublet of (<i>unit of distantce</i>) <i>ли́га</i> (líga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaxy, flaxen (made of, relating to, or characteristic of flax)</li><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) linaceous, relating or belonging to the flax family (Linaceae).</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) Any plant of the flax family, Linaceae.</li></ol><p>From <i>лён</i> (ljon, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, relational adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax mill</li></ol><p><i>лён</i> (ljon) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пряди́льня</i> (prjadílʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flaxseed, linseed</li></ol><p><i>лён</i> (ljon, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́мя</i> (sémja, “seed”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cling <i>(to)</i>, to stick <i>(to)</i></li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;figurative) to cultivate (someone), to fawn on</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;figurative) to feel drawn towards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flax, linen</li></ol><p><i>лён</i> (ljon) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flatterer, adulator</li></ol><p><i>льстить</i> (lʹstitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flattering, sycophantic</li><li>smooth-tongued, smooth-speaking</li></ol><p><i>льстить</i> (lʹstitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, to adulate</li><li>to flatter, to please</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>льстити</i> (lĭstiti) (also <i>лестити</i> (lestiti)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьstiti</i>, derived as if from <i>&#42;lьstь</i> (whence <i>лесть</i> (lestʹ)) + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being poured</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) laptop (a laptop computer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>laptop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving</li><li>amorous, lustful</li></ol><p><i>любо́вь</i> (ljubóvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>оби́льный</i> (obílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>любе́зный</i> (ljubéznyj): dearest</li></ol><p><i>любе́зн(ый)</i> (ljubézn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pay compliments, pay court</li></ol><p><i>любе́зный</i> (ljubéznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>friendly (in a friendly manner)</li></ol><p><i>любе́зный</i> (ljubéznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtesy, politeness, civility</li><li>kindness</li><li>favour/favor</li></ol><p><i>любе́зный</i> (ljubéznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amiable, affable, courteous (mild, benign)</li><li>obliging (ready to help)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) my good man, sir (form of address)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>любьзнъ</i> (ljubĭznŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favourite/favorite, pet, fondling, minion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) favorite, pet; teacher's pet; blue-eyed boy (<i>particularly someone who is manipulative and preys on someone's sympathies to the detriment of others</i>)</li></ol><p><i>люби́мец</i> (ljubímec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>favorite</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) beloved, darling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lovage, sea parsley (<i>Levisticum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>люби́сток</i> (ljubýstok), from Polish <i>lubszczek</i> (modern <i>lubczyk</i>), from Middle High German <i>lübestecke</i> (> German <i>Liebstöckel</i>), from Latin <i>levisticum</i>, earlier <i>ligusticum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>λιγυστικόν</i> (ligustikón), originally “Ligurian plant used for medicinal purposes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover, devotee, fan</li><li>amateur, dilettante</li></ol><p>From <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amateur, amateurish (non-professional)</li></ol><p><i>люби́тель</i> (ljubítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to love, to like, to be fond of, to grow fond of (more intensely than <i>нра́виться</i> (nrávitʹsja))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľubiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i>, whence English <i>love</i>, Sanskrit <i>लुभ्यति</i> (lubhyati, “to desire”), Sanskrit <i>लोभयति</i> (lobhayati, “to make crazy”) and Latin <i>libet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to love each other</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to love [(<i>optional</i>) <i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) there is loving [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated as English <i>love</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ljubljana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;folkloric) nicely, beautifully; prettily; pleasantly; well; correctly</li></ol><p><i>лю́бый</i> (ljúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiration</li></ol><p><i>любова́ться</i> (ljubovátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admire, to feast one's eyes (upon)</li></ol><p><i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover</li><li>paramour</li></ol><p>From <i>любо́вь</i> (ljubóvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fondly, lovingly</li><li>painstakingly</li></ol><p><i>любо́вный</i> (ljubóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love letter (letter about the author's love)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) love</li><li>amorous (indicating love or sexual desire)</li><li>loving</li></ol><p><i>любо́вь</i> (ljubóvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love triangle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love</li><li>affection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>любꙑ</i> (ljuby), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľuby</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>लोभ</i> (lobha, “desire, cupidity”) and English <i>love</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Любовь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lyubov or Liubov</li></ol><p>From <i>любо́вь</i> (ljubóvʹ, “love”); a calque of Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγάπη</i> (Agápē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agape (asexual love, spiritual love, altruistic love)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love at first sight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquisitiveness, curiosity</li></ol><p><i>любозна́тельный</i> (ljuboznátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curious, inquisitive</li></ol><p><i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>any, either, whichever one wants</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľubъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>любить</i> (ljubitʹ, “to like”) +&lrm; <i>-ой</i> (-oj). For semantic shift to "whichever" compare Latin <i>quilibet</i>, German <i>beliebig</i>, Spanish <i>qualquiera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at all costs, at any cost (sparing no effort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nosey parker, stickybeak (prying person, male or female)</li></ol><p>Literally, “inquisitive Varvara/Barbara”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>curiously, with curiosity</li></ol><p><i>любопы́тный</i> (ljubopýtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curious, inquisitive</li><li>interesting, intriguing</li></ol><p><i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́пыт</i> (ópyt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curiosity, interest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be curious, to ask, to pry</li></ol><p><i>любопы́тство</i> (ljubopýtstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;except&#32;short&#32;forms</i>) dear, sweet, cute, pleasant, pleasing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>любъ</i> (ljubŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľubъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people, folk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľudъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people, humans</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľudьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi): (<i>colloquial</i>) (worthless) people</li></ol><p><i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ишки</i> (-iški). Compare Latin <i>popellus</i> (“rabble”), diminutive of Latin <i>populus</i> (“people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) densely populated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crowded</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well-traveled, full of passers-by (<i>of a place</i>)</li></ol><p><i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man-eater, cannibal; a person, animal, or other creature that eats humans</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a cruel person, a murderer</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a tyrant, an oppressor</li></ol><p><i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>людое́д</i> (ljudojéd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľudьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatch, hatchway</li><li>port</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>luik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lux (unit of light)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deluxe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lūx</i>.</p><p>Derived from Latin <i>lūxus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luxemburgism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Luxembourg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cradle (oscillating bed for a baby)</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) suspended scaffold, cradle (lifting device)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) gun cradle (mount for the barrel of an artillery piece on a gun carriage)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sidecar (<i>of a motorcycle</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;regional) tobacco pipe</li></ol><p>From <i>люлю</i> (ljulju), an interjection used to lull babies. Compare to English <i>lull</i>, Swedish <i>lulla</i> (“to lull”), Icelandic <i>lúlla</i> (“to lull”).</p><p>From Turkic (Ottoman Turkish لوله (lüle)), ultimately from Persian لوله (lule).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumen (SI-unit for luminous flux)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>lumen</i>, from Latin <i>lumen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>luminescent</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) fluorescent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous&#32;or&#32;low&#32;colloquial</i>) aluminium, aluminum</li></ol><p>Apheretic form of <i>алюми́ний</i> (aljumínij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luminophore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;historical) Lumpenproletariat</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) miscreant, hoodlum, ruffian</li><li>snakefish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lumpen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Marxism</i>) lumpenproletariat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lumpenproletariat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumpenisation, degradation to lumpens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chandelier, lustre (<i>UK</i>), luster (<i>US</i>) (a branched, often ornate, lighting fixture suspended from the ceiling)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>lustre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lustration (restoration of credibility to a government by the purging earlier perpetrators)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) February</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лютъ</i> (ljutŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľutъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Protestantism</i>) Lutheran</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Lutheraner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Lutheran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Protestantism</i>) Lutheranism</li></ol><p>From <i>лютера́нин</i> (ljuteránin) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lutetium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttercup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>lutnia</i>, from Czech <i>loutna</i>, from Middle High German <i>lûte</i>, from Italian <i>liuto</i>, from Arabic اَلْعُود (al-ʕūd, “wood”); see English <i>lute</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fierceness, ferocity</li></ol><p><i>лю́тый</i> (ljútyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, cruel, grim</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Ukraine) February</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лютъ</i> (ljutŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľutъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alfalfa, lucerne (<i>Medicago sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lucerna</i> (“lamp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(musical note)</i> A</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A-flat major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A-sharp major (major key)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A-sharp minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>лега́вый</i> (legávyj)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>лега́вый</i> (legávyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to sway</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lęgati</i>. Cognate with Slovak <i>lengať</i> (“to dangle, to swing”). Per Vasmer, probably related to Lithuanian <i>lingúoti</i> (“to swing”), <i>li̇̀ngė</i> (“pole on which a cradle is suspended”), Latvian <i>lĩguôt</i> (“to swing, to sway”), also <i>lĩgât</i> (1sg. <i>lĩgãju</i>), Sanskrit <i>लङ्घति</i> (láṅghati, “to jump up, to bounce”), also <i>लङ्घयति</i> (laṅgháyati), Old Irish <i>lingid</i> (“to jump up, to bounce”), Old High German <i>lungar</i> (“quick, fast”), Old English <i>lungor</i> (“quick, fast”), Ancient Greek <i>ἐλαφρός</i> (elaphrós, “easy, agile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li></ol><p><i>ляга́ть</i> (ljagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock, to let out (<i>at</i>), to jump (<i>on</i>) (to tell something offensive of someone)</li></ol><p><i>ляга́ть</i> (ljagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wading pool</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) A French person, a frogeater</li></ol><p><i>лягу́шка</i> (ljagúška, “frog”) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frog; frog's, ranine</li></ol><p><i>лягу́шка</i> (ljagúška) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frog; frog's, ranine</li></ol><p><i>лягу́шка</i> (ljagúška) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frog</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) @ (at sign) (more common: <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka, “dog”))</li></ol><p><i>ляга́ть</i> (ljagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young frog</li></ol><p><i>лягуш(ка)</i> (ljaguš(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wretched</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;archaic) thigh, haunch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lędvьje</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lendʰ-</i> (“loins”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>रन्ध्र</i> (rándhra, “split, hole”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) thigh, haunch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clank, clang, clack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clank, to clang</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>лязг</i> (ljazg) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clank, to clang</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ля́згать</i> (ljázgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) doll, puppet (a toy in the form of a human)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) baby</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ля́лька</i> (ljálʹka), from Polish <i>lalka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) million</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lambda (the Greek letter Λ&#47;λ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>λάμβδα</i> (lámbda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap, sling, tug</li></ol><p>Occurs in such form from the 18th century, <i>ля́ма</i> (ljáma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), and with equal signification in Ukrainian <i>ля́мка</i> (ljámka), <i>ля́ма</i> (ljáma) and Belarusian <i>ля́мка</i> (ljámka).</p><p>Probably connected to Finnish <i>lämsä</i> and Karelian <i>lamca</i> and its Sami origins, while the similarity to Polish <i>lamówka</i> (“border, edging”) is coincidental, though there be a homonymic Belarusian <i>ля́мка</i> (ljámka, “edge, ridge”) borrowed from Polish <i>lama</i>, <i>lamka</i> (“fringe, hem”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Liaoning</li></ol><p>From <i>Ляони́н</i> (Ljaonín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) error, mistake; blooper, blunder</li></ol><p>Probably from an onomatopoeic or interjectory root of Proto-Slavic origin. Proposed cognates include (<i>vulgar</i>) <i>ля́па</i> (ljápa, “spot, stain; mistake”); (<i>dialectal</i>) <i>ля́па</i> (ljápa, “slap in the face”, noun), <i>аляповатый</i> (aljapovatyj, “crudely fashioned”), Ukrainian <i>ля́пати</i> (ljápaty, “splash; soil”, verb), <i>ля́пка</i> (ljápka, “blot, smudge”, noun), Belarusian <i>ля́паць</i> (ljápacʹ, “knock; speak harshly”, verb) Chernyshev also postulates a relationship to <i>ла́па</i> (lápa, “paw; big foot”), <i>ла́пaть</i> (lápatʹ, “paw, grope”). A possible relationship to the root in <i>ля́псус</i> (ljápsus, “blunder, slip”) remains to be clarified.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to make hastily, to throw together</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to slap, to splotch, to daub (paint on something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to whack, to hit</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to blurt out, to blab</li></ol><p>Probably from an onomatopoeic or interjectory root related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lopiti</i> (“to clap, bang, strike”) or <i>&#42;lapa</i> (“paw”). See more at <i>ляп</i> (ljap), <i>ля́па</i> (ljápa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(pharmacology,&#32;inorganic chemistry) silver nitrate</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>lapis (īnfernālis)</i>; compare Polish <i>lapis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to blab, to blurt out</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to hit, to whack, to smack</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;qualification to verb сказал,&#32;derogatory) to straight from the shoulder</li></ol><p><i>ляп</i> (ljap) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blunder, slip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lapsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) a spirit of the underworld, a demon</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;rare) scoundrel</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) whore, slut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>larva</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Romani <i>larva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;now&#32;sometimes&#32;slightly&#32;derogatory&#32;or&#32;ironic,&#32;ethnic slur) Pole, Polish man, Polack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>лѧхъ</i> (lęxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lęxъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lęděninъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track and field (group of athletic sports)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amble (an unhurried leisurely walk or stroll)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light industry (industry that does not require high capitalization or heavy machinery)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light, lightweight</li><li>easy, facile, slight</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lucky</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьgъkъ</i>, from pre-Slavic *<i>h₁ln̥gʷʰ-u-ko</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁lengʷʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) easy-going, enthusiastic, eager (readily taking part in new endeavours)</li></ol><p>Literally, “light on the rise”.</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raisu</i> (“a travel, journey”) (whence <i>рейс</i> (rejs)) (akin to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raizijaną</i> (“to cause (someone, something) to rise; raise”)) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rey-</i> (“to rise, arise”)). Also compare <i>анабасис</i> (anabasis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lung</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij, “light, easy”). Compare Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁lengʷʰ-</i> (“light, not heavy, agile, nimble”), with a sense of "the light organ" developing later. Compare Polish <i>lekkie</i>, English <i>lung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easiness, ease (the quality of being easy&#59; simplicity)</li><li>lightness (the state of having little weight)</li></ol><p><i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij): light, lightweight, easy, facile, slight</li></ol><p>From <i>лёг(кий)</i> (ljóg(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lung; pulmonary</li></ol><p><i>лёгкие</i> (ljóxkije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice</li><li>(slang,&#32;sports) skating rink, ice rink</li><li>(crime,&#32;drugs) methamphetamine</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ледъ</i> (ledŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ledъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ledús</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the lying position</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lying (for a long time), lying position</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) lair, den, place where animals lie in hiding</li></ol><p><i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>льнъ</i> (lĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lьnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;lína</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;linom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) loess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Löss</i> or (older orthography) <i>Löß</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;letъ</i>, from which the verb <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ) is also derived.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flying, flight</li></ol><p><i>лёт</i> (ljot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) airman, pilot, flyer, aviator</li></ol><p><i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female pilot of an aircraft, aviatress, airwoman</li></ol><p>From <i>лётчик</i> (ljótčik) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ле́вый</i> (lévyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>леса́</i> (lesá, “fishing line”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>леса́</i> (lesá, “scaffolding”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>лес</i> (les).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ле́то</i> (léto).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ле́ший</i> (léšij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>метр</i> (metr, “meter”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>мужско́й род</i> (mužskój rod, “masculine gender”)</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>милли-</i> (milli-, “milli-”) (<i>(0.001)</i>)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: М)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>метро́</i> (metró, “subway (station)”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>мужска́я</i> (mužskája, “men's room, men’s toilet”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá, “Moscow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hmm, pff (an expression of being puzzled or doubtful)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>uh, um (expressing hesitation in speech)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>м-да́</i> (m-dá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj, “little”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>мужско́й род</i> (mužskój rod, “masculine gender”, literally “m.”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>месте́чко</i> (mestéčko, “town, shtetl”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: М.)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>метро́</i> (metró, “subway (station)”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>мужска́я</i> (mužskája, “men's room, men’s toilet”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá, “Moscow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мо́жет быть</i> (móžet bytʹ, “may be, maybe, perhaps”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мину́вшего го́да</i> (minúvševo góda): last year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ме́жду про́чим</i> (méždu próčim, “by the way, incidentally; among other things”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мо́жет быть</i> (móžet bytʹ, “may be, maybe, perhaps”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мину́вшего го́да</i> (minúvševo góda): last year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mausoleum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mausoleum</i> (“mausoleum”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mausoleum of Maussollos at Halicarnassus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mausoleum</li></ol><p><i>мавзоле́й</i> (mavzoléj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moor (a member of a certain mixed race of Arab and Berber people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Maure</i> or Latin <i>Maurus</i>. Doublet of <i>му́рин</i> (múrin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mauritian</li></ol><p><i>Маври́кий</i> (Mavríkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mauritanian</li></ol><p><i>Маврита́ния</i> (Mavritánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mage (magician, wizard or sorcerer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>μάγος</i> (mágos). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;megʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Magadan</li></ol><p><i>Магада́н</i> (Magadán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>магази́н</i> (magazín)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>магази́н</i> (magazín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shop, store (establishment that sells goods)</li><li>magazine (ammunition holder enabling multiple rounds of ammunition to be fed to a gun)</li><li>honey storehouse/storeroom</li><li>hopper, dispenser, stacker</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) warehouse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Magazin</i> or Dutch <i>magazijn</i>, from Middle French <i>magasin</i> (“warehouse, store”), from Italian <i>magazzino</i> (“storehouse”), from Arabic مَخَازِن (maḵāzin, “storerooms, storehouses”).</p><p>The informal stress variant <i>мага́зин</i> (magázin) is possibly via Polish <i>magazyn</i>.</p><p>(<i>ammunition clip or chamber</i>): Compare natively formed Czech <i>zásobník</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) store, shop</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) magazine (ammunition holder in a gun)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) beehive honey storeroom</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) hopper, dispenser</li></ol><p><i>магази́н</i> (magazín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>магази́н</i> (magazín): (small) shop/store</li></ol><p><i>магази́н</i> (magazín) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maharaja</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>महाराजा</i> (mahārājā), from Sanskrit <i>महाराज</i> (mahārāja), compound of <i>महा</i> (mahā, “great”) and <i>राजन्</i> (rājan, “king”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epulum, festive meal, particularly on the occasion of a contract conclusion, or colloquially in any context</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic مَخَارِج (maḵārij), plural of مَخْرَج (maḵraj), but the chain of derivation is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>alchemy</i>) magistery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master (holder of a master's degree)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Grand Master (the head of a medieval chivalric or religious order, and their title)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>magister</i>. Doublet of <i>ма́стер</i> (máster), <i>маэ́стро</i> (maéstro), <i>ми́стер</i> (míster), and <i>мэтр</i> (mɛtr). From the Latin source the second component of <i>гроссме́йстер</i> (grossméjster) is also derived.</p><p>Also note a coordinate Latin term <i>minister</i> (whence <i>мини́стр</i> (minístr)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main line, main route, main waterway (air, rail, nautical, automotive)</li><li>thoroughfare, highway</li><li>freeway</li><li>trunk, trunkline, main</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) highway, freeway, main line</li><li>main, arterial (road, way)</li></ol><p><i>магистра́ль</i> (magistrálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master's degree course, graduate programme</li><li>magistracy (the office, authority or collective body of magistrates)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) magistracy (in Ancient Rome)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magical</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́гия</i> (mágija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic; supernatural occurrences or feats</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) magma; magmatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnate, tycoon</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>magnās</i> (via German, or Polish (per Chernykh less likely)), from <i>magnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) magnetar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>magnetar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnetism</li><li>magnetics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>magnétisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnetite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnesium</li></ol><p>From English <i>magnium</i> (obsolete synonym of <i>magnesium</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>μαγνησία</i> (magnēsía), after <i>Μαγνησία</i> (Magnēsía, “Magnesia”), a region in Thessaly.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>магни́т</i> (magnít)</li></ol><p><i>магни́т</i> (magnít) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnetic</li></ol><p><i>магни́т</i> (magnít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio cassette recorder (combination radio and cassette recorder)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>магнитофо́н</i> (magnitofón, “tape recorder”) +&lrm; <i>радио́ла</i> (radióla, “radiogram (combination radio and record player)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnetosphere (region around a planet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tape recorder, cassette deck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Magnetophon</i>, the brand name of an early tape recorder.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) recorder, tape recorder</li></ol><p><i>магнитофо́н</i> (magnitofón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnitude</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>magnitūdō</i> (<i>magnus</i> + <i>-tūdō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnolia (tree of the genus <i>Magnolia</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Mohammedan</li></ol><p><i>Магоме́т</i> (Magomét) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madagascan (native or inhabitant of island&#58;Madagascar) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Мадагаска́р</i> (Madagaskár) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) madam (polite term of address to a woman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>madame</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madapolam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;French context&#32;or&#32;the Russian Empire</i>) mademoiselle, Miss (polite term of address to an unmarried woman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mademoiselle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madeira (a kind of fortified wine produced in the Madeira Islands)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1765. Borrowed from Portuguese <i>madeira</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magenta (pinkish-purple colour&#47;color)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>magenta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mahjong (game for four players)</li></ol><p>From Cantonese <i>麻將</i>&nbsp;/ <i>麻将</i> (maa4 zoeng3) (standard Mandarin: 麻將／麻将 (<i>májiàng</i>)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мадемуазе́ль</i> (madɛmuazɛ́lʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mademoiselle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madonna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>madonna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Madras</li></ol><p><i>Мадра́с</i> (Madrás) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madrigal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Madrid (the capital of Spain)</li></ol><p><i>Мадри́д</i> (Madríd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magyar, Hungarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>magyar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hungarian; Magyar</li></ol><p><i>мадья́р</i> (madʹjár) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anxiety, worry</li><li>annoying and troublesome task</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ма́йка</i> (májka): (small) wifebeater (type of undershirt)</li></ol><p><i>ма́йка</i> (májka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) major</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rich kid, nepo baby (a child of wealthy parents who uses their parents' wealth without being a productive person themselves)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>majoritarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick end of a billiards cue, mace</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>masse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) luck, chance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wattle-and-daub cottage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oil, to grease, to lubricate</li><li>to paint<i> (lips)</i>, to put make-up on</li><li>to smear, to spread, to anoint</li><li>to soil, to stain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to daub, to paint poorly</li><li>to miss <i>(the mark)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;màzati</i>. Related to <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo, “butter, oil”).</p><p>Unrelated with Russian <i>мазу́т</i> (mazút), which, however, is secondary reinforced by the term.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil oneself, to stain oneself, to get dirty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to <i>(put on)</i> make up</li><li>to apply some ointment, to rub oneself with some ointment</li></ol><p><i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Israel,&#32;colloquial) air conditioner, A/C, usually able to heat as well as cool (a heat pump)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>מַזְגָּן</i> (mazgán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mazdaism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dauber (unskillful painter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a sloppy person</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) inept, poor artist</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) someone who makes misses targets (in shooting, in games, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor painting, daub</li></ol><p><i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brushstroke, dab (<i>of paint</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) swab, smear</li></ol><p><i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masochism</li></ol><p>From German <i>Masochismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masochist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>masochistic</li></ol><p><i>мазохи́ст</i> (mazoxíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mazurka</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>mazurek</i>, with the ending influenced by <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mazut, fuel oil</li></ol><p>Via Turkic from Arabic مَهْزُولَات&nbsp;f&nbsp;pl (mahzūlāt, “refuse, waste”), feminine plural of مَهْزُول (mahzūl). Influenced by <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, salve, liniment</li><li>…, lubricant</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) raw opium</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mazь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maize, corn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>maíz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) maize; corn</li></ol><p><i>маи́с</i> (maís) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>May</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>маи</i> (mai) from Byzantine Greek <i>Μάϊος</i> (Máïos) from Latin <i>māius</i> from <i>Maia</i> (perhaps) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;magya</i> (“she who is great”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mégh₂s</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) public square, marketplace, maidan, meidan</li><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) public square in Ukraine; maidan</li><li>(by extension,&#32;politics) a revolution, a rebellion, similar to the Ukrainian maidan movement.</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language (compare Turkish <i>meydan</i> (“square”)), ultimately from Persian and Arabic مَيْدَان (maydān). The extended meanings are from Ukrainian <i>майда́н</i> (majdán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) who has supported or participated in the Euromaidan</li></ol><p>From <i>майдан</i> (majdan) +&lrm; <i>-у́тый</i> (-útyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;slang,&#32;offensive) a supporter of the Euromaidan protests and the Ukrainian government.</li></ol><p>From <i>Евромайда́н</i> (Jevromajdán, “the Euromaidan protests of 2014”) and <i>Даун</i> (Daun) ("a person with Down's syndrome, retard, stupid").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undershirt, tank top, vest, singlet, wife-beater</li><li>T-shirt</li><li>jersey</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Майка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Майя (Majja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maiko (apprentice geisha)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>舞妓</i> (まいこ, maiko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cryptocurrencies,&#32;neologism) mining (<i>in a blockchain system</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mining</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Minecraft</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Minecraft</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;gaming,&#32;neologism) Minecrafter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Minecrafter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ма́йнкрафт</i> (májnkraft).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mayonnaise (a dressing made from raw egg yolks and oil)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mayonnaise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mayonnaise</li></ol><p><i>майоне́з</i> (majonéz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) major</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>maior</i>. Doublet of <i>мэр</i> (mɛr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marjoram, <i>Origanum majorana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) primogeniture (an exclusive right of inheritance belonging to the eldest son)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) entailed estate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Majorat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) May</li></ol><p><i>май</i> (maj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maya person</li><li>Maya language</li><li>lightweight cotton cloth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Maya</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Майя)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Maya, from Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μαῖᾰ</i> (Maîă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) poppy</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) poppyseeds</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>макъ</i> (makŭ, “poppy; poppyseeds; poppyseed”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;makъ</i> (“poppy”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂kos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macaque (any monkey of the genus <i>Macaca</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ryazan</i>) hypocrite, someone who applies concealment</li><li>(Siberia,&#32;Voronezh) buffoon, inadvertent person</li><li>(<i>Nizhny Novgorod Oblast</i>) daydreamer, one who engages in remote thinking</li><li>(historical,&#32;Nizhny Novgorod Oblast,&#32;Moscow) co-tax-farmer</li><li>(historical,&#32;Ryazan,&#32;Nizhny Novgorod Oblast,&#32;Moscow) landlord, pubkeeper</li><li>(<i>Vladimir Governorate</i>) inveterate fisher</li><li>method, wise, fashion</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Makarov pistol</li></ol><p>From Persian مکار (makkâr), from Arabic مَكَّار (makkār), from the root م ك ر (m k r).</p><p>Clipping of <i>пистоле́т Мака́рова</i> (pistolét Makárova).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Макар)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μακάριος</i> (Makários), from <i>μακάριος</i> (makários, “blessed, happy, fortunate”). Doublet of <i>Мака́рий</i> (Makárij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macaron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of macaroni</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baked macaroni pudding</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) macaroni, spaghetti twirler</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) an Italian</li></ol><p><i>макаро́ны</i> (makaróny) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) macaroni</li></ol><p><i>макаро́ны</i> (makaróny) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasta maker (person who makes pasta)</li><li>pasta maker (machine which makes pasta)</li></ol><p><i>макаро́ны</i> (makaróny) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik). Compare Belarusian <i>макаро́ншчык</i> (makarónščyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macaroni</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>maccheroni</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasta with ground meat, <i>makarony po-flotski</i></li></ol><p>Literally, “navy-style pasta”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>макаро́ны</i> (makaróny)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip (to lower into a liquid)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;makati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makgeolli (makkoli), a traditional alcoholic beverage in Korea, made of rice.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>막걸리</i> (makgeolli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(youth,&#32;colloquial) abbreviation of <i>Макдо́налдс</i> (Makdónalds); (<i>Britain</i>) Maccy D's, (Australia, New Zealand) Macca's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedonian (person from Macedonia)</li></ol><p><i>Македо́ния</i> (Makedónija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Macedonian</li></ol><p><i>Македо́ния</i> (Makedónija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Македонский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Македонски</i>&nbsp;m (Makedonski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ancient Macedonian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>model, mockup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maquette</i>, from Italian <i>macchietta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) model, mockup</li></ol><p><i>маке́т</i> (makét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makizushi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>巻き寿司</i> (makizushi, literally “rolled sushi”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mackintosh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mackintosh</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maquillage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) makeup</li></ol><p><i>макия́ж</i> (makijáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) McCarthyism</li></ol><p>From <i>Макка́рти</i> (Makkárti) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm) or borrowing from English <i>McCarthyism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a haggler, one who procures small sales</li><li>alternative form of <i>мокло́к</i> (moklók)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Makler</i> or Middle Dutch <i>makelare</i>, substituting its ending with <i>-а́к</i> (-ák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Makler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мокло́к</i> (moklók)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip (to lower into a liquid)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>South Korean idol fandom</i>) maknae (youngest member of an idol group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>막내</i> (mangnae), with the spelling likely influenced by English <i>maknae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poppy fruit, poppyhead</li><li>poppy seed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;architecture) onion dome</li><li>top, summit</li><li>cupola (<i>of a church</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crown (<i>of the head</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ма́ковый</i> (mákovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macramé (a form of decorative textile made by knotting and weaving)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>macramé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mackerel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>makreel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>macro-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macroclimate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macrocosm (a complex structure containing numerous smaller-scale structures)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macrolepiota (<i>Macrolepiota</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Macrolepiota</i>. Surface analysis <i>макро-</i> (makro-, “macro-”) +&lrm; <i>лепио́та</i> (lepióta, “<i>Lepiota</i>”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>genetics</i>) macromutation (large-scale mutation)</li></ol><p><i>макро-</i> (makro-) +&lrm; <i>мута́ция</i> (mutácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macron (short, straight, horizontal diacritical mark placed over a vowel)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Макрон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the French surname <i>Macron</i></li></ol><p>From French <i>Macron</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macrostructure</li></ol><p>From <i>макро-</i> (makro-) +&lrm; <i>структу́ра</i> (struktúra). Compare Ukrainian <i>макрострукту́ра</i> (makrostruktúra), Belarusian <i>макраструкту́ра</i> (makrastruktúra), Polish <i>makrostruktura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macrotheory</li></ol><p><i>макро-</i> (makro-, “macro-”) +&lrm; <i>тео́рия</i> (teórija, “theory”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>macroeconomic</li></ol><p><i>макроэконо́м(ика)</i> (makroekonóm(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maxi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maxim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maximalism</li></ol><p>Either borrowed whole or derived from <i>максима́ль(ный)</i> (maksimálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at most, at the most; as much as possible; to the maximum</li></ol><p><i>максима́льный</i> (maksimálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maximal</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>maximum</i>, neuter of the adjective <i>maximus</i> (“greatest”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maximum</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at most</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>maximum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wastepaper (unwanted paper that has been discarded)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Makulatur</i>, from Latin <i>maculātūra</i>. Further from Latin <i>maculō</i>, from Latin <i>macula</i>, related to Ancient Greek <i>σμάω</i> (smáō) (whence <i>σμῆγμα</i> (smêgma) and <i>σμηκτικός</i> (smēktikós)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>макулату́рный</i> (makulatúrnyj)</li></ol><p><i>макулату́рный</i> (makulatúrnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wastepaper</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory,&#32;especially&#32;of literature) of low quality</li></ol><p><i>макулату́ра</i> (makulatúra, “waste paper”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown, pate (of the head)</li><li>top, summit, vertex</li></ol><p>From <i>мак</i> (mak, “poppy”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška); from the resemblance of the crown of the head to a poppy head (compare <i>ма́ковка</i> (mákovka, “poppy, poppy seed, onion dome, cupola, crown of the head, top, summit”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мак</i> (mak).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too small; smaller than necessary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Malawian</li></ol><p><i>Мала́ви</i> (Malávi) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malagasy (native or inhabitant of Madagascar) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Malagasy (related to Madagascar, the Malagasy people or their language)</li></ol><p>Probably from English <i>Malagasy</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malaysian (person from Malaysia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malaysian (person from Malaysia)</li></ol><p><i>Мала́йзия</i> (Malájzija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Malaysian (relating to Malaysia or the Malay language)</li></ol><p><i>Мала́йзия</i> (Malájzija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Malay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Malay</i>, from Malay <i>Melayu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malanga (<i>Xanthosoma</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) sperm, cum</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) the penis</li></ol><p>Blend of Greek <i>μαλακία</i> (malakía, “wanking”) +&lrm; <i>молоко́</i> (molokó, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fur cap with lappets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) malachite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) malachite</li></ol><p><i>малахи́т</i> (malaxít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>малахи́т</i> (malaxít).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malayalam (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;somewhat&#32;derogatory) to draw (pictures), to paint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>malować</i>, ultimately from Middle High German <i>mâlên</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>least, slightest: superlative degree of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(orthography,&#32;typography) minuscule (lower-case letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, small</li><li>short</li><li>trifling, petty</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a little, a bit, somewhat</li></ol><p><i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o), with stress shift</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boy, lad</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) finger</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc) (ending-stressed), or from <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malian (a person from Mali or of Malian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Malian (relating to Mali or the Malian people)</li></ol><p><i>Мали́</i> (Malí) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) raspberries (fruit)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) raspberry (plant)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;uncountable) delight, heaven (a pleasant, worry-free place or situation)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) thieves' hideout, den</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;malina</i>.</p><p>Adapted from Yiddish, from Hebrew <i>מָלוֹן</i> (malón, “hotel, inn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the European robin (<i>Erithacus rubecula</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>мали́новый</i> (malínovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) raspberry</li><li>crimson (<i>color</i>)</li><li>having a pleasant, soft timbre</li></ol><p><i>мали́на</i> (malína) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij, “small”); teeny, teeny weeny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>little, few</li><li>too little, too few</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>малъ</i> (malŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;malъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mal-</i>, <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironically,&#32;usually in a question or exclamation) not little, not few</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;used as a particle) who knows, you never know (expresses unclarity, uncertainty, can be used on its own)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reasonably [more or less] well, to some extent, to an acceptable extent</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ма́л(ый)</i> (mál(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ски</i> (-ski)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>little by little, by degrees, gradually</li></ol><p>From <i>мало</i> (malo) +&lrm; <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ма́лу</i> (málu), an old short dative singular of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bit by bit, little by little (small amount at a time)</li></ol><p>From <i>мало</i> (malo) +&lrm; <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ма́лу</i> (málu), an old short dative singular of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unimportant, of little importance, insignificant</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothing and shoes</i>) a little too small</li></ol><p><i>мало-</i> (malo-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́тый</i> (-vátyj). The full form <i>малова́тый</i> (malovátyj) is seldom used.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) not quite enough, insufficiently</li></ol><p><i>мало-</i> (malo-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́тый</i> (-vátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardly probable, not likely, unlikely</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вероя́тный</i> (verojátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small-sized, compact</li></ol><p><i>мал-</i> (mal-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>габари́т</i> (gabarít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subliteracy</li></ol><p>From <i>малогра́мотн(ый)</i> (malográmotn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semiliterate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) illiterate (badly versed in an area of knowledge)</li><li>completed without sufficient knowledge or without following the rules</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) pusillanimity; cowardice</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дух</i> (dux) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>faint-heartedly, apprehensively, in a cowardly fashion, in a cowardly manner</li></ol><p><i>малоду́шный</i> (malodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faint-hearted, cowardly, craven</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faint-hearted person, coward</li></ol><p><i>малодушие</i> (malodušije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the little</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj, “little, not much”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Khitan small script</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лое</i> (máloje) +&lrm; <i>кида́ньское</i> (kidánʹskoje) +&lrm; <i>письмо́</i> (pisʹmó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barely viable</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>маложиттєзда́тний</i> (maložyttjezdátnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconspicuous, imperceptible, barely noticeable, nearly invisible</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заме́тить</i> (zamétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) stealth bomber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfamiliar, not well-known, little-known</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown (person), unfamiliar person, little-known person</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>знако́мый</i> (znakómyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insignificant, of little importance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little-known</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little-studied, understudied, underinvestigated</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>изу́ченный</i> (izúčennyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>малови́вчений</i> (malovývčenyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, not affluent</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>иму́щий</i> (imúščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little-used</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>испо́льзуемый</i> (ispólʹzujemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anemia (medical condition with decreased oxygen transport)</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) young child</li></ol><p><i>малоле́т(ний)</i> (malolét(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>juvenile, underage, minor</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juvenile, minor</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(early) childhood, infancy</li></ol><p><i>малоле́т(ний)</i> (malolét(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) subcompact (an automobile with a small-displacement engine)</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>литра́ж</i> (litráž) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, low-capacity (<i>of a container</i>)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) small-displacement (<i>of an internal combustion engine</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>литра́ж</i> (litráž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) low-powered, low-power</li><li>weak, feeble (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>poor, unwealthy (<i>of a person, social class, etc.</i>)</li><li>having low throughput (<i>of a factory, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мощный</i> (moščnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>малопоту́жний</i> (malopotúžnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>needy, poor, poverty-stricken</li></ol><p><i>мал-</i> (mal-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>обеспе́ченный</i> (obespéčennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sedentary, not mobile</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) expressionless, lackluster</li></ol><p><i>ма́ло</i> (málo) +&lrm; <i>подви́жный</i> (podvížnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stunted, undersized</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ро́слый</i> (róslyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) Little Russia, Little Russian</li></ol><p><i>Малоро́ссия</i> (Maloróssija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;offensive) Ukrainian</li></ol><p><i>Малоро́ссия</i> (Maloróssija) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insipid, empty</li></ol><p>From <i>малый</i> (malyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>содержа́ть</i> (soderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lightly salted</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj, “little”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>соль</i> (solʹ, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smallness, trifle</li><li>tiny bit, a little bit; somewhat (also used as an adverb)</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seldom-used (used within a limited&#47;small circle or scope)</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>употреби́мый</i> (upotrebímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scanty, few, not numerous</li><li>small</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>число́</i> (čisló) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) labia minora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little</li><li>short</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow, guy, chap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>малъ</i> (malŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;malъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mal-</i>, <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i>, whence English <i>small</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesser spotted eagle</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj, “small, little”) +&lrm; <i>подо́рлик</i> (podórlik, “spotted eagle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little kid, baby, little boy</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) shorty, shrimp</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing</i>) little child</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;malyšьka</i>. Can also be analysed as <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>малы́ш</i> (malýš): kid, baby</li></ol><p><i>малы́ш</i> (malýš) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>malva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mallow</li></ol><p><i>ма́льва</i> (málʹva) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Malvales.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Malagasy (native or inhabitant of Madagascar) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) Malagasy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>мальга́ш</i> (malʹgáš) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Maldives; Dhivehi</li></ol><p><i>Мальди́вы</i> (Malʹdívy) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) malpighiaceous</li></ol><p><i>мальпи́гия</i> (malʹpígija, “malpighia”) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Malpighiales.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) malpighia (<i>Malpighia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Malpighia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maltese (inhabitant of Malta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Maltese</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maltese language (short for мальти́йский язы́к (malʹtíjskij jazýk))</li></ol><p><i>Ма́льта</i> (Málʹta) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maltose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy</li><li>lad</li></ol><p><i>мале́ц</i> (maléc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whipping boy; scapegoat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boyish, puerile</li></ol><p><i>мальчи́ш(ка)</i> (malʹčíš(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urchin, boy</li><li>greenhorn</li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) synonym of <i>парни́шка</i> (parníška, “lad, guy”)</li></ol><p><i>ма́льч(ик)</i> (málʹč(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bachelor party (boys' party, usually before the wedding)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little boy</li></ol><p>Apparently derived from <i>ма́льчик</i> (málʹčik), with an unclear suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tiny, wee</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ю́сенький</i> (-júsenʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>diminutive</i>) baby (very young human being)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) whitebait</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little one (a young child); shorty, shrimp (a short person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maľavъka</i> (compare with Belarusian <i>маляўка</i> (maljawka), Polish dialectal <i>malawka</i>, Ukrainian <i>малявка</i> (maljavka)), from <i>&#42;maljava</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ъka</i> whence Russian dialectal <i>маля́ва</i> (maljáva, “fry”) (also, compare with Czech <i>malavý</i>, Macedonian <i>малав</i> (malav), Serbo-Croatian <i>malava</i>), further from <i>&#42;malъ</i> (“small; little”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ava</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(house) painter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ма́ляр</i> (máljar) or <i>маля́р</i> (maljár). First used in 1665.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) malaria; malarial</li></ol><p><i>маляри́я</i> (maljaríja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anopheles (mosquito) (member of the Anopheles genus of mosquitos)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) malaria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small fry, fry, guppy, small and young fish; whitebait</li><li>cub</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;rare) little boy; young, inexperienced person</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) a little, slightly</li></ol><p><i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mama, mummy, mommy, mum, mom (<i>mother</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) motherboard</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) female socket</li></ol><p>A nursery word. From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mama</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mā́ˀmāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂-méh₂</i>, a reduplication of <i>&#42;meh₂-</i> - the root of <i>&#42;méh₂tēr</i> (“mother”). Cognate with German <i>Muhme</i> (“aunt”), Latin <i>mamma</i> (“mother, nurse”), Irish <i>mam</i> (“mother”), Lithuanian <i>mama</i>, <i>moma</i> (“mother”) among others. Cf. Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tata</i>, <i>&#42;baba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mamaliga</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romanian <i>mămăligă</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(francophilic,&#32;fancy,&#32;emotional) mother, maman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mother, mamma, mummy</li><li>familiar term of address for an (elderly) woman</li></ol><p>From <i>мама</i> (mama) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša), derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;màma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rural Kazakh person, perceived to be uneducated.</li><li>a Kazakh nationalist, especially one who dislikes Russian-speakers or Russia.</li><li>a Kazakh who loves their own language and culture.</li><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;by extension) a yokel or a bumpkin, regardless of ethnic origin</li><li>(Siberia,&#32;by extension) a rural Yakut migrant from the countryside who behaves in an aggressive manner</li><li>(Siberia,&#32;by extension) a young applicant from the Yakut countryside who enrolls in a university</li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>Мәмбет</i> (Mämbet), a popular name among Kazakh men, which is also used in Kazakh slang to refer to a country bumpkin or yokel; ultimately Arabic مُحَمَّد (muḥammad).</p><p>Sense 5 was most likely brought to Yakutia from soldiers returning from the Soviet Army.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) mama's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) mama's boy, momma's boy (male overly attached to his mother)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mom’s, mother’s</li></ol><p><i>ма́ма</i> (máma) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mother</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) wet nurse</li></ol><p><i>ма́ма</i> (máma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To utter <i>ма́ма</i> (máma, “mother”) repeatedly; to call for one's mother persistently.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang) ignorant and/or narrow-minded (<i>about a follower of certain ideology or philosophical belief</i>)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang) armchair (giving unqualified, uninformed advice or opinions)</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> мамка (mamka).</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́мка</i> (mámka) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in), literally "momma's".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) nipple cactus (<i>Mammillaria</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Mammillaria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) gut, belly (as a symbol of greediness)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мамона</i> (mamona, “monkey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mammoth</li></ol><p>Probably from a Uralic language, like Proto-Mansi <i>&#42;mē̮ŋ-ońt</i> (“earth-horn”) combining Proto-Uralic <i>&#42;mëxe</i> (“earth”) (compare Northern Mansi <i>ма̄</i> (mā), Finnish <i>maa</i>) with <i>&#42;aŋtɜ</i> (“horn”) (compare Northern Mansi <i>а̄ньт</i> (ānʹt, “horn”) – otherwise displaced in many branches by PU <i>&#42;śarwe</i> cf. Finnish <i>sarvi</i> and Hungarian <i>szarv</i>); or Khanty.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mummy/mommy</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) brothel supervisor</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Used to express surprise, fear or other emotions&#59; slightly humorous, on a similar level to holy mackerel, yikes, etc.</li></ol><p><i>ма́мка</i> (mámka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;literally) motherfucker (one who engages in incest with their mother)</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;figuratively) member of internet discussion (in general, a minor) who often offends other members; a troll</li></ol><p><i>мам</i> (mam) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;endearing) mother, mum</li></ol><p><i>ма́ма</i> (máma) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mana (a form of supernatural energy in Polynesian religion or magical energy in video games)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) hemp stock from milk (at least whole milk, often condensed milk, sometimes sour cream)</li></ol><p>Probably <i>молоко́</i> (molokó, “milk”) with slangy suffix as <i>тюря́га</i> (tjurjága).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) manat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>манат</i> (manat) or Turkmen <i>манат</i> (manat), both of which are from Russian <i>моне́та</i> (monéta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (one's) things, belongings, gear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>comics</i>) manga (comic originating in Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>漫画<rp>(</rp><rt>まんが</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>manga</i>), from Middle Chinese 漫 (<i>MC manH</i>, “free, unrestrained”) + 畫 (<i>MC hweaH|hweak</i>, “drawing”). Doublet of <i>ма́нхва</i> (mánxva) and <i>маньхуа́</i> (manʹxuá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A mangaka; a professional manga or anime artist.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>漫画家<rp>(</rp><rt>まんがか</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>mangaka</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brazier, mangal, barbecue, charcoal grill (a portable cooking device using burning charcoal as the source of heat&#59; or an electric version of such a device)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) antidrone slat armor, cope cage</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic مَنْقَل (manqal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mango</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mango</i>, from Portuguese <i>manga</i>, from Malay <i>mangga</i>, from Tamil <i>மாங்காய்</i> (māṅkāy), from <i>மா</i> (mā, “mango species”) + <i>காய்</i> (kāy, “unripe fruit”), or from another Dravidian source with the same basic elements.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mango</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мангуста́н</i> (mangustán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mongoose</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mangouste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мангу́ст</i> (mangúst)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mangosteen (<i>Garcinia mangostana</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pussy, cunt, twat</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown, however may be possibly derived from Old Polish <i>mądo</i> (“testicle”), which in its turn comes from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫdo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab louse (<i>Pthirus pubis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>манда́</i> (mandá, “cunt”) +&lrm; <i>вошь</i> (vošʹ, “louse”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Possibly related with Polish <i>mendoweszka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandarin (citrus fruit)</li><li>Mandarin, Putonghua, Guoyu (also: путунхуа, литературный китайский)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tangerine, mandarin</li></ol><p><i>мандари́н</i> (mandarín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandate, credentials, warrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandolin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mandolin</li></ol><p><i>мандоли́на</i> (mandolína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) Mandarin popular music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Mandopop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrill (Mandrillus sphinx)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandu (a dumpling in Korean cuisine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>만두</i> (mandu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maneuver (a movement, often one performed with difficulty)</li></ol><p>See манёвр (manjóvr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>манёвренность</i> (manjóvrennostʹ)</li></ol><p><i>мане́вренный</i> (manévrennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>манёвренный</i> (manjóvrennyj)</li></ol><p><i>мане́вр</i> (manévr) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maneuvering</li><li>maneuver</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) redistribution</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) management, use</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) shunting</li></ol><p><i>маневри́ровать</i> (manevrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manoeuvre, to maneuver</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to redistribute</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to manage, to use</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) to shunt</li></ol><p>From French <i>manœuvrer</i>, probably through German <i>manövrieren</i>. By surface analysis, <i>манёвр</i> (manjóvr) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) maneuver</li></ol><p><i>маневр</i> (manevr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manège, riding arena, ring</li><li>playpen (for children)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) riding(-)school, manège, riding-academy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manège</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manège, riding arena</li></ol><p><i>мане́ж</i> (manéž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mannequin, dummy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mannequin</i>, from Dutch <i>mannekijn</i>, <i>manneken</i> (“little man”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mannequin</li></ol><p><i>манеке́н</i> (manekén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) manner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Manier</i>, from French <i>manière</i>, from Latin <i>manus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;man-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manner (way of performing or effecting&#59; method or style)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) manners</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manière</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act/behave mannered (with excessive or forced mannerisms)</li></ol><p><i>мане́ра</i> (manéra, “manner”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mannerism, affectation, preciosity</li></ol><p><i>мане́рный</i> (manérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mannered, affected</li><li>frilly, overly decorated</li></ol><p><i>мане́ра</i> (manéra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuff, manchette; wristband</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(psychiatry,&#32;obsolete) manic-depressive</li></ol><p><i>маниака́льный</i> (maniakálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>депресси́вный</i> (depressívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maniacal, manic</li></ol><p>Adapted borrowing of French <i>maniacal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Ancient Greek <i>μανιακός</i> (maniakós) (whence <i>манья́к</i> (manʹják)) + Latin <i>-ālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manicure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manucure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manicure</li></ol><p><i>маникю́р</i> (manikjúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manioc, cassava</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manioc, cassava</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manipulation</li></ol><p><i>манипули́ровать</i> (manipulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manipulate, to control, to handle, to operate [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a machine, tool, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;psychology) to manipulate, to control psychologically [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a person, someone's will, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>manipulieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manipulator (person who manipulates others)</li><li>circus performer who performs sleight of hand tricks, prestidigitator</li><li>manipulator (mechanical device for performing complex operations)</li><li>(<i>telegraphy</i>) telegraph key (<i>for entering Morse code</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) external pointing device (<i>e.g. a mouse or trackball</i>)</li></ol><p><i>манипул(и́ровать)</i> (manipul(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-я́тор</i> (-játor), after French <i>manipulateur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manipulation</li></ol><p>From French <i>manipulation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beckon, to wave <i>(to)</i></li><li>to attract, to lure, to entice, to allure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maniti</i> (“to deceive”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂-</i> (“good, timely”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifesto</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manifesto</li></ol><p><i>манифе́ст</i> (manifést) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mania (violent derangement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semolina</li><li>semolina porridge (breakfast cereal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be absent, to miss</li><li>to neglect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manquer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person with a lost or degraded cultural and ethnic identity and/or awareness about his/her ancestry, especially because of being affected by a dominant culture</li></ol><p>Introduced by the Kyrgyz author Chinghiz Aitmatov in his 1980 novel <i>The Day Lasts More Than a Hundred Years</i>, and originally denoted a <i>"prisoner of war who was turned into a slave by having his heads wrapped in camel skin"</i>, which supposedly resulted in that <i>"...A mankurt did not recognise his name, family or tribe — a mankurt did not recognise himself as a human being."</i> <i>(For citations, possible etymology, and more, see :w:Mankurt.)</i></p><p>However, this term quickly caught on in the sense of "a person deprived of cultural and ethnic identity," and became popular in the languages of Central Asia and Eastern Europe. Compare Bashkir <i>маңҡорт</i> (mañqort).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) manna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) semolina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) wave of the hand</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) nod</li></ol><p>Constructed as if from <i>&#42;манова́ть</i> (&#42;manovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije); compare <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;manǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter's whistle, call</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bait</li></ol><p><i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attic, garret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mansarde</i>, from a surname of French architect François Mansart.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mansard</li></ol><p><i>манса́рда</i> (mansárda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mansi</li></ol><p><i>Ма́нси</i> (Mánsi, “the Mansi people”) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>манти́лья</i> (mantílʹja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantilla (<i>Spanish headdress</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak, mantle, robe, gown</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) mantle</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>мантиꙗ</i> (mantija), from Latin <i>mantum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manteau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>मन्त्र</i> (mantra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mántras</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>маньто́у</i> (manʹtóu)</li></ol><p>Exact etymology unknown. Ultimately a doublet of <i>маньто́у</i> (manʹtóu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manti, mantou</li></ol><p>Similar to other words across Eurasia for a type of dumpling, from an unknown source. Compare Turkish <i>mantı</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manul; Pallas' cat</li></ol><p>From Mongolian <i>мануул</i> (manuul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manuscript</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufactory</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) drapery, textiles</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>manufaktura</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manufactory</li></ol><p><i>мануфакту́ра</i> (manufaktúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>мануфакту́рой</i> (manufaktúroj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manhwa (Korean comic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>만화(漫畫)</i> (manhwa), from Japanese <ruby>漫<rp>(</rp><rt>まん</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby><ruby>画<rp>(</rp><rt>が</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>manga</i>). Doublet of <i>ма́нга</i> (mánga) and <i>маньхуа́</i> (manʹxuá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indigenous people)</i> Manhattan (Algonquian-Wakashan)</li><li><i>(cocktail)</i> manhattan <i>(whiskey, vermouth, and bitters)</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Манхэттен)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manhattan (an island and borough of New York City)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Manhattan</li></ol><p><i>Манхэ́ттен</i> (Manxɛ́tten) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantou</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 饅頭／馒头 (<i>mántou</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manhua (Chinese comic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>漫畫</i>&nbsp;/ <i>漫画</i> (mànhuà), from Japanese <ruby>漫<rp>(</rp><rt>まん</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby><ruby>画<rp>(</rp><rt>が</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>manga</i>). Doublet of <i>ма́нга</i> (mánga) and <i>ма́нхва</i> (mánxva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manchu (person)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Manchu <i>ᠮᠠᠨᠵᡠ</i> (manju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manjurology (study of the culture of Manchu people, including Manjuristics)</li></ol><p>From <i>маньчжу́р</i> (manʹčžúr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Manchu; Manchurian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manchu (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>маньчжу́р</i> (manʹčžúr) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maniac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maniaque</i>. Further from Ancient Greek <i>μανιακός</i> (maniakós), from Ancient Greek <i>μανία</i> (manía) (whence <i>ма́ния</i> (mánija)). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, to mind; spiritual activity”) (whence <i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мана́га</i> (manága)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alluring, inviting, beckoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maneuver (a movement, often one performed with difficulty)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>manœuvre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maneuverability</li><li>(<i>military</i>) mobility</li></ol><p><i>манёвренный</i> (manjóvrennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maneuverable</li><li>(<i>military</i>) mobile</li></ol><p><i>манёвр</i> (manjóvr) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maoism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Maoism</i>) Maoist</li></ol><p>From <i>Ма́о</i> (Máo) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Maoism</i>) Maoist</li></ol><p>From <i>маои́ст</i> (maoíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maori</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moutai</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 茅臺／茅台 (<i>Máotái</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>март</i> (mart) or <i>ма́рта</i> (márta, “March”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marasmus</li><li>senility</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) idiocy, asininity</li></ol><p>Internationalism ultimately derived from Late Latin <i>marasmus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μαρασμός</i> (marasmós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a senile dement</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a marasmatic (an obtuse person who performs and permits behaviours devoid of logic or reason)</li></ol><p>From <i>мара́зм</i> (marázm, “senility, idiocy”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, “-ic, -ist”), but on an extended stem <i>маразмат-</i> (marazmat-), as if derived from an Ancient Greek <i>&#42;μάρασμα, &#42;μαρασματ-</i> (&#42;márasma, &#42;marasmat-)&nbsp;– extant in Early Modern Greek <i>μάρασμα</i> (márasma)&nbsp;– whereas the true etymon is the Ancient Greek <i>μαρασμός, μαρασμ-</i> (marasmós, marasm-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion fruit (<i>plant</i>)</li><li>passion fruit (<i>fruit</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Altai wapiti (<i>Cervus elaphus sibiricus</i>, syn. <i>Cervus canadensis sibiricus</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>馬闌勒</i> (mǎránlè /⁠maral⁠/).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Marathi</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marathi (language of Maharashtra)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to stain, to dirty, to smut</li><li>to sully, to stain, to tarnish, to blemish</li><li>to daub, to scribble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;marati</i> (“to stain, to sully”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the outer appearance of orderliness, toilet – personal, domestic or other cleaning</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish معرفت (maʼrifet, “knowledge; connaissance, adroitness; skill, trick; method, means”), from Arabic مَعْرِفَة (maʕrifa, “knowledge”). The central phrase for the meaning development is <i>навести́</i> (navestí) / <i>наводи́ть</i> (navodítʹ) марафе́т, which was first applied by criminals to mean distancing oneself from the crime scene or putting forward an alibi, so as not to appear responsible. The sense "cocaine" comes from the effects of the drug when satisfying an addiction.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marathon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μαραθών</i> (Marathṓn, “Marathon, a town northeast of Athens”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>марафо́н</i> (marafón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manganese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Manganerz</i> (“manganese ore”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manganese</li></ol><p><i>ма́рганец</i> (márganec) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) solution of potassium permanganate, used as a disinfectant</li></ol><p><i>марганцо́вый</i> (margancóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manganese</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) potassium permanganate</li></ol><p><i>ма́рганец</i> (márganec) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>margarine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daisy (shrub, flower)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfit, nonconformist (person not conforming to recognized moral norms)</li><li>outcast</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>marginal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marginalize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marginal (inessential, on the margins of society, in the margins of a manuscript, etc.)</li><li>asocial</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>marginal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or from German <i>marginal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(heat) haze, mirage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madder (<i>Rubia</i> gen. et spp., especially <i>Rubia tinctorum</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;marěna</i>. However usually not written with ѣ (jě) pre-1918. Dialectally there has also been stress on the third syllable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) margin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>margin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mari person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Eastern Mari <i>мари</i> (mari, “Mari language term for the Mari”), a name of Indo-Iranian origin, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;máryas</i> (“human”, literally “mortal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Mary Jane, marijuana, cannabis</li></ol><p>From <i>Марива́нна</i> (Marivánna).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мариванна)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>Ма́рья Ива́новна</i> (Márʹja Ivánovna)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of Maria's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Mariinsky (name attached to buildings and places named in honor of various Russian empresses named "Maria")</li></ol><p><i>Мари́я</i> (Maríja) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mari</li></ol><p>Eastern Mari <i>мари</i> (mari) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) mariculture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marimba</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marinade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) marinade</li></ol><p><i>марина́д</i> (marinád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marinated, pickled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marinate, to pickle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to delay, to shelve, to put off, to keep waiting, to keep, to make languish</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>marin(er)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marionette (string puppet)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) dummy, puppet, stooge</li></ol><p>From French <i>marionnette</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marijuana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stamp (small, adhesive paper document)</li><li>brand, make</li><li>grade, quality (of a product)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) LSD, or tab of LSD</li><li>mark (mostly historical unit of currency in various countries)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) mark (half-pound unit of weight mostly used for gold and silver)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) march, mark (medieval administrative unit used for borderlands)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) chip or token used for payment in taverns or point-counting in games</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Marke</i>, from French <i>marque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>margrave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Markgraf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>margravial; relating to a margrave</li></ol><p>From <i>маркгра́ф</i> (markgráf) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marker, highlighter (writing instrument)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) marker (morpheme that marks the grammatical role of words, phrases or sentences)</li><li>(<i>genetics</i>) genetic marker (known specific DNA sequence)</li><li>(<i>paintball</i>) paintball marker, paintball gun</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) bullet</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) marker beacon</li></ol><p>From English <i>marker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marketing (promotion, distribution and selling of a product or service&#59; the work of a marketer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>marketing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) marketing</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́рке́тинг</i> (márkéting) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marketeer</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>маркетоло́гия</i> (marketológija). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>merx</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marquis, marquess (title of nobility)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>marquis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) sunblind, sun blind, awning, marquee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>markedness</li></ol><p>From <i>маркированный</i> (markirovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sutler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mercatante</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marxism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marxism-Leninism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marxist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Marxist (following Marxism)</li></ol><p><i>маркси́ст</i> (marksíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Marxist-Leninist</li></ol><p><i>маркси́стский</i> (marksístskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́нинский</i> (léninskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gauze (a fabric)</li></ol><p><i>ма́рля</i> (márlja) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gauze (thin fabric with open weave)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>marli</i> (“gauze, muslin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marmalade, fruit jelly</li><li>fruit jelly candy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>marmelade</i>, from Portuguese <i>marmelada</i>, from <i>marmelo</i> (“quince”), from Latin <i>melimēlum</i> (“sweet apple”), from Ancient Greek <i>μελίμηλον</i> (melímēlon), from <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) + <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мармела́д</i> (marmelád) fruit jelly candy</li></ol><p><i>мармела́д</i> (marmelád) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) marmalade, fruit jelly candy</li></ol><p><i>мармела́д</i> (marmelád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marauder (someone who moves about in roving fashion looking for plunder)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) profiteer</li><li>looter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maraudeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>looting, marauding, pillaging, plundering</li></ol><p>From <i>мародёр</i> (marodjór) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Compare Ukrainian <i>мароде́рство</i> (marodérstvo), Belarusian <i>марадзёрства</i> (maradzjórstva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moroccan (person from Morocco)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Moroccan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) top <i>(on sailing vessels, a platform around the head of a lower mast)</i></li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) crow's nest <i>(observation platform at the top of a ship’s mast)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>mars</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Марс)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Mars</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Mars</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) topsail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>marszeil</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Марсель)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marseille (the capital city of Bouches-du-Rhône department, France; the capital city of the region of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Marseille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Martian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Martian</li></ol><p><i>марсиа́н(ин)</i> (marsián(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>space science</i>) Mars rover</li></ol><p>From <i>Марс</i> (Mars, “Mars”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod, “motion”). Compare Ukrainian <i>марсохі́д</i> (marsoxíd). Perhaps the first appearance in Russian around 1959.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>March</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мартъ</i> (martŭ), Old Church Slavonic <i>мартъ</i> (martŭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>Μάρτιος</i> (Mártios), from Latin <i>mārtius</i>, from Latin <i>Mavors</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martini</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>martini</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyrology (list of martyred Christian saints)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyrology (list of martyred Christian saints)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) a nonexistant month&#59; Neveruary</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>март</i> (mart, “March”) +&lrm; <i>октябрь</i> (oktjabrʹ, “October”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) March</li></ol><p><i>март</i> (mart) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marmoset, monkey</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) black-headed gull</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marzipan, marchpane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Marzipan</i>, from Italian <i>marzapane</i> (misinterpreted as if from Latin <i>Marci</i> <i>panis</i> ‘Mark's bread’), now thought originally to come from Martaban, a Burmese port known for its spice exports.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>march</li><li>(<i>music</i>) march</li><li>flight of stairs</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>forward! (command)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>marche</i> (“march”) via Polish <i>marsz</i> or German <i>Marsch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) marshal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Marschall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshalcy, marshalship</li></ol><p><i>ма́ршал</i> (máršal) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to march</li></ol><p>From French <i>marcher</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) via German <i>marschieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshmallow (confectionery)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>marshmallow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>route, itinerary, path, course</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Marschroute</i>, from <i>Marsch</i> (“march”) + <i>Route</i> (“route”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) router</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transport) marshrutka, minibus, fixed route taxi, (small) shuttle bus</li></ol><p>Colloquial shortening of <i>маршру́тное такси́</i> (maršrútnoje taksí, “routed taxicab”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transport</i>) marshrutka, minibus, fixed route taxi</li></ol><p>From <i>маршру́т</i> (maršrút, “route”), referring to a planned route that something follows, and <i>такси́</i> (taksí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) route</li></ol><p><i>маршру́т</i> (maršrút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) A man with caring and respectful qualities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mask</li></ol><p>From Middle French <i>masque</i> (“a covering to hide or protect the face”), from Italian <i>maschera</i> (“mask, disguise”). Possible via German <i>Maske</i> or French <i>masque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masquerade, masked ball</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mascarpone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mascarpone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mask, to disguise</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to camouflage</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>maskieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) or from <i>ма́ска</i> (máska) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on a mask</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to camouflage oneself</li><li>to dress up (wear a costume) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘as someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>маскирова́ть</i> (maskirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masking, disguising, concealment</li><li>(<i>military</i>) camouflage</li><li>(<i>military</i>) misinformation, disinformation</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́ска</i> (máska, “mask”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>camouflage, masking</li></ol><p><i>маскировка</i> (maskirovka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maskne</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>masculine (having male qualities, not feminine or effeminate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Ма́сленица</i> (Máslenica); Maslenitsa, Shrovetide, Butter Week, Crepe Week (the week before Lent, seven weeks before Easter)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) feast, high life</li><li>(regional,&#32;obsolete) butter churn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maslěnica</i>, from <i>&#42;maslěnъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ica</i>, from <i>&#42;maslo</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Масленица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maslenitsa, Shrovetide, Butter Week, Crepe Week (the week before Lent, seven weeks before Easter)</li><li>Lady Maslenitsa (a straw effigy burned on the last day of the Maslenitsa week)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maslěnica</i>, from <i>&#42;maslěnъjь</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ica</i>, from <i>&#42;maslo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil, grease, butter; oily, buttery</li><li>unctuous (of a person)</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́сл(о)</i> (másl(o)) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive</li><li>olive tree</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bullet, cap</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo, “oil, butter”) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína); borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>маслина</i> (maslina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maslo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) olive</li></ol><p><i>масли́н(а)</i> (maslín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to butter</li><li>to oil</li></ol><p><i>ма́сло</i> (máslo, “butter, oil”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butter</li><li>oil <i>(vegetable or mineral)</i></li><li>(<i>painting</i>) oil</li></ol><p>Inherited Old East Slavic <i>масло</i> (maslo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maslo</i>. Related to <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ, “to smear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) tautology, pleonasm; much of a muchness</li></ol><p>Literally, “buttery butter”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil tank (a tank designed to hold engine oil)</li></ol><p><i>ма́сло</i> (máslo, “oil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бак</i> (bak, “tank”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butter churn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creamery, dairy</li></ol><p><i>ма́сло</i> (máslo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creamery, dairy</li></ol><p><i>ма́сло</i> (máslo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oily, greasy, oleaginous</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́сляный</i> (másljanyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) grease</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) butter</li><li>oily, buttery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maslěnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container for butter</li><li>oilcan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slippery jack mushroom, sticky bun mushroom (<i>Suillus luteus</i>), an edible mushroom with a mucous oily yellow-brown cap.</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;firearms) cartridge (<i>for a handgun or rifle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>масл(о)</i> (masl(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(free) mason, Freemason</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maçon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Masonic</li></ol><p><i>масо́н</i> (masón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freemasonry, masonry</li></ol><p><i>масо́н</i> (masón) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mask</li></ol><p><i>ма́сок</i> (mások) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass media (public communication that reaches a large audience)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mass media</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass</li><li>mass, paste</li><li>mass, bulk</li><li>ground (electrical)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>massa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>massage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>massage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to massage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masseur</li></ol><p><i>масса́ж</i> (massáž) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>massif, mountain mass</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) array (any of various data structures)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>massive (in various senses)</li><li>clumpy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>massif</i>, with <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) suffix. Compare Ukrainian <i>маси́вний</i> (masývnyj), Belarusian <i>масі́ўны</i> (masíwny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concentrated, massed</li><li>massive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to massage</li><li>(military,&#32;transitive) to mass, to concentrate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>massieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass culture, popular culture</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ма́ссовая культу́ра</i> (mássovaja kulʹtúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>масс-ме́диа</i> (mass-média)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) paste</li></ol><p><i>ма́сса</i> (mássa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person who organises mass entertainments</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass entertainer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass meeting, rally</li><li>(film,&#32;theater) crowd scene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>crime</i>) mass murder, mass killing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass character</li></ol><p><i>ма́ссовый</i> (mássovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mass, bulk</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mass; popular</li></ol><p><i>ма́сса</i> (mássa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) skilled person; craftsman, etc.</li></ol><p><i>ма́ст(ер)</i> (mást(er)) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) used to express negative attitude towards something</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) Windows</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>must</i> <i>die</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master, craftsman, expert</li><li>foreman</li><li>repairman</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) wizard (program or script used to simplify complex operations)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мастеръ</i> (masterŭ, “Master of the Order”), from Old French <i>maistre</i>, from Latin <i>magister</i> (“master, chief, head”). Doublet of <i>маги́стр</i> (magístr), <i>маэ́стро</i> (maéstro), <i>ми́стер</i> (míster), and <i>мэтр</i> (mɛtr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jack of all trades</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master class</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) artisan, craftsman, worker</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) workman, factory worker</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trowel</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop, shop, repair shop</li><li>atelier, artist’s studio</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>мастерска́я ко́мната</i> (masterskája kómnata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>masterfully</li></ol><p><i>мастерско́й</i> (masterskój) +&lrm; <i>-и́</i> (-í)</p><p><i>ма́стерский</i> (másterskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мастерско́й</i> (masterskój)</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>masterful, masterly</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastery, skill</li><li>trade, craft</li></ol><p><i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastic, resin, gum</li><li>putty, lute</li><li>floor polish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μαστίχη</i> (mastíkhē).</p><p>Secondary reinforced by originally unrelated Russian <i>масти́тый</i> (mastítyj), <i>масть</i> (mastʹ), <i>масти́ть</i> (mastítʹ), <i>-маща́ть</i> (-maščátʹ) (akin to <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ), <i>ма́сло</i> (máslo)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>venerable, mature, expert</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>маститъ</i> (mastitŭ, “anointed”).</p><p>Originally unrelated with Russian <i>масти́ка</i> (mastíka), which is borrowed from Ancient Greek.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastodon</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) giant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masturbation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>masturbātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to masturbate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>masturbor</i> + <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>color</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) suit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mastь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scale (ratio of distances)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) scope</li><li>caliber</li><li>repute</li><li>standard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Maßstab</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scale up, to scale (to change the scale of, e.g. a process, font, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>масшта́б</i> (masštáb) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scalability</li></ol><p><i>масштаби́руемый</i> (masštabírujemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immensity, state of being large-scale</li></ol><p><i>масшта́бный</i> (masštábnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) scale</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) large-scale</li></ol><p><i>масшта́б</i> (masštáb, “scale (<i>dimension</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) checkmate, mate (Russian notation: ×)</li><li>(<i>singular only</i>) mat, matte finish, frost <i>(glass)</i></li><li>mat, floor mat (woven floor covering, or soft floor pad in athletics)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foul language; language that includes obscene words deriving from a small set of roots for sex, sexual organs and sexuality</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mat</i>, short for <i>échec et mat</i>, from Arabic شَاهُ مَاتَ (šāhu māta), ultimately from Persian شاه مات (šâh mât, “the king is amazed”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>mat</i>, probably from Latin <i>mattus</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>mat</i> or Dutch <i>mat</i>.</p><p>Abbreviated from the expression <i>ма́терная брань</i> (máternaja branʹ), ultimately from <i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matador (person whose aim is to kill the bull in a bullfight)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>matador</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maté (plant, herb, beverage, but unlike in Spanish usually not the vessel from which the beverage is consumed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>mate</i>.</p><p>&nbsp;<b>мате</b> on Russian&nbsp;Wikipedia</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>matematyka</i> (“mathematics”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mathematical</li></ol><p><i>матема́т(ика)</i> (matemát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) matricide (killing of one's mother)</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́терь</i> (máterʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) matricide (murderer of own mother)</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́терь</i> (máterʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material (matter from which physical things are made or which is otherwise used to create such things)</li><li>material (something, as data or an idea, that may be reworked into a new form or may serve as the basis for interpretations)</li><li>fabric, material</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>māteriālis</i> (“material (adjective)”) via German <i>Material</i> or Dutch <i>materiaal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>materialism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;transitive) to materialize, to reify, to make concrete or tangible</li></ol><p>English <i>materialize</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to materialize, to be realized, to be made concrete or tangible</li></ol><p><i>материализова́ть</i> (materializovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>materialist</li></ol><p>From or cognate with French <i>matérialiste</i> and/or German <i>Materialist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>materialistic</li></ol><p><i>материали́ст</i> (materialíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>materially, financially</li></ol><p><i>материа́льный</i> (materiálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>material and technical</li></ol><p><i>материа́льный</i> (materiálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>material</li><li>pecuniary, financial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continent</li><li>mainland</li><li>native soil</li><li>subsoil</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) matrix, gangue</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) parent tissue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continental</li></ol><p><i>матери́к</i> (materík) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mother's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) motherboard (primary circuit board of a computer)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>motherboard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mother's, motherly, maternal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maternal instinct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motherhood, maternity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;materinьstvo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dirty mouth, to badmouth, to abuse, to cuss out (to insult, to swear dirtily (at))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swear, to use filthy language (<i>using obscenities derived from sexual terms</i>)</li></ol><p><i>матери́ть</i> (materítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substance, matter</li><li>fabric, material, cloth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>materia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) obscene, bad, dirty, foul</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́тер-</i> (máter-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), based on the oblique stem of <i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”); compare <i>мат</i> (mat, “obscene language”), derived from the same word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cloth, fabric</li></ol><p><i>мате́рия</i> (matérija) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foul language, filthy language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person who uses vulgar or offensive language too much; who uses <i>мат</i> (mat) too much</li></ol><p><i>матерщи́на</i> (materščína) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;formal</i>) mother</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>материа́л</i> (materiál).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мате́рия</i> (matérija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maths, math (school subject)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>матема́тика</i> (matemátika) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete form of <i>мать</i> (matʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frost, to delustre, to depolish, to dull, to mat, to take the gloss off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;rare) mother</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) stud female (<i>in some domesticated animals</i>), queen bee</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) uterus, womb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;matъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matte, dull, lustreless</li><li>opaque, frosted</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) checkmate</li></ol><p><i>мат</i> (mat, “matte”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p><p><i>мат</i> (mat, “checkmate”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) uterus, womb; uterine</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) queen bee</li></ol><p><i>ма́тка</i> (mátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mattress (a pad on which a person can recline and sleep)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) the Stars and Stripes (the flag of the United States of America)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Matratze</i>, Middle High German <i>materaz</i>, from Old French <i>materas</i>, from Arabic مَطْرَح (maṭraḥ).</p><p>The second sense was from Soviet times, when people thought that the American flag looked like a mattress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mattress (a pad on which a person can recline and sleep)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Matratze</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matriarchy</li></ol><p><i>матриарх</i> (matriarx) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) extracellular matrix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matrimonial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matrix</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Matrize</i>, from French <i>matrice</i>, from Latin <i>mātrīx</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>ма́триця</i> (mátrycja), Belarusian <i>ма́трыца</i> (mátryca), Polish <i>matryca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) matrona (a wife of an honorable man in Ancient Rome)</li><li>matron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mātrōna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor, seaman (a common member of the crew of a vessel&#59; an enlisted man in the Navy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>matroos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) sailors</li></ol><p><i>матро́с</i> (matrós) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sailor; sailor's</li></ol><p><i>матро́с</i> (matrós, “sailor”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matryoshka, Russian doll</li><li>(<i>low&#32;colloquial</i>) a dolled up woman (usually a hijabi)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) synonym of <i>душица</i> (dušica, “oregano”)</li></ol><p>Diminutive form of female name <i>Матрёна</i> (Matrjóna), ultimately from Latin <i>mātrōna</i> (“matron”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother, mum (<i>parent</i>)</li><li>mother (<i>term of address</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мать</i> (matʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) match</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>match</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) equipment, materiel</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) knowledge, training, instructions, or manuals for operating military equipment</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>материа́льная часть</i> (materiálʹnaja častʹ, “material part”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matronymic</li></ol><p><i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мати</i> (mati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mā́ˀtē</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂tēr</i>, whence English <i>mother</i> and Sanskrit <i>मातृ</i> (mātṛ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>single mother (a woman raising a child on her own)</li></ol><p><i>мать</i> (matʹ) +&lrm; <i>одино́чка</i> (odinóčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mouth, when the person uses vulgar or offensive language; dirty mouth; specifically refers to the word <i><i>мат</i> (mat)</i> (offensive language)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) megaphone</li></ol><p>From <i>матюга́ть</i> (matjugátʹ, “to say obscenities”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) swear word</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swear, to curse, to use foul language, to cuss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inveterate, experienced, mature, out-and-out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;matorъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;meh₂-</i> (“to ripen, to mature”).</p><p>Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>матор</i> and Latin <i>maturus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mauser (firearm)</li></ol><p>Named after the original designers Wilhelm and Paul Mauser.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mafioso, mafiosi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mafiosi</i>, plural of <i>mafioso</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mafiosi, mafioso (member of the mafia)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the mafia</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) an unlucky entrepreneur</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>mafiosi</i> +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mafia</li><li>Mafia (Soviet and Russian party game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mafia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muffin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>muffin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>single rapid movement in the air, swing, wave, flap, stroke</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) rapid forward, backward or sideways movement of the foot or hand</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) single revolution, swing, stroke</li><li>step (<i>of an animal, usually a horse, when running</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maxъ</i>. Deverbal from <i>маха́ть</i> (maxátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave, to flap, to fling, to brandish [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;māxàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something big, a bulky and cumbersome thing</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>маши́на</i> (mašína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machination, shenanigans, gerrymander, fraud, contrivance, scheme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anarchism,&#32;communism) The theory and strategy of Makhno; Makhnovism, Makhnovshchina</li><li>(<i>history</i>) The Black Army and its allied military groups in Ukraine, Southern Russia and Kalmykia</li><li>(<i>history</i>) The Free Territory and the associated areas</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;figurative) chaos, mayhem</li></ol><p><i>Махно</i> (Maxno) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina). Literally, “the thing relating to Makhno”: Makhnovshchina.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave, to flap, to fling, to brandish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to leap, to jump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go (off), to travel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;maxnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>маха́ть</i> (maxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>махну́ти</i> (maxnúty), Belarusian <i>махну́ць</i> (maxnúcʹ). Polish <i>machnąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mechanics,&#32;also&#32;figurative) flywheel</li><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) handwheel</li></ol><p><i>махово́й</i> (maxovój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swing, wave, flap, stroke; swinging, waving, flapping</li><li>executed in the form of a swing, wave, flap, etc.</li></ol><p><i>мах</i> (max) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>махово́й</i> (maxovój)</li></ol><p><i>мах</i> (max) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) tiny</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) little (in age), young</li></ol><p>Derived form of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A coarse, strong type of tobacco, makhorka (<i>Nicotiana rustica</i>)</li></ol><p>Modification of <i>&#42;амерфо́ртский</i> (&#42;amerfórtskij, “Amersfoort (<i>relational</i>)”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from the Dutch city of Amersfoort, known for its tobacco industries.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) makhorka (a strong type of tobacco)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) specially made thread loops on fabric or knitwear</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) terrycloth</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>махо́рка</i> (maxórka).</p><p>Originally <i>мохра́</i> (moxrá), from <i>мох</i> (mox); respelled according to pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) terry cloth</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) double</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inveterate, double-dyed</li></ol><p><i>махра́</i> (maxrá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matzo, matzoh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>מַצָּה</i> (matsá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to touch, to caress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matsoni, matzoon (a yoghurt-like dairy product made in Armenia and Georgia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>მაწონი</i> (mac̣oni), from Armenian <i>մածուն</i> (macun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepmother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;màťexa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machismo (exaggerated masculinity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>machismo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macho</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>macho</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mast</li><li><i>(radio, electric)</i> tower</li></ol><p>From older <i>ма́шта</i> (mášta), from Dutch <i>mast</i> or Low German <i>mast</i>. Compare Polish <i>maszt&#47;maszta</i>. For the change <i>-шт-</i> (-št-) → <i>-чт-</i> (-čt-) compare <i>по́чта</i> (póčta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mung bean (<i>Vigna radiata</i>)</li></ol><p>1920s, from Kazakh <i>мәш</i> (mäş), from Persian ماش (mâš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) car, motor vehicle</li><li>machine</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) computer</li><li>(physics,&#32;colloquial) accelerator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Maschine</i> (“machine, engine”), from French <i>machine</i>, from Latin <i>māchina</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μᾱχᾰνᾱ́</i> (mākhănā́, “machine, tool”). Doublet of <i>махи́на</i> (maxína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mechanically</li><li>automatically, subconsciously</li><li>absentmindedly, without thought</li></ol><p><i>машина́льный</i> (mašinálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>automatic (acting without conscious thought)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machinist, engine driver; engineman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Maschinist</i> or French <i>machiniste</i>. First attested in 1721. By surface analysis, <i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist). Compare Ukrainian <i>машині́ст</i> (mašyníst), Belarusian <i>машыні́ст</i> (mašyníst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female typist</li></ol><p><i>машини́ст</i> (mašiníst) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>маши́на</i> (mašína): small car, machine or mechanism</li><li>miniature or toy car</li><li>small device for accomplishing work (e.g. typewriter, washing machine, clipper, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) typewriter</li></ol><p><i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>machine-readable</li></ol><p><i>маши́нный</i> (mašínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>чита́емый</i> (čitájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>artificial intelligence</i>) machine learning (a field of study concerned with the design and development of algorithms and techniques that allow computers to learn)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) machine</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) computer</li></ol><p><i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>translation studies</i>) machine translation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typewritten</li></ol><p><i>маши́нопись</i> (mašínopisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typewriting</li><li>typescript</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanical engineering</li><li>engineering, the work of an engineer</li></ol><p>From <i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строе́ние</i> (strojénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machinist, machine builder</li><li>mechanical engineer</li></ol><p><i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строи́тель</i> (stroítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engineering</li><li>machine-building</li></ol><p><i>маши́на</i> (mašína) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строи́тельный</i> (stroítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maestro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>maestro</i>. Further from Latin <i>magister</i>. Doublet of <i>маги́стр</i> (magístr), <i>ма́стер</i> (máster), <i>ми́стер</i> (míster), and <i>мэтр</i> (mɛtr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighthouse</li><li>beacon</li><li>Used as the call sign for some Soyuz missions: Soyuz T-3, Soyuz T-10 & Soyuz T-15.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;majakъ</i> (“lighthouse”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;màjati</i> (“to beckon”) + <i>&#42;-akъ</i> (“masculine nominalizing suffix”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Маяк)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mayak, the name of government radio broadcasting company in Russia</li><li>Mayak, the name of a nuclear waste reprocessing plant in Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pendulum</li></ol><p>From <i>ма́ять</i> (májatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pendulumlike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, to harass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;majati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to suffer, to languish</li></ol><p><i>ма́ять</i> (májatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loom, to appear indistinctly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang around, to linger</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be in the offing, to loom ahead</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>мая́к</i> (maják, “lighthouse”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(GPS-)tracker, a device to locate an item</li><li>emergency vehicle lighting, flashing light (emergency vehicle lighting)</li><li>diminutive of <i>мая́к</i> (maják), beacon</li></ol><p>From <i>мая́к</i> (maják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мо́жет быть</i> (móžet bytʹ, “may be, maybe, perhaps”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мб)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>мегаба́йт</i> (megabájt): MB (megabyte)</li></ol></html>:/m̩ˈhm̩/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>M, mega- (x 1,000,000)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mg, milligram</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Ukraine) alternative form of <i>угу́</i> (uhú, ugú); mhm (yes)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>мега-</i> (mega-).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>миллигра́мм</i> (milligrámm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) mist, haze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьгла</i> (mĭgla), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьgla</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;míglāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃migʰleh₂</i> (“mist, cloud”). Compare Polish <i>mgła</i> (“fog”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foggy, misty</li></ol><p><i>мгла</i> (mgla) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moment, instant, twinkling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мьгновениѥ</i> (mĭgnovenije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьgnovenьje</i>, from <i>&#42;mьgnǫti</i> (“to blink”), from <i>&#42;migati</i> (“to blink”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) blink of an eye, snap, jiffy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>momentarily, instantaneously</li></ol><p><i>мгнове́нный</i> (mgnovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>momentary, instantaneous</li></ol><p><i>мгнове́ние</i> (mgnovénije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мгнове́ние</i> (mgnovénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) oh well; too bad (an expression of mild disappointment or resignation)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>м-да́</i> (m-dá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) furniture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>meuble</i> via Polish <i>mebel</i> or German <i>Möbel</i>. Further from Latin <i>mōbilis</i>, from Latin <i>moveō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) furniture</li></ol><p><i>ме́бель</i> (mébelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>mega-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μέγας</i> (mégas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) megabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>мега-</i> (mega-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) megabit</li></ol><p>From <i>мега-</i> (mega-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megawatt</li></ol><p><i>мега-</i> (mega-) +&lrm; <i>ватт</i> (vatt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megajoule</li></ol><p><i>мега-</i> (mega-) +&lrm; <i>джоуль</i> (džoulʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megalith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) megalography</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) macrography</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μεγαλογραφία</i> (megalographía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megalopolis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “great, large, mighty”) + <i>πόλις</i> (pólis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megaton</li></ol><p><i>мега-</i> (mega-) +&lrm; <i>тонна</i> (tonna)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megaphone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oceanography</i>) megatsunami (an extremely large tsunami-like wave caused by an avalanche or landslide displacing large amounts of water very quickly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megaerg (unit of energy equal to one million ergs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) shrew, termagant, scold, virago, vixen, bitch (a malicious, quarrelsome woman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mégère</i> (or German <i>Megaere</i>), from Latin <i>Megaera</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μέγαιρα</i> (Mégaira, “Megaera”), one of the Erinyes in Greek mythology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>медици́на</i> (medicína, “medicine”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medallist, medal winner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medal</li></ol><p>From Middle French <i>medaille</i>, <i>medale</i>, from Italian <i>medaglia</i>, from Late Latin <i>medalia</i> (“half a denarius”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locket, medallion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) male nurse</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>медици́нский брат</i> (medicínskij brat, “medical brother”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) Ursa Major or Ursa Minor (see Большая Медведица, Малая Медведица)</li><li>tiger moth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole cricket</li><li>Medvedka (Russian anti-submarine missile)</li></ol><p><i>медве́д(ь)</i> (medvéd(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear</li><li>male bear</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) bear (<i>of any bear-like animal</i>)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) bear (<i>on a stock market</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) large and clumsy person</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) safe (see also <i>медвежа́тник</i> (medvežátnik))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>медвѣдь</i> (medvědĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;medvědь</i>. Literally, “one that eats mead/honey”. Compare Russian <i>медое́д</i> (medojéd), composed of the same roots. Also compare Sanskrit <i>मध्वद्</i> (madhvad, “honey- or mead-eating”).</p><p>Compare typologically Bulgarian <i>лакомец</i> (lakomec, “wolverine”), Macedonian <i>лакомец</i> (lakomec, “glutton, wolverine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear meat (as food)</li></ol><p>From <i>медвежа́т-</i> (medvežát-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>медвежо́нок</i> (medvežónok, “young bear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear hunter</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) bear trainer (an entertainer who exhibits a trained bear)</li><li>a breed of dog used for bear hunting</li><li>bear enclosure; bear cage</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) broad double-edged knife</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) safecracker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dog used for bear hunting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bear; bear’s, ursine</li></ol><p><i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear cub; young bear</li></ol><p><i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear's paw</li><li>acanthus (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) disservice, more harm than good (an ill turn or injury)</li></ol><p>From <i>медве́жий</i> (medvéžij, “bear's”) + <i>услу́га</i> (uslúga, “service”) - "bear's service".</p><p>According to Max Vasmer, possibly after Jean de La Fontaine's fable about a bear who broke his owner's head trying to kill a fly, in Russia spread thanks to Ivan Krylov's fable "<i>The Hermit and the Bear</i>" (Пусты́нник и медве́дь). The moral being "an obliging fool is more dangerous than an enemy." Compare German <i>Bärendienst</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) having the smell and/or taste of honey or prepared with honey</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) honey-colored</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) having a pleasant sound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>медве́дка</i> (medvédka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parliament in some Islamic countries or regions, majlis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish مجلس (meclis), from Arabic مَجْلِس (majlis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>media (means and institutions for publishing and broadcasting information)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>media</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) medial, mesial</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>mediālis</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mediāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) median</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;computing) media player</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>media player</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) plectrum ((music) small piece of plastic, metal, ivory, etc for plucking the strings)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) neurotransmitter, neuromediator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) multimedia file</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>media file</i>. By surface analysis, <i>медиа-</i> (media-) +&lrm; <i>файл</i> (fajl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medievalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) media, mass media</li></ol><p><i>ме́диа</i> (média) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>medicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) medicine, medication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical college, medical institute</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>медици́нский институ́т</i> (medicínskij institút, “medical institute”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meditation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine (field of study and profession)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>medycyna</i> (“medicine”), from Latin <i>medicina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medical, medicinal</li></ol><p><i>медици́на</i> (medicína) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical condition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ме́дленный</i> (médlennyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ме́дленно</i> (médlenno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slowly</li></ol><p><i>ме́дленный</i> (médlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowness</li></ol><p><i>ме́дленный</i> (médlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъdьlьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ме́длить</i> (médlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sluggishness, slowness, lethargy, inactivity</li></ol><p><i>медли́тельный</i> (medlítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sluggish, slow, tardy, slothful, inactive</li></ol><p><i>ме́длить</i> (médlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delay, to linger, to procrastinate, to hesitate, to tarry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъdьliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brazier, coppersmith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>copper-red</li></ol><p><i>ме́дный</i> (médnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) copper; cupric, cuprous, cupreous</li><li>copper colored, reddish-yellow</li></ol><p><i>медь</i> (medʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) dunderhead</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>front d'airain</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>brazen-faced</i>, <i>brass</i>, <i>bronze</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nectary</li><li>medovik (a layer cake popular in countries of the former Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>мёд</i> (mjod) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mead</li></ol><p><i>медо́в(ый)</i> (medóv(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) honey</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mead</li><li>honey yellow, amber, old gold (<i>color</i>)</li><li>honeyed</li></ol><p><i>мёд</i> (mjod) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeymoon</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>lune de miel</i>, itself a calque of English <i>honeymoon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey badger (<i>Mellivora capensis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>мёд</i> (mjod, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”). Compare <i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ, “bear”), composed of the same elements but going back to Proto-Slavic; and compare with Ukrainian <i>медоїд</i> (medojid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>мёд</i> (mjod, “honey”)</li></ol><p><i>мёд</i> (mjod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) melliferous plant</li></ol><p>From <i>мёд</i> (mjod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-нос</i> (-nos), combining form of <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to carry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeybee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>melliferous, nectariferous</li></ol><p><i>мёд</i> (mjod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-носи́ть</i> (-nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination (inspection by a doctor)</li></ol><p>Short for медици́нский (medicínskij) осмо́тр (osmótr) (medicínskij osmótr) "medical examination"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) honeyeater (Meliphagidae sp.)</li></ol><p>From <i>мёд</i> (mjod, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ, “to suck”, verb), literally “honeysucker”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) honeyguide</li></ol><p>From <i>медо́вый</i> (medóvyj, “honey”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>ука́зка</i> (ukázka, “orders, instructions”) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik, diminutive suffix). Compare English <i>honeyguide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical staff</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>медици́нский персона́л</i> (medicínskij personál, “medical staff”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first-aid post, aid station; infirmary</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>медици́нский пункт</i> (medicínskij punkt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) madrasa (an Islamic school)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مَدْرَسَة&nbsp;f (madrasa, “school”). The pronunciation of this word in many spoken varieties of Arabic has [æ] for underlying /a/, which explains the use of Russian е.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) (female) hospital nurse</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>медици́нская сестра́</i> (medicínskaja sestrá, “medical sister”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medusa, jellyfish</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Медуза)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Medusa</li><li>a female given name, Meduza, equivalent to English Medusa</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μέδουσα</i> (Médousa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣдь</i> (mědĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mědь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper (a copper coin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smooth snake</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;inanimate) verdigris</li></ol><p><i>медь</i> (medʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary&#32;formal</i>) between, among</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;межу</i> (&#42;mežu), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;meďu</i>. Doublet of <i>ме́жду</i> (méždu), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the meantime, meanwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>inter- (among, between)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abutment, abuttal, boundary, margin, division (as in land plots)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;meďa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;medjāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;medʰyeh₂</i>. Ultimately from the feminine form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i>.</p><p>Cognate with English <i>middle</i> and <i>mid</i>, Latin <i>medius</i>, Sanskrit <i>मध्य</i> (mádhya) and Ancient Greek <i>μέσος</i> (mésos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interbudgetary</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>бюдже́т</i> (bjudžét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inter-agency, interdepartmental</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домство</i> (védomstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intergalactic</li></ol><p><i>меж</i> (mež) + <i>галакти́ческий</i> (galaktíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intergovernmental, interstate</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рство</i> (gosudárstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) interjection</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́жду</i> (méždu, “between”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of Latin <i>interiectiō</i>.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>przymiotnik</i> (&lt; Latin <i>adiectīvum</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) interjection; interjectional (being or pertaining to an interjection)</li></ol><p><i>междоме́тие</i> (meždométije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>междоме́тие</i> (meždométije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestine war; feud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestine war; feud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between, among</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>междоу</i> (meždu). Compare inherited East Slavic <i>меж</i> (mež).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>between a rock and a hard place</li></ol><p>Literally, “between two fires”. Compare French <i>être pris entre deux feux</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>between a rock and a hard place</li></ol><p>Literally, “between a hammer and an anvil”. Compare French <i>entre le marteau et l'enclume</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) by the way, incidentally</li><li>among other things</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the meantime, meanwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>while</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>inter- (among, between)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>междугоро́дный</i> (meždugoródnyj)</li></ol><p><i>между-</i> (meždu-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intercity</li></ol><p><i>между-</i> (meždu-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the space between buildings</li></ol><p><i>между-</i> (meždu-) +&lrm; <i>дом</i> (dom) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>междудо́мие</i> (meždudómije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>between princes, dukes</li><li>inter-dukedom</li></ol><p><i>между-</i> (meždu-, “between”) +&lrm; <i>князь</i> (knjazʹ, “prince”) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an expert on international matters, such as a publicist, political writer</li></ol><p><i>междунаро́дный</i> (meždunaródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>international</li></ol><p>From <i>между-</i> (meždu-, “between”) +&lrm; <i>наро́д</i> (naród, “people; nation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) International Phonetic Alphabet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interfluve (the land between two rivers)</li></ol><p><i>между-</i> (meždu-) +&lrm; <i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Междуречье)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Mesopotamia (a geographic region in southwest Asia, spanning from the rivers Euphrates and Tigris, located in modern Iraq, eastern Syria, and southeast Turkey; the site of one of the most ancient civilizations in the history of man)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Mesopotamia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing from 116 to 117 AD and again from 198 to 637 AD)</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>Μεσοποταμία</i> (Mesopotamía), from <i>ме́жду</i> (méždu) +&lrm; <i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>междоусо́бица</i> (meždousóbica)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survey, to delineate</li></ol><p><i>межа́</i> (mežá) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boundary</li></ol><p><i>меж(а́)</i> (mež(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interstellar</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) intercellular</li></ol><p><i>меж</i> (mež) +&lrm; <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intercontinental</li></ol><p>From <i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>контине́нт</i> (kontinént) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>міжконтинента́льний</i> (mižkontynentálʹnyj), Belarusian <i>міжкантынента́льны</i> (mižkantynjentálʹny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) interdenominational</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>конфессиона́льный</i> (konfessionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interpersonal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>international</li><li>interethnic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interindustry</li><li>intersectoral, interbranch</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>о́трасль</i> (ótraslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) input-output table, input-output model</li></ol><p>Literally, “intersectoral balance”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interparliamentary</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>парла́мент</i> (parláment) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interparty</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>парти́йный</i> (partíjnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interplanetary</li></ol><p>From <i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>плане́тный</i> (planétnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intergovernmental</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>прави́тельство</i> (pravítelʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interracial</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>ра́са</i> (rása) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interregional</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>региона́льный</i> (regionálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>off-season</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>сезо́н</i> (sezón) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intermaxillary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anal cleft (groove between buttocks)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interethnic</li></ol><p><i>меж-</i> (mež-) +&lrm; <i>этни́ческий</i> (etníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable pulp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mězga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inner side of hide</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męzdra</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗms</i> (“flesh, meat”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>міздря́</i> (mizdrjá), Belarusian <i>мяздра́</i> (mjazdrá), Polish <i>miazdra</i> and Czech <i>mázdra</i> as well as Latin <i>membrum</i>. Related to <i>мя́со</i> (mjáso, “meat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mesozoic era</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>particle physics</i>) meson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mezzanine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mesopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) mesoscopic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, English <i>mesoscopic</i> +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mesosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) mezuzah</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>מְזוּזָה</i> (m'zuzá), via Yiddish <i>מזוזה</i> (mezuze).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>makeup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>makeup</i> in the 1990s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>email (system, message)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mail</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Maine Coon, a breed of cat.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Maine Coon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;uncountable</i>) mainstream (that which is common&#59; the norm)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mainstream</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meitnerium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mexican</li></ol><p><i>мексика́н(ский)</i> (meksikán(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Any jay of the genus <i>Cyanolyca</i>, found in the New World.</li></ol><p><i>мексика́нский</i> (meksikánskij, “Mexican”) +&lrm; <i>со́йка</i> (sójka, “jay”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mexican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalk</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) Cretaceous</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mělъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melancholy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land reclamation/improvement/melioration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) balayage, flamboyage, hair highlighting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) to make (hair) ombré</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>melieren</i> (“to mix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small-bore gun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fine, fine-grained</li><li>small, tiny, little, minute</li><li>small, insignificant, petty, minor</li><li>shallow</li><li>petty, small-minded</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little one</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣлъкъ</i> (mělŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mělъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shallowly (in a shallow manner, not deep)</li><li>finely, in small particles, (written) in very small letters (of texts)</li></ol><p><i>ме́лкий</i> (mélkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shallow water</li><li>period of shallow water</li><li>shallows (<i>of a river</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ме́лкий</i> (mélkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small-bore, small-calibre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) small fry (small fish)</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) small fry, small potatoes (insignificant people)</li></ol><p><i>ме́лк(ий)</i> (mélk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triviality of subject matter, unwillingness to tackle important questions (<i>of a conversation, a piece of writing, a work of art, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ме́лкий</i> (mélkij, “minor”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>те́ма</i> (téma, “subject, topic”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smalltime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>papermaking</i>) coated (<i>of paper or cardboard</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chalk</li><li>chalk-white</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) Cretaceous</li></ol><p><i>мел</i> (mel) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>melodious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tune, melody</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melodrama</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalk (a small piece of chalk used as a writing implement)</li><li>stick of pastel or similar</li><li>(rare,&#32;by extension) crayon</li></ol><p><i>мел</i> (mel) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;colloquial) music lover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small-minded, petty, mean-spirited</li></ol><p><i>ме́лочь</i> (méločʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collectively</i>) small things</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) change, coins of small denomination</li><li>trifle</li></ol><p>Derived from мелкий (melkij, “fine, small, petty”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trifle (anything that is of little importance or worth)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) petty cash</li></ol><p><i>мелочь</i> (meločʹ, “small change, small amount of money or objects”) +&lrm; <i>-ёвка</i> (-jóvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shallow, shoal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mělъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twinkle, glimpse</li></ol><p><i>мелька́ть</i> (melʹkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash, to gleam</li><li>to flit, to fly past</li><li>to loom, to turn up</li><li>to appear for a moment, to be glimpsed fleetingly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to shimmer, glisten”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melkite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Melkite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash, to gleam</li><li>to flit, to fly (past)</li><li>to loom, to turn up</li><li>to appear for a moment, to be glimpsed fleetingly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to shimmer, glisten”). Cognates include Lithuanian <i>mérkti</i> (“to blink, twinkle”), Ancient Greek <i>μέρα</i> (méra, “morning”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (maurgins, “morning”), English <i>morrow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in passing, cursorily, perfunctorily, briefly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miller</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мельник)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Melnik, equivalent to English Miller</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́льник</i> (mélʹnik, “miller”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мельница</i> (melĭnica, “device for grinding grains and groats”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melьnica</i>, from <i>&#42;melьnъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ica</i>, from <i>&#42;melti</i> (“to grind, mill”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mill</li></ol><p><i>ме́льница</i> (mélʹnica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flicker</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flash before one's eyes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flickering</li></ol><p><i>мельтеши́ть</i> (melʹtešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ме́лкий</i> (mélkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) small fry (small fish)</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) small fry, small potatoes (insignificant people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meme</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>meme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) meme</li></ol><p><i>мем</i> (mem) +&lrm; <i>-ас</i> (-as).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane</li><li>diaphragm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meme</li></ol><p>From <i>мем</i> (mem) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memorandum</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>memor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memorial (architecture)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>memoriālis</i>, from Latin <i>memoria</i> (whence also English <i>memory</i>), further from Latin <i>memor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>memorial</li></ol><p><i>мемориа́л</i> (memoriál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memoirist</li></ol><p><i>мемуа́ры</i> (memuáry) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) memoir</li><li>memoiristic</li></ol><p><i>мемуа́ры</i> (memuáry) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memoirs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mémoires</i>, plural of <i>mémoire</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>memoria</i> (whence also English <i>memory</i>), further from Latin <i>memor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange, barter, swap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maenad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) menarche (first menstruation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mendelevium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manager</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>manager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>management</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>management</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>less</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьне</i> (mĭne), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьnjes-</i> or Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьnješ-</i>. Cognate to Latin <i>minor</i>, <i>minus</i>, Ancient Greek <i>μινύθω</i> (minúthō), <i>μείον</i> (meíon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) minstrel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) the scale length (of a stringed instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mensura</i> (“measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measuring glass, graduate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meningitis</li></ol><p>Ultimately from New Latin <i>meningitis</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μῆνιγξ</i> (mêninx, “membrane”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(optics,&#32;physics,&#32;anatomy) meniscus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μηνίσκος</i> (mēnískos).</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>łąkotka</i>, <i>łękotka</i> **лякотка (Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lęťi</i>, <i>&#42;lękъ</i>, <i>&#42;lǫkъ</i>, Russian <i>отля́чить</i> (otljáčitʹ), <i>ля́кий</i> (ljákij), <i>ля́ка</i> (ljáka), <i>лук</i> (luk)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Russia,&#32;cryptozoology,&#32;folklore) An animal described by the Mansi as a "forest giant", with anatomical features similar to a Yeti, said to live in the area of Khantia-Mansia, Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mansi (compare Northern Mansi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menorah</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstrual cup (a receptacle worn inside the vagina during menstruation to collect menstrual fluid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstruation, period</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cop</li></ol><p>Possibly from Polish <i>menda</i> (“police officer (vulgar)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mentality</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mentalität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mentality</li></ol><p><i>мента́льный</i> (mentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mental (<i>referring to the mind</i>)</li><li>mental (<i>referring to the chin</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>mental</i> (“mental (<i>of the mind</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Latin <i>mēns</i> (“mind”).</p><p>Perhaps from French <i>mental</i> (“mental (<i>of the chin</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Latin <i>mentum</i> (“chin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) place where police resides, police station, cophouse</li><li>mint liquor, schnapps concocted with <i>Mentha</i></li></ol><p><i>мент</i> (ment) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>менто́вка</i> (mentóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;relational) cop (police officer)</li></ol><p><i>мент</i> (ment) +&lrm; <i>-овско́й</i> (-ovskój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) menthol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mentor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minuet</li></ol><p>From French <i>menuet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij); comparative degree of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj): less (to smaller extent), smaller, fewer</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ма́ло</i> (málo): less (to smaller extent), smaller, fewer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to lessen, small, little”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>मी</i> (mī, “to lessen, diminish”); Latin <i>minuō</i> whence English <i>minute</i>, <i>diminish</i>, etc.; Latin <i>minus</i>, <i>minor</i>, <i>minimum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Menshevik (a member of a faction of the Russian revolutionary movement)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́ньше</i> (ménʹše, “lesser”) +&lrm; <i>-еви́к</i> (-evík, “-ist”), literally “minoritarian”, alluding to <i>меньшинство́</i> (menʹšinstvó, “minority”, literally “lesser-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) Mensheviks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something smaller, less</li><li>the smallest</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>ме́ньший</i> (ménʹšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) and <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij): smaller, lesser, smallest, least</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьňьjь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“small, little”), whence English <i>minor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minority (subgroup that does not form a numerical majority)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́ньше</i> (ménʹše) +&lrm; <i>-инство́</i> (-instvó), calque of French <i>minorité</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>menu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money changer (person who will exchange currency)</li></ol><p><i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money-changing shop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swap, to barter (to give something to another person and take something in return)</li><li>to replace, to substitute or supersede (to get rid of an old thing or person and get a new one)</li><li>to modify, to change, to transform, to alter (to make something different gradually)</li><li>to exchange, to recapture (to lose one or more pieces or pawns almost simultaneously with the opponent)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to change</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to exchange, to trade</li></ol><p><i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a dry measure approximately equal to one pood of seeds, usually equated with the chetverik, standardized in 1902 as 26.239 liters</li><li>measure</li><li>degree, extent, limit</li><li>measure, step, action</li><li>way</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mě̀ra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure of precaution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) marl, malm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawnwork, drawn-thread work, openwork, needlework</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meringue (mixture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>meringue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meringue</li></ol><p><i>мере́нг(а)</i> (meréng(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die, to perish (in large numbers)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) to stand still, to sink, to miss a beat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мерети</i> (mereti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;merti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to seem <i>(to)</i>, to appear <i>(to)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>villain, scoundrel, rat, stinker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vile, disgusting, abominable, nasty, odious</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрьзъкъ</i> (mrĭzŭkŭ), native form would be <i>&#42;мёрзкий</i> (&#42;mjórzkij), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьrzъkъ</i> (“frosty, cold”). Cognate to <i>мёрзнуть</i> (mjórznutʹ), <i>моро́з</i> (moróz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frozen ground, frozen earth; frozen state, congelation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A person who is too sensitive to cold, who is cold when others are not.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) disgusting, foul, revolting</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́рз(кий)</i> (mérz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́кость</i> (pákostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abomination, meanness, vileness, loathsomeness</li><li>abomination, nasty thing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрьзость</i> (mrĭzostĭ), native form would be <i>&#42;мёрзость</i> (&#42;mjórzostʹ). By surface analysis, <i>ме́рз(кий)</i> (mérz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) meridian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard, criterion</li><li>measure, test</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měridlo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gelding</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Mercedes (car)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolic; compare Kalmyk <i>мөрн</i> (mörn, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merino, a sheep of a Spanish breed.</li><li>The wool of this sheep.</li><li>The fabric made from this wool.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>merino</i> or French <i>mérinos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) merino</li></ol><p><i>мерино́с</i> (merinós) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure</li><li>to try on</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měriti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure(s), measurements</li><li>measuring rod, yardstick</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) yardstick, criterion</li></ol><p><i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mercantile (relating to mercantilism)</li><li>petty, self-serving</li></ol><p>French <i>mercantile</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dark/dim</li><li>to fade, to wane</li><li>to pale <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьркнути</i> (mĭrknuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mь̀rknǫti</i>. Cognate to <i>морок</i> (morok), <i>мрак</i> (mrak), <i>мерца́ть</i> (mercátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) mercury (Hg)</li><li>mercury preparation as used in medicine</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Меркурий)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Mercury</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Mercury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hake (<i>Merluccius</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>merluza</i>, itself from French <i>merlu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrachan</li></ol><p>Disputed:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) astrakhan</li></ol><p><i>мерлу́шка</i> (merlúška) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a rhythmic or measured manner</li></ol><p><i>ме́рный</i> (mérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhythmic, measured</li><li>measuring</li></ol><p><i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undertaking, action, enterprise, measure (that which is undertaken)</li><li>event</li></ol><p><i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прия́тие</i> (prijátije). Per Shansky a calque of German <i>Maßnahme</i>. Known since 1791 (a letter from Suvorov A. V. to Khostov D. I.). Compare French <i>prendre des mesures</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Merc (a car of the Mercedes-Benz brand).</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>мерседес</i> (mersedes, “Mercedes-Benz”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thank you</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>merci</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly pale, ghastly, lifeless</li><li>deathly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрьтвьнъ</i> (mrĭtvĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse, cadaver, dead man, dead body</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrion (dead flesh&#59; carcasses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flicker, shimmer, twinkle</li></ol><p><i>мерца́ть</i> (mercátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shimmer, to twinkle, to glimmer, to flicker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрьцати</i> (mrĭcati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьrcati</i>. Doublet of native dialectal <i>мерка́ть</i> (merkátʹ), also compare <i>смерка́ться</i> (smerkátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miryachit</li></ol><p>Probably from Yakut</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) (fish) net</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;merža</i>. Doublet of <i>мре́жа</i> (mréža), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medley, jumble</li><li>mash, mush, batter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight (a physical confrontation between people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead</li><li>to mix with water (<i>e.g. of clay</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měsiti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;máiśīˀtei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) mass</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from French <i>messe</i>. Compare also Italian <i>messa</i>, German <i>Messe</i>. All from Latin <i>missa</i> (“dismissed”), from <i>ite, missa est</i> (“go, (the assembly) is dismissed”), last words of the Roman Rite of the Catholic Church.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) message (communication, concept or information conveyed&#59; underlying theme)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>message</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) messenger</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) messenger (instant messaging program)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>messenger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messiah</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мессия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messiah (country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Messias</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in some places, in places</li><li>partly, partially, in part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto)</li><li>A small village or town on the territory of the former Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.</li><li>A shtetl, small Jewish village.</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto, “place”). Sense&nbsp;2 and 3 are a semantic loan from Polish <i>miasteczko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep</li><li>to scatter</li><li>to swirl</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to snow strongly, to blizzard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mestì</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>local committee (<i>e.g. of a trade union</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>ме́стный комите́т</i> (méstnyj komitét, “local committee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) mestnichestvo</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto, “place, position”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>area, district, place, locality, region</li><li>terrain, ground</li></ol><p><i>ме́стный</i> (méstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>local</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) locative (relating to the case that expresses location)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto, “place”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, spot</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) region(s), province(s), parts (<i>especially if distant or rural</i>)</li><li>site, scene</li><li>job, position</li><li>piece (<i>of luggage</i>)</li><li>room, space</li><li>seat, place, room, bed (<i>where a passenger, visitor, etc. can sit, stay, or sleep</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>мѣсто</i>&nbsp;n&nbsp;inan (město), from Old East Slavic <i>мѣсто</i>&nbsp;n (město), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mě̀sto</i>&nbsp;n.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(place of) residence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place of residence</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жи́тельство</i> (žítelʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) pronoun</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>и́мя</i> (ímja) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of Latin <i>pronomen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pronominal</li></ol><p><i>местоиме́ние</i> (mestoiménije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location, position, whereabouts (place)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нахожде́ние</i> (naxoždénije). Compare Ukrainian <i>місцезнахо́дження</i> (misceznaxódžennja), Belarusian <i>месцазнахо́джанне</i> (mjescaznaxódžannje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location (place)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>положе́ние</i> (položénije). Compare Ukrainian <i>місцеполо́ження</i> (miscepolóžennja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residence, abode, seat, whereabouts</li></ol><p><i>место</i> (mesto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пребыва́ние</i> (prebyvánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location</li></ol><p><i>место</i> (mesto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>расположе́ние</i> (raspoložénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deposit, field (e.g. coal field), formation, bed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revenge, vengeance</li><li>vendetta</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьсть</i> (mĭstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьstь</i>, related to the verb <i>&#42;mьstiti</i> (“to avenge”).</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>месть</i> (mestʹ), Old Church Slavonic <i>мьсть</i> (mĭstĭ), Bulgarian <i>мъст</i> (mǎst), Czech <i>msta</i>, <i>pomsta</i>, Slovak <i>pomsta</i>, Polish <i>pomsta</i>, <i>zemsta</i>. See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьstiti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;French context</i>) monsieur, sir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>monsieur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>month</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) moon (the Earth's only permanent natural satellite, including a full moon)</li><li>moon (in <b>modern Russian</b> usually referring to a crescent or a gibbous moon, but not a full moon)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣсѧць</i> (měsęcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měsęcь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for months, taking months, lasting for months, month after month, months on end</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menologium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstruation</li></ol><p>Possibly short for <i>ме́сячные очище́ния</i> (mésjačnyje očiščénija, “monthly cleanings”) or <i>ме́сячные кро́ви</i> (mésjačnyje króvi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monthly</li><li>month-long</li><li>month-old</li><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) moon</li><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) moonlit</li></ol><p><i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) mark, label, sign</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) distinguishing characteristic (what distinguishes two otherwise identical or similar things)</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) profit sharing contract (contract where the participants equally share profits and losses)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) goal, target</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) object of desire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mēta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>meta-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(science fiction,&#32;Internet,&#32;computing) metaverse</li></ol><p>From English <i>metaverse</i> (ме́та- (méta-) meaning meta-, and вселе́нная (vselénnaja) meaning -verse), therefore <i>ме́та-</i> (méta-) +&lrm; <i>вселе́нная</i> (vselénnaja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metacinema</li></ol><p><i>мета-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>кино</i> (kino).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) metal (music genre)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>metal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal (chemical element forming metallic bonds or alloy containing such elements)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) metal (music style)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>metallum</i>. The musical sense is a semantic loan from English <i>metal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metalworker</li><li>specialist in metals or metallurgy</li><li>(<i>music</i>) musician who plays metal; member of a metal band</li><li>(<i>music</i>) metalhead, headbanger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) metal; metallic</li></ol><p><i>мета́лл</i> (metáll) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal detector</li></ol><p><i>мета́лл</i> (metáll) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>иска́тель</i> (iskátelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal construction, metalwork, metal structure</li></ol><p><i>мета́лл</i> (metáll) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>констру́кция</i> (konstrúkcija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrap metal, scrap</li></ol><p><i>мета́лл</i> (metáll, “metal”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лом</i> (lom, “scrap”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal processing, metalworking</li></ol><p><i>мета́лл</i> (metáll) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>обрабо́тка</i> (obrabótka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metallurgist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metallurgical, metallurgic</li></ol><p>From <i>металлурги́я</i> (metallurgíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metallurgy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metamorphosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>methamphetamine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) methane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throwing; casting; tossing</li><li>inconstancy, blowing hot and cold</li></ol><p><i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>methanoic acid, formic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) methanol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metamessage</li></ol><p><i>мета-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>сообще́ние</i> (soobščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) metastasis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metathesis (the transportation of letters, syllables or sounds within a word)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to toss</li><li>to spawn (roe)</li><li>to baste, to tack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>метати</i> (metati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;metàti</i> (“to throw”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;met-</i>.</p><p>From Old East Slavic <i>метати</i> (metati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;metàti</i> (“to throw”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;met-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to cast pearls before swine</li></ol><p>From the Bible, Matthew 7:6. Compare English <i>cast pearls before swine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush about</li><li>to toss, to toss about</li></ol><p><i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metaphysician</li></ol><p><i>мета-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>физик</i> (fizik). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>метафі́зик</i> (metafízyk), Belarusian <i>метафі́зік</i> (mjetafízik), Polish <i>metafizyk</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) metaphysics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>metaphysica</i>, from Byzantine Greek <i>μεταφυσικά</i> (metaphusiká), from the Ancient Greek title of the collection by Aristotle <i>τὰ μετὰ τὰ φυσικά</i> (tà metà tà phusiká). Compare Ukrainian <i>метафі́зика</i> (metafízyka), Belarusian <i>метафі́зіка</i> (mjetafízika), Polish <i>metafizyka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metaphysical</li></ol><p><i>метафи́зика</i> (metafízika) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metaphor</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>metaphora</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μεταφορά</i> (metaphorá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>figuratively (in a figurative manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metalanguage</li></ol><p><i>мета-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>язык</i> (jazyk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>мете́ль</i> (metélʹ, “blizzard, snowstorm”)</li></ol><p><i>мете́ль</i> (metélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snowstorm</li><li>full of snowstorms</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) broom, besom</li></ol><p><i>мете́ль</i> (metélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>метла́</i> (metlá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metempsychosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metempsychosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brooming, sweeping</li></ol><p><i>мет-</i> (met-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(meteorology,&#32;technical jargon) weather report</li></ol><p>Short for <i>метеогра́мма</i> (meteográmma), <i>метеонаблюде́ние</i> (meteonabljudénije), or <i>метеосво́дка</i> (meteosvódka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) meteor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bloating, flatulence, tympany, tympanites, meteorism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>μετεωρισμός</i> (meteorismós, “raising up, flatulence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) meteorite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meteorograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meteorologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meteorological</li></ol><p><i>метеороло́гия</i> (meteorológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meteorology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Meteorologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weather report</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>methyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) methionine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metis; mestizo; half-breed; bastard; mutt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>métis</i>, from Late Latin <i>mixticius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) crossbreeding</li></ol><p>From <i>метис</i> (metis) +&lrm; <i>-ация</i> (-acija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark, to label</li><li>to aim</li><li>to drive at, to mean</li><li>to aspire <i>(to a position)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mětiti</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁tis</i> (“measure”), from <i>&#42;meh₁-</i>. Akin to Latin <i>mētior</i> (“to measure”), Ancient Greek <i>μῆτις</i> (mêtis, “counsel, plan”), Sanskrit <i>माति</i> (māti, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marker, mark, label</li><li>the action of marking</li></ol><p><i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-aimed, sharp (shooter)</li><li>witty, well-aimed (remark, comment, article)</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ) +&lrm; <i>кий</i> (kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accurately</li><li>keenly</li><li>pointedly, aptly</li></ol><p><i>ме́ткий</i> (métkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accuracy (<i>when hitting a target</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sharpness, wittiness, sagacity</li></ol><p><i>ме́ткий</i> (métkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, to cast, to fling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;metnǫti</i> (“to throw”). By surface analysis, <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss, to dash</li></ol><p><i>метну́ть</i> (metnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>method, procedure</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>μέθοδος</i> (méthodos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) method</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>method(s), methodology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>methodologist</li><li>Methodist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>methodical</li><li>systematic</li></ol><p><i>мето́д(ика)</i> (metód(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) study guide, manual, tutorial</li></ol><p>From <i>методи́ч(еское)</i> (metodíč(eskoje)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), short for <i>методи́ческое посо́бие</i> (metodíčeskoje posóbije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>methodically, systematically</li></ol><p><i>методи́чный</i> (metodíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>methodical, systematic</li></ol><p><i>ме́тод</i> (métod) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>methodological</li></ol><p>From <i>методоло́гия</i> (metodológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>methodology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) metonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metonymic</li></ol><p><i>метони́мия</i> (metonímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metonymy</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>metonymia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μετωνυμίᾱ</i> (metōnumíā, “change of name”), from <i>μετά</i> (metá, “other”) + <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meter/metre <i>(SI unit of measure)</i></li><li>meter stick (metre stick)</li><li>tape measure, measuring tape (<i>at least 1 meter long</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mètre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;humorous) a small person, a person knee-high to a grasshopper</li></ol><p>Literally, “one meter tall with a cap on top”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(film) footage</li><li>length in meters</li><li>area in square meters</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>métrage</i>. By surface analysis, <i>метр</i> (metr) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headwaiter, maître d'hôtel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maître d'hôtel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth certificate</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) metrics</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) metric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metric</li><li>metrical</li><li>relating to birth certificates (<i>ме́трика</i>); metrical</li></ol><p><i>метр</i> (metr) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subway, underground</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>métro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meter</li><li>one-metre-long, one-metre-wide, one-metre-thick, one-metre-high</li></ol><p><i>метр</i> (metr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metrology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metronome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subway, underground</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>métropolitain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother country</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) metropolis</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>μητρόπολις</i> (mētrópolis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>метла́</i> (metlá): whisk</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) panicle</li><li>(animate,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) chick, broad, girl</li></ol><p><i>метла́</i> (metlá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mephedrone (4-methylmethcathinone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mephedrone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fur</li><li>bellows</li><li>goatskin, wineskin, waterskin</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>мѣхъ</i> (měxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měxъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;maišás</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moysós</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>міх</i> (mix), Belarusian <i>мех</i> (mjex), Old Church Slavonic <i>мѣхъ</i> (měxŭ), Serbo-Croatian <i>мијех</i>/<i>mijeh</i>, Polish <i>miech</i>, Czech <i>měch</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) (<~ <i>коза́</i> (kozá)); <i>лоси́ны</i> (losíny) (&lt; <i>лось</i> (losʹ)). Also compare English <i>goatskin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anime,&#32;manga,&#32;colloquial) mecha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>メカ</i> (meka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine operator; mechanization specialist; mechanic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanization (the use of machinery to replace human or animal labour)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mechanized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mechanize (equip with machinery)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanism, machinery (working parts of a machine as a group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) tank driver</li><li>driver/mechanic (<i>of heavy machinery</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanics</li><li>trick, knack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mechanically</li></ol><p><i>механи́ческий</i> (mexaníčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mechanical (related to mechanics&#59; done by machine)</li><li>automatic (acting without conscious thought)</li></ol><p><i>меха́н(ика)</i> (mexán(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fur</li></ol><p><i>мех</i> (mex) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrier (a person who sells, makes, repairs, alters, cleans, or otherwise deals in clothing made of fur)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrier's wife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the patron of science and art</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword (<i>usually with a straight blade</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mečь</i>. Further etymology unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marked, labeled, tagged</li></ol><p><i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosque</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language (compare Kazakh <i>мешіт</i> (meşıt), Tatar <i>мәчет</i> (mäçet), Turkmen <i>metjit</i>), ultimately from Arabic مَسْجِد (masjid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasy, daydream</li><li>dream (act or habit of imagining something, usually intensely but often unrealistically desired; something thus imagined)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мьчьта</i> (mĭčĭta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьčьta</i>, from <i>&#42;mikati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dreaming, daydreaming</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мьчьтаниѥ</i> (mĭčĭtanije). By surface analysis, <i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dreamer, daydreamer</li></ol><p><i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dreamily, musingly</li></ol><p><i>мечта́тельный</i> (mečtátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dreamy</li><li>pensive, tending to dream</li><li>visionary</li></ol><p><i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dream (to imagine something, usually intensely but often unrealistically desired; to desire, hope or assume such a thing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мьчьтати</i> (mĭčĭtati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьčьtati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear in one's dreams, to be a dream [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>] (<i>often of hopes or aspirations</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) there is dreaming [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘by someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>dream</i> with the instrumental object as the subect)</li></ol><p><i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>меч</i> (meč).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitator, mixer, blender (implement for shaking or mixing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měšadlъka</i>, from <i>&#42;měšadlo</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ъka</i>, from <i>&#42;měšati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-dlo</i>, from <i>&#42;měsiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ, “to stir, to mix, to blend”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mishmash, medley, hodgepodge</li></ol><p>From <i>ме́шанный</i> (méšannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mixed; blended</li></ol><p><i>мешать</i> (mešatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir</li><li>to mix, to mingle, to blend</li><li>to mix up, to confuse</li><li>to hinder, to impede, to prevent, to interfere with [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to bother, to make difficulties, to hurt a little</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣшати</i> (měšati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měšati</i>. Akin to <i>меси́ть</i> (mesítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mix, to mingle</li><li>to get confused</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;colloquial) to get in the way</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;colloquial) to interfere [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to linger, to delay, to tarry over</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mešьkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(cloths)</i> baggy</li><li><i>(person)</i> awkward, clumsy</li></ol><p><i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sackcloth</li></ol><p><i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bursiform (shaped like a purse or bag)</li></ol><p><i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otiosity, leisureliness</li></ol><p><i>ме́шкать</i> (méškatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dilatory</li></ol><p><i>мешкота́</i> (meškotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack (large bag for storage and handling)</li><li>(sports,&#32;informal) punching bag</li><li>(<i>military</i>) encirclement</li><li>clumsy person, galoot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣшькъ</i> (měšĭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měšьkъ</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;měxъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;maišás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moysós</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) (<~ <i>коза́</i> (kozá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) bag under the eye; eyebag</li></ol><p>Literally, “sack(s) under the eye(s)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мешо́к</i> (mešók)</li></ol><p><i>мешо́к</i> (mešók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) petty bourgeois, petit bourgeois (member of a social estate in the Russian Empire comprising the urban lower middle class)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) Philistine, narrow-minded person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>mieszczanin</i> (“burgher, townsman”), from <i>miasto</i> (“town”). By surface analysis, <i>место</i> (mesto) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>мещани́н</i> (meščanín)</li></ol><p><i>мещани́н</i> (meščanín) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) narrow-minded woman, woman with petty interests</li></ol><p><i>мещан(и́н)</i> (meščan(ín)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) petty bourgeoisie</li><li>narrow-minded, vulgar</li></ol><p><i>мещан(и́н)</i> (meščan(ín)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) petite bourgeoisie, urban lower middle class (social estate in the Russian Empire)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) narrowmindedness</li></ol><p><i>мещан(и́н)</i> (meščan(ín)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;humorous&#32;in modern usage) payment, reward, fee</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bribe</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) retribution, payback</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьзда</i> (mĭzda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьzdà</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) corruption, bribery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) E, mi (<i>note</i>)</li><li>mu (the modern Greek letter <i>Μ</i>&#47;<i>μ</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mi</i>, from Latin <i>mīra</i>, the first word of the third line of <i>Ut queant laxis</i>, the medieval hymn which solfège was based on.</p><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>μι</i> (mi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) E-flat major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moment, instant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;migъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: МиГ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>MiG, mig (fighter jet)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>А. И. Микоя́н и М. И. Гуре́вич</i> (A. I. Mikoján i M. I. Gurévič), designers of the fighter jets.</p></html>

<h3>Russian (Capitalized: Миг)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) MiG, mig (a series of fighter jets designed by Mikoyán and Gurévich... А. И. Микоян и М. И. Гуревич)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blinkenlight (a flashing light or beacon light)</li><li>emergency vehicle lighting, flashing light (emergency vehicle lighting)</li></ol><p><i>мига́ть</i> (migátʹ, “to blink”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blink</li><li>to twinkle</li><li><i>(lamp etc.)</i> to blink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;migati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blink</li><li>to twinkle</li><li><i>(lamp etc.)</i> to wink, to blink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mignǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мига́ть</i> (migátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a trice, in a flash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) migration, migratory</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>Migration</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migration (moving to live in another place)</li><li>migration (seasonal moving of animals)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) migration (changing a platform from an environment to another one)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Migration</i> or French <i>migration</i>, from Latin <i>migrātiō</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>мігра́ція</i> (mihrácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) migraine, sick headache</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>migraine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to migrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mussel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>μύδια</i> (mýdia), plural of <i>μύδι</i> (mýdi).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мидия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Media (a geographic region and ancient satrapy of the Persian Empire in northwestern Iran, originally inhabited by the Medes)# (<i>historical</i>) Media (a possible ancient kingdom ruled by the Medes from approximately 700 to 550 BCE, whose extent and sometimes even existence is debated)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mede</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociology</i>) misandry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mise en scène</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mise en scène</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misanthrope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misanthropic</li></ol><p><i>мизантро́п</i> (mizantróp) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Russian tarantula, <i>Lycosa singoriensis</i></li><li>(by extension,&#32;because the tarantula is so pestilent where it exists) a spider in general</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мизгирь</i> (mizgirĭ), мѣзгирь (mězgirĭ) once in ніти мѣзгиревыхъ тенетъ in the so-called Повѣсть о Стефанѣ Пермскомъ, on which basis one assumes a Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mězgyrь</i>, an archaism only attested in Great Russian and Ukrainian regiolects.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мизерико́рдия</i> (mizerikórdija)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misericord (ledge, sometimes ornately carved, attached to a folding church seat to provide support for a person standing for long periods)</li><li>misericord (medieval dagger, used for the mercy stroke to a wounded foe)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>misericordia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measly, scanty</li><li>wretched, miserable</li></ol><p>French <i>misère</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little finger</li><li>little toe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѣзиньць</i> (mězinĭcĭ, “youngest son”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mězinьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мизи́нец</i> (mizínec); (small) little finger, (small) little toe (small fifth finger or toe)</li></ol><p><i>мизи́нец</i> (mizínec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) AAA battery</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>мизи́нчиковая батаре́йка</i> (mizínčikovaja bataréjka, literally “little-finger battery”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;diminutive) little finger, little toe (fifth finger or toe)</li></ol><p><i>мизи́нчик</i> (mizínčik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) pump-cheek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>mik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mikado (emperor of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>帝</i> (みかど, mikado).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mycenaean, related to the city of Mycenae and its civilisation from ancient Greece.</li></ol><p><i>Мике́ны</i> (Mikény) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), Ancient Greek <i>Μυκῆναι</i> (Mukênai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mycologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mycology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>micro- (very small)</li><li>micro- (one millionth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minibus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microampere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microbe, germ</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microbiologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microbiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microblog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microwave (wave)</li></ol><p><i>микро-</i> (mikro-) +&lrm; <i>волна́</i> (volná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microwave oven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microwave (oven)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) microwave</li></ol><p><i>микроволна́</i> (mikrovolná) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any green alga of the genus <i>Microglena</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Microglena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microclimate</li></ol><p>From <i>мѝкро-</i> (mìkro-) +&lrm; <i>кли́мат</i> (klímat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microcosm (smaller system as representative of a larger one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>micrometer (device)</li><li>micrometer, micron (unit of measurement)</li></ol><p><i>микро-</i> (mikro-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr)</p><p>Same as 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>micron, micrometre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>micronic</li></ol><p>From <i>микро́н</i> (mikrón, “micron”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microorganism</li></ol><p><i>микро-</i> (mikro-) +&lrm; <i>органи́зм</i> (organízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microplastic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>microplastic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighborhood; microdistrict, microraion (residential complex in a current or former communist country)</li></ol><p>From <i>микро-</i> (mikro-, “micro-”) +&lrm; <i>райо́н</i> (rajón, “district”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microsecond</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>microscopical</li></ol><p><i>микроско́п</i> (mikroskóp) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microscopy</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>microscopie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) microchip, chip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microflora (microscopic plant life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>microphone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) microchip (integrated circuit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>microchip</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trace element</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) result of mixing several tracks</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mix</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mixer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) myxomatosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A myxophagan, any beetle of the suborder Myxophaga.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Myxophaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) mixed-sex competition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mixed</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) liquid mixture, liquid compounded drug</li><li>(<i>philately</i>) kiloware, stamp mixture, bulk mixture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mixtūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) mixer, mixing console</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mixer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angular mil (=<small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>6000</sub></small> of a circle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a cute child (especially a girl), a sweet girl, babe, cutie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) babe, cutie, cutie pie, hottie</li></ol><p>diminutive of ми́лая (mílaja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military blogger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) milady (English noblewoman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>milady</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ми́ло</i> (mílo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ми́ло</i> (mílo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Milików (a village in Poland) or Mileykov (several villages in Belarus and Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Миле́йково</i> (Miléjkovo) or <i>Миле́йков</i> (Miléjkov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Милейковский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Mileikowsky or Mileikowski, transferred from the place name</li></ol><p>From the relational adjective <i>милейко́вский</i> (milejkóvskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj, “dear, sweet”)</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>militarisation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to militarize (to train or equip for war)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>militarism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>militarist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) police, militsia (<i>during the Soviet period and in some post-Soviet successor states</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;relational) militia (<i>in the Russian Empire</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мили́ция</i> (milícija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>police officer, policeman, militsia officer (<i>during the Soviet period and in some post-Soviet successor states</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) militiaman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Milizionär</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>police, militsia (<i>during the Soviet period and in some post-Soviet successor states</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) militia (<i>in the Russian Empire</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>milicja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milkshake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>milkshake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) millettia (<i>Millettia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Millettia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>milli-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milliampere</li></ol><p><i>милли-</i> (milli-) +&lrm; <i>ампе́р</i> (ampér)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>billion, milliard (<i>a short scale billion</i>) (1,000,000,000, 10<sup>9</sup>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>milliard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billionaire</li></ol><p>From French <i>milliardaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milligram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milliliter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millimeter/millimetre <i>(SI unit of measure)</i></li></ol><p><i>милли-</i> (milli-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>million (1,000,000)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>million</i>, from Italian <i>milione</i>, from Latin <i>mīlle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millionaire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Millionär</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>millionth</li><li>worth millions</li><li>million-strong</li></ol><p><i>миллио́н</i> (millión) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geometry,&#32;rare) megagon (million-sided polygon)</li></ol><p><i>миллио́н</i> (millión) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millisecond (one one-thousandth of a second)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dearly, kindly, cordially</li><li>cutely</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show mercy <i>(to)</i>, to pardon, to grant pardon <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to caress, to fondle</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;colloquial) to caress together (with someone else)</li></ol><p><i>ми́ловать</i> (mílovatʹ, “to pardon”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>милова́ть</i> (milovátʹ, “to caress”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuteness (state of being cute (endearingly attractive))</li></ol><p>From <i>милови́дный</i> (milovídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretty, good-looking</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj): dear, darling, sweetheart (<i>addressed to a man</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) milord (English nobleman)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>milord</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy (relenting; forbearance to cause or allow harm to another)</li><li>charity, clemency</li></ol><p>From <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Literally, “kindheartedness”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciful, charitable, gracious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milosьrdьnъ</i>. Equivalent to <i>милосе́рдие</i> (milosérdije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>graciously, indulgently</li></ol><p><i>ми́лостивый</i> (mílostivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) merciful, gracious, kind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milostivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ми́лость</i> (mílostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(formal,&#32;historical) dear sir; greetings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms, handout (something given to the poor as charity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>милостꙑн҄и</i> (milostynʹi, “mercy”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milostyni</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ми́лость</i> (mílostʹ, “mercy”) +&lrm; <i>-ыня</i> (-ynja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy, charity, grace, kindness (behaviour, quality)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) good deed, favour/favor (act)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (good) favour/favor (acceptance or trust by someone)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) pardon, quarter</li><li>(historical,&#32;now&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;reproachful,&#32;as an address with a possessive pronoun) (your) grace</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) something cute, cuteness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milostь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cuteness, something cute</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feeling of cuteness</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>мило́к</i> (milók): dear, darling, sweetheart (<i>addressed to a man</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мило́к</i> (milók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;sarcastic,&#32;ironic) my dear, darling (a term for addressing women)</li></ol><p><i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) clipping of <i>ми́лфа</i> (mílfa, “MILF”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) MILF</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>MILF</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, sweet (having a pleasing disposition)</li><li>cute (in various senses)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beloved</li><li>(in direct address,&#32;also&#32;ironic) dear, darling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>милъ</i> (milŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;milъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasant person, my dear (fellow)</li></ol><p><i>ми́л(ый)</i> (míl(yj), “dear, sweet, cute”) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mime (pantomime actor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facial expression(s)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mimicry (the act or ability to simulate the appearance of someone or something else)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>past, by</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>past, by</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mimo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mimosa</li><li>(common gender,&#32;animate,&#32;colloquial) coddled or touchy person</li><li>Mimosa (Soviet salad)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mimosa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>мимо́зой</i> (mimózoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleeting, passing, transient</li></ol><p><i>ми́мо</i> (mímo) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in passing</li></ol><p>From <i>ми́мо</i> (mímo, “past, by”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ход</i> (-xod) +&lrm; <i>-ом</i> (-om, instrumental suffix), based on a combining form of <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to walk, to go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>min., minute</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>мину́та</i> (minúta).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мин)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Ming; the Ming dynasty (Chinese dynasty that lasted from 1368–1644 <small>C.E.</small>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>明</i> (Míng)</li><li>a surname, Ming, from Chinese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>明</i> (Míng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>мин</i> (min)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) mine (explosive device)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) mortar round</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;military) torpedo</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) mine (underground tunnel packed with explosives)</li><li>a grimace, a face, mien (a facial expression—often an unhappy or unpleasant one)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mine</i>, itself derived from French <i>mine</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>mine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booby trap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;Islam) minaret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>minaret</i>, from Ottoman Turkish مناره (minare), from Persian مناره (menâre), from Arabic مَنَارَة (manāra, “lighthouse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) amygdala (region of the brain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) almond-shaped</li><li>amygdaloid</li></ol><p><i>минда́ль</i> (mindálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almond</li><li>(formal,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) tonsil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almond (nut)</li><li>almond (tree)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>мигдалъ</i> (migdalŭ) possibly through Polish <i>migdał</i>, from Latin <i>amygdalus</i>, from Greek <i>ἀμύγδαλος</i> (amýgdalos), <i>ἀμυγδάλη</i> (amygdáli), <i>ἀμυγδάλιον</i> (amygdálion), of Semitic origin. Cognate with Belarusian <i>мігда́л</i> (mihdál), German <i>Mandel</i>, Romanian <i>migdal</i>, Ukrainian <i>мигда́ль</i> (myhdálʹ). See more at <i>amygdalum</i>. Doublet of <i>мандо́рла</i> (mandórla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be overly sentimental</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show tenderness to those who don't deserve it</li></ol><p><i>минда́ль</i> (mindálʹ, “almond”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) almond (nut or tree)</li></ol><p><i>минда́ль</i> (mindálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mineral</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>minéral</i> or German <i>Mineral</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>minerālis</i>, <i>minerāle</i>, from Latin <i>minera</i>. Compare Ukrainian and Belarusian <i>мінера́л</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) mineral water</li><li>(slang,&#32;singular only) mineral fertilizer</li></ol><p><i>минера́ль(ный)</i> (minerálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mineralogist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mineralogical</li></ol><p><i>минерало́гия</i> (mineralógija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mineralogy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mineral water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mineral resources; mineral and raw material</li></ol><p><i>минера́льный</i> (minerálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сырьево́й</i> (syrʹjevój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt lake</li></ol><p>From <i>минеральный</i> (mineralʹnyj, “mineral”) +&lrm; <i>озеро</i> (ozero, “lake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mineral</li><li>full of minerals (<i>especially of wine</i>)</li></ol><p><i>минера́л</i> (minerál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellatio, blowjob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>minette</i> (“muff diving”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disparaging</i>) cocksucker (<i>a man who performs fellatio</i>)</li></ol><p><i>минет</i> (minet) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ministry of Health</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>министе́рство здравоохране́ния</i> (ministérstvo zdravooxranénija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>mini- (a smaller than usual version of an item)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacuum aspiration abortion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minibar</li></ol><p><i>мини-</i> (mini-) +&lrm; <i>бар</i> (bar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miniskirt (skirt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miniature</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) thumbnail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>miniature</i>, from Italian <i>miniatura</i>, from Latin <i>miniō</i> (“to colour/color red”), from <i>minium</i> (“red lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miniature (smaller than normal)</li></ol><p><i>миниатю́ра</i> (miniatjúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minivan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>minivan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miniature golf, minigolf (informal form of golf)</li></ol><p><i>мини-</i> (mini-) +&lrm; <i>гольф</i> (golʹf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) minimalism</li></ol><p>Either borrowed whole or derived from <i>минима́ль(ный)</i> (minimálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minimalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a minimum, minimally</li></ol><p><i>минима́льный</i> (minimálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>minimal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minimization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to minimize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>minimize</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minimum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) mining (laying mines)</li></ol><p><i>мини́ровать</i> (minírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to mine; to sow mines (in an area)</li></ol><p>From <i>ми́на</i> (mína) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ), perhaps after German <i>minieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ministerial</li></ol><p><i>министе́р(ство)</i> (ministér(stvo)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ministry, department (a department of government, headed by a minister)</li></ol><p><i>мини́стр</i> (minístr) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minister (the politician who heads a ministry)</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from Latin <i>minister</i>, likely through French <i>ministre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minefield</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mine</li></ol><p><i>ми́на</i> (mína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>government</i>) ministry of defense</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>министе́рство оборо́ны</i> (ministérstvo oboróny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass, to elapse (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>to go by, to go past (<i>without any effect</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to turn (an age)</li><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) to evade</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to end, to finish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>миновати</i> (minovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;minovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamprey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>minóg</i>, from German <i>Neunauge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lamprey</li></ol><p><i>мино́га</i> (minóga) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>мино́гой</i> (minógoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lamprey</li></ol><p><i>мино́га</i> (minóga) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) mortar, trench mortar (lightweight, often portable artillery piece which transmits recoil to a base plate)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) rocket launcher (Katyusha and similar World War II-era systems)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) torpedo tube</li></ol><p>From <i>ми́на</i> (mína) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мёт</i> (-mjót), combining form of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ). Calque of German <i>Minenwerfer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) large torpedo boat capable of operating in the open sea (19th and early 20th century)</li></ol><p><i>ми́на</i> (mína, “torpedo”) (<i>archaic</i>) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) small, high-speed steam-powered torpedo boat (19th century)</li></ol><p><i>ми́на</i> (mína, “torpedo”) (<i>archaic</i>) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) minor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sad mood, melancholy, the blues</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) minor, subdeterminant, minor determinant</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>minor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Minsk</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ming</li></ol><p>From <i>Мин(ск)</i> (Min(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p><p>From <i>Мин</i> (Min) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Alaska pollock</li></ol><p><i>минта́й</i> (mintáj) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>): <i>Gadus chalcogrammus</i>, Alaska pollock (<i>species</i>) (formally Theragra chalcogramma)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>): Alaska pollock (meat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past, last</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minus</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) minus sign, −</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) disadvantage, defect, shortcoming</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a subzero temperature (below zero degrees Celsius)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) clipping of <i>минусо́вка</i> (minusóvka); backing track, instrumental</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>minus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minute</li><li>moment, instant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>minūta</i>, from <i>minūtus</i> (“small”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мину́та</i> (minúta): minute, (short) moment</li></ol><p><i>мину́та</i> (minúta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) minute</li><li>lasting one minute</li><li>momentary</li></ol><p><i>мину́та</i> (minúta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мину́тка</i> (minútka), diminutive of <i>мину́та</i> (minúta): minute, (short) moment</li></ol><p><i>мину́тка</i> (minútka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>just a moment, wait a minute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jiffy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass, to elapse ((of time) to pass or move by)</li><li>to go by, to go past (without any effect)</li><li>to turn (of age)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;minǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ministry of finance</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>министе́рство фина́нсов</i> (ministérstvo finánsov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>government</i>) ministry of digital development</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>министе́рство цифрово́го разви́тия</i> (ministérstvo cifrovóvo razvítija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Minsk</li></ol><p><i>Минск</i> (Minsk) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) alternative form of <i>мине́т</i> (minét)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disparaging</i>) cocksucker (<i>a man who performs fellatio</i>)</li></ol><p><i>минье́т</i> (minʹjét) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ministry of justice</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>министе́рство юсти́ции</i> (ministérstvo justícii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hematology,&#32;pathology) myoglobinemia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) myocardium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myopia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) peace</li><li>universe; world; planet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mirъ</i> (“peace; world”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;meiˀrás</i> (“peace”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey(H)-ró-s</i>.</p><p>The dual meaning of "peace" and "world" is unique to Slavic. The pre-1918 orthography had an orthographical distinction of the two identically pronounced words to avoid confusion.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mirage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Mirbelia</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Mirbelia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) myriad (ten thousand)</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;poetic) myriad (an uncountably large number)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>myriade</i> or German <i>Myriade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;miriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мир</i> (mir, “peace”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make peace <i>(with)</i>, to be reconciled <i>(with)</i></li><li>to accept, to put up <i>(with)</i>, to reconcile oneself <i>(to)</i>, to resign oneself <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) myrmecophily</li></ol><p>Likely from English <i>myrmecophily</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) myrmicine (<i>ant of the Myrmicinae</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Myrmicinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>peacefully</li></ol><p><i>ми́рный</i> (mírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peace</li><li>peaceful</li><li>friendly</li><li>(military,&#32;relational) civilian, non-combatant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mirьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military slang,&#32;collective) civilians, civvies</li></ol><p><i>ми́рный</i> (mírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrism</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѵро</i> (müro), from Ancient Greek <i>μύρον</i> (múron).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) Weltschmerz, mal du siècle, world-weariness (apathetic or pessimistic view of life&#59; depression concerning or discomfort with the human condition or state of the world)</li></ol><p>Literally, “sorrow of the world”. Calque of German <i>Weltschmerz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldview, weltanschauung</li></ol><p>From <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>воззре́ние</i> (vozzrénije); a calque of German <i>Weltanschauung</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) worldview</li></ol><p><i>мировоззре́ние</i> (mirovozzrénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) world; global, worldwide</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) first-rate, first-class, great</li><li>(<i>law</i>) resolved by settlement rather than trial</li><li>(<i>law</i>) petty (related to minor civil and criminal cases)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) petty-court judge (judge who deals with minor civil and criminal cases)</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir, “world; peace”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldview, weltanschauung</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>восприя́тие</i> (vosprijátije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) the universe, the cosmos, the world</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir, “world”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>здание</i> (zdanije, “structure”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мир</i> (mir): (small) world, microcosm</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>peacefully, peaceably</li></ol><p><i>миролюби́вый</i> (miroljubívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful, peaceable, peace-loving</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attitude, disposition (to the world)</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ощуще́ние</i> (oščuščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrismation, anointment</li></ol><p><i>ми́ро</i> (míro, “chrism”) +&lrm; <i>пома́зание</i> (pomázanije, “anointing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldview, world outlook, weltanschauung</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>понима́ние</i> (ponimánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>world order</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поря́док</i> (porjádok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;religion</i>) act/way of governing the world.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldview, weltanschauung</li></ol><p>From <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>созерца́ние</i> (sozercánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacemaker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>миротворьць</i> (mirotvorĭcĭ), from Old Church Slavonic. By surface analysis, <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peacekeeper, peacemaker</li><li>peacekeeping, peacemaking</li></ol><p><i>миротво́рец</i> (mirotvórec) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacekeeping</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тво́рчество</i> (tvórčestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The exudation of the Oil of Saints.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) myroblyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) myroblyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to secrete chrism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>world order</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>устро́йство</i> (ustrójstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) society, community</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) world</li><li>secular, worldly</li><li>(relational,&#32;law) arbitration</li></ol><p><i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrtle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) myrtle</li></ol><p><i>мирт</i> (mirt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мир</i> (mir): peace.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layman (someone who is not an ordained cleric or member of the clergy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, tureen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;misъka</i>, whence also Polish <i>miska</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>mēnsa</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>discus</i>, whence <i>чан</i> (čan), <i>доска</i> (doska), English <i>dish</i>, <i>desk</i>. Also compare <i>тра́пеза</i> (trápeza) (&lt; Ancient Greek <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miso</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>味噌</i> (みそ, miso).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miss</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Miss</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) missionary</li></ol><p><i>миссионе́р</i> (missionér) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mission</li><li>legation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>English-speaking countries</i>) mister, Mr.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mister</i>. Doublet of <i>маги́стр</i> (magístr), <i>ма́стер</i> (máster), <i>маэ́стро</i> (maéstro), and <i>мэтр</i> (mɛtr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) mystery (secret rite in Ancient Greece and Rome)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) medieval Western European drama on a biblical theme, with interludes, mystery play</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mysterium</i>, from Latin <i>mysterium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μυστήριον</i> (mustḗrion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mystic</li></ol><p>First attested in 1798. Borrowed from French <i>mystique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mysticism</li></ol><p>First attested in 1799. Borrowed from German <i>Mystik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoax, mystification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mysticism</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>mysticus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μυστικός</i> (mustikós, “secret, mystic”), from <i>μύστης</i> (mústēs, “one who has been initiated, initiate”), from <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō, “to close one's lips or eyes; initiate into the mysteries”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mystical, mystic</li></ol><p><i>ми́ст(ика)</i> (míst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) misiurka (a type of light helmet with a long chain mail aventail)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic مِصْر (miṣr, “Egypt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) rally, mass-meeting (a demonstration&#59; an event where people gather together to protest against a given cause or express solidarity)</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) meeting (gathering among business people to discuss their business)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>meeting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold a political rally, to hold a meeting</li></ol><p><i>ми́тинг</i> (míting) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mitochondrion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) mitre (a covering for the head, worn on solemn occasions by church dignitaries)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) metropolitan</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μητροπολίτης</i> (mētropolítēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>митрополи́т</i> (mitropolít).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Orthodox Christianity</i>) metropolis, archdiocese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>митрополиꙗ</i> (mitropolija), from Byzantine Greek <i>μητρόπολις</i> (mētrópolis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) #MeToo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myth, fable</li><li>legend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mythical</li></ol><p><i>миф</i> (mif) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mythological</li></ol><p><i>мифоло́гия</i> (mifológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mythology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μυθολογία</i> (muthología).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mikhail's, Michael's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Mikhailov, Mikhailovsk, Mikhailovsky (referring to various people or places with any of these names, or to things named after them)</li></ol><p>From one of the following (depending on the reference):</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Mycena</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Mycena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) midshipman, warrant officer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>midshipman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>target (butt or mark to shoot at)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мишень</i> (mišenĭ, “stamp, round plate, sign, tag”), from Turkic. Compare Turkish <i>nişan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teddy bear</li><li>bear, an endearing form of <i>медве́дь</i> (medvédʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ми́шка</i> (Míška), an endearing diminutive of <i>Михаи́л</i> (Mixaíl, “a usual name of a bear”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мишка)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>Михаи́л</i> (Mixaíl)</li><li>nickname of a bear in folklore</li><li>teddy bear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Jewish</i>) family, especially extended family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>משפּחה</i> (mishpukha), from Hebrew <i>מִשְׁפָּחָה</i> (mishpakhá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mixtec</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>mixteco</i>, from Classical Nahuatl <i>mixtēcah</i>, plural of <i>mixtēcatl</i> (“inhabitant of the place of clouds”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinsel</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) showiness, window dressing</li></ol><p>Unknown. Attested since the first half of the 17th century. In view of <i>мисю́рка</i> (misjúrka, “a kind of helmet-armour with a net”), Ukrainian <i>місю́рка</i> (misjúrka, “a kind of helmet-armour with a net; glass-pearl”) apparently borrowed from a Turkic language which changes /b/ to /m/, doublet of <i>би́сер</i> (bíser, “beads, pearls”) or whatever Turkic word that armour name hails from.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>миф</i> (mif).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>micro- (0.000001)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>микро-</i> (mikro-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>миллили́тр</i> (millilítr, “ml, milliliter”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>ми́на-лову́шка</i> (mína-lovúška, “booby trap”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infant, baby</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>младьньць</i> (mladĭnĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldenьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>младо́й</i> (mladój) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) infant</li><li>infantile</li></ol><p><i>младе́нец</i> (mladénec) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infancy, babyhood</li></ol><p><i>младе́нец</i> (mladénec) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) young, youthful, new</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>младъ</i> (mladŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldъ</i> (“young”). Doublet of <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) youth, adolescence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>младость</i> (mladostĭ), doublet of <i>мо́лодость</i> (mólodostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>мла́дший</i> (mládšij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój)</li></ol><p>Originally comparative degree of <i>младо́й</i> (mladój), which is now dated.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>younger, youngest (in an earlier period of life)</li><li>junior</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) mallet, hammer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>млатъ</i> (mlatŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moltъ</i>. Doublet of <i>мо́лот</i> (mólot), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) milk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>млѣко</i> (mlěko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melko</i>. Doublet of <i>молоко́</i> (molokó), the inherited Old East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mammal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mammalian</li></ol><p>From <i>мле́ко</i> (mléko) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ющий</i> (pitájuščij). Literally, “milk-feeder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to relax</li><li>to enjoy, to delight <i>(in)</i>, to luxuriate <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъd(ь)lěti</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>мъдлѣть</i> (mŭdlětĭ), Ukrainian <i>мліти</i> (mlity), Belarusian <i>млець</i> (mljecʹ), Slovene <i>medleti</i>, Czech <i>omdleti</i>, Polish <i>mdleć</i>. Related to <i>ме́дленный</i> (médlennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;dated) milky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>млѣчьнъ</i> (mlěčĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мле́ко</i> (mléko) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>моло́чный</i> (molóčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>minced oath</i>) damn, shit</li></ol><p>Euphemistic form of <i>бля</i> (blja, a vulgar curse).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mm, millimeter</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>миллиме́тр</i> (millimétr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>мно́жественное число́</i> (mnóžestvennoje čisló, “plural number”, literally “pl.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>мно́жественное число́</i> (mnóžestvennoje čisló, “plural number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>мно́жественное число́</i> (mnóžestvennoje čisló, “plural number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mnemonics (study of techniques for improving memory)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μνημονικά</i> (mnēmoniká, “mnemonics”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mnemonics (study of techniques for improving memory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opinion (<i>person's</i>)</li><li>opinion (official statement)</li></ol><p><i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ, “deem”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>мне́ние</i> (mnénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete spelling of <i>мне́нию</i> (mnéniju, “dative singular of мне́ние (mnénije, “opinion”)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imaginary</li><li>sham</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) imaginary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistrustfulness, suspiciousness</li><li>hypochondria</li></ol><p><i>мни́тельный</i> (mnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mistrustful, suspicious</li><li>hypochondriac</li></ol><p><i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, to imagine, to suspect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мьнѣти</i> (mĭněti) or <i>мьнити</i> (mĭniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьněti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;minḗˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i>. Cognate to Lithuanian <i>miñti</i> (“to recall, to mention”), Latvian <i>minêt</i> (“to mention”), Ancient Greek <i>μέμονα</i> (mémona, “to remember”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem; to appear</li></ol><p><i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;humorous) many times, often</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>many (people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much</li><li>many</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъnogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much</li><li>many</li><li>a lot of, plenty of</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) at most</li><li>бо́лее (bóleje, “more”)</li><li>наибо́лее (naibóleje, “the most”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъnogъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;managas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;monogʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>multi- (pertaining to many)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) lots and lots</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) at most (<i>sentential adverb</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) polyatomic</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-, “multi-, poly-”) +&lrm; <i>атомный</i> (atomnyj, “atomic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiturreted</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ба́шенный</i> (bášennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polytheist</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “many”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) polytheism</li></ol><p>From <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (bog) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygamy (<i>in all senses</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of Late Latin <i>polygamia</i> and Ancient Greek <i>πολυγαμία</i> (polugamía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a bit much (More than is reasonable)</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́тый</i> (-vátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>centuries-long, multicentury (lasting centuries)</li><li>centuries-old, multisecular</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój). Compare Ukrainian <i>багатовікови́й</i> (bahatovikovýj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many-eyed</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) polyphony</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyhedron</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) polyhedral</li><li>many-sided, multifaceted, versatile</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-, “many”) + <i>грань</i> (granʹ, “facet, border”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many children</li><li>large (<i>family</i>)</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>де́ти</i> (déti) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) many things, many a thing, much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygamist (one with many wives)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygyny</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жена́</i> (žená) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) multitasking (the ability to handle multiple tasks at the same time)</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-, “multi-”) +&lrm; <i>задача</i> (zadača, “problem, issue”) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>information technology</i>) capable of multitasking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>significantly, with meaning</li></ol><p><i>многозначи́тельный</i> (mnogoznačítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>significant, meaningful</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>значи́тельный</i> (značítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multidigit</li><li>having multiple meanings, polysemous</li><li>ambiguous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) meaningful</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) multivalued</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>зна́к</i> (znák, “digit”, literally “sign”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>-зна́чный</i> (-znáčnyj)</p><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ, “to mean”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>-зна́чный</i> (-znáčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiapartment, multifamily</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>кварти́ра</i> (kvartíra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multicellular, multicelled, pluricellular</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka, “cell”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>repeatedly (done several times)</li></ol><p><i>многокра́тный</i> (mnogokrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multifold, multiple</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) iterative, frequentative</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “multi-, multiple, many”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>крат</i> (krat, “-fold”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) iterative aspect, frequentative aspect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) the proclamation of the words <i>мно́гая ле́та</i> (mnógaja léta, “many years”) during solemn worship, weddings, etc.</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of many years, long-term</li><li>perennial (having a life cycle of more than two years&#59; of a plant)</li></ol><p><i>мно́го-</i> (mnógo-) +&lrm; <i>ле́тний</i> (létnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) perennial</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crowded</li><li>populous</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multidimensional, multivariate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) diverse, versatile</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multimillion, of many millions</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>миллио́н</i> (millión) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyandry</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>муж</i> (muž) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiethnic, polyethnic</li><li>multinational</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millipede; centipede</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “many”) +&lrm; <i>но́жка</i> (nóžka, “leg (diminutive)”) or <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “many”) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “leg”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) A centipede-eater; any atractaspidid snake of the genus <i>Aparallactus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>многоно́жка</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;anim (mnogonóžka, “centipede or millipede”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “-eater”), literally “centipede/millipede-eater”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>promising, hopeful</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>обеща́ющий</i> (obeščájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety; diversity; multiformity</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) manifold</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diverse, varied, multiform</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) many-eyed</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>о́ко</i> (óko) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly experienced</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>о́пыт</i> (ópyt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiplayer</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзовательский</i> (pólʹzovatelʹskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) multipolarity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) multipolar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reusable</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>раз</i> (raz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Space Transportation System (STS)</li><li>Space Shuttle, shuttle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>garrulous, vaniloquent</li></ol><p><i>мно́го-</i> (mnógo-) +&lrm; <i>речи́стый</i> (rečístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiarmed, multi-handed</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) flat-backed millipede (order Polydesmida)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Polydesmida</i>, as <i>много-</i> (mnogo-, “multi-”) +&lrm; <i>связь</i> (svjazʹ, “link, connection”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a, nominal suffix). Compare <i>многосвя́зный</i> (mnogosvjáznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polyspermic; polyspermous; multi-seeded</li></ol><p><i>много</i> (mnogo) +&lrm; <i>семян</i> (semjan) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verbosity, longwindedness</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>verbose, prolix, long-winded</li></ol><p><i>многосло́в(ие)</i> (mnogoslóv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) polysyllabic</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>слог&gt;слож-</i> (slog&gt;slož-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multilayered, multilayer</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>слой</i> (sloj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polysyndeton</li></ol><p>First attested in 1796. <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of Late Latin <i>polysyndeton</i>, from Byzantine Greek <i>πολυσύνδετον</i> (polusúndeton). Compare with <i>полиси́ндетон</i> (polisíndɛton).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>firearms</i>) multibarrel</li><li>(<i>music</i>) multipipe</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) multitrunk</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ствол</i> (stvol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) polygonal, multilateral</li><li>multilateral, multiparty</li><li>many-sided, versatile</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-suffering</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronautics</i>) multistage</li><li>multistage, consisting of several steps or phases</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ступе́нь</i> (stupénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multivolume</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>том</i> (tom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large-tonnage (weighing multiple tonnes)</li><li>many-hued, multihued (exhibiting multiple hues or shades of colour)</li><li>multitone (producing multiple musical tones)</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>то́нный</i> (tónnyj).</p><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>тон</i> (ton, “hue, tint (of colour); (music) tone”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis <i>(punctuation)</i></li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>то́ч(ка)</i> (tóč(ka), “period, dot”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p><p>Compare typologically Polish <i>wielokropek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of many thousands</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) dear, esteemed, highly respected (used as an honorific)</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “much”) +&lrm; <i>уважа́емый</i> (uvažájemyj, “respected”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) polygon</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterogeneous, multiform</li></ol><p><i>мно́гий</i> (mnógij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уклад</i> (uklad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) multilevel</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>у́ровень</i> (úrovenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multifunctional, multifunction</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>функциона́льный</i> (funkcionálʹnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>багатофункціона́льний</i> (bahatofunkcionálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;ironical) a combination of many (smart) steps (in sports, politics, movie plots, etc.) usually leading to success</li><li>multimove</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>многоходо́вка</i> (mnogoxodóvka)</li></ol><p><i>многоходо́вка</i> (mnogoxodóvka) +&lrm; <i>-о́чка</i> (-óčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multipurpose</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many-hour, multihour (lasting many hours, produced for many hours in a row, calculated for many hours, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>numerous</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “many”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>число́</i> (čisló, “number”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) polynomial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high-rise (tall building, one consisting of many storeys)</li></ol><p>From <i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>этаж</i> (etaž) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multistory</li></ol><p><i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multilingualism (the condition of being multilingual; the ability to speak multiple languages)</li></ol><p><i>много-</i> (mnogo-) +&lrm; <i>язык</i> (jazyk) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polyglot, polylingual, multilingual</li></ol><p><i>мно́го-</i> (mnógo-) +&lrm; <i>язы́чный</i> (jazýčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) plural</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multiplicity</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) plurality</li></ol><p><i>мно́жественный</i> (mnóžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multiple</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) plural</li></ol><p><i>мно́жество</i> (mnóžestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude, mass</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) set</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мъножьство</i> (mŭnožĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъnožьstvo</i>, from <i>&#42;mъnogъ</i> (“much, many”) + <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мно́гий</i> (mnógij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) multiplicand (number that is to be multiplied by another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) multiplier (number)</li></ol><p><i>мно́жить</i> (mnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to multiply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъnožiti</i>, from <i>&#42;mъnogъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мно́гий</i> (mnógij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>мно́жити</i> (mnóžyty), Belarusian <i>мно́жыць</i> (mnóžycʹ), Polish <i>mnożyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to multiply, to increase in number</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to reproduce</li></ol><p><i>мно́жить</i> (mnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moabite (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory) mobik</li></ol><p>From <i>моб(илизо́ванный)</i> (mob(ilizóvannyj), “mobilized”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, diminutive ending).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mobilization</li></ol><p><i>мобилиза́ция</i> (mobilizácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mobilization</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mobilisation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mobilize (e.g. troops)</li><li>to mobilize, to enlist efforts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mobile phone, cellphone</li></ol><p><i>моби́льный</i> (mobílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) mobile app</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mobility</li></ol><p><i>моби́льный</i> (mobílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mobile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mobile phone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;Ukraine) the Ukrainian language</li><li>(often&#32;derogatory,&#32;Belarus) the Belarusian language</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) language</li></ol><p>From Ukrainian and Belarusian <i>мо́ва</i> (móva). Doublet of <i>молва́</i> (molvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;rare,&#32;often&#32;ironic) bad manners, bad taste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mauvais ton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;rare) ugly woman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mauvaise</i> +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Ukrainian-speaker who hates the Russian language</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any Ukrainian-speaker</li></ol><p>From <i>мо́ва</i> (móva, “[Ukrainian] language”) +&lrm; <i>говню́к</i> (govnjúk, “shit-eater”), ultimately from Ukrainian <i>мова</i> (mova, “language”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxtail millet (<i>Setaria italica</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>mohár</i>, from Serbo-Croatian <i>mòhār</i>. Doublet of <i>мыше́й</i> (myšéj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mohican</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Mohican</i> + <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mogyla</i> (“sepulchral cairn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;informal,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;military,&#32;sometimes&#32;humorous) A mogilization, ostensibly precipitating mass casualties; mobilization to the grave.</li><li>(slang,&#32;informal,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;military,&#32;sometimes&#32;humorous,&#32;specifically) The 2022 Russian mobilization.</li><li>(slang,&#32;informal,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;military,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;sometimes&#32;humorous,&#32;specifically) A forced mobilization in Ukraine.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla, “grave”) +&lrm; <i>мобилиза́ция</i> (mobilizácija, “mobilization”), as if from <i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla, “grave”) +&lrm; <i>-иза́ция</i> (-izácija, “-ization”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla): (small) grave</li></ol><p><i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla, “grave”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) burial ground, sepulchre</li><li>burial place for radioactive or toxic waste</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) eastern imperial eagle (<i>Aquila heliaca</i>)</li><li>any plant of genus <i>Peganum</i>, often specifically Syrian rue (<i>Peganum harmala</i>)</li></ol><p><i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla, “grave”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grave, burial</li><li>deathly, sepulchral</li></ol><p><i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravedigger</li><li>(figurative,&#32;rhetorical) destroyer</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) cemetery beggar</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) graverobber</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) burying beetle</li></ol><p><i>моги́ла</i> (mogíla) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>магары́ч</i> (magarýč)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) mogul</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) strong, mighty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mogǫtьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty, powerful, potent, strong</li></ol><p><i>мог-</i> (mog-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful, potent</li></ol><p><i>могу́щество</i> (mogúščestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power</li></ol><p><i>могу́щий</i> (mogúščij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mod (<i>a representative of the youth movement in the UK in the 1960s–70s</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mod</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fashion, vogue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) habit</li><li>(<i>statistics</i>) mode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) modality</li></ol><p>From <i>модальный</i> (modalʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(grammar,&#32;computing) modal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) modal verb (an auxiliary verb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modelling/modeling, simulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to model, to design, to fashion, to shape</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;engineering) to simulate</li><li>(<i>art</i>) to model, to sculpture</li></ol><p><i>моде́ль</i> (modɛ́lʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>model (<i>model car, fashion model, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) mold/mould</li></ol><p>From French <i>modèle</i>, from Italian <i>modello</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modeller, fashion designer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) model (in various senses)</li></ol><p><i>моде́ль</i> (modɛ́lʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) modem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>modem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) moderato</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a movement or piece that starts as moderato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>moderato</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moderator (someone who moderates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) modern, contemporary, fashionable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) modern style</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>moderne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modernization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modernized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to modernize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modernism (movement of art)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modernist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mujahid (a Muslim engaging in jihad)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مُجَاهِد (mujāhid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) manufacturer of fashionable dress, fashion designer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) milliner, modiste</li></ol><p>First attested in 1803. From French <i>modiste</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modification</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>modificātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to modify</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>modifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dandy, male fashionista, fashionable man, fashion plate</li></ol><p><i>мо́да</i> (móda) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fashionably</li></ol><p><i>мо́дный</i> (módnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fashionable (characteristic of or influenced by a current popular trend or style)</li></ol><p><i>мо́да</i> (móda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;programming,&#32;etc.) module</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) absolute value, modulus (non-negative, real-valued magnitude of a real or complex number)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) modulus (coefficient expressing how much of a certain property is possessed by a certain substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>module</i> or Italian <i>modulo</i>, from Latin <i>modulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;general,&#32;architecture,&#32;computing,&#32;etc.) module; modular</li><li>(relational,&#32;mathematics) absolute value, modulus</li><li>(relational,&#32;physics) modulus</li></ol><p><i>мо́дуль</i> (módulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>мо́жет быть</i> (móžet bytʹ): maybe, perhaps, possibly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) maybe, perhaps, possibly</li></ol><p>Literally, “it may be”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) juniper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moždževelь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) juniper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosaic</li><li>(<i>phytopathology</i>) mosaic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mosaico</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>musaicum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mosaic</li><li>mixed, variegated (consisting of heterogeneous elements)</li></ol><p><i>моза́ика</i> (mozáika) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mozambican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) brain; marrow</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) brains (usually as food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mozgъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;masgás</i> (“marrow; brain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mosgʰós</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>mózg</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>мозак</i> (mozak), Persian مغز (maġz), Dutch <i>merg</i>, English <i>marrow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brains <i>(food)</i></li><li>brain, head, intelligence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to think through, to think hard</li></ol><p><i>мозг</i> (mozg) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brain</li><li>cerebral</li></ol><p><i>мозг</i> (mozg) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) brain stem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;informal) shrink (<i>psychiatrist</i> or <i>psychoanalyst</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a bothersome, captious, nitpicky person; a pain in the arse</li></ol><p><i>мозг</i> (mozg, “brain”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ёб</i> (job)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) amygdala (region of the brain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cerebellum</li></ol><p><i>мозжечо́к</i> (mozžečók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cerebellum</li></ol><p>From <i>мозг</i> (mozg) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók); calque of Latin <i>cerebellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horny, callous</li></ol><p><i>мозо́ль</i> (mozólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>callus, corn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mozolь</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>mozol</i> and Polish <i>modzel, mozół</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) my, mine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мои</i> (moi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mojь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;majas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;móyos</i> (“my; mine”). Cognate with Old Prussian <i>mais</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) capelin</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) the Ukrainian language</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;rare) the Belarusian language</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>maiva</i>.</p><p>Corruption of мо́ва (móva), making it similar to etymology 1 above, from Ukrainian and Belarusian <i>мо́ва</i> (móva, “language”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing</li><li>sink (basin), kitchen sink</li><li>washer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washerman (a man who washes for payment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moccasin (shoe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>moccasin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мокло́к</i> (moklók)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>in four-hooved beasts either of the two projections of the ilium, the <i>spina iliaca ventralis</i> or <i>tuber coxae</i></li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare <i>мосо́л</i> (mosól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wet</li><li>to soak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мокнути</i> (moknuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moknǫti</i> (“to become/get wet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milksop (weak, easily frightened or ineffectual person), weak and pitiful-looking person</li></ol><p>Literally, “wet chicken”. Compare French <i>poule mouillée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodlouse, roly-poly, pillbug, sowbug (any species of suborder Oniscidea)</li><li>common chickweed (<i>Stellaria media</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мо́крый</i> (mókryj, “wet”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlegm</li><li>wetness, humidity</li></ol><p>From <i>мокрый</i> (mokryj) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota).</p><p>The same as Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any mushroom of the genus <i>Gomphidius</i></li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) murder</li></ol><p><i>мо́кр(ый)</i> (mókr(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, damp</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;relational) murder, killing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мокръ</i> (mokrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mokrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mokshas (Moksha people)</li><li>Moksha (a person of Moksha ethnicity)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) people of the Russian Federation.</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) Russia.</li><li>(Jainism,&#32;Hinduism) moksha</li></ol><p>From toponym <i>Мокша</i> (Mokša, “Moksha River”). The name of the river is speculated to derive from a language of Baltic extraction. First attested use in early 12th century, William of Rubruck mentions Moksha as (Russian translation) <i>Моксель</i> (Mokselʹ).</p><p>Neologism attested in at least 2019. Possibly due to the name's similarity to <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá).</p><p>Transliteration of Sanskrit <i>मोक्ष</i> (mokṣa)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мокша)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river in Mordovia and Penza, Nizhny Novgorod and Ryazan Oblasts, Russia</li><li>several settlements in Mordovia, Tatarstan and Samara Oblast, Russia</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) Russia.</li></ol><p>The name of the river is speculated to derive from a language of Baltic extraction. First attested use in early 12th century, William of Rubruck mentions Moksha as (Russian translation) <i>Моксель</i> (Mokselʹ).</p><p>Neologism etymology is possibly due to the name's similarity to <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moksha</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Moksha (relating to the Mordvinian people or their Fennic language, spoken in the western and southern parts of Mordovia, an independent republic within Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jetty, mole, breakwater, pier (protective structure to protect areas from sea waves)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) he/she says, they say; apparently, purportedly, as if (<i>used informally to mark reported speech, often when the speaker suspects the said is not true or to merely indicate that the piece following refers to the past even if the wording suggests the present</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>môle</i>.</p><p>From <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ, “to say”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rumour/rumor, talk, hearsay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мълва</i> (mŭlva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъlva</i>; cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>млъва</i> (mlŭva, “noise, mourning”), Ukrainian <i>мо́ва</i> (móva, “language”), Belarusian <i>мо́ва</i> (móva, “language”), Polish <i>mowa</i> (“language”), Bulgarian <i>мълва́</i> (mǎlvá, “rumour”), Czech <i>mluva</i>. Doublet of <i>мо́ва</i> (móva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to say, to declare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъlviti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moldavian, Moldovan (person from Moldova or of Moldovan descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Moldavian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moldavian (language)</li></ol><p><i>Молда́вия</i> (Moldávija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) public prayer, church service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) molecule</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>molécule</i>, from Latin <i>molecula</i> (“a molecule”), diminutive of <i>moles</i> (“a mass”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>molecular</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>molekular</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oratory (private chapel)</li></ol><p>From <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja), with unexplained vowel change *i &gt; e before the suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>molybdenum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>molybdaenum</i>, from <i>molybdaena</i> (“any of various substances resembling lead”), from Ancient Greek <i>μολύβδαινα</i> (molúbdaina, “a plummet, piece of lead”), from <i>μόλυβδος</i> (mólubdos, “lead; graphite”), from an Anatolian word cognate with Lydian <i>𐤪𐤠𐤭𐤦𐤥𐤣𐤠</i> (mariwda, “dark”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;morkʷ-iyo-</i>, from a root <i>&#42;morkʷ-</i> (“dark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) molybdenum</li></ol><p><i>молибде́н</i> (molibdén) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;modlitva</i>, from Old East Slavic <i>молитва</i> (molitva) from <i>&#42;modliti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tva</i>. By surface analysis, <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) prayer book</li><li>(animate,&#32;literary,&#32;dated) devout worshipper, <i>feminine equivalent:</i> <i>моли́твенница</i> (molítvennica)</li></ol><p><i>моли́твен(ный)</i> (molítven(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>молитвьникъ</i> (molitvĭnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prayer</li><li>prayerful</li></ol><p><i>моли́тва</i> (molítva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implore, to entreat, to beseech</li><li>to pray, to beg</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>молити</i> (moliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;modlìti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;maldī́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meldʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray (to petition a higher being)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>молитисꙗ</i> (molitisja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;modliti sę</i> (“to pray”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figuratively) to fade, to grow quiet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьlknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) synonym of <i>торго́вый центр</i> (torgóvyj centr, “shopping centre”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mollusc</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mollusque</i>. Several steps omitting, from Latin <i>mollis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mollusc</li></ol><p><i>моллю́ск</i> (molljúsk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with lightning speed</li></ol><p><i>молниено́сный</i> (molnijenósnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lightning fast</li><li>hyperacute</li></ol><p><i>мо́лния</i> (mólnija) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Macedonian <i>молневит</i> (molnevit), Serbo-Croatian <i>муњевит</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Macedonian <i>секавичен</i> (sekavičen) (&lt; <i>секавица</i> (sekavica)), Polish <i>błyskawiczny</i>, <i>błyskawicowy</i> (&lt; <i>błyskawica</i>), German <i>blitzschnell</i>, <i>blitzgeschwind</i>, <i>blitzartig</i> (&lt; <i>Blitz</i>, whence <i>блиц</i> (blic)), Dutch <i>bliksemsnel</i>, Swedish <i>blixtsnabb</i>, Italian <i>fulminee</i>, Spanish <i>fulminante</i> (<&lt; Latin <i>fulmen</i>, akin to <i>fulgur</i>, whence English <i>fulgurant</i>), Norwegian Bokmål <i>lynrask</i>, Danish <i>lynhurtig</i>.</p><p>Outside PIE compare Mongolian <i>цахим</i> (caxim), Vietnamese <i>chớp nhoáng</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li><li>zipper, zip, zip fastener</li><li>express telegram</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cigarette lighter</li><li>Molniya 1 (the first Soviet communications satellite)</li><li>Molnija (Soviet and Russian watch brand)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мълниꙗ</i> (mŭlnija), <i>мълнии</i> (mŭlnii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъlnьji</i> or <i>&#42;mьlnьji</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;mъldnьji</i> or <i>&#42;mъldni</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mild-n-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥dʰ-n-</i> (“lightning”).</p><p>Slavic cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>му́ња</i> (munja), Bulgarian <i>мъ́лния</i> (mǎ́lnija), Polabian <i>måuńa</i> and Kashubian <i>môłniô</i>. Probable cognates outside Slavic include Latvian <i>milna</i> (“hammer of Pērkons”), Old Prussian <i>mealde</i>, Welsh <i>mellt</i> (“lightning”), Old Norse <i>Mjǫllnir</i> (“hammer of Thor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) bride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>молодёжь</i> (molodjóžʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój, “young”): very young</li></ol><p><i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become younger</li></ol><p><i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine fellow, fine girl</li><li>brave man, clever man (usually a young man)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>attaboy!, attagirl!, well done!, bravo!, good for you!, good for her!, good for him!</li></ol><p><i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravado</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) aye-green, houseleek <i>(Sempervivum)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to look younger</li></ol><p><i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) young animals, cubs</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) underwood, saplings</li><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) offspring, youngsters, young generation</li></ol><p>From <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-ня́к</i> (-nják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>youthfully, in a young manner</li></ol><p><i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newlywed, a newly married person (<i>usually plural</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>молодой</i> (molodoj) +&lrm; <i>жена́</i> (žená).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li><li>youthful</li><li>new</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥dus</i> (“soft, weak”), whence English <i>mild</i>, <i>melt</i> and <i>mollify</i>. Doublet of <i>младо́й</i> (mladój), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man, lad, boy, guy</li><li>young man, sir, master (an informal form of address, sometimes used to address not so young men or little boys)</li></ol><p>Literally, “young man”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth (state of being young)</li><li>youth (period in life)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldostь</i>. Equivalent to <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój, “young”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;rare) alternative form of <i>молоде́ц&#33;</i> (molodéc&#33;)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young man (usually perceived as suspicious or dangerous)</li></ol><p>From <i>молоде́ц</i> (molodéc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) synonym of <i>молоде́ц</i> (molodéc, “attaboy, attagirl, etc.”)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>attaboy!, attagirl!, well done!, fine fellow!, good boy!, good girl!, etc. (used to show encouragement or approval to a male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young shoots</li><li>whitebait, young fish</li><li>young animals</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) youth, young people</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>youth, youthful</li></ol><p><i>молодёжь</i> (molodjóžʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the young, youth, young people <i>(collective)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>молодежь</i> (molodežĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moldežь</i>, from <i>&#42;moldъ</i> (“young”) + <i>&#42;-ežь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-ёжь</i> (-jóžʹ). Compare Polish <i>młodzież</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young-looking, youthful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>мо́лодо</i> (mólodo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colostrum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>молозиво</i> (molozivo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melzivo</i>, from <i>&#42;melsti</i> (“to milk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue lettuce (used in the sense of молокан татарский)</li><li>Molokan</li></ol><p>From <i>молоко́</i> (molokó).</p><p>From <i>молоко́</i> (molokó), so named for the refusal of the Molokans to give up dairy consumption during fast days.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a Molokan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) thick fog</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(aviation,&#32;colloquial) thick clouds through which one is flying</li></ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the blank area around a shooting target, worth no points</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melko</i>. Doublet of <i>мле́ко</i> (mléko), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>молоко́м</i> (molokóm).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipsqueak</li></ol><p><i>молоко́</i> (molokó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-сос</i> (-sos), combining form of <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>молоке́</i> (moloké).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a pot wrapped into birchbark for shaking butter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melstvъ</i>. Compare мо́ло́ст (mólóst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large hammer (<i>e.g. a smith's forging hammer</i>), mallet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moltъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;molh₂-to-</i>, from <i>&#42;melh₂-</i>. Doublet of <i>млат</i> (mlat), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. Cognate with Latin <i>malleus</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>кий</i> (kij), <i>кия́нка</i> (kijánka), <i>кува́лда</i> (kuválda) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshing machine, thresher</li></ol><p><i>молотить</i> (molotitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thresh, to flail</li><li>to hit, to beat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat up someone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moltiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) hammer, mallet, maul</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) good guy <i>(used only in nominative, altered form of молодец)</i></li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nice one!, well done!</li></ol><p><i>мо́лот</i> (mólot) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) malleus (middle ear bone)</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>молото́к</i> (molotók, “hammer”)</li></ol><p><i>молото́к</i> (molotók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind, to mill (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk nonsense, bang on about</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mélˀtei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) threshing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>молотьба</i> (molotĭba), formally from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moltьba</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;moltiti</i>) or <i>&#42;meltьba</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;melti</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) sperm, cum, jizz</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) an unpleasant man, a bastard</li></ol><p>Blend of Greek <i>μαλακία</i> (malakía, “wanking”) +&lrm; <i>молоко́</i> (molokó, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spurge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melčajь</i>, further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to, made of, or characteristic of spurges.</li><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) euphorbiaceous, belonging to the family Euphorbiaceae, which contains the spurges, among others.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) A plant of the family Euphorbiaceae, a spurge (<i>sensu lato</i>).</li></ol><p>From <i>молоча́й</i> (moločáj, “spurge”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collectively,&#32;colloquial) dairy</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>моло́чные проду́кты</i> (molóčnyje prodúkty, “dairy products”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>молоко́</i> (molokó)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) jelly (a liquid that forms in the goiter or in the special glands of some birds and insects for feeding nestlings or larvae)</li><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) cream</li></ol><p><i>молоко́</i> (molokó) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) mammary gland (milk-secreting gland)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milkman</li><li>milk container</li></ol><p><i>молоко́</i> (molokó) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;pathology) candidiasis, thrush, milk fever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>milk-white</li></ol><p><i>моло́чн(ый)</i> (molóčn(yj), “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj, “white”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) milk, dairy; lactic</li><li>milky</li><li>milk-fed</li><li>milk-white</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;melčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>молоко́</i> (molokó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>мле́чный</i> (mléčnyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk tooth, baby tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently, tacitly</li><li>speechlessly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>молчали́вый</i> (molčalívyj): most taciturn</li></ol><p><i>молчали́в(ый)</i> (molčalív(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reticently</li><li>tacitly</li></ol><p><i>молчали́вый</i> (molčalívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taciturn (untalkative, silent)</li></ol><p><i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence (action or state of remaining silent; that is, abstention from speech, utterances, or the use of the voice)</li></ol><p><i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep silent, to be silent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьlčati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silent man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes moth (Tineidae)</li><li>(music,&#32;dated) minor</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) mole, gram molecule</li><li>(<i>timber industry</i>) a method of timber rafting in which logs are not tied together</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;dated) minor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>моль</i> (molĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plea, request, entreaty, supplication</li><li>prayer</li></ol><p>From <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьба́</i> (-ʹbá), from Old East Slavic <i>молити</i> (moliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;modlìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Malbrett</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Molieresque</li></ol><p><i>Молье́р</i> (Molʹjér) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>time</i>) moment</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) point, detail, aspect</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) moment</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) moment</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>mōmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) torque, moment of force</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>instantly, promptly, momentarily</li></ol><p><i>момента́льный</i> (momentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>instant, instantaneous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>momental</i> (“right now”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>momme (unit of weight (3.75 grams) used for pearls, paper, and silk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>匁<rp>(</rp><rt>もんめ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>monme</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>momo, Tibetan dumpling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tibetan <i>མོག་མོག</i> (mog mog), from Mandarin <i>馍馍</i> (mómo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Momordica</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Momordica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monarch, sovereign (ruler)</li><li>monarch butterfly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Monarch</i>, itself derived from Latin <i>monarcha</i>, in turn derived from Ancient Greek <i>μόναρχος</i> (mónarkhos). Attested since the 16th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monarchist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monarchical</li><li>monarchist</li></ol><p><i>мона́рхия</i> (monárxija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monarchy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>monarchya</i>, from Latin <i>monarchia</i>, itself from Ancient Greek <i>μοναρχία</i> (monarkhía). Attested since 1659.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) monastery</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) convent</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cloister</li><li>monastic, cloistral</li></ol><p><i>монасты́рь</i> (monastýrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monastery, convent</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>μοναστήριον</i> (monastḗrion), converged with words ending with "-ырь" (cf. пустырь). Further from Ancient Greek <i>μόνος</i> (mónos).</p><p>Compare natively formed Serbo-Croatian <i>самостан</i>, Slovene <i>samostan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>монасты́рь</i> (monastýrʹ): (small) monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>монасты́р(ь)</i> (monastýr(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Russian Old Church Slavonic <i>монахъ</i> (monaxŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>μοναχός</i> (monakhós, “monk, (<i>originally</i>) solitary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monastic</li></ol><p><i>мона́х</i> (monáx) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkhood; monasticism</li></ol><p><i>мона́х</i> (monáx) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nun</li></ol><p><i>мона́х</i> (monáx) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mongol, Mongolian <i>(person)</i></li><li>Mongolian <i>(horse)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Mongolian <i>ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ</i> (mongɣol), from Middle Mongol <i>ᠮᠣᠩᠬᠣᠯ</i> (mongqol).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humanities</i>) Mongolist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manjuristics (study of the Mongolic languages)</li></ol><p>From <i>монго́л</i> (mongól) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mongolian (relating to Mongolia or its peoples, languages, or cultures)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mongolian (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>монго́л</i> (mongól) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>moneta</i>, from Latin <i>monēta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monetization (the conversion of something into money)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>моне́та</i> (monéta): (small) coin</li></ol><p><i>моне́та</i> (monéta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monetary</li></ol><p><i>моне́та</i> (monéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;religion) monism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of traditional necklace made of coins, beads, colored stones etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;monisto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) monitor, a screen that displays graphics and images</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) hydraulic giant, hydraulic monitor, hydraulic gun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>monitor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monitoring</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>monitoring</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) monoarsenide (any arsenide containing a single atom of arsenic in each molecule or unit cell)</li></ol><p><i>моно-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>арсени́д</i> (arseníd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;Ukraine) one-party majority in parliament, mostly in Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>моно</i> (mono) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>большинство</i> (bolʹšinstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monogamy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>in Russia, a town with economy that is based solely or predominantly on a single factory or enterprise; company town, one-factory town, single-industry town</li></ol><p><i>моно-</i> (mono-, “mono-”) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod, “town”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monoclonal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monocle (a single lens)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) monoculture (cultivation of a single crop at a time)</li></ol><p><i>моно-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monolith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monolithic</li></ol><p><i>моноли́т</i> (monolít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monologue/monolog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>monologue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monomaniac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) monomer</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone, only, sole, single”) + <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part, share, portion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monopolization</li></ol><p><i>монополиз(и́ровать)</i> (monopoliz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monopolist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monopoly (<i>various senses</i>)</li><li>exclusive domain</li><li>(historical,&#32;pre-Soviet Russia) a government liquor store</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>monopōlium</i> via Polish <i>monopolia</i> or New High German <i>Monopolia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μονοπώλιον</i> (monopṓlion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) monopole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a government liquor store</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) vodka</li></ol><p>From <i>монопо́лия</i> (monopólija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) monopoly</li><li>exclusive</li><li>(relational,&#32;physics) monopole</li></ol><p><i>монопо́л(ия)</i> (monopól(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>монопо́ль</i> (monopólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monorail (a railroad system where the trains run on one rail&#59; a train running on a single rail)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) monorail</li></ol><p><i>моноре́льс</i> (monorélʹs) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) monotheism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) monotheist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) monotheistic</li></ol><p><i>монотеи́ст</i> (monotɛíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) monotypic (relating to or exhibiting monotypy)</li></ol><p>From <i>моноти́п</i> (monotíp) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>monotonously</li><li>in a monotone</li></ol><p><i>моното́нный</i> (monotónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monotonousness, monotony</li></ol><p><i>моното́нный</i> (monotónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monotonous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) monophthong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monochrome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to the state of Mon, the Mon people, the Mon language, or the Mon script.</li></ol><p><i>мо́н</i> (món, “Mon, a people and state of Myanmar”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) monstera (plant of the genus <i>Monstera</i>, endemic to Central and South America, some species of which are commonly used as houseplants)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i><i>Monstera</i></i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster (<i>a terrifying and dangerous creature</i>)</li><li>monster, fiend (<i>an evil person</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>monstre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembling, assemblage</li><li>mounting</li><li>montage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>montage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>installer, assembler, fitter</li></ol><p><i>монта́ж</i> (montáž) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly, fitting</li><li>(relational,&#32;cinematography) editing, cutting</li></ol><p><i>монта́ж</i> (montáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assemble (to put together)</li><li>to mount, to arrange (cinema)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>montieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowbar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>installer, assembler, fitter</li><li>electrician, lineman</li></ol><p>From <i>монт(и́ровать)</i> (mont(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monument</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monumental</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>monumental</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moped</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>moped</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) moped</li></ol><p><i>мопе́д</i> (mopéd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pug (<i>small breed of dog</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mops</i> or Dutch <i>mops</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) pestilence, plague, murrain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>моръ</i> (morŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moralizer, moralist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moralist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>moraliste</i>. Further from Latin <i>mōrālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morals; morality</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lecturing, reprimanding, telling someone off</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>mōrālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>morally (in a moral manner)</li><li>psychologically, mentally, emotionally</li></ol><p><i>мора́льный</i> (morálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moral and psychological</li></ol><p><i>мора́льный</i> (morálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>психологи́ческий</i> (psixologíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moral (relating to principles of right and wrong)</li><li>psychological, mental, emotional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moratorium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>morātōrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>morbid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morgue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blink</li><li>to wink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blink</li><li>to wink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face of an animal, muzzle, snout</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) face of a person (<i>usually ugly</i>), mug</li></ol><p>From late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrda</i>, possibly from an Iranian language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cute face, pretty face</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cutie (someone with a pretty face, especially a child or woman)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) breed of large hunting dog, similar to the extinct Medelyan but smaller</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) dog of this breed</li></ol><p>From <i>морда́ха</i> (mordáxa, “cutie, cute face”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) or directly from <i>мо́рда</i> (mórda, “muzzle, snout, mug”) +&lrm; <i>-а́шка</i> (-áška) (sense of "breed of dog" probably derived directly from <i>мо́рда</i> (mórda)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fist fight, scuffle (the one where people are beaten on the face)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) an impudent/insolent person; a boor; a cad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мо́рда</i> (mórda): (small) muzzle, face (of an animal)</li></ol><p><i>мо́рда</i> (mórda) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) mare (darker area on the Moon's surface)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a sea of, a large amount of, a large number of</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;móri</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>море</i> (more), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moře</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;móri</i>, whence English <i>mer-</i> and <i>marsh</i> and through Latin <i>marine</i>, <i>maritime</i>.</p><p>The astronomical sense is a calque of New Latin <i>mare</i> (“sea”), with which it is cognate.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lazarev Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laptev Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oceanography, science about seas and oceans</li></ol><p><i>мо́ре</i> (móre) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oceanographical</li></ol><p><i>мореве́д(ение)</i> (morevéd(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moraine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>moraine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) navigation, seafaring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seafarer (one who travels by sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) seafood</li></ol><p><i>мо́ре</i> (móre) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>проду́кт</i> (prodúkt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seafood</li></ol><p><i>мо́ре</i> (móre) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>проду́кты</i> (prodúkty)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walrus</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) winter swimmer, ice swimmer</li></ol><p>A Sami borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Samic <i>&#42;moršë</i>. Compare Finnish <i>mursu</i>, Skolt Sami <i>moršša</i>, English <i>morse</i>, and possibly the second element of translingual <i>Rosmarus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) female winter swimmer, ice swimmer</li></ol><p><i>морж</i> (morž) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) walrus</li></ol><p><i>морж</i> (morž, “walrus”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby walrus</li></ol><p><i>морж</i> (morž) +&lrm; <i>-онок</i> (-onok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mordant, chemical substance used to sear a surface</li></ol><p><i>мори́ть</i> (morítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exterminate, to kill with the help of a poison</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to exhaust, to wear out</li><li>to stain <i>(paint with mordant)</i></li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мор</i> (mor) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) carrot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carrot (root vegetable, usually orange)</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) diminutive of <i>морко́вь</i> (morkóvʹ); carrots</li></ol><p><i>морко́вь</i> (morkóvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carrot</li></ol><p><i>морко́вь</i> (morkóvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) carrot (plant)</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) carrots (roots of the plant used for eating)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mormon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mormyshka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezer</li><li>ice cream parlor</li><li>ice cream maker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ice cream</li></ol><p><i>моро́женое</i> (moróženoje) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>моро́женое</i> (moróženoje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice cream</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>моро́женый</i> (moróženyj, “frozen”), from <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ, “to freeze”) + <i>-еный</i> (-enyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice cream vendor</li></ol><p><i>моро́жен(ое)</i> (moróžen(oje)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frozen</li><li>chilled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezing weather, frost</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>морозъ</i> (morozŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morzъ</i>. Doublet of <i>мраз</i> (mraz), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>моро́з</i> (moróz): (light) frost</li></ol><p><i>моро́з</i> (moróz) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezer (part of a refrigerator)</li></ol><p><i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ, “to freeze”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezer (stand-alone appliance)</li></ol><p>From <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ, “to freeze, congeal”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze, to congeal</li><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Donbass,&#32;dialect) to ignore</li></ol><p><i>моро́з</i> (moróz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frosty</li></ol><p><i>моро́з</i> (moróz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare <i>мразный</i> (mraznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freezeproof</li></ol><p><i>моро́з</i> (moróz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misconstruction of <i>мразо́та</i> (mrazóta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freezeproof</li></ol><p><i>моро́з</i> (moróz) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>усто́йчивый</i> (ustójčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>моро́з</i> (moróz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) darkness</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) clouds, fog</li></ol><p>By pleophony from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>мрак</i> (mrak), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) hassle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drizzle</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be nervous, to fuss, to be slow, to act up, talk/do nonsense</li></ol><p>Compare Slovene <i>mršéti</i> (“to drizzle”), <i>mŕščati</i> (“to spout, to drizzle”), Latvian <i>mer̂ga</i>, <i>mar̂ga</i>, <i>mārgа</i> (“drizzle”), <i>merguôt</i>, <i>mirdzinât</i> (“to drizzle”). Also linked with Ancient Greek <i>βρέχω</i> (brékhō, “to wet”), <i>βροχετός</i> (brokhetós, “rain”). Sometimes also associated with Sanskrit <i>मर्षति</i> (marṣati, “to sprinkle, to spray”) or, alternatively, Russian <i>морга́ть</i> (morgátʹ, “to blink, to wink”). Association with Russian <i>ме́рзкий</i> (mérzkij, “vile”), Russian <i>мёрзнуть</i> (mjórznutʹ, “to freeze”), Russian <i>моро́з</i> (moróz, “frost”) is doubtful. Not related to Polish <i>zmierzch</i> (“dusk”), <i>mierzchnąć</i> (“to become dark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drizzle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool, to trick, to dupe, to pull the wool over someone's eyes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morčiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull someone's leg</li></ol><p>Literally, “to trick (someone's) head”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>мо́рочный</i> (móročnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) murky, hazy, dull, gloomy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) troublesome, drawn-out</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) murky, dull, gloomy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mȍrčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мо́рок</i> (mórok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mòrčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>моро́ка</i> (moróka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloudberry, <i>Rubus chamaemorus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from some Finnic source; compare Finnish <i>muurain</i> (“cloudberry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) marine (member of a marine corps)</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>морско́й пехоти́нец</i> (morskój pexotínec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mors (a traditional Russian non-carbonated drink made from wild berries, usually lingonberry and&#47;or cranberry)</li></ol><p>Attested in the 16th-century <i>Domostroy</i> as "вишневый морс" and "малиновый морс". Usually associated with <i>мороси́ть</i> (morosítʹ, “to drizzle”), but compare Romanian <i>mursă</i> (“mead, juice”). Association with Lithuanian <i>smarsas</i> (“fat, smell of greasy meat”) is doubtful. Alternatively derived from <i>Moosbeere</i> or akin to <i>моро́шка</i> (moróška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seasickness (a feeling of nausea caused by the motion of a ship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starfish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea snake</li></ol><p><i>морско́й</i> (morskój, “sea”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>змея́</i> (zmejá, “snake”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European plaice (Pleuronectes platessa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nautical chart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guinea pig</li></ol><p>Probably a calque of Polish <i>świnka morska</i>, which in turn is a calque of German <i>Meerschweinchen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porpoise (cetacean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barnacle in the suborder Lepadomorpha (marine crustacean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea turtle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiralty law (area of law that deals with ships at sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abalone (edible univalve mollusc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sea</li><li>nautical</li><li>maritime</li><li>naval</li><li>seaside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мо́ре</i> (móre) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moře</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;móri</i>, whence English <i>marine</i> and <i>maritime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>port terminal, passenger terminal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barnacle in the suborder Balanomorpha (marine crustacean)</li></ol><p><i>морско́й</i> (morskój, “sea”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>жёлудь</i> (žóludʹ, “acorn”), literally “sea acorn”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arachnology</i>) A mite of the family Halacaridae, a halacarid.</li></ol><p><i>морско́й</i> (morskój, “marine, water”) +&lrm; <i>кле́щ</i> (kléšč, “mite”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea horse (fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fur seal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea lion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rockfish (<i>Sebastes</i> sp.)</li></ol><p><i>морско́й</i> (morskój, “sea”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>о́кунь</i> (ókunʹ, “perch, bass”), literally “sea bass”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider crab (species of crab)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant seal (mammal of the genus <i>Mirounga</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angler (angler fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common sole (<i>Solea solea</i>)</li><li>tonguefish (Cynoglossidae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea urchin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) mortar (heavy, large-bore, short-barreled cannon used for indirect fire)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) attendant of sick people during epidemics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) morph, morpheme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morpheme</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>morphème</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) morphemics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) morpheme; morphemic</li></ol><p>From <i>морфе́ма</i> (morféma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morphine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>morphological</li></ol><p><i>морфоло́гия</i> (morfológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morphology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) pile (<i>of velvet or similar fabric</i>)</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional) tassel, tuft (<i>especially on dresses and horse tack</i>)</li></ol><p>Metathesis of <i>мохо́р, мохра́</i> (moxór, moxrá) (old form of <i>махра́</i> (maxrá)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrinkle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrščina</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>маршчы́на</i> (marščýna), Czech <i>mrština</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>мръщина</i> (mrŭština), Ukrainian <i>морщина</i> (morščyna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrinkled, puckered</li></ol><p><i>морщи́на</i> (morščína) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>морщи́на</i> (morščína): (small) wrinkle</li></ol><p><i>морщи́на</i> (morščína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrinkle (one's nose)</li><li>to purse (one's lips)</li><li>to furrow (one's forehead, one's brow)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to crease, to pucker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъrščiti</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>мръ́щя</i> (mrǎ́štja), Czech <i>smrsknout</i>, Slovene <i>mŕščiti</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>съмърсканъ</i> (sŭmŭrskanŭ), Polish <i>marszczyć</i>, Ukrainian <i>мо́рщити</i> (mórščyty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrinkle (to pucker or become uneven or irregular)</li><li>to knit one's brows</li><li>to make a wry face, to wince</li><li>to crease, to wrinkle up (of clothes)</li></ol><p><i>мо́рщить</i> (mórščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мор</i> (mor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaman, sailor, mariner (a member of the crew of a vessel&#59; a person experienced in nautical matters)</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) sea breeze (a strong, biting wind that blows in from the sea around the mouths of rivers)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mořakъ</i> (by surface analysis, <i>мо́ре</i> (móre, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moře</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;móri</i>, whence English <i>marine</i> and <i>maritime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>моря́к</i> (morják)</li></ol><p><i>моря́к</i> (morják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Belarus,&#32;ethnic slur) a Muscovite, someone from Moscow or the surrounding region.</li><li>(by&#32;extension,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;ethnic slur) any Russian person, Russki.</li><li>(Ukraine,&#32;archaic) any Imperial Russian soldier</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Ruthenian <i>москаль</i> (moskalʹ), from a contraction of Ottoman Turkish مسقولو (moskovlu, “Russian”, literally “Muscovite”). Its recent negative association originated from referring to soldiers from the Imperial Russian Army, and later applied to the Soviet Army and Russians in general. By surface analysis, <i>Москва</i> (Moskva) +&lrm; <i>-аль</i> (-alʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Muscovite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muscovite <i>(resident of Moscow)</i></li><li>Moskvich <i>(a common private passenger car)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandfly, phlebotomine (any fly of the subfamily Phlebotominae)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) mosquito, gnat</li><li>P-270 Moskit (Soviet supersonic ramjet powered anti-ship cruise missile)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>musca</i> + <i>-ittus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sandfly, phlebotomine (any fly of the subfamily Phlebotominae), mosquito, gnat</li></ol><p><i>моски́т</i> (moskít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moscovium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Moscow</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Muscovy</li></ol><p><i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>моско́вский</i> (moskóvskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Moscow All-Union State Trust for the Processing of Agricultural Products (<i>from 1922 to 1937</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>Моско́вский (Всесою́зный госуда́рственный трест по перерабо́тке) сельскохозя́йственных проду́ктов</i> (Moskóvskij (Vsesojúznyj gosudárstvennyj trest po pererabótke) selʹskoxozjájstvennyx prodúktov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridge</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) bridge (a prosthesis replacing one or several adjacent teeth)</li><li>axle</li><li>(gymnastics,&#32;dance) backbend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мостъ</i> (mostŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mostъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mastás</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мост</i> (most): (small) bridge; footbridge</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) (captain's) bridge</li></ol><p><i>мост</i> (most) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pave</li></ol><p><i>мост</i> (most) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planked gangway or footbridge over a swamp, river, ravine, etc.</li><li>boardwalk</li><li>pier, jetty</li><li>walkway around construction areas</li></ol><p><i>мост</i> (most) +&lrm; <i>-ки́</i> (-kí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roadway, carriage way</li><li>pavement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bridge</li></ol><p><i>мост</i> (most) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>мост</i> (most, “bridge”): footbridge</li></ol><p><i>мост</i> (most) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мост</i> (most). bridge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spendthrift, prodigal</li></ol><p>From <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ, “to reel, wind, squander”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coiler, reel, winder (a device or machine for coiling, winding, or unwinding something)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) youth gang, crew</li></ol><p>From <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reel, to wind</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rewind, to fast forward (e.g. tape on a cassette recorder)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wave, to shake</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squander, to waste</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do (time - in prison)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;motati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>)to fuss/rush about</li><li><i>idiomatic</i>:</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go frequently, to make frequent trips/visits <i>(to a place)</i></li></ol><p><i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motel (type of hotel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>motel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tune</li><li>motive, motif</li><li>motive, cause, reason (that which incites to action)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Motiv</i> or directly from French <i>motif</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>mōtīvus</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>подви́гнуть</i> (podvígnutʹ), <i>сподви́гнуть</i> (spodvígnutʹ), <i>дви́житель</i> (dvížitelʹ) akin to <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motivation; motivational</li></ol><p><i>мотива́ция</i> (motivácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motivation</li></ol><p>From French <i>motivation</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>mōtīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-motivated, grounded, reasonable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give reasons <i>(for)</i>, to justify <i>(by)</i>, to motivate <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>motivieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motivation</li></ol><p><i>мотиви́ровать</i> (motivírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motivational</li></ol><p><i>мотивиро́вка</i> (motiviróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go on an outing <i>(to a place)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash <i>(there and back)</i></li></ol><p><i>мотну́ть</i> (motnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purse, bunt (of seine)</li></ol><p><i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>motor (referring to something motorized)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-wheel tractor, walking tractor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small all-terrain vehicle, small ATV</li></ol><p><i>мото-</i> (moto-) +&lrm; <i>вездехо́д</i> (vezdexód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reel, swift <i>(to hold yarn)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;motovidlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skein; hank; clew; coil; ravel</li></ol><p><i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>biker club</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor, engine</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) taxi, motorcar</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) heart</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) action!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>motor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motorized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) motor skills, motor functions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worker servicing/repairing (automobile, aircraft or watercraft) engines or motors</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) motorboat (with an outboard engine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motor, engine</li><li>(relational,&#32;physiology) motor</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) lively, agile, nimble</li></ol><p><i>мото́р</i> (motór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scooter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мото́р</i> (motór): small motor</li></ol><p><i>мото́р</i> (motór) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motorized rifle (equipped with automatic weapons and armored vehicles highly capable of moving cross-country)</li></ol><p><i>мото-</i> (moto-) +&lrm; <i>стрелко́вый</i> (strelkóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motorcycle, motorbike, bike</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>motocycle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>мотоци́кл</i> (motocíkl)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motorcycle, motorbike</li></ol><p><i>мотоцикле́т</i> (motociklét, “motorcycle (dated)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motorcyclist</li></ol><p><i>мотоци́кл</i> (motocíkl) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) railway train</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>motrice</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoe, mattock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мотыка</i> (motyka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;motyka</i>, cognate to Latin <i>mateola</i> (“implement for digging in the soil”), English <i>mattock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth (<i>the small variety that swarms at night</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) butterfly, moth (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p>From a diminutive of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;motyľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъxъ</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;músos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mohair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mohair</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mojito (Cuban cocktail)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>mojito</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hairy, furry, wooly</li><li>shaggy, unkempt</li></ol><p>From obsolete <i>мохна́</i> (moxná, “bundle of hair or feathers”) + <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruffled fur or feathers of animals or birds</li><li>ruffled or unkempt hair of people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mozzarella</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>mozzarella</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mozartian</li></ol><p><i>Мо́царт</i> (Mócart) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>моча</i> (moča), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moča</i> / <i>&#42;močь</i> (“wetness, puddle, urine”), from <i>&#42;mok-</i> + <i>&#42;-ja</i>, <i>&#42;-jь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mok-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body scrubber, sponge, loofah</li><li>wisp of bast</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate) girl, chick, broad, slut, whore</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>моча́ло</i> (močálo, “bast”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bast (fibre made from the phloem of certain plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urea (CO(NH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>urinary, uric</li></ol><p><i>моча́</i> (močá) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) urinary bladder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urination (the process of eliminating liquid waste from the body)</li></ol><p><i>моча́</i> (močá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>испуска́ть</i> (ispuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>urinary, urethral</li></ol><p><i>моча́</i> (močá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>испуска́ть</i> (ispuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) urethra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) genitourinary (of, or relating to the genital and urinary systems of the body)</li></ol><p>From <i>моча́</i> (močá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>полово́й</i> (polovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ureter</li></ol><p><i>моча́</i> (močá) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ток</i> (tok) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brutal fight (a physical confrontation between people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to moisten, to douse, to humidify</li><li>to soak, to steep</li><li>to dike</li><li>to dunk</li><li>to pickle, to marinate</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill, to waste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;močiti</i>. Cognates include German <i>machen</i>, Ancient Greek <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō), Latin <i>mācerō</i>, Lithuanian <i>makonė</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urinate, to pee, to water, to micturate</li><li>to get wet, to get soaked</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to fight, to scuffle</li></ol><p><i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wetting, soaking</li><li>earlobe</li></ol><p><i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) (see <i>-мкнуть</i> (-mknutʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) can, be able to</li><li>may</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, might</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moťi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;megʰ-</i>, whence English <i>might</i>. Cognate to Ancient Greek <i>μῆχος</i> (mêkhos, “means, remedy”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maganą</i> (“to be able, may”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moťь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mógʰtis</i>, <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to allow, be able, help”). Cognate to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtiz</i> and its descendants like English <i>might</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>мощь</i> (moščʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickled apple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindler, cheat, cheater, fraud</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>мошна́</i> (mošná, “money bag”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), originally "<i>cutpurse</i>".</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>Beutelschneider</i> (&lt; <i>Beutel</i>), Czech <i>taškář</i> (&lt; <i>taška</i>).</p><p>For the original sense also see <i>карма́нник</i> (karmánnik) (&lt; <i>карма́н</i> (karmán)), English <i>pickpocket</i>, Czech <i>kapsář</i> (&lt; <i>kapsa</i>), Latin <i>crumēniseca</i> (&lt; <i>crumēna</i> + <i>secō</i> + <i>-a</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fraudulent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindle, fraud, cheating</li></ol><p><i>моше́нник</i> (mošénnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midge (a kind of gnat or gnatlike fly)</li><li>swarm of midges</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъši</i>. Related to <i>му́ха</i> (múxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collective) midges, swarms of midges</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) pouch, purse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mošьna</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mak-</i> (“small (leather) bag or pouch”), see also Lithuanian <i>mãkas</i>, Old High German <i>maga</i>, German <i>Magen</i> (“stomach”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) scrotum</li></ol><p><i>мошна́</i> (mošná, “pouch, purse (<i>dated</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scrotal</li></ol><p><i>мошо́нка</i> (mošónka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) reliquary</li></ol><p><i>мощи</i> (mošči, “relics”) +&lrm; <i>-евик</i> (-evik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) relics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мощи</i> (mošti), plural of <i>мошть</i> (moštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mogťi</i>. Cognate to <i>мощь</i> (moščʹ), <i>мочь</i> (močʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>мощный</i> (moščnyj)</li></ol><p><i>мощь</i> (moščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нее</i> (-neje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>мощный</i> (moščnyj)</li></ol><p><i>мощь</i> (moščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ней</i> (-nej)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>мо́щный</i> (móščnyj): most powerful</li></ol><p><i>мо́щный</i> (móščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mightily, extremely</li></ol><p><i>мо́щный</i> (móščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, capacity, rating</li><li>(<i>set theory</i>) cardinality</li></ol><p>From <i>мо́щный</i> (móščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty, powerful</li></ol><p><i>мощь</i> (moščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, force, power, strength</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мощь</i> (moštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;moťь</i>. Compare native <i>мочь</i> (močʹ) and Ukrainian <i>міч</i> (mič) (both inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мочь</i> (močĭ)) and Ukrainian <i>міць</i> (micʹ) and Belarusian <i>моц</i> (moc) (both borrowed from Polish <i>moc</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detergent (having the power to wash clean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hello</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) take one's hat off to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;folk poetic) frost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мразъ</i> (mrazŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morzъ</i>. Doublet of <i>моро́з</i> (moróz), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;uncommon,&#32;typically used by women on men) wasteman, vile person, scoundrel</li></ol><p><i>мразь</i> (mrazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;sometimes&#32;collectively) scum, dregs, despicable person, despicable people</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ме́рзкий</i> (mérzkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow, darkness</li><li>gloom, cheerlessness [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) despair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мракъ</i> (mrakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>мо́рок</i> (mórok), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscurant, obscurantist, bigot</li></ol><p><i>мрак</i> (mrak, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́с</i> (-bés, “-maniac”); the suffix <i>-бе́с</i> (-bés) is cognate with <i>беси́ться</i> (besítʹsja, “to be furious”). Historically, derives from <i>мракобе́сие</i> (mrakobésije), which was coined by Bestuzhev-Marlinsky in the 1810s and popularized by Belinsky.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscurantism (opposition to enlightenment and progress)</li></ol><p><i>мрак</i> (mrak, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́сие</i> (-bésije), from <i>беси́ться</i> (besítʹsja, “to rage”); calque of French <i>manie des ténèbres</i> (“mania of obscurity”), coined by Bestuzhev-Marlinsky in the 1810s and popularized by Belinsky. The suffix <i>-бе́сие</i> (-bésije) corresponds to Old Church Slavonic <i>-бѣ́сїе</i> (-bě́sije), used to translate Ancient Greek <i>-μᾰνία</i> (-mănía) in religious terminology, such as in Old Church Slavonic <i>идолобѣ́сїе</i> (idolobě́sije, “idolomania”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мраморъ</i> (mramorŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mormorъ</i>, a borrowing from Latin <i>marmor</i>. Displaced the inherited Old East Slavic <i>мороморъ</i> (moromorŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marbleized</li></ol><p><i>мра́мор</i> (mrámor) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) marble (crystalline limestone)</li><li>marble-like</li><li>white, smooth (<i>of a body part or its skin</i>)</li><li>marbled, spotted (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мра́мор</i> (mrámor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) marbled rock crab (<i>Pachygrapsus marmoratus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мра́морный</i> (mrámornyj, “marbled”) +&lrm; <i>краб</i> (krab, “crab”), a partial calque of translingual <i>Pachygrapsus marmoratus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to cover with gloom</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to make sad, to sadden</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрачити</i> (mračiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morčiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мрак</i> (mrak) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Doublet of <i>моро́чить</i> (moróčitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become gloomy, to darken</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>darkly, gloomily</li></ol><p><i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat gloomy</li></ol><p><i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li><li>obscurity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) gloominess, sadness, somberness</li></ol><p><i>мра́чный</i> (mráčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark (without moral or spiritual light)</li><li>obscure</li><li>gloomy, somber, grim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрачьнъ</i> (mračĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mȍrčьnъ</i>. Analyzable as <i>мрак</i> (mrak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Doublet of <i>мо́ро́чный</i> (móróčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;regional&#32;or&#32;biblical) (fish) net</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мрѣжа</i> (mrěža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;merža</i>. Doublet of <i>мерёжа</i> (merjóža), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avenger (one who avenges or vindicates&#59; as, an avenger of blood)</li></ol><p><i>мстить</i> (mstitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vindictiveness</li></ol><p><i>мсти́тельн(ый)</i> (mstítelʹn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vindictive, vengeful (having or showing a strong or unreasoning desire for revenge)</li></ol><p><i>мстить</i> (mstitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge, to revenge, to retaliate (to take vengeance) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьstiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;French context</i>) monsieur, sir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>m'sieur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) moo (sound a cow makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) horse's ass, asshole, dickhead</li></ol><p><i>му́ди</i> (múdi, “scrotum and testicles”) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak, personifying suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;anatomy) testicles, nuts, balls</li></ol><p>See мудо (mudo). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) asshole, dickhead; fuckwit</li></ol><p>From <i>мудо́</i> (mudó), <i>мудя́</i> (mudjá) or <i>муде́</i> (mudé), + <i>-и́ла</i> (-íla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;rare) scrotum, ballsack</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;rare) testicle, ball, nut</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫdo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to rough up, to bust one's ass</li></ol><p>Expressive from <i>му́ди</i> (múdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) asshole, shitass (mean or rude person)</li></ol><p>мудо́ (mudó, “ballsack”) + ёб (job, “fucker (suffix)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mudra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become smarter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sage (wise man)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫdrьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be too clever, to complicate things unnecessarily</li></ol><p><i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wisely (in a wise manner)</li></ol><p><i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫdrostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) wisdom</li></ol><p><i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) to philosophize</li></ol><p><i>му́дрство</i> (múdrstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мудръ</i> (mudrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫdrъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mandras</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mondʰh₁ros</i>, from <i>&#42;men(s)dʰh₁-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-rós</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>mandrùs</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mundraz</i> (German <i>munter</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) difficult (hard to understand or do)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intricate, complicated</li><li>strange, odd</li></ol><p><i>мудри́ть</i> (mudrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) great man (man at the forefront of his field or discipline)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѫжь</i> (mǫžĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫžь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mon-</i>, whence English <i>man</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mature, to grow (up), to reach manhood</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>муж</i> (muž) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;rare) male homosexual</li></ol><p><i>мужело́ж(ец)</i> (muželóž(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Eastern Orthodoxy,&#32;officialese) synonym of <i>мужело́жство</i> (muželóžstvo)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Church Slavonic <i>мѫжеложьство</i> (mǫželožĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>муж</i> (muž, “a man”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ, “to lay down”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male homosexual, sodomite, bugger</li><li>sodomite</li><li>pederast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Eastern Orthodoxy,&#32;officialese) buggery, sodomy, male homosexuality or homosexual behaviour</li><li>anal sex, gay sex</li><li>pederasty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Church Slavonic <i>мѫжеложьство</i> (mǫželožĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>муж</i> (muž, “a man”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ, “to lay down”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misandrist, manhater (hater of men)</li></ol><p><i>муж</i> (muž, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-, “hater”) +&lrm; <i>ненави́стник</i> (nenavístnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>androphobia (fear of men)</li></ol><p><i>мужененави́стник</i> (muženenavístnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>муж</i> (muž): hubby</li></ol><p><i>муж</i> (muž) +&lrm; <i>-енёк</i> (-enjók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manlike, mannish, masculine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) male, men's</li></ol><p><i>муж</i> (muž) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>му́жеский</i> (múžeskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bravely, courageously</li></ol><p><i>му́жественный</i> (múžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masculinity, manliness, virility</li></ol><p><i>му́жественный</i> (múžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manly, virile, masculine (having the characteristics of a man)</li><li>courageous, brave, gutsy</li></ol><p><i>му́жество</i> (múžestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courage</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>муж</i> (muž) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) man (male person); (UK) bloke, (US) dude, fella, guy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) husband</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;approving) a man as a bearer of masculine qualities; man, macho man, real man</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Term of address for men to each other</li><li>(rude,&#32;archaic,&#32;vernacular) coarse, uncouth man; boor</li><li>(historical,&#32;Tsarist Russia) commoner, peasant; mujik/muzhik</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) ordinary convict working conscientiously in a prison camp</li></ol><p>From <i>муж</i> (muž, “man (dated); husband”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) muzhik (pre-Revolutionary Russian peasant); muzhik's</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) boorish, rustic</li></ol><p><i>мужи́к</i> (mužík) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>мужи́к</i> (mužík, “man”): (<i>colloquial</i>) big man</li></ol><p><i>мужи́к</i> (mužík, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мужи́к</i> (mužík): a (little) man (male), a guy, chap</li></ol><p>From <i>мужи́к</i> (mužík) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>мужи́к</i> (mužík)</li><li>pejorative of <i>мужичо́к</i> (mužičók)</li></ol><p><i>мужи́к</i> (mužík) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>husband's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>men's room, men’s toilet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male honor</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;humorous) manhood (male genitalia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li><li>masculine</li><li>man's, gentleman's</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫžьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>муж</i> (muž, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) male sexual member, penis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) masculine gender</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man (male person)</li></ol><p>Attested since the 16th century as <i>мущи́на</i> (muščína), from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;мѫжьщина</i> (&#42;mǫžĭščina) or borrowed from Polish <i>mężczyzna</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫžьščina</i>, from <i>&#42;mǫžьskъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ina</i>, from <i>&#42;mǫžь</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мужско́й</i> (mužskój) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>мужчи́на</i> (mužčýna), Belarusian <i>мужчы́на</i> (mužčýna), Polish <i>mężczyzna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>мужчи́на</i> (mužčína, “man”): (<i>colloquial</i>) big man</li></ol><p><i>мужчи́на</i> (mužčína, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>муж</i> (muž).: husband</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>муж</i> (muž).: (dated,&#32;poetic) man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a Muse</li><li>muse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>museologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>музееве́дение</i> (muzejevédenije, “museology”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>museology</li></ol><p>From <i>музе́й</i> (muzéj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>museum</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>muzeum</i> or German <i>Museum</i>, from Latin <i>mūsēum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μουσεῖον</i> (mouseîon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) museum, museal</li></ol><p><i>музе́й</i> (muzéj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) museologist, museum worker</li></ol><p>From <i>музе́й</i> (muzéj) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play music, to make music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>musizieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) music</li></ol><p><i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) music</li></ol><p><i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>музыкальный факультет</i> (muzykalʹnyj fakulʹtet, “school of music (of a college)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) business</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>muzyka</i>. Displaced <i>гудьба́</i> (gudʹbá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music box</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musicality</li></ol><p><i>музыка́льный</i> (muzykálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>musical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) ear training exercise that consists in transcribing what one hears (melody, harmony, rhythm) in music notation</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>dictée musicale</i>, German <i>Musikdiktat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musical instrument (a device, object, contrivance or machine used to produce musical notes or sounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) musician</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a member of the Wagner Group</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Musikant</i>. By surface analysis, <i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka) +&lrm; <i>-а́нт</i> (-ánt). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>музика́нт</i> (muzykánt), Polish <i>muzykant</i>.</p><p>For sense 2, mocking the Wagner Group being named after the German composer Wilhelm Richard Wagner.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musicologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>музыкове́дение</i> (muzykovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musicology</li></ol><p><i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka): music</li></ol><p><i>му́зыка</i> (múzyka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour, fine meal</li><li>pain, anguish, torment, suffering, torture</li><li>ordeal</li><li>harassment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǭka</i> (“flour”). Akin to regional Serbo-Croatian <i>мука</i>/<i>muka</i> (“meal”), Slovene <i>moka</i>, Slovak <i>múka</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”), German and Old High German <i>mengen</i> (“to mix”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫka</i> (“torment, torture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour mill</li></ol><p><i>мука́</i> (muká) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule (the generally sterile male or female hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulatto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>mulato</i> or Portuguese <i>mulato</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) mullah (<i>a Muslim religious scholar and teacher</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>мулла</i> (mulla), from Arabic مولى. Attested since 17th century.</p><p>Replaced earlier (16th – 18th cc.) <i>молла</i> (molla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clipping of <i>мультфи́льм</i> (mulʹtfílʹm)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) million</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) clipping of <i>мультиакка́унт</i> (mulʹtiakkáunt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>multi- (pertaining to many)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multi-currency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;cosmology) multiverse</li></ol><p>From <i>мульти-</i> (mulʹti-) +&lrm; <i>вселенная</i> (vselennaja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;childish</i>) animated cartoon, animated film</li></ol><p>From a clipping of <i>мультфи́льм</i> (mulʹtfílʹm) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multiculturalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multimedia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>multimedia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) multimedia</li></ol><p><i>мультиме́диа</i> (mulʹtimédia) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multiplex (large cinema complex)</li><li>a set of TV or radio stations broadcast in one frequency band</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>multiplex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>animated (especially referring to cartoons)</li></ol><p><i>мультиплика́ция</i> (mulʹtiplikácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartoon (animated cartoon)</li><li>animated film</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animation (causing images to appear to move)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) multi-touch (a technology used widely on touchscreens and touchpads that accepts more than one touch at the same time)</li></ol><p>From English <i>multi-touch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartoon</li><li>animated film</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i><b>мульт</b>ипликационный <b>фильм</b></i> (<b>mulʹt</b>iplikacionnyj <b>filʹm</b>, “animated film”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mulch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mulch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>mulch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dummy, full-scale model, decoy, waxwork</li><li>anatomical dummy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>moulage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mummy (<i>embalmed corpse</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;indeclinable</i>) mumijo, natural shilajit, mineral tar, mineral pitch, mountain balsam <i>(mumijo is an organic resinous substance found at high altitudes in the Himalayas and used in traditional medicine to treat a variety of diseases. It is a dark brown or black tarry deposit in rock crevices.)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tajik <i>мумиё</i> (mumiyo), from Classical Persian مُومِیَا (mūmiyā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The FIFA World Cup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>mundial</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military) uniform</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>mundur</i>, from German <i>Montur</i>, from French <i>monture</i> (see there for more) and perhaps influenced by “mondiren”, an earlier form of German <i>montieren</i>. Ultimately from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;montāre</i>, from Latin <i>mōns, mōntis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouthpiece (part of a wind or brass instrument)</li><li>cigarette holder</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) curb bit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Mundstück</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>municipality</li><li>city hall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>municipal (relating to a city)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) purr (sound a contented cat makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, garbage, rubbish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mura</i>, cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;murъ</i> and further with Lithuanian <i>mauras</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) young grass; sward</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) glaze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;murava</i>, from <i>&#42;mura, &#42;murъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mauˀras</i> (“mud”). Cognate with Polish <i>murawa</i>.</p><p>From Latin <i>murra</i> (“precious stone”), ultimately from Persian. Compare مری (“little glass ball”) and مروارید (“pearl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ant</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;animate&#32;or&#32;inanimate) a special motor scooter with three wheels and a rear cargo area made in the USSR between 1959 and 1995 in Tula, officially capitalised - Муравей, some models were given different names: Турист, Тулица, the first model's name was Ту́ла (Túla).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;morvъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;morwi-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) carpenter ant (<i>Camponotus</i> spp.)</li></ol><p><i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj, “ant”) +&lrm; <i>древото́чец</i> (drevotóčec, “borer”), literally “borer ant”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) apartment building or high-rise</li></ol><p><i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anteater (mammal)</li></ol><p><i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formic acid, methanoic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ant; antlike</li></ol><p><i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antlion (nocturnal insects)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мураве́й</i> (muravéj), ant</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) goosebump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose bumps (raised skin caused by cold, excitement, or fear)</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>мура́шка</i> (muráška, “little ant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morza</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pussy-cat (affectionate name for cats)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Мурзик)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Murzik (a common name for cats)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Murzik (a name for dogs, especially small)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a black person; Maur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>муринъ</i> (murinŭ), ultimately from Latin <i>Maurus</i>. Cognates include Polish <i>Murzyn</i>, Ukrainian <i>му́рин</i> (múryn), Belarusian <i>му́рын</i> (múryn). Doublet of <i>мавр</i> (mavr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;inanimate) an ugly face</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;animate) a bad-mannered person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to purr</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hum</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Murmansk</li></ol><p><i>Му́рманск</i> (Múrmansk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Murmansk</li></ol><p><i>Му́рманск</i> (Múrmansk) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>construction</i>) to build out of bricks or stones and clay or mortar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>murować</i>, from Latin <i>mūrus</i> (“wall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) police station</li></ol><p>From <i>му́сор</i> (músor) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), modified by analogy with <i>звона́рня</i> (zvonárnja, “bell tower”), <i>гонча́рня</i> (gončárnja, “pottery workshop”), <i>бонда́рня</i> (bondárnja, “cooperage”), based on agent nouns ending in <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) muscat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nutmeg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) muscle</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) MySQL (<i>database</i>)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>mūsculus</i>.</p><p>Phono-semantic matching of English <i>MySQL</i>, influenced by the noun <i>му́скул</i> (múskul, “muscle”) through a Cyrillic reading of "My".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musculature, muscular system</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mūsculātūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>muscular, brawny (having strength)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musk</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>muscus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μόσχος</i> (móskhos), from Middle Persian (mwšk' /⁠mušk⁠/).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Muscovy duck</li></ol><p>Literally, “musk duck”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) musk</li></ol><p><i>му́скус</i> (múskus, “musk”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muslin, mousseline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) muslin</li></ol><p>From <i>мусли́н</i> (muslín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten with saliva</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wet with saliva</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave dirty marks on with dirty or sticky fingers</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend too much time (over); to discuss or to speak about over and over again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garbage, debris, refuse</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) cop, pig (policeman)</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Per Vasmer, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂d-</i>, and cognate with Ancient Greek <i>μύσος</i> (músos, “pollution, desecration”), Ancient Greek <i>μυσαρός</i> (musarós, “vile, covered with shame”), Old Irish <i>mosach</i> (“unclean”), Low German <i>mussig</i> (“dirty”).</p><p>Per Preobrazhensky and Trubachyov, cognate with dialectal terms <i>бу́сор</i> (búsor, “junk, old stuff”), <i>бу́сырь</i> (búsyrʹ), <i>бу́сорь</i> (búsorʹ), and probably borrowed from a Turkic language with alternation between /b/ and /m/; Trubachyov in particular characterizes Vasmer's derivation as unconvincing.</p><p>An expressive connection with <i>сор</i> (sor, “rubbish, trash”) is also suggested.</p><p>From the pre-1917 acronym <i>МУС</i> (MUS) for <i>Моско́вский уголо́вный сыск</i> (Moskóvskij ugolóvnyj sysk, “Moscow Criminal Detective Department”). Influenced by etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to litter, to leave litter about</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dumpster (large trash receptacle)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rubbish bin, garbage can</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) garbage truck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garbage</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) trash</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unwanted</li></ol><p><i>му́сор</i> (músor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garbage truck</li></ol><p>From <i>му́сор</i> (músor) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>воз</i> (voz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>му́сор</i> (músor): rubbish</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;slang) diminutive of <i>му́сор</i> (músor): cop, pig (police officer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garbage chute, refuse chute, refuse duct, rubbish chute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;relational) cop (police officer)</li></ol><p><i>му́сор</i> (músor) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garbage collector, dustman</li><li>scrapper, binner (a person who searches for valuable scraps in refuse)</li></ol><p><i>му́сор</i> (músor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mousse (dessert)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mousse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) monsoon (any of a number of winds associated with regions where most rain falls during a particular season)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mousson</i>, from Portuguese <i>monção</i>, from Arabic مَوْسِم (mawsim, “season”), from وَسَمَ (wasama, “to mark, to brand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustang</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muslim</li></ol><p>From Turkic (compare Tatar <i>мөселман</i> (möselman), Kazakh <i>мұсылман</i> (mūsylman)) + <i>-ин</i> (-in), ultimately from Classical Persian مُسَلْمَان (musalmān), from Arabic مُسْلِم (muslim). Doublet of <i>басурма́нин</i> (basurmánin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Muslim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutagen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>mūtātiō</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>мутаці́я</i> (mutacíja) and Polish <i>mutacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mutisia (<i>Mutisia</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Mutisia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to make (water) muddy</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to date, to have a relationship with</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to make up, to sort out</li><li>to cloud (the mind), to dull (the senses)</li><li>to incite to rebel</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be nauseated</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫtiti</i> (“to stir, trouble”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>mute</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) to become muddy (also of mind)</li></ol><p><i>мути́ть</i> (mutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) fishing net used to catch small fish</li><li>(<i>fishing</i>) seine net used to catch demersal fish</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) one who causes turmoil, attempts to muddy the waters</li></ol><p><i>мути́ть</i> (mutítʹ, “to make muddy”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat muddy</li></ol><p><i>му́тн(ый)</i> (mútn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turbid, murky (not clear)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫtьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ghastly, unpleasant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, hit repeatedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dregs, mud, blur; slime sediment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, froth, foam</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>мутить</i> (mutitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(fireproof)</i> muffle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Muffel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouflon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muff, handwarmer</li><li>(<i>machines</i>) clutch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) mufti</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic اَلْمُفْتِي (al-muftī) (<i>definite form</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) muff or coupling</li></ol><p><i>му́фт(а)</i> (múft(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fly (insect)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mùxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A mass migration or resettlement of Muslims from territories controlled by a non-Muslim majority or government.</li></ol><p><i>мухаджир</i> (muxadžir, “muhajir”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat (in games)</li></ol><p>Possibly, from German <i>mogeln</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cheating (<i>in games</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flyswatter, flyflap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flycatcher (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>му́ха</i> (múxa, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>-е́д</i> (-éd, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, rapidly, immediately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) flytrap, flypaper</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) Venus flytrap, sundew</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) flycatcher (muscicapid)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) centipede of the taxonomic order Scutigeromorpha</li></ol><p><i>муха́</i> (muxá, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лов</i> (lov, “hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare Polish <i>muchołówka</i>, Bulgarian <i>мухоловка</i> (muholovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fly agaric, <i>Amanita muscaria</i></li></ol><p>From <i>му́ха</i> (múxa, “fly”) + <i>мори́ть</i> (morítʹ, “to kill, exterminate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fly agaric, <i>Amanita muscaria</i></li></ol><p><i>мухомо́р</i> (muxomór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mukhoyar (an old Asian fabric of cotton blended with dyed wool or silk yarn, used to make garments in medieval Russia)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мухояръ</i> (muxojarŭ), ultimately from Arabic مُخَيَّر (muḵayyar, “choice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mukhtar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riverbend</li></ol><p>Cognate with Southern Selkup <i>муч</i> (muč)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>му́читься</i> (múčitʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>му́чать</i> (múčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment, torture, pain, anguish</li><li>ordeal</li></ol><p><i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyr</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) martyr, martyrdom</li><li>tormented (<i>look, face, etc.</i>) (expressing torment)</li></ol><p><i>му́ченик</i> (múčenik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyrdom</li></ol><p><i>му́ченик</i> (múčenik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tormentor; torturer</li></ol><p>From <i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ, “to torment, torture, wear out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>grievously, painfully (in a grievous manner)</li></ol><p><i>мучи́тельный</i> (mučítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grievous, painful (causing grief, pain or sorrow)</li></ol><p><i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torment, to anguish (cause to suffer pain)</li><li>to torture</li><li>to wear out, to bore, to tire out, to pester</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫ̀čiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agonize, to suffer (to experience pain)</li><li>to worry, to torment oneself</li></ol><p><i>му́чить</i> (múčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flour; farinaceous</li></ol><p><i>мука́</i> (muká) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fly</li></ol><p><i>му́ха</i> (múxa) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>му́ха</i> (múxa): a small fly, midge</li><li>foresight, sight (of a rifle or a similar weapon)</li><li>beauty spot, patch</li></ol><p><i>му́ха</i> (múxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musket</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>muszkiet</i>, from German <i>Muskete</i>, from French <i>mousquet</i>, from Italian <i>moschetto</i>. Further from Latin <i>musca</i> + <i>-ittus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) musketeer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mousquetaire</i> or from German <i>Musketier</i>, influenced by the common ending <i>-ёр</i> (-jór). Spelling with ш influenced by <i>мушке́т</i> (muškét), in turn probably influenced by <i>му́шка</i> (múška, “little fly; front sight on a firearm”) (<i>мушке́т</i> (muškét) derives ultimately from <i>moschetto</i> (literally “little fly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medlar</li><li>loquat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>muşmula</i>, from Ottoman Turkish مشمله (muşmula), from Greek <i>μούσμουλο</i> (moúsmoulo). As the Crimean Russian term, due to the submediterranean climate preferred by the plant it has succeeded in displacing the forms of the word <i>ши́шка</i> (šíška, “strobilus, bulb”) used for the medlar as the native Russian term.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drill; drilling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>musztra</i> &lt; Italian <i>mostra</i> &lt; Latin <i>monstrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>мужчи́на</i> (mužčína)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muezzin (the man who issues the call to prayer)</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic source, from Arabic مُؤَذِّن (muʔaḏḏin, “one who calls (to prayer), crier”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) MHAT (Moscow Art Theatre)</li></ol><p><i>МХАТ</i> (MXAT) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to whirl along</li><li>to rush/speed/tear along</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъčati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scurry, to dash, to race, to tear along</li></ol><p><i>мчать</i> (mčatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;inanimate) pearlwort (herb in the genus <i>Sagina</i>)</li><li>(zoology,&#32;inanimate&#32;or&#32;animate) bryozoan (invertebrate in the phylum Bryozoa)</li></ol><p><i>мх-</i> (mx-, “moss”) +&lrm; <i>-а́нка</i> (-ánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to make fleecy, to shaggy</li><li>(dated,&#32;regional) to cover with moss, to stuff or caulk with moss</li></ol><p>Reducible stem of <i>мох</i> (mox, “moss”) + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vengeance, revenge (revenge taken for an insult, injury, or other wrong)</li></ol><p><i>мстить</i> (mstitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we</li><li>I (only when used with <i>с</i> (s))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мꙑ</i> (my), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;my</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>мꙑ</i> (my). Also akin to Lithuanian <i>mẽs</i> and Old Armenian <i>մեք</i> (mekʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;Saint Petersburg,&#32;Estonia,&#32;Latvia) farmstead, manor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Estonian <i>mõis</i> or from a related Finnic language (cf. Finnish <i>moisio</i>, Livonian <i>mȯizõ</i>, Votic <i>mõizõ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to struggle (along), as if being tossed around</li><li>to wander, to drift around</li><li>to pace back and forth; to mope around</li></ol><p><i>-мы́кать</i> (-mýkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soap, to soap up</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) to send by e-mail</li></ol><p><i>мы́л(о)</i> (mýl(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) soap</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) lather</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) e-mail (by phono-semantic matching with English <i>mail</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mydlo</i>, from <i>&#42;myti</i> (“to wash”). By surface analysis, <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p><p>For the term and <i>помо́и</i> (pomói) relation with <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ), compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>ῥύμμα</i> (rhúmma) &lt; <i>ῥύπτω</i> (rhúptō, “to cleanse, to wash”). Also compare <i>σμῆμα</i> (smêma) &lt; <i>σμάω</i> (smáō). Also compare English <i>lye</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soapworks</li></ol><p><i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) a cleaning product, including detergents and body soaps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap opera (television serial)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lather</li><li>soapsuds</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap dish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) compact camera</li></ol><p><i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soapy (full of soap)</li></ol><p><i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap bubble</li><li>empty vessel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo)</li></ol><p><i>мы́ло</i> (mýlo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) sullen, disagreeable, unattractive, or poorly dressed woman</li></ol><p>From Komi-Permyak <i>мыныра</i> (mynyra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muong (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Vietnamese <i>Mường</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cape, promontory</li></ol><p>Attested first from the year 1651, of unknown provenance. Cognate with Belarusian <i>мыс</i> (mys), Ukrainian <i>мис</i> (mys), Czech <i>mys</i>, Slovak <i>mys</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mentally (in a mental manner)</li></ol><p><i>мы́сленный</i> (mýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mental</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtcrime</li></ol><p><i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>преступле́ние</i> (prestuplénije); calque of English <i>thoughtcrime</i>, from George Orwell's <i>Nineteen Eighty-Four</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conceivable, thinkable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thinker</li></ol><p><i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ, “to think”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thinking, intellectual</li></ol><p><i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mysliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be conceived/viewed</li></ol><p><i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) thought, judgement, mind</li></ol><p><i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought</li><li>idea</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myslь</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>myśl</i>, Macedonian <i>мисла</i> (misla), Bulgarian <i>мисъл</i> (misǎl), Slovene <i>misel</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>мисао</i> or misao, Czech <i>mysl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thinking, intelligent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strangles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordeal, toil, affliction</li></ol><p>From <i>мы́тарь</i> (mýtarʹ, “tax collector, publican”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>taxing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) customs duty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>washed, clean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to wash oneself</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myti sę</i>, reflexive of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myti</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>мия</i> (mija), Serbo-Croatian <i>мити</i>, Czech <i>mýt</i>, Polish <i>myć</i>; and with Lithuanian <i>maudyti</i>, Latvian <i>maut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ). <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moo, mooing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mumbling</li></ol><p><i>мыча́ть</i> (myčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moo</li><li>to bellow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mumble</li></ol><p>Compare (with different vocalism) Ukrainian <i>му́кати</i> (múkaty, “to moo”), Bulgarian <i>муча́</i> (mučá, “to moo”), also <i>му́кам</i> (múkam), Serbo-Croatian <i>му́кати</i> (“to moo”) (1sg. <i>му̑че̄м</i>), Slovene <i>múkati</i> (“to moo”), Czech <i>mukati</i> (“to moo”), Upper Sorbian <i>mučeć</i> (“to moo”), and more distantly Lithuanian <i>mū̃kia</i> (“it moos”), Ancient Greek <i>μῡκάομαι</i> (mūkáomai, “to moo, to roar”), Middle High German <i>mūhen</i> (“to moo”), also <i>mugen</i>, Middle Low German <i>mucken</i> (“to be quietly indignant”), Ancient Greek <i>μυγμός</i> (mugmós, “sigh”), <i>μύζω</i> (múzō, “to groan, to moo”), Latin <i>mūgiō</i> (“to moo”) (infinitive <i>mūgīre</i>), Sanskrit <i>मुञ्जति</i> (múñjati, “to make a sound”), also <i>मोजति</i> (mṓjati), Hittite (mūgāi-, “to lament, to resist”), etc. Ultimately onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) male equivalent of <i>мышь</i> (myšʹ):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mouse (animal, especially male)</li><li>computer mouse</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxtail, bristle grass, <i>Setaria</i> gen. et spp.</li></ol><p>Cognate with synonymous Ukrainian <i>миші́й</i> (myšíj), <i>мише́й</i> (myšéj), <i>муші́й</i> (mušíj), <i>муши́й</i> (mušýj), Belarusian <i>мяшэй</i> (mjašej), <i>мяжэй</i> (mjažej). All perhaps from the word for "fly (insect)" – Russian <i>му́ха</i> (múxa), Ukrainian <i>му́ха</i> (múxa), paralleled in Serbo-Croatian <i>muhar</i> / <i>мухар</i>, Slovak <i>mušec</i>. Some forms are influenced by the word for mouse — Russian <i>мышь</i> (myšʹ), Ukrainian <i>ми́ша</i> (mýša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mousetrap</li></ol><p><i>мышь</i> (myšʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muscular dystrophy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) muscle; muscular</li></ol><p><i>мы́шца</i> (mýšca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu lato) The Muridae.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu stricto) The Murinae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>мыши́ный</i> (myšínyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mouse, murine</li><li>mouse-gray (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>мышь</i> (myšʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>мышь</i> (myšʹ)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;computing,&#32;diminutive) mouse</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) armpit</li></ol><p>From <i>мышь</i> (myšʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). The computing term is a semantic loan from English <i>mouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ideation, thought, thinking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little mouse, baby mouse, young mouse</li></ol><p><i>мышь</i> (myšʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́нок</i> (-ónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мышо́нок</i> (myšónok): (cute / small) baby mouse</li></ol><p><i>мышо́нок</i> (myšónok, “baby mouse”) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muscle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mỳšь</i> (“mouse”) (referring to the mouse-like appearance of a flexing muscle), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;muh₂s</i>. For semantic development, compare to Latin <i>mūsculus</i> (“muscle”, literally “little mouse”) and Ancient Greek <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse; muscle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse (<i>animal</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) mouse</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>мꙑшь</i> (myšĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myšь</i> (“mouse”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;mū́ˀšis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;múHs</i>. The computing term is a semantic loan from English <i>mouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arsenic</li></ol><p><i>мышь</i> (myšʹ, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>-як</i> (-jak); compare English <i>rat poison</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arsenous, arsenious</li></ol><p><i>мышьяко́вый</i> (myšʹjakóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arsenous anhydride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) arsenic</li></ol><p><i>мышья́к</i> (myšʹják) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ma'am (polite term of address to a woman in the UK, US and other English-speaking countries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ma'am</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mayor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maire</i>. Further from Latin <i>maior</i>. Doublet of <i>майо́р</i> (majór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mayor's office</li><li>city hall, town hall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>mairie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master, maître</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>maître</i>. Further from Latin <i>magister</i>. Doublet of <i>маги́стр</i> (magístr), <i>ма́стер</i> (máster), <i>маэ́стро</i> (maéstro), and <i>ми́стер</i> (míster). From the Latin term the second component of <i>гроссме́йстер</i> (grossméjster) is also derived.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mu (the Greek letter Μ (M)&#47;μ (m))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>μῦ</i> (mû).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musical (stage performance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>musical</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any grass of the genus <i>Muhlenbergia</i>, a muhly.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Muhlenbergia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, from or relating to the city of Munich, capital of Bavaria, Germany</li></ol><p><i>Мю́нхен</i> (Mjúnxen) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muesli (breakfast dish)</li><li>granola cereal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Müsli</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>меня́</i> (menjá)</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>меня́</i> (menjá) and probably not a direct descendant of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mę</i> or Old Church Slavonic <i>мѧ</i> (mę).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft power (political influence that is extended by means of diplomacy, international assistance, cultural exchanges, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft power (political influence that is extended by means of diplomacy, international assistance, cultural exchanges, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft</li><li>fresh</li><li>gentle [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>по отноше́нию к</b> (po otnošéniju k, + dative) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>mellow</li><li>mild</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) soft, palatal, palatalized</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мѧкъкъ</i> (mękŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mękъkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;minˀkakas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn̥k-o-kó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;menk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of the letter Ь, ь, the soft sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly, mildly, gently</li></ol><p><i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to say the least</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>softhearted (gentle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness</li><li>mildness, gentleness</li></ol><p><i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) soldier beetle</li></ol><p>From <i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij, “soft”) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>molluscous (soft-bodied)</li><li>spineless (uncourageous)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij): soft, mild</li></ol><p>From <i>мя́г(кий)</i> (mjág(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>мя́гко</i> (mjáxko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mękyna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chaff</li></ol><p><i>мяки́н(а)</i> (mjakín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb (the soft inner part of bread)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to grow pulpy</li><li>to grow flabby, to become soft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mę̀knǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(soft) flesh (of meat); pulp (of fruits)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mumble, to dodder, to hum and haw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mumbler, wishy-washy person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meaty, fleshy</li><li><i>(fruit, vegetables)</i> pulpy</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butchershop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher (a person who prepares and sells meat)</li><li>(medicine,&#32;informal) butcher (bad surgeon)</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) butcher; butcher's</li></ol><p><i>мясни́к</i> (mjasník) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meat</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) meat store, meat market</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) meat</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) flesh, pulp</li><li>(biology,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) tissue, flesh</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) (cannon) fodder</li><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;slang,&#32;soccer) FC Spartak Moscow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męso</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗms</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>meso</i>, Czech <i>maso</i>, Polish <i>mięso</i>, and Slovene <i>meso</i>. Compare Old Prussian <i>mensa</i> (“meat”).</p><p>Sense 5 refers to the football club being supported by a meat factory in Moscow during the 1930s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carnivore</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) meat eating period</li></ol><p>From <i>мясо</i> (mjaso, “meat”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meat processing plant; meat packing plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) meat processing</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>перераба́тывающий</i> (pererabátyvajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mincing machine, meatgrinder, mincer</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) slaughterhouse, slaughter, bloodbath (a vicious, bloody battle)</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso, “meat”) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ, “to chop”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “feminine noun-forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packing house</li></ol><p><i>мя́со</i> (mjáso) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хлад</i> (xlad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mint (<i>Mentha</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męta</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>menta</i> (“mint”), from Ancient Greek <i>μίνθη</i> (mínthē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutiny, rebellion (armed uprising often comprising disloyal soldiers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мѧтєжь</i> (mętežĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mętežь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutineer, rebel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мѧтежьникъ</i> (mętežĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>мяте́ж</i> (mjatéž) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rebellious, mutinous</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) restless, passionate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>мѧтежьнъ</i> (mętežĭnŭ). Equivalent to <i>мяте́ж</i> (mjatéž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mint; minty</li></ol><p><i>мя́та</i> (mjáta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creased</li><li>rumpled, crumpled</li><li>crushed, flattened</li><li>weary, exhausted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to crumple, to wrinkle, to fumble, to crease</li><li>to knead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męti</i>; compare Old Church Slavonic <i>мѧти</i> (męti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to crease, to wrinkle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hesitate</li></ol><p><i>мять</i> (mjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) meow (sound a cat makes)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) mephedrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meow, to mew</li></ol><p><i>мяу</i> (mjau, “meow”) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meow, to mew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Pskov</i>) synonym of <i>мя́со</i> (mjáso)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball (object, generally spherical, used for playing games)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męčь</i>, related to мя́гкий (mjáxkij). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>м'яч</i> (mʺjač), Belarusian <i>мяч</i> (mjač), Czech <i>míč</i>, Slovene <i>mȇč</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>мяч</i> (mjač): (small soft) ball</li></ol><p><i>мяч</i> (mjač) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) honey</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) mead</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;médʰu</td></tr></table></p><p>From Old East Slavic <i>медъ</i> (medŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mȅdъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;médu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰu</i>. Cognate with English <i>mead</i>, Ancient Greek <i>μέθυ</i> (méthu, “wine”) (whence Russian <i>мети́л</i> (metíl), English <i>methyl</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frozen, congealed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>мерзлꙑй</i> (merzlyj), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьrzlъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>мёрз(нуть)</i> (mjórz(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze, to feel cold</li><li>to become frozen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьrznǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead</li><li>lifeless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьrtvъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥twós</i>, <i>&#42;mr̥tós</i>, ultimately from the root <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”), whence also English <i>murder</i>, <i>mortal</i>, <i>morbid</i> and French <i>mourir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мир</i> (mir): universe, world.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ме́дь</i> (médʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ме́лкий</i> (mélkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мел</i> (mel).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мель</i> (melʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ме́на</i> (ména).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мери́ло</i> (merílo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мерный</i> (mernyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>месте́чко</i> (mestéčko).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>местоиме́ние</i> (mestoiménije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мех</i> (mex, “fur”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>мех</i> (mex, “bellows”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ми́ро</i> (míro).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>м-да́</i> (m-dá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>на́шей э́ры</i> (nášej éry): AD (anno Domini), CE (Common Era)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>на́шей э́ры</i> (nášej éry): AD (anno Domini), CE (Common Era)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>onto, on</li><li>to, toward</li><li>to set out for (in the sense "to start an activity with the intention of finishing it"), to go for</li><li>(<i>duration, value, purpose</i>) for</li><li>about, for</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) over, by, divided by</li><li>(<i>time</i>) in, for</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on, upon</li><li>(<i>only with certain nouns</i>) in, at</li><li>(<i>places, only certain nouns</i>) nearby, by (in the sense of near) (interchangeable with <i>у</i> (u), which requires the genitive case)</li><li>by (using a means of transport to get somewhere (while <i>в</i> (v) is just 'to be in something'))</li><li>runs/operates only because of someone/something; works on, runs on</li><li>assigned to (<i>a certain responsibility</i>)</li><li>with, for</li><li>(<i>time</i>) in, for</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) being dominated by a certain emotional state</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) accomplishing something while being dominated by a certain emotional state and primarily because of it</li></ol></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>here you go! (encouraging someone to accept something, used when giving or providing something, i.e., "take it!", "here you goǃ")</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>на</i> (na), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;na</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;nō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en-</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;na</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>aboard (on board)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aboard, on board</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>aboard (on board)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on bare feet</li></ol><p>Literally, “on(to) a bare foot”. The form <i>бо́су</i> is an old short accusative form. The stem stress is etymologically expected; compare nominative <i>нога́</i> (nogá) with accusative <i>но́га</i> (nóga). The ending stressed form <i>босу́</i> is formed by analogy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a suspended or balanced position</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the wild, free, outside, at liberty, at large, on the loose</li><li>free, not in prison, not in jail, not locked up, not in confinement, on the loose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in every way (in all sorts of possible ways or simply intensely)</li></ol><p>Literally, “at all ways”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all-rounder, of all trades (a person who has many skills)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one hundred percent, completely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wherever one wishes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ingenuously, acting all innocent, with feigned ignorance, playing dumb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) almost committing a foul</li><li>on the edge of impropriety, inappropriateness, or illegality; highly risqué</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>currently, at the moment, as yet (up to the present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) outside the home, in the fresh air</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) today, now</li><li>used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#59; see <i>на</i> (na) and <i>двор</i> (dvor)</li></ol><p>Literally, “in the yard”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one of these days</li><li>the other day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a long time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on one's hind legs (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>(standing) straight up in the air</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) kicking up one's heels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>to your heart's content</li><li>you're welcome (in answer to a thanks for food or refreshments)</li><li>do as you want at your own risk, be my guest (sarcastically)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily, without difficulty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all over the place, everywhere (implying a continuous presenсe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) why?, what the heck is something for?</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) why the hell, what the hell for</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) what the hell</li></ol><p>Literally, “for what devil”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) hurriedly, in a quick-and-dirty way</li><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) by hand, without proper equipment or automation</li></ol><p>In reference to using the top of one's knee as an improvised work surface.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in flight</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) on the fly, quickly, skilfully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in my view, in my opinion, to my mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>standing; upright</li><li>walking</li><li>up; awake; having got up</li></ol><p>Literally, “on the legs”. Compare the same construction in Dutch <i>op de been</i>, German <i>auf den Beinen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) on the same wavelength, on the same page, in tune [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Literally, “on one wave”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by heart, by rote</li></ol><p>Cognate with Polish <i>na pamięć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) at first sight, at first blush, at a glance (on cursory examination)</li><li>on the face of it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afloat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>help!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>help&#33;</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vertically</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) and obsolete <i>поп</i> (pop, “figure in the game of Gorodki”) (named after <i>поп</i> (pop, “priest”)), probably used as euphemism for <i>попа</i> (popa, “butt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) without a permit, without papers, without a contract, illegally</li></ol><p>Literally, “having a bird's rights [to do something]”; compare to German <i>vogelfrei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily, without difficulty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a matter of fact, in fact, in actual fact, actually, in reality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) doing one's thing, in one's own world, (living) in one's own way</li></ol><p>Literally, “on one's wave”. Originally nautical and cadet slang.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on foot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, at once, immediately, without delay (e.g. make food or prepare for something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>A-1, first rate, excellent</li><li>well done</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in case (in the event (of))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to death</li><li>utterly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> на (na),&lrm; у́лице (úlice).</li><li>outside, outdoors</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> на (na),&lrm; у́лице (úlice).</li><li>outside, outdoors</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) what the hell for?</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;including with the verbs посла́ть, убира́ться, пойти́, идти́, etc.) the fuck away, off, the fuck out, the fuck up, fucking</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) completely, permanently, decisively</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck, fuck it, fuck that, the fuck</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock”). Compare Polish <i>na chuj</i>, Ukrainian <i>на хуй</i> (na xuj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) what the fuck for? why?</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) in a <i>grand</i> style, in opulence; on a large scale, in a big way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) onto, upon</li><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) to repletion, to satiation, to the full, enough, too much</li><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) a quantity of, a lot of</li><li>(added to nouns, forms adverbs with the meaning in the direction of the noun)</li><li>(added to nouns or adverbs, forms adverbs with the approximate meaning 'for' the noun or adverb, or in the state of the noun or adverb)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;na</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>на́бережная</i> (náberežnaja, “embankment”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add <i>(to)</i>, to increase</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knob (<i>on the end of a cane or stick</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;набалда́чник</i> (&#42;nabaldáčnik), from <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>балда́к</i> (baldák, “large glass, cone, head”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), from Tatar <i>балдак</i> (baldaq, “hilt of a saber, knob on a saber handle, smooth ring without a stone”), or from Ottoman Turkish بالداق (<i>baldak</i>, “(same meaning)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarm, tocsin (also figuratively)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic نَوْبَة (nawba, “change of the guard, musical troupe”), likely through a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incursion, raid, foray (<i>of barbarians, nomads, gangs, etc.</i>)</li><li>surge, rush, gust</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>набега́ть</i> (nabegátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>incursiō</i> (whence English <i>incursion</i>), <i>excursiō</i> (whence English <i>excursion</i>, Russian <i>экску́рсия</i> (ekskúrsija)), akin to <i>currō</i>. Also compare typologically Russian <i>наше́ствие</i> (našéstvije) akin to <i>ше́ствовать</i> (šéstvovatʹ), Latin <i>invasiō</i> (whence English <i>invasion</i>, Russian <i>инва́зия</i> (invázija), <i>инвази́вный</i> (invazívnyj)) akin to <i>vadō</i>. Also compare <i>налёт</i> (naljót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover a total <i>(distance)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(till one is)</i>, to run oneself <i>(to the point of)</i></li><li>to run <i>(against)</i>, to dash</li><li><i>(waves)</i> to roll in, to coast in</li><li>to run <i>(over)</i>, to cover</li><li>to come running <i>(together)</i></li><li><i>(liquid)</i> to accumulate <i>(quickly)</i></li><li><i>(cloth)</i> to ruck up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run <i>(against)</i>, to dash</li><li><i>(waves)</i> to roll in, to coast in</li><li>to run <i>(over)</i>, to cover</li><li>to come running <i>(together)</i></li><li><i>(liquid)</i> to accumulate <i>(quickly)</i></li><li><i>(cloth)</i> to ruck up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aslant, cocked, tilted to one side</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>бекре́нь</i> (bekrénʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without erasures or corrections, in final form</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an alley, usually pedestrian, running along a bank or a shore</li><li>quayside, embankment (an area alongside a quay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shoreside, shorefront</li></ol><p><i>на́ берег</i> (ná bereg, “ashore”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to fart (silently, as opposed to пердеть)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бздеть</i> (bzdetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stuff <i>(with)</i>, to pack <i>(with)</i>, to fill <i>(with)</i></li><li>to print</li><li>to tap <i>(on)</i>, to fix <i>(on)</i></li><li>to key in</li></ol><p><i>наби́ть</i> (nabítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, to cram [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into a location’</small>]</li><li>to be packed or crammed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to impose oneself [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘on something/someone or into somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>набива́ть</i> (nabivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set up, to compose, to type (a text on a computer)</li><li>to gather; to pick; to collect; to assemble; to take (a lot of)</li><li>to take on; to enlist, to recruit; to engage; to enrol, to make up</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nabirati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to accumulate (<i>of several people</i>)</li><li>to pile up, to accumulate (<i>of debts, work, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to collect, to summon up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘an emotion’</small>]</li><li>to amount, to add up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to experience, to undergo [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘an emotion’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to learn, to pick up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a skill, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be full, to tank up on alcohol, to load up, to get wasted/drunk</li></ol><p><i>набира́ть</i> (nabirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>packed, stuffed</li><li>crowded, congested</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stuff, to pack, to fill</li><li>to fix (onto)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to type (some text)</li><li>to break, to smash (a lot of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to try one's hand at, to get one's hand in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, to cram [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into a location’</small>]</li><li>to be packed or crammed [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to impose oneself [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘on something/someone or into somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наби́ть</i> (nabítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observer</li><li>election monitor</li></ol><p><i>наблюда́ть</i> (nabljudátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observation (skill), power / keenness of observation</li></ol><p><i>наблюда́тельный</i> (nabljudátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) observation, observational</li><li>observant</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) supervision, supervisory</li></ol><p><i>наблюда́тель</i> (nabljudátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch, to observe</li><li>to study</li><li>to supervise, to take care of</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>наблюдати</i> (nabljudati). Equivalent to <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>блюда́ть</i> (bljudátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be observed</li></ol><p><i>наблюда́ть</i> (nabljudátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observation</li><li>supervision</li><li>surveillance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) nawab</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nabob</i>, ultimately from Arabic نُوَّاب (nuwwāb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devotion, piety, godliness</li></ol><p><i>на́божный</i> (nábožnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pious, devout, religious</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>awry (obliquely, crookedly&#59; askew)</li><li>sideways</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>set, kit, outfit, collection (collection of various objects for a particular purpose, such as a set of tools)</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) font type case containing movable type</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) typesetting, composition</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) typesetting plate</li><li>enlistment, admission, levy, recruitment, enrollment</li><li>wire gage, gauge</li><li>bank of cells</li><li>dialing (telephony)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) climb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naborъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typesetter, compositor</li></ol><p><i>набо́р</i> (nabór) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sketch, to draw an outline <i>(of)</i></li><li>to sketch, to outline, to draft</li><li>to jot down</li><li>to throw <i>(on, over)</i>, to slip on, to slip over</li></ol><p><i>наброса́ть</i> (nabrosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>набро́сить</i> (nabrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to assault, to go <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down <i>(on)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i>, to jump all over</li></ol><p><i>набра́сывать</i> (nabrásyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set up, to compose, to type (a text on a computer)</li><li>to gather; to pick; to collect; to assemble; to take (a lot of)</li><li>to take on; to enlist, to recruit; to engage; to enrol, to make up</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to accumulate (<i>of several people</i>)</li><li>to pile up, to accumulate (<i>of debts, work, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to collect, to summon up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘an emotion’</small>]</li><li>to amount, to add up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to experience, to undergo [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘an emotion’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to learn, to pick up [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘a skill, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be full, to tank up on alcohol, to load up, to get wasted/drunk</li></ol><p><i>набра́ть</i> (nabrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to come across [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to hit upon [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘an idea’</small>]</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;low colloquial) to gather (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sketch, to draw an outline <i>(of)</i></li><li>to sketch, to outline, to draft</li><li>to jot down</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>прики́нуть</i> (prikínutʹ), <i>прики́дывать</i> (prikídyvatʹ), related to <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw <i>(on, over)</i>, to slip on, to slip over</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to assault, to go <i>(for)</i></li><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down <i>(on)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i>, to jump all over</li></ol><p><i>набро́сить</i> (nabrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sketch, draft, outline</li></ol><p><i>наброса́ть</i> (nabrosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;rare) to swell</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;rare) to swell up from moisture</li></ol><p><i>набря́кнуть</i> (nabrjáknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to swell</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to swell up from moisture</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бря́кнуть</i> (brjáknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heap up, to fill</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to screw over, to dupe</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>буты́ль</i> (butýlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swelling</li></ol><p><i>набуха(ть)</i> (nabuxa(tʹ), “to swell”) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell up, to swell</li><li>to toss, to pour, to pile up, etc. a large quantity of something (often too large)</li></ol><p><i>набу́хнуть</i> (nabúxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бу́хать</i> (búxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бу́хнуть</i> (búxnutʹ, “to swell”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delusion, hallucination</li><li>obsession</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>наваждениѥ</i> (navaždenije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>навадити</i> (navaditi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(on)</i>, to heap up <i>(on)</i>, to pile <i>(on)</i></li><li>to load <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li><li>to pile up</li><li>to come in crowds</li></ol><p><i>навали́ть</i> (navalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean all one's weight <i>(upon)</i>, to bring all one's weight to bear <i>(on)</i></li><li>to overburden, to weigh heavily <i>(on)</i></li><li>to come <i>(over)</i>, to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to have an attack <i>(of)</i></li><li>to fall <i>(on)</i></li><li>to go to work <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to dig <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>нава́ливать</i> (naválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(on)</i>, to heap up <i>(on)</i>, to pile <i>(on)</i></li><li>to load <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li><li>to pile up</li><li>to come in crowds</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean all one's weight <i>(upon)</i>, to bring all one's weight to bear <i>(on)</i></li><li>to overburden, to weigh heavily <i>(on)</i></li><li>to come <i>(over)</i>, to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to have an attack <i>(of)</i></li><li>to fall <i>(on)</i></li><li>to go to work <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to dig <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>навали́ть</i> (navalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in bulk, in a pile</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lots, a lot [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>навал(и́ть)</i> (naval(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ом</i> (-om).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) supporter of Alexei Navalny, a prominent Russian political figure and opposition leader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bulk; in bulk</li><li>blowing across the river; pressing a vessel or drifting ice to the pier, to the ice or to another object (<i>of the wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>навали́ть</i> (navalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Навальный)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Navalny</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>particularly</i>) Alexei Navalny, Russian opposition leader</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>нава́льный</i> (naválʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a supporter of Alexei Navalny, especially a young one</li></ol><p><i>Нава́льный</i> (Naválʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat on the surface of soup or other liquid</li><li>broth</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) profit</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>навари́ть</i> (navarítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook (some amount of food)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) to boil (a quantity of steel) in an open-hearth furnace</li><li>to lengthen by welding</li><li>to weld on</li><li>to profit</li></ol><p><i>навари́ть</i> (navarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook (some amount of food)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) to boil (a quantity of steel) in an open-hearth furnace</li><li>to lengthen by welding</li><li>to weld on</li><li>to profit</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Navajo <i>(indigenous North-American people)</i></li><li>Navajo <i>(the Athabaskan language of this people)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to cast <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>наве́ять</i> (navéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) induction</li><li>aiming, pointing, homing, guidance</li><li>establishing, putting</li></ol><p><i>навед-</i> (naved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>наве́даться</i> (navédatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) forever</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) forever (for all time, for all eternity&#59; for an infinite amount of time)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>probably, most likely</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) for sure, certainly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) probably, most likely</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) for sure, certainly, for certain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap (something) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to screw (something) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to eat a lot of (something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wind [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘around something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to screw [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to appear (<i>of tears in the eyes</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up, to appear unexpectedly (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>наверну́ть</i> (navernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>certainly, for sure</li><li>safely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up for (something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>верста́ть</i> (verstátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to screw [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to make (a quantity of something) by twisting, to twist out (a quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up, upward</li><li>upstairs (direction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above (in a higher place)</li><li>upstairs (located upstairs)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>верху́</i> (verxú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (a quantity of something) by twisting, to twist out (a quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>наверте́ть</i> (navertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awning, shed, lean-to (an open-sided structure with a roof)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) overhang</li><li>(sports,&#32;colloquial) lob, lobbed ball</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>наве́сить</i> (navésitʹ, “to hang on, suspend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slightly drunk, tipsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang up, to suspend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to load <i>(with)</i>, to tax <i>(with)</i>, to charge <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to lob</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intended for hanging, suspended</li></ol><p><i>наве́сить</i> (navésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>навесно́й</i> (navesnój)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) awning, canopy</li><li>(military,&#32;sports) having a steep, arcing trajectory</li></ol><p><i>наве́с</i> (navés) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to direct (<i>at</i>), to aim (<i>at</i>), to point (<i>at</i>)</li><li>to cover, to coat, to apply</li><li>to introduce, bring, produce, make, cause</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call on, to come and see</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-вести́ть</i> (-vestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slander, calumny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever (for all time, for all eternity&#59; for an infinite amount of time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh out <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>to hang up <i>(a number of)</i>, to suspend <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>to give it <i>(to)</i>, to give the business, to give the works</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang up, to suspend</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to load <i>(with)</i>, to tax <i>(with)</i>, to charge <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to lob</li><li>to weigh out <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>наве́сить</i> (navésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or from <i>наве́шать</i> (navéšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call on, to come and see</li></ol><p><i>навести́ть</i> (navestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to cast <i>(over)</i></li><li>to winnow <i>(some quantity)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on one's back, backwards, face up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coil, to wind, to reel</li></ol><p><i>нави́ть</i> (navítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ива</i> (-iva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coiling, winding, reeling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;computing) navigator</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>navigator</i>, reinforced by English in the computing sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) navigation; navigational</li></ol><p><i>навига́ция</i> (navigácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navigation</li></ol><p>Via Polish <i>nawigacja</i> from Latin <i>nāvigātiō</i> (“sailing, navigation”); introduced by Theophan Prokopovich.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang over (something)</li></ol><p><i>нави́снуть</i> (navísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang over (something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring on, to incur, to draw</li></ol><p><i>навле́к-</i> (navlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring on, to incur, to draw</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to direct (<i>at</i>), to aim (<i>at</i>), to point (<i>at</i>)</li><li>to cover, to coat, to apply</li><li>to introduce, bring, produce, make, cause</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire fear, to browbeat (to bully in an intimidating way)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying, training (<i>of a weapon</i>)</li><li>tipping off, tip-off</li><li>bridging operation</li><li>focusing</li><li>(<i>criminology</i>) inside job</li></ol><p><i>наводи́ть</i> (navodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood</li></ol><p><i>наводни́ть</i> (navodnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flood</li></ol><p><i>наводни́ть</i> (navodnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) gunlayer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tipper-off</li><li>person who finds a likely place for robbery, marker</li></ol><p><i>наводи́ть</i> (navodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наваждение</i> (navaždenije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, manure</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>воз</i> (voz), literally “something brought (on a cart)”. Analyzable as a deverbal from <i>навози́ть</i> (navozítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dung, manure</li></ol><p><i>наво́з</i> (navóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam, weaver's beam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillowcase, pillowslip</li></ol><p><i>на́волока</i> (návoloka, “pillowcase”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Root and suffix cognate with Polish <i>powłoczka</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Norwegian Nynorsk <i>putetrekk</i>, <i>dynetrekk</i> (<<+ <i>&#42;trakjaną</i> &lt; <i>&#42;trekaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to wind (something) up, to wrap (something) up circularly</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to heap up (a large quantity of something, in a disorderly way)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overcomplicate (something), to make (something) overly convoluted</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make a mess of (something), to do (something undesirable or erroneous)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to eat a lot of (something)</li></ol><p><i>навороти́ть</i> (navorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to heap up (a large quantity of something, in a disorderly way)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overcomplicate (something), to make (something) overly convoluted</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make a mess of (something), to do (something undesirable or erroneous)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) equipped with numerous additional features or modifications, tricked-out, loaded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nap (fabric) (to form a raised pile on)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ворси́ть</i> (vorsítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nap (fabric) (to form a raised pile on)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ворсова́ть</i> (vorsovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;transitive) to sharpen, to whet</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>востри́ть</i> (vostrítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to prick up one's ears, to perk up one's ears (to start listening attentively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie <i>(to someone)</i>, to tell lies <i>(to someone)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make mistakes <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>врать</i> (vratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do a lot of harm <i>(to by)</i>, to harm</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, unlikely, hardly likely</li><li>I don’t think so</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вряд</i> (vrjad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly, unlikely, hardly likely (used to express doubt about the veracity of some statement)</li><li>I don’t think so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever</li><li>once and for all</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>всегда</i> (vsegda)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(hunting,&#32;military,&#32;about shooting) offhand (quickly throwing up the gun and not leaning on anything with the elbows)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) off the top of one's head, offhand, without preparation, roughly, randomly</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>вски́дку</i> (vskídku, “a throwing/tossing up”, in the accusative case)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the opposite direction; coming, approaching</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>toward, towards</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>встре́чу</i> (vstréču), accusative singular of <i>встре́ча</i> (vstréča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inside out, wrong side out</li><li>the wrong way round</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skill, experience</li><li>acquired habit, practice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;navykъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with bulging (eyes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to get the hang of [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>навы́кнуть</i> (navýknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to get the hang of [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́кнуть</i> (-výknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not tucked into one's trousers/boots etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packing, loading (a packed animal)</li></ol><p><i>навью́чивать</i> (navʹjúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (a beast of burden)</li></ol><p><i>навью́чить</i> (navʹjúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (a beast of burden)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie <i>(on)</i>, to fasten <i> (to)</i></li><li>to make <i>(a number of)</i>, to knit</li><li>to impose <i>(on)</i>, to thrust <i>(on)</i>, to foist <i>(off)</i> <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stick, to get stuck</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p><p><i>навя́знуть</i> (navjáznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impose oneself, to obtrude oneself</li></ol><p><i>навяза́ть</i> (navjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get stuck, (<i>intransitive</i>) to stick</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>вя́знуть</i> (vjáznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intrusive, annoying</li><li>obsessive</li><li>haunting (<i>song</i>)</li></ol><p><i>навяза́ть</i> (navjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imposition, imposing</li><li>attaching, attachment, fastening</li></ol><p><i>навя́зывать</i> (navjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie <i>(on)</i>, to fasten <i> (to)</i></li><li>to make <i>(a number of)</i>, to knit</li><li>to impose <i>(on)</i>, to thrust <i>(on)</i>, to foist <i>(off)</i> <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>навяза́ть</i> (navjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impose oneself, to obtrude oneself</li></ol><p><i>навя́зывать</i> (navjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>наверста́ть</i> (naverstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to screw [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наверну́ть</i> (navernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wind [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘around something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to screw [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to appear (<i>of tears in the eyes</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up, to appear unexpectedly (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>навёртывать</i> (navjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defecate, to shit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil, to defile</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to do mischief, to play dirty tricks on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>га́дить</i> (gáditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nagaika (a type of short whip with a wide, flat, often weighted tip)</li></ol><p>From <i>нога́йский</i> (nogájskij, “Nogai”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Nagant M1895 revolver (a seven-shot, gas-seal revolver designed and produced by Belgian industrialist Léon Nagant for the Russian Empire.)</li></ol><p>Named after the original Belgian designers Léon and Émile <b>Nagant</b> (/naˈɡɑ̃/). Officially was called <i>револьве́р систе́мы Нага́на</i> (revolʹvér sistémy Nagána).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snuff <b>(candle)</b>, candle-snuff,</li><li>scale <b>(on metal)</b></li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>нагора́ть</i> (nagorátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken nugget</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nuggets</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) pin, peg, dowel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nagel</i> (“nail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoop, to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>нагиба́ть</i> (nagibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>нагиба́ть</i> (nagibátʹ)</li></ol><p>Modification of <i>нагиба́ть</i> (nagibátʹ), influenced by perfective <i>нагну́ть</i> (nagnútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stark naked (completely unclothed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li></ol><p><i>на́глый</i> (náglyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) an impudent, insolent person</li></ol><p>From <i>на́глый</i> (náglyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impertinently, impudently</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>наглъ</i> (naglŭ, “quick”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naglъ</i>. Per Vasmer, cognates include Sanskrit <i>अञ्जसा</i> (añjasā, “soon, suddenly”), <i>अञ्जस्</i> (añjas, “instantly, quickly”) and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌺𐍃</i> (anaks, “suddenly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) English-speaking Western countries; especially the United States and Britain.</li></ol><p>From <i>на́глый</i> (náglyj, “shameless”) +&lrm; <i>англосакс</i> (anglosaks, “Anglo-Saxon, Westerner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudence, insolence, effrontery; cheek</li></ol><p><i>на́глый</i> (náglyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tightly, hermetically</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>глу́хо</i> (glúxo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless, impudent, impertinent, insolent, barefaced</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>на́глый</i> (náglyj), from Old East Slavic <i>на́глꙑи</i> (náglyi), <i>на́глъ</i> (náglŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nãglъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see enough</li><li>to feast one's eyes</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by visual demonstration, visually, graphically, clearly</li></ol><p><i>нагля́дный</i> (nagljádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearness, obviousness</li><li>use of visual methods, use of visual aids</li></ol><p><i>нагля́дный</i> (nagljádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear</li><li>visual, graphic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up to</li><li>to equal (someone) in success</li><li>to make up for (lost time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘an emotion’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to pump, to supercharge</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гнести́</i> (gnestí). Compare Kashubian <i>nagniesc</i> and Polish <i>nagnieść</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumping, pressurization, inflating</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) aggravation, exacerbation (<i>of negative emotions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нагнета́ть</i> (nagnetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump, to pressurize, to inflate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to aggravate, to exacerbate (negative emotions)</li></ol><p>From <i>нагнет-</i> (nagnet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare Kashubian <i>nagniatac</i>, Polish <i>nagniatać</i>, and Silesian <i>nagniŏtać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoop, to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>нагну́ть</i> (nagnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to record, to read for recording, to have one's reading recorded</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander</li></ol><p><i>наговори́ть</i> (nagovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slander, calumny</li><li>incantation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to record, to read for recording, to have one's reading recorded</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander</li><li>to say, to tell (a lot)</li><li>to bewitch</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk one's fill; to have a good long talk</li></ol><p><i>наговори́ть</i> (nagovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nude, naked, bare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nagъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nogʷós</i> (“naked”); see there for the cognates.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nagoya</li></ol><p><i>Наго́я</i> (Nagója) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close to the skin, closely shaven</li><li>naked (<i>of metal</i>), drawn (<i>of a sword</i>), to the bare metal</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>го́ло</i> (gólo); but compare also <i>наго́й</i> (nagój, “naked”).</p><p>Apparently from <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>го́ло</i> (gólo), with stress shift (but see above).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decisively, completely (in terms of a military defeat)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>голову́</i> (golovú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>hydraulics</i> pileup</li><li><i>waves</i> run-up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telling-off, rating, scolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up to</li><li>to equal (someone) in success</li><li>to make up for (lost time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘an emotion’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mountainous, upland, highland</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>го́рный</i> (górnyj), from <i>гора́</i> (gorá, “mountain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highland, upland (mountainous land)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nudity, bareness, nakedness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at call, at the ready, in readiness, ready, prepared, on the lookout</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>гото́ве</i> (gotóve), with the second element having the form of a prepositional singular of a nonexistent noun based on the verb <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ). Attested at least since the mid-16th.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal a lot of, to plunder, to accumulate by robbing</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward (something of value given in return for an act)</li><li>award</li><li>decoration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>нагорода</i> (nahoroda) in the XVII (1696), from Polish <i>nagroda</i>. Remodeled after Old Church Slavonic <i>градъ, градити</i> (gradŭ, graditi) later in the XVIII. Falsely believed to derive from unattested Old Church Slavonic <i>&#42;награда</i> (&#42;nagrada).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward, to award</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-гради́ть</i> (-gradítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward, to award</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rewarding; decorating (with a prize, medal, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat, heating</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nagrěvъ</i>, from <i>&#42;grěvъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heating</li></ol><p><i>нагрева́ть</i> (nagrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat, to warm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swindle</li></ol><p><i>нагре́ть</i> (nagrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get warm/hot</li></ol><p><i>нагрева́ть</i> (nagrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat, to warm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swindle</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get warm/hot</li></ol><p><i>нагре́ть</i> (nagrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pile up, to heap up</li></ol><p><i>нагромозди́ть</i> (nagromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piling up, pileup, heap, heaping</li></ol><p>From <i>нагромозди́ть</i> (nagromozdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Compare Ukrainian <i>нагрома́дження</i> (nahromádžennja), Polish <i>nagromadzenie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pile up, to heap up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>громозди́ть</i> (gromozdítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to insult, to speak rudely to; to talk back to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>груби́ть</i> (grubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bib (item of clothing for babies)</li><li>(<i>armor</i>) breastplate</li><li>(<i>harness</i>) breast collar</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>нагръдник</i> (nagrǎdnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) breast; pectoral</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>грудь</i> (grudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load <i>(with)</i>, to lade <i>(with)</i>, to freight <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to load <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>нагрузи́ть</i> (nagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load <i>(with)</i>, to lade <i>(with)</i>, to freight <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to load <i>(with)</i>, to saddle <i>(with)</i>, to lumber <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loading</li><li>load, workload</li><li>(engineering,&#32;medicine,&#32;electrical engineering) load</li><li>condition, stipulation (<i>especially to purchase something</i>)</li><li>a burden</li></ol><p><i>нагрузи́ть</i> (nagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come unexpectedly (out of the blue)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break out, <i>(cold)</i> to strike</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гря́нуть</i> (grjánutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(livestock)</i> to fatten, to put on weight</li><li>to acquire, to get</li></ol><p><i>нагуля́ть</i> (naguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk/stroll to one's heart's content</li><li>to play/amuse oneself to one's heart's content</li><li>to sleep around to one's heart's content</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above, over</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>надъ</i> (nadŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>takes verbs, produces verbs: to extend, add</li><li>takes verbs, produces verbs: to apply an action or measure</li><li>takes verbs, produces verbs: to apply an action in incomplete manner</li><li>takes verbs, produces verbs: super-</li><li>takes nouns, produces nouns and adjectives: over, above</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give (<i>in some quantity or in several separate actions</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to push</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exert pressure <i>(on)</i>, to pressurize <i>(into)</i>, to pressure <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to push</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exert pressure <i>(on)</i>, to pressurize <i>(into)</i>, to pressure <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>надави́ть</i> (nadavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition; markup; raise; extra charge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move/push/pull <i>(up to)</i></li></ol><p><i>надви́г-</i> (nadvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, to draw near, to impend, to be imminent</li><li>to come <i>(over)</i>, to move <i>(onto)</i></li></ol><p><i>надвига́ть</i> (nadvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move/push/pull <i>(up to)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, to draw near, to impend, to be imminent</li><li>to come <i>(over)</i>, to move <i>(onto)</i></li></ol><p><i>надви́нуть</i> (nadvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above-water</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) surface</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two, in half, into two parts</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ambiguously, with a double meaning</li></ol><p><i>на́</i> (ná) +&lrm; <i>дво́е</i> (dvóje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tombstone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>located on or over a grave</li><li>located or made on a gravestone</li><li>funerary (<i>speech, weeping, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>гроб</i> (grob) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on, to get on</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>надзява́ць</i> (nadzjavácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be put on</li></ol><p><i>надева́ть</i> (nadevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope, expectation, prospect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>надежда</i> (nadežda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadeďa</i>. Doublet of <i>надёжа</i> (nadjóža), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Надежда)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nadezhda; its short form, Nadia, is common in English</li></ol><p>From <i>наде́жда</i> (nadéžda, “hope”); a calque of Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλπίς</i> (Elpís). See <i>Ве́ра</i> (Véra), <i>Любо́вь</i> (Ljubóvʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Allotment land, which was the designation for pieces of land granted to peasants by their former landlords and the state with the reforms of the 1860s. The allotment lands had minimum and maximum sizes and were usually collectively owned.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(certain quantity)</i> to make, to churn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to cause</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allotment, investment</li></ol><p><i>надели́ть</i> (nadelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allot, to give, to provide</li><li>to endow</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(certain quantity)</i> to make, to churn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to cause</li></ol><p><i>наде́лать</i> (nadélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allot, to give, to provide [<i>with</i> instrumental]</li><li>to endow</li></ol><p><i>надели́ть</i> (nadelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on, to get on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hope</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naděja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope</li><li>to rely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadějati sę</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warden, turnkey, supervisor, overseer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervision</li><li>surveillance</li><li>inspectorate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadъzorъ</i>. Cognate with <i>надзира́ть</i> (nadzirátʹ, “to supervise”). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadъ</i> and <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) surveillance</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) supervision, supervisory</li></ol><p><i>надзо́р</i> (nadzór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nadir (point of the sky)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kneecap, patella</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elytron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) elytron</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>кры́лья</i> (krýlʹja, “wings”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;formal) to have to (to do as a requirement)</li></ol><p>Since the late 17th century. <i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ), possibly a calque of German <i>obliegen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proper, fitting, suitable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken, fractured</li><li>cracked</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) (emotionally) broken, shattered, crushed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>arrogantly, haughtily</li></ol><p><i>надме́нный</i> (nadménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haughtiness, arrogance</li></ol><p><i>надме́нный</i> (nadménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supercilious, haughty, arrogant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>надъменъ</i> (nadŭmenŭ, “inflated, blown up”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadъmenъ</i> (“inflated, blown up”) - passive past participle of <i>&#42;nadǫti</i> (“inflate, blow up”). Doublet of <i>наду́тый</i> (nadútyj) and <i>до́мна</i> (dómna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>set theory</i>) superset</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>мно́жество</i> (mnóžestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>над</i> (nad): above, over</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>надо</i> (nado), <i>надобѣ</i> (nadobě), from dative/locative singular of <i>надоба</i> (nadoba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadoba</i>, from <i>&#42;na-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;doba</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>надъ</i> (nadŭ), <i>надо</i> (nado), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Exclamation expressing surprise, amazement or discontent; well, well, (would you) look at that, what do you know, fancy that, wow</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> на́до (nádo),&lrm; же (že).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>над-</i> (nad-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) need, necessity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>надоба</i> (nadoba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadoba</i> (whence Czech <i>nádoba</i> (“dish, container”)), from <i>&#42;na</i> + <i>&#42;doba</i> (whence Old Church Slavonic <i>доба</i> (doba, “use, advantage”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to need</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>добиться</i> (dobitʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, necessity</li></ol><p><i>на́добный</i> (nádobnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) necessary</li></ol><p><i>на́доба</i> (nádoba, “need”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bore, obsessive person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annoyance (the act of annoying)</li></ol><p>From <i>надоеда́ть</i> (nadojedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore</li><li>to bother, to pester, to molest, to worry, to annoy, to plague</li></ol><p><i>надое́д-</i> (nadojéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tiresome, troublesome, annoying, boring, bothersome, bothering, pesky</li></ol><p><i>надоеда́ть</i> (nadojedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore</li><li>to bother, to pester, to molest, to worry, to annoy, to plague</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ). Compare <i>приеда́ться</i> (prijedátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy, to pester</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for long, for a long time</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>до́лго</i> (dólgo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear slightly</li><li>to overstrain, to overtax, to undermine</li></ol><p><i>надо-</i> (nado-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear slightly</li><li>to overstrain oneself, to exert oneself to the utmost, to knock oneself out, to bust a gut</li><li>to shout, to cry, to cry at the top of one's lungs</li></ol><p><i>надорва́ть</i> (nadorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supraspinate</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>ость</i> (ostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to give the idea (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>score <i>(on the wood)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to label, to caption, to write an inscription on</li><li>to write (<i>something that is above something else, e.g. a missing letter above the line</i>)</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to label, to caption, to write an inscription on</li><li>to write (<i>something that is above something else, e.g. a missing letter above the line</i>)</li></ol><p><i>надписа́ть</i> (nadpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription, writing, label</li><li>dedication (<i>on a book</i>)</li><li>address (<i>on an envelope</i>)</li><li>legend (<i>on a coin, medal, map</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>надписа́ть</i> (nadpisátʹ, “to caption, to write an inscription on”) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), with typical stress shift (compare <i>по́дпись</i> (pódpisʹ), <i>за́пись</i> (zápisʹ), etc.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epinephros, adrenal gland</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>по́чка</i> (póčka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) suprarenal, adrenal</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>по́чка</i> (póčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nautical) to burnish</li></ol><p><i>надра́ить</i> (nadráitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nautical) to burnish</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>дра́ить</i> (dráitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incising, incision, slicing, notching</li><li>cut, incision, notch</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>надре́зать</i> (nadrézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>notched</li></ol><p>From <i>надре́з</i> (nadréz) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outrage, sacrilege</li><li>mockery, ridicule, abuse</li></ol><p><i>надруга́ть(ся)</i> (nadrugátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desecrate, to defile, to commit an outrage against [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to humiliate, to insult</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>руга́ться</i> (rugátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slight tear, rent</li><li>breakdown, anguish</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>надрыва́ть</i> (nadryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear slightly</li><li>to overstrain, to overtax, to undermine</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear slightly</li><li>to overstrain oneself, to exert oneself to the utmost, to knock oneself out, to bust a gut</li><li>to shout, to cry, to cry at the top of one's lungs</li></ol><p><i>надрыва́ть</i> (nadryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overseer, supervisor; jailer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build on, to overbuild, to raise (the height of something)</li></ol><p><i>надстро́ить</i> (nadstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build on, to overbuild, to raise (the height of something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raising, building on</li><li>superstructure, additional storey</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) superstructure</li></ol><p><i>надстро́ить</i> (nadstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlinear, superscript</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>строка́</i> (stroká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swindler, cheat, trickster</li></ol><p><i>надува́ть</i> (naduvátʹ, “to swindle”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindle, fraud, scam, skulduggery</li></ol><p><i>надува́ть</i> (naduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inflate, to puff out, to blow up</li><li>to drift <i>(by wind)</i></li><li>to give someone a chill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dupe, to swindle</li></ol><p><i>наду́ть</i> (nadútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fill out, to belly out, to swell out, to blow up, to inflate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be puffed up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sulk, to pout</li></ol><p><i>надува́ть</i> (naduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflatable (able to be inflated)</li></ol><p><i>надува́ть</i> (naduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflatable castle, bouncing castle, bouncy castle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced, artificial</li><li>unlikely, far-fetched</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make up one's mind, to decide</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to devise, to invent</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swollen, increased in volume</li><li>arrogant, haughty</li><li>pompous</li><li>disgruntled, offended, angry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inflate, to puff out, to blow up</li><li>to drift <i>(by wind)</i></li><li>to give someone a chill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dupe, to swindle</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ду́ть</i> (dútʹ). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>надути</i> and Polish <i>nadąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fill out, to belly out, to swell out, to blow up, to inflate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be puffed up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sulk, to pout</li></ol><p><i>наду́ть</i> (nadútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) to get angry, to get offended, to huff, to sulk</li></ol><p>Literally, “to sulk like a mouse in front of groats”. At first, according to Vladimir Dal, it meant to "put on airs" before the object of the observation, now it means "to sulk", "to feel offended".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dab scent, to spray, to splash</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>души́ть</i> (dušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fine file</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nadelfeile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>над</i> (nad).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nibble at, to bite at</li></ol><p><i>надъе́д-</i> (nadʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nibble at, to bite at</li></ol><p><i>над-</i> (nad-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;slang) to obtain by black means</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ды́бать</i> (dýbatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) hope; support, stronghold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>надежа</i> (nadeža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadeďa</i>. Doublet of <i>наде́жда</i> (nadéžda) borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reliably (in a reliable manner)</li></ol><p><i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reliability; trustworthiness; dependability; security</li></ol><p><i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reliable, dependable, trustworthy</li><li>firm, safe, sure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nadeďьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>надёжа</i> (nadjóža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;derogatory) scam, fuckover, lie(s), trick</li></ol><p><i>наеба́ть</i> (najebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-во</i> (-vo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to fuck someone over (to cheat someone)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Compare with Polish <i>najebać</i> and Serbo-Croatian <i>najebati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fall, to plunge, especially as a result of negligence; to fuck oneself up, to eat shit</li><li>to break down (of a device, etc.)</li><li>to fail, to go to smash</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ёбнуться</i> (jóbnutʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat one's fill/get one's fill, to eat plenty <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>наед-</i> (najed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tête-à-tête, privately</li><li>alone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flying visit</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) inroad, raid, incursion</li><li>running-down accident (traffic accident where someone gets run over)</li><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) zoom-in, dollying in</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) shakedown, armtwisting, bullying, bullyboy tactics</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>наезжа́ть</i> (najezžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horseman, equestrian</li><li><i>(horse)</i> trainer, jockey</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) ichneumon-fly</li></ol><p><i>нае́здить</i> (najézditʹ, “to run over, cross, ride”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run into, to hit [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to visit periodically [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come in large numbers</li><li>to seal, to smooth down (a road)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act foolishly or to talk nonsense</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ерунда́</i> (jerundá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat one's fill/get one's fill, to eat plenty <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run into, to hit [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come in large numbers</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) to assault verbally, to threat or blame in offensive manner</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pressing (e.g. of a button)</li></ol><p><i>нажа́ть</i> (nažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to push</li><li>to urge, to impel, to influence, to put pressure on</li><li>to press on, to press ahead</li><li>to make by pressing</li><li>to reap, to harvest (a specified quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emery (<i>stone or powder</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) emery paper, sandpaper</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) big club, battle axe (<i>16th to 18th century</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ناجاق (nacak, “hatchet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sandpaper</li></ol><p><i>нажда́к</i> (naždák) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandpaper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) emery</li></ol><p><i>нажда́к</i> (naždák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, profit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to earn, to profit</li></ol><p><i>нажи́ть</i> (nažítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bait</li></ol><p><i>наживи́ть</i> (naživítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pressure, push</li><li>emphasis</li><li>thick line</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to push</li><li>to urge, to impel, to influence, to put pressure on</li><li>to press on, to press ahead</li><li>to make by pressing</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reap, to harvest (a specified quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to earn, to profit</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) (<i>only in expressions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) to eat a lot, to eat one's fill</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) to get very drunk</li></ol><p><i>нажра́ть</i> (nažrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to badmouth, to slander (extremely)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to criticize (to one's heart's content)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the next day</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>за́втра</i> (závtra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back, backward, backwards</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>get back</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>зад</i> (zad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) behind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nasalized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nasalization</li></ol><p><i>наза́льный</i> (nazálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nasal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nasal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>nasal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Latin <i>nasālis</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néh₂s</i> (whence also <i>нос</i> (nos)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazarene</li></ol><p><i>Назарет</i> (Nazaret, “Nazareth”) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin, “-ene”), closely related to назѡрѧни́нъ (nazoręnínŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazarene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be very eager to get something</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>на-</i> (na-) + dialectal <i>за́рить</i> (záritʹ, “to interest, to excite, to annoy”) + <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from dialectal <i>за́ркий</i> (zárkij, “greedy, lustful; angry, grumpy”). Cognate (with vocalic alternation) with Old Czech <i>zóřiv</i> (“sharp, hot, frantic”), <i>zóřiti</i> (“to rampage”). For further etymology, often compared with Sanskrit <i>हर्यति</i> (háryati, “to love, to find pleasure, to thirst”), Ancient Greek <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “to be glad”), <i>χάρις</i>&nbsp;f (kháris, “grace, beauty”), Oscan <i>herest</i> (“he wants”), Umbrian <i>heriest</i> (“he wants”), Latin <i>horior</i> (“to encourage”), <i>hortor</i> (“to exhort, to urge”), Old High German <i>gern</i> (“greedy”), Avestan <i>𐬰𐬀𐬭𐬀</i>&nbsp;m (zara, “striving, purpose”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to call a lot and often</li><li>to ring a lot and often</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name, appellation, title</li></ol><p><i>назва́ть</i> (nazvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a relative, usually a brother</i>) sworn, blood (bounded by loyalty instead of genetic relationship)</li></ol><p><i>назва́ть</i> (nazvátʹ, “to name, to call”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, to specify</li><li>to call [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by some name’</small>]</li><li>to convene, to invite many people</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to identify oneself, to give one’s name</li><li>to be called, to be named</li></ol><p><i>назва́ть</i> (nazvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) land, ground, surface</li></ol><p><i>на́земь</i> (názemʹ, “to the ground”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) down, onto the ground</li></ol><p>Shortened form of <i>на зе́млю</i> (na zémlju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of Nazianzus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edification, moral (the moral significance or practical lesson)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to spite</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>зло</i> (zlo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appoint, to designate, to nominate</li><li>to fix, to settle, to set, to assign</li><li>to prescribe, to destine</li></ol><p><i>назна́чить</i> (naznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appointment (act of appointing; designation of a person to hold an office)</li><li>fixing, setting, assignment</li><li>destination, purpose</li><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) prescription</li></ol><p><i>назна́чить</i> (naznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appoint, to designate, to nominate</li><li>to fix, to settle, to set, to assign</li><li>to prescribe, to destine</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irritating, intrusive, pushy</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, original meaning may have been "noisy", in which case it may be derived from <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>зой</i> (zoj, “scream”) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj). Compare also <i>зои́ться</i> (zoítʹsja, “to rage, to rave”). Both <i>зой</i> (zoj) and <i>зои́ться</i> (zoítʹsja) are ultimately related to <i>зия́ть</i> (zijátʹ, “to yawn, to gape”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen, to become ripe, to mature, (abscess) to come to a head</li><li>to be about to happen, to boil up, to be brewing</li></ol><p><i>назре́ть</i> (nazrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen, to become ripe, to mature, (abscess) to come to a head</li><li>to be about to happen, to boil up, to be brewing</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>зре́ть</i> (zrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) inside out, off pat, thoroughly</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на зубо́к</i> (na zubók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, to specify</li><li>to call [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘by some name’</small>]</li><li>to convene, to invite many people</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to call a spade a spade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to identify oneself, to give one’s name</li><li>to be called, to be named</li></ol><p><i>называ́ть</i> (nazyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get very drunk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>prefix forming superlatives from comparative adjectives (or nonstandardly from positive adjectives)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;najь-</i>.</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>най-</i> (naj-), Polish <i>naj-</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>naj-</i>, Slovak <i>naj-</i> Slovene <i>naj-</i>, Czech <i>nej-</i>, Macedonian <i>нај-</i> (naj-), Bulgarian <i>най-</i> (naj-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(superlative)</i> the most, above all, most of all</li></ol><p><i>наи-</i> (nai-) +&lrm; <i>бо́лее</i> (bóleje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the greatest, the largest, the most</li><li>the worst (in negative context)</li><li>maximal, maximum</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>naively</li></ol><p><i>наи́вный</i> (naívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naïveté, naivety</li></ol><p><i>наи́вный</i> (naívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naive, ingenuous, unsophisticated</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>naïve</i>. Further from Latin <i>nātīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij): highest, greatest</li></ol><p><i>наи-</i> (nai-) +&lrm; <i>вы́сший</i> (výsšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced, feigned, faked, contrived, hollow, artificial, disingenuous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play (several songs, pieces or theatrical roles)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gain (some money) gambling or playing a musical instrument</li><li>to play (<i>quietly or only the main melody, on a musical instrument</i>)</li><li>to play (a song or piece) for a recording</li><li>to record the music of (a record or film)</li><li>to feign, to force, to fake (an emotion)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play (several songs, pieces or theatrical roles)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gain (some money) gambling or playing a musical instrument</li><li>to play (<i>quietly or only the main melody, on a musical instrument</i>)</li><li>to play (a song or piece) for a recording</li><li>to record the music of (a record or film)</li><li>to feign, to force, to fake (an emotion)</li></ol><p><i>наигра́ть</i> (naigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) upwards (following the path of a gentile hill)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inside out, on the wrong side</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>изна́нку</i> (iznánku)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by heart, by rote</li></ol><p>Compounded from на (na) + из (iz) + уста́ (ustá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>superlative</i>) the best possible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij): the best, optimal</li></ol><p>From <i>наи-</i> (nai-) +&lrm; <i>лу́чший</i> (lúčšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(superlative)</i> the least, least of all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name, title</li></ol><p><i>наименова́ть</i> (naimenovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, to call</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>именова́ть</i> (imenovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj), superlative degree of <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij): the smallest, the least</li></ol><p><i>наи-</i> (nai-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ньший</i> (ménʹšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) in particular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(US, Canadian English) cater-corner (diagonally across)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(US, Canadian English) cater-corner (diagonally across)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) heaven-sent inspiration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>наитие</i> (naitije); constructed as if <i>найти́</i> (najtí) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) superlative degree of <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worst (most inferior) (superlative degree of плохо́й (ploxój) and худо́й (xudój))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundling</li></ol><p><i>найд-</i> (najd-) +&lrm; <i>-ёныш</i> (-jónyš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hire</li></ol><p>From наём (najóm), itself deverbal from наня́ть (nanjátʹ, “to hire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to think, to consider</li><li>to come across</li><li>to be located, to be situated</li><li>to happen to have</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;najьti</i> (“find”). By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>знайти́</i> (znajtý), Belarusian <i>знайсці́</i> (znajscí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injunction, order, instruction</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nakazъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punishment</li><li>penalty, chastisement</li></ol><p><i>наказа́ть</i> (nakazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to bid, to order</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) punishable</li></ol><p><i>наказа́ть</i> (nakazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́емый</i> (-újemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to bid, to order</li></ol><p><i>наказа́ть</i> (nakazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incandescence</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) tension, heat</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>накали́ть</i> (nakalítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incandescence</li></ol><p><i>нака́ливать</i> (nakálivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat to a very high temperature (metal or something metallic)</li><li>to agitate, to stir up, to build up tension in</li></ol><p><i>накали́ть</i> (nakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>накаля́ться</i> (nakaljátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>нака́ливать</i> (nakálivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat to a very high temperature (metal or something metallic)</li><li>to agitate, to stir up, to build up tension in</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кали́ть</i> (kalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to heat up to a high temperature, to become very hot</li><li>to become extremely tense</li></ol><p><i>накали́ть</i> (nakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick</li><li>to pin (something) on</li></ol><p><i>наколо́ть</i> (nakolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>нака́ливать</i> (nakálivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>накали́ть</i> (nakalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to heat up to a high temperature, to become very hot</li><li>to become extremely tense</li></ol><p><i>накаля́ть</i> (nakaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incandescent, red-hot</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) tense, strained</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) red-hot, furious, ready to flare up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the day before</li><li>on the eve of</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на кану́не</i> (na kanúne).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass, to pile up</li><li>to stockpile</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass</li></ol><p><i>нака́пливать</i> (nakáplivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundswell (nautical sense)</li><li>(<i>billiards</i>) follow</li><li>rolling (on, onto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make smooth <i>(by rolling)</i></li><li>to roll on</li><li>to roll up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash off, to throw together, to fling off, to fling together, to tear off</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(on, onto)</i></li><li><i>(waves)</i> to roll in, to coast in</li><li><i>(weather)</i> to set in, <i>(events)</i> to begin, to break out</li><li><i>(feelings, mood)</i> to come <i>(over)</i>, to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to have an attack</li><li><i>(alcohol)</i> to drink strong liquor, to especially vodka</li><li><i>(computer)</i> to apply patches, including installation of updates and applying cracks</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make smooth <i>(by rolling)</i></li><li>to roll on</li><li>to roll <i>(on, onto)</i></li><li><i>(waves)</i> to roll in, to coast in</li><li><i>(weather)</i> to set in, <i>(events)</i> to begin, to break out</li><li><i>(feelings, mood)</i> to come <i>(over)</i>, to be seized <i>(with)</i>, to have an attack</li></ol><p>From <i>наката́ть</i> (nakatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>накати́ть</i> (nakatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump</li><li>to inflate</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump</li><li>to inflate</li></ol><p><i>накача́ть</i> (nakačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cape, cloak, tippet, throw-over, wrapper</li></ol><p><i>наки́д-</i> (nakíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw on, to throw over</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add</li></ol><p><i>наки́д-</i> (nakíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to assault, to go <i>(for)</i></li><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down <i>(on)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i>, to jump all over</li></ol><p><i>наки́дывать</i> (nakídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw on, to throw over</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i>, to attack, to assault, to go <i>(for)</i></li><li>to fall <i>(upon, on)</i>, to pounce <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down <i>(on)</i>, to jump <i>(on)</i>, to jump all over</li></ol><p><i>наки́нуть</i> (nakínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form bubbles/foam/scum</li><li>to seethe, to boil</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover plate, cover strap, cover piece (<i>of a lock</i>)</li><li>hairpiece, false hair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slip, blunder, mess-up, error</li></ol><p><i>накла́д(ывать)</i> (naklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invoice</li><li>shipping list</li><li>waybill</li><li>bill of lading</li><li>consignment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>laid on, superimposed</li><li>false (<i>hair, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>накла́д(ывать)</i> (naklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay (something) on/over (something else), to superimpose</li><li>to apply (bandage, face cream, etc.)</li><li>to pack, to load (a fair amount of something)</li><li>to put in (stitches)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick on, to paste on</li></ol><p><i>накле́ить</i> (nakléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick on, to paste on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sticker, label</li></ol><p><i>накле́ить</i> (nakléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination</li><li>slope, incline, tilt, incidence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naklonъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) mood, mode</li></ol><p><i>наклони́ть</i> (naklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tilt, to bend</li><li>to bow, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>наклони́ть</i> (naklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination, leaning, propensity</li></ol><p><i>накло́нный</i> (naklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sloping, slanting, inclined, oblique</li></ol><p><i>накло́н</i> (naklón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tilt, to bend</li><li>to bow, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>наклони́ть</i> (naklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>наклоня́ть</i> (naklonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anvil</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) incus</li></ol><p><i>накова́ть</i> (nakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>накова́лня</i> (nakoválnja), Czech <i>kovadlina</i>, Macedonian <i>накова́лна</i> (nakoválna), Polish <i>kowadło</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>наковањ</i>, Slovak <i>nákova</i>, Slovene <i>nakovalo</i>. More distantly cognate with Latin <i>incūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge onto, to attach by forging [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to forge (<i>in some specified quantity</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge onto, to attach by forging [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to forge (<i>in some specified quantity</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>накова́ть</i> (nakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy,&#32;medicine) skin</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee-pad</li><li>knee guard, kneepiece</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headdress</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tattoo</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) trick, ruse, practical joke</li></ol><p><i>наколо́ть</i> (nakolótʹ, “to pin on, to prick”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick</li><li>to pin (something) on</li><li>to slaughter (many animals)</li><li>to chop, to split (a good amount of wood)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosquito head net, mosquito net</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кома́р</i> (komár) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at last, finally</li><li>in conclusion</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as an emphasiser when demanding something to occur sooner &#47; expressing impatience; (<i>US</i>) already</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>коне́ц</i> (konéc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>finally!; oh, finally!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferrule, tip, point (extreme top of something)</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>коне́ц</i> (konéc) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulator, stockpiler (someone who accumulates money, property, etc.)</li><li>hoarder</li><li>(general,&#32;technical,&#32;computing) accumulator, storage device</li><li>reservoir (natural or artificial)</li><li>(<i>criminal jargon</i>) collection area, holding area, holding cell (in a prison or paddy wagon)</li><li>waiting area (in an airport, after passing security)</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cumulative, accumulative</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass, to pile up</li><li>to stockpile</li><li>to save up (money)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to save up</li><li>to hide, to conceal (feelings, knowledge, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>копи́ть</i> (kopítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulation; cumulation</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) accumulated funds, savings</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass</li><li>to stockpile</li></ol><p><i>накопи́ть</i> (nakopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accumulate, to gather, to amass</li></ol><p><i>накопля́ть</i> (nakopljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed</li><li>to keep, to maintain</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to speckle, to cover with specks</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кра́пать</i> (krápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drizzle, to rain lightly</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to speckle, to cover with specks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to make up, to put lipstick <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starch</li></ol><p><i>накрахма́лить</i> (nakraxmálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starch</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крахма́лить</i> (kraxmálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to make up, to put lipstick <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>накра́сить</i> (nakrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(also&#32;impersonal,&#32;transitive) to tilt, to tip, to bank</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крени́ть</i> (krenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tilt, to tip, to list</li></ol><p><i>накрени́ть</i> (nakrenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(also&#32;impersonal,&#32;transitive) to tilt, to tip</li></ol><p><i>накрени́ть</i> (nakrenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tilt, to tip, to list</li></ol><p><i>накреня́ть</i> (nakrenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very firmly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very much, very strongly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) resolutely, decisively</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>кре́пко</i> (krépko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>crosswise, crossways, criss-cross</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крест</i> (krest)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuke loudly, to scream at, to chew out [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap around, to wind on</li><li>to screw on</li><li>to produce (a usually large quantity of something) by twisting</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate (an amount, a result, etc. by fraud)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to depict, to tell (something intricate or implausible)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to instill (a feeling in someone)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap around, to wind on</li><li>to screw on</li><li>to produce (a usually large quantity of something) by twisting</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to inflate (an amount, a result, etc. by fraud)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to depict, to tell (something intricate or implausible)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to instil (a feeling in someone)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;usually with <i>себя́</i> (sebjá) and sometimes also with <i>сам</i> (sam)) to catastrophise, to overthink, to get overly worked up, to work oneself up (to get lost in depressive or catastrophic thoughts)</li></ol><p><i>накрути́ть</i> (nakrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover (<i>from above, e.g. with a lid</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hit, to place fire on a target, to trap (<i>from above, e.g. of artillery fire or bombing</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lay (<i>a table</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch (<i>in the act</i>)</li></ol><p><i>накры́ть</i> (nakrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to flop, to fizzle out, to break down, to conk down, to go kaput, to fold up, to go to smash</li></ol><p><i>накрыва́ть</i> (nakryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover (<i>from above, e.g. with a lid</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hit, to place fire on a target, to trap (<i>from above, e.g. of artillery fire or bombing</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lay (<i>a table</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch (<i>in the act</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to flop, to fizzle out, to break down, to conk down, to go kaput, to fold up, to go to smash</li></ol><p><i>накры́ть</i> (nakrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;slang) to go belly up, to go tits up, to go kaput, to go down the pan, to go up in smoke (to go to ruin&#59; to fail completely)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to be covered with a copper basin”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be fucked (broken, beyond repair, lit&#58; "to be covered with a cunt")</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be of no chance of achieving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binnacle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>nachthuis</i> (literally “nighthouse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a good bathe</li></ol><p><i>накупи́ть</i> (nakupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>нали́чные</i> (nalíčnyje): cash, money</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cash under the table, off the record</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>нали́чные</i> (nalíčnyje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (an amount of something) (<i>usually a large quantity</i>)</li></ol><p><i>налови́ть</i> (nalovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impose, to inflict (opinions, penalties, prohibitions, punishments, requirements, taxes)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to apply (bandages or plaster casts for injured people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>налагати</i> (nalagati). By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ). Compare it's neutral counterpart <i>накла́дывать</i> (nakládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to repair, to set right</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to organize, to arrange, to establish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to normalize, to smooth (relations)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tune, to adjust to a certain harmony (<i>of a musical instrument, song, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drive away</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to keep talking about, to harp on (<i>perfective only</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get right, to come to normal, to improve</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be planning, to be geared up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a habit <i>(of)</i>, to get accustomed <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>нала́дить</i> (naláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment, setting up, preparation for use (<i>especially of a machine</i>)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) set of machine settings</li></ol><p><i>нала́дить</i> (naláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handheld</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement, establishment</li><li>adjustment, tuning</li></ol><p><i>нала́живать</i> (naláživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to repair, to set right</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to organize, to arrange, to establish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to normalize, to smooth (relations)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tune, to adjust to a certain harmony (<i>of a musical instrument, song, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drive away</li></ol><p><i>нала́дить</i> (naláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get right, to come to normal, to improve</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be planning, to be geared up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a habit <i>(of)</i>, to get accustomed <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>нала́живать</i> (naláživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(of animals) to get enough liquid (e.g. water or milk) by lapping it up</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to get drunk, to get pissed</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лака́ть</i> (lakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break (an amount of something)</li></ol><p><i>налома́ть</i> (nalomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the left</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) on the side</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to apply oneself to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something, e.g. a task’</small>]</li><li>to wield energetically [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a tool, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to influence, to lean on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>налег-</i> (naleg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) without a load/cargo; empty (e.g. of pack animal, trucks)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на</i> (na, “on”) +&lrm; <i>легке</i> (lexke), the old short locative singular of <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naled, icing, ice (a layer of ice over something, especially over ground waters)</li><li>frazil (a collection of stray ice crystals that form in fast-moving water)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лёд</i> (ljod) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ). Compare Polish <i>naledź</i>, Ukrainian <i>на́лідь</i> (nálidʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick on</li><li>to model <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick on</li></ol><p><i>налепи́ть</i> (nalepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(upon, against)</i>, to come flying <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bump <i>(into)</i>, to run <i>(into)</i>, to hit, to collide <i>(with)</i></li><li>to raid</li><li><i>(wind)</i> to get up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall <i>(on, upon)</i>, to swoop down <i>(upon)</i>, to rush <i>(at, upon)</i></li><li>to have flown, to have a flying time <i>(of)</i>, to have covered <i>(a distance of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(upon, against)</i>, to come flying <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bump <i>(into)</i>, to run <i>(into)</i>, to hit, to collide <i>(with)</i></li><li>to raid</li><li><i>(wind)</i> to get up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall <i>(on, upon)</i>, to swoop down <i>(upon)</i>, to rush <i>(at, upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to apply oneself to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something, e.g. a task’</small>]</li><li>to wield energetically [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a tool, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to influence, to lean on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pouring in</li><li>volume <i>(of liquid poured into a container)</i></li><li>ripening</li><li>sap, juice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (<i>liquid into a container</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нали́ть</i> (nalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be poured, to be filled (into a container with liquid)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to drink a large amount of liquid</li><li>to ripen; become juicy; swell</li></ol><p><i>налива́ть</i> (nalivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sweet fruit or berry liqueur, nalivka, nalewka, nastoyka</li></ol><p><i>налива́ть</i> (nalivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burbot, eel-pot</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) burbot, eel-pot</li></ol><p><i>нали́м</i> (nalím) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get very drunk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick; to accumulate (on a surface) by sticking on it</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ли́пнуть</i> (lípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (in, out) (liquid)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be filled</li><li>to ripen, to become juicy</li></ol><p><i>нали́ть</i> (nalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obvious</li><li>present; on hand to the fore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be on hand, to be present</li></ol><p><i>нали́чие</i> (nalíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ествовать</i> (-estvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>availability, presence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trim, decorative molding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>availability</li><li>cash in hand</li></ol><p><i>нали́чный</i> (nalíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cash, hard cash</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>available, present, ready</li><li>(<i>related to</i>) cash</li></ol><p>Short for <i>нали́чные де́ньги</i> (nalíčnyje dénʹgi, “available money”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>available, present</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naličьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (an amount of something) (<i>usually a large quantity</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, duty, levy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nalogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) taxman</li></ol><p><i>нало́говый</i> (nalógovyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tax, taxation</li></ol><p><i>нало́г</i> (nalóg, “tax”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxation, taxing, imposition of tax</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxpayer</li></ol><p>From <i>нало́г</i> (nalóg) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плате́льщик</i> (platélʹščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imposition, applying</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;cinematography,&#32;informatics) superposition, superimposition</li></ol><p><i>наложи́ть</i> (naložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impose, to inflict (opinions, penalties, prohibitions, punishments, requirements, taxes)</li><li>to lay (something) on/over (something else), to superimpose</li><li>to apply (bandage, face cream, etc.)</li><li>to pack, to load (a fair amount of something)</li><li>to put in (stitches)</li><li>to shit, to defecate , usually in a stressful situation</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) concubine</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) mistress (female extramarital lover, often financially supported)</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть(ся)</i> (ložítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica); apparently not derived directly from <i>наложи́ть</i> (naložítʹ) or <i>наложи́ться</i> (naložítʹsja) due to incompatible meanings.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(church,&#32;dated,&#32;colloquial) analogion, lectern, desk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break (an amount of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the skin, closely</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>лы́сый</i> (lýsyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raid, foray, incursion (a sudden, rapid attack by a mobile force)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>law enforcement</i>) raid, swoop (<i>by police to make arrests, or by a criminal gang to plunder</i>)</li><li>(<i>military&#32;aviation</i>) air raid</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) artillery strike (a short, intense bombardment)</li></ol><li>a thin film, a thin coat, coating</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a touch, a hint (a small sign of some state or trait)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) flying time</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>налета́ть</i> (naletátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>petītiō</i> (whence English <i>petition</i>, Russian <i>пети́ция</i> (petícija)), <i>appetītus</i> (whence English <i>appetite</i>, Russian <i>аппети́т</i> (appetít)), both from <i>petō</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂eti</i>; French <i>voleur</i> &lt; <i>voler</i> <&lt; Latin <i>volō</i>. Also compare <i>набе́г</i> (nabég).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raider, robber engaged in a raid</li></ol><p><i>налёт</i> (naljót) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) salat</li><li>(Islam,&#32;by extension) prayer</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, from Persian نماز (namâz).</p><p>Compare</p><p>Kazakh, Kyrgyz <i>намаз</i> (namaz),</p><p>Uzbek <i>namoz</i>,</p><p>Bashkir <i>намаҙ</i> (namaź),</p><p>Turkish <i>namaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread <i>(on)</i>, to smear <i>(with)</i>, to daub <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind <i>(round)</i>, to reel <i>(on)</i>, to coil <i>(round)</i></li><li>to cover <i>(a distance of)</i></li></ol><p><i>намота́ть</i> (namotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to moisten</li><li>to soak, to steep</li><li>to prepare or preserve by soaking in water [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something, usually a specified quantity of a foodstuff’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>намочи́ть</i> (namočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the other day (lately)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hint (at), to allude, to imply (refer to something indirectly or by suggestion)</li></ol><p><i>намекну́ть</i> (nameknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). For details, see <i>смека́ть</i> (smekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allude, to hint, to imply</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be going to, to intend, to be about to, to mean (to intend&#59; plan on doing)</li></ol><p><i>наме́р(ение)</i> (namér(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going to, intending to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intention, intent, purpose (course intended to follow)</li></ol><p><i>наме́рить</i> (naméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>intentionally (intentional manner, on purpose)</li></ol><p>From <i>наме́ренный</i> (namérennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intentional</li><li>deliberate</li></ol><p><i>наме́рение</i> (namérenije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to measure out [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘enough (usually a large amount) of material’</small>] [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a specific (usually large) amount, quantity, or size’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to measure (<i>something to be</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘amount, quantity, or size’</small>]</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to intend, to plan</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tightly, fast, permanently</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>мертво́</i> (mertvó) or from <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>мёртво</i> (mjórtvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep together <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>to cause to drift <i>(by wind)</i> <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) deputy, governor-general</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) viceroyalty (administrative division of the Russian Empire)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to design, to plan, to contemplate, to lay down (a program)</li><li>to select, to nominate</li><li>to mark, to make a mark on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be outlined, to take shape, to begin to show</li><li>to be planned, to be in the offing</li></ol><p><i>наме́тить</i> (namétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to design, to plan, to contemplate, to lay down (a program)</li><li>to select, to nominate</li><li>to mark, to make a mark on</li></ol><p><i>наме́тить</i> (namétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be outlined, to take shape, to begin to show</li><li>to be planned, to be in the offing</li></ol><p><i>намеча́ть</i> (namečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Namibian (male Namibian person)</li></ol><p><i>Нами́бия</i> (Namíbija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Namibian</li></ol><p><i>Нами́бия</i> (Namíbija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by far, much (in comparisons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wet</li></ol><p><i>намо́к(нуть)</i> (namók(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wet</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мо́кнуть</i> (móknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzle (device to stop an animal from biting)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мо́рда</i> (mórda) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knit, to furrow (one's forehead, one's brow)</li></ol><p><i>намо́рщить</i> (namórščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knit, to furrow (one's forehead, one's brow)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мо́рщить</i> (mórščitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind <i>(round)</i>, to reel <i>(on)</i>, to coil <i>(round)</i></li><li>to cover <i>(a distance of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winding, reeling</li></ol><p><i>намота́ть</i> (namotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to moisten</li><li>to soak, to steep</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only,&#32;transitive) to splash, to spill water on</li><li>to prepare or preserve by soaking in water [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something, usually a specified quantity of a foodstuff’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be too clever, to complicate things unnecessarily</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мудри́ть</i> (mudrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to rub with soap</li></ol><p><i>намы́лить</i> (namýlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soap, to soap up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>мы́лить</i> (mýlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hint, suggestion</li><li>allusion, implication</li><li>tip (piece of private information)</li><li>inkling</li><li>insinuation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>намека́ть</i> (namekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наме́стничество</i> (naméstničestvo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Nanai person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, to carry (<i>in some quantity, in several stages</i>)</li></ol><p><i>наноси́ть</i> (nanosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhea, nandu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>ñandú</i>, of Paraguayan Guarani origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putting, inflicting</li></ol><p><i>нанес-</i> (nanes-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drift (on); to carry, to waft, to deposit, to wash ashore (e.g. of snow, sand, etc.)</li><li>to mark (on); to draw (e.g. onto a map)</li><li>to inflict (on); to cause (to); to bring</li><li>to bring (too much of something)</li><li>to lay (eggs, a lot)</li><li>to apply (cream, ointment, make-up, etc.)</li><li>(<i>in expressions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘beads, mushrooms, etc.’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘a necklace, fringe, etc.’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>низа́ть</i> (nizátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘beads, mushrooms, etc.’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘a necklace, fringe, etc.’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наниза́ть</i> (nanizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>employer, hirer (person or entity which employs others)</li></ol><p><i>нанима́ть</i> (nanimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hire, to employ (to obtain the services of in return for fixed payment)</li><li>to rent</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek employment, to apply for work</li></ol><p><i>нанима́ть</i> (nanimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nankeen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nankeen</li></ol><p><i>на́нка</i> (nánka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>nano- (1 000 000 000th part of)</li><li>nano- (nanotechnology industry)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew, again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanoscale</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>диапазо́н</i> (diapazón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanogold (nanoparticle of metallic gold)</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanometal</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>мета́лл</i> (metáll)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanometre (subunit of length)</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanoobject</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>объе́кт</i> (obʺjékt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alluvium, alluvion</li><li><i>(sand, snow)</i> drift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanosecond (measure of time)</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deposit, to pile up (sand or snow)</li><li>to apply (cream, ointment, make-up, etc.)</li><li>to mark (on); to draw (e.g. onto a map)</li><li>to inflict (on); to cause (to); to bring</li><li>to bear, to carry (<i>in some quantity, in several stages</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired from carrying</li></ol><p><i>наноси́ть</i> (nanosítʹ, “to deposit, to pile up”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>наноси́ть</i> (nanosítʹ, “to bear, to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alluvial</li><li>alien, borrowed</li><li>superficial</li></ol><p><i>наноси́ть</i> (nanosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanotechnology</li></ol><p>From <i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>техноло́гия</i> (texnológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nanoparticle</li></ol><p><i>нано-</i> (nano-) +&lrm; <i>части́ца</i> (častíca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nanoelectronic</li></ol><p><i>наноэлектро́ника</i> (nanoelektrónika) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hire, to engage</li><li>to rent, to lodge</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get a job, to get hired</li></ol><p><i>наня́ть</i> (nanjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to promise</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>обеща́ть</i> (obeščátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inside out, back to front</li><li>vice versa, the other way round, conversely</li><li>on the contrary, to the contrary</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>оборо́т</i> (oborót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) at random, randomly, blindly, haphazardly (without preliminary consideration of how something will turn out; without thinking)</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>обу́м</i> (obúm, “in vain, at random”), further from <i>об</i> (ob) + <i>ум</i> (um, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with a sweeping movement, roundhouse, in a full-blown smacky fashion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, definitely</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>отре́з</i> (otréz), known since the 18th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attack, to fall on, to assault, to descend <i>(on)</i></li><li>to come across, to come upon, to hit on</li><li><i>(feeling, condition)</i> to come <i>(over)</i>, to grip, to seize, to overcome</li><li>to fall <i>(some quantity)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ambush (to attack by ambush&#59; to waylay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attacker, assailant</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) forward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, assault</li><li>aggression</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) forward, offense</li></ol><p><i>напа́д-</i> (napád-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attacks</li><li>accusations, cavils</li><li>carping, faultfinding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give to drink, to water</li><li>to make drunk</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to suffuse</li><li>to solder on</li></ol><p><i>напои́ть</i> (napoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>напая́ть</i> (napajátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>napalm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>napalm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partner, mate, associate (person united with another or others in an act, enterprise, or business&#59; a partner or colleague&#59; friend, buddy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, to cut</li><li>to rip/unstitch/undo <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p><i>напоро́ть</i> (naporótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(upon, against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(into)</i>, to run up <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut oneself <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>напа́рывать</i> (napáryvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dose, pull, shot (of a psychoactive substance)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic نَفَس (nafas, “breath; puff, draw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, bad luck, trouble</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attack, to fall on, to assault, to descend <i>(on)</i></li><li>to come across, to come upon, to hit on</li><li><i>(feeling, condition)</i> to come <i>(over)</i>, to grip, to seize, to overcome</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;napastь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solder on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пая́ть</i> (pajátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melody, tune</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hum (a melody)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sing</li><li>to record, to have one's singing recorded on</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to slander</li></ol><p><i>напе́ть</i> (napétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in eager rivalry, vying with each other</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>перебо́й</i> (perebój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) (of rifle, pistol, sword, knife, etc.) held in front of one, at the ready, at the high port, in a firing position</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trying to overtake the opponent or one another</li><li>in a hurry, alternately ahead of each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in spite, in defiance, contrary</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>counter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>across; cutting someone's way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) bosom friend, intimate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) foxglove (<i>Digitalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>перст</i> (perst) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka); compare <i>напёрсток</i> (napjórstok, “thimble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) forward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beforehand, in advance</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>перёд</i> (perjód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hum (a melody)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sing</li><li>to record, to have one's singing recorded on</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to slander</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пе́ть</i> (pétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to print (to copy something on a surface, especially by machine)</li><li>to type (to use a typewriter or to enter text or commands into a computer using a keyboard)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to be imprinted (<i>in one's memory, heart, soul, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake <i>(certain quantity)</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to heat by the sun</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quench thirst</li><li>to get drunk</li></ol><p><i>напи́ться</i> (napítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lie</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw (a quantity of)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>file, rasp (tool)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>напили́ть</i> (napilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), but the verb "to file" is actually <i>опили́ть</i> (opilítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press, to push</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to emphasize, to give emphasis <i>(to)</i>, to point up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing, way of writing, spelling</li></ol><p><i>написа́ть</i> (napisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;napisati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”). Cognate with Latin <i>pingō</i> (whence English <i>paint</i> and <i>picture</i>), and with Persian نوشتن.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sate, to satiate</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beverage</li><li>alcoholic beverage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;napitъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quench thirst</li><li>to get drunk</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overfeed (<i>usually forcibly or against someone's desires</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to overdose, to give too much medicine to (someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stuff, to cram (someone with useless or unnecessary knowledge or information)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to oversupply (useless or unnecessary knowledge or information)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пи́чкать</i> (píčkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spit <i>(upon)</i>, to not give a damn <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘a quantity of things’</small>]</li><li>(low&#32;colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to blather, to jabber [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘lies, rumors, etc.’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘a quantity of things’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наплет-</i> (naplet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>influx, flow, rush</li><li><i>(on a tree)</i> excrescence, burl</li><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) fade in, motion blur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim, to float [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into something/someone’</small>], to bump into while swimming or floating</li><li>to move/sweep [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘over something/someone, e.g. clouds over the sun’</small>]</li><li>to accumulate while flowing, to flood (<i>in some amount, often a lot; of liquid or a viscous material such as mud</i>)</li><li>to emanate (<i>of sounds or smells</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to materialize (<i>of thoughts or feelings</i>)</li><li>to form a residue</li></ol><p><i>наплы́ть</i> (naplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bump into (something) while swimming or floating</li><li>to sweep over (something)</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)] to swim or float towards (something)</li><li>(<i>of liquid or a viscous material</i>) to flood</li><li>(<i>of sounds or smells</i>) to emanate</li><li>(<i>of thoughts or feelings</i>) to materialize</li><li>to form a residue</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>like, not unlike</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>подо́бие</i> (podóbije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give to drink, to water</li><li>to make drunk</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to suffuse</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for show</li><li>on display</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>пока́з</i> (pokáz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>napoleon (multi-layered pastry)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) napoleon (12-pounder cannon used by the Second French Empire)</li><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) napoleon (20-franc gold coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Napoléon</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Наполеон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Napoleon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) napoleon (20-franc gold coin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>napoléon d'or</i> (“gold napoleon”). Compare to <i>луидо́р</i> (luidór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Napoleonic</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) grand, ambitious</li></ol><p><i>Наполео́н</i> (Napoleón) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crawl [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make by crawling (e.g. holes in the knees of one's pants)</li></ol><p><i>наползти́</i> (napolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>по́лзать</i> (pólzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crawl [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filling (up) (action)</li></ol><p><i>напо́лнить</i> (napólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) filler, filling (substance)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) filler, filling machine</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) person who fills</li></ol><p><i>напо́лнить</i> (napólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill, to fill up</li><li>to blow out</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li></ol><p><i>напо́лнить</i> (napólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill, to fill up</li><li>to blow out</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li></ol><p><i>наполня́ть</i> (napolnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in half</li><li>by halves</li><li>partially, partly, half</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>installed on the floor</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reminder (something that reminds)</li></ol><p><i>напомина́ть</i> (napominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind (cause one to experience a memory&#59; bring to a person's notice)</li><li>to resemble, to look like</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind, to dun (cause one to experience a memory&#59; bring to a person's notice)</li><li>to resemble, to look like</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>помнить</i> (pomnitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pressure, force</li><li><i>water, steam</i> head</li><li>rush, push, vigour, energy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>energetic, pushy, forceful, aggressive, assertive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pressure</li><li>pressurized, under pressure</li></ol><p><i>напо́р</i> (napór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, to cut</li><li>to rip/unstitch/undo <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to bungle, to botch up</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to tell, to babble</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(upon, against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(into)</i>, to run up <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut oneself <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>напоро́ть</i> (naporótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the end, finally, after all</li><li>ultimately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>e.g.</li><li>for example</li><li>for instance</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>наприме́р</i> (naprimér).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to direct</li><li>to turn, to aim, to level, to point</li><li>to refer, to send, to assign, to detach</li><li>to sharpen</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ, “to govern, to rule, to drive, to steer”).</p><p>For the meaning development compare with Latin <i>dīrigō</i> (“to lay straight, to arrange in lines, to direct to a place, guide, steer”) (whence English <i>direct</i>) connected with <i>regō</i> (“to rule, to govern, to direct, to guide, steer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to head, to make one's way</li><li>to get going</li></ol><p><i>напра́вить</i> (naprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>direction, orientation</li><li>orientation, current, school, specialization (field of study)</li><li>assignment, directive, order, warrant</li><li>(<i>military</i>) operational direction</li><li>referral</li><li>area, front, issue (of a task or work effort)</li></ol><p>From <i>направля́ть</i> (napravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>direction, orientation</li><li>trend</li></ol><p><i>напра́вленный</i> (naprávlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>directional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to direct</li><li>to turn, to aim, to level, to point</li><li>to refer, to send, to assign, to detach</li><li>to sharpen</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) + <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ, “to govern”) + <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to head, to make one's way (intransitive)</li><li>to get going</li></ol><p><i>направля́ть</i> (napravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide (device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the right</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain</li></ol><p><i>напра́сный</i> (naprásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in vain, idle, wasted, useless</li><li>wrongful, unjust (e.g. accusation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust oneself upon, to offer oneself</li><li>to suggest itself, to arise (<i>of a thought or inference</i>)</li></ol><p><i>напроси́ться</i> (naprosítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for example, for instance, e.g.</li><li>for one</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>приме́р</i> (primér)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наприме́р</i> (naprimér).</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>примѣ́ръ</i> (primě́r)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for rent, for hire</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>прока́т</i> (prokát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>straight, directly, headlong; overcoming all obstacles</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) head-on, decisively; regardless of the circumstances</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) straight through, without a break</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>пролёт</i> (proljót)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust oneself upon, to offer oneself</li><li>to suggest itself, to arise (<i>of a thought or inference</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>introductory</i>) (no,) quite the opposite; no, in fact...</li><li>(<i>parenthetical</i>) on the contrary, in contrast, by contrast, conversely, on the other hand, however</li><li>(<i>as a connective</i>) rather, instead</li><li>opposite (on the opposite side of something, opposite something)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) the opposite [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>opposite (to/from)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absolutely, altogether, without a trace</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>прочь</i> (pročʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flex (the muscle)</li><li>to strain (the back, the ears)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to stress out</li></ol><p><i>напря́г-</i> (naprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strain oneself, to exert oneself</li></ol><p><i>напряга́ть</i> (naprjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effort, exertion</li><li>(physics,&#32;engineering) tension, strain, stress (force)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) voltage</li></ol><p><i>напряга́ть</i> (naprjagátʹ, “to strain”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tense situation</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) shortage, lack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tensely</li><li>intensely</li><li>intently</li></ol><p><i>напряжённый</i> (naprjažónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tensity, tenseness, tension (psychological state)</li></ol><p><i>напряжённый</i> (naprjažónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intense, intensive</li><li>strained</li><li>arduous (needing or using up much energy)</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;of a vowel) tense (produced with relative constriction of the vocal tract)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bluntly, point-blank (in a blunt manner)</li><li>directly, straight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bluntly, point-blank (in a blunt manner)</li><li>directly, straight</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пряму́ю</i> (prjamúju)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flex (a muscle)</li><li>to strain (the back, the ears)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to stress out</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-прячь</i> (-prjačʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strain oneself, to exert oneself</li></ol><p><i>напря́чь</i> (naprjáčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frightened</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let in, to allow to enter (a large amount of liquid, gas or small items)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to let in (someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>idiomatic</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unleash, to sic, to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘an animal, a person’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to strike, to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘fear, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artificial, fake, insincere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let in, to allow to enter (a large amount of liquid, gas or small items)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to let in (someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>idiomatic</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unleash, to sic, to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘an animal, a person’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to strike, to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘fear, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to botch, to mess up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mix up, to get wrong [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional)]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parting words, parting wishes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to tell [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘parting words, one's goodbye, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>напу́тствие</i> (napútstvije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pompous, puffy, flamboyant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to pull <i>(on)</i>, to get on</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;deprecative) to trick out <i>(in)</i>, to dude up <i>(in)</i>, to fig up, to fig out <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>напя́лить</i> (napjálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to pull <i>(on)</i>, to get on</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;deprecative) to trick out <i>(in)</i>, to dude up <i>(in)</i>, to fig up, to fig out <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>пя́лить</i> (pjálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sewing</i>) thimble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;napьrstъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>перст</i> (perst, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hybrid camel</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. Compare Kyrgyz <i>нар</i> (nar), Kazakh <i>нар</i> (nar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work <i>(a period of time)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to acquire</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundwork, development(s)</li><li>operating time, life</li></ol><p><i>нарабо́тать</i> (narabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on (a) par <i>(with)</i></li><li>equally <i>(with)</i>, along <i>(with)</i>, on an equal basis/footing <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p>Compare <i>на</i> (na) and <i>равно́</i> (ravnó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wide open</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>распахивать</i> (raspaxivatʹ, “to throw open”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a singsong manner, liltingly, melodically (<i>of speech</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>распе́в</i> (raspév)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growth, augmenting, rising</li></ol><p><i>нараста́ть</i> (narastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow on, to be building up</li><li>to increase, to grow</li><li>to accrue, to accumulate</li></ol><p><i>нарасти́</i> (narastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>growing, rising, increasing</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) progressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow on, to be building up</li><li>to increase, to grow</li><li>to accrue, to accumulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow, to develop, to graft on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lengthen, to augment</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to intensify, to increase, to build up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building-up; expansion; growth; accretion; escalation</li></ol><p><i>нара́щивать</i> (naráščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow, to develop, to graft on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lengthen, to augment</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to intensify, to increase, to build up</li></ol><p><i>нарасти́ть</i> (narastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narwhal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>narval</i> or any other European languages in the 1780s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(boil, abscess)</i> to gather, to come to a head</li><li>to pick <i>(a quantity of)</i></li><li>to tear <i>(a quantity of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(into)</i>, to run up <i>(on, against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ask <i>(for)</i>, to invite</li></ol><p><i>нарва́ть</i> (narvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people's deputy, MP</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный депута́т</i> (naródnyj deputát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backgammon</li></ol><p>Derived from Persian نرد (nard, “backgammon”), likely through Georgian <i>ნარდი</i> (nardi) and Armenian <i>նարդի</i> (nardi). The game is most popular in Persian-influenced areas in the Caucasus and Central Asia. During the Soviet era it gained popularity in the rest of the USSR.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread <b>(of a screw)</b></li><li>groove <b>(in rifling)</b></li><li>(<i>historical</i>) <b>(a portion of land)</b> lot, plot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to slice</li><li>to rifle, to thread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get hurt (by cutting), to leave a painful impression on one's body</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narězati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ).</p><p><i>наре́зать</i> (narézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting <i>(into pieces)</i></li><li>cuts, pieces</li><li>thread, rifling</li></ol><p><i>наре́зать</i> (narézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threaded, having rifles on it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, censure, reprimand (the act of blaming, criticizing, or condemning as wrong; reprehension)</li></ol><p><i>нарека́ть</i> (narekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije). sense&nbsp;2 is a semantic loan from Polish <i>narzekanie</i> (“complaining”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to name</li></ol><p><i>наре́к-</i> (narék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) dialect</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adverb (lexical category)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narěčьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>речь</i> (rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adverbial</li></ol><p><i>наре́чие</i> (naréčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nareťi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>officially declared; named, announced</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;specifically</i>) betrothed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiancé, betrothed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Narzan, brand of mineral water from Kislovodsk, Russia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Karachay-Balkar <i>нарсан</i> (narsan, “healing spring water”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Narzan (a brand of mineral water from Kislovodsk, Russia)</li></ol><p><i>нарза́н</i> (narzán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) drug addict, junkie</li></ol><p><i>наркома́н</i> (narkomán, “drug addict”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, “diminutive suffix, -er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to paint, to design, to crayon</li><li>to picture <i>(imagine)</i></li><li>to depict, to paint, to portray</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be pictured, to appear (in one's imagination)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show up (to appear suddenly or unexpectedly)</li></ol><p><i>нарисова́ть</i> (narisovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) appellative, nominal, denominative (relating to a common noun)</li><li>household, generic, common, symbolic, shorthand, metaphorical/synonymic (usually about a name)</li><li>nominal (as of a value on currency, etc.)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, Old Church Slavonic <i>нарицати</i> (naricati, “to call, name”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(illicit) drug business</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наркоти́ческий би́знес</i> (narkotíčeskij bíznɛs, “narcotics business”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug dealer (person who illegally sells drugs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narcosis, general anesthesia (loss of bodily sensation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>narcose</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ναρκόω</i> (narkóō, “to put to sleep”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) people's commissar</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный комисса́р</i> (naródnyj komissár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug addict</li></ol><p>From наркомания (narkomanija, “drug addiction”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug addiction, substance abuse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) people's commissariat</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный комиссариа́т</i> (naródnyj komissariát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) people's commissar for procurements</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный комисса́р загото́вок</i> (naródnyj komissár zagotóvok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) People's Commissariat of Meat and Dairy Industry (<i>from 1940 to 1946</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный комиссариа́т мясно́й и моло́чной промы́шленности</i> (naródnyj komissariát mjasnój i molóčnoj promýšlennosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) People's Commissariat of Electrical Industry</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный комиссариа́т электропромы́шленности</i> (naródnyj komissariát elektropromýšlennosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A scandal involving narcotic drugs.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>нарко-</i> (narko-, “narcotic”) +&lrm; <i>сканда́л</i> (skandál, “scandal”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;inanimate) drugs, dope</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate) junkie</li><li>(slang,&#32;animate,&#32;collective) junkies (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>нарко́тик</i> (narkótik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narcotic</li><li>drug, dope</li></ol><p>From French <i>narcotique</i>, Medieval Latin <i>narcoticum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ναρκόω</i> (narkóō, “to benumb”), from <i>νάρκη</i> (nárkē, “numbness, torpor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narcotic, (<i>relational</i>) drug</li></ol><p><i>нарко́тик</i> (narkótik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij); see also <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(illicit) drug business</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people</li><li>nation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narodъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>as used by the conservative right</i>) God-bearing people — the Russian nation, seen as the embodiment of spirituality and the Christian faith, carrying out the sacred task of defending all that is good and holy.</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) God-bearing people — the Russian nation, perceived as the embodiment of any negative quality that the speaker is ascribing to the Russian people.</li></ol><p>From народ + богоносец. Originally from Fyodor Dostoyevsky’s novel <i>Demons</i>: <i>"Единый народ «богоносец» — это русский народ..."</i> (<i>"Only one nation is 'god-bearing,' that's the Russian people..."</i>) Notice the difference between the styling used by Dostoyevsky—<i>народ «богоносец»</i>—and the modern preferred spelling <i>народ-богоносец</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>наро́д</i> (naród)</li><li>pejorative of <i>наро́д</i> (naród)</li></ol><p><i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give birth to, to bear (someone/something in a usually large quantity)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to be born</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be born in some (often large) quantity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to appear, to arise</li></ol><p><i>народи́ть</i> (narodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>наро́д</i> (naród): worthless or little people</li></ol><p><i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narodnik</li></ol><p>From <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) Narodnichestvo, Narodnism</li></ol><p><i>наро́дник</i> (naródnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Narodnichestvo, Narodnism</li></ol><p>From <i>наро́дник</i> (naródnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo). Compare Ukrainian <i>наро́дництво</i> (naródnyctvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nationality; people</li><li>ethnic or national character, national identity or spirit</li></ol><p><i>наро́дный</i> (naródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>people's</li><li>popular</li><li>popular, folk, vernacular</li><li>national</li><li>public</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narodьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>democracy, sovereignty of the people</li></ol><p><i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>δημοκρατία</i> (dēmokratía) (whence <i>демокра́тия</i> (demokrátija)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of Narodnaya Volya, a late 19th century far-left terrorist organization in Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>наро́д</i> (naród, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja, “will”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), based on the name of the organization <i>Наро́дная Во́ля</i> (Naródnaja Vólja, “Narodnaya Volya”, literally “People's Will”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>population</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) tenants</li></ol><p><i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>населе́ние</i> (naselénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наро́д</i> (naród).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give birth to, to bear (someone/something in a usually large quantity)</li></ol><p><i>народи́ть</i> (narodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to be born</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be born in some (often large) quantity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to appear, to arise</li></ol><p><i>нарожда́ть</i> (naroždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growth, tumor</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>нарасти́</i> (narastí), with the stressed vocalism found in past tense <i>наро́с</i> (narós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>specially, expressly</li><li>deliberately, intentionally</li></ol><p><i>нарочи́тый</i> (naročítyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intentional, deliberate, on purpose</li><li>feigned</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нарочитъ</i> (naročitŭ). By surface analysis, <i>наро́к</i> (narók) +&lrm; <i>-итый</i> (-ityj). Compare Ukrainian <i>нарочи́тий</i> (naročýtyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on purpose, deliberately</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naročьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>рок</i> (rok, “fate, destiny”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>нарочьнъ</i> (naročĭnŭ), Bulgarian <i>нарочно</i> (naročno), Polish <i>naroczny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) narrative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>narrative</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Nart</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ossetian <i>нарт</i> (nart).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>на́рты</i> (nárty)</li></ol><p>Uncertain:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sledge, sleigh, sled (<i>drawn by reindeer or dogs</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outwardly, externally</li></ol><p><i>нару́жный</i> (narúžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, looks; exterior</li></ol><p><i>нару́жный</i> (narúžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>external, outward (outside of something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ružь</i>. Cognate with Latvian <i>raugs</i>, <i>raudzīt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outwardly, openly, outside, towards the outside, out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oversleeve, armlet</li><li>water wing</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>рука́в</i> (rukáv) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeve, arm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rouge</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>румя́н(а)</i> (rumján(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) handcuffs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manacle, shackle</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) handcuff</li></ol><p><i>на́ручни</i> (náručni) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handcuffs, manacles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watch, wristwatch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn on the arm; wrist</li></ol><p><i>на руке́</i> (na ruké, “on hand”) [к → ч] + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to violate, to break</li><li>to upset, to disrupt</li></ol><p><i>нару́шить</i> (narúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get broken, to be violated</li></ol><p><i>наруша́ть</i> (narušátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breach, violation, infringement</li></ol><p><i>нару́шить</i> (narúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violator; trespasser; infringer; perpetrator; disturber; intruder</li></ol><p><i>нару́шить</i> (narúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to violate, to break</li><li>to upset, to disrupt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narušiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ). Compare Polish <i>naruszyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get broken, to be violated</li></ol><p><i>нару́шить</i> (narúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) daffodil</li><li>(literary&#32;or&#32;dated,&#32;animate) narcissistic man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>νάρκισσος</i> (nárkissos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narcissism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narcissistic</li></ol><p><i>нарци́сс</i> (narcíss) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) narcissistic personality disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plank bed (<i>often used in prisons</i>), bunk</li><li>(metonymic,&#32;colloquial) prison, jail</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>на́ри</i> (náry), Polish <i>nary</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abscess, boil</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>нарыва́ть</i> (naryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(boil, abscess)</i> to gather, to come to a head</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(into)</i>, to run up <i>(on, against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ask <i>(for)</i>, to invite</li></ol><p><i>нарыва́ть</i> (naryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) blister beetle</li></ol><p><i>нары́в</i> (narýv, “abscess, boil”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, diminutive nominative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘a quantity of something’</small>]</li><li>to dig up, to dig out</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outfit, attire, dress, formal wear (a formal outfit or one's best clothes, worn for a holiday&#59; usually a woman's)</li><li>assignment, commission, order</li><li>work order, invoice for services</li><li>(<i>military</i>) fatigue, duty (menial task)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) detail, detachment (<i>of soldiers</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>наряди́ть</i> (narjadítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>KP</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress up, to dress out, to apparel (to dress or clothe)</li><li>to adorn, to decorate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dispatch (someone) on an assignment</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to establish, to organize (e.g. an investigation)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ряди́ть</i> (rjadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress smartly</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress improperly or inappropriately</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress as, to dress in imitation of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наряди́ть</i> (narjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>наря́дный</i> (narjádnyj)</li></ol><p><i>наря́дн(ый)</i> (narjádn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-dressed (of a person)</li><li>smart, natty, spiffy (of clothes)</li></ol><p><i>наря́д</i> (narjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>along with, together</li><li>on level with</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labor organizer</li></ol><p>From <i>наря́д</i> (narjád) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress up, to dress out, to apparel (to dress or clothe)</li><li>to adorn, to decorate (нового́днюю ёлку)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dispatch (someone) on an assignment</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to establish, to organize (e.g. an investigation)</li></ol><p><i>наряди́ть</i> (narjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress smartly</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress improperly or inappropriately</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dress as, to dress in imitation of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наряжа́ть</i> (narjažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наре́чие</i> (naréčije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘several people or objects’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na) <small>‘in’</small>]</li><li>to attach (to stick, pin, sew, etc.) (something) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto’</small>]</li><li>to plant [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘several plants’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to spread, to implant, to introduce (ideas, feelings, doctrines, culture)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nozzle, mouthpiece</li><li>setting, putting on (action)</li><li>(fishing) bait</li><li>(engineering) excavator attachment</li></ol><p><i>насади́ть</i> (nasadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to implant, to instill, to ingraft</li></ol><p><i>насади́ть</i> (nasadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantation</li><li>stand (in biology, forestry)</li><li>planting</li><li>propagation, spreading</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get <i>(on)</i>, to haft <i>(on)</i>, to stick, to pin, to spit</li></ol><p><i>насади́ть</i> (nasadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go <i>(onto)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crowd <i>(into)</i>, to jam <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>наса́живать</i> (nasáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to litter, to soil</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>свиня́чить</i> (svinjáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle <i>(a melody)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>свиста́ть</i> (svistátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle <i>(a melody)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to rat <i>(on)</i>, to squeal <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to whistle</li></ol><p>From <i>насвиста́ть</i> (nasvistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>насвисте́ть</i> (nasvistétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, to accumulate, to settle (<i>of dust</i>)</li><li>to pounce [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pressure [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to press, to put pressure, to advance [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>насед-</i> (nased-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p>For semantic parallels, compare cognate English <i>set on</i>, <i>set upon</i>, <i>onset</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insect</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Hexapoda</li></ol><p>From <i>насека́ть</i> (nasekátʹ, “to make incisions, to notch”), a calque of French <i>insecte</i>, from <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insectivore</li></ol><p>From <i>насеко́мое</i> (nasekómoje) +&lrm; <i>-яд</i> (-jad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); calque of Latin <i>insectivorus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hexapoda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>population, inhabitants</li><li>peopling, settling</li></ol><p>From <i>насели́ть</i> (naselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to populate</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inhabit; to reside in some place</li><li>to populate</li></ol><p><i>насели́ть</i> (naselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly populated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, locality, settlement (inhabited area&#58; a village, town, or (a small) city)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roost, perch</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>насе́сть</i> (naséstʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, to accumulate, to settle (<i>of dust</i>)</li><li>to pounce [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pressure [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to press, to put pressure, to advance [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li><li>to sit down (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>насе́ст</i> (nasést)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p><p>For semantic parallels, compare cognate English <i>set on</i>, <i>set upon</i>, <i>onset</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incubate, to hatch (an egg) (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to receive (something unpleasant) as a result of a sedentary lifestyle</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incubate, to hatch (an egg) (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to receive (something unpleasant) as a result of a sedentary lifestyle</li></ol><p><i>насиде́ть</i> (nasidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violence, force, coercion</li><li>rape</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>насилиѥ</i> (nasilije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nasilьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) violator; oppressor; offender</li></ol><p>From <i>наси́ловать</i> (nasílovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to coerce, to constrain</li><li>to rape, to violate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>насиловати</i> (nasilovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nasilovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>difficultly, hardly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapist</li><li>violator</li><li>oppressor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>насильникъ</i> (nasilĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nasilьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forcibly, by force, against someone's will</li></ol><p><i>наси́льный</i> (nasílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) forcible, violent, by force</li></ol><p><i>наси́л(ие)</i> (nasíl(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forcibly</li><li>violently, coercively</li></ol><p><i>наси́льственный</i> (nasílʹstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violent</li><li>forcible, forced, forceful</li></ol><p><i>наси́льство</i> (nasílʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit</li></ol><p><i>насра́ть</i> (nasrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-сира́ть</i> (-sirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into a fence, etc.’</small>] while on horseback</li><li>to jump at, to attack from multiple sides (<i>of a group</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naskakati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pounce on, to jump onto [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to attack with unfounded accusations [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to collide with, to run into [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stumble into [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘e.g. a bad situation’</small>]</li><li>to jump [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into a fence, etc.’</small>] while on horseback</li><li>to jump at, to attack from multiple sides (<i>of a group</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наскок-</i> (naskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>наскака́ть</i> (naskakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rupestral (made on or placed on rock surfaces)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>скал(а́)</i> (skal(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brawl, to make a row</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сканда́лить</i> (skandálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all the way through, throughout, completely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lunge, pounce, swoop</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) attack</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>наскочи́ть</i> (naskočítʹ), with underlying final <i>к</i> (compare the imperfective <i>наска́кивать</i> (naskákivatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how much, how (to what extent)</li><li>as, as far as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hurriedly, hastily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pounce on, to jump onto [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to attack with unfounded accusations [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to collide with, to run into [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stumble into [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘e.g. a bad situation’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape up (together); to scratch up</li></ol><p><i>наскреб-</i> (naskreb-) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape up (together); to scratch up</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>скрести́</i> (skrestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape up (together); to scratch up</li></ol><p><i>наскрёб-</i> (naskrjób-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) to bore [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наску́чить</i> (naskúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bore [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;dated,&#32;colloquial) to get bored [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-ску́чить</i> (-skúčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enjoy, to be delighted</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>насладити сѧ</i> (nasladiti sę). By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>слади́ть</i> (sladítʹ, “to sweeten”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enjoy, to be delighted</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>наслаждати сѧ</i> (naslaždati sę). By surface analysis, <i>наслади́ться</i> (nasladítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure, enjoyment, delight</li><li>treat, feast</li><li>gratification</li><li>luxury</li><li>fruition, delectation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>наслаждениѥ</i> (naslaždenije). By surface analysis, <i>наслади́ться</i> (nasladítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasurable, enjoyable</li></ol><p><i>наслажде́ние</i> (naslaždénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to layer on, to apply layers to</li></ol><p><i>наслои́ть</i> (nasloítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(troubles etc)</i> to send, to inflict <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nasъlati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heritage, legacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>successor, heir, legatee (someone who inherits, or is designated to inherit, the property of another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritance, inheriting</li></ol><p><i>насле́довать</i> (naslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to inherit (property, traditions, vices, etc.)</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to receive an inheritance [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to have the right to receive an inheritance [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heredity</li></ol><p><i>насле́дственный</i> (naslédstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hereditary, inherited</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heirloom, heritage, legacy (property passed from a deceased person to others)</li><li>heritage (cultural phenomena passed on from predecessors)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) inheritance</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>след</i> (sled) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stratification, layering</li><li>layer</li></ol><p><i>наслои́ть</i> (nasloítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to layer on, to apply layers to</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>слои́ть</i> (sloítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear plenty[a lot] <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(very) familiar by hearsay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear a lot <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>на́ смерть</i> (ná smertʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mock, to jeer, to deride, to ridicule, to laugh at [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeer, sneer, gibe, mock, mockery, ridicule, derision, scoff, gleek</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>насме́шливый</i> (nasméšlivyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mockingly, archly, sarcastically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mockery</li></ol><p><i>насме́шливый</i> (nasméšlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mocking, derisive, sarcastic</li></ol><p><i>насмеха́ть(ся)</i> (nasmexátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runny nose, rhinorrhea</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) common cold</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) + deverbal from <i>сморка́ться</i> (smorkátʹsja, “to blow one's nose”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see as much as one wanted <i>(of)</i>, to see enough <i>(of)</i>, to have a good look <i>(at)</i></li><li>to see a lot <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for keeps, for good, permanently, forever</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>совсе́м</i> (sovsém)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pump</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Насос)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antlia (constellation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pump station, pumproom.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pump</li></ol><p><i>насо́с</i> (nasós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hurriedly, hastily, in a hurry, in haste, carelessly, in a slapdash manner</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>спех</i> (spex)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gossip, to tittle-tattle</li><li>to talk scandal</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>спле́тничать</i> (splétničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to harm (<i>someone</i>), to wrong (<i>someone</i>)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;impersonal,&#32;usually in the infinitive even when translated with a finite verb) for (someone) to not give a shit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>not give a shit</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solid crust formed on snow after thawing and refreezing</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, formed as if from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;настъ</i> (&#42;nastŭ), from <i>на</i> (na) + &#42;стъ, combining form of the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>. Compare Old East Slavic <i>обнастывати</i> (obnastyvati, “to cover”), <i>рознастити</i> (roznastiti, “to uncover”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come, to begin (of time)</li></ol><p><i>наста́ть</i> (nastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place, to set <i>(many)</i></li><li>to piece (on), to add, to add a piece, to put on</li><li>to lengthen</li><li>to aim, to level, to point</li><li>to instruct, to admonish, to edify, to exhort</li><li>to teach</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cuckold</li></ol><p>Literally, “to put horns on (someone)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>directions, instructions</li><li>admonition, precept, exhortation</li><li>(<i>military</i>) manual</li></ol><p><i>наста́вить</i> (nastávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place, to set <i>(many)</i></li><li>to piece (on), to add, to add a piece, to put on</li><li>to lengthen</li><li>to aim, to level, to point</li><li>to instruct, to admonish, to edify, to exhort</li><li>to teach</li></ol><p><i>наста́вить</i> (nastávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tutor, mentor, preceptor</li><li>instructor, tutor-guide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insist, to persist, to press on, to put one's foot down</li><li>to draw, to extract, to infuse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come, to begin (of time)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wide open</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>стежь</i> (stežʹ, “road, path”). Compare Belarusian <i>на́сцеж</i> (náscjež).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up <i>(with)</i>, to come close <i>(to)</i>, to approach</li><li>to catch</li></ol><p><i>насти́гнуть</i> (nastígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up <i>(with)</i>, to come close <i>(to)</i>, to approach</li><li>to catch</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́гнуть</i> (-stígnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flooring, planking, deck</li><li>covering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>насти́гнуть</i> (nastígnutʹ) (<i>conjugation is the same, only the infinitive is different</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́чь</i> (-stíčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liqueur, nastoyka</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) tincture</li></ol><p><i>настоя́ть</i> (nastojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>persistently, obstinately</li></ol><p><i>насто́йчивый</i> (nastójčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persistence (property of being persistent)</li><li>insistence</li></ol><p><i>насто́йчивый</i> (nastójčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>persistent</li><li>insistent</li></ol><p><i>настоя́ть</i> (nastojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tabletop game, board game</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>насто́льная игра́</i> (nastólʹnaja igrá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, so much, as much, thus much</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tabletop game, board game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desklamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tabletop, desktop</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table tennis, ping pong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alert, to put on guard</li></ol><p><i>насторожи́ть</i> (nastorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick up one's ears</li></ol><p>Literally, “to put one's ears on guard”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick up one's ears, to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>настора́живать</i> (nastoráživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on guard, on the lookout</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>сто́роже</i> (stórože).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>насторожённый</i> (nastorožónnyj)</li></ol><p><i>насторо́женный</i> (nastoróžennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vigilance, watchfulness, alertness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alert, awake (<i>experiencing alertness; of a person</i>)</li><li>alert (<i>expressing alertness; of a look, tone, face, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li><li>wary (<i>experiencing wariness; of a person</i>)</li><li>wary (<i>expressing wariness; of a look, tone, face, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alert, to put on guard</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сторожи́ть</i> (storožítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick up one's ears</li></ol><p>Literally, “to put one's ears on guard”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick up one's ears, to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>насторожи́ть</i> (nastorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an alert manner</li><li>warily</li></ol><p><i>насторожённый</i> (nastorožónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alert, awake (<i>experiencing alertness; of a person</i>)</li><li>alert (<i>expressing alertness; of a look, tone, face, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li><li>wary (<i>experiencing wariness; of a person</i>)</li><li>wary (<i>expressing wariness; of a look, tone, face, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insistence, insisting</li></ol><p><i>настоя́ть</i> (nastojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prior, abbot, father superior (superior or head of an abbey or monastery)</li></ol><p><i>настоя́ть</i> (nastojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insistently, urgently</li></ol><p><i>настоя́тельный</i> (nastojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very stubborn, persistent</li><li>urgent, pressing</li></ol><p><i>настоя́ть</i> (nastojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insist, to persist, to press on</li><li>to draw, to extract, to infuse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) vespid</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Vespidae</li></ol><p><i>настоя́щий</i> (nastojáščij, “true”) +&lrm; <i>оса́</i> (osá, “wasp”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the present</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present tense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present</li><li>true, real, genuine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>&#42;настоѩщь</i> (&#42;nastojęštĭ), present active imperfective participle of <i>настоꙗти</i> (nastojati, “to happen; to approach (of time)”). Compare <i>настоѩщиꙗ жизнь</i> (nastojęštija žiznĭ, “this life, this world”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Any buzzard of the genus <i>Buteo</i>, a buteo.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The genus <i>Buteo</i>.</li></ol><p><i>настоя́щий</i> (nastojáščij, “true”) +&lrm; <i>каню́к</i> (kanjúk, “buzzard”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A longhorn beetle of the subfamily Cerambycinae, a cerambycine.</li></ol><p><i>настоя́щий</i> (nastojáščij, “true”) +&lrm; <i>уса́ч</i> (usáč, “longhorn beetle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) bombycid</li></ol><p><i>настоя́щий</i> (nastojáščij, “true”) +&lrm; <i>шелкопря́д</i> (šelkoprjád, “silkworm”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to tune (a musical instrument)</li><li>(radio,&#32;television) to tune (a radio or television)</li><li>to put, to cause to be [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in a specified mood’</small>]</li><li>to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>про́тив</b> (prótiv, + genitive) <small>‘against someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in the mood (for something)</li></ol><p><i>настра́ивать</i> (nastráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mood, spirits, sentiment (mental state)</li></ol><p><i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predisposed, inclined (<i>to do something</i>)</li><li>in a (given) mood</li><li>adjusted, tuned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to build [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘houses, buildings, etc. in quantity’</small>]</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to tune (a musical instrument)</li><li>(radio,&#32;television) to tune (a radio or television)</li><li>to put, to cause to be [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘in a specified mood’</small>]</li><li>to incite [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>про́тив</b> (prótiv, + genitive) <small>‘against someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in the mood (for something)</li></ol><p><i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mood, spirit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nastrojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment, setting, setup, retrofitting, tuning, preference</li></ol><p><i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) preferences, settings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuner</li></ol><p><i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scribble, to dash off</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>строчи́ть</i> (stročítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) offensive</li><li>aggressive, active, persistent</li></ol><p><i>наступа́ть</i> (nastupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to step on</li><li>to come, to begin, to set in</li><li>to attack, to advance, to be on the offensive</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to step on</li><li>to come, to begin, to set in</li><li>(this perfective form is rarely in this sense) to attack, to advance, to be on the offensive</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to make the same mistake again</li></ol><p>Literally, “to step twice on one and the same rake”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offensive, onset, advance (an attack)</li><li>beginning, coming</li></ol><p><i>наступи́ть</i> (nastupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pass information by knocking (e.g. Morse code); to knock a rhythm</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to squeal on, to rat on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pushy, aggressive, insolent, brazen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to promise</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сули́ть</i> (sulítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to frown: only used in expressions, with брови.</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>су́пить</i> (súpitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frown, to scowl</li></ol><p><i>насу́пить</i> (nasúpitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the opposite side</li><li>across the way</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>vis-à-vis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up (something in plenty)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vital, essential, urgent, necessary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>насѫщьнъ</i> (nasǫštĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count</li><li>to number</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count</li><li>to number</li><li>to include, to contain</li></ol><p><i>насчита́ть</i> (nasčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to number, to amount <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>насчи́тывать</i> (nasčítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) about, concerning, regarding</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на</i> (na, “on, for”) +&lrm; <i>счёт</i> (sčot, “count”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(troubles etc)</i> to send, to inflict <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew, to pour (to distribute objects or pieces of something, of friable objects, not liquids)</li><li>to sputter, to pelt, to spout</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>насы́пать</i> (nasýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to add insult to injury, to rub it in (to further a loss with mockery or indignity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) embankment, mound</li><li>dike, dam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, to satiate, to sate</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to saturate</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-сы́тить</i> (-sýtitʹ). Not directly from <i>сыти́ть</i> (sytítʹ, “to satisfy, to saturate”) because of the differing stress pattern and iotation (<i>сычу́</i> (syčú) but <i>насы́щу</i> (nasýšču)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full (after eating)</li><li>to be saturated</li></ol><p><i>насы́тить</i> (nasýtitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, to satiate, to sate</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to saturate</li></ol><p><i>насы́тить</i> (nasýtitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full (after eating)</li><li>to be saturated</li></ol><p><i>насыща́ть</i> (nasyščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satiation, saturating</li></ol><p><i>насы́тить</i> (nasýtitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saturation</li><li>body (comparative viscosity, solidity or substance)</li></ol><p><i>насы́щенный</i> (nasýščennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat, rich, saturated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into/against something’</small>]</li><li>to lead, to guide [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>натолкну́ть</i> (natolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bump into, to hit</li><li>to encounter, to come across</li></ol><p><i>ната́лкивать</i> (natálkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat thoroughly</li><li>to melt (a lot of something)</li></ol><p><i>натопи́ть</i> (natopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry or drag (an amount of something, in several batches)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal (an amount of something)</li><li>to store (an amount of something)</li><li>to train (an animal)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to teach superficially, to coach superficially</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to gather or extract from several sources</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry or drag (an amount of something, in several batches)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal (an amount of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to store (an amount of something)</li><li>to train (an animal)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to teach superficially, to coach superficially</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to gather or extract from several sources</li><li>to pull on (an article of clothing)</li></ol><p><i>натаска́ть</i> (nataskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry or drag (an amount of something, in several batches)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to store (an amount of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal (an amount of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull on (an article of clothing)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) to do in a bad or wrong way</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) here you are!; there you are!, here! (said formally or to a group of people when giving someone something, i.e., "take it!")</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>an exclamation of surprise</i>)</li></ol><p>Imperative plural of <i>на</i> (na).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn directly on the body</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub <i>(with)</i>, to rub <i>(on, in)</i></li><li>to chafe, to rub sore</li><li>to rub, to polish</li><li>to grate</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub oneself [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>натере́ть</i> (naterétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to experience, to suffer a lot [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something unpleasant or painful’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) natively, naturally, as found in nature (about a chemical, protein, etc.)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) natively (in a manner present from birth)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) natively (in a manner designed for a particular platform)</li></ol><p><i>нати́вный</i> (natívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) native, natural, unmodified, as found in nature (about a chemical, protein, etc.)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) native (present from birth)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) native (designed for a particular platform)</li></ol><p>Latin <i>nātīvus</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub <i>(with)</i>, to rub <i>(on, in)</i></li><li>to chafe, to rub sore</li><li>to rub, to polish</li><li>to grate</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub oneself [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>натира́ть</i> (natirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onslaught, charge, onset, impact</li><li>pressure</li><li>stamping, impression</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) + deverbal from <i>ти́скать</i> (tískatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run against, to stumble on, to hit on</li><li>to stumble upon, to come across, to encounter by chance</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Member of or servant for NATO.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) NATO</li></ol><p><i>НА́ТО</i> (NÁTO) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into/against something’</small>]</li><li>to lead, to guide [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bump into, to hit</li><li>to encounter, to come across</li></ol><p><i>натолкну́ть</i> (natolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat thoroughly</li><li>to melt (a lot of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on an empty stomach</li></ol><p>From <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>то́щий</i> (tóščij) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak). Compare Polish <i>na czczo</i> (“on an empty stomach”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to train, to coach</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>тренирова́ть</i> (trenirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to train</li></ol><p><i>натренирова́ть</i> (natrenirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sodium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>natrium</i>, from German <i>Natron</i> + <i>-ium</i>, from Spanish <i>natrón</i>, from Arabic نَطْرُون (naṭrūn), from Ancient Greek <i>νίτρον</i> (nítron, “nitre”), ultimately from Egyptian <i>nṯrj</i> (“natron”): <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76670467.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="R9" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) effort, strain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to pull (taut)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strain</li><li>to stretch enough</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ту́жить</i> (túžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strained, forced</li></ol><p><i>нату́га</i> (natúga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;humorous) nature (the natural world)</li><li>nature, character (of a person)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) body (of an organism)</li><li>reality</li><li>(<i>art</i>) model (to draw/paint from), (living) subject</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) kind (goods, products etc. as a means of payment instead of money)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>nātūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heterosexual (a heterosexual person)</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding</i>) natty (someone who has not enhanced his gains by use of anabolic steroids)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(art,&#32;literature,&#32;philosophy,&#32;theater,&#32;sociology) naturalism</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) exaggerated focus on gritty details</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>naturalisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) naturalist (follower of naturalism)</li><li>(<i>sciences</i>) naturalist (natural scientist, one who studies nature)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from French <i>naturaliste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural fodder, non-industrial dog-food or other nourishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) naturally, of course</li></ol><p><i>натура́льный</i> (naturálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsistence economy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) natural number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural, real</li><li><i>(food)</i> organic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bodybuilding&#32;slang</i>) natural bodybuilding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in-situ, field, full-scale, actual, real, outdoor, exterior</li></ol><p><i>нату́ра</i> (natúra, “nature”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>model (person)</li></ol><p>From <i>нату́ра</i> (natúra) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run against, to stumble on, to hit on</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-тыка́ть</i> (-tykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>наткну́ться</i> (natknútʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recoil pad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>still life</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nature morte</i> (literally “dead nature”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to pull (taut)</li><li>to pull on</li></ol><p><i>натяг-</i> (natjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stretch, to be or become taut or elastic</li></ol><p><i>натя́гивать</i> (natjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) with a heavy load/cargo; not empty (e.g. of pack animal, trucks)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>на</i> (na, “on”) +&lrm; <i>тяжеле</i> (tjažele), the old short locative singular of <i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tension (state of an elastic object)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretching, pulling</li><li>stretch, strained interpretation</li></ol><p><i>натяг-</i> (natjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tight</li><li>stiff, strained</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to pull (taut)</li><li>to pull on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stretch, to be or become taut or elastic</li></ol><p><i>натяну́ть</i> (natjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nahuatl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at random</li><li>by guesswork</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>угад(а́ть)</i> (ugad(átʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>T-square (rule with a short perpendicular crosspiece at one end)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at random, haphazardly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>science</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) lesson</li><li>Nauka (module of the International Space Station)</li><li>Nauka (Soviet and Russian publisher)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nauka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>science-intensive, knowledge-based, high-tech (associated with or requiring significant scientific research)</li></ol><p>From <i>нау́ка</i> (naúka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ёмкий</i> (jómkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to abet, to egg on, to stir up (to assist or encourage in crime)</li></ol><p><i>нау́ськать</i> (naúsʹkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nautilus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the next morning, on the morrow</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>у́тро</i> (útro)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;with verbs of motion) running away, running for the hills (saving oneself)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach (<i>often not in an academic setting</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> dative <small>‘a skill, principle, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn (<i>often not in an academic setting</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘a skill, principle, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>научи́ть</i> (naučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>scientifically</li></ol><p><i>нау́чный</i> (naúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific and research</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) popular science</li><li>popular-scientific</li></ol><p><i>нау́чный</i> (naúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>популя́рный</i> (populjárnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific-practical, scientific and practical</li></ol><p><i>нау́чный</i> (naúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>практи́ческий</i> (praktíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) research and production</li></ol><p><i>нау́чный</i> (naúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>произво́дственный</i> (proizvódstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific and technical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naučьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>нау́ка</i> (naúka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) Scientific director.</li><li>(higher education,&#32;doctorate) Thesis supervisor.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>researcher, research fellow (academic research position requiring a PhD-equivalent degree)</li></ol><p>Literally, “scientific employee”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(socialism,&#32;obsolete) scientific socialism (society governed by scientific reasoning, not will).</li><li>(<i>socialism</i>) scientific socialism (socialism that starts from the analysis of capitalism, revealing its inner workings and contradictions, in order to develop a plan to move from capitalism to socialism).</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>socialisme scientifique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>radio</i>) headphone, earphone</li><li>(archaic,&#32;slang) earflap</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) informer, telltale; slanderer &lsqb;from 18th c.&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>у́хо</i> (úxo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare Polish <i>zausznik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headphone, earphone (listening device)</li><li>earmuffs, ear-flaps, ear-laps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(female)</i> telltale, informer, tattletale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to incite, to instigate, to abet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>на фига́</i> (na figá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) petroleum, oil, naphtha (naturally-occurring liquid petroleum)</li><li>naphtha (petroleum distillate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) naphthalene</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) mothballs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naphthalene</li></ol><p><i>нафтали́н</i> (naftalín) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) naphthalene</li></ol><p><i>нафтали́н</i> (naftalín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;vulgar,&#32;Internet slang) clipping of <i>на хуй</i> (na xuj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>нахуй</i> (naxuj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an impudent or insolent person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naxalъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be insolent, to be impudent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impudently, insolently, brazenly, shamelessly</li></ol><p><i>наха́льный</i> (naxálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impudent, insolent, impertinent</li><li>saucy, cheeky</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>наха́л</i> (naxál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudence, insolence, impertinence, effrontery, sauce, cheek</li></ol><p><i>наха́л</i> (naxál) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to praise, to extol, to tout (a lot)</li></ol><p><i>нахвали́ть</i> (naxvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to praise, to extol, to tout (a lot)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanger-on, sponger, freeloader</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>παράσιτος</i> (parásitos) (&lt; <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) + <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “food”)) (whence <i>парази́т</i> (parazít), English <i>parasite</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitism (about people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull down over one's eyes (e.g. a hat)</li></ol><p><i>нахлобу́чить</i> (naxlobúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull down over one's eyes (e.g. a hat)</li></ol><p>Probably an expressive formation influenced by <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ, “to clap”) and also <i>клобу́к</i> (klobúk, “cowl, hood”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep (over), to rush (onto)</li><li>to flow, to surge</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хлы́нуть</i> (xlýnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sullen, frowning, gloomy, morose, stern (<i>of a look or person</i>)</li><li>furrowed (<i>of the brows, expressing a stern or morose feeling</i>)</li><li>gloomy, cloudy (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frown</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хму́рить</i> (xmúritʹ), from <i>хму́рый</i> (xmúryj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frown</li><li>to gloom, to lour (of rain clouds)</li></ol><p><i>нахму́рить</i> (naxmúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to think, to consider</li><li>to come across</li><li>to happen to have</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be found, to turn up</li><li>to be located, to be situated (<i>no perfective form</i>)</li><li>to be in some condition (<i>no perfective form</i>)</li><li>to happen to have, to be found, to be discovered</li><li>to walk for a long time</li><li>to tire oneself by walking</li></ol><p><i>находи́ть</i> (naxodítʹ, “to find”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-, “to repletion, to satiation, to the full”) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to walk”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>find, discovery (anything that is found, especially by good fortune)</li></ol><p><i>находи́ть</i> (naxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Находка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nakhodka (a port city in Primorsky Krai, Russia)</li><li>Nakhodka Bay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingenuity, resourcefulness, inventiveness</li></ol><p><i>нахо́дчивый</i> (naxódčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resourceful</li></ol><p><i>наход(и́ть)</i> (naxod(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finding</li><li>being (at)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruffle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look morose or sullen</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хохлить</i> (xoxlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brazenness, unceremoniousness</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>храп</i> (xrap, “snore, snoring”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boldly, brazenly, unceremoniously</li></ol><p>Instrumental case of <i>нахра́п</i> (naxráp).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Ukrainian propaganda post, Ukrainian fake news</li><li>(military slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Ukrainian attack, Ukrainian offensive (<i>especially if overhyped or unsuccessful</i>)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>набе́г</i> (nabég, “incursion, raid”) +&lrm; <i>хрюк</i> (xrjuk, “oinking”), or made of <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>хрюк</i> (xrjuk). Russian jokes often stereotype Ukrainians as eating salo (salted pig fat); after the War in Donbas and the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine, in Russian nationalist discourse, this portrayal progressed to a dehumanizing association of enemy Ukrainian soldiers or Ukrainians in general with pigs (as opposed to merely pig-eaters).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used slightly more commonly</i>) alternative form of <i>на́ хуй</i> (ná xuj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) alternative form of <i>на хуя́</i> (na xujá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to get very drunk, to get shitfaced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write or draw by scratching</li><li>to write or draw carelessly or illegibly</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write or draw by scratching</li><li>to write or draw carelessly or illegibly</li></ol><p><i>нацара́пать</i> (nacarápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>national guard</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>национа́льная гва́рдия</i> (nacionálʹnaja gvárdija). Compare also Ukrainian <i>нацгвардія</i> (nachvardija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim (to point or direct a missile weapon)</li></ol><p><i>наце́лить</i> (nacélitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim at, to target [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to prepare to, to get ready to [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наце́ливать</i> (nacélivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim, to point</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>це́лить</i> (célitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim at, to target [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to prepare to, to get ready to [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>наце́лить</i> (nacélitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise the price of</li></ol><p><i>нацени́ть</i> (nacenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise the price of</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>markup (<i>of a price</i>), margin</li><li>extra charge, surcharge</li><li>increase in price</li></ol><p><i>нацени́ть</i> (nacenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten <i>(to)</i>, to hook on <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put on, to fasten on, to pin on</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten <i>(to)</i>, to hook on <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put on, to fasten on, to pin on</li></ol><p><i>нацепи́ть</i> (nacepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Nazi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nazi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nazismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) A Nazi.</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) A nationalist.</li></ol><p>From нацист (nacist) or националист (nacionalist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) National Bolshevism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) national traitor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nationalverräter</i> (“national traitor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>National Socialism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nationalsozialismus</i>, apparently in the Russian language first appears in 1933.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>National Socialist (<i>an adherent of National Socialism</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Nationalsozialist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nationalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nationalize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) nationalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nationalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nationalist</li></ol><p><i>национали́ст</i> (nacionalíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) national culture; national-cultural</li></ol><p><i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnic group; ethnicity</li><li>(<i>less common</i>) nationality (the status of belonging to a particular nation, a particular country)</li></ol><p><i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national</li><li>ethnic; traditional for an ethnic group</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>national park</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Nazi</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) A perceived enemy of Russia in general.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Nazi</li></ol><p><i>наци́ст</i> (nacíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nation, people</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>nacja</i>, from Latin <i>nātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>national leader</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>национа́льный ли́дер</i> (nacionálʹnyj líder).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>national park</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>национа́льный парк</i> (nacionálʹnyj park).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, commencement, onset, start</li><li>source, origin</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) principles, basis, basics</li><li>command, authority</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>начѧло</i> (načęlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;načędlo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>нача́ть</i> (načátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“new, fresh”), whence English <i>recent</i> and <i>ceno-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) origin (the point at which the axes of a coordinate system intersect)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief, boss, superior, master, principal, commander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>начѧльникъ</i> (načęlĭnikŭ). Also analyzable as <i>нача́ло</i> (načálo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Cognate with Czech <i>náčelník</i>, Polish <i>naczelnik</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>načelnik</i>, and Ukrainian <i>начальник</i> (načalʹnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stationmaster (the person in charge of a railroad station)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) overbearing, imperious</li></ol><p><i>нача́льник</i> (načálʹnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initial, first; opening</li><li>primary, elementary, basic</li></ol><p><i>нача́ло</i> (načálo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bossy, domineering, overbearing</li></ol><p><i>нача́льство</i> (načálʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, direction</li><li>authorities</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chief, head, boss</li></ol><p><i>нача́ло</i> (načálo) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, to start, to commence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>начѧти</i> (načęti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;načęti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-чать</i> (-čatʹ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“new, fresh”), whence English <i>recent</i> and <i>ceno-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, to start, to set in, to break out</li></ol><p><i>нача́ть</i> (načátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the alert, on guard, keeping an eye out</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>на</i> (na) + a deverbal of <i>чека́ть</i> (čekátʹ) in the locative case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tracing, inscribing</li><li>outlining</li><li>outline, inscription</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) written statement, description</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) instruction, order</li></ol><p><i>начерта́ть</i> (načertátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inscribe, to trace</li><li>to outline, to delineate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>черта́</i> (čertá) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to draw up, to plot</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read aloud</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to deposit (any amount)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undertaking; beginning</li></ol><p><i>начина́ть</i> (načinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, to start</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>-чина́ть</i> (-činátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, to start, to set in, to break out</li></ol><p>From <i>начина́ть</i> (načinátʹ) and <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginner</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) engaging in an activity for the first time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sharpen (a usually large quantity of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repair (a usually large quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>начини́ть</i> (načinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up the inside of (something), to stuff</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to equip in abundance (<i>e.g. with knowledge, instruments, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sharpen (a usually large quantity of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repair (a usually large quantity of something)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ, “to cause, to administer”)</p><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ, “to repair”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filling, stuffing</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) internals (internal equipment, parts)</li></ol><p><i>начини́ть</i> (načinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up the inside of (something), to stuff</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to equip in abundance (<i>e.g. with knowledge, instruments, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>начини́ть</i> (načinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charging, extra sum</li></ol><p><i>начи́слить</i> (načíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to charge</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to credit <i>(to an account)</i>, to credit <i>(an account with)</i>, to post</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to calculate, to reckon</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to authorize, to assign</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to charge</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to credit <i>(to an account)</i>, to credit <i>(an account with)</i>, to post</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to calculate, to reckon</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to authorize, to assign</li></ol><p><i>начи́слить</i> (načíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to polish, to shine</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clean</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) outright, decidedly, openly, flatly</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, frankly, without equivocation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erudition</li></ol><p><i>начита́нный</i> (načitánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-read, (of a person) that reads a lot, that has read a lot of books</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read in any quantity (<i>usually a large quantity</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discover by reading</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘any amount of something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read a lot</li></ol><p><i>начита́ть</i> (načitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read in (usually) large quantities</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discover by reading</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read aloud</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to deposit (any amount)</li></ol><p><i>начита́ть</i> (načitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘any amount of something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read a lot</li></ol><p><i>начи́тывать</i> (načítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to sneeze (into something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to give the cold shoulder</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чиха́ть</i> (čixátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to polish, to shine</li></ol><p><i>начи́стить</i> (načístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) office head, office chief</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>нача́льник канцеля́рии</i> (načálʹnik kanceljárii).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nachos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>nachos</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) chief of staff</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>нача́льник шта́ба</i> (načálʹnik štába).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) our, ours</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) referring to an ingroup, e.g. one's compatriots, relatives, or associates</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of the letter н in the Old Russian alphabet.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нашь</i> (našĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;našь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grope for</li></ol><p><i>наша́рить</i> (našáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find, to touch (<i>after groping for something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>ша́рить</i> (šáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;rare) sal ammoniac, ammonium chloride (rare mineral)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) short for <i>нашатырный спирт</i> (našatyrnyj spirt, “ammoniac-water mixture”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>нашатырь</i> (našatyrʹ) (since 16th c.), from Persian نشادر (nošâdor, nošâder, “sal ammoniac”) probably via some Turkic intermediary, from Middle Persian <i>𐭠𐭭𐭥𐭱𐭠𐭲𐭥𐭥𐭩</i> (*ʾnwšʾdwr /⁠*anōšādur⁠/, “immortal fire”), combined from <i>𐭠𐭭𐭥𐭱</i> (anōš /⁠ʾnwš⁠/, “elixir, antidote”, literally “immortal”) and <i>𐭠𐭲𐭥𐭥𐭩</i> (ʾdwr' /⁠ādur⁠/, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anno Domini, Common Era, A.D.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to whisper</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to whisper in (someone's) ear [(<i>optional</i>) <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to suggest secretly, to gossip [(<i>optional</i>) <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to utter a charm or spell [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on something, giving it magical power’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invasion, incursion, inroad, irruption</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>нашьствиѥ</i> (našĭstvije), <i>нашествиѥ</i> (našestvije). By surface analysis, <i>наше́д</i> (našéd) +&lrm; <i>-ствие</i> (-stvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</li><li>to sew onto (something)</li><li>to sew in large amounts</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>наши́ть</i> (našítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing</li><li>sewn-on patch or strip</li><li>(<i>military</i>) stripe (indicating rank), chevron</li><li>(<i>military</i>) insignia, badge</li></ol><p><i>нашива́ть</i> (našivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slice, to shred, to chop (in thin slices or pieces)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>шинкова́ть</i> (šinkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a lot of noise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause a sensation</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>шуме́ть</i> (šumétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>наш</i> (naš).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to whisper</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to whisper in (someone's) ear [(<i>optional</i>) <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to suggest secretly, to gossip [(<i>optional</i>) <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to utter a charm or spell [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on something, giving it magical power’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>нашепта́ть</i> (našeptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally&#32;and&#32;figuratively</i>) to grope <i>(for, after)</i>, to feel about <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally&#32;and&#32;figuratively</i>) to grope <i>(for, after)</i>, to feel about <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>нащу́пать</i> (naščúpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to snitch, to inform [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>] (<i>especially of children</i>)</li></ol><p><i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>я́бедничать</i> (jábedničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in real life, while awake, not in a dream</li></ol><p>Constructed as if from <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>яву́</i> (javú), from &#42;ява́ (compare Ukrainian <i>ява́</i> (javá, “reality”), and Russian <i>явь</i> (javʹ) with the same meaning).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) naiad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to iterate furiously</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to toss off, to jerk it</li></ol><p>From <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>я́рый</i> (járyj) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;humorous) liar, cheater</li></ol><p><i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck with, to annoy”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik, “a doer”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking over, fuckover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to fuck someone over (to cheat someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>наеба́ть</i> (najebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rent, hire</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>наня́ть</i> (nanjátʹ, “to hire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercenary, hireling (person employed to fight)</li></ol><p><i>наём</i> (najóm) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assassination (killing or murder for political reasons)</li><li>contract killing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hired, rented</li></ol><p><i>наём</i> (najóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hired) assassin (one who, motivated by political reasons, intentionally kills a particular person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>м-да́</i> (m-dá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>нрав</i> (nrav)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>negative in full</i>) not, no, -n't, without</li><li>Idiomatic negative usage</li><li>(<i>partial negative</i>) perhaps not, whether... or not</li><li>will never (<i>implying impossibility to do something</i>)</li><li>(usually not translated, gives affirmative meaning for expression, especially in exclamations)</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(With prepositions and pronouns <b>не</b> actually is a prefix, in spite of the fact that it is written separately.)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) no (used to show disagreement or negation)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ne</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ne</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;né</i>. Cognates include Lithuanian <i>ne</i>, Old English <i>ne</i>, German <i>nicht</i>, <i>nie</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni), Irish <i>ní</i>, Latin <i>ne</i>, Sanskrit <i>न</i> (ná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a valid, well-founded, justified manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at odds (in disagreement, conflicting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncomfortable with something, under the weather</li><li>out of place</li></ol><p>A mistranslation of French <i>ne pas être dans son assiette</i>, where the word <i>assiette</i> (“place, condition; plate”) was originally referring to <i>place, condition</i> rather than <i>plate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of sorts</li><li>out of one's mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;in the negative) to not see the forest for the trees (to be overwhelmed by detail to the point where it obscures the overall situation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>let alone, to say nothing of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> нечего (nečevo),&lrm; за (za).</li><li>you're welcome, not at all, don't mention it</li><li>not worth mentioning, nothing to speak of</li><li>no problem</li></ol><p>Literally, “there's nothing for”. See also German <i>da nicht für</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of place, out of turn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uneasily, uncomfortably</li><li>out of sorts, under the weather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>non-stop, tirelessly, relentlessly</li></ol><p>Literally, “without laying the hands”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>isn't it so? (used as an expression of affirmation after a statement)</li></ol><p>Same construction as in Scandinavian languages (compare Norwegian Nynorsk <i>ikkje sant</i>, Danish <i>ikke sandt</i>, Swedish <i>inte sant</i> and Norwegian Bokmål <i>ikke sant</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more than once, time and again, repeatedly, many a time</li></ol><p>Literally, “not once”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>God forbid; you never know</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unhurriedly</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) + <i>спеша́</i> (spešá), adverbial participle of <i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to keep an eye on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>you're welcome, not at all, don't mention it</li></ol><p>Literally, “not worth the gratitude”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (that's/it's) not the point, (that/it) doesn't matter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) it doesn't matter, it's not important</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>not counting, apart from, exclusive of, less</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> не (ne),&lrm; так (tak).</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wrong (amiss)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>right; isn't it so?, used as an expression of affirmation after a statement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the wrong thing, the wrong one, something wrong</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> не (ne),&lrm; то (to).</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) introduces an alternative outcome or a potential repercussion or, or else; lest</li></ol><p>Literally, “not that”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not that, not like, not as if (used to express that something potentially assumed is not actually the case, or at least not to the same degree)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> не (ne),&lrm; тот (tot).</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the wrong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>не́-а</i> (né-a)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>un-, non-, in-, im-, ir-, dis-, mis- (not)</li></ol><p>Prefix use of <i>не</i> (ne, “not”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nuh-uh, nope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inadequacy</li><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) mental instability</li></ol><p><i>неадеква́тный</i> (neadɛkvátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbalanced, unreasonable, inordinate, unreliable, inappropriate, inadequate</li><li>crazy, disturbed, out of one's mind</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>адеква́тный</i> (adɛkvátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexact, unpunctual</li><li>careless</li><li>untidy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>аккуратный</i> (akkuratnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neanderthal (member of the now extinct species <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Neanderthal (of or pertaining to Homines neanderthalenses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsafe, fairly dangerous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>безопа́сный</i> (bezopásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-indifferent, not indifferent, somewhat interested</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>безразли́чный</i> (bezrazlíčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;ironic) not unknown, rather famous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>безызве́стный</i> (bezyzvéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rather interesting, not uninteresting (<i>often translated as simply "interesting"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>интере́с</i> (interés) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), lit. "not without interest"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only,&#32;poetic) heavens, skies</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>небеса</i> (nebesa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cerulean, azure, sky-blue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) celestial body, heavenly body (natural object beyond earth's atmosphere)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavenly, celestial</li></ol><p><i>небеса́</i> (nebesá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>LGBTQ</i>) non-binary</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>бина́рный</i> (binárnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbeautifulness, unattractiveness</li><li>blameworthiness, unbecomingness</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благови́дн(ый)</i> (blagovídn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbeautiful, unattractive</li><li>unbecoming</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благо-</i> (blago-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingratitude</li></ol><p><i>неблагода́рный</i> (neblagodárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungrateful</li><li>thankless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благода́рный</i> (blagodárnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malevolent</li><li>ill-disposed</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благожела́тельный</i> (blagoželátelʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrustworthy, unreliable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благонадёжный</i> (blagonadjóžnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble, bad luck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhappy, unfavourable/unfavorable, bad</li><li>problem</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>благополу́чный</i> (blagopolúčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfavorable, negative</li><li>inauspicious</li><li>adverse, ill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imprudence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;regional) reindeer calf younger than one year old</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tundra Nenets <i>ня̆блюй</i> (nyăblyuy°).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sky</li><li>heaven, firmament</li><li>air</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>небо</i> (nebo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nebo</i>. Doublet of <i>нёбо</i> (njóbo), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not rich or wealthy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;ironic</i>) deity, divine/heavenly being, ivory tower dweller</li></ol><p><i>не́бо</i> (nébo, “sky”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жи́тель</i> (žítelʹ, “dweller”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little, short, not great</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) firmament, the vault of heaven, the dome of the sky</li></ol><p>From <i>не́бо</i> (nébo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сво́д</i> (svód). Parallel to <i>небосклон</i> (nebosklon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skyline, horizon, the place at which the earth and sky appear to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>не́бо</i> (nébo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>скло́н</i> (sklón). Coined by the poet Ivan Ivanovich Martynov (Иван Иванович Мартынов) in 1793. It has also been interpreted as a calque of the Latin term declinatio caeli (<i>inclination of the sky</i>), meaning geographical latitude.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skyscraper</li></ol><p>From <i>не́бо</i> (nébo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-скрёб</i> (-skrjób), combining form of <i>скрести́</i> (skrestí, “to scrape”) (compare 1sg. present <i>скребу́</i> (skrebú)). Calque of English <i>skyscraper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) I suppose, most likely, probably, I dare say</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sure, no fear</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>не бо́йся</i> (ne bójsja, literally “do not fear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) neglect, disregard</li></ol><p>From <i>небречь</i> (nebrečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>negligently, perfunctorily (in a negligent manner)</li><li>jauntily (in a dapper or stylish manner)</li></ol><p><i>небре́жный</i> (nebréžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness, negligence</li><li>nonchalance</li></ol><p><i>небре́жный</i> (nebréžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, negligent, mindless, slipshod</li><li>casual, nonchalant, offhand</li></ol><p>From <i>небре́чь</i> (nebréčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Old Church Slavonic <i>небрѣщи</i> (nebrěšti). Compare native Russian <i>бе́режный</i> (bérežnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>пренебрега́ть</i> (prenebregátʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>небрѣщи</i> (nebrěšti)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unshaven</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>бри́тый</i> (brítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unremarkable, brightless, inconspicuous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>бро́ский</i> (bróskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprecedented, unheard-of</li><li>imaginary, fantastic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>быва́лый</i> (byvályj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tale, fable, yarn, invention (story)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>были́ца</i> (bylíca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-existence; nonbeing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>небꙑтиѥ</i> (nebytije). By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>бытие</i> (bytije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>no matter (how)</li><li>poorly, not very well</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>it doesn't matter, it's OK</li></ol><p><i>нева́жный</i> (nevážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unimportant</li><li>poor; pitiable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ва́жный</i> (vážnyj, “important”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roly-poly (<i>a toy</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>не</i> (ne, “not”) and the verb <i>валять</i> (valjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not far off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) soon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) unknown, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ве́данный</i> (védannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) there is no telling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) there is no way of knowing</li></ol><p><i>неве́домый</i> (nevédomyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown, unbeknown, unbeknownst</li><li>unfamiliar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boor, lout, ill-mannered person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>невѣжа</i> (nevěža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevěďa</i> (“ignorant person &lt; ignorance”), from <i>&#42;ne-</i> + <i>&#42;věďa</i>, from <i>&#42;věděti</i> (“to know”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>неве́жда</i> (nevéžda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignoramus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>невѣжда</i> (nevěžda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevěďa</i> (“ignorant person &lt; ignorance”), from <i>&#42;ne-</i> + <i>&#42;věďa</i>, from <i>&#42;věděti</i> (“to know”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>неве́жа</i> (nevéža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ignorant</li></ol><p><i>неве́жество</i> (nevéžestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li></ol><p><i>неве́жа</i> (nevéža) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impolite (not polite)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ве́жливый</i> (véžlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bad luck</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>везе́ние</i> (vezénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bad luck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlucky</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little, not big, not large, insignificant</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incredulity, unbelief (religious disbelief)</li><li>disbelief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incorrectly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ве́рно</i> (vérno, “correctly”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infidelity, unfaithfulness (unfaithfulness in marriage or other moral obligation)</li><li>disloyalty</li><li>incorrectness</li></ol><p><i>неве́рный</i> (nevérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorrect (erroneous)</li><li>unfaithful</li><li>unsteady (<i>e.g. gait</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infidel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevěrьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рный</i> (vérnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) improbable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вероподо́бный</i> (veropodóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unbelievably, incredibly (in a manner one does not believe)</li></ol><p><i>невероя́тный</i> (neverojátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improbable</li><li>unbelievable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “im-”) +&lrm; <i>вероя́тный</i> (verojátnyj, “probable”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbelieving, disbelieving, nonbelieving</li><li>irreligious, faithless</li><li>pagan</li><li>ungodly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atheist, disbeliever, nonbeliever, unbeliever, pagan, infidel</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>ве́рующий</i> (vérujuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unhappily, sadly</li></ol><p>From <i>невесёлый</i> (nevesjólyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) or from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ве́село</i> (véselo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weightlessness</li></ol><p><i>невесо́мый</i> (nevesómyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) imponderable, weightless</li><li>negligible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>весо́мый</i> (vesómyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiancée, bride</li><li>marriageable girl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevěsta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman's daughter-in-law</li><li>sister-in-law (the wife of one's brother or brother-in-law)</li></ol><p>From <i>неве́ста</i> (nevésta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Akin to Belarusian няве́стка.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) God knows (no one knows)</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>весть</i> (vestʹ), obsolete third singular present indicative of <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerless, joyless, mirthless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have headache due to various emotions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adversity, misfortune</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>regardless of</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>взира́я</i> (vzirája, present adverbial participle of <i>взира́ть</i> (vzirátʹ, “to look, to gaze”)) +&lrm; <i>на</i> (na), literally “not [by] looking on”; compare <i>regardless</i> (literally “without look”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dislike, to take a dislike to</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) accidentally, by chance</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unintentionally</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вз-</i> (vz-) +&lrm; <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>чай</i> (čaj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, plain, homely, unpretentious, modest, ordinary, average</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>взра́чный</i> (vzráčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonexplosive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undemanding, not particular</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>взыска́тельный</i> (vzyskátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) something unprecedented, surprising, not seen before</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вида́ть</i> (vidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ).</p><p>For the meaning development compare with Czech <i>zázrak</i> (“miracle”), Slovak <i>zázrak</i> (“miracle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>previously unseen or unknown</li><li>extraordinary, exceptional, unprecedented</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ви́данный</i> (vídannyj), past passive participle of <i>вида́ть</i> (vidátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) invisible hand</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>invisible hand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine) invisible being</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) invisible hairpin, bobby pin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>invisibly (in a way that can not be seen)</li></ol><p><i>неви́димый</i> (nevídimyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevidimъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ви́димый</i> (vídimyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>innocently (guiltlessly, harmlessly, naively or virginally)</li></ol><p><i>неви́нный</i> (nevínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innocence (lack of ability or intention to harm or damage)</li></ol><p><i>неви́нный</i> (nevínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, guiltless</li><li>harmless, innocuous</li><li>naive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вина́</i> (viná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Невинный)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Nevinny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innocence (absence of responsibility for a crime)</li></ol><p><i>невино́вный</i> (nevinóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, not guilty <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вино́вный</i> (vinóvnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tasteless, insipid, unappetizing</li><li>(figurative,&#32;ironic) unpleasant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insanity, lunacy</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>вменя́емость</i> (vmenjájemostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insane</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) not in control of one's emotions</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вменя́емый</i> (vmenjájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonintervention, noninterference</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вмеша́тельство</i> (vmešátelʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inattention</li><li>disregard, negligence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inattentive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>внима́тельный</i> (vnimátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indistinctly, inarticulately, in a confused way</li></ol><p><i>невня́тный</i> (nevnjátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indistinct, inarticulate, confused</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вня́тный</i> (vnjátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seine</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) window grate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevodъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irretrievable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of restraint</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>воздержа́ние</i> (vozderžánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of restraint</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>возде́ржанный</i> (vozdéržannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impossibly</li></ol><p><i>невозмо́жный</i> (nevozmóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impossibility</li></ol><p><i>невозмо́жный</i> (nevozmóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impossible (not possible)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>возмо́жный</i> (vozmóžnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>imperturbably, calmly, in a cool manner</li></ol><p><i>невозмути́мый</i> (nevozmutímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperturbability, unflappability</li></ol><p><i>невозмути́мый</i> (nevozmutímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unflappable, imperturbable, unruffled, calm</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>возмути́ть</i> (vozmutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To force someone to do something contrary to his personal will and wishes</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave, captive</li></ol><p><i>нево́ля</i> (nevólja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>involuntarily</li><li>automatically</li><li>unintentionally, unwittingly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced</li><li>involuntary, unintentional</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery, captivity, bondage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nevoľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unimaginable, inconceivable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вообрази́мый</i> (voobrazímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unarmed (defenceless and lacking weapon)</li><li>unaided, naked, bare (of the eye, a look)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the naked eye</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-bred, bad-mannered, ill-mannered, uncivilized (having bad manners)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of receptivity, unreceptiveness</li><li>insensitivity, immunity, insusceptibility, resistance</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>восприимчивость</i> (vospriimčivostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unneeded, not in demand</li><li>unclaimed (letter, parcel, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>востре́бованный</i> (vostrébovannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inappropriately, out of place, not to the point, malapropos, inopportunely</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>впопа́д</i> (vpopád)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a great deal <i>(of)</i>, piles <i>(of)</i>, bags <i>(of)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unintelligible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вразуми́тельный</i> (vrazumítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;psychiatry) neurasthenic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unscathed, unharmed, unhurt, safe, intact</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>neuro-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) obsessive-compulsive disorder</li></ol><p>Literally, “neurosis of obsessive conditions”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>study of neurotic disorders</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"neurosotherapy", an unconventional, nontraditional and widely criticized psychotherapeutic treatment of neuroses, practiced by no more than 1% of doctors worldwide. The general idea is to treat a neurosis by trying to produce its symptoms in the patient.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurology</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>neurologia</i>. By surface analysis, <i>невро-</i> (nevro-) +&lrm; <i>-логия</i> (-logija). Also from French <i>névrologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neuropathologist</li><li>neurologist (<i>former usage up till about 1980, incorrect, occasionally still found</i>)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>невропатоло́гия</i> (nevropatológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neuropathology</li><li>neurology (<i>former usage up till about 1980, incorrect, occasionally still found</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurotic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Neva (river in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Нева́</i> (Nevá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Невский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nevsky</li><li>used in names, such as street names</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>being impatient (in anticipation of something), hard to bear, hard to be patient, hard to wait</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the one who walks through the wrong door</li></ol><p>This word comes from the phrase "зашёл не в ту дверь" that Philipp Kirkorov said in his video apologizing for participating in Nastya Ivleeva's so-called naked party (голая вечеринка) in December 2023.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) magnificent, wonderful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disadvantageously, at a loss (in a disadvantageous manner)</li></ol><p><i>невы́годный</i> (nevýgodnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprofitable, non-lucrative, unremunerative, not paying</li><li>disadvantageous, unfavourable/unfavorable</li><li>unattractive</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вы́годный</i> (výgodnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period of not leaving a given city or country</li><li>prohibition on leaving a given city or country</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вы́езд</i> (výjezd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restricted to travel abroad; not allowed to travel abroad; not permitted to travel abroad (esp. for security reasons)</li><li>travel banned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unbearably</li></ol><p><i>невыноси́мый</i> (nevynosímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbearable, intolerable, insufferable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonpayment</li><li>arrears</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вы́плата</i> (výplata)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonfulfilment; failure to carry out; failure to execute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infeasible, impracticable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>выполни́мый</i> (vypolnímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ineffable, inexpressible</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вы́разить</i> (výrazitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-имый</i> (-imyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexpressive, inarticulate</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>вырази́тельный</i> (vyrazítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not tall, not high, short (<i>height</i>), low</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>неве́ста</i> (nevésta).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>неве́стка</i> (nevéstka).</li></ol><p><i>невѣ́ста</i> (nevě́sta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luxury, comfort, prosperity</li><li>bliss, delight, contentment, languor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;něga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) negative</li><li>negative, negative information, feedback or mood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>negatively</li></ol><p><i>негати́вный</i> (negatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negative (<i>usually in the sense "unfavourable/unfavorable/adverse/detrimental" or "critical (expressing criticism) / pessimistic"</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;stilted) somewhere</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>где</i> (gde). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ні́де</i> (níde), Belarusian <i>не́дзе</i> (njédzje), Serbo-Croatian <i>не̏где</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>non-hermetically, unhermetically, in a non-airtight manner</li></ol><p><i>негермети́чный</i> (negermetíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpressurised, non-airtight, leaky, leaking, non-hermetic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>гермети́чный</i> (germetíčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflexible, stiff, rigid</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>гибкий</i> (gibkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhygienic</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-, not”) +&lrm; <i>гигиенический</i> (gigijeničeskij, “hygienic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unspoken rule, unwritten rule, oral law (rule that is understood but does not exist in written form)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspoken, unwritten, oral, implicit (implicit or understood but not formally articulated)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negligée</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>négligé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shallow, not too deep</li><li>shallow, superficial, skin-deep</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensible, clever enough</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) naughty child; rascal; rogue; wretch, mischiefmaker</li></ol><p><i>него́дный</i> (negódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unfitness, lack of fitness</li><li>uselessness</li></ol><p><i>него́дный</i> (negódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfit</li><li>improper, unsuitable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scoundrel, rascal, good-for-nothing, worthless person</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>го́дный</i> (gódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indignation, resentment (anger or displeasure felt out of belief that others have engaged in wrongdoing or mistreatment)</li></ol><p><i>негодова́ть</i> (negodovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be indignant, to rail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>негодовати</i> (negodovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;negodovati</i> (“to be indignant”), related to <i>&#42;godìti</i> (“to please”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoundrel, villain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;negodějь</i>. Can also be analysed as <i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>годи́ться</i> (godítʹsja, “to be of good use”) +&lrm; <i>-яй</i> (-jaj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstate</li><li>nongovernmental</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рственный</i> (gosudárstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unpreparedness, unreadiness</li></ol><p>From <i>негото́вый</i> (negotóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unready, unprepared</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вый</i> (gotóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black (man); negro</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;offensive,&#32;rare) someone who works for peanuts or for nothing, a slave</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ghost writer (usually in the idiom <i>литерату́рный негр</i> (literatúrnyj negr))</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) abbreviation of <i>неграждани́н</i> (negraždanín): non-citizen (used primarily in Latvia and Estonia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nègre</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>niger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illiteracy</li><li>incompetence</li></ol><p><i>негра́мотный</i> (negrámotnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illiterate</li><li>ungrammatical</li><li>ignorant, unversed</li><li><i>(work)</i> crude, inexpert</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Negrito</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) nigger, black (man)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black, Negro</li></ol><p>From a hypothetical &#42;негритя́нин (whose feminine <i>негритя́нка</i> (negritjánka) is attested) + <i>-ский</i> (-skij). The form &#42;негритя́нин contains the same root as <i>негритёнок</i> (negritjónok) (an extended version of the root of <i>негр</i> (negr)) + <i>-я́нин</i> (-jánin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black child</li></ol><p><i>негр</i> (negr) +&lrm; <i>-ит-</i> (-it-) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anthropology</i>) Negroid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>négroïde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quiet, low (<i>of voice</i>)</li><li>less well-known</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quietly</li><li>in a low voice</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>гро́мко</i> (grómko, “loudly, aloud”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhumane (cruel and savage, not humane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of event) recent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>да́вний</i> (dávnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>recently, not long ago, lately</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>давно́</i> (davnó, “long ago”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not far, not far off, nearby, close</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>далеко́</i> (dalekó, “far, far off, far away”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>недалеко́</i> (nedalekó): not far, not far away, not far off, nearby, close</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close, near, nearby</li><li>close, near (in time); recent, soon</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;of a human) dim-witted, narrow-minded, stupid</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>далёкий</i> (daljókij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not in vain, not without reason; not for nothing; justly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>real estate</li></ol><p><i>недви́жимый</i> (nedvížimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immovable, immobile</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дви́жимый</i> (dvížimyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>explicitly, unequivocally, unambiguously</li></ol><p><i>недвусмы́сленный</i> (nedvusmýslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explicit, unequivocal, unambiguous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>двусмы́сленный</i> (dvusmýslennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) incapable, ineffective</li><li>(<i>law</i>) incompetent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;law) incompetent person</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дееспосо́бный</i> (dejesposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invalidity, inefficiency, ineffectiveness</li></ol><p><i>недействи́тельный</i> (nedejstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) invalid, void, null</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) past its expiry/expiration term, expired</li><li>untrue</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>действи́тельный</i> (dejstvítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indivisible</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) prime</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дели́мый</i> (delímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja): week</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a week's, weekly</li></ol><p><i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>week</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Sunday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>недѣлꙗ</i> (nedělja, “Sunday”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;neděľa</i> (“Sunday”). By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>де́л-</i> (dél-, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja). Originally used to mean Sunday, the day of rest.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incontinence (inability to restrain discharge)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enuresis, urinary incontinence, involuntary urination, bedwetting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unruly, undisciplined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) under-, insufficiently</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) to fail to do</li><li>(<i>used with nouns</i>) shoddy, wannabe</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>до-</i> (do-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble</li></ol><p>Neuter singular of <i>недо́брый</i> (nedóbryj, “hostile, evil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil wisher, illwisher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfair, dishonest, unscrupulous</li><li>improper, negligent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>добросо́вестный</i> (dobrosóvestnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unkind, hostile</li><li>bad, wicked, evil</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-done, half-cooked</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>до-</i> (do-) +&lrm; <i>варить</i> (varitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistrust (lack of trust)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “non-”) +&lrm; <i>доверие</i> (doverije, “trust”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with distrust/suspicion, distrustfully, mistrustfully</li></ol><p><i>недове́рчивый</i> (nedovérčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incredulity (unwillingness to believe&#59; doubt about the truth of something)</li></ol><p><i>недове́рчивый</i> (nedovérčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distrustful, incredulous, mistrustful, suspicious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дове́рчивый</i> (dovérčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>discontentedly</li></ol><p><i>недово́льный</i> (nedovólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissatisfied, discontented, displeased, malcontent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discontent, dissatisfaction, displeasure; resentment</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дово́льство</i> (dovólʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep/hold back</li></ol><p><i>недоговори́ть</i> (nedogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep/hold back</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupid, half-baked, lousy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unfinished</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unfinished</li></ol><p><i>недоде́лать</i> (nedodélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malnutrition</li></ol><p><i>недоеда́ть</i> (nedojedátʹ, “to be underfed”) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not eat enough, to not eat one's fill</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;intransitive) to be undernourished, to be malnourished, to go hungry</li></ol><p><i>недоед-</i> (nedojed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not eat enough, to not eat one's fill</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlawful, inadmissible, prohibited, unfair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unacceptable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overdue tax payment, arrears</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brief, short, not long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not long (of time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily</li></ol><p><i>недо́лгий</i> (nedólgij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-lived</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>долгове́чный</i> (dolgovéčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have no special liking/sympathy <i>(for)</i>, to have a dislike <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malaise, lethargy, malady</li></ol><p><i>недомога́ть</i> (nedomogátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije). Compare Polish <i>niedomagać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ail, to be unwell, to be ill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliberate omission (<i>in speech</i>)</li><li>allusion, innuendo</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessness, failure to think something through</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>мысль</i> (myslʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>premature birth, slink, preemie</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) wasteman, piece of trash</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>доноси́ть</i> (donosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), or <i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p><p>Compare <i>не́доросль</i> (nédoroslʹ). Compare typologically Latvian <i>bērns</i> (“child”), Lithuanian <i>bérnas</i> (“lad, servant”), <i>berniukas</i> (“boy”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barną</i>, Latin <i>forda</i> (“cow in calf”) (all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underestimate, to underrate, to undervalue</li></ol><p><i>недооцени́ть</i> (nedoocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underestimate, to underrate, to undervalue</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underestimation</li></ol><p><i>недооцени́ть</i> (nedoocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) to be too drunk, but not as much as intended. To drink too much, but still want more regardless.</li></ol><p>From <i>недо-</i> (nedo-, “under-”) +&lrm; <i>пере-</i> (pere-, “over-”) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ, “drink”). Literally, “underoverdrink”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underbake, not to bake enough</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not drink completely</li><li>to not drink enough, to not drink until satisfied</li></ol><p><i>недопи́ть</i> (nedopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not drink completely</li><li>to not drink enough, to not drink until satisfied</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>пи́ть</i> (pítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underpay (to pay someone less than the value of their work&#59; to pay someone insufficiently)</li><li>to underpay (to pay less than is due for something)</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underpay (to pay someone less than the value of their work&#59; to pay someone insufficiently)</li><li>to underpay (to pay less than is due for something)</li></ol><p><i>недоплати́ть</i> (nedoplatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misunderstanding, misapprehension</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inadmissibility, impermissibility</li></ol><p><i>недопусти́мый</i> (nedopustímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inadmissible, intolerable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>допусти́мый</i> (dopustímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-admission, barring, banning</li><li>preventing, stopping (from happening)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) imperfection, shortcoming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misunderstanding, misapprehension, misconception</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cheaply, inexpensively</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexpensive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not full-grown</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>ро́слый</i> (róslyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) adolescent nobleman who has not entered the civil service (<i>in Russia in the 18th century</i>)</li><li>(ironic,&#32;derogatory) uneducated, stupid young man</li></ol><p>From <i>недоро́слый</i> (nedoróslyj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), with stress shift typical of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understatement, vagueness, ambiguity, innuendo, things left unsaid</li></ol><p><i>недоска́занный</i> (nedoskázannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left unsaid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unsaid, to not say a lot (of), to not say everything (about)</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unsaid, to not say a lot (of), to not say everything (about)</li></ol><p><i>недосказа́ть</i> (nedoskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to in part / inattentively</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to something incompletely or not to the end</li></ol><p><i>недослу́шать</i> (nedoslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inattention</li><li>oversight, mistake</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>недосмотре́ть</i> (nedosmotrétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overlook</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail to look after [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to lack, to be needed</li><li>to miss</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>достава́ть</i> (dostavátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack, deficiency, deficit, shortage (an insufficient quantity)</li><li>defect, drawback, flaw, fault (an unwanted quality)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nedostatъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insufficiently (not sufficiently)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insufficiency, shortage, lack, inadequacy</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) insufficiency</li></ol><p><i>недоста́точный</i> (nedostátočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insufficient</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>доста́точный</i> (dostátočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lack, to be needed</li><li>to miss</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>доста́ть</i> (dostátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shortage</li></ol><p><i>недоста́ть</i> (nedostátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ча</i> (-ča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>missing, lacking, failing, deficient, short</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unachievable, unattainable, inaccessible, unreachable</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>достижи́мый</i> (dostižímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreliable, untrustworthy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>достове́рный</i> (dostovérnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unworthy <i>(of)</i></li><li>unworthy, disreputable, dishonourable/dishonorable, contemptible, unbecoming</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>досто́йный</i> (dostójnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfinished (<i>of a building</i>)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>достро́енный</i> (dostrójennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) unfinished construction</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>строй</i> (stroj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inaccessibility</li><li>unavailability</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) incomprehensibility</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) prohibitiveness (<i>of price</i>)</li></ol><p><i>недосту́пный</i> (nedostúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inaccessible, unavailable</li><li>prohibitive (of price)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lack of free time, being too busy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>досу́г</i> (dosúg)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not finish counting (something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss, to be short <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>недосчита́ть</i> (nedosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not finish counting (something)</li></ol><p><i>недосчита́ть</i> (nedosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss, to be short of</li></ol><p><i>недосчи́тывать</i> (nedosčítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>not having slept enough, lack of sleep</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>досыпа́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (dosypátʹ, “to sleep to end”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inaccessible, unattainable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>досяга́емый</i> (dosjagájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) touchy person, touch-me-not</li><li>jewelweed, touch-me-not (<i>Impatiens</i> gen. et spp.)</li><li>sensitive plant, touch-me-not (<i>Mimosa pudica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>не</i> (ne, “not”) +&lrm; <i>дотро́г-</i> (dotróg-, “touch”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a). The botany term is a calque of New Latin <i>Impatiens noli-tangere</i>; cf. не-тронь-меня́ (ne-tronʹ-menjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) duffer, wuss, klutz, slob</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) rube, simpleton</li></ol><p>From <i>недо-</i> (nedo-) + a form possibly derived from dialectal <i>тепти́</i> (teptí, “to beat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be at a loss, to be bewildered, to be perplexed</li></ol><p>From <i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>уме́ть</i> (umétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bewilderment, perplexity, quandary (a state of not knowing what to decide)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perplexedly, in perplexity, with a puzzled look</li></ol><p><i>недоуме́нный</i> (nedouménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perplexed, puzzled, confused</li></ol><p><i>недоуме́ние</i> (nedouménije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;swear word) idiot, imbecile, dolt</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>недоуме́нно</i> (nedouménno)</li></ol><p><i>недоумённый</i> (nedoumjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>недоуме́нный</i> (nedouménnyj)</li></ol><p><i>недоуме́ние</i> (nedouménije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not learn completely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not learn completely</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underfunding, underfinancing</li></ol><p><i>недофинанси́ровать</i> (nedofinansírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underfund, to underfinance</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-) +&lrm; <i>финанси́ровать</i> (finansírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortage</li><li>shortcoming, deficiency, flaw, mistake</li></ol><p>From <i>недо-</i> (nedo-) + a deverbal form of <i>честь</i> (čestʹ) (originally meaning "to count" as well as to "to read"; the meaning "to count" is now found in the compound <i>счесть</i> (sčestʹ)). For the vowel of <i>-чёт</i> (-čót), compare <i>счёт</i> (sčot, “count”), deverbal from <i>счесть</i> (sčestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) the state of not having (enough) sex</li></ol><p><i>недо-</i> (nedo-, “insufficiently”) +&lrm; <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ, “fuck”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) womb</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) bosom</li><li>subsoil, bowels of the earth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nědra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigilant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsoil usage</li></ol><p><i>не́дра</i> (nédra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзование</i> (pólʹzovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mineral rights holder, subsoil user</li></ol><p><i>не́дра</i> (nédra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзователь</i> (pólʹzovatelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;humorous) No Nut November (Internet challenge in which participants abstain from all forms of intentional nutting (ejaculation) for the whole month of November)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дрочить</i> (dročitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-абрь</i> (-abrʹ), the latter being a suffix found in the names of the last four months in Russian i.e. <i>сентябрь</i> (sentjabrʹ), <i>октябрь</i> (oktjabrʹ), <i>ноябрь</i> (nojabrʹ) and <i>декабрь</i> (dekabrʹ). Partial calque of English <i>No Nut November</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, foe</li></ol><p><i>не́-</i> (né-) +&lrm; <i>друг</i> (drug)</p><p>For the word formation compare Latin <i>inimīcus</i> (<i>in-</i> + <i>amīcus</i>) whence <i>enemy</i>. Also compare <i>неприя́тель</i> (neprijátelʹ) (<i>не-</i> (ne-) + <i>прия́тель</i> (prijátelʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfriendly, hostile</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>дру́жественный</i> (drúžestvennyj, “friendly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) disease, illness, malady (an abnormal condition of the body causing discomfort or dysfunction)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nedǫgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not bad, pretty good, good enough</li><li>attractive, pretty</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outstanding, remarkable, extraordinary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>дю́жинный</i> (djúžinnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) nonunique, ambiguous</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>еди́нственный</i> (jedínstvennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unnaturally</li><li>affectedly</li></ol><p><i>неесте́ственный</i> (nejestéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affectation, eccentricity, histrionics, pretense, tension, theatricality, strangeness</li></ol><p><i>неесте́ственный</i> (nejestéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnatural</li><li>affected</li><li>unusual, uncommon</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unexpectedly, out of the blue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unexpected</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жда(ть)</i> (žda(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-нный</i> (-nnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something unexpected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unwillingness, reluctance, disinclination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undesirable, unwanted, objectionable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жела́тельный</i> (želátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) than</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single (not married)</li><li>celibate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) unattached, associated with the single life (as of a bachelor)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жена́тый</i> (ženátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milksop, sissy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifeless</li><li>inanimate, inorganic</li><li>dull, lifeless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>живо́й</i> (živój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninhabited</li><li>uninhabitable, untenantable</li><li>nonresidential</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жило́й</i> (žilój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low-fat</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жи́рный</i> (žírnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pamper</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;Slavic mythology) evil or dangerous spirits</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;fantasy) undead</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the appearance of being uninhabited or abandoned</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;něžiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не́га</i> (néga) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bask</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lounge around, to luxuriate in being inactive</li></ol><p><i>не́жить</i> (néžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>не́жный</i> (néžnyj): gentlest, tenderest, most delicate; fondest</li></ol><p><i>не́жный</i> (néžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be gentle/tender (with someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act excessively gentle/tender</li></ol><p><i>не́жный</i> (néžnyj, “gentle, tender”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dearly, fondly, gently</li></ol><p><i>не́жный</i> (néžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenderness, caress, fondness, delicacy</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) kind words, endearments</li></ol><p>From <i>не́жный</i> (néžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, tender</li><li>fond [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <i>or</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>delicate, soft, fine</li><li>gentle, delicate, tender (sensitive or painful)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;něžьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не́га</i> (néga, “luxury, comfort, prosperity”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unforgettable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) forget-me-not</li></ol><p>First attested in 1778. From <i>не</i> (ne, “don't”) +&lrm; <i>забу́дь</i> (zabúdʹ, “forget”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), an all-European calque of Old French <i>ne m'oubliez mie</i> or German <i>Vergissmeinnicht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unforgettable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incompleteness</li></ol><p><i>незавершённый</i> (nezaveršónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incomplete, uncompleted, unfinished</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>завершённый</i> (zaveršónnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>незаве́ршений</i> (nezavéršenyj), Belarusian <i>незаве́ршаны</i> (njezavjéršany), Bulgarian <i>незавършен</i> (nezavǎršen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unenviable</li><li>reprehensible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зави́дный</i> (zavídnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>independently (in an independent manner)</li><li>regardless</li></ol><p><i>незави́симый</i> (nezavísimyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>independence</li></ol><p><i>незави́симый</i> (nezavísimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>independent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зави́симый</i> (zavísimyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bad luck</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зада́ча</i> (zadáča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unlucky</li></ol><p><i>незада́ча</i> (nezadáča) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shortly before; not long (before); a short time previously</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>задо́лго</i> (zadólgo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>illegally, unlawfully (in a manner contrary to the law)</li></ol><p><i>незако́нный</i> (nezakónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illegitimate, out of wedlock (of children)</li></ol><p><i>незако́нно</i> (nezakónno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ро́жденный</i> (róždennyj), obsolete form of <i>рождённый</i> (roždjónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illegality</li></ol><p><i>незако́нный</i> (nezakónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlawful, illegal</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зако́нный</i> (zakónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfinished</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зако́нченный</i> (zakónčennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>independent (used in a satirical and derogatory context in relation to Ukraine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>незале́жний</i> (nezaléžnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, without delay, promptly, forthwith</li></ol><p><i>незамедли́тельный</i> (nezamedlítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immediate</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>заме́длить</i> (zamédlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreplaceable</li><li>indispensable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>imperceptibly</li></ol><p><i>незаме́тный</i> (nezamétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconspicuous, invisible, undetectable, unnoticeable</li><li>plain, ordinary, insignificant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>заме́тный</i> (zamétnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnoticed, unseen</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>заме́ченный</i> (zaméčennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only of women</i>) single, celibate, unmarried (not married)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, uncomplicated</li><li>primitive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>замыслова́тый</i> (zamyslovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immemorial, very distant in time</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unforgettable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstained, stainless, spotless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unfairly</li></ol><p><i>незаслу́женный</i> (nezaslúžennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfair</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>заслу́женный</i> (zaslúžennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, unpretentious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зате́йливый</i> (zatéjlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outstanding, extraordinary, out of the common</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зауря́дный</i> (zaurjádnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there is no need (to), there is no use (to)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>заче́м</i> (začém)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insecurity, unprotectedness</li></ol><p><i>незащищённый</i> (nezaščiščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprotected, insecure, undefended</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>защищённый</i> (zaščiščónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbidden, uninvited</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зва́ный</i> (zványj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) not of these parts, not local</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) otherworldly, supernatural, mysterious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зде́шний</i> (zdéšnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for one to not feel or be well, for one to be under the weather [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>нездоро́вый</i> (nezdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sickly, unhealthy</li><li>unwholesome</li><li>morbid</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unhealthiness, ill health</li><li>illness, ailment</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>здоро́вье</i> (zdoróvʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extraterrestrial</li><li>unearthly, supernatural, heavenly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>земно́й</i> (zemnój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) gentle, mild</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignoramus</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>знай</i> (znaj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Незнайка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neznaika, Neznayka (<i>a character in the Russian children's literature and the protagonist of an animated film</i>), Dunno, Know-Nothing (<i>English translations</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>незнайка</i> (neznajka, “ignoramus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger, alien (person, etc. from outside)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>знако́мец</i> (znakómec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown (to), unfamiliar (to)</li><li>unfamiliar (with), unacquainted (with)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance, lack of knowledge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not noble; ignoble</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зна́тный</i> (znátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insignificantly (of such extremely small quantity or degree that it is not worth measuring)</li></ol><p><i>незначи́тельный</i> (neznačítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insignificant, unimportant, negligible, minor, marginal</li><li>small, slight, little</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>значи́тельный</i> (značítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unripe</li><li>immature</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зре́лый</i> (zrélyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зри́мый</i> (zrímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind, eyeless (unable to see)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зря́чий</i> (zrjáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmness, solidity, immutability</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unshakability, steadfastness</li></ol><p><i>незы́блемый</i> (nezýblemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, stable, unshakeable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>зы́блемый</i> (zýblemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inevitably</li></ol><p><i>неизбе́жный</i> (neizbéžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inevitability</li></ol><p><i>неизбе́жный</i> (neizbéžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inevitable, unavoidable, inescapable</li><li>usual, commonly used</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) impossible to get rid of, indestructible, unquenchable, indelible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>избыва́ть</i> (izbyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>неизбе́жный</i> (neizbéžnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unexplored, unknown, unfamiliar</li><li>unresearched, not investigated</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>изве́данный</i> (izvédannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertainty</li><li>obscurity</li></ol><p><i>неизве́стный</i> (neizvéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indelible</li><li>unforgettable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>изгла́дить</i> (izgláditʹ, “to erase; to weaken (impressions, feelings)”) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incurable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>излечи́мый</i> (izlečímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>invariably</li></ol><p><i>неизме́нный</i> (neizménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invariability, immutability</li></ol><p><i>неизме́нный</i> (neizménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invariable, permanent, immutable</li><li>true, unfailing, devoted</li><li>customary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invariable, unalterable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>изменя́емый</i> (izmenjájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immeasurably</li></ol><p><i>неизмери́мый</i> (neizmerímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immeasurable</li><li>immense, huge</li><li><i>(depth)</i> unfathomable, fathomless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>измери́мый</i> (izmerímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ineffable, inexpressible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ineffable, inexpressible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexplicable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence, lack, want</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incredibly, unbelievably</li></ol><p><i>неимове́рный</i> (neimovérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incredible</li><li>enormous</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare Old Church Slavonic <i>ѩти вѣрѫ</i> (jęti věrǫ, “to believe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, indigent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>иму́щий</i> (imúščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) noninvasive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>инвази́вный</i> (invazívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonintensive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>интенси́вный</i> (intensívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uninterestingly, without interest</li></ol><p><i>неинтере́сный</i> (neinterésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninteresting, boring</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) ugly, unattractive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>интере́сный</i> (interésnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insincerely</li></ol><p><i>неи́скренний</i> (neískrennij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insincere, hypocritical</li><li>feigned, not genuine</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>и́скренний</i> (ískrennij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insincerity, hypocrisy</li><li>lack of genuineness, falseness (<i>referring to a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>неи́скренний</i> (neískrennij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonfulfilment; failure to execute (especially a military order)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncorrectable, incorrigible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>исправи́мый</i> (ispravímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure, malfunction, defect, disrepair</li></ol><p><i>неиспра́вный</i> (neisprávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not working, broken, out of order, defective</li><li>careless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>испра́вный</i> (isprávnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>never running dry (<i>of a spring or well</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) inexhaustible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>иссяка́ть</i> (issjakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>furiously, violently</li></ol><p><i>неи́стовый</i> (neístovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fury, rage, craze, frenzy</li></ol><p><i>неи́стовый</i> (neístovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, to rave, to storm</li></ol><p><i>неи́стовство</i> (neístovstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>furious, violent, frantic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>и́стовый</i> (ístovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexhaustible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indestructible, ineradicable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>истреби́ть</i> (istrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexhaustible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>исче́рпать</i> (isčérpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) incalculable, innumerable, uncountably large</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) uncountable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>исчисли́мый</i> (isčislímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncountable noun (mass noun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncountable (about a noun which cannot be counted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nylon (substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nylon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nylon</li></ol><p><i>нейло́н</i> (nejlón, nɛjlón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) about the inability to resist something, to calm down, to calm down&#59; cannot wait [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘who’</small>]</li></ol><p>third-person singular present indicative imperfective of <i>неня́ться</i> (nenjátʹsja) (<i>archaic</i>), from <i>неня́ть</i> (nenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>neuro-</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron, “nerve”). Doublet of <i>невро-</i> (nevro-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) neurobionic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neurology,&#32;pathology) neurodegenerative (of, pertaining to, or resulting in the progressive loss of nerve cells and of neurologic function)</li></ol><p>From <i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>дегенерати́вный</i> (degeneratívnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>нейродегенерати́вний</i> (nejrodeheneratývnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neuroleptic, neuroplegic, antipsychotic (any of a group of drugs used to treat psychosis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neuron</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron, “nerve”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(artificial intelligence,&#32;anatomy) neural network</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>neural network</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neuron; neuronal, neural</li></ol><p><i>нейро́н</i> (nejrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurodivergence</li></ol><p><i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>отли́чие</i> (otlíčije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neurodivergent</li></ol><p><i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>отли́чный</i> (otlíčnyj) or <i>нейроотли́чие</i> (nejrootlíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neurodiversity</li></ol><p><i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>разнообра́зие</i> (raznoobrázije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neurodivergent</li></ol><p><i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>разнообра́зный</i> (raznoobráznyj) or <i>нейроразнообра́зие</i> (nejroraznoobrázije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neural network</li></ol><p><i>нейросе́т(ь)</i> (nejrosét(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neural network</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) neurosurgeon</li></ol><p><i>нейро-</i> (nejro-) +&lrm; <i>хиру́рг</i> (xirúrg)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neutralization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neutralize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>neutraliser</i> + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neutrality</li></ol><p>From German <i>Neutralität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neutral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neutrino</li></ol><p><i>нейтри́но</i> (nejtríno) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neutrino</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neutron (subatomic particle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) neutron star (degenerate star that has been so collapsed by gravity that its electrons and protons have been merged into neutrons by the intense pressure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neutron; neutronal</li></ol><p><i>нейтро́н</i> (nejtrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) plain, homely, not (very) attractive, not much to look at</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>каз(а́ться)</i> (kaz(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-capitalist</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>капиталисти́ческий</i> (kapitalistíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say "no" or "not" often</li><li>to not care</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>substandard, low-quality</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ка́чественный</i> (káčestvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>a certain, certain</li><li>some</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the old days, in former times, formerly, once</li></ol><p>From <i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>когда́</i> (kogdá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-commercial</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>комме́рческий</i> (kommérčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;computing) noncommutative</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>коммутати́вный</i> (kommutatívnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incompetence</li></ol><p><i>некомпете́нтный</i> (nekompeténtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incompetent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>out of control, uncontrolled, uncontrollable</li><li>unmonitored</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsistent, fallacious, faulty</li><li>improper, inappropriate, impolite</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>корре́ктный</i> (korréktnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some; certain (<i>adjectival</i>)</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) some, some people; certain people (<i>pronominal</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after a fashion, in a way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unattractively</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) improperly</li></ol><p><i>некраси́вый</i> (nekrasívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbeautifulness</li></ol><p><i>некраси́в(ый)</i> (nekrasív(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ugly, (<i>Canada, US</i>) homely</li><li>dissonant, unpleasant to the ear</li><li><i>(behaviour)</i> mean, ugly, not nice</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>краси́вый</i> (krasívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rather weak, not strong</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necrosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obituary</li><li>necrology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necropolis (large cemetery)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>νεκρόπολις</i> (nekrópolis, “city of the dead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necrophiliac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necrophilia (pathological attraction to dead bodies)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inopportunely, malapropos, at the wrong moment</li><li>irrelevantly, out of place, ineptly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>кста́ти</i> (kstáti)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nectar</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>νέκταρ</i> (néktar, “nourishment of the gods”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néḱtr̥h₂</i>, derived from the roots <i>&#42;neḱ-</i> (“to perish, disappear”) and <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“to overcome”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nectarine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nectary</li></ol><p><i>некта́р</i> (nektár) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nectarlike</li></ol><p><i>некта́р</i> (nektár) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>indefinite pronoun, somebody, someone</li><li>a certain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(proscribed,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>не́который</i> (nékotoryj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncultured</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>культу́рный</i> (kulʹtúrnyj, “cultured, cultivated, civilized”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amiss (wrong)</li></ol><p><i>нела́дный</i> (neládnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something bad or wrong; grief; trouble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wrong, bad</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дный</i> (ládnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disagreements, inability to get along</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лады́</i> (ladý)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfriendly, untender</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ла́сковый</i> (láskovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) illegal alien, illegal immigrant (person within a state without authorization)</li><li>(<i>espionage</i>) illegal (undercover spy)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>нелега́льный</i> (nelegálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>illegally (in a manner contrary to the law)</li></ol><p><i>нелега́льный</i> (nelegálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illegal (contrary to or forbidden by law)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лега́льный</i> (legálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not easily, with difficulty</li></ol><p><i>нелёгкий</i> (neljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absurdity, ridiculousness, nonsense, rubbish</li></ol><p><i>неле́пый</i> (nelépyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ridiculously, absurdly</li></ol><p><i>неле́пый</i> (nelépyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absurdity</li></ol><p><i>неле́пый</i> (nelépyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absurd, odd, ridiculous</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ле́пый</i> (lépyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flightless (unable to fly)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ющий</i> (letájuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonlinear</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лине́йный</i> (linéjnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frank, sincere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) (<i>archaic</i>) impartial, unbiased, unprejudiced</li><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) unpleasant, offensive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лицеприя́тный</i> (liceprijátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not useless, needed, necessary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ли́шний</i> (líšnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awkward, clumsy</li><li>uncomfortable</li><li>inconvenient, embarrassing</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ловкий</i> (lovkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>awkwardly</li><li>heavy-handedly</li></ol><p><i>нело́вкий</i> (nelóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awkwardness</li><li>clumsiness</li><li>discomfort (especially emotional)</li></ol><p><i>нело́вкий</i> (nelóvkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illogicality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, truthful, proper (moral excellence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nelma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nelma</li></ol><p><i>не́льма</i> (nélʹma) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disobligingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unloved</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>люби́мый</i> (ljubímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dislike (for)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsociable, reclusive, withdrawn, distant</li><li>empty of people, deserted</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) beast, monster, villain, a very cruel person</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi, “people”). Compare Ukrainian <i>не́люд</i> (néljud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil spirit, the devil, evil forces, especially in expressions with <i>занести́</i> (zanestí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult, not easy, hard</li><li>heavy, not light</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>неме́цкий</i> (neméckij): Germ., German</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) there is no, there are no [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘possessed by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>not have</i> with the object of <i>у</i> (u) as the subject)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonmagic</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>маги́ческий</i> (magíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quite a bit, a great deal</li><li>considerably</li></ol><p><i>нема́лый</i> (nemályj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of no small importance/account</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>малова́жный</i> (malovážnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rather large or big, considerable, sizeable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intangible, immaterial, nonmaterial</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) intangible, noneconomic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>материа́льный</i> (materiálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, forthwith</li></ol><p><i>неме́дленный</i> (nemédlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immediate</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ме́дленный</i> (médlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once, immediately</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne, “not”) + <i>ме́для</i> (médlja, “lingering; being slow; delaying”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) nemesia (<i>Nemesia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Nemesia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very much, loads, immeasurably much</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ме́ренный</i> (mérennyj, “measured”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) proboscis worm, ribbon worm (Nemertea sp.)</li></ol><p>Likely from the obsolete taxon of translingual <i>Nemertini</i>, now synonymous with the phylum Nemertea.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German customs and culture</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) foreign land, country, or government</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>Німеччина</i> (Nimeččyna).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Неметчина)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Germany (a country in Central Europe)</li></ol><p>From <i>немецкий</i> (nemeckij, “German”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina). Compare Ukrainian <i>Німеччина</i> (Nimeččyna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dumb, to mute</li><li>to become numb</li></ol><p><i>немо́й</i> (nemój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German, German man</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) non-Slavic European foreigner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нѣ́мьць</i> (ně́mĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němьcь</i> (“foreigner, German”), from <i>&#42;němъ</i> (“mute”) (whence Russian <i>немо́й</i> (nemój)).</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros), whence <i>ва́рвар</i> (várvar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German Shepherd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>German</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German (the German language)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němьčьskъ</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не́мец</i> (némec) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij), from <i>немо́й</i> (nemój, “mute”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Dane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) dislike of a superior for a subordinate</li><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) disfavor, disgrace</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ми́лость</i> (mílostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inevitably, unavoidably</li></ol><p><i>немину́емый</i> (neminújemyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inevitable, imminent, impending, unavoidable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>мину́-</i> (minú-) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female teacher of German language</li></ol><p><i>не́м(ец)</i> (ném(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>few, a few, some, several</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few, several (more than one, but not as many as usual or as expected)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a little, some</li></ol><p>From <i>не</i> (ne, “not”) +&lrm; <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo, “much”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little, not much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terse, laconic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>многосло́вный</i> (mnogoslóvnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not numerous, few</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>многочи́сленный</i> (mnogočíslennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>немно́го</i> (nemnógo, “a little bit”)</li></ol><p><i>немно́го</i> (nemnógo) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>немно́го</i> (nemnógo): a little, some [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>немно́го</i> (nemnógo) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dumb, mute</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) dumb (unable to speak) person, mute person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němъ</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>нѣмъ</i> (němŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elderly, not young</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muteness, dumbness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němota</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) malaise, malady, illness, feebleness (especially of old or sick people), sickness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infirm, sick, feeble, sickly</li></ol><p><i>не́мощь</i> (némoščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malaise, malady, illness, feebleness (especially of old or sick people), sickness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>немощь</i> (nemoštĭ). Doublet of <i>не́мочь</i> (némočʹ), the inherited form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Fritzes, Krauts (Germans)</li><li>(singular only,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Fritz, a Kraut (a German)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>не́мец</i> (némec, “a German”). Compare <i>детвора́</i> (detvorá) and <i>мошкара́</i> (moškará) for the unusual stress pattern on the suffix. Compare Ukrainian <i>німчура́</i> (nimčurá), Czech <i>Němčour</i>, Polish <i>niemczura</i>, synonymous and cognate with the Russian term.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstandard spelling of <i>немшённый</i> (nemšónnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmossed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unthinkably</li></ol><p><i>немы́слимый</i> (nemýslimyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unthinkable, inconceivable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwashed</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>мы́тый</i> (mýtyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate <i>(strong)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nenaviděti</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>ненавидѣти</i> (nenaviděti), Belarusian <i>ненаві́дзець</i> (njenavídzjecʹ), Ukrainian <i>нена́видіти</i> (nenávydity), Polish <i>nienawidzić</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>nenávidjeti</i>, Czech <i>nenávidět</i>, Slovak <i>nenávidieť</i>, and Bulgarian <i>ненави́ждам</i> (nenavíždam).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>презира́ть</i> (prezirátʹ), Latin <i>dēspiciō</i>.</p><p>Also compare <i>зави́довать</i> (zavídovatʹ), <i>оби́деть</i> (obídetʹ) (cognate via Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;viděti</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hater (someone who hates)</li></ol><p><i>не́нависть</i> (nénavistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hated, odious</li><li>hateful</li></ol><p><i>не́нависть</i> (nénavistʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred, hate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ненависть</i> (nenavistĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nenavistь</i>, from <i>&#42;nenaviděti</i> + <i>&#42;-tь</i>, from <i>&#42;ne</i> (“not”) + <i>&#42;naviděti</i>, from <i>&#42;na</i> (“on(to), in(to)”) + <i>&#42;viděti</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unobtrusive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>навя́зчивый</i> (navjázčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very cute, loveable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>very cute person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improper, wrong</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>надлежа́щий</i> (nadležáščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uselessness</li></ol><p><i>нена́добный</i> (nenádobnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unnecessary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>на́добный</i> (nádobnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a short while, not for long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreliable, insecure</li><li><i>(person)</i> untrustworthy, unreliable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unintentionally, accidentally, unknowingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inadvertently, accidentally, by accident, unintentionally</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;narokъ</i> (analysable as <i>не-</i> (ne-) + <i>на-</i> (na-) + <i>рок</i> (rok, “fate”) + <i>-ом</i> (-om, adverbial suffix)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weather</i>) rainy, inclement (implying foul or bad situation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fake, artificial, not real</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>настоя́щий</i> (nastojáščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad weather (unpleasant or destructive atmospheric conditions)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, related to наст (nast).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insatiable, gluttonous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>насы́т(ить)</i> (nasýt(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificiality</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>натура́льн(ый)</i> (naturálʹn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnatural, artificial, affected</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>натура́льный</i> (naturálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nenets</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tundra Nenets <i>ненэцьʼ</i> (nyenecʹ). Displaced <i>самоед</i> (samojed) in the 20th century as this term was considered derogatory.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nenets</li></ol><p><i>не́нец</i> (nénec) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abnormally (in an abnormal manner)</li></ol><p><i>ненорма́льный</i> (nenormálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abnormality, irregularity</li><li>insanity</li></ol><p><i>ненорма́льный</i> (nenormálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abnormal (not conforming to rule or system)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) not in one's right mind, crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-normative, nonstandard</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>нормати́вный</i> (normatívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uselessness</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) useless thing</li></ol><p><i>нену́жный</i> (nenúžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>needless, unnecessary, unneeded (not needed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterlily</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nénufar</i>, from Arabic نِلُوفَر (nilūfar), نِينُوفَر (nīnūfar), from Middle Persian <i>nylw(k)pl</i> (nīlōpal, “lotus, water-lily”), from Sanskrit <i>नीलोत्पल</i> (nīlotpala, “blue lotus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to not take</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>neo- (new)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rash, hasty</li><li>reckless, thoughtless, haphazard</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обду́манный</i> (obdúmannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninhabited</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обита́емый</i> (obitájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desert island (an uninhabited island)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vast, endless, boundless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обозри́мый</i> (obozrímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baseless (based on something that is not true)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprocessed (not processed), unwrought</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneducated, illiterate</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>образо́ванный</i> (obrazóvannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irreversibility</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) nonconvertibility</li></ol><p><i>необрати́мый</i> (neobratímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreversible</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) inconvertible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обрати́мый</i> (obratímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Neo-Buddhism</li></ol><p><i>нео-</i> (neo-, “new”) +&lrm; <i>будди́зм</i> (buddízm, “Buddhism”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbridled, ungovernable, uncontrollable, unrestrained (free from constraint, uninhibited)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обу́зданный</i> (obúzdannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>necessarily, obligatorily</li></ol><p><i>необходи́мый</i> (neobxodímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necessity, need</li></ol><p><i>необходи́мый</i> (neobxodímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indispensable</li><li>necessary, required</li><li>inevitable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обходимый</i> (obxodimyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsociable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>общи́тельный</i> (obščítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexplicable, inscrutable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>объясни́мый</i> (obʺjasnímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immense, vast, boundless</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>объя́т-</i> (obʺját-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unusually, uncommonly</li></ol><p><i>необыкнове́нный</i> (neobyknovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual, uncommon, extraordinary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exceptionally, extraordinarily, rarely, unusually</li><li>surprisingly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) very, extremely</li></ol><p><i>необыча́йный</i> (neobyčájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extraordinary, exceptional, unusual</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обы́чай</i> (obýčaj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unusually</li></ol><p><i>необы́чный</i> (neobýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singularity, queerness, unusualness</li></ol><p><i>необы́чный</i> (neobýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual, uncommon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>optionally</li><li>unnecessarily</li><li>unobligingly</li></ol><p><i>необяза́тельный</i> (neobjazátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optionalness, optionality, non-necessity</li><li>condition of not fulfilling one's promises</li></ol><p><i>необяза́тельный</i> (neobjazátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not obligatory, optional</li><li><i>(person)</i> unobliging, unreliable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>обяза́тельный</i> (objazátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>limitless, unbounded, unlimited, unrestricted</li><li>absolute (in terms of authority)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neodymium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neodymium</li></ol><p><i>неоди́м</i> (neodím) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>different, distinct, not the same</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>одина́ковый</i> (odinákovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ambiguously (in an ambiguous manner)</li></ol><p><i>неоднозна́чный</i> (neodnoznáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambiguity</li><li>ambivalence (coexistence of opposing attitudes)</li></ol><p><i>неоднозна́чный</i> (neodnoznáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) ambiguous, multiple-value</li><li>ambiguous</li><li>varied, diverse, mixed</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>однозна́чный</i> (odnoznáčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>repeatedly, time and again, more than once</li></ol><p><i>неоднокра́тный</i> (neodnokrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>repeated, reiterated</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>однокра́тный</i> (odnokrátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heterogeneity, dissimilarity</li></ol><p><i>неодноро́дный</i> (neodnoródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterogeneous</li><li>dissimilar</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>одноро́дный</i> (odnoródnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disapprobation, disapproval, animadversion (criticism, a critical remark)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-, dis-”) +&lrm; <i>одобре́ние</i> (odobrénije, “approval”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disapprovingly (in a disapproving manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unapproving, disapproving, disapprobatory</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>одобри́тельный</i> (odobrítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconquerable, invincible</li><li>irresistible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>одоле́ть</i> (odolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) inanimacy</li></ol><p><i>неодушевлённый</i> (neoduševljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inanimate</li><li>inanimate (referring to the form in the accusative of a noun denoting nonliving things, or of an adjective that modifies it)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>одушевлённый</i> (oduševljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neo-eugenics</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>евге́ника</i> (jevgénika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly, unexpectedly</li></ol><p><i>неожи́данный</i> (neožídannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surprise</li></ol><p><i>неожи́данный</i> (neožídannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sudden, unexpected (happening quickly and with little or no warning)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ожида́ть</i> (ožidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нный</i> (-nnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neoclassicism</li></ol><p>From <i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>классици́зм</i> (klassicízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) neoconservatism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>neoconservatism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfinished, incomplete</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>око́нченный</i> (okónčennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) neoliberal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modernism, neologism, neology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) neomorpheme, new morpheme</li></ol><p><i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>морфе́ма</i> (morféma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>νέον</i> (néon), neuter of <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neo-Nazism (the ideology held by neo-Nazis)</li></ol><p><i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>наци́зм</i> (nacízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) neo-Nazi</li></ol><p><i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>наци́ст</i> (nacíst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;politics) neo-Nazi</li></ol><p><i>неонаци́ст</i> (neonacíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;technology) neon</li></ol><p><i>нео́н</i> (neón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ineffable (beyond expression)</li><li>indescribable, unutterable</li><li>nondescript</li><li>beyond description</li><li>inexpressible</li><li>without a name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonpayment</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пла́та</i> (pláta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unidentified</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>опо́знанный</i> (opóznannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unidentified flying object</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unjustified, unfounded</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>опра́вданный</i> (oprávdannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) infinitive (verb form)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indefinitely, uncertainly, vaguely</li></ol><p><i>неопределённый</i> (neopredeljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indefinite pronoun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abeyance</li><li>uncertainty, incertitude, doubtfulness, ambiguity, indeterminacy, indetermination, vagueness</li><li>suspense</li></ol><p><i>неопределённый</i> (neopredeljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indefinite, uncertain, vague</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indefinite</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) infinitive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>определённый</i> (opredeljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) indefinite article</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incontrovertibly; unassailably</li></ol><p><i>неопровержи́мый</i> (neoproveržímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indisputable, irrefutable, incontrovertible, undeniable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>опрове́рг(нуть)</i> (oprovérg(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untidy, slovenly, sloppy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>опря́тный</i> (oprjátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inexperience</li></ol><p><i>нео́пытный</i> (neópytnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexperienced, green</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>о́пытный</i> (ópytnyj, “experienced”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology) inorganic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>органи́ческий</i> (organíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extraordinary, unusual</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ордина́рный</i> (ordinárnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance, incompetence</li></ol><p><i>неосведомлённый</i> (neosvedomljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninformed, ignorant, not knowledgeable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>осведомлённый</i> (osvedomljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unthoroughness, baselessness</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>основа́тель</i> (osnovátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconscious, subconscious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>осо́знанный</i> (osóznannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>conclusively, incontestably, indisputably, incontrovertibly</li></ol><p><i>неоспори́мый</i> (neosporímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undeniable, indisputable, unquestionable, incontestable, irrefutable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>оспори́мый</i> (osporímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any grass of the genus <i>Neostapfia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Neostapfia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incautiously (without caution)</li></ol><p><i>неосторо́жный</i> (neostoróžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness; imprudence</li></ol><p><i>неосторо́жный</i> (neostoróžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, imprudent, ill-advised, unwary, indiscreet, incautious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impracticable, unrealizable, infeasible, unachievable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) impalpable, intangible, untouchable (not able to be touched)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) too small, insignificant, elusive</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>осяза́емый</i> (osjazájemyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inevitably, unavoidably, inescapably</li></ol><p><i>неотврати́мый</i> (neotvratímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inevitability</li></ol><p><i>неотврати́мый</i> (neotvratímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inevitable, unavoidable, inescapable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отврат(и́ть)</i> (otvrat(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inseparable, inalienable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отдели́мый</i> (otdelímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indistinguishable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отличи́ть</i> (otličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) emergency medical service</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ambulance</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>неотло́жная медици́нская по́мощь</i> (neotlóžnaja medicínskaja pómoščʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pressing, urgent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отложи́ть</i> (otložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Czech <i>naléhavý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irresistible, irrefutable, incontrovertible</li><li>irresistible, fascinating</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отрази́ть</i> (otrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uninterruptedly, continuously, without a break</li><li>inseparably</li></ol><p><i>неотры́вный</i> (neotrývnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous, uninterrupted</li><li>inseparable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отры́в</i> (otrýv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>relentlessly</li></ol><p><i>неотсту́пный</i> (neotstúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relentless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отступ(и́ть)</i> (otstup(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inalienable, integral</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>отыма́ть</i> (otymátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncouth, rough, unpolished, coarse, vulgar</li><li>rough, not squared</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) neofascism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neofascist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) neofascist</li></ol><p><i>неофаши́ст</i> (neofašíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neophyte (<i>in various senses</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>néophyte</i>, from Latin <i>neophytus</i>, from Koine Greek <i>νεόφυτος</i> (neóphutos, “newly planted, (figuratively) new convert”). Piecewise root cognate with <i>новоприбы́вший</i> (novopribývšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unofficial (not official)</li><li>informal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>listlessness, reluctance, unwillingness</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>охо́та</i> (oxóta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unwillingly (in an unwilling manner)</li></ol><p><i>неохо́тный</i> (neoxótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwilling</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>охо́тный</i> (oxótnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) inestimable, invaluable, priceless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Correctly, not erroneously or by mistake.</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>ошибочно</i> (ošibočno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neopaganism (reconstructions or revivals of pre-Christian religious beliefs)</li></ol><p><i>нео-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>язычество</i> (jazyčestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nepali</li></ol><p><i>Непа́л</i> (Nepál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpaired</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>па́рный</i> (párnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untranslatable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-имый</i> (-imyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intolerable, unbearable</li><li>nonportable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>переноси́мый</i> (perenosímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) intransitive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>перехо́дный</i> (perexódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intransitive verb (action verb not taking a direct object)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspoken (not formally articulated or stated)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unspoken rule, unwritten rule, oral law (rule that is understood but does not exist in written form)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(gamer slang) non-player character</li></ol><p>Reading of "NPC" (non-player character) as EnPeeSee, Эн-Пи-Си.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonpayer, defaulter</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonpayment, default</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>платёж</i> (platjóž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insolvency</li></ol><p><i>неплатёжеспосо́бный</i> (neplatjožesposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insolvent (unable to pay)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>платёжеспосо́бный</i> (platjožesposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not badly</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>пло́хо</i> (plóxo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, not bad (useful for a particular purpose)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invincibility</li></ol><p><i>непобеди́мый</i> (nepobedímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invincible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) unfavorable, unattractive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience, defiance, insubordination (refusal to obey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clumsy, awkward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sluggish</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поворо́тливый</i> (povorótlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniqueness</li></ol><p><i>неповтори́мый</i> (nepovtorímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unique, unmatched, inimitable</li><li>unrepeatable</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>повтори́мый</i> (povtorímyj, “repeatable”), present passive participle of <i>повтори́ть</i> (povtorítʹ, “to repeat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad weather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nepogoda</i>. Compare Czech <i>nepohoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infallibility</li></ol><p><i>непогреши́мый</i> (nepogrešímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infallible</li><li>impeccable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>погреши́ть</i> (pogrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearby, within easy reach</li><li>around here, about</li><li>thereabouts, thereabout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unyielding, refractory</li><li>stubborn, intractable, hard-mouthed</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пода́тливый</i> (podátlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>motionlessly (in a motionless manner)</li></ol><p><i>неподви́жный</i> (nepodvížnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immobility</li></ol><p><i>неподви́жный</i> (nepodvížnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motionless (at rest, not moving)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подви́жный</i> (podvížnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unpreparedness</li></ol><p><i>неподгото́вленный</i> (nepodgotóvlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprepared, untrained (not receiving the necessary training)</li><li>not well-educated</li><li>unprepared, nonprepared (not cooked in advance)</li><li>unready</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подгото́вленный</i> (podgotóvlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genuine, real, authentic</li><li>sincere, unfeigned</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подде́льный</i> (poddélʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsupport</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “non-”) +&lrm; <i>подде́ржка</i> (poddéržka, “support”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorruptible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подкупа́ть</i> (podkupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improper, inappropriate, unbecoming, unseemly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подоба́ющий</i> (podobájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unaccountability (freedom from being held accountable)</li></ol><p><i>неподотчётный</i> (nepodotčótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unaccountable (free from accountability or control)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подотчётный</i> (podotčótnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuitable (not suitable&#59; unfit&#59; inappropriate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience (refusal to obey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very heavy</li><li>difficult or impossible to implement</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>подъём</i> (podʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unacceptable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steadfastness, perseverance, firmness.</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>поколебать</i> (pokolebatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, steadfast</li><li>unflinching</li><li>imperturbable, inflexible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поколеба́ть</i> (pokolebátʹ, “to shaken”) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unruly</li><li>rebellious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поко́рный</i> (pokórnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defiance, pride, rebellion (the feeling of being defiant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncovered</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>покры́тый</i> (pokrýtyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defect, fault, failure, malfunction</li><li><i>(usu. plural)</i> disagreement, problems</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пола́д(ить)</i> (polád(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incompleteness, imperfection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badness, deficiency, inferiority</li></ol><p><i>неполноце́нный</i> (nepolnocénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective, inferior</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>полноце́нный</i> (polnocénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incomplete</li><li>short</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>excessively, exorbitantly, unreasonably</li></ol><p><i>непоме́рный</i> (nepomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excessive, exorbitant, unreasonable, disproportionate</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the one who does not remember his ancestry</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнящий</i> (pómnjaščij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Непомнящий)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Nepomnyashchiy or Nepomniachtchi</li></ol><p>From <i>непо́мнящий</i> (nepómnjaščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incomprehension, lack of understanding</li><li>misunderstanding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) misunderstanding</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поня́тный</i> (ponjátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unintelligibly, incomprehensibly</li><li>strangely, oddly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>поня́тно</i> (ponjátno, “understandably, comprehensibly”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unintelligible, incomprehensible, obscure</li><li>strange, odd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreparable, irremediable, irretrievable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поправи́мый</i> (popravímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpopular</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>популя́рный</i> (populjárnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chastity, immaculacy, purity</li></ol><p><i>непоро́чный</i> (neporóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vestal, chaste, pure</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>поро́чный</i> (poróčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrespectable, dishonest</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>поря́дочный</i> (porjádočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninitiated</li><li>ill-informed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone uninitiated or ill-informed</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>посвящённый</i> (posvjaščónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fidgety person, fidget</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сед-</i> (sed-) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fidgety, restless</li></ol><p><i>непосе́да</i> (neposéda) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>backbreaking, excessive, over-demanding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inconsistency, incoherence, illogicalness</li></ol><p><i>непосле́довательный</i> (neposlédovatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsistent, incoherent, illogical</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>после́довательный</i> (poslédovatelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience (refusal to obey)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disobedient, refractory, naughty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>directly (in a straightforward way)</li></ol><p><i>непосре́дственный</i> (neposrédstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spontaneity, ingenuousness, artlessness, sincerity</li></ol><p><i>непосре́дственный</i> (neposrédstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immediate, direct, first-hand</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>посре́дственный</i> (posrédstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incomprehensible (in sense as something fully unintelligible or unfathomable)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>постижи́мый</i> (postižímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fickle, flighty, capricious, changeable (quick to change one’s opinion or allegiance)</li><li>giddy</li><li>impermanent, inconstant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inconstancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nepotism</li></ol><p>From French <i>népotisme</i>, from Italian <i>nepotismo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissimilarity, unlikeness</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>похо́жий</i> (poxóžij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissimilar, unlike, different</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>похо́жий</i> (poxóžij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untouched</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untruth, lie</li><li>not true</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nepravьda</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>пра́вда</i> (právda, “truth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>improbably</li></ol><p><i>неправдоподо́бный</i> (nepravdopodóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improbable, unlikely, infeasible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>правдоподо́бный</i> (pravdopodóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>iniquitously, unjustly, unrighteously</li></ol><p><i>непра́ведный</i> (neprávednyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) iniquitous, unjust, unrighteous</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пра́ведный</i> (právednyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incorrectly, wrongly</li><li>irregularly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>пра́вильно</i> (právilʹno, “correctly”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incorrectness</li><li>abnormality, irregularity</li></ol><p><i>непра́вильный</i> (neprávilʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorrect, wrong</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) irregular</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вильный</i> (právilʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irregular verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-governmental</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прави́тельственный</i> (pravítelʹstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illegal</li><li>illegitimate</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>правоме́рный</i> (pravomérnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incompetent</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) unauthorized; without authority</li><li>(literary,&#32;law) invalid, preterlegal, unqualified, ineligible</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>правомо́чный</i> (pravomóčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of being not right, not fair, or not honest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unjust, unfair</li><li><i>(in the)</i> wrong</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsurpassed, peerless, second to none, matchless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>превзойдённый</i> (prevzojdjónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impartial, unbiased</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>предвзя́тый</i> (predvzjátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unforeseen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unintentionally (in an unintentional manner)</li></ol><p><i>непреднаме́ренный</i> (neprednamérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpremeditated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unpredictability (the quality of being unpredictable)</li></ol><p><i>непредсказу́емый</i> (nepredskazújemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpredictable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;law) unrepresentative</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unpresentable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impartial, unbiased</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>предубеждённый</i> (predubeždjónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpremeditated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unpresentable</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>презента́бельный</i> (prezentábelʹnyj, “beautiful”, dated, dialectal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steadfastness, firmness, unshakability</li><li>inflexibility</li></ol><p><i>непрекло́нный</i> (nepreklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncompromising</li><li>steadfast</li><li>inflexible, unbending</li><li>adamant, inexorable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incessant, continuous, uninterrupted</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прекраща́ющийся</i> (prekraščájuščijsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inviolable, invariable, immutable</li><li>incontestable, indisputable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without fail, certainly</li></ol><p><i>непреме́нный</i> (nepreménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indispensable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>непрѣмѣньнъ</i> (neprěměnĭnŭ). Compare inherited <i>переме́нный</i> (pereménnyj, “variable”), <i>переме́на</i> (pereména, “change”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irresistible</li><li>insuperable, insurmountable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>преодоли́мый</i> (preodolímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unquestionable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пререка́емый</i> (prerekájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incessantly (in a manner without pause or stop)</li></ol><p><i>непреры́вный</i> (neprerývnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continuity</li></ol><p>From <i>непреры́вный</i> (neprerývnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous, uninterrupted, unbroken</li><li>contiguous (connecting without a break)</li><li>ongoing, continued, continual</li><li>sustained</li><li>incessant, ceaseless, unceasing, perpetual</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прерыва́ть</i> (preryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) unceasingly, incessantly, ceaselessly</li></ol><p><i>непреста́нный</i> (neprestánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) ceaseless, unceasing, incessant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>преста́ть</i> (prestátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нный</i> (-ánnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal, everlasting, imperishable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>преходя́щий</i> (prexodjáščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untidy</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>при́бранный</i> (príbrannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfriendly, ungracious, impolite</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cheerless, dreary, bleak (e.g. environment)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>приве́тливый</i> (privétlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unattractive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>привлека́тельный</i> (privlekátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lack of habit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unusually, not habitually</li></ol><p><i>непривы́чный</i> (neprivýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual</li><li>unaccustomed</li><li>not accustomed to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unattached, unfastened, untethered, unbound, loose (not tightly fixed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsightly, unattractive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пригляд(е́ть)</i> (prigljad(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfit, unsuitable, ineligible, useless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>приго́дный</i> (prigódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprepared, untrained</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unacceptable (unsatisfactory&#59; not acceptable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untouchable (<i>vile or unpleasant to the touch</i>); outcast</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an untouchable; pariah; outcaste; Harijan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) restless, not knowing what to do</li><li>unsettled, homeless</li></ol><p>Has the form of <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;прика́янный</i> (&#42;prikájannyj), putative past passive participle of <i>&#42;прика́ять</i> (&#42;prikájatʹ); compare <i>ка́яться</i> (kájatʹsja, “to repent”), <i>окая́нный</i> (okajánnyj, “damned, cursed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inviolability</li></ol><p><i>неприкоснове́нный</i> (neprikosnovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untouchable, inviolable, immune, sacrosanct</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) reserve (kept for an exigency)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>неприкосновеньнъ</i> (neprikosnovenĭnŭ). Equivalent to <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прикосну́ться</i> (prikosnútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency reserve, emergency stock, emergency cache, reserve stock (a stock of food, medicine, fuel, ammunition, etc. reserved for use in an emergency)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfastened, unattached, loose (not tightly fixed)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прикреплённый</i> (prikrepljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overt, bold, uncovered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impropriety, rudeness, indecency</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прили́чие</i> (prilíčije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indecently (in an indecent manner)</li></ol><p><i>неприли́чный</i> (neprilíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indecent, improper, unbecoming, indecorous, unseemly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прили́чный</i> (prilíčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inapplicable, unsuitable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperceptible, inconspicuous</li><li>unremarkable, unobtrusive, ordinary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>приме́тный</i> (primétnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intransigence, implacability</li><li>irreconcilability</li></ol><p><i>непримири́мый</i> (neprimirímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreconcilable, unappeasable, implacable</li><li>incompatible, irreconcilable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>примири́ть</i> (primirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without embarrassment</li><li>naturally, spontaneously, without constraint</li></ol><p><i>непринуждённый</i> (neprinuždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effortlessness, ease</li><li>nonchalance</li></ol><p><i>непринуждённый</i> (neprinuždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconstrained</li><li>informal</li><li>unforced, unstrained</li><li>at ease (free of anxiety)</li><li>easy, free, natural, casual (speech, manners, etc.)</li><li>spontaneous, unstudied</li><li>unrestrained</li><li>unaffected</li><li>cavalier</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>принуждённый</i> (prinuždjónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscenity</li></ol><p><i>непристо́йный</i> (nepristójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscene, indecent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>присто́йный</i> (pristójnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inaccessible, unapproachable, forbidding</li><li>impregnable, unassailable</li><li>unapproachable, haughty</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>при́ступ</i> (prístup) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, unpretentious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прихотли́вый</i> (prixotlívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a hostile manner</li></ol><p><i>неприя́зненный</i> (neprijáznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile, inimical, unfriendly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прия́зненный</i> (prijáznennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enmity, hostility (hostile or unfriendly disposition)</li><li>aversion (fixed dislike)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) enemy, adversary</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;neprijateľь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>прия́тель</i> (prijátelʹ).</p><p>For the word formation compare Latin <i>inimīcus</i> (<i>in-</i> + <i>amīcus</i>), whence <i>enemy</i>. Also compare <i>не́друг</i> (nédrug) (<i>не-</i> (ne-) + <i>друг</i> (drug)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aversion (fixed dislike)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disagreeably, unpleasantly</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прия́тно</i> (prijátno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slovenliness, unpleasantness</li><li>trouble</li></ol><p><i>неприя́тный</i> (neprijátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disagreeable, unpleasant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прия́тный</i> (prijátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitch-black</li><li>impenetrable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прогляд(е́ть)</i> (progljad(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brief, short (<i>especially of duration</i>)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>продолжи́тельный</i> (prodolžítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thoughtlessly, ad hoc (on the spur of the moment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opaque (non-transparent)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прозра́чный</i> (prozráčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) unproductive, non-productive; wasteful, wasted</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>производи́тельный</i> (proizvodítelʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>involuntarily</li></ol><p><i>непроизво́льный</i> (neproizvólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>involuntary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>произво́льный</i> (proizvólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpronounceable</li><li>silent (pronunciation of letters)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waterproof, watertight</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>промока́ть</i> (promokátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impenetrable, impervious, impermeable, proof, tight</li><li>impenetrable, imperturbable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexcusable, unforgivable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прости́тельный</i> (prostítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty</li></ol><p><i>непросто́й</i> (neprostój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not simple, complex, complicated</li><li>not ordinary</li><li>not basic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>просто́й</i> (prostój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) non-resistance, laissez-faire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-professional, amateur</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>профессиона́л</i> (professionál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonprofessionalism, amateurism</li></ol><p><i>непрофессиона́ль(ный)</i> (neprofessionálʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-professional, amateur</li><li>amateurish, low-quality</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>профессиона́льный</i> (professionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impassable</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) utter, rank, hopeless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>проходи́мый</i> (proxodímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frailness, lack of strength/solidity, fragility</li><li>insecurity</li></ol><p><i>непро́чный</i> (nepróčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-durable</li><li>fragile</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>про́чный</i> (próčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninvited, unasked-for, uncalled-for, unwanted, unwarranted, unsolicited, unbidden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, not straight</li><li>roundabout</li><li>not straightforward, not candid, insincere</li><li>indirect, collateral</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neptunium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flighty, frivolous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inept, careless, foolish, stupid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) someone who is flighty or frivolous</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) dolt, idiot, simpleton</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>путёвый</i> (putjóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dustless, dust-free</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easy, cushy (<i>of work</i>)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пы́льный</i> (pýlʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-drinking, abstinent, teetotaling</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пью́щий</i> (pʹjúščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idle, out of service/work</li><li>unemployed, out of work</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тающий</i> (rabótajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-working</li><li>non-operational (hours, machinery, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́чий</i> (rabóčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inequality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a person, or an attitude or approach) caring, not indifferent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unevenly (in an unequal manner)</li></ol><p><i>неравноме́рный</i> (neravnomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unevenness</li></ol><p><i>неравноме́рный</i> (neravnomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneven</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>равноме́рный</i> (ravnomérnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unequal</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negligent, remiss, careless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ради́вый</i> (radívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mess; muddle; confusion; shambles; stir; tangle; SNAFU; muss</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разбер-</i> (razber-, “to sort out”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa), using the present-tense stem of <i>разобра́ться</i> (razobrátʹsja). Literally, “thing that can't be sorted out”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>illegibly, indecipherably</li><li>inarticulately</li><li>undiscerningly, in an undemanding manner</li><li>unscrupulously</li></ol><p><i>неразбо́рчивый</i> (nerazbórčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illegible, indecipherable</li><li>inarticulate</li><li>undemanding, undiscerning</li><li>unscrupulous, unprincipled</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разбо́рчивый</i> (razbórčivyj).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>illegible</i> (<<~ Latin <i>in-</i> + <i>legō</i> + <i>-ibilis</i> <+ Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undeveloped</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>развито́й</i> (razvitój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of development, underdevelopment</li></ol><p>From <i>неразвито́й</i> (nerazvitój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), with stress as in the short masculine singular form <i>нера́звит</i> (nerázvit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taciturn (untalkative, silent)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разгово́рчивый</i> (razgovórčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indiscernible, indistinguishable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inseparable, always together</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разлучи́ть</i> (razlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unexploded (ordnance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insoluble, unsolvable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разреши́мый</i> (razrešímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indestructible, ‘not to be destroyed’.</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>разрушить</i> (razrušitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-имый</i> (-imyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unbreakably</li><li>inseparably, inextricably</li></ol><p><i>неразры́вный</i> (nerazrývnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbreakable</li><li>indissoluble, inseparable, inextricable</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разры́в</i> (razrýv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or perhaps <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разры́вный</i> (razrývnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolishness, lack of wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>неразу́мный</i> (nerazúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwise, unintelligent</li><li>injudicious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>разу́мный</i> (razúmnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonproliferation</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>распростране́ние</i> (rasprostranénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) insoluble, indissoluble, nonsoluble</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>раствори́мый</i> (rastvorímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sluggish, slow, inefficient</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>расторопный</i> (rastoropnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nerve</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>nervus</i>, perhaps via German <i>Nerv</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make nervous, to unnerve</li><li>to irritate</li><li>to fidget</li><li>to get on someone's nerves</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nervous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>не́рвы</i> (nérvy) (<i>this pejorative form is used in plural only</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нерв</i> (nerv) +&lrm; <i>-и́шки</i> (-íški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nervous system</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be nervous, to feel nervous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nervously</li></ol><p><i>не́рвный</i> (nérvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nerve gas, nerve agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nervousness</li></ol><p><i>не́рвный</i> (nérvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nerve; nervous</li><li>nervous, easily excitable or provoked</li><li>nerve-wracking, creating a lot of worry</li></ol><p><i>нерв</i> (nerv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nervousness</li></ol><p><i>нерво́зный</i> (nervóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nervous</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>nervōsus</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stressful situation, nerve-wracking situation, severe hassle</li></ol><p><i>нерв</i> (nerv) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>трёпка</i> (trjópka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unreality</li><li>infeasibility, impracticability</li></ol><p><i>нереа́льный</i> (nereálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreal, unrealistic</li><li>exceptional</li><li>cool, awesome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common, frequent, not rare, usual</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not infrequently, quite often, not rarely</li></ol><p><i>нере́дкий</i> (nerédkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprofitable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>рента́бельный</i> (rentábelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spawning</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>нерестъ</i> (nerestŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nerstъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;nérśtas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spawn</li></ol><p><i>не́рест</i> (nérest) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indecision, irresolution, indecisiveness</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>реши́мость</i> (rešímostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indecisively, hesitantly</li></ol><p><i>нереши́тельный</i> (nerešítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indecision, irresolution</li><li>hesitation, wavering, hesitancy, hesitance</li><li>uncertainty, ambivalence, doubtfulness</li><li>indetermination</li><li>infirmity of purpose</li><li>dither</li><li>shilly-shally</li><li>half-heartedness</li></ol><p><i>нереши́тельный</i> (nerešítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indecisive</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>реши́тельный</i> (rešítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undecided, unsettled</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>решённый</i> (rešónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stainless steel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-rusting, non-corrosive, rustproof, corrosion-resistant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sockeye salmon (<i>Oncorhynchus nerka</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>nieriä</i> into Russian during the colonization of the Kamchatka (end of 17<sup>th</sup>, beginning of 18<sup>th</sup> century) by Finnish-Russian bilinguals, + <i>-ка</i> (-ka) ending. The meaning of the Finnish word is fixed to “saibling” now but it did not need to be so at that time. Doublet of <i>нериас</i> (nerias), <i>нериус</i> (nerius), and <i>не́ргас</i> (nérgas), northwestern Russian dialectal forms borrowed from Karelian <i>nieries</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unevenly (in an uneven manner)</li></ol><p><i>неро́вный</i> (neróvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unevenness, roughness</li><li>unequality</li></ol><p><i>неро́вный</i> (neróvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone drastically different from another mentally, morally or socially, unequal</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ро́вня</i> (róvnja) (stem-stressed) or from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ровня́</i> (rovnjá) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unromantic (not romantic)</li><li>unromantic, nonromantic (having no interest in romance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) seal of the genus <i>Pusa</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>нерпа</i> (nerpa), first attested from the 2nd half of the 16th century. Borrowed from Finnic language, probably from Livvi <i>ńоrрра</i>, compare Karelian <i>ńorppa</i>, Ludian <i>ńorp</i>, Finnish <i>norppa</i> (“ringed seal”); ultimately possible from Proto-Samic <i>&#42;noarvē</i> (“seal”), probable substrate loanword, like Proto-Samic <i>&#42;nuorjō</i> (“seal”). Compare Proto-Eskimo <i>&#42;nayyiʀ</i> (“ringed seal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seal of the genus <i>Pusa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) one who has stained himself with unworthy, vile or immoral acts, someone you cannot shake hands with</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>рукопожа́тный</i> (rukopožátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) not made by hand, not of human making</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-Russian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a non-Russian person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;usually&#32;collective) aliens, foreigners, outlanders (from the Russian perspective); people with no Russian ethnic or cultural relation</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>русь</i> (rusʹ); literally, “non-Russian”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbreakable, inviolable, indissoluble</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ру́шимый</i> (rúšimyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) messy, unkempt person</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ря́ха</i> (rjáxa, “snappy dresser”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laxness, slovenliness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laxness, slovenliness</li></ol><p><i>неря́шливый</i> (nerjášlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untidy, slovenly</li><li>careless, sloppy</li></ol><p>From <i>неря́ха</i> (nerjáxa, “slob”) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj), from <i>ря́ха</i> (rjáxa, “dandy”), shortening of <i>ряжо́ха</i> (rjažóxa, “dandy”), from <i>ряди́ться</i> (rjadítʹsja, “to dress up”), from <i>ряд</i> (rjad, “row”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unauthorized</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>санкциони́рованный</i> (sankcionírovannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unworkable, impracticable, infeasible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сбы́точный</i> (sbýtočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indigestion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) stale</li><li>stagnant (<i>of air or water</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) already familiar, not new; outdated</li><li>in long use</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>све́жий</i> (svéžij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unfreedom, slavery, captivity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untimely, inopportune</li><li>late, tardy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>своевре́менный</i> (svojevrémennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual, unnatural, uncharacteristic</li><li>extrinsic</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сво́йственный</i> (svójstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbendable</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unyielding, persistent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сгиба́емый</i> (sgibájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noncompliant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сгово́рчивый</i> (sgovórčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unrestraint (lack of restraint)</li></ol><p><i>несде́ржанный</i> (nesdéržannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfulfilled</li><li>unrestrained, violent</li><li>impetuous, lacking self control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying out, performance, execution; fulfilment</li></ol><p><i>нес-</i> (nes-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unserious, not serious, light-headed, frivolous</li><li>unfounded</li><li>unimportant, insignificant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incompressibility</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сжима́ем(ый)</i> (sžimájem(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not strong, weak</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asymmetric, asymmetrical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>asynchronously</li><li>unsynchronized</li></ol><p><i>несинхронизи́рованный</i> (nesinxronizírovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asynchronous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>asynchronously</li></ol><p><i>несинхро́нный</i> (nesinxrónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asynchronous (not synchronous)</li><li>not in sync</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsystematic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ineffable, indescribable</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) very strong, exceptional, extraordinary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungainly, awkward, gawky, clumsy</li><li>absurd</li><li>incoherent, inarticulate (<i>speech, etc.</i>)</li><li>inharmonious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>скла́дный</i> (skládnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indeclinable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>склоня́емый</i> (sklonjájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhat, slightly, rather; some</li><li>multiple, a couple</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>a few, several, some [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ско́лько</i> (skólʹko, “amount; many”), literally “no specific amount”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interminable, never-ending</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immodest</li><li>indiscreet, indelicate</li><li>brazen</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>скро́мный</i> (skrómnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>нескро́мний</i> (neskrómnyj), Belarusian <i>няскро́мны</i> (njaskrómny), Polish <i>nieskromny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconcealed, undisguised</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>скрыва́емый</i> (skryvájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-Slavic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-verbal</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>слове́сный</i> (slovésnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) non-syllabic</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>слогово́й</i> (slogovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, not sophisticated</li><li>simple, easy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobeyer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unheard-of</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>слы́ханный</i> (slýxannyj), past passive participle of <i>слыха́ть</i> (slyxátʹ, “to hear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inaudibly</li></ol><p><i>неслы́шный</i> (neslýšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inaudible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шный</i> (slýšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timid, shy</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сме́лый</i> (smélyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innumerable, countless, numberless, incalculable</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сме́та</i> (sméta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>despite</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>смотря́</i> (smotrjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in spite of, despite (<i>the action must be completed in advance</i>)</li><li>notwithstanding</li><li>although</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>although, in spite of the fact that, despite the fact that</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intolerable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noncompliance</li><li>nonobservance</li><li>infraction, violation</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>соблюде́ние</i> (sobljudénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) underage (in terms of individuals)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) minor (an individual below of the age of majority)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>совершенноле́тний</i> (soveršennolétnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperfect, mediocre</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) imperfect, imperfective</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>соверше́нный</i> (soveršénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) imperfective aspect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperfection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incompatibility</li></ol><p><i>несовмести́мый</i> (nesovmestímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incompatible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mismatch</li><li>misalignment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disagreement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>discordant, inconsonant, at loggerheads ((figuratively) unable to agree&#59; opposing)</li></ol><p><i>несогла́сие</i> (nesoglásije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not having been agreed upon, not having received content, unsanctioned</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) lacking agreement in grammatical forms</li><li>lacking coherence, structure, unity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indestructible, invincible, unconquerable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) without enough salt (only in expressions)</li></ol><p>From a rare negative adverbial со́лоно (sólono) of adjective солёный (soljónyj, “salty”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>undoubtedly, without doubt, doubtlessly</li></ol><p><i>несомне́нный</i> (nesomnénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doubtless, indubitable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disproportion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discrepancy, disparity, lack of correspondence</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>соответствие</i> (sootvetstvije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inapplicable, inappropriate, incommensurate, insufficient, misplaced, out of place, irrelevant</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>соотве́тствующий</i> (sootvétstvujuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned, cancelled</li><li>abortive, failed</li><li><i>(person)</i> unaccomplished</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>состоя́вшийся</i> (sostojávšijsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insolvency, failure</li><li>modest means</li><li>unfoundedness, groundlessness, flimsiness, unsoundness</li></ol><p><i>несостоя́тельный</i> (nesostojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insolvent</li><li>not wealthy, of modest means</li><li>unfounded, groundless, flimsy, unsound, untenable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>состоя́тельный</i> (sostojátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disagreement</li><li>discrepancy, inconsistency</li><li>contradiction</li><li>incorrect docking</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>состыко́вка</i> (sostykóvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unripe</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>спе́лый</i> (spélyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slowly, in a leisurely manner</li></ol><p><i>неспе́шный</i> (nespéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) slow, unhurried, leisurely</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>спе́шный</i> (spéšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inconvenient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restless</li><li>anxious, unsettled</li><li>choppy (<i>seas</i>)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>споко́йный</i> (spokójnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inability, incompetence, incapacity</li></ol><p><i>неспосо́бный</i> (nesposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncapable, incapable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unjustly, unfairly (in an unjust manner)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>справедли́во</i> (spravedlívo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injustice, unfairness</li></ol><p><i>несправедли́вый</i> (nespravedlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfair</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>справедли́вый</i> (spravedlívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) not without purpose, with some hidden design</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>спроста́</i> (sprostá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incomparably, matchlessly, peerlessly</li><li>far, by far</li></ol><p><i>несравне́нный</i> (nesravnénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) incomparable, matchless, unmatched, peerless</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сравни́ть</i> (sravnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instability</li></ol><p><i>нестаби́льный</i> (nestabílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>стаби́льный</i> (stabílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstandard</li><li>irregular</li><li>non-typical</li><li>custom</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>станда́ртный</i> (standártnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not aging; ageless.</li></ol><p><i>не</i> (ne) +&lrm; <i>старение</i> (starenije) +&lrm; <i>-яющий</i> (-jajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unbearably</li></ol><p><i>нестерпи́мый</i> (nesterpímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbearable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>стерпе́ть</i> (sterpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not shy, not coy (reserved)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) unconstrained</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>стесни́тельный</i> (stesnítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, to bring, to carry</li><li>to carry, to bear, to support</li><li>to lay (eggs)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to talk (about), to babble on (about)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be carried away, to go over the limits</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нести</i> (nesti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nestì</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurtle, to dash, to race, to tear, to scurry (to move rapidly, violently, or without control)</li><li>to come, <i>(sound)</i> to reach one's ears, to be heard</li><li>to lay eggs</li></ol><p><i>нести́</i> (nestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Nestorianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of agreement, inability to agree</li><li>discrepancy, inconsistency, disagreement</li><li>contradiction</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>стыкова́ть(ся)</i> (stykovátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нестыко́вка</i> (nestykóvka, “discrepancy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>нѣсть</i> (něstĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;religion) Nonpossessor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absurdity, ridiculousness, nonsense, rubbish</li></ol><p><i>несура́зный</i> (nesuráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absurdity (<i>in the abstract or concrete sense</i>)</li><li>awkwardness</li></ol><p><i>несура́зный</i> (nesuráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absurd, ridiculous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awkward, unsteady</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) + <i>сура́зный</i> (suráznyj, “prominent, beautiful”), from <i>су-</i> (su-, “with”) + <i>раз</i> (raz, “(originally) stroke, cut”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, rubbish</li></ol><p>From <i>несусве́т-</i> (nesusvét-) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) extreme, utter</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сусве́тный</i> (susvétnyj, “ordinary, common”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying hen, layer (hen kept to lay eggs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonessential, inessential, immaterial</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>суще́ственный</i> (suščéstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonexistent (not existent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(load-)bearing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfortunate, ill-fated, ill-starred, luckless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>счастли́вый</i> (sčastlívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhappy, unlucky</li><li>paltry, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) + Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъčęstьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>сча́стье</i> (sčástʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accident (mishap, casualty)</li><li>Neschastny Sluchai (Soviet and Russian rock band)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, bad luck</li><li>unhappiness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inedible</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>съедо́бный</i> (sʺjedóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>no</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>there is not, there are no [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘possessed by someone’</small>] (often idiomatically translated by English <i>not have</i> with the object of <i>у</i> (u) as the subject)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нѣту</i> (nětu), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nětu</i>, from <i>&#42;ne</i> (“not”) + <i>&#42;e</i>, short form of <i>&#42;estь</i> (“is”) + <i>&#42;tu</i> (“there, here”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>should have</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) no</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) as a rule, no</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tactless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>netball (game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>netball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impatiently</li></ol><p><i>нетерпели́вый</i> (neterpelívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impatient</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>терпели́вый</i> (terpelívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impatience (quality of being impatient)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intolerance</li></ol><p><i>нетерпи́мый</i> (neterpímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intolerant</li><li>intolerable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>терпи́мый</i> (terpímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>atypical</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>типи́чный</i> (tipíčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) imperishable, incorruptible (not subject to decay)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) eternal</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тле́нный</i> (tlénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a member of the radical strain of the Priestless Old Believer movement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsinking, unsinkable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bat, noctule, pipistrelle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;netopyřь</i>, a compound which first part <i>nekto-</i> (e-grade) is cognate to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i> (o-grade).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>leisurely, in an unhurried manner</li></ol><p><i>неторопли́вый</i> (netoroplívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, unhurried, leisurely</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>торопли́вый</i> (toroplívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inaccuracy, inexactitude</li></ol><p><i>нето́чный</i> (netóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) inaccurate, inexact, imprecise</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative medicine (any of various medical methods and practices used in place of conventional medicine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nontraditional, unconventional</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>традицио́нный</i> (tradiciónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not exacting, undemanding, lenient</li><li>undemanding, unpretentious, modest</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тре́бовательный</i> (trébovatelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intoxicated, drunk</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nontrivial</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тривиа́льный</i> (triviálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untouched</li><li>fresh, unsullied, virginal</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тро́нутый</i> (trónutyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>easily, without difficulty</li></ol><p><i>нетру́дный</i> (netrúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy, not difficult</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>net (<i>of weight</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>netto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) there is no, there are no [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) no</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нѣту</i> (nětu) and <i>нѣтъ</i> (nětŭ). From older <b>*не ѥ ту</b> (it is not here), where <b>ту</b> (тут, also colloquial <i>не́туть</i> (nétutĭ)), was originally an ethical dative (<i>-ть</i> (-tĭ), dative <i>-ти</i> (-ti)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconvincing</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>убеди́тельный</i> (ubedítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untidy</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>у́бранный</i> (úbrannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disrespect, lack of respect</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уваже́ние</i> (uvažénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flippant, disrespectful, irreverent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уважи́тельный</i> (uvažítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>timidly, uncertainly, hesitantly</li></ol><p><i>неуве́ренный</i> (neuvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertainty, ambivalence</li></ol><p><i>неуве́ренный</i> (neuvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncertain, hesitating, vacillating, unconfident (<i>in general; of a person or thing</i>)</li><li>unsteady, unstable (<i>in general; of a person or thing</i>)</li><li>uncertain, lacking confidence [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + instrumental) <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уве́ренный</i> (uvérennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) discrepancy, lack of coordination</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>увяза́ть</i> (uvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) Constantly burning, inextinguishable; unrelenting, permanently existing.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;neugasimъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>га́снуть</i> (gásnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disliked <i>(by)</i>, out of favour/out of favor <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уго́дный</i> (ugódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) restless</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) indefatigable, ever-active</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>угомон(и́ть)</i> (ugomon(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessful</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уда́вшийся</i> (udávšijsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, mishap, bad luck, failure</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уда́ча</i> (udáča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unlucky fellow / person; loser</li></ol><p><i>неуда́ча</i> (neudáča) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>poorly, abortively, unsuccessfully</li></ol><p><i>неуда́чный</i> (neudáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessful, unfortunate</li><li>unlucky</li></ol><p><i>неуда́ча</i> (neudáča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>irrepressibly</li><li>uncontrollably</li></ol><p><i>неудержи́мый</i> (neuderžímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irrepressible</li><li>uncontrollable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удержа́ть</i> (uderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unsurprisingly, no wonder, small wonder</li></ol><p><i>неудиви́тельный</i> (neudivítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsurprising</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удиви́тельный</i> (udivítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>неудо́бно</i> (neudóbno); awkward, inconvenient, uncomfortable</li></ol><p><i>неудо́бно</i> (neudóbno) +&lrm; <i>-енько</i> (-enʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uncomfortably</li><li>inconveniently</li><li>uneasily, ill at ease</li></ol><p><i>неудо́бный</i> (neudóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncomfortable, inconvenient</li><li>awkward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;neudobьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indigestible (of a food and information)</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удобовари́мый</i> (udobovarímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) indigestible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illegibility, unreadability</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удобочитаемость</i> (udobočitajemostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illegible, unreadable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удобочита́емый</i> (udobočitájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inconvenience</li><li>discomfort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unsatisfactorily</li></ol><p><i>неудовлетвори́тельный</i> (neudovletvorítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsatisfactory</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удовлетвори́тельный</i> (udovletvorítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissatisfaction, discontent</li></ol><p><i>неудовлетворённый</i> (neudovletvorjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsatisfied (expressing a lack of satisfaction) (<i>of a look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li><li>unsatisfied (experiencing a lack of satisfaction) (<i>of a person</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>удовлетворённый</i> (udovletvorjónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>displeasure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>really?, is it possible?, indeed</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>не</i> (ne, “not”) +&lrm; <i>уже́</i> (užé, “already”) +&lrm; <i>ли</i> (li, interrogative particle). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>невже́</i> (nevžé), Belarusian <i>няўжо́</i> (njawžó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>неуже́ли</i> (neužéli)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>struggling to live (<i>with others</i>)</li><li>socially inept</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ужив(а́ться)</i> (uživ(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) really?, is it possible?, indeed</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уж</i> (už) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unrecognizability</li></ol><p><i>неузнава́емый</i> (neuznavájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrecognizable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>узнава́емый</i> (uznavájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrecognized</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>у́знанный</i> (úznannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steadily, relentlessly</li><li>steadfastly</li><li>progressively</li></ol><p><i>неукло́нный</i> (neuklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steadfast, unflinching, steady</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>укло́н</i> (uklón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clumsily, awkwardly, uncouthly (done without care or finesse, often hurriedly or awkwardly)</li></ol><p><i>неуклю́жий</i> (neukljúžij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clumsiness</li></ol><p><i>неуклю́жий</i> (neukljúžij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clumsy, awkward</li></ol><p>Compare Old Czech <i>pokliuditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strictly, rigorously</li><li>unfailingly</li></ol><p><i>неукосни́тельный</i> (neukosnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strict, rigorous</li><li>unfailing</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>укосни́ть</i> (ukosnítʹ, “to delay, to hesitate”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indomitable, invincible, untameable</li><li>uncontrollable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>укроти́мый</i> (ukrotímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>imperceptibly, subtly</li></ol><p><i>неулови́мый</i> (neulovímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elusive, difficult to catch</li><li>imperceptible, subtle</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>улови́мый</i> (ulovímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) "uncatchable Joe", something widely known but has no interest to anyone</li></ol><p>Based on a joke that someone called "Joe" was never caught, not because he is hard to catch but because no-one wants to catch him.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clumsily, ineptly</li></ol><p><i>неуме́лый</i> (neumélyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unskilful, inept, incompetent</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уме́лый</i> (umélyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inability, lack of skill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misplaced, inappropriate, out of place, unsuitable, unfitting, irrelevant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уме́стный</i> (uméstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) klutz, duffer, sad sack (an inept or clumsy person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid, unintelligent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inexorably, implacably (in an inexorable manner; relentlessly)</li></ol><p><i>неумоли́мый</i> (neumolímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexorable, implacable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>умоли́ть</i> (umolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirty person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inadvertently, accidentally, unintentionally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpremeditated, inadvertent, unintentional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpacked, loose (not packaged)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonpayment (failure to pay)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>out of control, uncontrolled, uncontrollable</li><li>erratic</li><li>ungovernable</li><li>unguided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) crop failure, poor harvest</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>урожа́й</i> (urožáj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion, disorder, mess</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) squabbling, arguing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure, lack of success</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>успе́х</i> (uspéx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>constantly, incessantly, relentlessly</li><li>tirelessly</li></ol><p><i>неуста́нный</i> (neustánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constant, incessant, relentless</li><li>tireless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уст(а́ть)</i> (ust(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́нный</i> (-ánnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;law) penal damages, stipulated damages (in a civil law system, the monetary penalty for nonfulfillment of a contractual obligation)</li></ol><p><i>неусто́йка</i> (neustójka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) penal damages, stipulated damages (in a civil law system, the monetary penalty for nonfulfillment of a contractual obligation)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) failure</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>устоя́ть</i> (ustojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instability, unsteadiness</li></ol><p><i>неусто́йчивый</i> (neustójčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstable, unsteady, fluctuating, changeable</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) labile</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>усто́йчивый</i> (ustójčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fearlessness (quality of being fearless)</li></ol><p><i>неустраши́мый</i> (neustrašímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undaunted, fearless, intrepid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of being unsettled</li><li>disorderliness, disorder</li></ol><p><i>неустро́енный</i> (neustrójennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsettled</li><li>disorderly</li><li>unsuccessful, not established (person)</li><li>not provided for</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>устро́енный</i> (ustrójennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noncompliant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>усту́пчивый</i> (ustúpčivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uninspiring</li><li>inauspicious</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>утеши́тельный</i> (utešítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsolable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tireless, indefatigable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>утоми́ть</i> (utomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose (not compact)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) ignoramus</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уч-</i> (uč-), the root of <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ, “to learn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impolite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>discourteous, impolite</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>учти́вый</i> (učtívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uncomfortably</li></ol><p><i>неую́тный</i> (neujútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncomfortable</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) causing loneliness or longing</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ую́тный</i> (ujútnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invulnerability</li></ol><p><i>неуязви́мый</i> (neujazvímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invulnerable</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уязви́мый</i> (ujazvímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indefatigable, irrepressible</li><li><i>(pain etc)</i> incessant</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>уём</i> (ujóm, “quiet, calm”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) nave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) informal (a member of a 1980s Soviet youth subculture)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>неформа́л</i> (neformál).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) informal (a member of a 1980s Soviet youth subculture)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>неформа́льные объедине́ния молодёжи</i> (neformálʹnyje obʺjedinénija molodjóži, “informal youth associations”) – social groups, clubs and gangs formed in response to "formal" youth associations such as Komsomol.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>informal</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>форма́льный</i> (formálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nephrite, jade</li><li>nephritis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil tank farm, oil terminal</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ба́за</i> (báza)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil and gas</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>газ</i> (gaz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker in the oil and gas industry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil production</li><li>oil-production industry</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>добы́ча</i> (dobýča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil refining</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>перераба́тывающий</i> (pererabátyvajuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil refining</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>перерабо́тка</i> (pererabótka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil refinery worker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil pipeline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil pipeline</li></ol><p>From <i>нефтепрово́д</i> (nefteprovód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>нафтопрові́дни́й</i> (naftoprovídnýj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) mineral oil, oil product</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>проду́кт</i> (prodúkt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil spill boat, pollution control ship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>petrochemical</li></ol><p><i>нефтехи́мия</i> (neftexímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petrochemistry</li></ol><p>From <i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>хи́мия</i> (xímija), probably a calque of French <i>pétrochimie</i> (“petrochemistry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) oil, petroleum, mineral oil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نفت (neft).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil industry worker, oilman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oil, petroleum</li></ol><p><i>нефть</i> (neftʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortage (not enough, not sufficient)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsophisticated, artless, guileless, simple-minded (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simple, unsophisticated</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad (not good)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bad, badly (in a bad manner)</li><li>unwell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unwillingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-Christian (person who is not a Christian)</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-, “non-”) +&lrm; <i>христиани́н</i> (xristianín, “Christian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) there is no need (to), there is no use (to)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) there is nothing</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) indicates a requirement to stop</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>хуй</i> (xuj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purposeless, inexpedient</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>целесообра́зный</i> (celesoobráznyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscene, indecent</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) contrary to the rules of censorship</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>цензу́рный</i> (cenzúrnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>неча́янно</i> (nečájanno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rarely, infrequently</li></ol><p><i>неча́стый</i> (nečástyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infrequent, rare</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ча́стый</i> (částyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inadvertently, accidentally, unintentionally</li></ol><p><i>неча́янный</i> (nečájannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unexpected</li><li>unintentional, accidental</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>ча́янный</i> (čájannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhuman</li><li>superhuman</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A man who is wicked, evil, blasphemous.</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>честный</i> (čestnyj, “honest”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec, “doer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impious, profane, ungodly, unholy, wicked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dishonest, unfair</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Slavic&#32;mythology,&#32;collective) evil spirit(s); evil forces</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not purebred</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>чистопоро́дный</i> (čistoporódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncleanness</li><li>impurity</li><li>defectiveness, sloppiness, inaccuracy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) depravity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dishonesty</li><li>evilness, diabolicalness</li><li>dirt</li></ol><p><i>нечи́стый</i> (nečístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewage, scum, filth, night-soil</li><li>impurities</li></ol><p>Has the form of the plural of <i>нечистота́</i> (nečistotá, “uncleanness, impurity, dirt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dirty, unclean</li><li>defective, sloppy, inaccurate</li><li>dishonest, morally bankrupt</li><li>impure</li><li>evil, diabolical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;folklore) evil spirits, unholy creatures</li><li>(collective,&#32;derogatory) riffraff, scum, vermin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inarticulate, unintelligible</li></ol><p>From <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>членоразде́льный</i> (členorazdélʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>something</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;něčьto</i>. Morphologically from <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>что</i> (što).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indistinct, unsharp, fuzzy, blurred; illegible, difficult (<i>of handwriting or a font</i>)</li><li>vague, indistinct, uncertain, undefined</li><li>unstreamlined, uncoordinated, not efficient, inefficient</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>чёткий</i> (čótkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) odd number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>odd <i>(not divisible by two)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstaff, not part of staff, freelance, adjunct (about a worker)</li><li>freelance (about work)</li><li>(<i>specialist</i>) not nominal, nonstandard, nonroutine, abnormal, unusual, unplanned, exceptional, emergency, extraordinary</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>шта́тный</i> (štátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) really?, is it possible?, indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) serious, considerable, substantial</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>шу́точный</i> (šútočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mercilessly, ruthlessly</li></ol><p><i>неща́дный</i> (neščádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciless, ruthless</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>щад(и́ть)</i> (ščad(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inefficiency</li></ol><p><i>неэффекти́вный</i> (neeffektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inefficient</li><li>ineffective</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;highly&#32;formal</i>) alternative form of <i>нея̈</i> (nejǫ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure to appear, no-show</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>я́вка</i> (jávka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dim, faint (<i>of light</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) pale, impure (<i>of colors or sounds</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) imperceptible</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) not producing a strong impression</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ordinary, nonunique</li></ol><p><i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>я́ркий</i> (járkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vaguely</li><li>thick, thickly</li><li>darkly</li><li>ambiguously (in an ambiguous manner)</li><li>indefinitely</li><li>fuzzily</li><li>uncertainly</li></ol><p><i>нея́сный</i> (nejásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambiguity (something liable to more than one interpretation)</li><li>obscurity, opacity</li><li>haziness, fuzziness, indistinctness</li><li>nebulosity</li></ol><p><i>нея́сный</i> (nejásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclear (ambiguous&#59; liable to more than one interpretation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tawny owl (Strix, a genus of owls)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nejęsytь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>neither, nor</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not a</li><li><i>(between a personal pronoun and the verb in the affirmative)</i> whatever, however</li><li><i>(before какой, кто, что)</i> any, no</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nu (the modern Greek letter Ν&#47;ν)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ни</i> (ni), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ni</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>νι</i> (ni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under no circumstances, on no account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) not for all the tea in China</li></ol><p>Literally, “Not for any gingerbread”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for free, for nothing</li><li>never, not for any reason (expressing great reluctance)</li><li>for no good reason</li><li>(not) for anything</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>none, nothing</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not even a bit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not once</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unexpectedly, all of a sudden, without rhyme or reason, apropos of nothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) in the small hours; very early in the morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without rhyme or reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) neither fish nor fowl</li></ol><p>Literally, “neither that nor this”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) nothing at all or very little, fuck all</li></ol><p>ни (ni) + фиг (fig)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;slang,&#32;mildly&#32;vulgar) expressing surprise or amazement, especially in an unexpected situation. wow, holy crap.</li></ol><p><i>ни фига</i> (ni figa) +&lrm; <i>себе</i> (sebe)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) jack shit, fuck all, fucking nothing</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) no fucking way, fuck no</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking hell (<i>an exclamation of great surprise</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking wow, what the fuck, fucking hell, fuck me (<i>expresses surprise or anger</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>no-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) nibble (unit of memory equal to half a byte)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nibble</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornfield</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) field</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ňiva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to offset, to eliminate, to level, to negate, to neutralise, to mitigate, to even out</li><li>(<i>geodesy</i>) to determine the height of a point on the earth's surface relative to another point or to sea level</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>nivellieren</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) nigger, coon, blackie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nigga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) nigger (а person descended from the native people of sub-Saharan Africa&#59; a black person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nigger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nowhere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nikъde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nigerian</li></ol><p><i>Ниге́рия</i> (Nigérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nihilism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nihilist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nigirizushi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>握り寿司</i> (nigirizushi, literally “gripped sushi”), from the way the sushi rice is molded in the hand.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dutchman</li></ol><p><i>Нидерла́нды</i> (Niderlándy) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dutch <i>(language)</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dutch</li></ol><p><i>Нидерла́нды</i> (Niderlándy) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>below, beneath, under</li><li>lower, down (from a higher to a lower position, downwards)</li><li>shorter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nižьjь</i>, from <i>&#42;niz-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьjь</i>.</p><p>See Russian <i>низ</i> (niz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nizhny Novgorod, Nizhegorodian</li></ol><p><i>ни́же</i> (níže) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) following, subsequent</li></ol><p>From <i>низкий</i> (nizkij) +&lrm; <i>сле́дующий</i> (slédujuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underwear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lower Sorbian</li></ol><p>From <i>ни́жний</i> (nížnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>лу́жицкий</i> (lúžickij), a calque of German <i>niedersorbisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower, inferior</li><li>under-, bottom</li></ol><p>From <i>низ</i> (niz, “bottom”) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij). See also <i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Нижний)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>Ни́жний Но́вгород</i> (Nížnij Nóvgorod)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lower lip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom, lower part</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nizъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nei-ǵʰ-</i>? (Derksen) or <i>&#42;ni-</i> (“down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>down, de-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘beads, mushrooms, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>to string [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a necklace, fringe, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>из</b> (iz, + genitive) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to decorate by stringing [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nizati</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>ни́зати</i> (1sg. <i>ни̑же̄м</i>), Slovene <i>nízati</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>) (1sg. <i>nȋžem</i>, <i>nȋzam</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>)), Bulgarian <i>ни́жа</i> (níža), Church Slavonic <i>низати</i> (nizati), Polish <i>nanizać</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>nizaś</i>. Related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nьziti</i>; compare Russian <i>вонзи́ть</i> (vonzítʹ), <i>пронзи́ть</i> (pronzítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to throw down</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to throw down</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij, “low”)</li></ol><p><i>ни́з(кий)</i> (níz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow, lowland</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nizina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low</li><li>despicable</li><li>deep (of a sound or voice, low in frequency or pitch)</li><li>short (of a person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nizъkъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ni-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>basely, meanly</li><li>low, close to the ground level</li></ol><p><i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low-skilled</li></ol><p>From <i>низкий</i> (nizkij) +&lrm; <i>квалифици́рованный</i> (kvalificírovannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociology</i>) low-income (<i>designed or related to people with little to no monetary security</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij, “low”) +&lrm; <i>обеспе́чение</i> (obespéčenije, “security, assurance, safety, etc.”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poorly-paid, low-paid, underpaid, low-paying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) low Earth orbit, low-orbit</li></ol><p><i>ни́зк(ий)</i> (nízk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>орбита́льный</i> (orbitálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) flatterer, adulator</li></ol><p><i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>покло́н</i> (poklón) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulation, sycophancy, obsequiousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulation, sycophancy, obsequiousness</li></ol><p><i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij) +&lrm; <i>покло́н</i> (poklón) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undersized, shortish, dwarf</li></ol><p><i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ро́слый</i> (róslyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technology</i>) low-precision, low-accuracy (<i>of a machine, measurement, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol><p><i>низ-</i> (niz-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowland</li></ol><p><i>ни́зменный</i> (nízmennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) low-lying</li><li>mean, base, vile</li></ol><p><i>ни́зом</i> (nízom) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>низ-</i> (niz-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower</li><li>downstream, from/on the lower reaches</li><li>local</li></ol><p><i>низ</i> (niz) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to descend, to go down [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to somewhere’</small>]</li><li>(poetic,&#32;figurative) to descend, to fall [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘onto somewhere’</small>] (<i>of the night, an emotion, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>низъити</i> (nizŷiti). By surface analysis, <i>низо-</i> (nizo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>along the bottom, on low ground, taking a lower road</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowness</li><li>baseness; meanness; pettiness; ignominy</li></ol><p><i>низ</i> (niz) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to throw down</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ни́зкий</i> (nízkij, “low”)</li><li>the lowest, inferior</li><li>lower, subordinate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nižьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) Internet handle, nickname, alias</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nick(name)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niqab (veil which covers the face)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic نِقَاب (niqāb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by no means, not at all, in no way, nowise</li><li>it appears, it seems, it looks like</li></ol><p>From <i>ни-</i> (ni-) +&lrm; <i>как</i> (kak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) no sir; negative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) not any of possible variants, no, none; (in a negative context) whatever, whatsoever, absolutely</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;of a person's state) out of it, senseless, e.g. very drunken or exhausted</li></ol><p><i>ни</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>како́й</i> (kakój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nicaraguan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nickel</li></ol><p><i>ни́кель</i> (níkelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nickel-plated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plate with nickel, to nickel</li></ol><p><i>ни́кель</i> (níkelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nickel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nikitsky (street in Moscow, monastery, botanical gardens in Yalta, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>Ники́т(ский)</i> (Nikít(skij)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;niknǫti</i>. Slavic cognates include Czech <i>vzniknout</i> (“to arise, originate”) and Serbo-Croatian <i>niknuti</i> (“to sprout, spring”). Further cognates include Ancient Greek <i>νεῖκος</i> (neîkos, “quarrel, strife”) and Ancient Greek <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never, nevermore (entails the meaning "always" or "for now on")</li></ol><p>Compare Lithuanian <i>niekada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) not any, no</li></ol><p><i>ни</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>кой</i> (koj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to Emperor Nicholas I of Russia</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to Emperor Nicholas II of Russia</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to Grand Duke Nicholas Nikolaevich the Elder</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayev</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Mykolaiv</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevo</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Mikałajeva</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Mykolaieve</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikałajova</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevka</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Mikałajeŭka</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Mykolaivka</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nicolaevca</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolaevka</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevsk</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevsky</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevskaya</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to (a) Nikolayevskoye</li></ol><p><i>Никола́й</i> (Nikoláj) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p><p><i>Никола́ев</i> (Nikolájev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>Никола́ево</i> (Nikolájevo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>Никола́евка</i> (Nikolájevka) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>Никола́евск</i> (Nikolájevsk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p><p><i>Никола́евский</i> (Nikolájevskij) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p><p><i>Никола́евская</i> (Nikolájevskaja) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p><p><i>Никола́евское</i> (Nikolájevskoje) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Николаевский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numerous settlements in Russia, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Nikolayevsky (a posyolok in Karaulnoye selsoviet, Kamyzyak Raion, Astrakhan Oblast, Southern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Nikolayevsky (a posyolok, the administrative centre of Zabodskoy rural settlement, Tarbagatay Raion, Republic of Buryatia, Far Eastern Federal District, Russia, founded in 1805)</li><li>Nikolayevsky (a posyolok in Trushniki rural settlement, Chernushka Raion, Perm Krai, Volga Federal District, Russia)</li><li>(fully <i>Никола́евский 2-й</i>) 2nd Nikolayevsky (a khutor, the administrative centre of Nikolayevsky rural settlement, Proletarsk Raion, Rostov Oblast, Southern Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Nikolayevsky (a khutor in Verkhnekardailsky rural settlement, Novonikolayevsky Raion, Volgograd Oblast, Southern Federal District, Russia)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>никола́евский</i> (nikolájevskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) A member of the Orthodox Church (in Old Believers' speech)</li></ol><p><i>Ни́кон</i> (Níkon) +&lrm; <i>-ианин</i> (-ianin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nicotine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nicotine</li></ol><p><i>никоти́н</i> (nikotín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nixtamalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> nobody, no one; <i>(with negative)</i> anybody, anyone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>никъто</i> (nikŭto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nikъto</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ни-</i> (ni-) +&lrm; <i>кто</i> (kto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to nowhere, nowhither</li></ol><p>From <i>ни-</i> (ni-) +&lrm; <i>куда́</i> (kudá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) bad, useless, in no respect good</li></ol><p><i>никуда́</i> (nikudá) +&lrm; <i>-ы́шный</i> (-ýšnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>никчёмный</i> (nikčómnyj): worthlessness, uselessness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthless, useless, good for nothing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>нікче́мний</i> (nikčémnyj) in late 19th or early 20th century, from Old Ruthenian <i>никче́мный</i> (nikčémnyj), possibly from Old Polish <i>nikczemny</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ни</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>к</i> (k) +&lrm; <i>чему́</i> (čemú) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) nielsbohrium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nile</li></ol><p><i>Ни́л</i> (Níl) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) Nile perch (<i>Lates niloticus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ни́льский</i> (nílʹskij, “Nile”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>о́кунь</i> (ókunʹ, “perch, bass”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not in the least, not at all</li></ol><p><i>ни</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>ма́ло</i> (málo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halo, aureole, nimbus</li><li>glory</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>nimbus</i>. Doublet of <i>не́бо</i> (nébo), <i>нёбо</i> (njóbo), <i>небула́йзер</i> (nebulájzer, “nebulizer”), and <i>небуля́рный</i> (nebuljárnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) nymph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) nymphaea (<i>Nymphaea</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Nymphaea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nymphomaniac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ним</i> (nim):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>instrumental of <i>онъ</i> (on)</li><li>instrumental of <i>оно́</i> (onó)</li><li>dative of <i>они́</i> (oní)</li><li>dative of <i>онѣ́</i> (oně́)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ninja, shinobi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>忍者</i>, <i>にんじゃ</i> (ninja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ningxia</li></ol><p>From <i>Нинся́</i> (Ninsjá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niobium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from nowhere</li></ol><p><i>ни</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>отку́да</i> (otkúda)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) never; by no means</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cheaply, at a very low price, for nothing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unconcerned; easily tolerated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) nipple</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nipple</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) nirvana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>निर्वाण</i> (nirvāṇa, “blown or put out, extinguished”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>низ-</i> (niz-) (<i>before voiceless consonants</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not in the least, not at all, none at all (of quantity)</li></ol><p>From <i>ни-</i> (ni-) +&lrm; <i>ско́лько</i> (skólʹko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fall, to fall down, to hang down, to flow down (e.g. of hair)</li></ol><p><i>нис-</i> (nis-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godsent, God-given</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to send or bestow from above (as of a deity or similar figure)</li></ol><p><i>нис-</i> (nis-) +&lrm; <i>послать</i> (poslatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to send or bestow from above (as of a deity or similar figure)</li></ol><p><i>нис-</i> (nis-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to descend</li></ol><p><i>нис-</i> (nis-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) nit (candela per square meter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliberate misspelling of <i>не такой</i> (ne takoj)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>не тако́й</i> (ne takój)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>нитако́й как фсе</i> (nitakój kak fse).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread</li><li>string (<i>of beads, of pearls</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нить</i> (nitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нить</i> (nitʹ), diminutive of <i>ни́тка</i> (nítka): (short or thin) thread</li></ol><p><i>ни́тка</i> (nítka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) nitrate (salt or ester of nitric acid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) nitride (compound with nitrogen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) nitrite (salt of nitrous acid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrylic fiber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acrylic fiber</li></ol><p><i>нитро́н</i> (nitrón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nitrofuran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread</li><li>filament</li><li>topic</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>нить</i> (nitĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nitь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;nī́ˀtis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ни́тяный</i> (nítjanyj, “thread (<i>relational</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>нить</i> (nitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thread</li></ol><p><i>нить</i> (nitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) niphaea (<i>Niphaea</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Niphaea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nihonium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar,&#32;swear word) alternative form of <i>ни хуя́</i> (ni xujá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>них</i> (nix):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>genitive/accusative/prepositional of <i>они́</i> (oní)</li><li>genitive/accusative/prepositional of <i>онѣ́</i> (oně́)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;stilted&#32;or&#32;literary) prone, face down</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ниць</i> (nicĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nĩcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) so-so, not bad, fair</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>never mind!; that's all right!; no problem!</li></ol><p>From <i>ни-</i> (ni-) +&lrm; <i>чего́</i> (čevó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wow!</li></ol><p>ничего (ničevo) + себе (sebe)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nothing at all, not a thing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>nobody's (belonging to nobody (possessive form of nobody))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nobody's</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;sports) tied</li></ol><p><i>ниче́й</i> (ničéj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>ничья́</i> (ničʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>prone, face down</li></ol><p>From <i>ниц</i> (nic), <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>ничко́м</i> (nyčkóm), Bulgarian <i>ни́чком</i> (níčkom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> nothing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ничьто</i> (ničĭto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ničьto</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ни-</i> (ni-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>что́</i> (štó, “what”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) without a shadow of a doubt; asking no question; nothing wavering (<i>while committing a rash act</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ничто́же сꙋмнѧ́щесѧ</i> (ničtóže sumnę́štesę); an expression used in several New Testament passages, including 1 Cor. 10:25 and 10:27 (corresponding to “asking no question” in King James Version) and James 1:6 (corresponding to “nothing wavering” in King James Version).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) nonentity (an unimportant or insignificant person)</li><li>smallness, pettiness, nothingness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insignificance</li><li>wretchedness</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) nonentity</li></ol><p><i>ничто́жный</i> (ničtóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trifling, insignificant, contemptible</li><li>worthless, paltry, despicable, mean</li><li>(<i>law</i>) null and void</li></ol><p>From Russian <i>ничто́</i> (ništó, “nothing”). Compare Russian <i>никчёмный</i> (nikčómnyj, “worthless, useless, good for nothing”), Russian <i>никуды́шный</i> (nikudýšnyj, “bad, useless, in no respect good”), Russian <i>никаку́щий</i> (nikakúščij, “in no respect good”). Also compare Latin <i>nēquam</i> (“worthless, good-for-nothing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not a bit, not in the slightest, by no means</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) draw, tie (result of a game)</li></ol><p>From <i>ничья́ игра́</i> (ničʹjá igrá, “nobody's game”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(nonstandard,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;proscribed) contraction of <i>ничего́</i> (ničevó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) niche, recess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>niche</i>. Further from Latin <i>nīdus</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nisdós</i> (whence also Russian <i>гнездо́</i> (gnezdó), English <i>nest</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tasty (mostly sweet) food; cookie</li><li>food that has been thrown away but is going to be eaten by someone else (mostly due to lack of money and not proper freeganism)</li><li>something regarded as good and gotten for free</li></ol><p>From Fenya criminal argot. Borrowed from Romani <i>nyst</i> and from Yiddish <i>נישט</i> (nisht). Now common in colloquial speech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become impoverished, to become destitute</li></ol><p><i>ни́щий</i> (níščij) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) beggar, tramp</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) cheapskate</li></ol><p><i>ни́щий</i> (níščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beggarly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) miserable</li></ol><p><i>ни́щий</i> (níščij) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to beg, to panhandle</li></ol><p><i>ни́щенство</i> (níščenstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty, destitution, penury, misery</li><li>beggars, beggary</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;niščeta</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ни́щий</i> (níščij) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, beggarly, destitute</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar, pauper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;niščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, yet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>but</li><li>Noh</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>same as но́-о-о (nó-o-o)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nu</i>. Cognate with English <i>now</i> and Latin <i>num</i> ("now") and <i>nunc</i> (&lt; *num + <i>ce</i>, "now").</p><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>能</i> (のう, Nō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) no pasarán (slogan, "they shall not pass")</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>no pasarán</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>giddyup (<i>in urging a horse forward</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noahide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nobel</li></ol><p><i>Но́бель</i> (Nóbelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobelium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innovator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>novātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innovative</li></ol><p><i>нова́тор</i> (novátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innovation (act of innovating)</li></ol><p><i>нова́тор</i> (novátor) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelty, innovation</li><li>(<i>law</i>) novation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Novgorod, Novgorodian</li></ol><p><i>Но́вгород</i> (Nóvgorod) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;novějь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short story</li><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) novel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>novella</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>novemdecillion (10<sup>60</sup>, or 10<sup>114</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brand new (utterly new)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) novice, newcomer</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novial (an international auxiliary language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelty, newness (state of being new)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virgin soil</li><li>freshly-reaped corn</li><li>piece of unbleached linen</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-ина́</i> (-iná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelty</li><li>new product, new arrival</li></ol><p><i>новина́</i> (noviná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) beginner, novice, newbie, neophyte</li><li>(neologism,&#32;inanimate) novichok (<i>nerve agent</i>)</li></ol><p>May equivalent to новый + -це + -ко. Cognate with Persian نوچه.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>newly</li></ol><p>From <i>новый</i> (novyj, “new”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Englander</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>А́нглия</i> (Ánglija) +&lrm; <i>-ча́нин</i> (-čánin), based on <i>Но́вая А́нглия</i> (Nóvaja Ánglija, “New England”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) neo-Aramaic</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>араме́йский</i> (araméjskij, “Aramaic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) (new) recruit</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>брани́ть</i> (branítʹ, “to abuse, to scold”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newlywed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newlywed</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>брак</i> (brak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innovation</li></ol><p><i>ново-</i> (novo-) +&lrm; <i>вве́дение</i> (vvédenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>New Year's</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij), based on <i>Но́вый год</i> (Nóvyj god, “New Year”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Year's dinner</li><li>New Year's table; the table used for the New Year's dinner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a New Year's party, especially one for children</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to celebrate the New Year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Modern Greek</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Modern Greek</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гре́ческий</i> (gréčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Novodevichy (referring to various locations named as such, e.g. Novodevichy Convent, Novodevichy Cemetery&#59; the name originally referred to the convent)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́вица</i> (dévica, “maiden”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij). Literally, “New Maidens”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something new</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) New Testament, neo-testamentary</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заве́т</i> (zavét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Zealander <i>(male)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Зела́ндия</i> (Zelándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), based on <i>Но́вая Зела́ндия</i> (Nóvaja Zelándija, “New Zealand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>related to</i>) New Zealand</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Зела́ндия</i> (Zelándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), based on <i>Но́вая Зела́ндия</i> (Nóvaja Zelándija, “New Zealand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) newly-made, newly-fledged</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>испечённый</i> (ispečónnyj, “baked”). Compare typologically German <i>frischgebacken</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Neoptera.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Neoptera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new moon</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>луна́</i> (luná) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>новолу́ние</i> (novolúnije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new formation</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) neoplasm</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ние</i> (obrazovánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new settler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) a female person who has recently died</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) recently deceased</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) a person who has recently died</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newly arrived</li></ol><p>From <i>ново-</i> (novo-) +&lrm; <i>прибы́вший</i> (pribývšij). Piecewise root cognate with <i>неофи́т</i> (neofít).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newly acquired, newfound</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>приобретённый</i> (priobretjónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonstandard form of <i>новорождённый</i> (novoroždjónnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newborn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newborn child</li><li>one celebrating his birthday</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рождённый</i> (roždjónnyj, “born”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new home</li><li>housewarming</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Novosibirsk, from or related to Novosibirsk</li></ol><p><i>Новосиби́р-</i> (Novosibír-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>news (especially, radio or television program)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) news; containing socially, politically or economically significant news</li></ol><p><i>но́вость</i> (nóvostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new building</li><li>new construction site</li><li>newly constructed neighborhood</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>стро́йка</i> (strójka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelty</li><li>news</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new settler</li><li>new tenant, inhabitant of a new residence</li><li>someone about to move to a new place</li></ol><p>From <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-сёл</i> (-sjól), combining form of <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ, “to settle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic</i>) newly/just brought to light, latter-day</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>я́вленный</i> (jávlennyj, “shown”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Newspeak (from George Orwell's "1984")</li><li>new language (generally with pejorative connotations); new argot</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>яз(ы́к)</i> (jaz(ýk)), literally “newlang”; calque of English <i>Newspeak</i>, from George Orwell's <i>Nineteen Eighty-Four</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innovation, novelty</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-шество</i> (-šestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>new</li><li>novel</li><li>recent</li><li>modern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nòvъ</i>, related to English <i>new</i> and <i>novel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>high style</i>) newness, novelty, something new in people's lives</li><li>unplowed land, virgin land</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) grain from a new harvest</li></ol><p><i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg</li><li>foot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нога</i> (noga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;noga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nogai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) leggy, long-legged, having nice and long legs</li></ol><p><i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>но́готь</i> (nógotʹ)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) marigold <i>(Calendula)</i></li><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) tip of the beak (<i>of geese, swans, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>но́готь</i> (nógotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ногото́к</i> (nogotók), diminutive of <i>но́готь</i> (nógotʹ)</li></ol><p><i>ногото́к</i> (nogotók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nail (of finger or toe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nogъtь</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃nogʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;h₃nogʷʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nail (<i>of the finger or toe</i>); ungual</li></ol><p><i>но́готь</i> (nógotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife</li><li>blade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nožь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) knife</li><li>bladed (having a blade) (<i>of machinery, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нож</i> (nož) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нога́</i> (nogá)</li></ol><p>From <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka). Compare Ukrainian <i>ні́женька</i> (níženʹka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нож</i> (nož): little knife</li></ol><p><i>нож</i> (nož) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>но́жик</i> (nóžik)</li></ol><p><i>но́жик</i> (nóžik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нога́</i> (nogá): small leg</li><li>trotter <i>(food)</i></li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) leg</li><li>stem <i>of a glass or letter</i></li><li>stalk <i>of a mushroom</i></li><li>drumstick (<i>chicken leg</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) chicken leg</li></ol><p>Importation of chicken legs from the United States began during the presidency of George HW Bush.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) scissors, shears, clipper, cutter</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) disproportion, discrepancy</li></ol><p>From <i>нож</i> (nož).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foot, leg</li><li>foot-powered</li></ol><p><i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheath, scabbard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handsaw (a small saw for cutting wood, designed to be used with one hand)</li><li>hacksaw (a small saw for cutting metal, designed to be used with one hand)</li></ol><p><i>нож</i> (nož) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ножево́й</i> (noževój)</li></ol><p><i>нож</i> (nož) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>нога́</i> (nogá)</li></ol><p><i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>porous</li></ol><p><i>ноздр(я́)</i> (nozdr(já)) +&lrm; <i>-ева́тый</i> (-evátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nostril</li></ol><p><i>ноздря́</i> (nozdrjá) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nostril</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nozdra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shaman of the Sami people</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami <i>но̄ййт</i> (nōjjt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>нойд</i> (nojd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>нойд</i> (nojd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noyan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) knockout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>knockout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>combat sports</i>) to knock out</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to greatly shock or upset</li></ol><p><i>нока́ут</i> (nokáut) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) knockdown (act or the condition of being knocked down)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>knockdown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) noctuid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Noctuidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) noctuine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Noctuinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) nocturne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nocturne</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>nocturnus</i>, further from Latin <i>nox</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ноль</i> (nolʹ); a (little) zero</li><li>a little circle (symbol)</li></ol><p>From <i>ноль</i> (nolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zero (0)</li><li>nil, nothing</li><li>precisely, exactly (time)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cipher, nobody, nonentity, a paltry person</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) nothing, naught</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Null</i>, from earlier form <i>Nulla</i>, from Italian <i>nulla</i>, from Latin <i>nūlla</i>, feminine of <i>nūllus</i> (“no one”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;né</i>, <i>&#42;óynos</i> and <i>&#42;-lós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>o'clock (sharp), hundred hours (used to indicate a time exactly on the hour)</li></ol><p>Pronunciation of the two zeros, representing the minutes in a written time.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nomad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) nomadology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nomenclature</li><li>(historical,&#32;collective) nomenklatura</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nomenclatural, nomenclatorial</li><li>high-ranking</li></ol><p><i>номенклату́ра</i> (nomenklatúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominal number</li><li>number (various senses)</li><li>size (of clothes or shoes)</li><li>room (in a hotel)</li><li>number, issue (e.g. of a magazine or a newspaper)</li><li>item, turn, trick (e.g., in a circus)</li></ol><p>First attested as <i>ну́мер</i> (númer), which is now dated, from German <i>Nummer</i>. The form <i>но́мер</i> (nómer) may have been influenced by the French <i>nombre</i>, or this variation simply reflected a perception of the short <i>u</i> in German as closer to the Russian <i>о</i>. Compare the case of the doublets <i>нуль</i> (nulʹ) and <i>ноль</i> (nolʹ), and the corresponding German <i>Null</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephone number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>нумера́ция</i> (numerácija)</li></ol><p><i>номерова́ть</i> (nomerovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ordinal number</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) size (<i>of clothes or shoes</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) number, issue (<i>e.g. of a magazine or a newspaper</i>)</li><li>numbered</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valet (<i>of a hotel or a similar facility</i>)</li></ol><p><i>но́мер</i> (nómer) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>нумерова́ть</i> (numerovátʹ)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>нумерова́ть</i> (numerovátʹ), influenced by <i>но́мер</i> (nómer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>но́мер</i> (nómer, “hotel room, license plate, etc.”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ticket, check (especially one issued in a coat check)</li></ol><p><i>но́мер</i> (nómer) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) face value, par</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) nominal</li><li>nominal, titular, formal</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) rated</li></ol><p><i>номина́л</i> (nominál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) nominative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nomination</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>nominacja</i>, from Latin <i>nominatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nominate (to name someone as candidate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>non-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) now, nowadays, today, currently (as opposed to the past)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nъně</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nonillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>30</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonconformist</li></ol><p>From <i>нон-</i> (non-) +&lrm; <i>конформи́ст</i> (konformíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) nonsense</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nonsense</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) now, nowadays, today, currently (as opposed to the past)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́не</i> (nóne). Compare <i>ны́нче</i> (nýnče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, burrow, form (burrowed animal dwelling)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foxhole, dugout</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) small, dark room; hole, diggings</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noradrenaline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a Norwegian (a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norwegian</li></ol><p><i>Норве́гия</i> (Norvégija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norwegian language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Norwegian</li></ol><p><i>Норве́гия</i> (Norvégija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a Norwegian (a person)</li></ol><p>From Norwegian Bokmål <i>Norge</i> (“Norway”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) north</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) norther (north wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>noord</i> (“north”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) northwest</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) northwester (<i>wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>норд</i> (nord) +&lrm; <i>вест</i> (vest)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) northeast</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) northeaster (<i>wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>норд</i> (nord) +&lrm; <i>ост</i> (ost)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nori (red algae of the genus <i>Pyropia</i>&#59; used in the preparation of sushi in a form of thin sheets)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>海苔</i> (のり, nori).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Norilsk, of Norilsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Нори́ль(ск)</i> (Norílʹ(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нора́</i> (norá)</li><li>mink (<i>animal and fur</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mink</li></ol><p><i>но́рка</i> (nórka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) abbreviation of <i>норма́льно</i> (normálʹno); fine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>norm, standard</li><li>rate, quota</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>norma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>normalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to normalise</li></ol><p>French <i>normalis(er)</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>normally</li><li>OK, it’s all right</li></ol><p><i>норма́льный</i> (normálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>statistics</i>) normal distribution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>normalcy (state of being normal)</li></ol><p><i>норма́льный</i> (normálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>normal</li><li>proper</li><li>sane</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) perpendicular to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>norm, standard, (in plural) rules, (in plural) regulation(s).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Normativ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regulatory</li></ol><p><i>нормати́вный</i> (normatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>правово́й</i> (pravovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>normative, standard</li></ol><p>From German <i>normativ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standardization, regulation (establishment of norms or legal limits)</li><li>standardization (<i>of the rates of production</i>)</li><li>rationing</li><li>(<i>algebra</i>) absolute value (type of function in advanced algebra, a generalization of the basic concept of absolute value)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) valuation of an asset</li></ol><p>From <i>нормирова́ть</i> (normirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije) (suffix-stressed) or <i>норми́ровать</i> (normírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to standardize, to regulate (to establish norms or legal limits)</li><li>to standardize (<i>the rates of production</i>)</li><li>to ration</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) to valuate (an asset)</li></ol><p>From <i>но́рма</i> (nórma) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ) (final-syllable-stressed variant) or <i>но́рма</i> (nórma) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ) (antepenultimate-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) normie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>normies</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in a burrow (<i>of an animal</i>)</li><li>employed to hunt animals in burrows</li></ol><p><i>нора́</i> (norá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) temper, disposition</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>норовъ</i> (norovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;norvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>нрав</i> (nrav).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) restive, jibbing, tetchy</li></ol><p><i>но́ров</i> (nórov) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim</li><li>to strive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nose</li><li>bow, prow <i>(boats)</i></li><li>forepart, point</li><li>horn <i>(of an anvil)</i></li><li>headland, promontory, point <i>(geology)</i></li><li>lip <i>(for pouring)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nosъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néh₂s</i>. Cognate with English <i>nose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) having a large nose</li></ol><p><i>нос</i> (nos) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big-nosed, nasute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nosatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-nosed person or animal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>proboscis monkey</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nosačь</i> (“big-nosed man/animal”), from <i>&#42;nosъ</i> (“nose”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ačь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one that can be worn/put on (of clothes)</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>нос</i> (nos): (little) nose</li><li>bill, beak (<i>of an insect or bird</i>)</li><li>spout (a tube through which liquid is poured or discharged)</li><li>face (forward part of some tools)</li><li>toe (that part of a shoe or sock covering the toe)</li></ol><p><i>нос</i> (nos) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretcher (simple litter designed to carry a sick, injured, or dead person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrier, bearer, holder</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “carry”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native speaker</li></ol><p>Literally, “language carrier”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to bear (a burden)</li><li>(<i>of wind, water, etc.</i>) to carry along, to drive</li><li>to have something (e.g. a name, sideburns, a gun...)</li><li>to wear (clothes, shoes or makeup)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nositi</i>, iterative form of <i>&#42;nesti</i> (whence also <i>нести́</i> (nestí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to rush around, to run/drive around (on a high speed)</li><li>to float around, to fly around</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fuss over, to pay too much attention to, to be obsessed with (an idea)</li><li>(of smells, sounds, etc.) to spread</li><li>to wear, to be worn (of clothes)</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>нос</i> (nos): (<i>colloquial</i>) big nose</li></ol><p><i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carrying</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wearing (clothes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bearing (a child)</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) durable, strong, not easily worn out (<i>of clothes or shoes</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carrying a lot of eggs</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to wear”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nose; nasal</li><li>(relational,&#32;nautical) prow, bow; forward</li></ol><p><i>нос</i> (nos) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handkerchief (cloth for wiping the face, eyes, nose or hands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sock</li><li>toe (<i>of a boot, shoe or sock</i>)</li><li>toe (<i>of a foot or hoof; especially the tip of the toe</i>)</li><li>projection, nose (<i>e.g. of a plane</i>)</li><li>diminutive of <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”)</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók); compare etymology 2. Sense development is "little nose" > "projection" > "toe" > "sock". Less likely from <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to wear”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p><p><i>нос</i> (nos) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) nose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinoceros</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog, “horn”), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ῥινόκερως</i> (rhinókerōs). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>носорі́г</i> (nosoríh), Belarusian <i>насаро́г</i> (nasaróh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rhinoceros</li></ol><p><i>носоро́г</i> (nosoróg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rhinoceros</li></ol><p><i>носоро́г</i> (nosoróg) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>носо́к</i> (nosók): (small) sock</li></ol><p><i>носо́к</i> (nosók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be nostalgic, to feel nostalgia</li></ol><p>Derived from New Latin <i>nostalgia</i> (probably via an intermediate language) + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nostalgic</li></ol><p><i>ностальги́я</i> (nostalʹgíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nostalgia</li></ol><p>Ultimately from New Latin <i>nostalgia</i>, coined from Ancient Greek <i>νόστος</i> (nóstos, “returning home”) + <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”), translating German <i>Heimweh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coati</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>нос</i> (nos).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying, bearing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) note</li><li>(<i>diplomatic</i>) note, memorandum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) notary</li><li>notarial</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>notarial</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homily, lecture, reprimand</li><li>notation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>но́та</i> (nóta): (faint) note</li></ol><p><i>но́та</i> (nóta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) music</li></ol><p><i>но́ты</i> (nóty) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) music, sheet music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>nōtа</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>know-how</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>know-how</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>no-name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) no-name</li></ol><p><i>ноуне́йм</i> (nounéjm) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) laptop</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>ноутбу́к</i> (noutbúk, “laptop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) laptop, notebook computer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>notebook</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laptop</li></ol><p><i>ноутбу́к</i> (noutbúk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) a kind of tub used for performing household jobs, particularly bathing small children and kneading dough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ночьвы</i> (nočĭvy), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nъťьvy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend the night, stay for the night, to stay overnight</li></ol><p><i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noctiluca scintillans</li></ol><p><i>ноч(ь)</i> (noč(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ): night</li></ol><p><i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodging for the night</li><li>overnight stay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ночьлѣгъ</i> (nočĭlěgŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;noťьlěgъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ, “night”) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>нічлі́г</i> (ničlíh), Belarusian <i>начле́г</i> (načljéh), Polish <i>nocleg</i>, Czech <i>nocleh</i>, Slovak <i>nocľah</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doss-house, doss, flophouse (sleeping-place for the homeless)</li></ol><p><i>ночле́г</i> (nočlég) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overnighter (one who stays overnight)</li><li>one who stays overnight in a doss-house</li></ol><p>From <i>ночле́г</i> (nočlég) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) doss, doss-house, night-lodging</li></ol><p><i>ночле́г</i> (nočlég) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth, night fly</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;euphemistic) prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightshirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) nightlight</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) moonlighter, someone who works at night</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) pilot specializing in night flights</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) nocturnal bird, nocturnal animal</li></ol><p><i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) A mouse-eared bat or myotis (of the genus <i>Myotis</i>).</li></ol><p>From <i>ночни́к</i> (nočník, diminutive of 'night', literally “nightling; nightlet”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) night, nighttime</li><li>nocturnal</li></ol><p>From <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightclub (establishment that is open late at night)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightshirt</li></ol><p><i>ночн(о́й)</i> (nočn(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night, nighttime (period of time from sundown to sunup)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;nókʷts</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ночь</i> (nočĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;noťь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;náktis</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i>. Doublet of <i>нощь</i> (noščʹ, “night (archaic)”), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at night, by night</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) overnight stay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend the night, stay for the night, to stay overnight (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>ночева́ть</i> (nočevátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>ночева́ть</i> (nočevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load, burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying</li><li>wearing (clothes)</li><li>bearing (a child)</li></ol><p><i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>де́нно и но́щно</i> (dénno i nóščno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;ecclesiastic&#32;or&#32;Church Slavonic) night, nighttime (period of time from sundown to sunup)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>нощь</i> (noštĭ) / <i>ⱀⱁⱋⱐ</i> (noštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;noťь</i>. Doublet of <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ), inherited from Old East Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ноя́брь</i> (nojábrʹ) or <i>ноя́бря</i> (nojábrja, “November”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>November</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ноꙗбрь</i> (nojabrĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>ноѩбр҄ь</i> (nojębrʹĭ), from Koine Greek <i>Νοέμβριος</i> (Noémbrios). Ultimately from Latin <i>november</i>, from <i>novem</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i> (“nine”) + Latin <i>-ber</i> from <i>-bris</i>, an adjectival suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) November</li></ol><p><i>ноя́брь</i> (nojábrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>e.g., for example, for instance</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>наприме́р</i> (naprimér).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>character, temperament</li><li>disposition, temper</li><li>manner, custom, discipline</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) customs</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нравъ</i> (nravŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>нравъ</i> (nravŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;norvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>но́ров</i> (nórov), from Old East Slavic <i>норовъ</i> (norovŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated with English <i>like</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>нрав</i> (nrav) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moral instruction, moral edification, moralizing</li><li>moral (<i>at the end of a story</i>)</li></ol><p><i>нрав</i> (nrav) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уче́ние</i> (učénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>morally, ethically</li></ol><p><i>нра́вственный</i> (nrávstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morals, morality</li></ol><p><i>нра́вственный</i> (nrávstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moral (referring to morality)</li><li>moral, ethical (conforming to a standard of right behavior)</li></ol><p><i>нра́вство</i> (nrávstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) custom, practice</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) mutual attraction, love</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нравьство</i> (nravĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>нрав</i> (nrav) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>well</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) yeah, yep (impolite, sometimes implying a question - so what?)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sarcastic</i>) yeah right, get off with you! (<i>disbelief</i>)</li><li>well, yes; sure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well, well then, right (<i>in the sense of marking a transition to a new thought, action or summary, as if pronounced – in either English or Russian – ending with a falling tone and a subsequent pausa</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>now!, come!, come on!</li><li>well, let's see</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>there there (conveys comfort)</li><li>well well (pondering or consideration)</li><li>come on (expression of disbelief)</li></ol><p><i>ну</i> (nu) +&lrm; <i>ну</i> (nu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) now then, well</li></ol><p><i>ну</i> (nu) +&lrm; <i>-с</i> (-s)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>noob</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nubian (<i>male</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ну́бия</i> (Núbija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Compare Ukrainian <i>нубі́єць</i> (nubíjecʹ) and Belarusian <i>нубі́ец</i> (nubíjec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nubian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ну́бия</i> (Núbija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;derogatory) nouveau riche, new money, newly rich person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nouveau riche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nougat (mixture consisting of egg white and a sweetener, variously mixed with almonds or hazelnuts, or used without nuts as a filler in candy bars)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nougat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nudism (the belief in or practice of social, non-sexual nudity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nudist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) to force, to compel</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) to tire, to exhaust</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk annoyingly or monotonously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to annoy, to bother</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause melancholy, to bore</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to persistently beg, to pester (<i>у</i> (u))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нꙋдити</i> (nuditi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nuditi</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>нуди́ти</i> (nudýty), Belarusian <i>нудзі́ць</i> (nudzícʹ), Old Church Slavonic <i>ноудити</i> (nuditi, “to force, to compel”) and <i>нѫдити</i> (nǫditi) (per Vasmer, with secondary nasalization), Bulgarian <i>ну́дя</i> (núdja, “to force, to compel”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ну̏дити</i> (“to offer, to obtain”) (1sg. <i>ну̏дӣм</i>), Slovene <i>núditi</i> (“to force”) (1sg. <i>nȗdim</i>), Czech <i>nudit</i> (“to bore”), Slovak <i>nudiť</i> (“to bore”), Polish <i>nudzić</i> (“to bore, to annoy”).</p><p>Apparently related to <i>ну́дить</i> (núditʹ, “to compel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boring</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>нудни́й</i> (nudnýj), from Old Ruthenian <i>нꙋдны́й</i> (nudnýj, “boring”), from Old East Slavic <i>нꙋдьнъ</i> (nudĭnŭ, “gruelling, difficult”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nudьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ну́да</i> (núda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) need, want</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нужа</i> (nuža), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nuďa</i>. Doublet of <i>нужда́</i> (nuždá), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, want, request</li><li>need, indigence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ноужда</i> (nužda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nuďa</i>. Displaced the inherited East Slavic form <i>нужа</i> (nuža).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to be hard up, to be needy</li></ol><p><i>нужда́</i> (nuždá) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>needy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) toilet, lavatory, loo</li></ol><p><i>ну́жа</i> (núža) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary, needed [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>для</b> (dlja, + genitive) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘for an action to be done’</small>]</li><li>right, proper, relevant, appropriate, desired (of certain objects or qualities to achieve a goal)</li></ol><p><i>ну́жа</i> (núža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) guardsman, warrior of a Mongolian khan</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Mongolian <i>нөхөр</i> (nöxör).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) nucleotide</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>nucleotide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nucleotide</li></ol><p><i>нуклеоти́д</i> (nukleotíd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nucleus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) nucleon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zero</li><li>zeroth</li><li>(slang,&#32;in the plural) the noughties (2000s)</li></ol><p><i>нуль</i> (nulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zero (0)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brand new</li></ol><p><i>нуль</i> (nulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) number</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) room (in a hotel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numbering, enumeration</li><li>number system</li></ol><p><i>нумерова́ть</i> (numerovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to number, to enumerate</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from Polish <i>numerować</i> or from German <i>numer(ieren)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ). Also derivable from <i>ну́мер</i> (númer, “(<i>archaic</i>) number”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numismatist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>numismate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numismatics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>numismatique</i> or German <i>Numismatik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>numismatic</li></ol><p><i>нумизма́тика</i> (numizmátika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nunatak (mountaintop surrounded by glacial ice)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) nunation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nunchaku</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>双節棍</i> (ぬんちゃく, nunchaku) or <i>ヌンチャク</i> (nunchaku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman Catholicism</i>) nuncio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>nūntius</i> (“envoy”). Doublet of <i>ано́нс</i> (anóns), <i>денонса́ция</i> (denonsácija), and <i>проно́нс</i> (pronóns).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to hang (one's head sadly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chickpea, garbanzo</li></ol><p>Via Turkic from Persian نخود (noxod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chickpea</li></ol><p><i>нут</i> (nut) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coypu, nutria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>nutria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inside, the inward nature (especially of a person), gut feeling</li><li>inside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to hide something/someone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, nowadays, today, at present</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>нꙑнѣ</i> (nyně), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyně</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nu</i> (“now”), compare with Ukrainian <i>ни́ні</i> (nýni), German <i>nun</i>, English <i>now</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present, today's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) now, nowadays, today</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>нꙑнѣ</i> (nyně) (whence <i>ны́не</i> (nýne)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyně</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nu</i> (“now”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyrnǫti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>нирıати</i> (nirıati), Belarusian <i>ны́рка</i> (nýrka), Bulgarian <i>ни́рна</i> (nírna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dive</li><li>pochard</li></ol><p><i>ныря́ть</i> (nyrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p><p><i>ныря́ть</i> (nyrjátʹ, “to dive”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p><p>For the meaning development compare English <i>duck</i> (&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dūkaną</i> (“to dive, to bend down”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diver</li></ol><p><i>ныря́ть</i> (nyrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diving</li></ol><p><i>ныря́ть</i> (nyrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyřati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>нырну́ть</i> (nyrnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whimper, to whine, to yammer, to snivel, to bellyache, to cry (to complain peevishly)</li><li>to ache</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyti</i> (“to yearn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ньюйо́ркец</i> (nʹjujórkec).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) New York</li><li>from New York</li></ol><p><i>Нью-Йо́рк</i> (Nʹju-Jórk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>New Yorker, person from New York</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newsmaker (one whose actions make the headlines)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>newsmaker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newsroom (the office of a news organisation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>newsroom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newton (derived unit of force)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>newton</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ньютон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Newton</i>; particularly, the English mathematician, physicist, astronomer, theologian, and author Isaac Newton, December 25, 1642 – March 20, 1726/27</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Newton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) NEP (New Economic Policy)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>но́вая экономи́ческая поли́тика</i> (nóvaja ekonomíčeskaja polítika).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: НЭП)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) NEP (New Economic Policy)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>но́вая экономи́ческая поли́тика</i> (nóvaja ekonomíčeskaja polítika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Nepman</li></ol><p>From <i>НЭП</i> (NEP, “New Economic Policy”) + German <i>Mann</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nepman</li></ol><p><i>нэ́пман</i> (nɛ́pman) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>netsuke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>根付</i> (netsuke).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) nude</li><li>nu (the Greek letter Ν&#47;ν)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nu</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>νῦ</i> (nû).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuance, shade</li><li>a small detail, a piece of additional information</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>nuance</i> (“nuance, shade, hue”). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>nūbēs</i>.</p><p>For the meaning development compare Italian <i>sfumatura</i> (&lt; Latin <i>fūmus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) nude</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>nudes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Nüshu (syllabic script which was used exclusively among the ethnic Yao women of Jiangyong, Hunan, China)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>女書</i>&nbsp;/ <i>女书</i> (Nǚshū, literally “women's script”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ню̀й-шу́</i> (njùj-šú)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) sniveller, crybaby</li></ol><p><i>ню́(хать)</i> (njú(xatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, from or relating to the city of Nuremberg, Bavaria, Germany</li></ol><p><i>Ню́рнберг</i> (Njúrnberg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Belarusian <i>нюрнбергскі</i> (njurnbjerhski), Polish <i>norymberski</i>, Czech <i>norimberský</i>, Ukrainian <i>ню́рнберзький</i> (njúrnberzʹkyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scent, nose (the sense of smell)</li><li>gut feeling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ňuxъ</i>, itself of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nose, sniffer</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) scent</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pursuer, stalker</li></ol><p><i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sniffer (<i>of drugs or tobacco</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(for) smelling</li></ol><p><i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snuff (fine-ground tobacco)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to sniff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ňuxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) aroma tester in the perfume industry (working by smelling perfume samples)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person who sniffs (e.g. tobaccos, drugs, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) commonly used by Russian-speaking anime communities. Often used as a synonym of "hello" or "how cute".</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>にゃあ</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ня́ня</i> (njánja)</li></ol><p><i>ня́нька</i> (njánʹka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nurse, to take care of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ня́ня</i> (njánja, “nurse, nanny, babysitter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nurse</li><li>nanny</li><li>babysitter</li></ol><p>Since ancient times, from reduplication in children’s speech. The related form <i>ня́нька</i> (njánʹka) was first attested in the 12th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bottom layer of a shallow lake full of scum and mud</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) scum (algae)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cute girl, babe, cutie</li></ol><p>Probably Finno-Ugric.</p><p>From Japanese <i>にゃあ</i> (“meow, sound of a cat”) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cute girl, babe, cutie</li></ol><p><i>ня́ша</i> (njáša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cute</li></ol><p><i>ня́ша</i> (njáša) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) palate (roof of the mouth); palatal</li></ol><p>From <i>нёбо</i> (njóbo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palate</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>небо</i> (nebo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nebo</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;néba</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nébʰos</i>. Doublet of <i>не́бо</i> (nébo), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>нём</i> (njom):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>prepositional of <i>онъ</i> (on)</li><li>prepositional of <i>оно́</i> (onó)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>не́кий</i> (nékij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>неме́цкий</i> (neméckij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>не́мец</i> (némec).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>немо́й</i> (nemój).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>нет</i> (net).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>О</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>O</i>/<i>o</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>about, of, on</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>literary</i>) against, upon</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>о</i> (o), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o</i>, ultimately a natural expression. Compare Ancient Greek <i>ὦ</i> (ô), Latin <i>ō</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ebʰi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh my God!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh my God!, oh God!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>prefix added to adjectives or nouns to produce factitive verbs in conjunction with <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), i.e. verbs with the meaning of making (someone or something) the mentioned characteristic or property</li><li>prefix added to adjectives or nouns to produce fientive verbs in conjunction with <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) &#43; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) or with <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), i.e. verbs with the meaning of becoming or receiving the mentioned characteristic or property</li><li>prefix added to verbs of motion: about, around</li><li>prefix added to nouns: close by, around</li><li>prefix added to verbs: completely, to the full extent</li><li>prefix added to verbs, with the suffix <i>-ся</i> (-sja): to fail at (some action)</li><li>prefix added to verbs, with the suffix <i>-ся</i> (-sja): to recover from (some action)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ebʰi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh, ho, ho!; wow!; oh, my goodness!; oh, oh, oh! (expresses surprise, admiration, approval, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Japan, Korea, etc.</i>) miai, omiai (formal marriage interview)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>お見合い</i> (おみあい, o-miai).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ow</li><li>uh-oh (expressing fear)</li><li>golly (expressing surprise)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>о-пэ́р</i> (o-pɛ́r)</li></ol><p>From French <i>au pair</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;rare) au pair (a single girl or boy staying as a guest with a host family)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>au pair</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>о́рдер</i> (órder).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) initialism of <i>отде́льная артиллери́йская брига́да</i> (otdélʹnaja artilleríjskaja brigáda); separate artillery brigade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oasis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>oasis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>about, of, on</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>against, upon</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ab</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ebʰi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>о-</i> (o-) (<i>before certain letters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>both</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оба</i> (oba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oba</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰóh₁</i>, whence also Lithuanian <i>abù</i>, Latvian <i>abi</i>, Latin <i>ambo</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἄμφω</i> (ámphō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>used to express surprise, typically also implying content or irony</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch bolete (<i>Leccinum scabrum</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>both</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оба дъва</i> (oba dŭva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oba dъva</i>. By surface analysis, univerbation of <i>оба</i> (oba) +&lrm; <i>два</i> (dva). Compare Italian <i>ambedue</i>, Romanian <i>amândoi</i>, Polish <i>obydwa</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>obadva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to charm; to fascinate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bewitch; to enchant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) A sweet-talking, crafty or deceptive person.</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>обая́ть</i> (obajátʹ), <i>оба́ивать</i> (obáivatʹ, “to charm”). Possible link with the proper noun <i>Оба́им</i> (Obáim) / <i>Обо́им</i> (Obóim).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Northern) layabout; inactive person</li></ol><p>From Eastern dialectal <i>опа́кша, опаку́ша</i> (opákša, opakúša, “clumsy and sluggish person; left-hander”). From <i>о́пак</i> (ópak, “backwards”). Related to dialectal <i>пакша́</i> (pakšá, “left hand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stop thinking clearly, to take leave of one's senses</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to grow dumb with astonishment, to be dumb-founded, to drop one's jaw</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used to express astonishment.</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>балде́ть</i> (baldétʹ), or (per Vasmer) <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>балда́</i> (baldá) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fool, simpleton, booby, goof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) to bankrupt (to force into bankruptcy)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>банкро́т</i> (bankrót) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) to go bankrupt (to become bankrupt)</li></ol><p><i>обанкро́тить</i> (obankrótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) A stubborn, disobedient person.</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) idiot, fool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ob(ъ)turъ, &#42;ob(ъ)tura</i>, from the verb <i>&#42;ob(ъ)turiti</i>. Compare <i>&#42;turiti</i> (“to chase, drive away”). Related to <i>отурить, отурять</i> (oturitʹ, oturjatʹ, “capsize, confuse, deceive”).</p><p>According to Anikin, the word's age renders unlikely the Turkic etymology proposed by Vasmer, who derives the term from Turkic <i>bahadyr</i>, comparing Kazakh <i>батыр</i> (batyr, “hero”). Compare also Hindi <i>बहादुर</i> (bahādur, “brave”), Mongolian <i>баатар</i> (baatar, “hero, warrior”), Russian <i>богаты́рь</i> (bogatýrʹ, “warrior”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) but</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attraction, charm, fascination, spell (quality of inspiring delight or admiration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charming, fascinating</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ба́ять</i> (bájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to charm; to fascinate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bewitch; to enchant</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бая́ть</i> (bajátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) attractive person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collapse (of building, stock price, exchange rate, economy, etc.)</li><li>landslide, rockfall (fall of earth, rocks, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to fall, to crumble</li><li>to heap round</li><li>to roll [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in flour, bread crumbs, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обвали́ть</i> (obvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>обваля́ть</i> (obvaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to collapse, to cave in, to crumble</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) to collapse, to fall, to dive</li></ol><p><i>обва́ливать</i> (obválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to fall, to crumble</li><li>to heap round</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to collapse, to cave in, to crumble</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) to collapse, to fall, to dive</li></ol><p><i>обвали́ть</i> (obvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in flour, bread crumbs, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour boiling water (on)</li><li>to burn, to steam, to scald (with boiling water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour boiling water (<i>on</i>)</li><li>to burn, to steam, to scald (<i>with boiling water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry <i>(in church)</i>, to wed</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>венча́ть</i> (venčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be married <i>(in church)</i>, to get married <i>(in church)</i></li></ol><p><i>обвенча́ть</i> (obvenčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead (<i>someone around</i>)</li><li>to encircle, to surround</li><li>to outline, to contour</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to dodge, to outplay</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan, to cast one's eyes over</li></ol><p>Literally, “to surround with a glance”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoodwink, to cheat, to dupe, to swindle</li></ol><p>Literally, “to lead around one's finger”; cf. wrap around one's little finger.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan, to cast one's eyes over</li></ol><p>Literally, “to surround with the eyes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weathered, weather-beaten, weather-worn (<i>of a house, someone's face, etc.</i>)</li><li>chapped (<i>of the hands, lips, etc.</i>)</li><li>stale, dried-out (<i>of food</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chap</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́трить</i> (-vétritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn out, dilapidated, shabby</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>обветша́ть</i> (obvetšátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay; to dilapidate; to become decrepit</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ветша́ть</i> (vetšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind around; to coil around</li></ol><p><i>обви́ть</i> (obvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation, charge (act of accusing or charging with a crime)</li></ol><p><i>обвини́ть</i> (obvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accuser</li><li>(<i>law</i>) prosecutor</li></ol><p><i>обвини́ть</i> (obvinítʹ, “to accuse”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accusative (producing accusations&#59; accusatory)</li></ol><p><i>обвини́ть</i> (obvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse, to charge, to blame [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘of/with/for’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вини́ть</i> (vinítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be charged <i>(with)</i>, to be accused <i>(of)</i>, to be prosecuted <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>обвини́ть</i> (obvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accused (defendant)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accused, charged</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse, to charge [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘of/with’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обвини́ть</i> (obvinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be charged <i>(with)</i>, to be accused <i>(of)</i>, to be prosecuted <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>обвиня́ть</i> (obvinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to droop, to sag</li><li>to become/grow flabby</li></ol><p><i>обви́снуть</i> (obvísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to droop, to sag</li><li>to become/grow flabby</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind around; to coil around</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead (<i>someone around</i>)</li><li>to encircle, to surround</li><li>to outline, to contour</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to dodge, to outplay</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan, to cast one's eyes over</li></ol><p>Literally, “to surround with a glance”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoodwink, to cheat, to dupe, to swindle</li></ol><p>Literally, “to lead around one's finger”; cf. wrap around one's little finger.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan, to cast one's eyes over</li></ol><p>Literally, “to surround with the eyes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envelop</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to ensnare and subjugate to one's will</li></ol><p><i>обволо́к-</i> (obvolók-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envelop</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to ensnare and subjugate to one's will</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fascinate, to charm, to enchant, to captivate</li></ol><p><i>обворожи́ть</i> (obvorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob; to plunder</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ворова́ть</i> (vorovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fascinating, enchanting, bewitching</li></ol><p><i>обворожи́ть</i> (obvorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fascinate, to charm, to enchant, to captivate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ворожи́ть</i> (vorožítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encircle by tying [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something else’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>tie around</i> with the instrumental object as the direct object and the accusative object preceded by <i>around</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encircle by tying [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something else’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>tie around</i> with the instrumental object as the direct object and the accusative object preceded by <i>around</i>)</li></ol><p><i>обвяза́ть</i> (obvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw around</li><li>to pick (a bone)</li></ol><p><i>обглода́ть</i> (obglodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw around</li><li>to pick (a bone)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>глода́ть</i> (glodátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss</li><li>to provide <i>(for)</i>, to make a provision <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>обговори́ть</i> (obgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss</li><li>to provide <i>(for)</i>, to make a provision <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overtaking (e.g. by a vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overtake</li><li>to excel, to surpass</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be scorched, to be burnt on the edges</li><li>to get burned</li><li>to sunburn, to get sunburnt</li></ol><p><i>обгоре́ть</i> (obgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burned, charred</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sunburned</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scorched (<i>of earth, vegetation, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>обгоре́ть</i> (obgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be scorched, to be burnt on the edges</li><li>to get burned</li><li>to sunburn, to get sunburnt</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw round</li></ol><p><i>обгры́зть</i> (obgrýztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw round</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour over, to douse</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seize (with a feeling)</li></ol><p><i>обда́ть</i> (obdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour over, to douse</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seize (with a feeling)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do out of one's share, to not let have one's fair share, to share unfairly</li><li>to deprive</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do out of one's share, to not let have one's fair share, to share unfairly</li><li>to deprive</li></ol><p><i>обдели́ть</i> (obdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Pskov) a south-easterly wind</li></ol><p>According to Anikin, possibly related to the Indo-European cognates of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;desnъ(jь)</i> (“right”). Perhaps borrowed from the Baltic cognate: compare Lithuanian <i>dẽšinas</i> (“right”). For the meaning "south-east" Anikin compares Sanskrit <i>दक्षिण</i> (dákṣiṇa, “right; south”) and Irish <i>deas</i> (“right; to the south”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip, to peel, to skin, to flay, to flense</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scratch <i>(on)</i>, to scrape <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fleece, to rook</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) to rob blind</li></ol><p>Literally, “to strip (someone) like a young linden tree”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtful, thought-out; deliberate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to think over, to weigh</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection, pondering, deliberation</li></ol><p><i>обду́мывать</i> (obdúmyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to think over, to weigh</li></ol><p>From <i>обду́мать</i> (obdúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat</li></ol><p><i>обдури́ть</i> (obdurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>дури́ть</i> (durítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run (everywhere or many places)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop by (everywhere or many places)</li><li>to run around [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>вокру́г</b> (vokrúg, + genitive) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>to run [<i>with</i> <b>ми́мо</b> (mímo, + genitive) <small>‘past a location’</small>] (to bypass while running)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunch, dinner (the main meal of the day, which is served between noon and 3 p.m.)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) noon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obědъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to eat lunch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обѣдати</i> (obědati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obědati</i>, from <i>&#42;obědъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ati</i>, ultimately from <i>&#42;ěsti</i> (“to eat”). By surface analysis, <i>обе́д</i> (obéd, “dinner, lunch”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ, “infinitive suffix”). See also <i>объеда́ть</i> (obʺjedátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lunch/dinner (referring to the main meal of the day, which is eaten from about 1 or 2 p.m. to 3 p.m.)</li></ol><p><i>обе́д</i> (obéd) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunchbreak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dining table</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become poor, to become difficult</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бедне́ть</i> (bednétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to make poor or poorer, to impoverish</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) mass, liturgy</li></ol><p>From <i>обе́д</i> (obéd, “lunch”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), presumably due to its occurrence around lunchtime.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to make poor or poorer, to impoverish</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to become poor or poorer, to become impoverished</li></ol><p><i>обедни́ть</i> (obednítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p><p>Variant of <i>обедне́ть</i> (obednétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dine (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>обе́дать</i> (obédatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>обе́дать</i> (obédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run around [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>вокру́г</b> (vokrúg, + genitive) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>to run [<i>with</i> <b>ми́мо</b> (mímo, + genitive) <small>‘past a location’</small>] (to bypass while running)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run (everywhere or many places)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop by (everywhere or many places)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anesthetization, anesthesia</li></ol><p><i>обезбо́ливать</i> (obezbólivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anesthetize</li></ol><p><i>обезбо́лить</i> (obezbólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anaesthetic, painkiller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analgesic, anaesthetic, pain-relieving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anesthetize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>боль</i> (bolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) dewaterability</li></ol><p><i>обезво́живаемый</i> (obezvóživajemyj, “dewaterable”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dehydration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to render harmless</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безвре́д(ный)</i> (bezvréd(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearance, removal (of something harmful); decontamination</li></ol><p>From <i>обезвре́живать</i> (obezvréživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to render harmless</li></ol><p><i>обезвре́дить</i> (obezvréditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decapitate, to behead</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>глава́</i> (glavá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decapitate, to behead</li></ol><p><i>обезгла́вить</i> (obezglávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to immobilise, to render motionless</li></ol><p><i>обездви́жить</i> (obezdvížitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to immobilise, to render motionless</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бездви́ж(ный)</i> (bezdvíž(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destitute, indigent, impoverished, extremely poor</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) miserable, wretched</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indigent person, destitute person</li><li>miserable person, wretch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make destitute, to leave penniless</li></ol><p><i>обездо́лить</i> (obezdólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make destitute, to leave penniless</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>до́ля</i> (dólja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat-free, fatless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disinfection, sterilization, decontamination</li></ol><p><i>обеззара́живать</i> (obezzaráživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disinfect</li></ol><p><i>обеззара́зить</i> (obezzarázitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deforest</li></ol><p><i>обезле́сить</i> (obezlésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deforest</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лес</i> (les, “forest”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), or equivalently from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безле́с(ный)</i> (bezlés(nyj), “treeless”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depopulate</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), or equivalently from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безлю́д(ный)</i> (bezljúd(nyj), “uninhabited”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depopulate</li></ol><p><i>обезлю́дить</i> (obezljúditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose the ability to walk from fatigue, to "lose legs"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave someone without legs, to usually figuratively, to as in making someone extremely tired</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure, to make ugly</li></ol><p><i>обезобра́зить</i> (obezobrázitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure, to make ugly</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безобра́зие</i> (bezobrázije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безопа́с(ный)</i> (bezopás(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to disarm</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to calm (an angry person)</li></ol><p><i>обезору́жить</i> (obezorúžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to disarm</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to calm (an angry person)</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>ору́жие</i> (orúžije, “weapon, arms”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), or equivalently from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безору́ж(ный)</i> (bezorúž(nyj), “unarmed”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose the ability to use hands, to "lose hands"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave someone without hands, to usually figuratively, to as in making someone extremely tired</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>без-</i> (bez-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) to become insane, to go crazy, to go mad</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>безу́мие</i> (bezúmije) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkey, ape</li><li>(slang,&#32;rare) the @ sign</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обезьꙗ́на</i> (obezĭjána), <i>обозьꙗ́на</i> (obozĭjána), further borrowed from Persian ابوزینه (abuzina), بوزینه (buzina, “simian”). Cognates include Old Ruthenian <i>обезъꙗ́на</i> (obezʺjána).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) monkey</li></ol><p><i>обезья́на</i> (obezʹjána) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>обезья́на</i> (obezʹjána)</li></ol><p><i>обезья́на</i> (obezʹjána) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkey-house</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) jail in a Russian police station for pre-charge detention</li></ol><p><i>обезья́на</i> (obezʹjána, “ape”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ape, to monkey (imitate)</li><li>to act the ape/monkey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apeman</li></ol><p><i>обезья́на</i> (obezʹjána, “ape, monkey”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “man”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apishness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obelisk</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὀβελίσκος</i> (obelískos), diminutive of <i>ὀβελός</i> (obelós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whitewash, to redeem (to remove suspicion or condemnation)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to whiten (to make white)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бели́ть</i> (belítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whitewash, to redeem (to remove suspicion or condemnation from)</li><li>(historical,&#32;Russia,&#32;late 15th century to 1917) to exempt from taxes or duties (in whole or in part)</li></ol><p><i>обели́ть</i> (obelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amulet, charm</li></ol><p>Derived from оберега́ть (oberegátʹ), and then бере́чь (beréčʹ), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;berťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard <i>(against)</i>, to protect <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p>From the <i>оберег-</i> (obereg-) stem of <i>обере́чь</i> (oberéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>оберіга́ти</i> (oberiháty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard <i>(against)</i>, to protect <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap up</li><li>to wind <i>(around)</i>, to wrap <i>(around)</i></li><li>to turn</li><li>to turn over, to overturn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to arrange, to get done</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) to turn <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself (with, in)</li><li>to turn one's head, to turn around</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) to turn, to change [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘into something’</small>]</li><li>to manage, to get by; to wangle</li><li>to (go and) come back; (finance) to make a full circle</li></ol><p><i>оберну́ть</i> (obernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;physics) overtone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Oberton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exsanguinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse, to perplex, to baffle</li><li>to discourage, to dishearten, to dispirit</li></ol><p><i>обескура́жить</i> (obeskurážitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse, to perplex, to baffle</li><li>to discourage, to dishearten, to dispirit</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>кура́ж</i> (kuráž) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>securing, guaranteeing, ensuring</li><li>security, guarantee</li><li>provision, supply</li></ol><p>From <i>обеспе́чить</i> (obespéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije) (without stress shift) or from <i>обеспе́чить</i> (obespéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije) (with stress shift).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provision</li><li>means, wealth, material well-being/security, prosperity</li></ol><p><i>обеспе́ченный</i> (obespéčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-off, affluent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide, to supply</li><li>to assure, to secure, to guarantee, to ensure (to make sure and secure)</li></ol><p><i>обеспе́чить</i> (obespéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>обеспе́чивать</i> (obespéčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide, to supply</li><li>to assure, to secure, to guarantee, to ensure (to make sure and secure)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>беспе́ч(ный)</i> (bespéč(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>обеспе́чить</i> (obespéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concern, worry, anxiety</li></ol><p><i>обеспоко́енный</i> (obespokójennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worried, concerned, alarmed (experiencing concern, e.g. of a person)</li><li>worried, concerned, alarmed (expressing concern, e.g. of a look)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry, to trouble, to make anxious</li><li>to disturb, to perturb</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>беспоко́ить</i> (bespokóitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become anxious/uneasy</li><li>to become concerned <i>(over, about)</i></li></ol><p><i>обеспоко́ить</i> (obespokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to weaken, to get weak (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бесси́л(ие)</i> (bessíl(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enervate, to weaken, to deprive of strength (someone)</li></ol><p><i>обесси́лить</i> (obessílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enervate, to weaken, to deprive of strength (someone)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бесси́лие</i> (bessílije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to immortalize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бессме́рт(ный)</i> (bessmért(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depreciate, to devalue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depreciate, to devalue</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бес-</i> (bes-) +&lrm; <i>цен(а́)</i> (cen(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dehumanize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dehumanize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, to defame</li><li>to abuse (a woman)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бесче́стить</i> (besčéstitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, to defame</li><li>to abuse (a woman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vow, promise</li><li>pledge</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) + Old Russian and Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣтъ</i> (větŭ, “agreement”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;větъ</i> (“council, talk”). Related words include <i>наве́т</i> (navét), <i>изве́т</i> (izvét), <i>отве́т</i> (otvét), <i>приве́т</i> (privét), <i>сове́т</i> (sovét), <i>ве́че</i> (véče), <i>отвеча́ть</i> (otvečátʹ), <i>отве́тить</i> (otvétitʹ), <i>завеща́ть</i> (zaveščátʹ), and <i>совеща́ться</i> (soveščátʹsja). Probably cognate with Polish <i>witać</i> (“to welcome”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) promised</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обѣтованьнъ</i> (obětovanĭnŭ, “promised”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>обѣтованъ</i> (obětovanŭ, “promised”), which is the past passive participle of Old Church Slavonic <i>обѣтовати</i> (obětovati, “to promise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;rare) to promise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обѣчати</i> (oběčati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oběťati</i>. Doublet of <i>обеща́ть</i> (obeščátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise (vow)</li></ol><p><i>обеща́ть</i> (obeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>promised</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise, to pledge</li><li>to promise, to give grounds for expectation <i>(perfective is not used)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обѣщати</i> (oběštati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oběťati</i>. Displaced native Russian <i>обеча́ть</i> (obečátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough handle, stilt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appeal (application for the removal of a cause to a superior judge for reexamination)</li></ol><p><i>обжа́ловать</i> (obžálovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make/bear/lodge a complaint <i>(against)</i>, (<i>law</i>) to appeal <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жа́ловать</i> (žálovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry or roast on all sides</li></ol><p><i>обжа́рить</i> (obžáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry or roast on all sides</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wring out, to press out (<i>from all sides</i>)</li><li>to hug closely (<i>from all sides</i>), to surround closely</li><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to reap (the whole of)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, to scorch</li><li>to fire, to burn, to calcine</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn oneself</li></ol><p><i>обже́чь</i> (obžéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle (a place), to adapt (a place) for habitation</li></ol><p><i>обжи́ть</i> (obžítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, to scorch</li><li>to fire, to burn, to calcine</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wring out, to press out (<i>from all sides</i>)</li><li>to hug closely (<i>from all sides</i>), to surround closely</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to reap (the whole of)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>settled, adapted for habitation (<i>of an area</i>)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>обжи́тый</i> (obžítyj)/<i>о́бжитый</i> (óbžityj), past passive participle of <i>обжи́ть</i> (obžítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle (a place), to adapt (a place) for habitation</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glutton</li></ol><p>Related to <i>обжирать</i> (obžiratʹ, “gorging”), from <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gluttony</li></ol><p><i>обжо́ра</i> (obžóra) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire, to provide oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>завести́сь</i> (zavestísʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire, to provide oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>заводи́ться</i> (zavodítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to cut the back of the hair of someone as a part of an old military custom for being unfit for battles</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to condemn, to reject, to discard (someone) (by recognizing someone as generally unfit)</li></ol><p><i>обзаты́лить</i> (obzatýlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to cut the back of the hair of someone as a part of an old military custom for being unfit for battles</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to condemn, to reject, to discard (someone) (by recognizing someone as generally unfit)</li></ol><p><i>об–</i> (ob–) +&lrm; <i>заты́л(ок)</i> (zatýl(ok)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consecutively ring everybody or many on the phone</li></ol><p><i>обзвони́ть</i> (obzvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consecutively ring everybody or many on the phone</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>woodworks</i>) rough edge, wane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field of view/vision</li><li>review, overview, roundup, survey</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) inspection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obzorъ</i>. By surface analysis, deverbal from <i>обозре́ть</i> (obozrétʹ), with typical Indo-European nominal <i>o</i>-vocalism (compare <i>набо́р</i> (nabór) and <i>набра́ть</i> (nabrátʹ), the latter with future tense <i>наберу́</i> (naberú) etc. showing typical Indo-European present/future verbal <i>e</i>-vocalism).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name-calling</li></ol><p><i>обзыва́ть</i> (obzyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name-call, to call someone an offensive name, to give something an abusive or offensive name</li><li>to call out to someone, to attract someone's attention by shouting to them or calling their name</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obi (a kimono sash)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>帯</i> (おび, obi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to upholster, to pad</li><li>to strike off, to beat off, to knock off, to shake off</li></ol><p><i>оби́ть</i> (obítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upholstering</li><li>upholstery</li></ol><p><i>обива́ть</i> (obivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resentment (feeling), offense; grudges</li><li>hurt, grievance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvida</i>, from <i>&#42;obviděti</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;viděti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend, to hurt someone's feelings, to abuse, to insult</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take offence <i>(at)</i>, to be offended <i>(by)</i>, to feel hurt <i>(by)</i>, to resent</li></ol><p><i>оби́деть</i> (obídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>offensively (in an offensive manner)</li></ol><p><i>оби́дный</i> (obídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offensive, slighting</li><li>vexing, annoying, frustrating, disappointing</li></ol><p><i>оби́да</i> (obída) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>touchily, sensitively, quickly taking offence</li></ol><p><i>оби́дчивый</i> (obídčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>touchy, susceptible, quick to take offence</li></ol><p><i>оби́деть</i> (obídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offender</li></ol><p><i>оби́деть</i> (obídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend, to hurt someone's feelings, to abuse, to insult</li></ol><p><i>оби́деть</i> (obídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take offence <i>(at)</i>, to be offended <i>(by)</i>, to feel hurt <i>(by)</i>, to resent</li></ol><p><i>обижа́ть</i> (obižátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>resentfully</li></ol><p><i>оби́женный</i> (obížennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offended, having a grudge, resentful (<i>experiencing offense or resentment; of a person</i>)</li><li>offended, resentful (<i>expressing offense or resentment; of a look, tone, face, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obilьje</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>obilí</i> (“grain, cereals”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abundantly</li></ol><p><i>оби́льный</i> (obílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant, plentiful, rich</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obilъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>оби́лие</i> (obílije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hesitate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hesitate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) ambages, circumlocutions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick, to collect (<i>from a tree or garden</i>)</li><li>to clean out (to cheat someone out of their possessions)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to gather in folds or ruffles</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to rob</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obirati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhabited</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abode (slightly dated&#58; residence)</li></ol><p><i>обита́ть</i> (obitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habitation</li></ol><p><i>обита́ть</i> (obitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant, dweller</li></ol><p>From <i>обита́ть</i> (obitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell in, to live in, to inhabit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обитати</i> (obitati). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вита́ть</i> (vitátʹ, “to hover about”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living, dwelling, inhabiting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) abode, dwelling place</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) cloister, monastery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обитѣль</i> (obitělĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obitělь</i>, from <i>&#42;obitati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ělь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>обита́ть</i> (obitátʹ, “to inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-ель</i> (-elʹ).</p><p>Cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>obitelj</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to upholster, to pad</li><li>to strike off, to beat off, to knock off, to shake off</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom, use</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) rules of church singing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obixodъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>оби-</i> (obi-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll smooth, to smoothen by rolling</li><li>to wear smooth</li><li>to make round by rolling</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to make hackneyed, to make common, to deprive of one's particular nature</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repeat until perfected</li><li>to test out, to break in (<i>e.g. a car</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover in (e.g. breadcrumbs) by rolling</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll (something) around (something else)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to douse</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to travel around (an area)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testing out, breaking in</li><li>repeating until perfection</li><li>trial run</li></ol><p><i>обката́ть</i> (obkatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll smooth, to smoothen by rolling</li><li>to wear smooth</li><li>to make round by rolling</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to make hackneyed, to make common, to deprive of one's particular nature</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repeat until perfected</li><li>to test out, to break in (<i>e.g. a car</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover in (e.g. breadcrumbs) by rolling</li><li>to roll (something) around (something else)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to douse</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to travel around (an area)</li></ol><p><i>обката́ть</i> (obkatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>обкати́ть</i> (obkatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to douse</li></ol><p><i>обкати́ть</i> (obkatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(round)</i>, to edge, to cover</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) to close <i>(round)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay/put round oneself</li></ol><p><i>обкла́дывать</i> (obkládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regional/oblast committee <i>(esp. of a party or trade union)</i></li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>областно́й комите́т</i> (oblastnój komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob</li></ol><p><i>обкра́д-</i> (obkrád-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite around, to gnaw round</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>кус</i> (kus) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite around, to gnaw round</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>region (Russian administrative unit)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>о́бласть</i> (óblastʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) battue</li><li>roundup</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oblava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assess, to impose (tax, fine), to tax</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be liable</li></ol><p><i>облага́ть</i> (oblagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>show of benevolence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do a favor, to befriend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ennoble, to refine</li><li>to improve (e.g. animal breeds or quality of plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ennoble, to refine</li><li>to improve (e.g. animal breeds or quality of plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession, possessing, having; enjoyment (e.g. of rights)</li></ol><p><i>облада́ть</i> (obladátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possessor, owner, holder</li></ol><p><i>облада́ть</i> (obladátʹ, “to possess”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to possess, to have, to own [<i>with</i> instrumental of the thing possessed]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обладати</i> (obladati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvoldati</i>, imperfective of <i>&#42;obolsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblast administration</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>областна́я администра́ция</i> (oblastnája administrácija, “oblast administration”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blunder, to make a mistake, to screw up</li></ol><p><i>облажа́ть</i> (oblažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark at someone or something</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to call names, to scold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) cloud</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>облакъ</i> (oblakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obolkъ</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>оболоко</i> (oboloko). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>во́лок</i> (vólok) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) (akin to <i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ), <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off</li><li>to cajole, to persuade</li></ol><p>From <i>облома́ть</i> (oblomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>обломи́ть</i> (oblomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swindle, to cheat, to humbug</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat kindly</li><li>to commend</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to give protection to</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ласка́ть</i> (laskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat kindly</li><li>to commend</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to give protection to</li></ol><p><i>обласка́ть</i> (oblaskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oblast; regional</li></ol><p><i>о́бласть</i> (óblastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>region, oblast, province</li><li>dominion, sovereignty</li><li>domain, sphere, field (area of knowledge)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>область</i> (oblastĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obolstь</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>оболость</i> (obolostĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) wafer, host</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) capsule</li><li>paper seal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to array, to vest, to robe in (to clothe&#47;ornament)</li><li>to dress up, to deck out</li></ol><p><i>облачи́ть</i> (oblačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on, to wear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘vestments, robes or other attire of a formal and symbolic nature’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to put on, to wear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘any clothing’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>облача́ть</i> (oblačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attire, vestments (one's dress or clothes)</li></ol><p><i>облачи́ть</i> (oblačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to array, to vest, to robe in (to clothe&#47;ornament)</li><li>to dress up, to deck out</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>облачити</i> (oblačiti). By surface analysis, <i>об</i> (ob) +&lrm; <i>влачи́ть</i> (vlačítʹ). Doublet of <i>обволочи́ть</i> (obvoločítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on, to wear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘vestments, robes or other attire of a formal and symbolic nature’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to put on, to wear [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘any clothing’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>облачи́ть</i> (oblačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́блако</i> (óblako): (small) cloud</li></ol><p><i>о́блако</i> (óblako) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloudiness, clouds, cloud cover</li></ol><p><i>о́блачный</i> (óblačnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cloudy, made of, covered with or related to clouds</li><li>(<i>of weather</i>) sparsely cloudy, with a few separated clouds and gaps of clear sky between</li><li>(computing,&#32;data science) internet, virtual</li></ol><p><i>о́блако</i> (óblako) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark at someone or something</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to call names, to scold</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ла́ять</i> (lájatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;vulgar) to vomit (on someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li><i>(cloth)</i> to fit tightly, to cling <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>обле́г-</i> (oblég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alleviate, to lighten, to facilitate, to ease, to relieve (pain)</li></ol><p><i>облегчи́ть</i> (oblexčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relief, alleviation, easement</li></ol><p><i>облегчи́ть</i> (oblexčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alleviate, to lighten, to facilitate, to ease, to relieve (pain)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with relief, with a sense of relief</li></ol><p><i>облегчённый</i> (oblexčónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light (<i>in weight</i>), lightweight</li><li>simplified</li><li>lightened, relieved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered with ice, to ice over</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to freeze solid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to stiffen from cold</li></ol><p><i>обледене́ть</i> (obledenétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy</li></ol><p><i>обледене́ть</i> (obledenétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́лый</i> (-élyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered with ice, to ice over</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to freeze solid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to stiffen from cold</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ледене́ть</i> (ledenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;obsolete,&#32;military) to encircle</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лежать</i> (ležatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(fur etc)</i> to grow bare, to come out/off</li><li><i>(paint etc)</i> to peel off</li></ol><p><i>обле́зть</i> (obléztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shabby, bare</li></ol><p><i>обле́зть</i> (obléztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(fur etc)</i> to grow bare, to come out/off</li><li><i>(paint etc)</i> to peel off</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe <i>(in)</i></li><li>to invest <i>(with)</i>, to vest <i>(with)</i>, to envelop <i>(in)</i></li><li>to shape <i>(as)</i>, to present <i>(as)</i></li></ol><p><i>обле́к-</i> (oblék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to put on clothes, to don garment</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take the form of</li></ol><p><i>облека́ть</i> (oblekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick all over (with), to paste all over (with); to cover (with), to plaster (with)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to besiege, to cling (to); to swarm all over (of flies)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea buckthorn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sea buckthorn</li></ol><p><i>облепи́ха</i> (oblepíxa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick all over (with), to paste all over (with); to cover (with), to plaster (with)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to besiege, to cling (to); to swarm all over (of flies)</li></ol><p><i>облепи́ть</i> (oblepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(round)</i></li><li><i>(rumor, news etc)</i> to spread <i>(all over)</i></li><li><i>(leaves)</i> to fall, <i>(plant)</i> to lose/shed its leaves</li><li>to fly <i>(all over)</i></li><li>to test, to test-fly</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(round)</i></li><li><i>(rumor, news etc)</i> to spread <i>(all over)</i></li><li><i>(leaves)</i> to fall, <i>(plant)</i> to lose/shed its leaves</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li><i>(cloth)</i> to fit tightly, to cling <i>(to)</i></li><li>to clothe <i>(in)</i></li><li>to invest <i>(with)</i>, to vest <i>(with)</i>, to envelop <i>(in)</i></li><li>to shape <i>(as)</i>, to present <i>(as)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ).</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>облѣщи</i> (oblěšti). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ). Doublet of <i>обволо́чь</i> (obvolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to put on clothes, to don garment</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take the form of</li></ol><p><i>обле́чь</i> (obléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) circumflex (<i>of an accent or tone</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spilling (over), pouring (over), dousing (with water), affusion (pouring of liquid)</li></ol><p><i>облива́ть</i> (oblivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (over); to spill (over), to sluice (over)</li></ol><p><i>обли́ть</i> (oblítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a shower bath, to sponge down</li><li>to pour/spill over oneself</li></ol><p><i>облива́ть</i> (oblivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, debenture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>obligacja</i>, from Latin <i>obligātiō</i>, from <i>obligō</i> + <i>-tiō</i>, from <i>ob-</i> + Latin <i>ligō</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>обяза́тельство</i> (objazátelʹstvo) (<~ <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick, to lick all over, to lick clean</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick oneself</li></ol><p><i>облиза́ть</i> (oblizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick off</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лизну́ть</i> (liznútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick, to lick all over, to lick clean</li></ol><p><i>облиза́ть</i> (oblizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick oneself</li></ol><p><i>обли́зывать</i> (oblízyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face, look, aspect, appearance, figure, image</li><li>mindset, temper, character, make-up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oblikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with leaves, leafy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (over); to spill (over), to sluice (over)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ли́ть</i> (lítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a shower bath, to sponge down</li><li>to pour/spill over oneself</li></ol><p><i>обли́ть</i> (oblítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to face (<i>with stone, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facing, lining, cladding, revetment (<i>of stone, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>облицева́ть</i> (oblicevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to face (<i>with stone, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>облицева́ть</i> (oblicevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to blame, to denounce</li><li>to unmask, to expose</li><li>to reveal, to display, to manifest, to point</li></ol><p><i>обличи́ть</i> (obličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation</li><li>exposure</li></ol><p><i>обличи́ть</i> (obličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>обли́чье</i> (oblíčʹje)</li></ol><p><i>о́блик</i> (óblik) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to blame, to denounce</li><li>to unmask, to expose</li><li>to reveal, to display, to manifest, to point</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-личи́ть</i> (-ličítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, look</li><li>face; guise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обличье</i> (obličĭje), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obličьje</i>, from <i>&#42;oblikъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ьje</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;likъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>аблі́чча</i> (ablíčča), Ukrainian <i>обли́ччя</i> (oblýččja). By surface analysis, <i>о́блик</i> (óblik) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxation</li><li>tax, levy</li></ol><p><i>обложи́ть</i> (obložítʹ, “to tax”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assess, to impose (tax, fine), to tax</li><li>to put <i>(round)</i>, to edge, to cover</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) to close <i>(round)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay/put round oneself</li></ol><p><i>обложи́ть</i> (obložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover (front and back of a book or a magazine)</li></ol><p><i>обложи́ть</i> (obložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of rain</i>) completely covering the sky with rain clouds</li></ol><p><i>обложи́ть</i> (obložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>manufacturing</i>) flash (excess material that seeps into insufficiently tightly sealed gaps during casting of a moulded product)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) sleet</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) graupel, snow pellets</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обли́ть</i> (oblítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean one's elbows [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>облокоти́ться</i> (oblokotítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>о́блако</i> (óblako)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean one's elbows [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ло́коть</i> (lókotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking off (breaking a piece off of something)</li><li>something broken off, fragment</li><li>place of fracture</li><li>crack or bump at a place of fracture</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) molding</li><li>(<i>military</i>) breastwork</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) failure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) frustration</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обломи́ть</i> (oblomítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off</li><li>to cajole, to persuade</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oblomovism (indolent apathy)</li></ol><p><i>Обло́мов</i> (Oblómov) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina), after the nobleman who rarely leaves his bed in Ivan Goncharov's novel <i>Oblomov</i> (1859). Coined by Nikolay Dobrolyubov in an essay on that novel entitled «Что такое обломовщина?» (“What is Oblomovism?”), also published in 1859.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) debris, wreckage, flotsam</li></ol><p>From <i>обло́м</i> (oblóm) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), itself a deverbal of <i>обломи́ть</i> (oblomítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spoilsport</li></ol><p><i>обло́м</i> (oblóm) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel, to shell</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лупи́ть</i> (lupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to peel (<i>of skin, etc.</i>), to peel off (<i>of paint, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>облупи́ть</i> (oblupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peeled, husked</li><li>peeling, flaking off (<i>of skin, plaster, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel, to shell</li></ol><p><i>облупи́ть</i> (oblupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to peel (<i>of skin, etc.</i>), to peel off (<i>of paint, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>облу́пливать</i> (oblúplivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) irradiation (exposure to radiation)</li><li>exposure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boot (part of a carriage where the coachman sits)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>облукъ</i> (oblukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oblǫkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;lǫkъ</i> (“bow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) round, roundish, rounded</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) plump, fat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obьlъ</i>, from Old East Slavic <i>обьлъ</i> (obĭlŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alopecia, loss of hair</li></ol><p><i>облысе́ть</i> (oblysétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become bald</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>лысе́ть</i> (lysétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>любо́й</i> (ljubój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select</li></ol><p><i>облюбова́ть</i> (obljubovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) covered in vomit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;vulgar) to vomit (on someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test, to test-fly</li></ol><p><i>облета́ть</i> (obletátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to daub all over</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dunk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip (to lower into a liquid)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>макну́ть</i> (maknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception</li><li>deceit, fraud</li><li>trick</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обману́ть</i> (obmanútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) blende (<i>from the fact that they are compounds of metals but are not metallic in appearance</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deception, deceit, lie</li><li>(<i>art</i>) trompe l'oeil</li></ol><p><i>обману́ть</i> (obmanútʹ, “to deceive”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dishonest, fraudulent, deceptive, deceitful, false (dishonest; based on fraud or deception)</li></ol><p><i>обма́н</i> (obmán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive, to cheat, to trick, to swindle</li><li>to disappoint, to let down</li></ol><p>Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>obmanuti</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a mistake by making false expectations; to overreach oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceptive, delusive</li></ol><p><i>обман(у́ть)</i> (obman(útʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheat, deceiver, fraud, faker, trickster</li></ol><p><i>обман</i> (obman) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive, to cheat, to trick, to swindle</li><li>to disappoint, to let down</li></ol><p><i>обману́ть</i> (obmanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a mistake by making false expectations; to overreach oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dirty mouth, to badmouth, to abuse, to cuss out (to insult, to swear dirtily (at))</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>матери́ть</i> (materítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind around, to wrap around</li></ol><p><i>обмота́ть</i> (obmotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange, to swap</li></ol><p><i>обменя́ть</i> (obmenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange, to swap</li></ol><p><i>обме́нивать</i> (obménivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exchange</li></ol><p><i>обме́н</i> (obmén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange, to swap</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange, to swap</li></ol><p><i>обменя́ть</i> (obmenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measurement (act of measuring)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) false measuring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to faint, to lose consciousness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go numb, to freeze (<i>from fright, shock, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skip a beat (<i>of one's heart</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure (all dimensions)</li><li>to cheat in measure</li><li>to look up and down, to scan (with one's eyes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure <i>(all dimensions)</i></li><li>to cheat in measure, to give short measure</li><li>to scan (with one's eyes)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>обме́рить</i> (obméritʹ)</li><li>to measure (ascertain the quantity of a unit)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat in measuring, to give short measure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dusting (dust removing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to faint, to lose consciousness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go numb, to freeze (<i>from fright, shock, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skip a beat (<i>of one's heart</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-мира́ть</i> (-mirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to secularize (<i>oneself</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to secularize (<i>oneself</i>)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мирск(о́й)</i> (mirsk(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to carefully think through or think over</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мозгова́ть</i> (mozgovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to think through, to think over</li></ol><p><i>обмозгова́ть</i> (obmozgovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a slip in speaking</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to utter casually</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mention, to let slip</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare Polish <i>obmówić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slip of the tongue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) threshing</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) threshed grain; (amount of) grain left after threshing</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обмолоти́ть</i> (obmolotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to thresh</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>молоти́ть</i> (molotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frostbite, to cause frostbite in (a body part)</li></ol><p><i>обморо́зить</i> (obmorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frostbite, to cause frostbite in (a body part)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faint, swoon, syncope (the act of fainting)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obmorkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;o(b)</i> + <i>&#42;morkъ</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool, to trick, to dupe, to pull the wool over someone's eyes</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>моро́чить</i> (moróčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull someone's leg</li></ol><p>Literally, “to trick (someone's) head”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind around, to wrap around</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winding</li><li>puttee</li></ol><p><i>обмота́ть</i> (obmotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outfit, uniform (a set of clothing)</li><li>fitting out (with uniform)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash, to bathe</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to celebrate, to drink <i>(to a purchase or deal)</i></li></ol><p><i>обмы́ть</i> (obmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash, to bathe</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to celebrate, to drink <i>(to a purchase or deal)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become soft, to soften</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become flabby, to go limp</li></ol><p><i>обмя́кнуть</i> (obmjáknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become soft, to soften</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become flabby, to go limp</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>мя́кнуть</i> (mjáknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>нагле́ть</i> (naglétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure, to reassure, to encourage</li><li>to raise hopes (of), to give hope</li></ol><p><i>обнадёжить</i> (obnadjóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure, to reassure, to encourage</li><li>to raise hopes (of), to give hope</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>надёжа</i> (nadjóža) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undress, to bare</li><li>to uncover, to expose</li><li>to unsheathe, to draw</li><li>to bare, to unleaf</li><li>to reveal</li></ol><p><i>обнажи́ть</i> (obnažítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off one's clothes, to become naked</li><li>to wear overly revealing clothes</li><li>to become exposed (<i>of body parts</i>)</li><li>to become exposed (<i>by removing the top layer</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘by some cause’</small>]</li><li>to lose vegetation, to become bare (<i>e.g. of trees</i>)</li><li>to become clear, to be revealed</li><li>to become defenseless, to be exposed to the enemy</li></ol><p><i>обнажа́ть</i> (obnažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undress, to bare</li><li>to uncover, to expose</li><li>to unsheathe, to draw</li><li>to bare, to unleaf</li><li>to reveal</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>наго́й</i> (nagój) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off one's clothes, to become naked</li><li>to wear overly revealing clothes</li><li>to become exposed (<i>of body parts</i>)</li><li>to become exposed (<i>by removing the top layer</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘by some cause’</small>]</li><li>to lose vegetation, to become bare (<i>e.g. of trees</i>)</li><li>to become clear, to be revealed</li><li>to become defenseless, to become exposed to the enemy</li></ol><p><i>обнажи́ть</i> (obnažítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nude</li></ol><p><i>обнажённый</i> (obnažónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked, bare (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) not having leaves</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sincere, candid</li><li>naked (<i>vice etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>cash, redeem (<i>a cheque, a coupon, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promulgation, announcement, publication</li></ol><p><i>обнаро́довать</i> (obnaródovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promulgate, to publish</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finding, discovery, detecting</li></ol><p><i>обнару́жить</i> (obnarúžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disclose, to show, to reveal, to display</li><li>to discover, to find out, to detect, to reveal, to spot</li></ol><p><i>обнару́жить</i> (obnarúžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be revealed, to be discovered, to be found, to show</li></ol><p><i>обнару́живать</i> (obnarúživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disclose, to show, to reveal, to display</li><li>to discover, to find out, to detect, to reveal, to spot</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>нару́ж(ный)</i> (narúž(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be revealed, to be discovered, to be found, to show</li></ol><p><i>обнару́жить</i> (obnarúžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out (clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break in (clothing) (to wear to the point of feeling comfortable), to feel more comfortable <i>(in an item of clothes, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>обноси́ть</i> (obnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose <i>(with)</i></li><li>to serve <i>(round)</i></li><li>to miss [pass over] while serving, to fail to serve <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hug, to embrace</li><li>to engulf, to envelop</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hug, to embrace one another</li></ol><p><i>обнима́ть</i> (obnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) hug, cuddle</li></ol><p><i>обнима́ние</i> (obnimánije) +&lrm; <i>-а́шка</i> (-áška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(in expressions only)</i></li></ol><p><i>обнима́ть</i> (obnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impoverishment, destitution</li></ol><p><i>обнища́ть</i> (obniščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become impoverished, to become destitute</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>нища́ть</i> (niščátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new thing, novelty, new acquisition, new dress</li></ol><p>From <i>обнови́ть</i> (obnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a); deverbal.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renovate, to refresh, to repair, to make as good as new</li><li>to renew, to update</li><li>to use/try out for the first time, to give a first trial, <i>(clothes)</i> to wear for the first time</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-нови́ть</i> (-novítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get renewed</li></ol><p><i>обнови́ть</i> (obnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) new thing, recently acquired thing (<i>usually clothing</i>)</li></ol><p><i>обно́ва</i> (obnóva) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal</li><li>renovation</li><li>update</li><li>aggiornamento (reform of some teachings of Roman Catholic Church in 1962–65)</li></ol><p><i>обнови́ть</i> (obnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renovate, to refresh, to repair, to make as good as new</li><li>to renew, to update</li><li>to use/try out for the first time, to give a first trial, <i>(clothes)</i> to wear for the first time, to christen</li></ol><p><i>обнови́ть</i> (obnovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/get renewed</li></ol><p><i>обновля́ть</i> (obnovljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose <i>(with)</i></li><li>to serve <i>(round)</i></li><li>to miss [pass over] while serving, to fail to serve <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out (clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break in (clothing) (to wear to the point of feeling comfortable), to feel more comfortable <i>(in an item of clothes, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to zeroize, to set to zero, to zero out, to /dev/zero</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) to finish off, to waste, to khalass</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>нуль</i> (nulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sniff around, to sniff on all sides</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sniff around, to sniff on all sides</li></ol><p><i>обню́хать</i> (obnjúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hug, to embrace</li><li>to engulf, to envelop</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hug, to embrace one another</li></ol><p><i>обня́ть</i> (obnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>об</i> (ob): about, of, on; against, upon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ebʰi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>об-</i> (ob-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick, to collect (<i>from a tree or garden</i>)</li><li>to clean out (to cheat someone out of their possessions)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to gather in folds or ruffles</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to rob</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to generalize, to summarize</li></ol><p><i>обобщи́ть</i> (obobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generalization</li><li>summarizing</li></ol><p><i>обобщи́ть</i> (obobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to generalize, to summarize</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-общи́ть</i> (-obščítʹ), or derived as a factitive verb from <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generalized, abstract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrich, to make rich</li><li>to enrich, to make worthy</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to concentrate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обогатити</i> (obogatiti). By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бога́тый</i> (bogátyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrich oneself</li></ol><p><i>обогати́ть</i> (obogatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrich, to make rich</li><li>to enrich, to make worthy</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to concentrate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обогащати</i> (obogaštati). By surface analysis, <i>обогати́ть</i> (obogatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrich oneself</li></ol><p><i>обогаща́ть</i> (obogaščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enrichment</li><li>improvement, enrichment</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) concentration</li></ol><p><i>обогати́ть</i> (obogatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overtake</li><li>to excel, to surpass</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to round, to skirt, to double</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming, heating</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обогрева́ть</i> (obogrevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heater</li></ol><p><i>обогрева́ть</i> (obogrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat, to warm</li><li>to give one's warmth/affection, to comfort</li></ol><p><i>обогре́ть</i> (obogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat, to warm</li><li>to give one's warmth/affection, to comfort</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rim, felloe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvodъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́бод</i> (óbod, “rim”)</li><li>bezel</li></ol><p><i>о́бод</i> (óbod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) colon <i>(digestive system)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rim</li><li>(relational,&#32;medicine) colon; colonic</li></ol><p><i>ободо́к</i> (obodók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip, to peel, to skin, to flay, to flense</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scratch <i>(on)</i>, to scrape <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fleece, to rook</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob blind</li></ol><p>Literally, “to strip (someone) like a young linden tree”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encouragement; cheering up</li></ol><p><i>ободри́ть</i> (obodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encourage, to hearten, to cheer up, to reassure</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бодри́ть</i> (bodrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encourage, to hearten, to cheer up, to reassure</li></ol><p><i>ободри́ть</i> (obodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adorable, adored</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoration (the act of adoring)</li></ol><p><i>обожа́ть</i> (obožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adorer, admirer</li></ol><p><i>обожа́ть</i> (obožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adore, to love</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to deify, to revere, worship</li></ol><p>Imperfective form of now dated <i>обо́жить</i> (obóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) further from <i>бог</i> (bog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wait <i>(for a while)</i>, to wait <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apotheosis, deification</li><li>deification, idolization, worshipping</li></ol><p>From <i>обожестви́ть</i> (obožestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deify, to divinize</li></ol><p><i>обожестви́ть</i> (obožestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string of carts, wagon train, string of sledges</li><li>(<i>military</i>) transport, train</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obozъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>воз</i> (voz). Compare cognate Polish <i>obóz</i> (“camp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name-call, to call someone an offensive name, to give something an abusive or offensive name</li><li>to call out to someone, to attract someone's attention by shouting to them or calling their name</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embitter <i>(someone)</i>, to rouse the spite <i>(of someone)</i></li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>злить</i> (zlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow/get angry</li></ol><p><i>обозли́ть</i> (obozlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to confuse one thing or person with another, to mistake, to not recognize</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denote, to designate, to indicate</li><li>to mark, to label</li></ol><p><i>обозна́чить</i> (oboznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to appear</li></ol><p><i>обознача́ть</i> (oboznačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designation</li></ol><p><i>обозна́чить</i> (oboznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denote, to designate, to indicate</li><li>to mark, to label</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to appear</li></ol><p><i>обозна́чить</i> (oboznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) observer; reviewer; editor (newspaper, television, etc)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) (<i>computing</i>) (web) browser</li></ol><p><i>обозрева́ть</i> (obozrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survey, to view, to look round</li><li>to review, to overview</li></ol><p><i>обозре́ть</i> (obozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>review, commentary, survey</li><li>revue</li><li>viewing, surveying</li></ol><p><i>обозре́ть</i> (obozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survey, to view, to look round</li><li>to review, to overview</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreseeable (able to be foreseen or anticipated)</li></ol><p>From <i>обозре́ть</i> (obozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallpaper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>обо́и</i> (obói): wallpaper</li></ol><p><i>обо́и</i> (obói) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) charger, cartridge clip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) range, string</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) iron ring</li></ol><p>From <i>объя́ть</i> (obʺjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), compare <i>по́йма</i> (pójma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go around, to bypass (to pass by going around)</li><li>to walk through, to wander around, to go around, to tour, to go to (many places in an area)</li><li>to overtake, to outrank, to beat, to outperform (a peer or competitor)</li><li>to evade, to circumvent, to avoid, to bypass (the law etc.)</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cost; to come to</li><li>to treat [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a specific way’</small>]</li><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) to be able to do without</li><li>to turn out, to work out</li></ol><p><i>обойти́</i> (obojtí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>красть</i> (krastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obol <i>(Ancient Greek coin)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to refute, to confute</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>лжи́вый</i> (lžívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alburnum, sapwood</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Ancient Greek <i>φελλός</i> (phellós, “cork”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, envelope, shell</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) capsule, outward form</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) membrane</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) jacket, casing</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) shell (<i>environment</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>оболочка</i> (oboločka), from <i>оболочити</i> (oboločiti) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka) or from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvolčьka</i>, from <i>&#42;obvolka</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьka</i>, from <i>&#42;obelťi</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;velťi</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>obłóczka</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>Bezug</i> (&lt; <i>ziehen</i> <&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teuhaną</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i>), the term also functions as a suffix meaning <i>case</i> or <i>cover</i>, as in <i>Kopfkissenbezug</i> (compare <i>на́волочка</i> (návoločka), Polish <i>powłoczka</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) nincompoop, simpleton</li></ol><p>Attested no later than 1839.</p><p>Possibly a blend of болта́ть (boltátʹ, “to dangle, to babble”) and Latin <i>-tus</i>, from seminary jargon influenced by Latin <i>stultus</i> (“foolish, stupid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seducer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seductive, beguiling, alluring</li></ol><p><i>обольсти́ть</i> (obolʹstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to seduce (<i>sexually</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to seduce, to deceive</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>льстить</i> (lʹstitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seduced by, to be beguiled by, to succumb or give in to (<i>as a result of seduction or deception</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обольсти́ть</i> (obolʹstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to seduce (<i>sexually</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to seduce, to deceive</li></ol><p><i>обольсти́ть</i> (obolʹstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seduced by, to be beguiled by, to succumb or give in to (<i>as a result of seduction or deception</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обольща́ть</i> (obolʹščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seduction</li><li>self-deception, illusion</li><li>temptation</li></ol><p><i>обольсти́ть</i> (obolʹstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be stupefied</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>млеть</i> (mletʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mossy, moss-grown</li></ol><p><i>обомше́ть</i> (obomšétʹ, “to become overgrown with moss”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered or overgrown with moss</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>мох</i> (mox) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense of smell, olfaction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvonjanьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to nose, to olfact</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>воня́ть</i> (vonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cords with which bast shoes are fastened to the foot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obora</i> (“string, twine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn</li><li>to turn over</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;imperfective) to arrange, to get done</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) to turn <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>обороти́ть</i> (oborotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around</li><li>to turn into [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обора́чивать</i> (oboráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person dressed in rags; toerag; ragamuffin</li></ol><p><i>оборва́ть</i> (oborvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нец</i> (-ánec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ragged, tattered, torn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear/pluck off/round</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to break, to tear</li><li>to break off (ties) <i>(<i>figurative</i>), the figurative shift made in <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</i></li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to rip</li></ol><p><i>оборва́ть</i> (oborvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person dressed in rags</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person dressed in rags</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>борзе́ть</i> (borzétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flounce</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blockhead, dimwit, dumbass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defence, defense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bornь</i>. Related to <i>брань</i> (branʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defensive</li></ol><p><i>оборони́ть</i> (oboronítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend</li></ol><p><i>оборо́на</i> (oboróna) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend oneself <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hold the line</li></ol><p><i>оборони́ть</i> (oboronítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) defense industry, military-industrial complex</li></ol><p><i>оборо́на</i> (oboróna) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) defense</li></ol><p><i>оборо́на</i> (oboróna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>readiness for defense</li></ol><p><i>обороноспосо́бный</i> (oboronosposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready for defense, prepared for defense; defensible</li></ol><p><i>оборо́на</i> (oboróna) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend</li></ol><p><i>оборони́ть</i> (oboronítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend oneself <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hold the line</li></ol><p><i>обороня́ть</i> (oboronjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(full) rotation, revolution, turn</li><li>reverse side, back (of a page, sheet, document, object, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) use, usage, circulation</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) turnover</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) turn of events</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) phrase, linguistic construction</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обороти́ть</i> (oborotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) figure of speech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>werewolf (also werefox and any other werebeast)</li><li>shapeshifter, turnskin</li><li>(<i>neologism</i>) a secret criminal</li></ol><p>Derived from оборот (oborot, “shifting, changing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn over</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) to turn <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvortiti</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;vortiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ). Doublet of <i>обрати́ть</i> (obratítʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>back (side); reverse (side); other (side)</li><li>(economics,&#32;finance) current; circulating; working (in active circulation&#59; in operational flow)</li><li>(business,&#32;accounting) relating to turnover (the movement of debits and credits)</li><li>(commerce,&#32;logistics) returnable; reusable</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) turnaround; turnback (used for turning around, so that a vehicle can go in the opposite direction)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) involving a turn of phrase; idiomatic</li><li>(<i>orthography</i>) reversed (turned around&#59; facing the other way)</li></ol><p><i>оборо́т</i> (oborót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equipment, machinery, hardware</li></ol><p><i>обору́довать</i> (oborúdovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip, to fit out, to rig</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) and <i>ору́довать</i> (orúdovatʹ, “to handle, to manipulate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substantiation, basing, argument, grounds (fact or statement used to support a proposition&#59; a reason)</li></ol><p><i>обоснова́ть</i> (obosnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a valid, well-founded, well-motivated, justified manner</li></ol><p><i>обосно́ванный</i> (obosnóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>validity, good reasons</li></ol><p><i>обосно́ванный</i> (obosnóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-founded, grounded, valid, sound, justified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substantiate, to justify, to validate (e.g. an argument)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle down <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>обоснова́ть</i> (obosnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substantiate, to justify, to validate (e.g. an argument)</li></ol><p><i>обоснова́ть</i> (obosnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle somewhere</li></ol><p><i>обосно́вывать</i> (obosnóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to isolate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>осо́бо</i> (osóbo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isolation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detached, separate, isolated, solitary (apart from&#59; not connected to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to isolate</li></ol><p><i>обосо́бить</i> (obosóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit on something</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to talk shit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;объсьрати</i> (&#42;obŭsĭrati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)sьrati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>сра́ть</i> (srátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit one's pants, to shit oneself</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to get frightened, to chicken out</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to disgrace oneself, to shit the bed</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to piss one's pants, to piss oneself</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to get frightened, to chicken out</li></ol><p><i>обо-</i> (obo-) +&lrm; <i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intensification, worsening, aggravation, exacerbation, sharpening</li></ol><p><i>обостри́ть</i> (obostrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escalate, to intensify</li><li>to aggravate, to strain, to exacerbate</li><li>to whet</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>остри́ть</i> (ostrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become sharp(er)</li><li>to become more sensitive/acute</li><li>to worsen, to become aggravated/strained</li></ol><p><i>обостри́ть</i> (obostrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escalate, to intensify</li><li>to aggravate, to strain, to exacerbate</li><li>to whet</li></ol><p><i>обостри́ть</i> (obostrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become sharp(er)</li><li>to become more sensitive/acute</li><li>to worsen, to become aggravated/strained</li></ol><p><i>обостря́ть</i> (obostrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>keen, acute (<i>senses</i>)</li><li>strained (<i>relationships</i>)</li><li>sharp (<i>facial features</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roadside, shoulder</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mutually</li></ol><p><i>обою́дный</i> (obojúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mutual, reciprocal</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>обою́ду</i> (obojúdu, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), the former from Old East Slavic <i>обоѭдоу</i> (obojǫdu) (by surface analysis, <i>о́ба</i> (óba) +&lrm; <i>-ю́ду</i> (-júdu), the latter suffix from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ǫdu</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-edged</li></ol><p><i>обою́дный</i> (obojúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process, to treat, to work on</li><li>to cultivate (soil)</li><li>to finish, to polish</li><li>to cleanse (a wound), to apply antiseptic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to indoctrinate, to influence</li></ol><p><i>обрабо́тать</i> (obrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be processed (to acquire the necessary properties in the process of use or work)</li></ol><p><i>обраба́тывать</i> (obrabátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process, to treat, to work on</li><li>to cultivate (soil)</li><li>to finish, to polish</li><li>to cleanse (a wound), to apply antiseptic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to indoctrinate, to influence</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be processed (to acquire the necessary properties in the process of use or work)</li></ol><p><i>обрабо́тать</i> (obrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>processing (act of taking something through a set of prescribed procedures)</li><li>adaptation (something which has been adapted, variation)</li></ol><p><i>обрабо́тать</i> (obrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happily</li></ol><p><i>обра́дованный</i> (obrádovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gladden, to please, to bring joy to</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ра́довать</i> (rádovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, to be glad, to be happy [<i>with</i> dative of the thing enjoyed]</li></ol><p><i>обра́довать</i> (obrádovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shape, form</li><li>appearance, look, style, image</li><li>type</li><li>figure</li><li>mode, manner, way</li><li>icon</li><li>icon, sacred image</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>образъ</i> (obrazŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrazъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>раз</i> (raz). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>образъ</i> (obrazŭ), Ukrainian <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz), Polish <i>obraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way of life</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specimen</li><li>example</li><li>sample</li></ol><p><i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon chest or icon shelf</li></ol><p><i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icon chest or icon shelf</li></ol><p><i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>figuratively (in a figurative manner)</li></ol><p><i>о́бразный</i> (óbraznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) imagery</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) graphic nature</li></ol><p><i>о́бразный</i> (óbraznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graphic, picturesque, vivid (image-bearing)</li></ol><p><i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formation</li><li>constitution</li><li>education</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ), <i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scholarship</li><li>education</li><li>educational attainment</li></ol><p><i>образо́ванный</i> (obrazóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>educated (having attained a level of higher education)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obrazovanshchina (class of people with superficial education but without the higher ethics of an educated person)</li></ol><p>From <i>образова́ние</i> (obrazovánije) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>educational, informative</li><li>formative</li></ol><p><i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, to make, to make up, to produce</li></ol><p>From <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear, to get to be</li><li>te educate oneself</li><li>to work out, to be fine</li></ol><p><i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, to make, to make up, to produce</li></ol><p><i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to one's senses</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to one's senses</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to one's senses</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exemplary (deserving honour&#47;honor, respect and admiration)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) model</li></ol><p><i>образе́ц</i> (obrazéc) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;derogatory</i>) sample, specimen</li></ol><p><i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frame (a painting), to put inside a frame</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to encircle, to frame (<i>e.g. of light around a face, trees around an alley, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, probably borrowed from Polish <i>obramować</i> (“to put a border around”), from Middle High German <i>brëmen</i> (“to put a border around”), from Middle High German <i>brëm</i> (“edge, border”); compare English <i>brim</i>. Meaning influenced by <i>ра́ма</i> (ráma, “frame”). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ра́ма</i> (ráma) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) (stem-stressed variant) or as <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ра́ма</i> (ráma) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frame, framing, setting</li></ol><p><i>обра́мить</i> (obrámitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frame (a painting), to put inside a frame</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to encircle, to frame (<i>e.g. of light around a face, trees around an alley, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>обра́мить</i> (obrámitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) or from <i>обрами́ть</i> (obramítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/be overgrown <i>(with)</i>, to become/be covered <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to acquire, to accumulate</li></ol><p><i>обрасти́</i> (obrastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/be overgrown <i>(with)</i>, to become/be covered <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to acquire, to accumulate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skim milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reversible</li><li>convertible</li></ol><p>Constructed as if the present passive participle of <i>обрати́ть</i> (obratítʹ, “to reverse”) (but this verb is perfective and has no present tense).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn; to direct</li><li>to turn into</li><li>to convert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обратити</i> (obratiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvortiti</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;vortiti</i>. Doublet of <i>обороти́ть</i> (oborotítʹ), the inherited Old East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn (to)</li><li>to speak (to); to address; to go to</li><li>to apply (to)</li><li>to appeal</li><li>to take to, to take (flight)</li></ol><p><i>обрати́ть</i> (obratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back, backwards</li><li>conversely, inversely</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) again</li></ol><p><i>обра́тный</i> (obrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>back, return, reverse</li><li>inverse</li><li>(<i>law</i>) retroactive</li><li>reciprocal</li></ol><p><i>обрати́ть</i> (obratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negotiability (of a document)</li><li>the intensity or frequency of the population's appeals for medical care to medical institutions</li><li>level of circulation</li></ol><p><i>обраща́емый</i> (obraščájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn; to direct</li><li>to turn into</li><li>to convert</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обращати</i> (obraščati). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay attention [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to draw attention [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn to, to turn into</li><li>to address, to apply, to appeal</li><li>to treat, to handle, to manage</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) to turn, to change [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘into someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to circulate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обращатисѧ</i> (obraščatisę). By surface analysis, <i>обраща́ть</i> (obraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversion, transformation</li><li>circulation</li><li>treatment, management, handling</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) manners</li><li>address, salutation</li><li>appeal</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) addressee</li></ol><p>From <i>обрати́ть</i> (obratítʹ), <i>обраща́ть</i> (obraščátʹ), from Common Slavonic <i>обратити</i> (obratiti, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inverted, directed towards, turned into</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge (<i>of book pages, a roof, etc.</i>)</li><li>sawn-off rifle, sawn-off shotgun</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) shoulder, verge (narrow strip of flat land adjacent to a highway)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) wooden vat (<i>made from a barrel sawn in half</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cut (a small wound)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обре́зать</i> (obrézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>обреза́ние</i>) cutting, paring, trimming, edging</li><li>(<i>обре́зание or обреза́ние</i>) circumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>обре́зать</i> (obrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije) or from <i>обреза́ть</i> (obrezátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije). Compare Belarusian <i>абразанне</i> (abrazannje), Ukrainian <i>обріза́ння</i> (obrizánnja), Polish <i>obrzezanie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to clip, to pare, to trim, to prune, to square</li><li>to cut</li><li>to circumcise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snub, to cut short</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>обре́зать</i> (obrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) scrap, snippet, clipping</li></ol><p><i>обре́з(ать)</i> (obréz(atʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to clip, to pare, to trim, to prune, to square</li><li>to cut</li><li>to circumcise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snub, to cut short</li></ol><p><i>обре́зать</i> (obrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to doom</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;northern) to assume, presume</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;northern) to eyeball, estimate by eye</li></ol><p><i>обре́к-</i> (obrék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burdensome, onerous</li><li>troublesome</li></ol><p><i>обремени́ть</i> (obremenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden</li><li>to encumber <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бре́мен-</i> (brémen-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) or <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бремени́ть</i> (bremenítʹ, “to burden”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden</li><li>to encumber <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>обремени́ть</i> (obremenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to gain, to acquire, to be blessed with</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обрѣсти</i> (obrěsti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrěsti</i>. Compare <i>встре́тить</i> (vstrétitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to gain, to acquire, to be blessed with</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обрѣтати</i> (obrětati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrětati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>обрет-</i> (obret-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare <i>встретить</i> (vstretitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide, to pass one's time</li></ol><p><i>обрета́ть</i> (obretátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquisition; appropriation; discovery, finding</li></ol><p>From <i>обрет-</i> (obret-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije) or borrowed directly from Old Church Slavonic <i>обрѣтениѥ</i> (obrětenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to doom</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a doomed manner</li></ol><p><i>обречённый</i> (obrečónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doom</li></ol><p><i>обречённый</i> (obrečónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doomed (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>doomed (<i>appearance, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li><li>predestined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) to make a lattice on, to provide with a lattice</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to strengthen by nailing laths to</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>реше́тить</i> (rešétitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) to make a lattice on, to provide with a lattice</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to strengthen by nailing laths to</li></ol><p><i>обреше́тить</i> (obrešétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lath, slat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outline</li><li>to outline, to depict, to delineate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outline</li><li>to outline, to depict, to delineate</li></ol><p><i>обрисова́ть</i> (obrisovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave, shave off</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>брить</i> (britʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) rent, tribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop</li><li><i>(remark etc)</i> to let drop/fall</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-рони́ть</i> (-ronítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim by cutting off a part</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to chop off, to cut off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cut off, to cut short, to interrupt abruptly (a person, one's sleep, etc.)</li><li>to hem</li></ol><p><i>обруби́ть</i> (obrubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim by cutting off a part</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to chop off, to cut off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cut off, to cut short, to interrupt abruptly (a person, one's sleep, etc.)</li><li>to hem</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stump</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call names, to curse, to scold, to swear</li><li>to tear to pieces, to criticize, to attack, to pan</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Russify, to assimilate and become like a Russian</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обручь</i> (obručĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrǫčь</i> (“wrist, bracelet, ring”), from <i>&#42;ob</i> + <i>&#42;rǫka</i> (“arm, hand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding ring, engagement ring (a ring symbolizing marriage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engagement, wedding (ring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betroth</li></ol><p><i>обручи́ть</i> (obručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/to become/to get engaged/betrothed [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обруча́ть</i> (obručátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engagement, betrothal (period of time when marriage is planned or promised)</li></ol><p><i>обручи́ть</i> (obručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betroth</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/to become/to get engaged/betrothed to, to affiance</li></ol><p><i>обручи́ть</i> (obručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fall, collapse</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) failure</li></ol><p><i>обру́шить</i> (obrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, to cause to fall [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li><li>to rain down, to unleash [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘blows, bombs, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li><li>to hurl [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘epithets’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘at someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>обру́шить</i> (obrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in, to collapse</li><li>to batter, to pound [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>] (<i>of the weather</i>)</li><li>to befall [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to attack [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to assail [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>] verbally</li></ol><p><i>обру́шивать</i> (obrúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, to cause to fall [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li><li>to rain down, to unleash [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘blows, bombs, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li><li>to hurl [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘epithets’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘at someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in, to collapse</li><li>to batter, to pound [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>] (<i>of the weather</i>)</li><li>to befall [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to attack [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to assail [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>] verbally</li></ol><p><i>обру́шить</i> (obrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>break, rupture</li><li>precipice, steep; bluff, cliff</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обрыва́ть</i> (obryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear/pluck off/round</li><li>to break, to tear</li><li>to break off (ties)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to rip</li><li>to retrench</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep, precipitous, abrupt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrap</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fragments, scraps, snatches</li></ol><p><i>обрыва́ть</i> (obryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) messy person</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>рыга́ть</i> (rygátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flabby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get abnormally loose skin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to knock up [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>] (to make pregnant)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>брюха́тить</i> (brjuxátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceremony, rite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obrędъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) a ritualist, one who takes part in rituals</li><li>a book describing how rites are properly done</li></ol><p><i>обря́д</i> (obrjád, “ritual”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rites</li></ol><p><i>обря́дный</i> (obrjádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ritual, ceremonial</li></ol><p>From <i>обря́д</i> (obrjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ritual, ceremonial</li></ol><p>From <i>обря́д</i> (obrjád) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>обря́д</i> (obrjád).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, to line (a house, a pond, a path, etc.) with plants or trees</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, to line (a house, a pond, a path, etc.) with plants or trees</li></ol><p><i>обсади́ть</i> (obsadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;card games) mistake in dealing cards</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) mistake, error of judgement</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bug, error in a computer program</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>обсдаться</i> (obsdatʹsja, “deal badly (cards), mess up”). From <i>об-</i> (ob-) indicating mistaken action + <i>сдать</i> (sdatʹ, “deal”). Compare similarly formed <i>огово́рка</i> (ogovórka), <i>опеча́тка</i> (opečátka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observatory (place where celestial bodies are observed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) obsessive-compulsive disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsession</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit around something</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsidian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit on something</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to talk shit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscurant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obscurantism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, inspection, survey</li></ol><p><i>обсле́довать</i> (obslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspect, to examine</li><li>to study, to investigate, to inquire <i>(into)</i>, to survey</li><li>to explore</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) staff, service personnel</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>слуга́</i> (slugá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service (customers), maintenance (machinery), hospitality (business)</li></ol><p><i>обслу́живать</i> (obslúživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve, to attend to, to wait on</li><li>to maintain, to service (e.g. equipment)</li><li>to cater for, to supply (for)</li></ol><p><i>обслужи́ть</i> (obslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve, to attend to, to wait on</li><li>to maintain, to service (e.g. equipment)</li><li>to cater for, to supply (for)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(very) dry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround <i>(with)</i>, to encircle <i>(with)</i></li><li>to furnish</li><li>to arrange</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trick, to cheat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get ahead <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to defeat</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround <i>(with)</i>, to encircle <i>(with)</i></li><li>to furnish</li><li>to arrange</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trick, to cheat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get ahead <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to defeat</li></ol><p><i>обста́вить</i> (obstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furniture, decor</li><li><i>(theatre)</i> scenery, set, decor</li><li>situation, conditions, environment, set-up</li><li>atmosphere, setting</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>станови́ться</i> (stanovítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in detail (with all particulars)</li></ol><p><i>обстоя́тельный</i> (obstojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detailed, circumstantial, thorough</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thorough, reliable (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>обстоя́ть</i> (obstojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumstance (that which attends, or relates to, or in some way affects, a fact or event)</li><li>(grammar,&#32;linguistics) adverbial modifier, adverb, adjunct</li></ol><p>From <i>обстоя́ть</i> (obstojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo), a calque of French <i>circonstance</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(in the third person with the words <i>дело</i> (delo), <i>всё</i> (vsjo), etc.) to be, to be going, to be getting on, to stand (of affairs)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build up</li></ol><p><i>обстро́ить</i> (obstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a house for oneself</li></ol><p><i>обстра́ивать</i> (obstráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firing, fire</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обстре́ливать</i> (obstrélivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to shell, to fire at</li></ol><p><i>обстреля́ть</i> (obstreljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to shell, to fire at</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>острига́ть</i> (ostrigátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>обстри́г-</i> (obstríg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>острига́ться</i> (ostrigátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>обстрига́ть</i> (obstrigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>остри́чь</i> (ostríčʹ)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>остри́чься</i> (ostríčʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>обстри́чь</i> (obstríčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build up</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a house for oneself</li></ol><p><i>обстро́ить</i> (obstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstruction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>obstrūctiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beset, to crowd, to encompass, to surround</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beset, to crowd, to encompass, to surround</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss, to consider, to talk over</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss, to consider</li><li>to talk over</li></ol><p><i>обсуди́ть</i> (obsudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discussion</li></ol><p>From <i>обсужда́ть</i> (obsuždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to short-change</li><li>to do the calculation(s) <i>(for)</i>, to count</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to short-change</li><li>to do the calculation(s) <i>(for)</i>, to count</li></ol><p><i>обсчита́ть</i> (obsčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to cover by sprinkling, to sprinkle over [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to cover densely, to dot</li><li>to cause (something loose) to fall</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to cover with a rash [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>обсы́пать</i> (obsýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) streamlined</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) evasive, roundabout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) fairing</li><li>(aviation,&#32;space science) nose cone</li></ol><p>From <i>обтека́ть</i> (obtekátʹ, “to flow around”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow around, to circumvent</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to streamline</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to circulate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bypass, to dodge, to ignore (something like obstacles or burdens)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to feel strongly ashamed and try not to show it</li></ol><p><i>обте́к-</i> (obték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe off, to dry off</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim, to square, to hew, to sharpen</li><li>to teach manners</li><li>to lick into a shape</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>теса́ть</i> (tesátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow around, to circumvent</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to streamline</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to circulate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bypass, to dodge, to ignore (something like obstacles or burdens)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to start something in advance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hire supporters for someone</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe off, to dry off</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen, to grind</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover furniture with fabric or lether</li><li>to hug, to fit close <i>(about clothes)</i></li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to bowse taut, to bowse down</li></ol><p><i>обтяг-</i> (obtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothes</i>) close-fitting, tight, snug</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover <i>(furniture)</i></li><li>to fit close <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to bowse taut, to bowse down</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim, to square, to hew, to sharpen</li><li>to teach manners</li><li>to lick into a shape</li></ol><p><i>обтеса́ть</i> (obtesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put shoes on someone's feet, to help someone put on his/her shoes</li><li>to provide with shoes</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to trick, to take in, to snooker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обувати</i> (obuvati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obuvati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put one's shoes on</li></ol><p><i>обува́ть</i> (obuvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́бувь</i> (óbuvʹ)</li></ol><p><i>о́бувь</i> (óbuvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shoe</li></ol><p><i>о́бувь</i> (óbuvʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footwear, shoes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обувь</i> (obuvĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obuvь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>обува́ть</i> (obuvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to char</li></ol><p><i>обу́глить</i> (obúglitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to char</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to grip, to overcome (<i>especially of feelings</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden, load</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) drag, liability, lame duck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обузъ</i> (obuzŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obvǫzъ</i>, <i>&#42;obvǫza</i>.</p><p>Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>обѫзъ</i> (obǫzŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curb, to bridle</li><li>to restrain, to control, to keep in check</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>узда́</i> (uzdá) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curb, to bridle</li><li>to restrain, to control, to keep in check</li></ol><p><i>обузда́ть</i> (obuzdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to seize, to grip, to overcome, to overwhelm (<i>especially of feelings</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>обу́рювати</i> (obúrjuvaty) of broader meaning “to irritate, to make irate, to revolt or shock, to enrage”, perfective обу́рити (obúryty), from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бу́рити</i> (búryty, “to stir, to shake; to flurry, to trouble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore somewhat</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>бури́ть</i> (burítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condition, to stipulate, to determine, to govern, to dictate</li></ol><p><i>обусло́вить</i> (obuslóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condition, to stipulate, to determine, to govern, to dictate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>усло́вие</i> (uslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caused by; depending on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause, to call forth</li><li>to stipulate, to determine</li></ol><p><i>обусло́вить</i> (obuslóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make comfortable, to equip with the necessary facilities/amenities</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to develop</li></ol><p><i>обустро́ить</i> (obustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle down, to provide oneself with the necessary conveniences/amenities, to make one's home more comfortable</li></ol><p><i>обустра́ивать</i> (obustráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make comfortable, to equip with the necessary facilities/amenities</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) to develop</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle down, to provide oneself with the necessary conveniences/amenities, to make one's home more comfortable</li></ol><p><i>обустро́ить</i> (obustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provision of the necessary facilities/amenities</li><li>(<i>military</i>) development</li></ol><p><i>обустро́ить</i> (obustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put shoes on someone's feet, to help someone put on his/her shoes</li><li>to provide with shoes</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to trick, to take in, to snooker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obuti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-уть</i> (-utʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put one's shoes on</li></ol><p><i>обу́ть</i> (obútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt (the thicker end of a tool or a weapon, especially an axe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обухъ</i> (obuxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obuxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, centred upon, or pertaining to Obukhiv (the city of Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, centred upon, or pertaining to Obukhivka (the rural settlement in Dnipro Raion, Dnipropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, by, for, or pertaining to Pavel Matveyevich Obukhov (1820–1869)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, served by, surrounding, or pertaining to Obukhovo (the former railway station named for the Obukhov metallurgical plant)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Obukhovo (the historical district of Saint Petersburg named for the Obukhovo railway station)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, near, or pertaining to Obukhovo (the municipal okrug of Neva district named for the historical district of Saint Petersburg)</li></ol></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning ability</li></ol><p><i>обуча́емый</i> (obučájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, to instruct, to train</li></ol><p><i>обучи́ть</i> (obučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get trained <i>(in)</i>, to receive training <i>(in)</i>, to learn, to study</li></ol><p><i>обуча́ть</i> (obučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning, education (the process of being taught)</li></ol><p><i>обучи́ть</i> (obučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, to instruct (<i>often not in an academic setting</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> dative <small>‘a skill, principle, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get trained <i>(in)</i>, to receive training <i>(in)</i>, to learn, to study</li></ol><p><i>обучи́ть</i> (obučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́бух</i> (óbux)</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) tubber</li></ol><p><i>о́бух</i> (óbux) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to grip, to overcome (especially of feelings)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обоуꙗти</i> (obujati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girth (<i>e.g. of a tree</i>)</li><li>(action of) embracing</li><li>embrace</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) comprehensive knowledge</li><li>(<i>military</i>) envelopment</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>обхвати́ть</i> (obxvatítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clasp</li><li>to embrace, to enfold</li><li>to grapple</li><li>to encompass</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clasp</li><li>to embrace, to enfold</li><li>to grapple</li><li>to encompass</li></ol><p><i>обхвати́ть</i> (obxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round</li><li>roundabout way, detour</li><li>turning movement</li><li>evasion, circumvention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polite, courteous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>обходи́(ться)</i> (obxodí(tʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go around, to bypass (to pass by going around)</li><li>to walk through, to wander around, to go around, to tour, to go to (many places in an area)</li><li>to overtake, to outrank, to beat, to outperform (a peer or competitor)</li><li>to evade, to circumvent, to avoid, to bypass (the law etc.)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cost; to come to</li><li>to treat [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a specific way’</small>]</li><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) to be able to do without</li><li>to turn out, to work out</li></ol><p><i>обходи́ть</i> (obxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trackman</li></ol><p><i>обхо́д</i> (obxód) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manners</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) common</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обьчии</i> (obĭčii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obьťь</i>. Doublet of <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ransack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rummage through</li></ol><p><i>обша́рить</i> (obšáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ransack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rummage through</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ша́рить</i> (šáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shabby, ragged, frayed, worn out, worn away</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edge, to border, to trim</li><li><i>(parcel, package etc)</i> to cover up</li><li>to plank, to revet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make clothes <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>обши́ть</i> (obšítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edging, bordering</li><li>trimming</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) boarding, panelling</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) sheathing</li><li>(<i>shipbuilding</i>) planking, plating</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) covering</li></ol><p><i>обшива́ть</i> (obšivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>оши́кать</i> (ošíkatʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>шикать</i> (šikatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extensively</li></ol><p><i>обши́рный</i> (obšírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vast, ample, extensive</li></ol><p>Cognates include Polish <i>obszerny</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trimmed, edged</li><li>cased, encased</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edge, to border, to trim</li><li><i>(parcel, package etc)</i> to cover up</li><li>to plank, to revet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make clothes <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapel (at the end of a sleeve), cuff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Aufschlag</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahaną</i> (whence English <i>slay</i>, <i>slag</i>). Doublet of <i>аншла́г</i> (anšlág), <i>дуршла́г</i> (duršlág), <i>форшла́г</i> (foršlág), <i>шлаг</i> (šlag), and <i>шлагба́ум</i> (šlagbáum) and more distantly doublet of <i>шлак</i> (šlak) and <i>шля́хта</i> (šljáxta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) dorm, dormitory</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>общежи́тие</i> (obščežítije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) common fund of a criminal group; mob money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate (with), to converse, to mix (with)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) combined arms; all-military (uniting all branches of the armed forces)</li></ol><p>From <i>обще</i> (obšče) +&lrm; <i>войска́</i> (vojská) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national, state, statewide</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рственный</i> (gosudárstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>available to everybody</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) topos, locus</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) commonplace, banality, platitude</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>locus commūnis</i>, itself a calque of Ancient Greek <i>κοινὸς τόπος</i> (koinòs tópos). The figurative sense developed by the early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pan-European</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormitory</li><li>hostel</li><li>bunkhouse</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) social life, common life, community</li></ol><p>From <i>обще-</i> (obšče-, “common”) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ, “to live”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generally known, well-known</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>изве́стный</i> (izvéstnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worldwide</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nationwide, national</li></ol><p>From <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Literally, “of all the people”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national, nationwide</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communication, intercourse (concept of information exchange)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general, nonspecialized (<i>education</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>образова́тельный</i> (obrazovátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foodservice</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>обще́ственное пита́ние</i> (obščéstvennoje pitánije, “public nutrition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>political science</i>) pan-post-Soviet (spanning all or most of the region which was once part of the Soviet Union, especially in reference to the post-Soviet countries (countries which were once part of the Soviet Union))</li></ol><p>From <i>обще-</i> (obšče-) +&lrm; <i>по̀стсове́тский</i> (pòstsovétskij, “post-Soviet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>universally recognized, admitted, generally acknowledged, generally accepted</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>при́знанный</i> (príznannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generally accepted, conventional</li></ol><p>From <i>обще-</i> (obšče-) +&lrm; <i>при́нятый</i> (prínjatyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-Russian, nationwide (in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij, “general, total”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij, “Russian (referring to Russia, the nation, rather than Russians as an ethnic group)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Common Slavonic, Common Slavic (spoken from 5th–9th centuries C.E.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pan-Soviet (spanning all or most of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>обще-</i> (obšče-) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public property</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>socially</li></ol><p><i>обще́ственный</i> (obščéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sociopolitical, (<i>relational</i>) public policy</li></ol><p><i>обще́ственный</i> (obščéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полити́ческий</i> (politíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public opinion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(general) public, community</li></ol><p><i>обще́ственный</i> (obščéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>social</li><li>public</li><li>common</li></ol><p><i>о́бщество</i> (óbščestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public transport, public transit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society</li><li>association, society, union</li><li>company (group of people together)</li><li>company (business enterprise)</li><li>clipping of <i>обществозна́ние</i> (obščestvoznánije): social science</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обьщьство</i> (obĭštĭstvo); see <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij, “common”) and <i>-ество</i> (-estvo). Cognates include Bulgarian <i>общество́</i> (obštestvó) and Macedonian <i>општество</i> (opštestvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limited liability company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social science, social studies</li></ol><p><i>о́бщество</i> (óbščestvo) +&lrm; <i>зна́ние</i> (znánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commonly-used, in common usage.</li></ol><p>From <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij, “common, public”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-, vocalic interfix) +&lrm; <i>употреби́ть</i> (upotrebítʹ, “to use, to apply”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common to all mankind, universal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>public</li><li>total, whole</li><li>general</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) common</li><li>(regional,&#32;Kazakhstan,&#32;slang) ellipsis of <i>общий салам</i> (obščij salam): a friendly collective greeting (mainly in Islamic regions); greetings to all</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обьщь</i> (obĭštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obьťь</i>. Doublet of <i>о́бчий</i> (óbčij), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) common gender (refers to nouns that are both masculine and feminine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lingua franca (common language used by people of diverse backgrounds to communicate with one another)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) common ground</li></ol><p>Literally, “common language.”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commune, collective society (primitive, often clan-based, society with collective ownership of the means of production)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) community of believers</li><li>community</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>обьщина</i> (obĭščina), from Old Church Slavonic <i>обьщина</i> (obĭština), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obьťina</i>. Doublet of <i>обчи́на</i> (občína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) commune, collective society</li><li>communal, collective</li><li>collectivist</li></ol><p><i>общи́на</i> (obščína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociability, communicativeness, outgoingness</li></ol><p><i>общи́тельный</i> (obščítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sociable (tending to socialize or be social; friendly; inviting; congenial)</li><li>associative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generality</li><li>community</li></ol><p>From <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>об</i> (ob).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to fuck someone over (to cheat someone)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare) raver, someone who goes out on rave parties.</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare) junkie, drunkard, someone in a very intoxicated state.</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>)</li></ol><p><i>объеба́ться</i> (obʺjebátʹsja, “to get intoxicated (vulgar)”) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os). Can also be analyzed as <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat, to swindle</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>Его́р</i> (Jegór) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). It is likely connected to the feast day of St. George (Yegor, Yuri), on April 23 and November 26, when the terms of agricultural obligations would expire; the word thus means “to deceive someone by (or before) St. George’s Day”. Compare this proverb: вот тебе, бабушка, и Юрьев день (vot tebe, babuška, i Jurʹjev denʹ, “Well, Granny, there’s your St. George’s Day”), which is said about sudden misfortunes or dashed hopes.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to gnaw away (from sides, edges, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat out of house and home (to be a burden to someone by eating too much, not leaving enough for the host)</li></ol><p><i>объе́д-</i> (obʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat too much, to overeat [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘candy, fast food, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>объеда́ть</i> (obʺjedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something delicious and enjoyable to eat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>association, union</li><li>unification</li><li>(<i>military</i>) refers to a major combined field force such as a front or an army group</li></ol><p>From <i>объедини́ть</i> (obʺjedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Literally, “unification”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite, to combine, to join, to amalgamate, to consolidate</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join hands, to unite, to combine, to amalgamate</li></ol><p><i>объедини́ть</i> (obʺjedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite, to combine, to join, to amalgamate, to consolidate</li></ol><p><i>объедини́ть</i> (obʺjedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join hands, to unite, to combine, to amalgamate</li></ol><p><i>объединя́ть</i> (obʺjedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united</li><li>amalgamated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) leftover scrap of food, leaving</li></ol><p><i>объе́д-</i> (obʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uneaten remains of hay, straw or other animal feed</li><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) leftovers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detour, diversion, deviation, circuit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obъjězdъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel <i>(all over)</i></li><li><i>(horses)</i> to break in, to train</li><li><i>(a car)</i> to run in</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest ranger</li><li>horse trainer</li></ol><p><i>объе́здить</i> (obʺjézditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel <i>(all over)</i></li><li>to go round, to make a detour, to take the bypass</li><li><i>(horses)</i> to break in, to train</li><li><i>(a car)</i> to run in</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object (general sense)</li><li>enterprise, institution, facility (place of economic or industrial activity)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) object</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) object</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lens, objective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>objectively (in an impartial, objective manner)</li></ol><p><i>объекти́вный</i> (obʺjektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) objective character</li><li>objectivity, impartiality, impartialness</li></ol><p><i>объекти́вный</i> (obʺjektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>objective</li><li>unbiased, impartial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;philosophy,&#32;linguistics) object; objective (referring to something with material existence, or to the noun which a verb performs its action on)</li></ol><p><i>объе́кт</i> (obʺjékt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) objective case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to gnaw away (from sides, edges, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat out of house and home (to be a burden to someone by eating too much, not leaving enough for the host)</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat too much, to overeat [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘candy, fast food, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>объе́сть</i> (obʺjéstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel <i>(all over)</i></li><li>to go round, to make a detour, to take the bypass</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>обыма́ть</i> (obymátʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, to tell, to advertise, to express</li><li>to announce, to proclaim, to declare</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up, to show up, to appear</li></ol><p><i>объяви́ть</i> (obʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declaration, announcement, proclamation (act of announcing)</li><li>announcement, notice (item placed somewhere for general information)</li></ol><p><i>объяви́ть</i> (obʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, to tell, to advertise, to express</li><li>to announce, to proclaim, to declare</li></ol><p><i>объяви́ть</i> (obʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ). Cognate with Belarusian <i>аб'яўляць</i> (abʺjawljacʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up, to show up, to appear</li></ol><p><i>объявля́ть</i> (obʺjavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation (the act or process of explaining)</li></ol><p><i>объясни́ть</i> (obʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explicable, explainable</li></ol><p><i>объясни́ть</i> (obʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explanatory</li></ol><p><i>объясни́ть</i> (obʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>-ясни́ть</i> (-jasnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain oneself</li><li>to be accounted for</li><li>to make oneself understood</li><li>to have a talk (with), to have it out (with)</li></ol><p><i>объясни́ть</i> (obʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain, to illustrate, to clarify, to illuminate</li><li>to account for</li></ol><p><i>объясни́ть</i> (obʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain oneself</li><li>to be accounted for</li><li>to make oneself understood</li></ol><p><i>объясня́ть</i> (obʺjasnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embrace (hug)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obъjętьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>объя́ть</i> (obʺjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to come <i>(over)</i>, to envelop, to fill</li><li>to comprehend, to grasp, to embrace</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>объя́тие</i> (obʺjátije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to fuck someone over (to cheat someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>объеба́ть</i> (obʺjebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volume, capacity, bulk</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) extent, range, scope</li></ol><p>From <i>об-</i> (ob-) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jęti</i> (“to take”). Compare German <i>Umfang</i> (“extent, scope”) from <i>umfangen</i> (“to embrace, to include”), which is regarded as a calque of Latin <i>circumferentia</i> from Greek <i>περιφέρεια</i> (periféreia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) voluminous, capacious, bulky</li></ol><p><i>объём</i> (obʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voluminosity, capacity</li><li>fullness</li></ol><p><i>объёмный</i> (obʺjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) volume</li><li>voluminous, roomy</li><li>by volume</li><li>volumetric</li><li>three-dimensional</li></ol><p><i>объём</i> (obʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>объём</i> (obʺjóm)</li></ol><p><i>объём</i> (obʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the average man/citizen, the man in the street</li><li>philistine</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) resident, inhabitant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>obywatel</i> in the 17th c.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) resident, inhabitant</li><li>philistine</li></ol><p><i>обыва́тель</i> (obyvátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use with <i>(good)</i> effect, to play up, to make <i>(great)</i> play <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(musical instrument)</i> to mellow</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use with <i>(good)</i> effect, to play up, to make <i>(great)</i> play <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(musical instrument)</i> to mellow</li></ol><p><i>обыгра́ть</i> (obygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commonness, ordinariness</li><li>everyday life</li></ol><p><i>обы́денный</i> (obýdennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordinary, commonplace, everyday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calcified, limed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practice, habit, wont, custom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) demosponge</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Demospongiae.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Demospongiae</i>, as <i>обыкнове́нный</i> (obyknovénnyj, “ordinary, normal”) +&lrm; <i>гу́бка</i> (gúbka, “sponge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>обыкнове́нный</i> (obyknovénnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>обыкнове́нно</i> (obyknovénno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>usually, ordinarily, habitually</li></ol><p><i>обыкнове́нный</i> (obyknovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>usual, ordinary, habitual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obyknovenъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>обыкнове́ние</i> (obyknovénije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) common warthog (<i>Phacochoerus africanus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>обыкнове́нный</i> (obyknovénnyj, “ordinary, usual”) +&lrm; <i>борода́вочник</i> (borodávočnik, “warthog”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>обнима́ть</i> (obnimátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(house or body) search</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search, to conduct a search (for houses)</li><li>to frisk, to pat down</li><li>to rummage</li><li>to scour</li></ol><p><i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search, to conduct a search (for houses)</li><li>to frisk, to pat down</li><li>to rummage</li><li>to scour</li></ol><p><i>обыска́ть</i> (obyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom, usage (habit or accepted practice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obyčajь</i>, from <i>&#42;vyknǫti</i> (whence <i>-вы́кнуть</i> (-výknutʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) usually, habitually, customarily</li></ol><p><i>обы́чный</i> (obýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>usual, habitual, customary</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obyčьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;obyťi</i> (<i>&#42;o(b)-</i> + <i>&#42;vyťi</i>) and <i>&#42;obyknǫti</i> (<i>&#42;o(b)-</i> + <i>&#42;vyknǫti</i>) + <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duty</li><li>(<i>military</i>) service</li></ol><p><i>обя́занный</i> (objázannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obliged, obligated</li><li>must, be (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>necessarily, without fail</li></ol><p><i>обяза́тельный</i> (objazátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obligatoriness, obligation; compulsion</li></ol><p><i>обяза́тельный</i> (objazátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obligatory, mandatory, compulsory</li><li>obliging</li><li>invariable, everpresent</li></ol><p>обяза́ть (objazátʹ) + -тельный (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obligation</li><li>liability</li><li>engagement</li></ol><p><i>обяза́ть</i> (objazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo). Further akin to <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>облига́ция</i> (obligácija) (<&lt; Latin <i>ligō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oblige, to bind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>обѩзати</i> (objęzati). By surface analysis, <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engage, to undertake <i>(to)</i>, to commit oneself, to pledge oneself, to bind oneself</li></ol><p><i>обяза́ть</i> (objazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oblige, to bind</li></ol><p><i>обяза́ть</i> (objazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engage, to undertake <i>(to)</i>, to commit oneself, to pledge oneself, to bind oneself</li><li>to be under an obligation</li></ol><p><i>обя́зывать</i> (objázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrapper, wrapping</li></ol><p><i>оберт-</i> (obert-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Akin to <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ).</p><p>Compare Czech <i>vrstva</i>, Polish <i>warstwa</i> (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrsta</i> (whence also <i>верста́</i> (verstá))), also akin to <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap up</li><li>to wind <i>(around)</i>, to wrap <i>(around)</i></li></ol><p><i>оберт-</i> (obert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>обе́дать</i> (obédatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>обе́д</i> (obéd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>обе́т</i> (obét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oval</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Oval</i> or French <i>ovale</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>ovālis</i>, from Latin <i>ōvum</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ōwyóm</i> (whence also Ancient Greek <i>ᾠόν</i> (ōión), English <i>egg</i>, Russian <i>яйцо</i> (jajco)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipticity, ovality</li></ol><p><i>ова́льный</i> (oválʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oval</li></ol><p><i>ова́л</i> (ovál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) Any crab in the family Cancridae, a cancrid.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Cancridae, a family of crabs.</li></ol><p><i>ова́льный</i> (oválʹnyj, “ovalesque, ovalescent”) +&lrm; <i>краб</i> (krab, “crab”), literally “oval crab”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ovation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a widower or a widow</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>вдове́ть</i> (vdovétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) ram (male sheep)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) an Aries (a person born with the Aries star sign)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovьnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;awinas</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Овен)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Aries (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a ram)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Aries (zodiac sign for the ram, covering March 21 — April 19)</li><li>(astrology,&#32;animate) Aries (a person with the Aries star sign)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovьnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;awinas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sheep’s, ovine</li></ol><p><i>овца́</i> (ovcá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>овца́</i> (ovcá): sheep, lamb (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>овца́</i> (ovcá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to envelop</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barn (for storing crops), drying barn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jevinъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to capture, to become the owner of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to overcome [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a feeling’</small>]</li><li>to get control over, to dominate [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to master, to become proficient at [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a skill’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>овладе́ть</i> (ovladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capture</li><li>mastery, mastering</li></ol><p><i>овладе́ть</i> (ovladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to capture, to become the owner of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to overcome [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a feeling’</small>]</li><li>to get control over, to dominate [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to master, to become proficient at [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a skill’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) gadfly, botfly (an insect of genus <i>Oestrus</i>)</li><li>Ovod (Soviet and Russian cruise missile)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>овощь</i> (ovoštĭ, “fruit”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovoťe</i>. Compare native Ukrainian <i>о́воч</i> (óvoč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable warehouse</li></ol><p><i>о́вощ</i> (óvošč) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ба́за</i> (báza)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable grower, olericulturist</li></ol><p>From <i>о́вощ</i> (óvošč, “vegetable”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olericulture, vegetable growing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vegetable</li></ol><p><i>о́вощ</i> (óvošč) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravine (a deep narrow valley)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>врагъ</i> (vragŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vorgъ</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>овра́г</i> (ovrág).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild oats</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) oatmeal (cereal)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) oatmeal porridge, gruel</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) bunting, yellow bunting, yellowhammer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oats, oat</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) oatmeal</li></ol><p><i>овёс</i> (ovjós) +&lrm; <i>-я́ный</i> (-jányj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Tuesday</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of the phrase <i>во вто́рник</i> (vo vtórnik). Compare Belarusian <i>аўторак</i> (awtórak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ovulation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ōvum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;inanimate) ovulation</li><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) breeder, brood mare (a woman who is preoccupied with becoming pregnant, giving birth, or rearing children)</li></ol><p><i>овуля́ция</i> (ovuljácija) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheep (a domestic sheep of either gender)</li><li>ewe (a female domestic sheep)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;informal) cow (a stupid woman)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>овьца</i> (ovĭca), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovьca</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;áwis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ówis</i>. See also English <i>ewe</i> and <i>ovine</i>, Latin <i>ovis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musk ox</li></ol><p>From овца́ (ovcá) "sheep" + бык (byk) "bull". Calque of genus name <i>Ovibos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;ethnic slur) a "sheep fucker", a person from the North Caucasus (Chechen, Avar etc.) or Central Asia (Tajik, Uzbek)</li></ol><p>From <i>овца́</i> (ovcá, “sheep”) and <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”). Literally, “sheep-fucker”. Compare English <i>goatfucker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepdog, herding dog</li><li>German Shepherd</li></ol><p><i>овча́р</i> (ovčár) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepfold</li></ol><p><i>овча́р</i> (ovčár) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dressed) sheepskin</li></ol><p><i>овца́</i> (ovcá) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;obsolete,&#32;poetic) this (visible and close)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;obsolete) some</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>овъ</i> (ovŭ) or borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>овъ</i> (ovŭ), both from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovъ</i> (“this”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;awas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew-o-s</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oats</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ovьsъ</i>, which is a cognate with Latin <i>avena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oganesson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stub, remnant, cinder (<i>of a mostly-burned candle, electrode, etc.</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate) weakling, wimp (<i>especially of a teenager</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) rowdy, hooligan, rascal (<i>especially of a teenager</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>гор(е́ть)</i> (gor(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), with unclear replacement of о with а.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to round, to skirt, to double</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table of contents</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce, to proclaim, to read out</li><li>to divulge, to make public</li><li>to fill with sound, to resound</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>гласи́ть</i> (glasítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publicity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) vocalisation (presence or supplying the vowels (diacritics), e.g. in Arabic or Hebrew)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce, to proclaim, to read out</li><li>to divulge, to make public</li><li>to fill with sound, to resound</li></ol><p><i>огласи́ть</i> (oglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>announcement, publication, proclaiming</li></ol><p><i>огласи́ть</i> (oglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thill; shaft (pole)</li></ol><p>Akin to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъglobъ</i> (whence Bulgarian <i>сглоб</i> (sglob), Serbo-Croatian <i>зглоб</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow/become deaf</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>гло́хнуть</i> (glóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get dumber, to become stupid or more stupid</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to become infatuated</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>глупе́ть</i> (glupétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deafen</li><li>to stun (<i>especially with a heavy blow</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оглуши́ть</i> (oglušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deafeningly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) resoundingly, stunningly</li></ol><p><i>оглуши́тельный</i> (oglušítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deafening</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) resounding, stunning</li></ol><p><i>оглуши́ть</i> (oglušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deafen</li><li>to stun (<i>especially with a heavy blow</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>глуши́ть</i> (glušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to inspect, to look over</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look round; to have a look around</li></ol><p><i>огляде́ть</i> (ogljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) looking back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) care, caution</li></ol><p><i>огляде́ть</i> (ogljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to inspect, to look over</li></ol><p><i>огляде́ть</i> (ogljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look / glance back (at); to turn (back) to look at something</li><li>to look round / around; to have a look around</li></ol><p><i>огля́дывать</i> (ogljádyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to inspect, to look over (<i>once</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look / glance back (at); to turn (back) to look at</li></ol><p><i>огляну́ть</i> (ogljanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) fever</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fire</li><li>fiery, passionate</li><li>fervent, ardent, boisterous, perky, full of life/pep</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój). <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) comes ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥gʷnis</i>, whence English <i>igneous</i> (“containing, resembling or produced by fire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fire-breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>active volcano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire-thrower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) flamethrower</li></ol><p>From <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-мёт</i> (-mjót), combining form of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”). Calque of German <i>Flammenwerfer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flamethrower</li></ol><p><i>огнемёт</i> (ognemjót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) flamethrower operator</li></ol><p>From <i>огнемёт</i> (ognemjót) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fire</li><li>fiery</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flammable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire-worshipper</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>покло́нник</i> (poklónnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire worship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fire-retardant</li></ol><p>From <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) firearm(s), artillery (any type of weapon launching a projectile through combustion of a chemical propellant, from handguns to artillery pieces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firing (of weapons)</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>стрела́</i> (strelá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire extinguisher</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>туши́ть</i> (tušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ), calque of German <i>Feuerlöscher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>refractory, fire-resistant</li></ol><p>From <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>упо́рный</i> (upórnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinderbox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) In Kievan Rus', this was a member of the elite class. The word is mentioned in 12th century and later manuscripts.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where there was a fire (with ashes and charcoals)</li><li>augmentative of <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ): large fire</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>огнище</i> (ognišče), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ogňišče</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ, “fire”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gymnopilus (any mushroom of the genus <i>Gymnopilus</i>)</li><li>pulse pod borer moth (<i>Etiella zinckenella</i>)</li><li>box tree moth (<i>Cydalima perspectalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>огонёк</i> (ogonjók) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>whoa; oh, ho!; wow!; oh, my goodness!; oh, oh! (expresses surprise, admiration, approval, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander</li><li>to stipulate</li><li>to mention, to specify</li></ol><p><i>оговори́ть</i> (ogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a slip of the tongue</li><li>to make a reservation</li></ol><p><i>огова́ривать</i> (ogovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defamation, slander</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander</li><li>to stipulate</li><li>to mention, to specify</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a slip of the tongue</li><li>to make a reservation or stipulation</li></ol><p><i>оговори́ть</i> (ogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservation, proviso, stipulation, ifs</li><li>slip of the tongue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ifs, ands, or buts</li><li>caveat, clause, reservation, disclaimer</li></ol><p><i>оговори́ть</i> (ogovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bare, to strip</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to expose</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>го́лый</i> (gólyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbridled, rampant, frenzied, wild, extreme</li></ol><p>From <i>оголте́ть</i> (ogoltétʹ, “to go crazy”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bare, to strip</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to expose</li></ol><p><i>оголи́ть</i> (ogolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nude, naked</li><li>bare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire <i>(also figurative)</i></li><li>light</li><li>fire (of weapons)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Fire! (command issued to order people to shoot)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>огнь</i> (ognĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ogňь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ugnis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥gʷnis</i>. Cognate with Latvian <i>uguns</i>, Latin <i>ignis</i> (whence English <i>ignition</i> and <i>igneous</i>, Latin borrowings), Sanskrit <i>अग्नि</i> (agní), Albanian <i>e enjte</i>. Compare also Hittite <i>𒀀𒀝𒉌𒅖</i> (a-ak-ni-iš), an Indo-Aryan borrowing. Doublet of <i>Агни</i> (Agni).</p><p>The second <b>o</b>-letter is not from an <b>ъ</b>-extra-short sound, but has the same origin as in extra sounds in <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) and <i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ). Because of it, the original stress was not <b>ого́нь</b>, but <b>о́гонь</b>. Anyway, the original stress today is probably not preserved in any dialect.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly fire (weapons fire coming from allied or friendly forces, as opposed to fire coming from enemy forces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) ogonek (diacritical mark as in <i>Ąą</i>, <i>Ęę</i>, <i>Įį</i>, <i>Ǫǫ</i> or <i>Ųų</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>ogonek</i> (literally “little tail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ): light, spark</li><li>a condiment from shredded tomatoes, horseradish, garlic and salt</li></ol><p><i>ого́нь</i> (ogónʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence in, to enclose</li></ol><p><i>огороди́ть</i> (ogorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable garden, kitchen garden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence in, to enclose</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>городи́ть</i> (gorodítʹ, “to surround with a fence”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>market gardener, truck farmer (one who grows produce for sale at a market)</li></ol><p><i>огоро́д</i> (ogoród) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vegetable garden, kitchen garden</li></ol><p><i>огоро́д</i> (ogoród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take aback, to disconcert</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, perhaps a modification of <i>огороди́ть</i> (ogorodítʹ, “to fence in”), influenced by <i>лапо́шить</i> (lapóšitʹ, “to bother”), <i>копоши́ться</i> (kopošítʹsja, “to swarm”).</p><p>Per Shansky and Krylov from <i>горо́шить</i> (goróšitʹ), from <i>горо́х</i> (goróx). Compare <i>как об сте́нку горо́х</i> (kak ob sténku goróx), <i>сы́пать как горо́х</i> (sýpatʹ kak goróx). Also compare <i>оста́ться на боба́х</i> (ostátʹsja na bobáx) (&lt; <i>боб</i> (bob)), <i>на оре́хи</i> (na oréxi) (&lt; <i>оре́х</i> (oréx)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to upset, to distress, to grieve</li></ol><p><i>огорчи́ть</i> (ogorčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be upset, to be distressed</li></ol><p><i>огорча́ть</i> (ogorčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affliction, grief, chagrin, disappointment</li></ol><p><i>огорчи́ть</i> (ogorčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to upset, to distress, to grieve</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) or perhaps <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>горчи́ть</i> (gorčítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be upset, to be distressed</li></ol><p><i>огорчи́ть</i> (ogorčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upset, distressed (<i>experiencing upset or distress; of a person</i>)</li><li>upset, distressed (<i>expressing upset or distress; of a face, look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ogre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ogre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob, to sack, to loot, to plunder</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery (act or practice of robbing)</li></ol><p><i>огра́бить</i> (ográbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence (barrier), fencing, hedge</li><li>(<i>neuroanatomy</i>) claustrum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ограда</i> (ograda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ogorda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard</li><li>to protect (with fences), to fence in</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>огра́да</i> (ográda) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) or <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-гради́ть</i> (-gradítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>огра́да</i> (ográda, “fence, hedge”)</li></ol><p><i>огра́да</i> (ográda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard</li><li>to protect (with fences), to fence in</li></ol><p><i>огради́ть</i> (ogradítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure; barrier, fence; railings</li><li>protection, prevention</li></ol><p><i>огражда́ть</i> (ograždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to facet</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>грани́ть</i> (granítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limitation, restriction, constraint</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чить</i> (ograníčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limitedness, scantiness</li><li>narrow-mindedness</li></ol><p><i>ограни́ченный</i> (ograníčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bordered, confined, limited</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confine, to limit, to restrict, to cut down</li><li>to constrain, to bound</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чить</i> (ograníčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limit oneself</li><li>to content oneself with</li><li>to not go beyond</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чивать</i> (ograníčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limiter (type of device)</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чить</i> (ograníčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restrictive, restraining, limiting, binding, limitary</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чить</i> (ograníčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confine, to limit, to restrict, to cut down</li><li>to constrain, to bound</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>грани́чить</i> (graníčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limit oneself</li><li>to content oneself with</li><li>to not go beyond</li></ol><p><i>ограни́чить</i> (ograníčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit strongly, to punch strongly, to sock, to club</li></ol><p><i>огре́ть</i> (ogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit strongly, to punch strongly, to sock, to club</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) missed or poorly processed spot in a field after plowing, sowing, harvesting, etc.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flaw, blemish</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>грех</i> (grex)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very large; ginormous</li></ol><p><i>огро́мный</i> (ogrómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vastness, hugeness</li></ol><p><i>огро́мный</i> (ogrómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>huge, vast, enormous</li></ol><p>Cognate with Czech <i>ohromný</i>, Slovak <i>ohromný</i>, Polish <i>ogromny</i>, Macedonian <i>огромен</i> (ogromen), Bulgarian <i>огро́мен</i> (ogrómen), Slovene <i>ogromen</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>о̏гро̄ман</i> / <i>ȍgrōman</i>.</p><p><i>огроми́ть</i> (ogromítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), by surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>громи́ть</i> (gromítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), compare <i>громи́ла</i> (gromíla). Also compare <i>отгро́хать</i> (otgróxatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>обгрыза́ть</i> (obgryzátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>огры́зть</i> (ogrýztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snarl <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snarl, to talk back</li></ol><p><i>огрыза́ть</i> (ogryzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snarl <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snarl, to talk back</li></ol><p><i>огры́зть</i> (ogrýztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bit, end</li><li>stub, stump</li><li>apple core</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>обгры́зть</i> (obgrýztʹ)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>грызть</i> (gryztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rump of an animal</li><li>backside</li><li>fur or leather skinned from the back of an animal</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) dummy, fool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Oghuz (a group of southwestern Turkic people, including the Turks and Turkmens)</li></ol><p><i>огу́з</i> (ogúz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the quality of being indiscriminate, groundless, unfounded (e.g. accusation)</li></ol><p><i>огу́льный</i> (ogúlʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) wholesale, universal</li><li>indiscriminate, groundless, unfounded (e.g. accusation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>ogólny</i>, which is from <i>ogół</i> (“entirety”), cognate with Belarusian <i>агу́льны</i> (ahúlʹny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cucumber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>огурецъ</i> (ogurec) (огꙋрецъ; mid-16th c.), semi-calque from Old Ruthenian <i>огꙋрокъ</i> (ohurok) (1519+), itself a partial calque from Old Polish <i>ogurek</i> (1394+), from Byzantine Greek <i>ἀγγούριον</i> (angoúrion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cucumber</li></ol><p><i>огуре́ц</i> (oguréc) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>огуре́ц</i> (oguréc): (small) cucumber, gherkin</li></ol><p><i>огуре́ц</i> (oguréc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend (to allow to be used temporarily) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) to borrow (to receive (something) temporarily) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>одолжи́ть</i> (odolžítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give presents</li><li>to endow <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>одари́ть</i> (odarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give presents</li><li>to endow <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дари́ть</i> (darítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give presents</li><li>to endow <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>одари́ть</i> (odarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talent, aptitude, genius</li><li>cleverness</li><li>capability</li></ol><p><i>одарённый</i> (odarjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endowed (<i>with</i>)</li><li>gifted, talented</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress, to clothe</li><li>to provide clothes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cover</li></ol><p><i>оде́ть</i> (odétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress (oneself)</li></ol><p><i>одева́ть</i> (odevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes, clothing, garments</li><li>surfacing, top dressing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;odeďa</i>. Doublet of <i>одёжа</i> (odjóža), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>одёжа</i> (odjóža) or <i>оде́жда</i> (odéžda): nasty clothing</li></ol><p><i>одёжа</i> (odjóža) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cologne, eau de Cologne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>eau de Cologne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stiffen</li><li>to get numb</li><li>to lignify, to become wood</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>деревене́ть</i> (derevenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win, to gain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>одержати</i> (oderžati) in the 17th century, from Polish <i>odzierżeć</i>, which is a partial calque of Latin <i>obtineo</i>. Equivalent to <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defeat, to prevail over</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p>Literally, “to gain the top part”, or more loosely translated as “to gain the upper hand”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win, to gain</li></ol><p><i>одержа́ть</i> (oderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defeat, to prevail over</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p>Literally, “to gain the top part”, or more loosely translated as “gain the upper hand”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsession, diabolism, possession</li></ol><p><i>одержи́мый</i> (oderžímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>одержи́мість</i> (oderžýmistʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessed <i>(by)</i>, obsessed (by)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the possessed</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>одержа́ть</i> (oderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odessite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Odessa; Odessan</li></ol><p>From <i>Оде́сса</i> (Odéssa, Odɛ́ssa, “Odessa”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix). Compare Ukrainian <i>оде́ський</i> (odésʹkyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dressed, clothed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress, to clothe</li><li>to provide clothes</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cover</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;proscribed) to put on (clothing, equipment)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oděti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress (oneself)</li></ol><p><i>оде́ть</i> (odétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blanket, counterpane</li><li>quilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>одѣꙗло</i> (odějalo, “garment, blanket”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>одѣꙗло</i> (odějalo, “outerwear, cloak”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣти</i> (děti, “to set, to place”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)dějadlo</i>, from <i>&#42;děti</i> (“to do, to place”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>, whence English <i>face</i> and <i>do</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;archaic) raiment, attire, garb, habiliments (<i>usually festive, solemn</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>одѣѩиѥ</i> (odějęije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ojibwe (language)</li><li>Ojibwe (people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ojibwe (language)</li><li>Ojibwe (people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one (1)</li><li>alone</li><li>only (especially with negative connotation)</li><li>a, a certain, some</li><li>(<i>contrast</i>) some (do something or have some quality), while others (do not do it; do something else; do not have this quality; or have another or the opposite quality)</li><li><i>(mark)</i> one <i>(out of five)</i>, poor; F mark, F grade</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>одинъ</i> (odinŭ), <i>единъ</i> (jedinŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edinъ</i>. Doublet of <i>един-</i> (jedin-).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Один)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) Odin, the Germanic or Norse god of wisdom, war, magic, and death.</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>Óðinn</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Wōdanaz</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdaz</i> (“rage, manic inspiration, furor poeticus”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₂t-</i> (“to be excited”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one by one (individually)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>the same, one and the same</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the same one(s), one and the same</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) completely alone/lonely (with no family, friends or partners)</li></ol><p>Literally, “single like a finger”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one and only, sole</li></ol><p><i>оди́н</i> (odín) +&lrm; <i>еди́нственный</i> (jedínstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>equally, evenly (in an equal manner)</li></ol><p><i>одина́ковый</i> (odinákovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal, identical, the same</li></ol><p><i>оди́н</i> (odín) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single (<i>not double</i>), unary</li></ol><p>From <i>оди́н</i> (odín), probably as misinterpreted <i>ордина́рный</i> (ordinárnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hendecahedron</li></ol><p><i>оди́ннадцать</i> (odínnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eleven-year period</li><li>eleventh anniversary, eleventh birthday</li></ol><p><i>оди́ннадцать</i> (odínnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eleven-year</li><li>eleven-year-old</li></ol><p><i>оди́ннадцать</i> (odínnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hendecagon</li></ol><p><i>оди́ннадцать</i> (odínnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eleven-storey</li></ol><p><i>оди́ннадцат(ь)</i> (odínnadcat(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleven (11)</li></ol><p>From <i>оди́н</i> (odín, “one”) +&lrm; <i>-надцать</i> (-nadcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>11×, eleven times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solitary, single, lone</li><li>lonely, lonesome</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>single person, lone person (<i>by oneself</i>)</li><li>lonely person</li></ol><p><i>оди́н</i> (odín) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alone</li><li>in a lonely manner</li></ol><p><i>одино́кий</i> (odinókij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loneliness, solitude</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loner, singleton (one who lacks or avoids company)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) solitary <i>(confinement)</i> cell, one-man cell</li><li>(<i>rowing</i>) single scull</li></ol><p><i>одино́кий</i> (odinókij) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) solitary wasp</li><li>potter wasp, eumenine</li></ol><p><i>одино́чный</i> (odinóčnyj, “singular, solo, solitary”) +&lrm; <i>оса́</i> (osá, “wasp”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single</li></ol><p><i>оди́н</i> (odín) +&lrm; <i>-о́чный</i> (-óčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оди́н</i> (odín).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оди́н и то́т же</i> (odín i tót že).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>odious, extremely unpleasant</li></ol><p>Borrowed during the second half of the 19th century from French <i>odieux</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), itself a borrowing from Latin <i>odiōsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>odyssey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run wild</li><li>to become wild, to grow wild</li><li>to become shy or unsociable</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дича́ть</i> (dičátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once, one day, once upon a time</li><li>once (only one time)</li></ol><p><i>оди́н</i> (odín) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>however, yet, still</li><li>(regional,&#32;Siberia) probably</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>you don't say!, no really!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in short, in a word</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the same thing, the same, one and the same</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>uni- (one)</li></ol><p>From <i>оди́н</i> (odín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>simultaneously</li></ol><p>From <i>одновреме́нный</i> (odnovreménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) or from <i>одновре́менный</i> (odnovrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o), depending on stress.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simultaneous, synchronous (at the same time)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-eyed, single-eyed, monocular</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) one-year (relating to a period lasting for one year)</li></ol><p>From <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-humped</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>горб</i> (gorb) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dromedary (<i>Camelus dromedarius</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “one-humped camel”. Compare <i>двуго́рбый верблю́д</i> (dvugórbyj verbljúd, “Bactrian camel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) monohedron</li></ol><p>From <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “one-side-er”. Compare Ukrainian <i>одногра́нник</i> (odnohránnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) odnodvorets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having only one type of yer</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ер</i> (jer) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Unikonta.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unambiguously</li><li>unequivocally</li></ol><p><i>однозна́чный</i> (odnoznáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonymous</li><li>having only one meaning</li><li>single-digit</li><li>unambiguous, unequivocal, explicit</li><li>straightforward</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ, “to mean”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the same name (as something already mentioned)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>имен-</i> (imen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) schoolmate, college mate, fellow student</li></ol><p>From <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ка́ша</i> (káša) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), based on the old dialectal meaning "joint meal, voluntary association/society" of <i>ка́ша</i> (káša).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>compāniō</i> (whence <i>компаньо́н</i> (kompanʹón)) (&lt; <i>con-</i> + <i>pānis</i> + <i>-ō</i>), English <i>mate</i> (<&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + <i>&#42;matiz</i> (whence also <i>meat</i>)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classmate, schoolmate (someone who is in the same class in school)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single-celled, unicellular, monocellular</li></ol><p>From <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka, “cell”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>однокліти́нний</i> (odnoklitýnnyj), Belarusian <i>аднакле́тачны</i> (adnakljétačny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) single-track railway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-room (consisting of only a living room)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occurring once, single (as opposed to multiple)</li><li>unitary, one-fold, single</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) semelfactive, momentary (referring to a verb form that indicates a momentary or punctiliar action)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-, “uni-, one, single”) +&lrm; <i>крат</i> (krat, “-fold”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) semelfactive aspect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coursemate, fellow student, student of the same year</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single-edged</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ле́звие</i> (lézvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one-woman man.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) single-member (referring to an electoral district represented by a single candidate)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>манда́т</i> (mandát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single, for one</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unidirectional</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>напра́вленный</i> (naprávlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concrete verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-legged</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monotony, uniformity, sameness, lack of diversity</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monotonous, uniform, unvarying</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) fellow party member, party colleague (member of the same party)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>па́ртия</i> (pártija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Compare Ukrainian <i>однопарті́єць</i> (odnopartíjecʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) brother soldier</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>полк</i> (polk) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unisexual</li><li>same-sex</li><li>homosexual</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol, “sex, gender”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>same-sex marriage, gay marriage (marriage between two people of the same sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) single-track railway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) single-use item or accessory</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disposable vape</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>раз</i> (raz) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), or <i>однора́зовый</i> (odnorázovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposable</li><li>single, one-time</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>раз</i> (raz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peer-to-peer</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ранг</i> (rang) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homogeneity</li></ol><p><i>одноро́дный</i> (odnoródnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homogeneous, uniform</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-armed, one-handed</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single-handled <i>(for one hand)</i></li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single-row; inline (<i>of skates</i>)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>in-line skate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow-villager</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>сельча́нин</i> (selʹčánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monospermic; monospermous; one-seeded</li></ol><p><i>одно</i> (odno) +&lrm; <i>семян</i> (semjan) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) monosyllabic</li><li>curt, terse, abrupt</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-, “one”) +&lrm; <i>слог</i> (slog, “syllable”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix), a calque of English <i>monosyllabic</i> or Late Latin <i>monosyllabicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>univalve (<i>mollusk</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) single-door, single-flap</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>ство́рка</i> (stvórka) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unilateral, one-sided</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same type</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>тип</i> (tip) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single-volume</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>том</i> (tom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the same mother but a different father, half- (<i>of brothers and sisters</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>утро́б(а)</i> (utrób(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Literally, “of one womb”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an (unrelated) person with the same surname, a namesake</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>фами́лия</i> (famílija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a short period of time, (just) one hour (in expressions)</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-storeyed, single-storey</li></ol><p><i>одно-</i> (odno-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wait a minute, one moment, please</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptance, approval (an agreeing to the action of another)</li></ol><p><i>одобря́ть</i> (odobrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>approvingly (in an approving manner)</li></ol><p><i>одобри́тельный</i> (odobrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>approving, approbative</li></ol><p><i>одо́брить</i> (odóbritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approbate, to approve, to pass, to sanction, to authorize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approbate, to approve, to pass, to sanction, to authorize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acclaiming, friendly</li><li>approbative (expressing approval)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpower, to subdue</li><li>to seize, to overcome</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cope, to manage</li></ol><p><i>одоле́ть</i> (odolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>одоле́нь</i> (odolénʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valerian (<i>Valeriana officinalis</i>)</li><li>water lily (<i>Nymphaea</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;odolěnъ</i> (“valerian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome, to overpower, to conquer</li><li>to seize, to overcome</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cope, to manage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>одолѣти</i> (odolěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;odolěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favor, service</li></ol><p><i>одолжи́ть</i> (odolžítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>commodō</i> (&lt; <i>commodum</i>, akin to <i>commodus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend (to allow to be used temporarily) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘some of something’</small>, <i>along with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) to borrow (to receive (something) temporarily) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘some of something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>долг</i> (dolg) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>одоле́й</i> (odoléj).</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>одоле́й</i> (odoléj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>одоле́нь</i> (odolénʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) odometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) bed</li><li>stretcher</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>одръ</i> (odrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;odrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandelion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think better of it, to change one's mind</li><li>to come to one's senses</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to grok, to get to grasp</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дупло́</i> (dupló, “hole, cavity”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), “getting all holes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grok, to grasp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fool</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дура́к</i> (durák) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupefied, crazed, woozy, zonked</li></ol><p><i>одуре́ть</i> (odurétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дуре́ть</i> (durétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intoxicate, to stupefy, to cloud the consciousness</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to mislead, to misinform</li></ol><p><i>одурма́нить</i> (odurmánitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intoxicate, to stupefy, to cloud the consciousness</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to mislead, to misinform</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дурма́нить</i> (durmánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupor, torpor</li></ol><p><i>одуре́ть</i> (odurétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to inspire, to spiritualize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li><li>exalted, lofty, sublime, enlightened</li><li>animate, alive (<i>especially in regard to normally inanimate objects, such as dolls</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to animate</li><li>to inspire</li></ol><p><i>одушеви́ть</i> (oduševítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) animacy</li></ol><p><i>одушевлённый</i> (oduševljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>animated</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) animate</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>адушаўлёны</i> (adušawljóny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortness of breath, short wind, breathlessness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) clothes, clothing, garments</li></ol><p>Inherited East Slavic form of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;odeďa</i>. Doublet of <i>оде́жда</i> (odéžda), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clothes</li></ol><p><i>одёжа</i> (odjóža) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straightening out, pulling down (<i>esp. of clothing</i>)</li></ol><p><i>одёргивать</i> (odjórgivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(clothes etc)</i> to straighten out, to pull down <i>(into shape)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull up, to call to order</li></ol><p><i>одёрг-</i> (odjórg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(clothes etc)</i> to straighten out, to pull down <i>(into shape)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull up, to call to order</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Southern Russia) glaze, glaze ice, glazed frost, verglas, sleet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>о́желедь</i> (óželedʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)želdь</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)- &#43; &#42;želdь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace (jewelry)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)žerdlьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>жерело́</i> (žereló, “mouth, muzzle, throat; opening”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). Cognates include Bulgarian <i>огърлица</i> (ogǎrlica), Serbo-Croatian <i>огрлица</i>, Czech <i>náhrdelník</i>, Slovak <i>náhrdelník</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, to embitter</li></ol><p><i>ожесточи́ть</i> (ožestočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitterness, obduracy, anger, cruelty</li></ol><p><i>ожесточи́ть</i> (ožestočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, to embitter</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardhearted, bitter, embittered, hardened</li><li>desperate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn oneself, to sting oneself</li></ol><p><i>оже́чь</i> (ožéčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return to life, to revive</li><li>to perk up, to come alive</li></ol><p><i>ожи́ть</i> (ožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animator (one who animates something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring back to life</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to revive, to bring back (something that has been lost or forgotten)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to imbue physical or emotional energy into (e.g. into a person or street)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to improve the appearance of (e.g. a building)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>живи́ть</i> (živítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become animated, to liven up</li><li>to be bucked up</li></ol><p><i>оживи́ть</i> (oživítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revival, reanimation</li><li>liveliness</li></ol><p><i>оживи́ть</i> (oživítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring back to life</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to revive, to bring back (of something that has been lost or forgotten)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to imbue physical or emotional energy (ex. into a person, a street)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to improve the appearance (ex. of a building)</li></ol><p><i>оживи́ть</i> (oživítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become animated, to liven up</li><li>to be bucked up</li></ol><p><i>оживля́ть</i> (oživljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a lively manner, vibrantly, vividly</li></ol><p><i>оживлённый</i> (oživljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excited, animated (<i>experiencing excitement, animation, or liveliness; of a person</i>)</li><li>animated, lively, boisterous, busy (<i>expressing or showing signs of excitement, animation or liveliness; of a look, face, tone, conversation, streets, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) waiting room</li></ol><p><i>ожида́ть</i> (ožidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation, waiting, pending</li></ol><p><i>ожида́ть</i> (ožidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ожида́ние</i> (ožidánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expect, to anticipate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ožidati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-жида́ть</i> (-židátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be expected</li></ol><p><i>ожида́ть</i> (ožidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;low colloquial) to become fat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) porcine, morbidly obese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) porcine, morbidly obese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obesity, corpulence</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>жир</i> (žir) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to become fat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return to life, to revive</li><li>to perk up, to come alive</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ). Compare Czech <i>ožit</i>, Kashubian <i>òżëc</i>, and Polish <i>ożyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burn (a physical injury caused by heat or caustic chemicals), scorch</li><li>scald</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) blight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause anxiety, to disquiet, to worry</li><li>to add someone (more trouble, work, responsibilities, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>забо́тить</i> (zabótitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be concerned <i>(with)</i></li><li>to attend <i>(to)</i>, to see <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>озабо́тить</i> (ozabótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anxiously, apprehensively</li></ol><p><i>озабо́ченный</i> (ozabóčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preoccupation, concern, anxiety</li></ol><p><i>озабо́ченный</i> (ozabóčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preoccupied [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with/about something’</small>]</li><li>anxious, worried (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause anxiety, to disquiet, to worry</li><li>to add someone (more trouble, work, responsibilities, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>озабо́тить</i> (ozabótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be concerned <i>(with)</i></li><li>to attend <i>(to)</i>, to see <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>озабо́чивать</i> (ozabóčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entitle, to head</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>загла́вие</i> (zaglávije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entitle, to head</li></ol><p><i>озагла́вить</i> (ozaglávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wonderingly, quizzically, in a perplexed manner</li></ol><p><i>озада́ченный</i> (ozadáčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>puzzled, perplexed (<i>experiencing puzzlement or confusion; of a person</i>)</li><li>puzzled, perplexed (<i>expressing or showing signs of puzzlement or confusion; of a look, face, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to puzzle, to perplex</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to give a task/job</li></ol><p><i>озада́чить</i> (ozadáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to puzzle, to perplex</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to give a task/job</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зада́ча</i> (zadáča) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irradiation</li><li>strikening, aha moment, aha/bingo factor</li><li>inspiration</li></ol><p><i>озари́ть</i> (ozarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light up; to illuminate</li><li>to brighten, to lighten</li><li><i>thought or idea</i> to strike, to dawn upon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>озарити</i> (ozariti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ozariti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>заря́</i> (zarjá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light up; to illuminate</li><li>to brighten, to lighten</li><li><i>thought or idea</i> to strike, to dawn up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>озарꙗти</i> (ozarjati). By surface analysis, <i>озар-</i> (ozar-) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fly into a rage</li><li>to become a beast, to become brutalized</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run wild, to run riot, to go crazy, to go nuts</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>звере́ть</i> (zverétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(pharmacology,&#32;humorous) a fictional drug that helps one become brutal</li></ol><p>From <i>озвере́ть</i> (ozverétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to be voiced (of a consonant)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зво́нкий</i> (zvónkij) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) voicing (of a consonant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dub, to post-synch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to voice, to give utterance <i>(to)</i>, to read</li></ol><p><i>озву́чить</i> (ozvúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dub, to post-synch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to voice, to give utterance <i>(to)</i>, to read</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dubbing, post-synching, voice recording</li></ol><p><i>озву́чить</i> (ozvúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>health-improving</li></ol><p><i>оздорови́ть</i> (ozdorovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make healthier</li><li>to improve</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>здоро́вый</i> (zdoróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invigoration, recovery, improvement (of health, economy, sanitary conditions, etc.), making oneself or someone healthier</li></ol><p><i>оздорови́ть</i> (ozdorovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make healthier</li><li>to improve</li></ol><p><i>оздорови́ть</i> (ozdorovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planting of greenery, trees and gardens, landscaping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) against the ground</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ob zemь</i>, the latter part an alternative form of <i>&#42;zemľa</i> (“earth, land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>озеро</i> (ozero), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ȅzero</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;éźeran</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́зеро</i> (ózero, “lake”): small lake, pond</li></ol><p><i>о́зеро</i> (ózero) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Aussie (an Australian)</li></ol><p>From Australian English <i>Aussie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Aussie (an Australian)</li></ol><p>From Australian English <i>Aussie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) autumn, (before) winter (<i>of crops</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зима́</i> (zimá) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look around (oneself)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-зира́ть</i> (-zirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>обозли́ться</i> (obozlítʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>озли́ть</i> (ozlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embitter</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зло́ба</i> (zlóba) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), or less likely from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зло́бить</i> (zlóbitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become embittered</li></ol><p>From <i>озло́бить</i> (ozlóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exasperation, bitterness, animosity</li></ol><p><i>озло́бить</i> (ozlóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>embittered, spiteful, resentful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embitter</li></ol><p><i>озло́бить</i> (ozlóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become embittered</li></ol><p>From <i>озлобля́ть</i> (ozlobljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquaint, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become acquainted with, to get to know</li></ol><p><i>ознако́мить</i> (oznakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familiarization, acquaintance</li></ol><p><i>ознако́мить</i> (oznakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquaint, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>ознако́мить</i> (oznakómitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become acquainted with, to get to know</li></ol><p><i>ознакомля́ть</i> (oznakomljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark, to celebrate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>знаменова́ть</i> (znamenovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mean, to signify</li><li>to stand for, to represent, to betoken, to denote</li></ol><p><i>озна́чить</i> (oznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) specified, previously-named, above-mentioned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to mean, to signify</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to stand for, to represent, to betoken, to denote</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зна́чить</i> (znáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chill, fever, shivering</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>ознобить</i> (oznobitʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Lower Sorbian <i>zymnica</i> (akin to <i>зима́</i> (zimá)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gild</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make (someone) rich, to generously endow</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зо́лото</i> (zóloto) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ozone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mischief; hellion; prankster; naughty or mischievous person, especially a child</li></ol><p><i>озор(но́й)</i> (ozor(nój)) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get naughty; to make mischief</li></ol><p>From <i>озорни́к</i> (ozorník).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mischievous, naughty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic. Akin to Russian <i>позо́р</i> (pozór), <i>зазо́рный</i> (zazórnyj), <i>озира́ться</i> (ozirátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mischief, naughtiness (vexatious or annoying conduct)</li></ol><p><i>озор(но́й)</i> (ozor(nój)) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cold, to freeze, to feel cold</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>зя́бнуть</i> (zjábnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lake; lacustrine</li></ol><p><i>о́зеро</i> (ózero) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh! <i>(surprise tinged with fright)</i></li><li>oops!</li><li>ouch!</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Compare Polish <i>oj</i> and Yiddish <i>אוי</i> (oy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a sound <i>ой</i> (oj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the ecumene, the inhabited world, particularly from the point of view of the Ancient Greeks</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>οἰκουμένη</i> (oikouménē). Doublet of <i>экуме́на</i> (ekuména), which was borrowed from Late Latin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet) alternative form of <i>оке́й</i> (okéj)</li></ol><p>Distorted form of <i>оке́й</i> (okéj) from English <i>OK</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ОК)</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>оке́й</i> (okéj)</li></ol><p>Orthographic borrowing from English <i>OK</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near, about</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>о́коло</i> (ókolo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rendering</li></ol><p><i>оказа́ть</i> (okazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to render, to do, to provide (help, service, support, etc.)</li><li>to exert (influence)</li><li>to put, to exert (pressure)</li><li>to accord (respect)</li><li>to offer (resistance)</li><li>to give (someone a reception)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ). Compare Polish <i>okazać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out, to be found, to prove (to be)</li><li>to find oneself</li><li>to be found</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p><i>оказа́ть</i> (okazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) opportunity</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unexpected event</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>occasiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to render, to do, to provide (help, service, support, etc.)</li><li>to exert (influence)</li><li>to put, to exert (pressure)</li><li>to accord (respect)</li><li>to offer (resistance)</li><li>to give (someone a reception)</li></ol><p><i>оказа́ть</i> (okazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out, to be found, to prove (to be)</li><li>to find oneself</li><li>to be found</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p><i>ока́зывать</i> (okázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>showing, rendering</li><li>exerting (influence)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edge, to hem (to provide with a hem, edge, border, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кайма́</i> (kajmá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edge, to hem (to provide with a hem, edge, border, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>окайми́ть</i> (okajmítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calx, cinder, dross, slag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fossil, petrifaction</li></ol><p><i>окамене́лый</i> (okamenélyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>petrified, turned to stone</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) petrified (<i>with fear, shock, etc.</i>)</li><li>hardened, embittered, unfeeling</li></ol><p><i>окамене́ть</i> (okamenétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn to stone, to petrify</li><li><i>(with fear, shock etc)</i> to petrify, to be petrified</li><li>to harden, to become embittered</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>камене́ть</i> (kamenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end, to complete</li><li>to graduate</li></ol><p><i>око́нчить</i> (okónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end, to be over</li></ol><p><i>ока́нчивать</i> (okánčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) the dialectal unreduced pronunciation of unstressed о as [o] (as heard in Northern Russian and standard Ukrainian).</li></ol><p><i>о́кать</i> (ókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up the ground around (something)</li><li>to surround with a ditch, to dig a ditch around, to entrench</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stain (in various places or with many drops)</li></ol><p><i>окопа́ть</i> (okopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>ока́пать</i> (okápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to dig in (to dig trenches for protection against attack)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stain oneself (in various places or with many drops)</li></ol><p><i>ока́пывать</i> (okápyvatʹ, “to dig around”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>ока́пывать</i> (okápyvatʹ, “to stain (<i>in various places or with many drops</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caricature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocarina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to douse [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ока́чивать</i> (okáčivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>окати́ть</i> (okatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock pellet, rolled briquette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hopper car</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce an unstressed <i>о</i> like an <i>о</i> (without the vowel reduction, as in some parts of Northern Russia, considered non-standard)</li></ol><p><i>о</i> (o) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to douse [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>окати́ть</i> (okatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) damned, cursed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>окаꙗньнъ</i> (okajanĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>окай-</i> (okaj-) +&lrm; <i>-анный</i> (-annyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oceanarium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oceanographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ocean, oceanic</li></ol><p><i>океа́н</i> (okeán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>OK, okay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>okay</i>, <i>OK</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan (with one's eyes)</li></ol><p><i>окид-</i> (okid-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan (with one's eyes)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxidation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;dated) oxide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) occasionalism</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nonce word, occasionalism</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>occasionālis</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ока́зия</i> (okázija) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occultism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occultist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occult</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>occultus</i> (“hidden, secret”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occupier, occupant, invader</li><li>(biology,&#32;zoology) an animal that steals and lives in the nest of another animal.</li><li>(specifically,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;derogatory) a Russian soldier who takes part in the 2022–present Russo–Ukrainian war</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) occupying</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) occupation</li></ol><p><i>оккупа́ция</i> (okkupácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occupation (control of a country or region by a hostile army)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>occupātio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occupy, to invade</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>okkupieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gross pay</li><li>a riza (a thin metal cover protecting an icon)</li><li>a treasure binding, a jewelled bookbinding (a luxurious book cover)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>About a beard: full-bodied and thick.</li></ol><p><i>о</i> (o) +&lrm; <i>кладь</i> (kladʹ) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slander, to defame, to malign</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>клевета́ть</i> (klevetátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover by gluing (e.g. a wall with wallpaper)</li></ol><p><i>окле́ить</i> (okléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover by gluing (e.g. a wall with wallpaper)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to recuperate, to recover</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to come to, to wake up</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get out of a difficult situation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hail, to call <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>окли́кнуть</i> (oklíknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hail, to call <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кли́кнуть</i> (klíknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okъno</i>. Related to Russian <i>о́ко</i> (óko, “eye”).</p><p>For semantic parallels, compare Old English <i>ēagþȳrel</i> (“window”, literally “eye-hole”) and Old Norse <i>vindauga</i> (“window”, literally “wind-eye”) (whence English <i>window</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) eye (used in proverbs and expressions)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>око</i> (oko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oko</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eye for an eye, tit for tat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackles, manacles, gyves, fetters, irons (paired wrist or ankle restraints)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bondage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick one's heels, to lounge about</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>колдова́ть</i> (koldovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense, drivel, rubbish</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó, “wheel”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica). Compare <i>околеси́ть</i> (okolesítʹ, “to talk nonsense, to speak in a roundabout way, to beat around the bush”) (also <i>околёсить</i> (okoljósitʹ)), <i>околёсок</i> (okoljósok, “nonsense”). Sense derivation is "detour" → "roundabout speech" → "nonsense".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;often&#32;figuratively) to freeze to death</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;of animals;&#32;by extension,&#32;of people,&#32;derogatory) to drop dead</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>коле́ть</i> (kolétʹ, “to freeze, to become numb”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) village fence, village gate</li><li>outskirts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near, around, about, by</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okolo</i>, from <i>&#42;kolo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>near-, para-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;economics) state-affiliated, state-controlled, quasi-state (formally non-governmental, but in practice closely affiliated with or controlled by state authorities)</li></ol><p><i>о́коло</i> (ókolo, “near”) +&lrm; <i>госуда́рственный</i> (gosudárstvennyj, “state, public”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near-Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>около-</i> (okolo-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>навколозе́мний</i> (navkolozémnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) parauterine, periuterine</li></ol><p><i>около-</i> (okolo-) +&lrm; <i>ма́тка</i> (mátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) neighbourhood</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) ward, town district</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) police station</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>колпа́к</i> (kolpák) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>околе́сица</i> (okolésica)</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó, “wheel”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica). See <i>околе́сица</i> (okolésica) for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extremity (furthest point)</li></ol><p><i>оконе́чный</i> (okonéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windowpane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) window</li></ol><p>From <i>окон-</i> (okon-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare <i>о́кон</i> (ókon), genitive plural of <i>окно́</i> (oknó, “window”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>okonomiyaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>お好み焼き</i> (おこのみやき, okonomiyaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disconcert, to discomfit, to embarrass, to put in an awkward or embarrassing position</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>конфу́зить</i> (konfúzitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disconcerted, to be embarrassed, to get into an embarrassing situation</li></ol><p><i>оконфу́зить</i> (okonfúzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>окно́</i> (oknó): (small) window</li></ol><p><i>окно́</i> (oknó) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, close, termination, completion, conclusion</li><li>graduation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ending, inflection</li><li>(sex,&#32;slang) finishing</li></ol><p><i>око́нчить</i> (okónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>finally, definitively</li></ol><p><i>оконча́тельный</i> (okončátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final, ultimate; definitive</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflectional</li></ol><p><i>оконча́ть</i> (okončátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>ока́нчивать</i> (okánčivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>око́нчить</i> (okónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to end, to complete</li><li>to graduate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish, to end, to be over</li></ol><p><i>око́нчить</i> (okónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) foxhole, dugout, trench</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>окопъ</i> (okop) (att. 1678), from Old Ruthenian <i>окопъ</i> (okop) (att. 1578), from Middle Polish <i>okop</i> (16th c.), from <i>okopać</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kop-</i>, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kep-</i> (“to strike, beat”).</p><p>Probable cognates include Ancient Greek <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, to cut, to shake”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up the ground around (something)</li><li>to surround with a ditch, to dig a ditch around</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to dig in (to dig trenches for protection against attack)</li></ol><p><i>окопа́ть</i> (okopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foxhole; trench</li></ol><p>From <i>око́п</i> (okóp, “foxhole”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ham, gammon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>окорокъ</i> (okorokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okorkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;korkъ</i> (“leg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lambing</li><li>having kittens</li><li>lambing time</li><li>time of having kittens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth to kittens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become numb from the cold</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кочене́ть</i> (kočenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>око́шко</i> (okóško), diminutive of <i>окно́</i> (oknó) (small) window; counter (in a ticket office, bank, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>око́шко</i> (okóško) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>окно́</i> (oknó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>okra; gumbo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outskirts (the edges or areas around a city or town)</li><li>fringe, margin</li><li>periphery</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Ukraine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)krajina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>край</i> (kraj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) outskirts; marginal, peripheral (relating to or located at the outskirts or margins)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)krajinьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>окра́ина</i> (okráina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coloring, hue (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>окра́сить</i> (okrásitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tincture, to paint, to dye</li><li>to tinge <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colouring/coloring, painting, dyeing</li><li>colouring/coloring, colouration/coloration, colour/color</li><li>tinge, tint</li></ol><p><i>окра́сить</i> (okrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tincture, to paint, to dye</li><li>to tinge <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>окра́сить</i> (okrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow stronger</li><li>to get firmly established</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кре́пнуть</i> (krépnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) around, nearby, in the neighborhood</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) around, near</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>окрьстъ</i> (okrĭstŭ). By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кре́ст</i> (krést). Compare Bulgarian <i>о́кръст</i> (ókrǎst), Polabian <i>vuokárst</i> (“circle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baptize, to christen</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nickname</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surroundings (area surrounding someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>environs, vicinity, neighbourhood</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) neighbourhood</li></ol><p><i>окре́стный</i> (okréstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surrounding, local, neighboring</li></ol><p>From obsolete <i>окре́ст</i> (okrést, “in the neighborhood”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peremptory shout/cry</li><li>hail, hallo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stain with blood</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>крова́вый</i> (krovávyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) or directly from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>крова́вить</i> (krovávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodstained, bloody</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stain with blood, to bloody</li></ol><p><i>окрова́вить</i> (okrovávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspersion, affusion (a sprinkling of holy water)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) okroshka</li></ol><p><i>окро́шка</i> (okróška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;rare) to crumble</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кроши́ть</i> (krošítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>okroshka</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) medley, hotchpotch, hodgepodge</li></ol><p>From <i>окроши́ть</i> (okrošítʹ, “to crumble”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>district, okrug</li><li>subprefecture (of Japan)</li><li>county (of the USA or Canada)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) circuit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>округъ</i> (okrugŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okrǫgъ</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;krǫgъ</i> (“circle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbourhood, area</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rounding</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) rounding, rounding off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to round <i>(off)</i>, to make round</li><li><i>(numbers)</i> to round <i>(off)</i> <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(esp. wealth)</i> to increase, to add <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кру́глый</i> (krúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become round</li></ol><p><i>округли́ть</i> (okruglítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roundness, circularity</li></ol><p><i>окру́глый</i> (okrúglyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rounded</li><li>smooth, measured</li><li>complete (sentences, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>круг</i> (krug) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to round <i>(off)</i>, to make round</li><li><i>(numbers)</i> to round <i>(off)</i> <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(esp. wealth)</i> to increase, to add <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>округли́ть</i> (okruglítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become round</li></ol><p><i>округля́ть</i> (okrugljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rounded, rounded off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather round</li><li>to encircle, to ring in</li><li>to surround, to lavish</li><li>to encircle, to round up</li></ol><p><i>окружи́ть</i> (okružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>environment, ambient (something that surrounds)</li><li>environment (the natural world or ecosystem)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milieu, social surroundings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>others, surrounding people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surrounding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entourage, environment, surroundings, milieu</li><li>(<i>military</i>) encirclement</li></ol><p><i>окружи́ть</i> (okružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather round</li><li>to encircle, to ring in</li><li>to surround, to lavish</li><li>to encircle, to round up</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>кружи́ть</i> (kružítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) district</li><li>circular</li></ol><p><i>о́круг</i> (ókrug) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumference, circle</li></ol><p>From <i>окружно́й</i> (okružnój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Cognate with Belarusian <i>акру́жнасць</i> (akrúžnascʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire, to uplift spiritually</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire, to uplift spiritually</li></ol><p><i>окрыли́ть</i> (okrylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>аксами́т</i> (aksamít)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) oxymoron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὀξύμωρον</i> (oxúmōron).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acute accent (acute accent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to do the sign of the cross (on someone)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to baptise</li></ol><p>From <i>окрести́ть</i> (okrestítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) to do the sign of the cross (on oneself)</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;figuratively,&#32;usually as an imperative exclamation) to come to one's senses, to wake up, to pull oneself together, to collect oneself, to think, to calm down</li></ol><p><i>оксти́ть</i> (okstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Oxonian, Oxford</li></ol><p>From <i>О́ксфорд</i> (Óksford) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) oxymoron (figure of speech)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>октя́брь</i> (oktjábrʹ) or <i>октя́бря</i> (oktjábrja, “October”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octave</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>octō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octahedron</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octet</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>octō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>octillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>27</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>octodecillion (10<sup>57</sup>, or 10<sup>108</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hash (the &#35; symbol)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>October</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>октѧбрь</i> (oktębrĭ). Etymologically, this form is "Octember", and was formed by analogy with the other three month names ending in <i>-ѧбрь</i> (-jębrĭ). The parallel form <i>октомбрь</i> (oktombrĭ), <i>октѫбрь</i> (oktǫbrĭ) is found in Old Church Slavonic, next to <i>октоврь</i> (oktovrĭ). Ultimately borrowed, via Byzantine Greek <i>Ὀκτώβριος</i> (Oktṓbrios), from Latin <i>Octōber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) October</li></ol><p><i>октя́брь</i> (oktjábrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"Little Octobrist" (any child of 6 to 9 years old, grades 1 to 3, in the Former Soviet Union. This is the earliest of three stages before becoming a full member of the Communist Party).</li></ol><p>From <i>октябрь</i> (oktjabrʹ), referring to the October Revolution of 1917, and <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyepiece</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the plural) glasses, spectacles</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>oculaire</i>, from Latin <i>oculāris</i> (compare English <i>ocular</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, to plunge</li></ol><p><i>окун-</i> (okun-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge <i>(into, in)</i>, to dip <i>(into)</i></li><li>to plunge <i>(into, in)</i>, to become (utterly) absorbed/engrossed <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>окуна́ть</i> (okunátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dip, to plunge (something into something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okǫpnǫti</i>.</p><p>For the <i><b>п</b></i> (<b>p</b>) dropping, compare <i>тону́ть</i> (tonútʹ) (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topnǫti</i>, akin to <i>&#42;topiti</i> (whence <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge <i>(into, in)</i>, to dip <i>(into)</i></li><li>to plunge <i>(into, in)</i>, to become (utterly) absorbed/engrossed <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>окуну́ть</i> (okunútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perch</li><li>bass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>о́кунь</i> (ókunĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;òkunь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>о́кунь</i> (ókunʹ): perch, bass</li></ol><p><i>о́кунь</i> (ókunʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover of expenditure, recoupment</li></ol><p><i>окупа́емый</i> (okupájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reimburse, to pay back, to compensate (expenses)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) to make amends for, to atone for, to redeem</li></ol><p><i>окупи́ть</i> (okupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reimbursed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to pay off, to be worth it in the end (<i>of difficulties, hardships, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>окупа́ть</i> (okupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reimburse, to pay back, to compensate (expenses)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) to make amends for, to atone for, to redeem</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reimbursed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to pay off, to be worth it in the end (<i>of difficulties, hardships, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>окупи́ть</i> (okupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigarette butt, cigar butt</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>окури́ть</i> (okurítʹ), itself from <i>о-</i> (o-) + <i>курить</i> (kuritʹ, “to smoke tobacco”). Compare Ukrainian <i>недокурок</i> (nedokurok, “cigarette butt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap <i>(round)</i></li><li>to cloak <i>(in)</i>, to shroud <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ку́тать</i> (kútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap (<i>round</i>)</li><li>to cloak (<i>in</i>), to shroud (<i>in</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>оку́тать</i> (okútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to loosen the soil around the plants, piling it to the base of the stems or trunks</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to influence someone or something to achieve the desired effect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to loosen the soil around the plants, piling it to the base of the stems or trunks</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to influence someone or something to achieve the desired effect</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ку́ча</i> (kúča) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ecclesiastical</i>) any alcoholic beverage except wine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>олъ</i> (olŭ, “fermented liquor; strong drink”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ȍlъ</i> (“beer; fermented liquor”). Doublet of <i>эль</i> (elʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ола́дья</i> (oládʹja)</li></ol><p>From <i>ола́дья</i> (oládʹja) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resembling oladyi</li></ol><p><i>ола́дь(и)</i> (oládʹ(i)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick pancake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ола́дьꙗ</i> (oládĭja), further borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐλάδιον</i> (eládion), diminutive of <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil, olive-oil”).</p><p>Compare Belarusian <i>ала́дка</i> (aládka), Ukrainian <i>ола́док</i> (oládok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alderman, guildmaster (headman, ruler, governor, or warden of a guild)</li><li>alderman (member of a municipal legislative body in a city or town)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>alderman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) Oldowan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oleate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ice-covered, frozen</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ледене́(ть)</i> (ledené(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reindeer herder</li></ol><p>From <i>оле́нь</i> (olénʹ, “deer”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reindeer herding, reindeer breeding</li></ol><p>From <i>оленево́д</i> (olenevód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) deer, reindeer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venison</li></ol><p><i>оле́нь</i> (olénʹ, “deer”) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína, suffix denoting a type of meat)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deer</li><li>buck, stag (male deer)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) dimwit, fool, narrow-minded individual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;elỳ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fawn, young deer, deer yearling</li></ol><p><i>оле́нь</i> (olénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive oil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) olive</li><li>olive-green, olivaceous (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оли́вка</i> (olívka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olivier salad, Russian salad (traditional Russian salad of boiled vegetables with mayonnaise)</li></ol><p>From the French given name <i>Olivier</i>,</p><p>named after Russian&#32;chef of Belgian origin&#32;Lucien Olivier, who invented it in the 1860s. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>олів'є́</i> (olivʺjé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oligarch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὀλίγοι</i> (olígoi, “few”) + <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “rule”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oligarchic</li></ol><p><i>олига́рх</i> (oligárx) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oligarchy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὀλιγαρχία</i> (oligarkhía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>herpetology</i>) kukri snake (<i>Oligodon</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Oligodon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) oligophrenic (person suffering from oligophrenia)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὀλίγοι</i> (olígoi, “few”) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind, soul”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) oligophrenia, mental deficiency, mental retardation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὀλίγοι</i> (olígoi, “few”) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind, soul”) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija, “-ia”, disease suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olympiad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Olympian (Ancient Greek god living on Mount Olympus)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) serene, solemn, stately person</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) arrogant, inaccessible person</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) Olympian (participant in the modern Olympic Games)</li></ol><p><i>Оли́мп</i> (Olímp, “Olympus”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ец</i> (-íjec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) sports sweatshirt with a zip fastener</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Olympic, of Olympus, Olympian</li></ol><p><i>Оли́мпия</i> (Olímpija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embody, to personify</li></ol><p><i>олицетвори́ть</i> (olicetvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>олово</i> (olovo, “lead, tin”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olovo</i> (“lead, tin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stanniferous</li></ol><p><i>о́лово</i> (ólovo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-носи́ть</i> (-nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tin (made of tin)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оловѧнꙑи</i> (olovęnyi), <i>оловѣнꙑи</i> (olověnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olověnъ</i>, <i>&#42;olověnьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;olovo</i> (“lead”) + <i>&#42;-ěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin soldier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Olonets</li></ol><p><i>Оло́нец</i> (Olónec) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) blockhead, dimwit (stupid person)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer from <i>во́лух</i> (vólux), from <i>вол</i> (vol, “ox”), i.e. initially <i>воло́вий пасту́х</i> (volóvij pastúx, “ox shepherd”). Dismissed by Chernykh. Per Chernykh akin to <i>волхв</i> (volxv) (note <i>о́лух царя́ небе́сного</i> (ólux carjá nebésnovo)). Alternatively from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;оглухъ</i> (&#42;ogluxŭ), whence Ukrainian <i>оглух</i> (ohlux, “someone hard of hearing”), due to association between <i>глухова́тый</i> (gluxovátyj, “hard of hearing”) and <i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj, “stupid”). Attested since 1769.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gannet, sulid (sea bird)</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>о́лух</i> (ólux).</p><p>Compare typologically Translingual <i>Morus</i>, German <i>Tölpel</i>, Polish <i>głuptak</i> (akin to <i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj), <i>глупе́ц</i> (glupéc), also note <i>глупы́ш</i> (glupýš, “silly fellow, naive kid, silly little thing; fulmar”)). Also compare English <i>booby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alder (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olьxa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élis-</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>јо́ха</i>, <i>jóha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alder</li></ol><p><i>ольх(а́)</i> (olʹx(á)) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alder forest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Olsztyn</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>О́льштын</i> (Ólʹštyn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dipper (any bird of the genus <i>Cinclus</i>, especially the white-throated dipper (<i>Cinclus cinclus</i>))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ohm (Ω)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inula, elecampane (<i>Inula</i> spp., especially <i>Inula helenium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;omanъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Оман)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oman (a country in West Asia in the Middle East)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Omani</li></ol><p><i>Ома́н</i> (Omán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оман</i> (oman, “elecampane”).</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Оманъ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Ома́н</i> (Omán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>homard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lobster</li></ol><p><i>ома́р</i> (omár) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;Ukraine) initialism of <i>отде́льная механизи́рованная брига́да</i> (otdélʹnaja mexanizírovannaja brigáda); separate mechanized brigade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ombudsman (official who investigates complaints)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ombudsman</i>, from Swedish <i>ombudsman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemlock, conium</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oměgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omega (the Greek letter Ω&#47;ω)</li><li>omega (the old Cyrillic letter Ѡ&#47;ѡ, used in Church Slavonic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὦ μέγα</i> (ô méga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>оме́гой</i> (omégoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Umayyad</li></ol><p><i>Омейя́д</i> (Omejjád) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistletoe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;emela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abomination (the feeling of extreme disgust)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting, loathsome, sickening, revolting</li></ol><p>From <i>омерзить</i> (omerzitʹ, “to disgust”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omicron (the Greek letter Ο (O)&#47;ο (o))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὂ μικρόν</i> (ò mikrón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omelette</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) blowjob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>omelette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablution (the act of washing or cleansing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>омъвеньѥ</i> (omŭvenĭje). By surface analysis, <i>омы́ть</i> (omýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije). Doublet of <i>омы́тие</i> (omýtije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homoglyph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejuvenate</li></ol><p><i>омолоди́ть</i> (omolodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejuvenate</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>молоди́ть</i> (molodítʹ) or perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>молодо́й</i> (molodój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rejuvenation</li></ol><p><i>омолоди́ть</i> (omolodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) homonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riot policeman</li></ol><p><i>ОМО́Н</i> (OMÓN) +&lrm; <i>-овец</i> (-ovec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) homophone (words with the same pronunciation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὁμόφωνος</i> (homóphōnos), from <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) + <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omophorion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to cloud</li><li>to darken, to cloud, to overshadow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>омрачати</i> (omračati). By surface analysis, <i>омрачи́ть</i> (omračítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to cloud</li><li>to darken, to cloud, to overshadow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>омрачити</i> (omračiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obmorčiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>мрачи́ть</i> (mračítʹ). Doublet of <i>обморо́чить</i> (obmoróčitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Omsk (city and oblast in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Om (river in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Ом(ск)</i> (Om(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p><p><i>Омь</i> (Omʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Coregonus autumnalis</i>, whitefish (<i>species</i>) (prized for its high fat content)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(underwater)</i> pool, slough</li><li>whirlpool</li><li>slough</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-мут</i> (-mut), combining form of <i>му́тный</i> (mútnyj, “murky, turbid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to wash</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to pour over from all sides, to lap (<i>of water</i>)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to surround with its water (<i>of the sea or ocean</i>)</li></ol><p><i>омы́ть</i> (omýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to wash</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>third-person masculine singular pronoun: he, it.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>онъ</i> (onŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;onъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;anas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énos</i> (“that”). Inflected forms from Old East Slavic <i>и</i> (i), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;is</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ís</i> and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>. See о́ный (ónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, it (<i>third-person feminine singular pronoun</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ona</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onanism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onanist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muskrat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ondatra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;feminine) they</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dumb, to mute</li><li>to become numb</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>неме́ть</i> (nemétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>the third-person plural</i>) they</li><li>(the third-person singular,&#32;usually&#32;humorous,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated) they</li><li>(the third-person singular,&#32;gender-neutral,&#32;proscribed) they</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>они</i> (oni), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;onъ</i>.</p><p>The prefixation of /n/ after prepositions comes from Proto-Slavic prepositions such as <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> (“with”) (compare Ancient Greek <i>σύν</i> (sún) and Latin <i>cum</i>), that originally ended in <i>&#42;-n</i> and governed oblique cases. Since the prepositions and the pronouns occurred together so often, it was easy to lose track of which word the final <i>&#42;-n</i> belonged to, and so it was reinterpreted as part of the pronouns; compare Old English <i>an</i>, which was reinterpreted in the same way. Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> <i>&#42;jьmi</i> became modern Russian <i>с ни́ми</i> (s ními), and this rule was extended to all prepositions governing any third-person pronoun.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onyx</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ὄνυξ</i> (ónux). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃negʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) onychiurid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Onychiuridae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) a person belonging to a legendary tribe from Chukchi legends</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chukchi.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oncologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>онколо́гия</i> (onkológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oncological</li></ol><p><i>онколо́гия</i> (onkológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oncology</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) cancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>online (connected to the Internet or a computer network)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>online</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) An online auction, an auction held digitally over the Internet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>online auction</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>online game</li></ol><p>From <i>онла́йн</i> (onlájn) +&lrm; <i>игра́</i> (igrá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female OnlyFans model</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly in Korean media,&#32;women's speech) unnie (elder sister, or an affectionate term of address for a somewhat older woman)</li><li>(<i>South Korean idol fandom</i>) unnie (older female member of an idol group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>언니</i> (eonni, “older sister of a woman”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>it (<i>third-person neuter singular pronoun</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ono</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) onomastics</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὀνομαστικός</i> (onomastikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onomatopoeia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) onosma (<i>Onosma</i> species)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Onosma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onsen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>温泉</i> (おんせん, onsen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ontologically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ontological</li></ol><p><i>онтоло́гия</i> (ontológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) ontology</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) ontology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>он</i> (on).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) that, that very, the aforementioned</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) that other (as opposed to this)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) otherworldly, unearthly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>онъ</i> (onŭ, “that, yonder”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;onъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;anas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énos</i> (“that”). See он (on).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;feminine) Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оне́</i> (oné, “they”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) UNO (United Nations Organization)</li></ol><p><i>ОО́Н</i> (OÓN) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;zoology) oonopid, goblin spider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Oonopidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>An exclamation accompanying some energetic action (often unexpected), like a jump, sudden movement, etc.</li><li>An exclamation encouraging some energetic action.</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>whoops!; oops! <i>(as when stumbling and almost tripping or almost stepping on something to be avoided)</i></li><li>alley-oop <i>(also used as an exclamation when dogs do tricks)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall (off) (<i>of leaves, fruits etc.</i>)</li><li>to subside, to lessen, to diminish, to sink in</li></ol><p><i>опад-</i> (opad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being late</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be late</li></ol><p><i>опозда́ть</i> (opozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) back, backwards, the other way round</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opaque (sort of faience)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>опако</i> (opako), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opakъ</i> (“the other way round”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>opaque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) opal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disgrace</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>опалъка</i> (opalŭka, “anger”), from <i>палити</i> (paliti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to singe (animal, feathers, etc.)</li><li>to sear, to burn, to scorch</li></ol><p><i>опали́ть</i> (opalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to singe (animal, feathers, etc.)</li><li>to sear, to burn, to scorch</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) opal</li><li>opal-colored (milky-white with yellow or blue)</li></ol><p><i>опа́л</i> (opál) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formwork</li></ol><p>From <i>опалубить</i> (opalubitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgraced, fallen into disfavour/disfavor, fallen into disgrace</li></ol><p><i>опа́ла</i> (opála) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sear, to burn, to scorch</li></ol><p><i>опали́ть</i> (opalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leavened dough</li><li>leaven, sourdough</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maggot (dipterous insect's larva that eats decomposing flesh)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>опа́риш</i> (opáryš), Belarusian <i>апа́рыш</i> (apáryš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be afraid of, to fear [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to avoid, to beware of, to refrain from [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘some/something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пасти́</i> (pastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, anxiety, apprehension, alarm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dread, fear</li></ol><p>From <i>опас(а́ться)</i> (opas(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cautiously, fearfully</li></ol><p><i>опа́сливый</i> (opáslivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cautious, wary</li><li>fearful</li><li>anxious</li></ol><p><i>опаса́ть(ся)</i> (opasátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dangerously, perilously</li></ol><p><i>опа́сный</i> (opásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, peril</li><li>dangerousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)pasьnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>опа́сный</i> (opásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dangerous, perilous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)pasьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall (off) (<i>of leaves, fruits etc.</i>)</li><li>to subside, to lessen, to diminish, to sink in</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardianship, wardship, tutelage, custody</li><li>trusteeship</li><li>guardians, board of guardians</li><li>care, surveillance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)peka</i>, a calque of Latin <i>prōcūrātor</i>. Compare Czech <i>рéčе</i>, Ukrainian <i>опі́ка</i> (opíka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be guardian to, to be trustee to, to have the wardship (of); to be warden to</li><li>to patronize; to watch over, to take care of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opekati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian, trustee, patron</li><li>(<i>law</i>) custodian of a child</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opekunъ</i>, from <i>&#42;peťi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>опека́ть</i> (opekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tutelary, custodian</li></ol><p><i>опеку́н</i> (opekún) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardianship; tutelage</li><li>custodianship</li></ol><p><i>опеку́н</i> (opekún) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) police operative/agent, member of a police task force, fed</li></ol><p>Short for <i>операти́вник</i> (operatívnik), <i>оперуполномо́ченный</i> (operupolnomóčennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) opera</li><li>opera house</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) category, vein (in the following constructions)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>opera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fairy opera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>opéra-féerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) operand</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>operand</i> or French <i>opérande</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>operandum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>операти́вная па́мять</i> (operatívnaja pámjatʹ); RAM</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brief operational meeting on current affairs or work</li><li>(<i>business</i>) written operational message</li><li>(<i>business</i>) car for operational officers of the Russian Ministry of Internal Affairs</li></ol><p><i>операти́вный</i> (operatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field investigator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>efficiently, promptly, quickly</li></ol><p><i>операти́вный</i> (operatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) operational-search (referring to pre-trial investigative activities when a crime may have been committed)</li></ol><p><i>операти́вно</i> (operatívno) +&lrm; <i>разыскно́й</i> (razysknój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>efficiency</li></ol><p><i>операти́вный</i> (operatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>efficient, prompt, quick</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) operative, surgical</li><li>(<i>military</i>) strategical, operation(s), operational</li><li>operation(s)</li><li>(economy,&#32;finance) operational</li></ol><p>Perhaps German <i>operativ</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Latin <i>operātīvus</i>, from Latin <i>operō</i> (&lt; Latin <i>opus</i>, akin to <i>ops</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>operator, operative</li><li>machine-man, mechanic</li><li>cameraman</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) surgeon</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) functional, operator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>operating room (in a hospital)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>software</i>) operating system</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) operating</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) operations</li></ol><p>German <i>Operation</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>operation</li></ol><p>Attested since the reign of Peter the Great in the military and surgical sense; from Latin <i>operatio</i>, via Polish <i>operacja</i> or German <i>Operation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass ahead, to take a lead, to outstrip, to leave behind</li><li>to do something ahead of someone; to beat someone to the punch</li><li>to outdo, to surpass</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пе́ред</i> (péred) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass ahead, to take a lead, to outstrip, to leave behind</li><li>to do something ahead of someone</li><li>to outdo, to surpass</li></ol><p><i>опереди́ть</i> (operedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticipatory, advanced, leading</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advance, lead, outrunning</li></ol><p><i>опереди́ть</i> (operedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feathering, plumage</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) empennage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>operetta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>operetta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on/against something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, to rest [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on/against something/someone’</small>]</li><li>to rely (on), to depend on, to be guided (by)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;transitive) to operate on (someone)</li><li>(military,&#32;intransitive) to operate, to conduct military operations</li><li>(finance,&#32;intransitive) to carry out a commercial or financial transaction</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to operate, to use [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a machine, calculator, computer, etc.’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fully fledged</li><li>to become independent, stand on one's own feet/legs</li></ol><p><i>опери́ть</i> (operítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) opera</li><li>operatic</li></ol><p>From <i>о́пера</i> (ópera, “opera”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>security officer, detective</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>операти́вный уполномо́ченный</i> (operatívnyj upolnomóčennyj, “operations agent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>операти́вный штаб</i> (operatívnyj štab)</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>операти́вный штаб</i> (operatívnyj štab).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sadden, to grieve</li><li>to upset, to disappoint</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>печа́лить</i> (pečálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become sad, to grieve</li></ol><p><i>опеча́лить</i> (opečálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal, to seal up</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to typo, to make a typo</li></ol><p><i>опеча́тать</i> (opečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>опеча́тка</i> (opečátka) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typo, misprint (a spelling or typographical error)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal, to seal up</li></ol><p><i>опеча́тать</i> (opečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be taken aback</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пе́ший</i> (péšij, “unmounted, pedestrian”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ); original sense "to be thrown off a horse".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drink out of house and home (to drink at someone's expense, causing them material damage)</li></ol><p><i>опи́ть</i> (opítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat, to strike</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пиздю́ль</i> (pizdjúlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat, to strike</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пиздю́ль</i> (pizdjúlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>о́пиум</i> (ópium)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to file or saw all sides of</li></ol><p><i>опили́ть</i> (opilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to file or saw all sides of</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) shaving, filing, sawdust</li></ol><p><i>опили́ть</i> (opilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawdust, filings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on/against something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, to rest [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘on/against something/someone’</small>]</li><li>to rely (on), to depend on, to be guided (by)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пирать</i> (piratʹ), the latter being a variant of <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ, “to carry, move, trudge”), q. v.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>description</li></ol><p><i>описа́ть</i> (opisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) circumscribed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>описа́ние</i> (opisánije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>descriptive</li></ol><p><i>описа́ть</i> (opisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to describe, to depict, to portray (to represent in words)</li><li>to make / take an inventory (of), to inventory</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to describe, to circumscribe</li><li>to urinate (on something)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”)</p><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пи́сать</i> (písatʹ, “to pee, to piss”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a slip <i>(of a pen)</i></li><li>to wet oneself</li></ol><p><i>описа́ть</i> (opisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>опи́сать</i> (opísatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slip of the pen, miswriting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to describe, to depict, to portray (to represent in words)</li><li>to make / take an inventory (of), to inventory</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to describe, to circumscribe</li><li>to urinate (on something)</li></ol><p><i>описа́ть</i> (opisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>опи́сать</i> (opísatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a slip <i>(of a pen)</i></li><li>to wet oneself</li></ol><p><i>опи́сывать</i> (opísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventory, making of an inventory</li><li>list, inventory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drink out of house and home (to drink at someone's expense, causing them material damage)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>opium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewail, to bemoan</li><li>to deplore, to mourn (over someone or something), to mourn the loss of</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewail, to bemoan</li><li>to deplore, to mourn (over someone or something), to mourn the loss of</li></ol><p><i>опла́кать</i> (oplákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment (the act of paying)</li></ol><p><i>оплати́ть</i> (oplatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay, to repay, to pay off</li><li>to remunerate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay, to repay, to pay off</li><li>to remunerate</li></ol><p><i>оплати́ть</i> (oplatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit all over</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to humiliate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to braid <i>(with)</i>, to twine <i>(round)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get round, to trick</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to braid <i>(with)</i>, to twine <i>(round)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get round, to trick</li></ol><p><i>оплет-</i> (oplet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slap in the face, box on the ear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insemination, fertilization (act of making pregnant)</li></ol><p><i>оплодотвори́ть</i> (oplodotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inseminate, to fertilize, to impregnate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плодотвори́ть</i> (plodotvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inseminate, to fertilize, to impregnate</li></ol><p><i>оплодотвори́ть</i> (oplodotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stronghold, bulwark</li></ol><p><i>о</i> (o) +&lrm; <i>плот</i> (plot). Akin to <i>плете́нь</i> (pleténʹ), <i>плести́</i> (plestí).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ба́шня</i> (bášnja), <i>бастион́</i> (bastioń) several steps omitting from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bastijan</i> (&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bastaz</i>, whence also English <i>bast</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a mistake, to blunder</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плоша́ть</i> (plošátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inadvertence, negligence</li></ol><p><i>опло́шный</i> (oplóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wrong, erroneous</li></ol><p><i>оплоша́ть</i> (oplošátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to swim around</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swell up; to become bloated; to gutter (of a candle)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit all over</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to humiliate</li></ol><p><i>оплева́ть</i> (oplevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify (to communicate knowledge to (an)other(s))</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>по́весть</i> (póvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify (to communicate knowledge to (an)other(s))</li></ol><p><i>оповести́ть</i> (opovestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notification, notice, alert</li></ol><p><i>оповести́ть</i> (opovestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opodeldoc</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delay</li><li>being late, lateness, tardiness</li></ol><p><i>опозда́ть</i> (opozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be late</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>по́зд(ний)</i> (pózd(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to identify</li></ol><p><i>опозна́ть</i> (opoznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identification</li></ol><p><i>опозна́ть</i> (opoznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to identify</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>позна́ть</i> (poznátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, to defame</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>позо́рить</i> (pozóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) gaize, marlstone (a hard, porous sedimentary rock with a high silica content)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) limestone</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) box, flask, box-form, casting-box (<i>for founding</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)poka</i>. Compare Polish <i>opoka</i>, Czech <i>opuka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landslide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>militiaman, militia member</li></ol><p><i>ополче́ние</i> (opolčénije) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>militia, home guard, territorial army</li><li>(dated,&#32;law) levy</li></ol><p><i>ополчи́ть</i> (opolčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm someone to fight in a war or conflict</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to encourage or cause someone to fight someone or against something; to cause hostility or animosity against someone or something</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>полк</i> (polk) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to one's senses, to collect oneself</li><li>to regain consciousness</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>only in an expression</i> во весь <b>опор</b> (at full tilt, at full speed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, prop, rest</li><li>buttress, pier (anything that serves to support something)</li><li>foothold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)pora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>опорожня́ть</i> (oporožnjátʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>опорожни́ть</i> (oporožnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>опоро́жнить</i> (oporóžnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) A person with a musculoskeletal disorder.</li><li>(soccer,&#32;informal) short for <i>опо́рный полузащи́тник</i> (opórnyj poluzaščítnik); defensive midfielder</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) short for <i>опо́рный пункт</i> (opórnyj punkt); strongpoint, stronghold (an anchor point in a defensive fighting position, fortified so as to not be easily overrun)</li><li>(<i>automotive&#32;slang</i>) short for <i>опо́рный подши́пник</i> (opórnyj podšípnik); thrust bearing</li></ol><p><i>опо́рный</i> (opórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tanks</i>) road wheels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) support</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) foothold</li><li>supporting, bearing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) model (serving as an example for others)</li></ol><p><i>опо́ра</i> (opóra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) strongpoint, stronghold (an anchor point in a defensive fighting position, fortified so as to not be easily overrun)</li><li>(military,&#32;politics) foothold (a military base or airfield providing a nation strategic presence in a certain country or region)</li><li>(<i>law enforcement</i>) station, base</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evacuation, emptying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to empty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>опорознити</i> (oporozniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oporzdьniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to empty</li></ol><p>From <i>опорожни́ть</i> (oporožnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) or <i>опоро́жнить</i> (oporóžnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defame, to discredit, to smear</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>поро́чить</i> (poróčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proxy war</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mediate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>посреди́</i> (posredí) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;literary) mediated; transmitted indirectly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophical</i>) To express in an indirect way; to serve as a means of transmission.</li><li>To mediate.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>средство</i> (sredstvo) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opossum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to annoy, to disgust [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>посты́леть</i> (postýletʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the hair of the dog that bit you, to freshen the nip (to take a shot in the morning to help make one's hangover go away)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>похмели́ться</i> (poxmelítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) taking the hair of the dog that bit you, freshening the nip (taking a shot in the morning to help make one's hangover go away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the alcohol one drinks to make one's hangover go away</li></ol><p><i>опохмел-</i> (opoxmel-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A male from, or citizen of, the Russian city of Opochka.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) bedchamber</li></ol><p><i>опочива́ть</i> (opočivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) to rest, to repose</li></ol><p><i>опочи́ть</i> (opočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) to rest, to repose</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) to die, to pass away (<i>perfective only</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>почи́ть</i> (počítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird, to engirdle</li><li>to surround, to encircle, to girdle</li></ol><p><i>о</i> (o) +&lrm; <i>-поя́сать</i> (-pojásatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird, to engirdle</li><li>to surround, to encircle, to girdle</li></ol><p><i>опоя́сать</i> (opojásatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppositionist (a person who opposes&#59; especially a member of an official opposition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) opposition; oppositional</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>оппози́ция</i> (oppozícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) opposition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>opposition</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opponent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opportunism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opportunist (someone who takes advantage of any opportunity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting, mounting, casing, rim, frame, holder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>justification, explanation</li><li>excuse, reason</li><li>acquittal, vindication (legal decision)</li></ol><p><i>оправда́ть</i> (opravdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>justified, reasonable, sound, understandable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exculpatory, exonerative</li><li>justificatory</li></ol><p><i>оправда́ть</i> (opravdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to justify, to warrant, to authorize, to vindicate</li><li>to acquit, to discharge; to exonerate</li><li>to excuse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opravьdati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to justify/vindicate oneself, to justify one's actions</li><li>to try to prove one's innocence, to justify oneself, to give excuses</li><li>to prove/come true, to be justified; to prove to be correct</li></ol><p><i>оправда́ть</i> (opravdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to justify, to warrant, to authorize, to vindicate</li><li>to acquit, to discharge; to exonerate</li><li>to excuse</li></ol><p><i>оправда́ть</i> (opravdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to justify/vindicate oneself, to justify one's actions</li><li>to try to prove one's innocence, to justify oneself, to give excuses</li><li>to prove/come true, to be justified; to prove to be correct</li></ol><p><i>опра́вдывать</i> (oprávdyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to put in order, to set right</li><li>to set (by mounting), to mount</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover, to pick up</li><li><i>(clothes)</i> to put in order</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ease oneself, to do the deed</li></ol><p><i>опра́вить</i> (oprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrel (object used as an aid for shaping a material)</li><li>arbor (bar for supporting cutting tools)</li><li>dolly (a camera cart)</li></ol><p><i>опра́вить</i> (oprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to put in order, to set right</li><li>to set (by mounting), to mount</li></ol><p><i>опра́вить</i> (oprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover, to pick up</li><li><i>(clothes)</i> to put in order</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ease oneself, to do the deed</li></ol><p><i>оправля́ть</i> (opravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mandrel (object used as an aid for shaping a material)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) arbor (bar for supporting cutting tools)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) dolly (a camera cart)</li></ol><p>From <i>опра́вка</i> (oprávka, “mandrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to question, to interrogate, to examine</li><li>to poll, to interrogate</li></ol><p><i>опроси́ть</i> (oprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>definition</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) attribute, modifier</li><li>(<i>law</i>) ruling, decision</li></ol><p><i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>determinant</li><li>qualifier</li></ol><p><i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ, “to determine”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attributive</li><li>adherent</li></ol><p><i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to determine (to set the limits of)</li><li>to define</li><li>to detect, to identify</li><li>to assess</li><li>to appoint, to assign</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>опрѣдѣлити</i> (oprěděliti). By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>преде́л</i> (predél) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>defīniō</i> (&lt; <i>fīnio</i> &lt; <i>fīnis</i>, note <i>фина́л</i> (finál), <i>фи́ниш</i> (fíniš), <i>дефини́ция</i> (definícija)), Latin <i>determinō</i> (&lt; <i>terminō</i> &lt; <i>terminus</i>, note <i>те́рмин</i> (términ), <i>термина́л</i> (terminál), <i>термина́льный</i> (terminálʹnyj), <i>термина́тор</i> (terminátor)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape, to be / become formed (<i>of one's character</i>); to clarify itself</li><li>to make a decision, to find / determine one's position in an issue or a conflict</li><li>to enter, to enlist (in); to find a place, to get employment</li></ol><p><i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to determine (to set the limits of)</li><li>to define</li><li>to detect, to identify</li><li>to assess</li><li>to appoint, to assign</li></ol><p><i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape, to be / become formed (<i>of one's character</i>); to clarify itself</li><li>to find / determine one's position</li><li>to enter, to enlist (in); to find a place, to get employment</li></ol><p><i>определя́ть</i> (opredeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>determiner (class of words)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) defining, decisive (the most significant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>определённый</i> (opredeljónnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>определённо</i> (opredeljónno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>definitely</li></ol><p><i>определённый</i> (opredeljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinctness, determinacy</li></ol><p><i>определённый</i> (opredeljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>definite, certain, fixed</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) definite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) definite article</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intertrigo</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пре(ть)</i> (pre(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-л</i> (-l) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water distiller</li></ol><p><i>опресни́ть</i> (opresnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desalinate, to desalinize, to distil</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пре́сный</i> (présnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desalinate, to desalinize, to distil</li></ol><p><i>опресни́ть</i> (opresnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) oprichnik (a bodyguard of Russian Tsar Ivan the Terrible)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a cruel, callous performer of the government orders (especially when suppressing dissidents or protesters)</li></ol><p><i>опри́чный</i> (opríčnyj, “separate”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) oprichnina</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) oprichniks</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;politics) cruel suppression of civil liberties</li></ol><p>from <i>опри́чный</i> (opríčnyj), ultimately from <i>опри́чь</i> (opríčʹ, “besides”), Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>опри́чь</i> (opríčĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) except, besides</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) separately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test, to sample</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>про́бовать</i> (próbovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refute, to disprove</li><li>to deny, to disclaim</li></ol><p><i>опрове́ргнуть</i> (oprovérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refute, to disprove</li><li>to deny, to disclaim</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refutation; disproof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overturn, to topple</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to overthrow</li><li>to frustrate, to upset</li></ol><p><i>опроки́д-</i> (oprokíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overturn, to tip over, to capsize</li></ol><p><i>опроки́дывать</i> (oprokídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overturn, to topple</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to overthrow</li><li>to frustrate, to upset</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(alcoholic beverage)</i> to knock back</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>проки́нуть</i> (prokínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overturn, to tip over, to capsize</li></ol><p><i>опроки́нуть</i> (oprokínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>recklessly (in a reckless manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rash, impulsive, impetuous</li></ol><p>From <i>о́прометь</i> (óprometʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>headlong, very hastily</li></ol><p>Lexicalized instrumental case of <i>о́прометь</i> (óprometʹ, “fast run”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>questioning, interrogation</li><li>oral test</li><li>survey, poll, inquiry</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) interrogation, inquiry, poll</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to question, to interrogate, to examine</li><li>to poll, to interrogate</li><li>to survey</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>questionnaire</li></ol><p><i>опроси́ть</i> (oprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protest (of a bill)</li><li>(<i>laws</i>) to appeal</li></ol><p><i>опротестова́ть</i> (oprotestovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become disgusted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tidiness</li></ol><p>From <i>опря́тный</i> (oprjátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tidy, neat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) wholesale</li></ol><p>Attested first from the year 1862, of unknown provenance.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optician, optometrist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optimal (the best)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optimization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to optimize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>optimiser</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optimism, hopefulness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optimist (a person who expects a favorable outcome)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optimistic</li></ol><p><i>оптими́ст</i> (optimíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optimistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optical</li></ol><p><i>о́пт(ика)</i> (ópt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wholesaler</li></ol><p><i>опто́вый</i> (optóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>optical fibre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) optical fibre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholesale</li></ol><p><i>опт</i> (opt) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholesale (in bulk or large quantity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publication (act of publishing), issuance</li></ol><p><i>опубликова́ть</i> (opublikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish, to issue</li><li>to make public</li><li>to promulgate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>публикова́ть</i> (publikovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish, to issue</li><li>to make public</li><li>to promulgate</li></ol><p><i>опубликова́ть</i> (opublikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) opuntia, prickly pear</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Opuntia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) opus</li><li>(<i>ironically</i>) opus, piece of writing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowering, sinking, descent</li><li>omission</li></ol><p><i>опуска́ть</i> (opuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let down, to lower, to sink (to allow to descend)</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li><li>(prison slang,&#32;vulgar) to rape</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to give up</li></ol><p>Literally, “to lower one's arms”. Compare French <i>baisser les bras</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to descend</li></ol><p><i>опуска́ть</i> (opuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become empty</li><li>to become deserted</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пусте́ть</i> (pustétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let down, to lower, to sink (to allow to descend)</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) to rape a man, making him an <i>опу́щенный</i> (opúščennyj, “bitch, punk”)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to give up</li></ol><p>Literally, “to lower one's arms”. Compare French <i>baisser les bras</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to descend</li><li>to sit down, to lie down, to settle (<i>on something</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fall, to sink into poverty or vice</li></ol><p><i>опусти́ть</i> (opustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devastate, to ravage, to lay waste</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to empty</li><li>to drain someone's spirit, to have a devastating effect <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>опустоши́ть</i> (opustošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emptying</li><li>desolation, devastation, ruin, destruction, ravage</li></ol><p><i>опустоши́ть</i> (opustošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devastate, to ravage, to lay waste</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to empty</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to guzzle</li><li>to drain someone's spirit, to have a devastating effect <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пу́стошь</i> (pústošʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressed, spiritually empty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desertification</li></ol><p>From <i>опусты́нивать</i> (opustýnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije), from <i>пусты́ня</i> (pustýnja, “desert”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enmesh <i>(in)</i>, to entangle <i>(in)</i>, to wind <i>(round)</i></li><li>to ensnare, to rope in</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enmesh <i>(in)</i>, to entangle <i>(in)</i>, to wind <i>(round)</i></li><li>to ensnare, to rope in</li></ol><p><i>опу́тать</i> (opútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to start acting unreasonably, rudely or stupidly, to go crazy or out of one's mind</li></ol><p><i>опу́хнуть</i> (opúxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to start acting unreasonably, rudely or stupidly, to go crazy or out of one's mind</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tumour, tumor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim with fur, to fur</li><li>to cover (<i>of snow, hoarfrost or rime</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest border, forest edge</li><li>fur trimming, edging</li></ol><p>From <i>опуши́ть</i> (opušítʹ, “to decorate/sheathe the edges of (something) with fur”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), transferred from the action of decorating the edges of something to the edge itself, then narrowed specifically to a forest edge.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) downcast, depressed (<i>look, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) option</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pollination</li></ol><p><i>опыли́ть</i> (opylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experience</li><li>experiment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>опꙑтъ</i> (opytŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)pytъ</i>, from <i>&#42;obpytati</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;pytati</i> (“to ask, examine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) experimental design; developmental</li></ol><p><i>о́пытный</i> (ópytnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>констру́кторский</i> (konstrúktorskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expertise, proficiency</li></ol><p><i>о́пытный</i> (ópytnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>experienced</li><li>experimental</li></ol><p><i>о́пыт</i> (ópyt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inebriation, (alcoholic) intoxication, drunkenness</li></ol><p><i>опьяне́ть</i> (opʹjanétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get intoxicated/tipsy/inebriated</li><li>to get intoxicated <i>(with joy, success etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пьяне́ть</i> (pʹjanétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пьяни́ть</i> (pʹjanítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk</li></ol><p><i>опьяни́ть</i> (opʹjanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again (<i>occurring another time</i>)</li><li>(usually in the combination <i>опя́ть же</i> (opjátʹ že)) again (used when re-emphasising something already stated)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opętь</i>. Cognates include Czech <i>opět</i> and Serbo-Croatian <i>opet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (but) again, and what's more</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) on the other hand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey fungus of the genus <i>Armillaria</i> of edible parasitic mushrooms with a yellow cap and thin stalk that live on trees and woody shrubs, especially <i>Armillaria mellea</i>, that usually grow in groups on tree roots, stumps, and felled trees.</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>пень</i> (penʹ, “stump”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Literally, “something growing about a stump”. Compare Polish <i>opieńka</i>, Slovak <i>podpňovka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) yelling, shouting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gang, horde, mob, crowd, caboodle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;inanimate) oracle</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>ōrāculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) ard, wooden plough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oradlo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ора́ть</i> (orátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mouth; oral</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>oral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or French <i>oral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>ора́льний</i> (orálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oral sex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orangutan</li></ol><p>From Malay <i>orang</i> (“person, man”) + <i>hutan</i> (“forest”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) orange (tree)</li><li>orange, a secondary color made by mixing red and yellow</li></ol><p>First attested in 1754. From <i>&#42;оранж</i> (&#42;oranž) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), borrowed from French <i>orange</i> (“orange (fruit)”). Displaced Middle Russian <i>наранжи</i> (naranži, pl.), Old East Slavic <i>наранзи</i> (naranzi, pl.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hothouse, greenhouse, conservatory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>orangerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orator, speaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speechify, to perorate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive,&#32;of humans) to yell, to bawl, to scream, to shout</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive,&#32;of animals) to emit loud characteristic sounds (mooing, bleating, roaring, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive,&#32;disapproving) to talk too loudly, to shout, to yell</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to scold [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to sing loudly, to sing at the top of one's lungs</li><li>(youth,&#32;neologism) to laugh out loud, to have fun</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to plow/plough</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂res-</i>. Related to Latin <i>ōrō</i> (and English <i>orate</i>), Old Armenian <i>ուրանամ</i> (uranam), Sanskrit <i>अर्चयति</i> (arcayati), Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬵-</i> (rah-).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>орати</i> (orati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oràti</i>. Cognate to Lithuanian <i>árti</i>, Latin <i>arō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀρόω</i> (aróō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orbit</li><li>sphere of influence</li><li>eye-socket</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>orbita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orbital, orbiting</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>orbital</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orgasm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὀργασμός</i> (orgasmós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orgasmic, orgastic</li></ol><p>From <i>орга́зм</i> (orgázm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) organ</li><li>body, agency, authority</li><li>organ, publication</li><li>(<i>music</i>) organ (<i>large musical instrument with pipes</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὄργανον</i> (órganon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organizer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) organizer; organizational</li><li>organizing</li></ol><p><i>организа́тор</i> (organizátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organizational and legal</li></ol><p><i>организацио́нный</i> (organizaciónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>правово́й</i> (pravovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organizational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organization, arrangement</li><li>organization</li><li>setup, structure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>system of organs making up the body of a living thing</li><li>organism (living thing, usually a small and simplistic one)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a complex structure or mechanism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>organisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organized crime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organization (state of being well-organized)</li></ol><p><i>организо́ванный</i> (organizóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to organise, to arrange (to set up, organise)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>organizāre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be/become organized</li></ol><p><i>организова́ть</i> (organizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to organise, to arrange (to set up, organise)</li></ol><p><i>организова́ть</i> (organizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be/become organized</li></ol><p><i>организо́вывать</i> (organizóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) organic chemistry</li><li>organic matter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) organic fertilizer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) organic residue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fossil</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) living organism</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) naturalness, organicity</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>organique</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>organically</li></ol><p><i>органи́ческий</i> (organíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>organ(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>organically</li></ol><p><i>органи́чный</i> (organíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>organ(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orgiastic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orgy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὄργια</i> (órgia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organizing committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>организацио́нный комите́т</i> (organizaciónnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>graph theory</i>) digraph (an oriented graph, directed graph)</li></ol><p>Syllabic abbreviation of <i>ориенти́рованный граф</i> (orijentírovannyj graf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office equipment</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>организацио́нная те́хника</i> (organizaciónnaja téxnika, “organizing equipment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horde</li></ol><p>From a Kipchak language, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;ordu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>honors</i>) decoration, order (pl: <i>ордена́</i> (ordená))</li><li>(<i>organizations</i>) order (pl: <i>о́рдены</i> (órdeny))</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) order (pl: <i>о́рдены</i> (órdeny))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Orden</i>. Further from Latin <i>ōrdō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Order of the Red Banner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrant</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) order</li><li>(<i>law</i>) writ</li><li>(<i>advertising</i>) coupon</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrest warrant (an official document that authorizes law-enforcement officials to arrest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) orderly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordinary</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ōrdinārius</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) ordinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Orenburg (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Оренбу́рг</i> (Orenbúrg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halo, aureole</li><li>aura</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>aureolus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nut (tree&#59; seed)</li><li>walnut wood</li><li>nut coal; chestnut coal</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) ass, booty (buttocks, typically female)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orěxъ</i>. Compare Albanian <i>arrë</i> (“walnut”), Ancient Greek <i>ἄρυα</i> (árua, “walnut”), Latvian <i>riẽksts</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>òrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nutcracker, nuthatch (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оре́х</i> (oréx) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nut</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) walnut</li><li>walnut, nut-brown (<i>color</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оре́х</i> (oréx) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>оре́х</i> (oréx): (small) nut</li></ol><p><i>оре́х</i> (oréx) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) hazel</li><li>hazel grove</li><li>commercial grade of anthracite</li></ol><p><i>оре́х</i> (oréx) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Орешник)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Russian intermediate-range ballistic missile.</li></ol><p>See оре́шник (oréšnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origami (the Japanese art of paper folding)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>折り紙</i> (おりがみ, origami), from <i>折り</i> (おり, ori, “to fold”) + <i>紙</i> (かみ, kami, “paper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>original (object from which all later copies and variations are derived)</li><li>eccentric</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>orīginālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singularity, originality</li></ol><p><i>оригина́льный</i> (originálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>original (first in a series&#59; fresh, different)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orientalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orientation</li><li>understanding</li><li>direction of attention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reference point</li><li>guideline</li><li>orientator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oriented</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) competent, knowledgeable</li><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics) directional, oriented</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to orient, to orientate, to direct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to orient, to orientate (oneself), to get one's bearings (to familiarize with a situation or circumstance)</li></ol><p><i>ориенти́ровать</i> (orijentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orientation</li></ol><p><i>ориенти́ровать</i> (orijentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orienting, orientative</li><li>approximate</li></ol><p><i>ориентиро́вка</i> (orijentiróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oriya (language)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Odia <i>ଓଡ଼ିଆ</i> (oṛiā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) orc (humanoid monster warrior)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a police officer or titushky working for former Ukrainian president Yanukovych</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang) a Russian person with little to no desirable qualities (unattractive, low intelligence, uncultured, etc.)</li><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) Russian invader (a Russian, DPR, or LPR soldier participating in the 2022 invasion of Ukraine)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;by extension) Any Russian person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>orc</i>.</p><p>The pejorative usage appeared as early as in 2014, when Euromaidan activists referred Yanukovych's supporters and riot policemen as "orcs". Later it was extended in Ukrainian military slang as a term for the Russian enemy during the War in Donbass and was widely popularized by an official Armed Forces of Ukraine Facebook post on 25 February 2022.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) orchestra (large group of musicians who play together on various instruments)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) the Wagner Group</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>orchēstra</i>, most likely via German <i>Orchester</i> in the 1700s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchestra musician</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a Wagner Group soldier</li></ol><p><i>орке́стр</i> (orkéstr) +&lrm; <i>-а́нт</i> (-ánt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to orchestrate (to write or transcribe music for an orchestra)</li></ol><p><i>орке́стр</i> (orkéstr) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) orchestra pit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) orchestra; orchestral</li></ol><p><i>орке́стр</i> (orkéstr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orkish</li></ol><p>From <i>орк</i> (ork) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea eagle, erne (any member of the <i>Haliaeetus</i> genus)</li><li>Orlan (Soviet and Russian space suit)</li></ol><p><i>орёл</i> (orjól, “eagle”) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eagle</li><li>aquiline</li></ol><p><i>орёл</i> (orjól) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Oryol (city in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Orlov (surname)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>Орёл</i> (Orjól) +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orlov Trotter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eaglet (an eagle chick)</li><li>a member of a Soviet pioneer camp <i>Орлёнок</i> (or it's modern equivalent) or a boy who is acting like one</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a teenager who was fighting on the Red side during the Civil War in Russia, Belarussia and Ukraine (1917-24)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a teenager who took part in the defence of Lemberg fighting on the Polish side during the Polish-Ukrainian War (1918-19)</li></ol><p><i>орёл</i> (orjól) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament (element of decoration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornithologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornithology (scientific study of birds)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornithomimus (<i>Ornithomimus</i>-genus dinosaur)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from translingual <i>Ornithomimus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/become frightened, to grow timid</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>робе́ть</i> (robétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orography (all senses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain, high ground”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Orontian</li></ol><p><i>Оро́нт</i> (Orónt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оро́нтский</i> (oróntskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) irrigation</li></ol><p><i>ороси́ть</i> (orosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle, to wash</li><li>to irrigate, to water</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>роса́</i> (rosá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle, to wash</li><li>to irrigate, to water</li></ol><p><i>ороси́ть</i> (orosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irrigation</li></ol><p><i>ороси́ть</i> (orosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) unit of vector length</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) crosscut, cross drift (horizontal underground excavation with no direct exit to the surface)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person with orthodox views</li><li>Orthodox Jew</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθόδοξος</i> (orthódoxos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orthodox</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ортодо́кс</i> (ortodóks) +&lrm; <i>-альный</i> (-alʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthodoxy</li></ol><p><i>ортодо́кс</i> (ortodóks) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthodontist (orthodontic dentist)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthoclase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthopedist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;youth) LOL</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool, instrument, implement</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pawn, puppet (a person acting under another's control)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) gun, cannon, artillery piece</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>орѫдие</i> (orǫdije), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>орѫдиѥ</i> (orǫdije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orǫdьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gun</li></ol><p><i>ору́дие</i> (orúdije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work <i>(with)</i>, to handle, to manipulate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to be active</li></ol><p><i>ору́дие</i> (orúdije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armory, armoury (place where arms are kept, an arsenal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaponmaker, armourer</li></ol><p>From ору́жие (orúžije, “weaponry”) and -ник (-nik, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) weapon</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gun</li></ol><p><i>ору́жие</i> (orúžije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armiger, armor-bearer (squire carrying the armour of a knight)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) weapon(s), weaponry, arm(s)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>орѫжие</i> (orǫžije), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>орѫжиѥ</i> (orǫžije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orǫžьje</i>. Doublet of <i>ружьё</i> (ružʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) weapon(s) of mass destruction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) weapon(s) of mass destruction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Orwellian</li></ol><p><i>О́руэлл</i> (Óruell) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A spelling that is in accordance with orthographic rules, usually etymological or historical rather than phonetic.</li><li>A consistently reproducible way to represent phonomorphological features of a given language in writing, such as <b>-ого</b> for the Russian masculine genitive singular of adjectives, instead of the phonetic spelling <b>-ава</b>: но́вого (nóvovo) (nóvəvə).</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “straight, right, true”) +&lrm; <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “letter, writing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orthographic</li></ol><p><i>орфогра́фия</i> (orfográfija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spelling, orthography</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθογραφία</i> (orthographía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orthoepic</li></ol><p>From <i>орфоэ́пия</i> (orfoépija, “orthoepy”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthoepy (the correct pronunciation of words)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὄρχις</i> (órkhis, “orchid, testicle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) orchid</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) orchidaceous</li></ol><p>From <i>орхиде́я</i> (orxidéja, “orchid”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orgeat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cudgel, pole (large stick)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) string bean (very tall, awkward, and clumsy person)</li></ol><p>Compare typologically <i>балда́</i> (baldá), <i>дуби́на</i> (dubína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) heads (the side of a coin bearing a coat of arms, e.g. Russia's double-headed eagle)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a brave man</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>орьлъ</i> (orĭlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orьlъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;arélis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃érō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Орёл)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oryol (a city, the administrative center of Oryol Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Aquila (constellation)</li></ol><p>See <i>орёл</i> (orjól, “eagle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>орфогра́фия</i> (orfográfija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wobʰseh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siege</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)sada</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check, to rein in, to rein back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take down a peg</li><li>to lay siege <i>(to)</i>, to besiege, to beset, to beleaguer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pester <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to precipitate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draft, depth (<i>of a ship</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precipitation</li><li>fallout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sediment, deposition, deposit</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) after-taste, feeling of resentment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)sadъ</i> with ending <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Related to <i>сад</i> (sad) and <i>сажать</i> (sažatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>оса́док</i> (osádok, “after-taste”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sediment, deposit; sedimentary</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) precipitation</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) fallout</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) draft, depth (<i>of a ship</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оса́док</i> (osádok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>оса́дки</i> (osádki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>оса́дка</i> (osádka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay siege <i>(to)</i>, to besiege, to beset, to beleaguer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pester <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to precipitate</li></ol><p><i>осади́ть</i> (osadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check, to rein in, to back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take down a peg</li></ol><p><i>осади́ть</i> (osadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>posture, bearing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hosanna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>osanna</i>, <i>hosanna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὡσαννά</i> (hōsanná).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run wild, to go crazy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сатане́ть</i> (satanétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to master, to cope with (making knowledge or method into one's "own")</li><li>to settle, to open up, to develop (new territories)</li><li>to develop; to become familiar with the use (of lands or machines)</li><li>to acclimate (in terms of plants)</li></ol><p><i>осво́ить</i> (osvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make oneself familiar <i>(with)</i>, to get the feel <i>(of)</i></li><li>to feel comfortable <i>(with)</i>, to adapt <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>осва́ивать</i> (osváivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informant, informer</li><li>whistleblower</li></ol><p><i>осве́домить</i> (osvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домо</i> (védomo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire (after, for; about); to want to know (about)</li></ol><p><i>осве́домить</i> (osvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform</li></ol><p><i>осве́домить</i> (osvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire (after, for; about); to want to know (about)</li></ol><p><i>осведомля́ть</i> (osvedomljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness (the state or quality of being aware of something)</li></ol><p><i>осведомлённый</i> (osvedomljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aware (<i>of</i>)</li><li>knowledgeable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refresh (<i>also one's memory, knowledge, etc.</i>)</li><li>to touch up (colors in a painting)</li></ol><p><i>освежи́ть</i> (osvežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flay (to strip the skin off, to skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freshener (something that freshens)</li></ol><p><i>освежи́ть</i> (osvežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air freshener (device containing substances that neutralize unpleasant odours)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>refreshing</li></ol><p><i>освежи́ть</i> (osvežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refresh (<i>also one's memory, knowledge, etc.</i>)</li><li>to touch up (colors in a painting)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>све́жий</i> (svéžij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) illuminator, lighting technician</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) illuminator (device)</li></ol><p><i>освети́ть</i> (osvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to light up, to illuminate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to elucidate, to illustrate, to throw light (upon), to shed light on</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>освѣтити</i> (osvětiti). By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light up, to brighten</li></ol><p><i>освети́ть</i> (osvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of an image</i>) dodge, dodging</li><li>(<i>of colour/color</i>) lightening</li><li>(<i>of a liquid</i>) clarification</li><li>(<i>of waste water</i>) defecation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to light up, to illuminate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to elucidate, to illustrate, to throw light (upon), to shed light on</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>освѣщати</i> (osvěštati). By surface analysis, <i>освети́ть</i> (osvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light up, to brighten</li></ol><p><i>освеща́ть</i> (osveščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting; illumination</li><li>elucidation; coverage (e.g. in the press)</li></ol><p><i>освети́ть</i> (osvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illumination (state of being illuminated)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) luminosity</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) availability of information</li></ol><p><i>освещённый</i> (osveščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illuminated, lit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medical) examination, inspection, muster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberator, emancipator</li></ol><p><i>освободи́ть</i> (osvobodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liberation</li></ol><p><i>освободи́ть</i> (osvobodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free, to liberate, to emancipate, to set free</li><li>to free (from); to exempt (from)</li><li>to dismiss</li><li>to clear, to empty</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>свобо́д(ный)</i> (svobód(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free, to break free, to free oneself (from), to get loose</li><li>to become empty; to become vacant</li><li>to become free, to get free time (e.g. after work)</li></ol><p><i>освободи́ть</i> (osvobodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free, to liberate, to emancipate, to set free</li><li>to free (from); to exempt (from)</li><li>to dismiss</li><li>to clear, to empty</li></ol><p><i>освободи́ть</i> (osvobodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get free, to break free, to free oneself (from), to get loose</li><li>to become empty; to become vacant</li><li>to become free, to get free time (e.g. after work)</li></ol><p><i>освобожда́ть</i> (osvoboždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberation, emancipation, freeing</li><li>liberation, deliverance</li><li>freeing, vacating (the act of vacating something; moving out of something)</li><li>exemption</li></ol><p><i>освобожда́ть</i> (osvoboždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) releasee, released prisoner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastering</li><li>(<i>finances</i>) disbursement</li></ol><p><i>осво́ить</i> (osvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to master, to cope with (making knowledge or method into one's "own")</li><li>to settle, to open up, to develop (new territories)</li><li>to develop; to become familiar with the use (of lands or machines)</li><li>to acclimate (in terms of plants)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сво́й</i> (svój) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make oneself familiar <i>(with)</i>, to get the feel <i>(of)</i></li><li>to feel comfortable <i>(with)</i>, to adapt <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>осво́ить</i> (osvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sanctify, to hallow, to consecrate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sanctify, to hallow, to consecrate</li></ol><p><i>освяти́ть</i> (osvjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consecration, sanctification</li></ol><p><i>освяти́ть</i> (osvjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>here, now, there</li><li>lo (look, see, behold)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) axle</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) axis; axial</li></ol><p><i>ось</i> (osʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to sink</li><li>to gravitate</li></ol><p><i>осе́д-</i> (oséd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saddle, to put on a halter</li><li>to straddle</li><li>to override</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>седла́ть</i> (sedlátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sedentarily (not moving from place to place)</li></ol><p><i>осе́длый</i> (osédlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sedentary lifestyle (as opposed to nomadism)</li></ol><p><i>осе́длый</i> (osédlyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sedentary, settled (as opposed to nomadic or migratory)</li></ol><p><i>оседа́ть</i> (osedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to misfire</li><li>to stop short</li></ol><p><i>осек-</i> (osek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whetstone, hone (sharpening stone)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osьla</i> or <i>&#42;osla̋</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-l-</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>осла</i> (osla), Slovak <i>osla</i>, Slovene <i>ósla</i>, Polish <i>osełka</i>. More distant cognates include Armenian <i>ասեղ</i> (aseġ, “needle”) and dialectal German <i>Achel</i> (“awn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insemination (act of making pregnant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) to overhang, to spread <i>(above)</i>, to spread as a canopy <i>(over)</i></li><li>to dawn <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>осѣнити</i> (osěniti). By surface analysis, <i>о</i> (o) +&lrm; <i>сень</i> (senʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumnal equinox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) autumn</li><li>autumnal</li></ol><p><i>о́сень</i> (ósenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colchicum (<i>Colchicum</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>о́сень</i> (ósenʹ, “autumn, fall”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare Belarusian <i>восе́ннік</i> (vosjénnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also figuratively</i>) autumn, fall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осень</i> (osenĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;esenь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-en-</i>, whence English <i>earn</i> and German <i>Ernte</i> (“harvest”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>есень</i> (jesenʹ) and <i>есеня</i> (jesenja), cognate with Belarusian <i>во́сень</i> (vósjenʹ) and Ukrainian <i>о́сінь</i> (ósinʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in autumn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) to overhang, to spread <i>(above)</i>, to spread as a canopy <i>(over)</i></li><li>to dawn <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>осени́ть</i> (osenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get angry</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>серча́ть</i> (serčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to sink</li><li>to gravitate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to settle, to subside</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ossete, Ossetian</li></ol><p>From <i>Осе́тия</i> (Osétija) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ossetian</li></ol><p><i>осети́н</i> (osetín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sturgeon (<i>as prepared meat</i>)</li></ol><p><i>осётр</i> (osjótr) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sturgeon</li></ol><p><i>осётр</i> (osjótr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfire</li></ol><p><i>осе́к-</i> (osék-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to misfire</li><li>to stop short</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>се́чь</i> (séčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpower</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage, to cope, to master</li></ol><p><i>оси́лить</i> (osílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpower</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage, to cope, to master</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-си́лить</i> (-sílitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;opsina</i>, <i>&#42;opsa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)osp-eh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;(H)osp-</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Ukrainian <i>оси́на</i> (osýna), Belarusian <i>асі́на</i> (asína), Lithuanian <i>ẽpušė</i>, Latvian <i>apse</i>, Old Prussian <i>abse</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aspō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspen forest or grove</li></ol><p>From <i>оси́на</i> (osína) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aspen</li></ol><p><i>оси́на</i> (osína) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspen hawk-moth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wasp</li></ol><p><i>оса́</i> (osá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become husky/hoarse</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>си́пнуть</i> (sípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become an orphan</li><li>to be deserted</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сироте́ть</i> (sirotétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to illuminate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to illuminate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grin, bared teeth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show (one's teeth)</li></ol><p><i>оска́лить</i> (oskálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bared (<i>of teeth</i>)</li><li>to bare one's teeth (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to grin, to smile</li></ol><p><i>оска́ливать</i> (oskálivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show (one's teeth)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ска́лить</i> (skálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bared (<i>of teeth</i>)</li><li>to bare one's teeth (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to grin, to smile</li></ol><p><i>оска́лить</i> (oskálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desecration, defilement</li><li>defacement, vandalism</li></ol><p><i>оскверни́ть</i> (oskvernítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, to profane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оскврьнити</i> (oskvrĭniti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, to profane</li></ol><p><i>оскверни́ть</i> (oskvernítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to open (the mouth or face) in a grin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bare (the teeth) in a grin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>склабити сѩ</i> (sklabiti sję, “to smile”), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skolbiti</i>. Cognate with Old East Slavic <i>осколобление</i> (oskoloblenije, “smile”), Czech <i>šklebit</i> (“to grin”), <i>ošklíbat sе</i> (“to grin”), Slovak <i>skľabiť sa</i> (“to grin”), Old Polish <i>skɫobiony</i>. Distantly related to Old Norse <i>skelpa</i> (“grimace”, literally “crack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grin, to smile broadly (<i>showing the teeth</i>)</li></ol><p><i>оскла́бить</i> (osklábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to open (the mouth or face) in a grin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bare (the teeth) in a grin</li></ol><p><i>оскла́бить</i> (osklábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grin, to smile broadly (<i>showing the teeth</i>)</li></ol><p><i>осклабля́ть</i> (osklabljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splinter, fragment, sliver, shard</li></ol><p>Related to <i>скала́</i> (skalá, “crag”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing/soreness of the mouth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offensive, abusive</li></ol><p><i>оскорби́ть</i> (oskorbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insult, to offend, to outrage</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>скорбь</i> (skorbʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel insulted, to get offended [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>оскорби́ть</i> (oskorbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insult, offence, affront, outrage, abuse <i>(speech)</i></li></ol><p><i>оскорби́ть</i> (oskorbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insult, to offend, to outrage</li></ol><p><i>оскорби́ть</i> (oskorbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel insulted, to get offended [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>оскорбля́ть</i> (oskorbljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Oscan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oscan (language)</li></ol><p><i>оск</i> (osk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, to become weaker, to become/grow weak</li><li>to slacken, to relax, to ease, to abate, to subside</li><li>to loosen, to become looser</li></ol><p><i>ослабе́ть</i> (oslabétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, to become weaker, to become/grow weak</li><li>to slacken, to relax, to ease, to abate, to subside</li><li>to loosen, to become looser</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>слабе́ть</i> (slabétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enfeeble, to weaken</li><li>to relax, to slacken, to loosen</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakening, slackening, relaxation</li></ol><p><i>осла́бить</i> (oslábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enfeeble, to weaken</li><li>to relax, to slacken, to loosen</li></ol><p><i>осла́бить</i> (oslábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, to become weaker, to become/grow weak</li><li>to slacken, to relax, to ease, to abate, to subside</li><li>to loosen, to become looser</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́бнуть</i> (slábnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dazzlingly, radiantly, shiningly</li></ol><p><i>ослепи́тельный</i> (oslepítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blinding</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dazzling</li></ol><p><i>ослепи́ть</i> (oslepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blind</li><li>to dazzle</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>слепи́ть</i> (slepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) blindness, bedazzlement (inability to perceive or understand)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blind</li><li>to dazzle</li></ol><p><i>ослепи́ть</i> (oslepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/go blind, to lose one's sight</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сле́пнуть</i> (slépnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>осёл</i> (osjól)</li></ol><p><i>осёл</i> (osjól) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) donkey, ass</li><li>asinine</li></ol><p><i>осёл</i> (osjól) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complication (the act or process of complicating&#59; the state of being complicated&#59; intricate or confused relation of parts&#59; entanglement)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) complication (a disease or diseases, or adventitious circumstances or conditions, coexistent with and modifying a primary disease, but not necessarily connected with it)</li></ol><p><i>осложни́ть</i> (osložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complicate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become complicated</li><li><i>(disease)</i> to cause complications</li></ol><p><i>осложни́ть</i> (osložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complicate</li></ol><p><i>осложни́ть</i> (osložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become complicated</li><li><i>(disease)</i> to cause complications</li></ol><p><i>осложня́ть</i> (osložnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disobey, to follow not [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘some bidding’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шаться</i> (slúšatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) disobeyer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mishear</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey foal.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) stupid, stubborn person (usually young).</li></ol><p><i>осёл</i> (osjól) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ottoman (of the Islamic empire of Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Осма́н</i> (Osmán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to survey, to inspect</li><li>to see, to see round, to look round</li></ol><p><i>осмотре́ть</i> (osmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look about, to look around</li><li>to look round, to get one's bearings, to see how the land lies</li></ol><p><i>осма́тривать</i> (osmátrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ridicule, to scoff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow bolder</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>смеле́ть</i> (smelétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>смелеть</i> (smeletʹ) +&lrm; <i>-в</i> (-v) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сме́лый</i> (smélyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ridicule, to scoff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) osmium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>osmium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) osmometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>osmosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>osmotic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, inspection, (sightseeing) tour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to survey, to inspect</li><li>to see, to see round, to look round</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look about, to look around</li><li>to look round, to get one's bearings, to see how the land lies</li></ol><p><i>осмотре́ть</i> (osmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence, circumspection</li></ol><p><i>осмотри́тельный</i> (osmotrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prudent, circumspect</li><li>wary</li></ol><p><i>осмотре́ть</i> (osmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apprehension, comprehension, understanding (act of grasping with the intellect)</li></ol><p><i>осмы́слить</i> (osmýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meaningful, intelligent, reasonable, sensible (<i>expressing intelligence, making sense; usually of a face, look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, to grasp</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to analyze, to interpret</li></ol><p><i>осмы́слить</i> (osmýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, to grasp</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to analyze, to interpret</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ); or perhaps <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>смы́слить</i> (smýslitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, to grasp</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to analyze, to interpret</li></ol><p><i>осмы́слить</i> (osmýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to rig</li><li>to fit out, to equip, to outfit</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>снасть</i> (snastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of rigging (a ship, etc.)</li><li>rigging (<i>on a ship</i>)</li><li>fishing tackle</li><li>technological equipment for building machine tools, etc.</li></ol><p><i>оснасти́ть</i> (osnastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to rig</li><li>to fit out, to equip, to outfit</li></ol><p><i>оснасти́ть</i> (osnastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rigging, fitting out</li><li>equipment, accessories, fixtures</li></ol><p><i>оснасти́ть</i> (osnastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being equipped, equipment</li></ol><p><i>оснащённый</i> (osnaščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basis, foundation, base</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fundamentals, essential principles, ABC, basics, foundation</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) stem</li><li>(<i>textile</i>) warp</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>основа</i> (osnova), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osnova</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>base, foundation, pedestal</li><li>foundation, establishment</li><li>grounds, reason, argument</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) base</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) base</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) base of degree or logarithm</li></ol><p><i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder</li></ol><p><i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ, “to found, establish”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thoroughly, well</li></ol><p><i>основа́тельный</i> (osnovátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thoroughness</li><li>soundness, well-foundedness</li></ol><p><i>основа́тельный</i> (osnovátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-grounded, well-founded</li><li>solid, sound, thorough, substantial</li><li>stout, solid</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bulky</li></ol><p><i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to found, to establish</li><li>to base upon, to build upon</li></ol><p>Related to, and possibly derived from, <i>осно́ва</i> (osnóva) (unless the latter is a deverbal). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>osnòvati</i> / <i>осно̀вати</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to settle down, to stay</li><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to arise, to form</li></ol><p><i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>core, essential, fundamental</li><li>the main part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>basic, fundamental, main, primary, principal, cardinal</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) basic</li></ol><p><i>осно́ва</i> (osnóva) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>basic, fundamental</li></ol><p><i>осно́ва</i> (osnóva, “basis, foundation”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полага́ющий</i> (polagájuščij), present active participle of <i>полага́ть</i> (polagátʹ, “to think, to consider”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder, initiator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to found, to establish</li><li>to base upon, to build upon</li></ol><p><i>основа́ть</i> (osnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be based (<i>imperfect only</i>)</li><li>to settle, to settle down, to stay</li><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to arise, to form</li></ol><p><i>осно́вывать</i> (osnóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person, personage, individual</li></ol><p>Initially attested in the 18th century, most likely either borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>осо́ба</i> (osóba, “person, appearance, species, hypostasis”) or Middle Polish <i>osoba</i> (with identical meanings), from Old Polish <i>osoba</i>, from Old Czech <i>osoba</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osoba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>especially</li></ol><p><i>осо́бенный</i> (osóbennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peculiarity, feature, characteristic (important or main item)</li></ol><p>From <i>осо́бенный</i> (osóbennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special (unique)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osobьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>осо́бый</i> (osóbyj) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) especially; particularly</li></ol><p><i>особли́вый</i> (osoblívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o). Cognate with Polish <i>osobliwie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) particular, special</li></ol><p>From <i>осо́ба</i> (osóba) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj). Cognate with Belarusian <i>асаблі́вы</i> (asablívy) and Polish <i>osobliwy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mansion, detached house</li></ol><p>From <i>осо́бый</i> (osóbyj) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) separately, by oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>особня́к</i> (osobnják): small mansion</li></ol><p><i>особня́к</i> (osobnják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately</li><li>differently</li><li>especially, particularly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>осо́бый</i> (osóbyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special (distinct)</li></ol><p><i>осо́ба</i> (osóba) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) individual (of an organism), specimen</li></ol><p>Cognate to <i>осо́ба</i> (osóba), <i>осо́бый</i> (osóbyj). Since the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggiornamento (reform of some teachings of Roman Catholic Church in 1962–65)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to fathom, to realize, to be aware</li></ol><p><i>осозна́ть</i> (osoznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness (the state of consciousness)</li></ol><p><i>осозна́ть</i> (osoznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consciously (in a conscious manner&#59; knowingly, volitionally)</li></ol><p><i>осо́знанный</i> (osóznannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscious, deliberate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to fathom, to realize, to be aware</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>созна́ть</i> (soznátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sedge</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, possibly cognate with <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ, “to cut, to chop”) (1sg. <i>секу́</i> (sekú)). Others propose a connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂еḱ-</i> (“sharp”) (whence Russian <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj)), relating the term to Lithuanian <i>ašakà</i> (“fishbone”), also <i>ãšaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elongated sedge (<i>Carex elongata</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Carex elongata</i>, as <i>осо́ка</i> (osóka, “sedge”) +&lrm; <i>удлинённый</i> (udlinjónnyj, “elongated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Sonchus</i>, sow thistle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осътъ</i> (osŭtŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osъtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smallpox, variola</li><li>chickenpox</li><li>cowpox</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осъпа</i> (osŭpa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osъpa</i>; related to <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ), <i>сыпь</i> (sypʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, challenge, contestation, impugnment, appeal <i>(against)</i>, litigation</li></ol><p><i>оспа́ривать</i> (ospárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contest, to dispute, to call into question</li><li>to contend <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>оспо́рить</i> (ospóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contestable, deniable, challengeable</li></ol><p><i>оспо́рить</i> (ospóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contest, to dispute, to call into question</li><li>to contend <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>спо́рить</i> (spóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame, to disgrace, to dishonor</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>срами́ть</i> (sramítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ossuary (a place where the dead are buried)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) east</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) easter (east wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>oost</i> (“east”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, to stay</li><li>to be left</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) to lose at durak</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to be made a fool of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide by (to remain faithful to&#59; to stand to&#59; to adhere to) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to leave alone</li><li>to abandon</li><li>to give up</li><li>to drop, to stop</li><li>to keep, to reserve</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave alone, to leave in peace, to let be</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abandonment, reservation, dereliction</li></ol><p><i>оста́вить</i> (ostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to leave alone</li><li>to abandon</li><li>to give up</li><li>to drop, to stop</li><li>to keep, to reserve</li></ol><p><i>оста́вить</i> (ostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remaining, residual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remaining, the rest of</li><li>otherwise</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to bring to stop</li><li>to stop short, to restrain</li><li>to fix, to direct, to concentrate</li></ol><p><i>останови́ть</i> (ostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to come to a stop, to pause, to pull up</li><li>to put up, to stop, to stay</li><li>to dwell on</li><li>to decide in favor, to decide on, to settle on</li></ol><p><i>остана́вливать</i> (ostanávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remains, remnants</li></ol><p>The plural of <i>оста́нок</i> (ostánok) (<i>archaic</i>), from Old Church Slavonic <i>останъкъ</i> (ostanŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostanъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ostankino (a district of Moscow)</li></ol><p><i>Оста́нкино</i> (Ostánkino) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to bring to stop</li><li>to stop short, to restrain</li><li>to fix, to direct, to concentrate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-станови́ть</i> (-stanovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop</li><li>to put up (at)</li><li>to dwell (on)</li></ol><p><i>останови́ть</i> (ostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stop, pause</li><li>break, pause</li><li>bus stop, stopping place</li></ol><p><i>останови́ть</i> (ostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remainder, rest, residue, remnant</li><li>residuum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostatъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>оста́ться</i> (ostátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permanent, residual, vestigial</li></ol><p><i>оста́ток</i> (ostátok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, to stay</li><li>to be left</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) to lose at durak</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to be made a fool of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide by (to remain faithful to&#59; to stand to&#59; to adhere to) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ostrogoth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glazing (covering with glass, or installation of glass)</li></ol><p><i>остекли́ть</i> (osteklítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glaze (to cover with glass, to install glass in)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>стекло́</i> (stekló) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glaze (to cover with glass, to install glass in)</li></ol><p><i>остекли́ть</i> (osteklítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) osteoporosis</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) + <i>πῶρος</i> (pôros, “tuff, a porous type of rock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frenzy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get extremely angry, to aggressive or vicious</li></ol><p>From <i>сте́рва</i> (stérva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get extremely angry, to aggressive or vicious</li></ol><p>From <i>сте́рва</i> (stérva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn</li></ol><p><i>остере́г-</i> (osterég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beware <i>(of)</i>, to be on one's guard <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>остерега́ть</i> (osteregátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>стере́чь</i> (steréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beware <i>(of)</i>, to be on one's guard <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>остере́чь</i> (osteréčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) Baltic Germans, Baltic</li></ol><p>German <i>Ostsee</i> +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spinose (<i>particularly&#32;in&#32;botany</i>)</li><li>spinal (<i>particularly&#32;in&#32;anatomy</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ость</i> (ostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skeleton</li><li>frame, framework</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupefaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be dumbfounded</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>столбене́ть</i> (stolbenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockhead, dimwit (stupid person)</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>столп</i> (stolp). The expansion with о is a result of the second pleophony (<i>второе полногласие</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>осторо́жный</i> (ostoróžnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>осторо́жный</i> (ostoróžnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carefully, cautiously, with caution, with care</li><li>guardedly, warily</li><li>prudently</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>look out!, look sharp!, watch out!, be careful!</li><li>beware, danger!</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>асцяро́жна</i> (ascjaróžna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, caution, prudence</li></ol><p><i>осторо́жный</i> (ostoróžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cautious, careful, wary, prudent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostoržьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́рож</i> (stórož) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make fed up, to make sick and tired [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>черт(е́й)</i> (čert(éj)) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) ostracism (under the Athenian democracy)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively) exile, banishment (punishment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literature</i>) defamiliarisation, ostranenie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reprimand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) warning</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>страст-</i> (strast-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) common cold; upper respiratory tract infection</li></ol><p>Literally, “acute respiratory viral infection”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj): sharpest; keenest; most acute</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to crop, to bob, to shingle</li></ol><p><i>остри́г-</i> (ostríg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut one's hair, to get a haircut</li></ol><p><i>острига́ть</i> (ostrigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sharpen, to whet</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be witty, to make witticisms, to crack jokes</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinworm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to crop, to bob, to shingle</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut one's hair, to get a haircut</li></ol><p><i>остри́чь</i> (ostríčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, spike, tip</li><li>edge, cutting edge</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>остриѥ</i> (ostrije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostrьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>acutely (in an acute manner)</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li><li>isle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>островъ</i> (ostrovŭ). Akin to <i>струя́</i> (strujá), <i>стру́мень</i> (strúmenʹ).</p><p>Compare Slovene <i>ótok</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ascension Island (an island and constituent part of the British overseas territory of Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha, located in the South Atlantic Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wrangel Island (an island of the Chukotka Autonomous Okrug, in the far northeast of Russia in the Arctic Ocean, on the 180 degree longitude meridian)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kolguyev Island (marshy island in Nenets Autonomous Okrug, Russia in the Barents Sea, about 400 miles northeast of Arkhangelsk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cook Islands (a country and archipelago of Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pitcairn Islands (a small archipelago and overseas territory of the United Kingdom in the southern Pacific Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wallis and Futuna (an archipelago and overseas collectivity of France in Oceania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>islander</li></ol><p>From <i>о́стров</i> (óstrov) +&lrm; <i>-итянин</i> (-itjanin). Compare Ukrainian <i>острів'я́нин</i> (ostrivʺjányn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) island; insular</li></ol><p><i>о́стров</i> (óstrov) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>islet</li></ol><p><i>о́стров</i> (óstrov) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) stockaded town</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) jail, gaol</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj). Note <i>острога</i> (ostroga), compare typologically <i>частоко́л</i> (častokól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing spear, harpoon, leister</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostroga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>эстраго́н</i> (estragón)</li></ol><p>Modified under the influence of <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acute accent (acute accent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;relational) prison, gaol</li><li>(historical,&#32;relational) ostrog</li></ol><p>From <i>остро́г</i> (ostróg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-pointed, pointed</li></ol><p><i>острый</i> (ostryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>конец</i> (konec) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holly</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj, “sharp, pointed”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Ostropomycetidae.</li></ol><p>Partial calque of translingual <i>Ostropomycetidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witty person, wit</li><li>joke teller</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́в(о)</i> (slóv(o), “speech”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acuity (acuteness)</li><li>sharpness (quality of being sharp (as a knife))</li><li>joke, witticism, one-liner</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acute-angled (geometry)</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acute triangle (triangle all of the angles of which are acute)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wit, wittiness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wittily, cleverly</li></ol><p><i>остроу́мный</i> (ostroúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>witty</li><li>clever</li></ol><p><i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp, pointed</li><li>acute</li><li>keen</li><li>critical</li><li>(<i>of food</i>) hot, spicy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostrъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱrós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witty person, wit</li></ol><p><i>остриё</i> (ostrijó) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>студи́ть</i> (studítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool</li></ol><p><i>остуди́ть</i> (ostudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a mistake, to do wrong, to go wrong, to go astray, to leave the straight and narrow</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a mistake, to do wrong, to go wrong, to go astray, to leave the straight and narrow</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooling-down</li></ol><p><i>остыва́ть</i> (ostyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool down (to become cooler in temperature)</li><li>to calm down, to chill out</li></ol><p>From <i>осты́ть</i> (ostýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ) or from <i>осты́нуть</i> (ostýnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>осты́ть</i> (ostýtʹ)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сты́нуть</i> (stýnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool down (to become cooler in temperature)</li><li>to calm down, to chill out</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>стыть</i> (stytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) awn, beard</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a Khanty person</li><li>(archaic,&#32;rare) a Ket or a Selkup</li></ol><p>From Turkic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to blame</li><li>to convict</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, to blame</li><li>to convict</li></ol><p><i>осуди́ть</i> (osudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conviction (judgement of guilt)</li><li>animadversion, condemnation (criticism, a critical remark)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condemned man</li><li>convicted man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/grow/look pinched, to become thin and hollow-cheeked, to look drawn/haggard, to become peaky</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry, to drain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drain, to drink up</li></ol><p><i>осуши́ть</i> (osušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry, to drain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drain, to drink up</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drainage, draining</li></ol><p><i>осуши́ть</i> (osušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>practicable, realizable, feasible, achievable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to realize, to accomplish, to fulfill, to put into practice</li><li>to carry out, to implement</li><li>to exercise (in terms of rights or control)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>существо́</i> (suščestvó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be carried out; to be realized; to come true; to come to be; to be put into effect</li></ol><p><i>осуществи́ть</i> (osuščestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realization, implementation (the act of making real)</li><li>enforcement</li></ol><p><i>осуществи́ть</i> (osuščestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to realize, to accomplish, to fulfill, to put into practice</li><li>to carry out, to implement</li><li>to exercise (in terms of rights or control)</li></ol><p><i>осуществи́ть</i> (osuščestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be carried out; to be realized; to come true; to come to be; to be put into effect</li></ol><p><i>осуществля́ть</i> (osuščestvljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make happy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>счастли́вый</i> (sčastlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), or less likely <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>счастли́вить</i> (sčastlívitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew <i>(with)</i>, to shower <i>(on)</i></li><li>to shower <i>(with)</i>, to heap <i>(on)</i></li><li><i>(leaves, petals)</i> to shed</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ). Compare Czech <i>osypat</i> or Polish <i>osypać</i>.</p><p><i>осы́пать</i> (osýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble</li><li>(<i>flowers, leaves</i>) to fall <i>(off)</i>, <i>(crops)</i> to shed its grain</li></ol><p><i>осы́пать</i> (osýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>осыпа́ть</i> (osypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scree, talus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axis</li><li>axle</li><li>(<i>computer slang</i>) operating system</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ось</i> (osĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osь</i> (“axle”).</p><p>In the sense of "operating system", is an adaptation of the English acronym OS in the same sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axis of evil (states supposedly sponsoring terrorism and seeking weapons of mass destruction)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>axis of evil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octopus</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;oḱtṓw</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>осмь</i> (osmĭ) (Modern Russian <i>во́семь</i> (vósemʹ)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osmь</i> (“eight”) + genitive plural of <i>&#42;noga</i> (“foot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) octopus</li></ol><p><i>осьмино́г</i> (osʹminóg) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) tangible, perceptible</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively) noticeable, visible, significant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense of touch</li></ol><p><i>осяза́ть</i> (osjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tactile, tactual</li><li>sensible, tangible, palpable</li></ol><p><i>осязать</i> (osjazatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel (<i>by touching</i>)</li><li>to perceive, to be palpably aware (<i>of</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>осѧѕати</i> (osędzati) (1sg. <i>осѧѕаѭ</i> (osędzajǫ) or <i>осѧжѫ</i> (osęžǫ)), variant (showing the Slavic progressive palatalization) of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obsęgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey, ass (animal)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idiot, fool, stupid or naive person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;osьlъ</i> (“donkey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sturgeon</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) VIP, big cheese</li><li>Osetr (class of submarines of the Imperial Russian Navy)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;esetrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from, away from, of, with (in the following senses):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to denote the starting point of the movement of something&#58;</li><li>to denote a reference point in comparison with the position of something else&#58;</li><li>used with certain verbs to denote literal or figurative distance from the indirect object&#58;</li><li>(with <i>до</i> (do)) to denote a range&#58;</li><li>to denote the cause or origin of some condition&#58;</li><li>used in a few set phrases to denote the nature of an action&#58;</li></ol><li>from, since, of (about a time of origin)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>отъ</i> (otŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from time to time, at times, now and then, every now and then, now and again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the heart, sincerely</li><li>to one's heart's content</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) thank you</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) from top to bottom, back to front, inside out, thoroughly, fully, completely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on behalf of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the beginning</li><li>ab initio (referring to the time from when a legal document comes into force)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with effort, with some goodwill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) away from</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъ-</i>; see <i>&#42;otъ</i> for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aftergrass, aftermath, eddish, fog grass, regrowth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otaku</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>オタク</i>/<i>おたく</i> (otaku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat (e.g. a building)</li></ol><p><i>отопи́ть</i> (otopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock <i>(of sheep)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic. Compare Karaim <i>otar</i> (“pasture; flock of sheep”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out, to discard [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something’</small> <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘part of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out, to discard [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all of something’</small> <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘part of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отба́вить</i> (otbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run back</li><li>to run away</li><li>to finish running</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run back</li><li>to run away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleach (chemical)</li></ol><p><i>отбе́ливать</i> (otbélivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blanch, to whiten, to make something white</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бели́ть</i> (belítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat off (an attack; enemies), to repulse</li><li>to knock off, to break off</li><li>to parry, to return (a ball)</li><li>to take, to snatch away (by force)</li><li>to liberate (by force)</li><li>to beat, to drum, to strike (a rhythm)</li></ol><p><i>отби́ть</i> (otbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight off, to ward off, to beat off, to repulse, to defend oneself</li><li>to come off, to fall off</li><li>to fall behind from a group, to get lost</li></ol><p><i>отбива́ть</i> (otbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutlet, chop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intended for beating back (a blow) (<i>of a weapon</i>)</li><li>beaten (made by beating, softened by beating, e.g. meat)</li></ol><p><i>отбива́ть</i> (otbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select, to single out</li><li>to take away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat off (an attack; enemies), to repulse</li><li>to knock off, to break off</li><li>to parry, to return (a ball)</li><li>to take, to snatch away (by force)</li><li>to liberate (by force)</li><li>to beat, to drum, to strike (a rhythm)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight off, to ward off, to beat off, to repulse, to defend oneself</li><li>to come off, to fall off</li><li>to fall behind from a group, to get lost</li></ol><p><i>отби́ть</i> (otbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show one's gratitude, to show one's appreciation <i>(of)</i></li><li>to return/repay someone's kindness/favour/favor</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>благодари́ть</i> (blagodarítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection, gleam, sheen</li><li>reflection, imprint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(military or police)</i> over</li><li>all clear signal, ceasefire signal</li><li>(<i>military</i>) retreat</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) ring off</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъbojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crash barrier</li></ol><p><i>отбить</i> (otbitʹ, “to knock off, beat off”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intended for chipping or breaking</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъbojьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackhammer, pneumatic drill, road breaker, demolition hammer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection, choice (process or act of selecting)</li><li>picking, sampling</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отбира́ть</i> (otbirátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>select, choice, picked</li></ol><p><i>отбо́р</i> (otbór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) selection, choice</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) qualifying</li></ol><p><i>отбо́рка</i> (otbórka, “selection, choosing”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to throw aside</li><li>to make retreat (army)</li><li>to move back, to move to the previous position</li><li>to cast (a shadow)</li></ol><p><i>отбро́сить</i> (otbrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) garbage</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отбро́сить</i> (otbrósitʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>ēiectāmenta</i> (&lt; <i>ēiectō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to throw aside</li><li>to make retreat (army)</li><li>to move back, to move to the previous position</li><li>to cast (a shadow)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waste, refuse (useless products, garbage)</li><li>dregs, scum</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>отбро́с</i> (otbrós).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>ēiectāmenta</i> (plural of <i>ēiectāmentum</i>; &lt; <i>ēiectō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave</li><li>to serve (in prison)</li></ol><p><i>отбы́ть</i> (otbýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li><li>completion, serving out (<i>of a term in office, prison sentence, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отбы́ть</i> (otbýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart (from or for)</li><li>to complete (a prison sentence, military duty, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravery, valour, courage</li></ol><p>Possibly from Polish <i>odwaga</i>, from Middle High German <i>wāge</i>, from Old High German <i>wāga</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>відва́га</i> (vidváha), Belarusian <i>адва́га</i> (adváha).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break of the habit <i>(of)</i>, to teach not <i>(to)</i></li><li>to scare away, to drive off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́дить</i> (-váditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out of the habit [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отва́дить</i> (otváditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break of the habit <i>(of)</i>, to teach not <i>(to)</i></li><li>to scare away, to drive off</li></ol><p><i>отва́дить</i> (otváditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out of the habit [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><li>to dare, to venture, to have the courage</li></ol><p><i>отва́живать</i> (otváživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>отва́житься</i> (otvážitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, to venture, to have the courage</li></ol><p><i>отва́ж-</i> (otváž-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, bravely, doughtily</li></ol><p><i>отва́жный</i> (otvážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valiant, brave, courageous, gallant</li></ol><p><i>отва́га</i> (otvága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li><li>mouldboard, earthboard</li><li>(<i>minery</i>) dump, slag-heap</li><li>sailing off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) something amazing, cool or extraordinary; something rad, something wicked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pull off, to heave off; to cast off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lavish (money), to pay out a large sum of money (generously)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go away, to piss off; to leave someone alone</li></ol><p>From <i>отвали́ть</i> (otvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or from <i>отваля́ть</i> (otvaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall off, to peel off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lean back</li></ol><p><i>отва́ливать</i> (otválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pull off, to heave off; to cast off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lavish (money), to pay out a large sum of money (generously)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go away, to piss off; to leave someone alone</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall off, to peel off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lean back</li></ol><p><i>отвали́ть</i> (otvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broth</li><li>decoction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiled; prepared as part of a broth or by means of decoction</li></ol><p><i>отва́р</i> (otvár) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try, to taste</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try, to taste</li></ol><p><i>отве́дать</i> (otvédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away (by vehicle), to drive away, to take (someone or something by vehicle)</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí, “to carry, transport”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject, to repel, to repudiate, to scout (reject with contempt), to waive, to disallow</li></ol><p><i>отве́ргнуть</i> (otvérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject, to repel, to repudiate, to scout (reject with contempt), to waive, to disallow</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ, “to throw”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freezing</li></ol><p>From <i>отвердева́ть</i> (otverdevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije), from <i>отверде́ть</i> (otverdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ), from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тверде́ть</i> (tverdétʹ), from <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj, “hard, solid”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze, solidify</li><li>to harden, to become firm</li></ol><p><i>отверде́ть</i> (otverdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardening; induration</li><li>callosity</li></ol><p><i>отверде́ть</i> (otverdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harden, to become firm</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тверде́ть</i> (tverdétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) rejection</li></ol><p>From <i>отве́ргнуть</i> (otvérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Compare former past passive participle <i>отве́рженный</i> (otvéržennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) a state of rejection, exclusion, or being cast out</li></ol><p><i>отве́рженный</i> (otvéržennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rejected, outcast</li></ol><p>Formerly the past passive participle of <i>отве́ргнуть</i> (otvérgnutʹ), from <i>отве́рг(нуть)</i> (otvérg(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to open up</li></ol><p><i>отверза́ть</i> (otverzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>to fold down, to turn down (e.g. a blanket)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li><li>to unscrew, to loosen</li><li>to turn on (a faucet, a tap)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away, to turn one's back (on)</li><li>to come unscrewed</li></ol><p><i>отверну́ть</i> (otvernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening, aperture, hole, perforation</li><li>mesh (<i>of a screen</i>)</li><li>orifice, mouth, vent, passage, inlet, outlet, port</li><li>break, gap</li><li>bore</li><li>span (<i>of a bridge</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come unscrewed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to wriggle out; to get out; to get off; to escape</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plummet, plumb, plumb-line</li><li>vertical slope, vertical face; sheer cliff</li><li>(slang,&#32;eating disorders) body weight loss seen upon a new weighing on the scale</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъvěsъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh out</li><li>to make, to take (a bow)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal (a blow)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep, plumb, sheer, precipitous, vertical</li></ol><p><i>отве́с</i> (otvés) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead away; to take / draw aside; to take away</li><li>to divert</li><li>to avert, to parry; to deflect; to draw off; to ward off</li><li>to allocate, to assign</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer, reply, response</li><li>responsibility</li><li>Otvet (Russian anti-submarine missile)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъvětъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch, branching, embranchment, offshoot, outshoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer, to reply</li><li>to be responsible for, to be accountable for, to be liable for</li><li>to account for, to answer for</li><li>to be held to account for, to be called to account for</li></ol><p><i>отве́т</i> (otvét) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reciprocal, in return, in answer</li></ol><p><i>отве́т</i> (otvét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>responsibly</li><li>crucially</li></ol><p><i>отве́тственный</i> (otvétstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>responsibility</li></ol><p><i>отве́тственный</i> (otvétstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>responsible (<i>various senses</i>)</li><li>in charge</li><li>crucial, critical</li></ol><p>From <i>отве́тство</i> (otvétstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) answer</li></ol><p><i>отве́т</i> (otvét) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) answer</li></ol><p><i>отве́т</i> (otvét) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to reply, to answer</li></ol><p><i>отве́тство</i> (otvétstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defendant, the accused, respondent</li></ol><p><i>отве́тить</i> (otvétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer, to reply</li><li>to be responsible for, to be accountable for, to be liable for</li><li>to account for, to answer for</li><li>to be held to account for, to be called to account for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъvěťati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>отве́тить</i> (otvétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh out</li><li>to make, to take (a bow)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give, to deal (a blow)</li></ol><p><i>отве́сить</i> (otvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unscrew</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>винти́ть</i> (vintítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unscrew</li></ol><p><i>отвинти́ть</i> (otvintítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to sag</li></ol><p><i>отви́снуть</i> (otvísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to sag</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distract, to divert</li></ol><p><i>отвле́к-</i> (otvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distracted, to deflect/divert one's attention away</li><li>to digress</li><li>to abstract oneself</li></ol><p><i>отвлека́ть</i> (otvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distraction</li><li>abstraction</li></ol><p><i>отвле́к-</i> (otvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distract, to divert</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distracted, to deflect/divert one's attention away</li><li>to digress</li><li>to abstract oneself</li></ol><p><i>отвле́чь</i> (otvléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abstract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withdrawal</li><li>rejection, objection, challenge</li><li>allotment</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) pipe bend</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) tap, tapping, bend</li><li>(<i>naval</i>) spreader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead away; to take / draw aside; to take away</li><li>to divert</li><li>to avert, to parry; to deflect; to draw off; to ward off</li><li>to allocate, to assign</li><li>to withdraw</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li><li>to recuse</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abductor (muscle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abductive (anatomy)</li><li>deviating, diverting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win <i>(from)</i>, to win over, to win back <i>(from)</i></li><li>to fight <i>(for some time)</i></li><li>to finish (be through with) fighting</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>воева́ть</i> (vojevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish fighting, to be through with fighting</li></ol><p><i>отвоева́ть</i> (otvojevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away (by vehicle), to drive away, to take (someone or something by vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>to fold down, to turn down (e.g. a blanket)</li><li>to loosen, to remove (e.g. a stone from a fence)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li></ol><p><i>отвороти́ть</i> (otvorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to turn away, to avert one's face/eyes</li><li>to break (<i>with someone</i>), to turn away (<i>from someone</i>), to turn one's back</li><li>to come unscrewed</li><li>to bend back</li></ol><p><i>отвора́чивать</i> (otvoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open (a door, a gate, or a window)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъvorìti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-твори́ть</i> (-tvorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li></ol><p><i>отвори́ть</i> (otvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapel, flap (each of the two triangular pieces of cloth on a suit)</li><li>(boot) top</li><li>cuff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to fold down, to turn down (e.g. a blanket)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to loosen, to remove (e.g. a stone from a fence)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ). Compare <i>отврати́ть</i> (otvratítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open (a door, a gate, or a window)</li></ol><p><i>отвори́ть</i> (otvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li></ol><p><i>отворя́ть</i> (otvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win <i>(from)</i>, to win over, to win back <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>отвоева́ть</i> (otvojevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something abominable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>отврати́тельный</i> (otvratítelʹnyj): disgustingest</li></ol><p><i>отврати́тельн(ый)</i> (otvratítelʹn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disgustingly, nauseatingly, abominably</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) horribly, very badly</li></ol><p><i>отврати́тельный</i> (otvratítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting (repulsive, distasteful)</li></ol><p><i>отврати́ть</i> (otvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>turpis</i>, possibly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avert, to ward off, to prevent</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to avert, to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>to keep (from), to prevent (from)</li><li>to turn (off), to put (off), to cause aversion (in)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отъвратити</i> (otŭvratiti). By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ, “to turn”). Doublet of <i>отвороти́ть</i> (otvorotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avert, to ward off, to prevent</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to avert, to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>to keep (from), to prevent (from)</li><li>to turn (off), to put (off), to cause aversion (in)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отъвращати</i> (otŭvraštati). By surface analysis, <i>от</i> (ot) +&lrm; <i>вращать</i> (vraščatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aversion, disgust, repugnance</li></ol><p><i>отврати́ть</i> (otvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out of the habit <i>(of)</i>, to break oneself of the habit <i>(of)</i></li><li>to become estranged <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>отвы́кнуть</i> (otvýknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out of the habit <i>(of)</i>, to break oneself of the habit <i>(of)</i></li><li>to become estranged <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъvyknǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́кнуть</i> (-výknutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie</li><li>to disconnect (account, profile)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get loose</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie</li></ol><p><i>отвяза́ть</i> (otvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get loose</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>отвяза́ться</i> (otvjazátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screwdriver, turnscrew (tool)</li><li>screwdriver (a cocktail made with orange juice and vodka)</li><li>screwing off (action)</li></ol><p>From <i>отверте́ть</i> (otvertétʹ, “to turn off, to screw off”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to turn away</li><li>to fold down, to turn down (e.g. a blanket)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li><li>to unscrew, to loosen</li><li>to turn on (a faucet, a tap)</li></ol><p><i>отверну́ть</i> (otvernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess, to solve a riddle</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess, to solve a riddle</li></ol><p><i>отгада́ть</i> (otgadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie-down, bending, tying down (agr.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbend, to straighten</li><li>to roll up or down (the edge of something, e.g. one's sleeves)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verb form</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verbal noun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) deverbal, verbal (derived from a verb)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>глаго́л</i> (glagól, “verb”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissuade (someone from), to put someone off, to talk/reason (someone out of)</li></ol><p><i>отговори́ть</i> (otgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an action of dissuading (someone from), to put someone off, to talk/reason (someone out of)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) incantation, spell from something evil</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отговори́ть</i> (otgovorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissuade (someone from), to put someone off, to talk/reason (someone out of)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excuse, pretext</li></ol><p><i>отговори́ть</i> (otgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dissuasion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echo</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>го́лос</i> (gólos) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away</li><li>to try to get rid of, to suppress (a mental or physical state)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence off, to partition off, to screen off</li></ol><p><i>отгороди́ть</i> (otgorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence oneself off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to isolate oneself</li></ol><p><i>отгора́живать</i> (otgoráživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence off, to partition off, to screen off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>городи́ть</i> (gorodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence oneself off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to isolate oneself</li></ol><p><i>отгороди́ть</i> (otgorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispatch, to ship</li></ol><p><i>отгрузи́ть</i> (otgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispatch, to ship</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispatch, shipping, shipment</li></ol><p><i>отгрузи́ть</i> (otgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paid time off in compensation for overtime work</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отгуля́ть</i> (otguljátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take compensatory leave [a compensatory holiday], to take <i>(a certain number of)</i> days off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish taking time off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to celebrate (an occasion)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish celebrating (an occasion)</li></ol><p><i>отгуля́ть</i> (otguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take time off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>отде́л</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;inan (otdél)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>отделе́ние</i>&nbsp;n&nbsp;inan (otdelénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give back, to return</li><li>to give (away), to hand over, to give up, to give over</li><li>to send</li><li>to devote</li><li>to cast (anchor)</li><li>to recoil (of a gun)</li><li>to smell like or taste of [<i>with</i> instrumental]</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to give exclusive rights to something in exchange for money</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give exclusive control over (a project, etc., to someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to realize, to be or become aware, to understand</li></ol><p>Likely a calque of French <i>se rendre compte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to surrender oneself <i>(to)</i>, to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to yield <i>(to)</i>, to put out <i>(to)</i> <i>(have sex)</i></li><li>to resound, to reverberate, to ring</li><li>to make</li></ol><p><i>отдава́ть</i> (otdavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) removal; estrangement</li></ol><p><i>отдали́ть</i> (otdalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold away, to move away</li><li>to postpone, to put off</li><li>to estrange, to alienate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-дали́ть</i> (-dalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away <i>(from)</i></li><li>to drift apart, to become estranged</li></ol><p><i>отдали́ть</i> (otdalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold away, to move away</li><li>to postpone, to put off</li><li>to estrange, to alienate</li></ol><p><i>отдали́ть</i> (otdalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away <i>(from)</i></li><li>to drift apart, to become estranged</li></ol><p><i>отдаля́ть</i> (otdaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>distantly, remotely</li></ol><p><i>отдалённый</i> (otdaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remoteness</li><li>distance</li></ol><p><i>отдалённый</i> (otdaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remote, distant, outlying, far</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a reciprocal gift, to reciprocate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a reciprocal gift, to reciprocate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дари́ть</i> (darítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give back, to return</li><li>to give (away), to hand over, to give up, to give over</li><li>to send</li><li>to devote</li><li>to cast (anchor)</li><li>to recoil (of a gun)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to give exclusive rights to something in exchange for money</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give exclusive control over (a project, etc., to someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to surrender oneself <i>(to)</i>, to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to yield <i>(to)</i>, to put out <i>(to)</i> <i>(have sex)</i></li><li>to resound, to reverberate, to ring</li><li>to make</li></ol><p><i>отда́ть</i> (otdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>return</li><li>(military,&#32;engineering) recoil, kick</li><li>efficiency, output</li><li>(<i>naval</i>) dropping, casting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department, division, bureau, office, section</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ). Compare Polish <i>oddział</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to trim <i>(up)</i></li><li>to trim <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spoil, to ruin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to wallop, to lick, to give the works, to give the business, to tan someone's hide</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid <i>(of)</i>, to get away <i>(from)</i>, to throw off, to shake off, to be through <i>(with)</i>, to finish <i>(with)</i></li><li>to escape <i>(with)</i>, to get away <i>(with)</i>, to get off <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get away <i>(with)</i>, to get off <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>отде́лать</i> (otdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department, division, branch</li><li>(the act of) separation</li><li>secretion</li><li>compartment</li><li>(<i>military</i>) squad, section</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) police station or a local police department. contraction of <i>отделение милиции</i> (otdelenije milicii) (older). contraction of <i>отделение полиции</i> (otdelenije policii) (newer).</li><li>secession</li><li>section, sector, part (e.g. of a train, shop or a bag)</li></ol><p><i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separable</li></ol><p><i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to detach, to part</li><li>to disjoint, to divorce, to separate off</li><li>to divide, to serve as boundary</li><li>to secrete</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to get detached, to come off</li><li>to set up on one's own</li></ol><p><i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finishing, trimming, decoration work/finish</li><li>finish, decoration, trimmings</li></ol><p><i>отде́лать</i> (otdélatʹ, “to finish, to trim”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) finishing</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) decoration</li></ol><p><i>отде́лка</i> (otdélka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to trim <i>(up)</i></li><li>to trim <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spoil, to ruin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to wallop, to lick, to give the works, to give the business, to tan someone's hide</li></ol><p><i>отде́лать</i> (otdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get rid <i>(of)</i>, to get away <i>(from)</i>, to throw off, to shake off, to be through <i>(with)</i>, to finish <i>(with)</i></li><li>to escape <i>(with)</i>, to get away <i>(with)</i>, to get off <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get away <i>(with)</i>, to get off <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>отде́лывать</i> (otdélyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately (in a separate manner)</li></ol><p><i>отде́льный</i> (otdélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separateness</li></ol><p><i>отде́льный</i> (otdélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separate, independent, detached</li><li>individual, single</li></ol><p><i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to detach, to part</li><li>to disjoint, to divorce, to separate off</li><li>to divide, to serve as boundary</li><li>to secrete</li></ol><p><i>отдели́ть</i> (otdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to get detached, to come off</li><li>to set up on one's own</li></ol><p><i>отделя́ть</i> (otdeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) squad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) squad leader, squad commander</li></ol><p>From <i>отделе́ние</i> (otdelénije, “squad”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) respite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest, to relax, to have a rest</li><li>to be resting, to be on holiday, to take a vacation</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дуба́сить</i> (dubásitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away (dust, steam, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>отду́ть</i> (otdútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become puffed up</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to puff, to pant</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to bulge (<i>of pockets</i>)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to take the fall for, to take the blame for</li></ol><p><i>отдува́ть</i> (otduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away (dust, steam, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to thrash</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become puffed up</li></ol><p><i>отду́ть</i> (otdútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vent; outlet; airhole; air valve</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vent, something that gives relief to frustrations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest</li><li>relaxation, respite</li><li>holiday (in the sense of vacation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to relax, to have a rest, to take a rest</li><li>to be on holiday, to be on vacation</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ды́хать</i> (dýxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacationer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover one's breath</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дыша́ть</i> (dyšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respite, short rest pause</li><li>misspelling of <i>одышка</i> (odyška)</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дышать</i> (dyšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jerk away, to pull away, to yank away, to take away, to withdraw</li><li>to draw aside, to pull aside</li></ol><p><i>отдёрг-</i> (otdjórg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jerk away, to pull away, to yank away, to take away, to withdraw</li><li>to draw aside, to pull aside</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>отде́л</i> (otdél).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to be swollen, to become dropsical</li><li><i>(a candle)</i> to drip</li></ol><p><i>оте́к-</i> (oték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calve, <i>(deer)</i> to fawn</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>телиться</i> (telitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hotel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>hôtel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe clean, to wipe dry</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li><li>ancestor</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) senior, superior, first of, chief, sensei</li><li>familiar term of address for an elderly man</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) referring to a man who cares about somebody or something like a father</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) founder, initiator of something</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) root, basis, source</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>отьць</i> (otĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otьcь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;átta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding father</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>основа́тель</i> (osnovátelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paternal, fatherly</li></ol><p><i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>native, home</li><li>patriotic</li></ol><p><i>оте́чество</i> (otéčestvo, “fatherland”) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatherland, motherland, native land, native country, country</li><li>mother country, home country, homeland</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo). Doublet of <i>о́тчество</i> (ótčestvo) and <i>отцо́вство</i> (otcóvstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to be swollen, to become dropsical</li><li><i>(a candle)</i> to drip</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze dry</li><li>to squeeze out (liquid)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push back, to drive back, to force to retreat</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to force to separate under pressure (e.g. a valve, a layer of rock)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extort, to expropriate, to seize or take the possession of something by physical force, menace or other illegal and ingenuine means</li><li>to finish harvesting, to finish reaping</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to deliver (to set free from danger etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anneal</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become obsolete</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-жива́ть</i> (-živátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annealing (act of heating solid metal or glass)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>burn part of an item</li><li>to anneal</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something out of the ordinary, to go beyond the ordinary, to be extraordinary or eccentric</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a squeezing out (of liquid)</li><li>(slang,&#32;economics) extortion</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отжима́ть</i> (otžimátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>push-up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze dry</li><li>to squeeze out (liquid)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push back, to drive back, to force to retreat</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to force to separate under pressure (e.g. a valve, a layer of rock)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extort, to expropriate, to seize or take the possession of something by physical force, menace or other illegal and ingenuine means</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish harvesting, to finish reaping</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echo</li><li>repercussion</li><li>response</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otzovist, recallist</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>зов</i> (zov) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst). Formed as if based on &#42;отзо́в — actually <i>отзы́в</i> (otzýv, “recall (of an ambassador)”) — formed by analogy with <i>зов</i> (zov), <i>вы́зов</i> (výzov), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opinion, review, response, comment</li><li>feedback (also обратная связь)</li><li>reference, testimonial</li><li>response, echo</li><li><i>(to the password)</i> reply</li><li>recall (of an ambassador, delegate, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recall (product), to summon (political or diplomatic personnel)</li><li>to take aside</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to respond (to), to answer; to echo</li><li>to resound</li><li>to speak [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>], to give feedback</li><li>to tell <i>(upon, on)</i></li><li><i>(pain)</i> to extend <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a taste <i>(of)</i>, to smack <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>отзыва́ть</i> (otzyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindheartedness, sympathy</li><li>empathy</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) latency</li></ol><p><i>отзы́вчивый</i> (otzývčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forthcoming, responsive, sympathetic</li></ol><p><i>отзыва́ть(ся)</i> (otzyvátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe clean, to wipe dry</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refusal, denial, repudiation, rejection</li><li>renunciation, disavowal</li><li>breakdown, failure</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отказа́ть</i> (otkazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny, to refuse, to decline</li><li>to break, to fail, to stop working</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to bequeath, to leave</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ). A false friend with Belarusian <i>адказа́ць</i> (adkazácʹ, “to answer, to respond, to reply”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abandon</li><li>to abdicate</li><li>to refuse</li><li>to forgo</li><li>to renounce</li></ol><p><i>отказа́ть</i> (otkazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;historical) otkaznik; refusenik</li><li>(<i>military</i>) conscientious objector</li></ol><p><i>отка́з</i> (otkáz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny, to refuse, to decline</li><li>to break, to fail, to stop working</li></ol><p><i>отказа́ть</i> (otkazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abandon</li><li>to abdicate</li><li>to refuse</li><li>to forgo</li><li>to renounce</li></ol><p><i>отка́зывать</i> (otkázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calibrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calibrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split off, to chip off, to cut off</li><li>to separate something attached or nailed</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something unexpected (pleasant or unpleasant)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something tricky, complex or dexterous</li></ol><p><i>отколо́ть</i> (otkolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off <i>(from)</i></li><li>to break <i>(with)</i>, to split off <i>(from)</i>, splinter off <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>отка́лывать</i> (otkályvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out</li><li>to exhume, to disinter, to disentomb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig up, to unearth, to find</li></ol><p><i>откопа́ть</i> (otkopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recoil</li><li>retreat</li><li>ramp, slope</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) undo, rollback</li><li>kickback (clandestine payment for providing an opportunity of chargeable transaction)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>откати́ть</i> (otkatítʹ, “to roll away, to roll back”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll away</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rebound</li></ol><p><i>откати́ть</i> (otkatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough up</li></ol><p><i>отка́шлять</i> (otkášljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear one's throat</li></ol><p><i>отка́шливать</i> (otkášlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ка́шлять</i> (kášljatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear one's throat</li></ol><p><i>отка́шлять</i> (otkášljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>folding</li><li>strained (<i>using a colander</i>)</li></ol><p><i>откид-</i> (otkid-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to cast away</li><li>to turn down (e.g. a seat)</li></ol><p><i>отки́д-</i> (otkíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean back, to settle back</li></ol><p><i>отки́дывать</i> (otkídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, to cast away</li><li>to turn down (e.g. a seat)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean back, to settle back</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to come out of prison after having served time</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to die, kick the bucket</li></ol><p><i>отки́нуть</i> (otkínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set aside</li><li>to lay by, to save (money for some purchase)</li><li>to put off, to delay, to adjourn, to postpone</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to shelve, to postpone indefinitely</li></ol><p>Compare Dutch <i>op de lange baan schuiven</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bid adieu</li></ol><p><i>откла́няться</i> (otklánjatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bid adieu</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>кла́няться</i> (klánjatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel off</li></ol><p><i>откле́ить</i> (otkléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echo, repercussion</li><li>response</li><li><i>(to the password)</i> reply</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) comments; reaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to respond, to answer (to a call, challenge, appeal, etc.)</li><li>to respond (with a letter), to comment (in the press)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to sound as an answer, to like an echo</li></ol><p><i>откли́кнуться</i> (otklíknutʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to respond, to answer (to a call, challenge, appeal, etc.)</li><li>to respond (with a letter), to comment (in the press)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to sound as an answer, to like an echo</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>кли́кнуть</i> (klíknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deviation (act of deviating)</li></ol><p><i>отклони́ть</i> (otklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deflect</li><li>to decline, to turn down</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move to the side, to tilt, to be deflected</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to swerve, to deviate [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from something’</small>]</li><li>to dodge, to evade [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to change [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘the subject, topic, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отклони́ть</i> (otklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deflect</li><li>to decline, to turn down</li></ol><p><i>отклони́ть</i> (otklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move to the side, to tilt, to be deflected</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to swerve, to deviate [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from something’</small>]</li><li>to dodge, to evade [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to change [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘the subject, topic, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отклоня́ть</i> (otklonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deviating</li><li>deflecting</li><li>digressive, excursive</li><li>aberrant</li><li>divergent</li><li>tangential</li><li>declinatory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to disconnect, to turn off, to switch off, to shut down</li></ol><p><i>отключи́ть</i> (otključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become disconnected, to switch off, to shut down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go out <i>(of it)</i>, to conk out, to flake out, to crash out</li></ol><p><i>отключа́ть</i> (otključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shutdown (action of closing a computer)</li><li>cutoff, disconnection</li><li>deactivating</li></ol><p><i>отключи́ть</i> (otključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to disconnect, to turn off, to switch off, to shut down</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become disconnected, to switch off, to shut down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go out <i>(of it)</i>, to conk out, to flake out, to crash out</li></ol><p><i>отключи́ть</i> (otključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge (to shape metal)</li><li>to unfetter</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge (to shape metal)</li><li>to unfetter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) since when; since</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) from where</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split off, to chip off, to cut off</li><li>to separate something attached or nailed</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something unexpected (pleasant or unpleasant)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to do something tricky, complex or dexterous</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>колошма́тить</i> (kološmátitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out</li><li>to exhume, to disinter, to disentomb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig up, to unearth, to find</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten <i>(an animal etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-fed, corpulent, fat, plump</li><li>fattened (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slope, slant, scarp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish cutting</li><li>to cut off a portion of?</li></ol><p><i>открои́ть</i> (otkroítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detach, to unfasten</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to unpin, to undock</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detach, to unfasten</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to unpin, to undock</li></ol><p><i>открепи́ть</i> (otkrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to cross oneself in protection of evil spirits</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to disown (to refuse to own or to refuse to acknowledge one’s own)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revelation (the act of revealing or disclosing)</li><li>afflatus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отъкръвениѥ</i> (otŭkrŭvenije, “revelation”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>отъкрꙑти</i> (otŭkryti, “to open, reveal”). Related to <i>откры́ть</i> (otkrýtʹ, “to open, reveal”). Doublet of <i>откры́тие</i> (otkrýtije, “opening, discovery, revelation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frank, to have a frank and sincere talk (e.g. with close friends, revealing something personal)</li></ol><p><i>открове́нный</i> (otkrovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frankly, candidly, openly</li></ol><p><i>открове́нный</i> (otkrovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candour, sincerity, openness</li></ol><p><i>открове́нный</i> (otkrovénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sincere</li><li>frank, candid, straightforward</li><li>revealing (<i>of clothing</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отъкръвеньнъ</i> (otŭkrŭvenĭnŭ, “open”), from <i>отъкръвенъ</i> (otŭkrŭvenŭ, “opened”) +&lrm; <i>-ьнъ</i> (-ĭnŭ). Doublet of <i>откры́тый</i> (otkrýtyj, “open, sincere”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish cutting</li><li>to cut off a portion of?</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>крои́ть</i> (kroítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>кромса́ть</i> (kromsátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>открыва́шка</i> (otkryváška)</li></ol><p><i>открыва́ть</i> (otkryvátʹ, “to open”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening (action of opening)</li></ol><p><i>открыва́ть</i> (otkryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to turn on</li><li>to discover</li><li>to disclose</li><li>to reveal</li><li>to unveil</li><li>to inaugurate</li></ol><p><i>откры́ть</i> (otkrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;ironic) to discover that Queen Anne's dead (to rediscover and announce common knowledge)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to discover America.”. Compare Polish <i>odkrywać Amerykę</i>, Italian <i>scoprire l'America</i>, French <i>redécouvrir l'Amérique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to come to light</li><li>to confide, to open up, to declare oneself</li></ol><p><i>открыва́ть</i> (otkryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) opener; can opener; bottle opener</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>в откры́тую</i> (v otkrýtuju)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening</li><li>discovery</li><li>revelation</li><li>inauguration</li><li>unveiling</li></ol><p><i>откры́ть</i> (otkrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post card</li><li>greeting card, birthday card, etc.</li></ol><p>Short for <i>откры́тое письмо́</i> (otkrýtoje pisʹmó, “open letter”), with suffix <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>avowedly, openly, frankly, publicly (in a frank, open, honest, or too honest manner)</li></ol><p><i>откры́тый</i> (otkrýtyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open sea, high seas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>openness</li></ol><p><i>откры́тый</i> (otkrýtyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>open (not closed)</li><li>public</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to turn on</li><li>to discover</li><li>to disclose</li><li>to reveal</li><li>to unveil</li><li>to inaugurate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>отъкрꙑти</i> (otŭkryti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъkryti</i>, from <i>&#42;otъ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;kryti</i> (“to cover, hide”). By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ).</p><p>The sense "to discover" is a calque of French <i>découvrir</i> or German <i>entdecken</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;ironic) to discover that Queen Anne's dead (to rediscover and announce common knowledge)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to discover America”. Compare Polish <i>odkryć Amerykę</i>, Italian <i>scoprire l'America</i>, Ukrainian <i>відкри́ти Аме́рику</i> (vidkrýty Améryku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to come to light</li><li>to confide, to open up, to declare oneself</li></ol><p><i>откры́ть</i> (otkrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whence, from where</li><li>from which</li><li>whence</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>куда́</i> (kudá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from somewhere or other, from some place or another</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from anywhere, from somewhere</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) from anywhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from somewhere, somewhence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;economics) tax farming</li><li>lease</li><li>payoff</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>откупи́ть</i> (otkupítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to buy up</li></ol><p><i>откупи́ть</i> (otkupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to ransom oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to free oneself by bribing or paying off, to buy one's freedom, to bribe one's way out [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to finish bathing or swimming</li></ol><p><i>откупа́ть</i> (otkupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ться</i> (kupátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to buy up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to ransom oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to free oneself by bribing or paying off, to buy one's freedom, to bribe one's way out [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>откупи́ть</i> (otkupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uncork</li></ol><p><i>отку́порить</i> (otkúporitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uncork</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ку́порить</i> (kúporitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite off, to take a bite <i>(of)</i></li><li>to snap off, to nip off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite off, to take a bite <i>(of)</i></li><li>to snap off, to nip off</li></ol><p><i>откуси́ть</i> (otkusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take part in lunch, tea, etc.; to have a bite</li><li>to taste, to taste-test, to try (<i>food</i>)</li><li>to finish eating</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ку́шать</i> (kúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (an animal, bird, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>отлови́ть</i> (otlovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) postponement, deferment, procrastination</li></ol><p><i>отлага́ть</i> (otlagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to set aside</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to put off, to delay, to adjourn, to postpone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отълагати</i> (otŭlagati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъlagati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ). Compare it's neutral counterpart <i>откла́дывать</i> (otkládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to fine-tune</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to debug</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) debugging</li></ol><p><i>отла́дить</i> (otláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to fine-tune</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to debug</li></ol><p><i>отла́дить</i> (otláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to varnish, to lacquer</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лакирова́ть</i> (lakirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off (to separate a piece from a whole by breaking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (a body part) to fall asleep or go numb</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to lie (<i>for a specified amount of time, due to sickness</i>)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie (in a horizontal position) for some time</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to convalesce, to recover one's strength by lying</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to ripen (<i>as a result of lying for some time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отлежа́ть</i> (otležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take off, to unstick</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take off, to unstick</li></ol><p><i>отлепи́ть</i> (otlepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to fly off</li><li>to rebound, to bounce back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come off, to break off</li><li>to complete a flight</li><li>to fly <i>(for a certain period)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to fly off</li><li>to rebound, to bounce back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come off, to break off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebb, low tide</li><li>tinge, tint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour off, to pump out</li><li>to found, to cast</li><li>to flow back, to ebb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a leak, to make water</li><li>to be shot (<i>with a colour/color</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отли́ть</i> (otlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;dialectal,&#32;northwestern) to beat up, to thrash (someone)</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;rare) to suck off, to fellate (to give oral sex to) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour off, to pump out</li><li>to found, to cast</li><li>to flow back, to ebb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a leak, to make water</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, to tell (from) (to see someone or something clearly or distinctly)</li></ol><p><i>отличи́ть</i> (otličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to differ [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>] (not to have the same characteristics)</li><li>to be notable <i>(for)</i></li><li>to distinguish oneself, to excel</li><li>(<i>disapprovable</i>) to make an exhibition of oneself</li></ol><p><i>отлича́ть</i> (otličátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference (quality of being different)</li><li>distinction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>characteristic, distinctive, distinguishing</li></ol><p><i>отличи́ть</i> (otličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, to tell (from) (to see someone or something clearly or distinctly)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-личи́ть</i> (-ličítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to differ [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something’</small>] (not to have the same characteristics)</li><li>to be notable (<i>for</i>)</li><li>to distinguish oneself, to excel</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to make an exhibition of oneself</li></ol><p><i>отличи́ть</i> (otličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high achiever</li></ol><p>From <i>отличи́ть</i> (otličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) or <i>отли́чно</i> (otlíčno, “excellent”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very good, very well, excellently, perfectly</li><li>differently, in a different way</li></ol><p><i>отли́чный</i> (otlíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>different</li><li>excellent, perfect, first-class, first-rate, prime</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъličьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>отли́чие</i> (otlíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catching (of animals or birds)</li><li>catch (animal or bird caught)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отлови́ть</i> (otlovítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish catching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time catching</li><li>to catch (an animal, bird, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ). Compare Polish <i>odłowić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, gently sloping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deposit; sediment, sedimentation, precipitation, deposition</li></ol><p><i>отложи́ть</i> (otložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ablative</li></ol><p>From <i>отложи́ть</i> (otložítʹ, “to set aside”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective&ndash;forming suffix). Calque of Latin <i>ablātīvus</i> (“related to taking away”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ablative case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set aside</li><li>to lay by, to save (money for some purchase)</li><li>to put off, to delay, to adjourn, to postpone</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) to lay <i>(eggs, spawn etc.)</i></li><li>to fold/turn back</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to shelve, to postpone indefinitely</li></ol><p>Compare Dutch <i>op de lange baan schuiven</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off (to separate a piece from a whole by breaking)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off (to separate a piece from a whole by breaking)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in two</li><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p><i>отломи́ть</i> (otlomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrash, to flog, to spank, to beat, to batter</li><li>to beat, to drub, to clobber</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лупи́ть</i> (lupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to separate, to remove</li><li>to exclude, to excommunicate, to ostracize, to shun</li></ol><p><i>отлучи́ть</i> (otlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to go away</li></ol><p><i>отлуча́ть</i> (otlučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excommunication, exclusion, ostracization</li></ol><p><i>отлучи́ть</i> (otlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to separate, to remove</li><li>to exclude, to excommunicate, to ostracize, to shun</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ, “to unite (originally, to separate)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to go away</li></ol><p><i>отлучи́ть</i> (otlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence (state of being absent)</li></ol><p><i>отлучи́ть</i> (otlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (a body part) to fall asleep or go numb</li></ol><p><i>отлежа́ть</i> (otležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie (in a horizontal position) for some time</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to convalesce, to recover one's strength by lying</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to ripen (<i>as a result of lying for some time</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to lie in wait</li></ol><p><i>отлёживать</i> (otljóživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flying away, take-off, departure (e.g. of an airplane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) excuse, pretext</li></ol><p><i>отма́зать</i> (otmázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to intentionally not answer, to keep silent in response</li></ol><p><i>отмолча́ться</i> (otmolčátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signal with a flag</li><li>to finish waving</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover, to travel (a distance)</li><li>to wave away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>маха́ть</i> (maxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to wave away, to chase away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover, to travel (a distance)</li></ol><p><i>отмахну́ть</i> (otmaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>отмаха́ть</i> (otmaxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave/fan off/away</li><li>to wave away, to wave aside, to brush aside</li></ol><p><i>отма́хивать</i> (otmáxivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to wave away, to chase away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave/fan off/away</li><li>to wave away, to wave aside, to brush aside</li></ol><p><i>отмахну́ть</i> (otmaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave (<i>of the hand</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) go-ahead signal</li></ol><p><i>отмах(ну́ться)</i> (otmax(nútʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To separate, split apart with a border or demarcation.</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>между</i> (meždu) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissociate oneself, to isolate oneself (from)</li><li>to refuse to acknowledge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand bar, sand bank</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мель</i> (melʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancellation (act of cancelling)</li><li>abolishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abolish</li><li>to cancel, to countermand, to disaffirm, to reverse</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to repeal, to rescind, to abrogate, to revoke, to call off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent, superb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abolish</li><li>to cancel, to countermand, to disaffirm, to reverse</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to repeal, to rescind, to abrogate, to revoke, to call off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die (<i>of part of an organism or plant</i>)</li><li>to die out, to disappear</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure off, to mete out</li><li>to cover (a distance)</li></ol><p><i>отме́рить</i> (otméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure, to measure off, to mete out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cover (walk, drive, etc.) <i>a distance</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dole out, to give out (in small portions)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>отме́рить</i> (otméritʹ)</li><li>to measure off, to mete out</li><li>to cover (a distance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep aside</li><li>to brush aside, to reject, to give up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) revenge</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) repositioning</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>месть</i> (mestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep aside</li><li>to brush aside, to reject, to give up</li><li>to finish piling up (haystack, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>отмет-</i> (otmet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, sign</li><li>trace, imprint</li><li>different-colored spot on an animal</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) distinctive trace of someone's influence</li></ol><p><i>отме́тить</i> (otmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to note</li><li>to mark off, to make a note <i>(of)</i></li><li>to mention, to point to, to record</li><li>to celebrate, to mark by celebration, to commemorate, to observe (<i>anniversary, ceremony</i>)</li><li>to register <i>(out)</i>, to sign out</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check in, to sign in, to sign up, to register (<i>e.g. one's arrival or departure</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to stand out, to make a mark</li></ol><p><i>отме́тить</i> (otmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, tick</li><li>note, record</li><li>(<i>academic</i>) mark, grade</li><li>mark, level</li></ol><p>From <i>отме́тить</i> (otmétitʹ, “to mark, to note”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ, “to mark, to label”). See there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to note</li><li>to mark off, to make a note <i>(of)</i></li><li>to mention, to point to, to record</li><li>to celebrate, to mark by celebration, to commemorate, to observe (<i>anniversary, ceremony</i>)</li><li>to register <i>(out)</i>, to sign out</li></ol><p><i>отме́тить</i> (otmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check in, to sign in, to sign up, to register (<i>e.g. one's arrival or departure</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to stand out, to make a mark</li></ol><p><i>отмеча́ть</i> (otmečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die (<i>of part of an organism or plant</i>)</li><li>to die out, to disappear</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-мира́ть</i> (-mirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to intentionally not answer, to keep silent in response</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get frostbite on (a body part)</li></ol><p><i>отморо́зить</i> (otmorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get frostbite on (a body part)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) thug, scumbag, monster (a person utterly lacking moral scruples, a criminal capable of barbaric violence)</li></ol><p><i>отморо́зить</i> (otmorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to rough up, to bust one's ass</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мудо́хать</i> (mudóxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to strike, to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>муту́зить</i> (mutúzitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laundering (<i>of money etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отмыва́ть</i> (otmyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money laundering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash clean</li><li>to wash off, to wash away</li><li>to launder</li></ol><p><i>отмы́ть</i> (otmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clean due to washing</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wash off (<i>of paint, dirt, stains, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to be purified by washing</li></ol><p><i>отмыва́ть</i> (otmyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash clean</li><li>to wash off, to wash away</li><li>to launder</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clean due to washing</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wash off (<i>of paint, dirt, stains, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to be purified by washing</li></ol><p><i>отмы́ть</i> (otmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock pick (tool)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) key (<i>to understand something</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) unlocking, unlatching</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) unfastening</li></ol><p><i>отмыка́ть</i> (otmykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to refuse</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>не́кать</i> (nékatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascription, attribution, appropriation, reference</li><li>assignment, classification, categorization</li></ol><p><i>отнес-</i> (otnes-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to deliver</li><li>(of wind) to carry (off, away), to sweep (away)</li><li>to relate, to attribute, to include</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a specified way’</small>]</li><li>to express one's opinion [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to address officially [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отнести́</i> (otnestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (from)</li><li>to subtract; to take away; to abstract</li><li>to deduct, to take away, to take from, to take off</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) to take away (from); to bereave (of)</li><li>to withdraw; to remove (hands)</li><li>to rob (of), to deprive (of)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to amputate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>relatively (proportionally)</li><li>rather</li><li>concerning, regarding, with respect to, as regards [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>относи́тельный</i> (otnosítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) relative pronoun (pronoun that introduces a relative clause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relativity</li></ol><p><i>относи́тельный</i> (otnosítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relative</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) relative (as in relative clause)</li><li>comparative</li></ol><p><i>относи́ть</i> (otnosítʹ, “to refer”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective suffix), perhaps a calque of Latin <i>relātīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refer</li><li>to attribute, to ascribe (to associate ownership or authorship with)</li><li>to relegate</li><li>to take</li><li>to charge</li><li>to appropriate</li><li>to take to</li><li>to take back</li><li>to wear until worn off or until stopping to wear, to wear for a while</li><li>to carry, to bear for a while</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a specified way’</small>]</li><li>to express one's opinion [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to date from</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to concern, to relate, to apply (to be relevant) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to address officially [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out (<i>of clothing, shoes, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>относи́ть</i> (otnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attitude</li><li>treatment</li><li>relation, regard, respect, consideration</li><li>ratio</li><li>official letter</li></ol><p><i>относи́ть</i> (otnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>henceforth, henceforward, from now on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with&#32;не</i>) not at all, by no means</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) totally, completely</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отънѭдь</i> (otŭnjǫdĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (from)</li><li>to subtract; to take away; to abstract</li><li>to deduct, to take away, to take from, to take off</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) to take away (from); to bereave (of)</li><li>to withdraw; to remove (hands)</li><li>to rob (of), to deprive (of)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to amputate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>от</i> (ot): from, since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>от-</i> (ot-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to eat lunch; to have lunch</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>обе́дать</i> (obédatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to reflect</li><li>to depict, to represent, to reproduce</li><li>to display</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to project</li></ol><p><i>отобрази́ть</i> (otobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection; representation</li><li>imaging, display, echoing (of entered characters on a display)</li></ol><p><i>отобрази́ть</i> (otobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to reflect</li><li>to depict, to represent, to reproduce</li><li>to display</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to project</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, to select, to single out</li><li>to take away, to strip (of)</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) from all sides</li></ol><p><i>ото</i> (oto) +&lrm; <i>всю́ду</i> (vsjúdu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away</li><li>to try to get rid of, to suppress (a mental or physical state)</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbend, to straighten</li><li>to roll up or down (the edge of something, e.g. one's sleeves)</li></ol><p>From <i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ). Compare Polish <i>odgiąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move aside, to shift away</li><li>to postpone</li></ol><p><i>отодви́г-</i> (otodvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move aside, to draw/move back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be postponed</li></ol><p><i>отодвига́ть</i> (otodvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move aside, to shift away</li><li>to postpone</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move aside, to draw/move back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be postponed</li></ol><p><i>отодви́нуть</i> (otodvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear off, to strip off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a sound flogging</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) <i>(use sexually)</i> to bang, to screw</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to identify</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тождеств(о)</i> (toždestv(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identification</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;psychology) identification</li></ol><p><i>отождестви́ть</i> (otoždestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to identify</li></ol><p><i>отождестви́ть</i> (otoždestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>отождестви́ть</i> (otoždestvítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тожеств(о)</i> (tožestv(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>отождествля́ть</i> (otoždestvljátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>отожестви́ть</i> (otožestvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take aside</li><li>to recall, to withdraw, to call back</li><li>to revoke, to rescind</li><li>to retract, to withdraw</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to respond (to), to answer; to echo</li><li>to resound</li><li>to speak [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>], to give feedback</li></ol><p><i>отозва́ть</i> (otozvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to go aside, to move away, to move aside</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to withdraw, to fall back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be out for a while, to go away temporarily (with the intention to come back soon)</li><li><i>(отойти от те́мы)</i> to digress</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come around, to recover, to come back to normal (e.g. after a shock or disease)</li><li><i>(to change the owner)</i> to pass to</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revenge oneself, to avenge (to take vengeance for)</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>мстить</i> (mstitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heating</li></ol><p><i>отопи́ть</i> (otopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat (e.g. a building)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heating (a system that raises the temperature of a room or building)</li></ol><p><i>отопи́ть</i> (otopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) isolation, alienation, loneliness, lack of communication with others</li></ol><p><i>ото́рванный</i> (otórvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>isolated, separated, alienated (<i>from other people</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear/pull away</li><li>to tear from</li><li>to divert, to interrupt, to disturb</li><li>to separate</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come off; to tear off; to be torn off</li><li>(of an aircraft) to take off</li><li>to turn away, to tear oneself away (from work, a book. etc.)</li><li>to separate, to disengage (from pursuit)</li><li>to lose contact (with someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a break, to rest from daily routine</li></ol><p><i>оторва́ть</i> (otorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otorhinolaryngologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be perplexed/nonplussed, to be struck dumb</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о́торопь</i> (ótoropʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shock, startle, confusion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obtorpь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>clothing</i>) trim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, to send off, to dispatch</li><li>to send back</li><li>to refer <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъsъlati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>сла́ть</i> (slátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop sleeping</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sleep for a while</li></ol><p><i>ото-</i> (oto-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch up on sleep; to rest a lot after not having enough of sleep for a while</li></ol><p><i>отоспа́ть</i> (otospátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a falling away or falling off</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) dead plant matter still attached to the plant</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отпада́ть</i> (otpadátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall off, to fall away</li><li>to break away <i>(from)</i></li><li>to be dismissed, to fall away</li><li><i>(wish, desire)</i> to pass</li><li>to stop falling</li></ol><p><i>отпад-</i> (otpad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall off, to fall away</li><li>to break away <i>(from)</i></li><li>to be dismissed, to fall away</li><li><i>(wish, desire)</i> to pass</li><li>to become redundant, to fall out of use</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial/funeral service (in church), dirge</li></ol><p><i>отпева́ть</i> (otpevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform a funeral ritual for (someone)</li></ol><p><i>отпе́ть</i> (otpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlock</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to unlock, to become unlocked</li><li>to disavow, to deny [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отпере́ть</i> (otperétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to finish singing</li><li>to perform a funeral ritual for (someone)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to print, to type</li><li>to imprint</li><li>to unseal, to open up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to say brusquely/resolutely/emphatically</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imprinted, to leave tracks (<i>e.g. of one's feet in the snow</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be imprinted, to remain indelibly (<i>in the mind, soul, etc.</i>)</li><li>to manifest outwardly, to become evident, to be visible</li></ol><p><i>отпеча́тать</i> (otpečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imprint, impress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъpečatьkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>отпеча́тать</i> (otpečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to print, to type</li><li>to imprint</li><li>to unseal, to open up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to say brusquely/resolutely/emphatically</li></ol><p><i>отпеча́тать</i> (otpečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imprinted, to leave tracks (<i>e.g. of one's feet in the snow</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be imprinted, to remain indelibly (<i>in the mind, soul, etc.</i>)</li><li>to manifest outwardly, to become evident, to be visible</li></ol><p><i>отпеча́тывать</i> (otpečátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a sip <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>отпи́ть</i> (otpítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlock</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to unlock, to become unlocked</li><li>to disavow, to deny [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отпира́ть</i> (otpirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give the runaround, to reply beside the point (to)</li><li>to unsubscribe</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to write back</li></ol><p><i>отписа́ть</i> (otpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsubscription</li><li>formal response failing to address an issue or request</li></ol><p><i>отписа́ться</i> (otpisátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a sip <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пи́ть</i> (pítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove away, to shove off (e.g. a boat from the shore)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove aside</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>отпих(ну́ть)</i> (otpix(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove away, to shove off (e.g. a boat from the shore)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove aside</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пихну́ть</i> (pixnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay back, to recompense (with money)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to pay back, to exact revenge</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim away</li><li>to sail off</li></ol><p><i>отплы́ть</i> (otplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing off, departure from shore</li></ol><p><i>отплы́ть</i> (otplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim away</li><li>to sail off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dance with a lot of enthusiasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) answer, reply</li><li>retort, reproof, rebuff, rebuke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>odpowiedź</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish crawling</li></ol><p><i>отползти́</i> (otpolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>по́лзать</i> (pólzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to polish, to shine, to buff</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>полирова́ть</i> (polirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repulse, rebuff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bud, to gemmate, to propagate by gemmation</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>почкова́ться</i> (počkovátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bud, to gemmate, to propagate by gemmation</li></ol><p><i>отпочкова́ться</i> (otpočkovátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sender (someone who sends)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch, to forward</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, to set off (to leave)</li></ol><p><i>отпра́вить</i> (otprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sending off, forwarding, dispatch, shipping</li></ol><p><i>отпра́вить</i> (otprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure (the act of departing)</li></ol><p><i>отпра́вить</i> (otprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch, to forward</li><li>to exercise, to perform, to discharge</li></ol><p><i>отпра́вить</i> (otprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, to set off (to leave)</li></ol><p><i>отправля́ть</i> (otpravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initial</li></ol><p>From <i>отпра́вить</i> (otprávitʹ, “to send off, to dispatch”) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój). Compare <i>отправле́ние</i> (otpravlénije, “departure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to celebrate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пра́здновать</i> (prázdnovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask permission for an absence</li></ol><p><i>отпроси́ться</i> (otprosítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get permission for an absence</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ться</i> (prosítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;botany) offshoot</li><li>(animate,&#32;archaic,&#32;now&#32;ironic) offspring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recoil, to spring back, to start back</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пря́нуть</i> (prjánutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten off, to scare away</li></ol><p><i>отпуга́ть</i> (otpugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten off, to scare away</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пугну́ть</i> (pugnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leave, vacation, holiday, furlough</li><li>(business,&#32;uncountable) release, issue, delivery, distribution (<i>of goods to a customer or client</i>)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) tempering</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a prayer of absolution</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отпуска́ть</i> (otpuskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, to let off</li><li>to release, to set free</li><li>to dismiss, to give leave</li><li>to supply, to serve, to issue, to give out, to sell</li><li>to allot, to assign, to allow, to provide</li><li>to slacken, to relax, to turn loose, to loosen</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to remit, to forgive</li><li><i>(hair or beard)</i> to grow, to let grow</li><li><i>(metal)</i> to temper, to draw</li><li><i>(pain)</i> to lessen, to ease</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vacation</li><li>selling (<i>price</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о́тпуск</i> (ótpusk) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, to let off</li><li>to release, to set free</li><li>to dismiss, to give leave</li><li>to supply, to serve, to issue, to give out, to sell</li><li>to allot, to assign, to allow, to provide</li><li>to slacken, to relax, to turn loose, to loosen</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to remit, to forgive</li><li><i>(hair or beard)</i> to grow, to let grow</li><li><i>(metal)</i> to temper, to draw</li><li><i>(pain)</i> to lessen, to ease</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) release, setting free</li><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) remission (<i>of sins, guilt</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отпусти́ть</i> (otpustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work off (usually a debt)</li><li>to work (for a certain period of time)</li><li>to improve, to polish up (through testing by identifying and eliminating flaws)</li><li>to complete, to perfect (a certain task)</li></ol><p><i>отрабо́тать</i> (otrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wear out (<i>of tools or machine parts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отраба́тывать</i> (otrabátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exhaust</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) waste</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work off (usually a debt)</li><li>to work (for a certain period of time)</li><li>to improve, to polish up (through testing by identifying and eliminating flaws)</li><li>to complete, to perfect (a certain task)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wear out (<i>of tools or machine parts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отрабо́тать</i> (otrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working off, paying by work</li><li>development, fine-tuning</li><li>training, practicing</li></ol><p><i>отрабо́тать</i> (otrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison</li><li>bane</li><li>execrable/abominable/repulsive food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poisoner (person who poisons something or someone)</li></ol><p><i>отрави́ть</i> (otravítʹ, “to poison”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poison, to envenom</li><li>to spoil</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poison oneself</li><li>to get poisoned</li><li>to have food poisoning</li></ol><p><i>отрави́ть</i> (otravítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poisoning (administration of a poison)</li><li>intoxication (the state of being poisoned)</li></ol><p><i>отрави́ть</i> (otravítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poison, to envenom</li><li>to spoil</li></ol><p><i>отрави́ть</i> (otravítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poison oneself</li><li>to get poisoned</li><li>to have a food poisoning</li></ol><p><i>отравля́ть</i> (otravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delight, joy, comfort</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasing, pleasant, comforting</li></ol><p><i>отра́да</i> (otráda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repel, to ward off, to refute, to parry</li><li>to reflect, to mirror</li></ol><p><i>отрази́ть</i> (otrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reflected, to reverberate</li><li>to affect, to have an impact on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отража́ть</i> (otražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection, reverberation</li><li>repulse, parry, warding off</li></ol><p><i>отрази́ть</i> (otrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reflected (<i>of an image, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repel, to ward off, to refute, to parry</li><li>to reflect, to mirror</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъraziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reflected, to reverberate</li><li>to affect, to have an impact on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отрази́ть</i> (otrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рапортова́ть</i> (raportovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) branch, sector; sectoral</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) industry</li></ol><p><i>о́трасль</i> (ótraslʹ, “branch, sector, field”) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch, field (area in business or of knowledge, research)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otraslъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow to some amount (<i>of hair or nails</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отрасти́</i> (otrastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow to some amount (<i>of hair or nails</i>)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow out, to cause to grow (hair, nails, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grow out, to cause to grow (hair, nails, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>отрасти́ть</i> (otrastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to react, to respond [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>реаги́ровать</i> (reagírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(also plural,&#32;dated) scum, worthless remnants, waste, rabble, refuse</li><li>(also plural,&#32;derogatory) the dregs of society, morally debased or criminal elements</li><li>rags, tatters</li></ol><p>Associated with <i>тереби́ть</i> (terebítʹ). The presence of ⟨ре⟩ instead of ⟨ере⟩ is associated with the late loss of the pre-stressed vowel, or is associated with Old Novgorod or Old Pskov liquid metathesis.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust, to regulate, to tune up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>регули́ровать</i> (regulírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edit</li><li>to edit, to lead the publication of (<i>as an editor</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to edit</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>редакти́ровать</i> (redaktírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut</li><li>perforated line (<i>for tearing</i>)</li><li>length of material</li></ol><p>Deverbal noun from <i>отре́зать</i> (otrézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li><li>to snip off</li><li>to divide, to apportion</li><li><i>(speech)</i> to cut short, to snap out</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>отре́зать</i> (otrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sober <i>(up)</i>, to become/grow sober</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>трезве́ть</i> (trezvétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, section, segment</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) segment</li></ol><p><i>отре́зать</i> (otrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to cut off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disown, to disavow, to renounce, to give up, to repudiate</li><li>to abdicate</li></ol><p><i>отрек-</i> (otrek-) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquaint, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рекомендова́ть</i> (rekomendovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repair, to refit, to recondition</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ремонти́ровать</i> (remontírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehearse</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>репети́ровать</i> (repetírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also plural</i>) old clothes, rags, tatters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, to refurbish</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>реставри́ровать</i> (restavrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to review, to give feedback on</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рецензи́ровать</i> (recenzírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renunciation</li><li>abdication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disown, to disavow, to renounce, to give up, to repudiate</li><li>to abdicate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъreťi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove from office</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disconnect, to isolate</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to untie</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to liberate (<i>from</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отреши́ть</i> (otrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to abandon, to renounce, to keep away from [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отреша́ть</i> (otrešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove from office</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disconnect, to isolate</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to untie</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to liberate (<i>from</i>)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to abandon, to renounce, to keep away from [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отреши́ть</i> (otrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detachment (indifference)</li></ol><p><i>отрешённый</i> (otrešónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detached, alienated, indifferent (<i>in general, when referring to a person; not in the sense "cut off (from something)", which is the participial usage</i>)</li><li>indifferent (<i>look, face, etc.; expressing indifference</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to reject, to turn down</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ри́нуть</i> (rínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) rendering, enveloping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negation, denial</li></ol><p><i>отрица́ть</i> (otricátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>negatively</li><li>adversely</li></ol><p><i>отрица́тельный</i> (otricátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negative (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>отрица́ть</i> (otricátʹ, “to deny, negate”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny (to assert that something is not true)</li><li>to refute, to repudiate, to negate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>отърицати</i> (otŭricati). Compare the native Russian <i>отрека́ться</i>&nbsp;impf (otrekátʹsja, “disown, disavow; abdicate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spur (<i>of a mountain chain</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рог</i> (rog).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) never, for the whole life (since birth till present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) never, for the whole life (since birth till present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;ironic) boy, adolescent (<i>7–12 years old</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) junior retainer, soldier (<i>in a medieval prince's armed retinue</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otrokъ</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>īnfāns</i> (<i>in-</i> + <i>fāns</i>, p.a.p. of <i>for</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>process, outgrowth</li><li>appendage (an external body part that projects from the body)</li><li>sprout, sucker</li><li>slip</li><li>rame</li><li>ramification</li><li>spur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boyhood, adolescence</li></ol><p><i>о́трок</i> (ótrok) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) privately owned peasant land, located inside the village, a category which came into existence after the Stolypin reforms of 1906.</li><li>a place where a tree/branch is cut</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>руб</i> (rub), the stem of <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chop off, to cut off</li></ol><p><i>отруби́ть</i> (otrubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bran</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otrǫba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ, “to cut, hack, axe”) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i, “plural ending”).</p><p>See отруби́ть (otrubítʹ, verb), perfective form of руби́ть (rubítʹ, “to cut, hack, axe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chop off, to cut off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tearing off, breaking</li><li>breakaway</li><li>alienation; isolation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отрыва́ть</i> (otryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear/pull away</li><li>to tear from</li><li>to divert, to interrupt, to disturb</li><li>to separate</li><li>to unearth, to dig out</li><li>to disinter</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>отры́ть</i> (otrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come off; to tear off; to be torn off</li><li>(of an aircraft) to take off</li><li>to turn away, to tear oneself away (from work, a book. etc.)</li><li>to separate, to disengage (from pursuit)</li><li>to lose contact (with someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a break, to rest from daily routine</li></ol><p><i>отрыва́ть</i> (otryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brokenly, disconnectedly, shortly</li></ol><p><i>отры́вистый</i> (otrývistyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abrupt, jerky</li><li>disconnected (<i>e.g. thoughts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отрыва́ть</i> (otryvátʹ, “to tear away”) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment, extract, passage</li></ol><p><i>отрыва́ть</i> (otryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Compare parallel from the same root with another prefix: Polish <i>urywek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regurgitate, to throw up</li><li>to burp, to belch out</li></ol><p><i>отрыгну́ть</i> (otrygnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regurgitate, to throw up</li><li>to burp, to belch out</li><li>to burp once, to belch out once</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рыгну́ть</i> (rygnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burp, belch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) burping sound</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) burping smell</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) remnant (<i>of something obsolete</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отрыг(ну́ть)</i> (otryg(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unearth, to dig out, to excavate</li><li>to disinter</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) detachment, squadron, troop</li><li>group, team</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) order</li></ol><p>From <i>отряди́ть</i> (otrjadítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depute, to dispatch</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) group, team</li></ol><p><i>отря́д</i> (otrjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depute, to despatch</li></ol><p><i>отряди́ть</i> (otrjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, to shake down</li></ol><p><i>отряхну́ть</i> (otrjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to shake off, to get rid of, to eliminate [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘e.g. a negative feeling’</small>]</li><li>to spread one's feathers or fur by shaking the body (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отря́хивать</i> (otrjáxivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, to shake down</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тряхну́ть</i> (trjaxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to shake off, to get rid of, to eliminate [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘e.g. a negative feeling’</small>]</li><li>to spread one's feathers or fur by shaking the body (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отряхну́ть</i> (otrjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aspirate (to remove a liquid or gas by suction)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to suck off (to fellate a man until he ejaculates)</li></ol><p><i>отсоса́ть</i> (otsosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection, reflected light, glow, gleam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine <i>(with)</i>, to gleam <i>(with)</i></li><li>to be reflected</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stand/be in the light</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) one's own words inserted in some context, in someone else's speech</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) someone's baseless judgment or action</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>себя</i> (sebja) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sift out</li><li><i>(students)</i> to eliminate, to screen</li></ol><p><i>отсе́ять</i> (otséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compartment, module</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheel well <i>(airframe)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to chop off, to sever</li></ol><p><i>отсе́к-</i> (otsék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting off, severance</li></ol><p><i>отсе́к-</i> (otsék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) cutoff</li><li>(<i>font</i>) serif</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to chop off, to sever</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sift out</li><li><i>(students)</i> to eliminate, to screen</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay <i>(for)</i></li><li>to get numb <i>(by sitting)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ), similar to German absitzen</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide for a while, to take cover</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit it out, to not participate</li></ol><p><i>отсиде́ть</i> (otsidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) stint (<i>especially in jail or of inactivity</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отсиде́ть</i> (otsidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay <i>(for)</i></li><li>to get numb <i>(by sitting)</i></li></ol><p><i>отсиде́ть</i> (otsidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide for a while, to take cover</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit it out, to not participate</li></ol><p><i>отси́живать</i> (otsíživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gallop, to cover by galloping</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъskakati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump aside/away, to jump/leap back</li><li>to rebound, to recoil, to bounce off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break off, to come off, to be torn off</li></ol><p><i>отскок-</i> (otskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>отскака́ть</i> (otskakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump aside/away, to jump/leap back</li><li>to rebound, to recoil, to bounce off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break off, to come off, to be torn off</li><li>to achieve/get a positive result in the game (to win or to draw), due to ricochets, luck, happy accidents, etc., when the final result does not correspond to the logic, the course of the match, the alignment in the game — when a team won or drew, not because it played better.</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to monitor, to trace</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tracking, tracing, monitoring</li></ol><p><i>отсле́живать</i> (otsléživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to monitor, to trace</li></ol><p><i>отследи́ть</i> (otsledítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve out; to finish serving; to serve one's time (e.g. in the army)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be worn out (of clothes, shoes, vehicles, etc.)</li><li>to finish a service (church)</li></ol><p><i>отслужи́ть</i> (otslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve out; to finish serving; to serve one's time (e.g. in the army)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be worn out (of clothes, shoes, vehicles, etc.)</li><li>to finish a service (church)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to take a picture <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>снять</i> (snjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sort out, reject, cull</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сортирова́ть</i> (sortirovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sort out, reject, cull</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suction, suctioning, sucking</li><li>vacuum plunger, sucker</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) blowjob</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary layer bleed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aspirate (to remove a liquid or gas by suction)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to suck off (to fellate a man until he ejaculates)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to postpone, to delay, to defer, to adjourn, to respite</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extend</li></ol><p><i>отсро́чить</i> (otsróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to postpone, to delay, to defer, to adjourn, to respite</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to extend</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postponement, deferment, adjournment</li><li>prolongation, extension</li></ol><p><i>отсро́чить</i> (otsróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lagging, lag</li></ol><p><i>отстава́ть</i> (otstavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall behind, to lag (to be below average in performance)</li><li>to be slow (<i>of a clock</i>)</li><li>to be backward / behind; to be retarded</li><li>to come / fall off, to peel off</li><li>to let alone, to leave alone, to stop annoying</li></ol><p><i>отста́ть</i> (otstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set/put aside</li><li>to thrust out</li><li>to dismiss, to discharge</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) as you were!</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resignation, retirement</li></ol><p><i>отста́вить</i> (otstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set/put aside</li><li>to thrust out</li><li>to dismiss, to discharge</li></ol><p><i>отста́вить</i> (otstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>retired</li></ol><p><i>отста́в(ка)</i> (otstáv(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assertion</li></ol><p><i>отста́ивать</i> (otstáivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend, to protect, to hold against enemy attacks</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to maintain, to assert</li><li>to desilt</li><li>to stand (<i>on one's feet</i>) (somewhere) from beginning to end</li><li>to tire out (one's legs) from standing too long</li></ol><p><i>отстоя́ть</i> (otstojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backwardness (state of being backward)</li></ol><p><i>отста́лый</i> (otstályj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outdated, retrograde</li><li>backward, retarded (in terms of development of a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall behind, to lag (to be below average in performance)</li><li>to be slow (<i>of a clock</i>)</li><li>to be backward / behind; to be retarded</li><li>to come / fall off, to peel off</li><li>to let alone, to leave alone, to stop annoying</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbuckle, to unzip, to unbutton, to unfasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a kickback</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sedimentation</li><li>sediment, sludge</li><li>(figurative,&#32;slang) shlock, junk</li><li>(<i>transport</i>) vehicular storage, long-term parking</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отстоя́ть</i> (otstojátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sump</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long-term car park</li></ol><p><i>отсто́й</i> (otstój) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad, low-quality, tasteless, uninteresting, soggy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend, to protect, to hold against enemy attacks</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to maintain, to assert</li><li>to desilt</li><li>to stand (<i>on one's feet</i>) (somewhere) from beginning to end</li><li>to tire out (one's legs) from standing too long</li><li>to be away (<i>from</i>), to be distant (<i>from</i>), to be apart, to be located (a certain distance from)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, to complete the construction</li></ol><p><i>отстро́ить</i> (otstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish the building/construction of one's house</li><li>to rebuild</li><li><i>(city, buildings)</i> to be rebuilt</li></ol><p><i>отстра́ивать</i> (otstráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal, discharge, suspending, ban</li><li>defamiliarization</li></ol><p><i>отстрани́ть</i> (otstranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push aside, to remove</li><li>to discharge, to dismiss</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>стран-</i> (stran-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away <i>(from)</i>, to dodge</li><li>to keep away <i>(from)</i>, to keep aloof <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>отстрани́ть</i> (otstranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push aside, to remove</li><li>to discharge, to dismiss</li></ol><p><i>отстрани́ть</i> (otstranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away <i>(from)</i>, to dodge</li><li>to keep away <i>(from)</i>, to keep aloof <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>отстраня́ть</i> (otstranjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aloof, distantly (without sympathy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detachment, estrangement, alienation</li></ol><p><i>отстранённый</i> (otstranjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) killing by shooting</li><li>forcible separation, ejection, jettisoning</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отстреля́ть</i> (otstreljátʹ).</p><p>Deverbal from <i>отстрели́ть</i> (otstrelítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot off (a body part)</li><li>to cause to forcibly separate, to eject, to jettison</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) to kill by shooting</li><li>to shoot (a predetermined amount of ammunition)</li></ol><p><i>отстрели́ть</i> (otstrelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>отстреля́ть</i> (otstreljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> 